globalThis.CYDOC_DATA={"schemaVersion":1,"name":"Syrnck","version":"0.1.0","defaultLocale":"en","locales":[{"code":"en","label":"English"},{"code":"fr","label":"Français"}],"aliases":{"ARCHITECTURE.md":"architecture","ARCHIVE_RETENTION.md":"archive-retention","ASSET_LOCKS.md":"asset-locks","AUDIT.md":"audit","BACKUPS.md":"backups","CHECKOUT_RETENTION.md":"checkout-retention","COMPACTION.md":"compaction","EXPORT.md":"export","FILE_COMPARISON.md":"file-comparison","FILE_RANGES.md":"file-ranges","GIT_ASSETS.md":"git-assets","GIT_FILTER_PROCESS.md":"git-filter-process","GIT_LFS_HISTORY.md":"git-lfs-history","GIT_LFS_MIGRATION.md":"git-lfs-migration","GIT_RETENTION.md":"git-retention","INDEXES.md":"indexes","LOCALIZATION.md":"localization","MAINTENANCE.md":"maintenance","NATIVE_AMEND.md":"native-amend","NATIVE_ARCHIVES.md":"native-archives","NATIVE_BISECT.md":"native-bisect","NATIVE_BRANCHES.md":"native-branches","NATIVE_CHECKOUT.md":"native-checkout","NATIVE_COMPARISON.md":"native-comparison","NATIVE_FILE_HISTORY.md":"native-file-history","NATIVE_HARD_RESET.md":"native-hard-reset","NATIVE_HISTORY.md":"native-history","NATIVE_HUNKS.md":"native-hunks","NATIVE_LINES.md":"native-lines","NATIVE_MERGE.md":"native-merge","NATIVE_REBASE.md":"native-rebase","NATIVE_REBASE_TODO.md":"native-rebase-todo","NATIVE_REPLAY.md":"native-replay","NATIVE_RESET.md":"native-reset","NATIVE_RESOLUTIONS.md":"native-resolutions","NATIVE_SQUASH.md":"native-squash","NATIVE_STAGE_REBASE.md":"native-stage-rebase","NATIVE_STAGING.md":"native-staging","NATIVE_STASH.md":"native-stash","NATIVE_TAGS.md":"native-tags","NATIVE_WATCH.md":"native-watch","NATIVE_WORKSPACE.md":"native-workspace","NETWORK_COMPRESSION.md":"network-compression","OPERATIONS.md":"operations","PACKS.md":"packs","PARALLEL_TRANSFERS.md":"parallel-transfers","PROTOCOL.md":"protocol","REFERENCE_TABLES.md":"reference-tables","REPAIR_COPY.md":"repair-copy","SELECTIVE_RETENTION.md":"selective-retention","SERVER_ACCESS.md":"server-access","SERVER_READ_LEASES.md":"server-read-leases","SERVER_UPLOAD_LEASES.md":"server-upload-leases","SETTINGS.md":"settings","TEXT_DIFF.md":"text-diff","TEXT_MERGE.md":"text-merge","TEXT_PATCHES.md":"text-patches","TRANSFER_POLICY.md":"transfer-policy","TRANSFER_SESSIONS.md":"transfer-sessions","TRANSFERS.md":"transfers","UPLOAD_INVENTORIES.md":"upload-inventories","USAGE.md":"usage","WORKING_INDEX.md":"working-index","WORKSPACE_ABI.md":"workspace-abi","README.md":"overview","README.fr.md":"overview","THIRD_PARTY.md":"licenses","LICENSE":"licenses","PERFORMANCE.md":"performance","ROADMAP.md":"limits","CRASH_RECOVERY.md":"crash-recovery","MIGRATION_CPP.md":"compatibility","PUBLISHING.md":"licenses"},"pages":[{"id":"api-c-abi","title":"C ABI: ownership and errors","description":"C ABI: ownership and errors","section":"API","parent":"integrations","order":100,"kind":"api","symbol":"syrnck_store_open","source":"include/syrnck.h:19","revision":"3a43c7e65bba03865086e2ab8b537c7295dfd209c75e7d3ef8d664edcbafcade","locale":"en","html":"\u003cp>Include \u003ccode>&lt;syrnck.h&gt;\u003c/code> and use the matching \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> library; define SYRNCK_STATIC for static linking.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Handles and buffers\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-c-abi/ownership\" aria-label=\"Link to Handles and buffers\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Open with syrnck_store_open and close exactly once. Serialize calls on each handle, including close.\nWorkspace sessions retain no Store; open it only for queries. Paths and strings are UTF-8. Buffers belong to the caller unless a\nfunction explicitly allocates a string; release those strings with syrnck_string_free.\nIDs need 65 bytes including NUL. Size-query conventions differ between JSON strings and binary\nobject reads; follow the comments beside each declaration in the distributed header.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors\">Status and diagnostics\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-c-abi/errors\" aria-label=\"Link to Status and diagnostics\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>ABI 1 returns 0 on success, 1 on ordinary error, 2 on an unknown exception and 3 on cooperative\ncancellation for operation-enabled calls. No C++ object or exception crosses the boundary.\nCopy syrnck_last_error or use syrnck_error_copy on the same OS thread immediately after failure.\nDiagnostics are canonical English and limited to 4095 bytes. Do not free the thread-local pointer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"catalogs\">Prepare and publish catalogs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-c-abi/catalogs\" aria-label=\"Link to Prepare and publish catalogs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>syrnck_object_put creates an unpinned chunk or canonical native metadata object. C++ computes\nthe typed ID and rejects duplicate JSON fields. syrnck_ingest_buffer_ex (&lt;=16 MiB) and\nsyrnck_prepare_file_ex (streamed) prepare unpinned manifests without temporary plaintext.\nPublish a root before releasing the Store to GC; legacy ingest_file_ex still pins under ffi/ID.\nsyrnck_refs_json reads the typed reference table. syrnck_refs_compare_json compares a complete\ngroup of name/expected/value entries and verifies all replacement closures before one publication.\nA false comparison is not permission to retarget an old plan. On nonzero status the reset applied\noutput proves nothing: an IO error can follow atomic publication, so reload and establish its outcome.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/go-binding/catalog-transactions\">catalog transactions\u003c/a> for ownership and an example.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-actions\">Guarded native workspace actions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-c-abi/native-actions\" aria-label=\"Link to Guarded native workspace actions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>workspace_native_state_json_ex reads target, typed revision, materialization and checkout state.\nCapture and checkout require the exact SyrnckNativeExpected target/revision/policy object.\nCapture retains local working changes; plan is read-only; checkout_owned uses the existing native\njournal and refuses dirty work. recover_owned requires the exact persisted owner and candidate.\nCapture reports source reads/manifest reuse; session-only strong stamps never trust status indexes.\nFull object closure verification remains required; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance/incremental-capture\">capture\u003c/a>.\ncheckout_status_json_ex reads an exact core journal/receipt without applying it. Completed receipts\nprove past application with owner/source/target evidence; subsequent local edits remain separate.\nrecover_owned_preserving_json_ex explicitly archives regular-file edits and creates exclusive\nconflict copies before ordinary strict recovery. Default recover_owned stays strict.\nA session is bound to one Store path, registered context and working directory. Mutations\ninvalidate observation continuity; successful capture seeds its complete inventory instead.\nOn error reload state: publication may already have completed.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\">native actions\u003c/a> for recovery and locking contracts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Observation and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-c-abi/operations\" aria-label=\"Link to Observation and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Cancellation handles are one-shot and may be requested from another thread or progress callback.\nThe owning application must serialize destruction against users. A callback borrows its strings\nonly for its duration. Read \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">workspace sessions\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/go-binding\">Go\u003c/a>, and\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/file-ranges\">verified ranges\u003c/a> for complete API contracts and examples.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Include \u003csyrnck.h> and use the matching syrnck_ffi library; define SYRNCK_STATIC for static linking. Handles and buffers # Open with syrnck_store_open and close exactly once. Serialize calls on each handle, including close. Workspace sessions retain no Store; open it only for queries. Paths and strings are UTF-8. Buffers belong to the caller unless a function explicitly allocates a string; release those strings with syrnck_string_free. IDs need 65 bytes including NUL. Size-query conventions differ between JSON strings and binary object reads; follow the comments beside each declaration in the distributed header. Status and diagnostics # ABI 1 returns 0 on success, 1 on ordinary error, 2 on an unknown exception and 3 on cooperative cancellation for operation-enabled calls. No C++ object or exception crosses the boundary. Copy syrnck_last_error or use syrnck_error_copy on the same OS thread immediately after failure. Diagnostics are canonical English and limited to 4095 bytes. Do not free the thread-local pointer. Prepare and publish catalogs # syrnck_object_put creates an unpinned chunk or canonical native metadata object. C++ computes the typed ID and rejects duplicate JSON fields. syrnck_ingest_buffer_ex (\u003c=16 MiB) and syrnck_prepare_file_ex (streamed) prepare unpinned manifests without temporary plaintext. Publish a root before releasing the Store to GC; legacy ingest_file_ex still pins under ffi/ID. syrnck_refs_json reads the typed reference table. syrnck_refs_compare_json compares a complete group of name/expected/value entries and verifies all replacement closures before one publication. A false comparison is not permission to retarget an old plan. On nonzero status the reset applied output proves nothing: an IO error can follow atomic publication, so reload and establish its outcome. See catalog transactions for ownership and an example. Guarded native workspace actions # workspace_native_state_json_ex reads target, typed revision, materialization and checkout state. Capture and checkout require the exact SyrnckNativeExpected target/revision/policy object. Capture retains local working changes; plan is read-only; checkout_owned uses the existing native journal and refuses dirty work. recover_owned requires the exact persisted owner and candidate. Capture reports source reads/manifest reuse; session-only strong stamps never trust status indexes. Full object closure verification remains required; see capture . checkout_status_json_ex reads an exact core journal/receipt without applying it. Completed receipts prove past application with owner/source/target evidence; subsequent local edits remain separate. recover_owned_preserving_json_ex explicitly archives regular-file edits and creates exclusive conflict copies before ordinary strict recovery. Default recover_owned stays strict. A session is bound to one Store path, registered context and working directory. Mutations invalidate observation continuity; successful capture seeds its complete inventory instead. On error reload state: publication may already have completed. See native actions for recovery and locking contracts. Observation and cancellation # Cancellation handles are one-shot and may be requested from another thread or progress callback. The owning application must serialize destruction against users. A callback borrows its strings only for its duration. Read workspace sessions , Go , and verified ranges for complete API contracts and examples.","headings":[{"id":"ownership","text":"Handles and buffers","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Status and diagnostics","level":2},{"id":"catalogs","text":"Prepare and publish catalogs","level":2},{"id":"native-actions","text":"Guarded native workspace actions","level":2},{"id":"operations","text":"Observation and cancellation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/go-binding/catalog-transactions","#/en/performance/incremental-capture","#/en/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/go-binding","#/en/file-ranges"]},{"id":"api-store","title":"C++ Store API","description":"C++ Store API","section":"API","parent":"integrations","order":100,"kind":"api","symbol":"syrnck::Store","source":"include/syrnck/core.hpp:298","revision":"14995bd6ba7df6651831f0ff47edfe60fc672269d7f0087494b9371ca7e62b74","locale":"en","html":"\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>#include &lt;syrnck/core.hpp&gt;\u003c/code> and link \u003ccode>Syrnck::core\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Ownership and concurrency\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-store/ownership\" aria-label=\"Link to Ownership and concurrency\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store(root, key, create, options)\u003c/code> owns an exclusive repository lock. Keep the handle alive\nfor the complete operation and serialize all calls, including destruction. It is movable,\nnot copyable. Internal ingest workers do not make the handle safe for concurrent callers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objects\">Objects and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-store/objects\" aria-label=\"Link to Objects and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ingest(input, chunking)\u003c/code> returns a manifest ID and statistics; it does not publish a reference.\n\u003ccode>get\u003c/code> verifies an object&#39;s typed identity. \u003ccode>reconstruct\u003c/code> verifies chunks and the complete file.\n\u003ccode>set_ref\u003c/code> validates its complete closure before retention. \u003ccode>compare_update_refs\u003c/code> publishes\na group only when every expected value still matches; false means the caller must refresh.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/storage\">storage\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-ranges\">ranges\u003c/a>, and \u003ca href=\"#/en/reference-tables\">reference tables\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors\">Errors and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/api-store/errors\" aria-label=\"Link to Errors and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Catch \u003ccode>syrnck::Error\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>MissingObject\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Cancelled\u003c/code> as specialized errors.\nOperationOptions supplies a stop token and synchronous progress observer; do not reenter\nthe Store from callbacks. Follow each operation&#39;s publication boundary and retention rules.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">progress and cancellation\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/settings\">repository settings\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Use #include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> and link Syrnck::core . Ownership and concurrency # Store(root, key, create, options) owns an exclusive repository lock. Keep the handle alive for the complete operation and serialize all calls, including destruction. It is movable, not copyable. Internal ingest workers do not make the handle safe for concurrent callers. Objects and retention # ingest(input, chunking) returns a manifest ID and statistics; it does not publish a reference. get verifies an object's typed identity. reconstruct verifies chunks and the complete file. set_ref validates its complete closure before retention. compare_update_refs publishes a group only when every expected value still matches; false means the caller must refresh. See storage , ranges , and reference tables . Errors and cancellation # Catch syrnck::Error , with MissingObject and Cancelled as specialized errors. OperationOptions supplies a stop token and synchronous progress observer; do not reenter the Store from callbacks. Follow each operation's publication boundary and retention rules. See progress and cancellation and repository settings .","headings":[{"id":"ownership","text":"Ownership and concurrency","level":2},{"id":"objects","text":"Objects and retention","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Errors and cancellation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/storage","#/en/file-ranges","#/en/reference-tables","#/en/operations","#/en/settings"]},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture","description":"Architecture guide.","section":"Getting started","order":10,"revision":"4","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"architecture-de-syrnck\">Syrnck architecture\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/architecture-de-syrnck\" aria-label=\"Link to Syrnck architecture\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>One engine supports large assets, standalone revisions and block reuse for file synchronization. Read the workflow guides for exact contracts and the \u003ca href=\"#/en/limits\">limits\u003c/a> for unfinished work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objectif-et-decisions\">Goals and decisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/objectif-et-decisions\" aria-label=\"Link to Goals and decisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Git keeps ordinary code diff/merge/rebase/blame/tags/hooks/review. Standalone commands develop those workflows over shared immutable objects. C++20/CMake provide native libraries, CLI, C ABI and Go integration. The storage engine has no Git/HTTP dependency; adapters depend on it. RAII and operation ownership govern lifetimes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The static architecture diagram requires no interactive graph or 3 D dependency. \u003ca href=\"https://epicgames.github.io/lore/explanation/system-design/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lore’s design\u003c/a> also separates storage from revision control; this does not make formats compatible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"diagram\">\u003csvg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Syrnck\" viewBox=\"0 0 730 362\" width=\"730\" height=\"362\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\u003cdefs>\u003cmarker id=\"arrow-architecture-0\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\u003cpath d=\"M0 0 10 5 0 10Z\" style=\"fill:var(--accent,#278169);stroke:none\"/>\u003c/marker>\u003c/defs>\u003cpath d=\"M210 31L250 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M355 62L355 140\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M500 31L460 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M250 171L210 311\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M355 202L355 280\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M500 311L460 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003crect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"105\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Git / filters\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">CLI / native history\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"500\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"605\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">C / Go / BEP adapter\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"140\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">C++20 core\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"0\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"105\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">CAS / manifests / revisions\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Refs / retention\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"500\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"605\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">HTTP / block server\u003c/text>\u003c/svg>\u003cfigcaption>Syrnck\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003ch2 id=\"fichiers-et-revisions\">Files and revisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/fichiers-et-revisions\" aria-label=\"Link to Files and revisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Typed domain-separated BLAKE3 IDs identify chunks. Complete manifests contain total size, file hash, chunking policy and ordered full block lists. Sharing requires no parent relation. Revisions contain a complete sorted path→manifest/executable snapshot, parents, message and timestamp. Paged Merkle metadata remains future work, while every revision must remain complete.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Changing block 2 toX stores/transfers X plus metadata; reconstructing versionB does not replayA. Shared blocks remain live while B retains them even if A is retired. The engine does not parse Unreal objects. Compression/encryption or whole-asset rewrites can change most bytes and reduce deduplication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"politique-de-decoupage\">Chunking policy\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/politique-de-decoupage\" aria-label=\"Link to Chunking policy\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>FastCDC2020 defaults:256 KiB minimum,1 MiB target,4 MiB maximum; fixed sizes also supported. CDC can recover common boundaries after insertions/deletions; average is not guaranteed. File-type policy and real-corpus tuning remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00001-fast-cdc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lore’s FastCDC choice\u003c/a> is a reference. \u003ca href=\"https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/bep-v1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Syncthing BEP\u003c/a> uses fixed 128 KiB..16 MiB blocks except the tail. Fixed chunking alone cannot make Syrnck objects/network compatible: hashes, metadata, identities, versions and messages differ. The separate \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing\">BEP adapter\u003c/a> bridges regular file blocks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mode-git\">Git mode\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/mode-git\" aria-label=\"Link to Git mode\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Text remains ordinary Git blobs. Selected \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> assets pass required clean/smudge filters into Syrnck pointers/reconstructed bytes. Failure cannot masquerade as a hydrated asset. The pointer differs from \u003ca href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git LFS\u003c/a>; GitHub/GitLab pointer hosting does not provide Syrnck block service/previews/LFS compatibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Offline \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration\">snapshot LFS migration\u003c/a> verifies SHA-256 data and builds only changed trees under a retained complete catalog before prepared Git ref publication. HEAD/index/workspace stay unchanged; \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history\">full history migration\u003c/a> separately converts ancestry to new refs. Pending symbolic refs are checked under the Git lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Complete filtered manifests are pinned; canonical unhydrated pointers survive without incomplete pins for pointer-only clones. \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-retention\">Retention reconciliation\u003c/a> inventories refs/reflogs/stashes/all worktree indexes, verifies complete files and adds missing pins atomically without deleting unobserved pins. \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets\">Catalogs\u003c/a> bind a parentless revision to each commit, retained locally and immutable remotely. Git-net verifies catalogs/pointers around transfer. Opt-in pre-push sends complete ancestry before Git publication but is bypassable and not a distributed transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Checkout policies local/download/skip select hydration. Download uses pointer manifest, read lease and persistent sessions independently of HEAD, verifying size/blocks/complete hash before output. \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-filter-process\">Filter-process\u003c/a> shares Store/cache across sequential requests without delayed hydration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mode-autonome\">Standalone mode\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/mode-autonome\" aria-label=\"Link to Standalone mode\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Native history provides complete commits, frozen staging, branches/tags, first-parent log, new-directory export and archived history detachment. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-comparison\">Status/diff\u003c/a> inspect without importing. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-staging\">Staging\u003c/a> retains bytes/base; commit atomically moves head and consumes index, refusing stale bases.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/text-patches\">Text patches\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-merge\">three-way text merge\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge\">native merge\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase\">linear/interactive rebase\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay\">cherry-pick/revert\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stash\">stash\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-file-history\">blame/exact rename history\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-bisect\">bisect\u003c/a> exist with bounded policies. Merge-preserving rebase, structured authors and signatures remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">Workspace preflight\u003c/a> separates base/index/work and content/mode/representation, requires clean tracked files for checkout, and includes ignored paths for collisions. Links/protected or ambiguous portable paths fail. Clean case/file-directory changes use the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\">namespace executor\u003c/a>. Optional plan verification does not replace execution validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Recoverable checkout verifies source/target closures, retains a canonical-root-bound journal and prepares whole files before exclusive moves. Source backups survive until atomic branch/archive/target publication. Resume/abort is explicit; after publication only cleanup can resume. Workspace moves are sequential without external-editor lock; private bytes stay plaintext even with encrypted objects. GC/native writes block during active recovery; receipts/archives remain until explicit retirement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HEAD is a chunk at \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code>, binding directory/selection; absence means main. Named descriptors keep v1; \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">detached HEAD\u003c/a> uses v2 and private revision/index roots. Switch journalv2 (namespacev4), or v7 for detached targets, guards old/new HEAD and both tips and publishes selection with the committed journal, preserving tips. CLI defaults and \u003ccode>Store::native_target()\u003c/code> resolve the selection. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">Linked workspaces\u003c/a> scope HEAD/index/cache/journal and receipts under real \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/\u003c/code> roots, share objects/named branches, and enforce branch reservations. Linked receipts use v8 with recorded context and layout; backups retain portable selections rather than absolute paths. Static partial workspaces retain complete logical revisions/indexes and project physical files with a canonical policy. Their v9 checkout receipts freeze both policies; dynamic selections and workspace administration remain incomplete.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tags use target and optional annotation refs published atomically. Expected-tip deletion archives target/annotation/receipt and restore preserves archive. Pages bound JSON to 16 MiB. Selectors reject ambiguous branch/tag names. Signatures, server native branch policy and atomic multi-root transfer remain future work. Text storage may share chunks without exposing block-level differences in the user interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stockage-physique-et-packs\">Physical storage and packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/stockage-physique-et-packs\" aria-label=\"Link to Physical storage and packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Logicalv1 objects can be loose or in indexed immutable packs. Pack appends new loose-only data; compact regroups. Explicit maintenance selects physicalv2/v3, never opening alone. All outputs are written/readback-verified before atomic config publication and removal. GC preserves fully live packs, drops dead ones and rewrites partial survivors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Indexes use 256-record pages, validated fences/digests, negative-key filters and independent 4 MiB page LRU. Opening still verifies whole indexes and duplicate keys. Limits 1,048,576 entries/pack,1024 packs,4,194,304 total. GC/fsck stream packed marks; loose keys/stack/JSON still consume proportional memory. Public objects/closure materialize collections. Compaction merges pages and sorts loose keys only. Read sessions reuse at most eight LRU pack handles and close before publication/removal. Maintenance is exclusive; every envelope remains verified, including both copies in fsck. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs\">packs\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">indexes\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/audit\">audit\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/compaction\">compaction\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pipeline-d-ingestion\">Ingest pipeline\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/pipeline-d-ingestion\" aria-label=\"Link to Ingest pipeline\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Optional 2..64 workers encode new chunks; default is sequential. Coordinator owns CDC, hashes, deduplication, manifest order and atomic writes. Workers own reusable Zstd contexts and never mutate Store/refs. Raw byte budget and 2*workers pending-task cap provide backpressure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Raw inputs are released before encoded results return. Encoded buffers/read buffers/codecs/metadata add memory beyond that budget. Flat serialized manifests stop at 16 MiB during reading, which does not bound JSON allocations. First-occurrence block order determines publication; manifest waits for every task. Errors/cancellation stop and join workers; existing refs stay intact, already published orphan chunks may remain for retry/GC. Internal workers do not make a Store concurrently callable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-et-parcours-de-validation\">Cache and validation traversal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/cache-et-parcours-de-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Cache and validation traversal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Handle-local cache is never disk authority. Entries require authenticated decrypt, bounded decompress, ID/format validation and bind typed keys to exact envelope BLAKE3. Every later access rereads/hashes the envelope before reuse; changed bytes evict and fully validate, missing data stays missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Independent LRUs default 8192 stamps/64 MiB decoded bytes, excluding indexes/work buffers. Stamps can survive decoded eviction. Cache retains nothing through GC, never persists across open and clears decoded buffers before release. Authorized-process plaintext remains distinct from at-rest encryption.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Closure validation hashes each full file while traversing ordered blocks and records dependencies including parents. Shared blocks may be reread for distinct full-file hashes. Fsck streams all stored data; GC validates retained roots before deletion. Both bypass caches and restore policy even after failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conservation-et-suppression\">Retention and removal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/conservation-et-suppression\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention and removal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Branches, tags, indexes, archives, pins and received references retain transitive dependencies including parents. Exclusive Store locking prevents other writers during GC; missing/corrupt retained objects stop deletion. Three separate actions: remove a commit from a branch, release retention roots, physically collect unreachable objects. Detachment archives the old head first; use \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">backup-protected retirement\u003c/a> to release archives. Fully unretained collected versions cannot be reconstructed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">Transfer journals\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">upload leases\u003c/a> and read leases protect in-progress work with selective retention/inventories. Releases, verified backups and grace policies need deliberate administration; distributed GC cannot rely on one client’s roots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"synchronisation-et-serveur\">Synchronization and server\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/synchronisation-et-serveur\" aria-label=\"Link to Synchronization and server\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Agent compares typed IDs, asks what is missing, transfers verified objects and publishes only a complete closure with expected-value CAS. One Store server supports shared-token or \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">configured identities/permissions\u003c/a>, serializes storage and excludes offline GC/other Store processes while running. Logical objects are at most 16 MiB, missing batches 4096, server pool 16 threads. \u003ca href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers\">Parallel transfer\u003c/a> bounds requests/buffers while Store access stays coordinated.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Block transfer does not resolve concurrent edits. Resident causal versions, deletions, rename/conflict policy and multi-file publication remain separate work. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">Read leases\u003c/a> retain exact roots even after source-ref removal and renew independently, releasing after local completion. \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">Inventories\u003c/a> declare keys before presence checks for selective GC; old leases retain global protection. \u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">Receipt pruning\u003c/a> is explicit. Distributed GC remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C/Go expose object IO, closures, pins and workspace observation. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing\">BEP adapter\u003c/a> uses the upstream Syncthing library’s fixed SHA-256 file blocks without changing Syrnck storage; it is not a full daemon or CDC extension between Syncthing peers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chiffrement\">Encryption\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/chiffrement\" aria-label=\"Link to Encryption\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Objects are compressed then encrypted at rest with XChaCha20-Poly1305, random 256-bit key/192-bit nonce, typed ID authenticated as associated data. Manifests/revisions are encrypted; config, refs, IDs and stored sizes remain visible. Key is supplied through SYRNCK_KEY, not config. No password derivation, in-place rotation, recovery or OS vault. Process/environment owners are trusted. Backups can reencrypt to another key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">Network Zstd\u003c/a> level 1 negotiates logical frames, falling back to raw for poor gain/budget. Decoder validates before import; network counters/throttling include retries separately from successful logical bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Transfers expose decoded objects through HTTPS to a trusted server. End-to-end encryption would need opaque metadata, dedup/equality-leakage policy, rollback protection and device revocation. \u003ca href=\"https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/untrusted.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Syncthing untrusted encryption\u003c/a> binds blocks to file/offset, reducing correlation but preventing the desired cross-file/position deduplication if adopted unchanged. Its TLS certificate/device identity is distinct from Syrnck token/proxy transport.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"integrite-et-limites-actuelles\">Integrity and current limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/architecture/integrite-et-limites-actuelles\" aria-label=\"Link to Integrity and current limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Objects use temporary files and atomic publication; refs publish after dependency verification. Reconstruction checks every chunk/complete hash and refuses overwrite. Directory export prepares a complete tree and publishes exclusively to a new path; kill may leave staging. Checkout/switch in existing workspaces journal sequential moves, not a globally atomic tree.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Scanner excludes Git/Store/cache and ignore rules, refuses links/special files, requires portable UTF-8 paths and uses utf8proc normalization/casefold for collisions. ACLs,xattrs,hardlinks,empty directories are not stored. Unix executable bits are supported; Windows keeps snapshot metadata. Size/mtime checks around reads do not provide a filesystem snapshot: stabilize files during commit. Power-loss, network shares and writers bypassing locks need further validation. Original code is MIT; \u003ca href=\"#/en/licenses\">dependency notices\u003c/a> retain their own terms.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Syrnck architecture # One engine supports large assets, standalone revisions and block reuse for file synchronization. Read the workflow guides for exact contracts and the limits for unfinished work. Goals and decisions # Git keeps ordinary code diff/merge/rebase/blame/tags/hooks/review. Standalone commands develop those workflows over shared immutable objects. C++20/CMake provide native libraries, CLI, C ABI and Go integration. The storage engine has no Git/HTTP dependency; adapters depend on it. RAII and operation ownership govern lifetimes. The static architecture diagram requires no interactive graph or 3 D dependency. Lore’s design also separates storage from revision control; this does not make formats compatible. Git / filters CLI / native history C / Go / BEP adapter C++20 core CAS / manifests / revisions Refs / retention HTTP / block server Syrnck Files and revisions # Typed domain-separated BLAKE3 IDs identify chunks. Complete manifests contain total size, file hash, chunking policy and ordered full block lists. Sharing requires no parent relation. Revisions contain a complete sorted path→manifest/executable snapshot, parents, message and timestamp. Paged Merkle metadata remains future work, while every revision must remain complete. Changing block 2 toX stores/transfers X plus metadata; reconstructing versionB does not replayA. Shared blocks remain live while B retains them even if A is retired. The engine does not parse Unreal objects. Compression/encryption or whole-asset rewrites can change most bytes and reduce deduplication. text Copy Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4] Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4] Chunking policy # FastCDC2020 defaults:256 KiB minimum,1 MiB target,4 MiB maximum; fixed sizes also supported. CDC can recover common boundaries after insertions/deletions; average is not guaranteed. File-type policy and real-corpus tuning remain future work. Lore’s FastCDC choice is a reference. Syncthing BEP uses fixed 128 KiB..16 MiB blocks except the tail. Fixed chunking alone cannot make Syrnck objects/network compatible: hashes, metadata, identities, versions and messages differ. The separate BEP adapter bridges regular file blocks. Git mode # Text remains ordinary Git blobs. Selected .gitattributes assets pass required clean/smudge filters into Syrnck pointers/reconstructed bytes. Failure cannot masquerade as a hydrated asset. The pointer differs from Git LFS ; GitHub/GitLab pointer hosting does not provide Syrnck block service/previews/LFS compatibility. Offline snapshot LFS migration verifies SHA-256 data and builds only changed trees under a retained complete catalog before prepared Git ref publication. HEAD/index/workspace stay unchanged; full history migration separately converts ancestry to new refs. Pending symbolic refs are checked under the Git lock. Complete filtered manifests are pinned; canonical unhydrated pointers survive without incomplete pins for pointer-only clones. Retention reconciliation inventories refs/reflogs/stashes/all worktree indexes, verifies complete files and adds missing pins atomically without deleting unobserved pins. Catalogs bind a parentless revision to each commit, retained locally and immutable remotely. Git-net verifies catalogs/pointers around transfer. Opt-in pre-push sends complete ancestry before Git publication but is bypassable and not a distributed transaction. Checkout policies local/download/skip select hydration. Download uses pointer manifest, read lease and persistent sessions independently of HEAD, verifying size/blocks/complete hash before output. Filter-process shares Store/cache across sequential requests without delayed hydration. Standalone mode # Native history provides complete commits, frozen staging, branches/tags, first-parent log, new-directory export and archived history detachment. Status/diff inspect without importing. Staging retains bytes/base; commit atomically moves head and consumes index, refusing stale bases. Text patches , three-way text merge , native merge , linear/interactive rebase , cherry-pick/revert , stash , blame/exact rename history and bisect exist with bounded policies. Merge-preserving rebase, structured authors and signatures remain future work. Workspace preflight separates base/index/work and content/mode/representation, requires clean tracked files for checkout, and includes ignored paths for collisions. Links/protected or ambiguous portable paths fail. Clean case/file-directory changes use the namespace executor . Optional plan verification does not replace execution validation. Recoverable checkout verifies source/target closures, retains a canonical-root-bound journal and prepares whole files before exclusive moves. Source backups survive until atomic branch/archive/target publication. Resume/abort is explicit; after publication only cleanup can resume. Workspace moves are sequential without external-editor lock; private bytes stay plaintext even with encrypted objects. GC/native writes block during active recovery; receipts/archives remain until explicit retirement. HEAD is a chunk at workspace/HEAD , binding directory/selection; absence means main. Named descriptors keep v1; detached HEAD uses v2 and private revision/index roots. Switch journalv2 (namespacev4), or v7 for detached targets, guards old/new HEAD and both tips and publishes selection with the committed journal, preserving tips. CLI defaults and Store::native_target() resolve the selection. Linked workspaces scope HEAD/index/cache/journal and receipts under real workspaces/NAME/ roots, share objects/named branches, and enforce branch reservations. Linked receipts use v8 with recorded context and layout; backups retain portable selections rather than absolute paths. Static partial workspaces retain complete logical revisions/indexes and project physical files with a canonical policy. Their v9 checkout receipts freeze both policies; dynamic selections and workspace administration remain incomplete. Tags use target and optional annotation refs published atomically. Expected-tip deletion archives target/annotation/receipt and restore preserves archive. Pages bound JSON to 16 MiB. Selectors reject ambiguous branch/tag names. Signatures, server native branch policy and atomic multi-root transfer remain future work. Text storage may share chunks without exposing block-level differences in the user interface. Physical storage and packs # Logicalv1 objects can be loose or in indexed immutable packs. Pack appends new loose-only data; compact regroups. Explicit maintenance selects physicalv2/v3, never opening alone. All outputs are written/readback-verified before atomic config publication and removal. GC preserves fully live packs, drops dead ones and rewrites partial survivors. Indexes use 256-record pages, validated fences/digests, negative-key filters and independent 4 MiB page LRU. Opening still verifies whole indexes and duplicate keys. Limits 1,048,576 entries/pack,1024 packs,4,194,304 total. GC/fsck stream packed marks; loose keys/stack/JSON still consume proportional memory. Public objects/closure materialize collections. Compaction merges pages and sorts loose keys only. Read sessions reuse at most eight LRU pack handles and close before publication/removal. Maintenance is exclusive; every envelope remains verified, including both copies in fsck. See packs , indexes , audit , compaction . Ingest pipeline # Optional 2..64 workers encode new chunks; default is sequential. Coordinator owns CDC, hashes, deduplication, manifest order and atomic writes. Workers own reusable Zstd contexts and never mutate Store/refs. Raw byte budget and 2*workers pending-task cap provide backpressure. Raw inputs are released before encoded results return. Encoded buffers/read buffers/codecs/metadata add memory beyond that budget. Flat serialized manifests stop at 16 MiB during reading, which does not bound JSON allocations. First-occurrence block order determines publication; manifest waits for every task. Errors/cancellation stop and join workers; existing refs stay intact, already published orphan chunks may remain for retry/GC. Internal workers do not make a Store concurrently callable. Cache and validation traversal # Handle-local cache is never disk authority. Entries require authenticated decrypt, bounded decompress, ID/format validation and bind typed keys to exact envelope BLAKE3. Every later access rereads/hashes the envelope before reuse; changed bytes evict and fully validate, missing data stays missing. Independent LRUs default 8192 stamps/64 MiB decoded bytes, excluding indexes/work buffers. Stamps can survive decoded eviction. Cache retains nothing through GC, never persists across open and clears decoded buffers before release. Authorized-process plaintext remains distinct from at-rest encryption. Closure validation hashes each full file while traversing ordered blocks and records dependencies including parents. Shared blocks may be reread for distinct full-file hashes. Fsck streams all stored data; GC validates retained roots before deletion. Both bypass caches and restore policy even after failure. Retention and removal # Branches, tags, indexes, archives, pins and received references retain transitive dependencies including parents. Exclusive Store locking prevents other writers during GC; missing/corrupt retained objects stop deletion. Three separate actions: remove a commit from a branch, release retention roots, physically collect unreachable objects. Detachment archives the old head first; use backup-protected retirement to release archives. Fully unretained collected versions cannot be reconstructed. Transfer journals , upload leases and read leases protect in-progress work with selective retention/inventories. Releases, verified backups and grace policies need deliberate administration; distributed GC cannot rely on one client’s roots. Synchronization and server # Agent compares typed IDs, asks what is missing, transfers verified objects and publishes only a complete closure with expected-value CAS. One Store server supports shared-token or configured identities/permissions , serializes storage and excludes offline GC/other Store processes while running. Logical objects are at most 16 MiB, missing batches 4096, server pool 16 threads. Parallel transfer bounds requests/buffers while Store access stays coordinated. Block transfer does not resolve concurrent edits. Resident causal versions, deletions, rename/conflict policy and multi-file publication remain separate work. Read leases retain exact roots even after source-ref removal and renew independently, releasing after local completion. Inventories declare keys before presence checks for selective GC; old leases retain global protection. Receipt pruning is explicit. Distributed GC remains future work. C/Go expose object IO, closures, pins and workspace observation. The BEP adapter uses the upstream Syncthing library’s fixed SHA-256 file blocks without changing Syrnck storage; it is not a full daemon or CDC extension between Syncthing peers. Encryption # Objects are compressed then encrypted at rest with XChaCha20-Poly1305, random 256-bit key/192-bit nonce, typed ID authenticated as associated data. Manifests/revisions are encrypted; config, refs, IDs and stored sizes remain visible. Key is supplied through SYRNCK_KEY, not config. No password derivation, in-place rotation, recovery or OS vault. Process/environment owners are trusted. Backups can reencrypt to another key. Network Zstd level 1 negotiates logical frames, falling back to raw for poor gain/budget. Decoder validates before import; network counters/throttling include retries separately from successful logical bytes. Transfers expose decoded objects through HTTPS to a trusted server. End-to-end encryption would need opaque metadata, dedup/equality-leakage policy, rollback protection and device revocation. Syncthing untrusted encryption binds blocks to file/offset, reducing correlation but preventing the desired cross-file/position deduplication if adopted unchanged. Its TLS certificate/device identity is distinct from Syrnck token/proxy transport. Integrity and current limits # Objects use temporary files and atomic publication; refs publish after dependency verification. Reconstruction checks every chunk/complete hash and refuses overwrite. Directory export prepares a complete tree and publishes exclusively to a new path; kill may leave staging. Checkout/switch in existing workspaces journal sequential moves, not a globally atomic tree. Scanner excludes Git/Store/cache and ignore rules, refuses links/special files, requires portable UTF-8 paths and uses utf8proc normalization/casefold for collisions. ACLs,xattrs,hardlinks,empty directories are not stored. Unix executable bits are supported; Windows keeps snapshot metadata. Size/mtime checks around reads do not provide a filesystem snapshot: stabilize files during commit. Power-loss, network shares and writers bypassing locks need further validation. Original code is MIT; dependency notices retain their own terms. Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4] Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]","headings":[{"id":"architecture-de-syrnck","text":"Syrnck architecture","level":1},{"id":"objectif-et-decisions","text":"Goals and decisions","level":2},{"id":"fichiers-et-revisions","text":"Files and revisions","level":2},{"id":"politique-de-decoupage","text":"Chunking policy","level":2},{"id":"mode-git","text":"Git mode","level":2},{"id":"mode-autonome","text":"Standalone mode","level":2},{"id":"stockage-physique-et-packs","text":"Physical storage and packs","level":2},{"id":"pipeline-d-ingestion","text":"Ingest pipeline","level":2},{"id":"cache-et-parcours-de-validation","text":"Cache and validation traversal","level":2},{"id":"conservation-et-suppression","text":"Retention and removal","level":2},{"id":"synchronisation-et-serveur","text":"Synchronization and server","level":2},{"id":"chiffrement","text":"Encryption","level":2},{"id":"integrite-et-limites-actuelles","text":"Integrity and current limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4]\nVersion B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]","assets":[],"links":["#/en/limits","https://epicgames.github.io/lore/explanation/system-design/","https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00001-fast-cdc/","https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/bep-v1.html","#/en/syncthing","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md","#/en/git-lfs-migration","#/en/git-lfs-history","#/en/git-retention","#/en/git-assets","#/en/git-filter-process","#/en/native-comparison","#/en/native-staging","#/en/text-patches","#/en/text-merge","#/en/native-merge","#/en/native-rebase","#/en/native-replay","#/en/native-stash","#/en/native-file-history","#/en/native-bisect","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions","#/en/native-detached","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/packs","#/en/indexes","#/en/audit","#/en/compaction","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-access","#/en/parallel-transfers","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/selective-retention","#/en/syncthing","#/en/network-compression","https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/untrusted.html","#/en/licenses"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"archive-retention","title":"Retire native archives with a verified backup","description":"Retire native archives with a verified backup guide.","section":"Storage","order":20,"parent":"storage","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup\">Retire native archives with a verified backup\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/archive-retention/retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup\" aria-label=\"Link to Retire native archives with a verified backup\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native archives deliberately retain old revision closures after reset, rebase,\ncheckout, amend and index rewrites. Immutable \u003ccode>native-fetch/REVISION\u003c/code> roots also\nretain fetched history. GC keeps those versions until their roots are explicitly\nretired.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The C++ API in \u003ccode>syrnck/retention.hpp\u003c/code> and the \u003ccode>backup retire-plan\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>backup retire\u003c/code>\ncommands retire selected simple archive roots and complete branch/tag/workspace archive\ngroups in one reference transaction.\nA separate verified \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">backup\u003c/a> must reconstruct every selected\nrevision, including every parent revision and complete file payload.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --kind reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the exact \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> value from the read-only preview. The plan binds the complete\ncurrent reference table, sorted selected references and verified backup snapshot.\nAn intervening reference change or a different backup invalidates it. The apply\ncommand recomputes coverage and performs one compare-and-swap transaction.\nCancellation before publication leaves all selected roots in place. It does not\ndelete payloads or run GC; inspect a later GC preview separately.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Supported roots are the simple revision archives recognized by \u003ccode>native archives\u003c/code>:\n\u003ccode>reset\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reset-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>rebase-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stage-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>squash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>amend\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>fetch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>publish\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stash\u003c/code> and detached-history archives. Exact \u003ccode>native-fetch/REVISION\u003c/code> roots are\nalso supported. Select 1..1024 unique references. Heads, tags, indexes,\nand asset-lock roots are refused. Structured archives use whole-group selectors:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Selector\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Roots removed together\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/branches/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Descriptor, target revision and optional frozen index\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Descriptor, target revision and optional annotation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/workspaces/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Descriptor, HEAD, physical work and every original context root\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Discover IDs with \u003ccode>native branch-archives\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native tag-archives\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>native workspace-archives\u003c/code>; all accept\n\u003ccode>--after ID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--limit N\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. Inspect a particular archive before\nretiring it. A member such as \u003ccode>archive/branches/ID/index\u003c/code> or\n\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/target\u003c/code> cannot be retired independently. Incomplete groups,\nunexpected members, mismatched annotation bindings and invalid staging baselines\nare refused. The verified backup must contain the exact complete group under its\noriginal reference names; the same payload objects under other names do not\nprovide a recoverable archive recipe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Group rows add \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>roots\u003c/code> map to the preview&#39;s \u003ccode>references\u003c/code> array.\n\u003ccode>kind\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>branch-group\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tag-group\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>workspace-group\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> is the archived tip.\n\u003ccode>removed_references\u003c/code> counts the expanded roots, including descriptors and optional\nindex/annotation roots. The limit of 1..1,024 applies to unique selectors. Reports\nare bounded below 16 MiB including JSON escaping. Simple roots and groups can be\nmixed in one atomic transaction. Recreated branches/tags with the same original\nnames, current HEAD, working files and unrelated roots remain untouched.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The primary Store uses \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. The secondary backup uses\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code>, or no key if that variable is absent. The two Stores can\nhave independent envelope keys. The backup remains unchanged and exclusively\nlocked during verification and publication. Native checkout recovery must be\nfinished before retirement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Other local roots may still retain the selected revision after its archive is\nremoved. Retirement therefore does not promise immediate disk savings. When\nthe local version becomes unreachable and is later collected, its verified\nbackup remains the recovery source. Keep that backup under an appropriate\nretention policy; this operation does not prevent a future administrator from\ndeleting it. Restore that backup to a separate Store with \u003ccode>backup restore\u003c/code>, then\nuse \u003ccode>native branch-restore ID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native tag-restore ID\u003c/code> or\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/restore\">workspace archive restoration\u003c/a> there. Restoring directly\ninside a verified backup changes its references and invalidates its descriptor.\nAutomatic age/release policies and distributed GC remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/checkout-retention\">Completed checkout retirement\u003c/a> handles receipt/target\npairs and optional checkout-source archives together. It also supports retained\ntarget roots restored from a backup without local workspace receipts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Apply runs its final progress observer before revalidating the backup and exact\nplan. Final verification/publication uses cancellation without further callbacks,\nso observer mutations of source refs or backup evidence cannot authorize stale\nretirement.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Retire native archives with a verified backup # Native archives deliberately retain old revision closures after reset, rebase, checkout, amend and index rewrites. Immutable native-fetch/REVISION roots also retain fetched history. GC keeps those versions until their roots are explicitly retired. The C++ API in syrnck/retention.hpp and the backup retire-plan / backup retire commands retire selected simple archive roots and complete branch/tag/workspace archive groups in one reference transaction. A separate verified backup must reconstruct every selected revision, including every parent revision and complete file payload. text Copy syrnck native archives --kind reset syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck gc Use the exact plan value from the read-only preview. The plan binds the complete current reference table, sorted selected references and verified backup snapshot. An intervening reference change or a different backup invalidates it. The apply command recomputes coverage and performs one compare-and-swap transaction. Cancellation before publication leaves all selected roots in place. It does not delete payloads or run GC; inspect a later GC preview separately. Supported roots are the simple revision archives recognized by native archives : reset , reset-index , rebase-index , stage-index , squash , amend , checkout , fetch , publish , bisect , stash and detached-history archives. Exact native-fetch/REVISION roots are also supported. Select 1..1024 unique references. Heads, tags, indexes, and asset-lock roots are refused. Structured archives use whole-group selectors: Selector Roots removed together archive/branches/ID Descriptor, target revision and optional frozen index archive/tags/ID Descriptor, target revision and optional annotation archive/workspaces/ID Descriptor, HEAD, physical work and every original context root Discover IDs with native branch-archives , native tag-archives and native workspace-archives ; all accept --after ID , --limit N and --verify . Inspect a particular archive before retiring it. A member such as archive/branches/ID/index or archive/tags/ID/target cannot be retired independently. Incomplete groups, unexpected members, mismatched annotation bindings and invalid staging baselines are refused. The verified backup must contain the exact complete group under its original reference names; the same payload objects under other names do not provide a recoverable archive recipe. text Copy syrnck native branch-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID Group rows add name and a roots map to the preview's references array. kind is branch-group , tag-group or workspace-group ; revision is the archived tip. removed_references counts the expanded roots, including descriptors and optional index/annotation roots. The limit of 1..1,024 applies to unique selectors. Reports are bounded below 16 MiB including JSON escaping. Simple roots and groups can be mixed in one atomic transaction. Recreated branches/tags with the same original names, current HEAD, working files and unrelated roots remain untouched. The primary Store uses SYRNCK_KEY . The secondary backup uses SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY , or no key if that variable is absent. The two Stores can have independent envelope keys. The backup remains unchanged and exclusively locked during verification and publication. Native checkout recovery must be finished before retirement. Other local roots may still retain the selected revision after its archive is removed. Retirement therefore does not promise immediate disk savings. When the local version becomes unreachable and is later collected, its verified backup remains the recovery source. Keep that backup under an appropriate retention policy; this operation does not prevent a future administrator from deleting it. Restore that backup to a separate Store with backup restore , then use native branch-restore ID , native tag-restore ID or workspace archive restoration there. Restoring directly inside a verified backup changes its references and invalidates its descriptor. Automatic age/release policies and distributed GC remain future work. Completed checkout retirement handles receipt/target pairs and optional checkout-source archives together. It also supports retained target roots restored from a backup without local workspace receipts. Apply runs its final progress observer before revalidating the backup and exact plan. Final verification/publication uses cancellation without further callbacks, so observer mutations of source refs or backup evidence cannot authorize stale retirement. syrnck native archives --kind reset syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck gc syrnck native branch-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","headings":[{"id":"retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup","text":"Retire native archives with a verified backup","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native archives --kind reset\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck native branch-archives --verify\nsyrnck native tag-archives --verify\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","assets":[],"links":["#/en/backups","#/en/native-workspace-archives/restore","#/en/checkout-retention"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"asset-locks","title":"Repository-local asset leases","description":"Repository-local asset leases guide.","section":"Storage","order":30,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"repository-local-asset-leases\">Repository-local asset leases\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/asset-locks/repository-local-asset-leases\" aria-label=\"Link to Repository-local asset leases\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck::AssetLocks\u003c/code> provides an atomic lease table in an existing Store. The C++\nAPI and bilingual CLI support grouped acquisition, renewal, release and expiry\npruning. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">Server access\u003c/a> connects this table to authenticated\nHTTP identities, remote lock operations and branch-publication enforcement.\nThe local C++ caller supplies a trusted owner identifier. A fence is a\nstale-operation guard, not an authentication credential.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"acquire-and-inspect\">Acquire and inspect\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/asset-locks/acquire-and-inspect\" aria-label=\"Link to Acquire and inspect\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks list --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --lang fr locks list\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All paths in one acquisition publish together, or none do. This supports related\nUnreal asset files without partially reserving a group. Locks name exact\nrepository-relative paths; they do not lock directory subtrees or scan working\nfiles. Unicode normalization and case folding identify the same portable path.\nDuplicate portable aliases in a request are rejected. Every active lock blocks\nanother acquisition, including one requested by its existing owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An acquisition plan reads metadata only and stores no objects. A ready plan\nexits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON conflicts. The plan does not reserve\npaths or guarantee a future generation. Acquisition recomputes current state\nunder the Store lock. Other invalid commands emit a diagnostic without a partial\nsuccess result. JSON fields and reason codes remain English in both languages.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>locks list\u003c/code> includes owner, original path spelling, fence, acquisition/expiry\ntimestamps and an \u003ccode>active\u003c/code> flag. Expired records remain visible until replaced,\nreleased or pruned. Pages default to 100 entries, with a limit in 1..1000 and an\noptional \u003ccode>--after PATH\u003c/code> using the previous page&#39;s \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> value. Ordering uses\nportable folded paths.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"renew-release-and-prune\">Renew, release and prune\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/asset-locks/renew-release-and-prune\" aria-label=\"Link to Renew, release and prune\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck locks prune --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the fence returned by acquisition. Renew/release require the owner and every\nclaimed fence to match. A single mismatch rejects the complete batch. CLI batches\nuse one shared fence; C++ claims can carry a different fence for each path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Renewal preserves the acquisition time and fence, extends expiry without\nshortening it, and requires the entire group to remain active through publication.\nExpired locks require a new acquisition. Release accepts an expired matching\nclaim, but a reallocated path rejects the former owner&#39;s claim. Pruning previews\nexpired records by default; \u003ccode>--delete\u003c/code> removes the selected expired records in\none expected-reference transaction. Active records remain protected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each acquisition advances a Store-wide unsigned 64-bit fencing generation. A\nretained counter survives release, expiry pruning and GC, so normal API operations\nnever recycle an old generation. Exhaustion refuses new acquisition. Expired paths\ncan be acquired directly without a preceding prune; replacement receives the new\ngeneration. Manual changes to the internal roots bypass this component&#39;s contract.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Durations are in 1..86400 seconds. Production uses Unix wall-clock seconds; the\nservice calling the API must supply a trustworthy clock. Publication rechecks\nexpiry and refuses an observed backwards clock movement. A supplied C++ clock is\nan application dependency, not a client-controlled lease parameter; the CLI has\nno clock override.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-and-api\">Storage and API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/asset-locks/storage-and-api\" aria-label=\"Link to Storage and API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The table uses ordinary immutable chunk objects and one atomic reference update:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>asset-locks/clock\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retained fencing generation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>asset-locks/paths/BLAKE3_FOLDED_PATH\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current immutable lease record\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The record contains \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>owner\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>expires_at\u003c/code>. Readers validate its schema, canonical bytes, path identity,\ntimestamps and relationship to the fencing counter. Existing object encryption\nprotects stored records at rest. Packing and GC retain current table roots;\nobsolete unreferenced records can be collected. Lock records do not retain file\ncontents: branches, revisions, tags and pins retain the asset versions themselves.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The table holds at most 4096 records, including expired entries. Requests contain\n1..1024 paths; each path is at most 4096 UTF-8 bytes. Owner identifiers contain\n1..256 UTF-8 bytes without control characters. Existing repository path rules\napply. Applications must preserve the Store&#39;s exclusive lock and avoid callback\nreentry. Cancellation before reference publication preserves the visible table\nand can leave complete unreferenced metadata objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/asset_locks.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>AssetLocks \u003cspan class=\"token function\">locks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> acquired \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> locks\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">acquire\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> generation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> acquired\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"locks\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">uint64_t\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">locks\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">renew\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> generation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">                       \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> generation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3600\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Repository-local asset leases # syrnck::AssetLocks provides an atomic lease table in an existing Store. The C++ API and bilingual CLI support grouped acquisition, renewal, release and expiry pruning. Server access connects this table to authenticated HTTP identities, remote lock operations and branch-publication enforcement. The local C++ caller supplies a trusted owner identifier. A fence is a stale-operation guard, not an authentication credential. Acquire and inspect # text Copy syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --limit 100 syrnck --lang fr locks list All paths in one acquisition publish together, or none do. This supports related Unreal asset files without partially reserving a group. Locks name exact repository-relative paths; they do not lock directory subtrees or scan working files. Unicode normalization and case folding identify the same portable path. Duplicate portable aliases in a request are rejected. Every active lock blocks another acquisition, including one requested by its existing owner. An acquisition plan reads metadata only and stores no objects. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON conflicts. The plan does not reserve paths or guarantee a future generation. Acquisition recomputes current state under the Store lock. Other invalid commands emit a diagnostic without a partial success result. JSON fields and reason codes remain English in both languages. locks list includes owner, original path spelling, fence, acquisition/expiry timestamps and an active flag. Expired records remain visible until replaced, released or pruned. Pages default to 100 entries, with a limit in 1..1000 and an optional --after PATH using the previous page's next value. Ordering uses portable folded paths. Renew, release and prune # text Copy syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune syrnck locks prune --delete Use the fence returned by acquisition. Renew/release require the owner and every claimed fence to match. A single mismatch rejects the complete batch. CLI batches use one shared fence; C++ claims can carry a different fence for each path. Renewal preserves the acquisition time and fence, extends expiry without shortening it, and requires the entire group to remain active through publication. Expired locks require a new acquisition. Release accepts an expired matching claim, but a reallocated path rejects the former owner's claim. Pruning previews expired records by default; --delete removes the selected expired records in one expected-reference transaction. Active records remain protected. Each acquisition advances a Store-wide unsigned 64-bit fencing generation. A retained counter survives release, expiry pruning and GC, so normal API operations never recycle an old generation. Exhaustion refuses new acquisition. Expired paths can be acquired directly without a preceding prune; replacement receives the new generation. Manual changes to the internal roots bypass this component's contract. Durations are in 1..86400 seconds. Production uses Unix wall-clock seconds; the service calling the API must supply a trustworthy clock. Publication rechecks expiry and refuses an observed backwards clock movement. A supplied C++ clock is an application dependency, not a client-controlled lease parameter; the CLI has no clock override. Storage and API # The table uses ordinary immutable chunk objects and one atomic reference update: Reference Purpose asset-locks/clock Retained fencing generation asset-locks/paths/BLAKE3_FOLDED_PATH Current immutable lease record The record contains version , type , path , owner , fence , created_at and expires_at . Readers validate its schema, canonical bytes, path identity, timestamps and relationship to the fencing counter. Existing object encryption protects stored records at rest. Packing and GC retain current table roots; obsolete unreferenced records can be collected. Lock records do not retain file contents: branches, revisions, tags and pins retain the asset versions themselves. The table holds at most 4096 records, including expired entries. Requests contain 1..1024 paths; each path is at most 4096 UTF-8 bytes. Owner identifiers contain 1..256 UTF-8 bytes without control characters. Existing repository path rules apply. Applications must preserve the Store's exclusive lock and avoid callback reentry. Cancellation before reference publication preserves the visible table and can leave complete unreferenced metadata objects. cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp> syrnck :: AssetLocks locks ( store ) ; auto acquired = locks . acquire ( \"alice\" , { \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"Content/Map.ubulk\" } ) ; const auto generation = acquired [ \"locks\" ] [ 0 ] [ \"fence\" ] . get \u003c std :: uint64_t > ( ) ; locks . renew ( \"alice\" , { { \"Content/Map.uasset\" , generation } , { \"Content/Map.ubulk\" , generation } } , 3600 ) ; syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --limit 100 syrnck --lang fr locks list syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune syrnck locks prune --delete #include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp> syrnck::AssetLocks locks(store); auto acquired = locks.acquire(\"alice\", {\"Content/Map.uasset\", \"Content/Map.ubulk\"}); const auto generation = acquired[\"locks\"][0][\"fence\"].get\u003cstd::uint64_t>(); locks.renew(\"alice\", {{\"Content/Map.uasset\", generation}, {\"Content/Map.ubulk\", generation}}, 3600);","headings":[{"id":"repository-local-asset-leases","text":"Repository-local asset leases","level":1},{"id":"acquire-and-inspect","text":"Acquire and inspect","level":2},{"id":"renew-release-and-prune","text":"Renew, release and prune","level":2},{"id":"storage-and-api","text":"Storage and API","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks list --limit 100\nsyrnck --lang fr locks list\nsyrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks prune\nsyrnck locks prune --delete\n#include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp>\n\nsyrnck::AssetLocks locks(store);\nauto acquired = locks.acquire(\"alice\", {\"Content/Map.uasset\", \"Content/Map.ubulk\"});\nconst auto generation = acquired[\"locks\"][0][\"fence\"].get\u003cstd::uint64_t>();\nlocks.renew(\"alice\", {{\"Content/Map.uasset\", generation},\n                       {\"Content/Map.ubulk\", generation}}, 3600);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/server-access"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"audit","title":"Streaming integrity audits","description":"Streaming integrity audits guide.","section":"Storage","order":40,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"streaming-integrity-audits\">Streaming integrity audits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/audit/streaming-integrity-audits\" aria-label=\"Link to Streaming integrity audits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::fsck()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>syrnck fsck\u003c/code> verify every logical object in the active store,\nincluding unreachable revisions, manifests and chunks. This is a read-only audit;\nretention and deletion remain the responsibility of \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After a known interrupted GC, dead loose metadata can temporarily outlive its\ndead dependencies and fail this complete audit. Retained versions remain\nreconstructible; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\">resuming interrupted cleanup\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The audit reloads the configuration and validates the complete active pack\nindexes. It bypasses decoded data and validation caches, checks references, then\nenumerates revisions, manifests and chunks without constructing a complete\ninventory of IDs. Pack page fences select only pages intersecting the requested\nobject kind. A page can contain several kinds; each record is filtered while its\noriginal position in the pack is preserved.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Metadata comes before standalone chunks. A manifest&#39;s file hash is checked while\nreading its blocks in order, so enumeration does not first decode every chunk\nand then read those chunks again to hash its file. Shared blocks and repeated\npositions are still read wherever required by a file&#39;s complete hash. This does\nnot promise one read per object for every dependency graph.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Every read still validates the envelope, authentication when encrypted, object ID\nand object format. Missing dependencies and incorrect complete file hashes fail\nthe audit, including those found only in unreachable metadata. A logical object\npresent both loose and packed counts once, but both physical copies are checked.\nAn inactive pack is outside the active inventory and contributes no retention root.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-lifetime\">Memory and lifetime\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/audit/memory-and-lifetime\" aria-label=\"Link to Memory and lifetime\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Packed objects use one mark bit per index entry, rounded up separately for each\npack. For 1,048,576 entries in one pack, the useful bitmap is 131,072 bytes. The\npage directory, negative-key filter and configurable index page cache have their\nown costs, described in \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">paged indexes\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Objects absent from the pack catalog require a compact set of typed IDs, including\nall loose-only objects audited. References, the current dependency stack, decoded\nobjects, JSON and allocator overhead also use memory. Large individual manifests\nand revisions remain materialized. Total engine memory is not globally bounded\nindependently of project size.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each enumeration page owns its buffer, so nested lookups remain valid even when\nthe index page cache is disabled. Nested read sessions share bounded file handles;\ntheir outermost return or exception closes the handles. The ordinary decoded-cache\npolicy is restored after success or failure. The store retains its exclusive lock\nthroughout the operation. Public \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> still return complete\ncollections. Packing/compaction now use \u003ca href=\"#/en/compaction\">streaming plans\u003c/a>, while\ntheir loose-only input keys still need sorting.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>No storage, network, C ABI or logical object format changes are needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification-and-measurement\">Interpreting resource use\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/audit/verification-and-measurement\" aria-label=\"Link to Interpreting resource use\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For interpreting process memory and tuning cache budgets, see \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance\">performance and resource limits\u003c/a>. Synthetic benchmark fixtures do not establish throughput or capacity for an Unreal project. An integrity audit is read-only and checks actual stored bytes, independently of cache statistics.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"progress-and-cancellation\">Progress and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/audit/progress-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to Progress and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> accepts \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code> and the CLI supports \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> or\n\u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code>. Counters track decoded payload reads without materializing\nthe inventory. Interrupted audits close their read sessions and restore normal\ncache use; store contents and retention roots are unchanged. See the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">operation contract and limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Streaming integrity audits # Store::fsck() and syrnck fsck verify every logical object in the active store, including unreachable revisions, manifests and chunks. This is a read-only audit; retention and deletion remain the responsibility of gc . After a known interrupted GC, dead loose metadata can temporarily outlive its dead dependencies and fail this complete audit. Retained versions remain reconstructible; see resuming interrupted cleanup . The audit reloads the configuration and validates the complete active pack indexes. It bypasses decoded data and validation caches, checks references, then enumerates revisions, manifests and chunks without constructing a complete inventory of IDs. Pack page fences select only pages intersecting the requested object kind. A page can contain several kinds; each record is filtered while its original position in the pack is preserved. Metadata comes before standalone chunks. A manifest's file hash is checked while reading its blocks in order, so enumeration does not first decode every chunk and then read those chunks again to hash its file. Shared blocks and repeated positions are still read wherever required by a file's complete hash. This does not promise one read per object for every dependency graph. Every read still validates the envelope, authentication when encrypted, object ID and object format. Missing dependencies and incorrect complete file hashes fail the audit, including those found only in unreachable metadata. A logical object present both loose and packed counts once, but both physical copies are checked. An inactive pack is outside the active inventory and contributes no retention root. Memory and lifetime # Packed objects use one mark bit per index entry, rounded up separately for each pack. For 1,048,576 entries in one pack, the useful bitmap is 131,072 bytes. The page directory, negative-key filter and configurable index page cache have their own costs, described in paged indexes . Objects absent from the pack catalog require a compact set of typed IDs, including all loose-only objects audited. References, the current dependency stack, decoded objects, JSON and allocator overhead also use memory. Large individual manifests and revisions remain materialized. Total engine memory is not globally bounded independently of project size. Each enumeration page owns its buffer, so nested lookups remain valid even when the index page cache is disabled. Nested read sessions share bounded file handles; their outermost return or exception closes the handles. The ordinary decoded-cache policy is restored after success or failure. The store retains its exclusive lock throughout the operation. Public objects() and closure() still return complete collections. Packing/compaction now use streaming plans , while their loose-only input keys still need sorting. No storage, network, C ABI or logical object format changes are needed. Interpreting resource use # For interpreting process memory and tuning cache budgets, see performance and resource limits . Synthetic benchmark fixtures do not establish throughput or capacity for an Unreal project. An integrity audit is read-only and checks actual stored bytes, independently of cache statistics. Progress and cancellation # fsck accepts OperationOptions and the CLI supports --progress or --progress-json . Counters track decoded payload reads without materializing the inventory. Interrupted audits close their read sessions and restore normal cache use; store contents and retention roots are unchanged. See the operation contract and limits .","headings":[{"id":"streaming-integrity-audits","text":"Streaming integrity audits","level":1},{"id":"memory-and-lifetime","text":"Memory and lifetime","level":2},{"id":"verification-and-measurement","text":"Interpreting resource use","level":2},{"id":"progress-and-cancellation","text":"Progress and cancellation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection","#/en/indexes","#/en/compaction","#/en/performance","#/en/operations"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"backups","title":"Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair","description":"Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair guide.","section":"Storage","order":50,"parent":"storage","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair\">Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair\" aria-label=\"Link to Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The C++ API in \u003ccode>syrnck/backup.hpp\u003c/code> creates logical Store snapshots. A backup\ncontains every object reachable from its retained roots, including parent\nrevisions, archived heads, tags, staging snapshots, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stash\">stashes\u003c/a>\nand the asset-lock fencing\ncounter. Complete file hashes are verified before a destination becomes visible.\nThe source remains unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The destination must be a new directory with an existing parent, outside the\nsource Store. An existing destination is never replaced, even if empty. Create\nand restore write into a private sibling directory, flush it, then publish it\nwith an exclusive directory rename. Cancellation or an exception before that\nrename exposes no partial destination. A hard process termination can leave an\nunpublished \u003ccode>.syrnck-export-*\u003c/code> sibling; it is not a valid backup until published.\nRerunning the command creates a new private staging directory. Automatic\nretirement of those abandoned directories remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stop the server before opening its Store for backup. The exclusive Store lock\nprevents simultaneous normal writers. Active checkouts, incomplete transfer\nsessions and unexpired active read/upload leases block backup creation. Complete\nor explicitly retire those operations first. Asset leases and their fencing\ncounter are retained, with their original expiry times.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These are backups of retained storage, not filesystem images. Working files,\nuncommitted edits outside staging, ignored/untracked files, Git metadata,\nunreferenced objects, transfer journals and private checkout directories are\nnot copied. Absolute \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> bindings and completed checkout receipts\nare explicitly omitted; their retained source/target revision roots remain.\nRestore produces an ordinary independent Store. Export a chosen revision to\nrecover its files. Rebinding/recreating a working workspace is a separate step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-and-change-encryption-keys\">Verify and change encryption keys\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/verify-and-change-encryption-keys\" aria-label=\"Link to Verify and change encryption keys\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each backup has a canonical \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code> descriptor recording hashes of its\nreference table and settings, the object count, decoded byte count, creation\ntime and omitted references. \u003ccode>backup verify\u003c/code> checks this descriptor, all\nretained dependencies, every reconstructed file hash and the complete object\ninventory. Changing backup references/settings or adding an unreferenced object\ninvalidates verification. The descriptor is an integrity receipt, not a signed\nor externally authenticated root. Keep backups protected from modification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Primary Store encryption uses \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. For create/restore, optional\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> selects the destination key. If omitted, an encrypted source\nkeeps the current key; a plaintext source produces a plaintext destination.\nThe C++ API requires a destination key for encrypted sources and refuses a\nsilent plaintext downgrade. Object IDs remain stable when envelopes are\nre-encrypted. Copying to a new key does not revoke old backups or hide equality\nbetween matching object IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>To verify a backup, put its own key in \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>, because it is the primary\nStore for that command. To restore with another key, use the backup key in\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> and the new key in \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code>. Keys are 32 bytes encoded as\n64 hexadecimal characters. Store keys securely outside Git and backup paths.\nThe backup descriptor/reference table is not encrypted; object envelopes use\nthe existing at-rest encryption. This is not end-to-end encryption, a password\nKDF, an OS key vault or live distributed key rotation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Restoration fully verifies the backup before creating a destination. It imports\ndecoded objects into fresh envelopes, retains the reference table/settings and\nverifies the resulting Store. The restored Store does not keep \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code>,\nso normal future revisions do not leave a misleading immutable-backup receipt.\nKeep the original backup until restored revisions have been exported and checked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repair-missing-retained-dependencies\">Repair missing retained dependencies\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/repair-missing-retained-dependencies\" aria-label=\"Link to Repair missing retained dependencies\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">Archive retirement\u003c/a> uses a verified backup to remove\nselected simple archive/fetch roots and complete branch/tag archive groups through a bound preview and one reference\ntransaction. It does not run GC automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store damaged-store fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For an encrypted repair source, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> is its key. \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>\ncontinues to open the damaged destination. Repair does not assume both keys are\nequal; an unset secondary key means the repair source is plaintext.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The default command is a read-only plan with \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>corrupt\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>references_hash\u003c/code>. A blocked plan exits 1 with JSON. Applying a blocked plan\nfails without a partial repair. Other invalid commands emit a diagnostic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The backup is fully verified first. The planner traverses the destination&#39;s\nretained portable roots, reading local objects and using backup objects only\nwhere a dependency is absent. It verifies complete reconstructed file hashes\nthrough this combined view before any write. A backup can therefore repair an\nolder asset while newer complete local revisions remain untouched. It need not\ncontain those newer revisions. A required object absent from both Stores blocks\nthe operation before writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code> imports only missing verified objects. Each import publishes a complete\nenvelope atomically; cancellation/crash can leave a verified subset repaired.\nRerun the plan/apply operation to finish. Existing roots and objects are not\nremoved or replaced, and a completed repair is idempotent. Plan output is capped\nat 16 MiB. The traversal currently keeps object identities in memory, as the\nexisting closure API does; it is not a disk-backed inventory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Corrupt existing objects block the whole repair. They are preserved for\ninspection. This first repair API does not replace corrupt loose objects,\nrebuild lost/corrupt packs or repair a corrupt reference table/configuration.\nRestore into a new Store when existing storage cannot be safely repaired.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/repair-copy\">\u003ccode>backup repair-copy-plan\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>backup repair-copy\u003c/code>\u003c/a> can rebuild\nthe current retained graph in a new verified Store, recovering missing/corrupt\nobjects from an older backup while keeping intact newer source data. Neither\ninput&#39;s existing objects, pack files or references are replaced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-limits\">Validation and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/validation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Validation and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ch2 id=\"linked-workspaces\">Portable linked workspace snapshots\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/linked-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Link to Portable linked workspace snapshots\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Backups and repaired copies preserve each registered linked selection as portable\nmetadata plus a retained exact revision, including its frozen index. Absolute\nregistration/HEAD paths and checkout receipts are omitted. Feature-free backups\nwith these exclusions use marker v2; required-reader Stores use v3. Existing\nv1/v2 backups remain supported.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After restoring the Store inside a new primary directory, run\n\u003ccode>native workspace-restore NAME NEW_DIRECTORY [--branch NAME | --detach]\u003c/code>.\nThe default reuses the original named branch only if its tip still matches and\nno other workspace selects it. A changed/reserved branch requires another name\nor detached restoration. Prepared bytes are materialized when an index exists;\nHEAD remains the committed revision. Unprepared working edits are not backed up.\nAn interrupted complete export can be retried only if all exported files still\nmatch. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/restore\">workspace restoration\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reader-features\">Required-reader preservation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/backups/reader-features\" aria-label=\"Link to Required-reader preservation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A feature-gated Store produces backup marker v3, binding its nonempty\n\u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code> list to the backup configuration. Verification rejects a\nchanged or stripped requirement, including a marker downgraded to v1/v2 while\nthe Store still requires features. Legacy feature-free v1/v2 backups remain\nsupported. Restore and repaired copies preserve the source requirements while\nretaining their independently chosen encryption key and existing physical\nexport rules. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/protocol/required-store-features\">required-reader semantics\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair # The C++ API in syrnck/backup.hpp creates logical Store snapshots. A backup contains every object reachable from its retained roots, including parent revisions, archived heads, tags, staging snapshots, stashes and the asset-lock fencing counter. Complete file hashes are verified before a destination becomes visible. The source remains unchanged. text Copy syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files The destination must be a new directory with an existing parent, outside the source Store. An existing destination is never replaced, even if empty. Create and restore write into a private sibling directory, flush it, then publish it with an exclusive directory rename. Cancellation or an exception before that rename exposes no partial destination. A hard process termination can leave an unpublished .syrnck-export-* sibling; it is not a valid backup until published. Rerunning the command creates a new private staging directory. Automatic retirement of those abandoned directories remains future work. Stop the server before opening its Store for backup. The exclusive Store lock prevents simultaneous normal writers. Active checkouts, incomplete transfer sessions and unexpired active read/upload leases block backup creation. Complete or explicitly retire those operations first. Asset leases and their fencing counter are retained, with their original expiry times. These are backups of retained storage, not filesystem images. Working files, uncommitted edits outside staging, ignored/untracked files, Git metadata, unreferenced objects, transfer journals and private checkout directories are not copied. Absolute workspace/HEAD bindings and completed checkout receipts are explicitly omitted; their retained source/target revision roots remain. Restore produces an ordinary independent Store. Export a chosen revision to recover its files. Rebinding/recreating a working workspace is a separate step. Verify and change encryption keys # Each backup has a canonical backup.json descriptor recording hashes of its reference table and settings, the object count, decoded byte count, creation time and omitted references. backup verify checks this descriptor, all retained dependencies, every reconstructed file hash and the complete object inventory. Changing backup references/settings or adding an unreferenced object invalidates verification. The descriptor is an integrity receipt, not a signed or externally authenticated root. Keep backups protected from modification. Primary Store encryption uses SYRNCK_KEY . For create/restore, optional SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY selects the destination key. If omitted, an encrypted source keeps the current key; a plaintext source produces a plaintext destination. The C++ API requires a destination key for encrypted sources and refuses a silent plaintext downgrade. Object IDs remain stable when envelopes are re-encrypted. Copying to a new key does not revoke old backups or hide equality between matching object IDs. To verify a backup, put its own key in SYRNCK_KEY , because it is the primary Store for that command. To restore with another key, use the backup key in SYRNCK_KEY and the new key in SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY . Keys are 32 bytes encoded as 64 hexadecimal characters. Store keys securely outside Git and backup paths. The backup descriptor/reference table is not encrypted; object envelopes use the existing at-rest encryption. This is not end-to-end encryption, a password KDF, an OS key vault or live distributed key rotation. Restoration fully verifies the backup before creating a destination. It imports decoded objects into fresh envelopes, retains the reference table/settings and verifies the resulting Store. The restored Store does not keep backup.json , so normal future revisions do not leave a misleading immutable-backup receipt. Keep the original backup until restored revisions have been exported and checked. Repair missing retained dependencies # Archive retirement uses a verified backup to remove selected simple archive/fetch roots and complete branch/tag archive groups through a bound preview and one reference transaction. It does not run GC automatically. text Copy syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply syrnck --store damaged-store fsck For an encrypted repair source, SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY is its key. SYRNCK_KEY continues to open the damaged destination. Repair does not assume both keys are equal; an unset secondary key means the repair source is plaintext. The default command is a read-only plan with ready , missing , corrupt and references_hash . A blocked plan exits 1 with JSON. Applying a blocked plan fails without a partial repair. Other invalid commands emit a diagnostic. The backup is fully verified first. The planner traverses the destination's retained portable roots, reading local objects and using backup objects only where a dependency is absent. It verifies complete reconstructed file hashes through this combined view before any write. A backup can therefore repair an older asset while newer complete local revisions remain untouched. It need not contain those newer revisions. A required object absent from both Stores blocks the operation before writes. --apply imports only missing verified objects. Each import publishes a complete envelope atomically; cancellation/crash can leave a verified subset repaired. Rerun the plan/apply operation to finish. Existing roots and objects are not removed or replaced, and a completed repair is idempotent. Plan output is capped at 16 MiB. The traversal currently keeps object identities in memory, as the existing closure API does; it is not a disk-backed inventory. Corrupt existing objects block the whole repair. They are preserved for inspection. This first repair API does not replace corrupt loose objects, rebuild lost/corrupt packs or repair a corrupt reference table/configuration. Restore into a new Store when existing storage cannot be safely repaired. backup repair-copy-plan and backup repair-copy can rebuild the current retained graph in a new verified Store, recovering missing/corrupt objects from an older backup while keeping intact newer source data. Neither input's existing objects, pack files or references are replaced. Validation and limits # Portable linked workspace snapshots # Backups and repaired copies preserve each registered linked selection as portable metadata plus a retained exact revision, including its frozen index. Absolute registration/HEAD paths and checkout receipts are omitted. Feature-free backups with these exclusions use marker v2; required-reader Stores use v3. Existing v1/v2 backups remain supported. After restoring the Store inside a new primary directory, run native workspace-restore NAME NEW_DIRECTORY [--branch NAME | --detach] . The default reuses the original named branch only if its tip still matches and no other workspace selects it. A changed/reserved branch requires another name or detached restoration. Prepared bytes are materialized when an index exists; HEAD remains the committed revision. Unprepared working edits are not backed up. An interrupted complete export can be retried only if all exported files still match. See workspace restoration . Required-reader preservation # A feature-gated Store produces backup marker v3, binding its nonempty required_features list to the backup configuration. Verification rejects a changed or stripped requirement, including a marker downgraded to v1/v2 while the Store still requires features. Legacy feature-free v1/v2 backups remain supported. Restore and repaired copies preserve the source requirements while retaining their independently chosen encryption key and existing physical export rules. See required-reader semantics . syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply syrnck --store damaged-store fsck","headings":[{"id":"verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair","text":"Verified offline backups, restore and missing-object repair","level":1},{"id":"verify-and-change-encryption-keys","text":"Verify and change encryption keys","level":2},{"id":"repair-missing-retained-dependencies","text":"Repair missing retained dependencies","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-limits","text":"Validation and limits","level":2},{"id":"linked-workspaces","text":"Portable linked workspace snapshots","level":2},{"id":"reader-features","text":"Required-reader preservation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001\nsyrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify\nsyrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store\nsyrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck\nsyrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files\nsyrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001\nsyrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply\nsyrnck --store damaged-store fsck","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-stash","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/repair-copy","#/en/native-workspaces/restore","#/en/protocol/required-store-features"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"checkout-retention","title":"Completed checkout receipt retirement","description":"Completed checkout receipt retirement guide.","section":"Storage","order":60,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"completed-checkout-receipt-retirement\">Completed checkout receipt retirement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/checkout-retention/completed-checkout-receipt-retirement\" aria-label=\"Link to Completed checkout receipt retirement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Completed checkouts retain both an immutable receipt and a target revision root.\nTheir source archive independently protects the previous files. These roots also\ncover switch, hard reset and stash journals. They deliberately survive normal GC.\nThe API in \u003ccode>syrnck/checkout_retention.hpp\u003c/code> provides paginated inspection and\nexplicit, backup-fenced retirement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default removes \u003ccode>checkouts/receipts/ID\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>checkouts/completed/ID\u003c/code> together.\nAdd \u003ccode>--include-source-archives\u003c/code> to both commands to also retire that transaction&#39;s\n\u003ccode>archive/checkout/ID\u003c/code> root in the same reference transaction. No file bytes,\nworking files, branch heads, staging indexes or private recovery directories are\nremoved. Later GC remains an independent, explicit operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coverage-and-fences\">Coverage and fences\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/checkout-retention/coverage-and-fences\" aria-label=\"Link to Coverage and fences\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Select 1..1024 unique canonical transaction IDs. The plan binds the source Store\nlocation, complete reference table, selected receipt/target/source pairs, archive\noption and exact verified backup snapshot. A changed selection, source refs,\nbackup or option invalidates the plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The separate backup must pass full descriptor, inventory and file verification.\nIt must reconstruct every selected target, and each source archive being removed,\nwith all revision parents, manifests and payloads. Receipt metadata contains local\nworkspace paths and is intentionally omitted by backup; the portable revision\nroots remain covered. Such restored Stores expose entries with\n\u003ccode>retained_only: true\u003c/code>, which can also be retired with backup coverage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pending checkouts block inspection/retirement. Orphan pending pins, contradictory\nreceipt/target/source pairs, linked checkout directories and any remaining private\nrecovery data cause refusal. Retirement never cleans or adopts unknown files.\nFinish the existing checkout&#39;s normal recovery first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application runs its final progress observer before revalidating the complete\nbackup and exact plan. Final verification and the CAS use cancellation only,\nwithout further observer callbacks. A callback that changes source refs or the\nbackup therefore cannot retire roots using stale evidence. Cancellation before\npublication preserves all selected references. A process failure sees the entire\nold or new group; rerun inspection/planning after a lost command response.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The primary Store uses \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>; the backup uses \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code>. Keep the\nbackup and its key under an appropriate retention policy. Other pins, branches,\nstashes or archives may still retain the same versions, so retirement does not\npromise immediate disk savings. A later backup deletion remains an administrator&#39;s\nresponsibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"historical-receipts\">Historical receipts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/checkout-retention/historical-receipts\" aria-label=\"Link to Historical receipts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout-status --id ID\u003c/code> validates the immutable historical record without\nrequiring an old symbolic HEAD selection or an explicitly retired source snapshot\nto remain live. Those objects are required when \u003cstrong>actively recovering\u003c/strong> a journal,\nwhere their binding and snapshot validation still run. Historical receipt IDs and\nrecord hashes remain checked. If its source archive was retired and the source\nrevision was collected, status returns \u003ccode>files: null\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>files_available: false\u003c/code>\ninstead of inventing file actions. File reconstruction uses retained revision roots or\nthe verified backup; a receipt alone does not keep retired file bytes reachable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After receipt retirement, querying that receipt returns unknown transaction. The\nbackup retains its source/target files, while normal backup restore intentionally\ndoes not recreate local workspace receipts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-limits\">Validation and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/checkout-retention/validation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Validation and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Pages contain at most 1000 entries. The reference table and retirement reports\nretain their 16 MiB limits. Inspection can audit complete displayed closures with\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> without writing files. File payload verification uses the existing\nstreaming engine; the current graph traversal still keeps object identities in\nmemory.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Completed checkout receipt retirement # Completed checkouts retain both an immutable receipt and a target revision root. Their source archive independently protects the previous files. These roots also cover switch, hard reset and stash journals. They deliberately survive normal GC. The API in syrnck/checkout_retention.hpp provides paginated inspection and explicit, backup-fenced retirement. text Copy syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100 syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID The default removes checkouts/receipts/ID and checkouts/completed/ID together. Add --include-source-archives to both commands to also retire that transaction's archive/checkout/ID root in the same reference transaction. No file bytes, working files, branch heads, staging indexes or private recovery directories are removed. Later GC remains an independent, explicit operation. Coverage and fences # Select 1..1024 unique canonical transaction IDs. The plan binds the source Store location, complete reference table, selected receipt/target/source pairs, archive option and exact verified backup snapshot. A changed selection, source refs, backup or option invalidates the plan. The separate backup must pass full descriptor, inventory and file verification. It must reconstruct every selected target, and each source archive being removed, with all revision parents, manifests and payloads. Receipt metadata contains local workspace paths and is intentionally omitted by backup; the portable revision roots remain covered. Such restored Stores expose entries with retained_only: true , which can also be retired with backup coverage. Pending checkouts block inspection/retirement. Orphan pending pins, contradictory receipt/target/source pairs, linked checkout directories and any remaining private recovery data cause refusal. Retirement never cleans or adopts unknown files. Finish the existing checkout's normal recovery first. Application runs its final progress observer before revalidating the complete backup and exact plan. Final verification and the CAS use cancellation only, without further observer callbacks. A callback that changes source refs or the backup therefore cannot retire roots using stale evidence. Cancellation before publication preserves all selected references. A process failure sees the entire old or new group; rerun inspection/planning after a lost command response. The primary Store uses SYRNCK_KEY ; the backup uses SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY . Keep the backup and its key under an appropriate retention policy. Other pins, branches, stashes or archives may still retain the same versions, so retirement does not promise immediate disk savings. A later backup deletion remains an administrator's responsibility. Historical receipts # native checkout-status --id ID validates the immutable historical record without requiring an old symbolic HEAD selection or an explicitly retired source snapshot to remain live. Those objects are required when actively recovering a journal, where their binding and snapshot validation still run. Historical receipt IDs and record hashes remain checked. If its source archive was retired and the source revision was collected, status returns files: null and files_available: false instead of inventing file actions. File reconstruction uses retained revision roots or the verified backup; a receipt alone does not keep retired file bytes reachable. After receipt retirement, querying that receipt returns unknown transaction. The backup retains its source/target files, while normal backup restore intentionally does not recreate local workspace receipts. Validation and limits # Pages contain at most 1000 entries. The reference table and retirement reports retain their 16 MiB limits. Inspection can audit complete displayed closures with --verify without writing files. File payload verification uses the existing streaming engine; the current graph traversal still keeps object identities in memory. syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100 syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","headings":[{"id":"completed-checkout-receipt-retirement","text":"Completed checkout receipt retirement","level":1},{"id":"coverage-and-fences","text":"Coverage and fences","level":2},{"id":"historical-receipts","text":"Historical receipts","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-limits","text":"Validation and limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"compaction","title":"Streaming packing and compaction","description":"Streaming packing and compaction guide.","section":"Storage","order":70,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"streaming-packing-and-compaction\">Streaming packing and compaction\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compaction/streaming-packing-and-compaction\" aria-label=\"Link to Streaming packing and compaction\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck pack\u003c/code> appends packs for new loose-only objects. \u003ccode>syrnck pack --compact\u003c/code>\nregroups all active objects, including unreachable objects, into new packs. Use\n\u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> to remove unreachable objects according to references and pins. Compaction\npreserves complete manifests and every block needed to reconstruct any stored\nrevision; it does not introduce chains of binary deltas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both operations audit the store first, including complete file hashes and both\ncopies of loose/packed duplicates. They preserve each selected envelope exactly,\nwithout recompression or encryption-key changes. Reads prefer a loose copy when\nthe same object is also packed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordered-input-and-two-passes\">Ordered input and two passes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compaction/ordered-input-and-two-passes\" aria-label=\"Link to Ordered input and two passes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Packed IDs are already sorted within each immutable index. Compaction merges\nthese indexes with a heap containing one head per pack and one owned page of 256\nrecords per pack. Nested object lookups cannot invalidate the cursor&#39;s buffers,\neven with the index cache disabled. The cursor uses the existing bounded index\nhandle pool and closes its session on exhaustion, cancellation or failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only loose-only IDs are collected and sorted, as compact typed keys of 33 bytes\neach before vector capacity and allocator overhead. Packed duplicates do not add\nanother key. An incremental pack consumes this sorted loose sequence; compaction\nmerges it with the packed sequence. It does not build a complete packed-ID list.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The first pass checks strict input ordering, count, envelope lengths, object and\nbyte budgets, and output-pack capacity. It retains only a count, a byte size and\na BLAKE3 sequence digest for each output pack. Capacity or malformed-source\nfailures occur before payload reads or output creation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The second pass requests a fresh cursor and writes the planned packs in order.\nEach pack&#39;s digest binds the typed IDs and selected envelope lengths to the first\npass. Changed IDs, lengths or counts fail the batch. Source or codec errors also\nstop the operation. Previously completed files in a failed batch remain inactive\nuntil a later maintenance cleanup; they do not replace the active catalog.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Every new envelope is read back and verified by page enumeration before a single\natomic catalog publication. All old-catalog cursors and read sessions end before\npublication. A fresh loose-tree scan then removes copies whose keys are confirmed\nin the published catalog. Inactive generated packs are removed last. Publication\nand crash-recovery rules are unchanged; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs\">pack recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-remaining-limits\">Memory and remaining limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compaction/memory-and-remaining-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Memory and remaining limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>There are no complete packed-ID lists for planning, output partitions, readback\nor loose cleanup. The merged input retains at most 11,520 bytes of record data\nper active pack, or 11.25 MiB at the existing 1,024-pack limit. Heap entries,\ncursor metadata, catalog snapshots, index directories/filters, page caches,\nenvelopes, codecs and the initial integrity audit have additional costs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Loose-only ID sorting remains proportional to the number of loose-only objects.\nThe initial audit&#39;s loose marks and metadata also have proportional costs.\nPublic \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> still return complete collections. This is not\na global constant-memory guarantee for the engine, nor does it raise catalog\nlimits or provide concurrent online maintenance. The exclusive store lock covers\nthe operation. No on-disk format, object ID, CLI option or C ABI changes are needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tests-and-reproduction\">Planning maintenance\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compaction/tests-and-reproduction\" aria-label=\"Link to Planning maintenance\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compaction requires space for the complete new generation before old packs are removed. Use a verified backup and inspect the result before destructive collection. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs/publication-et-reprise\">pack publication and recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026\">Performance boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compaction/windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026\" aria-label=\"Link to Performance boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Performance depends on the object layout, codecs, storage and cache state. Streaming plans reduce packed-ID inventories; they do not guarantee higher throughput. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance\">resource budgets\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Streaming packing and compaction # syrnck pack appends packs for new loose-only objects. syrnck pack --compact regroups all active objects, including unreachable objects, into new packs. Use gc to remove unreachable objects according to references and pins. Compaction preserves complete manifests and every block needed to reconstruct any stored revision; it does not introduce chains of binary deltas. Both operations audit the store first, including complete file hashes and both copies of loose/packed duplicates. They preserve each selected envelope exactly, without recompression or encryption-key changes. Reads prefer a loose copy when the same object is also packed. Ordered input and two passes # Packed IDs are already sorted within each immutable index. Compaction merges these indexes with a heap containing one head per pack and one owned page of 256 records per pack. Nested object lookups cannot invalidate the cursor's buffers, even with the index cache disabled. The cursor uses the existing bounded index handle pool and closes its session on exhaustion, cancellation or failure. Only loose-only IDs are collected and sorted, as compact typed keys of 33 bytes each before vector capacity and allocator overhead. Packed duplicates do not add another key. An incremental pack consumes this sorted loose sequence; compaction merges it with the packed sequence. It does not build a complete packed-ID list. The first pass checks strict input ordering, count, envelope lengths, object and byte budgets, and output-pack capacity. It retains only a count, a byte size and a BLAKE3 sequence digest for each output pack. Capacity or malformed-source failures occur before payload reads or output creation. The second pass requests a fresh cursor and writes the planned packs in order. Each pack's digest binds the typed IDs and selected envelope lengths to the first pass. Changed IDs, lengths or counts fail the batch. Source or codec errors also stop the operation. Previously completed files in a failed batch remain inactive until a later maintenance cleanup; they do not replace the active catalog. Every new envelope is read back and verified by page enumeration before a single atomic catalog publication. All old-catalog cursors and read sessions end before publication. A fresh loose-tree scan then removes copies whose keys are confirmed in the published catalog. Inactive generated packs are removed last. Publication and crash-recovery rules are unchanged; see pack recovery . Memory and remaining limits # There are no complete packed-ID lists for planning, output partitions, readback or loose cleanup. The merged input retains at most 11,520 bytes of record data per active pack, or 11.25 MiB at the existing 1,024-pack limit. Heap entries, cursor metadata, catalog snapshots, index directories/filters, page caches, envelopes, codecs and the initial integrity audit have additional costs. Loose-only ID sorting remains proportional to the number of loose-only objects. The initial audit's loose marks and metadata also have proportional costs. Public objects() and closure() still return complete collections. This is not a global constant-memory guarantee for the engine, nor does it raise catalog limits or provide concurrent online maintenance. The exclusive store lock covers the operation. No on-disk format, object ID, CLI option or C ABI changes are needed. Planning maintenance # Compaction requires space for the complete new generation before old packs are removed. Use a verified backup and inspect the result before destructive collection. See pack publication and recovery . Performance boundaries # Performance depends on the object layout, codecs, storage and cache state. Streaming plans reduce packed-ID inventories; they do not guarantee higher throughput. See resource budgets .","headings":[{"id":"streaming-packing-and-compaction","text":"Streaming packing and compaction","level":1},{"id":"ordered-input-and-two-passes","text":"Ordered input and two passes","level":2},{"id":"memory-and-remaining-limits","text":"Memory and remaining limits","level":2},{"id":"tests-and-reproduction","text":"Planning maintenance","level":2},{"id":"windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026","text":"Performance boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/packs","#/en/packs/publication-et-reprise","#/en/performance"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"compatibility","title":"Compatibility and C++20 migration","description":"Compatibility and C++20 migration","section":"Getting started","order":20,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"formats\">Preserved formats\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compatibility/formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Preserved formats\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The engine, CLI, server, agent, Git filters and C ABI are C++20/CMake. Native C dependencies require no Rust compiler/runtime. Logical v1 typed BLAKE3 IDs, canonical manifests/revisions, FastCDC 2020 boundaries (fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0), SYR1/Zstd/XChaCha20-Poly1305 envelopes, physical v1 configuration/refs, Git pointers and HTTP v1 remain the compatibility baseline. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/protocol\">format reference\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>New physical pack formats require supporting readers; explicit packing may select v2/v3, opening alone never converts. V3 stays v3 even after reducing pack count. Paged index access preserves existing binary bytes/hashes and adds no disk fields. Back up before enabling features unsupported by old binaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adaptations\">Explicit differences and distribution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/compatibility/adaptations\" aria-label=\"Link to Explicit differences and distribution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Unicode normalization/casefold via utf8proc may reject equivalent names accepted by simpler lowercase checks. Ignore rules support nested glob/negation rules with documented scanner limits. C exceptions never cross the ABI: status 2 now means unknown C++ exception, and supported cancellable calls add status 3. ABI 1 gains symbols; pair the binding with matching rebuilt headers/library.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HTTPS uses libcurl with Schannel on Windows and OpenSSL 3 on Unix; redirects are disabled. The HTTP service is loopback-only. Binaries/libraries must be built for each OS/architecture. Static C++ libraries require their dependencies at final link. Define SYRNCK_STATIC for the static C ABI. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/integrations\">integrations\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/go-binding\">Go\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/licenses\">licenses\u003c/a> and the repository build instructions.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Preserved formats # The engine, CLI, server, agent, Git filters and C ABI are C++20/CMake. Native C dependencies require no Rust compiler/runtime. Logical v1 typed BLAKE3 IDs, canonical manifests/revisions, FastCDC 2020 boundaries (fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0), SYR1/Zstd/XChaCha20-Poly1305 envelopes, physical v1 configuration/refs, Git pointers and HTTP v1 remain the compatibility baseline. See format reference . New physical pack formats require supporting readers; explicit packing may select v2/v3, opening alone never converts. V3 stays v3 even after reducing pack count. Paged index access preserves existing binary bytes/hashes and adds no disk fields. Back up before enabling features unsupported by old binaries. Explicit differences and distribution # Unicode normalization/casefold via utf8proc may reject equivalent names accepted by simpler lowercase checks. Ignore rules support nested glob/negation rules with documented scanner limits. C exceptions never cross the ABI: status 2 now means unknown C++ exception, and supported cancellable calls add status 3. ABI 1 gains symbols; pair the binding with matching rebuilt headers/library. HTTPS uses libcurl with Schannel on Windows and OpenSSL 3 on Unix; redirects are disabled. The HTTP service is loopback-only. Binaries/libraries must be built for each OS/architecture. Static C++ libraries require their dependencies at final link. Define SYRNCK_STATIC for the static C ABI. See integrations , Go , licenses and the repository build instructions.","headings":[{"id":"formats","text":"Preserved formats","level":2},{"id":"adaptations","text":"Explicit differences and distribution","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/protocol","#/en/integrations","#/en/go-binding","#/en/licenses"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"crash-recovery","title":"Interruption and recovery","description":"Interruption and recovery","section":"Storage","order":80,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"before-recovery\">Inspect before recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/crash-recovery/before-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Inspect before recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Open the same Store with its original key and preserve evidence before retrying. Store opening never changes workspace files. For a pending checkout/switch/merge/replay/rebase/reset/stash operation, inspect \u003ccode>syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/code>, then choose \u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> to finish or \u003ccode>native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/code> to restore originals before publication. After publication only cleanup may finish. Do not edit private slots or internal roots. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">checkout states and limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Missing/corrupt retained data is an error. Use a verified \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">backup\u003c/a> or \u003ca href=\"#/en/repair-copy\">repair copy\u003c/a> for recoverable missing objects, preserving corrupted evidence; GC is not general repair.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interrupted-garbage-collection\">Interrupted garbage collection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\" aria-label=\"Link to Interrupted garbage collection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>After a known interrupted GC, dead loose metadata can outlive its dead packed dependencies. Retained closures remain protected, but fsck also audits unreachable metadata and may report those missing dependencies. With the same Store/key, preview \u003ccode>syrnck gc\u003c/code>, inspect retained roots, resume the already requested deletion with \u003ccode>syrnck gc --delete\u003c/code>, then run \u003ccode>syrnck fsck\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>GC revalidates all retained closures/file hashes before deleting; absent/corrupt retained data aborts. This procedure completes cleanup, never recovers versions whose roots were deliberately removed. Do not use it for an unexplained integrity error. Keep required pins/archives and verified backups first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-operations\">Other operations and temporary data\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/crash-recovery/other-operations\" aria-label=\"Link to Other operations and temporary data\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Retry interrupted packing with pack/compact after correcting the IO issue; active config selects the published generation. Ingestion can leave complete unreferenced chunks reusable on retry. Retry restoration only if the destination is absent; if already published, verify and use it without overwriting. \u003ca href=\"#/en/export\">Directory export\u003c/a> and backup publication can leave private sibling staging directories after a kill; they are not automatically retired.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stale \u003ccode>.tmp&lt;random-id&gt;\u003c/code> files do not enter object inventory or retain data. Automatic global temporary reclamation is not implemented. Inactive generated pack/index cleanup is a separate validated mechanism. Process-termination recovery does not establish power-loss, controller-cache, network-filesystem or hostile-writer guarantees.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Inspect before recovery # Open the same Store with its original key and preserve evidence before retrying. Store opening never changes workspace files. For a pending checkout/switch/merge/replay/rebase/reset/stash operation, inspect syrnck native checkout-status , then choose native checkout-recover to finish or native checkout-recover --abort to restore originals before publication. After publication only cleanup may finish. Do not edit private slots or internal roots. See checkout states and limits . Missing/corrupt retained data is an error. Use a verified backup or repair copy for recoverable missing objects, preserving corrupted evidence; GC is not general repair. Interrupted garbage collection # After a known interrupted GC, dead loose metadata can outlive its dead packed dependencies. Retained closures remain protected, but fsck also audits unreachable metadata and may report those missing dependencies. With the same Store/key, preview syrnck gc , inspect retained roots, resume the already requested deletion with syrnck gc --delete , then run syrnck fsck . GC revalidates all retained closures/file hashes before deleting; absent/corrupt retained data aborts. This procedure completes cleanup, never recovers versions whose roots were deliberately removed. Do not use it for an unexplained integrity error. Keep required pins/archives and verified backups first. Other operations and temporary data # Retry interrupted packing with pack/compact after correcting the IO issue; active config selects the published generation. Ingestion can leave complete unreferenced chunks reusable on retry. Retry restoration only if the destination is absent; if already published, verify and use it without overwriting. Directory export and backup publication can leave private sibling staging directories after a kill; they are not automatically retired. Stale .tmp\u003crandom-id> files do not enter object inventory or retain data. Automatic global temporary reclamation is not implemented. Inactive generated pack/index cleanup is a separate validated mechanism. Process-termination recovery does not establish power-loss, controller-cache, network-filesystem or hostile-writer guarantees.","headings":[{"id":"before-recovery","text":"Inspect before recovery","level":2},{"id":"interrupted-garbage-collection","text":"Interrupted garbage collection","level":2},{"id":"other-operations","text":"Other operations and temporary data","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-checkout","#/en/backups","#/en/repair-copy","#/en/export"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"export","title":"Transactional native exports","description":"Transactional native exports guide.","section":"Storage","order":80,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"transactional-native-exports\">Transactional native exports\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/export/transactional-native-exports\" aria-label=\"Link to Transactional native exports\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native export REVISION NEW_DIRECTORY\u003c/code> validates the revision and its complete\ndependency closure, reconstructs every file into a new sibling staging directory,\nand publishes the directory with one exclusive rename. The destination is absent\nuntil the complete tree is ready. Existing files, symlinks and directories,\nincluding an empty directory created concurrently, are never replaced.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In C++, \u003ccode>Store::export_revision(id, destination, OperationOptions)\u003c/code> accepts the\nsame stop token and synchronous observer used by reconstruction. \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code>\nevents cover the initial closure; \u003ccode>exporting\u003c/code> events report aggregate restored\nbytes and a known total for the current snapshot, with the current relative\nfile path when available. Empty files are included. A completed exporting event\nmeans preparation finished. The subsequent \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event is the last\ncancellable callback before the directory rename. Successful return/exit is the\npublication result. References and source objects are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The staging directory is named \u003ccode>.syrnck-export-&lt;32 lowercase hex digits&gt;\u003c/code> in the\ndestination&#39;s existing parent. This keeps staging and publication on the same\nfilesystem. Unix staging roots use owner-only permissions, retained by the\npublished root. Files contain their ordinary decoded bytes; executable metadata\nis restored on Unix.\nCancellation, callback exceptions and ordinary write/publication failures clean\nup only this invocation&#39;s staging directory. Output paths remain validated\nrevision paths, reconstructed from complete manifests.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The implementation uses exclusive rename primitives: Windows\n\u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-movefileexw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MoveFileExW without replacement\u003c/a>,\nLinux \u003ca href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">renameat 2 with RENAME_NOREPLACE\u003c/a>,\nand macOS \u003ca href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/urlresourcevalues/volumesupportsexclusiverenaming\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">renamex_np with RENAME_EXCL\u003c/a>.\nIf the OS or filesystem does not support exclusive directory rename, export\nfails and removes its staging directory. There is no fallback that could\noverwrite an empty destination. Files are individually flushed; Unix directory\nentries are flushed before publication and the parent is flushed afterwards.\nThe final rename is a commit point: cancellation arriving after it starts can\nbe too late. A final durability error can also leave a complete published tree.\nThese operations do not establish power-loss guarantees for every filesystem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Hard termination can leave a staging directory, while the destination is either\nabsent or complete. The normal reader does not automatically remove such\ndirectories: confirm ownership and whether their contents are still needed\nbefore removing them. Retry to a missing destination builds a fresh staging\ntree. No staged file is treated as stored revision data or as a retention root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reconstruction streams file blocks, but the initial public \u003ccode>closure\u003c/code> still\nmaterializes its object-key set, and revision/manifest JSON keeps the existing\nsize limits. This is a complete-directory export to a new location. For an existing working tree, use the separate \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">recoverable checkout\u003c/a> and conflict-resolution workflows.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Transactional native exports # native export REVISION NEW_DIRECTORY validates the revision and its complete dependency closure, reconstructs every file into a new sibling staging directory, and publishes the directory with one exclusive rename. The destination is absent until the complete tree is ready. Existing files, symlinks and directories, including an empty directory created concurrently, are never replaced. text Copy syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json In C++, Store::export_revision(id, destination, OperationOptions) accepts the same stop token and synchronous observer used by reconstruction. verifying events cover the initial closure; exporting events report aggregate restored bytes and a known total for the current snapshot, with the current relative file path when available. Empty files are included. A completed exporting event means preparation finished. The subsequent publishing event is the last cancellable callback before the directory rename. Successful return/exit is the publication result. References and source objects are unchanged. The staging directory is named .syrnck-export-\u003c32 lowercase hex digits> in the destination's existing parent. This keeps staging and publication on the same filesystem. Unix staging roots use owner-only permissions, retained by the published root. Files contain their ordinary decoded bytes; executable metadata is restored on Unix. Cancellation, callback exceptions and ordinary write/publication failures clean up only this invocation's staging directory. Output paths remain validated revision paths, reconstructed from complete manifests. The implementation uses exclusive rename primitives: Windows MoveFileExW without replacement , Linux renameat 2 with RENAME_NOREPLACE , and macOS renamex_np with RENAME_EXCL . If the OS or filesystem does not support exclusive directory rename, export fails and removes its staging directory. There is no fallback that could overwrite an empty destination. Files are individually flushed; Unix directory entries are flushed before publication and the parent is flushed afterwards. The final rename is a commit point: cancellation arriving after it starts can be too late. A final durability error can also leave a complete published tree. These operations do not establish power-loss guarantees for every filesystem. Hard termination can leave a staging directory, while the destination is either absent or complete. The normal reader does not automatically remove such directories: confirm ownership and whether their contents are still needed before removing them. Retry to a missing destination builds a fresh staging tree. No staged file is treated as stored revision data or as a retention root. Reconstruction streams file blocks, but the initial public closure still materializes its object-key set, and revision/manifest JSON keeps the existing size limits. This is a complete-directory export to a new location. For an existing working tree, use the separate recoverable checkout and conflict-resolution workflows. syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"transactional-native-exports","text":"Transactional native exports","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress\nsyrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-movefileexw","https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html","https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/urlresourcevalues/volumesupportsexclusiverenaming","#/en/native-checkout"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"file-comparison","title":"Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans","description":"Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans guide.","section":"Storage","order":90,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans\">Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/file-comparison/manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans\" aria-label=\"Link to Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::compare_files(source_manifest, target_manifest, operation)\u003c/code> compares\ntwo verified v1 manifests without reading their file payloads. Its ordered\nEnglish JSON has version 1, the source/target IDs and sizes, \u003ccode>same_content\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>reused_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>new_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unique_new_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blocks\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>needed_chunks\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--summary\u003c/code> removes the two block lists from stdout after planning; it reduces\noutput size, not planning memory. Progress stays on stderr, with English/French\ntext or stable JSON. No reference is changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The C ABI function \u003ccode>syrnck_file_compare_json\u003c/code> uses caller-owned output and size\nqueries, including the final NUL byte. Go&#39;s \u003ccode>CompareFiles\u003c/code> returns typed\n\u003ccode>FileComparison\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>FileBlock\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>NeededChunk\u003c/code> values. Go caps comparison JSON\nat 128 MiB before decoding. Query/read requires planning twice; no native output\npointer is retained. Rebuild consumers against this release&#39;s header/library.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each target block has its \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, target \u003ccode>offset\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>source_offset\u003c/code>.\nThe latter is the first matching chunk&#39;s byte offset in the source, or null\nwhen the source manifest has no matching chunk. Moved and repeated chunks are\nreusable. Their declared sizes must agree everywhere an ID appears.\n\u003ccode>needed_chunks\u003c/code> lists each absent chunk once, in first target occurrence order.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>reused_bytes + new_bytes\u003c/code> equals the target file size. \u003ccode>new_bytes\u003c/code> counts every\ntarget occurrence; \u003ccode>unique_new_bytes\u003c/code> counts distinct absent chunks once. These\nare logical plaintext bytes. They exclude manifests, protocol framing, encryption\nand compression, and do not measure network traffic. An already populated\ndestination Store may need fewer objects still.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>same_content\u003c/code> compares the declared size and complete file hash independently\nof chunk layout. Identical bytes can have different chunk boundaries and yield\nno chunk reuse. This planner does not search inside chunks or emit binary deltas.\nIt builds a reconstruction recipe without changing stored formats or references.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is a metadata plan: source and target chunks may be absent. A source byte\noffset is not proof that a mutable local file still has those bytes. Consumers\nmust verify copied/imported chunks against their IDs and verify the completed\nfile against its target manifest before publication. Existing verified object\nimport, reconstruction and reference publication provide those checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only one manifest is read when both IDs are equal, otherwise two. Metadata and\nthe emitted plan grow with their block counts, under the existing 16 MiB limit\nper input manifest. Lookup costs are O((source + target blocks) log(unique IDs));\nno file-size payload buffer is allocated. Manifest parsing still reads complete\nmetadata and has no persistent index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Progress uses \u003ccode>planning\u003c/code>; its counters represent processed block entries across\nboth manifests, rather than file bytes. Cancellation and callbacks follow the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">operation contract\u003c/a>. The operation is read only and does not\npin its input manifests; callers must maintain their intended retention roots.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans # Store::compare_files(source_manifest, target_manifest, operation) compares two verified v1 manifests without reading their file payloads. Its ordered English JSON has version 1, the source/target IDs and sizes, same_content , reused_bytes , new_bytes , unique_new_bytes , blocks and needed_chunks . text Copy syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary --summary removes the two block lists from stdout after planning; it reduces output size, not planning memory. Progress stays on stderr, with English/French text or stable JSON. No reference is changed. The C ABI function syrnck_file_compare_json uses caller-owned output and size queries, including the final NUL byte. Go's CompareFiles returns typed FileComparison , FileBlock and NeededChunk values. Go caps comparison JSON at 128 MiB before decoding. Query/read requires planning twice; no native output pointer is retained. Rebuild consumers against this release's header/library. Each target block has its id , target offset , size and source_offset . The latter is the first matching chunk's byte offset in the source, or null when the source manifest has no matching chunk. Moved and repeated chunks are reusable. Their declared sizes must agree everywhere an ID appears. needed_chunks lists each absent chunk once, in first target occurrence order. reused_bytes + new_bytes equals the target file size. new_bytes counts every target occurrence; unique_new_bytes counts distinct absent chunks once. These are logical plaintext bytes. They exclude manifests, protocol framing, encryption and compression, and do not measure network traffic. An already populated destination Store may need fewer objects still. same_content compares the declared size and complete file hash independently of chunk layout. Identical bytes can have different chunk boundaries and yield no chunk reuse. This planner does not search inside chunks or emit binary deltas. It builds a reconstruction recipe without changing stored formats or references. This is a metadata plan: source and target chunks may be absent. A source byte offset is not proof that a mutable local file still has those bytes. Consumers must verify copied/imported chunks against their IDs and verify the completed file against its target manifest before publication. Existing verified object import, reconstruction and reference publication provide those checks. Only one manifest is read when both IDs are equal, otherwise two. Metadata and the emitted plan grow with their block counts, under the existing 16 MiB limit per input manifest. Lookup costs are O((source + target blocks) log(unique IDs)); no file-size payload buffer is allocated. Manifest parsing still reads complete metadata and has no persistent index. Progress uses planning ; its counters represent processed block entries across both manifests, rather than file bytes. Cancellation and callbacks follow the operation contract . The operation is read only and does not pin its input manifests; callers must maintain their intended retention roots. syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary","headings":[{"id":"manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans","text":"Manifest comparison and chunk reuse plans","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json\nsyrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary","assets":[],"links":["#/en/operations"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"file-ranges","title":"Verified file ranges","description":"Verified file ranges guide.","section":"Storage","order":100,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"verified-file-ranges\">Verified file ranges\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/file-ranges/verified-file-ranges\" aria-label=\"Link to Verified file ranges\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The C++ Store can read a byte range from an existing file manifest without\nreconstructing the complete file:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reconstruct_range\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>manifest\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> output_stream\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> offset\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_file_range\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>manifest\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> offset\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The CLI supports the same exclusive export:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both options are required together. Omitting both retains ordinary full-file\nreconstruction. \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> keeps stdout empty and reports range-relative\ncounters on stderr.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The additive C ABI exports \u003ccode>syrnck_file_read_range\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>syrnck_restore_file_range\u003c/code>; use matching headers and libraries from this release.\nThe Go methods are \u003ccode>ReadRange\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>RestoreRange\u003c/code>. Buffered C/Go reads are capped\nat 16 MiB per call and stage bytes before copying them to the caller, so failure\nleaves the caller&#39;s output untouched. Streaming file exports have no such range\nlength cap. The existing per-chunk and manifest limits still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A C size query with \u003ccode>(NULL, 0)\u003c/code> validates metadata and bounds without reading\npayloads. A non-NULL buffer requests actual verification, even for a zero-length\nread of an empty file. On insufficient capacity the required length is returned;\nother failures reset it to zero. The Go wrapper requests verified bytes directly\nand allocates the requested output range; C adds an equally sized staging buffer.\nC buffered reads validate bounds before allocating, then load the manifest again\nduring reconstruction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Offsets and lengths are unsigned 64-bit byte counts. Both are exact: the range\nmust fit inside the declared file size, with no truncation or integer wraparound.\nA zero-length range is valid at any offset through EOF. The destination contains\nonly the requested bytes, starting at destination offset zero.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The manifest is read and validated, then only intersecting chunks are loaded.\nEach selected chunk is verified in full through the existing content-addressed\nobject reader, including authentication/decompression for encrypted objects.\nIts actual length must match the manifest before any of its bytes are emitted.\nUnrelated chunks may be absent; their availability and integrity are not audited.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A partial read cannot validate the complete file hash. A range covering the\nentire file, including an empty file, uses ordinary reconstruction and verifies\nthat hash as well. To verify every dependency and the whole file before use,\nretain the existing full reconstruction or closure verification operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stream output may contain a prefix if a later chunk, callback or output write\nfails. File output is prepared in a temporary sibling and published exclusively\nafter success. Existing destinations are preserved; cancellation, invalid ranges\nand corrupt selected chunks do not publish a destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Progress uses \u003ccode>reconstructing\u003c/code>, counting bytes relative to the requested range;\n\u003ccode>total_bytes\u003c/code> is its length. Cancellation and callback rules follow\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">the operation contract\u003c/a>. The Store&#39;s existing exclusive lock\nand caller serialization requirements still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This operation works with existing v1 manifests and loose, single-pack or\nmulti-pack storage. It parses the complete manifest and scans block metadata\nto locate the range; it does not introduce a paged manifest or a persistent\noffset index. C++ streaming payload memory is bounded by one selected decoded\nchunk, alongside manifest metadata, codec scratch and configured caches. At a chunk boundary,\neven a small range can require reading and decoding an entire chunk.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Verified file ranges # The C++ Store can read a byte range from an existing file manifest without reconstructing the complete file: cpp Copy store . reconstruct_range ( manifest , output_stream , offset , length , operation ) ; store . restore_file_range ( manifest , new_destination , offset , length , operation ) ; The CLI supports the same exclusive export: text Copy syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress Both options are required together. Omitting both retains ordinary full-file reconstruction. --progress-json keeps stdout empty and reports range-relative counters on stderr. The additive C ABI exports syrnck_file_read_range and syrnck_restore_file_range ; use matching headers and libraries from this release. The Go methods are ReadRange and RestoreRange . Buffered C/Go reads are capped at 16 MiB per call and stage bytes before copying them to the caller, so failure leaves the caller's output untouched. Streaming file exports have no such range length cap. The existing per-chunk and manifest limits still apply. A C size query with (NULL, 0) validates metadata and bounds without reading payloads. A non-NULL buffer requests actual verification, even for a zero-length read of an empty file. On insufficient capacity the required length is returned; other failures reset it to zero. The Go wrapper requests verified bytes directly and allocates the requested output range; C adds an equally sized staging buffer. C buffered reads validate bounds before allocating, then load the manifest again during reconstruction. Offsets and lengths are unsigned 64-bit byte counts. Both are exact: the range must fit inside the declared file size, with no truncation or integer wraparound. A zero-length range is valid at any offset through EOF. The destination contains only the requested bytes, starting at destination offset zero. The manifest is read and validated, then only intersecting chunks are loaded. Each selected chunk is verified in full through the existing content-addressed object reader, including authentication/decompression for encrypted objects. Its actual length must match the manifest before any of its bytes are emitted. Unrelated chunks may be absent; their availability and integrity are not audited. A partial read cannot validate the complete file hash. A range covering the entire file, including an empty file, uses ordinary reconstruction and verifies that hash as well. To verify every dependency and the whole file before use, retain the existing full reconstruction or closure verification operation. Stream output may contain a prefix if a later chunk, callback or output write fails. File output is prepared in a temporary sibling and published exclusively after success. Existing destinations are preserved; cancellation, invalid ranges and corrupt selected chunks do not publish a destination. Progress uses reconstructing , counting bytes relative to the requested range; total_bytes is its length. Cancellation and callback rules follow the operation contract . The Store's existing exclusive lock and caller serialization requirements still apply. This operation works with existing v1 manifests and loose, single-pack or multi-pack storage. It parses the complete manifest and scans block metadata to locate the range; it does not introduce a paged manifest or a persistent offset index. C++ streaming payload memory is bounded by one selected decoded chunk, alongside manifest metadata, codec scratch and configured caches. At a chunk boundary, even a small range can require reading and decoding an entire chunk. store.reconstruct_range(manifest, output_stream, offset, length, operation); store.restore_file_range(manifest, new_destination, offset, length, operation); syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress","headings":[{"id":"verified-file-ranges","text":"Verified file ranges","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1"],"languages":["cpp","text"],"code":"store.reconstruct_range(manifest, output_stream, offset, length, operation);\nstore.restore_file_range(manifest, new_destination, offset, length, operation);\nsyrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress","assets":[],"links":["#/en/operations"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"git-assets","title":"Git asset catalogs and retained bundles","description":"Git asset catalogs and retained bundles guide.","section":"Git","order":200,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles\">Git asset catalogs and retained bundles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-assets/git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles\" aria-label=\"Link to Git asset catalogs and retained bundles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Syrnck reads pointers from the \u003cstrong>committed Git tree\u003c/strong>, independently of dirty\nworking files, the index and checkout filters. Git keeps source code and its\nnormal history. A deterministic Syrnck revision describes only the assets needed\nby one Git commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Existing LFS snapshots can be converted with the separate \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration\">offline migration\u003c/a>.\nIt verifies cached SHA-256 objects, imports native blocks and retains the new\ncatalog before publishing a child commit on a new branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git catalog HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>catalog\u003c/code> is read-only and needs no Syrnck store or credentials. Its English JSON\ncontains \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, the resolved full Git \u003ccode>commit\u003c/code>, and ordered \u003ccode>assets\u003c/code> entries\nwith \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, Git \u003ccode>blob\u003c/code> ID, Syrnck \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code> ID, logical \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>executable\u003c/code>.\nSHA-1 and SHA-256 Git repositories are supported; annotated commit tags resolve\nto their commit. The source revision is resolved once before reading the tree.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>prepare-assets\u003c/code> uses the repository&#39;s common Git store (\u003ccode>git-common-dir/syrnck\u003c/code>)\nunless \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> is supplied. Initialize filters/store first with \u003ccode>git install\u003c/code>.\nIt checks each pointer size against its manifest and verifies the full object\nclosure, including file hashes, before publishing \u003ccode>git-assets/COMMIT\u003c/code> locally.\nA failure preserves existing pins. Different worktrees share the common store;\nthe Store&#39;s existing exclusive process lock still applies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The bundle uses the existing revision format: no parents, timestamp zero,\nmessage \u003ccode>Syrnck Git assets v1 COMMIT\u003c/code>, and asset paths/manifests/executable flags.\nIt contains no source-code files and does not replace a Git commit. Identical\ncatalogs produce identical bundle IDs. Path validation, portable case collisions\nand the 16 MiB metadata limit apply. Bundle pins retain complete versions through\nGC independently of later changes to Git refs. They currently require explicit\nunpinning to release. \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-retention\">Retention inventory and protection\u003c/a>\ncan now observe refs, reflogs and worktree indexes, verify their assets and add\nper-repository pins atomically. Automatic retirement remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"grouped-transfers\">Grouped transfers\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-assets/grouped-transfers\" aria-label=\"Link to Grouped transfers\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The local Git commit must already exist. These commands use the common Git store\nunless \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> overrides it. Tokens/keys remain in their usual environment;\n\u003ccode>--ca\u003c/code>, compression, retries and shared traffic/buffer limits work as for the\nordinary agent. The result contains \u003ccode>commit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>assets\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bundle\u003c/code>, and the nested\n\u003ccode>transfer\u003c/code> statistics/session receipt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push prepares the verified bundle, transfers its deduplicated closure using the\nexisting persistent upload inventory/lease protocol, and publishes the single\nserver reference \u003ccode>git-COMMIT\u003c/code>. That reference is immutable through this command:\nan existing different root fails before transfer rather than being overwritten.\nThe final server CAS verifies the complete closure. Another commit, including a\nchange only to source code, has its own reference. Git itself is not pushed by\nthis command. A repeated upload reuses verified objects and session recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pull reconstructs the expected bundle ID from the locally committed pointers,\nthen requires the matching server reference. It downloads into\n\u003ccode>git-stage/BUNDLE_ID\u003c/code>, verifies all pointer size contracts and file hashes, and\npublishes \u003ccode>git-assets/COMMIT\u003c/code> only after those checks. The staging pin is then\nremoved. A failed validation/interruption can retain a staging pin and journal;\nrerun \u003ccode>git pull-assets\u003c/code> after fixing the cause to finish validation/publication.\nAn explicit \u003ccode>agent resume\u003c/code> only finishes its underlying staging transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>No working file or Git index is modified by grouped transfer. For a fresh clone,\nuse \u003ccode>git clone --no-checkout\u003c/code>, run \u003ccode>syrnck git install\u003c/code>, pull the desired commit&#39;s\nassets, then explicitly check out that commit. This is an explicit hydration\nworkflow. On-demand smudging is available through the opt-in policy below;\nthe \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-filter-process\">resident filter\u003c/a> reuses one Store per Git command.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The transaction covers one catalog&#39;s publication. Multiple Git commits use\nseparate roots, and the Git server and block server do not participate in one\ndistributed transaction. A failed Git publication can leave retained block roots.\nDo not delete those roots while corresponding Git history must stay reconstructible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones\">On-demand checkout and pointer-only clones\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-assets/on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones\" aria-label=\"Link to On-demand checkout and pointer-only clones\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">cd project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">git checkout --force HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code> in the environment for missing assets, and \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> when\nusing an encrypted local store (\u003ccode>git install --encrypted\u003c/code>). The server and local\nstore can use different encryption keys. HTTPS verifies certificates. The\ngenerated filter uses system CAs; a custom filter invocation can supply \u003ccode>--ca\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The default policy is \u003ccode>local\u003c/code>: missing objects fail checkout without block\nnetwork access. \u003ccode>download\u003c/code> explicitly selects a configured Git remote&#39;s \u003cstrong>block\u003c/strong>\nURL. A missing manifest or chunk triggers a persistent pull of the exact manifest\nfrom stdin&#39;s pointer, protected by a required renewable server read lease. It\ndoes not consult \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>, the worktree file, or a catalog reference: selected paths\nand old versions can be checked out while \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code> still names another commit.\nOnly that manifest&#39;s missing objects are downloaded. The server must still have\nits full closure; retained catalogs from pre-push/grouped uploads provide that\nretention. Servers without read-lease support fail this opt-in download path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ordinary content and fully available assets need no token, endpoint validation,\nor network request. Existing corrupt objects are errors, rather than silently\nreplaced from the server. Before reconstructing pointer content, smudge verifies\nthe pointer&#39;s size, every block, and the complete file hash. Successful assets\nare pinned as \u003ccode>git/MANIFEST\u003c/code>. Failed downloads emit no reconstructed bytes and\nretain verified partial objects through the ordinary transfer journal for retry\nand GC. The pull first publishes \u003ccode>git-fetch/MANIFEST\u003c/code>; a failed size check can\nretain this staging pin. Rerun checkout after fixing the pointer/cause to finish\nits Git pin and staging cleanup. A generic \u003ccode>agent resume\u003c/code> only completes the\nstaging transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git configure-checkout skip\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">git checkout --force HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git configure-checkout local\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>skip\u003c/code> writes canonical pointers into the worktree. It opens no Store and needs\nno key, token or block server. Clean accepts an unhydrated canonical pointer\nunchanged, so \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git add\u003c/code> work in a pointer-only clone. It creates\nno retention pin for an incomplete closure; pre-push/prepare still require all\nreferenced assets. A present manifest&#39;s wrong size or detected corruption still\nfails clean. Malformed Syrnck pointers fail in all modes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1\u003c/code> is a one-invocation/environment override, even without a\nrepository or store; \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> uses the regular policy. Other values fail. For example,\nset it to \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> in PowerShell before the Git checkout and remove it afterward with\n\u003ccode>Remove-Item Env:SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE\u003c/code>. Git filter configuration is repository-local\nand is not transferred by clone; install it explicitly in every new repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Changing policy updates configuration only. Git may keep an existing pointer\nwhose index/stat already match, even with \u003ccode>checkout-index --force\u003c/code>. To hydrate\nthat indexed path after enabling download, preserve any desired edits, remove\n\u003cstrong>only its pointer workfile\u003c/strong>, then run \u003ccode>git checkout -- Map.uasset\u003c/code>. For example,\nPowerShell can remove that pointer with \u003ccode>Remove-Item -LiteralPath Map.uasset\u003c/code>;\nPOSIX shells can use \u003ccode>rm -- Map.uasset\u003c/code>. These are explicit working-file changes;\npolicy configuration itself never removes files. For a selective checkout in a\nfresh no-checkout clone, check out \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> first so Git sees the filter\nrule. Normal source-code paths retain their ordinary Git behavior.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The policy is one local Git config value, \u003ccode>syrnck.checkout\u003c/code>, containing \u003ccode>local\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>skip\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>download:REMOTE\u003c/code>. Installed smudge commands pass \u003ccode>--checkout-policy\u003c/code>;\nmanual \u003ccode>git smudge\u003c/code> remains local unless given that flag. \u003ccode>git install\u003c/code> upgrades\nthe exact earlier Syrnck-generated smudge command, preserving custom commands by\nrefusing to overwrite them. Common worktree storage/policy and the Store&#39;s\nexclusive process lock still apply. New installations use the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/git-filter-process\">resident filter\u003c/a> for sequential requests in one Git command;\nthe single-file commands remain available. Deferred hydration and parallel\ncheckouts remain future work.\nPreflight verification adds a full read before output; memory remains bounded by\nexisting object/metadata budgets and the closure&#39;s visited keys, not file size.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>git_checkout\u003c/code> suite exercises an encrypted clone, selected paths and old\nversions, offline local reuse, pointer-only status/add, store-free skip, malformed\ninput, unavailable endpoint/retry with partial objects, corruption refusal,\nsize contracts, offline staging cleanup, and owned filter upgrades against the production block server.\nGit&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_filter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">filter contract\u003c/a> supplies\nstdin content and consumes stdout; it does not make a multi-file checkout atomic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"opt-in-pre-push-hook\">Opt-in pre-push hook\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-assets/opt-in-pre-push-hook\" aria-label=\"Link to Opt-in pre-push hook\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git install-hook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">git push origin main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The hook is installed only by the explicit \u003ccode>install-hook\u003c/code> command. Ordinary\nfilter installation does not install it. The block URL is stored locally as\n\u003ccode>remote.origin.syrnckUrl\u003c/code>, separately from Git&#39;s transport URL. CLI configuration\nrejects credentials/query/fragment URLs and non-loopback HTTP. The generated\nscript contains only quoted executable/store paths, inherits token/key environment\nvariables, and forwards Git&#39;s hook arguments/stdin. No token is written to Git\nconfig or the script. The automatic invocation uses system CAs; for private CAs,\nintegrate the CLI invocation with \u003ccode>--ca PATH\u003c/code> into an existing hook manually.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Git&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks#_pre_push\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pre-push input\u003c/a> identifies each\nlocal tip and proposed remote update. The hook resolves non-deleted tips to\ncommits and walks their \u003cstrong>entire locally reachable ancestry\u003c/strong>, including merge\nparents, using \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rev-list\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\u003ccode>rev-list\u003c/code>\u003c/a>. It does not\nassume assets are present merely because Git advertises an old remote commit.\nAnnotated commit tags and overlapping multi-ref pushes share this traversal.\nAn asset removed from the newest tree still needs publishing for its historical\ncommit. Each nonempty catalog is verified, uploaded and pinned before the hook\nallows Git to continue. Upload-lease support is required for this hook.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The block client is created lazily. Deletion-only and entirely code-only histories\nneed no block endpoint/token or block network request. A current tree with no\nassets does not bypass checks on older asset-bearing commits. Missing manifests,\nbad pointer sizes, unavailable blocks, wrong immutable roots, unsupported leases\nand network errors fail the hook and abort that Git push. Rerun the push after\nfixing the cause; verified uploads and publication receipts are reusable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Installation is atomic and idempotent for the exact generated script. An existing\ndifferent hook, a symlinked hooks path or configured \u003ccode>core.hooksPath\u003c/code> is refused\nwithout overwriting user files. Existing hook workflows must call the equivalent\n\u003ccode>syrnck --store COMMON_STORE git pre-push &quot;$@&quot;\u003c/code> themselves, preserve stdin, and\npropagate its failure. Shared Git worktrees use the common hooks/store location.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is a \u003cstrong>local client hook\u003c/strong>, and Git&#39;s \u003ccode>--no-verify\u003c/code> bypasses it. It does not\nenforce a policy on GitHub/GitLab or make Git/block publication atomic across\nservers. Cancellation or a later Git push failure can leave complete block roots\nretained, and a multi-ref Git push uses Git&#39;s own atomicity rules. Server-side Git\nenforcement, automatic orphan-root expiry and distributed publication remain\nfuture work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The current hook rejects shallow/grafted histories and tags which cannot resolve\nto commits. Fetch a complete local Git history and its pointer blobs before use.\nMissing promisor data fails locally. It accepts at most 4,096 update lines,\n1 MiB stdin, 4 KiB per line and 100,000 unique reachable commits; history output\nis limited to 8 MiB. It rescans committed trees on each invocation and does not\nclaim incremental production throughput. Presence/catalog indexing remains work\nfor larger projects.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>git_pre_push\u003c/code> suite runs real pushes to isolated \u003cstrong>local\u003c/strong> bare Git repos\nand an encrypted loopback block server. It checks intermediate/deleted asset\nversions, annotated tags and multiple refs, old-version clone/checkout, missing\nhistorical blocks, unavailable endpoints, retry, existing-hook preservation,\nSHA-256 linked worktrees, code-only/deletion flows and invalid/shallow/grafted input. It does not push this\nsource repository or contact GitHub.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-local-git-requirements\">Bounds and local Git requirements\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-assets/bounds-and-local-git-requirements\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounds and local Git requirements\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use Git \u003cstrong>2.45 or newer\u003c/strong>. Local plumbing uses \u003ccode>--no-replace-objects\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--no-lazy-fetch\u003c/code>, so missing promisor objects fail instead of initiating a fetch.\n\u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-ls-tree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git tree enumeration\u003c/a> uses NUL-delimited\npaths and sizes; only regular blobs of at most 1,024 bytes can be pointer\ncandidates. Large ordinary Git blobs are never loaded into the catalog process.\nSymlinks and submodule entries are excluded; submodules need their own setup.\nA candidate starting with the exact Syrnck pointer header must parse canonically.\nOther blobs are ordinary Git content.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The tree response is limited to 64 MiB and 262,144 entries. Unique candidate\nblobs are read in batches of at most 128 using raw\n\u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\u003ccode>cat-file --batch\u003c/code>\u003c/a>; each response is\nlimited to 160 KiB. Child stderr is limited to 16 KiB. The process helper spools\nchild output to temporary files before bounded reads; this does not cap temporary\ndisk usage or all metadata/RSS. Cancellation is checked between Git commands,\nbatch parsing and Store operations; a Git child already running may finish.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Git asset catalogs and retained bundles # Syrnck reads pointers from the committed Git tree , independently of dirty working files, the index and checkout filters. Git keeps source code and its normal history. A deterministic Syrnck revision describes only the assets needed by one Git commit. Existing LFS snapshots can be converted with the separate offline migration . It verifies cached SHA-256 objects, imports native blocks and retains the new catalog before publishing a child commit on a new branch. text Copy syrnck git catalog HEAD syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD catalog is read-only and needs no Syrnck store or credentials. Its English JSON contains version , the resolved full Git commit , and ordered assets entries with path , Git blob ID, Syrnck manifest ID, logical size and executable . SHA-1 and SHA-256 Git repositories are supported; annotated commit tags resolve to their commit. The source revision is resolved once before reading the tree. prepare-assets uses the repository's common Git store ( git-common-dir/syrnck ) unless --store is supplied. Initialize filters/store first with git install . It checks each pointer size against its manifest and verifies the full object closure, including file hashes, before publishing git-assets/COMMIT locally. A failure preserves existing pins. Different worktrees share the common store; the Store's existing exclusive process lock still applies. The bundle uses the existing revision format: no parents, timestamp zero, message Syrnck Git assets v1 COMMIT , and asset paths/manifests/executable flags. It contains no source-code files and does not replace a Git commit. Identical catalogs produce identical bundle IDs. Path validation, portable case collisions and the 16 MiB metadata limit apply. Bundle pins retain complete versions through GC independently of later changes to Git refs. They currently require explicit unpinning to release. Retention inventory and protection can now observe refs, reflogs and worktree indexes, verify their assets and add per-repository pins atomically. Automatic retirement remains future work. Grouped transfers # text Copy syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease The local Git commit must already exist. These commands use the common Git store unless --store overrides it. Tokens/keys remain in their usual environment; --ca , compression, retries and shared traffic/buffer limits work as for the ordinary agent. The result contains commit , assets , bundle , and the nested transfer statistics/session receipt. Push prepares the verified bundle, transfers its deduplicated closure using the existing persistent upload inventory/lease protocol, and publishes the single server reference git-COMMIT . That reference is immutable through this command: an existing different root fails before transfer rather than being overwritten. The final server CAS verifies the complete closure. Another commit, including a change only to source code, has its own reference. Git itself is not pushed by this command. A repeated upload reuses verified objects and session recovery. Pull reconstructs the expected bundle ID from the locally committed pointers, then requires the matching server reference. It downloads into git-stage/BUNDLE_ID , verifies all pointer size contracts and file hashes, and publishes git-assets/COMMIT only after those checks. The staging pin is then removed. A failed validation/interruption can retain a staging pin and journal; rerun git pull-assets after fixing the cause to finish validation/publication. An explicit agent resume only finishes its underlying staging transfer. No working file or Git index is modified by grouped transfer. For a fresh clone, use git clone --no-checkout , run syrnck git install , pull the desired commit's assets, then explicitly check out that commit. This is an explicit hydration workflow. On-demand smudging is available through the opt-in policy below; the resident filter reuses one Store per Git command. The transaction covers one catalog's publication. Multiple Git commits use separate roots, and the Git server and block server do not participate in one distributed transaction. A failed Git publication can leave retained block roots. Do not delete those roots while corresponding Git history must stay reconstructible. On-demand checkout and pointer-only clones # text Copy git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project cd project syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin git checkout --force HEAD Set SYRNCK_TOKEN in the environment for missing assets, and SYRNCK_KEY when using an encrypted local store ( git install --encrypted ). The server and local store can use different encryption keys. HTTPS verifies certificates. The generated filter uses system CAs; a custom filter invocation can supply --ca . The default policy is local : missing objects fail checkout without block network access. download explicitly selects a configured Git remote's block URL. A missing manifest or chunk triggers a persistent pull of the exact manifest from stdin's pointer, protected by a required renewable server read lease. It does not consult HEAD , the worktree file, or a catalog reference: selected paths and old versions can be checked out while HEAD still names another commit. Only that manifest's missing objects are downloaded. The server must still have its full closure; retained catalogs from pre-push/grouped uploads provide that retention. Servers without read-lease support fail this opt-in download path. Ordinary content and fully available assets need no token, endpoint validation, or network request. Existing corrupt objects are errors, rather than silently replaced from the server. Before reconstructing pointer content, smudge verifies the pointer's size, every block, and the complete file hash. Successful assets are pinned as git/MANIFEST . Failed downloads emit no reconstructed bytes and retain verified partial objects through the ordinary transfer journal for retry and GC. The pull first publishes git-fetch/MANIFEST ; a failed size check can retain this staging pin. Rerun checkout after fixing the pointer/cause to finish its Git pin and staging cleanup. A generic agent resume only completes the staging transfer. text Copy syrnck git configure-checkout skip git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout local skip writes canonical pointers into the worktree. It opens no Store and needs no key, token or block server. Clean accepts an unhydrated canonical pointer unchanged, so git status and git add work in a pointer-only clone. It creates no retention pin for an incomplete closure; pre-push/prepare still require all referenced assets. A present manifest's wrong size or detected corruption still fails clean. Malformed Syrnck pointers fail in all modes. SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 is a one-invocation/environment override, even without a repository or store; 0 uses the regular policy. Other values fail. For example, set it to 1 in PowerShell before the Git checkout and remove it afterward with Remove-Item Env:SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE . Git filter configuration is repository-local and is not transferred by clone; install it explicitly in every new repository. Changing policy updates configuration only. Git may keep an existing pointer whose index/stat already match, even with checkout-index --force . To hydrate that indexed path after enabling download, preserve any desired edits, remove only its pointer workfile , then run git checkout -- Map.uasset . For example, PowerShell can remove that pointer with Remove-Item -LiteralPath Map.uasset ; POSIX shells can use rm -- Map.uasset . These are explicit working-file changes; policy configuration itself never removes files. For a selective checkout in a fresh no-checkout clone, check out .gitattributes first so Git sees the filter rule. Normal source-code paths retain their ordinary Git behavior. The policy is one local Git config value, syrnck.checkout , containing local , skip , or download:REMOTE . Installed smudge commands pass --checkout-policy ; manual git smudge remains local unless given that flag. git install upgrades the exact earlier Syrnck-generated smudge command, preserving custom commands by refusing to overwrite them. Common worktree storage/policy and the Store's exclusive process lock still apply. New installations use the resident filter for sequential requests in one Git command; the single-file commands remain available. Deferred hydration and parallel checkouts remain future work. Preflight verification adds a full read before output; memory remains bounded by existing object/metadata budgets and the closure's visited keys, not file size. The git_checkout suite exercises an encrypted clone, selected paths and old versions, offline local reuse, pointer-only status/add, store-free skip, malformed input, unavailable endpoint/retry with partial objects, corruption refusal, size contracts, offline staging cleanup, and owned filter upgrades against the production block server. Git's filter contract supplies stdin content and consumes stdout; it does not make a multi-file checkout atomic. Opt-in pre-push hook # text Copy syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git install-hook git push origin main The hook is installed only by the explicit install-hook command. Ordinary filter installation does not install it. The block URL is stored locally as remote.origin.syrnckUrl , separately from Git's transport URL. CLI configuration rejects credentials/query/fragment URLs and non-loopback HTTP. The generated script contains only quoted executable/store paths, inherits token/key environment variables, and forwards Git's hook arguments/stdin. No token is written to Git config or the script. The automatic invocation uses system CAs; for private CAs, integrate the CLI invocation with --ca PATH into an existing hook manually. Git's pre-push input identifies each local tip and proposed remote update. The hook resolves non-deleted tips to commits and walks their entire locally reachable ancestry , including merge parents, using rev-list . It does not assume assets are present merely because Git advertises an old remote commit. Annotated commit tags and overlapping multi-ref pushes share this traversal. An asset removed from the newest tree still needs publishing for its historical commit. Each nonempty catalog is verified, uploaded and pinned before the hook allows Git to continue. Upload-lease support is required for this hook. The block client is created lazily. Deletion-only and entirely code-only histories need no block endpoint/token or block network request. A current tree with no assets does not bypass checks on older asset-bearing commits. Missing manifests, bad pointer sizes, unavailable blocks, wrong immutable roots, unsupported leases and network errors fail the hook and abort that Git push. Rerun the push after fixing the cause; verified uploads and publication receipts are reusable. Installation is atomic and idempotent for the exact generated script. An existing different hook, a symlinked hooks path or configured core.hooksPath is refused without overwriting user files. Existing hook workflows must call the equivalent syrnck --store COMMON_STORE git pre-push \"$@\" themselves, preserve stdin, and propagate its failure. Shared Git worktrees use the common hooks/store location. This is a local client hook , and Git's --no-verify bypasses it. It does not enforce a policy on GitHub/GitLab or make Git/block publication atomic across servers. Cancellation or a later Git push failure can leave complete block roots retained, and a multi-ref Git push uses Git's own atomicity rules. Server-side Git enforcement, automatic orphan-root expiry and distributed publication remain future work. The current hook rejects shallow/grafted histories and tags which cannot resolve to commits. Fetch a complete local Git history and its pointer blobs before use. Missing promisor data fails locally. It accepts at most 4,096 update lines, 1 MiB stdin, 4 KiB per line and 100,000 unique reachable commits; history output is limited to 8 MiB. It rescans committed trees on each invocation and does not claim incremental production throughput. Presence/catalog indexing remains work for larger projects. The git_pre_push suite runs real pushes to isolated local bare Git repos and an encrypted loopback block server. It checks intermediate/deleted asset versions, annotated tags and multiple refs, old-version clone/checkout, missing historical blocks, unavailable endpoints, retry, existing-hook preservation, SHA-256 linked worktrees, code-only/deletion flows and invalid/shallow/grafted input. It does not push this source repository or contact GitHub. Bounds and local Git requirements # Use Git 2.45 or newer . Local plumbing uses --no-replace-objects and --no-lazy-fetch , so missing promisor objects fail instead of initiating a fetch. Git tree enumeration uses NUL-delimited paths and sizes; only regular blobs of at most 1,024 bytes can be pointer candidates. Large ordinary Git blobs are never loaded into the catalog process. Symlinks and submodule entries are excluded; submodules need their own setup. A candidate starting with the exact Syrnck pointer header must parse canonically. Other blobs are ordinary Git content. The tree response is limited to 64 MiB and 262,144 entries. Unique candidate blobs are read in batches of at most 128 using raw cat-file --batch ; each response is limited to 160 KiB. Child stderr is limited to 16 KiB. The process helper spools child output to temporary files before bounded reads; this does not cap temporary disk usage or all metadata/RSS. Cancellation is checked between Git commands, batch parsing and Store operations; a Git child already running may finish. syrnck git catalog HEAD syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project cd project syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout skip git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout local syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git install-hook git push origin main","headings":[{"id":"git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles","text":"Git asset catalogs and retained bundles","level":1},{"id":"grouped-transfers","text":"Grouped transfers","level":2},{"id":"on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones","text":"On-demand checkout and pointer-only clones","level":2},{"id":"opt-in-pre-push-hook","text":"Opt-in pre-push hook","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-local-git-requirements","text":"Bounds and local Git requirements","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git catalog HEAD\nsyrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json\nsyrnck git prepare-assets HEAD\nsyrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease\nsyrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease\ngit clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project\ncd project\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\nsyrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin\ngit checkout --force HEAD\nsyrnck git configure-checkout skip\ngit checkout --force HEAD\nsyrnck git configure-checkout local\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\nsyrnck git install-hook\ngit push origin main","assets":[],"links":["#/en/git-lfs-migration","#/en/git-retention","#/en/git-filter-process","#/en/git-filter-process","https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_filter","https://git-scm.com/docs/githooks#_pre_push","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rev-list","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-ls-tree","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"git-filter-process","title":"Resident Git filters","description":"Resident Git filters guide.","section":"Git","order":120,"parent":"git-assets","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"resident-git-filters\">Resident Git filters\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-filter-process/resident-git-filters\" aria-label=\"Link to Resident Git filters\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck git install\u003c/code> now also configures a repository-local\n\u003ccode>filter.syrnck.process\u003c/code> command. A Git operation can send multiple clean/smudge\nrequests to one process. The CLI opens its Store lazily and retains its bounded\nverified-object cache until Git closes stdin. There is no background service.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">git add Content\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">git checkout another-branch\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Existing Syrnck-generated single-file filters are retained and the exact earlier\nsmudge command is upgraded when needed. A different existing clean, smudge or\nprocess command is refused before any filter config is written. For repositories\nwithout an inherited process setting, \u003ccode>git config --local --unset filter.syrnck.process\u003c/code> restores the single-file filters; \u003ccode>git install\u003c/code> enables the\nresident process again. Custom filters require manual integration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"behavior-and-performance-scope\">Behavior and performance scope\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-filter-process/behavior-and-performance-scope\" aria-label=\"Link to Behavior and performance scope\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The same clean/smudge engine and \u003ccode>local\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>download\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>skip\u003c/code>\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets/on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones\">checkout policies\u003c/a> apply.\nConfiguration and environment are read at launch. Skip-smudge needs no Store or\nkey, even for an entire session; a clean request still requires local storage.\nDownload creates the remote client only when an object is missing and requires\nthe existing renewable read leases. Ordinary verified local content stays offline.\nTransfer journals, staged pins and complete Git pins keep their usual semantics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once opened, the Store keeps its \u003cstrong>exclusive process lock for the Git command&#39;s\nlifetime\u003c/strong>, including time waiting for the next request. Other commands against\nthat Store still fail fast while it is busy. Requests are sequential. Worktrees\nshare their common on-disk Store, but independent filter processes do not share\na decoded cache. No \u003ccode>delay\u003c/code> capability, parallel checkout scheduler, daemon or\nmulti-file checkout transaction is introduced.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Keeping a handle avoids repeated Store/index initialization and lets repeated\nobjects reuse verified decoding. Each access still rereads and fingerprints the\ncurrent envelope, so changed/corrupt bytes cannot hide behind the cache. This is\nnot a throughput claim for Unreal project corpora; those benchmarks remain work.\nFull integrity verification before smudge output also remains in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"streaming-and-bounds\">Streaming and bounds\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-filter-process/streaming-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Link to Streaming and bounds\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The implementation follows Git&#39;s\n\u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_long_running_filter_process\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">filter-process contract\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/long-running-process-protocol\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">version negotiation\u003c/a>.\nIt negotiates version 2 and the offered \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>smudge\u003c/code> capabilities only.\nBinary bodies use \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/protocol-common#_pkt_line_format\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pkt-line framing\u003c/a>:\n65,516 payload bytes per packet, distinct empty-data and flush packets, and no\nconversion of content bytes. Text records accept an optional final LF. Metadata\nlists are limited to 64 KiB and 256 records; unknown request headers are ignored,\nwhile duplicate keys or missing/unsupported commands/pathnames fail that file.\nPathnames are borrowed context, never paths opened by the protocol adapter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>No response is sent until the request body&#39;s terminating flush has arrived.\nNormal Syrnck clean consumes the file directly into the ingestion pipeline and\nthen emits its small pointer. Normal pointer smudge consumes the short pointer,\nverifies its closure, and streams the reconstructed asset directly into packets.\nNeither path needs a full-asset temporary copy for protocol framing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A generic handler may write before consuming its input; ordinary raw content\npassing through smudge does this. Output is held in at most 64 KiB before spilling\nto a private temporary stream. A successful spill is read back in bounded packets\nonly after input completion. The temporary file closes/deletes with the request,\nincluding on failure. Spill bytes are plaintext and temporary disk usage can grow\nwith a raw pass-through file; encrypted object storage does not encrypt this\nfallback stream. Packet buffers, the Store cache, ingestion pipeline, visited\nobject keys and metadata add their own memory costs. These are not total RSS or\ntemporary-disk quotas.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failures-and-shutdown\">Failures and shutdown\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-filter-process/failures-and-shutdown\" aria-label=\"Link to Failures and shutdown\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A file error drains its remaining framed input, reports an English/French CLI\ndiagnostic on stderr, and returns \u003ccode>status=error\u003c/code>. The next valid request can still\nsucceed. If content already started, the final status list overrides its earlier\nsuccess; Git must reject the file. The installed \u003ccode>filter.syrnck.required=true\u003c/code>\nsetting makes failures authoritative. No diagnostic is mixed into binary stdout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Malformed framing, oversized metadata, protocol IO failure or cancellation ends\nthe process. The CLI exits 130 for cooperative cancellation and 1 for fatal\nerrors. A normal EOF between requests exits 0, including after a per-file error:\nthe client reads each framed status rather than treating process exit alone as\nthe result. Reads blocked on a pipe need data/EOF before cancellation can finish.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The public C++ \u003ccode>git::filter_process\u003c/code> adapter takes unframed handler callbacks; it\nhas no HTTP dependency. It reports recoverable errors through an optional\ndiagnostic callback and throws fatal errors. Handlers must honor cancellation,\nfinish output checks, and use the normal Store/observer serialization contracts.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Resident Git filters # syrnck git install now also configures a repository-local filter.syrnck.process command. A Git operation can send multiple clean/smudge requests to one process. The CLI opens its Store lazily and retains its bounded verified-object cache until Git closes stdin. There is no background service. text Copy syrnck git install git add Content git checkout another-branch Existing Syrnck-generated single-file filters are retained and the exact earlier smudge command is upgraded when needed. A different existing clean, smudge or process command is refused before any filter config is written. For repositories without an inherited process setting, git config --local --unset filter.syrnck.process restores the single-file filters; git install enables the resident process again. Custom filters require manual integration. Behavior and performance scope # The same clean/smudge engine and local / download / skip checkout policies apply. Configuration and environment are read at launch. Skip-smudge needs no Store or key, even for an entire session; a clean request still requires local storage. Download creates the remote client only when an object is missing and requires the existing renewable read leases. Ordinary verified local content stays offline. Transfer journals, staged pins and complete Git pins keep their usual semantics. Once opened, the Store keeps its exclusive process lock for the Git command's lifetime , including time waiting for the next request. Other commands against that Store still fail fast while it is busy. Requests are sequential. Worktrees share their common on-disk Store, but independent filter processes do not share a decoded cache. No delay capability, parallel checkout scheduler, daemon or multi-file checkout transaction is introduced. Keeping a handle avoids repeated Store/index initialization and lets repeated objects reuse verified decoding. Each access still rereads and fingerprints the current envelope, so changed/corrupt bytes cannot hide behind the cache. This is not a throughput claim for Unreal project corpora; those benchmarks remain work. Full integrity verification before smudge output also remains in place. Streaming and bounds # The implementation follows Git's filter-process contract and version negotiation . It negotiates version 2 and the offered clean / smudge capabilities only. Binary bodies use pkt-line framing : 65,516 payload bytes per packet, distinct empty-data and flush packets, and no conversion of content bytes. Text records accept an optional final LF. Metadata lists are limited to 64 KiB and 256 records; unknown request headers are ignored, while duplicate keys or missing/unsupported commands/pathnames fail that file. Pathnames are borrowed context, never paths opened by the protocol adapter. No response is sent until the request body's terminating flush has arrived. Normal Syrnck clean consumes the file directly into the ingestion pipeline and then emits its small pointer. Normal pointer smudge consumes the short pointer, verifies its closure, and streams the reconstructed asset directly into packets. Neither path needs a full-asset temporary copy for protocol framing. A generic handler may write before consuming its input; ordinary raw content passing through smudge does this. Output is held in at most 64 KiB before spilling to a private temporary stream. A successful spill is read back in bounded packets only after input completion. The temporary file closes/deletes with the request, including on failure. Spill bytes are plaintext and temporary disk usage can grow with a raw pass-through file; encrypted object storage does not encrypt this fallback stream. Packet buffers, the Store cache, ingestion pipeline, visited object keys and metadata add their own memory costs. These are not total RSS or temporary-disk quotas. Failures and shutdown # A file error drains its remaining framed input, reports an English/French CLI diagnostic on stderr, and returns status=error . The next valid request can still succeed. If content already started, the final status list overrides its earlier success; Git must reject the file. The installed filter.syrnck.required=true setting makes failures authoritative. No diagnostic is mixed into binary stdout. Malformed framing, oversized metadata, protocol IO failure or cancellation ends the process. The CLI exits 130 for cooperative cancellation and 1 for fatal errors. A normal EOF between requests exits 0, including after a per-file error: the client reads each framed status rather than treating process exit alone as the result. Reads blocked on a pipe need data/EOF before cancellation can finish. The public C++ git::filter_process adapter takes unframed handler callbacks; it has no HTTP dependency. It reports recoverable errors through an optional diagnostic callback and throws fatal errors. Handlers must honor cancellation, finish output checks, and use the normal Store/observer serialization contracts. syrnck git install git add Content git checkout another-branch","headings":[{"id":"resident-git-filters","text":"Resident Git filters","level":1},{"id":"behavior-and-performance-scope","text":"Behavior and performance scope","level":2},{"id":"streaming-and-bounds","text":"Streaming and bounds","level":2},{"id":"failures-and-shutdown","text":"Failures and shutdown","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git install\ngit add Content\ngit checkout another-branch","assets":[],"links":["#/en/git-assets/on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones","https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes#_long_running_filter_process","https://git-scm.com/docs/long-running-process-protocol","https://git-scm.com/docs/protocol-common#_pkt_line_format"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"git-lfs-history","title":"Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history","description":"Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history guide.","section":"Git","order":130,"parent":"git-assets","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history\">Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history/offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history\" aria-label=\"Link to Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> can convert every commit reachable from one tip, including all\nparents of merges, onto a new branch. Source references, HEAD, the index and\nworking files remain intact. No Git LFS server or \u003ccode>git-lfs\u003c/code> process is used.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">git log --graph converted-history\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both commands accept \u003ccode>--directory PROJECT\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--lfs-objects CACHE/objects\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>--max-commits 10000\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--strip-signatures\u003c/code>. Migration uses the existing\nStore&#39;s ingestion settings unless explicitly overridden. Repository filters\ncan be initialized separately with \u003ccode>syrnck git install\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"complete-preflight\">Complete preflight\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history/complete-preflight\" aria-label=\"Link to Complete preflight\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Traversal reads raw commit parents, without replacements or history\nsimplification. Missing ancestors fail the operation; a shallow boundary is\nnever silently treated as a new root. The selected argument is resolved once\nto an immutable commit before traversal. Moving the source branch later does\nnot change that selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before importing assets, migration checks every distinct source tree&#39;s LFS\npointers and attribute updates, audits every distinct cached object with\nSHA-256 and verifies existing Syrnck asset dependencies. Historical assets\ndeleted from the latest snapshot still need their cached bytes. Missing,\ntruncated and corrupt old-only objects prevent any candidate or Store write\nduring this preflight. The cache is checked again against the exact bytes\nconsumed by ingestion, so later cache changes cannot publish incorrect assets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The read-only preview needs no Store or encryption key. It reports per-object\naudit status, per-commit parents/tree, counts and whether signature removal is\nrequired. Like the snapshot preview, it audits LFS cache data rather than the\navailability of existing Syrnck assets or the final branch transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preserved-history-and-signatures\">Preserved history and signatures\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history/preserved-history-and-signatures\" aria-label=\"Link to Preserved history and signatures\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The rewrite preserves ordered parents, merges, roots, empty commits, raw\nauthor/committer headers (including dates/time zones), encoding headers,\nmessages and other accepted extension headers. Unaffected commits keep their\noriginal IDs. Every changed tree uses the same literal per-file attributes\nand regular-file pointer conversion as \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration\">snapshot migration\u003c/a>.\nUnconverted code, executable modes, symlink blobs and Gitlinks keep their IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A rewritten commit cannot retain its old signature. By default, any \u003ccode>gpgsig\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>gpgsig-sha256\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>mergetag\u003c/code> header on a commit that needs rewriting blocks\nmigration. Review the preview, then explicitly permit removal if appropriate:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These headers, including continuation lines, are removed only from rewritten\ncommits and counted in the result. Unaffected signed commits retain their exact\nobjects. There is no automatic re-signing, tag rewriting or mutation of other\nbranches. Repeat the operation for other tips when needed; CAS objects deduplicate\nacross runs, but references and signatures require their own deliberate policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-publication\">Retention and publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history/retention-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention and publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For \u003cstrong>every converted historical commit\u003c/strong> containing Syrnck assets, migration\nverifies and retains its full catalog at \u003ccode>git-assets/COMMIT\u003c/code> before publishing\nthe final branch. A later asset deletion, independent Git history cleanup,\nSyrnck GC or packing therefore does not discard those protected versions.\nExisting native assets are included alongside converted LFS assets. Empty\nasset catalogs need no Store root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The final new branch is created through Git&#39;s prepared reference transaction,\nincluding the same symbolic-reference/worktree checks and user hook handling\nas snapshot migration. It never replaces an existing branch. Cancellation is\nhonored before publication; after commit begins, an exact observed commit\nreconciles a lost acknowledgement. An unresolved outcome returns exit 3 and\nrequires inspecting the reported reference and commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Failures or interruptions can leave valid unreferenced objects and already\nprepared catalog pins. They are safe to reuse, but no migration journal\nautomatically deletes them. There is no atomic cross-system transaction\nbetween Git and the Store: protection precedes branch publication. Upload of\nblocks and Git publication remain separate operations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Successful JSON contains the selected source, new tip/branch, counts,\n\u003ccode>stripped_signature_headers\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reconciled\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code> array mapping each\nsource ID to its converted commit, tree and optional retained bundle. Keep this\nmapping when reviewing or separately migrating tags. The local LFS cache is\nnever removed by migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-validation\">Bounds and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history/bounds-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounds and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The traversal defaults to 10,000 commits (configurable from 1 to 100,000), with\nat most 1,024 parents per commit, 1 MiB per raw commit and a 64 MiB budget for\nraw commit bytes plus estimated node metadata. Graph containers and allocator\noverhead are additional memory. There are at most 65,536 distinct LFS objects;\nthe existing per-snapshot tree/attribute limits still apply. Both preview and\nresult JSON are bounded at 16 MiB. Payloads stream through the bounded native\npipeline; trees, graph metadata and mapping tables remain in memory. Git\nsubprocesses are currently invoked per commit/tree, so this is an offline\nconversion tool rather than a tuned large-history server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Git&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/gitformat-signature\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">commit object format\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-ref\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">reference transactions\u003c/a> define the\nmetadata and publication mechanisms used here.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history # syrnck_git can convert every commit reachable from one tip, including all parents of merges, onto a new branch. Source references, HEAD, the index and working files remain intact. No Git LFS server or git-lfs process is used. text Copy syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress git log --graph converted-history Both commands accept --directory PROJECT , --lfs-objects CACHE/objects , --max-commits 10000 and --strip-signatures . Migration uses the existing Store's ingestion settings unless explicitly overridden. Repository filters can be initialized separately with syrnck git install . Complete preflight # Traversal reads raw commit parents, without replacements or history simplification. Missing ancestors fail the operation; a shallow boundary is never silently treated as a new root. The selected argument is resolved once to an immutable commit before traversal. Moving the source branch later does not change that selection. Before importing assets, migration checks every distinct source tree's LFS pointers and attribute updates, audits every distinct cached object with SHA-256 and verifies existing Syrnck asset dependencies. Historical assets deleted from the latest snapshot still need their cached bytes. Missing, truncated and corrupt old-only objects prevent any candidate or Store write during this preflight. The cache is checked again against the exact bytes consumed by ingestion, so later cache changes cannot publish incorrect assets. The read-only preview needs no Store or encryption key. It reports per-object audit status, per-commit parents/tree, counts and whether signature removal is required. Like the snapshot preview, it audits LFS cache data rather than the availability of existing Syrnck assets or the final branch transaction. Preserved history and signatures # The rewrite preserves ordered parents, merges, roots, empty commits, raw author/committer headers (including dates/time zones), encoding headers, messages and other accepted extension headers. Unaffected commits keep their original IDs. Every changed tree uses the same literal per-file attributes and regular-file pointer conversion as snapshot migration . Unconverted code, executable modes, symlink blobs and Gitlinks keep their IDs. A rewritten commit cannot retain its old signature. By default, any gpgsig , gpgsig-sha256 or mergetag header on a commit that needs rewriting blocks migration. Review the preview, then explicitly permit removal if appropriate: text Copy syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures These headers, including continuation lines, are removed only from rewritten commits and counted in the result. Unaffected signed commits retain their exact objects. There is no automatic re-signing, tag rewriting or mutation of other branches. Repeat the operation for other tips when needed; CAS objects deduplicate across runs, but references and signatures require their own deliberate policy. Retention and publication # For every converted historical commit containing Syrnck assets, migration verifies and retains its full catalog at git-assets/COMMIT before publishing the final branch. A later asset deletion, independent Git history cleanup, Syrnck GC or packing therefore does not discard those protected versions. Existing native assets are included alongside converted LFS assets. Empty asset catalogs need no Store root. The final new branch is created through Git's prepared reference transaction, including the same symbolic-reference/worktree checks and user hook handling as snapshot migration. It never replaces an existing branch. Cancellation is honored before publication; after commit begins, an exact observed commit reconciles a lost acknowledgement. An unresolved outcome returns exit 3 and requires inspecting the reported reference and commit. Failures or interruptions can leave valid unreferenced objects and already prepared catalog pins. They are safe to reuse, but no migration journal automatically deletes them. There is no atomic cross-system transaction between Git and the Store: protection precedes branch publication. Upload of blocks and Git publication remain separate operations. Successful JSON contains the selected source, new tip/branch, counts, stripped_signature_headers , reconciled and a commits array mapping each source ID to its converted commit, tree and optional retained bundle. Keep this mapping when reviewing or separately migrating tags. The local LFS cache is never removed by migration. Bounds and validation # The traversal defaults to 10,000 commits (configurable from 1 to 100,000), with at most 1,024 parents per commit, 1 MiB per raw commit and a 64 MiB budget for raw commit bytes plus estimated node metadata. Graph containers and allocator overhead are additional memory. There are at most 65,536 distinct LFS objects; the existing per-snapshot tree/attribute limits still apply. Both preview and result JSON are bounded at 16 MiB. Payloads stream through the bounded native pipeline; trees, graph metadata and mapping tables remain in memory. Git subprocesses are currently invoked per commit/tree, so this is an offline conversion tool rather than a tuned large-history server. Git's commit object format and reference transactions define the metadata and publication mechanisms used here. syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress git log --graph converted-history syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures","headings":[{"id":"offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history","text":"Offline conversion of complete Git LFS history","level":1},{"id":"complete-preflight","text":"Complete preflight","level":2},{"id":"preserved-history-and-signatures","text":"Preserved history and signatures","level":2},{"id":"retention-and-publication","text":"Retention and publication","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-validation","text":"Bounds and validation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress\ngit log --graph converted-history\nsyrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures","assets":[],"links":["#/en/git-lfs-migration","https://git-scm.com/docs/gitformat-signature","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-ref"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"git-lfs-migration","title":"Offline Git LFS migration","description":"Offline Git LFS migration guide.","section":"Git","order":140,"parent":"git-assets","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"offline-git-lfs-migration\">Offline Git LFS migration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/offline-git-lfs-migration\" aria-label=\"Link to Offline Git LFS migration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Syrnck can convert the Git LFS assets of one committed snapshot into native\nSyrnck pointers. It creates one child commit on a new branch. The source commit,\nHEAD, index and working files are preserved, including uncommitted changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is a snapshot migration. Earlier commits still contain their original LFS\npointers and need their original LFS storage to reconstruct those versions.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history\">Complete reachable history conversion\u003c/a> is available as a\nseparate command. Downloading LFS objects and converting LFS extension pipelines\nremain unimplemented.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-and-inspect\">Prepare and inspect\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/prepare-and-inspect\" aria-label=\"Link to Prepare and inspect\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Make the source snapshot&#39;s LFS objects available locally using your existing LFS\ntools or an offline copy. Syrnck never contacts an LFS server and does not invoke\n\u003ccode>git-lfs\u003c/code>. Inspect the committed pointers and verify their cached contents:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The preview needs Git, but no Syrnck Store or encryption key. It reads committed\nblobs directly, so a \u003ccode>git clone --no-checkout\u003c/code> is sufficient for the Git side.\nThe default cache is \u003ccode>lfs/objects\u003c/code> beneath the Git common directory, shared by\nlinked worktrees. Each object must have the usual layout:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For a copied cache or a repository with custom \u003ccode>lfs.storage\u003c/code>, supply the objects\ndirectory explicitly. Syrnck refuses to guess the meaning of a custom storage\nsetting. Relative overrides are resolved against the process working directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>JSON lists each path, Git blob ID, LFS SHA-256 OID, declared size, executable\nflag and status: \u003ccode>verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>size_mismatch\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>hash_mismatch\u003c/code>.\nDuplicate OIDs are hashed once. \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> is true only for a nonempty catalog\nwhose LFS objects all verify. Exit status is 0 when ready, 1 otherwise.\nMalformed pointers and unsafe cache entries fail the operation. The cache root,\nits two shard directories and object files must not be symbolic links; ancestors\nabove the supplied root are not checked for symbolic links.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The preview audits LFS content only. It does not establish that a target branch,\nattribute files, Git author identity, existing Syrnck assets or user hooks will\npermit migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"convert-a-snapshot\">Convert a snapshot\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/convert-a-snapshot\" aria-label=\"Link to Convert a snapshot\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Initialize the Syrnck Store and repository filters, then choose an unused branch:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">git diff HEAD converted-assets\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>git install\u003c/code> writes local filter configuration as a separate setup step. The\nmigration itself does not change Git configuration or install hooks. It requires\nan existing Store, a local Git author identity and all source LFS objects.\n\u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--lfs-objects\u003c/code> work as for the preview. Without pipeline\noverrides, the CLI uses the Store&#39;s saved ingestion settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For each unique LFS object, migration streams its bytes through the production\nchunking, compression and optional encryption pipeline. SHA-256 covers the exact\nbytes consumed by that pipeline; both the consumed size and digest must match\nthe original pointer. Native manifests and blocks keep their existing BLAKE3\nidentities and formats. A repeated asset reuses the imported manifest.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The new Git tree replaces only the selected LFS pointer blobs and necessary\n\u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> blobs. Each converted path receives a literal, anchored basename\nrule in its immediate parent directory, after existing rules:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This overrides inherited LFS selection and disables text transformations for\nthat asset. Spaces and brackets are escaped. Other files and unchanged subtrees\nretain their Git object IDs; executable bits, symbolic-link blobs and submodule\nentries are preserved. Only regular-file LFS pointers are converted. A changed\nattributes file must be a regular file, contain no NUL bytes and fit the size\nlimit. An LFS-managed \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> file cannot be converted by this operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before publication, Syrnck verifies the new commit&#39;s entire native asset catalog,\nincluding any Syrnck assets already present in the source snapshot. It retains\nthe complete bundle under \u003ccode>git-assets/NEW_COMMIT\u003c/code>. GC and packing can then\npreserve all of its dependencies independently of Git history retention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Finally, Git prepares a reference transaction and holds the target reference\nlock. Syrnck checks the reference again while that lock is held, then commits\nthe new branch. This also protects dangling symbolic references on older Git\nversions that treat their target OID as absent. Existing references and branches\nselected by an unborn HEAD or linked worktree are refused. Existing Git\n\u003ccode>reference-transaction\u003c/code> hooks run and can reject publication; commit-tree does\nnot invoke ordinary commit hooks or request commit signing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Successful JSON includes \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>commit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>converted_assets\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>unique_objects\u003c/code>, the retained \u003ccode>bundle\u003c/code>, the converted \u003ccode>assets\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>reconciled\u003c/code>.\nReview the branch and check it out explicitly when ready. Git source publication\nand Syrnck block upload remain separate operations; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets\">Git asset transfers\u003c/a>.\nThe existing LFS cache is read only and remains in its original form even when\nthe Syrnck Store is encrypted at rest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cancellation-and-recovery\">Cancellation and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/cancellation-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Cancellation and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Both commands accept \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> on stderr. Ctrl+C requests\ncooperative cancellation, with exit 130 before publication starts. Child Git\ncommands and hooks can delay cancellation; a blocked pipe read must return\nbefore the operation can proceed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once the final Git commit command has begun, migration finishes or reconciles\npublication without further cancellation checks. A late interrupt can therefore\nreturn success with the branch created. If acknowledgement is lost, an exact\nreference match to the prepared commit returns success with \u003ccode>reconciled: true\u003c/code>.\nAn unresolved publication returns exit 3: inspect the reported reference and\ncommit before retrying. Never infer failure to publish from a lost response.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Failures before bundle retention can leave unreferenced, valid CAS objects;\nnormal \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code> can collect them. A prepared bundle pin remains after later\nfailure or cancellation, protecting its blocks even if no branch was created.\nGit may also retain unreachable blobs, trees or the prepared commit. There is no\nmigration journal or automatic deletion of prepared pins. Inspect \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>, the\nreported Git reference and its catalog before deliberately removing an unused\npin. A retry can reuse already imported objects and requires an absent target\nbranch. Neither failure nor success deletes the LFS cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"library-and-limits\">Library and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/library-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Library and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/integrations\">git_lfs.hpp\u003c/a> exposes pointer parsing, catalog\ninspection, cache resolution, preview and migration through \u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code>, with\nno HTTP dependency. C++ callers provide an already open Store and optional\n\u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code>; callbacks follow the \u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">operation contract\u003c/a>.\nSHA-256 uses the privately vendored \u003ca href=\"#/en/licenses\">PicoSHA2 dependency\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The implementation currently limits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Git tree output to 64 MiB and 262,144 entries, including directories.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pointer candidates to regular blobs of at most 1,024 bytes, read in batches\nof 128 with a 160 KiB response cap. Accepted canonical LFS pointers are\nstrictly smaller than 1,024 bytes; legacy versions, extensions and unknown\nfields are refused.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A migration to 65,536 asset paths, 4,096 changed attributes directories and\n1 MiB per changed attributes file, with 16 MiB across all resulting attribute\ncontents. Native bundle metadata must also fit its\nexisting 16 MiB format limit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SHA-256 scratch batches to 64 KiB. Asset ingestion uses the existing bounded\nblock pipeline without a complete asset spool; tree metadata, visited IDs,\nparsed pointers and change maps still grow with the snapshot.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Transaction response lines to 4 KiB and each acknowledgement&#39;s diagnostic\noutput to 16 KiB. Git stderr uses a temporary file; user hook execution time\nand that file&#39;s size do not have a separate quota.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Pointer semantics follow the \u003ca href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git LFS specification\u003c/a>.\nTree and reference writes use Git&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-mktree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mktree\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-ref\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">update-ref transactions\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Offline Git LFS migration # Syrnck can convert the Git LFS assets of one committed snapshot into native Syrnck pointers. It creates one child commit on a new branch. The source commit, HEAD, index and working files are preserved, including uncommitted changes. This is a snapshot migration. Earlier commits still contain their original LFS pointers and need their original LFS storage to reconstruct those versions. Complete reachable history conversion is available as a separate command. Downloading LFS objects and converting LFS extension pipelines remain unimplemented. Prepare and inspect # Make the source snapshot's LFS objects available locally using your existing LFS tools or an offline copy. Syrnck never contacts an LFS server and does not invoke git-lfs . Inspect the committed pointers and verify their cached contents: text Copy syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress The preview needs Git, but no Syrnck Store or encryption key. It reads committed blobs directly, so a git clone --no-checkout is sufficient for the Git side. The default cache is lfs/objects beneath the Git common directory, shared by linked worktrees. Each object must have the usual layout: text Copy OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID For a copied cache or a repository with custom lfs.storage , supply the objects directory explicitly. Syrnck refuses to guess the meaning of a custom storage setting. Relative overrides are resolved against the process working directory. text Copy syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects JSON lists each path, Git blob ID, LFS SHA-256 OID, declared size, executable flag and status: verified , missing , size_mismatch or hash_mismatch . Duplicate OIDs are hashed once. ready is true only for a nonempty catalog whose LFS objects all verify. Exit status is 0 when ready, 1 otherwise. Malformed pointers and unsafe cache entries fail the operation. The cache root, its two shard directories and object files must not be symbolic links; ancestors above the supplied root are not checked for symbolic links. The preview audits LFS content only. It does not establish that a target branch, attribute files, Git author identity, existing Syrnck assets or user hooks will permit migration. Convert a snapshot # Initialize the Syrnck Store and repository filters, then choose an unused branch: text Copy syrnck git install syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress git diff HEAD converted-assets git install writes local filter configuration as a separate setup step. The migration itself does not change Git configuration or install hooks. It requires an existing Store, a local Git author identity and all source LFS objects. --directory and --lfs-objects work as for the preview. Without pipeline overrides, the CLI uses the Store's saved ingestion settings. For each unique LFS object, migration streams its bytes through the production chunking, compression and optional encryption pipeline. SHA-256 covers the exact bytes consumed by that pipeline; both the consumed size and digest must match the original pointer. Native manifests and blocks keep their existing BLAKE3 identities and formats. A repeated asset reuses the imported manifest. The new Git tree replaces only the selected LFS pointer blobs and necessary .gitattributes blobs. Each converted path receives a literal, anchored basename rule in its immediate parent directory, after existing rules: text Copy \"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf This overrides inherited LFS selection and disables text transformations for that asset. Spaces and brackets are escaped. Other files and unchanged subtrees retain their Git object IDs; executable bits, symbolic-link blobs and submodule entries are preserved. Only regular-file LFS pointers are converted. A changed attributes file must be a regular file, contain no NUL bytes and fit the size limit. An LFS-managed .gitattributes file cannot be converted by this operation. Before publication, Syrnck verifies the new commit's entire native asset catalog, including any Syrnck assets already present in the source snapshot. It retains the complete bundle under git-assets/NEW_COMMIT . GC and packing can then preserve all of its dependencies independently of Git history retention. Finally, Git prepares a reference transaction and holds the target reference lock. Syrnck checks the reference again while that lock is held, then commits the new branch. This also protects dangling symbolic references on older Git versions that treat their target OID as absent. Existing references and branches selected by an unborn HEAD or linked worktree are refused. Existing Git reference-transaction hooks run and can reject publication; commit-tree does not invoke ordinary commit hooks or request commit signing. Successful JSON includes source , commit , branch , converted_assets , unique_objects , the retained bundle , the converted assets and reconciled . Review the branch and check it out explicitly when ready. Git source publication and Syrnck block upload remain separate operations; see Git asset transfers . The existing LFS cache is read only and remains in its original form even when the Syrnck Store is encrypted at rest. Cancellation and recovery # Both commands accept --progress or --progress-json on stderr. Ctrl+C requests cooperative cancellation, with exit 130 before publication starts. Child Git commands and hooks can delay cancellation; a blocked pipe read must return before the operation can proceed. Once the final Git commit command has begun, migration finishes or reconciles publication without further cancellation checks. A late interrupt can therefore return success with the branch created. If acknowledgement is lost, an exact reference match to the prepared commit returns success with reconciled: true . An unresolved publication returns exit 3: inspect the reported reference and commit before retrying. Never infer failure to publish from a lost response. Failures before bundle retention can leave unreferenced, valid CAS objects; normal gc --delete can collect them. A prepared bundle pin remains after later failure or cancellation, protecting its blocks even if no branch was created. Git may also retain unreachable blobs, trees or the prepared commit. There is no migration journal or automatic deletion of prepared pins. Inspect refs , the reported Git reference and its catalog before deliberately removing an unused pin. A retry can reuse already imported objects and requires an absent target branch. Neither failure nor success deletes the LFS cache. Library and limits # git_lfs.hpp exposes pointer parsing, catalog inspection, cache resolution, preview and migration through syrnck_git , with no HTTP dependency. C++ callers provide an already open Store and optional IngestOptions ; callbacks follow the operation contract . SHA-256 uses the privately vendored PicoSHA2 dependency . The implementation currently limits: Git tree output to 64 MiB and 262,144 entries, including directories. Pointer candidates to regular blobs of at most 1,024 bytes, read in batches of 128 with a 160 KiB response cap. Accepted canonical LFS pointers are strictly smaller than 1,024 bytes; legacy versions, extensions and unknown fields are refused. A migration to 65,536 asset paths, 4,096 changed attributes directories and 1 MiB per changed attributes file, with 16 MiB across all resulting attribute contents. Native bundle metadata must also fit its existing 16 MiB format limit. SHA-256 scratch batches to 64 KiB. Asset ingestion uses the existing bounded block pipeline without a complete asset spool; tree metadata, visited IDs, parsed pointers and change maps still grow with the snapshot. Transaction response lines to 4 KiB and each acknowledgement's diagnostic output to 16 KiB. Git stderr uses a temporary file; user hook execution time and that file's size do not have a separate quota. Pointer semantics follow the Git LFS specification . Tree and reference writes use Git's mktree and update-ref transactions . syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects syrnck git install syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress git diff HEAD converted-assets \"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf","headings":[{"id":"offline-git-lfs-migration","text":"Offline Git LFS migration","level":1},{"id":"prepare-and-inspect","text":"Prepare and inspect","level":2},{"id":"convert-a-snapshot","text":"Convert a snapshot","level":2},{"id":"cancellation-and-recovery","text":"Cancellation and recovery","level":2},{"id":"library-and-limits","text":"Library and limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress\nOBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID\nsyrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress\ngit diff HEAD converted-assets\n\"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf","assets":[],"links":["#/en/git-lfs-history","#/en/git-assets","#/en/integrations","#/en/operations","#/en/licenses","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-mktree","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-ref"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"git-retention","title":"Git retention inventory and verified protection","description":"Git retention inventory and verified protection guide.","section":"Git","order":150,"parent":"git-assets","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection\">Git retention inventory and verified protection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-retention/git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection\" aria-label=\"Link to Git retention inventory and verified protection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Syrnck can inspect the assets still observable through Git and add complete\nretention roots before local block maintenance. Inspection is explicit; it does\nnot run automatically inside Git filters, packing or GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git retention-scan --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git retention --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git retention --apply --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All commands accept \u003ccode>--directory PATH\u003c/code>. The scan needs Git and no Store, key,\ncheckout filters or working-file contents. The other commands use the common\nGit Store by default; \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> can select an initialized custom Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-scan-observes\">What the scan observes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-retention/what-the-scan-observes\" aria-label=\"Link to What the scan observes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each registered worktree is traversed explicitly through Git, including the\nmain worktree or bare repository. The traversal covers:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>All references and their ancestors, including branches, remote-tracking refs,\nannotated/lightweight tags, stash and private worktree refs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>All objects mentioned in each worktree&#39;s reflogs and their reachable history.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HEAD, including detached HEAD, and all indexed objects, including conflict\nstages and an index in an unborn repository.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Scanning each worktree explicitly also covers private refs that Git 2.45&#39;s\nglobal traversal omits from other worktrees. Shared objects are deduplicated\nbefore inspecting blob contents. Ordinary large blobs are filtered from history\ntraversal; only blobs of at most 1,024 bytes are read as pointer candidates.\nObject IDs and sizes are inspected in batches, without executing clean/smudge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Any canonical Syrnck pointer-shaped blob in that object set is included,\nregardless of attributes, path or file mode. Blob tags and symbolic-link blobs\ncan therefore retain extra assets conservatively. Duplicate manifest IDs are\nmerged; contradictory declared sizes or malformed pointers fail the scan.\nNo LFS object is converted or fetched by this command.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Unstaged working-file contents, untracked files, arbitrary alternate indexes,\nunregistered worktrees, submodule repositories and operational pseudo-refs such\nas \u003ccode>ORIG_HEAD\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>FETCH_HEAD\u003c/code> are not additional roots. Already retained pins\nremain protected. Run separate scans for submodule repositories as needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The scan returns English JSON with \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, a path-derived \u003ccode>repository\u003c/code>\nidentity, an object-set \u003ccode>fingerprint\u003c/code>, counts for \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code>, and\nsorted \u003ccode>manifests\u003c/code> containing ID and size. This is a metadata inventory, not\nproof that local Syrnck blocks exist. The common Git directory&#39;s canonical path\ndetermines the namespace; moving a repository can create a different namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preview-and-apply\">Preview and apply\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-retention/preview-and-apply\" aria-label=\"Link to Preview and apply\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>git retention\u003c/code> reads the inventory and current Store references, then verifies\nevery observed manifest&#39;s declared size, complete block closure and whole-file\nhash. It returns the scan fields plus:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>add\u003c/code>: missing per-repository pins and their object keys.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>unobserved_pins\u003c/code>: existing pins not observed through this inventory, with\n\u003ccode>repository\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>legacy\u003c/code> scope labels.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>verification_error\u003c/code>: whether all observed assets verified;\na verification error is canonical English diagnostic data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>applied\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>added\u003c/code>: whether the requested update completed and its count.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The default preview changes nothing. Missing or corrupt assets produce\n\u003ccode>ready: false\u003c/code>, exit 1, and no additions, including with \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code>. Fetch or\nrestore the missing content separately, then retry. A successful empty scan is\nready and needs no new pins.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code>, the inventory is read again before publication. A changed object\nset aborts with a retry diagnostic. New roots use:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The new roots are verified again and added in one compare-and-swap reference\ntransaction. Either every addition is published, or none is. Existing matching\npins are reused; a conflicting object under this namespace is an error.\nUser references, native branches, transfer roots and existing Git pins are\npreserved. A repeat with no additions returns \u003ccode>applied: true, added: 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The command writes neither Git history nor HEAD, index, configuration or working\nfiles. It makes no network request and invokes no Git publication hooks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete\">Unobserved does not mean safe to delete\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-retention/unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete\" aria-label=\"Link to Unobserved does not mean safe to delete\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>This release only adds protections. It never removes pins automatically.\n\u003ccode>unobserved_pins\u003c/code> is an inspection report, not a deletion plan or a reclaimable\nbyte estimate. Legacy \u003ccode>git/MANIFEST\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git-assets/COMMIT\u003c/code> pins may belong to\nanother repository sharing the Store, or intentionally preserve deleted history.\nOld per-repository pins also remain until deliberately removed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Git and Syrnck do not share one transaction lock for refs, reflogs, indexes and\nworktrees. Rechecking detects a change between the two scans, but does not freeze\nGit after the second scan. Additions safely retain the captured snapshot; they\ncannot promise coverage of concurrent or later Git changes. Coordinate Git\nactivity and rerun before maintenance that relies on this inventory. Dirty\nworking files do not become retained versions through inspection alone.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Automatic retirement needs further coordination and a recovery policy. Until\nthen, inspect the report and retain desired versions before deliberate manual\nunpinning. Ordinary \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> remains a preview; \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code> only collects objects\nunreachable from all remaining pins and other supported retention roots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"transactions-cancellation-and-limits\">Transactions, cancellation and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/git-retention/transactions-cancellation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Transactions, cancellation and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The C++ \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-store\">Store API\u003c/a> exposes \u003ccode>RefUpdate\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>compare_update_refs(updates, operation)\u003c/code> for atomic multi-reference updates.\nEach update has a name, optional expected old key and optional new key. Null\nexpected means absent; null new key means removal. Duplicate names are errors.\nAn expected-value mismatch returns false without changing references. All new\nclosures and file hashes are verified before publishing one \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> through\nthe existing atomic writer. Other references are preserved. Callers must\nserialize Store access, including callbacks, as for other Store methods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The references document has a 16 MiB write limit, checked before publication.\nKnown-unique, sorted keys are emitted without repeated linear duplicate searches.\nAn oversized transaction leaves the previous document intact and readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All Git retention commands support text/JSON progress and cooperative\ncancellation. Scanning counts object entries; verification uses the existing\nclosure progress contract. Cancellation before reference publication leaves\npins unchanged. There are no callbacks or cancellation checks after publication;\nan operation already committed can complete successfully after an interrupt.\nChild Git commands must finish before a pending cancellation can be observed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Current bounds are 4,096 worktrees, 1 MiB worktree-list output, 524,288 unique\nGit objects, 65,536 unique manifests and 64 MiB traversal output per worktree.\nMetadata headers are read in batches of 512 (64 KiB response cap); small blob\nbodies in batches of 128 (160 KiB cap). Ordinary blob payloads are not retained\nin memory across batches. IDs, sorted sets, fingerprints, pins and result JSON\nstill grow with repository history. A full shared history is traversed once per\nworktree; there is no persistent incremental inventory yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Shallow histories, graft history and environment overrides \u003ccode>GIT_NAMESPACE\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>GIT_INDEX_FILE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GIT_SHALLOW_FILE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GIT_DIR\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GIT_WORK_TREE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>GIT_COMMON_DIR\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY\u003c/code> are refused. Missing/inaccessible registered worktrees\nor unavailable Git objects fail before publication. Lazy object fetching,\nreplace objects and fsmonitor are disabled for the inspection commands.\nTraversal output on stderr is also refused, including warnings about reflogs\nthat reference pruned commits even when Git exits successfully. Disable Git\ntracing or repair the reported Git metadata before retrying.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Traversal follows Git&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rev-list\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rev-list object options\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">batched object inspection\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Git retention inventory and verified protection # Syrnck can inspect the assets still observable through Git and add complete retention roots before local block maintenance. Inspection is explicit; it does not run automatically inside Git filters, packing or GC. text Copy syrnck git retention-scan --progress syrnck git retention --progress syrnck git retention --apply --progress All commands accept --directory PATH . The scan needs Git and no Store, key, checkout filters or working-file contents. The other commands use the common Git Store by default; --store can select an initialized custom Store. What the scan observes # Each registered worktree is traversed explicitly through Git, including the main worktree or bare repository. The traversal covers: All references and their ancestors, including branches, remote-tracking refs, annotated/lightweight tags, stash and private worktree refs. All objects mentioned in each worktree's reflogs and their reachable history. HEAD, including detached HEAD, and all indexed objects, including conflict stages and an index in an unborn repository. Scanning each worktree explicitly also covers private refs that Git 2.45's global traversal omits from other worktrees. Shared objects are deduplicated before inspecting blob contents. Ordinary large blobs are filtered from history traversal; only blobs of at most 1,024 bytes are read as pointer candidates. Object IDs and sizes are inspected in batches, without executing clean/smudge. Any canonical Syrnck pointer-shaped blob in that object set is included, regardless of attributes, path or file mode. Blob tags and symbolic-link blobs can therefore retain extra assets conservatively. Duplicate manifest IDs are merged; contradictory declared sizes or malformed pointers fail the scan. No LFS object is converted or fetched by this command. Unstaged working-file contents, untracked files, arbitrary alternate indexes, unregistered worktrees, submodule repositories and operational pseudo-refs such as ORIG_HEAD or FETCH_HEAD are not additional roots. Already retained pins remain protected. Run separate scans for submodule repositories as needed. The scan returns English JSON with version , a path-derived repository identity, an object-set fingerprint , counts for objects and commits , and sorted manifests containing ID and size. This is a metadata inventory, not proof that local Syrnck blocks exist. The common Git directory's canonical path determines the namespace; moving a repository can create a different namespace. Preview and apply # git retention reads the inventory and current Store references, then verifies every observed manifest's declared size, complete block closure and whole-file hash. It returns the scan fields plus: add : missing per-repository pins and their object keys. unobserved_pins : existing pins not observed through this inventory, with repository or legacy scope labels. ready and verification_error : whether all observed assets verified; a verification error is canonical English diagnostic data. applied and added : whether the requested update completed and its count. The default preview changes nothing. Missing or corrupt assets produce ready: false , exit 1, and no additions, including with --apply . Fetch or restore the missing content separately, then retry. A successful empty scan is ready and needs no new pins. With --apply , the inventory is read again before publication. A changed object set aborts with a retry diagnostic. New roots use: text Copy git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID The new roots are verified again and added in one compare-and-swap reference transaction. Either every addition is published, or none is. Existing matching pins are reused; a conflicting object under this namespace is an error. User references, native branches, transfer roots and existing Git pins are preserved. A repeat with no additions returns applied: true, added: 0 . The command writes neither Git history nor HEAD, index, configuration or working files. It makes no network request and invokes no Git publication hooks. Unobserved does not mean safe to delete # This release only adds protections. It never removes pins automatically. unobserved_pins is an inspection report, not a deletion plan or a reclaimable byte estimate. Legacy git/MANIFEST and git-assets/COMMIT pins may belong to another repository sharing the Store, or intentionally preserve deleted history. Old per-repository pins also remain until deliberately removed. Git and Syrnck do not share one transaction lock for refs, reflogs, indexes and worktrees. Rechecking detects a change between the two scans, but does not freeze Git after the second scan. Additions safely retain the captured snapshot; they cannot promise coverage of concurrent or later Git changes. Coordinate Git activity and rerun before maintenance that relies on this inventory. Dirty working files do not become retained versions through inspection alone. Automatic retirement needs further coordination and a recovery policy. Until then, inspect the report and retain desired versions before deliberate manual unpinning. Ordinary gc remains a preview; gc --delete only collects objects unreachable from all remaining pins and other supported retention roots. Transactions, cancellation and limits # The C++ Store API exposes RefUpdate and compare_update_refs(updates, operation) for atomic multi-reference updates. Each update has a name, optional expected old key and optional new key. Null expected means absent; null new key means removal. Duplicate names are errors. An expected-value mismatch returns false without changing references. All new closures and file hashes are verified before publishing one refs.json through the existing atomic writer. Other references are preserved. Callers must serialize Store access, including callbacks, as for other Store methods. The references document has a 16 MiB write limit, checked before publication. Known-unique, sorted keys are emitted without repeated linear duplicate searches. An oversized transaction leaves the previous document intact and readable. All Git retention commands support text/JSON progress and cooperative cancellation. Scanning counts object entries; verification uses the existing closure progress contract. Cancellation before reference publication leaves pins unchanged. There are no callbacks or cancellation checks after publication; an operation already committed can complete successfully after an interrupt. Child Git commands must finish before a pending cancellation can be observed. Current bounds are 4,096 worktrees, 1 MiB worktree-list output, 524,288 unique Git objects, 65,536 unique manifests and 64 MiB traversal output per worktree. Metadata headers are read in batches of 512 (64 KiB response cap); small blob bodies in batches of 128 (160 KiB cap). Ordinary blob payloads are not retained in memory across batches. IDs, sorted sets, fingerprints, pins and result JSON still grow with repository history. A full shared history is traversed once per worktree; there is no persistent incremental inventory yet. Shallow histories, graft history and environment overrides GIT_NAMESPACE , GIT_INDEX_FILE , GIT_SHALLOW_FILE , GIT_DIR , GIT_WORK_TREE , GIT_COMMON_DIR and GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY are refused. Missing/inaccessible registered worktrees or unavailable Git objects fail before publication. Lazy object fetching, replace objects and fsmonitor are disabled for the inspection commands. Traversal output on stderr is also refused, including warnings about reflogs that reference pruned commits even when Git exits successfully. Disable Git tracing or repair the reported Git metadata before retrying. Traversal follows Git's rev-list object options and batched object inspection . syrnck git retention-scan --progress syrnck git retention --progress syrnck git retention --apply --progress git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID","headings":[{"id":"git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection","text":"Git retention inventory and verified protection","level":1},{"id":"what-the-scan-observes","text":"What the scan observes","level":2},{"id":"preview-and-apply","text":"Preview and apply","level":2},{"id":"unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete","text":"Unobserved does not mean safe to delete","level":2},{"id":"transactions-cancellation-and-limits","text":"Transactions, cancellation and limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git retention-scan --progress\nsyrnck git retention --progress\nsyrnck git retention --apply --progress\ngit-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID","assets":[],"links":["#/en/api-store","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rev-list","https://git-scm.com/docs/git-cat-file"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"go-binding","title":"Go binding","description":"Go binding guide.","section":"API","order":10,"parent":"integrations","revision":"9","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"binding-go-du-moteur-c\">Go binding for the C++ engine\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/binding-go-du-moteur-c\" aria-label=\"Link to Go binding for the C++ engine\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Local module \u003ccode>syrnck.local/bindings/go\u003c/code> exposes Stores, CDC/fixed ingest, verified object IO/import, closures, pins and reconstruction with no external Go dependencies. Consumer projects currently need a local \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> until a permanent module address is published.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The engine remains C++20. Windows amd64/arm64 uses the system DLL loader without cgo or a C compiler; Linux/macOS uses cgo linking \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code>. Windows amd64 has local execution coverage; Linux/macOS and ARM64 still require validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tester\">Load the native library\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/tester\" aria-label=\"Link to Load the native library\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Build the matching C++ library. On Windows set \u003ccode>SYRNCK_LIBRARY\u003c/code> to the absolute trusted DLL path before first \u003ccode>Open\u003c/code>; it stays loaded for process lifetime. Unix uses cgo: configure \u003ccode>CGO_LDFLAGS=-L&lt;directory&gt;\u003c/code> and the loader path or distribution rpath. Go 1.22+ is required.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exemple\">Example\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/exemple\" aria-label=\"Link to Example\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Create\u003c/code> requires a new/empty directory; it is not open-or-create. \u003ccode>Options.Key\u003c/code> has zero or 32 bytes. The Go array is not retained. The application owns key lifetime and clearing its copies. The example copies a complete retained file between two local Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">source, err := syrnck.Open(&quot;source-store&quot;, syrnck.Options{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">defer source.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">destination, err := syrnck.Open(&quot;destination-store&quot;, syrnck.Options{Create: true})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">defer destination.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">id, err := source.Ingest(&quot;Map.uasset&quot;, syrnck.Chunking{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">    syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: &quot;manifest&quot;, ID: id}, &quot;sync/map-v1&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">fmt.Printf(&quot;%d bytes copied\\n&quot;, stats.Bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">return destination.Restore(id, &quot;Map-restored.uasset&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"catalog-transactions\">Prepare and publish compound state\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/catalog-transactions\" aria-label=\"Link to Prepare and publish compound state\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>PrepareBytes(data, chunking)\u003c/code> ingests at most 16 MiB from memory without a\nplaintext temporary file. \u003ccode>PrepareFile(path, chunking)\u003c/code> streams a file without\nthat input limit. Both return \u003cstrong>unpinned\u003c/strong> manifests, unlike the legacy \u003ccode>Ingest\u003c/code>\nmethod. \u003ccode>Put(kind, data)\u003c/code> creates an unpinned typed object: chunks stay exact;\nC++ validates and canonically encodes manifest/revision JSON and computes its\nnative BLAKE3 identity. Unknown fields, duplicate/escaped duplicate keys, invalid\nschemas, excessive nesting and oversized objects are rejected. Go callers do\nnot need to reproduce the native canonical encoder or object hash algorithm.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Keep the Store open through preparation and publication. GC after closing can\ncollect unpinned objects. Retain all data dependencies in a complete native\nrevision or explicit roots; an opaque chunk containing object IDs does not\nautomatically retain the referenced objects. A native revision&#39;s files and\nparents trace their complete dependencies through GC and backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Refs()\u003c/code> returns an independent \u003ccode>map[string]ObjectKey\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>CompareRefs(updates)\u003c/code>\natomically guards and replaces/removes a group of up to 65536 references, with\nat most 16 MiB of JSON. Every replacement closure and whole-file hash is verified\nbefore one reference-table publication. Duplicate names are errors. A nil\n\u003ccode>Expected\u003c/code> requires absence; a nil \u003ccode>Value\u003c/code> removes the reference. Nil/empty groups\nare no-ops. The result is meaningful only when \u003ccode>err == nil\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">if old, ok := current[&quot;ffi/catalog/main&quot;]; ok { expected = &amp;old }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">// Application-defined JSON describes a complete native revision whose files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">// reference the prepared manifests, with any required prior revision parents.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, &quot;revision&quot;, revisionJSON)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">    {Name: &quot;ffi/catalog/main&quot;, Expected: expected, Value: &amp;candidate},\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L13\" data-line=\"13\">if err != nil { return err } // Reload and establish the outcome before retrying.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L14\" data-line=\"14\">if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A stale expectation changes no member and returns false without an error.\nRefresh/replan instead of changing the expected value of an old operation.\nAn IO failure can occur after atomic publication: on error, the boolean is not\nevidence that publication did not happen. Reopen/read the table and compare the\nexact proposed group before retrying. The table is always wholly old or wholly\nnew, never a mixture produced by this transaction. Persist intent where a\nhigher-level workflow must reconcile an unknown outcome after a crash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All five operations have \u003ccode>Context\u003c/code> variants. Preparation and CAS propagate\nnative cancellation through ingestion/verification and before publication;\nsuccessful completion wins a late stop request. \u003ccode>PutContext\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>RefsContext\u003c/code>\ncheck cancellation before their bounded native call; encoding/compression/read\nalready in flight can finish. Failed preparation may leave complete unreferenced\nobjects, with no publication. Concurrent external file writers remain the\napplication&#39;s responsibility. These primitives support durable protocol state;\nthey do not implement Syncthing folder reconciliation or workspace checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"concurrence-et-conservation\">Concurrency and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/concurrence-et-conservation\" aria-label=\"Link to Concurrency and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Do not copy a \u003ccode>Store\u003c/code>. Methods/Close share a mutex. Native call/error retrieval stays on the same OS thread; no Go pointer is retained by C++. Close explicitly to release the process lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Ingest\u003c/code> pins automatically under \u003ccode>ffi/&lt;manifest&gt;\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Import\u003c/code> verifies ID/format without publication. \u003ccode>Pin\u003c/code> verifies the complete closure/file hashes before replacement; distinct names retain versions. \u003ccode>Unpin\u003c/code> may make data eligible for GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CopyMissing\u003c/code> is a local two-handle integration: stable lock order, verified presence comparison, one object at a time, then pin publication. Retry reuses objects across reopen. Cancellation is between objects. Interrupted unpublished data needs protection from GC until resume; this binding cannot run GC concurrently, but collection after close can remove unpinned data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Object bodies are 16 MiB maximum; complete closure lists load in memory up to 128 MiB in Go. Buffer reads currently query size then read, verifying twice. Pagination, persistent leases, buffer reuse and boundary overhead remain performance work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers\">Ranges and file comparison\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers\" aria-label=\"Link to Ranges and file comparison\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ReadRange(id,offset,length)\u003c/code> returns an exact range up to 16 MiB/call. Selected chunks are verified; a full-file range also checks the complete hash. \u003ccode>RestoreRange\u003c/code> streams to a new exclusive destination without that length cap. Out-of-range requests fail without truncation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CompareFiles(source,target)\u003c/code> returns typed reusable/new bytes, target blocks with optional source offset and a deduplicated list absent from source metadata. It does not prove a working file still has those bytes. Verify reused data and completed file before publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Methods share mutex/OS-thread rules, change no pins and cannot cancel mid-native-call. Comparison JSON is bounded 128 MiB then decoded. Matching rebuilt headers/library are required; ABI1 gains additive symbols. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-ranges\">ranges\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-comparison\">comparison\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-observation\">Workspace observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/workspace-observation\" aria-label=\"Link to Workspace observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenWorkspace(project,storePath,WorkspaceOptions{})\u003c/code> arms the watcher and retains the full inventory without an open Store. Use \u003ccode>Status(store,WorkspaceSelection{})\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Combined:true\u003c/code> for staging+working changes. Close the caller-opened Store after queries; \u003ccode>Wait(ctx,timeout)\u003c/code> returns typed native/polling hints. Batches remain retained until successful status, across waits/cancellation. \u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> requests a complete audit; schedule those independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Zero options use Windows/Linux notifications with polling fallback,4096 hints,65536 registrations. Calls/Close serialize; close explicitly. Status returns canonical \u003ccode>json.RawMessage\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>OpenWorkspaceContext\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>StatusContext\u003c/code> pass cancellation to recursive registration/hashing; Wait interrupts native waits. Context can abandon serialization waits. Each native source is per-call and released only after callback shutdown. Completed native results win over late cancellation; OS IO/JSON may finish first. Go does not expose C progress callbacks. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">ownership and limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"windows-test-cleanup\">Closing on Windows\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/windows-test-cleanup\" aria-label=\"Link to Closing on Windows\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Close Stores/sessions explicitly before removing their directories. The trusted DLL remains loaded for the process lifetime.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"syncthing\">Syncthing\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/syncthing\" aria-label=\"Link to Syncthing\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The optional \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing\">Syncthing module\u003c/a> adapts manifests to fixed SHA-256 BEP blocks using the pinned Syncthing v2.1.7 library. Authenticated device connections and differential transfers are tested against its real daemon. Syrnck objects and CDC retain their own format. Its native-backed catalog persists folder indexes, vectors, tombstones and verified data; staged downloads avoid holding Store across network waits. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-controller\">continuous file controller\u003c/a> applies verified versions to native workspaces with owned recovery. Full directory/symlink semantics, copies for concurrent protocol versions, discovery, a pairing UI and OS service installation remain unfinished.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registered-workspaces\">Registered workspaces\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/registered-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Link to Registered workspaces\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Open with \u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code> and select \u003ccode>store.UseWorkspace(&quot;edit&quot;)\u003c/code>\nbefore each status. Empty names use primary. Mismatched context fails; selection\nfailure leaves the prior name intact. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/registered-contexts\">linked contexts\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-workspace-actions\">Native capture and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/native-workspace-actions\" aria-label=\"Link to Native capture and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace.NativeState(store)\u003c/code> reads the selected native context, reference,\nrevision, materialization and pending checkout. \u003ccode>state.Expected()\u003c/code> freezes the\nexact target/revision/policy identity. Use it for \u003ccode>Capture(store, expected, message)\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>PlanCheckout(store, expected, candidate)\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>CheckoutOwned(store, expected, candidate, owner)\u003c/code>. Capture retains local changes;\ncheckout requires clean working files and preserves a recoverable owned journal.\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned(store, target, candidate, owner)\u003c/code> resumes only that exact pending\noperation. All five methods have Context variants and enforce the session&#39;s\nexact Store/context/directory binding. Mutation errors require re-reading state\nbecause publication may already have occurred. See the \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\">native action contract\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext(ctx, store, transaction)\u003c/code> reads one exact journal or\ncompleted receipt with the core decoder. It verifies the bound Store/context/\ndirectory. A committed receipt proves past application even if the user has\nedited the working file since; it does not assert that the workspace is clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreserving[Context]\u003c/code> explicitly archives regular-file edits and\ncreates exclusive conflict copies before resuming the exact owned native journal.\nUse the \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits\">active-edit contract\u003c/a>; ordinary\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned\u003c/code> remains strict, and every mutation error requires reloading state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Capture I/O report\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/go-binding/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Link to Capture I/O report\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>CaptureReportContext\u003c/code> returns the revision and source read/reuse counters.\n\u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code> keeps its revision-only result. Session manifests can survive\nStore reopens, with complete enumeration and object verification. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/performance/incremental-capture\">capture boundaries\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Go binding for the C++ engine # Local module syrnck.local/bindings/go exposes Stores, CDC/fixed ingest, verified object IO/import, closures, pins and reconstruction with no external Go dependencies. Consumer projects currently need a local replace until a permanent module address is published. The engine remains C++20. Windows amd64/arm64 uses the system DLL loader without cgo or a C compiler; Linux/macOS uses cgo linking syrnck_ffi . Windows amd64 has local execution coverage; Linux/macOS and ARM64 still require validation. Load the native library # Build the matching C++ library. On Windows set SYRNCK_LIBRARY to the absolute trusted DLL path before first Open ; it stays loaded for process lifetime. Unix uses cgo: configure CGO_LDFLAGS=-L\u003cdirectory> and the loader path or distribution rpath. Go 1.22+ is required. Example # Create requires a new/empty directory; it is not open-or-create. Options.Key has zero or 32 bytes. The Go array is not retained. The application owns key lifetime and clearing its copies. The example copies a complete retained file between two local Stores. go Copy source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{}) if err != nil { return err } defer source.Close() destination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true}) if err != nil { return err } defer destination.Close() id, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{}) if err != nil { return err } stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination, syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\") if err != nil { return err } fmt.Printf(\"%d bytes copied\\n\", stats.Bytes) return destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\") Prepare and publish compound state # PrepareBytes(data, chunking) ingests at most 16 MiB from memory without a plaintext temporary file. PrepareFile(path, chunking) streams a file without that input limit. Both return unpinned manifests, unlike the legacy Ingest method. Put(kind, data) creates an unpinned typed object: chunks stay exact; C++ validates and canonically encodes manifest/revision JSON and computes its native BLAKE3 identity. Unknown fields, duplicate/escaped duplicate keys, invalid schemas, excessive nesting and oversized objects are rejected. Go callers do not need to reproduce the native canonical encoder or object hash algorithm. Keep the Store open through preparation and publication. GC after closing can collect unpinned objects. Retain all data dependencies in a complete native revision or explicit roots; an opaque chunk containing object IDs does not automatically retain the referenced objects. A native revision's files and parents trace their complete dependencies through GC and backups. Refs() returns an independent map[string]ObjectKey . CompareRefs(updates) atomically guards and replaces/removes a group of up to 65536 references, with at most 16 MiB of JSON. Every replacement closure and whole-file hash is verified before one reference-table publication. Duplicate names are errors. A nil Expected requires absence; a nil Value removes the reference. Nil/empty groups are no-ops. The result is meaningful only when err == nil : go Copy current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx) if err != nil { return err } var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey if old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old } // Application-defined JSON describes a complete native revision whose files // reference the prepared manifests, with any required prior revision parents. candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON) if err != nil { return err } applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{ {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate}, }) if err != nil { return err } // Reload and establish the outcome before retrying. if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan } A stale expectation changes no member and returns false without an error. Refresh/replan instead of changing the expected value of an old operation. An IO failure can occur after atomic publication: on error, the boolean is not evidence that publication did not happen. Reopen/read the table and compare the exact proposed group before retrying. The table is always wholly old or wholly new, never a mixture produced by this transaction. Persist intent where a higher-level workflow must reconcile an unknown outcome after a crash. All five operations have Context variants. Preparation and CAS propagate native cancellation through ingestion/verification and before publication; successful completion wins a late stop request. PutContext and RefsContext check cancellation before their bounded native call; encoding/compression/read already in flight can finish. Failed preparation may leave complete unreferenced objects, with no publication. Concurrent external file writers remain the application's responsibility. These primitives support durable protocol state; they do not implement Syncthing folder reconciliation or workspace checkout. Concurrency and retention # Do not copy a Store . Methods/Close share a mutex. Native call/error retrieval stays on the same OS thread; no Go pointer is retained by C++. Close explicitly to release the process lock. Ingest pins automatically under ffi/\u003cmanifest> . Import verifies ID/format without publication. Pin verifies the complete closure/file hashes before replacement; distinct names retain versions. Unpin may make data eligible for GC. CopyMissing is a local two-handle integration: stable lock order, verified presence comparison, one object at a time, then pin publication. Retry reuses objects across reopen. Cancellation is between objects. Interrupted unpublished data needs protection from GC until resume; this binding cannot run GC concurrently, but collection after close can remove unpinned data. Object bodies are 16 MiB maximum; complete closure lists load in memory up to 128 MiB in Go. Buffer reads currently query size then read, verifying twice. Pagination, persistent leases, buffer reuse and boundary overhead remain performance work. Ranges and file comparison # ReadRange(id,offset,length) returns an exact range up to 16 MiB/call. Selected chunks are verified; a full-file range also checks the complete hash. RestoreRange streams to a new exclusive destination without that length cap. Out-of-range requests fail without truncation. CompareFiles(source,target) returns typed reusable/new bytes, target blocks with optional source offset and a deduplicated list absent from source metadata. It does not prove a working file still has those bytes. Verify reused data and completed file before publication. Methods share mutex/OS-thread rules, change no pins and cannot cancel mid-native-call. Comparison JSON is bounded 128 MiB then decoded. Matching rebuilt headers/library are required; ABI1 gains additive symbols. See ranges and comparison . Workspace observation # OpenWorkspace(project,storePath,WorkspaceOptions{}) arms the watcher and retains the full inventory without an open Store. Use Status(store,WorkspaceSelection{}) or Combined:true for staging+working changes. Close the caller-opened Store after queries; Wait(ctx,timeout) returns typed native/polling hints. Batches remain retained until successful status, across waits/cancellation. Invalidate requests a complete audit; schedule those independently. Zero options use Windows/Linux notifications with polling fallback,4096 hints,65536 registrations. Calls/Close serialize; close explicitly. Status returns canonical json.RawMessage . OpenWorkspaceContext / StatusContext pass cancellation to recursive registration/hashing; Wait interrupts native waits. Context can abandon serialization waits. Each native source is per-call and released only after callback shutdown. Completed native results win over late cancellation; OS IO/JSON may finish first. Go does not expose C progress callbacks. See ownership and limits . Closing on Windows # Close Stores/sessions explicitly before removing their directories. The trusted DLL remains loaded for the process lifetime. Syncthing # The optional Syncthing module adapts manifests to fixed SHA-256 BEP blocks using the pinned Syncthing v2.1.7 library. Authenticated device connections and differential transfers are tested against its real daemon. Syrnck objects and CDC retain their own format. Its native-backed catalog persists folder indexes, vectors, tombstones and verified data; staged downloads avoid holding Store across network waits. The continuous file controller applies verified versions to native workspaces with owned recovery. Full directory/symlink semantics, copies for concurrent protocol versions, discovery, a pairing UI and OS service installation remain unfinished. Registered workspaces # Open with WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} and select store.UseWorkspace(\"edit\") before each status. Empty names use primary. Mismatched context fails; selection failure leaves the prior name intact. See linked contexts . Native capture and recovery # workspace.NativeState(store) reads the selected native context, reference, revision, materialization and pending checkout. state.Expected() freezes the exact target/revision/policy identity. Use it for Capture(store, expected, message) , PlanCheckout(store, expected, candidate) and CheckoutOwned(store, expected, candidate, owner) . Capture retains local changes; checkout requires clean working files and preserves a recoverable owned journal. RecoverOwned(store, target, candidate, owner) resumes only that exact pending operation. All five methods have Context variants and enforce the session's exact Store/context/directory binding. Mutation errors require re-reading state because publication may already have occurred. See the native action contract . CheckoutStatusContext(ctx, store, transaction) reads one exact journal or completed receipt with the core decoder. It verifies the bound Store/context/ directory. A committed receipt proves past application even if the user has edited the working file since; it does not assert that the workspace is clean. RecoverOwnedPreserving[Context] explicitly archives regular-file edits and creates exclusive conflict copies before resuming the exact owned native journal. Use the active-edit contract ; ordinary RecoverOwned remains strict, and every mutation error requires reloading state. Capture I/O report # CaptureReportContext returns the revision and source read/reuse counters. CaptureContext keeps its revision-only result. Session manifests can survive Store reopens, with complete enumeration and object verification. See capture boundaries . source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{}) if err != nil { return err } defer source.Close() destination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true}) if err != nil { return err } defer destination.Close() id, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{}) if err != nil { return err } stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination, syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\") if err != nil { return err } fmt.Printf(\"%d bytes copied\\n\", stats.Bytes) return destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\") current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx) if err != nil { return err } var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey if old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old } // Application-defined JSON describes a complete native revision whose files // reference the prepared manifests, with any required prior revision parents. candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON) if err != nil { return err } applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{ {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate}, }) if err != nil { return err } // Reload and establish the outcome before retrying. if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }","headings":[{"id":"binding-go-du-moteur-c","text":"Go binding for the C++ engine","level":1},{"id":"tester","text":"Load the native library","level":2},{"id":"exemple","text":"Example","level":2},{"id":"catalog-transactions","text":"Prepare and publish compound state","level":2},{"id":"concurrence-et-conservation","text":"Concurrency and retention","level":2},{"id":"plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers","text":"Ranges and file comparison","level":2},{"id":"workspace-observation","text":"Workspace observation","level":2},{"id":"windows-test-cleanup","text":"Closing on Windows","level":2},{"id":"syncthing","text":"Syncthing","level":2},{"id":"registered-workspaces","text":"Registered workspaces","level":2},{"id":"native-workspace-actions","text":"Native capture and recovery","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Capture I/O report","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-2-L13","code-2-L14"],"languages":["go"],"code":"source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer source.Close()\ndestination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer destination.Close()\n\nid, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{})\nif err != nil { return err }\nstats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination,\n    syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\")\nif err != nil { return err }\nfmt.Printf(\"%d bytes copied\\n\", stats.Bytes)\nreturn destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\")\ncurrent, err := store.RefsContext(ctx)\nif err != nil { return err }\nvar expected *syrnck.ObjectKey\nif old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old }\n\n// Application-defined JSON describes a complete native revision whose files\n// reference the prepared manifests, with any required prior revision parents.\ncandidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON)\nif err != nil { return err }\napplied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{\n    {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate},\n})\nif err != nil { return err } // Reload and establish the outcome before retrying.\nif !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }","assets":[],"links":["#/en/file-ranges","#/en/file-comparison","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/syncthing","#/en/syncthing/folder-controller","#/en/workspace-abi/registered-contexts","#/en/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions","#/en/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits","#/en/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"indexes","title":"Paged pack indexes","description":"Paged pack indexes guide.","section":"Storage","order":10,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs\">Paged pack indexes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/indexes/lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs\" aria-label=\"Link to Paged pack indexes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The engine reads \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code> indexes directly from disk, retaining page fences/digests, a negative-key filter and a bounded page cache instead of a complete ID/location table. No physical v2/v3, object, envelope or network bytes change; no auxiliary index or automatic conversion is added.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-et-recherche\">Validation and lookup\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/indexes/validation-et-recherche\" aria-label=\"Link to Validation and lookup\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Opening and auditing stream the entire index with 256 records (11,520 bytes). Header/count/types, strict order, contiguous ranges and the configuration’s complete BLAKE3 index hash are verified. Each page records first/last key and digest; each pack has a roughly 10-bit-per-entry presence filter with seven probes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A negative rejects absent keys without page IO. A positive still requires the index; it does not validate an object. Binary search selects a candidate page. Index file identity and size are checked when opened; each reread page must match its validated digest before addresses are used. Changed, truncated or missing pages fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Indexes are independently sorted. Overlapping key intervals trigger a merged traversal checking that no key belongs to two active packs. This holds one page per participating pack plus a head heap: up to 11.25 MiB of page data at 1,024 packs. Disjoint intervals avoid this merge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-et-snapshot\">Cache and snapshot\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/indexes/cache-et-snapshot\" aria-label=\"Link to Cache and snapshot\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Verified hot pages and the last address are a snapshot of the opened catalog under its lock. External index changes may remain invisible on hot entries; reread pages must match validated digests. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> reload and completely hash indexes rather than using old pages as evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This concerns addresses only. Envelopes are still reread and checked before decoded-cache reuse; authentication, ID and format rules are unchanged. Filters, directories and pages retain no objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reglages-et-compteurs\">Configuration and counters\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/indexes/reglages-et-compteurs\" aria-label=\"Link to Configuration and counters\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The page cache defaults to 4 MiB, independently of the 64 MiB decoded cache. The global option accepts 0..1024 MiB; zero disables page/last-address caching while retaining directory/filter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ uses \u003ccode>StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes\u003c/code> as the fourth \u003ccode>Store\u003c/code> constructor argument. Maintenance’s temporary readers inherit it. It budgets page data per reader, shared across that catalog’s indexes; temporary snapshots, structures and buffers add costs. Oversized pages use temporary buffers without admission.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>io_stats()\u003c/code> reports \u003ccode>index_directory_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_filter_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_pages\u003c/code>, including useful capacities but excluding allocator/container overhead, paths, JSON, temporary buffers and OS pages. They are not process RSS. \u003ccode>clear_cache()\u003c/code> releases decoded/page caches and keeps the validated directory; \u003ccode>reset_io_stats()\u003c/code> keeps caches and memory snapshots.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Indexes contain no decoded file data; types, IDs, sizes and offsets remain visible. Reconstruction, closure, ingest and inventory sessions reuse at most eight LRU index handles plus eight possible data-pack handles. Nested sessions share them; outer return/exception and retirement close them. Isolated lookups open/close their missing-page handle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get &lt;manifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"memoire-et-limites\">Memory and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/indexes/memoire-et-limites\" aria-label=\"Link to Memory and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The directory uses 98 bytes per page and the filter approximately 10 bits per entry. At 1,048,576 entries: 401,408 directory bytes, 1,310,720 filter bytes, before cache/overhead; the unchanged index file is 47,185,936 bytes. Index construction also streams records instead of preparing the entire file buffer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Limits remain 1,048,576 objects/pack, 1,024 packs, 4,194,304 packed objects total. Opening validates linearly; random lookups may cost more IO than an in-memory table, especially without cache. Filters reduce but do not eliminate false positives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>GC marks packed entries with one bit and streams dead/survivor inventories and replacement readback. Enumeration pages own their buffers, safe across nested lookups even without cache. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> likewise enumerates revisions, manifests and chunks by type/page fences without a full inventory, preserving boundary positions. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/audit\">audits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Public \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> still materialize IDs. Packing/compaction retain only loose keys for sorting; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/compaction\">streaming plans\u003c/a>. GC/audit still need compact IDs absent from packs, dependency stacks and current-file JSON. There is no global project-independent RAM ceiling. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs/memoire-du-gc\">GC budgets\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance\">performance\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Paged pack indexes # The engine reads SYRIDX01 indexes directly from disk, retaining page fences/digests, a negative-key filter and a bounded page cache instead of a complete ID/location table. No physical v2/v3, object, envelope or network bytes change; no auxiliary index or automatic conversion is added. Validation and lookup # Opening and auditing stream the entire index with 256 records (11,520 bytes). Header/count/types, strict order, contiguous ranges and the configuration’s complete BLAKE3 index hash are verified. Each page records first/last key and digest; each pack has a roughly 10-bit-per-entry presence filter with seven probes. A negative rejects absent keys without page IO. A positive still requires the index; it does not validate an object. Binary search selects a candidate page. Index file identity and size are checked when opened; each reread page must match its validated digest before addresses are used. Changed, truncated or missing pages fail. Indexes are independently sorted. Overlapping key intervals trigger a merged traversal checking that no key belongs to two active packs. This holds one page per participating pack plus a head heap: up to 11.25 MiB of page data at 1,024 packs. Disjoint intervals avoid this merge. Cache and snapshot # Verified hot pages and the last address are a snapshot of the opened catalog under its lock. External index changes may remain invisible on hot entries; reread pages must match validated digests. fsck and gc reload and completely hash indexes rather than using old pages as evidence. This concerns addresses only. Envelopes are still reread and checked before decoded-cache reuse; authentication, ID and format rules are unchanged. Filters, directories and pages retain no objects. Configuration and counters # The page cache defaults to 4 MiB, independently of the 64 MiB decoded cache. The global option accepts 0..1024 MiB; zero disables page/last-address caching while retaining directory/filter. C++ uses StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes as the fourth Store constructor argument. Maintenance’s temporary readers inherit it. It budgets page data per reader, shared across that catalog’s indexes; temporary snapshots, structures and buffers add costs. Oversized pages use temporary buffers without admission. io_stats() reports index_directory_bytes , index_filter_bytes , index_cached_bytes , index_cached_pages , including useful capacities but excluding allocator/container overhead, paths, JSON, temporary buffers and OS pages. They are not process RSS. clear_cache() releases decoded/page caches and keeps the validated directory; reset_io_stats() keeps caches and memory snapshots. Indexes contain no decoded file data; types, IDs, sizes and offsets remain visible. Reconstruction, closure, ingest and inventory sessions reuse at most eight LRU index handles plus eight possible data-pack handles. Nested sessions share them; outer return/exception and retirement close them. Isolated lookups open/close their missing-page handle. text Copy syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck Memory and limits # The directory uses 98 bytes per page and the filter approximately 10 bits per entry. At 1,048,576 entries: 401,408 directory bytes, 1,310,720 filter bytes, before cache/overhead; the unchanged index file is 47,185,936 bytes. Index construction also streams records instead of preparing the entire file buffer. Limits remain 1,048,576 objects/pack, 1,024 packs, 4,194,304 packed objects total. Opening validates linearly; random lookups may cost more IO than an in-memory table, especially without cache. Filters reduce but do not eliminate false positives. GC marks packed entries with one bit and streams dead/survivor inventories and replacement readback. Enumeration pages own their buffers, safe across nested lookups even without cache. fsck likewise enumerates revisions, manifests and chunks by type/page fences without a full inventory, preserving boundary positions. See audits . Public objects() / closure() still materialize IDs. Packing/compaction retain only loose keys for sorting; see streaming plans . GC/audit still need compact IDs absent from packs, dependency stacks and current-file JSON. There is no global project-independent RAM ceiling. See GC budgets and performance . syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck","headings":[{"id":"lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs","text":"Paged pack indexes","level":1},{"id":"validation-et-recherche","text":"Validation and lookup","level":2},{"id":"cache-et-snapshot","text":"Cache and snapshot","level":2},{"id":"reglages-et-compteurs","text":"Configuration and counters","level":2},{"id":"memoire-et-limites","text":"Memory and limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\nsyrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck","assets":[],"links":["#/en/audit","#/en/compaction","#/en/packs/memoire-du-gc","#/en/performance"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"integrations","title":"Library integrations","description":"Library integrations","section":"API","order":700,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"cpp\">C++20\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/integrations/cpp\" aria-label=\"Link to C++20\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use the distributed headers and CMake targets. \u003ccode>syrnck_core\u003c/code> owns storage and local revision/workspace operations; \u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> adds Git integration; \u003ccode>syrnck_net\u003c/code> adds HTTP; \u003ccode>syrnck_git_net\u003c/code> connects Git catalogs to transfers. The \u003ccode>Syrnck::core\u003c/code> alias is available in the source build. Static C++ archives require their native dependencies to be linked as well.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-store\">Store API\u003c/a> documents lock ownership, object verification and publication. \u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">Progress\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">backups\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text differences\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-watch\">workspace observation\u003c/a> describe operation-specific contracts. This reference uses explicit annotations from public distributed headers; it does not infer overload semantics or distribute implementation source listings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-go\">C and Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/integrations/c-go\" aria-label=\"Link to C and Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> exports the C ABI; \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi_static\u003c/code> uses SYRNCK_STATIC. Keep matching binary/header versions and never pass C++ objects across the boundary. Follow \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-c-abi\">C ownership/error rules\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/go-binding\">Go integration\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">embedded workspace sessions\u003c/a>. Windows uses dynamic DLL calls; Unix Go builds use cgo. Serialize handle operations, and capture native errors on the calling OS thread.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The separate \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing\">Syncthing adapter\u003c/a> implements the regular-file block-data subset against its pinned reference dependency. It does not manage device pairing, causal versions, deletions or a resident sync daemon.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"platforms\">Platforms and interoperability\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/integrations/platforms\" aria-label=\"Link to Platforms and interoperability\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Build separate binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS and each architecture. The source supports MSVC and GCC/Clang with C++20. Native dependencies include BLAKE3, Zstandard, Monocypher, utf8proc, JSON and the optional networking libraries. \u003ca href=\"#/en/licenses\">Notices\u003c/a> travel with distribution.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a minimal embedded build, disable CLI, network, benchmarks and tests; documentation generation can also be disabled explicitly. No Unreal headers or Unreal runtime are required by the engine. A future engine plugin is separate work. Lore and Syncthing designs inform some choices, but native Syrnck object/wire formats are distinct. BEP compatibility belongs to the explicit adapter, not to FastCDC or BLAKE3 objects.\u003c/p>\n","text":"C++20 # Use the distributed headers and CMake targets. syrnck_core owns storage and local revision/workspace operations; syrnck_git adds Git integration; syrnck_net adds HTTP; syrnck_git_net connects Git catalogs to transfers. The Syrnck::core alias is available in the source build. Static C++ archives require their native dependencies to be linked as well. The Store API documents lock ownership, object verification and publication. Progress , backups , text differences and workspace observation describe operation-specific contracts. This reference uses explicit annotations from public distributed headers; it does not infer overload semantics or distribute implementation source listings. C and Go # syrnck_ffi exports the C ABI; syrnck_ffi_static uses SYRNCK_STATIC. Keep matching binary/header versions and never pass C++ objects across the boundary. Follow C ownership/error rules , Go integration and embedded workspace sessions . Windows uses dynamic DLL calls; Unix Go builds use cgo. Serialize handle operations, and capture native errors on the calling OS thread. The separate Syncthing adapter implements the regular-file block-data subset against its pinned reference dependency. It does not manage device pairing, causal versions, deletions or a resident sync daemon. Platforms and interoperability # Build separate binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS and each architecture. The source supports MSVC and GCC/Clang with C++20. Native dependencies include BLAKE3, Zstandard, Monocypher, utf8proc, JSON and the optional networking libraries. Notices travel with distribution. For a minimal embedded build, disable CLI, network, benchmarks and tests; documentation generation can also be disabled explicitly. No Unreal headers or Unreal runtime are required by the engine. A future engine plugin is separate work. Lore and Syncthing designs inform some choices, but native Syrnck object/wire formats are distinct. BEP compatibility belongs to the explicit adapter, not to FastCDC or BLAKE3 objects.","headings":[{"id":"cpp","text":"C++20","level":2},{"id":"c-go","text":"C and Go","level":2},{"id":"platforms","text":"Platforms and interoperability","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/api-store","#/en/operations","#/en/backups","#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-watch","#/en/api-c-abi","#/en/go-binding","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/syncthing","#/en/licenses"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"licenses","title":"License and third-party notices","description":"License and third-party notices","section":"Syrnck","order":999,"revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"license\">Syrnck license\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/licenses/license\" aria-label=\"Link to Syrnck license\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Syrnck’s original code is MIT. The following original notice applies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">MIT License\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">copies or substantial portions of the Software.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">SOFTWARE.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"dependencies\">Components and distribution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/licenses/dependencies\" aria-label=\"Link to Components and distribution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Native components retain their individual licenses. The table below preserves the original notice. The installed engine also supplies complete applicable license files under share/licenses/Syrnck. The optional Syncthing Go module is MPL-2.0, downloaded separately; MIT does not relicense it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The local reader uses CyDoc 0.1.0 (MIT), marked (MIT) and Prism (MIT); esbuild (MIT) builds the reader. Their license files ship in DocData/licenses. The authoring dependency is pinned to the vendored CyDoc archive; the reader loads no remote library. No optional media module or Three.js is included.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Pinned version\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>License / notice\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>BLAKE3 C implementation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.8.7\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>c/\u003c/code> of the official blake3 1.8.7 crate, matching the Rust prototype&#39;s hash implementation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Apache-2.0 / CC0; LLVM exception where applicable, included in \u003ccode>third_party/blake3/LICENSE_*\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>FastCDC 2020 port and gear/mask tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nathan Fiedler&#39;s fastcdc crate, \u003ccode>v2020::cut_gear\u003c/code> and tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; \u003ccode>third_party/fastcdc/LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Monocypher\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4.0.3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://monocypher.org/download/monocypher-4.0.3.tar.gz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official download\u003c/a>, \u003ccode>src/monocypher.c\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>src/monocypher.h\u003c/code>, optional \u003ccode>monocypher-ed25519.c\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>.h\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BSD-2-Clause / CC0; included \u003ccode>LICENCE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>nlohmann/json\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3.12.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/tag/v3.12.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official release\u003c/a>, single-header distribution\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>LICENSE.MIT\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>cpp-httplib\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.28.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/tree/v0.28.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>, HTTP server only\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>utf8proc\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2.10.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/JuliaStrings/utf8proc/tree/v2.10.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT and Unicode data terms; included \u003ccode>LICENSE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>PicoSHA2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/okdshin/PicoSHA2/tree/161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>, unmodified \u003ccode>picosha2.h\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>third_party/picosha2/LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Zstd\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.5.7\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/facebook/zstd/releases/download/v1.5.7/zstd-1.5.7.tar.gz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official release archive\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BSD-3-Clause / GPL-2.0 option; licenses in the fetched archive\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>curl / libcurl\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>8.16.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/download/curl-8.16.0.tar.xz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official archive\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>curl license; \u003ccode>COPYING\u003c/code> in the fetched archive\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.1.0, \u003ccode>e038c474577e1f305df3eba421f2b9c03b0f1d8d\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/MrMybal/CyDoc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CyDoc\u003c/a>, packed archive in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/vendor\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/vendor/CyDoc.LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>marked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>17.0.6\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/marked.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Prism\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.30.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/Prism.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>esbuild\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.25.12\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Documentation build tool, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/esbuild.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n","text":"Syrnck license # Syrnck’s original code is MIT. The following original notice applies. text Copy MIT License Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE. Components and distribution # Native components retain their individual licenses. The table below preserves the original notice. The installed engine also supplies complete applicable license files under share/licenses/Syrnck. The optional Syncthing Go module is MPL-2.0, downloaded separately; MIT does not relicense it. The local reader uses CyDoc 0.1.0 (MIT), marked (MIT) and Prism (MIT); esbuild (MIT) builds the reader. Their license files ship in DocData/licenses. The authoring dependency is pinned to the vendored CyDoc archive; the reader loads no remote library. No optional media module or Three.js is included. Component Pinned version Source License / notice BLAKE3 C implementation 1.8.7 c/ of the official blake3 1.8.7 crate, matching the Rust prototype's hash implementation Apache-2.0 / CC0; LLVM exception where applicable, included in third_party/blake3/LICENSE_* FastCDC 2020 port and gear/mask tables fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0 Nathan Fiedler's fastcdc crate, v2020::cut_gear and tables MIT; third_party/fastcdc/LICENSE Monocypher 4.0.3 Official download , src/monocypher.c , src/monocypher.h , optional monocypher-ed25519.c / .h BSD-2-Clause / CC0; included LICENCE.md nlohmann/json 3.12.0 Official release , single-header distribution MIT; included LICENSE.MIT cpp-httplib 0.28.0 Official repository , HTTP server only MIT; included LICENSE utf8proc 2.10.0 Official repository MIT and Unicode data terms; included LICENSE.md PicoSHA2 161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29 Official repository , unmodified picosha2.h MIT; included third_party/picosha2/LICENSE Zstd 1.5.7 Official release archive BSD-3-Clause / GPL-2.0 option; licenses in the fetched archive curl / libcurl 8.16.0 Official archive curl license; COPYING in the fetched archive CyDoc 0.1.0, e038c474577e1f305df3eba421f2b9c03b0f1d8d CyDoc , packed archive in .CyDocBuild/vendor MIT; .CyDocBuild/vendor/CyDoc.LICENSE marked 17.0.6 CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/marked.txt Prism 1.30.0 CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/Prism.txt esbuild 0.25.12 Documentation build tool, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/esbuild.txt MIT License Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.","headings":[{"id":"license","text":"Syrnck license","level":2},{"id":"dependencies","text":"Components and distribution","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21"],"languages":["text"],"code":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.","assets":[],"links":["https://monocypher.org/download/monocypher-4.0.3.tar.gz","https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/tag/v3.12.0","https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/tree/v0.28.0","https://github.com/JuliaStrings/utf8proc/tree/v2.10.0","https://github.com/okdshin/PicoSHA2/tree/161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29","https://github.com/facebook/zstd/releases/download/v1.5.7/zstd-1.5.7.tar.gz","https://curl.se/download/curl-8.16.0.tar.xz","https://github.com/MrMybal/CyDoc"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"limits","title":"Current limits and planned work","description":"Current limits and planned work","section":"Syrnck","order":900,"revision":"21","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"workspace\">Workspace and versioning\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/limits/workspace\" aria-label=\"Link to Workspace and versioning\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">Named and detached HEAD\u003c/a> are supported. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">Linked native workspaces\u003c/a> retain separate HEADs/indexes and portable backup snapshots. Partial materialization, recoverable selection changes, hard reset and stash with matching selections are available. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives\">Archived unregistration and restoration\u003c/a> preserve captured dirty work, complete HEAD/index trees and context roots. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation\">Linked relocation\u003c/a> preserves the whole directory on one filesystem, with resume/abort and case changes; cross-filesystem and primary/Store moves remain work to complete. The persistent working index and observer avoid some repeated hashing but do not produce an atomic filesystem snapshot or a complete event history. Native OS events may overflow; conservative rescans and polling remain necessary.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Exact-content renames are recognized, while edited renames/copies, their merge/rebase integration and recursive synthesis of multiple merge bases remain incomplete. Legacy v1 revisions have no author or signature fields. Optional \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">v2 authorship\u003c/a> records authors/committers and Ed25519 signatures; \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-signatures\">server policies\u003c/a> can authorize keys and identities. Registration is manual and policy changes require restart; OS signing-key vaults and distributed revocation remain incomplete. Read each rebase/merge mode&#39;s constraints; available commands are not universal Git compatibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sync\">Team operation and continuous synchronization\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/limits/sync\" aria-label=\"Link to Team operation and continuous synchronization\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Configured servers provide users/roles, branch checks and expiring asset leases. Native history transfer and Git catalog publication remain separate from a distributed multi-server transaction. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent\">resident publication agent\u003c/a> captures local edits, deletions and exact renames, retains offline snapshots and retries guarded native pushes. The optional bidirectional mode applies incoming descendants through recoverable checkout and merges compatible concurrent histories. Unresolved conflicts, acknowledged remote deletions/rewinds and rewritten local history are blocked. The agent resumes only its own guarded checkout/merge transactions, including offline recovery of prepared signed targets without a private signing key. Manual or unrelated recovery stays explicit. The explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent/conflict-copies\">conflict-copy policy\u003c/a> preserves concurrent file contents; namespace collisions still block. OS service installation remains unfinished.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The Syncthing adapter handles regular-file block indexes/requests/responses and \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/device-connections\">pinned device connections\u003c/a>. A real upstream daemon is tested for differential transfers in both directions. A native-backed \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-catalog\">folder catalog\u003c/a> persists device index generations, version vectors, tombstones and verified files; conflicts remain explicit and historical data stays retained. Real-daemon reconnection after an offline rename is tested. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-controller\">continuous controller\u003c/a> now applies regular files/tombstones to actual native workspaces, with owned recovery and live-daemon reconnection tests. Explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">concurrent-version copies\u003c/a> merge regular-file vectors while retaining different losing contents. Directory/symlink metadata, OS service installation, discovery, a pairing UI, catalog membership management and automatic history retirement remain unfinished.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security\">Security, retention and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/limits/security\" aria-label=\"Link to Security, retention and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Objects can be encrypted at rest, with separately keyed backups/restores. Trusted servers receive plaintext objects over the secured transport. End-to-end encryption, password-based keys, system key vaults, distributed rotation/revocation, automatic replica repair and distributed GC remain separate work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Retention is explicit. Archives preserve versions until their protecting roots are retired; a verified backup can protect manual retirement, but automatic age/release policies are not implemented. Process interruption and power-loss durability are different guarantees. No operation establishes universal power-loss, network-filesystem or hostile external-writer safety.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">active-checkout edit preservation\u003c/a>\narchives regular-file contents before conflict-copy moves and resumes the strict\nowned journal. Missing-file/deletion intent and ambiguous namespace changes still\nneed resolution. The retained edit archive groups currently require manual\nretention management; automatic age/backup-based retirement remains unfinished.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Workspace and versioning # Named and detached HEAD are supported. Linked native workspaces retain separate HEADs/indexes and portable backup snapshots. Partial materialization, recoverable selection changes, hard reset and stash with matching selections are available. Archived unregistration and restoration preserve captured dirty work, complete HEAD/index trees and context roots. Linked relocation preserves the whole directory on one filesystem, with resume/abort and case changes; cross-filesystem and primary/Store moves remain work to complete. The persistent working index and observer avoid some repeated hashing but do not produce an atomic filesystem snapshot or a complete event history. Native OS events may overflow; conservative rescans and polling remain necessary. Exact-content renames are recognized, while edited renames/copies, their merge/rebase integration and recursive synthesis of multiple merge bases remain incomplete. Legacy v1 revisions have no author or signature fields. Optional v2 authorship records authors/committers and Ed25519 signatures; server policies can authorize keys and identities. Registration is manual and policy changes require restart; OS signing-key vaults and distributed revocation remain incomplete. Read each rebase/merge mode's constraints; available commands are not universal Git compatibility. Team operation and continuous synchronization # Configured servers provide users/roles, branch checks and expiring asset leases. Native history transfer and Git catalog publication remain separate from a distributed multi-server transaction. The resident publication agent captures local edits, deletions and exact renames, retains offline snapshots and retries guarded native pushes. The optional bidirectional mode applies incoming descendants through recoverable checkout and merges compatible concurrent histories. Unresolved conflicts, acknowledged remote deletions/rewinds and rewritten local history are blocked. The agent resumes only its own guarded checkout/merge transactions, including offline recovery of prepared signed targets without a private signing key. Manual or unrelated recovery stays explicit. The explicit conflict-copy policy preserves concurrent file contents; namespace collisions still block. OS service installation remains unfinished. The Syncthing adapter handles regular-file block indexes/requests/responses and pinned device connections . A real upstream daemon is tested for differential transfers in both directions. A native-backed folder catalog persists device index generations, version vectors, tombstones and verified files; conflicts remain explicit and historical data stays retained. Real-daemon reconnection after an offline rename is tested. The continuous controller now applies regular files/tombstones to actual native workspaces, with owned recovery and live-daemon reconnection tests. Explicit concurrent-version copies merge regular-file vectors while retaining different losing contents. Directory/symlink metadata, OS service installation, discovery, a pairing UI, catalog membership management and automatic history retirement remain unfinished. Security, retention and recovery # Objects can be encrypted at rest, with separately keyed backups/restores. Trusted servers receive plaintext objects over the secured transport. End-to-end encryption, password-based keys, system key vaults, distributed rotation/revocation, automatic replica repair and distributed GC remain separate work. Retention is explicit. Archives preserve versions until their protecting roots are retired; a verified backup can protect manual retirement, but automatic age/release policies are not implemented. Process interruption and power-loss durability are different guarantees. No operation establishes universal power-loss, network-filesystem or hostile external-writer safety. Explicit active-checkout edit preservation archives regular-file contents before conflict-copy moves and resumes the strict owned journal. Missing-file/deletion intent and ambiguous namespace changes still need resolution. The retained edit archive groups currently require manual retention management; automatic age/backup-based retirement remains unfinished.","headings":[{"id":"workspace","text":"Workspace and versioning","level":2},{"id":"sync","text":"Team operation and continuous synchronization","level":2},{"id":"security","text":"Security, retention and recovery","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-detached","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/native-workspace-archives","#/en/native-workspace-relocation","#/en/native-authorship","#/en/server-signatures","#/en/resident-agent","#/en/resident-agent/conflict-copies","#/en/syncthing/device-connections","#/en/syncthing/folder-catalog","#/en/syncthing/folder-controller","#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts","#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"localization","title":"Language and localization policy","description":"Language and localization policy guide.","section":"Getting started","order":170,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"language-and-localization-policy\">Language and localization policy\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/localization/language-and-localization-policy\" aria-label=\"Link to Language and localization policy\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>English is Syrnck&#39;s source language and default interface language. French is the second built-in language. New interfaces must support per-session language selection and English fallback from their first implementation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"command-line-selection\">Command-line selection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/localization/command-line-selection\" aria-label=\"Link to Command-line selection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --help\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --lang fr --help\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --lang en --store data fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--lang\u003c/code> takes precedence over \u003ccode>SYRNCK_LANG\u003c/code>. If neither is set, English is used regardless of \u003ccode>LANG\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LC_ALL\u003c/code>, or Windows regional settings. \u003ccode>en\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>fr\u003c/code> are supported, including regional forms such as \u003ccode>en-US\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>fr_FR\u003c/code>. Unsupported explicit values fail with an error. Output uses UTF-8.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The CLI translates help, status text, its server&#39;s listening banner, and diagnostics owned by Syrnck. A complete catalog is compiled into the library, so an installed executable does not need external locale files. Translation introduces no global mutable language or process-locale change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"library-and-protocol-boundaries\">Library and protocol boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/localization/library-and-protocol-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to Library and protocol boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/i18n.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> language \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse_language\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fr\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> display \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">localize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"object hash mismatch\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> language\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The core keeps canonical English exception text. A caller translates it for display with \u003ccode>localize\u003c/code>; another caller can select a different language concurrently. The default argument is \u003ccode>Language::english\u003c/code>. Unknown operating-system, standard-library, or dependency messages retain their original text.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Do not translate stored bytes, paths, user-authored messages, command/option names, reference names, JSON keys, object kinds, IDs, hashes, Git pointers, or protocol fields. C and Go ABI diagnostics also retain their canonical English form; language belongs to their application&#39;s presentation layer. HTTP error bodies remain canonical diagnostics independent of the server console&#39;s selected language. These boundaries keep automation, content hashes, and peers interoperable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"maintaining-translations\">Documentation languages\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/localization/maintaining-translations\" aria-label=\"Link to Documentation languages\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The local manual contains complete English and French editions with common page and anchor IDs. The CLI passes its selected language to contextual help. The reader can change language independently; unavailable routes fall back to English. Each translation records the English revision it covers. Developer maintenance instructions are kept separately from this manual.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Language and localization policy # English is Syrnck's source language and default interface language. French is the second built-in language. New interfaces must support per-session language selection and English fallback from their first implementation. Command-line selection # text Copy syrnck --help syrnck --lang fr --help syrnck --lang en --store data fsck --lang takes precedence over SYRNCK_LANG . If neither is set, English is used regardless of LANG , LC_ALL , or Windows regional settings. en and fr are supported, including regional forms such as en-US and fr_FR . Unsupported explicit values fail with an error. Output uses UTF-8. The CLI translates help, status text, its server's listening banner, and diagnostics owned by Syrnck. A complete catalog is compiled into the library, so an installed executable does not need external locale files. Translation introduces no global mutable language or process-locale change. Library and protocol boundaries # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp> auto language = syrnck :: parse_language ( \"fr\" ) ; auto display = syrnck :: localize ( \"object hash mismatch\" , language ) ; The core keeps canonical English exception text. A caller translates it for display with localize ; another caller can select a different language concurrently. The default argument is Language::english . Unknown operating-system, standard-library, or dependency messages retain their original text. Do not translate stored bytes, paths, user-authored messages, command/option names, reference names, JSON keys, object kinds, IDs, hashes, Git pointers, or protocol fields. C and Go ABI diagnostics also retain their canonical English form; language belongs to their application's presentation layer. HTTP error bodies remain canonical diagnostics independent of the server console's selected language. These boundaries keep automation, content hashes, and peers interoperable. Documentation languages # The local manual contains complete English and French editions with common page and anchor IDs. The CLI passes its selected language to contextual help. The reader can change language independently; unavailable routes fall back to English. Each translation records the English revision it covers. Developer maintenance instructions are kept separately from this manual. syrnck --help syrnck --lang fr --help syrnck --lang en --store data fsck #include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp> auto language = syrnck::parse_language(\"fr\"); auto display = syrnck::localize(\"object hash mismatch\", language);","headings":[{"id":"language-and-localization-policy","text":"Language and localization policy","level":1},{"id":"command-line-selection","text":"Command-line selection","level":2},{"id":"library-and-protocol-boundaries","text":"Library and protocol boundaries","level":2},{"id":"maintaining-translations","text":"Documentation languages","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck --help\nsyrnck --lang fr --help\nsyrnck --lang en --store data fsck\n#include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp>\n\nauto language = syrnck::parse_language(\"fr\");\nauto display = syrnck::localize(\"object hash mismatch\", language);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"maintenance","title":"Maintenance progress and cancellation","description":"Maintenance progress and cancellation guide.","section":"Storage","order":180,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"maintenance-progress-and-cancellation\">Maintenance progress and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/maintenance/maintenance-progress-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to Maintenance progress and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>gc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> support \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> on stderr, along with cooperative Ctrl+C. Their ordinary final\nJSON reports on stdout are unchanged. In C++, use\n\u003ccode>Store::gc(remove, OperationOptions)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Store::pack(PackOptions, OperationOptions)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Preparation is cancellable: retained-file validation, physical inventory\nplanning, loose-key sorting, pack construction and verified readback check the\nstop token. An observer exception also aborts preparation. The old catalog,\nreferences and existing source files remain intact. Temporary files are removed\non cooperative unwind. Completed immutable packs prepared before cancellation\ncan remain inactive; the next successful maintenance cleanup reclaims generated\ninactive pack/index files. Opening the store does not select them automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Phases identify the counters&#39; scope. \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> counts decoded payload reads.\n\u003ccode>planning\u003c/code> counts physical envelope bytes inspected for the maintenance plan.\n\u003ccode>packing\u003c/code> counts verified source envelopes supplied to the new pack writer;\nit is not a durable-write counter and excludes pack headers/indexes. The packing\ntotal is known after planning. GC may alternate packing and verification as it\nprepares each partially live pack. A \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event is the final callback and\ncancellation check before committing maintenance. Phase completion describes\npreparation; only successful return/exit confirms the entire operation finished.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once the catalog has been published, maintenance finishes loose-object and\nobsolete-pack cleanup without further observer callbacks or cancellation checks.\nIf no catalog change is needed, the same boundary precedes the first destructive\ndeletion. This prevents a cooperative stop from stranding dead loose metadata\nafter its packed dependencies have been removed. A stop that arrives after that\nboundary can therefore finish with exit 0. The CLI may appear quiet during this\nfinal cleanup. Dry-run GC never enters destructive cleanup and remains\ncancellable throughout its work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ordinary IO failures and hard termination can still interrupt committed cleanup.\nThe existing \u003ca href=\"#/en/crash-recovery\">crash recovery procedure\u003c/a> applies: retained roots\nstay complete, and a known interrupted GC may need its cleanup resumed before a\nfull audit of unreachable metadata succeeds. These options do not change object\nformats, physical pack versions, retention rules or exclusive-store locking.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Native IO, codec calls and index validation already in progress may finish before\nthe next stop check. Existing memory costs remain: one mark bit per packed\nobject for GC, sorting of loose-only keys for packing and separate metadata and\ntraversal allocations. Progress/cancellation add constant-size counters and\ncallbacks rather than another full object inventory.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Maintenance progress and cancellation # gc , gc --delete , pack and pack --compact support --progress and --progress-json on stderr, along with cooperative Ctrl+C. Their ordinary final JSON reports on stdout are unchanged. In C++, use Store::gc(remove, OperationOptions) or Store::pack(PackOptions, OperationOptions) . Preparation is cancellable: retained-file validation, physical inventory planning, loose-key sorting, pack construction and verified readback check the stop token. An observer exception also aborts preparation. The old catalog, references and existing source files remain intact. Temporary files are removed on cooperative unwind. Completed immutable packs prepared before cancellation can remain inactive; the next successful maintenance cleanup reclaims generated inactive pack/index files. Opening the store does not select them automatically. Phases identify the counters' scope. verifying counts decoded payload reads. planning counts physical envelope bytes inspected for the maintenance plan. packing counts verified source envelopes supplied to the new pack writer; it is not a durable-write counter and excludes pack headers/indexes. The packing total is known after planning. GC may alternate packing and verification as it prepares each partially live pack. A publishing event is the final callback and cancellation check before committing maintenance. Phase completion describes preparation; only successful return/exit confirms the entire operation finished. Once the catalog has been published, maintenance finishes loose-object and obsolete-pack cleanup without further observer callbacks or cancellation checks. If no catalog change is needed, the same boundary precedes the first destructive deletion. This prevents a cooperative stop from stranding dead loose metadata after its packed dependencies have been removed. A stop that arrives after that boundary can therefore finish with exit 0. The CLI may appear quiet during this final cleanup. Dry-run GC never enters destructive cleanup and remains cancellable throughout its work. Ordinary IO failures and hard termination can still interrupt committed cleanup. The existing crash recovery procedure applies: retained roots stay complete, and a known interrupted GC may need its cleanup resumed before a full audit of unreachable metadata succeeds. These options do not change object formats, physical pack versions, retention rules or exclusive-store locking. Native IO, codec calls and index validation already in progress may finish before the next stop check. Existing memory costs remain: one mark bit per packed object for GC, sorting of loose-only keys for packing and separate metadata and traversal allocations. Progress/cancellation add constant-size counters and callbacks rather than another full object inventory.","headings":[{"id":"maintenance-progress-and-cancellation","text":"Maintenance progress and cancellation","level":1}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/crash-recovery"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-amend","title":"Native commit amendment","description":"Native commit amendment guide.","section":"Version control","order":190,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-commit-amendment\">Native commit amendment\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-amend/native-commit-amendment\" aria-label=\"Link to Native commit amendment\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Amendment replaces an existing branch tip while preserving its ordered parent\nlist, including every parent of a merge revision. The previous revision remains\nimmutable and is retained under \u003ccode>archive/amend/OLD_ID\u003c/code>. The branch move, archive\nroot and optional staging consumption publish in one atomic reference update.\nExisting tags continue to identify their original revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli\">CLI\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-amend/cli\" aria-label=\"Link to CLI\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\"># Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\"># Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native add Content/Maps Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\"># Reuse every existing file entry and change the message.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\"># Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All modes require \u003ccode>-m\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>--message\u003c/code>. The selected native branch is the default;\n\u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code> overrides it without changing symbolic HEAD. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> is an\nexact lowercase 64-character revision ID, never a branch/tag selector. If omitted,\nthe CLI captures the selected branch&#39;s current revision while holding the\nexclusive Store lock. There must already be a tip; amendment cannot create an\nunborn branch. \u003ccode>--message-only\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> require \u003ccode>--amend\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Snapshot source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Staging behavior\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Working files\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--amend\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete current directory, using ordinary commit exclusions and ingestion settings\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Any existing index is rejected\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read and ingested, never written\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--amend --staged\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Immutable branch staging snapshot\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Must exist and have the current tip as baseline; consumed atomically\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Never accessed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--amend --message-only\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Existing tip&#39;s file entries\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Any existing index is rejected\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Never accessed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>An unchanged prepared snapshot is accepted for a message correction. Staged and\nmessage modes are mutually exclusive and reject directory/pipeline arguments.\nDirectory mode accepts \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> as ordinary\ncommit does, including workspace binding checks. All modes refuse to run while a\nnative checkout or switch requires recovery. A stale index requires inspection\nand explicit reset; amendment never silently changes its baseline.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The new revision uses the supplied message and current UTC timestamp in seconds.\nWith a \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">native identity\u003c/a>, amendment preserves the original\nauthor and records the current committer and a new optional signature in v2.\nRewriting an existing v2 revision requires an identity; an old signature is never\nreused. Without an identity, legacy v1 stays unchanged. An amendment\nwhose complete serialized metadata is identical can return the existing ID;\nthere is no random nonce that forces a new object identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-interruption-and-restoration\">Retention, interruption and restoration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-amend/retention-interruption-and-restoration\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention, interruption and restoration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Before publication, the engine verifies the complete replacement closure and\nthe archive closure, including reconstructed file hashes. A missing old block\nprevents publication even if the new snapshot no longer needs that block.\nExpected branch, index and archive values are checked together. An existing\n\u003ccode>archive/amend/OLD_ID\u003c/code> can be reused only if it points to that same revision;\nan unrelated object at that name is an error.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation, callback failure or invalid input before reference publication\nkeeps the old branch, index and archives. Preparation may leave immutable,\nunreferenced objects that a retry can reuse or GC can collect. A process stopped\nduring publication leaves the complete old or complete new reference set.\nOnce publication starts, it may complete despite cancellation; there are no\ncallbacks or cancellation checks after a successful publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After an interrupted invocation, inspect \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code>. If the expected old tip remains, retry with its exact \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> ID. If\nthe replacement tip and \u003ccode>archive/amend/OLD_ID\u003c/code> exist, publication completed. When\nthe replacement has a different ID, retrying with the old expected ID fails\ninstead of amending that replacement a second time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Archives have no automatic expiry or dedicated pruning command. They are GC\nroots for the old revision, its ancestors and all referenced file blocks. Even\nwhen ordinary history no longer visits an old tip, it remains exportable:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the existing checkout/switch preflight and recovery workflow to restore an\narchived snapshot into a working directory. Generic \u003ccode>unpin\u003c/code> can remove an archive\nroot; removing the last retaining root makes its exclusive blocks eligible for\nGC. Retention is a local storage guarantee, not an access-control boundary.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">C++ API and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-amend/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ API and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> expected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resolve_native_revision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"heads/main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> corrected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Corrected snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> prepared \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> corrected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Selected correction\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> renamed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> prepared\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Corrected message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every API requires the exact expected tip ID. The directory overload accepts\n\u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code>, defaulting to repository settings. Staged/message APIs accept\n\u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>; callbacks must not reenter the Store. Metadata-only here\ndescribes snapshot selection: publication still reads and verifies stored file\npayloads. All CLI modes support English/French progress and cooperative Ctrl+C\ncancellation; JSON progress and returned IDs are language-independent.\nNative amendment is not yet exposed through the C ABI or Go binding.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native commit amendment # Amendment replaces an existing branch tip while preserving its ordered parent list, including every parent of a merge revision. The previous revision remains immutable and is retained under archive/amend/OLD_ID . The branch move, archive root and optional staging consumption publish in one atomic reference update. Existing tags continue to identify their original revision. CLI # text Copy # Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory. syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\" # Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone. syrnck native add Content/Maps Source syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\" # Reuse every existing file entry and change the message. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\" # Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\" All modes require -m / --message . The selected native branch is the default; --branch NAME overrides it without changing symbolic HEAD. --expect is an exact lowercase 64-character revision ID, never a branch/tag selector. If omitted, the CLI captures the selected branch's current revision while holding the exclusive Store lock. There must already be a tip; amendment cannot create an unborn branch. --message-only and --expect require --amend . Mode Snapshot source Staging behavior Working files --amend Complete current directory, using ordinary commit exclusions and ingestion settings Any existing index is rejected Read and ingested, never written --amend --staged Immutable branch staging snapshot Must exist and have the current tip as baseline; consumed atomically Never accessed --amend --message-only Existing tip's file entries Any existing index is rejected Never accessed An unchanged prepared snapshot is accepted for a message correction. Staged and message modes are mutually exclusive and reject directory/pipeline arguments. Directory mode accepts --directory , --workers and --buffer-mib as ordinary commit does, including workspace binding checks. All modes refuse to run while a native checkout or switch requires recovery. A stale index requires inspection and explicit reset; amendment never silently changes its baseline. The new revision uses the supplied message and current UTC timestamp in seconds. With a native identity , amendment preserves the original author and records the current committer and a new optional signature in v2. Rewriting an existing v2 revision requires an identity; an old signature is never reused. Without an identity, legacy v1 stays unchanged. An amendment whose complete serialized metadata is identical can return the existing ID; there is no random nonce that forces a new object identity. Retention, interruption and restoration # Before publication, the engine verifies the complete replacement closure and the archive closure, including reconstructed file hashes. A missing old block prevents publication even if the new snapshot no longer needs that block. Expected branch, index and archive values are checked together. An existing archive/amend/OLD_ID can be reused only if it points to that same revision; an unrelated object at that name is an error. Cancellation, callback failure or invalid input before reference publication keeps the old branch, index and archives. Preparation may leave immutable, unreferenced objects that a retry can reuse or GC can collect. A process stopped during publication leaves the complete old or complete new reference set. Once publication starts, it may complete despite cancellation; there are no callbacks or cancellation checks after a successful publication. After an interrupted invocation, inspect native head , refs and native staged . If the expected old tip remains, retry with its exact --expect ID. If the replacement tip and archive/amend/OLD_ID exist, publication completed. When the replacement has a different ID, retrying with the old expected ID fails instead of amending that replacement a second time. Archives have no automatic expiry or dedicated pruning command. They are GC roots for the old revision, its ancestors and all referenced file blocks. Even when ordinary history no longer visits an old tip, it remains exportable: text Copy syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID Use the existing checkout/switch preflight and recovery workflow to restore an archived snapshot into a working directory. Generic unpin can remove an archive root; removing the last retaining root makes its exclusive blocks eligible for GC. Retention is a local storage guarantee, not an access-control boundary. C++ API and validation # cpp Copy auto expected = store . resolve_native_revision ( \"heads/main\" ) ; auto corrected = store . amend_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected , \"Corrected snapshot\" ) ; auto prepared = store . amend_staged ( \"main\" , corrected , \"Selected correction\" , operation ) ; auto renamed = store . amend_message ( \"main\" , prepared , \"Corrected message\" , operation ) ; Every API requires the exact expected tip ID. The directory overload accepts IngestOptions , defaulting to repository settings. Staged/message APIs accept OperationOptions ; callbacks must not reenter the Store. Metadata-only here describes snapshot selection: publication still reads and verifies stored file payloads. All CLI modes support English/French progress and cooperative Ctrl+C cancellation; JSON progress and returned IDs are language-independent. Native amendment is not yet exposed through the C ABI or Go binding. # Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory. syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\" # Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone. syrnck native add Content/Maps Source syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\" # Reuse every existing file entry and change the message. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\" # Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\" syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID auto expected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"heads/main\"); auto corrected = store.amend_directory(project, \"main\", expected, \"Corrected snapshot\"); auto prepared = store.amend_staged(\"main\", corrected, \"Selected correction\", operation); auto renamed = store.amend_message(\"main\", prepared, \"Corrected message\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-commit-amendment","text":"Native commit amendment","level":1},{"id":"cli","text":"CLI","level":2},{"id":"retention-interruption-and-restoration","text":"Retention, interruption and restoration","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"C++ API and validation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"# Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory.\nsyrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\"\n\n# Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone.\nsyrnck native add Content/Maps Source\nsyrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\"\n\n# Reuse every existing file entry and change the message.\nsyrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\"\n\n# Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected.\nsyrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\"\nsyrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\nauto expected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"heads/main\");\nauto corrected = store.amend_directory(project, \"main\", expected, \"Corrected snapshot\");\nauto prepared = store.amend_staged(\"main\", corrected, \"Selected correction\", operation);\nauto renamed = store.amend_message(\"main\", prepared, \"Corrected message\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-archives","title":"Native history archives","description":"Native history archives guide.","section":"Version control","order":200,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-history-archives\">Native history archives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-archives/native-history-archives\" aria-label=\"Link to Native history archives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native history mutations retain former revisions and prepared bytes as ordinary\nGC roots. The archive browser lists those roots without changing references,\nobjects or working files. It also covers existing amendment, checkout and history\ndetachment archives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Retained data\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>reset\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/reset/OLD_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Former branch tip and its full history\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>reset-index\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Former prepared snapshot and its baseline history\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>rebase-index\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Index before replay onto a new head, including its original baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stage-index\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/stage-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Frozen index before text hunk/row selection or external patch staging, including its original baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>squash\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/squash/OLD_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Original chain, including intermediate file versions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>amend\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/amend/OLD_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Former amended tip and its parents\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/checkout/TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Source revision retained by completed checkout\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>detached\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/OLD_ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Original tip retained by history detachment or replacement of an inactive detached HEAD line\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Tag deletion records use the existing \u003ccode>native tag-archive\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>tag-restore\u003c/code>\nworkflow and are excluded. Branch deletion records use\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches/archived-branch-deletion\">\u003ccode>native branch-archives\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>branch-archive\u003c/code>\u003c/a>\nand are also excluded. Custom/unknown archive namespaces are excluded; \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>\nremains the generic complete reference inventory. The browser checks owned archive\nnames, revision target kinds and ID identity. Checkout names identify a transaction\nrather than the target revision. A manually corrupted owned archive is an error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pages-and-verification\">Pages and verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-archives/pages-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Pages and verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--kind\u003c/code> optionally selects one table category. \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> is 1..1000, default 100.\nRows are ordered by full reference name. \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> is the exclusive cursor for\nthe next page when \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code> is true, otherwise null. A cursor remains usable if\nits reference has since been removed, but its category must match an explicit\nfilter. Pages reflect the current reference table on each invocation; they are\nnot a persistent snapshot across mutations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each row contains \u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kind\u003c/code>, exact \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> ID, file/parent counts,\nthe revision&#39;s timestamp and a message preview of at most 256 UTF-8 bytes without\nsplitting a code point. \u003ccode>message_bytes\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>message_truncated\u003c/code> describe the full\nmessage. The timestamp belongs to the stored revision, not the archive event.\n\u003ccode>native archive-show\u003c/code> returns the same row for one full reference; use \u003ccode>inspect revision ID\u003c/code> for complete revision metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Default queries read only reference/revision metadata. They can list a revision\nwhose blocks are missing. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally audits each returned revision&#39;s\ncomplete closure and reconstructed file hashes; missing/corrupt retained bytes\nfail the query. List verification covers the returned page, not undisplayed\narchives. Progress/cancellation and English/French diagnostics are supported;\nJSON fields remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The compact report is bounded to 16 MiB. The underlying reference table is still\nloaded in full; pagination bounds returned rows and revision metadata reads,\nrather than reference-table memory. Plain/encrypted and loose/packed Stores use\nthe same logical roots. No format or retention policy changes are introduced by\ninspection.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovering-stored-versions\">Recovering stored versions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-archives/recovering-stored-versions\" aria-label=\"Link to Recovering stored versions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use the row&#39;s \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> ID to export files or create a recovery branch:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native switch recovered\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The existing checkout/switch clean-workspace and recovery rules apply. A prepared\nindex archive can be exported in the same way to recover its frozen bytes; the\nbrowser does not automatically restore a branch/index pair or alter working edits.\nWorking files never imported into the Store are outside archive retention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Archives do not expire automatically. Generic reference removal can drop a root;\nwhen its last retaining root disappears, exclusive objects become eligible for\ndestructive GC. The browser neither prunes archives nor promises disk reclamation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_archives\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reset-index\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archive \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive/reset/\"\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">+\u003c/span> old_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Available through C++ and CLI. Native history C/Go bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Partial text staging retains replaced indexes under \u003ccode>archive/stage-index/ID\u003c/code>, visible with \u003ccode>--kind stage-index\u003c/code>. These roots retain their complete old preparations and baseline through GC; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">partial text staging\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native history archives # Native history mutations retain former revisions and prepared bytes as ordinary GC roots. The archive browser lists those roots without changing references, objects or working files. It also covers existing amendment, checkout and history detachment archives. text Copy syrnck native archives --limit 100 syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100 syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify Kind Reference Retained data reset archive/reset/OLD_ID Former branch tip and its full history reset-index archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID Former prepared snapshot and its baseline history rebase-index archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID Index before replay onto a new head, including its original baseline stage-index archive/stage-index/INDEX_ID Frozen index before text hunk/row selection or external patch staging, including its original baseline squash archive/squash/OLD_ID Original chain, including intermediate file versions amend archive/amend/OLD_ID Former amended tip and its parents checkout archive/checkout/TRANSACTION_ID Source revision retained by completed checkout detached archive/OLD_ID Original tip retained by history detachment or replacement of an inactive detached HEAD line Tag deletion records use the existing native tag-archive / tag-restore workflow and are excluded. Branch deletion records use native branch-archives / branch-archive and are also excluded. Custom/unknown archive namespaces are excluded; refs remains the generic complete reference inventory. The browser checks owned archive names, revision target kinds and ID identity. Checkout names identify a transaction rather than the target revision. A manually corrupted owned archive is an error. Pages and verification # --kind optionally selects one table category. --limit is 1..1000, default 100. Rows are ordered by full reference name. next_after is the exclusive cursor for the next page when has_more is true, otherwise null. A cursor remains usable if its reference has since been removed, but its category must match an explicit filter. Pages reflect the current reference table on each invocation; they are not a persistent snapshot across mutations. Each row contains reference , kind , exact revision ID, file/parent counts, the revision's timestamp and a message preview of at most 256 UTF-8 bytes without splitting a code point. message_bytes and message_truncated describe the full message. The timestamp belongs to the stored revision, not the archive event. native archive-show returns the same row for one full reference; use inspect revision ID for complete revision metadata. Default queries read only reference/revision metadata. They can list a revision whose blocks are missing. --verify additionally audits each returned revision's complete closure and reconstructed file hashes; missing/corrupt retained bytes fail the query. List verification covers the returned page, not undisplayed archives. Progress/cancellation and English/French diagnostics are supported; JSON fields remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. The compact report is bounded to 16 MiB. The underlying reference table is still loaded in full; pagination bounds returned rows and revision metadata reads, rather than reference-table memory. Plain/encrypted and loose/packed Stores use the same logical roots. No format or retention policy changes are introduced by inspection. Recovering stored versions # Use the row's revision ID to export files or create a recovery branch: text Copy syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify syrnck native switch recovered The existing checkout/switch clean-workspace and recovery rules apply. A prepared index archive can be exported in the same way to recover its frozen bytes; the browser does not automatically restore a branch/index pair or alter working edits. Working files never imported into the Store are outside archive retention. Archives do not expire automatically. Generic reference removal can drop a root; when its last retaining root disappears, exclusive objects become eligible for destructive GC. The browser neither prunes archives nor promises disk reclamation. cpp Copy auto page = store . native_archives ( \"reset-index\" , { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto archive = store . native_archive ( \"archive/reset/\" + old_id , true , operation ) ; Available through C++ and CLI. Native history C/Go bindings remain future work. Partial text staging retains replaced indexes under archive/stage-index/ID , visible with --kind stage-index . These roots retain their complete old preparations and baseline through GC; see partial text staging . syrnck native archives --limit 100 syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100 syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify syrnck native switch recovered auto page = store.native_archives(\"reset-index\", {}, 100, true, operation); auto archive = store.native_archive(\"archive/reset/\" + old_id, true, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-history-archives","text":"Native history archives","level":1},{"id":"pages-and-verification","text":"Pages and verification","level":2},{"id":"recovering-stored-versions","text":"Recovering stored versions","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native archives --limit 100\nsyrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify\nsyrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100\nsyrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify\nsyrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID\nsyrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify\nsyrnck native switch recovered\nauto page = store.native_archives(\"reset-index\", {}, 100, true, operation);\nauto archive = store.native_archive(\"archive/reset/\" + old_id, true, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-branches/archived-branch-deletion","#/en/native-hunks"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-authorship","title":"Native authors and signatures","description":"Original authors, current committers and optional Ed25519 signatures.","section":"Version control","order":305,"parent":"native-history","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"identity\">Configure an identity\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-authorship/identity\" aria-label=\"Link to Configure an identity\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native log --json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native log --show-signature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native identity --clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Names and addresses are UTF-8 text, limited to 256 and 320 bytes, without control\ncharacters. The timezone is minutes east of UTC, in -1439..1439; the default is 0.\nAddresses are recorded literally, without proving mailbox ownership. JSON fields,\nIDs and key fingerprints are identical in the English and French interfaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The optional machine-local \u003ccode>native-identity.json\u003c/code> inside the Store contains only\nthe version, type, name, email and timezone. It applies to all workspaces using\nthat Store. Logical backups do not copy this file. Clearing the profile preserves\nexisting revisions and the additive reader requirement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Paired \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_NAME\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_EMAIL\u003c/code> override the saved profile for\none invocation; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_TIMEZONE\u003c/code> supplies an optional offset. A newly created\nrevision uses legacy v1 when no identity is configured, including after an\nexplicit clear. Rewriting an authored v2 revision requires a current identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signing\">Sign without storing a private key\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-authorship/signing\" aria-label=\"Link to Sign without storing a private key\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An optional \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SIGNING_SEED\u003c/code> supplies a 32-byte Ed25519 seed encoded as 64\nhexadecimal characters. Supply it through a controlled process environment;\ndo not put a real seed in command arguments, scripts or a profile file. The CLI\nconsumes and removes the variable from its process environment and wipes its\nowned temporary buffers. Unrelated commands discard it before spawning tools.\nThis does not promise erasure of earlier copies maintained by the OS or caller.\nAn identity without a seed creates an unsigned v2 revision with \u003ccode>signature: null\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In the C++ library, identity and signing keys are explicit handle state:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/core.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice@example.invalid\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">60\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                          syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">SigningKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">generate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> revision \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Add asset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">clear_native_identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The Store owns the moved key exclusively. The library does not read the CLI\nprofile or environment automatically. \u003ccode>native_identity()\u003c/code> returns public profile\nand key information; no private-key export is provided.\n\u003ccode>take_native_identity()\u003c/code> detaches a move-only \u003ccode>NativeIdentityContext\u003c/code> containing\nthe profile and optional signing key. Move both into the next Store with\n\u003ccode>use_native_identity()\u003c/code> before releasing the context. This supports short-lived\nlocked handles in a resident process without copying or exporting seed bytes.\nThe former handle becomes unconfigured; taking an empty context returns null.\nChanging or taking identity inside a progress callback is refused. See the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/signing-api\">signing primitive\u003c/a> for seed ownership and strict verification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rewrites\">Author and committer rules\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-authorship/rewrites\" aria-label=\"Link to Author and committer rules\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A v2 revision records \u003ccode>author\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>committer\u003c/code> and an optional signature. Each person\nstamp has \u003ccode>name,email,timestamp,timezone_offset\u003c/code>; the committer time equals the\nrevision timestamp. An original author may be null when rewriting legacy history.\nThe current committer signs the complete resulting revision, including its files\nand parent IDs. An old signature is never copied onto changed metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fresh directory/staged commits, new merge commits, reverts and standalone squashes\nuse the current person as author and committer. Amend, cherry-pick, linear rebase\nand history detachment preserve the original author and record the new committer.\nRebase squash/fixup steps preserve the first retained commit&#39;s original author.\nAn unchanged/fast-forward result retains the exact existing revision. Internal\nindex, stash and recovery snapshots remain v1; they do not invent authorship.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rebase freezes one committer time for both candidate-calculation passes. With\nan identity configured, \u003ccode>--committer-time SECONDS\u003c/code> on rebase-plan/rebase-todo/rebase\nmakes a preview reproducible with the same identity, key and inputs. Without\nthat option, separate invocations can choose different times and candidate IDs.\nThe report exposes \u003ccode>committer_timestamp\u003c/code>. Existing checkout recovery reads frozen\nsigned candidates and does not need the signing private key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native log --json\u003c/code>, file history, blame and archive summaries expose v2 authors,\ncommitters and public signing-key IDs. Plain log shows the author and a different\ncommitter; \u003ccode>--show-signature\u003c/code> adds the key ID or unsigned status. Legacy summary\nfields and default first-parent log traversal remain compatible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format\">Signed bytes and compatibility\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-authorship/format\" aria-label=\"Link to Signed bytes and compatibility\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Revision v2 is canonical compact JSON in the order\n\u003ccode>version,parents,message,timestamp,author,committer,signature,files\u003c/code>.\nThe signature object contains exactly \u003ccode>algorithm,key_id,public_key,value\u003c/code>:\n\u003ccode>ed25519\u003c/code>, the fingerprint, 32-byte public key and 64-byte signature; binary\nvalues are lowercase hexadecimal. The signed message is ASCII\n\u003ccode>Syrnck native revision signature v1:\u003c/code> followed by the 64-character lowercase\nBLAKE3 hash of the canonical v2 revision with \u003ccode>signature\u003c/code> set to null.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Object import, reads and audits validate the signature, its fingerprint and the\ncanonical revision. Altering the author, parent, timestamp, message or file map\ninvalidates a signature. \u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code> is enabled before storing a v2\nobject or saving an identity profile. Historical readers reject the extended\nStore configuration; envelope, manifest, pack, HTTP object and C ABI encodings\nare unchanged. Logical backups/restores retain this reader requirement. V1 and\nv2 revisions can coexist in the same graph.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trust\">Trust and remaining limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-authorship/trust\" aria-label=\"Link to Trust and remaining limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A valid signature proves possession of its signing key for those bytes. A name,\nemail and timestamp remain claims by the signer. Server access v1 uses authenticated\nprincipals, branch/path ACLs and asset locks; \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-signatures\">access v2\u003c/a>\ncan also require approved signatures and bind committer keys/identities to\naccounts. It checks current policy for newly admitted history, preserving the\nexisting branch baseline. An OS signing-key vault and distributed revocation\nremain separate work. Native history authorship APIs are currently C++/CLI; C/Go object\nimport and reconstruction can read validated v2 objects without a signing seed.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Configure an identity # text Copy syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60 syrnck native identity syrnck native log --json syrnck native log --show-signature syrnck native identity --clear Names and addresses are UTF-8 text, limited to 256 and 320 bytes, without control characters. The timezone is minutes east of UTC, in -1439..1439; the default is 0. Addresses are recorded literally, without proving mailbox ownership. JSON fields, IDs and key fingerprints are identical in the English and French interfaces. The optional machine-local native-identity.json inside the Store contains only the version, type, name, email and timezone. It applies to all workspaces using that Store. Logical backups do not copy this file. Clearing the profile preserves existing revisions and the additive reader requirement. Paired SYRNCK_USER_NAME and SYRNCK_USER_EMAIL override the saved profile for one invocation; SYRNCK_USER_TIMEZONE supplies an optional offset. A newly created revision uses legacy v1 when no identity is configured, including after an explicit clear. Rewriting an authored v2 revision requires a current identity. Sign without storing a private key # An optional SYRNCK_SIGNING_SEED supplies a 32-byte Ed25519 seed encoded as 64 hexadecimal characters. Supply it through a controlled process environment; do not put a real seed in command arguments, scripts or a profile file. The CLI consumes and removes the variable from its process environment and wipes its owned temporary buffers. Unrelated commands discard it before spawning tools. This does not promise erasure of earlier copies maintained by the OS or caller. An identity without a seed creates an unsigned v2 revision with signature: null . In the C++ library, identity and signing keys are explicit handle state: cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> store . use_native_identity ( { \"Alice\" , \"alice@example.invalid\" , 60 } , syrnck :: SigningKey :: generate ( ) ) ; auto revision = store . commit_directory ( project , \"main\" , \"Add asset\" ) ; store . clear_native_identity ( ) ; The Store owns the moved key exclusively. The library does not read the CLI profile or environment automatically. native_identity() returns public profile and key information; no private-key export is provided. take_native_identity() detaches a move-only NativeIdentityContext containing the profile and optional signing key. Move both into the next Store with use_native_identity() before releasing the context. This supports short-lived locked handles in a resident process without copying or exporting seed bytes. The former handle becomes unconfigured; taking an empty context returns null. Changing or taking identity inside a progress callback is refused. See the signing primitive for seed ownership and strict verification. Author and committer rules # A v2 revision records author , committer and an optional signature. Each person stamp has name,email,timestamp,timezone_offset ; the committer time equals the revision timestamp. An original author may be null when rewriting legacy history. The current committer signs the complete resulting revision, including its files and parent IDs. An old signature is never copied onto changed metadata. Fresh directory/staged commits, new merge commits, reverts and standalone squashes use the current person as author and committer. Amend, cherry-pick, linear rebase and history detachment preserve the original author and record the new committer. Rebase squash/fixup steps preserve the first retained commit's original author. An unchanged/fast-forward result retains the exact existing revision. Internal index, stash and recovery snapshots remain v1; they do not invent authorship. Rebase freezes one committer time for both candidate-calculation passes. With an identity configured, --committer-time SECONDS on rebase-plan/rebase-todo/rebase makes a preview reproducible with the same identity, key and inputs. Without that option, separate invocations can choose different times and candidate IDs. The report exposes committer_timestamp . Existing checkout recovery reads frozen signed candidates and does not need the signing private key. native log --json , file history, blame and archive summaries expose v2 authors, committers and public signing-key IDs. Plain log shows the author and a different committer; --show-signature adds the key ID or unsigned status. Legacy summary fields and default first-parent log traversal remain compatible. Signed bytes and compatibility # Revision v2 is canonical compact JSON in the order version,parents,message,timestamp,author,committer,signature,files . The signature object contains exactly algorithm,key_id,public_key,value : ed25519 , the fingerprint, 32-byte public key and 64-byte signature; binary values are lowercase hexadecimal. The signed message is ASCII Syrnck native revision signature v1: followed by the 64-character lowercase BLAKE3 hash of the canonical v2 revision with signature set to null. Object import, reads and audits validate the signature, its fingerprint and the canonical revision. Altering the author, parent, timestamp, message or file map invalidates a signature. authored-revisions-v1 is enabled before storing a v2 object or saving an identity profile. Historical readers reject the extended Store configuration; envelope, manifest, pack, HTTP object and C ABI encodings are unchanged. Logical backups/restores retain this reader requirement. V1 and v2 revisions can coexist in the same graph. Trust and remaining limits # A valid signature proves possession of its signing key for those bytes. A name, email and timestamp remain claims by the signer. Server access v1 uses authenticated principals, branch/path ACLs and asset locks; access v2 can also require approved signatures and bind committer keys/identities to accounts. It checks current policy for newly admitted history, preserving the existing branch baseline. An OS signing-key vault and distributed revocation remain separate work. Native history authorship APIs are currently C++/CLI; C/Go object import and reconstruction can read validated v2 objects without a signing seed. syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60 syrnck native identity syrnck native log --json syrnck native log --show-signature syrnck native identity --clear #include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> store.use_native_identity({\"Alice\", \"alice@example.invalid\", 60}, syrnck::SigningKey::generate()); auto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Add asset\"); store.clear_native_identity();","headings":[{"id":"identity","text":"Configure an identity","level":2},{"id":"signing","text":"Sign without storing a private key","level":2},{"id":"rewrites","text":"Author and committer rules","level":2},{"id":"format","text":"Signed bytes and compatibility","level":2},{"id":"trust","text":"Trust and remaining limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60\nsyrnck native identity\nsyrnck native log --json\nsyrnck native log --show-signature\nsyrnck native identity --clear\n#include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp>\nstore.use_native_identity({\"Alice\", \"alice@example.invalid\", 60},\n                          syrnck::SigningKey::generate());\nauto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Add asset\");\nstore.clear_native_identity();","assets":[],"links":["#/en/signing-api","#/en/server-signatures"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-bisect","title":"Persistent native bisection without checkout","description":"Persistent native bisection without checkout guide.","section":"Version control","order":210,"parent":"native-history","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout\">Persistent native bisection without checkout\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-bisect/persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout\" aria-label=\"Link to Persistent native bisection without checkout\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck::NativeBisect\u003c/code> narrows a known good/bad range through a revision DAG,\nincluding merges. Its state is persistent and each mark uses an expected state\nID. The search does not move branches, alter staging or change working files.\nTesting a candidate remains an explicit caller action.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native bisect-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Start requires distinct revisions with the known good revision reachable from\nthe initial bad revision. Normal native revision selectors work in the CLI.\nThe JSON report contains \u003ccode>session\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>state\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>candidate_count\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>next_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>first_bad\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>good\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>skipped\u003c/code> and the current \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code> revision.\nUse the exact suggested revision and \u003ccode>state\u003c/code> from the latest report when\nrecording a test result. Another update makes an earlier state token invalid.\nA new random session identity prevents a stale token from becoming valid when\nthe same range is searched again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Known good revisions exclude their complete ancestry. The remaining candidates\nare ancestors of the current bad revision. For each eligible test revision,\nthe engine counts how many candidates remain if it is bad and how many remain\nif it is good, selecting the most balanced split with a deterministic ID tie\nbreak. Bitset ancestry propagation handles merges without counting shared\nancestors twice. The current known bad revision is not offered as another test.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>status: found\u003c/code> identifies one \u003ccode>first_bad\u003c/code> revision. A skipped/unbuildable\ncandidate remains a possible introduction point. If no unskipped test remains,\nthe report is \u003ccode>ambiguous\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>first_bad\u003c/code> stays null and \u003ccode>possible_first_bad\u003c/code> lists\nthe remaining possibilities. It does not silently choose one skipped commit.\nAs with a normal binary regression search, outcomes should describe the same\ntest with a defect that persists through descendants; contradictory or changing\ntest criteria cannot establish a reliable first introduction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"state-retention-and-recovery\">State, retention and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-bisect/state-retention-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to State, retention and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>One active search is supported per workspace context. Primary \u003ccode>bisect/active\u003c/code> points to a strict\ncanonical version-1 chunk descriptor; \u003ccode>bisect/pin\u003c/code> retains the initial bad\nrevision and all its ancestor/file dependencies. Starting publishes state and\npin together, after complete file verification. Removing or moving a visible\nbranch during the search does not allow GC to collect its test history.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Marks publish a new immutable state object through an expected-reference\ntransaction. Old state objects can be collected; there is no growing snapshot\narchive on every mark. Cancellation/crash before reference publication leaves\nthe previous active state; complete orphan metadata can remain. Fresh processes\nread the same search and continue with \u003ccode>bisect-status\u003c/code>. No runtime cache is\nrequired for correctness. Corrupt descriptors, missing/mismatched pins,\nout-of-history results and contradictory good/bad metadata are rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reset ends an unfinished or finished search, moving its history protection to\n\u003ccode>archive/bisect/INITIAL_BAD\u003c/code> and retaining its final descriptor under\n\u003ccode>bisect/receipts/SESSION\u003c/code>. These changes publish together. The old file versions\nremain reconstructable after GC. \u003ccode>native archives --kind bisect\u003c/code> inspects the\nhistory roots; \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">backup-protected retirement\u003c/a> can later\nremove them explicitly. A new search is then independent of the old session.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The revision/metadata traversal is bounded at 100000 revisions, 1000000 parent\nlinks and a 64 MiB estimated graph-metadata payload budget. At most 4096 unresolved\ncandidate revisions are admitted. The dense ancestry bitsets use at most 2 MiB;\ncontainer/cache/allocator memory is additional. Narrow an overly large initial\nrange with a later known good revision. There is no persistent commit-graph\nindex yet, so repeated status calls can reread revision metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Linked contexts retain their own scoped active state, history pin and receipts;\nselect them with \u003ccode>--workspace NAME\u003c/code>. Searches in different workspaces can remain\nactive independently while Store mutations still serialize through its lock.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-file-history\">Blame and exact rename history\u003c/a> are separate queries.\nInteractive bisect checkout, automatic command execution, rename-aware predicate\nselection remain work to complete. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">Optional revision signatures\u003c/a>\nare verified when authored metadata is read; they do not authenticate a user&#39;s\ngood/bad bisect classification.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Persistent native bisection without checkout # syrnck::NativeBisect narrows a known good/bad range through a revision DAG, including merges. Its state is persistent and each mark uses an expected state ID. The search does not move branches, alter staging or change working files. Testing a candidate remains an explicit caller action. text Copy syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION syrnck native bisect-status syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID Start requires distinct revisions with the known good revision reachable from the initial bad revision. Normal native revision selectors work in the CLI. The JSON report contains session , state , status , candidate_count , next_revision , first_bad , good , skipped and the current bad revision. Use the exact suggested revision and state from the latest report when recording a test result. Another update makes an earlier state token invalid. A new random session identity prevents a stale token from becoming valid when the same range is searched again. Known good revisions exclude their complete ancestry. The remaining candidates are ancestors of the current bad revision. For each eligible test revision, the engine counts how many candidates remain if it is bad and how many remain if it is good, selecting the most balanced split with a deterministic ID tie break. Bitset ancestry propagation handles merges without counting shared ancestors twice. The current known bad revision is not offered as another test. status: found identifies one first_bad revision. A skipped/unbuildable candidate remains a possible introduction point. If no unskipped test remains, the report is ambiguous , first_bad stays null and possible_first_bad lists the remaining possibilities. It does not silently choose one skipped commit. As with a normal binary regression search, outcomes should describe the same test with a defect that persists through descendants; contradictory or changing test criteria cannot establish a reliable first introduction. State, retention and recovery # One active search is supported per workspace context. Primary bisect/active points to a strict canonical version-1 chunk descriptor; bisect/pin retains the initial bad revision and all its ancestor/file dependencies. Starting publishes state and pin together, after complete file verification. Removing or moving a visible branch during the search does not allow GC to collect its test history. Marks publish a new immutable state object through an expected-reference transaction. Old state objects can be collected; there is no growing snapshot archive on every mark. Cancellation/crash before reference publication leaves the previous active state; complete orphan metadata can remain. Fresh processes read the same search and continue with bisect-status . No runtime cache is required for correctness. Corrupt descriptors, missing/mismatched pins, out-of-history results and contradictory good/bad metadata are rejected. Reset ends an unfinished or finished search, moving its history protection to archive/bisect/INITIAL_BAD and retaining its final descriptor under bisect/receipts/SESSION . These changes publish together. The old file versions remain reconstructable after GC. native archives --kind bisect inspects the history roots; backup-protected retirement can later remove them explicitly. A new search is then independent of the old session. The revision/metadata traversal is bounded at 100000 revisions, 1000000 parent links and a 64 MiB estimated graph-metadata payload budget. At most 4096 unresolved candidate revisions are admitted. The dense ancestry bitsets use at most 2 MiB; container/cache/allocator memory is additional. Narrow an overly large initial range with a later known good revision. There is no persistent commit-graph index yet, so repeated status calls can reread revision metadata. Linked contexts retain their own scoped active state, history pin and receipts; select them with --workspace NAME . Searches in different workspaces can remain active independently while Store mutations still serialize through its lock. Blame and exact rename history are separate queries. Interactive bisect checkout, automatic command execution, rename-aware predicate selection remain work to complete. Optional revision signatures are verified when authored metadata is read; they do not authenticate a user's good/bad bisect classification. syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION syrnck native bisect-status syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID","headings":[{"id":"persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout","text":"Persistent native bisection without checkout","level":1},{"id":"state-retention-and-recovery","text":"State, retention and recovery","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION\nsyrnck native bisect-status\nsyrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory\nsyrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID","assets":[],"links":["#/en/archive-retention","#/en/native-file-history","#/en/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-blame","title":"Native line attribution","description":"Verified blame through merge parents and exact renames.","section":"Version control","order":251,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Attribute lines\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-blame/usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Attribute lines\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native blame REVISION PATH\u003c/code> attributes each line of a committed UTF-8 text file\nto the revision and path that introduced it. It reconstructs and verifies stored\nbytes without reading the workspace, modifying references or importing objects.\nThe C++ entry point is \u003ccode>native_blame\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>syrnck/history.hpp\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The JSON \u003ccode>lines\u003c/code> array keeps final line numbers, origin \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, origin \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>,\none-based \u003ccode>source_line\u003c/code> and exact \u003ccode>content\u003c/code>, including LF/CRLF or a missing final\nnewline. Empty files have zero lines. \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code> indexes origin revision timestamps\nand bounded message previews. V2 entries also expose \u003ccode>author\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>committer\u003c/code> and\nthe public \u003ccode>signing_key\u003c/code> (null when unsigned). Legacy v1 has no such fields;\nthis command does not invent them. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">identity and trust\u003c/a>.\nJSON keys and identifiers remain English.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lineage\">Merges and renames\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-blame/lineage\" aria-label=\"Link to Merges and renames\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>By default every ordered parent can supply unchanged lines. The first matching\nparent takes precedence, then the remaining lines are compared with later\nparents. Only lines unmatched in every parent originate at the merge itself.\n\u003ccode>--first-parent\u003c/code> restricts attribution to the first-parent chain. Matching uses\nthe bounded Myers line-edit engine; repeated identical lines can admit several\nvalid alignments. No whitespace normalization is applied.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Unique exact-content renames are followed in either chunk representation, using\nthe complete-file hash and size. Executable-mode changes do not create new text\norigins. Multiple possible removed sources or added destinations stop following;\nedited renames and copies are not inferred. \u003ccode>--no-follow\u003c/code> treats a newly named\nfile as an addition. A binary parent cannot supply textual line origins.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Budgets and errors\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-blame/limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Budgets and errors\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The default traversal permits 1000 distinct revisions (1..10000), 64 MiB aggregate\ndecoded revision/manifest metadata and reconstructed file bytes (1..256 MiB), and\nthe \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text engine budgets\u003c/a>. These input/work budgets do not bound\nevery allocation in the process. Reconstructed content is cached per manifest\nonly for this query. Existing retained roots still protect the inspected history.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>status: complete\u003c/code> exits 0. Binary input returns \u003ccode>status: binary\u003c/code>, reason \u003ccode>non_text\u003c/code>,\nand exit 1. An exhausted revision/input/file/line/work/trace/output budget returns\n\u003ccode>status: limited\u003c/code>, a reason and exit 1. Both non-complete reports contain empty\n\u003ccode>lines\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code>; partial attribution is never presented as complete.\nMissing paths, corrupt/missing objects, invalid arguments and cancellation fail\nthe command with no partial report. Progress and diagnostics use the selected\ninterface language.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Attribute lines # native blame REVISION PATH attributes each line of a committed UTF-8 text file to the revision and path that introduced it. It reconstructs and verifies stored bytes without reading the workspace, modifying references or importing objects. The C++ entry point is native_blame in syrnck/history.hpp . text Copy syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow The JSON lines array keeps final line numbers, origin revision , origin path , one-based source_line and exact content , including LF/CRLF or a missing final newline. Empty files have zero lines. commits indexes origin revision timestamps and bounded message previews. V2 entries also expose author , committer and the public signing_key (null when unsigned). Legacy v1 has no such fields; this command does not invent them. See identity and trust . JSON keys and identifiers remain English. Merges and renames # By default every ordered parent can supply unchanged lines. The first matching parent takes precedence, then the remaining lines are compared with later parents. Only lines unmatched in every parent originate at the merge itself. --first-parent restricts attribution to the first-parent chain. Matching uses the bounded Myers line-edit engine; repeated identical lines can admit several valid alignments. No whitespace normalization is applied. Unique exact-content renames are followed in either chunk representation, using the complete-file hash and size. Executable-mode changes do not create new text origins. Multiple possible removed sources or added destinations stop following; edited renames and copies are not inferred. --no-follow treats a newly named file as an addition. A binary parent cannot supply textual line origins. Budgets and errors # The default traversal permits 1000 distinct revisions (1..10000), 64 MiB aggregate decoded revision/manifest metadata and reconstructed file bytes (1..256 MiB), and the text engine budgets . These input/work budgets do not bound every allocation in the process. Reconstructed content is cached per manifest only for this query. Existing retained roots still protect the inspected history. text Copy syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728 syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608 status: complete exits 0. Binary input returns status: binary , reason non_text , and exit 1. An exhausted revision/input/file/line/work/trace/output budget returns status: limited , a reason and exit 1. Both non-complete reports contain empty lines and commits ; partial attribution is never presented as complete. Missing paths, corrupt/missing objects, invalid arguments and cancellation fail the command with no partial report. Progress and diagnostics use the selected interface language. syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728 syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Attribute lines","level":2},{"id":"lineage","text":"Merges and renames","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Budgets and errors","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp\nsyrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-authorship","#/en/text-diff"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-branches","title":"Native branches and recoverable switching","description":"Native branches and recoverable switching guide.","section":"Version control","order":220,"parent":"native-history","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-branches-and-recoverable-switching\">Native branches and recoverable switching\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/native-branches-and-recoverable-switching\" aria-label=\"Link to Native branches and recoverable switching\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The native CLI normally selects a named branch and also supports\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">detached HEAD\u003c/a>. Existing Stores without a HEAD\ndescriptor implicitly select \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, including when that branch is unborn.\nReading HEAD never creates it. A successful switch records an explicit selection\nbound to the canonical workspace directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch feature REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native switch feature --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native switch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native branch NAME REVISION\u003c/code> creates a branch without selecting it or changing\nworking files. \u003ccode>native switch NAME\u003c/code> restores that branch&#39;s exact head and selects\nit. \u003cstrong>Neither branch head moves during switch\u003c/strong>. \u003ccode>native checkout REVISION\u003c/code>\ncontinues to restore an exact revision and move the selected branch, with an\narchive of its previous head. Both use the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">recoverable checkout\u003c/a>\nexecutor. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">\u003ccode>native switch --detach REVISION\u003c/code>\u003c/a> selects a\nrevision independently of named branches. Switch never creates a named branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"branch-inventory-and-verified-creation\">Branch inventory and verified creation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/branch-inventory-and-verified-creation\" aria-label=\"Link to Branch inventory and verified creation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native branches\u003c/code> lists existing \u003ccode>heads/\u003c/code> references in byte-sorted name order.\nIt returns the current \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> descriptor, a \u003ccode>branches\u003c/code> array, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, an\nexclusive \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> name cursor and \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>. An unborn selected\nbranch appears in \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, without an invented entry in the array. Empty pages\nhave a null cursor. \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> is 1..1000, default 100; a cursor need not still\nexist. Pages reflect the references present during each call, rather than a\nsnapshot spanning several invocations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each row includes \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>, file/parent counts, the revision\ntimestamp and a UTF-8-safe message preview of at most 256 bytes, with\n\u003ccode>message_bytes\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>message_truncated\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>stale_index\u003c/code> expose\nthe branch&#39;s frozen preparation, including stale indexes, without reading working\nfiles. Orphan indexes without a branch remain accessible through \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>native staged --branch NAME\u003c/code>; the inventory does not adopt them as branch heads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Metadata-only inventory does not audit block payloads. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits the full\nclosures of the displayed heads and indexes, including their baseline histories.\nThe selected head outside that page is not an additional verified root. Reports\nare bounded to 16 MiB. The implementation reads the reference table once for the\npage, then resolves its rows without copying that table per branch; the table\nitself is still loaded in full.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Creation accepts an existing exact revision in C++; the CLI also accepts normal\nrevision selectors. It verifies the complete retained data and publishes only if\nboth the destination head and staging slot are absent. Existing heads and orphan\nindexes are errors. A pending checkout/switch blocks creation. There is no force\nreplacement. Creation neither selects the branch nor checks out its files; if\nHEAD already symbolically names that unborn branch, its revision resolves to the\nnew head afterwards. Dirty working files remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Branch creation stays quiet on stdout in the CLI; its C++ result reports the\ncreated name/revision. Inventory emits English JSON in either interface language.\nBoth commands accept the ordinary progress/cancellation options. Reference\npublication uses the exclusive Store lock and expected absent slots. Cancellation,\nmissing retained bytes or observer failure before publication preserves existing\nreferences and working files. No callbacks run after the commit point.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"archived-branch-deletion\">Archived branch deletion\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/archived-branch-deletion\" aria-label=\"Link to Archived branch deletion\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Deletion removes an inactive branch&#39;s \u003ccode>heads/NAME\u003c/code> and optional \u003ccode>staging/NAME\u003c/code>\nreferences in the same transaction that publishes a retained archive. Its complete\ntip history and every frozen index entry/baseline stay reconstructible after GC.\nThe selected symbolic branch is protected, even when another branch points to the\nsame revision. A pending checkout/switch blocks all branch mutations. Working\nfiles and HEAD selection remain unchanged, including dirty or untracked files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An index, including a clean or stale one, requires \u003ccode>--archive-index\u003c/code>. Without that\noption the plan reports \u003ccode>staging_present\u003c/code>. The CLI requires an exact \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>\ntip ID, and optionally \u003ccode>--expect-index\u003c/code>; when archiving an index without that\nsecond option, it captures the current index under the Store lock. The C++ API\ntakes the expected optional index: null requires an absent slot, and an ID\nauthorizes archiving exactly that snapshot. Changed tip/index values fail before\npublication. There is no automatic dropping of unmerged versions: deletion retains\nthe entire archived history, without requiring an ancestor relationship.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">backup-protected retirement\u003c/a> can later remove a\ncomplete \u003ccode>archive/branches/ID\u003c/code> group. Its descriptor, tip and optional frozen\nindex must all be retained in the verified independent backup. This does not\ndelete a recreated live branch with the original name or run GC automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The read-only plan reports \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, a proposed \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> ID, \u003ccode>archive_existing\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>archive_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. A ready plan exits 0;\na blocked plan exits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid arguments or missing required\nmetadata produce diagnostics. The application adds \u003ccode>state: deleted\u003c/code>. Original\nbranch/index fields and \u003ccode>archive_existing\u003c/code> describe the inputs to that operation.\nApplication always audits complete retained file data before creating an archive\nrecord. Metadata-only plans do not audit block payloads; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> does.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Archive identity is deterministic from the original name, tip and optional index.\nRepeating deletion of that exact state reuses the same complete retained archive.\nAn existing partial or conflicting root group blocks deletion. Cancellation or\nobserver failure before publication preserves the previous references; preparing\nan archive can leave complete unreferenced metadata. Inspect the branch and archive\nbefore retrying an interrupted application. The operation uses the ordinary atomic\nreference writer and does not need a working-file journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each archive has an ordinary chunk record with canonical JSON \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>type: native-branch-archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> and nullable \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Retained object\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/record\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Archive descriptor chunk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/target\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Original revision and its full history\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/index\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Optional original staging snapshot and baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The browser validates descriptor identity, exact root groups and branch-bound\nstaging metadata. \u003ccode>branch-archives\u003c/code> returns bounded pages sorted by archive ID,\nwith an exclusive \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> ID cursor, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> is 1..1000. Each row identifies its \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> and original \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, with\nthe same revision/index summary as the branch inventory, excluding \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>branch-archive\u003c/code> returns one such row in its \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> field. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits\nall displayed roots. Index baselines remain exactly as recorded, including stale\nand initially unborn preparations. Archives have no automatic expiry. Generic\nroot removal can release their retention; keep required archives through GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Branch archives use this dedicated browser. The ordinary \u003ccode>native archives\u003c/code>\ncommand continues to list revision archives such as checkout, reset and amend.\nStorage/object formats are unchanged; the descriptor and its explicit dependency\nroots use existing chunk/revision/reference mechanisms.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> deletion \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_delete_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> removed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">delete_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_tip\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> saved \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_branch_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"restore-an-archived-branch\">Restore an archived branch\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/restore-an-archived-branch\" aria-label=\"Link to Restore an archived branch\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Restoration recreates the archived head and optional frozen index in one\nreference transaction. Both destination slots must be absent; an existing branch\nor orphan index blocks it, even if it points to the same object. A pending\ncheckout/switch also blocks restoration. The source archive remains retained.\nWorking files and symbolic HEAD selection are unchanged; if that selection already\nnames the unborn destination, its revision resolves to the restored head afterwards.\nUse the ordinary explicit checkout/switch workflow to restore working files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Without \u003ccode>--as\u003c/code>, the original name and exact staging ID are restored. \u003ccode>--as NAME\u003c/code>\nchooses another branch name. When an index exists, the engine creates an ordinary\nstaging revision changing only its branch-bound internal message. Its frozen file\nentries, executable modes, timestamp and parents stay intact. The candidate ID is\ndeterministic and may not be stored until application. Initially unborn indexes\nkeep an empty parent list. Stale indexes remain stale; inspect them with\n\u003ccode>native staged --branch NAME\u003c/code> and explicitly use\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase\">staging rebase\u003c/a> if their changes must move onto the head.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The read-only report includes \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>original_name\u003c/code>, destination \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, source \u003ccode>previous_index\u003c/code>, proposed \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_rewritten\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>index_base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stale_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>.\nA ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON. Application always verifies\nthe complete archive closure before storing a rewritten index or publishing\nreferences, and adds \u003ccode>state: restored\u003c/code> to its result. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits previewed\nsource data; metadata-only planning does not read file payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Publication guards the retained archive group, both absent destination slots and\nthe current HEAD object. Source roots remain unchanged. Cancellation or observer\nfailure before publication preserves the old reference table; a renamed index\ncandidate can remain unreferenced. After interruption, inspect the destination\nand archive before retrying. A completed destination is a collision, rather than\nan implicit replacement. Original archived versions remain available through GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_restore_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>archive_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restored \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>archive_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity\">CLI defaults and workspace identity\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity\" aria-label=\"Link to CLI defaults and workspace identity\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>When omitted, \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> resolves the current branch or detached target for native commit (ordinary\nor staged), add, staged, reset, status (ordinary or combined), checkout-plan,\ncheckout, log and history detachment. Explicit \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> keeps its existing\nmeaning: operate on that named branch, without changing HEAD. Explicit\n\u003ccode>status --revision\u003c/code> selects the requested snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Snapshot arguments also accept \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-tags\">native tags and revision selectors\u003c/a>.\nOrdinary branch switching takes a literal branch name; a tag is restored with\n\u003ccode>native checkout tags/NAME\u003c/code>, which moves the selected branch and archives its\nprevious head. Use \u003ccode>native switch --detach tags/NAME\u003c/code> to detach instead.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> reports English JSON fields in both interface languages:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>version\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Report schema version, currently 1\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current symbolic branch name, or null for detached HEAD\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Its current revision ID, or null for an unborn branch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>explicit\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whether an explicit descriptor exists\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>directory\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bound canonical workspace, or null for legacy implicit HEAD\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>HEAD resolves the branch dynamically. A later commit or checkout on that branch\nchanges \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> without rewriting the descriptor. Generic deletion of its\nbranch leaves an unborn symbolic selection; it does not select another branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once HEAD is explicit, workspace commands require the same canonical directory.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">Linked workspaces\u003c/a> have independent HEADs and indexes;\n\u003ccode>--workspace NAME\u003c/code> selects one and defaults native \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> to its registered\npath. Primary commands retain the \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> default. A named branch can be selected\nin one workspace at a time. Relocation, sparse materialization and unborn\ndestination switching remain future work. Low-level C++ methods with an\nexplicit branch argument keep their existing contract; they do not implicitly\nresolve HEAD. Applications can use \u003ccode>native_target()\u003c/code> to select a branch or the\nopaque detached target; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">detached HEAD\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preflight-publication-and-recovery\">Preflight, publication and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/preflight-publication-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Preflight, publication and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native switch-plan NAME [--verify]\u003c/code> is read-only. It starts with ordinary\ncheckout preflight against the current branch, targeting the destination head.\nIt adds \u003ccode>operation: &quot;switch&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>destination_branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head_object\u003c/code> (the current\ndescriptor ID or null) and \u003ccode>destination_staging_snapshot\u003c/code>. An index on either\nbranch blocks switch, even if it has no staged changes; a destination index adds\nthe \u003ccode>target_staging_present\u003c/code> conflict. A completed blocked plan emits JSON and\nexits 1, while invalid arguments or missing required objects emit no report.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Switch requires the destination to exist and point to a revision. Every tracked\nsource file must be clean by complete hash. Untracked/ignored collisions, links,\nportable aliases and file/directory transitions follow the existing checkout\npolicy. There is no force, overwrite or automatic index reset option. A clean\nswitch to the already selected branch is a read-only no-op. Different branches\nat the same revision still publish a new symbolic selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The transaction checks the expected source head, destination head, both staging\nslots and previous HEAD before workspace application. Preparation verifies\ncomplete closures and reconstructs targets. Exclusive file moves preserve\noriginals. The final atomic reference transaction publishes HEAD together with\nthe committed journal, source archive, target retention and receipt; both branch\nheads are guarded without being moved. Before that boundary, HEAD remains the\noriginal selection, even if working files have partly changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Inspect and recover with the existing commands:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Before publication, abort restores originals and keeps the old HEAD. Resume\nfinishes the new selection. After publication, recovery can only finish cleanup.\nIf a branch, staging slot or HEAD was changed through generic reference/admin\noperations, recovery refuses rather than replacing that state. Store opening\ndoes not automatically change working files. File moves are sequential and\nexternal editors must be quiesced. Prepared targets and original backups remain\nplaintext, including with encrypted object storage; all existing checkout\nresource, cancellation, retention and private-data limits apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"representation-and-c-api\">Representation and C++ API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-branches/representation-and-c-api\" aria-label=\"Link to Representation and C++ API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> retains an ordinary chunk containing canonical JSON with\n\u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type: &quot;native-head&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>. It benefits from\nexisting compression/encryption/packing, with no new object kind or physical\nformat. The descriptor contains no frozen revision pointer. Branch references\ncontinue to retain their own complete closures during GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Switch uses checkout journal version 2, adding \u003ccode>destination_branch\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>previous_head\u003c/code> (nullable chunk ID) and \u003ccode>next_head\u003c/code> (chunk ID) to its hashed\nidentity. Version 1 checkout journals and receipts remain readable. GC is blocked\nwhile either version is active. Older descriptor objects can be collected after\nlater switches; completed receipts remain inspectable because they do not need\nthose old descriptors to resolve their recorded fields. Active journals and HEAD\nare local workspace state and must not be edited or replicated as ordinary\nrelease roots. Older binaries do not interpret the new selection; use updated\nbinaries for native workspace commands after enabling it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> head \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> branches \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_branches\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> created \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_switch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> switched \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">switch_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The C ABI and Go binding do not expose native branch operations yet.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native branches and recoverable switching # The native CLI normally selects a named branch and also supports detached HEAD . Existing Stores without a HEAD descriptor implicitly select main , including when that branch is unborn. Reading HEAD never creates it. A successful switch records an explicit selection bound to the canonical workspace directory. text Copy syrnck native head syrnck native branch feature REVISION syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify syrnck native switch feature --progress syrnck native status --combined syrnck native add Source Content/Maps syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\" syrnck native log syrnck native switch main native branch NAME REVISION creates a branch without selecting it or changing working files. native switch NAME restores that branch's exact head and selects it. Neither branch head moves during switch . native checkout REVISION continues to restore an exact revision and move the selected branch, with an archive of its previous head. Both use the recoverable checkout executor. native switch --detach REVISION selects a revision independently of named branches. Switch never creates a named branch. Branch inventory and verified creation # text Copy syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100 syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress native branches lists existing heads/ references in byte-sorted name order. It returns the current head descriptor, a branches array, has_more , an exclusive next_after name cursor and objects_verified . An unborn selected branch appears in head , without an invented entry in the array. Empty pages have a null cursor. --limit is 1..1000, default 100; a cursor need not still exist. Pages reflect the references present during each call, rather than a snapshot spanning several invocations. Each row includes name , revision , current , file/parent counts, the revision timestamp and a UTF-8-safe message preview of at most 256 bytes, with message_bytes / message_truncated . index , index_base and stale_index expose the branch's frozen preparation, including stale indexes, without reading working files. Orphan indexes without a branch remain accessible through refs and native staged --branch NAME ; the inventory does not adopt them as branch heads. Metadata-only inventory does not audit block payloads. --verify audits the full closures of the displayed heads and indexes, including their baseline histories. The selected head outside that page is not an additional verified root. Reports are bounded to 16 MiB. The implementation reads the reference table once for the page, then resolves its rows without copying that table per branch; the table itself is still loaded in full. Creation accepts an existing exact revision in C++; the CLI also accepts normal revision selectors. It verifies the complete retained data and publishes only if both the destination head and staging slot are absent. Existing heads and orphan indexes are errors. A pending checkout/switch blocks creation. There is no force replacement. Creation neither selects the branch nor checks out its files; if HEAD already symbolically names that unborn branch, its revision resolves to the new head afterwards. Dirty working files remain unchanged. Branch creation stays quiet on stdout in the CLI; its C++ result reports the created name/revision. Inventory emits English JSON in either interface language. Both commands accept the ordinary progress/cancellation options. Reference publication uses the exclusive Store lock and expected absent slots. Cancellation, missing retained bytes or observer failure before publication preserves existing references and working files. No callbacks run after the commit point. Archived branch deletion # text Copy syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify Deletion removes an inactive branch's heads/NAME and optional staging/NAME references in the same transaction that publishes a retained archive. Its complete tip history and every frozen index entry/baseline stay reconstructible after GC. The selected symbolic branch is protected, even when another branch points to the same revision. A pending checkout/switch blocks all branch mutations. Working files and HEAD selection remain unchanged, including dirty or untracked files. An index, including a clean or stale one, requires --archive-index . Without that option the plan reports staging_present . The CLI requires an exact --expect tip ID, and optionally --expect-index ; when archiving an index without that second option, it captures the current index under the Store lock. The C++ API takes the expected optional index: null requires an absent slot, and an ID authorizes archiving exactly that snapshot. Changed tip/index values fail before publication. There is no automatic dropping of unmerged versions: deletion retains the entire archived history, without requiring an ancestor relationship. Explicit backup-protected retirement can later remove a complete archive/branches/ID group. Its descriptor, tip and optional frozen index must all be retained in the verified independent backup. This does not delete a recreated live branch with the original name or run GC automatically. The read-only plan reports branch , a proposed archive ID, archive_existing , archive_index , ready , objects_verified and blockers . A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid arguments or missing required metadata produce diagnostics. The application adds state: deleted . Original branch/index fields and archive_existing describe the inputs to that operation. Application always audits complete retained file data before creating an archive record. Metadata-only plans do not audit block payloads; --verify does. Archive identity is deterministic from the original name, tip and optional index. Repeating deletion of that exact state reuses the same complete retained archive. An existing partial or conflicting root group blocks deletion. Cancellation or observer failure before publication preserves the previous references; preparing an archive can leave complete unreferenced metadata. Inspect the branch and archive before retrying an interrupted application. The operation uses the ordinary atomic reference writer and does not need a working-file journal. Each archive has an ordinary chunk record with canonical JSON version: 1 , type: native-branch-archive , name , revision and nullable index : Reference Retained object archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/record Archive descriptor chunk archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/target Original revision and its full history archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/index Optional original staging snapshot and baseline The browser validates descriptor identity, exact root groups and branch-bound staging metadata. branch-archives returns bounded pages sorted by archive ID, with an exclusive next_after ID cursor, has_more and objects_verified . --limit is 1..1000. Each row identifies its archive and original name , with the same revision/index summary as the branch inventory, excluding current . branch-archive returns one such row in its archive field. --verify audits all displayed roots. Index baselines remain exactly as recorded, including stale and initially unborn preparations. Archives have no automatic expiry. Generic root removal can release their retention; keep required archives through GC. Branch archives use this dedicated browser. The ordinary native archives command continues to list revision archives such as checkout, reset and amend. Storage/object formats are unchanged; the descriptor and its explicit dependency roots use existing chunk/revision/reference mechanisms. cpp Copy auto deletion = store . plan_delete_native_branch ( \"feature\" , true , true , operation ) ; auto removed = store . delete_native_branch ( \"feature\" , expected_tip , expected_index , operation ) ; auto saved = store . native_branch_archive ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true , operation ) ; Restore an archived branch # text Copy syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic Restoration recreates the archived head and optional frozen index in one reference transaction. Both destination slots must be absent; an existing branch or orphan index blocks it, even if it points to the same object. A pending checkout/switch also blocks restoration. The source archive remains retained. Working files and symbolic HEAD selection are unchanged; if that selection already names the unborn destination, its revision resolves to the restored head afterwards. Use the ordinary explicit checkout/switch workflow to restore working files. Without --as , the original name and exact staging ID are restored. --as NAME chooses another branch name. When an index exists, the engine creates an ordinary staging revision changing only its branch-bound internal message. Its frozen file entries, executable modes, timestamp and parents stay intact. The candidate ID is deterministic and may not be stored until application. Initially unborn indexes keep an empty parent list. Stale indexes remain stale; inspect them with native staged --branch NAME and explicitly use staging rebase if their changes must move onto the head. The read-only report includes archive , original_name , destination name , revision , source previous_index , proposed index , index_rewritten , index_base , stale_index , current , ready , objects_verified and blockers . A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON. Application always verifies the complete archive closure before storing a rewritten index or publishing references, and adds state: restored to its result. --verify audits previewed source data; metadata-only planning does not read file payloads. Publication guards the retained archive group, both absent destination slots and the current HEAD object. Source roots remain unchanged. Cancellation or observer failure before publication preserves the old reference table; a renamed index candidate can remain unreferenced. After interruption, inspect the destination and archive before retrying. A completed destination is a collision, rather than an implicit replacement. Original archived versions remain available through GC. cpp Copy auto preview = store . plan_restore_native_branch ( archive_id , \"recovered\" , true , operation ) ; auto restored = store . restore_native_branch ( archive_id , \"recovered\" , operation ) ; CLI defaults and workspace identity # When omitted, --branch resolves the current branch or detached target for native commit (ordinary or staged), add, staged, reset, status (ordinary or combined), checkout-plan, checkout, log and history detachment. Explicit --branch keeps its existing meaning: operate on that named branch, without changing HEAD. Explicit status --revision selects the requested snapshot. Snapshot arguments also accept native tags and revision selectors . Ordinary branch switching takes a literal branch name; a tag is restored with native checkout tags/NAME , which moves the selected branch and archives its previous head. Use native switch --detach tags/NAME to detach instead. native head reports English JSON fields in both interface languages: Field Meaning version Report schema version, currently 1 branch Current symbolic branch name, or null for detached HEAD revision Its current revision ID, or null for an unborn branch explicit Whether an explicit descriptor exists directory Bound canonical workspace, or null for legacy implicit HEAD HEAD resolves the branch dynamically. A later commit or checkout on that branch changes revision without rewriting the descriptor. Generic deletion of its branch leaves an unborn symbolic selection; it does not select another branch. Once HEAD is explicit, workspace commands require the same canonical directory. Linked workspaces have independent HEADs and indexes; --workspace NAME selects one and defaults native --directory to its registered path. Primary commands retain the . default. A named branch can be selected in one workspace at a time. Relocation, sparse materialization and unborn destination switching remain future work. Low-level C++ methods with an explicit branch argument keep their existing contract; they do not implicitly resolve HEAD. Applications can use native_target() to select a branch or the opaque detached target; see detached HEAD . Preflight, publication and recovery # native switch-plan NAME [--verify] is read-only. It starts with ordinary checkout preflight against the current branch, targeting the destination head. It adds operation: \"switch\" , destination_branch , head_object (the current descriptor ID or null) and destination_staging_snapshot . An index on either branch blocks switch, even if it has no staged changes; a destination index adds the target_staging_present conflict. A completed blocked plan emits JSON and exits 1, while invalid arguments or missing required objects emit no report. Switch requires the destination to exist and point to a revision. Every tracked source file must be clean by complete hash. Untracked/ignored collisions, links, portable aliases and file/directory transitions follow the existing checkout policy. There is no force, overwrite or automatic index reset option. A clean switch to the already selected branch is a read-only no-op. Different branches at the same revision still publish a new symbolic selection. The transaction checks the expected source head, destination head, both staging slots and previous HEAD before workspace application. Preparation verifies complete closures and reconstructs targets. Exclusive file moves preserve originals. The final atomic reference transaction publishes HEAD together with the committed journal, source archive, target retention and receipt; both branch heads are guarded without being moved. Before that boundary, HEAD remains the original selection, even if working files have partly changed. Inspect and recover with the existing commands: text Copy syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID Before publication, abort restores originals and keeps the old HEAD. Resume finishes the new selection. After publication, recovery can only finish cleanup. If a branch, staging slot or HEAD was changed through generic reference/admin operations, recovery refuses rather than replacing that state. Store opening does not automatically change working files. File moves are sequential and external editors must be quiesced. Prepared targets and original backups remain plaintext, including with encrypted object storage; all existing checkout resource, cancellation, retention and private-data limits apply. Representation and C++ API # workspace/HEAD retains an ordinary chunk containing canonical JSON with version: 1 , type: \"native-head\" , directory and branch . It benefits from existing compression/encryption/packing, with no new object kind or physical format. The descriptor contains no frozen revision pointer. Branch references continue to retain their own complete closures during GC. Switch uses checkout journal version 2, adding destination_branch , previous_head (nullable chunk ID) and next_head (chunk ID) to its hashed identity. Version 1 checkout journals and receipts remain readable. GC is blocked while either version is active. Older descriptor objects can be collected after later switches; completed receipts remain inspectable because they do not need those old descriptors to resolve their recorded fields. Active journals and HEAD are local workspace state and must not be edited or replicated as ordinary release roots. Older binaries do not interpret the new selection; use updated binaries for native workspace commands after enabling it. cpp Copy auto head = store . native_head ( ) ; auto branches = store . native_branches ( { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto created = store . create_native_branch ( \"feature\" , revision_id , operation ) ; auto plan = store . plan_switch ( project , \"feature\" , true , operation ) ; auto switched = store . switch_directory ( project , \"feature\" , operation ) ; The C ABI and Go binding do not expose native branch operations yet. syrnck native head syrnck native branch feature REVISION syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify syrnck native switch feature --progress syrnck native status --combined syrnck native add Source Content/Maps syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\" syrnck native log syrnck native switch main syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100 syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify auto deletion = store.plan_delete_native_branch(\"feature\", true, true, operation); auto removed = store.delete_native_branch(\"feature\", expected_tip, expected_index, operation); auto saved = store.native_branch_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation); syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic auto preview = store.plan_restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", true, operation); auto restored = store.restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", operation); syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID auto head = store.native_head(); auto branches = store.native_branches({}, 100, true, operation); auto created = store.create_native_branch(\"feature\", revision_id, operation); auto plan = store.plan_switch(project, \"feature\", true, operation); auto switched = store.switch_directory(project, \"feature\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-branches-and-recoverable-switching","text":"Native branches and recoverable switching","level":1},{"id":"branch-inventory-and-verified-creation","text":"Branch inventory and verified creation","level":2},{"id":"archived-branch-deletion","text":"Archived branch deletion","level":2},{"id":"restore-an-archived-branch","text":"Restore an archived branch","level":2},{"id":"cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity","text":"CLI defaults and workspace identity","level":2},{"id":"preflight-publication-and-recovery","text":"Preflight, publication and recovery","level":2},{"id":"representation-and-c-api","text":"Representation and C++ API","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-7-L4","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native head\nsyrnck native branch feature REVISION\nsyrnck native switch-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native switch feature --progress\nsyrnck native status --combined\nsyrnck native add Source Content/Maps\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\"\nsyrnck native log\nsyrnck native switch main\nsyrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100\nsyrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress\nsyrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify\nsyrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nauto deletion = store.plan_delete_native_branch(\"feature\", true, true, operation);\nauto removed = store.delete_native_branch(\"feature\", expected_tip, expected_index, operation);\nauto saved = store.native_branch_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation);\nsyrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID\nsyrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify\nsyrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic\nauto preview = store.plan_restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", true, operation);\nauto restored = store.restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", operation);\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\nauto head = store.native_head();\nauto branches = store.native_branches({}, 100, true, operation);\nauto created = store.create_native_branch(\"feature\", revision_id, operation);\nauto plan = store.plan_switch(project, \"feature\", true, operation);\nauto switched = store.switch_directory(project, \"feature\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-detached","#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-detached","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/native-stage-rebase","#/en/native-tags","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/native-detached"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-checkout","title":"Recoverable native checkout","description":"Recoverable native checkout guide.","section":"Version control","order":230,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"recoverable-native-checkout\">Recoverable native checkout\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-native-checkout\" aria-label=\"Link to Recoverable native checkout\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout\u003c/code> restores an exact stored revision into an existing workspace\nand moves the explicitly selected native branch to it. The previous branch head\nis archived before any of its retention roots are released. This is the first,\nrestricted executor of the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">checkout preflight\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default branch is the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches\">current native branch\u003c/a>, implicitly\n\u003ccode>main\u003c/code> in legacy Stores; the default directory is \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>. Selecting \u003ccode>--branch feature\u003c/code>\nmoves that branch, using its current snapshot as the workspace baseline.\n\u003ccode>native switch BRANCH\u003c/code> selects a different branch while preserving both heads.\nThe Store must be strictly inside the canonical workspace directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The CLI resolves \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-tags\">revision selectors\u003c/a>, including native tags,\nbefore preflight. C++ callers can resolve them explicitly with\n\u003ccode>resolve_native_revision()\u003c/code>; the checkout API itself retains revision-ID inputs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"starting-and-publishing\">Starting and publishing\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/starting-and-publishing\" aria-label=\"Link to Starting and publishing\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The initial policy requires every tracked file to match its baseline by complete\ncontent hash and supported executable metadata. An existing staging index blocks\ncheckout, even if it has no staged changes. Ignored and untracked collisions,\nlinks/junctions, protected paths and ambiguous portable aliases are refused.\nClean tracked case changes and file/directory transitions use the namespace\nexecutor described below. There is no \u003ccode>--force\u003c/code> option.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a revision change, execution repeats preflight and verifies the complete\nsource and target closures, including historical parents and file hashes,\nbefore publishing its journal.\nThus a metadata-only plan is insufficient to execute checkout. A clean checkout\nwhose target already equals the branch head returns \u003ccode>state: &quot;unchanged&quot;\u003c/code> without\ncreating a transaction. Unrelated untracked and ignored files remain untouched.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For each changed file the engine first reconstructs and verifies the complete\ntarget into a private slot. After rechecking the workspace, it moves the original\nto a backup slot and installs the prepared target with exclusive, no-overwrite\nmoves. Deletions retain their original file in a backup. Representation-only\nchanges need no file move; mode-only changes currently prepare a complete file.\nExecutable bits are applied on Unix. Windows preserves their snapshot metadata\nwithout applying it to the filesystem or treating it as a local modification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All desired tracked files are verified again after the last public progress\ncallback. One compare-and-swap reference transaction then moves the branch,\nmarks checkout committed, archives the source and retains the target and receipt.\nOnly after that publication does the engine remove verified private data and\nrelease the active journal/pending roots. Success returns \u003ccode>state: &quot;committed&quot;\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>pending: false\u003c/code>, the transaction \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, and its \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> reference or null for\nan unborn branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Publication is atomic for the branch/journal references. \u003cstrong>Workspace file moves\nare sequential\u003c/strong>, so readers can see intermediate contents. Stop external\nwriters while checking out; this is not an atomic filesystem snapshot, workspace\nlock or defense against hostile concurrent namespace replacement. An IO error\nor cancellation after journal creation requires explicit recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspecting-resuming-and-aborting\">Inspecting, resuming and aborting\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/inspecting-resuming-and-aborting\" aria-label=\"Link to Inspecting, resuming and aborting\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>checkout-status\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>pending: false\u003c/code> when no operation is active. Otherwise\nit reports the immutable transaction identity, branch, source, target, canonical\nworkspace, state, private \u003ccode>data_directory\u003c/code>, and affected paths with their numeric\nslot names. \u003ccode>--id\u003c/code> can inspect the active transaction or a completed receipt.\nJSON fields and state names remain English with either interface language.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>State\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Resume\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Abort\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>preparing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish reconstruction, recheck the clean baseline, then apply\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Discard prepared outputs without touching working files\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>applying\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Validate backups/installed files and finish application\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Restore originals in reverse action order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>evacuating\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish backing up changed sources and removing required empty directories\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Uninstall targets, then restore sources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>installing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Validate/install all targets, then publish\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Uninstall all targets, then restore sources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>uninstalling\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue the requested target removal\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue target removal\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>restoring\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue the requested source restoration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue source restoration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>aborting\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue the requested rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>discarding\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish discarding preparation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish discarding preparation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>aborted\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish cleanup without replaying file moves\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish cleanup\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>committed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finish cleanup without changing the published branch\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Refused; branch publication already succeeded\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Recovery always uses the recorded workspace/branch; it accepts no override.\nOpening a Store does not automatically change its working files. The journal\nis bound to the canonical workspace location, so copying or relocating it is\nnot a supported recovery procedure. A completed recovery is idempotent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If a working file or private output has changed, recovery preserves those bytes\nand refuses the unsafe step. Inspect \u003ccode>checkout-status\u003c/code> and preserve such edits\nbefore retrying. A source edited immediately before its backup move is restored\nverbatim during abort. Rollback persists its \u003ccode>aborted\u003c/code> marker before removing\nprepared outputs, so interrupted cleanup cannot mistake a later working file\nfor an installed target. Newly created empty parent directories may remain\nafter abort. The executor does not recursively remove working directories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>While checkout is pending, GC (including preview) and native commit, add, reset,\nstaged commit and history detachment are blocked. Packing and read-only\ninspection remain available. Generic reference/admin operations are not a\ncheckout override: changing a branch or internal retention root can make\nrecovery refuse to proceed. Do not edit or replicate the active checkout roots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"owned-recovery\">Guarded recovery by a resident agent\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/owned-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Guarded recovery by a resident agent\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent\">bidirectional resident agent\u003c/a> marks its incoming checkouts\nand merge checkouts with an explicit owner ID. Journal v11 freezes that owner,\ncontext and both materialization definitions, with layout 1 or 3 for ordinary or\nnamespace application. The owner participates in the immutable transaction ID.\n\u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> is established before durable observations or owned journals;\nolder readers reject the Store instead of ignoring the new recovery state.\nUnowned checkout encodings remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_directory_owned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> owner_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout_owned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> owner_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The recovery method checks the owner, selected context, current physical workspace\nand HEAD target before applying anything. The ID is a coordination tag, not an\nauthentication credential against a trusted local Store writer. An agent also\nrequires its retained remote observation to be an ancestor of the prepared target.\nAnother endpoint, workspace or publication-only session leaves it blocked.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Active owned transactions can finish offline without a signing private key: the\nexact incoming or signed merge target is already frozen and retained. Recovery\ndoes not compose or re-sign a revision. Starting a new signed operation still\nrequires the signing identity. Manual or unrelated transactions require the\nordinary explicit \u003ccode>checkout-recover\u003c/code>; opening a Store alone performs no recovery.\nCompleted owned receipts remain inspectable and retained under the same rules\nas other receipts. Backups preserve reader requirements and complete revision\ndata, without activating old physical checkout state in a restored directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"journal-retention-and-private-data\">Journal, retention and private data\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/journal-retention-and-private-data\" aria-label=\"Link to Journal, retention and private data\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The immutable journal is an ordinary chunk object, so existing compression,\nencryption and pack support apply without changing logical/physical formats.\nIts identity hashes the version, nonce, canonical directory, branch and source/\ntarget revisions. Switch journals add destination and expected/new HEAD fields\nin version 2. Namespace checkout/switch journals use versions 3/4 and an explicit\n\u003ccode>namespace_transition: true\u003c/code> flag; versions 1/2 remain supported. Each phase publishes\na new journal object.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset\">Hard reset\u003c/a> uses version 5, which distinguishes the\ncaptured working snapshot from the original branch head/index and records the\nsource directory profile. Versions 1–4 keep their existing semantics.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Retained value\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkouts/active\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current journal object; at most one checkout per Store\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkouts/pending/ID/target\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete target closure until cleanup\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkouts/pending/ID/source\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete original closure when a source exists\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/checkout/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Previous head after successful publication\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkouts/completed/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Published target after successful publication\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkouts/receipts/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Committed journal for later inspection\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The active/pending roots are released only after cleanup. Archives, completed\ntargets and receipts remain retained; automatic pruning is not implemented.\nDeleting a branch therefore does not alone discard checkout history. Removing\nall roots and running destructive GC intentionally makes those versions\nunrecoverable. An aborted transaction leaves no permanent receipt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Private files live at \u003ccode>STORE/checkouts/ID/{new,old}/N.bin\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>They contain decoded\nplaintext even in an encrypted Store\u003c/strong>, just as the workspace does. Existing\nobject encryption does not encrypt these temporary targets or original-file\nbackups. Unix private directories use owner-only permissions; Windows relies on\nthe Store&#39;s inherited ACLs. Filesystem encryption is needed to protect these\nworking bytes at rest. Prepared targets require extra disk space; backups are\nmoved original files. Source, private slots and destinations must support\nexclusive moves on the same filesystem. Cross-filesystem moves fail with the\njournal retained for recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cleanup validates known slot names, types and expected hashes before removing\nfiles, and removes only empty directories. Unexpected or changed private data\nis retained with an error. Windows may briefly defer deletion of a file; cleanup\nretries only nonrecursive empty-directory removal for up to about 500 ms.\nOther IO failures leave the journal available for another recovery attempt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-progress-and-limits\">C++, progress and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/c-progress-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to C++, progress and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> pending \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> resumed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> aborted \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> receipt \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>transaction_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Callbacks are synchronous and must not reenter the Store. Preflight/comparison\nuse their existing scanning, planning and verification events. Preparation adds\nper-file reconstruction; application/rollback report \u003ccode>exporting\u003c/code> with logical\nchanged-file byte totals, interspersed with per-file verification. Counters can\nrestart between these phases and on recovery. The last observer before branch\npublication is \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> with path \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>. Cancellation or observer failure\nbefore that commit point keeps the original branch, but may leave a partial\nworkspace and pending journal. After publication, cleanup finishes without\nfurther observers or cancellation. An error after publication can therefore\nreport failure with a committed branch; inspect status and resume cleanup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The shared 1 MiB content buffer, snapshot tables, manifest-summary caches,\n16 MiB object limits and bounded workspace inventory still apply. This version\nperforms complete preflight/final hashes and closure verification, reconstructs\nwhole changed files, and hashes backups before cleanup. It does not yet use\nin-place block patching or parallel materialization; the advisory work index does not replace these checks.\nThe C ABI and Go binding do not expose native checkout yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recoverable-namespace-transitions\">Recoverable namespace transitions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\" aria-label=\"Link to Recoverable namespace transitions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Clean tracked files can change case, including parent directories, or cross\nfile/directory boundaries. For example, \u003ccode>Source/Code.cpp\u003c/code> can become\n\u003ccode>source/code.cpp\u003c/code>, a tracked \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> file can become \u003ccode>Content/Map.uasset\u003c/code>, and\na fully tracked \u003ccode>Content/...\u003c/code> tree can become a \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> file. Ordinary renamed\npaths are already represented as removals/additions; rename detection and\nhistory following remain separate work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Planning keeps the full clean-tracked policy, checks exact portable directory\nspellings, and reports \u003ccode>namespace_transition: true\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>remove_directories\u003c/code> when\nthe operation requires this executor. Each affected directory must contain only\nthe source snapshot&#39;s files and their known parent directories. Untracked or\nignored descendants, unknown empty directories, links and protected storage\nblock the entire operation before journal publication. Snapshots with inconsistent\ncase spellings for the same portable parent are rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The executor prepares every target, then backs up \u003cstrong>all changed source files\u003c/strong>\nbefore installing any target. It removes only the snapshot-derived directories\nneeded for the transition, deepest first, and only while empty. There is no\nrecursive removal. It persists \u003ccode>installing\u003c/code> before creating target parents and\nmoving the prepared files. Final hashes, exact namespace checks and atomic\nbranch/archive/journal publication retain the existing guarantees.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Abort first moves installed targets back into their prepared slots, removes\nonly required empty target directories, persists \u003ccode>restoring\u003c/code>, then restores the\noriginal sources and their spelling. Distinct durable phases prevent resumed\nrollback from confusing restored originals with still-installed targets.\nChanged working files and newly appeared obstructions are preserved and block\nunsafe recovery. Opening the Store still performs no recovery automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Switch, merge, replay and linear rebase use the shared checkout executor; branch\nswitch preserves both tips and publishes its HEAD descriptor with the journal.\nNo new object, pointer, revision or network format is introduced. Older engines\ncannot recover namespace journals and must reject their unsupported versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Version 6 supports \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stash\">stash application\u003c/a>: the branch head stays\nfixed while the working target, optional frozen index and stash pop are\npublished together. All previous journal versions remain readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/checkout-retention\">Completed receipt retirement\u003c/a> removes selected\nreceipt/target groups only after independent backup coverage. Historical receipt\ninspection does not require an obsolete HEAD selection or deliberately retired\nsource snapshot; active recovery keeps the full binding/snapshot checks.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Recoverable native checkout # native checkout restores an exact stored revision into an existing workspace and moves the explicitly selected native branch to it. The previous branch head is archived before any of its retention roots are released. This is the first, restricted executor of the checkout preflight . text Copy syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID The default branch is the current native branch , implicitly main in legacy Stores; the default directory is . . Selecting --branch feature moves that branch, using its current snapshot as the workspace baseline. native switch BRANCH selects a different branch while preserving both heads. The Store must be strictly inside the canonical workspace directory. The CLI resolves revision selectors , including native tags, before preflight. C++ callers can resolve them explicitly with resolve_native_revision() ; the checkout API itself retains revision-ID inputs. Starting and publishing # The initial policy requires every tracked file to match its baseline by complete content hash and supported executable metadata. An existing staging index blocks checkout, even if it has no staged changes. Ignored and untracked collisions, links/junctions, protected paths and ambiguous portable aliases are refused. Clean tracked case changes and file/directory transitions use the namespace executor described below. There is no --force option. For a revision change, execution repeats preflight and verifies the complete source and target closures, including historical parents and file hashes, before publishing its journal. Thus a metadata-only plan is insufficient to execute checkout. A clean checkout whose target already equals the branch head returns state: \"unchanged\" without creating a transaction. Unrelated untracked and ignored files remain untouched. For each changed file the engine first reconstructs and verifies the complete target into a private slot. After rechecking the workspace, it moves the original to a backup slot and installs the prepared target with exclusive, no-overwrite moves. Deletions retain their original file in a backup. Representation-only changes need no file move; mode-only changes currently prepare a complete file. Executable bits are applied on Unix. Windows preserves their snapshot metadata without applying it to the filesystem or treating it as a local modification. All desired tracked files are verified again after the last public progress callback. One compare-and-swap reference transaction then moves the branch, marks checkout committed, archives the source and retains the target and receipt. Only after that publication does the engine remove verified private data and release the active journal/pending roots. Success returns state: \"committed\" , pending: false , the transaction id , and its archive reference or null for an unborn branch. Publication is atomic for the branch/journal references. Workspace file moves are sequential , so readers can see intermediate contents. Stop external writers while checking out; this is not an atomic filesystem snapshot, workspace lock or defense against hostile concurrent namespace replacement. An IO error or cancellation after journal creation requires explicit recovery. Inspecting, resuming and aborting # checkout-status returns pending: false when no operation is active. Otherwise it reports the immutable transaction identity, branch, source, target, canonical workspace, state, private data_directory , and affected paths with their numeric slot names. --id can inspect the active transaction or a completed receipt. JSON fields and state names remain English with either interface language. State Resume Abort preparing Finish reconstruction, recheck the clean baseline, then apply Discard prepared outputs without touching working files applying Validate backups/installed files and finish application Restore originals in reverse action order evacuating Finish backing up changed sources and removing required empty directories Uninstall targets, then restore sources installing Validate/install all targets, then publish Uninstall all targets, then restore sources uninstalling Continue the requested target removal Continue target removal restoring Continue the requested source restoration Continue source restoration aborting Continue the requested rollback Continue rollback discarding Finish discarding preparation Finish discarding preparation aborted Finish cleanup without replaying file moves Finish cleanup committed Finish cleanup without changing the published branch Refused; branch publication already succeeded Recovery always uses the recorded workspace/branch; it accepts no override. Opening a Store does not automatically change its working files. The journal is bound to the canonical workspace location, so copying or relocating it is not a supported recovery procedure. A completed recovery is idempotent. If a working file or private output has changed, recovery preserves those bytes and refuses the unsafe step. Inspect checkout-status and preserve such edits before retrying. A source edited immediately before its backup move is restored verbatim during abort. Rollback persists its aborted marker before removing prepared outputs, so interrupted cleanup cannot mistake a later working file for an installed target. Newly created empty parent directories may remain after abort. The executor does not recursively remove working directories. While checkout is pending, GC (including preview) and native commit, add, reset, staged commit and history detachment are blocked. Packing and read-only inspection remain available. Generic reference/admin operations are not a checkout override: changing a branch or internal retention root can make recovery refuse to proceed. Do not edit or replicate the active checkout roots. Guarded recovery by a resident agent # The bidirectional resident agent marks its incoming checkouts and merge checkouts with an explicit owner ID. Journal v11 freezes that owner, context and both materialization definitions, with layout 1 or 3 for ordinary or namespace application. The owner participates in the immutable transaction ID. resident-sync-v1 is established before durable observations or owned journals; older readers reject the Store instead of ignoring the new recovery state. Unowned checkout encodings remain unchanged. cpp Copy store . checkout_directory_owned ( project , \"main\" , target , owner_id , operation ) ; store . recover_checkout_owned ( project , \"main\" , owner_id , operation ) ; The recovery method checks the owner, selected context, current physical workspace and HEAD target before applying anything. The ID is a coordination tag, not an authentication credential against a trusted local Store writer. An agent also requires its retained remote observation to be an ancestor of the prepared target. Another endpoint, workspace or publication-only session leaves it blocked. Active owned transactions can finish offline without a signing private key: the exact incoming or signed merge target is already frozen and retained. Recovery does not compose or re-sign a revision. Starting a new signed operation still requires the signing identity. Manual or unrelated transactions require the ordinary explicit checkout-recover ; opening a Store alone performs no recovery. Completed owned receipts remain inspectable and retained under the same rules as other receipts. Backups preserve reader requirements and complete revision data, without activating old physical checkout state in a restored directory. Journal, retention and private data # The immutable journal is an ordinary chunk object, so existing compression, encryption and pack support apply without changing logical/physical formats. Its identity hashes the version, nonce, canonical directory, branch and source/ target revisions. Switch journals add destination and expected/new HEAD fields in version 2. Namespace checkout/switch journals use versions 3/4 and an explicit namespace_transition: true flag; versions 1/2 remain supported. Each phase publishes a new journal object. Hard reset uses version 5, which distinguishes the captured working snapshot from the original branch head/index and records the source directory profile. Versions 1–4 keep their existing semantics. Reference Retained value checkouts/active Current journal object; at most one checkout per Store checkouts/pending/ID/target Complete target closure until cleanup checkouts/pending/ID/source Complete original closure when a source exists archive/checkout/ID Previous head after successful publication checkouts/completed/ID Published target after successful publication checkouts/receipts/ID Committed journal for later inspection The active/pending roots are released only after cleanup. Archives, completed targets and receipts remain retained; automatic pruning is not implemented. Deleting a branch therefore does not alone discard checkout history. Removing all roots and running destructive GC intentionally makes those versions unrecoverable. An aborted transaction leaves no permanent receipt. Private files live at STORE/checkouts/ID/{new,old}/N.bin . They contain decoded plaintext even in an encrypted Store , just as the workspace does. Existing object encryption does not encrypt these temporary targets or original-file backups. Unix private directories use owner-only permissions; Windows relies on the Store's inherited ACLs. Filesystem encryption is needed to protect these working bytes at rest. Prepared targets require extra disk space; backups are moved original files. Source, private slots and destinations must support exclusive moves on the same filesystem. Cross-filesystem moves fail with the journal retained for recovery. Cleanup validates known slot names, types and expected hashes before removing files, and removes only empty directories. Unexpected or changed private data is retained with an error. Windows may briefly defer deletion of a file; cleanup retries only nonrecursive empty-directory removal for up to about 500 ms. Other IO failures leave the journal available for another recovery attempt. C++, progress and limits # cpp Copy auto result = store . checkout_directory ( project , \"main\" , target_id , operation ) ; auto pending = store . checkout_status ( ) ; auto resumed = store . recover_checkout ( false , operation ) ; auto aborted = store . recover_checkout ( true , operation ) ; auto receipt = store . checkout_status ( transaction_id ) ; Callbacks are synchronous and must not reenter the Store. Preflight/comparison use their existing scanning, planning and verification events. Preparation adds per-file reconstruction; application/rollback report exporting with logical changed-file byte totals, interspersed with per-file verification. Counters can restart between these phases and on recovery. The last observer before branch publication is publishing with path checkout . Cancellation or observer failure before that commit point keeps the original branch, but may leave a partial workspace and pending journal. After publication, cleanup finishes without further observers or cancellation. An error after publication can therefore report failure with a committed branch; inspect status and resume cleanup. The shared 1 MiB content buffer, snapshot tables, manifest-summary caches, 16 MiB object limits and bounded workspace inventory still apply. This version performs complete preflight/final hashes and closure verification, reconstructs whole changed files, and hashes backups before cleanup. It does not yet use in-place block patching or parallel materialization; the advisory work index does not replace these checks. The C ABI and Go binding do not expose native checkout yet. Recoverable namespace transitions # Clean tracked files can change case, including parent directories, or cross file/directory boundaries. For example, Source/Code.cpp can become source/code.cpp , a tracked Content file can become Content/Map.uasset , and a fully tracked Content/... tree can become a Content file. Ordinary renamed paths are already represented as removals/additions; rename detection and history following remain separate work. Planning keeps the full clean-tracked policy, checks exact portable directory spellings, and reports namespace_transition: true and remove_directories when the operation requires this executor. Each affected directory must contain only the source snapshot's files and their known parent directories. Untracked or ignored descendants, unknown empty directories, links and protected storage block the entire operation before journal publication. Snapshots with inconsistent case spellings for the same portable parent are rejected. The executor prepares every target, then backs up all changed source files before installing any target. It removes only the snapshot-derived directories needed for the transition, deepest first, and only while empty. There is no recursive removal. It persists installing before creating target parents and moving the prepared files. Final hashes, exact namespace checks and atomic branch/archive/journal publication retain the existing guarantees. Abort first moves installed targets back into their prepared slots, removes only required empty target directories, persists restoring , then restores the original sources and their spelling. Distinct durable phases prevent resumed rollback from confusing restored originals with still-installed targets. Changed working files and newly appeared obstructions are preserved and block unsafe recovery. Opening the Store still performs no recovery automatically. Switch, merge, replay and linear rebase use the shared checkout executor; branch switch preserves both tips and publishes its HEAD descriptor with the journal. No new object, pointer, revision or network format is introduced. Older engines cannot recover namespace journals and must reject their unsupported versions. Version 6 supports stash application : the branch head stays fixed while the working target, optional frozen index and stash pop are published together. All previous journal versions remain readable. Completed receipt retirement removes selected receipt/target groups only after independent backup coverage. Historical receipt inspection does not require an obsolete HEAD selection or deliberately retired source snapshot; active recovery keeps the full binding/snapshot checks. syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID store.checkout_directory_owned(project, \"main\", target, owner_id, operation); store.recover_checkout_owned(project, \"main\", owner_id, operation); auto result = store.checkout_directory(project, \"main\", target_id, operation); auto pending = store.checkout_status(); auto resumed = store.recover_checkout(false, operation); auto aborted = store.recover_checkout(true, operation); auto receipt = store.checkout_status(transaction_id);","headings":[{"id":"recoverable-native-checkout","text":"Recoverable native checkout","level":1},{"id":"starting-and-publishing","text":"Starting and publishing","level":2},{"id":"inspecting-resuming-and-aborting","text":"Inspecting, resuming and aborting","level":2},{"id":"owned-recovery","text":"Guarded recovery by a resident agent","level":2},{"id":"journal-retention-and-private-data","text":"Journal, retention and private data","level":2},{"id":"c-progress-and-limits","text":"C++, progress and limits","level":2},{"id":"recoverable-namespace-transitions","text":"Recoverable namespace transitions","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify\nsyrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\nstore.checkout_directory_owned(project, \"main\", target, owner_id, operation);\nstore.recover_checkout_owned(project, \"main\", owner_id, operation);\nauto result = store.checkout_directory(project, \"main\", target_id, operation);\nauto pending = store.checkout_status();\nauto resumed = store.recover_checkout(false, operation);\nauto aborted = store.recover_checkout(true, operation);\nauto receipt = store.checkout_status(transaction_id);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-workspace","#/en/native-branches","#/en/native-tags","#/en/resident-agent","#/en/native-hard-reset","#/en/native-stash","#/en/checkout-retention"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-comparison","title":"Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison","description":"Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison guide.","section":"Version control","order":240,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison\">Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-comparison/native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison\" aria-label=\"Link to Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The native engine can inspect a working directory and compare two revision\nsnapshots without importing files or publishing references. These operations\nreturn canonical English JSON. Progress and diagnostics support English and\nFrench, using the ordinary \u003ccode>--lang\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> options.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native status --combined --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"working-directory-status\">Working-directory status\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-comparison/working-directory-status\" aria-label=\"Link to Working-directory status\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The optional \u003ca href=\"#/en/working-index\">\u003ccode>--indexed\u003c/code> working hash cache\u003c/a> has a separate\nreuse contract and can write local cache data. The full-read/read-only contract\nbelow describes ordinary status without that flag.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The default baseline is the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches\">current native branch\u003c/a>,\nimplicitly \u003ccode>heads/main\u003c/code> for legacy Stores. \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code> selects another native\nbranch; a missing branch is an empty baseline, with every selected file reported\nas untracked. \u003ccode>--revision ID\u003c/code> selects an exact revision and cannot be combined\nwith \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>. A missing or invalid explicit revision is an error.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--combined\u003c/code> selects the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">combined workspace report\u003c/a>:\nrecorded base versus index, then index versus working files. It is a separate\nreport schema and cannot be combined with \u003ccode>--revision\u003c/code>. Ordinary status keeps\nthe branch-versus-workspace contract below.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>status\u003c/code> reads every selected regular file in full and computes its BLAKE3 file\nhash. A matching size or modification time never substitutes for reading the\ncontents. This detects edits that preserve both size and timestamp. The report\ncontains \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tracked_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>workspace_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>hashed_bytes\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>counts\u003c/code> and sorted \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A clean result compares working bytes with manifest hashes; it does not audit\nthe stored chunk payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>untracked\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A selected working file has no baseline entry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>removed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A baseline file is absent from the selected working snapshot\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>modified\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A common path has different content or executable metadata\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>unchanged\u003c/code> count\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Content and executable metadata match the baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Each change has \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>content_changed\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>executable_changed\u003c/code>. A missing side is null. File summaries contain \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>size\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>executable\u003c/code>; a working file has a null manifest because\nstatus does not ingest it. \u003ccode>content_changed\u003c/code> is true for additions/removals;\n\u003ccode>executable_changed\u003c/code> compares modes only when both sides exist. \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code> requires\nno changes, including untracked files. A successful dirty status still exits 0.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The scanner is shared with \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code>: nested \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code> rules,\nnegations, \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code> exclusions, the Store directory, portable path checks\nand case-folding collision rules apply to both. The Store must reside inside\nthe inspected directory. An ignore file must be a regular file. Selected\nsymlinks and special files are rejected; excluded paths are not followed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ignore rules apply to the complete candidate snapshot, including tracked files.\nAdding a tracked file to \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code> therefore reports it as \u003ccode>removed\u003c/code>, even\nif it still exists on disk. The next ordinary native commit also excludes it.\nThis follows the current native commit contract. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-staging\">Native staging\u003c/a>\ncan freeze selected entries independently of subsequent workspace changes.\nExecutable bits are inspected on Unix. Native Windows scans record false, as\nnative commits already do, and working-directory comparison ignores executable\ndifferences against stored Unix metadata. Snapshot-to-snapshot comparison still\nreports those differences on every platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revision-snapshot-comparison\">Revision snapshot comparison\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-comparison/revision-snapshot-comparison\" aria-label=\"Link to Revision snapshot comparison\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native diff\u003c/code> compares the two snapshots&#39; file entries, using only verified\nrevision and manifest metadata. It requires all file manifests, including\nunchanged files, but reads no chunk payloads and does not traverse revision\nparents. It can inspect metadata-only partial Stores. It does not prove that\nthe referenced chunks or historical parents are available; use \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> for a\ncomplete integrity audit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The CLI accepts \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-tags\">native revision selectors\u003c/a> for diff operands\nand \u003ccode>status --revision\u003c/code>: exact IDs, HEAD and unambiguous branch/tag names.\nExplicit \u003ccode>heads/NAME\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code> select a namespace. C++ comparison methods\ncontinue to receive IDs; resolution is available separately.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The report contains \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>equal\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target_files\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>counts\u003c/code> and sorted \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>. Status names are \u003ccode>added\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>removed\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>modified\u003c/code>,\nwith an \u003ccode>unchanged\u003c/code> count. Change summaries have the same fields as working\nstatus, plus \u003ccode>manifest_changed\u003c/code>. A different manifest can describe identical\nfile bytes with different chunking: that entry is modified with\n\u003ccode>manifest_changed: true\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>content_changed: false\u003c/code>. Executable-only changes\nare also distinguished. Revision messages, timestamps and parents do not affect\nfile-entry equality. Renames are reported as removal plus addition.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This command compares snapshot metadata. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">Recoverable native checkout\u003c/a>\nrestores an exact revision separately. \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">Text differences\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-file-history\">exact rename history\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge\">merge/resolutions\u003c/a> have separate explicit commands; ordinary snapshot comparison reports removals/additions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-and-operation-boundaries\">C++ and operation boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-comparison/c-and-operation-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ and operation boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">status_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> baseline_revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> empty_baseline \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">status_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> difference \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">compare_revisions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>baseline_revision\u003c/code> is an optional string; omitting it selects an empty baseline\nin the library. Branch resolution belongs to the CLI. The C ABI and Go binding\ndo not yet expose these native revision operations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Callbacks are synchronous on the calling thread and must not reenter the Store.\n\u003ccode>scanning\u003c/code> identifies visited directories, \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> reports per-file bytes,\nand \u003ccode>planning\u003c/code> counts metadata/comparison entries. Counters reset between stages.\nCancellation or observer failure aborts without returning a partial report or\nchanging objects, references or working files. CLI JSON is written only after\nsuccess. The usual exclusive Store lock and normal opening/recovery rules apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The content buffer is 1 MiB. A per-operation FIFO caches at most 1,024 manifest\nsummaries. Snapshot tables and the complete report remain in memory, and each\nmanifest/revision retains its 16 MiB logical limit. The shared scanner rejects\na provably oversized flat snapshot before its file-entry lower bound exceeds\n16 MiB. This is not a global process-memory limit or a persistent work index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Status detects size, timestamp, type and Unix-mode changes observed around an\nindividual read, including growth during hashing. It does not lock the working\ndirectory or provide an atomic filesystem snapshot. Concurrent changes can\nrequire retrying; changes after a file was read can make a report stale. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/working-index\">working index\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-watch\">watcher\u003c/a> provide advisory acceleration; destructive operations independently validate current data.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison # The native engine can inspect a working directory and compare two revision snapshots without importing files or publishing references. These operations return canonical English JSON. Progress and diagnostics support English and French, using the ordinary --lang , --progress and --progress-json options. text Copy syrnck native status syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json syrnck native status --combined --branch main syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION Working-directory status # The optional --indexed working hash cache has a separate reuse contract and can write local cache data. The full-read/read-only contract below describes ordinary status without that flag. The default baseline is the current native branch , implicitly heads/main for legacy Stores. --branch NAME selects another native branch; a missing branch is an empty baseline, with every selected file reported as untracked. --revision ID selects an exact revision and cannot be combined with --branch . A missing or invalid explicit revision is an error. --combined selects the combined workspace report : recorded base versus index, then index versus working files. It is a separate report schema and cannot be combined with --revision . Ordinary status keeps the branch-versus-workspace contract below. status reads every selected regular file in full and computes its BLAKE3 file hash. A matching size or modification time never substitutes for reading the contents. This detects edits that preserve both size and timestamp. The report contains base , clean , tracked_files , workspace_files , hashed_bytes , counts and sorted changes . A clean result compares working bytes with manifest hashes; it does not audit the stored chunk payloads. Status Meaning untracked A selected working file has no baseline entry removed A baseline file is absent from the selected working snapshot modified A common path has different content or executable metadata unchanged count Content and executable metadata match the baseline Each change has path , status , source , target , content_changed and executable_changed . A missing side is null. File summaries contain manifest , size , file_hash and executable ; a working file has a null manifest because status does not ingest it. content_changed is true for additions/removals; executable_changed compares modes only when both sides exist. clean requires no changes, including untracked files. A successful dirty status still exits 0. The scanner is shared with native commit : nested .syrnckignore rules, negations, .git / .syrnck exclusions, the Store directory, portable path checks and case-folding collision rules apply to both. The Store must reside inside the inspected directory. An ignore file must be a regular file. Selected symlinks and special files are rejected; excluded paths are not followed. Ignore rules apply to the complete candidate snapshot, including tracked files. Adding a tracked file to .syrnckignore therefore reports it as removed , even if it still exists on disk. The next ordinary native commit also excludes it. This follows the current native commit contract. Native staging can freeze selected entries independently of subsequent workspace changes. Executable bits are inspected on Unix. Native Windows scans record false, as native commits already do, and working-directory comparison ignores executable differences against stored Unix metadata. Snapshot-to-snapshot comparison still reports those differences on every platform. Revision snapshot comparison # native diff compares the two snapshots' file entries, using only verified revision and manifest metadata. It requires all file manifests, including unchanged files, but reads no chunk payloads and does not traverse revision parents. It can inspect metadata-only partial Stores. It does not prove that the referenced chunks or historical parents are available; use fsck for a complete integrity audit. The CLI accepts native revision selectors for diff operands and status --revision : exact IDs, HEAD and unambiguous branch/tag names. Explicit heads/NAME and tags/NAME select a namespace. C++ comparison methods continue to receive IDs; resolution is available separately. The report contains source , target , equal , source_files , target_files , counts and sorted changes . Status names are added , removed and modified , with an unchanged count. Change summaries have the same fields as working status, plus manifest_changed . A different manifest can describe identical file bytes with different chunking: that entry is modified with manifest_changed: true and content_changed: false . Executable-only changes are also distinguished. Revision messages, timestamps and parents do not affect file-entry equality. Renames are reported as removal plus addition. This command compares snapshot metadata. Recoverable native checkout restores an exact revision separately. Text differences , exact rename history and merge/resolutions have separate explicit commands; ordinary snapshot comparison reports removals/additions. C++ and operation boundaries # cpp Copy auto status = store . status_directory ( project , baseline_revision , operation ) ; auto empty_baseline = store . status_directory ( project ) ; auto difference = store . compare_revisions ( source_id , target_id , operation ) ; baseline_revision is an optional string; omitting it selects an empty baseline in the library. Branch resolution belongs to the CLI. The C ABI and Go binding do not yet expose these native revision operations. Callbacks are synchronous on the calling thread and must not reenter the Store. scanning identifies visited directories, verifying reports per-file bytes, and planning counts metadata/comparison entries. Counters reset between stages. Cancellation or observer failure aborts without returning a partial report or changing objects, references or working files. CLI JSON is written only after success. The usual exclusive Store lock and normal opening/recovery rules apply. The content buffer is 1 MiB. A per-operation FIFO caches at most 1,024 manifest summaries. Snapshot tables and the complete report remain in memory, and each manifest/revision retains its 16 MiB logical limit. The shared scanner rejects a provably oversized flat snapshot before its file-entry lower bound exceeds 16 MiB. This is not a global process-memory limit or a persistent work index. Status detects size, timestamp, type and Unix-mode changes observed around an individual read, including growth during hashing. It does not lock the working directory or provide an atomic filesystem snapshot. Concurrent changes can require retrying; changes after a file was read can make a report stale. The working index and watcher provide advisory acceleration; destructive operations independently validate current data. syrnck native status syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json syrnck native status --combined --branch main syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION auto status = store.status_directory(project, baseline_revision, operation); auto empty_baseline = store.status_directory(project); auto difference = store.compare_revisions(source_id, target_id, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison","text":"Native working-directory status and snapshot comparison","level":1},{"id":"working-directory-status","text":"Working-directory status","level":2},{"id":"revision-snapshot-comparison","text":"Revision snapshot comparison","level":2},{"id":"c-and-operation-boundaries","text":"C++ and operation boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status\nsyrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT\nsyrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json\nsyrnck native status --combined --branch main\nsyrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION\nauto status = store.status_directory(project, baseline_revision, operation);\nauto empty_baseline = store.status_directory(project);\nauto difference = store.compare_revisions(source_id, target_id, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/working-index","#/en/native-branches","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/native-staging","#/en/native-tags","#/en/native-checkout","#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-file-history","#/en/native-merge","#/en/working-index","#/en/native-watch"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-detached","title":"Detached native HEAD","description":"Explore revisions and commit independently of named branches.","section":"Version control","order":221,"parent":"native-branches","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Select a detached revision\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-detached/usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Select a detached revision\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native switch --detach tags/release\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native branch rescue HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native switch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>switch --detach REVISION\u003c/code> restores a committed snapshot without selecting or\nmoving a named branch. It accepts the same revision selectors as checkout.\n\u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> reports \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode: &quot;detached&quot;\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>reference: &quot;workspace/detached&quot;\u003c/code> and the committed revision. No fake branch\nappears in \u003ccode>native branches\u003c/code>. This differs from \u003ccode>detach-history\u003c/code>, which creates\na new ancestry root for the selected target.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Default native commits, staging, status, history, rewrite operations, stash and\nworkspace observation use the detached revision and its independent index.\nCommits parent the previous detached revision. An explicit \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>\ncontinues to operate on that named branch without selecting it. Native remote\npublication requires a named branch; create a rescue branch before publishing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout REVISION\u003c/code> moves the currently selected target: a branch when\nattached, the private revision when detached. \u003ccode>native switch NAME\u003c/code> returns to\na named branch and restores its files; neither branch tip moves during switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Preflight and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-detached/recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Preflight and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Plans are read-only. Dirty tracked files, an index on either target, untracked\ncollisions and unsafe namespaces block detachment or reattachment under the\nordinary checkout rules. There is no implicit overwrite or index reset.\nFile/directory transitions use the same recoverable namespace executor.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before detaching an attached workspace, the destination is verified and retained\nin a private slot. Replacing an inactive detached line archives its previous\ntip under \u003ccode>archive/OLD_ID\u003c/code>. An interruption during this preparation leaves the\noriginal HEAD and working files intact, with an additional retained candidate.\nOnce the checkout journal exists, use \u003ccode>native checkout-status\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>--abort\u003c/code>). HEAD changes with the committed journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Retention and formats\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-detached/retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention and formats\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The private revision root \u003ccode>workspace/detached\u003c/code> and optional index root\n\u003ccode>workspace/staging\u003c/code> retain complete object closures through GC. Switching away\nkeeps the detached tip. Archives, rescue branches and tags retain older lines;\nportable backups retain these private data roots while omitting machine-bound\nHEAD. Usual explicit retirement rules still apply to archives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Named HEAD descriptors keep canonical version 1. Detached descriptors use\nversion 2 with \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code> and the same directory/type fields. Checkout\nrecords involving a detached target use version 7 with \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> naming\nthe corresponding layout 1 through 6. Named-only records keep their existing\nformats. Detached stash records use version 2; named stash records stay version 1.\nOlder executors must refuse the new records rather than misread their targets.\nObject/revision formats, physical pack formats and C ABI versions are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">Embedding\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-detached/api\" aria-label=\"Link to Embedding\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::native_target()\u003c/code> returns a named branch or the opaque selector \u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code>.\nPass it to native APIs that take an operational branch/target argument. Snapshot\nAPIs still take revision IDs; use \u003ccode>resolve_native_revision(&quot;HEAD&quot;)\u003c/code> first.\n\u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code> cannot be a real branch name. Legacy plan/status fields named \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>\ncontain this target selector when detached, while \u003ccode>native_head().branch\u003c/code> is null.\nThe C/Go workspace APIs follow detached HEAD when their selector is omitted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_detached_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ready\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">bool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">detach_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> target \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> changes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each Store handle selects one context. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">Linked workspaces\u003c/a>\nshare objects and branches while retaining independent HEADs and indexes. Sparse\nmaterialization, in-place relocation and archived unregistering remain separate work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Select a detached revision # text Copy syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify syrnck native switch --detach tags/release syrnck native head syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\" syrnck native branch rescue HEAD syrnck native switch main switch --detach REVISION restores a committed snapshot without selecting or moving a named branch. It accepts the same revision selectors as checkout. native head reports branch: null , mode: \"detached\" , reference: \"workspace/detached\" and the committed revision. No fake branch appears in native branches . This differs from detach-history , which creates a new ancestry root for the selected target. Default native commits, staging, status, history, rewrite operations, stash and workspace observation use the detached revision and its independent index. Commits parent the previous detached revision. An explicit --branch NAME continues to operate on that named branch without selecting it. Native remote publication requires a named branch; create a rescue branch before publishing. native checkout REVISION moves the currently selected target: a branch when attached, the private revision when detached. native switch NAME returns to a named branch and restores its files; neither branch tip moves during switch. Preflight and recovery # Plans are read-only. Dirty tracked files, an index on either target, untracked collisions and unsafe namespaces block detachment or reattachment under the ordinary checkout rules. There is no implicit overwrite or index reset. File/directory transitions use the same recoverable namespace executor. Before detaching an attached workspace, the destination is verified and retained in a private slot. Replacing an inactive detached line archives its previous tip under archive/OLD_ID . An interruption during this preparation leaves the original HEAD and working files intact, with an additional retained candidate. Once the checkout journal exists, use native checkout-status and native checkout-recover (or --abort ). HEAD changes with the committed journal. Retention and formats # The private revision root workspace/detached and optional index root workspace/staging retain complete object closures through GC. Switching away keeps the detached tip. Archives, rescue branches and tags retain older lines; portable backups retain these private data roots while omitting machine-bound HEAD. Usual explicit retirement rules still apply to archives. Named HEAD descriptors keep canonical version 1. Detached descriptors use version 2 with branch: null and the same directory/type fields. Checkout records involving a detached target use version 7 with layout_version naming the corresponding layout 1 through 6. Named-only records keep their existing formats. Detached stash records use version 2; named stash records stay version 1. Older executors must refuse the new records rather than misread their targets. Object/revision formats, physical pack formats and C ABI versions are unchanged. Embedding # Store::native_target() returns a named branch or the opaque selector :HEAD . Pass it to native APIs that take an operational branch/target argument. Snapshot APIs still take revision IDs; use resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\") first. :HEAD cannot be a real branch name. Legacy plan/status fields named branch contain this target selector when detached, while native_head().branch is null. The C/Go workspace APIs follow detached HEAD when their selector is omitted. cpp Copy auto plan = store . plan_detached_checkout ( workspace , revision , true , operation ) ; if ( plan [ \"ready\" ] . get \u003c bool > ( ) ) store . detach_checkout ( workspace , revision , operation ) ; auto target = store . native_target ( ) ; auto changes = store . workspace_status ( workspace , target , operation ) ; Each Store handle selects one context. Linked workspaces share objects and branches while retaining independent HEADs and indexes. Sparse materialization, in-place relocation and archived unregistering remain separate work. syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify syrnck native switch --detach tags/release syrnck native head syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\" syrnck native branch rescue HEAD syrnck native switch main auto plan = store.plan_detached_checkout(workspace, revision, true, operation); if (plan[\"ready\"].get\u003cbool>()) store.detach_checkout(workspace, revision, operation); auto target = store.native_target(); auto changes = store.workspace_status(workspace, target, operation);","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Select a detached revision","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Preflight and recovery","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Retention and formats","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"Embedding","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify\nsyrnck native switch --detach tags/release\nsyrnck native head\nsyrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\"\nsyrnck native branch rescue HEAD\nsyrnck native switch main\nauto plan = store.plan_detached_checkout(workspace, revision, true, operation);\nif (plan[\"ready\"].get\u003cbool>())\n  store.detach_checkout(workspace, revision, operation);\nauto target = store.native_target();\nauto changes = store.workspace_status(workspace, target, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-workspaces"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-file-history","title":"Exact renames and first-parent file history","description":"Exact renames and first-parent file history guide.","section":"Version control","order":250,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history\">Exact renames and first-parent file history\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-file-history/exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history\" aria-label=\"Link to Exact renames and first-parent file history\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The C++ APIs in \u003ccode>syrnck/history.hpp\u003c/code> add metadata-only exact rename comparison\nand file-history inspection. Neither API reads working files, changes references\nnor reconstructs asset payloads. Their content comparisons use verified\nmanifest metadata; they do not replace a complete file-integrity audit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"exact-rename-comparison\">Exact rename comparison\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-file-history/exact-rename-comparison\" aria-label=\"Link to Exact rename comparison\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native diff --renames\u003c/code> first compares snapshots, then pairs a removed path\nwith an added path when their complete-file BLAKE3 hash and size match exactly\nand the pairing is unique in both directions. It works across directories,\ncase/Unicode spelling changes and different chunking/compression representations.\nExecutable-mode changes are retained separately in the renamed row.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Renamed rows have \u003ccode>status: renamed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>path\u003c/code> (destination), \u003ccode>old_path\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>similarity: 100\u003c/code>, source/target metadata and distinct content/mode/manifest\nchange flags. Counts add \u003ccode>renamed\u003c/code> while subtracting matched additions/removals.\nThe ordinary diff command keeps its existing addition/removal behavior.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If identical removed/added contents have several possible pairings, the result\nkeeps the additions/removals, sets \u003ccode>rename_ambiguous\u003c/code> on the affected rows and\nreports ambiguity counts. Existing unchanged copies do not become renames.\nEdited renames, similarity heuristics, copy detection, swaps between existing\npaths and rename-aware merge/rebase conflict resolution are not inferred.\nReport serialization is bounded at 16 MiB.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"follow-a-file-lineage\">Follow a file lineage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-file-history/follow-a-file-lineage\" aria-label=\"Link to Follow a file lineage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native log-file REVISION PATH\u003c/code> walks the first-parent history and reports\nchanges affecting that file. It follows unique exact renames backwards, skips\nunrelated/empty commits and includes content, mode, representation and deletion\nevents. A path deleted or renamed away at the starting revision can still show\nits prior history. Each entry carries the revision, path at that revision,\nbounded message preview, timestamp, parents and the snapshot change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The first addition ends the lineage. An ambiguous rename origin also ends it,\nwith \u003ccode>stop_reason: ambiguous_rename\u003c/code>, rather than selecting an arbitrary source.\n\u003ccode>--no-follow\u003c/code> ends at additions under the selected path, including renamed-in\nfiles. Merge commits are compared with their first parent; other parents are\nreported as metadata but not traversed. This is not all-parent blame or a\ngeneral history-search engine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pages default to 100 changes (1..1000). The traversal independently defaults to\n1000 inspected revisions (1..10000), so an unchanged file does not require an\nunbounded walk. An incomplete page returns \u003ccode>next_revision\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>next_path\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Together those fields form an explicit continuation cursor, including a path\nchanged by a rename. \u003ccode>complete\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code> distinguish \u003ccode>added\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>ambiguous_rename\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>page_limit\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>traversal_limit\u003c/code>. Read-only queries\ndo not pin their versions; normal retained roots must still protect them from\nan intervening administrative retirement/GC.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Exact renames and first-parent file history # The C++ APIs in syrnck/history.hpp add metadata-only exact rename comparison and file-history inspection. Neither API reads working files, changes references nor reconstructs asset payloads. Their content comparisons use verified manifest metadata; they do not replace a complete file-integrity audit. text Copy syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000 syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow Exact rename comparison # native diff --renames first compares snapshots, then pairs a removed path with an added path when their complete-file BLAKE3 hash and size match exactly and the pairing is unique in both directions. It works across directories, case/Unicode spelling changes and different chunking/compression representations. Executable-mode changes are retained separately in the renamed row. Renamed rows have status: renamed , path (destination), old_path , similarity: 100 , source/target metadata and distinct content/mode/manifest change flags. Counts add renamed while subtracting matched additions/removals. The ordinary diff command keeps its existing addition/removal behavior. If identical removed/added contents have several possible pairings, the result keeps the additions/removals, sets rename_ambiguous on the affected rows and reports ambiguity counts. Existing unchanged copies do not become renames. Edited renames, similarity heuristics, copy detection, swaps between existing paths and rename-aware merge/rebase conflict resolution are not inferred. Report serialization is bounded at 16 MiB. Follow a file lineage # native log-file REVISION PATH walks the first-parent history and reports changes affecting that file. It follows unique exact renames backwards, skips unrelated/empty commits and includes content, mode, representation and deletion events. A path deleted or renamed away at the starting revision can still show its prior history. Each entry carries the revision, path at that revision, bounded message preview, timestamp, parents and the snapshot change. The first addition ends the lineage. An ambiguous rename origin also ends it, with stop_reason: ambiguous_rename , rather than selecting an arbitrary source. --no-follow ends at additions under the selected path, including renamed-in files. Merge commits are compared with their first parent; other parents are reported as metadata but not traversed. This is not all-parent blame or a general history-search engine. Pages default to 100 changes (1..1000). The traversal independently defaults to 1000 inspected revisions (1..10000), so an unchanged file does not require an unbounded walk. An incomplete page returns next_revision and next_path : text Copy syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25 Together those fields form an explicit continuation cursor, including a path changed by a rename. complete and stop_reason distinguish added , ambiguous_rename , root , page_limit and traversal_limit . Read-only queries do not pin their versions; normal retained roots must still protect them from an intervening administrative retirement/GC. syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000 syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25","headings":[{"id":"exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history","text":"Exact renames and first-parent file history","level":1},{"id":"exact-rename-comparison","text":"Exact rename comparison","level":2},{"id":"follow-a-file-lineage","text":"Follow a file lineage","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow\nsyrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-hard-reset","title":"Recoverable native hard reset","description":"Recoverable native hard reset guide.","section":"Version control","order":260,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"recoverable-native-hard-reset\">Recoverable native hard reset\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/recoverable-native-hard-reset\" aria-label=\"Link to Recoverable native hard reset\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Hard reset replaces the selected branch&#39;s tracked workspace and clears its\nfrozen staging index. Before replacing local data, it captures complete file\ncontents and retains the original head and index through a recovery snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset HEAD --hard\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> selects the workspace. The primary Store must be below its workspace;\nlinked workspaces use their registered directory and shared Store. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>\nmust identify the currently selected branch. Existing workspace\nbinding checks apply. A pending checkout/switch/reset must first be recovered\nor aborted. Soft/mixed reset and staging-only reset retain their existing APIs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-is-replaced-and-protected\">What is replaced and protected\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/what-is-replaced-and-protected\" aria-label=\"Link to What is replaced and protected\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The read-only plan inventories the namespace and hashes the actual selected\nworking files, independently of ignore rules, file size and modification time.\nIt includes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Present paths tracked by the current head or frozen staging snapshot.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Untracked regular files obstructing a target file/directory or spelling change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Observed directories needed to remove and reverse those namespace transitions,\nincluding empty directories.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Missing tracked files are represented by their absence, then restored when the\ntarget contains them. Modified tracked files, staged additions/deletions and\nstale frozen indexes can be reset. Case changes and file/directory transitions\nuse the existing evacuation/install journal. Untracked files outside those\nobstructions remain in place. The plan lists \u003ccode>obstructing_untracked\u003c/code> paths\nexplicitly; those files leave the working tree but remain reconstructible from\nthe recovery snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Links/reparse points, special files, ambiguous portable names and protected\n\u003ccode>.git\u003c/code>/Store entries are not adopted. Such obstructions block reset before\ncandidate creation. Directory removal is nonrecursive and requires emptiness\nafter the captured regular files have been evacuated.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> binds the canonical workspace and Store locations, selected HEAD,\noriginal head/index, target, exact working path spellings/content hashes/modes\nand source directory profile. Restoring a file&#39;s old size and timestamp does\nnot bypass this fence. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally checks the complete retained\nhead/index/target closures and reconstructed file hashes; it does not change\nthe plan ID. A C++ application must supply the plan ID. The CLI can capture a\nfresh plan immediately before application when \u003ccode>--expect-plan\u003c/code> is omitted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"application-and-recovery\">Application and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/application-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Application and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Application verifies all old retained versions and the target before importing\nnew working contents. Each imported file must still match the planned complete\nhash, size and mode. A canonical recovery revision stores the captured files\nand has the original head and optional index as parents. This preserves both\nthe dirty working bytes and independently staged bytes through later GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The version 5 checkout journal records separate working-source and branch-head\nidentities, the original staging snapshot, HEAD selection and the observed\ndirectory profile. Initial publication pins the complete source and target\nbefore working files move. Final publication updates the branch, removes the\nindex and creates the recovery archive in one reference transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Recovery resumes installation or cleanup. Abort before final reference\npublication restores pre-reset working bytes, missing-file state, directory\nspellings and captured empty directories; the head and index remain unchanged.\nAfter publication, recovery finishes cleanup. Store opening performs no implicit\nworkspace recovery. Concurrently changed or newly obstructed paths cause a\nrefusal that preserves the observed data for explicit recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Successful JSON includes \u003ccode>operation: reset\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode: hard\u003c/code>, the previous revision\nand index, \u003ccode>recovery_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> and the normal checkout receipt.\nThe archive points to the recovery revision. Its parents keep the entire old\nhistory and frozen index reconstructible independently of branch retention.\nFor example:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Revision export restores files; empty-directory metadata is part of the journal\nand is used for abort, rather than tracked in flat revision objects. Completed\narchives remain retained until explicitly retired with verified backup coverage.\nA clean same-head reset with no index returns \u003ccode>state: unchanged\u003c/code> without writes.\nPreparation failures can leave valid unreferenced CAS objects; a published\njournal must be recovered before GC. Checkout prepared files/backups remain\nplaintext, as in \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">ordinary checkout\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial-workspaces\">Partial workspaces\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Link to Partial workspaces\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Hard reset supports static \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">partial workspaces\u003c/a>. It resets\nthe selected physical paths while advancing the complete logical branch and\nclearing its complete frozen index. Unselected logical entries do not become\nphysical deletions or extra recovery files. An explicit empty selection permits\na metadata-only reset and leaves unrelated local files in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The plan uses version 2 and binds both the canonical \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code> prefix\narray and its retained \u003ccode>materialization_object\u003c/code>. The v9 journal uses layout 5\nwith equal frozen source/target definitions. Reference publication checks the\npolicy root together with HEAD, branch and index; a changed selection invalidates\nthe operation. Full-view plans and journals keep their original formats.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Recovery stores the actual captured physical tree without applying the mask\nagain. For example, a regular file \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> can obstruct a selected\n\u003ccode>Content/A/leaf\u003c/code> while the complete head also contains excluded \u003ccode>Content/B\u003c/code>.\nReset archives the physical file, installs the selected leaf and leaves \u003ccode>B\u003c/code>\nunmaterialized. The recovery revision retains the complete old head/index as\nparents, so their hidden blocks survive GC and portable backup. Abort restores\nthe original regular file. Excluded local paths that obstruct a selected target\nare listed explicitly and archived; unrelated excluded files stay in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api-bounds-and-validation\">API, bounds and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/api-bounds-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to API, bounds and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The core-only C++ APIs are declared in \u003ccode>syrnck/workspace.hpp\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_hard_reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_hard_reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                         target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Flat revision metadata, reports, source profile and inventory each retain their\n16 MiB budgets. At most 100,000 source-directory entries are accepted and\ninventory traversal remains limited to 128 directory levels. File bytes use\nthe configured streaming ingestion pipeline; the operation still inventories\nand hashes its selected working files. This is separate from a watcher/index.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Recoverable native hard reset # Hard reset replaces the selected branch's tracked workspace and clears its frozen staging index. Before replacing local data, it captures complete file contents and retains the original head and index through a recovery snapshot. text Copy syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native reset HEAD --hard --directory selects the workspace. The primary Store must be below its workspace; linked workspaces use their registered directory and shared Store. --branch must identify the currently selected branch. Existing workspace binding checks apply. A pending checkout/switch/reset must first be recovered or aborted. Soft/mixed reset and staging-only reset retain their existing APIs. What is replaced and protected # The read-only plan inventories the namespace and hashes the actual selected working files, independently of ignore rules, file size and modification time. It includes: Present paths tracked by the current head or frozen staging snapshot. Untracked regular files obstructing a target file/directory or spelling change. Observed directories needed to remove and reverse those namespace transitions, including empty directories. Missing tracked files are represented by their absence, then restored when the target contains them. Modified tracked files, staged additions/deletions and stale frozen indexes can be reset. Case changes and file/directory transitions use the existing evacuation/install journal. Untracked files outside those obstructions remain in place. The plan lists obstructing_untracked paths explicitly; those files leave the working tree but remain reconstructible from the recovery snapshot. Links/reparse points, special files, ambiguous portable names and protected .git /Store entries are not adopted. Such obstructions block reset before candidate creation. Directory removal is nonrecursive and requires emptiness after the captured regular files have been evacuated. plan binds the canonical workspace and Store locations, selected HEAD, original head/index, target, exact working path spellings/content hashes/modes and source directory profile. Restoring a file's old size and timestamp does not bypass this fence. --verify additionally checks the complete retained head/index/target closures and reconstructed file hashes; it does not change the plan ID. A C++ application must supply the plan ID. The CLI can capture a fresh plan immediately before application when --expect-plan is omitted. Application and recovery # Application verifies all old retained versions and the target before importing new working contents. Each imported file must still match the planned complete hash, size and mode. A canonical recovery revision stores the captured files and has the original head and optional index as parents. This preserves both the dirty working bytes and independently staged bytes through later GC. The version 5 checkout journal records separate working-source and branch-head identities, the original staging snapshot, HEAD selection and the observed directory profile. Initial publication pins the complete source and target before working files move. Final publication updates the branch, removes the index and creates the recovery archive in one reference transaction. text Copy syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Recovery resumes installation or cleanup. Abort before final reference publication restores pre-reset working bytes, missing-file state, directory spellings and captured empty directories; the head and index remain unchanged. After publication, recovery finishes cleanup. Store opening performs no implicit workspace recovery. Concurrently changed or newly obstructed paths cause a refusal that preserves the observed data for explicit recovery. Successful JSON includes operation: reset , mode: hard , the previous revision and index, recovery_revision , archive , plan and the normal checkout receipt. The archive points to the recovery revision. Its parents keep the entire old history and frozen index reconstructible independently of branch retention. For example: text Copy syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify Revision export restores files; empty-directory metadata is part of the journal and is used for abort, rather than tracked in flat revision objects. Completed archives remain retained until explicitly retired with verified backup coverage. A clean same-head reset with no index returns state: unchanged without writes. Preparation failures can leave valid unreferenced CAS objects; a published journal must be recovered before GC. Checkout prepared files/backups remain plaintext, as in ordinary checkout . Partial workspaces # Hard reset supports static partial workspaces . It resets the selected physical paths while advancing the complete logical branch and clearing its complete frozen index. Unselected logical entries do not become physical deletions or extra recovery files. An explicit empty selection permits a metadata-only reset and leaves unrelated local files in place. The plan uses version 2 and binds both the canonical materialization prefix array and its retained materialization_object . The v9 journal uses layout 5 with equal frozen source/target definitions. Reference publication checks the policy root together with HEAD, branch and index; a changed selection invalidates the operation. Full-view plans and journals keep their original formats. Recovery stores the actual captured physical tree without applying the mask again. For example, a regular file Content can obstruct a selected Content/A/leaf while the complete head also contains excluded Content/B . Reset archives the physical file, installs the selected leaf and leaves B unmaterialized. The recovery revision retains the complete old head/index as parents, so their hidden blocks survive GC and portable backup. Abort restores the original regular file. Excluded local paths that obstruct a selected target are listed explicitly and archived; unrelated excluded files stay in place. API, bounds and validation # The core-only C++ APIs are declared in syrnck/workspace.hpp : cpp Copy auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_hard_reset ( store , project , \"main\" , target , true ) ; auto applied = syrnck :: native_hard_reset ( store , project , \"main\" , expected_head , target , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) ) ; Flat revision metadata, reports, source profile and inventory each retain their 16 MiB budgets. At most 100,000 source-directory entries are accepted and inventory traversal remains limited to 128 directory levels. File bytes use the configured streaming ingestion pipeline; the operation still inventories and hashes its selected working files. This is separate from a watcher/index. syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native reset HEAD --hard syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", target, true); auto applied = syrnck::native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", expected_head, target, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>());","headings":[{"id":"recoverable-native-hard-reset","text":"Recoverable native hard reset","level":1},{"id":"what-is-replaced-and-protected","text":"What is replaced and protected","level":2},{"id":"application-and-recovery","text":"Application and recovery","level":2},{"id":"partial-workspaces","text":"Partial workspaces","level":2},{"id":"api-bounds-and-validation","text":"API, bounds and validation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify\nsyrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native reset HEAD --hard\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nsyrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files\nsyrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files\nsyrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", target, true);\nauto applied = syrnck::native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", expected_head,\n                                         target, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>());","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-workspaces"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-history","title":"Native revision ancestry","description":"Native revision ancestry guide.","section":"Version control","order":300,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-revision-ancestry\">Native revision ancestry\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-history/native-revision-ancestry\" aria-label=\"Link to Native revision ancestry\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native ancestry\u003c/code> examines the complete revision graph reachable from two\nsnapshots. It reports both ancestry directions and all best common ancestors.\nIt does not choose an arbitrary base when a criss-cross merge has several.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The JSON result contains \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_is_ancestor\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>target_is_ancestor\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>related\u003c/code>, sorted \u003ccode>merge_bases\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>visited_revisions\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>parent_links\u003c/code>. A revision is its own ancestor. Unrelated histories return an\nempty base list and \u003ccode>related: false\u003c/code>, with a successful command exit status.\nThe CLI accepts the existing native revision selectors. The C++ API takes IDs:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_ancestry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>left_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> right_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeGraphLimits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Traversal is iterative, with linear graph work after revision metadata decoding.\nEach distinct revision is loaded once. All parent edges, including merge parents,\nparticipate; duplicate parent entries count toward the link budget. Defaults are\n100,000 revisions and 1,000,000 links. Explicit limits allow 1..1,000,000 revisions\nand 0..10,000,000 links. Exceeding a budget fails without a partial answer.\nThe base-list size is also bounded conservatively below a 16 MiB JSON report.\nMemory scales with visited revisions and parent links; revision metadata itself\nremains subject to the existing 16 MiB object limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only revision objects are read. File manifests and block payloads are not needed,\nso this query does not establish file integrity. Missing or corrupt revision\nmetadata anywhere in the reachable graph is an error, even if a likely answer\nwas found earlier. There is no shallow-history fallback or implicit network fetch.\nUse \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> or a verified snapshot operation for file integrity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Queries never change refs, stored files or working files. Planning progress,\ncooperative cancellation and English/French diagnostics follow the existing\noperation contract. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. C ABI and Go exposure\nof native history operations remain future work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native revision ancestry # native ancestry examines the complete revision graph reachable from two snapshots. It reports both ancestry directions and all best common ancestors. It does not choose an arbitrary base when a criss-cross merge has several. text Copy syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000 The JSON result contains source , target , source_is_ancestor , target_is_ancestor , related , sorted merge_bases , visited_revisions and parent_links . A revision is its own ancestor. Unrelated histories return an empty base list and related: false , with a successful command exit status. The CLI accepts the existing native revision selectors. The C++ API takes IDs: cpp Copy auto report = store . native_ancestry ( left_id , right_id , syrnck :: NativeGraphLimits { } , operation ) ; Traversal is iterative, with linear graph work after revision metadata decoding. Each distinct revision is loaded once. All parent edges, including merge parents, participate; duplicate parent entries count toward the link budget. Defaults are 100,000 revisions and 1,000,000 links. Explicit limits allow 1..1,000,000 revisions and 0..10,000,000 links. Exceeding a budget fails without a partial answer. The base-list size is also bounded conservatively below a 16 MiB JSON report. Memory scales with visited revisions and parent links; revision metadata itself remains subject to the existing 16 MiB object limit. Only revision objects are read. File manifests and block payloads are not needed, so this query does not establish file integrity. Missing or corrupt revision metadata anywhere in the reachable graph is an error, even if a likely answer was found earlier. There is no shallow-history fallback or implicit network fetch. Use fsck or a verified snapshot operation for file integrity. Queries never change refs, stored files or working files. Planning progress, cooperative cancellation and English/French diagnostics follow the existing operation contract. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. C ABI and Go exposure of native history operations remain future work. syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000 auto report = store.native_ancestry(left_id, right_id, syrnck::NativeGraphLimits{}, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-revision-ancestry","text":"Native revision ancestry","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json\nsyrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000\nauto report = store.native_ancestry(left_id, right_id, syrnck::NativeGraphLimits{}, operation);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-hunks","title":"Native partial text staging and reset","description":"Native partial text staging and reset guide.","section":"Version control","order":280,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-partial-text-staging-and-reset\">Native partial text staging and reset\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hunks/native-partial-text-staging-and-reset\" aria-label=\"Link to Native partial text staging and reset\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Select complete numbered text hunks from an existing file without changing the\nworking copy. This complements ordinary file/directory staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both commands accept \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--directory PATH\u003c/code>, progress/cancellation,\nand the same \u003ccode>--context\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code> options as \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text diff\u003c/a>. English is the default\ninterface; French is also available. JSON keys, plan IDs and hunk numbers are\nlanguage independent. Application returns JSON, rather than the quiet ID output\nof ordinary \u003ccode>native add\u003c/code>.\nThe output budget bounds text-diff generation; numbering and context metadata\nare additional. The complete preview/application report is capped at 16 MiB.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"review-and-bind-the-selection\">Review and bind the selection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hunks/review-and-bind-the-selection\" aria-label=\"Link to Review and bind the selection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The read-only plan compares the frozen index (or branch head without an index)\nto the observed working file. Its \u003ccode>diff.hunks\u003c/code> have one-based \u003ccode>number\u003c/code> fields.\nContext controls grouping; adjacent edits in one hunk are selected together.\nNumbers belong only to that exact plan. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-lines\">Individual changed rows\u003c/a>\ncan also be selected with separate line commands. Neither form edits external\npatch text or presents an interactive prompt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The report includes the working diff, \u003ccode>operation: stage-hunks\u003c/code>, branch/head/index/\nbase state, optional old-index \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>text_options\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>plan_id\u003c/code>. The ID is the existing chunk-domain BLAKE3 ID of the canonical preview\nJSON before adding \u003ccode>plan_id\u003c/code>; no plan object is stored. It binds the directory,\npath, branch state, source manifest, observed working hash, available mode,\nnumbered hunks, limits and readiness. Different interface languages do not change\nit. After applying hunks, the returned report retains the original preview ID,\nwhile result fields describe publication; its entire JSON is not that preview&#39;s\nhash input anymore.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application recomputes the preview and requires the exact expected ID. Changes\nto working content, branch/index state or options require a fresh preview. Pass\nthe same nondefault options on both commands. Selected numbers must be unique,\npositive, in range, and nonempty (at most 100,000). Input order is irrelevant to\nthe selected bytes. After successful application, preview again before choosing\nremaining changes: their numbers can change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ready previews exit 0. Blocked previews exit 1 with JSON; ordinary errors exit\nnonzero without a result. Blockers include pending checkout, stale index,\nnon-text/limited diff, no text changes, file-presence changes and conflicting\nold-index archives. Preview verification covers this file, not unrelated stored\npayloads or full history. The complete replacement and retained closures are\nverified during publication; missing unrelated data can still block application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scope-and-exact-bytes\">Scope and exact bytes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hunks/scope-and-exact-bytes\" aria-label=\"Link to Scope and exact bytes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Both source and working file must exist as regular files. Empty existing files,\nCRLF/LF, Unicode text and missing final newlines are supported. Binary files,\nnew/untracked files and complete file deletions require ordinary \u003ccode>native add\u003c/code>.\nAn unborn branch is supported when an existing frozen initial index already\ncontains the selected file. Stale indexes must be explicitly rebased or reset.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The candidate starts from the complete source index/head file map. It replaces\nonly the selected path&#39;s manifest, retaining all other frozen files and the\nsource executable flag. Mode changes and alternate chunking representations\nare not selected as text. Individual hunks are copied byte-exactly; unselected\nregions remain source bytes. Selecting all hunks reproduces the observed working\ncontent, within the configured file budget. A subset can be larger than either\ninput when it keeps unselected deletions and includes selected additions; it is\nrefused before ingestion if it exceeds that budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The targeted workspace reader enforces ignore rules, exact path spelling,\nnamespace/link/storage guards, bounded reads and observed-change detection.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff/compare-a-working-file\">working-file guarantees\u003c/a>. No workspace\nfiles, branch heads or symbolic HEAD are changed by partial staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"atomic-retention-and-recovery\">Atomic retention and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Atomic retention and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The selected bytes are ingested using default CDC and repository compression/\nworker settings. The new ordinary v1 staging revision has the branch-bound\nmarker, timestamp zero and original head parent (or no parent initially).\nNo logical object format or encryption envelope changes are introduced.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>One reference transaction guards the branch head, current index, symbolic HEAD\nand optional archive. It publishes the new \u003ccode>staging/BRANCH\u003c/code> and, when an index\nalready existed, \u003ccode>archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID\u003c/code> retaining the complete former\nprepared state and its baseline. Exact existing archives can be reused; a\nconflicting target is refused. Inspect them through:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Working path metadata is checked again after ingestion and immediately before\nreference publication, after progress observers. Cancellation, observer failure,\ndetected working changes and failed closure checks preserve the old references.\nUnreferenced complete objects can remain and are reusable or collectible. Once\nthe atomic reference write starts, it may complete; no user callback runs after\npublication. After interruption, inspect the index/archive and obtain a fresh\nplan. Reusing the old successful plan does not silently apply it a second time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is not filesystem snapshot isolation or a guarantee against a hostile\nwriter restoring timestamps between checks. No workspace journal is needed\nbecause this operation never writes working files. Archives remain retained\nthrough GC until explicitly removed through generic reference administration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ callers use \u003ccode>plan_stage_hunks(directory, branch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>stage_hunks(directory, branch, path, expected_plan_id, numbers, limits, operation)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"remove-prepared-hunks\">Remove prepared hunks\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-hunks/remove-prepared-hunks\" aria-label=\"Link to Remove prepared hunks\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The reset preview compares the original staging baseline to the frozen index,\nin the same forward direction as \u003ccode>diff-text --staged\u003c/code>. It adds one-based\n\u003ccode>number\u003c/code> fields to those hunks. Selected numbers identify changes to remove;\nunselected changes remain prepared. The engine builds the result from the\nbaseline plus the retained hunks, preserving exact bytes even when insertions\nand deletions change line offsets. Preview again after each application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both reset commands accept \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>, progress/cancellation and the five text\noptions listed above. They have no \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> argument: they do not inspect\nworking files, ignore rules or workspace paths. Dirty, absent, binary or\ndirectory-replaced working paths therefore do not affect the plan or result.\nThe plan uses \u003ccode>operation: reset-hunks\u003c/code> and frozen \u003ccode>source_revision\u003c/code> (baseline)\nand \u003ccode>target_revision\u003c/code> (index); its identity binds that content, branch state,\nlimits and blockers. It does not bind a working directory or working hash.\nEnglish and French output have identical JSON. Ready/blocked exit conventions,\nselection validation, budgets and exact expected-plan checks match partial add.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A frozen index is required (\u003ccode>staging_required\u003c/code> otherwise), and the file must\nexist in both the baseline and index. New files, complete deletions and an\nunborn branch&#39;s initial additions use ordinary file-level \u003ccode>native reset\u003c/code>.\nPending checkout, stale index, binary/limited comparisons, no text changes and\narchive collisions also block partial reset. A mode-only change cannot be\nremoved as a text hunk. Selecting all hunks restores baseline content while\nkeeping the index&#39;s executable flag, other frozen files and the staging root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Publication uses the same verified atomic reference transaction and\n\u003ccode>archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID\u003c/code> retention as partial add. The complete previous\nindex remains reconstructible after GC and after committing the remaining\nprepared changes. Cancellation, observer failures and incomplete retained\nclosures preserve the old references; unreachable complete objects may remain.\nNo working-file revalidation or workspace journal is needed for reset because\nthe operation reads only stored snapshots and writes only objects/references.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ callers use \u003ccode>plan_reset_hunks(branch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>reset_hunks(branch, path, expected_plan_id, numbers, limits, operation)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native partial text staging and reset # Select complete numbered text hunks from an existing file without changing the working copy. This complements ordinary file/directory staging. text Copy syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native staged syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\" Both commands accept --branch NAME , --directory PATH , progress/cancellation, and the same --context , --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes options as text diff . English is the default interface; French is also available. JSON keys, plan IDs and hunk numbers are language independent. Application returns JSON, rather than the quiet ID output of ordinary native add . The output budget bounds text-diff generation; numbering and context metadata are additional. The complete preview/application report is capped at 16 MiB. Review and bind the selection # The read-only plan compares the frozen index (or branch head without an index) to the observed working file. Its diff.hunks have one-based number fields. Context controls grouping; adjacent edits in one hunk are selected together. Numbers belong only to that exact plan. Individual changed rows can also be selected with separate line commands. Neither form edits external patch text or presents an interactive prompt. The report includes the working diff, operation: stage-hunks , branch/head/index/ base state, optional old-index archive , text_options , ready , blockers and plan_id . The ID is the existing chunk-domain BLAKE3 ID of the canonical preview JSON before adding plan_id ; no plan object is stored. It binds the directory, path, branch state, source manifest, observed working hash, available mode, numbered hunks, limits and readiness. Different interface languages do not change it. After applying hunks, the returned report retains the original preview ID, while result fields describe publication; its entire JSON is not that preview's hash input anymore. Application recomputes the preview and requires the exact expected ID. Changes to working content, branch/index state or options require a fresh preview. Pass the same nondefault options on both commands. Selected numbers must be unique, positive, in range, and nonempty (at most 100,000). Input order is irrelevant to the selected bytes. After successful application, preview again before choosing remaining changes: their numbers can change. Ready previews exit 0. Blocked previews exit 1 with JSON; ordinary errors exit nonzero without a result. Blockers include pending checkout, stale index, non-text/limited diff, no text changes, file-presence changes and conflicting old-index archives. Preview verification covers this file, not unrelated stored payloads or full history. The complete replacement and retained closures are verified during publication; missing unrelated data can still block application. Scope and exact bytes # Both source and working file must exist as regular files. Empty existing files, CRLF/LF, Unicode text and missing final newlines are supported. Binary files, new/untracked files and complete file deletions require ordinary native add . An unborn branch is supported when an existing frozen initial index already contains the selected file. Stale indexes must be explicitly rebased or reset. The candidate starts from the complete source index/head file map. It replaces only the selected path's manifest, retaining all other frozen files and the source executable flag. Mode changes and alternate chunking representations are not selected as text. Individual hunks are copied byte-exactly; unselected regions remain source bytes. Selecting all hunks reproduces the observed working content, within the configured file budget. A subset can be larger than either input when it keeps unselected deletions and includes selected additions; it is refused before ingestion if it exceeds that budget. The targeted workspace reader enforces ignore rules, exact path spelling, namespace/link/storage guards, bounded reads and observed-change detection. See working-file guarantees . No workspace files, branch heads or symbolic HEAD are changed by partial staging. Atomic retention and recovery # The selected bytes are ingested using default CDC and repository compression/ worker settings. The new ordinary v1 staging revision has the branch-bound marker, timestamp zero and original head parent (or no parent initially). No logical object format or encryption envelope changes are introduced. One reference transaction guards the branch head, current index, symbolic HEAD and optional archive. It publishes the new staging/BRANCH and, when an index already existed, archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID retaining the complete former prepared state and its baseline. Exact existing archives can be reused; a conflicting target is refused. Inspect them through: text Copy syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY Working path metadata is checked again after ingestion and immediately before reference publication, after progress observers. Cancellation, observer failure, detected working changes and failed closure checks preserve the old references. Unreferenced complete objects can remain and are reusable or collectible. Once the atomic reference write starts, it may complete; no user callback runs after publication. After interruption, inspect the index/archive and obtain a fresh plan. Reusing the old successful plan does not silently apply it a second time. This is not filesystem snapshot isolation or a guarantee against a hostile writer restoring timestamps between checks. No workspace journal is needed because this operation never writes working files. Archives remain retained through GC until explicitly removed through generic reference administration. C++ callers use plan_stage_hunks(directory, branch, path, limits, operation) and stage_hunks(directory, branch, path, expected_plan_id, numbers, limits, operation) . Remove prepared hunks # text Copy syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified The reset preview compares the original staging baseline to the frozen index, in the same forward direction as diff-text --staged . It adds one-based number fields to those hunks. Selected numbers identify changes to remove; unselected changes remain prepared. The engine builds the result from the baseline plus the retained hunks, preserving exact bytes even when insertions and deletions change line offsets. Preview again after each application. Both reset commands accept --branch , progress/cancellation and the five text options listed above. They have no --directory argument: they do not inspect working files, ignore rules or workspace paths. Dirty, absent, binary or directory-replaced working paths therefore do not affect the plan or result. The plan uses operation: reset-hunks and frozen source_revision (baseline) and target_revision (index); its identity binds that content, branch state, limits and blockers. It does not bind a working directory or working hash. English and French output have identical JSON. Ready/blocked exit conventions, selection validation, budgets and exact expected-plan checks match partial add. A frozen index is required ( staging_required otherwise), and the file must exist in both the baseline and index. New files, complete deletions and an unborn branch's initial additions use ordinary file-level native reset . Pending checkout, stale index, binary/limited comparisons, no text changes and archive collisions also block partial reset. A mode-only change cannot be removed as a text hunk. Selecting all hunks restores baseline content while keeping the index's executable flag, other frozen files and the staging root. Publication uses the same verified atomic reference transaction and archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID retention as partial add. The complete previous index remains reconstructible after GC and after committing the remaining prepared changes. Cancellation, observer failures and incomplete retained closures preserve the old references; unreachable complete objects may remain. No working-file revalidation or workspace journal is needed for reset because the operation reads only stored snapshots and writes only objects/references. C++ callers use plan_reset_hunks(branch, path, limits, operation) and reset_hunks(branch, path, expected_plan_id, numbers, limits, operation) . syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native staged syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\" syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified","headings":[{"id":"native-partial-text-staging-and-reset","text":"Native partial text staging and reset","level":1},{"id":"review-and-bind-the-selection","text":"Review and bind the selection","level":2},{"id":"scope-and-exact-bytes","text":"Scope and exact bytes","level":2},{"id":"atomic-retention-and-recovery","text":"Atomic retention and recovery","level":2},{"id":"remove-prepared-hunks","text":"Remove prepared hunks","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\"\nsyrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify\nsyrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify\nsyrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY\nsyrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified","assets":[],"links":["#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-lines","#/en/text-diff/compare-a-working-file"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-lines","title":"Native individual text-line selection","description":"Native individual text-line selection guide.","section":"Version control","order":290,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-individual-text-line-selection\">Native individual text-line selection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-lines/native-individual-text-line-selection\" aria-label=\"Link to Native individual text-line selection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Prepare or remove individual added/removed rows inside a text hunk. Existing\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">whole-hunk commands\u003c/a> remain available. Working files are never\nwritten by either form of selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the numbers from the corresponding fresh preview, with the same options.\nThe add and reset plans have different identities and may have different rows.\nEnglish is the default interface; French is available with \u003ccode>--lang fr\u003c/code>. JSON\nkeys, numbers and plan IDs are language independent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numbered-change-rows\">Numbered change rows\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-lines/numbered-change-rows\" aria-label=\"Link to Numbered change rows\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each preview includes \u003ccode>selection_unit: line\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>change_count\u003c/code> and the ordinary\nnumbered \u003ccode>diff.hunks\u003c/code>. Each \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code> row additionally has a global\none-based \u003ccode>change_number\u003c/code>, in diff order across all hunks. Context rows have no\nchange number. \u003ccode>--lines\u003c/code> selects these change numbers, not working-file line\nnumbers or positions within a hunk. The list must be nonempty, unique, positive\nand in range, with at most 100,000 numbers; input order does not change the bytes.\nMore than 100,000 change rows blocks the entire preview with \u003ccode>selection_limit\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For a replacement, old and new rows are independent:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">change_number  kind    text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">1              remove old A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">2              remove old B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">3              add    new A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">4              add    new B\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Adding \u003ccode>--lines 2,4\u003c/code> removes \u003ccode>old B\u003c/code> and inserts \u003ccode>new B\u003c/code>, retaining \u003ccode>old A\u003c/code>.\nAdding only \u003ccode>--lines 3\u003c/code> keeps both old rows and inserts \u003ccode>new A\u003c/code> after them.\nAdding only \u003ccode>--lines 1\u003c/code> deletes \u003ccode>old A\u003c/code> without inserting a replacement.\nThe original diff order determines insertion positions; selected rows cannot be\nreordered or edited. This does not validate syntax or compilation of the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For reset, the same forward baseline-to-index numbering identifies changes to\nremove. Unselected change rows remain prepared. The result is constructed from\nthe original baseline plus those retained rows, avoiding shifted-line matching.\nResetting all rows restores baseline content while retaining the index&#39;s mode,\nother files and staging root. Obtain a fresh preview after every application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal\">Exact bytes and selection refusal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-lines/exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal\" aria-label=\"Link to Exact bytes and selection refusal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Selected text retains its exact UTF-8, LF/CRLF and final-newline bytes. No newline\nis inserted automatically. A selection that would append a logical line after\nan unterminated retained line is refused before ingestion:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">source: old (no final LF)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">target: new (with final LF)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Selecting only the added row would concatenate \u003ccode>old\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>new\u003c/code>; select both\nthe removal and addition to replace the line. The equivalent invalid partial\nreset is also refused. A ready preview means the comparison is eligible for\nselection; some individual combinations may still fail this boundary rule or\nthe result-size budget. A result can be larger than either input when it keeps\nunselected deletions while inserting selected additions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plans-storage-and-retention\">Plans, storage and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-lines/plans-storage-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Plans, storage and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The add preview (\u003ccode>operation: stage-lines\u003c/code>) compares the frozen index/head to the\nobserved working file. It accepts \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> and uses the existing targeted\nworking reader&#39;s ignore, namespace, link, size and observed-change checks.\nApplication revalidates working path metadata after ingestion and immediately\nbefore reference publication, after the progress observer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The reset preview (\u003ccode>operation: reset-lines\u003c/code>) compares the index&#39;s original\nbaseline to its frozen bytes. It has no \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> argument and never reads\nworking content. Working paths may be absent, dirty, ignored or replaced by a\ndirectory. Both sides of the stored comparison must contain the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both forms require existing text files, a fresh index when present, and no\npending checkout. New files, complete presence changes, binary/limited diffs,\nmode-only changes and archive conflicts follow the whole-hunk restrictions.\nAn unborn branch can add selected lines against an existing initial index;\nits initial file additions require file-level reset.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All commands accept \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>, progress/cancellation and the text options\n\u003ccode>--context\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code>.\nThese keep \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text diff&#39;s bounds\u003c/a>. Row numbering and state metadata\nare additional to the diff output budget; the complete report is capped at\n16 MiB. Changes to options, numbered diff, branch/index/base, readiness or\nobserved working content require a new exact \u003ccode>--expect-plan\u003c/code>. A whole-hunk\nplan cannot authorize a line application. Ready previews exit 0; blocked\npreviews exit 1 with JSON. Invalid selections fail without a result.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Publication reuses the verified atomic reference transaction, preserving other\nfrozen entries and the source index/head executable flag. It publishes the\nreplacement staging root together with \u003ccode>archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID\u003c/code>,\nwhen an old index exists. The complete previous preparation and its baseline\nremain reconstructible after GC and after committing the new selections.\nCancellation, observer exceptions and failed closure verification preserve the\nold references; complete unreachable objects may remain. No callback runs after\nreference publication. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery\">retention and interruption\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ callers use \u003ccode>plan_stage_lines(directory, branch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>stage_lines(directory, branch, path, expected_plan, numbers, limits, operation)\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>plan_reset_lines(branch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>reset_lines(branch, path, expected_plan, numbers, limits, operation)\u003c/code>.\nNo logical object or encryption format changes are introduced. Rebuild C++\nconsumers against the updated header.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native individual text-line selection # Prepare or remove individual added/removed rows inside a text hunk. Existing whole-hunk commands remain available. Working files are never written by either form of selection. text Copy syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3 syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4 Use the numbers from the corresponding fresh preview, with the same options. The add and reset plans have different identities and may have different rows. English is the default interface; French is available with --lang fr . JSON keys, numbers and plan IDs are language independent. Numbered change rows # Each preview includes selection_unit: line , change_count and the ordinary numbered diff.hunks . Each add or remove row additionally has a global one-based change_number , in diff order across all hunks. Context rows have no change number. --lines selects these change numbers, not working-file line numbers or positions within a hunk. The list must be nonempty, unique, positive and in range, with at most 100,000 numbers; input order does not change the bytes. More than 100,000 change rows blocks the entire preview with selection_limit . For a replacement, old and new rows are independent: text Copy change_number kind text 1 remove old A 2 remove old B 3 add new A 4 add new B Adding --lines 2,4 removes old B and inserts new B , retaining old A . Adding only --lines 3 keeps both old rows and inserts new A after them. Adding only --lines 1 deletes old A without inserting a replacement. The original diff order determines insertion positions; selected rows cannot be reordered or edited. This does not validate syntax or compilation of the result. For reset, the same forward baseline-to-index numbering identifies changes to remove. Unselected change rows remain prepared. The result is constructed from the original baseline plus those retained rows, avoiding shifted-line matching. Resetting all rows restores baseline content while retaining the index's mode, other files and staging root. Obtain a fresh preview after every application. Exact bytes and selection refusal # Selected text retains its exact UTF-8, LF/CRLF and final-newline bytes. No newline is inserted automatically. A selection that would append a logical line after an unterminated retained line is refused before ingestion: text Copy source: old (no final LF) target: new (with final LF) Selecting only the added row would concatenate old and new ; select both the removal and addition to replace the line. The equivalent invalid partial reset is also refused. A ready preview means the comparison is eligible for selection; some individual combinations may still fail this boundary rule or the result-size budget. A result can be larger than either input when it keeps unselected deletions while inserting selected additions. Plans, storage and retention # The add preview ( operation: stage-lines ) compares the frozen index/head to the observed working file. It accepts --directory and uses the existing targeted working reader's ignore, namespace, link, size and observed-change checks. Application revalidates working path metadata after ingestion and immediately before reference publication, after the progress observer. The reset preview ( operation: reset-lines ) compares the index's original baseline to its frozen bytes. It has no --directory argument and never reads working content. Working paths may be absent, dirty, ignored or replaced by a directory. Both sides of the stored comparison must contain the file. Both forms require existing text files, a fresh index when present, and no pending checkout. New files, complete presence changes, binary/limited diffs, mode-only changes and archive conflicts follow the whole-hunk restrictions. An unborn branch can add selected lines against an existing initial index; its initial file additions require file-level reset. All commands accept --branch , progress/cancellation and the text options --context , --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work , --max-output-bytes . These keep text diff's bounds . Row numbering and state metadata are additional to the diff output budget; the complete report is capped at 16 MiB. Changes to options, numbered diff, branch/index/base, readiness or observed working content require a new exact --expect-plan . A whole-hunk plan cannot authorize a line application. Ready previews exit 0; blocked previews exit 1 with JSON. Invalid selections fail without a result. Publication reuses the verified atomic reference transaction, preserving other frozen entries and the source index/head executable flag. It publishes the replacement staging root together with archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID , when an old index exists. The complete previous preparation and its baseline remain reconstructible after GC and after committing the new selections. Cancellation, observer exceptions and failed closure verification preserve the old references; complete unreachable objects may remain. No callback runs after reference publication. See retention and interruption . C++ callers use plan_stage_lines(directory, branch, path, limits, operation) , stage_lines(directory, branch, path, expected_plan, numbers, limits, operation) , plan_reset_lines(branch, path, limits, operation) and reset_lines(branch, path, expected_plan, numbers, limits, operation) . No logical object or encryption format changes are introduced. Rebuild C++ consumers against the updated header. syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3 syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4 change_number kind text 1 remove old A 2 remove old B 3 add new A 4 add new B source: old (no final LF) target: new (with final LF)","headings":[{"id":"native-individual-text-line-selection","text":"Native individual text-line selection","level":1},{"id":"numbered-change-rows","text":"Numbered change rows","level":2},{"id":"exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal","text":"Exact bytes and selection refusal","level":2},{"id":"plans-storage-and-retention","text":"Plans, storage and retention","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3\nsyrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4\nchange_number  kind    text\n1              remove old A\n2              remove old B\n3              add    new A\n4              add    new B\nsource: old (no final LF)\ntarget: new (with final LF)","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-hunks","#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-merge","title":"Native merge","description":"Native merge guide.","section":"Version control","order":300,"parent":"native-history","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-merge\">Native merge\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/native-merge\" aria-label=\"Link to Native merge\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native merge-plan\u003c/code> computes the topology and complete proposed file snapshot\nwithout changing objects, references or working files. It shares file-level\nthree-way decisions with \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay\">native replay\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default target is the selected native branch. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>\noverride the operation&#39;s branch and workspace. The CLI accepts native revision\nselectors; the C++ API takes exact source/base IDs. \u003ccode>--max-revisions\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--max-links\u003c/code> have the same bounds/defaults as \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-history\">ancestry queries\u003c/a>.\nThe complete input graph must be available; there is no shallow fallback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"topology\">Topology\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/topology\" aria-label=\"Link to Topology\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Proposed snapshot\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>up_to_date\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Source is already an ancestor of the target, including equality\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact current branch snapshot\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>fast_forward\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current target is an ancestor of source\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact source snapshot, including its manifest representation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>merge\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Neither revision contains the other\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Three-way file merge against the selected base\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>A unique best common ancestor is selected automatically. Multiple best bases\nproduce \u003ccode>multiple_merge_bases\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>--base\u003c/code> must select one of the reported best\nancestors. An older common ancestor is rejected. This explicit choice does not\nsynthesize Git&#39;s recursive virtual merge base. No common ancestor produces\n\u003ccode>unrelated_histories\u003c/code> unless \u003ccode>--allow-unrelated-histories\u003c/code> explicitly selects the\nempty tree as baseline. An explicit base cannot be supplied for unrelated roots.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The file merger combines independent edits and content/mode changes on the same\nfile. Different concurrent contents, add/add and delete/modify cases remain\nconflicts. The combined snapshot is checked for case and file/directory prefix\ncollisions. Content equality uses file hash, size and mode; rechunking alone is\nnot an edit. Fast-forward nevertheless adopts the exact source revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When snapshot conflicts are absent, the existing clean-tracked checkout preflight\nchecks working files, ignored/untracked obstructions, protected paths, links,\nstaging and active recovery. A topology-only divergent merge can be ready even\nwith no changed file bytes. Up-to-date plans also use this conservative preflight.\nExplicit file choices and custom manifests can resolve conflicts with\n\u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code>; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">native resolutions\u003c/a>.\nBounded text merging can be enabled explicitly as described below. Rename\ndetection and recursive base synthesis remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"opt-in-text-composition\">Opt-in text composition\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Link to Opt-in text composition\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The C++ \u003ccode>NativeMergeOptions::text_merge\u003c/code> optional enables text composition with\nthe supplied \u003ccode>TextDiffOptions\u003c/code>. Leaving it empty preserves the default file-level\ndecisions and metadata-only input inspection. Explicit \u003ccode>resolutions\u003c/code> are applied\nfirst; only remaining \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> conflicts are candidates for automatic text merge.\nAdd/add, delete/modify, binary data, overlapping edits and namespace conflicts\nremain blocking. This does not change fast-forward representation or topology.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Default text limits, explicit opt-in.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_tip\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each candidate&#39;s complete three inputs are reconstructed and hash-verified even\nwithout full-history verification. File-size and batch byte ceilings are checked\nbefore payload reads. At most 64 content conflicts are attempted, with a 16 MiB\naggregate charge for verified inputs plus clean results. Per-file work, line,\ntrace and serialized output limits also apply. Skipped or limited files keep\ntheir original conflict and report the reason; the operation never chooses a\npartial result to satisfy a budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The CLI exposes the same opt-in for both plan and apply:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code> set the\nper-file text budgets and require \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code>. Plan and apply should use the\nsame settings; apply always recomputes against the expected current head.\nEnglish/French diagnostics and progress use the existing language and stderr\ncontrols. JSON fields and reason codes stay language-independent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The plan&#39;s \u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code> object reports limits, attempt/byte counts, generated\nmanifest count and per-path status/ranges/target metadata. Disabled mode uses\n\u003ccode>null\u003c/code>. Reports omit merged plaintext. Ready workspace actions refer to exact\npredicted manifest IDs which need not exist in the Store yet. Planning does not\ncreate any objects, including when one file composes and another remains blocked.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation\">virtual file preparation\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Apply first recomputes the full plan, verifies input histories and checks all\nworkspace blockers. Only then does it materialize generated chunks/manifests,\nrequiring exact agreement with predicted IDs, and create the two-parent revision.\nThe existing recoverable checkout then publishes the complete result. Failure\nbefore journal publication can leave unreferenced generated objects, with no\nbranch/workspace change; normal GC can collect them. Once a journal exists,\nresume/abort uses its retained candidate without needing to rerun text merge.\nCancellation and observer exceptions preserve these boundaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conflict-copies\">Preserve concurrent file contents\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/conflict-copies\" aria-label=\"Link to Preserve concurrent file contents\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> is an explicit file-level preservation policy. Explicit\n\u003ccode>--resolutions\u003c/code> choices apply first; optional bounded text composition runs next.\nRemaining content and add/add conflicts keep one version at the original path\nand the other in a sibling copy. The lexicographically smaller complete revision\nID wins the original path, so reversing the two peers produces the same file\nnamespace. This ordering is deterministic; it does not imply newer or better data.\nConcurrent delete/modify retains the modified file at its original path and\nrecords \u003ccode>choice: keep_modified\u003c/code>. Both parent histories preserve the deletion.\nIdentical concurrent deletions still delete the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A sibling name combines at most 64 UTF-8 bytes of the stem, \u003ccode>.syrnck-conflict-\u003c/code>,\nthe losing revision&#39;s complete ID and 16 hex digits of the original path hash.\nExtensions of at most 16 UTF-8 bytes, including \u003ccode>.uasset\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>.umap\u003c/code>, are kept.\nNo timestamp, hostname or random suffix influences the result. Reports include\n\u003ccode>conflict_copies\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> entries with \u003ccode>choice: conflict_copy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>copy_path\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>copy_revision\u003c/code>, original conflict \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code> and both file descriptors.\nThe generated names are ordinary tracked files; review and remove them in a\nlater commit after choosing the desired asset version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Any occupied generated name or parent in an input snapshot blocks with\n\u003ccode>conflict_copy_collision\u003c/code>, including case aliases, directories and files that\nwould otherwise be deleted. Untracked/ignored working obstructions still block\ncheckout. Case and file/directory conflicts, ambiguous bases and unrelated\nhistories retain their normal policies. This option does not infer an edited\nrename or compose binary data. Copies share the existing manifests and chunks;\nthere is no extra binary payload in the Store for the preserved version.\nApply verifies the complete input histories and uses the usual signed revision\nand recoverable checkout. The frozen candidate, including its copy paths,\nresumes without recomputing policy or requiring its signing key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In C++, set \u003ccode>NativeMergeOptions::conflict_copies = true\u003c/code>. The default remains\nfalse. Cherry-pick, revert and rebase keep their existing resolution policies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reports-and-validation\">Reports and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/reports-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Reports and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The JSON report contains \u003ccode>mode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, selected \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, all\n\u003ccode>merge_bases\u003c/code>, graph traversal counts, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_files\u003c/code>,\nproposed \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>, file \u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code>, accepted \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> choices,\n\u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code>, topology \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> preflight.\nWith topology blockers, \u003ccode>result_files\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> are null. With file\nconflicts, \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> is null. Otherwise its target ID is null because the plan\ndoes not write a candidate revision. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1\nwith JSON on stdout. Invalid input or missing metadata produces a diagnostic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally verifies both input histories&#39; complete closures and\nreconstructed file hashes, including custom resolution manifests. Without it,\nstored revision/manifest metadata is read\nand working files are hashed, but incoming file block payloads are not audited.\nCompact reports and candidate metadata are bounded as for replay. Memory scales\nwith the bounded ancestry graph, snapshots and report.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>base \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> selected_best_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Optional explicit best ancestor.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Planning supports cooperative cancellation and English/French progress/errors.\nJSON fields and reason codes are language-independent. Callbacks must not reenter\nthe Store. Native merge is not exposed through the C ABI or Go binding yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-recover\">Apply and recover\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-merge/apply-and-recover\" aria-label=\"Link to Apply and recover\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge release --ff-only\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Apply recomputes the plan, always verifies both input closures and reconstructed\nfile hashes, and requires the exact target tip inspected by the caller. The CLI\ncaptures it under the Store lock when \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> is omitted. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> accepts\nan exact ID. The optional message defaults to \u003ccode>Merge SOURCE_ID\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Up-to-date returns \u003ccode>state: unchanged\u003c/code> without writing objects or refs. Fast-forward\nchecks out the exact source revision without creating a new commit. Divergence\ncreates a revision with parents \u003ccode>[old_target, source]\u003c/code> and the supplied\nmessage/current UTC timestamp. A \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">configured identity\u003c/a>\ncreates v2 with the current person as author/committer and an optional signature;\notherwise it stays v1. A same-tree divergent history still creates this\ntwo-parent commit. Source branches/tags and symbolic HEAD do not move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--no-ff\u003c/code> requests a two-parent commit even when a fast-forward is possible.\n\u003ccode>--ff-only\u003c/code> refuses divergence with the \u003ccode>fast_forward_required\u003c/code> blocker. These\nflags are mutually exclusive and work in both plan and apply. They do not bypass\nbase selection, unrelated-history opt-in, file conflicts or workspace preflight.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After preparing the candidate, apply uses the existing checkout journal, file\nbackups, atomic branch/archive/receipt publication and explicit recovery:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Abort is available before branch publication; afterwards recovery only finishes\ncleanup. An active journal blocks GC and new native history mutations. Candidate\nmetadata written before a failed/cancelled journal publication can remain\nunreferenced. Opening the Store never applies workspace recovery automatically.\nThe \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">checkout data and durability limits\u003c/a> apply, including\nplaintext private prepared files/backups in encrypted Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both histories remain reachable through the new two-parent commit after a\ndivergent merge. Fast-forward already contains the old target in its ancestry.\nCheckout archives additionally retain previous heads. These roots preserve old\nfile blocks through destructive GC. Reverting a merge uses the existing\n\u003ccode>native revert MERGE_ID --mainline N -m MESSAGE\u003c/code> operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The direct result adds \u003ccode>operation: merge\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_revision\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>merged_revision\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>base\u003c/code> to the checkout result. Recovery reports the generic\ncheckout journal identity and its recorded target; these response labels do not\nchange the journal format. Native revision v1 is unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Native merge # native merge-plan computes the topology and complete proposed file snapshot without changing objects, references or working files. It shares file-level three-way decisions with native replay . text Copy syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories The default target is the selected native branch. --branch and --directory override the operation's branch and workspace. The CLI accepts native revision selectors; the C++ API takes exact source/base IDs. --max-revisions and --max-links have the same bounds/defaults as ancestry queries . The complete input graph must be available; there is no shallow fallback. Topology # Mode Meaning Proposed snapshot up_to_date Source is already an ancestor of the target, including equality Exact current branch snapshot fast_forward Current target is an ancestor of source Exact source snapshot, including its manifest representation merge Neither revision contains the other Three-way file merge against the selected base A unique best common ancestor is selected automatically. Multiple best bases produce multiple_merge_bases ; --base must select one of the reported best ancestors. An older common ancestor is rejected. This explicit choice does not synthesize Git's recursive virtual merge base. No common ancestor produces unrelated_histories unless --allow-unrelated-histories explicitly selects the empty tree as baseline. An explicit base cannot be supplied for unrelated roots. The file merger combines independent edits and content/mode changes on the same file. Different concurrent contents, add/add and delete/modify cases remain conflicts. The combined snapshot is checked for case and file/directory prefix collisions. Content equality uses file hash, size and mode; rechunking alone is not an edit. Fast-forward nevertheless adopts the exact source revision. When snapshot conflicts are absent, the existing clean-tracked checkout preflight checks working files, ignored/untracked obstructions, protected paths, links, staging and active recovery. A topology-only divergent merge can be ready even with no changed file bytes. Up-to-date plans also use this conservative preflight. Explicit file choices and custom manifests can resolve conflicts with --resolutions JSON_FILE ; see native resolutions . Bounded text merging can be enabled explicitly as described below. Rename detection and recursive base synthesis remain future work. Opt-in text composition # The C++ NativeMergeOptions::text_merge optional enables text composition with the supplied TextDiffOptions . Leaving it empty preserves the default file-level decisions and metadata-only input inspection. Explicit resolutions are applied first; only remaining content conflicts are candidates for automatic text merge. Add/add, delete/modify, binary data, overlapping edits and namespace conflicts remain blocking. This does not change fast-forward representation or topology. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; // Default text limits, explicit opt-in. auto preview = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_tip , source_id , \"Merge feature\" , settings , operation ) ; Each candidate's complete three inputs are reconstructed and hash-verified even without full-history verification. File-size and batch byte ceilings are checked before payload reads. At most 64 content conflicts are attempted, with a 16 MiB aggregate charge for verified inputs plus clean results. Per-file work, line, trace and serialized output limits also apply. Skipped or limited files keep their original conflict and report the reason; the operation never chooses a partial result to satisfy a budget. The CLI exposes the same opt-in for both plan and apply: text Copy syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432 --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes set the per-file text budgets and require --text-merge . Plan and apply should use the same settings; apply always recomputes against the expected current head. English/French diagnostics and progress use the existing language and stderr controls. JSON fields and reason codes stay language-independent. The plan's text_merge object reports limits, attempt/byte counts, generated manifest count and per-path status/ranges/target metadata. Disabled mode uses null . Reports omit merged plaintext. Ready workspace actions refer to exact predicted manifest IDs which need not exist in the Store yet. Planning does not create any objects, including when one file composes and another remains blocked. See virtual file preparation . Apply first recomputes the full plan, verifies input histories and checks all workspace blockers. Only then does it materialize generated chunks/manifests, requiring exact agreement with predicted IDs, and create the two-parent revision. The existing recoverable checkout then publishes the complete result. Failure before journal publication can leave unreferenced generated objects, with no branch/workspace change; normal GC can collect them. Once a journal exists, resume/abort uses its retained candidate without needing to rerun text merge. Cancellation and observer exceptions preserve these boundaries. Preserve concurrent file contents # text Copy syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID --conflict-copies is an explicit file-level preservation policy. Explicit --resolutions choices apply first; optional bounded text composition runs next. Remaining content and add/add conflicts keep one version at the original path and the other in a sibling copy. The lexicographically smaller complete revision ID wins the original path, so reversing the two peers produces the same file namespace. This ordering is deterministic; it does not imply newer or better data. Concurrent delete/modify retains the modified file at its original path and records choice: keep_modified . Both parent histories preserve the deletion. Identical concurrent deletions still delete the file. A sibling name combines at most 64 UTF-8 bytes of the stem, .syrnck-conflict- , the losing revision's complete ID and 16 hex digits of the original path hash. Extensions of at most 16 UTF-8 bytes, including .uasset and .umap , are kept. No timestamp, hostname or random suffix influences the result. Reports include conflict_copies , resolved entries with choice: conflict_copy , copy_path , copy_revision , original conflict reason and both file descriptors. The generated names are ordinary tracked files; review and remove them in a later commit after choosing the desired asset version. Any occupied generated name or parent in an input snapshot blocks with conflict_copy_collision , including case aliases, directories and files that would otherwise be deleted. Untracked/ignored working obstructions still block checkout. Case and file/directory conflicts, ambiguous bases and unrelated histories retain their normal policies. This option does not infer an edited rename or compose binary data. Copies share the existing manifests and chunks; there is no extra binary payload in the Store for the preserved version. Apply verifies the complete input histories and uses the usual signed revision and recoverable checkout. The frozen candidate, including its copy paths, resumes without recomputing policy or requiring its signing key. In C++, set NativeMergeOptions::conflict_copies = true . The default remains false. Cherry-pick, revert and rebase keep their existing resolution policies. Reports and validation # The JSON report contains mode , head , source , selected base , all merge_bases , graph traversal counts, ready , objects_verified , result_files , proposed changes , file conflicts , accepted resolved choices, resolution_context , topology blockers and workspace preflight. With topology blockers, result_files and workspace are null. With file conflicts, workspace is null. Otherwise its target ID is null because the plan does not write a candidate revision. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid input or missing metadata produces a diagnostic. --verify additionally verifies both input histories' complete closures and reconstructed file hashes, including custom resolution manifests. Without it, stored revision/manifest metadata is read and working files are hashed, but incoming file block payloads are not audited. Compact reports and candidate metadata are bounded as for replay. Memory scales with the bounded ancestry graph, snapshots and report. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . base = selected_best_base ; // Optional explicit best ancestor. auto plan = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; Planning supports cooperative cancellation and English/French progress/errors. JSON fields and reason codes are language-independent. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. Native merge is not exposed through the C ABI or Go binding yet. Apply and recover # text Copy syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\" syrnck native merge release --ff-only syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\" Apply recomputes the plan, always verifies both input closures and reconstructed file hashes, and requires the exact target tip inspected by the caller. The CLI captures it under the Store lock when --expect is omitted. --expect accepts an exact ID. The optional message defaults to Merge SOURCE_ID . Up-to-date returns state: unchanged without writing objects or refs. Fast-forward checks out the exact source revision without creating a new commit. Divergence creates a revision with parents [old_target, source] and the supplied message/current UTC timestamp. A configured identity creates v2 with the current person as author/committer and an optional signature; otherwise it stays v1. A same-tree divergent history still creates this two-parent commit. Source branches/tags and symbolic HEAD do not move. --no-ff requests a two-parent commit even when a fast-forward is possible. --ff-only refuses divergence with the fast_forward_required blocker. These flags are mutually exclusive and work in both plan and apply. They do not bypass base selection, unrelated-history opt-in, file conflicts or workspace preflight. After preparing the candidate, apply uses the existing checkout journal, file backups, atomic branch/archive/receipt publication and explicit recovery: text Copy syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Abort is available before branch publication; afterwards recovery only finishes cleanup. An active journal blocks GC and new native history mutations. Candidate metadata written before a failed/cancelled journal publication can remain unreferenced. Opening the Store never applies workspace recovery automatically. The checkout data and durability limits apply, including plaintext private prepared files/backups in encrypted Stores. Both histories remain reachable through the new two-parent commit after a divergent merge. Fast-forward already contains the old target in its ancestry. Checkout archives additionally retain previous heads. These roots preserve old file blocks through destructive GC. Reverting a merge uses the existing native revert MERGE_ID --mainline N -m MESSAGE operation. The direct result adds operation: merge , mode , previous_revision , merged_revision and base to the checkout result. Recovery reports the generic checkout journal identity and its recorded target; these response labels do not change the journal format. Native revision v1 is unchanged. cpp Copy auto applied = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , \"Merge feature\" , settings , operation ) ; syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default text limits, explicit opt-in. auto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); auto result = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_tip, source_id, \"Merge feature\", settings, operation); syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432 syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.base = selected_best_base; // Optional explicit best ancestor. auto plan = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\" syrnck native merge release --ff-only syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\" syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort auto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-merge","text":"Native merge","level":1},{"id":"topology","text":"Topology","level":2},{"id":"opt-in-text-composition","text":"Opt-in text composition","level":2},{"id":"conflict-copies","text":"Preserve concurrent file contents","level":2},{"id":"reports-and-validation","text":"Reports and validation","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-recover","text":"Apply and recover","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID\nsyrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default text limits, explicit opt-in.\nauto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nauto result = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_tip, source_id,\n                                    \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.base = selected_best_base; // Optional explicit best ancestor.\nauto plan = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nsyrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\"\nsyrnck native merge release --ff-only\nsyrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\"\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nauto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-replay","#/en/native-history","#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation","#/en/native-authorship","#/en/native-checkout"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-rebase-todo","title":"Editable and interactive native rebase","description":"Editable and interactive native rebase guide.","section":"Version control","order":320,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"editable-and-interactive-native-rebase\">Editable and interactive native rebase\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase-todo/editable-and-interactive-native-rebase\" aria-label=\"Link to Editable and interactive native rebase\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native linear rebase supports a complete reviewed sequence, either from an\neditable JSON document or a terminal prompt. Both routes use the same C++\nplanner, conflict resolutions, text composition and recoverable checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Keep the document outside tracked workspace files (for example, under the\nStore) so it does not obstruct checkout. The template contains exact \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>onto\u003c/code> IDs and one step per source commit. Move rows to change order;\nretain every source exactly once and use \u003ccode>drop\u003c/code> explicitly to remove one.\nUnknown/missing fields, duplicate JSON keys/sources, foreign commits, stale\ncontexts and incomplete ranges are rejected before creating candidates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"type\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"native-rebase-todo\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"head\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"HEAD_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"BASE_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"onto\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ONTO_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"steps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"SECOND_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reword\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"New message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"FIRST_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"squash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"THIRD_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fixup\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"FOURTH_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"drop\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L13\" data-line=\"13\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Replace the illustrative IDs with the 64 lowercase hexadecimal IDs emitted\nby the template. Messages may contain Unicode, newlines and empty text.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pick\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replay the source delta and preserve its message/timestamp.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>drop\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replay nothing; retain its original history in the checkout archive.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>reword\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replay the delta, keep its timestamp and use the required \u003ccode>message\u003c/code>.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>squash\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replay the delta into the preceding picked result, retaining that result&#39;s timestamp/parent and appending the source message after two newlines.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>fixup\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replay the delta into the preceding picked result, retaining its message, timestamp and parent.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Only \u003ccode>reword\u003c/code> accepts a \u003ccode>message\u003c/code>. Squash/fixup require a preceding picked\nresult, ignoring intervening drops. A fold replaces the previous virtual\ncandidate rather than introducing an extra parent/commit. Provisional folded\nobjects are not stored. Original source versions remain protected by the old\nhead archive; an unpublished provisional snapshot is not a retained version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each delta is still measured against the source commit&#39;s \u003cstrong>actual original\nparent\u003c/strong>, even after reordering or dropping earlier commits. Dropping a\ndependency can therefore cause a conflict. It does not silently apply the\ndependent source&#39;s complete snapshot. Existing \u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code> can resolve those replay conflicts. Resolution contexts include\nthe current virtual head, so changing earlier actions invalidates stale choices.\nA dropped source cannot have file resolutions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An explicit sequence is honored even when BASE equals ONTO. Dropping all rows\nmoves the branch to ONTO through the normal checkout journal. Without a todo,\nthe original same-base no-op and oldest-first behavior remain available.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terminal-input\">Terminal input\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase-todo/terminal-input\" aria-label=\"Link to Terminal input\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The CLI prints source IDs and escaped message previews on stderr. Enter the\ncomplete desired sequence, one action and \u003cstrong>full\u003c/strong> revision ID per line:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">reword SECOND_ID New message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">squash FIRST_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">fixup THIRD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">drop FOURTH_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A line containing \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> confirms the sequence; \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> alone selects the original\npick sequence. EOF before confirmation fails without writing candidates or\nchanging files/references. Ctrl+C requests cancellation. Commands are not\nexecuted as shell text. Action names remain English in both interface languages.\nUse a JSON document for multiline messages. \u003ccode>--todo\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--interactive\u003c/code> are\nmutually exclusive; interactive prompts cannot be combined with JSON progress.\n\u003ccode>rebase-plan --interactive\u003c/code> reviews without applying. Plain result JSON stays\non stdout, separate from the prompts. Redirected input uses UTF-8. Windows\nconsole input is read as UTF-16 and converted without changing the console&#39;s\ncode page or interpreting message backslashes as path separators.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-recovery-and-scope\">Limits, recovery and scope\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase-todo/limits-recovery-and-scope\" aria-label=\"Link to Limits, recovery and scope\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The ordinary linear-range, clean-workspace and complete-file verification\nrequirements apply. Todo JSON and aggregate interactive input are limited to\n16 MiB, with at most 100,000 rows and 1 MiB per explicit/combined new message.\nRebase reports remain independently bounded at 16 MiB and now include \u003ccode>todo\u003c/code>,\nper-step \u003ccode>action\u003c/code>, fold \u003ccode>replaces\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_commits\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>dropped_commits\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>completed_commits\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>replayed_commits\u003c/code> count non-dropped source deltas, while\n\u003ccode>result_commits\u003c/code> counts the resulting chain after folds. The template is also\navailable in a normal rebase plan; a non-linear range has no todo template.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application verifies all inputs, materializes surviving commits and uses the\nexisting journal to install the final files, publish the branch and archive\nthe old head. Recover or abort an interrupted checkout with the documented\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase/apply-and-recover\">rebase recovery commands\u003c/a>. A failed or\ncancelled preparation can leave unreferenced valid objects for later GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is interactive \u003cstrong>linear\u003c/strong> rewriting. Merge-preserving rebase, editor\nlaunching, split/edit stops, automatic fixup matching and execution of test\ncommands are not implemented by these actions.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Editable and interactive native rebase # Native linear rebase supports a complete reviewed sequence, either from an editable JSON document or a terminal prompt. Both routes use the same C++ planner, conflict resolutions, text composition and recoverable checkout. text Copy syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD Keep the document outside tracked workspace files (for example, under the Store) so it does not obstruct checkout. The template contains exact head , base and onto IDs and one step per source commit. Move rows to change order; retain every source exactly once and use drop explicitly to remove one. Unknown/missing fields, duplicate JSON keys/sources, foreign commits, stale contexts and incomplete ranges are rejected before creating candidates. json Copy { \"version\" : 1 , \"type\" : \"native-rebase-todo\" , \"head\" : \"HEAD_ID\" , \"base\" : \"BASE_ID\" , \"onto\" : \"ONTO_ID\" , \"steps\" : [ { \"source\" : \"SECOND_ID\" , \"action\" : \"reword\" , \"message\" : \"New message\" } , { \"source\" : \"FIRST_ID\" , \"action\" : \"squash\" } , { \"source\" : \"THIRD_ID\" , \"action\" : \"fixup\" } , { \"source\" : \"FOURTH_ID\" , \"action\" : \"drop\" } ] } Replace the illustrative IDs with the 64 lowercase hexadecimal IDs emitted by the template. Messages may contain Unicode, newlines and empty text. Action Result pick Replay the source delta and preserve its message/timestamp. drop Replay nothing; retain its original history in the checkout archive. reword Replay the delta, keep its timestamp and use the required message . squash Replay the delta into the preceding picked result, retaining that result's timestamp/parent and appending the source message after two newlines. fixup Replay the delta into the preceding picked result, retaining its message, timestamp and parent. Only reword accepts a message . Squash/fixup require a preceding picked result, ignoring intervening drops. A fold replaces the previous virtual candidate rather than introducing an extra parent/commit. Provisional folded objects are not stored. Original source versions remain protected by the old head archive; an unpublished provisional snapshot is not a retained version. Each delta is still measured against the source commit's actual original parent , even after reordering or dropping earlier commits. Dropping a dependency can therefore cause a conflict. It does not silently apply the dependent source's complete snapshot. Existing --resolutions JSON_FILE and --text-merge can resolve those replay conflicts. Resolution contexts include the current virtual head, so changing earlier actions invalidates stale choices. A dropped source cannot have file resolutions. An explicit sequence is honored even when BASE equals ONTO. Dropping all rows moves the branch to ONTO through the normal checkout journal. Without a todo, the original same-base no-op and oldest-first behavior remain available. Terminal input # text Copy syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive The CLI prints source IDs and escaped message previews on stderr. Enter the complete desired sequence, one action and full revision ID per line: text Copy reword SECOND_ID New message squash FIRST_ID fixup THIRD_ID drop FOURTH_ID . A line containing . confirms the sequence; . alone selects the original pick sequence. EOF before confirmation fails without writing candidates or changing files/references. Ctrl+C requests cancellation. Commands are not executed as shell text. Action names remain English in both interface languages. Use a JSON document for multiline messages. --todo and --interactive are mutually exclusive; interactive prompts cannot be combined with JSON progress. rebase-plan --interactive reviews without applying. Plain result JSON stays on stdout, separate from the prompts. Redirected input uses UTF-8. Windows console input is read as UTF-16 and converted without changing the console's code page or interpreting message backslashes as path separators. Limits, recovery and scope # The ordinary linear-range, clean-workspace and complete-file verification requirements apply. Todo JSON and aggregate interactive input are limited to 16 MiB, with at most 100,000 rows and 1 MiB per explicit/combined new message. Rebase reports remain independently bounded at 16 MiB and now include todo , per-step action , fold replaces , result_commits and dropped_commits . completed_commits / replayed_commits count non-dropped source deltas, while result_commits counts the resulting chain after folds. The template is also available in a normal rebase plan; a non-linear range has no todo template. Application verifies all inputs, materializes surviving commits and uses the existing journal to install the final files, publish the branch and archive the old head. Recover or abort an interrupted checkout with the documented rebase recovery commands . A failed or cancelled preparation can leave unreferenced valid objects for later GC. This is interactive linear rewriting. Merge-preserving rebase, editor launching, split/edit stops, automatic fixup matching and execution of test commands are not implemented by these actions. syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD { \"version\": 1, \"type\": \"native-rebase-todo\", \"head\": \"HEAD_ID\", \"base\": \"BASE_ID\", \"onto\": \"ONTO_ID\", \"steps\": [ {\"source\": \"SECOND_ID\", \"action\": \"reword\", \"message\": \"New message\"}, {\"source\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"action\": \"squash\"}, {\"source\": \"THIRD_ID\", \"action\": \"fixup\"}, {\"source\": \"FOURTH_ID\", \"action\": \"drop\"} ] } syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive reword SECOND_ID New message squash FIRST_ID fixup THIRD_ID drop FOURTH_ID .","headings":[{"id":"editable-and-interactive-native-rebase","text":"Editable and interactive native rebase","level":1},{"id":"terminal-input","text":"Terminal input","level":2},{"id":"limits-recovery-and-scope","text":"Limits, recovery and scope","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-2-L13","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5"],"languages":["text","json"],"code":"syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"type\": \"native-rebase-todo\",\n  \"head\": \"HEAD_ID\",\n  \"base\": \"BASE_ID\",\n  \"onto\": \"ONTO_ID\",\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\"source\": \"SECOND_ID\", \"action\": \"reword\", \"message\": \"New message\"},\n    {\"source\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"action\": \"squash\"},\n    {\"source\": \"THIRD_ID\", \"action\": \"fixup\"},\n    {\"source\": \"FOURTH_ID\", \"action\": \"drop\"}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive\nreword SECOND_ID New message\nsquash FIRST_ID\nfixup THIRD_ID\ndrop FOURTH_ID\n.","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-rebase/apply-and-recover"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-rebase","title":"Native linear rebase","description":"Native linear rebase guide.","section":"Version control","order":310,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-linear-rebase\">Native linear rebase\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/native-linear-rebase\" aria-label=\"Link to Native linear rebase\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native rebase planning replays the explicitly selected linear range \u003ccode>(BASE, HEAD]\u003c/code>\nonto another revision. \u003ccode>BASE\u003c/code> is excluded and must be reached by following the\nbranch&#39;s single-parent chain. There is no implicit merge-base selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase-todo\">editable or interactive sequence\u003c/a> can reorder, drop,\nreword, squash and fix up commits. The ordinary behavior below applies when no\nexplicit sequence is supplied.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The CLI accepts normal native selectors; C++ takes exact revision IDs. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>\nselects the target branch, defaulting to the current native branch. \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>\nselects its workspace, subject to existing workspace binding checks. The workspace\nmust have clean tracked files, no obstructing untracked/ignored paths, no staging\nindex and no pending checkout/switch recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"file-replay-and-topology\">File replay and topology\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/file-replay-and-topology\" aria-label=\"Link to File replay and topology\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Commits replay oldest first with the shared three-way file merger. Each original\nparent is the baseline, the previous virtual result is current, and the original\ncommit is incoming. Independent file/content/mode changes combine. Content,\nadd/delete and portable namespace conflicts stop planning at the first conflicted\ncommit. No later commit is treated as applied. Optional text composition is\ndescribed below; rename detection remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A merge inside the selected range is a \u003ccode>merge_in_range\u003c/code> blocker; a merge at the\nexcluded boundary is allowed. Reaching a root before BASE gives\n\u003ccode>base_not_on_linear_history\u003c/code>. Rebase never guesses a different range or silently\nflattens merge parents. The default limit is 1,000 selected commits, configurable\nwith \u003ccode>--max-commits\u003c/code> in 1..100,000. Exceeding it fails the bounded traversal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With BASE equal to ONTO, a valid range is already up to date and preserves exact\ncommit identities. An empty range (HEAD equals BASE) proposes moving the branch\nto ONTO. Otherwise every original commit gets one new parent, its preceding\nvirtual result. Original messages are preserved. Legacy replay without an identity also preserves\noriginal timestamps; authored replay preserves the original author and records\nthe current committer with one frozen timestamp. Logically empty\nreplays are retained as commits instead of being silently dropped. Representation\nchanges alone are not content edits, matching cherry-pick&#39;s file-level policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-only-report-and-bounds\">Read-only report and bounds\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/read-only-report-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Link to Read-only report and bounds\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The JSON report includes \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>onto\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode\u003c/code>, ordered \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code>, replay\n\u003ccode>steps\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>completed_commits\u003c/code>, proposed \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, workspace preflight, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. A step contains original \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>, its virtual \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, proposed\n\u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>empty\u003c/code>, file changes and conflicts. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan\nexits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid options or missing required metadata produce\ndiagnostics. A blocked range or conflicted result has null target/workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Virtual candidate IDs use the ordinary revision object identity algorithm and\ncomplete candidate metadata validation. Planning does not store candidate objects\nor move any reference/file. A proposed ID may therefore not be inspectable in the\nStore. Each candidate preserves the source message and original author and uses the\ncomputed file map and single new parent. With an identity configured,\n\u003ccode>--committer-time SECONDS\u003c/code> fixes the committer clock; the report exposes\n\u003ccode>committer_timestamp\u003c/code>. Separate invocations without that option can choose\ndifferent clocks and IDs. Fixed inputs, identity, signing key and clock give\nthe same IDs. Without an identity, legacy source timestamps are retained. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally audits complete original-head/base/onto closures\nand reconstructed file hashes. Metadata-only planning does not audit block payloads.\nText-enabled plans also reconstruct the eligible conflicted files, even without\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. Each step returns its empty \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> and accepted \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> rows;\nthe report&#39;s top-level context is a template for the resolution series below.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reports and each candidate&#39;s flat revision metadata are limited to 16 MiB. The\nplanner retains a bounded ID list/report and the current decoded snapshots, rather\nthan every candidate&#39;s complete file map. It may read revision metadata more than\nonce. A large number of per-step changes can reach the report limit before the\ncommit-count limit. This is not a streaming tree representation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>max_commits \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">500\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                              settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Planning supports English/French progress/errors and cooperative cancellation;\nJSON fields/reason codes remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store.\nThe planning API is exposed through C++ and CLI; C/Go native history bindings,\nmerge preservation remain future work. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase-todo\">Interactive linear rebase\u003c/a> is available.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-recover\">Apply and recover\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/apply-and-recover\" aria-label=\"Link to Apply and recover\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Apply recomputes and verifies the entire ready plan before storing candidates.\nIt then repeats the deterministic calculation, storing complete new revisions\noldest first, and checks that the final ID matches the verified preview. It does\nnot retain every candidate&#39;s full snapshot in memory. Each candidate remains an\nordinary immutable v1 or authored v2 revision. Empty commits follow the same\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship/rewrites\">author/committer rules\u003c/a>. Recovery uses frozen\ncandidates and does not need a signing private key.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only the final candidate is applied to the workspace through the existing\nrecoverable checkout journal. Intermediate steps never move the branch or working\nfiles. The old target tip is retained in \u003ccode>archive/checkout/TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/code>, while\nthe final new chain retains ONTO and every replayed candidate. Original intermediate\nrevisions and their file blocks therefore remain reconstructible after GC.\nUse \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-archives\">the archive browser\u003c/a> with \u003ccode>--kind checkout\u003c/code> to locate them.\nSource branches/tags and symbolic HEAD selection do not move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> is an exact old tip ID; otherwise the CLI captures it under the Store\nlock. The C++ API requires it explicitly. Up-to-date application returns\n\u003ccode>state: unchanged\u003c/code> without writes, but still verifies stored data and workspace\npreflight. An empty range checks out the exact ONTO revision without new commits.\nConflicts, staging, dirty tracked files or an incomplete history prevent application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation before journal publication preserves branch/workspace and can leave\ncomplete unreferenced candidates. After publication, inspect and explicitly\nresume or abort the checkout journal. Abort is allowed before branch publication;\nafterwards recovery only completes cleanup. Opening the Store never applies\nrecovery automatically. Active journals block GC and new history mutations.\nPrepared files/backups are plaintext even with encrypted object storage; the\nexisting \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">checkout durability/data limits\u003c/a> apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The direct result adds \u003ccode>operation: rebase\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>onto\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replayed_commits\u003c/code> to the checkout receipt. Recovered journals report\ntheir generic checkout identity and recorded target. Revision/journal formats\nare unchanged. Retention roots have no automatic expiry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                       old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"resolve-conflicts-by-step\">Resolve conflicts by step\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/resolve-conflicts-by-step\" aria-label=\"Link to Resolve conflicts by step\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Both commands accept \u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code>. Copy the plan&#39;s top-level\n\u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> into that file, then append the conflicted step&#39;s\n\u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> to its \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code> array and fill that entry&#39;s \u003ccode>files\u003c/code> choices.\nReplan with the same BASE/ONTO arguments. Keep earlier choices when adding a\nlater step&#39;s context; the top-level template always starts with an empty array.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The wrapper has exactly \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, original branch \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, excluded \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>onto\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code>. Each step uses the shared \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">file resolution schema\u003c/a>\nwith \u003ccode>operation: rebase\u003c/code>, original commit \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, its original parent as both\n\u003ccode>base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>, and its current virtual parent as \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>. For the first step,\nthat virtual head is ONTO; later heads are the preceding candidate IDs. Use the\nreturned contexts, including every exact ID, rather than constructing them by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choices select \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>, or an imported custom\n\u003ccode>file\u003c/code> manifest and executable mode. They apply to complete entries and are\nlimited to the original step&#39;s conflicts. Namespace collisions are checked again\nafter choices. A partial series remains blocked and writes no candidates or\nworking files. Rebase retains a now-empty step as a commit with its original\nmessage/date. Custom payloads are audited with \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> and always before apply.\nPin imported manifests if GC may run before a completed application retains them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Changing the original head, BASE or ONTO rejects the wrapper. Changing an earlier\nchoice changes subsequent virtual IDs, so later stale contexts are rejected too.\nSources outside the selected range, duplicate source steps, duplicate JSON keys,\nunknown fields and malformed entry choices are errors. Choices for later steps\nare validated when replay reaches them; no conflicted prefix is published.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The whole document is bounded to 16 MiB, 100,000 steps and a total of 100,000 file\nchoices. Raw decoding additionally limits nesting to 64 and total object fields\nto 1,100,010. Existing single-operation decoders keep their own limits. The C++\nseries stores steps by original source ID:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeRebaseResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> step \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"steps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>steps\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>resolutions \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Optional bounded text composition\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-rebase/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Link to Optional bounded text composition\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code> attempts UTF-8 three-way composition of remaining content conflicts\nafter explicit file choices. It preserves exact line endings, missing final\nnewlines and independent executable changes. Binary files, overlapping edits,\nadd/delete conflicts and namespace collisions remain blocking. No conflict markers\nor partial history are published. Empty replayed commits remain in the chain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\">text merge limits\u003c/a> apply, including\n\u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code>. One batch\ncovers the \u003cstrong>whole range\u003c/strong>: at most 64 file attempts and 16 MiB of input plus clean\nresult bytes. Reading a previous virtual result consumes input budget again.\nLimits do not reset between commits. Budget exhaustion blocks the whole operation.\nBudget flags require \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The top-level \u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code> contains cumulative counters and limits. Each step&#39;s\n\u003ccode>text_merge.files\u003c/code> contains only that step&#39;s attempts, with statuses, reasons,\nconflict coordinates and proposed manifest metadata; its counters are cumulative.\nReports contain no merged plaintext. Explicit choices have priority and consume\nno automatic text attempt. Keep \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code>, budgets and earlier choices when\nusing later virtual-step resolution contexts, because changing a preceding result\nchanges the expected virtual parent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Planning keeps generated content in a bounded memory cache, so a later step can\nmerge an earlier result without storing it. Exact CDC manifest IDs and revision\nIDs remain reproducible. Apply first verifies the complete plan and workspace,\nthen materializes \u003cstrong>all\u003c/strong> generated manifests, including intermediate versions,\nbefore writing the candidate chain and starting recoverable checkout. Earlier\nsteps never publish if a later step conflicts. Cancellation before the journal\ncan leave complete unreferenced objects; the branch and workspace stay unchanged.\nThe new chain retains every intermediate result through GC, and the checkout\narchive retains the complete original history.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                     old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Native linear rebase # Native rebase planning replays the explicitly selected linear range (BASE, HEAD] onto another revision. BASE is excluded and must be reached by following the branch's single-parent chain. There is no implicit merge-base selection. An editable or interactive sequence can reorder, drop, reword, squash and fix up commits. The ordinary behavior below applies when no explicit sequence is supplied. text Copy syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500 The CLI accepts normal native selectors; C++ takes exact revision IDs. --branch selects the target branch, defaulting to the current native branch. --directory selects its workspace, subject to existing workspace binding checks. The workspace must have clean tracked files, no obstructing untracked/ignored paths, no staging index and no pending checkout/switch recovery. File replay and topology # Commits replay oldest first with the shared three-way file merger. Each original parent is the baseline, the previous virtual result is current, and the original commit is incoming. Independent file/content/mode changes combine. Content, add/delete and portable namespace conflicts stop planning at the first conflicted commit. No later commit is treated as applied. Optional text composition is described below; rename detection remains future work. A merge inside the selected range is a merge_in_range blocker; a merge at the excluded boundary is allowed. Reaching a root before BASE gives base_not_on_linear_history . Rebase never guesses a different range or silently flattens merge parents. The default limit is 1,000 selected commits, configurable with --max-commits in 1..100,000. Exceeding it fails the bounded traversal. With BASE equal to ONTO, a valid range is already up to date and preserves exact commit identities. An empty range (HEAD equals BASE) proposes moving the branch to ONTO. Otherwise every original commit gets one new parent, its preceding virtual result. Original messages are preserved. Legacy replay without an identity also preserves original timestamps; authored replay preserves the original author and records the current committer with one frozen timestamp. Logically empty replays are retained as commits instead of being silently dropped. Representation changes alone are not content edits, matching cherry-pick's file-level policy. Read-only report and bounds # The JSON report includes head , base , onto , mode , ordered commits , replay steps , completed_commits , proposed target , workspace preflight, ready and blockers . A step contains original source / parent , its virtual head , proposed target , empty , file changes and conflicts. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid options or missing required metadata produce diagnostics. A blocked range or conflicted result has null target/workspace. Virtual candidate IDs use the ordinary revision object identity algorithm and complete candidate metadata validation. Planning does not store candidate objects or move any reference/file. A proposed ID may therefore not be inspectable in the Store. Each candidate preserves the source message and original author and uses the computed file map and single new parent. With an identity configured, --committer-time SECONDS fixes the committer clock; the report exposes committer_timestamp . Separate invocations without that option can choose different clocks and IDs. Fixed inputs, identity, signing key and clock give the same IDs. Without an identity, legacy source timestamps are retained. --verify additionally audits complete original-head/base/onto closures and reconstructed file hashes. Metadata-only planning does not audit block payloads. Text-enabled plans also reconstruct the eligible conflicted files, even without --verify . Each step returns its empty resolution_context and accepted resolved rows; the report's top-level context is a template for the resolution series below. Reports and each candidate's flat revision metadata are limited to 16 MiB. The planner retains a bounded ID list/report and the current decoded snapshots, rather than every candidate's complete file map. It may read revision metadata more than once. A large number of per-step changes can reach the report limit before the commit-count limit. This is not a streaming tree representation. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeRebaseOptions settings ; settings . max_commits = 500 ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase ( project , \"feature\" , old_base , new_base , settings , true , operation ) ; Planning supports English/French progress/errors and cooperative cancellation; JSON fields/reason codes remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. The planning API is exposed through C++ and CLI; C/Go native history bindings, merge preservation remain future work. Interactive linear rebase is available. Apply and recover # text Copy syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Apply recomputes and verifies the entire ready plan before storing candidates. It then repeats the deterministic calculation, storing complete new revisions oldest first, and checks that the final ID matches the verified preview. It does not retain every candidate's full snapshot in memory. Each candidate remains an ordinary immutable v1 or authored v2 revision. Empty commits follow the same author/committer rules . Recovery uses frozen candidates and does not need a signing private key. Only the final candidate is applied to the workspace through the existing recoverable checkout journal. Intermediate steps never move the branch or working files. The old target tip is retained in archive/checkout/TRANSACTION_ID , while the final new chain retains ONTO and every replayed candidate. Original intermediate revisions and their file blocks therefore remain reconstructible after GC. Use the archive browser with --kind checkout to locate them. Source branches/tags and symbolic HEAD selection do not move. --expect is an exact old tip ID; otherwise the CLI captures it under the Store lock. The C++ API requires it explicitly. Up-to-date application returns state: unchanged without writes, but still verifies stored data and workspace preflight. An empty range checks out the exact ONTO revision without new commits. Conflicts, staging, dirty tracked files or an incomplete history prevent application. Cancellation before journal publication preserves branch/workspace and can leave complete unreferenced candidates. After publication, inspect and explicitly resume or abort the checkout journal. Abort is allowed before branch publication; afterwards recovery only completes cleanup. Opening the Store never applies recovery automatically. Active journals block GC and new history mutations. Prepared files/backups are plaintext even with encrypted object storage; the existing checkout durability/data limits apply. The direct result adds operation: rebase , mode , previous_revision , base , onto and replayed_commits to the checkout receipt. Recovered journals report their generic checkout identity and recorded target. Revision/journal formats are unchanged. Retention roots have no automatic expiry. cpp Copy auto applied = store . rebase_directory ( project , \"feature\" , expected_id , old_base , new_base , settings , operation ) ; Resolve conflicts by step # Both commands accept --resolutions JSON_FILE . Copy the plan's top-level resolution_context into that file, then append the conflicted step's resolution_context to its steps array and fill that entry's files choices. Replan with the same BASE/ONTO arguments. Keep earlier choices when adding a later step's context; the top-level template always starts with an empty array. text Copy syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID The wrapper has exactly version: 1 , original branch head , excluded base , onto and steps . Each step uses the shared file resolution schema with operation: rebase , original commit source , its original parent as both base and parent , and its current virtual parent as head . For the first step, that virtual head is ONTO; later heads are the preceding candidate IDs. Use the returned contexts, including every exact ID, rather than constructing them by hand. Choices select current , incoming , base , remove , or an imported custom file manifest and executable mode. They apply to complete entries and are limited to the original step's conflicts. Namespace collisions are checked again after choices. A partial series remains blocked and writes no candidates or working files. Rebase retains a now-empty step as a commit with its original message/date. Custom payloads are audited with --verify and always before apply. Pin imported manifests if GC may run before a completed application retains them. Changing the original head, BASE or ONTO rejects the wrapper. Changing an earlier choice changes subsequent virtual IDs, so later stale contexts are rejected too. Sources outside the selected range, duplicate source steps, duplicate JSON keys, unknown fields and malformed entry choices are errors. Choices for later steps are validated when replay reaches them; no conflicted prefix is published. The whole document is bounded to 16 MiB, 100,000 steps and a total of 100,000 file choices. Raw decoding additionally limits nesting to 64 and total object fields to 1,100,010. Existing single-operation decoders keep their own limits. The C++ series stores steps by original source ID: cpp Copy auto choices = syrnck :: NativeRebaseResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; auto step = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"steps\" ] [ 0 ] [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; step . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; choices . steps [ step . source ] = step ; settings . resolutions = choices ; Optional bounded text composition # text Copy syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID --text-merge attempts UTF-8 three-way composition of remaining content conflicts after explicit file choices. It preserves exact line endings, missing final newlines and independent executable changes. Binary files, overlapping edits, add/delete conflicts and namespace collisions remain blocking. No conflict markers or partial history are published. Empty replayed commits remain in the chain. The text merge limits apply, including --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes . One batch covers the whole range : at most 64 file attempts and 16 MiB of input plus clean result bytes. Reading a previous virtual result consumes input budget again. Limits do not reset between commits. Budget exhaustion blocks the whole operation. Budget flags require --text-merge . The top-level text_merge contains cumulative counters and limits. Each step's text_merge.files contains only that step's attempts, with statuses, reasons, conflict coordinates and proposed manifest metadata; its counters are cumulative. Reports contain no merged plaintext. Explicit choices have priority and consume no automatic text attempt. Keep --text-merge , budgets and earlier choices when using later virtual-step resolution contexts, because changing a preceding result changes the expected virtual parent. Planning keeps generated content in a bounded memory cache, so a later step can merge an earlier result without storing it. Exact CDC manifest IDs and revision IDs remain reproducible. Apply first verifies the complete plan and workspace, then materializes all generated manifests, including intermediate versions, before writing the candidate chain and starting recoverable checkout. Earlier steps never publish if a later step conflicts. Cancellation before the journal can leave complete unreferenced objects; the branch and workspace stay unchanged. The new chain retains every intermediate result through GC, and the checkout archive retains the complete original history. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeRebaseOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase ( project , \"feature\" , old_base , new_base , settings , true ) ; auto result = store . rebase_directory ( project , \"feature\" , expected_head , old_base , new_base , settings ) ; syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500 syrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings; settings.max_commits = 500; auto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true, operation); syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort auto applied = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_id, old_base, new_base, settings, operation); syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID auto choices = syrnck::NativeRebaseResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); auto step = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"steps\"][0][\"resolution_context\"]); step.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; choices.steps[step.source] = step; settings.resolutions = choices; syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); auto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true); auto result = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_head, old_base, new_base, settings);","headings":[{"id":"native-linear-rebase","text":"Native linear rebase","level":1},{"id":"file-replay-and-topology","text":"File replay and topology","level":2},{"id":"read-only-report-and-bounds","text":"Read-only report and bounds","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-recover","text":"Apply and recover","level":2},{"id":"resolve-conflicts-by-step","text":"Resolve conflicts by step","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Optional bounded text composition","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500\nsyrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.max_commits = 500;\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base,\n                              settings, true, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nauto applied = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_id,\n                                       old_base, new_base, settings, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeRebaseResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nauto step = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"steps\"][0][\"resolution_context\"]);\nstep.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nchoices.steps[step.source] = step;\nsettings.resolutions = choices;\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace();\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true);\nauto result = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_head,\n                                     old_base, new_base, settings);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-rebase-todo","#/en/native-rebase-todo","#/en/native-authorship/rewrites","#/en/native-archives","#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-replay","title":"Native cherry-pick and revert","description":"Native cherry-pick and revert guide.","section":"Version control","order":330,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-cherry-pick-and-revert\">Native cherry-pick and revert\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-replay/native-cherry-pick-and-revert\" aria-label=\"Link to Native cherry-pick and revert\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The replay planner applies a selected commit&#39;s file-level delta to a branch\nsnapshot. Cherry-pick applies parent -&gt; source; revert applies source -&gt; parent.\nThe target branch can contain later independent changes. All planning is read-only.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--mainline N\u003c/code> selects a 1-based parent. Merge revisions require it explicitly;\nan ordinary commit defaults to parent 1. A root revision uses an empty tree and\ndoes not accept a mainline option. The existing native selectors work in the CLI;\nthe C++ API takes exact IDs. A plan prints JSON and exits 0 when ready, or 1 with\nthe same JSON when empty, conflicted or blocked. Invalid arguments and damaged\nrequired metadata instead produce diagnostics on stderr.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-way-decisions\">Three-way decisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-replay/three-way-decisions\" aria-label=\"Link to Three-way decisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The planner compares the delta&#39;s baseline, current branch and incoming snapshot\nby complete logical file hash, size and executable metadata. Rechunking the same\nbytes is not a content edit. Identical changes or an unchanged incoming file\npreserve the current manifest. A change on only one side is selected. Independent\ncontent and executable-mode changes on the same existing file can combine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Different concurrent file contents produce \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> conflicts. Other reasons\nare \u003ccode>add_add\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>delete_modify\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>modify_delete\u003c/code>. Conflicting text or binary\ncontent can be resolved with \u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code>; see the shared\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">resolution schema and workflow\u003c/a>. Bounded text composition\ncan be enabled in the options-based planner described below. Rename detection\nremains future work. Independent additions are also\nchecked together for portable \u003ccode>case_collision\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>file_directory_collision\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The report contains the operation, branch, expected \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, selected \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>mainline\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>empty\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_files\u003c/code>, proposed\n\u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code>, accepted \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> choices and \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code>.\nChanged entries include complete manifest/hash/size/mode\nsummaries. Conflict entries show baseline, current and incoming summaries; path\ncollision entries identify the other path. Unresolved conflicts prevent application of the\nentire result, including otherwise compatible files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When snapshot conflicts are absent, \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> contains the ordinary\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">checkout preflight\u003c/a>. It checks all tracked working files,\nignored/untracked obstructions, protected paths, links, portable collisions,\nstaging and pending checkout state. Its \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> is null because the candidate\nrevision has not been written. A clean result with no logical changes has\n\u003ccode>empty: true\u003c/code> and is not ready. With snapshot conflicts, \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> is null;\nno partial candidate is treated as a valid checkout destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally verifies both input histories&#39; complete closures and\nreconstructed file hashes. Without it, the planner reads revision/manifest\nmetadata and hashes working files, but does not verify incoming/custom block payloads.\nThese read-only queries can still detect missing required metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The compact serialized report is limited to 16 MiB. Candidate file metadata also\nhas a conservative limit leaving 4 KiB for the revision header/message. Memory\nscales with the three input snapshots, result and bounded report; this is not a\nstreaming tree format. User-supplied long messages remain subject to the ordinary\nrevision object limit when a result is later committed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                              syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> inverse \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> merge_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                                 syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>revert\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Callbacks must not reenter the Store. Progress and cooperative cancellation use\nthe existing English/French interface; report fields and conflict reason codes\nare language-independent. C ABI and Go replay bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Optional bounded text composition\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Link to Optional bounded text composition\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>plan_replay_with_options\u003c/code> accepts a \u003ccode>NativeReplayOptions\u003c/code> value containing\noptional \u003ccode>mainline\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resolutions\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code> settings. Existing replay\nentry points keep their defaults and remain source compatible. With no\n\u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code>, file-level behavior is unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Default per-file text limits.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Explicit file choices are applied first. Only remaining existing-file \u003ccode>content\u003c/code>\nconflicts are candidates for the \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-merge\">bounded text engine\u003c/a>.\nCherry-pick uses the selected parent as text base and source as incoming;\nrevert uses source as text base and the selected parent as incoming. Both use\nthe current branch content as the current side. This direction preserves later\nindependent changes when removing a selected commit&#39;s delta. Root, add/delete,\nnamespace, binary and overlapping cases keep their existing conflict rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code> report is null when disabled. When enabled it contains the\nsame limits, counters and per-path status/ranges/target metadata as native merge.\nEach attempted file reconstructs and hash-verifies all three inputs, even without\nfull-history verification. File-size and aggregate limits are checked before\npayload reads. At most 64 conflicts are attempted and verified inputs plus clean\nresults charge at most 16 MiB, in addition to the per-file text limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Clean results have exact predicted v1 manifest IDs and internal candidate bytes;\nplanning writes no objects or workspace files and reports no merged plaintext.\nExplicit all-current resolution can still be empty, which never becomes ready.\nOne unresolved file blocks the entire replay. Existing workspace, staging,\npending-journal, mainline and resolution-context checks still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>replay_directory_with_options\u003c/code> applies the same settings after checking the\nexact expected head. It recomputes the complete plan and verifies both input\nclosures before materializing any generated bytes. Empty, conflicted, staged or\nworkspace-blocked operations do not materialize text. Generated manifests must\nmatch their predicted IDs before the single-parent candidate is written.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">replay_directory_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>revert\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Undo selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">    settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The normal checkout journal retains the complete candidate, including generated\nmanifests, for resume or abort. Cancellation before journal creation may leave\nunreferenced complete objects but cannot move the branch or edit working files.\nOnce published, the resulting file&#39;s manifest directly retains all of its blocks;\nreconstructing it does not require the unrelated source&#39;s original history.\nRetain a source branch/tag/pin when that history should also survive GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The CLI exposes the same opt-in, including explicit resolution documents:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code> set\nper-file budgets and require \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code>. Mainline, resolutions, expected head,\nrequired message, progress and English/French diagnostics retain their normal\nmeaning. Use the same options for plan and application. Any remaining conflict,\nincluding a budget refusal, blocks all generated files in that replay.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-a-planned-change\">Apply a planned change\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-replay/apply-a-planned-change\" aria-label=\"Link to Apply a planned change\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>-m\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>--message\u003c/code> is required, as for ordinary native commits. Branch/directory\ndefaults and selectors match the planner. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>, when supplied, must be an\nexact lowercase 64-character old tip ID. Otherwise the CLI captures the selected\nbranch tip while holding the exclusive Store lock. The API always requires it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">replay_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                      syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Apply selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Apply recomputes the plan and always verifies both input closures and file hashes.\nIt rejects unresolved conflicts, empty results, pending checkout/switch recovery and any\nexisting staging index. The clean-tracked checkout policy applies to all current\nfiles and destination paths; there is no force mode. A new ordinary v1 revision\nuses the supplied message, current UTC timestamp and exactly one parent: the\nprevious target-branch tip. Cherry-pick does not add the source as a merge parent.\nMerge input needs the selected mainline; the result still has one parent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The engine then applies this immutable candidate through the existing recoverable\ncheckout journal. Working files change before the atomic branch/archive/receipt\npublication; the branch remains at the old tip until every required file is\nverified. The old tip, its history and file blocks remain reconstructible through\nthe new parent chain and checkout archive. Symbolic HEAD and the source&#39;s branch\nor tags do not move. To preserve an unrelated source&#39;s complete original history,\nkeep its own branch, tag or pin; replay imports a delta rather than its ancestry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The result uses the checkout receipt fields, including \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>state\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>operation\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>replayed_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_revision\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>mainline\u003c/code> added to the direct command response. A recovered journal reports its\ngeneric checkout identity and recorded target, without these response-only labels.\nThere is no revision-format or journal-format change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interruption-abort-and-retention\">Interruption, abort and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-replay/interruption-abort-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Interruption, abort and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Before a journal exists, cancellation/failure preserves the old branch and\nworkspace. Candidate preparation may leave an unreferenced immutable revision.\nAfter journal publication, interruption requires the existing explicit workflow:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\"># Or, before branch publication:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Resume finishes the recorded candidate without recomputing the original replay.\nAbort restores original working files while retaining the old branch. Once the\nbranch has published, recovery can only finish cleanup. Opening the Store does\nnot modify working files, and GC is blocked while a journal is active. Inspect\nthe resulting head before retrying a completed command: an old \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> then\nfails instead of creating another commit. Empty replays are rejected rather\nthan creating an empty commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Completed new revisions and their parent history survive destructive GC. An\naborted, unpublished candidate may be collected after its temporary pins are\nremoved. Checkout archives and receipts have no automatic expiry. Private\nprepared files and backups are plaintext even with encrypted object storage;\nsee \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">checkout recovery and data limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native cherry-pick and revert # The replay planner applies a selected commit's file-level delta to a branch snapshot. Cherry-pick applies parent -> source; revert applies source -> parent. The target branch can contain later independent changes. All planning is read-only. text Copy syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory . syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2 --mainline N selects a 1-based parent. Merge revisions require it explicitly; an ordinary commit defaults to parent 1. A root revision uses an empty tree and does not accept a mainline option. The existing native selectors work in the CLI; the C++ API takes exact IDs. A plan prints JSON and exits 0 when ready, or 1 with the same JSON when empty, conflicted or blocked. Invalid arguments and damaged required metadata instead produce diagnostics on stderr. Three-way decisions # The planner compares the delta's baseline, current branch and incoming snapshot by complete logical file hash, size and executable metadata. Rechunking the same bytes is not a content edit. Identical changes or an unchanged incoming file preserve the current manifest. A change on only one side is selected. Independent content and executable-mode changes on the same existing file can combine. Different concurrent file contents produce content conflicts. Other reasons are add_add , delete_modify and modify_delete . Conflicting text or binary content can be resolved with --resolutions JSON_FILE ; see the shared resolution schema and workflow . Bounded text composition can be enabled in the options-based planner described below. Rename detection remains future work. Independent additions are also checked together for portable case_collision and file_directory_collision . The report contains the operation, branch, expected head , selected source , parent / mainline , ready , empty , objects_verified , result_files , proposed changes , conflicts , accepted resolved choices and resolution_context . Changed entries include complete manifest/hash/size/mode summaries. Conflict entries show baseline, current and incoming summaries; path collision entries identify the other path. Unresolved conflicts prevent application of the entire result, including otherwise compatible files. When snapshot conflicts are absent, workspace contains the ordinary checkout preflight . It checks all tracked working files, ignored/untracked obstructions, protected paths, links, portable collisions, staging and pending checkout state. Its target is null because the candidate revision has not been written. A clean result with no logical changes has empty: true and is not ready. With snapshot conflicts, workspace is null; no partial candidate is treated as a valid checkout destination. --verify additionally verifies both input histories' complete closures and reconstructed file hashes. Without it, the planner reads revision/manifest metadata and hashes working files, but does not verify incoming/custom block payloads. These read-only queries can still detect missing required metadata. The compact serialized report is limited to 16 MiB. Candidate file metadata also has a conservative limit leaving 4 KiB for the revision header/message. Memory scales with the three input snapshots, result and bounded report; this is not a streaming tree format. User-supplied long messages remain subject to the ordinary revision object limit when a result is later committed. cpp Copy auto plan = store . plan_replay ( project , \"main\" , selected_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , { } , true , operation ) ; auto inverse = store . plan_replay ( project , \"main\" , merge_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: revert , 2 , false , operation ) ; Callbacks must not reenter the Store. Progress and cooperative cancellation use the existing English/French interface; report fields and conflict reason codes are language-independent. C ABI and Go replay bindings remain future work. Optional bounded text composition # plan_replay_with_options accepts a NativeReplayOptions value containing optional mainline , resolutions and text_merge settings. Existing replay entry points keep their defaults and remain source compatible. With no text_merge , file-level behavior is unchanged. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeReplayOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; // Default per-file text limits. // settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent. auto plan = store . plan_replay_with_options ( project , \"main\" , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , settings , true , operation ) ; Explicit file choices are applied first. Only remaining existing-file content conflicts are candidates for the bounded text engine . Cherry-pick uses the selected parent as text base and source as incoming; revert uses source as text base and the selected parent as incoming. Both use the current branch content as the current side. This direction preserves later independent changes when removing a selected commit's delta. Root, add/delete, namespace, binary and overlapping cases keep their existing conflict rules. The text_merge report is null when disabled. When enabled it contains the same limits, counters and per-path status/ranges/target metadata as native merge. Each attempted file reconstructs and hash-verifies all three inputs, even without full-history verification. File-size and aggregate limits are checked before payload reads. At most 64 conflicts are attempted and verified inputs plus clean results charge at most 16 MiB, in addition to the per-file text limits. Clean results have exact predicted v1 manifest IDs and internal candidate bytes; planning writes no objects or workspace files and reports no merged plaintext. Explicit all-current resolution can still be empty, which never becomes ready. One unresolved file blocks the entire replay. Existing workspace, staging, pending-journal, mainline and resolution-context checks still apply. replay_directory_with_options applies the same settings after checking the exact expected head. It recomputes the complete plan and verifies both input closures before materializing any generated bytes. Empty, conflicted, staged or workspace-blocked operations do not materialize text. Generated manifests must match their predicted IDs before the single-parent candidate is written. cpp Copy auto applied = store . replay_directory_with_options ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: revert , \"Undo selected changes\" , settings , operation ) ; The normal checkout journal retains the complete candidate, including generated manifests, for resume or abort. Cancellation before journal creation may leave unreferenced complete objects but cannot move the branch or edit working files. Once published, the resulting file's manifest directly retains all of its blocks; reconstructing it does not require the unrelated source's original history. Retain a source branch/tag/pin when that history should also survive GC. The CLI exposes the same opt-in, including explicit resolution documents: text Copy syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\" syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432 syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\" --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes set per-file budgets and require --text-merge . Mainline, resolutions, expected head, required message, progress and English/French diagnostics retain their normal meaning. Use the same options for plan and application. Any remaining conflict, including a budget refusal, blocks all generated files in that replay. Apply a planned change # text Copy syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\" syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\" -m / --message is required, as for ordinary native commits. Branch/directory defaults and selectors match the planner. --expect , when supplied, must be an exact lowercase 64-character old tip ID. Otherwise the CLI captures the selected branch tip while holding the exclusive Store lock. The API always requires it: cpp Copy auto applied = store . replay_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , \"Apply selected changes\" , { } , operation ) ; Apply recomputes the plan and always verifies both input closures and file hashes. It rejects unresolved conflicts, empty results, pending checkout/switch recovery and any existing staging index. The clean-tracked checkout policy applies to all current files and destination paths; there is no force mode. A new ordinary v1 revision uses the supplied message, current UTC timestamp and exactly one parent: the previous target-branch tip. Cherry-pick does not add the source as a merge parent. Merge input needs the selected mainline; the result still has one parent. The engine then applies this immutable candidate through the existing recoverable checkout journal. Working files change before the atomic branch/archive/receipt publication; the branch remains at the old tip until every required file is verified. The old tip, its history and file blocks remain reconstructible through the new parent chain and checkout archive. Symbolic HEAD and the source's branch or tags do not move. To preserve an unrelated source's complete original history, keep its own branch, tag or pin; replay imports a delta rather than its ancestry. The result uses the checkout receipt fields, including target , state and archive , with operation , replayed_revision , previous_revision and mainline added to the direct command response. A recovered journal reports its generic checkout identity and recorded target, without these response-only labels. There is no revision-format or journal-format change. Interruption, abort and retention # Before a journal exists, cancellation/failure preserves the old branch and workspace. Candidate preparation may leave an unreferenced immutable revision. After journal publication, interruption requires the existing explicit workflow: text Copy syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover # Or, before branch publication: syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Resume finishes the recorded candidate without recomputing the original replay. Abort restores original working files while retaining the old branch. Once the branch has published, recovery can only finish cleanup. Opening the Store does not modify working files, and GC is blocked while a journal is active. Inspect the resulting head before retrying a completed command: an old --expect then fails instead of creating another commit. Empty replays are rejected rather than creating an empty commit. Completed new revisions and their parent history survive destructive GC. An aborted, unpublished candidate may be collected after its temporary pins are removed. Checkout archives and receipts have no automatic expiry. Private prepared files and backups are plaintext even with encrypted object storage; see checkout recovery and data limits . syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory . syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2 auto plan = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", selected_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, {}, true, operation); auto inverse = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", merge_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, 2, false, operation); syrnck::NativeReplayOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default per-file text limits. // settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent. auto plan = store.plan_replay_with_options(project, \"main\", source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, settings, true, operation); auto applied = store.replay_directory_with_options(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, \"Undo selected changes\", settings, operation); syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\" syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432 syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\" syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\" syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\" auto applied = store.replay_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, \"Apply selected changes\", {}, operation); syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover # Or, before branch publication: syrnck native checkout-recover --abort","headings":[{"id":"native-cherry-pick-and-revert","text":"Native cherry-pick and revert","level":1},{"id":"three-way-decisions","text":"Three-way decisions","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Optional bounded text composition","level":2},{"id":"apply-a-planned-change","text":"Apply a planned change","level":2},{"id":"interruption-abort-and-retention","text":"Interruption, abort and retention","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify\nsyrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory .\nsyrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2\nauto plan = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", selected_id,\n                              syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, {}, true, operation);\nauto inverse = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", merge_id,\n                                 syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, 2, false, operation);\nsyrnck::NativeReplayOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default per-file text limits.\n// settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent.\nauto plan = store.plan_replay_with_options(project, \"main\", source_id,\n    syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, settings, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.replay_directory_with_options(project, \"main\", expected_id,\n    source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, \"Undo selected changes\",\n    settings, operation);\nsyrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\"\nsyrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432\nsyrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\"\nsyrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\"\nsyrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\"\nauto applied = store.replay_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick,\n                                      \"Apply selected changes\", {}, operation);\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\n# Or, before branch publication:\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/text-merge","#/en/native-checkout"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-reset","title":"Native branch reset","description":"Native branch reset guide.","section":"Version control","order":340,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-branch-reset\">Native branch reset\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-reset/native-branch-reset\" aria-label=\"Link to Native branch reset\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Branch reset moves an existing branch to a selected revision while leaving every\nworking file untouched. It archives the old tip and any previous staging snapshot\nin the same atomic reference update. Ordinary \u003ccode>native reset [PATH...]\u003c/code> continues\nto unstage files; moving a branch requires an explicit mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Branch\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Prepared snapshot\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Working files\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--soft\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Selected target\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Preserve file entries and rebase the staging baseline onto the target\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Untouched\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--mixed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Selected target\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Archive and clear the index\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Untouched\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Without an explicit index, soft reset freezes the old tip&#39;s complete file entries\ninto a staging snapshot based on the new target. \u003ccode>native commit --staged\u003c/code> can then\nrecommit those bytes as a child of the target. Existing prepared bytes are retained,\nincluding changes different from the old tip and later working files. A stale index\nblocks soft reset; mixed reset explicitly archives and clears it. An index whose\nidentity/metadata is invalid is rejected in both modes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The target can be any valid native revision; ancestry is not required. The CLI\naccepts the usual native selectors. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> overrides the selected branch\nwithout changing symbolic HEAD. API methods require exact IDs and an existing\nbranch. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> guards the exact old branch tip; if omitted, the CLI captures it\nunder the exclusive Store lock. Staging is captured at invocation and checked in\nthe same publication, rather than frozen by an earlier plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plans emit JSON and exit 0 when ready, 1 with JSON for blockers. They read metadata\nonly; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits complete head/target/index closures and reconstructed file\nhashes. The soft plan includes the proposed staged comparison against the target.\nReports and prepared revision metadata retain the existing 16 MiB limits.\nMemory grows with decoded flat snapshots and the bounded staged comparison.\nFilesystem working contents are neither inspected\nnor validated, so dirty/untracked files remain available for subsequent status.\nPending checkout/switch recovery, conflicting archive roots or a stale soft\nindex prevent publication. A reset to the same tip is a no-op, except mixed reset\nstill archives and clears an existing index.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-interruption\">Retention and interruption\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-reset/retention-and-interruption\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention and interruption\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The previous tip remains rooted at \u003ccode>archive/reset/OLD_ID\u003c/code>. Any former index remains\nrooted at \u003ccode>archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>, including its baseline history. These are\nordinary revision roots; all their manifests and blocks survive destructive GC\nin plaintext/encrypted and loose/packed Stores. Tags and source branches do not\nmove. The operation verifies all old and new retained closures before publication;\na missing old block prevents even mixed reset from discarding its only root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The branch, index, history archive and index archive publish through one atomic\n\u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> replacement. Cancellation or observer failure before publication keeps\nthe old references. Soft reset preparation can leave an unreferenced immutable\nstaging revision. A killed publisher leaves the entire old or entire new root set;\ninspect \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code> before retrying with the old\nexpected tip. There is no working-file journal to resume because no files change.\nNo callbacks or cancellation checks run after publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Archives have no automatic expiry. Find retained versions using\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-archives\">the archive browser\u003c/a>, then export the old ID or create a recovery branch:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Generic reference tools can remove these roots; removing the last root permits GC\nto collect exclusive data. Working edits never ingested by commit/add are not\nstored by reset. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset\">Recoverable hard reset\u003c/a> also captures dirty working bytes. Automatic restoration of a former branch/index pair remains separate work. Native revision v1 is unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_reset_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResetMode\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>soft\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                                 syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResetMode\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>soft\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>C++ and CLI expose these operations; native history C/Go bindings remain future\nwork. Progress/errors support English and French; JSON field/reason names remain\nEnglish. Callbacks must not reenter the Store.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native branch reset # Branch reset moves an existing branch to a selected revision while leaving every working file untouched. It archives the old tip and any previous staging snapshot in the same atomic reference update. Ordinary native reset [PATH...] continues to unstage files; moving a branch requires an explicit mode. text Copy syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID Mode Branch Prepared snapshot Working files --soft Selected target Preserve file entries and rebase the staging baseline onto the target Untouched --mixed Selected target Archive and clear the index Untouched Without an explicit index, soft reset freezes the old tip's complete file entries into a staging snapshot based on the new target. native commit --staged can then recommit those bytes as a child of the target. Existing prepared bytes are retained, including changes different from the old tip and later working files. A stale index blocks soft reset; mixed reset explicitly archives and clears it. An index whose identity/metadata is invalid is rejected in both modes. The target can be any valid native revision; ancestry is not required. The CLI accepts the usual native selectors. --branch overrides the selected branch without changing symbolic HEAD. API methods require exact IDs and an existing branch. --expect guards the exact old branch tip; if omitted, the CLI captures it under the exclusive Store lock. Staging is captured at invocation and checked in the same publication, rather than frozen by an earlier plan. Plans emit JSON and exit 0 when ready, 1 with JSON for blockers. They read metadata only; --verify audits complete head/target/index closures and reconstructed file hashes. The soft plan includes the proposed staged comparison against the target. Reports and prepared revision metadata retain the existing 16 MiB limits. Memory grows with decoded flat snapshots and the bounded staged comparison. Filesystem working contents are neither inspected nor validated, so dirty/untracked files remain available for subsequent status. Pending checkout/switch recovery, conflicting archive roots or a stale soft index prevent publication. A reset to the same tip is a no-op, except mixed reset still archives and clears an existing index. Retention and interruption # The previous tip remains rooted at archive/reset/OLD_ID . Any former index remains rooted at archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID , including its baseline history. These are ordinary revision roots; all their manifests and blocks survive destructive GC in plaintext/encrypted and loose/packed Stores. Tags and source branches do not move. The operation verifies all old and new retained closures before publication; a missing old block prevents even mixed reset from discarding its only root. The branch, index, history archive and index archive publish through one atomic refs.json replacement. Cancellation or observer failure before publication keeps the old references. Soft reset preparation can leave an unreferenced immutable staging revision. A killed publisher leaves the entire old or entire new root set; inspect refs , native head and native staged before retrying with the old expected tip. There is no working-file journal to resume because no files change. No callbacks or cancellation checks run after publication. Archives have no automatic expiry. Find retained versions using the archive browser , then export the old ID or create a recovery branch: text Copy syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files Generic reference tools can remove these roots; removing the last root permits GC to collect exclusive data. Working edits never ingested by commit/add are not stored by reset. Recoverable hard reset also captures dirty working bytes. Automatic restoration of a former branch/index pair remains separate work. Native revision v1 is unchanged. cpp Copy auto plan = store . plan_reset_branch ( \"main\" , target_id , syrnck :: NativeResetMode :: soft , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . reset_branch ( \"main\" , expected_id , target_id , syrnck :: NativeResetMode :: soft , operation ) ; C++ and CLI expose these operations; native history C/Go bindings remain future work. Progress/errors support English and French; JSON field/reason names remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files auto plan = store.plan_reset_branch(\"main\", target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, true, operation); auto result = store.reset_branch(\"main\", expected_id, target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-branch-reset","text":"Native branch reset","level":1},{"id":"retention-and-interruption","text":"Retention and interruption","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify\nsyrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\nsyrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files\nauto plan = store.plan_reset_branch(\"main\", target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft,\n                                    true, operation);\nauto result = store.reset_branch(\"main\", expected_id, target_id,\n                                 syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-archives","#/en/native-hard-reset"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-resolutions","title":"Native conflict resolutions","description":"Native conflict resolutions guide.","section":"Version control","order":350,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-conflict-resolutions\">Native conflict resolutions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-resolutions/native-conflict-resolutions\" aria-label=\"Link to Native conflict resolutions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native merge, cherry-pick, revert, rebase and staging rebase share explicit file-level conflict choices.\nTheir plan commands remain read-only. Supplying choices does not bypass topology,\nobject verification, clean tracked files or checkout recovery requirements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-and-apply\">Prepare and apply\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-resolutions/prepare-and-apply\" aria-label=\"Link to Prepare and apply\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Copy the first plan&#39;s \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> object into \u003ccode>choices.json\u003c/code>, then fill\nits \u003ccode>files\u003c/code> map with the desired choices. Preserve every other field. A blocked\nplan exits 1 with its JSON on stdout. Merge topology blockers must be resolved\nfirst; their \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> is null. Keep the same base/mainline options\nwhen previewing and applying the choices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The same \u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code> option works with \u003ccode>cherry-pick-plan\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>cherry-pick\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revert-plan\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>revert\u003c/code>. The application commands recompute\nthe plan and verify all input histories and custom file payloads before applying\nthe complete result through the existing recoverable checkout journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The document binds the operation, exact target \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, exact \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, three-way\n\u003ccode>base\u003c/code> and selected replay \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>. Merge has a null \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>; cherry-pick&#39;s\nbase is its selected parent; revert&#39;s base is the source revision and its incoming\nsnapshot is the selected parent. Root replay has a null parent. Changing the\ntarget, source, merge base or selected replay parent rejects the document before\npublication. In particular, changing a merge revert&#39;s \u003ccode>--mainline\u003c/code> cannot reuse\nchoices made for another parent. Branch/tag selectors are resolved to exact IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Example document, with illustrative IDs to replace using the actual plan:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"operation\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"merge\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"head\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"parent\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token null keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"incoming\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"Content/Obsolete.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"remove\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"choices\">Choices\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-resolutions/choices\" aria-label=\"Link to Choices\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Choice\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Result for the path\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>current\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete current branch entry, including its manifest and executable mode\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete incoming entry; for revert this is the selected parent&#39;s version\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>base\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete baseline entry used by the three-way decision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Delete the path from the result\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>file\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use an existing Store manifest and the explicitly supplied executable mode\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Selecting an absent side deletes the path. Non-\u003ccode>file\u003c/code> entries contain only\n\u003ccode>choice\u003c/code>. A custom entry has exactly these three fields:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"file\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"manifest\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"executable\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>To combine content manually, save the resolved file outside the tracked workspace,\nimport it with \u003ccode>syrnck put PATH\u003c/code>, then use the returned \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code> ID in this\nentry. CLI \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> retains its manifest under \u003ccode>files/ID\u003c/code>. Low-level \u003ccode>Store::ingest\u003c/code> does not publish a pin: keep a reference when importing through that API if maintenance may run before application. Completed\ncommits retain their selected manifests and blocks through their normal history\nand archive roots, including encrypted and packed Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For compatible edits on distinct text lines, \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\">the bounded text merge preview\u003c/a>\ncan produce a reviewed custom file from three explicit snapshots. It does not\nautomatically change a native operation&#39;s file-level conflict decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only paths involved in the original unresolved plan&#39;s file conflicts or either\nside of its namespace collisions can have choices. Overrides of clean paths are rejected.\nThe engine reapplies portable case/prefix collision checks after all choices.\nAn explicit choice can therefore still leave a namespace or workspace blocker.\nExisting checkout limitations on case renames and file/directory transitions\ncontinue to apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Partial resolutions keep the whole operation blocked; compatible files are not\npublished separately. The report&#39;s \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> rows show accepted choices and\ntheir resulting entry, while \u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code> contains remaining conflicts. An\nall-current replay with no remaining logical delta is empty and cannot create\nan empty commit. A divergent merge can record its two parents with unchanged\nfile contents.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-recovery\">Validation and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-resolutions/validation-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Validation and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Without \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>, the plan reads custom manifest metadata but does not audit\nits block payloads. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> and application check the complete custom file,\nincluding its reconstructed hash. Missing or damaged selected data prevents\npublication. Plan callbacks/cancellation have the existing read-only contracts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Editable JSON is limited to 16 MiB, 100,000 paths, depth 64 and 400,010 total\nobject fields. The root contains exactly the seven documented fields. Unknown\nfields, duplicate keys, invalid paths/IDs and incorrectly typed choices are\nrejected. The C++ typed API applies the same schema and conservative size budget;\nuse \u003ccode>decode\u003c/code> for untrusted editable JSON to detect duplicate keys before a DOM\ncan discard them. IDs are lowercase 64-character hexadecimal values.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Resolution documents do not create persistent conflict state, modify tracked\nfiles or retain their input objects. Keep the necessary branch/tag/pin roots\nuntil application. Native \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\">merge\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition\">cherry-pick/revert\u003c/a> can\nexplicitly enable bounded text composition after user choices. Bounded text composition is also available explicitly in \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase\">rebase\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase\">staging rebase\u003c/a>. Rename integration and an interactive conflict editor remain future work. Recovery after application starts uses\n\u003ccode>native checkout-status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>native checkout-recover [--abort]\u003c/code>; it finishes or\nrolls back the recorded candidate without needing the original JSON document.\nRebase accepts a wrapper binding the original head, excluded base and onto revision,\nwith per-step contexts in its \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code> array; see the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-rebase/resolve-conflicts-by-step\">rebase resolution workflow\u003c/a>.\nStaging rebase uses a single context with \u003ccode>operation: rebase-index\u003c/code>, the frozen\nindex as \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, and its recorded baseline as both \u003ccode>base\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>; see\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/resolve-index-conflicts\">index conflict resolution\u003c/a>.\nRevision and checkout-journal formats are unchanged. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/apply-and-recover\">merge recovery\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay/interruption-abort-and-retention\">replay retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>resolutions \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L7\" data-line=\"7\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Resolve asset merge\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Replay uses \u003ccode>plan_replay_resolved\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>replay_directory_resolved\u003c/code> with a context\nobtained from the corresponding replay plan; existing replay API signatures\nremain available. These features are exposed through C++ and CLI; C ABI and Go\nnative history bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native conflict resolutions # Native merge, cherry-pick, revert, rebase and staging rebase share explicit file-level conflict choices. Their plan commands remain read-only. Supplying choices does not bypass topology, object verification, clean tracked files or checkout recovery requirements. Prepare and apply # text Copy syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID Copy the first plan's resolution_context object into choices.json , then fill its files map with the desired choices. Preserve every other field. A blocked plan exits 1 with its JSON on stdout. Merge topology blockers must be resolved first; their resolution_context is null. Keep the same base/mainline options when previewing and applying the choices. The same --resolutions JSON_FILE option works with cherry-pick-plan , cherry-pick , revert-plan and revert . The application commands recompute the plan and verify all input histories and custom file payloads before applying the complete result through the existing recoverable checkout journal. The document binds the operation, exact target head , exact source , three-way base and selected replay parent . Merge has a null parent ; cherry-pick's base is its selected parent; revert's base is the source revision and its incoming snapshot is the selected parent. Root replay has a null parent. Changing the target, source, merge base or selected replay parent rejects the document before publication. In particular, changing a merge revert's --mainline cannot reuse choices made for another parent. Branch/tag selectors are resolved to exact IDs. Example document, with illustrative IDs to replace using the actual plan: json Copy { \"version\" : 1 , \"operation\" : \"merge\" , \"head\" : \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\" , \"source\" : \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\" , \"base\" : \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\" , \"parent\" : null , \"files\" : { \"Content/Map.uasset\" : { \"choice\" : \"incoming\" } , \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\" : { \"choice\" : \"remove\" } } } Choices # Choice Result for the path current Complete current branch entry, including its manifest and executable mode incoming Complete incoming entry; for revert this is the selected parent's version base Complete baseline entry used by the three-way decision remove Delete the path from the result file Use an existing Store manifest and the explicitly supplied executable mode Selecting an absent side deletes the path. Non- file entries contain only choice . A custom entry has exactly these three fields: json Copy { \"choice\" : \"file\" , \"manifest\" : \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\" , \"executable\" : false } To combine content manually, save the resolved file outside the tracked workspace, import it with syrnck put PATH , then use the returned manifest ID in this entry. CLI put retains its manifest under files/ID . Low-level Store::ingest does not publish a pin: keep a reference when importing through that API if maintenance may run before application. Completed commits retain their selected manifests and blocks through their normal history and archive roots, including encrypted and packed Stores. For compatible edits on distinct text lines, the bounded text merge preview can produce a reviewed custom file from three explicit snapshots. It does not automatically change a native operation's file-level conflict decisions. Only paths involved in the original unresolved plan's file conflicts or either side of its namespace collisions can have choices. Overrides of clean paths are rejected. The engine reapplies portable case/prefix collision checks after all choices. An explicit choice can therefore still leave a namespace or workspace blocker. Existing checkout limitations on case renames and file/directory transitions continue to apply. Partial resolutions keep the whole operation blocked; compatible files are not published separately. The report's resolved rows show accepted choices and their resulting entry, while conflicts contains remaining conflicts. An all-current replay with no remaining logical delta is empty and cannot create an empty commit. A divergent merge can record its two parents with unchanged file contents. Validation and recovery # Without --verify , the plan reads custom manifest metadata but does not audit its block payloads. --verify and application check the complete custom file, including its reconstructed hash. Missing or damaged selected data prevents publication. Plan callbacks/cancellation have the existing read-only contracts. Editable JSON is limited to 16 MiB, 100,000 paths, depth 64 and 400,010 total object fields. The root contains exactly the seven documented fields. Unknown fields, duplicate keys, invalid paths/IDs and incorrectly typed choices are rejected. The C++ typed API applies the same schema and conservative size budget; use decode for untrusted editable JSON to detect duplicate keys before a DOM can discard them. IDs are lowercase 64-character hexadecimal values. Resolution documents do not create persistent conflict state, modify tracked files or retain their input objects. Keep the necessary branch/tag/pin roots until application. Native merge and cherry-pick/revert can explicitly enable bounded text composition after user choices. Bounded text composition is also available explicitly in rebase and staging rebase . Rename integration and an interactive conflict editor remain future work. Recovery after application starts uses native checkout-status and native checkout-recover [--abort] ; it finishes or rolls back the recorded candidate without needing the original JSON document. Rebase accepts a wrapper binding the original head, excluded base and onto revision, with per-step contexts in its steps array; see the rebase resolution workflow . Staging rebase uses a single context with operation: rebase-index , the frozen index as source , and its recorded baseline as both base and parent ; see index conflict resolution . Revision and checkout-journal formats are unchanged. See merge recovery and replay retention . cpp Copy auto choices = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; choices . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . resolutions = choices ; auto preview = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; auto applied = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , \"Resolve asset merge\" , settings , operation ) ; Replay uses plan_replay_resolved and replay_directory_resolved with a context obtained from the corresponding replay plan; existing replay API signatures remain available. These features are exposed through C++ and CLI; C ABI and Go native history bindings remain future work. syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID { \"version\": 1, \"operation\": \"merge\", \"head\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\", \"source\": \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\", \"base\": \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\", \"parent\": null, \"files\": { \"Content/Map.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"incoming\"}, \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"remove\"} } } {\"choice\": \"file\", \"manifest\": \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\", \"executable\": false} auto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); choices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.resolutions = choices; auto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); auto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, \"Resolve asset merge\", settings, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-conflict-resolutions","text":"Native conflict resolutions","level":1},{"id":"prepare-and-apply","text":"Prepare and apply","level":2},{"id":"choices","text":"Choices","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-recovery","text":"Validation and recovery","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5","code-4-L6","code-4-L7"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"operation\": \"merge\",\n  \"head\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n  \"source\": \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\",\n  \"base\": \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"files\": {\n    \"Content/Map.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"incoming\"},\n    \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"remove\"}\n  }\n}\n{\"choice\": \"file\", \"manifest\": \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\", \"executable\": false}\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nchoices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.resolutions = choices;\nauto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      \"Resolve asset merge\", settings, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution","#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition","#/en/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition","#/en/native-rebase","#/en/native-stage-rebase","#/en/native-rebase/resolve-conflicts-by-step","#/en/native-stage-rebase/resolve-index-conflicts","#/en/native-merge/apply-and-recover","#/en/native-replay/interruption-abort-and-retention"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-squash","title":"Native commit squash","description":"Native commit squash guide.","section":"Version control","order":360,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-commit-squash\">Native commit squash\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-squash/native-commit-squash\" aria-label=\"Link to Native commit squash\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Squash replaces the last 2..100,000 linear commits with one commit containing the\nexact former tip&#39;s file entries. It changes neither prepared nor working files.\nThe complete original history remains retained in an archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The range includes the current tip and follows its single-parent chain. The new\ncommit&#39;s parent is the parent of the oldest included commit, or no parent when\nthe range reaches the root. All manifests, executable modes and representation\nchoices come from the old tip; no files are reconstructed or reingested to build\nthe candidate. The new message is required and the timestamp is current UTC.\nThe complete candidate remains subject to the ordinary 16 MiB revision limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A merge inside the selected range is a \u003ccode>merge_in_range\u003c/code> blocker. Squash does not\nsilently select a merge parent. A merge immediately before the range is preserved\nas the new parent, including its existing ancestry. Requesting more commits than\nexist produces \u003ccode>count_exceeds_history\u003c/code>. Any staging snapshot, including a clean\none, blocks the operation, as do pending checkout recovery and conflicting archive\nroots. Clear/commit staging explicitly before squash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plans are read-only. They report the old \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, requested \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, visited\n\u003ccode>revisions\u003c/code> from newest to oldest, proposed \u003ccode>parents\u003c/code>, file count, archive reference,\n\u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> and blockers. Incomplete ranges have null \u003ccode>parents\u003c/code>. A ready plan exits 0;\na blocked plan exits 1 with its JSON on stdout. Invalid counts/metadata instead\nproduce diagnostics. The compact report is limited to 16 MiB. Memory grows with\nthe bounded revision-ID list and currently decoded flat revision metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits the old head&#39;s complete retained closure and reconstructed file\nhashes. Apply always verifies it before preparing any candidate, then verifies\nreplacement/archive roots before publication. Metadata-only preview does not\npromise that historical block payloads are available. Missing ancestor bytes\nblock publication even if the new tip snapshot no longer uses them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-and-retained-history\">Publication and retained history\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-squash/publication-and-retained-history\" aria-label=\"Link to Publication and retained history\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The branch moves to the new revision and \u003ccode>archive/squash/OLD_HEAD_ID\u003c/code> retains the\nentire original chain in one atomic reference update. The expected absent staging\nroot is checked in that transaction too. Tags, other branches and symbolic HEAD\nselection do not change. Working files may contain uncommitted edits; the engine\ndoes not inspect them or include them in the candidate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> overrides the selected branch. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> requires an exact old revision\nID; if omitted, the CLI captures the current tip under the exclusive Store lock.\nThe C++ API always requires that expected ID. A process interruption leaves the\ncomplete old or new reference set. Before publication, cancellation or callback\nfailure preserves the old references; an immutable unpublished candidate may\nremain. There is no working-file journal. Inspect \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> before\nretrying with the original expected tip. No callback or stop check runs after\nreference publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-archives\">archive browser\u003c/a> locates the original heads and prepared\nversions. Archives have no automatic expiry and retain every intermediate file version\nthrough destructive GC in plaintext/encrypted and loose/packed Stores. To recover\na former version, export its ID or create a branch at it. Removing the last root\nwith generic reference tools permits GC to collect its exclusive data. Squash\nshortens visible branch history; it does not reclaim archived history automatically.\nWith a \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">native identity\u003c/a>, the standalone squash creates\nan authored v2 revision with the current person as author and committer and a new\noptional signature. This differs from rebase squash, which preserves its first\nretained original author. Legacy v1 remains unchanged without an identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_squash\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">squash_commits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Implement asset streaming\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>English/French progress, cooperative cancellation and the usual callback\nnon-reentrancy contract apply. Exposure is through C++ and CLI; native history\nC/Go bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native commit squash # Squash replaces the last 2..100,000 linear commits with one commit containing the exact former tip's file entries. It changes neither prepared nor working files. The complete original history remains retained in an archive. text Copy syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID The range includes the current tip and follows its single-parent chain. The new commit's parent is the parent of the oldest included commit, or no parent when the range reaches the root. All manifests, executable modes and representation choices come from the old tip; no files are reconstructed or reingested to build the candidate. The new message is required and the timestamp is current UTC. The complete candidate remains subject to the ordinary 16 MiB revision limit. A merge inside the selected range is a merge_in_range blocker. Squash does not silently select a merge parent. A merge immediately before the range is preserved as the new parent, including its existing ancestry. Requesting more commits than exist produces count_exceeds_history . Any staging snapshot, including a clean one, blocks the operation, as do pending checkout recovery and conflicting archive roots. Clear/commit staging explicitly before squash. Plans are read-only. They report the old head , requested count , visited revisions from newest to oldest, proposed parents , file count, archive reference, ready and blockers. Incomplete ranges have null parents . A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with its JSON on stdout. Invalid counts/metadata instead produce diagnostics. The compact report is limited to 16 MiB. Memory grows with the bounded revision-ID list and currently decoded flat revision metadata. --verify audits the old head's complete retained closure and reconstructed file hashes. Apply always verifies it before preparing any candidate, then verifies replacement/archive roots before publication. Metadata-only preview does not promise that historical block payloads are available. Missing ancestor bytes block publication even if the new tip snapshot no longer uses them. Publication and retained history # The branch moves to the new revision and archive/squash/OLD_HEAD_ID retains the entire original chain in one atomic reference update. The expected absent staging root is checked in that transaction too. Tags, other branches and symbolic HEAD selection do not change. Working files may contain uncommitted edits; the engine does not inspect them or include them in the candidate. --branch overrides the selected branch. --expect requires an exact old revision ID; if omitted, the CLI captures the current tip under the exclusive Store lock. The C++ API always requires that expected ID. A process interruption leaves the complete old or new reference set. Before publication, cancellation or callback failure preserves the old references; an immutable unpublished candidate may remain. There is no working-file journal. Inspect native head and refs before retrying with the original expected tip. No callback or stop check runs after reference publication. The archive browser locates the original heads and prepared versions. Archives have no automatic expiry and retain every intermediate file version through destructive GC in plaintext/encrypted and loose/packed Stores. To recover a former version, export its ID or create a branch at it. Removing the last root with generic reference tools permits GC to collect its exclusive data. Squash shortens visible branch history; it does not reclaim archived history automatically. With a native identity , the standalone squash creates an authored v2 revision with the current person as author and committer and a new optional signature. This differs from rebase squash, which preserves its first retained original author. Legacy v1 remains unchanged without an identity. cpp Copy auto preview = store . plan_squash ( \"main\" , 3 , true , operation ) ; auto applied = store . squash_commits ( \"main\" , expected_id , 3 , \"Implement asset streaming\" , operation ) ; English/French progress, cooperative cancellation and the usual callback non-reentrancy contract apply. Exposure is through C++ and CLI; native history C/Go bindings remain future work. syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID auto preview = store.plan_squash(\"main\", 3, true, operation); auto applied = store.squash_commits(\"main\", expected_id, 3, \"Implement asset streaming\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-commit-squash","text":"Native commit squash","level":1},{"id":"publication-and-retained-history","text":"Publication and retained history","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify\nsyrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nauto preview = store.plan_squash(\"main\", 3, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.squash_commits(\"main\", expected_id, 3,\n                                    \"Implement asset streaming\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-archives","#/en/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-stage-rebase","title":"Rebase a native staging index","description":"Rebase a native staging index guide.","section":"Version control","order":370,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"rebase-a-native-staging-index\">Rebase a native staging index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/rebase-a-native-staging-index\" aria-label=\"Link to Rebase a native staging index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native rebase-index-plan\u003c/code> replays a frozen index&#39;s changes onto its branch&#39;s\ncurrent head. It reads stored snapshots and manifest metadata, without scanning,\ningesting or modifying working files. Dirty, ignored and untracked working files\ndo not affect the plan. This operates on native staging, independently of Git&#39;s index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"replay-and-preview\">Replay and preview\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/replay-and-preview\" aria-label=\"Link to Replay and preview\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The recorded staging parent is the baseline, the current branch head is current,\nand the complete frozen index is incoming. Independent file and executable-mode\nchanges combine through the shared three-way file merger. Content, add/delete,\ncase and file/directory collisions block the result. Optional text composition is\ndescribed below; rename detection remains future work. A pending checkout/switch\njournal also blocks the plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The branch must have a head and an index. An index prepared before the first\nbranch commit can be replayed with an empty baseline after a head exists. There\nis no ancestor requirement: the new head is an explicit destination. The CLI\ndefaults to the selected native branch; \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> does not change that selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An already fresh index is unchanged, preserving its exact ID and prepared file\nentries. A stale index proposes an ordinary v1 staging snapshot with the current\nhead as its parent, the branch&#39;s staging marker and timestamp zero. Empty results\nremain explicit indexes. The proposed ID is deterministic and is not written by\nplanning; it need not yet be inspectable in the Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Logical content and executable changes follow the ordinary replay policy. An\nexplicitly prepared representation-only change is also retained when the new\nhead still has the baseline&#39;s content and manifest; independent mode changes can\ncombine with it. If upstream replaced the representation too, upstream wins when\nthe logical contents match. A changed upstream file cannot keep an obsolete\nprepared representation of its old contents.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reports contain \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, original \u003ccode>index\u003c/code> and nullable \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stale\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code>,\nproposed \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>empty\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>counts\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. Change rows\ncompare the proposed index with the current head and distinguish content, mode\nand manifest differences. For a blocked result they describe a provisional merge;\n\u003ccode>target\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>result_files\u003c/code> are null. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1\nwith JSON on stdout. Invalid or missing inputs produce diagnostics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plans do not write candidate objects or references. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally audits\nboth complete retained closures, including historical reconstructed file hashes.\nWithout text composition, metadata-only plans do not audit block payloads.\nText-enabled plans reconstruct eligible unresolved content files and verify their\nhashes even without \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. Reports and candidate revision\nmetadata have the existing 16 MiB bounds; snapshots are still flat file maps.\nThe report includes an empty \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> and accepted \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> rows.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The API supports cooperative cancellation and progress. Owned core diagnostics\nare English; CLI help, errors and progress support English and French. JSON keys\nand reason codes remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. Native\nhistory C/Go bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes\">Apply and retain prepared bytes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes\" aria-label=\"Link to Apply and retain prepared bytes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Application verifies the complete ready plan before creating the replacement\nindex. Its ID must match the preview. One reference transaction checks the\nexpected branch and index, retains the old index under\n\u003ccode>archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>, and replaces \u003ccode>staging/BRANCH\u003c/code>. The branch tip,\nsymbolic HEAD and working files stay unchanged. Both expected IDs are exact;\nthe CLI captures omitted values under the exclusive Store lock. C++ requires\nthem explicitly. Conflicts or a pending checkout block application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A fresh index returns \u003ccode>state: unchanged\u003c/code> without writes, while still auditing\nretained bytes. A changed index returns \u003ccode>state: applied\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_index\u003c/code> and\nthe new \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, alongside the original plan fields. \u003ccode>changed\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>stale\u003c/code> describe\nthe input index; \u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code> describes the resulting fresh index. A result\nequal to the current head stays an explicit clean index; staged commit continues\nto require changed entries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation, missing retained blocks, callback failure or an archive collision\nbefore publication preserve the old references. Candidate creation can leave\ncomplete unreferenced metadata, reusable on retry or collectible by GC. There is\nno working-file journal because this operation does not change working files.\nPublication uses the existing atomic reference writer, with no callbacks or\ncancellation checks after its commit point. An interrupted operation leaves the\nold or the complete new root set. Inspect \u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code> before retrying.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The archived index retains all prepared manifests, blocks and its original\nbaseline history through GC, even after committing or clearing the replacement\nindex. Archives have no automatic expiry. The ordinary revision/reference formats\nand encryption-at-rest behavior are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"resolve-index-conflicts\">Resolve index conflicts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/resolve-index-conflicts\" aria-label=\"Link to Resolve index conflicts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Both commands accept \u003ccode>--resolutions JSON_FILE\u003c/code>, using the shared\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">file resolution schema\u003c/a>. Copy the plan&#39;s\n\u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code> into a JSON file and fill its \u003ccode>files\u003c/code> map. Its operation is\n\u003ccode>rebase-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> is the current branch tip, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code> is the frozen index,\nand \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>parent\u003c/code> are both its recorded baseline (null for an initial index).\nAll IDs must match when planning/application runs. Changed source indexes or\nbranch tips require a new plan and choices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Choices select a complete entry from \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> (the new branch head), \u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code>\n(the old prepared index), \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>, or a custom \u003ccode>file\u003c/code> manifest with an\nexplicit executable mode. Only original conflict paths may have choices.\nNamespace collisions are checked again after applying them. Incomplete choices\nremain blocked and publish nothing. Non-conflicting paths cannot be overridden\nthrough a resolution file. Empty choices on a fresh index keep its exact ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Raw decoding rejects duplicate JSON fields, unknown fields, invalid paths/IDs and\nmalformed choices, with the existing 16 MiB, 100,000-path and depth/field limits.\nCustom manifests are checked by metadata-only planning; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> and application\naudit their payloads as well. Pin imported files if GC may run before application\nretains them. A successfully published index protects their blocks; a later staged\ncommit retains the selected contents in the ordinary revision chain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage_resolved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage_resolved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                          choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Optional bounded text composition\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Link to Optional bounded text composition\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code> composes independent UTF-8 edits between the recorded staging base,\ncurrent head and frozen index. It never reads working text; later dirty bytes stay\nuntouched. Explicit \u003ccode>--resolutions\u003c/code> choices take priority. Remaining binary,\noverlapping, add/delete and namespace conflicts block the entire publication.\nLine endings and missing final newlines are preserved, and executable mode is\ncombined independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\">native text batch limits\u003c/a> apply:\n64 attempts and 16 MiB total input plus clean result bytes, with the usual\n\u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code> per-file\nlimits. Budget flags require \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code>. Reports add \u003ccode>text_merge\u003c/code>, including\nstatus/reason/coordinates and proposed manifest metadata per attempted path.\nThey include no merged plaintext. An already fresh index preserves its exact ID\nand reports zero text attempts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Planning reconstructs only eligible unresolved content files, verifies their\nhashes, and predicts canonical CDC manifest IDs without writing objects. Apply\nverifies all retained inputs before materializing generated data and creating the\ncandidate. The old index archive and new staging reference publish in one expected\nhead/index transaction. A conflict publishes neither. Cancellation before that\ntransaction can leave complete unreferenced objects while references and working\nfiles remain unchanged. The archived old index and its base remain reconstructible\nafter GC; a later staged commit retains the complete merged snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeStageRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                               expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The named options methods preserve compatibility with existing plain/resolved\nentry points.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Rebase a native staging index # native rebase-index-plan replays a frozen index's changes onto its branch's current head. It reads stored snapshots and manifest metadata, without scanning, ingesting or modifying working files. Dirty, ignored and untracked working files do not affect the plan. This operates on native staging, independently of Git's index. text Copy syrnck native staged syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature Replay and preview # The recorded staging parent is the baseline, the current branch head is current, and the complete frozen index is incoming. Independent file and executable-mode changes combine through the shared three-way file merger. Content, add/delete, case and file/directory collisions block the result. Optional text composition is described below; rename detection remains future work. A pending checkout/switch journal also blocks the plan. The branch must have a head and an index. An index prepared before the first branch commit can be replayed with an empty baseline after a head exists. There is no ancestor requirement: the new head is an explicit destination. The CLI defaults to the selected native branch; --branch does not change that selection. An already fresh index is unchanged, preserving its exact ID and prepared file entries. A stale index proposes an ordinary v1 staging snapshot with the current head as its parent, the branch's staging marker and timestamp zero. Empty results remain explicit indexes. The proposed ID is deterministic and is not written by planning; it need not yet be inspectable in the Store. Logical content and executable changes follow the ordinary replay policy. An explicitly prepared representation-only change is also retained when the new head still has the baseline's content and manifest; independent mode changes can combine with it. If upstream replaced the representation too, upstream wins when the logical contents match. A changed upstream file cannot keep an obsolete prepared representation of its old contents. Reports contain head , original index and nullable base , stale , changed , proposed target , archive , ready , objects_verified , result_files , empty , counts , changes , conflicts , resolution_context and blockers . Change rows compare the proposed index with the current head and distinguish content, mode and manifest differences. For a blocked result they describe a provisional merge; target and result_files are null. A ready plan exits 0; a blocked plan exits 1 with JSON on stdout. Invalid or missing inputs produce diagnostics. Plans do not write candidate objects or references. --verify additionally audits both complete retained closures, including historical reconstructed file hashes. Without text composition, metadata-only plans do not audit block payloads. Text-enabled plans reconstruct eligible unresolved content files and verify their hashes even without --verify . Reports and candidate revision metadata have the existing 16 MiB bounds; snapshots are still flat file maps. The report includes an empty resolution_context and accepted resolved rows. cpp Copy auto plan = store . plan_rebase_stage ( \"feature\" , true , operation ) ; The API supports cooperative cancellation and progress. Owned core diagnostics are English; CLI help, errors and progress support English and French. JSON keys and reason codes remain English. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. Native history C/Go bindings remain future work. Apply and retain prepared bytes # text Copy syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native staged syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\" syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify Application verifies the complete ready plan before creating the replacement index. Its ID must match the preview. One reference transaction checks the expected branch and index, retains the old index under archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID , and replaces staging/BRANCH . The branch tip, symbolic HEAD and working files stay unchanged. Both expected IDs are exact; the CLI captures omitted values under the exclusive Store lock. C++ requires them explicitly. Conflicts or a pending checkout block application. A fresh index returns state: unchanged without writes, while still auditing retained bytes. A changed index returns state: applied , previous_index and the new index , alongside the original plan fields. changed / stale describe the input index; native staged describes the resulting fresh index. A result equal to the current head stays an explicit clean index; staged commit continues to require changed entries. Cancellation, missing retained blocks, callback failure or an archive collision before publication preserve the old references. Candidate creation can leave complete unreferenced metadata, reusable on retry or collectible by GC. There is no working-file journal because this operation does not change working files. Publication uses the existing atomic reference writer, with no callbacks or cancellation checks after its commit point. An interrupted operation leaves the old or the complete new root set. Inspect native staged before retrying. The archived index retains all prepared manifests, blocks and its original baseline history through GC, even after committing or clearing the replacement index. Archives have no automatic expiry. The ordinary revision/reference formats and encryption-at-rest behavior are unchanged. cpp Copy auto result = store . rebase_stage ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , operation ) ; Resolve index conflicts # Both commands accept --resolutions JSON_FILE , using the shared file resolution schema . Copy the plan's resolution_context into a JSON file and fill its files map. Its operation is rebase-index , head is the current branch tip, source is the frozen index, and base / parent are both its recorded baseline (null for an initial index). All IDs must match when planning/application runs. Changed source indexes or branch tips require a new plan and choices. text Copy syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID Choices select a complete entry from current (the new branch head), incoming (the old prepared index), base , remove , or a custom file manifest with an explicit executable mode. Only original conflict paths may have choices. Namespace collisions are checked again after applying them. Incomplete choices remain blocked and publish nothing. Non-conflicting paths cannot be overridden through a resolution file. Empty choices on a fresh index keep its exact ID. Raw decoding rejects duplicate JSON fields, unknown fields, invalid paths/IDs and malformed choices, with the existing 16 MiB, 100,000-path and depth/field limits. Custom manifests are checked by metadata-only planning; --verify and application audit their payloads as well. Pin imported files if GC may run before application retains them. A successfully published index protects their blocks; a later staged commit retains the selected contents in the ordinary revision chain. cpp Copy auto choices = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; choices . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; auto preview = store . plan_rebase_stage_resolved ( \"feature\" , choices , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . rebase_stage_resolved ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , choices , operation ) ; Optional bounded text composition # text Copy syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID --text-merge composes independent UTF-8 edits between the recorded staging base, current head and frozen index. It never reads working text; later dirty bytes stay untouched. Explicit --resolutions choices take priority. Remaining binary, overlapping, add/delete and namespace conflicts block the entire publication. Line endings and missing final newlines are preserved, and executable mode is combined independently. The native text batch limits apply: 64 attempts and 16 MiB total input plus clean result bytes, with the usual --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes per-file limits. Budget flags require --text-merge . Reports add text_merge , including status/reason/coordinates and proposed manifest metadata per attempted path. They include no merged plaintext. An already fresh index preserves its exact ID and reports zero text attempts. Planning reconstructs only eligible unresolved content files, verifies their hashes, and predicts canonical CDC manifest IDs without writing objects. Apply verifies all retained inputs before materializing generated data and creating the candidate. The old index archive and new staging reference publish in one expected head/index transaction. A conflict publishes neither. Cancellation before that transaction can leave complete unreferenced objects while references and working files remain unchanged. The archived old index and its base remain reconstructible after GC; a later staged commit retains the complete merged snapshot. cpp Copy syrnck :: NativeStageRebaseOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase_stage_with_options ( \"feature\" , settings , true ) ; auto result = store . rebase_stage_with_options ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , settings ) ; The named options methods preserve compatibility with existing plain/resolved entry points. syrnck native staged syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature auto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage(\"feature\", true, operation); syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native staged syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\" syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify auto result = store.rebase_stage(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, operation); syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID auto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); choices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; auto preview = store.plan_rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", choices, true, operation); auto result = store.rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, choices, operation); syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck::NativeStageRebaseOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); auto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", settings, true); auto result = store.rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, settings);","headings":[{"id":"rebase-a-native-staging-index","text":"Rebase a native staging index","level":1},{"id":"replay-and-preview","text":"Replay and preview","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes","text":"Apply and retain prepared bytes","level":2},{"id":"resolve-index-conflicts","text":"Resolve index conflicts","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Optional bounded text composition","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native staged\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage(\"feature\", true, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\"\nsyrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify\nauto result = store.rebase_stage(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nchoices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nauto preview = store.plan_rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", choices, true, operation);\nauto result = store.rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index,\n                                          choices, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck::NativeStageRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace();\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", settings, true);\nauto result = store.rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", expected_head,\n                                               expected_index, settings);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-staging","title":"Native staging and selected commits","description":"Native staging and selected commits guide.","section":"Version control","order":380,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-staging-and-selected-commits\">Native staging and selected commits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/native-staging-and-selected-commits\" aria-label=\"Link to Native staging and selected commits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The standalone engine can prepare selected files, inspect the frozen snapshot,\nremove selections and publish a commit without reading the working files again.\nThis operates on native branches; Git retains its own ordinary index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset Source --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native add .\u003c/code> selects the entire candidate workspace. \u003ccode>native reset\u003c/code> without\npaths discards the branch&#39;s staging index. None of these commands changes working\nfiles. The CLI defaults to the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches\">current native branch\u003c/a>,\nimplicitly \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> in legacy Stores; \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> overrides it without selecting it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"selection-and-frozen-contents\">Selection and frozen contents\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/selection-and-frozen-contents\" aria-label=\"Link to Selection and frozen contents\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Paths select an exact file or all entries below a directory prefix. They are\nrelative to \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> (default \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>), rather than relative to a Git root.\nForward slashes are portable; the CLI also normalizes platform path separators,\n\u003ccode>./\u003c/code> and a trailing slash. The C++ API expects validated repository paths or \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>.\nWildcards, absolute paths and paths outside the root are rejected. Use \u003ccode>--\u003c/code>\nbefore a path starting with \u003ccode>-\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each add updates the existing index, initially copied from the branch snapshot.\nUnselected entries retain their previously prepared versions. Selected working\nfiles are ingested immediately. A selected tracked/prepared path absent from the\ncandidate workspace is removed from the index, including tracked files excluded\nby current ignore rules. A path that matches neither a selected working file nor\nan indexed/baseline file is an error; an untracked empty directory has no entry.\nOverlapping selections and repeated paths are allowed. \u003ccode>dir\u003c/code> does not select\n\u003ccode>dir2\u003c/code>. Select their common ancestor to stage a file/directory or case change\nthat would otherwise leave conflicting entries in the snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The scanner and exclusions are shared with native commits/status: nested\n\u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>, portable path/case collision checks and regular-file restrictions\napply. The Store must be inside the workspace. The scanner currently visits the\nwhole candidate workspace and ingests only selected files. An invalid unselected\npath can therefore still reject the scan. Staging does not use the advisory \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-watch\">watcher\u003c/a> or \u003ca href=\"#/en/working-index\">hash cache\u003c/a> to skip these reads.\nScanning and flat revision metadata retain the existing 16 MiB limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Add prints the immutable staging revision ID. It describes a complete snapshot,\nincluding unchanged files, and can be inspected/exported with the existing\nrevision tools. Repeating an add with the same branch base and file entries\nproduces the same ID. Later working edits do not change prepared bytes. Adding\nthe path again explicitly replaces its prepared version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-reset-and-commit\">Inspect, reset and commit\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/inspect-reset-and-commit\" aria-label=\"Link to Inspect, reset and commit\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code> reads only revision/manifest metadata and emits English JSON:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Explicit native branch name\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>base\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Branch revision recorded when this index started, or null\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>head\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current branch revision, or null\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>snapshot\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Staging revision ID, or null if no index exists\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stale\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current branch revision differs from the recorded base\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Indexed file entries equal the recorded baseline\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>staged_files\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Total files in the candidate snapshot, including unchanged files\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>counts\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Added/removed/modified entries and unchanged count\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Change rows follow \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-comparison\">native revision comparisons\u003c/a>, including\ncontent, executable and manifest changes. Representation-only changes can be\nstaged even when file bytes match. A missing index reports a clean comparison\nagainst the current branch; an unborn branch uses an empty baseline. A stale\nindex remains inspectable against its original base. Inspection does not audit\npayload availability; publication and GC verify full retained closures.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native status\u003c/code> continues to compare the working directory with the branch (or\nan explicit revision). Use \u003ccode>native status --revision STAGING_ID\u003c/code> to compare\nworking bytes with the prepared snapshot. \u003ccode>native status --combined\u003c/code> returns\nboth comparisons together, retaining stale-index information. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">combined native workspace status\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native reset PATH...\u003c/code> restores selected index entries from the recorded base,\nor removes newly added entries. It does not read or restore working files.\nIt leaves an explicit index, even when all entries match the baseline.\n\u003ccode>native reset\u003c/code> clears the entire index and is idempotent when none exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native commit --staged -m MESSAGE\u003c/code> publishes the exact index, sets the recorded\nbase as its parent and consumes the index. An unborn branch produces a root\ncommit. It requires an index with changed file entries. It accepts ordinary\nprogress/cancellation options, but rejects directory/ingestion options because\nit does not ingest working files. The ordinary \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code> still snapshots\nthe whole workspace and now refuses when that branch has an index; commit the\nselection or reset it explicitly first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If another operation moves/removes the branch, add, selective reset and staged\ncommit refuse the stale index. Inspect/export it if needed, then explicitly\nclear it with \u003ccode>native reset\u003c/code>, or use \u003ccode>native rebase-index-plan\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>native rebase-index\u003c/code> to replay its frozen changes onto an existing branch head.\nThe latter archives the old index and preserves working files; conflicts block\npublication. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stage-rebase\">staging rebase\u003c/a>. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">Numbered text hunks\u003c/a> or \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-lines\">individual changed rows\u003c/a> can be added or removed for existing files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/text-patches\">External unified patches\u003c/a> can also be prepared against frozen files with a bound preview, strict context/positions and atomic previous-index retention, without working-file reads.\nEditable/interactive patches remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-publication\">Retention and publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/retention-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention and publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each branch has one \u003ccode>staging/BRANCH\u003c/code> reference to an ordinary v1 revision object.\nIts fixed timestamp, branch-bound internal message and optional baseline parent\nidentify the index. It contains a full file map, not a chain of staging edits.\n\u003ccode>heads/BRANCH\u003c/code> remains unchanged until commit. These internal revision objects\nare not added as parents of user commits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The staging root protects all prepared manifests/chunks and the baseline history\nagainst GC, including while the branch itself is absent. Plain/encrypted and\nloose/single-pack/multi-pack Stores use the same logical mechanism. The ordinary\nformat and encryption-at-rest behavior are unchanged. Generic reference tools\ncan remove these protections; do not drop \u003ccode>staging/\u003c/code> roots for active indexes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Add and selective reset validate complete replacement closures before one\nreference transaction. Staged commit verifies all required objects, then moves\nthe branch and removes the index in a single atomic \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> replacement.\nExpected branch/index values are checked together. No callback or cancellation\ncheck runs after this publication. Full reset atomically removes only the index.\nThe exclusive Store lock remains held throughout each operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation, source changes, invalid paths, missing dependencies and callback\nfailure before publication preserve the previously published branch/index.\nIngestion or revision preparation may leave unreferenced immutable objects;\nretry can reuse them, or a later explicit GC can collect them. Workspace files\nare never rolled back because staging never writes them. Scanning is not an\natomic filesystem snapshot: individual files are checked around ingestion, and\nconcurrent edits can require retrying.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">C++ API and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ API and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native commit --amend --staged -m MESSAGE\u003c/code> can replace the existing tip using\nthe same prepared snapshot, including a clean index for a message correction.\nIt preserves the old tip&#39;s parents and atomically archives the old revision while\nconsuming the index. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-amend\">native amendment and retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> snapshot \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">stage_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Maps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">staged_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> remaining \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> commit \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An overload of \u003ccode>stage_directory\u003c/code> accepts \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code>; the default uses\nrepository ingestion settings. The other operations accept \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>.\nCallbacks run synchronously on the caller and must not reenter the Store.\nAdd reports scan/ingestion progress; publication verifies closures and ends with\n\u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> at \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>. C ABI and Go native-revision bindings remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-prepared-text\">Inspect prepared text\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-staging/inspect-prepared-text\" aria-label=\"Link to Inspect prepared text\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>native diff-text --staged PATH [--branch NAME] [--unified]\u003c/code> for a verified, bounded line diff against the frozen index’s original base. Stale indexes keep their baseline and later working-file changes are not read. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text diff contracts and limits\u003c/a>. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">Numbered hunk selection\u003c/a> is available for existing text files.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native staging and selected commits # The standalone engine can prepare selected files, inspect the frozen snapshot, remove selections and publish a commit without reading the working files again. This operates on native branches; Git retains its own ordinary index. text Copy syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress syrnck native staged --branch main syrnck native reset Source --branch main syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main native add . selects the entire candidate workspace. native reset without paths discards the branch's staging index. None of these commands changes working files. The CLI defaults to the current native branch , implicitly main in legacy Stores; --branch overrides it without selecting it. Selection and frozen contents # Paths select an exact file or all entries below a directory prefix. They are relative to --directory (default . ), rather than relative to a Git root. Forward slashes are portable; the CLI also normalizes platform path separators, ./ and a trailing slash. The C++ API expects validated repository paths or . . Wildcards, absolute paths and paths outside the root are rejected. Use -- before a path starting with - . Each add updates the existing index, initially copied from the branch snapshot. Unselected entries retain their previously prepared versions. Selected working files are ingested immediately. A selected tracked/prepared path absent from the candidate workspace is removed from the index, including tracked files excluded by current ignore rules. A path that matches neither a selected working file nor an indexed/baseline file is an error; an untracked empty directory has no entry. Overlapping selections and repeated paths are allowed. dir does not select dir2 . Select their common ancestor to stage a file/directory or case change that would otherwise leave conflicting entries in the snapshot. The scanner and exclusions are shared with native commits/status: nested .syrnckignore , portable path/case collision checks and regular-file restrictions apply. The Store must be inside the workspace. The scanner currently visits the whole candidate workspace and ingests only selected files. An invalid unselected path can therefore still reject the scan. Staging does not use the advisory watcher or hash cache to skip these reads. Scanning and flat revision metadata retain the existing 16 MiB limits. Add prints the immutable staging revision ID. It describes a complete snapshot, including unchanged files, and can be inspected/exported with the existing revision tools. Repeating an add with the same branch base and file entries produces the same ID. Later working edits do not change prepared bytes. Adding the path again explicitly replaces its prepared version. Inspect, reset and commit # native staged reads only revision/manifest metadata and emits English JSON: Field Meaning branch Explicit native branch name base Branch revision recorded when this index started, or null head Current branch revision, or null snapshot Staging revision ID, or null if no index exists stale Current branch revision differs from the recorded base clean Indexed file entries equal the recorded baseline staged_files Total files in the candidate snapshot, including unchanged files counts , changes Added/removed/modified entries and unchanged count Change rows follow native revision comparisons , including content, executable and manifest changes. Representation-only changes can be staged even when file bytes match. A missing index reports a clean comparison against the current branch; an unborn branch uses an empty baseline. A stale index remains inspectable against its original base. Inspection does not audit payload availability; publication and GC verify full retained closures. native status continues to compare the working directory with the branch (or an explicit revision). Use native status --revision STAGING_ID to compare working bytes with the prepared snapshot. native status --combined returns both comparisons together, retaining stale-index information. See combined native workspace status . native reset PATH... restores selected index entries from the recorded base, or removes newly added entries. It does not read or restore working files. It leaves an explicit index, even when all entries match the baseline. native reset clears the entire index and is idempotent when none exists. native commit --staged -m MESSAGE publishes the exact index, sets the recorded base as its parent and consumes the index. An unborn branch produces a root commit. It requires an index with changed file entries. It accepts ordinary progress/cancellation options, but rejects directory/ingestion options because it does not ingest working files. The ordinary native commit still snapshots the whole workspace and now refuses when that branch has an index; commit the selection or reset it explicitly first. If another operation moves/removes the branch, add, selective reset and staged commit refuse the stale index. Inspect/export it if needed, then explicitly clear it with native reset , or use native rebase-index-plan and native rebase-index to replay its frozen changes onto an existing branch head. The latter archives the old index and preserves working files; conflicts block publication. See staging rebase . Numbered text hunks or individual changed rows can be added or removed for existing files. External unified patches can also be prepared against frozen files with a bound preview, strict context/positions and atomic previous-index retention, without working-file reads. Editable/interactive patches remain future work. Retention and publication # Each branch has one staging/BRANCH reference to an ordinary v1 revision object. Its fixed timestamp, branch-bound internal message and optional baseline parent identify the index. It contains a full file map, not a chain of staging edits. heads/BRANCH remains unchanged until commit. These internal revision objects are not added as parents of user commits. The staging root protects all prepared manifests/chunks and the baseline history against GC, including while the branch itself is absent. Plain/encrypted and loose/single-pack/multi-pack Stores use the same logical mechanism. The ordinary format and encryption-at-rest behavior are unchanged. Generic reference tools can remove these protections; do not drop staging/ roots for active indexes. Add and selective reset validate complete replacement closures before one reference transaction. Staged commit verifies all required objects, then moves the branch and removes the index in a single atomic refs.json replacement. Expected branch/index values are checked together. No callback or cancellation check runs after this publication. Full reset atomically removes only the index. The exclusive Store lock remains held throughout each operation. Cancellation, source changes, invalid paths, missing dependencies and callback failure before publication preserve the previously published branch/index. Ingestion or revision preparation may leave unreferenced immutable objects; retry can reuse them, or a later explicit GC can collect them. Workspace files are never rolled back because staging never writes them. Scanning is not an atomic filesystem snapshot: individual files are checked around ingestion, and concurrent edits can require retrying. C++ API and validation # native commit --amend --staged -m MESSAGE can replace the existing tip using the same prepared snapshot, including a clean index for a message correction. It preserves the old tip's parents and atomically archives the old revision while consuming the index. See native amendment and retention . cpp Copy auto snapshot = store . stage_directory ( project , \"main\" , { \"Source\" , \"Content/Maps\" } ) ; auto report = store . staged_status ( \"main\" , operation ) ; auto remaining = store . reset_stage ( \"main\" , { \"Source\" } , operation ) ; auto commit = store . commit_staged ( \"main\" , \"Selected changes\" , operation ) ; store . reset_stage ( \"main\" ) ; An overload of stage_directory accepts IngestOptions ; the default uses repository ingestion settings. The other operations accept OperationOptions . Callbacks run synchronously on the caller and must not reenter the Store. Add reports scan/ingestion progress; publication verifies closures and ends with publishing at refs . C ABI and Go native-revision bindings remain future work. Inspect prepared text # Use native diff-text --staged PATH [--branch NAME] [--unified] for a verified, bounded line diff against the frozen index’s original base. Stale indexes keep their baseline and later working-file changes are not read. See text diff contracts and limits . Numbered hunk selection is available for existing text files. syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress syrnck native staged --branch main syrnck native reset Source --branch main syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main auto snapshot = store.stage_directory(project, \"main\", {\"Source\", \"Content/Maps\"}); auto report = store.staged_status(\"main\", operation); auto remaining = store.reset_stage(\"main\", {\"Source\"}, operation); auto commit = store.commit_staged(\"main\", \"Selected changes\", operation); store.reset_stage(\"main\");","headings":[{"id":"native-staging-and-selected-commits","text":"Native staging and selected commits","level":1},{"id":"selection-and-frozen-contents","text":"Selection and frozen contents","level":2},{"id":"inspect-reset-and-commit","text":"Inspect, reset and commit","level":2},{"id":"retention-and-publication","text":"Retention and publication","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"C++ API and validation","level":2},{"id":"inspect-prepared-text","text":"Inspect prepared text","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native staged --branch main\nsyrnck native reset Source --branch main\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main\nauto snapshot = store.stage_directory(project, \"main\", {\"Source\", \"Content/Maps\"});\nauto report = store.staged_status(\"main\", operation);\nauto remaining = store.reset_stage(\"main\", {\"Source\"}, operation);\nauto commit = store.commit_staged(\"main\", \"Selected changes\", operation);\nstore.reset_stage(\"main\");","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-branches","#/en/native-watch","#/en/working-index","#/en/native-comparison","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/native-stage-rebase","#/en/native-hunks","#/en/native-lines","#/en/text-patches","#/en/native-amend","#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-hunks"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-stash","title":"Native stash","description":"Native stash guide.","section":"Version control","order":390,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-stash\">Native stash\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/native-stash\" aria-label=\"Link to Native stash\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native stash captures complete working files and the frozen staging index before\ncleaning the selected branch&#39;s workspace. Applying it later restores local work\nwithout advancing the branch. The API is in \u003ccode>syrnck/stash.hpp\u003c/code> and has no network\nor CLI dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash list --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the canonical 64-character stash ID returned by \u003ccode>push\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>list\u003c/code>. There are\nno mutable positional aliases. \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> selects the primary workspace or\nthe registered linked directory sharing the Store. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> must identify\nthe selected named or detached target. Existing pending\ncheckout/switch/reset/stash operations must first be recovered.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"save-and-scope\">Save and scope\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/save-and-scope\" aria-label=\"Link to Save and scope\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Saving includes paths tracked by the head or frozen index, regardless of ignore\nrules, and regular untracked files obstructing a tracked namespace transition.\n\u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code> additionally captures regular files selected by the shared\nnative scanner, respecting \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>. Ignored unrelated files stay in place.\nLinks/reparse points, special files, protected Store/Git data and ambiguous names\nare refused when they obstruct the operation; the optional scanner also refuses\nselected links/reparse points. Its directory depth limit is 128.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The complete working snapshot has the old head and optional frozen index as\nparents. Its own files retain working contents, while the separate index parent\nretains staged contents even when they differ. Missing tracked paths remain\nmissing in the saved work. The cleanup target is the current head. Empty\ndirectories are not versioned in revision snapshots, though the reset journal\nretains observed directories needed to abort a namespace transition.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stash metadata and a monotonically increasing generation counter publish\natomically with the initial version 5 hard-reset journal and snapshot pins.\nWorking files only move after that publication. Failure before publication can\nleave valid unreferenced CAS objects; interruption afterward leaves a retained\nstash and recoverable cleanup. Aborting cleanup restores the original workspace\nand index, while keeping the stash as an additional recovery copy. A clean save\nwithout an index returns \u003ccode>stash: null\u003c/code> and writes nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>list --before GENERATION --limit N\u003c/code> pages newest first. It reads and validates\nactive metadata and paired work references without auditing all file payloads.\nMessages in list entries are previews of at most 256 UTF-8 bytes; \u003ccode>show\u003c/code> returns\nthe full message. \u003ccode>show --verify\u003c/code> audits the complete saved closure. JSON keys,\nIDs, user messages and operation names are stable across English/French output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reapply-conflicts-and-index\">Reapply, conflicts and index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/reapply-conflicts-and-index\" aria-label=\"Link to Reapply, conflicts and index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>apply-plan\u003c/code> is read-only. Application requires its exact \u003ccode>--expect-plan\u003c/code> ID and\nrecomputes the plan before writing candidates. A dirty tracked workspace,\nexisting frozen index, active checkout or obstructing untracked data blocks\napplication. It does not overwrite existing local work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Saved working files merge against their original head and the currently selected\nhead. With \u003ccode>--index\u003c/code>, staged files merge separately against the \u003cstrong>original\nindex&#39;s own parent\u003c/strong>, preserving later head changes even when the saved index\nwas stale. The new index is based on the current branch head. Without \u003ccode>--index\u003c/code>,\nstaged changes remain part of the working result without creating a frozen index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Conflicting file edits block the entire operation. Optional \u003ccode>--text-merge\u003c/code> uses\nthe existing bounded, explicit text merge engine. Binary files still require a\nchoice. The plan exposes separate \u003ccode>working.resolution_context\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>index.resolution_context\u003c/code> documents:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Fill the contexts&#39; \u003ccode>files\u003c/code> maps using the existing \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>remove\u003c/code> or explicit verified \u003ccode>file\u003c/code> choices. Operations are named \u003ccode>stash\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>stash-index\u003c/code>; head/source/base/parent IDs bind choices to the selected inputs.\nText merging shares the existing aggregate attempt and byte limits across both\nresults. Repeat the same merge/resolution options when applying a plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The version 6 checkout journal distinguishes the published branch head from the\nworking target. The branch head remains unchanged. Final publication installs\nthe optional new index and, for \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code>, archives/removes the stash in \u003cstrong>one CAS\u003c/strong>\nwith the committed receipt. \u003ccode>apply\u003c/code> keeps the active stash. The retained working\nrevision also keeps the original stash and new index reachable after GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Before final publication, abort restores the clean pre-apply workspace and leaves\nthe active stash available. After publication, recovery completes cleanup. Plain\nworking/prepared/rollback files follow the same local confidentiality limits as\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">ordinary checkout\u003c/a>. Store opening never resumes implicitly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial-workspaces\">Partial workspaces\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/partial-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Link to Partial workspaces\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Stash supports static \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">partial workspaces\u003c/a>, including\ndetached HEAD and explicit empty selections. Saving captures the selected\nphysical working tree and any regular-file obstructions needed to clean it.\n\u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code> follows that same selection and ignore rules. Unrelated\nexcluded local files remain in place. The complete old head/index stay retained\nas parents, including all hidden entries and their blocks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Partial descriptors use version 3 with an inline canonical \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code>\narray. Full named/detached descriptors retain versions 1/2. Apply requires the\nsaved and current prefix definitions to match exactly; crossing between full\nand partial views or different selections is refused before candidate writes.\nThe partial apply plan uses v2 and binds the definition and policy object root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Working replay compares projected base/current trees against the actual saved\nphysical tree. Index replay remains complete, so \u003ccode>--index\u003c/code> restores hidden\nprepared edits and preserves independent hidden changes in the newer head.\nWithout \u003ccode>--index\u003c/code>, hidden staged data remains available in the saved index\nparent rather than being materialized outside the selection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Save uses v9 checkout layout 5 and apply/pop use layout 6 with frozen equal\nsource/target definitions. Layout 6 projects the logical source and restores\nthe full physical candidate. For example, a saved regular file \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> can\nreplace the selected \u003ccode>Content/A/leaf\u003c/code> during apply without adding excluded\nlogical \u003ccode>Content/B\u003c/code> to that physical namespace. Abort restores the previous\nclean projected view; final publication combines the complete index and pop\narchive changes while leaving logical HEAD unchanged. GC and portable backup\nretain the descriptor, physical work and full index/history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drop-restore-and-retention\">Drop, restore and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/drop-restore-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Drop, restore and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>drop\u003c/code> is fenced by the immutable record ID and moves the active metadata/work\nreferences to archive references. \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code> uses the same archival protection. Neither\ndeletes objects. Restore preserves the original generation and requires that the\nactive ID is absent. Archived records and work roots survive backup/restore and\nGC. Work roots can be explicitly retired with \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">verified backup coverage\u003c/a>;\na retired stash cannot be restored from the live Store without restoring its\narchive protection/data from a retained backup. Independent checkout archives\nand completed receipts can continue to retain shared objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-verification\">Bounds and verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-stash/bounds-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounds and verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>At most 4,096 active stashes and 1,000 list rows per page are supported. Messages\nare at most 1 MiB. Existing 16 MiB revision, inventory and report bounds apply.\nThe engine streams file bytes through the configured ingestion pipeline. It\nstill inventories/hashes files; this is separate from a persistent working index\nor watcher.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On Windows hosts with the legacy canonical path-length limit, deep paths can\nbe refused by the OS before the scanner reaches 128 levels. Full Windows long-path support is still an independent compatibility task.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native stash # Native stash captures complete working files and the frozen staging index before cleaning the selected branch's workspace. Applying it later restores local work without advancing the branch. The API is in syrnck/stash.hpp and has no network or CLI dependency. text Copy syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\" syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\" syrnck native stash list --limit 100 syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID Use the canonical 64-character stash ID returned by push or list . There are no mutable positional aliases. --directory selects the primary workspace or the registered linked directory sharing the Store. --branch must identify the selected named or detached target. Existing pending checkout/switch/reset/stash operations must first be recovered. Save and scope # Saving includes paths tracked by the head or frozen index, regardless of ignore rules, and regular untracked files obstructing a tracked namespace transition. --include-untracked additionally captures regular files selected by the shared native scanner, respecting .syrnckignore . Ignored unrelated files stay in place. Links/reparse points, special files, protected Store/Git data and ambiguous names are refused when they obstruct the operation; the optional scanner also refuses selected links/reparse points. Its directory depth limit is 128. The complete working snapshot has the old head and optional frozen index as parents. Its own files retain working contents, while the separate index parent retains staged contents even when they differ. Missing tracked paths remain missing in the saved work. The cleanup target is the current head. Empty directories are not versioned in revision snapshots, though the reset journal retains observed directories needed to abort a namespace transition. Stash metadata and a monotonically increasing generation counter publish atomically with the initial version 5 hard-reset journal and snapshot pins. Working files only move after that publication. Failure before publication can leave valid unreferenced CAS objects; interruption afterward leaves a retained stash and recoverable cleanup. Aborting cleanup restores the original workspace and index, while keeping the stash as an additional recovery copy. A clean save without an index returns stash: null and writes nothing. list --before GENERATION --limit N pages newest first. It reads and validates active metadata and paired work references without auditing all file payloads. Messages in list entries are previews of at most 256 UTF-8 bytes; show returns the full message. show --verify audits the complete saved closure. JSON keys, IDs, user messages and operation names are stable across English/French output. Reapply, conflicts and index # apply-plan is read-only. Application requires its exact --expect-plan ID and recomputes the plan before writing candidates. A dirty tracked workspace, existing frozen index, active checkout or obstructing untracked data blocks application. It does not overwrite existing local work. Saved working files merge against their original head and the currently selected head. With --index , staged files merge separately against the original index's own parent , preserving later head changes even when the saved index was stale. The new index is based on the current branch head. Without --index , staged changes remain part of the working result without creating a frozen index. Conflicting file edits block the entire operation. Optional --text-merge uses the existing bounded, explicit text merge engine. Binary files still require a choice. The plan exposes separate working.resolution_context and index.resolution_context documents: text Copy syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID Fill the contexts' files maps using the existing current , incoming , base , remove or explicit verified file choices. Operations are named stash and stash-index ; head/source/base/parent IDs bind choices to the selected inputs. Text merging shares the existing aggregate attempt and byte limits across both results. Repeat the same merge/resolution options when applying a plan. The version 6 checkout journal distinguishes the published branch head from the working target. The branch head remains unchanged. Final publication installs the optional new index and, for pop , archives/removes the stash in one CAS with the committed receipt. apply keeps the active stash. The retained working revision also keeps the original stash and new index reachable after GC. text Copy syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Before final publication, abort restores the clean pre-apply workspace and leaves the active stash available. After publication, recovery completes cleanup. Plain working/prepared/rollback files follow the same local confidentiality limits as ordinary checkout . Store opening never resumes implicitly. Partial workspaces # Stash supports static partial workspaces , including detached HEAD and explicit empty selections. Saving captures the selected physical working tree and any regular-file obstructions needed to clean it. --include-untracked follows that same selection and ignore rules. Unrelated excluded local files remain in place. The complete old head/index stay retained as parents, including all hidden entries and their blocks. Partial descriptors use version 3 with an inline canonical materialization array. Full named/detached descriptors retain versions 1/2. Apply requires the saved and current prefix definitions to match exactly; crossing between full and partial views or different selections is refused before candidate writes. The partial apply plan uses v2 and binds the definition and policy object root. Working replay compares projected base/current trees against the actual saved physical tree. Index replay remains complete, so --index restores hidden prepared edits and preserves independent hidden changes in the newer head. Without --index , hidden staged data remains available in the saved index parent rather than being materialized outside the selection. Save uses v9 checkout layout 5 and apply/pop use layout 6 with frozen equal source/target definitions. Layout 6 projects the logical source and restores the full physical candidate. For example, a saved regular file Content can replace the selected Content/A/leaf during apply without adding excluded logical Content/B to that physical namespace. Abort restores the previous clean projected view; final publication combines the complete index and pop archive changes while leaving logical HEAD unchanged. GC and portable backup retain the descriptor, physical work and full index/history. Drop, restore and retention # text Copy syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify drop is fenced by the immutable record ID and moves the active metadata/work references to archive references. pop uses the same archival protection. Neither deletes objects. Restore preserves the original generation and requires that the active ID is absent. Archived records and work roots survive backup/restore and GC. Work roots can be explicitly retired with verified backup coverage ; a retired stash cannot be restored from the live Store without restoring its archive protection/data from a retained backup. Independent checkout archives and completed receipts can continue to retain shared objects. Bounds and verification # At most 4,096 active stashes and 1,000 list rows per page are supported. Messages are at most 1 MiB. Existing 16 MiB revision, inventory and report bounds apply. The engine streams file bytes through the configured ingestion pipeline. It still inventories/hashes files; this is separate from a persistent working index or watcher. On Windows hosts with the legacy canonical path-length limit, deep paths can be refused by the OS before the scanner reaches 128 levels. Full Windows long-path support is still an independent compatibility task. syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\" syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\" syrnck native stash list --limit 100 syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify","headings":[{"id":"native-stash","text":"Native stash","level":1},{"id":"save-and-scope","text":"Save and scope","level":2},{"id":"reapply-conflicts-and-index","text":"Reapply, conflicts and index","level":2},{"id":"partial-workspaces","text":"Partial workspaces","level":2},{"id":"drop-restore-and-retention","text":"Drop, restore and retention","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-verification","text":"Bounds and verification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\"\nsyrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\"\nsyrnck native stash list --limit 100\nsyrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify\nsyrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json\nsyrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nsyrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID\nsyrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify\nsyrnck native stash restore STASH_ID\nsyrnck native archives --kind stash --verify","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/archive-retention"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-tags","title":"Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives","description":"Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives guide.","section":"Version control","order":400,"parent":"native-history","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives\">Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-tags/native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives\" aria-label=\"Link to Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Native tags name committed revisions independently of a branch. A lightweight\ntag retains its target; supplying a message also stores an annotation with the\noriginal UTF-8 text and creation time. These operations change references and\nmetadata, without reading or changing working files, staging or HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag release/v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tags --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default target for creation is the current committed HEAD. Local working\nedits and staged bytes are not included. An unborn HEAD requires an explicit\nexisting revision. Creation verifies the target&#39;s entire closure, including\nparents and complete file hashes, before publication. Existing names are refused,\neven when the requested target is identical. There is no force/move operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Names follow native reference syntax: ASCII letters, digits, slash, dot, hyphen\nand underscore, with no empty, \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>..\u003c/code> components. Names cannot start with\n\u003ccode>-\u003c/code> and must fit both reference namespaces below (at most 187 bytes). Messages\naccept up to 65,536 UTF-8 bytes; an explicit empty message creates an annotated\ntag. Operating-system command-line limits can impose a smaller CLI bound.\nAnnotations are unsigned metadata; tagger identities and signatures remain\nfuture work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-only-reports-and-pagination\">Read-only reports and pagination\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-tags/read-only-reports-and-pagination\" aria-label=\"Link to Read-only reports and pagination\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native tags [--after NAME] [--limit N]\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, sorted \u003ccode>tags\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code>. The default limit is 100; the allowed range is\n1..1,000. Each tag contains \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>annotation\u003c/code> (chunk ID or null),\n\u003ccode>message\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>timestamp\u003c/code>. The last two are null for lightweight tags. Times are\nUTC Unix seconds. JSON keys and values owned by the engine remain English in\nboth interface languages; user-authored messages are preserved.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pages are additionally bounded below 16 MiB of serialized JSON, including\nescaping. A page can therefore contain fewer tags than its requested limit.\nUse the returned \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> as the next request&#39;s exclusive cursor. The cursor\nis a name, so deleting that tag does not invalidate pagination. Each call uses\none reference snapshot; insertions/deletions between calls can change the global\nview. There is no persistent listing snapshot or total-count scan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The page bound is not a process-memory limit. Reference maps, decoded-object\ncaches, the current annotation and serialization buffers consume additional\nmemory. Listing validates annotations in the current page and checks for orphan\nannotation roots. It avoids loading every revision tree or file payload.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native tag-show NAME\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tag\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>native tag-archive ID\u003c/code> returns the archived tag plus its \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> ID and\n\u003ccode>deleted_at\u003c/code> time. Both accept \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> to audit the full target closure.\nDefault reports inspect retained references and annotation/record contents;\nthey do not prove file chunks or historical revisions are available. Missing or\ninvalid annotations, mismatched target IDs and incomplete archive root sets are\nrefused. Inspection does not repair or write Store data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native tag-archives [--after ID] [--limit N] [--verify]\u003c/code> lists deleted tags in\narchive-ID order. It returns \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archives\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>. Each row contains \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tag\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>deleted_at\u003c/code>. Limits\nare 1..1,000 (default 100), with the same 16 MiB escaped-JSON bound. The exclusive\nID cursor remains usable if that archive is later retired. Every page uses one\nreference snapshot and validates complete archive groups and their annotations.\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits the complete closures of the displayed groups only; default\nlisting avoids revision trees and file payloads. It does not create an inventory\nsnapshot across requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"archived-deletion-and-restoration\">Archived deletion and restoration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-tags/archived-deletion-and-restoration\" aria-label=\"Link to Archived deletion and restoration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tag-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Deletion requires the exact expected revision ID, as returned by inspection.\n\u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> accepts an ID rather than a moving branch/tag selector. One atomic\nreference transaction removes the public tag and annotation roots and publishes\nan archive record, target root and optional annotation root. The response contains\nthe archive ID. If verification or the expected-target check fails, no tag is\nremoved. Archives retain complete target history and shared chunks during GC,\neven after all branch and public tag roots disappear.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If interruption loses the deletion response, inspect the public tag and\n\u003ccode>native tag-archives\u003c/code>, then use \u003ccode>native tag-archive ID\u003c/code> to identify the original\nname and target. An archived deletion has \u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/record\u003c/code> entries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Restoration verifies the archive and recreates its original name, target,\nmessage and annotation time in one reference transaction. It refuses a name\nalready occupied by a tag or annotation root. The archive itself remains\nretained. Tags can be deleted and recreated at another revision deliberately;\nthe deleted version remains available through its archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">backup-protected retirement\u003c/a> accepts\n\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID\u003c/code> and removes its complete root group atomically. The verified\nindependent backup retains its original recipe and payloads for later restoration.\nAutomatic archive pruning is not implemented. Generic reference/admin APIs can\nremove these roots, so API-level immutability is not an authorization or signature\npolicy. Removing every protecting root and running destructive GC intentionally\nmakes that version unavailable. Retain all archive roots when preserving or\ntransferring an archive; the record chunk alone does not retain its target.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-revision-selectors\">Native revision selectors\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-tags/native-revision-selectors\" aria-label=\"Link to Native revision selectors\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Native tag creation, branch creation, \u003ccode>status --revision\u003c/code>, snapshot diff,\ncheckout preflight/execution and revision export accept these selectors:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Selector\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Resolution\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>64 lowercase hexadecimal characters\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact revision ID, with priority over identically named labels\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Current named or detached committed revision; unborn HEAD is refused\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heads/NAME\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Explicit native branch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Explicit native tag, with annotation consistency checks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>NAME\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A branch or tag, provided only one namespace contains that name\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>A bare name shared by a branch and tag is ambiguous even if both point to the\nsame revision. Use its explicit namespace. A tag named \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>, or one named with\na 64-character hexadecimal string, remains addressable with \u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code>.\nResolution validates the selected revision metadata, without traversing its\nparents, manifests or file chunks. It is separate from full verification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native switch NAME\u003c/code> still selects a literal branch. \u003ccode>native checkout TAG\u003c/code>\nrestores that revision and moves the selected target, archiving its old head;\nuse \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">\u003ccode>native switch --detach TAG\u003c/code>\u003c/a> to detach instead. The explicit C++ snapshot methods retain\ntheir revision-ID arguments; applications can call \u003ccode>resolve_native_revision()\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-cancellation-and-api\">Storage, cancellation and API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-tags/storage-cancellation-and-api\" aria-label=\"Link to Storage, cancellation and API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Value\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Target revision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tag-metadata/NAME\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Optional canonical annotation chunk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/record\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Canonical deletion record chunk, whose object ID is ID\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/target\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Archived target revision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/annotation\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Original annotation chunk when present\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Annotation and deletion records use existing chunk objects, compression and\nencryption at rest. Their strict schemas bind names, target IDs and annotation\nIDs. The archive also records a fresh nonce and deletion time. No object kind,\npointer, C ABI or physical storage format changes. Generic object/reference\ntransfers do not yet negotiate an atomic annotated-tag/archive group; dedicated\nserver tag policies and coordinated replication remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All commands support progress and cooperative cancellation. Mutations use the\nexisting complete-closure compare-and-swap reference publication. Cancellation\nor an observer exception before publication keeps all old roots; newly written\nunreferenced metadata chunks can remain for reuse or GC. After publication there\nare no further cancellation points or callbacks. Process interruption leaves\neither the old reference set or the fully published new set. Tag operations are\nalso allowed while a workspace checkout is pending; they do not alter its branch,\nHEAD or internal recovery roots. Callbacks must not reenter the Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> selected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resolve_native_revision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"HEAD\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> created \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Release notes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> shown \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> removed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">delete_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archived_page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag_archives\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> saved \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restored \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The C ABI and Go binding do not expose native tag operations yet.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives # Native tags name committed revisions independently of a branch. A lightweight tag retains its target; supplying a message also stores an annotation with the original UTF-8 text and creation time. These operations change references and metadata, without reading or changing working files, staging or HEAD. text Copy syrnck native tag release/v1 syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify The default target for creation is the current committed HEAD. Local working edits and staged bytes are not included. An unborn HEAD requires an explicit existing revision. Creation verifies the target's entire closure, including parents and complete file hashes, before publication. Existing names are refused, even when the requested target is identical. There is no force/move operation. Names follow native reference syntax: ASCII letters, digits, slash, dot, hyphen and underscore, with no empty, . or .. components. Names cannot start with - and must fit both reference namespaces below (at most 187 bytes). Messages accept up to 65,536 UTF-8 bytes; an explicit empty message creates an annotated tag. Operating-system command-line limits can impose a smaller CLI bound. Annotations are unsigned metadata; tagger identities and signatures remain future work. Read-only reports and pagination # native tags [--after NAME] [--limit N] returns version , sorted tags , has_more and next_after . The default limit is 100; the allowed range is 1..1,000. Each tag contains name , revision , annotation (chunk ID or null), message and timestamp . The last two are null for lightweight tags. Times are UTC Unix seconds. JSON keys and values owned by the engine remain English in both interface languages; user-authored messages are preserved. Pages are additionally bounded below 16 MiB of serialized JSON, including escaping. A page can therefore contain fewer tags than its requested limit. Use the returned next_after as the next request's exclusive cursor. The cursor is a name, so deleting that tag does not invalidate pagination. Each call uses one reference snapshot; insertions/deletions between calls can change the global view. There is no persistent listing snapshot or total-count scan. The page bound is not a process-memory limit. Reference maps, decoded-object caches, the current annotation and serialization buffers consume additional memory. Listing validates annotations in the current page and checks for orphan annotation roots. It avoids loading every revision tree or file payload. native tag-show NAME returns version , tag and objects_verified . native tag-archive ID returns the archived tag plus its archive ID and deleted_at time. Both accept --verify to audit the full target closure. Default reports inspect retained references and annotation/record contents; they do not prove file chunks or historical revisions are available. Missing or invalid annotations, mismatched target IDs and incomplete archive root sets are refused. Inspection does not repair or write Store data. native tag-archives [--after ID] [--limit N] [--verify] lists deleted tags in archive-ID order. It returns version , archives , has_more , next_after and objects_verified . Each row contains archive , tag and deleted_at . Limits are 1..1,000 (default 100), with the same 16 MiB escaped-JSON bound. The exclusive ID cursor remains usable if that archive is later retired. Every page uses one reference snapshot and validates complete archive groups and their annotations. --verify audits the complete closures of the displayed groups only; default listing avoids revision trees and file payloads. It does not create an inventory snapshot across requests. Archived deletion and restoration # text Copy syrnck native tag-show release/v1 syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck gc --delete syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID Deletion requires the exact expected revision ID, as returned by inspection. --expect accepts an ID rather than a moving branch/tag selector. One atomic reference transaction removes the public tag and annotation roots and publishes an archive record, target root and optional annotation root. The response contains the archive ID. If verification or the expected-target check fails, no tag is removed. Archives retain complete target history and shared chunks during GC, even after all branch and public tag roots disappear. If interruption loses the deletion response, inspect the public tag and native tag-archives , then use native tag-archive ID to identify the original name and target. An archived deletion has archive/tags/ID/record entries. Restoration verifies the archive and recreates its original name, target, message and annotation time in one reference transaction. It refuses a name already occupied by a tag or annotation root. The archive itself remains retained. Tags can be deleted and recreated at another revision deliberately; the deleted version remains available through its archive. Explicit backup-protected retirement accepts archive/tags/ID and removes its complete root group atomically. The verified independent backup retains its original recipe and payloads for later restoration. Automatic archive pruning is not implemented. Generic reference/admin APIs can remove these roots, so API-level immutability is not an authorization or signature policy. Removing every protecting root and running destructive GC intentionally makes that version unavailable. Retain all archive roots when preserving or transferring an archive; the record chunk alone does not retain its target. Native revision selectors # Native tag creation, branch creation, status --revision , snapshot diff, checkout preflight/execution and revision export accept these selectors: Selector Resolution 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters Exact revision ID, with priority over identically named labels HEAD Current named or detached committed revision; unborn HEAD is refused heads/NAME Explicit native branch tags/NAME Explicit native tag, with annotation consistency checks NAME A branch or tag, provided only one namespace contains that name A bare name shared by a branch and tag is ambiguous even if both point to the same revision. Use its explicit namespace. A tag named HEAD , or one named with a 64-character hexadecimal string, remains addressable with tags/NAME . Resolution validates the selected revision metadata, without traversing its parents, manifests or file chunks. It is separate from full verification. native switch NAME still selects a literal branch. native checkout TAG restores that revision and moves the selected target, archiving its old head; use native switch --detach TAG to detach instead. The explicit C++ snapshot methods retain their revision-ID arguments; applications can call resolve_native_revision() . Storage, cancellation and API # Reference Value tags/NAME Target revision tag-metadata/NAME Optional canonical annotation chunk archive/tags/ID/record Canonical deletion record chunk, whose object ID is ID archive/tags/ID/target Archived target revision archive/tags/ID/annotation Original annotation chunk when present Annotation and deletion records use existing chunk objects, compression and encryption at rest. Their strict schemas bind names, target IDs and annotation IDs. The archive also records a fresh nonce and deletion time. No object kind, pointer, C ABI or physical storage format changes. Generic object/reference transfers do not yet negotiate an atomic annotated-tag/archive group; dedicated server tag policies and coordinated replication remain future work. All commands support progress and cooperative cancellation. Mutations use the existing complete-closure compare-and-swap reference publication. Cancellation or an observer exception before publication keeps all old roots; newly written unreferenced metadata chunks can remain for reuse or GC. After publication there are no further cancellation points or callbacks. Process interruption leaves either the old reference set or the fully published new set. Tag operations are also allowed while a workspace checkout is pending; they do not alter its branch, HEAD or internal recovery roots. Callbacks must not reenter the Store. cpp Copy auto selected = store . resolve_native_revision ( \"HEAD\" , operation ) ; auto created = store . create_native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , selected , \"Release notes\" , operation ) ; auto page = store . native_tags ( { } , 100 , operation ) ; auto shown = store . native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , true , operation ) ; auto removed = store . delete_native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , selected , operation ) ; auto archived_page = store . native_tag_archives ( { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto saved = store . native_tag_archive ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true , operation ) ; auto restored = store . restore_native_tag ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , operation ) ; The C ABI and Go binding do not expose native tag operations yet. syrnck native tag release/v1 syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify syrnck native tag-show release/v1 syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck gc --delete syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID auto selected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\", operation); auto created = store.create_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, \"Release notes\", operation); auto page = store.native_tags({}, 100, operation); auto shown = store.native_tag(\"release/v1\", true, operation); auto removed = store.delete_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, operation); auto archived_page = store.native_tag_archives({}, 100, true, operation); auto saved = store.native_tag_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation); auto restored = store.restore_native_tag(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives","text":"Native tags, revision selectors and retained archives","level":1},{"id":"read-only-reports-and-pagination","text":"Read-only reports and pagination","level":2},{"id":"archived-deletion-and-restoration","text":"Archived deletion and restoration","level":2},{"id":"native-revision-selectors","text":"Native revision selectors","level":2},{"id":"storage-cancellation-and-api","text":"Storage, cancellation and API","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native tag release/v1\nsyrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\"\nsyrnck native tags --limit 100\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\nsyrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1\nsyrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID\nsyrnck native tag-archives --verify\nsyrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck gc --delete\nsyrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID\nauto selected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\", operation);\nauto created = store.create_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, \"Release notes\", operation);\nauto page = store.native_tags({}, 100, operation);\nauto shown = store.native_tag(\"release/v1\", true, operation);\nauto removed = store.delete_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, operation);\nauto archived_page = store.native_tag_archives({}, 100, true, operation);\nauto saved = store.native_tag_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation);\nauto restored = store.restore_native_tag(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/archive-retention","#/en/native-detached"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-watch","title":"Resident native workspace observation","description":"Resident native workspace observation guide.","section":"Version control","order":410,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"resident-native-workspace-observation\">Resident native workspace observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/resident-native-workspace-observation\" aria-label=\"Link to Resident native workspace observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native watch\u003c/code> keeps a stream of current workspace status. Windows uses\nrecursive directory notifications and Linux uses recursive inotify\nregistrations. Other platforms, explicit \u003ccode>--polling\u003c/code>, unavailable native\nregistration and exceeded registration limits use the polling backend.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native watch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native watch --journal --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native watch-journal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native watch-journal --clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The command emits one compact JSON document per line and flushes each frame.\nJSON keys, event types and reason codes are English in both interface\nlanguages; error messages and stderr progress support English/French. It runs\nuntil interrupted unless a duration or status-update limit is supplied.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This observes changes. It does not create commits, replace files, publish\nreferences, transfer objects, acquire asset locks or resolve concurrent edits.\nIt is the watcher foundation for a future resident synchronization agent. The\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">C ABI and Go workspace sessions\u003c/a> expose the same watcher and\ninventory for embedding, with internally retained batches and caller-owned Store\nhandles. The CLI checkpoint journal is not exposed by those sessions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"status-and-event-lifecycle\">Status and event lifecycle\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/status-and-event-lifecycle\" aria-label=\"Link to Status and event lifecycle\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The watcher is armed before the initial query. Startup always clears the\ndisposable \u003ca href=\"#/en/working-index\">working hash index\u003c/a> and reads selected working\nfiles completely. Consequently a restart observes edits made while the watcher\nwas offline, independently of an earlier cached hash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Normal notifications are coalesced for a fixed bounded interval. Each batch\nupdates a complete in-memory inventory. Files and directory subtrees named by\nnative hints are reselected with the ordinary ignore rules. Regular files in\nthose scopes are read completely, even if their size and timestamps match;\nunrelated subtrees are neither enumerated nor read. Targeted observations do\nnot rewrite the persisted hash cache. Creations, removals, renames and\nfile/directory transitions replace their complete inventory prefixes. A missing,\nignored or regular-file ancestor broadens the scope so stale sibling entries\ncannot survive a deleted/replaced directory. Similar names such as \u003ccode>asset\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>assets\u003c/code> remain distinct prefixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Overlapping hints coalesce to their enclosing scope. A nested \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>\nchange reselects its entire parent subtree, including nested rules and newly\nincluded files. Root ignore changes still force a complete byte scan. Portable\ncase collisions, file/directory collisions and the v1 metadata size bound are\nvalidated across retained and newly selected entries before hashing scoped\ncontents. Selection failures, case-spelling ambiguity, protected storage hints\nand scope budget exhaustion fall back to a full scan; unresolved filesystem or\nnamespace errors remain errors. Symlink/reparse ignore files are refused by\nboth scan paths. Invalid ignore patterns produce a recoverable scan error.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Deletions and renamed paths are observed as\ncurrent filesystem state; event names are hints, not a transaction log or\nguaranteed rename pairs. \u003ccode>--combined\u003c/code> preserves separate staging and working\nchanges, including stale staging. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--revision\u003c/code> follow the same\nselection rules as native status; combined status cannot select a revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Native idle waits do not enumerate project directories or open the Store.\nThe Store exists only during a query and closes before output, waiting or\ncoalescing, so ordinary CLI operations can acquire its exclusive lock.\nActivity in the local cache, object storage and \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code> does not feed back into\nthe observer. Changes to the actual Store&#39;s \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> remain\nvisible. A differently spelled Store alias is checked for filesystem identity\non Windows; a separate \u003ccode>Vault\u003c/code> directory beside a custom \u003ccode>vault\u003c/code> Store on a\ncase-sensitive filesystem is not excluded.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only changed semantic status, completed full scans and new errors produce\nframes. Reused/hash-byte counters and cache generation changes alone do not\nproduce updates. The polling fallback enumerates the workspace at its query\ninterval, but unchanged semantic results are also quiet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"sequence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"type\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"status\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"reason\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"initial\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"backend\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"windows\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"full_scan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"overflow\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"status\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token null keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"clean\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"tracked_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"workspace_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"hashed_bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"counts\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"untracked\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"removed\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"modified\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"unchanged\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"working_index\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"mode\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"stat-v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"previous\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"missing\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"hashed_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"reused_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"supported_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"cached_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"truncated\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"object\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The nested \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> has the ordinary or combined status fields. Working reports\nalso include \u003ccode>observation\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>mode: full|indexed|targeted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>full_scan\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>inspected_files\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>retained_files\u003c/code>. Targeted reports also include\n\u003ccode>scanned_scopes\u003c/code>, the sorted replacement prefixes after overlap coalescing and\nancestor resolution. A removal can inspect zero files and hash zero bytes. Only enumerated queries include the\npersisted \u003ccode>working_index\u003c/code> metrics; targeted queries retain a complete session\ninventory, including files unsupported by the stat cache. A full fallback sets\nthe frame&#39;s \u003ccode>full_scan\u003c/code> flag as well. Report comparison still visits the\nin-memory file maps and reads revision metadata; this is not constant-time\nstatus for large histories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sequence\u003c/code> increases within this process, including error frames. Backends are\n\u003ccode>windows\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>inotify\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>polling\u003c/code>. Status reasons are:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reason\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>initial\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>First successful complete observation, including delayed startup\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>filesystem\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A native notification changed semantic status\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>polling\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A polling query changed semantic status\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>audit\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Scheduled complete byte scan\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>rescan\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Native registration/topology requires a complete byte scan\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>overflow\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Notification/path information was lost or exceeded its budget\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>recovered\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Complete scan after a previously available observation failed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations\">Lost events, audits and unavailable observations\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations\" aria-label=\"Link to Lost events, audits and unavailable observations\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Notification overflow, malformed/incomplete native records, path-budget\noverflow and native backend failure request a full scan and discard incomplete\npath lists. The next query clears the hash cache and reads all selected bytes.\nNon-UTF-8 native names also discard hints and request a full scan; diagnostic\ndisplay text replaces invalid encoding instead of breaking the JSON stream.\nLinux directory creation and move-in events extend registrations on the same\ninotify instance before returning their subtree hints. Each new directory is\narmed before its children are enumerated; the subsequent complete observation\nof that subtree covers earlier writes, and later events stay queued. Unrelated\nregistrations and descriptor/path mappings remain intact. Overlapping additions\ncoalesce, and the persistent directory count/name budget includes both existing\nwatches and queued additions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Deletion, move-out, self-move, ignored-watch and unmount events still rebuild\nthe instance and request a complete byte scan. Existing descriptor paths are\nnever relabeled from rename cookies. Unknown descriptors, malformed records,\noverflow, path/descriptor aliasing and failed/over-budget additive registration\nalso require recovery; unavailable/quota-limited registration can fall back to\npolling. Partial native instances are closed before recovery, and cancellation\nduring extension invalidates continuity for the next wait. Native backend\nfailures can switch to polling.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Independent periodic audits also read all selected working bytes. Normal\nnotifications and polling queries do not postpone their schedule. These scans\ncover notification limitations such as remote filesystem changes, missed\nmetadata notifications and memory-mapped writes. The default is five minutes;\nlarge projects can choose another bounded interval.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An occupied Store, active recoverable checkout or failed scan emits an error\nframe with \u003ccode>type: &quot;error&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>code: &quot;scan_failed&quot;\u003c/code>, a translated \u003ccode>message\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>status: null\u003c/code>. The last successful observation is not represented as current.\nIdentical consecutive errors are suppressed. Retrying waits at least one\nsecond, and successful recovery forces a full scan. The process holds no\nStore lock while retrying. Permanent workspace-root identity changes require\nrestarting the watcher instead of silently following another directory.\nThe observer retains a directory handle for that identity for the session;\nthis does not prevent working writes or acquire a Store lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The command still observes a changing filesystem. It does not provide a locked\nor atomic filesystem snapshot, does not preserve intermediate file versions,\nand does not authorize other operations to skip their own validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal\">Optional persistent checkpoint journal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal\" aria-label=\"Link to Optional persistent checkpoint journal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--journal\u003c/code> records the current coalesced batch before processing native hints\nor a full scan. It writes an \u003ccode>observed\u003c/code> checkpoint before emitting successful\nstatus, or an \u003ccode>unavailable\u003c/code> checkpoint after a scan error when storage remains\naccessible. A checkpoint is one bounded replacement record, not a replayable\nhistory of individual filesystem writes. It contains the workspace and query\nselection digests, random session identity, session generation, previous\nsession/state, backend, bounded path hints and the semantic status digest,\nbase revision, clean flag and file count. It does not store working contents\nor the complete status report.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The stream additionally contains \u003ccode>journal: {session, generation, durable, previous}\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>durable: true\u003c/code> means the corresponding checkpoint was saved before\noutput; it does not acknowledge delivery to the consumer. \u003ccode>sequence\u003c/code> remains\nprocess-local. A process can terminate after checkpoint publication but before\nstdout delivery, so consumers must reestablish current state after reconnecting.\nA busy/unavailable Store can prevent error checkpointing; the error frame then\nhas \u003ccode>durable: false\u003c/code>. Corrupt journals are preserved and block journal mode\nuntil explicitly cleared. Ordinary observation without \u003ccode>--journal\u003c/code> remains\navailable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native watch-journal\u003c/code> reads the last checkpoint, or reports \u003ccode>state: absent\u003c/code>.\nAn \u003ccode>observed\u003c/code> record means the last query finished, not that an observer is\ncurrently alive or that its result is still current. A \u003ccode>pending\u003c/code> record can\nremain after interruption. Startup always creates a new session and performs\na complete byte scan, whether the previous record was pending, observed or\nunavailable; offline edits never resume from the old inventory. The first\ncheckpoint records the old session/state for diagnosis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native watch-journal --clear\u003c/code> explicitly removes the local record, including\nmalformed records within the file-size bound. It does not alter refs, staging,\nobjects or working files. Journal mode holds a separate \u003ccode>watch.lock\u003c/code> for the\nsession, preventing another journal owner or a clear while it runs. The Store\nlock is still released between queries; checkpoint inspection and other CLI\noperations remain available. Non-journal observers need no session lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>watch-journal\u003c/code> file is self-contained: \u003ccode>SYW1\u003c/code>, a 64-byte chunk digest and\nthe existing compressed Store envelope. Payloads use the Store&#39;s existing\nAEAD key when encrypted, including hint names. The digest and encryption mode\nremain visible. The plaintext record is limited to 2 MiB, the stored file to\n3 MiB, and paths to 65,536 entries with the same 1 MiB accounting budget as\ncoalesced hints. Writes use the normal temporary-file flush and atomic durable\nreplacement, with a publication fence after progress callbacks. There is no\nobject reference or temporary retained root; GC and pack compaction cannot\nremove the checkpoint. Logical backups omit this machine-local record.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Native idle waits perform no checkpoint writes. Unchanged polling also keeps\nthe last observed checkpoint without pending writes or repeated flushes. Changed\npolling results are checkpointed before output, and full audits write pending\nthen observed. The journal is recovery metadata for the observer; causal\nversions, transfer outboxes and exactly-once network publication are separate\nwork.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-resource-use\">Limits and resource use\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/limits-and-resource-use\" aria-label=\"Link to Limits and resource use\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The workspace and Store directories must exist. An unreadable root or\nunavailable directory identity is an error before observation starts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Default\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Accepted range\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--interval-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20..60000; polling cadence and native loop wakeup cap\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--debounce-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>75\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0..5000; fixed coalescing window\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--audit-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>300000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1000..3600000; complete working-byte audit interval\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--duration-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 for unlimited, otherwise 100..86400000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-updates\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 for unlimited, otherwise 1..1000000 successful status frames\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-paths\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4096\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1..65536 unique hints per coalesced batch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Duration limits are checked between observations; they do not turn an\nindividual filesystem call into a timed operation. Errors do not consume the\nsuccessful-update limit. Duration expiry returns 1 if the latest observation\nremains unavailable, otherwise 0. Interruption uses the ordinary CLI\ncancellation behavior.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Windows uses a DWORD-aligned 64 KiB notification buffer and a bounded completion\ncopy. Linux uses a 64 KiB read buffer, at most 65,536 directory registrations,\n128 levels and 16 MiB of accounted relative names. Registration limits may\nalso be set lower by the OS. Both native hints and the coalesced CLI batch\nhave a 1 MiB path-accounting budget. Sets, path objects, maps, snapshots,\nreports, kernel queues and allocation overhead are additional; these limits\nare not a total RAM budget. Large output consumers must keep reading; queued\nevents can overflow while output is blocked, requiring a later full scan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Scoped C++ observations accept at most 65,536 hints and 1 MiB of accounted hint\nnames. Each scope traversal accounts ancestor/scope directory names and ignore\nbytes against 16 MiB; gathered selected names also have a conservative 16 MiB\nbudget. Crossing a scope budget requests a full scan. The ordinary full scan\nand the retained+replacement union still enforce the v1 revision metadata\nlower bound. These are accounting limits, not a total RAM or syscall budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">C++ API and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ API and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/watcher.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>WorkspaceWatcher \u003cspan class=\"token function\">watcher\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store_path\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> hints \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> watcher\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">wait\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>chrono\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">500\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>hints\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>full_scan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">    \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">watcher\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">invalidate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// The caller lost its own observation state.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The API has one caller; cancellation may be requested from another thread.\n\u003ccode>invalidate()\u003c/code> makes the next wait return a full-scan request immediately and\ndoes not discard pending notifications. \u003ccode>wait\u003c/code> accepts 0..60000 milliseconds;\n0 checks immediately. Native and polling waits check cancellation at most\n50 ms apart, apart from filesystem/syscall latency. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">C ABI and Go sessions\u003c/a> expose workspace observation and watch batches; the CLI checkpoint journal remains CLI-only.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>WorkspaceObservation(project, store_path)\u003c/code> provides \u003ccode>status(store, revision, hints, operation)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace_status(store, branch, hints, operation)\u003c/code>.\nArm a watcher first, feed every batch without gaps, and request full scans for\nlost continuity. A new observation, \u003ccode>invalidate()\u003c/code>, any failed query and periodic\naudits discard incremental assumptions. Polling enumerates with the persisted\nstat cache between byte audits. Closing and reopening a Store does not lose the\nsession inventory, and collecting its unrooted cache objects does not truncate\nthat inventory. Mutation preflights continue to validate independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"synthetic-performance-measurement\">Observation boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-watch/synthetic-performance-measurement\" aria-label=\"Link to Observation boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>File and subtree changes use targeted observation when continuity is preserved. Linux creates/imports extend registrations per subtree; removals and moves of existing watched directories still rebuild the instance and force a complete byte scan. Real Linux/macOS validation remains necessary. Replayable event history, causal versions, tombstones, transfer scheduling, OS service packaging and full Syncthing daemon/folder interoperability remain separate work. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance\">performance boundaries\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>API references: \u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ReadDirectoryChangesW\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">inotify\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Resident native workspace observation # native watch keeps a stream of current workspace status. Windows uses recursive directory notifications and Linux uses recursive inotify registrations. Other platforms, explicit --polling , unavailable native registration and exceeded registration limits use the polling backend. text Copy syrnck native watch syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10 syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000 syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048 syrnck native watch --journal --combined syrnck native watch-journal syrnck native watch-journal --clear The command emits one compact JSON document per line and flushes each frame. JSON keys, event types and reason codes are English in both interface languages; error messages and stderr progress support English/French. It runs until interrupted unless a duration or status-update limit is supplied. This observes changes. It does not create commits, replace files, publish references, transfer objects, acquire asset locks or resolve concurrent edits. It is the watcher foundation for a future resident synchronization agent. The C ABI and Go workspace sessions expose the same watcher and inventory for embedding, with internally retained batches and caller-owned Store handles. The CLI checkpoint journal is not exposed by those sessions. Status and event lifecycle # The watcher is armed before the initial query. Startup always clears the disposable working hash index and reads selected working files completely. Consequently a restart observes edits made while the watcher was offline, independently of an earlier cached hash. Normal notifications are coalesced for a fixed bounded interval. Each batch updates a complete in-memory inventory. Files and directory subtrees named by native hints are reselected with the ordinary ignore rules. Regular files in those scopes are read completely, even if their size and timestamps match; unrelated subtrees are neither enumerated nor read. Targeted observations do not rewrite the persisted hash cache. Creations, removals, renames and file/directory transitions replace their complete inventory prefixes. A missing, ignored or regular-file ancestor broadens the scope so stale sibling entries cannot survive a deleted/replaced directory. Similar names such as asset and assets remain distinct prefixes. Overlapping hints coalesce to their enclosing scope. A nested .syrnckignore change reselects its entire parent subtree, including nested rules and newly included files. Root ignore changes still force a complete byte scan. Portable case collisions, file/directory collisions and the v1 metadata size bound are validated across retained and newly selected entries before hashing scoped contents. Selection failures, case-spelling ambiguity, protected storage hints and scope budget exhaustion fall back to a full scan; unresolved filesystem or namespace errors remain errors. Symlink/reparse ignore files are refused by both scan paths. Invalid ignore patterns produce a recoverable scan error. Deletions and renamed paths are observed as current filesystem state; event names are hints, not a transaction log or guaranteed rename pairs. --combined preserves separate staging and working changes, including stale staging. --branch or --revision follow the same selection rules as native status; combined status cannot select a revision. Native idle waits do not enumerate project directories or open the Store. The Store exists only during a query and closes before output, waiting or coalescing, so ordinary CLI operations can acquire its exclusive lock. Activity in the local cache, object storage and .git does not feed back into the observer. Changes to the actual Store's refs.json and config.json remain visible. A differently spelled Store alias is checked for filesystem identity on Windows; a separate Vault directory beside a custom vault Store on a case-sensitive filesystem is not excluded. Only changed semantic status, completed full scans and new errors produce frames. Reused/hash-byte counters and cache generation changes alone do not produce updates. The polling fallback enumerates the workspace at its query interval, but unchanged semantic results are also quiet. json Copy { \"version\" : 1 , \"sequence\" : 1 , \"type\" : \"status\" , \"reason\" : \"initial\" , \"backend\" : \"windows\" , \"full_scan\" : true , \"overflow\" : false , \"paths\" : [ ] , \"status\" : { \"version\" : 1 , \"base\" : null , \"clean\" : true , \"tracked_files\" : 0 , \"workspace_files\" : 0 , \"hashed_bytes\" : 0 , \"counts\" : { \"untracked\" : 0 , \"removed\" : 0 , \"modified\" : 0 , \"unchanged\" : 0 } , \"changes\" : [ ] , \"working_index\" : { \"version\" : 1 , \"mode\" : \"stat-v1\" , \"previous\" : \"missing\" , \"hashed_files\" : 0 , \"reused_files\" : 0 , \"supported_files\" : 0 , \"cached_files\" : 0 , \"truncated\" : false , \"object\" : \"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\" } } } The nested status has the ordinary or combined status fields. Working reports also include observation with mode: full|indexed|targeted , full_scan , inspected_files and retained_files . Targeted reports also include scanned_scopes , the sorted replacement prefixes after overlap coalescing and ancestor resolution. A removal can inspect zero files and hash zero bytes. Only enumerated queries include the persisted working_index metrics; targeted queries retain a complete session inventory, including files unsupported by the stat cache. A full fallback sets the frame's full_scan flag as well. Report comparison still visits the in-memory file maps and reads revision metadata; this is not constant-time status for large histories. sequence increases within this process, including error frames. Backends are windows , inotify and polling . Status reasons are: Reason Meaning initial First successful complete observation, including delayed startup filesystem A native notification changed semantic status polling A polling query changed semantic status audit Scheduled complete byte scan rescan Native registration/topology requires a complete byte scan overflow Notification/path information was lost or exceeded its budget recovered Complete scan after a previously available observation failed Lost events, audits and unavailable observations # Notification overflow, malformed/incomplete native records, path-budget overflow and native backend failure request a full scan and discard incomplete path lists. The next query clears the hash cache and reads all selected bytes. Non-UTF-8 native names also discard hints and request a full scan; diagnostic display text replaces invalid encoding instead of breaking the JSON stream. Linux directory creation and move-in events extend registrations on the same inotify instance before returning their subtree hints. Each new directory is armed before its children are enumerated; the subsequent complete observation of that subtree covers earlier writes, and later events stay queued. Unrelated registrations and descriptor/path mappings remain intact. Overlapping additions coalesce, and the persistent directory count/name budget includes both existing watches and queued additions. Deletion, move-out, self-move, ignored-watch and unmount events still rebuild the instance and request a complete byte scan. Existing descriptor paths are never relabeled from rename cookies. Unknown descriptors, malformed records, overflow, path/descriptor aliasing and failed/over-budget additive registration also require recovery; unavailable/quota-limited registration can fall back to polling. Partial native instances are closed before recovery, and cancellation during extension invalidates continuity for the next wait. Native backend failures can switch to polling. Independent periodic audits also read all selected working bytes. Normal notifications and polling queries do not postpone their schedule. These scans cover notification limitations such as remote filesystem changes, missed metadata notifications and memory-mapped writes. The default is five minutes; large projects can choose another bounded interval. An occupied Store, active recoverable checkout or failed scan emits an error frame with type: \"error\" , code: \"scan_failed\" , a translated message and status: null . The last successful observation is not represented as current. Identical consecutive errors are suppressed. Retrying waits at least one second, and successful recovery forces a full scan. The process holds no Store lock while retrying. Permanent workspace-root identity changes require restarting the watcher instead of silently following another directory. The observer retains a directory handle for that identity for the session; this does not prevent working writes or acquire a Store lock. The command still observes a changing filesystem. It does not provide a locked or atomic filesystem snapshot, does not preserve intermediate file versions, and does not authorize other operations to skip their own validation. Optional persistent checkpoint journal # --journal records the current coalesced batch before processing native hints or a full scan. It writes an observed checkpoint before emitting successful status, or an unavailable checkpoint after a scan error when storage remains accessible. A checkpoint is one bounded replacement record, not a replayable history of individual filesystem writes. It contains the workspace and query selection digests, random session identity, session generation, previous session/state, backend, bounded path hints and the semantic status digest, base revision, clean flag and file count. It does not store working contents or the complete status report. The stream additionally contains journal: {session, generation, durable, previous} . durable: true means the corresponding checkpoint was saved before output; it does not acknowledge delivery to the consumer. sequence remains process-local. A process can terminate after checkpoint publication but before stdout delivery, so consumers must reestablish current state after reconnecting. A busy/unavailable Store can prevent error checkpointing; the error frame then has durable: false . Corrupt journals are preserved and block journal mode until explicitly cleared. Ordinary observation without --journal remains available. native watch-journal reads the last checkpoint, or reports state: absent . An observed record means the last query finished, not that an observer is currently alive or that its result is still current. A pending record can remain after interruption. Startup always creates a new session and performs a complete byte scan, whether the previous record was pending, observed or unavailable; offline edits never resume from the old inventory. The first checkpoint records the old session/state for diagnosis. native watch-journal --clear explicitly removes the local record, including malformed records within the file-size bound. It does not alter refs, staging, objects or working files. Journal mode holds a separate watch.lock for the session, preventing another journal owner or a clear while it runs. The Store lock is still released between queries; checkpoint inspection and other CLI operations remain available. Non-journal observers need no session lock. The watch-journal file is self-contained: SYW1 , a 64-byte chunk digest and the existing compressed Store envelope. Payloads use the Store's existing AEAD key when encrypted, including hint names. The digest and encryption mode remain visible. The plaintext record is limited to 2 MiB, the stored file to 3 MiB, and paths to 65,536 entries with the same 1 MiB accounting budget as coalesced hints. Writes use the normal temporary-file flush and atomic durable replacement, with a publication fence after progress callbacks. There is no object reference or temporary retained root; GC and pack compaction cannot remove the checkpoint. Logical backups omit this machine-local record. Native idle waits perform no checkpoint writes. Unchanged polling also keeps the last observed checkpoint without pending writes or repeated flushes. Changed polling results are checkpointed before output, and full audits write pending then observed. The journal is recovery metadata for the observer; causal versions, transfer outboxes and exactly-once network publication are separate work. Limits and resource use # The workspace and Store directories must exist. An unreadable root or unavailable directory identity is an error before observation starts. Option Default Accepted range --interval-ms 500 20..60000; polling cadence and native loop wakeup cap --debounce-ms 75 0..5000; fixed coalescing window --audit-ms 300000 1000..3600000; complete working-byte audit interval --duration-ms 0 0 for unlimited, otherwise 100..86400000 --max-updates 0 0 for unlimited, otherwise 1..1000000 successful status frames --max-paths 4096 1..65536 unique hints per coalesced batch Duration limits are checked between observations; they do not turn an individual filesystem call into a timed operation. Errors do not consume the successful-update limit. Duration expiry returns 1 if the latest observation remains unavailable, otherwise 0. Interruption uses the ordinary CLI cancellation behavior. Windows uses a DWORD-aligned 64 KiB notification buffer and a bounded completion copy. Linux uses a 64 KiB read buffer, at most 65,536 directory registrations, 128 levels and 16 MiB of accounted relative names. Registration limits may also be set lower by the OS. Both native hints and the coalesced CLI batch have a 1 MiB path-accounting budget. Sets, path objects, maps, snapshots, reports, kernel queues and allocation overhead are additional; these limits are not a total RAM budget. Large output consumers must keep reading; queued events can overflow while output is blocked, requiring a later full scan. Scoped C++ observations accept at most 65,536 hints and 1 MiB of accounted hint names. Each scope traversal accounts ancestor/scope directory names and ignore bytes against 16 MiB; gathered selected names also have a conservative 16 MiB budget. Crossing a scope budget requests a full scan. The ordinary full scan and the retained+replacement union still enforce the v1 revision metadata lower bound. These are accounting limits, not a total RAM or syscall budget. C++ API and validation # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp> syrnck :: WorkspaceWatcher watcher ( project , store_path , { } , stop ) ; // Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction. auto hints = watcher . wait ( std :: chrono :: milliseconds ( 500 ) , stop ) ; if ( hints . full_scan ) { // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints. } watcher . invalidate ( ) ; // The caller lost its own observation state. The API has one caller; cancellation may be requested from another thread. invalidate() makes the next wait return a full-scan request immediately and does not discard pending notifications. wait accepts 0..60000 milliseconds; 0 checks immediately. Native and polling waits check cancellation at most 50 ms apart, apart from filesystem/syscall latency. The C ABI and Go sessions expose workspace observation and watch batches; the CLI checkpoint journal remains CLI-only. WorkspaceObservation(project, store_path) provides status(store, revision, hints, operation) and workspace_status(store, branch, hints, operation) . Arm a watcher first, feed every batch without gaps, and request full scans for lost continuity. A new observation, invalidate() , any failed query and periodic audits discard incremental assumptions. Polling enumerates with the persisted stat cache between byte audits. Closing and reopening a Store does not lose the session inventory, and collecting its unrooted cache objects does not truncate that inventory. Mutation preflights continue to validate independently. Observation boundaries # File and subtree changes use targeted observation when continuity is preserved. Linux creates/imports extend registrations per subtree; removals and moves of existing watched directories still rebuild the instance and force a complete byte scan. Real Linux/macOS validation remains necessary. Replayable event history, causal versions, tombstones, transfer scheduling, OS service packaging and full Syncthing daemon/folder interoperability remain separate work. See performance boundaries . API references: ReadDirectoryChangesW , inotify . syrnck native watch syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10 syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000 syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048 syrnck native watch --journal --combined syrnck native watch-journal syrnck native watch-journal --clear {\"version\":1,\"sequence\":1,\"type\":\"status\",\"reason\":\"initial\",\"backend\":\"windows\",\"full_scan\":true,\"overflow\":false,\"paths\":[],\"status\":{\"version\":1,\"base\":null,\"clean\":true,\"tracked_files\":0,\"workspace_files\":0,\"hashed_bytes\":0,\"counts\":{\"untracked\":0,\"removed\":0,\"modified\":0,\"unchanged\":0},\"changes\":[],\"working_index\":{\"version\":1,\"mode\":\"stat-v1\",\"previous\":\"missing\",\"hashed_files\":0,\"reused_files\":0,\"supported_files\":0,\"cached_files\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"object\":\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"}}} #include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp> syrnck::WorkspaceWatcher watcher(project, store_path, {}, stop); // Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction. auto hints = watcher.wait(std::chrono::milliseconds(500), stop); if (hints.full_scan) { // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints. } watcher.invalidate(); // The caller lost its own observation state.","headings":[{"id":"resident-native-workspace-observation","text":"Resident native workspace observation","level":1},{"id":"status-and-event-lifecycle","text":"Status and event lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations","text":"Lost events, audits and unavailable observations","level":2},{"id":"optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal","text":"Optional persistent checkpoint journal","level":2},{"id":"limits-and-resource-use","text":"Limits and resource use","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"C++ API and validation","level":2},{"id":"synthetic-performance-measurement","text":"Observation boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native watch\nsyrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10\nsyrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000\nsyrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048\nsyrnck native watch --journal --combined\nsyrnck native watch-journal\nsyrnck native watch-journal --clear\n{\"version\":1,\"sequence\":1,\"type\":\"status\",\"reason\":\"initial\",\"backend\":\"windows\",\"full_scan\":true,\"overflow\":false,\"paths\":[],\"status\":{\"version\":1,\"base\":null,\"clean\":true,\"tracked_files\":0,\"workspace_files\":0,\"hashed_bytes\":0,\"counts\":{\"untracked\":0,\"removed\":0,\"modified\":0,\"unchanged\":0},\"changes\":[],\"working_index\":{\"version\":1,\"mode\":\"stat-v1\",\"previous\":\"missing\",\"hashed_files\":0,\"reused_files\":0,\"supported_files\":0,\"cached_files\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"object\":\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"}}}\n#include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp>\nsyrnck::WorkspaceWatcher watcher(project, store_path, {}, stop);\n// Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction.\nauto hints = watcher.wait(std::chrono::milliseconds(500), stop);\nif (hints.full_scan) {\n    // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints.\n}\nwatcher.invalidate(); // The caller lost its own observation state.","assets":[],"links":["#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/working-index","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/performance","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw","https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-workspace-archives","title":"Archive and restore a workspace","description":"Unregister linked workspaces while retaining dirty files, prepared changes and history.","section":"Version control","order":223,"parent":"native-workspaces","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"unregister\">Unregister a linked workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/unregister\" aria-label=\"Link to Unregister a linked workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the exact \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> from the preview, with the same \u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code>\nchoice. The commands select the named linked context themselves. The primary\nworkspace cannot be unregistered, and pending checkouts must be recovered first.\nThe preview reads files and references without publishing objects or metadata.\nChanged captured contents, selection or context references invalidate the plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application captures the physical working state and retains a canonical archive\nbefore removing the registration and every \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/\u003c/code> reference in one\nreference transaction. It leaves the original directory and all its files in\nplace and releases the branch reservation. Shared branches and primary HEAD\nremain unchanged. The C++ Store handle returns to the primary context.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contents\">What is retained\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/contents\" aria-label=\"Link to What is retained\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The archive keeps three independent states:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The complete committed HEAD, including files outside a partial selection.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The complete frozen index, when present, with its original parent, even if stale.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A physical recovery revision containing selected tracked files, their local\nchanges and regular-file obstructions to the logical tree. Deletions remain\nabsent from that physical snapshot. \u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code> additionally captures\nselected nonignored regular files discovered by the native scanner.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Every original context reference is retained through an explicit typed archive\nroot, including inactive indexes and completed recovery/history references.\nGC, packing and \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">backups\u003c/a> preserve their object closures.\nIgnored and unselected local files remain in the original directory; they are\nnot promised in the recovery snapshot. Empty directories are not versioned.\nUnsafe paths, links or protected metadata can block a plan; inspect \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> and\nthe reported blockers before applying it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The inventory accepts \u003ccode>--after ID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--limit N\u003c/code> (1..1000, default 100) and\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. IDs are stable archive object IDs; pagination orders them by ID.\n\u003ccode>workspace-archive\u003c/code> displays the saved reference recipe and physical revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore\">Restore into a new directory\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/restore\" aria-label=\"Link to Restore into a new directory\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run restoration from the primary context. The destination&#39;s parent must exist;\nthe destination must not overlap the primary, Store or another registered\nworkspace. An existing directory is reused only if its regular files and modes\nmatch the complete saved physical snapshot. Extra or changed files and links\nare refused. An existing exact snapshot may contain additional empty directories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The original named or detached selection is the default. \u003ccode>--branch NEW_NAME\u003c/code>\nrestores under another branch; \u003ccode>--detach\u003c/code> creates a private HEAD. A named branch\nmust be absent or still point at the saved HEAD and be unreserved. Restoration\nnever resets an advanced branch. HEAD retains the committed revision, the\nworking directory contains the captured dirty bytes, and the prepared index\nremains separate. Changing its selector rewrites only the index message; it\ndoes not silently rebase a stale index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The physical export is published before registration. Retry the same command\nand plan after interruption: an exact completed export is reused, and an exact\ncompleted registration is idempotent. Changes to files or bindings are refused.\nThe saved partial selection is restored, while physical ancestor obstructions\nare restored in full rather than filtered out. Earlier inactive context roots\nremain accessible through the archive; they are not reactivated in the new name.\nThe new context also pins its physical recovery revision independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Backups and archive retirement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Backups and archive retirement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Retirement requires the verified backup to retain the exact complete archive\ngroup. A member such as \u003ccode>/record\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/roots/...\u003c/code> cannot be\nretired individually. Retirement removes roots, without deleting working files\nor running GC. A restored context keeps its physical recovery pin even after\nthe source archive is retired. Its selected HEAD/index remain ordinary roots;\ninactive original context references then depend on the verified backup or\nother surviving roots. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">archive retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For recovery after local GC, restore that backup into a separate Store, then\nrun \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore-plan\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore\u003c/code> there with\nthe recovered primary directory supplied through \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>. Historical\nabsolute source bindings are recipes, not active registrations; the original\ndirectory need not exist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">C++ API and current limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives/api\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ API and current limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_unregistration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archived \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">unregister_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> archived\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L8\" data-line=\"8\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>detached \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L9\" data-line=\"9\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restore \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_archive_restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L11\" data-line=\"11\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_workspace_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L12\" data-line=\"12\">    destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> restore\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The header also exposes archive inspection and paginated inventory. Operations\naccept cancellation and progress callbacks. Files and refs are rechecked after\nthe last publishing callback, without calling observers during final publication.\nExternal writers still require coordination; this is not an atomic filesystem\nsnapshot. Process-interruption tests do not establish power-loss durability.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation\">Linked relocation on one filesystem\u003c/a> is available;\ncross-filesystem moves and automatic archive age/release policies remain separate work. Encrypted Stores protect archived objects at rest; working exports and\ntheir temporary files have the usual plaintext workspace semantics.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Unregister a linked workspace # text Copy syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native workspace-archives --verify syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify Use the exact plan from the preview, with the same --include-untracked choice. The commands select the named linked context themselves. The primary workspace cannot be unregistered, and pending checkouts must be recovered first. The preview reads files and references without publishing objects or metadata. Changed captured contents, selection or context references invalidate the plan. Application captures the physical working state and retains a canonical archive before removing the registration and every workspaces/NAME/ reference in one reference transaction. It leaves the original directory and all its files in place and releases the branch reservation. Shared branches and primary HEAD remain unchanged. The C++ Store handle returns to the primary context. What is retained # The archive keeps three independent states: The complete committed HEAD, including files outside a partial selection. The complete frozen index, when present, with its original parent, even if stale. A physical recovery revision containing selected tracked files, their local changes and regular-file obstructions to the logical tree. Deletions remain absent from that physical snapshot. --include-untracked additionally captures selected nonignored regular files discovered by the native scanner. Every original context reference is retained through an explicit typed archive root, including inactive indexes and completed recovery/history references. GC, packing and backups preserve their object closures. Ignored and unselected local files remain in the original directory; they are not promised in the recovery snapshot. Empty directories are not versioned. Unsafe paths, links or protected metadata can block a plan; inspect ready and the reported blockers before applying it. The inventory accepts --after ID , --limit N (1..1000, default 100) and --verify . IDs are stable archive object IDs; pagination orders them by ID. workspace-archive displays the saved reference recipe and physical revision. Restore into a new directory # text Copy syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined Run restoration from the primary context. The destination's parent must exist; the destination must not overlap the primary, Store or another registered workspace. An existing directory is reused only if its regular files and modes match the complete saved physical snapshot. Extra or changed files and links are refused. An existing exact snapshot may contain additional empty directories. The original named or detached selection is the default. --branch NEW_NAME restores under another branch; --detach creates a private HEAD. A named branch must be absent or still point at the saved HEAD and be unreserved. Restoration never resets an advanced branch. HEAD retains the committed revision, the working directory contains the captured dirty bytes, and the prepared index remains separate. Changing its selector rewrites only the index message; it does not silently rebase a stale index. The physical export is published before registration. Retry the same command and plan after interruption: an exact completed export is reused, and an exact completed registration is idempotent. Changes to files or bindings are refused. The saved partial selection is restored, while physical ancestor obstructions are restored in full rather than filtered out. Earlier inactive context roots remain accessible through the archive; they are not reactivated in the new name. The new context also pins its physical recovery revision independently. Backups and archive retirement # text Copy syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN Retirement requires the verified backup to retain the exact complete archive group. A member such as /record , /head , /work or /roots/... cannot be retired individually. Retirement removes roots, without deleting working files or running GC. A restored context keeps its physical recovery pin even after the source archive is retired. Its selected HEAD/index remain ordinary roots; inactive original context references then depend on the verified backup or other surviving roots. See archive retention . For recovery after local GC, restore that backup into a separate Store, then run workspace-archive-restore-plan and workspace-archive-restore there with the recovered primary directory supplied through --directory . Historical absolute source bindings are recipes, not active registrations; the original directory need not exist. C++ API and current limits # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store . use_native_workspace ( \"edit\" ) ; auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_unregistration ( store , true , true ) ; auto archived = syrnck :: unregister_native_workspace ( store , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true ) ; auto id = archived [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) ; syrnck :: NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings ; settings . detached = true ; auto restore = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_archive_restore ( store , primary , id , \"recovered\" , destination , settings , true ) ; syrnck :: restore_native_workspace_archive ( store , primary , id , \"recovered\" , destination , restore [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , settings ) ; The header also exposes archive inspection and paginated inventory. Operations accept cancellation and progress callbacks. Files and refs are rechecked after the last publishing callback, without calling observers during final publication. External writers still require coordination; this is not an atomic filesystem snapshot. Process-interruption tests do not establish power-loss durability. Linked relocation on one filesystem is available; cross-filesystem moves and automatic archive age/release policies remain separate work. Encrypted Stores protect archived objects at rest; working exports and their temporary files have the usual plaintext workspace semantics. syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native workspace-archives --verify syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN #include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store.use_native_workspace(\"edit\"); auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_unregistration(store, true, true); auto archived = syrnck::unregister_native_workspace( store, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true); auto id = archived[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(); syrnck::NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings; settings.detached = true; auto restore = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_archive_restore( store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, settings, true); syrnck::restore_native_workspace_archive(store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, restore[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), settings);","headings":[{"id":"unregister","text":"Unregister a linked workspace","level":2},{"id":"contents","text":"What is retained","level":2},{"id":"restore","text":"Restore into a new directory","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Backups and archive retirement","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"C++ API and current limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5","code-4-L6","code-4-L7","code-4-L8","code-4-L9","code-4-L10","code-4-L11","code-4-L12"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native workspace-archives --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\nsyrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN\n#include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp>\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"edit\");\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_unregistration(store, true, true);\nauto archived = syrnck::unregister_native_workspace(\n    store, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true);\nauto id = archived[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>();\nsyrnck::NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings;\nsettings.detached = true;\nauto restore = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_archive_restore(\n    store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, settings, true);\nsyrnck::restore_native_workspace_archive(store, primary, id, \"recovered\",\n    destination, restore[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), settings);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/backups","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/native-workspace-relocation"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-workspace-relocation","title":"Move a linked workspace","description":"Recoverable directory relocation, including case changes and unversioned local data.","section":"Version control","order":224,"parent":"native-workspaces","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Preview and move\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Preview and move\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run from the primary context and use the exact preview&#39;s \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code>. The source must\nbe a registered linked workspace. The destination&#39;s parent must exist and be on\nthe same filesystem; the destination must be absent. An existing case alias of\nthe source is allowed for a change such as \u003ccode>project-edit\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>Project-Edit\u003c/code>.\nAn independently occupied case variant is refused on case-sensitive filesystems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The operation moves the entire directory object, including dirty and staged\nworking files, ignored and unselected files, empty directories and descendant\nlinks. It does not traverse links, reconstruct files from HEAD or reingest\nworking data. Logical HEAD, index, partial selection and shared branches stay\nunchanged. Only the linked registration and HEAD directory binding change.\nThe primary directory and Store remain in place.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Planning reads directory identities and metadata without writing objects or\nchanging files. Its identity binds the complete reference table, old bindings,\nsource directory identity and both parent identities. It does not freeze file\ncontents: edits inside the same directory object move with that object. Keep\neditors, watchers and other tools coordinated during the operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Resume or cancel a pending move\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Resume or cancel a pending move\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use the primary context for recovery, even if the old linked directory is\ntemporarily absent. A pending transaction identifies the physical location as\n\u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>destination\u003c/code>. Forward recovery reaches the destination;\nabort returns the same directory object to its original path before retaining\nthe original bindings. Neither path recursively deletes working data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The journal is retained before the first filesystem move. Every relocation uses\na unique sibling bridge named \u003ccode>.syrnck-relocate-NONCE\u003c/code>, including changes of\ncase. Exclusive renames refuse an occupied destination. Registration and HEAD\npublish together only after the physical destination has the saved directory\nidentity. After interruption, recovery inspects actual identities rather than\nassuming a rename completed. Do not remove or repurpose the bridge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rerunning \u003ccode>workspace-relocate\u003c/code> with the same name, destination and plan resumes\nits matching pending move. A completed matching operation returns its retained\nreceipt when the current bindings and destination identity still match. A changed\nbinding or replaced directory requires inspection; it is not silently adopted.\nStatus/recovery return \u003ccode>pending: false, state: none\u003c/code> when no transaction is active.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety\">Safety and scope\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/safety\" aria-label=\"Link to Safety and scope\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The \u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code> reader requirement is enabled before journaling.\nOlder readers refuse the Store. During a pending move, other reference changes,\nnative mutations, GC and backup creation are blocked until recovery finishes.\nDirectory and parent replacements, root links/reparse points, ambiguous identity,\noverlaps and foreign occupied paths are refused. A raw reference edit cannot\nrebind a live linked registration around this protocol.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Directory identities use Windows volume/file IDs or POSIX device/inode IDs.\nUnsupported identity providers fail closed. Final callbacks run before renewed\nidentity/reference checks, with no observers inside the final publication.\nThis coordinates Syrnck writers; arbitrary external filesystem writers still\nrequire coordination. Process-interruption checks do not establish power-loss\ndurability or qualify every network filesystem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cross-filesystem moves and relocation of the primary workspace or Store are\nnot implemented by these commands. Completed relocation receipts remain\nretained; their age-based cleanup is not automated. Store backups retain\nlogical objects and receipts, while unprepared local working bytes still need\nan explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives\">workspace archive\u003c/a> or a filesystem\nbackup. Moving a directory does not turn its plaintext working files into\nencrypted files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">C++ API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/api\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_relocation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">relocate_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> pending \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_workspace_relocation_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_native_workspace_relocation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Pass \u003ccode>true\u003c/code> for abort. These are C++ operations; the C and Go interfaces keep\ntheir existing ABI. Progress/cancellation may leave a pending move, whose\nretained journal is required for subsequent resume or abort.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format\">Journal format\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/format\" aria-label=\"Link to Journal format\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-relocations/active\u003c/code> retains a canonical native-workspace-relocation\nv1 chunk with fourteen fields: \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nonce\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>destination\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>identity\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_parent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>destination_parent\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>registration\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>refs_hash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code>. Bindings are stored inline, without\nhidden object dependencies. Existing context roots remain in place while pending.\nThe central reference validator accepts only the exact start/finalization\ntransitions. Finalization removes the active root and adds\n\u003ccode>archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/aborted\u003c/code>, retaining the record.\nNormal object, revision, pack and C ABI versions remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Preview and move # text Copy syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head Run from the primary context and use the exact preview's plan . The source must be a registered linked workspace. The destination's parent must exist and be on the same filesystem; the destination must be absent. An existing case alias of the source is allowed for a change such as project-edit to Project-Edit . An independently occupied case variant is refused on case-sensitive filesystems. The operation moves the entire directory object, including dirty and staged working files, ignored and unselected files, empty directories and descendant links. It does not traverse links, reconstruct files from HEAD or reingest working data. Logical HEAD, index, partial selection and shared branches stay unchanged. Only the linked registration and HEAD directory binding change. The primary directory and Store remain in place. Planning reads directory identities and metadata without writing objects or changing files. Its identity binds the complete reference table, old bindings, source directory identity and both parent identities. It does not freeze file contents: edits inside the same directory object move with that object. Keep editors, watchers and other tools coordinated during the operation. Resume or cancel a pending move # text Copy syrnck native workspace-relocation-status syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort Use the primary context for recovery, even if the old linked directory is temporarily absent. A pending transaction identifies the physical location as source , bridge or destination . Forward recovery reaches the destination; abort returns the same directory object to its original path before retaining the original bindings. Neither path recursively deletes working data. The journal is retained before the first filesystem move. Every relocation uses a unique sibling bridge named .syrnck-relocate-NONCE , including changes of case. Exclusive renames refuse an occupied destination. Registration and HEAD publish together only after the physical destination has the saved directory identity. After interruption, recovery inspects actual identities rather than assuming a rename completed. Do not remove or repurpose the bridge. Rerunning workspace-relocate with the same name, destination and plan resumes its matching pending move. A completed matching operation returns its retained receipt when the current bindings and destination identity still match. A changed binding or replaced directory requires inspection; it is not silently adopted. Status/recovery return pending: false, state: none when no transaction is active. Safety and scope # The workspace-relocation-v1 reader requirement is enabled before journaling. Older readers refuse the Store. During a pending move, other reference changes, native mutations, GC and backup creation are blocked until recovery finishes. Directory and parent replacements, root links/reparse points, ambiguous identity, overlaps and foreign occupied paths are refused. A raw reference edit cannot rebind a live linked registration around this protocol. Directory identities use Windows volume/file IDs or POSIX device/inode IDs. Unsupported identity providers fail closed. Final callbacks run before renewed identity/reference checks, with no observers inside the final publication. This coordinates Syrnck writers; arbitrary external filesystem writers still require coordination. Process-interruption checks do not establish power-loss durability or qualify every network filesystem. Cross-filesystem moves and relocation of the primary workspace or Store are not implemented by these commands. Completed relocation receipts remain retained; their age-based cleanup is not automated. Store backups retain logical objects and receipts, while unprepared local working bytes still need an explicit workspace archive or a filesystem backup. Moving a directory does not turn its plaintext working files into encrypted files. C++ API # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store . use_native_workspace ( \"\" ) ; auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_relocation ( store , \"edit\" , destination ) ; syrnck :: relocate_native_workspace ( store , \"edit\" , destination , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , operation ) ; auto pending = syrnck :: native_workspace_relocation_status ( store ) ; syrnck :: recover_native_workspace_relocation ( store , false , operation ) ; Pass true for abort. These are C++ operations; the C and Go interfaces keep their existing ABI. Progress/cancellation may leave a pending move, whose retained journal is required for subsequent resume or abort. Journal format # workspace-relocations/active retains a canonical native-workspace-relocation v1 chunk with fourteen fields: version , type , name , nonce , source , destination , bridge , identity , source_parent , destination_parent , registration , head , refs_hash , plan . Bindings are stored inline, without hidden object dependencies. Existing context roots remain in place while pending. The central reference validator accepts only the exact start/finalization transitions. Finalization removes the active root and adds archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed or /aborted , retaining the record. Normal object, revision, pack and C ABI versions remain unchanged. syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck native workspace-relocation-status syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort #include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store.use_native_workspace(\"\"); auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_relocation(store, \"edit\", destination); syrnck::relocate_native_workspace(store, \"edit\", destination, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation); auto pending = syrnck::native_workspace_relocation_status(store); syrnck::recover_native_workspace_relocation(store, false, operation);","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Preview and move","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Resume or cancel a pending move","level":2},{"id":"safety","text":"Safety and scope","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"C++ API","level":2},{"id":"format","text":"Journal format","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit\nsyrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-status\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-recover\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort\n#include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp>\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"\");\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_relocation(store, \"edit\", destination);\nsyrnck::relocate_native_workspace(store, \"edit\", destination,\n    plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);\nauto pending = syrnck::native_workspace_relocation_status(store);\nsyrnck::recover_native_workspace_relocation(store, false, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-workspace-archives"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-workspace","title":"Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight","description":"Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight guide.","section":"Version control","order":420,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight\">Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace/native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight\" aria-label=\"Link to Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The native engine provides a combined branch/index/workspace status and a\nread-only checkout plan. Neither operation changes files, objects, references\nor staging. A plan does not execute a checkout; the separate\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">recoverable checkout\u003c/a> prepares verified outputs, rechecks\nthe workspace and journals file moves before publishing the branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both commands default to the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches\">current native branch\u003c/a>\n(\u003ccode>main\u003c/code> for legacy Stores) and directory \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>. The Store must be\ninside the workspace; checkout planning requires it strictly below the workspace\nroot. Explicit HEAD binds CLI workspace commands to that canonical root. Output\nkeys, action names and conflict codes are English in both interface languages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"combined-status\">Combined status\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace/combined-status\" aria-label=\"Link to Combined status\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--indexed\u003c/code> can opt into the \u003ca href=\"#/en/working-index\">persistent working hash cache\u003c/a>\nfor the unstaged side. That mode writes disposable cache data; ordinary combined\nstatus keeps the full-read/read-only contract described here.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native status --combined\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>staged\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>unstaged\u003c/code>. The nested \u003ccode>staged\u003c/code> report is the existing\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-staging\">\u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code> report\u003c/a>: its recorded base is compared with\nthe immutable index. The nested \u003ccode>unstaged\u003c/code> report is a\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-comparison\">working-directory status\u003c/a> against that index. With no\nindex, the effective comparison is against the branch head; an unborn branch\nuses an empty baseline.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A path can appear in both reports. For example, stage \u003ccode>prepared\u003c/code> over baseline\n\u003ccode>original\u003c/code>, then restore the working file to \u003ccode>original\u003c/code>. Ordinary branch status\nis clean, but combined status reports a staged modification and an unstaged\nmodification. This keeps the pending commit distinct from later working edits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An index whose base differs from the current branch remains inspectable against\nits recorded base. Working files are still compared with its frozen snapshot.\nOverall \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code> requires both comparisons to be clean and the index to be fresh.\nStaleness alone therefore makes the combined report unclean. A dirty report\nstill exits 0. \u003ccode>--combined\u003c/code> cannot be combined with \u003ccode>--revision\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The working scan retains ordinary status ignore rules, regular-file requirements\nand full BLAKE3 hashing. It does not ingest files or audit chunk payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checkout-plan-and-verification\">Checkout plan and verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace/checkout-plan-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Checkout plan and verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout-plan TARGET\u003c/code> compares the current \u003ccode>heads/BRANCH\u003c/code> snapshot with\nan exact target revision. It inventories the actual workspace and hashes every\ntracked baseline file in full. Timestamps and sizes never establish equality.\nThe first policy, \u003ccode>clean-tracked-v1\u003c/code>, requires \u003cstrong>all tracked files to be clean\u003c/strong>,\nincluding files whose target entry would remain unchanged. Any explicit staging\nindex blocks this preflight, including an index without staged changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The plan includes:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Baseline revision (or null) and exact target revision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>staging_snapshot\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Existing index ID, or null\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>policy\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Preflight policy and whether it found no blockers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whether complete target closure verification was requested and succeeded\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>executable_metadata_supported\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>False on Windows; executable bits cannot be inspected/applied there\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>inventory_entries\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Files, directories and opaque/protected entries inventoried\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>hashed_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Working baseline file bytes read, excluding stored-object verification\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>restore_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logical target bytes for proposed create/replace actions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>counts\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>actions\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Proposed snapshot changes and unchanged count\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>create_directories\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Missing target parent directories, sorted\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Sorted unique blockers with \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>obstruction\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Actions are sorted by repository path. Each action contains \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>target\u003c/code>\nfile summaries with manifest, size, file hash and executable metadata. Missing\nsides are null. These actions describe the candidate snapshot even if conflicts\nblock it; they are not an ordered transaction program.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>create\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Target file has no baseline entry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>replace\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Existing file has different target contents\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Baseline file has no target entry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>set_executable\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Contents match and executable metadata differs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>reuse\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Contents/mode match but manifest representation differs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>unchanged\u003c/code> count\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>File entries match; no action row\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>restore_bytes\u003c/code> is a file-level logical count, not network traffic or unique\nchunk bytes. \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-comparison\">Manifest reuse plans\u003c/a> provide block-level\ncomparison separately. Windows plans retain desired executable changes in the\nsnapshot comparison, but do not use local executable bits as a cleanliness test.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Default planning requires baseline/target revision and file-manifest metadata.\nIt reads no target chunk payloads or historical parents and can plan from a\nmetadata-only partial Store. Thus \u003ccode>ready: true, objects_verified: false\u003c/code> means\nthe filesystem preflight passed; it does not prove reconstruction is possible.\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> additionally verifies the target&#39;s entire closure, including parents\nand file hashes, as ordinary native export does. Missing/corrupt dependencies\nfail the command without returning a partial plan. Verification still runs when\nthe filesystem report contains blockers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A completed ready plan exits 0. A completed blocked plan emits its JSON and\nexits 1. Invalid arguments, IO errors, missing required metadata or verification\nerrors emit a diagnostic on stderr and no JSON report.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits\">Protected paths and first-policy limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace/protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Protected paths and first-policy limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Unlike ordinary status, checkout preflight does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> apply \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>.\nIgnored files are inventoried so they cannot hide a local edit or collision.\nUnrelated untracked/ignored files are preserved by the proposed action list;\ntheir contents are not hashed. A pre-existing untracked target file blocks the\nplan even if its bytes happen to match the desired target.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Conflict reason\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tracked_modified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tracked_missing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Local tracked edit/deletion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tracked_mode_changed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Local Unix executable metadata differs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>staging_present\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stale_staging\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The branch has a pending or stale index; path is empty\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>checkout_pending\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A checkout journal requires recovery; path is empty\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>untracked_collision\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A target would occupy an untracked path, or an affected directory contains untracked/ignored entries\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>file_directory_transition\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A file/directory conversion or ancestor obstruction includes an entry outside the tracked transition\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>path_spelling\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A name matches only after Unicode normalization/case folding\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>portable_collision\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Multiple physical names fold to the same affected name\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>symlink_obstruction\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>special_file\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An affected path or ancestor is not an ordinary file/directory\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>protected_path\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The operation would enter the Store or another protected entry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Static symlinks and Windows reparse points, including junctions, are opaque.\nThe inventory never deliberately traverses them. \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code> names and\nthe actual Store directory are protected and their contents are excluded from\nthe workspace inventory. Custom Store names receive the same protection.\nClean tracked case/normalization changes and file/directory transitions are\nplanned through the \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\">recoverable namespace executor\u003c/a>.\nSuch plans add \u003ccode>namespace_transition: true\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>remove_directories\u003c/code>. Only\nsnapshot-owned files and their known parent directories can be displaced;\nunknown empty directories and ignored descendants remain protected. Parent\nspellings inside each snapshot must be consistent under portable folding.\nNo force, overwrite, automatic index reset or adoption option is implemented.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-progress-and-resource-boundaries\">C++, progress and resource boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspace/c-progress-and-resource-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to C++, progress and resource boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> verified \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All callbacks are synchronous on the caller and must not reenter the Store.\n\u003ccode>scanning\u003c/code> reports directories/inventory entries, \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> reports working\nfile bytes or stored closure progress, and \u003ccode>planning\u003c/code> reports metadata/action\nentries. Counters reset between phases. Planning finishes with a complete\n\u003ccode>planning\u003c/code> event and never emits a \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event. Cancellation or observer\nfailure returns no partial report and changes no data. The ordinary exclusive\nStore lock and Store opening/recovery rules still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The working content buffer is 1 MiB. Each snapshot load uses a FIFO cache of up\nto 1,024 manifest summaries. Physical inventory has a 16 MiB entry accounting\nbudget (\u003ccode>64 + UTF-8 path bytes + folded path bytes\u003c/code> per entry) and a maximum\ndirectory depth of 128. This accounting is not a total RAM bound: maps, reports,\nJSON and allocation overhead are additional. Existing 16 MiB object limits\nand full-closure verification collections also remain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These are observations of a changing filesystem, not an atomic snapshot or a\nworkspace lock. Size/type/time/mode changes observed around a file read cause\nfailure; later edits can invalidate a completed report. The executor must\nprepare verified contents, revalidate its expectations and use a recovery\njournal. Neither \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> nor a prior plan authorizes it to skip those steps.\nThe C ABI and Go binding do not yet expose native workspace operations.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight # The native engine provides a combined branch/index/workspace status and a read-only checkout plan. Neither operation changes files, objects, references or staging. A plan does not execute a checkout; the separate recoverable checkout prepares verified outputs, rechecks the workspace and journals file moves before publishing the branch. text Copy syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify Both commands default to the current native branch ( main for legacy Stores) and directory . . The Store must be inside the workspace; checkout planning requires it strictly below the workspace root. Explicit HEAD binds CLI workspace commands to that canonical root. Output keys, action names and conflict codes are English in both interface languages. Combined status # --indexed can opt into the persistent working hash cache for the unstaged side. That mode writes disposable cache data; ordinary combined status keeps the full-read/read-only contract described here. native status --combined returns version , branch , clean , staged and unstaged . The nested staged report is the existing native staged report : its recorded base is compared with the immutable index. The nested unstaged report is a working-directory status against that index. With no index, the effective comparison is against the branch head; an unborn branch uses an empty baseline. A path can appear in both reports. For example, stage prepared over baseline original , then restore the working file to original . Ordinary branch status is clean, but combined status reports a staged modification and an unstaged modification. This keeps the pending commit distinct from later working edits. An index whose base differs from the current branch remains inspectable against its recorded base. Working files are still compared with its frozen snapshot. Overall clean requires both comparisons to be clean and the index to be fresh. Staleness alone therefore makes the combined report unclean. A dirty report still exits 0. --combined cannot be combined with --revision . The working scan retains ordinary status ignore rules, regular-file requirements and full BLAKE3 hashing. It does not ingest files or audit chunk payloads. Checkout plan and verification # native checkout-plan TARGET compares the current heads/BRANCH snapshot with an exact target revision. It inventories the actual workspace and hashes every tracked baseline file in full. Timestamps and sizes never establish equality. The first policy, clean-tracked-v1 , requires all tracked files to be clean , including files whose target entry would remain unchanged. Any explicit staging index blocks this preflight, including an index without staged changes. The plan includes: Field Meaning source , target Baseline revision (or null) and exact target revision staging_snapshot Existing index ID, or null policy , ready Preflight policy and whether it found no blockers objects_verified Whether complete target closure verification was requested and succeeded executable_metadata_supported False on Windows; executable bits cannot be inspected/applied there inventory_entries Files, directories and opaque/protected entries inventoried hashed_bytes Working baseline file bytes read, excluding stored-object verification restore_bytes Logical target bytes for proposed create/replace actions counts , actions Proposed snapshot changes and unchanged count create_directories Missing target parent directories, sorted conflicts Sorted unique blockers with path , reason and obstruction Actions are sorted by repository path. Each action contains source / target file summaries with manifest, size, file hash and executable metadata. Missing sides are null. These actions describe the candidate snapshot even if conflicts block it; they are not an ordered transaction program. Action Meaning create Target file has no baseline entry replace Existing file has different target contents remove Baseline file has no target entry set_executable Contents match and executable metadata differs reuse Contents/mode match but manifest representation differs unchanged count File entries match; no action row restore_bytes is a file-level logical count, not network traffic or unique chunk bytes. Manifest reuse plans provide block-level comparison separately. Windows plans retain desired executable changes in the snapshot comparison, but do not use local executable bits as a cleanliness test. Default planning requires baseline/target revision and file-manifest metadata. It reads no target chunk payloads or historical parents and can plan from a metadata-only partial Store. Thus ready: true, objects_verified: false means the filesystem preflight passed; it does not prove reconstruction is possible. --verify additionally verifies the target's entire closure, including parents and file hashes, as ordinary native export does. Missing/corrupt dependencies fail the command without returning a partial plan. Verification still runs when the filesystem report contains blockers. A completed ready plan exits 0. A completed blocked plan emits its JSON and exits 1. Invalid arguments, IO errors, missing required metadata or verification errors emit a diagnostic on stderr and no JSON report. Protected paths and first-policy limits # Unlike ordinary status, checkout preflight does not apply .syrnckignore . Ignored files are inventoried so they cannot hide a local edit or collision. Unrelated untracked/ignored files are preserved by the proposed action list; their contents are not hashed. A pre-existing untracked target file blocks the plan even if its bytes happen to match the desired target. Conflict reason Meaning tracked_modified , tracked_missing Local tracked edit/deletion tracked_mode_changed Local Unix executable metadata differs staging_present , stale_staging The branch has a pending or stale index; path is empty checkout_pending A checkout journal requires recovery; path is empty untracked_collision A target would occupy an untracked path, or an affected directory contains untracked/ignored entries file_directory_transition A file/directory conversion or ancestor obstruction includes an entry outside the tracked transition path_spelling A name matches only after Unicode normalization/case folding portable_collision Multiple physical names fold to the same affected name symlink_obstruction , special_file An affected path or ancestor is not an ordinary file/directory protected_path The operation would enter the Store or another protected entry Static symlinks and Windows reparse points, including junctions, are opaque. The inventory never deliberately traverses them. .git and .syrnck names and the actual Store directory are protected and their contents are excluded from the workspace inventory. Custom Store names receive the same protection. Clean tracked case/normalization changes and file/directory transitions are planned through the recoverable namespace executor . Such plans add namespace_transition: true and remove_directories . Only snapshot-owned files and their known parent directories can be displaced; unknown empty directories and ignored descendants remain protected. Parent spellings inside each snapshot must be consistent under portable folding. No force, overwrite, automatic index reset or adoption option is implemented. C++, progress and resource boundaries # cpp Copy auto status = store . workspace_status ( project , \"main\" , operation ) ; auto preview = store . plan_checkout ( project , \"main\" , target_id ) ; auto verified = store . plan_checkout ( project , \"main\" , target_id , true , operation ) ; All callbacks are synchronous on the caller and must not reenter the Store. scanning reports directories/inventory entries, verifying reports working file bytes or stored closure progress, and planning reports metadata/action entries. Counters reset between phases. Planning finishes with a complete planning event and never emits a publishing event. Cancellation or observer failure returns no partial report and changes no data. The ordinary exclusive Store lock and Store opening/recovery rules still apply. The working content buffer is 1 MiB. Each snapshot load uses a FIFO cache of up to 1,024 manifest summaries. Physical inventory has a 16 MiB entry accounting budget ( 64 + UTF-8 path bytes + folded path bytes per entry) and a maximum directory depth of 128. This accounting is not a total RAM bound: maps, reports, JSON and allocation overhead are additional. Existing 16 MiB object limits and full-closure verification collections also remain. These are observations of a changing filesystem, not an atomic snapshot or a workspace lock. Size/type/time/mode changes observed around a file read cause failure; later edits can invalidate a completed report. The executor must prepare verified contents, revalidate its expectations and use a recovery journal. Neither ready nor a prior plan authorizes it to skip those steps. The C ABI and Go binding do not yet expose native workspace operations. syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify auto status = store.workspace_status(project, \"main\", operation); auto preview = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id); auto verified = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id, true, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight","text":"Native workspace inspection and checkout preflight","level":1},{"id":"combined-status","text":"Combined status","level":2},{"id":"checkout-plan-and-verification","text":"Checkout plan and verification","level":2},{"id":"protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits","text":"Protected paths and first-policy limits","level":2},{"id":"c-progress-and-resource-boundaries","text":"C++, progress and resource boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json\nsyrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify\nauto status = store.workspace_status(project, \"main\", operation);\nauto preview = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id);\nauto verified = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id, true, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-branches","#/en/working-index","#/en/native-staging","#/en/native-comparison","#/en/file-comparison","#/en/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"native-workspaces","title":"Linked native workspaces","description":"Independent working directories, HEADs and indexes over one shared Store.","section":"Version control","order":222,"parent":"native-branches","revision":"9","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Create and select a workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Create and select a workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspaces\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The primary workspace retains the legacy layout. A registered linked workspace\nhas its own canonical directory, HEAD, frozen staging index, detached revision,\ncheckout receipts, bisect state, working cache and watch journal. Objects and\nnamed branches remain shared; file data is not copied between Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-add NAME DIRECTORY\u003c/code> runs from the primary context. The parent directory\nmust exist. An absent destination is created; existing files are preserved and\nordinary checkout collision rules apply. With \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>, the branch must\nalready exist and be available. \u003ccode>--revision REVISION\u003c/code> initializes detached HEAD;\nomitting both uses the primary committed revision, detached. An unborn primary\nstarts a linked workspace at a retained empty snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Names contain 1..64 lowercase ASCII letters, digits or internal hyphens. \u003ccode>primary\u003c/code>\nand Windows device names are reserved. Registered roots must be disjoint from\neach other, the primary workspace and the Store; links and portable aliases do\nnot provide an escape from these checks. At most 1,024 linked contexts are registered.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--workspace NAME\u003c/code> selects a context for that invocation, with \u003ccode>primary\u003c/code> as an\nexplicit spelling of the default. Use \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> to locate the shared Store when\nrunning from another directory. Native workspace commands default \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>\nto the registered root when a linked context is selected. An explicit directory\nmust resolve to that root. \u003ccode>native workspaces\u003c/code> includes the primary selection and\nreports each linked directory, branch/revision and pending checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial\">Partial working directories\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/partial\" aria-label=\"Link to Partial working directories\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Repeated \u003ccode>--include PATH\u003c/code> options select literal repository-relative files or\ndirectory prefixes, using portable Unicode/case matching. Prefixes must be\ndisjoint; wildcards and exclusions are not supported. \u003ccode>--no-files\u003c/code> explicitly\nselects an empty working directory. Omitting both options retains the complete\nworking directory. Root and ancestor \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code> controls needed by selected\npaths are included automatically with canonical spelling.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Revisions and frozen indexes always retain the complete logical file tree and\nall its objects locally. Whole-directory commit, stage and amend preserve\nomitted entries; working status, index and observation compare only the physical\nselection. Explicit working-file edits outside that selection are refused.\nDirectory staging/commit requires an existing target revision to preserve omitted\nentries; create a new branch from the complete selected revision first.\nSnapshot diffs and \u003ccode>native export\u003c/code> still address the complete tree. Partial\nmaterialization does not implement lazy block downloading or save Store space.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Checkout/switch, detached HEAD and portable restoration use the recorded\nselection, including file/directory and case transitions. Excluded local files\nare preserved; a namespace obstruction is refused. Backups retain the selection\nand the complete index. Restoration materializes the selected index files and\nverifies exactly that projected tree before binding the workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Direct policy-reference edits are refused while the workspace is active. Use the\nrecoverable transition below to change its selection. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\">Hard reset\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stash/partial-workspaces\">stash\u003c/a> support partial working files.\nC++ creation uses \u003ccode>register_partial_native_workspace(primary, name, directory, prefixes, operation)\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>native_materialization()\u003c/code> reports the policy.\nC/Go can select and observe a partial context created by CLI/C++.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"materialization-transitions\">Change the current selection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/materialization-transitions\" aria-label=\"Link to Change the current selection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Choose exactly one of repeated \u003ccode>--include PATH\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--no-files\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--all-files\u003c/code>.\nThe read-only plan must be ready; apply requires its exact ID. \u003ccode>--expect ID\u003c/code>\noptionally fences the current complete HEAD explicitly. The C++ APIs in\n\u003ccode>syrnck/workspace.hpp\u003c/code> are \u003ccode>plan_native_materialization\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>set_native_materialization\u003c/code>: a null optional prefix vector selects all files;\nan explicit empty vector selects none.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This works for primary and linked directories with named or detached HEAD.\nA changed selection requires clean tracked files and an empty frozen index.\nUse stash or commit to retain local edits first. Growing a selection refuses\nuntracked or ignored obstructions; shrinking removes only verified tracked\nfiles. Unrelated local data stays in place. Empty directories are not versioned\nand may remain. An identical selection returns \u003ccode>unchanged\u003c/code>, preserving dirty\nwork and its index without writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The transition keeps complete logical history and HEAD unchanged. Journal v10,\nlayout 7, freezes both projections. Its final reference transaction publishes\nthe new policy together with the committed receipt. Interruption before that\npublication leaves the old policy active: \u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> resumes;\n\u003ccode>native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/code> restores the old files and policy. After\npublication only cleanup remains. The observer and working index adopt the\nnew projection on their next observation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> are established before\njournaling, so older readers refuse the Store. An interruption may leave those\nadditive requirements enabled; retry is safe. Backups retain requirements and\npolicy metadata, while working directories still need explicit restoration.\nAll retained file objects remain local; the operation does not implement lazy\nasset fetching.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"isolation\">Isolation and branch reservations\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/isolation\" aria-label=\"Link to Isolation and branch reservations\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Staging in one workspace never consumes another workspace&#39;s index. Commits and\nrewrite operations use the selected target. A named branch can be selected by\nonly one workspace at a time; switch plans report \u003ccode>branch_in_other_workspace\u003c/code>.\nBranch changes through reference publication also check reservations, so an\nexplicit branch argument cannot move a foreign workspace&#39;s branch behind its\nworking files. Switch or detach that workspace first to release its branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>One Store handle keeps its exclusive repository lock. Independent processes open\nand close it around operations; watchers release it while waiting. A pending\ncheckout blocks new native mutations and GC across the Store until recovered in\nthe owning context. Filesystem application is journaled per workspace rather\nthan globally atomic across working directories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"observation\">Observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/observation\" aria-label=\"Link to Observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Linked observation uses native root notifications where available and checks\nthe external Store&#39;s reference/configuration identities. Metadata publication\nrequests a complete observation without emitting paths outside the workspace.\nUnsupported stamps or notification failures use polling. Periodic complete\naudits remain necessary; notifications are advisory. Each context has separate\nworking-cache and journal files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"performance\">Working-index benchmark\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/performance\" aria-label=\"Link to Working-index benchmark\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Omit \u003ccode>--linked\u003c/code> to measure primary; Unix builds use \u003ccode>build/syrnck\u003c/code>. The fixture\nuses a synthetic 64 MiB file and verifies equivalent full/indexed status and a\nsame-size edit whose modification time is restored. On local Windows x64,\n8 November 2026, five warm linked observations each reused one file and hashed\nzero bytes; full observations each hashed 64 MiB. Median CLI times were 68 ms\nfull and 24 ms indexed (primary: 75 ms and 20 ms). The file was cached by the OS;\nother regression tests were running. These measurements demonstrate avoided\nreads on this fixture, not Unreal-project throughput or cross-platform latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This separate fixture uses two synthetic assets totaling 128 MiB and 25 bytes of\ncode. Local Windows x64, 8 November 2026, five observations without the indexed\ncache: the complete view hashed 134,217,753 bytes per observation, while the\n\u003ccode>Source\u003c/code> view hashed 25 bytes. Median CLI times were 105 ms and 18 ms respectively.\nThe files had just been written/ingested, without flushing the OS cache; other\nregression tests were running. The Store\nretained all objects; the measurement concerns working status only.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore\">Backup and restoration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/restore\" aria-label=\"Link to Backup and restoration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Backups omit active directory bindings and local checkout receipts. They retain\nall indexes, detached data and completed target roots, plus a portable selection\ndescriptor and an exact revision root for each linked workspace. Backup format\nv2 supports these exclusions for ungated experimental sources. A Store with\nrequired reader features uses backup marker v3, which additionally binds those\nrequirements. V1/v2 backups remain readable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-restore NAME DIRECTORY\u003c/code> binds a portable snapshot to a new directory.\nIt restores the original branch when its tip is unchanged and unreserved.\n\u003ccode>--branch NEW_NAME\u003c/code> can create a rescue branch at the saved revision; \u003ccode>--detach\u003c/code>\nrestores independent HEAD. An occupied branch with another tip is never reset\nimplicitly. Frozen index content is materialized as working files, retaining\nthe committed HEAD separately. Unprepared dirty working bytes are not part of a\nlogical Store backup. Renaming the index&#39;s target archives its original snapshot\nbefore publishing the replacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Restoration exclusively publishes the exported physical selection before binding\nthe context. If interrupted before binding, rerun the command: an existing tree\nmust match the selected saved content and modes. Changed exports, extra files,\nlinks and protected metadata are refused without overwriting them. The Store\nkeeps the portable roots until an explicit retention operation removes them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspaces\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Registration atomically retains a valid empty detached state before initial\nmaterialization. If initialization is blocked or interrupted, the registered\ncontext remains inspectable; switch, checkout or recover in that context.\nRegistration does not move the primary HEAD or discard its prepared changes.\nCheckout records bind their context and exact canonical directory; another\ncontext cannot execute their recovery. Completed receipts remain local to their\ncontext and are inspectable after later selections and GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The Windows x64 campaign verified all 808 observed IO process-termination\nboundaries for linked registration and checkout entry/exit/movement, with plain\nand encrypted Stores, resume/abort, namespace transitions, independent production\ninspection and post-recovery GC. This tests process interruption with the OS and\nfilesystem running; power-loss behavior is a separate validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The partial-workspace campaign additionally verified all 716 observed process\ntermination boundaries for registration and checkout with the same plain/encrypted,\nresume/abort, fresh-reader and post-recovery GC checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">C++, C and Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/api\" aria-label=\"Link to C++, C and Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">register_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L3\" data-line=\"3\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">switch_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> changes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// primary, handle-local selection\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>C uses \u003ccode>syrnck_store_use_workspace\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex\u003c/code>. A linked observation session requires a\nStore selected to the same name. Selection failure preserves the previous name.\nGo exposes \u003ccode>Store.UseWorkspace(name)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code>;\nthe existing cancellation and serialized-handle rules apply. Empty names select\nprimary in these APIs. These are additive ABI 1 symbols requiring matching libraries.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"formats\">Formats and current scope\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Formats and current scope\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Primary reference layouts and checkout records v1..v7 stay unchanged. Linked\nmetadata lives under real \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/\u003c/code> references. Named indexes use the\nBLAKE3 digest of the branch name in their slot, preserving the existing maximum\nbranch length. The retained snapshot still records the complete branch name.\nComplete linked checkout records use v8 with \u003ccode>context\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> (1..6).\nPartial checkout records use v9 with frozen \u003ccode>source_materialization\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>target_materialization\u003c/code> definitions, context and layout (1..6). Layout 5 supports\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\">recoverable partial hard reset\u003c/a>, and\nlayout 6 restores \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-stash/partial-workspaces\">partial stashes\u003c/a>. The current\nstatic v9 selection requires equal source/target definitions. Selection changes\nuse v10/layout 7 with different frozen definitions. A canonical v1\nmaterialization chunk is retained at \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/workspace/materialization\u003c/code>.\nObject/revision, pack and C ABI versions are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Linked contexts can be \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives\">archived, unregistered and restored\u003c/a>,\npreserving dirty work separately from HEAD and the index. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation\">Whole-directory relocation\u003c/a>\nsupports linked contexts on one filesystem, including changes of case. Cross-filesystem\nand primary/Store relocation remain work to complete. Every workspace still\nrequires its complete retained objects locally.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compatibility\">Reader compatibility\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/native-workspaces/compatibility\" aria-label=\"Link to Reader compatibility\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Registration and portable restore establish the \u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> reader\nrequirement before publishing a linked binding. Historical executables refuse\nthe Store, including ordinary ref commands, so they cannot bypass branch\nreservations. Upgraded readers retain support for legacy configurations. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/settings/reader-features\">inspection and explicit upgrade\u003c/a>. Partial creation\nalso establishes \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> before publishing its policy. Readers\nsupporting linked contexts alone refuse this additional requirement.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Create and select a workspace # text Copy syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\" The primary workspace retains the legacy layout. A registered linked workspace has its own canonical directory, HEAD, frozen staging index, detached revision, checkout receipts, bisect state, working cache and watch journal. Objects and named branches remain shared; file data is not copied between Stores. workspace-add NAME DIRECTORY runs from the primary context. The parent directory must exist. An absent destination is created; existing files are preserved and ordinary checkout collision rules apply. With --branch NAME , the branch must already exist and be available. --revision REVISION initializes detached HEAD; omitting both uses the primary committed revision, detached. An unborn primary starts a linked workspace at a retained empty snapshot. Names contain 1..64 lowercase ASCII letters, digits or internal hyphens. primary and Windows device names are reserved. Registered roots must be disjoint from each other, the primary workspace and the Store; links and portable aliases do not provide an escape from these checks. At most 1,024 linked contexts are registered. --workspace NAME selects a context for that invocation, with primary as an explicit spelling of the default. Use --store to locate the shared Store when running from another directory. Native workspace commands default --directory to the registered root when a linked context is selected. An explicit directory must resolve to that root. native workspaces includes the primary selection and reports each linked directory, branch/revision and pending checkout. Partial working directories # text Copy syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files Repeated --include PATH options select literal repository-relative files or directory prefixes, using portable Unicode/case matching. Prefixes must be disjoint; wildcards and exclusions are not supported. --no-files explicitly selects an empty working directory. Omitting both options retains the complete working directory. Root and ancestor .syrnckignore controls needed by selected paths are included automatically with canonical spelling. Revisions and frozen indexes always retain the complete logical file tree and all its objects locally. Whole-directory commit, stage and amend preserve omitted entries; working status, index and observation compare only the physical selection. Explicit working-file edits outside that selection are refused. Directory staging/commit requires an existing target revision to preserve omitted entries; create a new branch from the complete selected revision first. Snapshot diffs and native export still address the complete tree. Partial materialization does not implement lazy block downloading or save Store space. Checkout/switch, detached HEAD and portable restoration use the recorded selection, including file/directory and case transitions. Excluded local files are preserved; a namespace obstruction is refused. Backups retain the selection and the complete index. Restoration materializes the selected index files and verifies exactly that projected tree before binding the workspace. Direct policy-reference edits are refused while the workspace is active. Use the recoverable transition below to change its selection. Hard reset and stash support partial working files. C++ creation uses register_partial_native_workspace(primary, name, directory, prefixes, operation) ; native_materialization() reports the policy. C/Go can select and observe a partial context created by CLI/C++. Change the current selection # text Copy syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID Choose exactly one of repeated --include PATH , --no-files or --all-files . The read-only plan must be ready; apply requires its exact ID. --expect ID optionally fences the current complete HEAD explicitly. The C++ APIs in syrnck/workspace.hpp are plan_native_materialization and set_native_materialization : a null optional prefix vector selects all files; an explicit empty vector selects none. This works for primary and linked directories with named or detached HEAD. A changed selection requires clean tracked files and an empty frozen index. Use stash or commit to retain local edits first. Growing a selection refuses untracked or ignored obstructions; shrinking removes only verified tracked files. Unrelated local data stays in place. Empty directories are not versioned and may remain. An identical selection returns unchanged , preserving dirty work and its index without writes. The transition keeps complete logical history and HEAD unchanged. Journal v10, layout 7, freezes both projections. Its final reference transaction publishes the new policy together with the committed receipt. Interruption before that publication leaves the old policy active: native checkout-recover resumes; native checkout-recover --abort restores the old files and policy. After publication only cleanup remains. The observer and working index adopt the new projection on their next observation. materialization-transitions-v1 and sparse-workspaces-v1 are established before journaling, so older readers refuse the Store. An interruption may leave those additive requirements enabled; retry is safe. Backups retain requirements and policy metadata, while working directories still need explicit restoration. All retained file objects remain local; the operation does not implement lazy asset fetching. Isolation and branch reservations # Staging in one workspace never consumes another workspace's index. Commits and rewrite operations use the selected target. A named branch can be selected by only one workspace at a time; switch plans report branch_in_other_workspace . Branch changes through reference publication also check reservations, so an explicit branch argument cannot move a foreign workspace's branch behind its working files. Switch or detach that workspace first to release its branch. One Store handle keeps its exclusive repository lock. Independent processes open and close it around operations; watchers release it while waiting. A pending checkout blocks new native mutations and GC across the Store until recovered in the owning context. Filesystem application is journaled per workspace rather than globally atomic across working directories. Observation # text Copy syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal Linked observation uses native root notifications where available and checks the external Store's reference/configuration identities. Metadata publication requests a complete observation without emitting paths outside the workspace. Unsupported stamps or notification failures use polling. Periodic complete audits remain necessary; notifications are advisory. Each context has separate working-cache and journal files. Working-index benchmark # text Copy python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json Omit --linked to measure primary; Unix builds use build/syrnck . The fixture uses a synthetic 64 MiB file and verifies equivalent full/indexed status and a same-size edit whose modification time is restored. On local Windows x64, 8 November 2026, five warm linked observations each reused one file and hashed zero bytes; full observations each hashed 64 MiB. Median CLI times were 68 ms full and 24 ms indexed (primary: 75 ms and 20 ms). The file was cached by the OS; other regression tests were running. These measurements demonstrate avoided reads on this fixture, not Unreal-project throughput or cross-platform latency. text Copy python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json This separate fixture uses two synthetic assets totaling 128 MiB and 25 bytes of code. Local Windows x64, 8 November 2026, five observations without the indexed cache: the complete view hashed 134,217,753 bytes per observation, while the Source view hashed 25 bytes. Median CLI times were 105 ms and 18 ms respectively. The files had just been written/ingested, without flushing the OS cache; other regression tests were running. The Store retained all objects; the measurement concerns working status only. Backup and restoration # text Copy syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project Backups omit active directory bindings and local checkout receipts. They retain all indexes, detached data and completed target roots, plus a portable selection descriptor and an exact revision root for each linked workspace. Backup format v2 supports these exclusions for ungated experimental sources. A Store with required reader features uses backup marker v3, which additionally binds those requirements. V1/v2 backups remain readable. workspace-restore NAME DIRECTORY binds a portable snapshot to a new directory. It restores the original branch when its tip is unchanged and unreserved. --branch NEW_NAME can create a rescue branch at the saved revision; --detach restores independent HEAD. An occupied branch with another tip is never reset implicitly. Frozen index content is materialized as working files, retaining the committed HEAD separately. Unprepared dirty working bytes are not part of a logical Store backup. Renaming the index's target archives its original snapshot before publishing the replacement. Restoration exclusively publishes the exported physical selection before binding the context. If interrupted before binding, rerun the command: an existing tree must match the selected saved content and modes. Changed exports, extra files, links and protected metadata are refused without overwriting them. The Store keeps the portable roots until an explicit retention operation removes them. Recovery # text Copy syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort Registration atomically retains a valid empty detached state before initial materialization. If initialization is blocked or interrupted, the registered context remains inspectable; switch, checkout or recover in that context. Registration does not move the primary HEAD or discard its prepared changes. Checkout records bind their context and exact canonical directory; another context cannot execute their recovery. Completed receipts remain local to their context and are inspectable after later selections and GC. The Windows x64 campaign verified all 808 observed IO process-termination boundaries for linked registration and checkout entry/exit/movement, with plain and encrypted Stores, resume/abort, namespace transitions, independent production inspection and post-recovery GC. This tests process interruption with the OS and filesystem running; power-loss behavior is a separate validation. The partial-workspace campaign additionally verified all 716 observed process termination boundaries for registration and checkout with the same plain/encrypted, resume/abort, fresh-reader and post-recovery GC checks. C++, C and Go # cpp Copy store . register_native_workspace ( primary , \"edit\" , directory , operation ) ; store . use_native_workspace ( \"edit\" ) ; store . switch_directory ( directory , \"feature\" , operation ) ; auto changes = store . workspace_status ( directory , store . native_target ( ) , operation ) ; store . use_native_workspace ( \"\" ) ; // primary, handle-local selection C uses syrnck_store_use_workspace and syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex . A linked observation session requires a Store selected to the same name. Selection failure preserves the previous name. Go exposes Store.UseWorkspace(name) and WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} ; the existing cancellation and serialized-handle rules apply. Empty names select primary in these APIs. These are additive ABI 1 symbols requiring matching libraries. Formats and current scope # Primary reference layouts and checkout records v1..v7 stay unchanged. Linked metadata lives under real workspaces/NAME/ references. Named indexes use the BLAKE3 digest of the branch name in their slot, preserving the existing maximum branch length. The retained snapshot still records the complete branch name. Complete linked checkout records use v8 with context and layout_version (1..6). Partial checkout records use v9 with frozen source_materialization and target_materialization definitions, context and layout (1..6). Layout 5 supports recoverable partial hard reset , and layout 6 restores partial stashes . The current static v9 selection requires equal source/target definitions. Selection changes use v10/layout 7 with different frozen definitions. A canonical v1 materialization chunk is retained at workspaces/NAME/workspace/materialization . Object/revision, pack and C ABI versions are unchanged. Linked contexts can be archived, unregistered and restored , preserving dirty work separately from HEAD and the index. Whole-directory relocation supports linked contexts on one filesystem, including changes of case. Cross-filesystem and primary/Store relocation remain work to complete. Every workspace still requires its complete retained objects locally. Reader compatibility # Registration and portable restore establish the linked-workspaces-v1 reader requirement before publishing a linked binding. Historical executables refuse the Store, including ordinary ref commands, so they cannot bypass branch reservations. Upgraded readers retain support for legacy configurations. See inspection and explicit upgrade . Partial creation also establishes sparse-workspaces-v1 before publishing its policy. Readers supporting linked contexts alone refuse this additional requirement. syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\" syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort store.register_native_workspace(primary, \"edit\", directory, operation); store.use_native_workspace(\"edit\"); store.switch_directory(directory, \"feature\", operation); auto changes = store.workspace_status(directory, store.native_target(), operation); store.use_native_workspace(\"\"); // primary, handle-local selection","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Create and select a workspace","level":2},{"id":"partial","text":"Partial working directories","level":2},{"id":"materialization-transitions","text":"Change the current selection","level":2},{"id":"isolation","text":"Isolation and branch reservations","level":2},{"id":"observation","text":"Observation","level":2},{"id":"performance","text":"Working-index benchmark","level":2},{"id":"restore","text":"Backup and restoration","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Recovery","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"C++, C and Go","level":2},{"id":"formats","text":"Formats and current scope","level":2},{"id":"compatibility","text":"Reader compatibility","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-6-L1","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-9-L1","code-9-L2","code-9-L3","code-9-L4","code-9-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature\nsyrnck native workspaces\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\"\nsyrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization\nsyrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal\npython scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json\npython scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json\nsyrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot\nsyrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck\nsyrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project\nsyrnck native workspaces\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort\nstore.register_native_workspace(primary, \"edit\", directory, operation);\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"edit\");\nstore.switch_directory(directory, \"feature\", operation);\nauto changes = store.workspace_status(directory, store.native_target(), operation);\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"\"); // primary, handle-local selection","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces","#/en/native-stash/partial-workspaces","#/en/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces","#/en/native-stash/partial-workspaces","#/en/native-workspace-archives","#/en/native-workspace-relocation","#/en/settings/reader-features"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"network-compression","title":"Negotiated object compression","description":"Negotiated object compression guide.","section":"Transfers","order":430,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"negotiated-object-compression\">Negotiated object compression\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/network-compression/negotiated-object-compression\" aria-label=\"Link to Negotiated object compression\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Object uploads and downloads use Zstandard by default when the server advertises\nsupport. Compression changes the wire representation only. BLAKE3 object IDs,\nfull manifests, at-rest encryption/compression, local session records and final\nreference publication retain their existing contracts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck serve --no-compression\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These commands require the usual initialized store, token and HTTPS setup.\n\u003ccode>auto\u003c/code> is the default; \u003ccode>off\u003c/code> sends and requests logical bytes directly; \u003ccode>zstd\u003c/code>\nrequires support when an object actually needs transferring. Even in \u003ccode>zstd\u003c/code>\nmode, small or incompressible objects can travel raw. The server can disable\nthe extension with \u003ccode>--no-compression\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>ServeOptions::object_compression=false\u003c/code>).\nIn C++, append \u003ccode>TransferOptions::compression\u003c/code> with\n\u003ccode>net::ObjectCompression::{automatic, disabled, zstd}\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"negotiation-and-representation\">Negotiation and representation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/network-compression/negotiation-and-representation\" aria-label=\"Link to Negotiation and representation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>GET /v1/capabilities\u003c/code> adds the integer \u003ccode>object_zstd\u003c/code>, either \u003cstrong>0\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>1\u003c/strong>.\nThe client negotiates lazily before the first missing object, once per operation.\nAn absent field or a 404 capability endpoint permits identity fallback in auto\nmode. Invalid types/versions, authentication errors and other request failures\nfail the operation. A fully reused transfer or completed session needs no\ncompression negotiation. Resumption can select a different mode without\nchanging the session identity or its expected reference.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Message\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Representation\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object PUT with \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One Zstandard frame\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object PUT without that header\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Original logical bytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object GET with \u003ccode>Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Client accepts a frame or logical bytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object GET without that header\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logical bytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object response with \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One Zstandard frame\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Object response without that header\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logical bytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Object bodies use \u003ccode>application/octet-stream\u003c/code>. Compression applies to chunks,\nmanifests and revisions. JSON control requests and object acknowledgements stay\nuncompressed. GET replies include \u003ccode>Vary: Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These are \u003cstrong>Syrnck protocol headers\u003c/strong>, deliberately separate from HTTP\n\u003ccode>Content-Encoding\u003c/code>. This keeps encoded-byte accounting and bounded decoding\ninside the engine instead of an HTTP library&#39;s automatic decoder. The client\nrequests HTTP identity and disables automatic HTTP content decoding. Unexpected\nHTTP content codings, duplicate object encoding headers, unsupported object\ncodings or encoding fields in trailers fail. A response cannot introduce\ncompressed objects unless the client accepted them. The server rejects encoded\nPUTs when disabled, unknown/duplicate encodings and encoded control requests\nwith 415. The custom header value is exactly \u003ccode>zstd\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Custom coding rejection happens after reading the bounded request body, so\nan unread body cannot reset the connection and hide its 415 response. Unsupported\nHTTP \u003ccode>Content-Encoding\u003c/code> can be rejected earlier by the HTTP library; clients\nmust also tolerate a connection failure on such unsupported requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HTTPS proxies must preserve both custom headers, \u003ccode>Vary\u003c/code> and the object bytes.\nA proxy which strips the representation header causes verification failure;\nclients do not reinterpret opaque failures as a reason to retry without\ncompression. Old clients still receive logical bodies from new servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-verification\">Bounds and verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/network-compression/bounds-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounds and verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Both encoded and logical object bodies are limited to \u003cstrong>16 MiB\u003c/strong>. A supported\nframe has a known content size, no dictionary, and a window no larger than\n16 MiB. Skippable frames, concatenated frames, trailing bytes, truncated frames,\nunknown-size frames and oversized declarations are rejected. The frame header\nis validated before allocating the decoded output; the streaming decoder also\ncaps its window. Frames are not themselves an authentication boundary: the Store\nstill verifies the logical object&#39;s type, BLAKE3 ID and schema before import.\nFinal publication still verifies the complete dependency closure and file hashes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Encoding uses Zstandard level \u003cstrong>1\u003c/strong>, attempts objects of at least \u003cstrong>1 KiB\u003c/strong>, and\nrequires a saving of at least \u003cstrong>64 bytes\u003c/strong>. If the candidate cannot fit within\nthe currently available staging credit, or compression cannot achieve that\nsaving, the original body is sent. No speculative larger encoded buffer is\nallocated. Compression and Store work remain on the coordinator; cancellation\nis checked before and after compression and between decoding steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers\">object buffer budget\u003c/a> includes the original upload,\nthe candidate encoded vector while both coexist, and the reserved acknowledgement.\nThe peak counter includes this preparation stage even if the candidate is\ndiscarded. After encoding, only the selected upload vector remains charged.\nDownloads keep their 16 MiB reservation and decode directly into their response\nvector without retaining an entire encoded copy. The 17 MiB CLI minimum still\nworks. Codec contexts, transport buffers, Store caches and metadata remain\noutside this body-credit budget; this is not a process RSS limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-retries-and-recovery\">Traffic, retries and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/network-compression/traffic-retries-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Traffic, retries and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> continues to count successful \u003cstrong>logical\u003c/strong> bytes once.\nTwo additional result fields count HTTP object-body bytes in each direction:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Uploaded object request bodies across all attempts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downloaded object response bodies across all attempts, including PUT acknowledgements\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>These fields exclude HTTP headers, TLS framing and capability/presence/lease/ref\ntraffic. Partial and repeated attempts count; a fully reused transfer reports\nzero. \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-policy\">Rate targets\u003c/a> and transfer progress also use encoded\nbody bytes. They must not be derived from \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> after compression.\nError/cancellation exits do not return a successful statistics object.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Idempotent upload retries retain the same selected encoded body. Interrupted\ndownloads restart the object with a fresh decoder; partial decoded bytes never\nenter the Store. Compression/header/format errors are not retried. Previously\nverified objects remain reusable through the ordinary persistent session and\nlease mechanisms. The final reference CAS is still never retried automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-remaining-work\">Current limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/network-compression/validation-and-remaining-work\" aria-label=\"Link to Current limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compression context reuse, CPU budgets, real asset corpus measurements, cross-platform execution and presence caching remain further work. Wire savings depend on the data; compression does not establish a universal throughput gain.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Negotiated object compression # Object uploads and downloads use Zstandard by default when the server advertises support. Compression changes the wire representation only. BLAKE3 object IDs, full manifests, at-rest encryption/compression, local session records and final reference publication retain their existing contracts. text Copy syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off syrnck serve --no-compression These commands require the usual initialized store, token and HTTPS setup. auto is the default; off sends and requests logical bytes directly; zstd requires support when an object actually needs transferring. Even in zstd mode, small or incompressible objects can travel raw. The server can disable the extension with --no-compression ( ServeOptions::object_compression=false ). In C++, append TransferOptions::compression with net::ObjectCompression::{automatic, disabled, zstd} . Negotiation and representation # GET /v1/capabilities adds the integer object_zstd , either 0 or 1 . The client negotiates lazily before the first missing object, once per operation. An absent field or a 404 capability endpoint permits identity fallback in auto mode. Invalid types/versions, authentication errors and other request failures fail the operation. A fully reused transfer or completed session needs no compression negotiation. Resumption can select a different mode without changing the session identity or its expected reference. Message Representation Object PUT with Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd One Zstandard frame Object PUT without that header Original logical bytes Object GET with Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd Client accepts a frame or logical bytes Object GET without that header Logical bytes Object response with Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd One Zstandard frame Object response without that header Logical bytes Object bodies use application/octet-stream . Compression applies to chunks, manifests and revisions. JSON control requests and object acknowledgements stay uncompressed. GET replies include Vary: Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding . These are Syrnck protocol headers , deliberately separate from HTTP Content-Encoding . This keeps encoded-byte accounting and bounded decoding inside the engine instead of an HTTP library's automatic decoder. The client requests HTTP identity and disables automatic HTTP content decoding. Unexpected HTTP content codings, duplicate object encoding headers, unsupported object codings or encoding fields in trailers fail. A response cannot introduce compressed objects unless the client accepted them. The server rejects encoded PUTs when disabled, unknown/duplicate encodings and encoded control requests with 415. The custom header value is exactly zstd . Custom coding rejection happens after reading the bounded request body, so an unread body cannot reset the connection and hide its 415 response. Unsupported HTTP Content-Encoding can be rejected earlier by the HTTP library; clients must also tolerate a connection failure on such unsupported requests. HTTPS proxies must preserve both custom headers, Vary and the object bytes. A proxy which strips the representation header causes verification failure; clients do not reinterpret opaque failures as a reason to retry without compression. Old clients still receive logical bodies from new servers. Bounds and verification # Both encoded and logical object bodies are limited to 16 MiB . A supported frame has a known content size, no dictionary, and a window no larger than 16 MiB. Skippable frames, concatenated frames, trailing bytes, truncated frames, unknown-size frames and oversized declarations are rejected. The frame header is validated before allocating the decoded output; the streaming decoder also caps its window. Frames are not themselves an authentication boundary: the Store still verifies the logical object's type, BLAKE3 ID and schema before import. Final publication still verifies the complete dependency closure and file hashes. Encoding uses Zstandard level 1 , attempts objects of at least 1 KiB , and requires a saving of at least 64 bytes . If the candidate cannot fit within the currently available staging credit, or compression cannot achieve that saving, the original body is sent. No speculative larger encoded buffer is allocated. Compression and Store work remain on the coordinator; cancellation is checked before and after compression and between decoding steps. The object buffer budget includes the original upload, the candidate encoded vector while both coexist, and the reserved acknowledgement. The peak counter includes this preparation stage even if the candidate is discarded. After encoding, only the selected upload vector remains charged. Downloads keep their 16 MiB reservation and decode directly into their response vector without retaining an entire encoded copy. The 17 MiB CLI minimum still works. Codec contexts, transport buffers, Store caches and metadata remain outside this body-credit budget; this is not a process RSS limit. Traffic, retries and recovery # transferred_bytes continues to count successful logical bytes once. Two additional result fields count HTTP object-body bytes in each direction: Field Meaning object_upload_bytes Uploaded object request bodies across all attempts object_download_bytes Downloaded object response bodies across all attempts, including PUT acknowledgements These fields exclude HTTP headers, TLS framing and capability/presence/lease/ref traffic. Partial and repeated attempts count; a fully reused transfer reports zero. Rate targets and transfer progress also use encoded body bytes. They must not be derived from transferred_bytes after compression. Error/cancellation exits do not return a successful statistics object. Idempotent upload retries retain the same selected encoded body. Interrupted downloads restart the object with a fresh decoder; partial decoded bytes never enter the Store. Compression/header/format errors are not retried. Previously verified objects remain reusable through the ordinary persistent session and lease mechanisms. The final reference CAS is still never retried automatically. Current limits # Compression context reuse, CPU budgets, real asset corpus measurements, cross-platform execution and presence caching remain further work. Wire savings depend on the data; compression does not establish a universal throughput gain. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off syrnck serve --no-compression","headings":[{"id":"negotiated-object-compression","text":"Negotiated object compression","level":1},{"id":"negotiation-and-representation","text":"Negotiation and representation","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-verification","text":"Bounds and verification","level":2},{"id":"traffic-retries-and-recovery","text":"Traffic, retries and recovery","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-remaining-work","text":"Current limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off\nsyrnck serve --no-compression","assets":[],"links":["#/en/parallel-transfers","#/en/transfer-policy"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"operations","title":"Progress and cooperative cancellation","description":"Progress and cooperative cancellation guide.","section":"Getting started","order":440,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"progress-and-cooperative-cancellation\">Progress and cooperative cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/operations/progress-and-cooperative-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to Progress and cooperative cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The C++ ingestion, native commit, reconstruction and Git-filter interfaces can\nreport progress and accept cancellation. Native commits and clean filters use\nrepository pipeline defaults; explicit \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code> replace those defaults.\nThe CLI accepts \u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>put\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native add\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>git clean\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git migrate-lfs\u003c/code> without changing saved preferences.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli\">CLI\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/operations/cli\" aria-label=\"Link to CLI\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>get\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>compare\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native commit/add/staged/reset/status/checkout-plan/checkout/checkout-recover/switch-plan/switch/diff/diff-text/export\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>git clean/smudge/filter-process\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native tag/tags/tag-show/tag-delete/tag-archives/tag-archive/tag-restore\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native branch/branches/branch-delete-plan/branch-delete/branch-archives/branch-archive\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native branch-restore-plan/branch-restore\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native ancestry/cherry-pick-plan/revert-plan/cherry-pick/revert/merge-plan/merge\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native reset-plan/squash-plan/squash/archives/archive-show\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>native rebase-plan/rebase/rebase-index-plan/rebase-index\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>git lfs-preview/migrate-lfs/retention-scan/retention\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>agent push/pull\u003c/code> accept mutually\nexclusive \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code>. Both write only to stderr; stdout\nkeeps its ordinary JSON, revision ID, Git pointer or binary stream. With neither\nflag, successful commands stay quiet on stderr. Other commands reject these\nflags. Plain source data passing through smudge and existing clean pointers do\nnot need block processing and may produce no progress events.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Text uses the selected interface language and quotes paths to escape newlines\nand control characters. JSON is language-independent, with one object per line:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"phase\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ingesting\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1048576\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"total_bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">8388608\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"complete\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"buffered_blocks\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>total_bytes\u003c/code> is null when unknown. Native commit paths are relative; put/get\nreport the source/destination argument (Windows paths use forward slashes).\nEvents are throttled to about ten per second for an unchanged phase/path;\nphase/path changes and file completion are always emitted. Scanning and branch\npublication events are not aggregate project-byte totals. A nonzero exit code\nis authoritative even if an earlier file event had \u003ccode>complete: true\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>LFS preview reports verification progress; migration adds ingestion and branch\npublication events. Its prepared Git transaction finishes without cancellation\nafter the final commit command begins. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-migration/cancellation-and-recovery\">LFS migration recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/file-ranges\">File range reads\u003c/a> report bytes relative to the\nrequested range. \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-comparison\">Manifest comparisons\u003c/a> use planning counters\nin block entries across both manifests, rather than bytes. Both support\ncancellation before output publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/git-retention\">Git retention\u003c/a> scans metadata, verifies observed\nassets and publishes all added protections together. Cancellation before that\npublication changes no pin; unobserved pins are never removed automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-comparison\">Native status and snapshot comparison\u003c/a> are read-only:\nstatus hashes selected working files, while revision comparison reads only\nrevision/manifest metadata. Planning events count entries rather than bytes;\nstatus verification events report bytes within the current file. Cancellation\nand callback failure produce no partial report or Store publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">Combined status and checkout preflight\u003c/a> are also read-only.\nCombined status keeps the staged and unstaged comparisons separate. Checkout\nplanning inventories ignored/untracked paths, hashes tracked files and optionally\nverifies the complete target closure with \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. It ends with a complete\nplanning event; blockers are a completed JSON result with CLI exit 1. Neither\noperation publishes files/references or emits a publishing callback.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">Native checkout\u003c/a> journals preparation and sequential file\nmoves, with explicit resume/abort. Cancellation after journal creation can leave\na partial workspace while the original branch remains unchanged. The last\nobserver before atomic branch/journal publication is \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> with path\n\u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>; afterwards cleanup has no callbacks or cancellation. A cleanup error\ncan leave a committed journal: inspect \u003ccode>native checkout-status\u003c/code> and resume,\nas abort is refused after publication. GC and native workspace writes remain\nblocked until the active journal has been cleaned. Prepared targets and original\nbackups contain plaintext even when Store objects are encrypted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-branches\">Native branch switching\u003c/a> uses the same executor and commit\npoint. HEAD remains on the source branch until it publishes atomically with\nthe committed journal; the two branch heads are checked and kept. Abort before\npublication retains the old selection. \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> is read-only and accepts\nno progress options; \u003ccode>switch-plan\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>switch\u003c/code> support progress/cancellation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-tags\">Native tags\u003c/a> support progress/cancellation for creation, paged\nlisting, inspection, archived deletion and restoration. Mutations verify the\ncomplete retained closure before the \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event at \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>, then publish\nall tag/annotation/archive roots together. Cancellation before that point keeps\nthe old references; new unreachable metadata may remain. No observer or stop\ncheck follows publication. Read-only reports default to metadata inspection;\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> adds complete target verification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-staging\">Native staging\u003c/a> freezes selected files during \u003ccode>native add\u003c/code>;\n\u003ccode>staged\u003c/code> reads metadata and \u003ccode>reset\u003c/code> changes only the index. Staged commit never\nreads working files. Before publication, cancellation or observer failure keeps\nboth branch and index unchanged; preparation may leave unreachable objects.\nThe final \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event identifies \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>. Branch movement and index removal\npublish together, with no cancellation or observer call after that boundary.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge\">Native merge\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay\">replay\u003c/a> plan without writing\nobjects or files; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audits their input closures and any custom resolution\nmanifests. Apply recomputes a verified plan and uses the recoverable checkout\nexecutor. Cancellation before journal publication preserves branch/workspace;\nafterwards inspect/resume/abort the journal as for checkout. Explicit\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">resolution documents\u003c/a> bind the inputs and selected parent;\npartial choices never publish a partial snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-reset\">Branch reset\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-squash\">squash\u003c/a> preserve working\nfiles. Plans read metadata, with optional full stored-data verification. Application\nverifies retained data and atomically publishes branch, index and archive roots;\nthe final observer is \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> at \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>, with no callbacks after publication.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-archives\">Archive queries\u003c/a> are read-only and verify only returned roots\nwhen requested. Cancellation never publishes partial pages or reference changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These commands handle Ctrl+C, Windows Ctrl+Break and SIGINT/SIGTERM where\navailable. Cooperative cancellation reports an English/French diagnostic and\nexits with code \u003cstrong>130\u003c/strong>. The handler only records an atomic flag; an ordinary\nmonitor thread requests the C++ stop token. A read blocked on a pipe or device\nmust return before cancellation can finish. Repeated interrupts request the\nsame stop; forced process termination still uses the separate crash-recovery\npath. Committed maintenance cleanup finishes without further cancellation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The implementation follows the C++ requirements for\n\u003ca href=\"https://eel.is/c++draft/support.signal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">signal-safe operations\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"https://eel.is/c++draft/atomics.flag\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">lock-free atomic flags\u003c/a>, and the Windows\n\u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/console/handlerroutine\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">console handler contract\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-operations\">C++ operations\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/operations/c-operations\" aria-label=\"Link to C++ operations\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>C++ observers receive a \u003ccode>ProgressEvent\u003c/code> synchronously on the calling coordinator\nthread. Phases are scanning, ingesting, reconstructing, verifying, exporting,\ntransferring, planning, packing and publishing. Events contain processed bytes, an optional total,\noptional path context, a completion flag for the reported scope and the current\nbuffered-block count. Native commits provide each relative file path and its\nobserved size. An ordinary stream import has no known total. Empty files still\nreport progress and completion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Paths are borrowed for the duration of the callback. Copy them if retaining an\nevent. Callbacks must not reenter or mutate the same Store. An exception stops\nthe operation and joins ingest workers. Input-byte progress includes queued\nwork; it does not claim all blocks have already reached durable storage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>stop_source cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> options \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">settings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">ingest_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>stop \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get_token\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>progress \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ProgressEvent \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop().\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> revision \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cancellation is checked between reads, blocks, directory entries and publications.\nCurrent native IO/codec work may finish before control returns. Worker threads\nonly encode data; the coordinator performs writes and callbacks. A stopped import\ncan leave reusable unreferenced blocks. If cancellation arrives after manifest\npublication, that complete unreferenced manifest can also remain. A completed\ningestion event describes the current file&#39;s processing, rather than final\nbranch/pin or destination publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Native commits validate their complete closure before publishing a branch and\ncheck cancellation through this traversal and the final atomic reference write.\n\u003ccode>set_ref\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>compare_set_ref\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>closure\u003c/code> also accept a stop token directly.\nCancellation before publication leaves the previous reference intact. A request\nthat races with an atomic publication already in progress can arrive too late;\nonce publication starts, the operation completes that commit point.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>reconstruct\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>restore_file\u003c/code> accept \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code> with a stop token and\ncallback. Direct reconstruction into a caller-owned stream can leave partial\noutput on failure. \u003ccode>restore_file\u003c/code> stages output atomically, checks cancellation\nbefore publication and removes its temporary file on cooperative cancellation.\nAn existing destination is never overwritten. Git&#39;s required filters report\nfailure rather than presenting a cancelled conversion as successful; clean emits\nits pointer only after the manifest has been retained.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck(OperationOptions)\u003c/code> reports aggregate \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> progress without building\na complete object list. Bytes count decoded payload reads, including repeated\nblocks needed for complete file hashes and secondary packed copies; the total\nis unknown. Completion is emitted only after all checks pass. Stop checks run\nbetween objects, blocks and loose-tree entries. Format/index validation already\nin progress must finish first. Cancellation or an observer exception closes read\nsessions and restores normal cache use without modifying objects or references.\nCLI audit failures emit no partial success message on stdout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>closure(roots, verify_files, stop, progress)\u003c/code> offers the same verification\nprogress while retaining its existing materialized return value. Callers that\nonly need to audit the entire store should use streaming \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Multi-file native exports stage a complete tree before exclusive publication.\nSee \u003ca href=\"#/en/export\">export guarantees, progress and recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Network requests also support these options, with a separate final remote\npublication commit point and exit 3 for uncertain publication. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfers\">transfer cancellation, progress and recovery\u003c/a>.\nPersistent transfer sessions retain their complete source or available downloaded\nobjects during GC until completed or deliberately discarded; records are checked\nbefore GC planning. \u003ccode>agent prune\u003c/code> previews completed receipts and \u003ccode>agent prune --delete\u003c/code>\nremoves only those receipts. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">selective retention and pruning\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">journal recovery and retention\u003c/a>.\nLegacy server upload leases block offline GC until expiry, publication or deliberate\nrelease. \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">Inventory-enabled uploads\u003c/a> retain declared objects\nselectively, with committed page/hash validation. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">server leases and fenced publication\u003c/a>.\nActive \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">read leases\u003c/a> retain their complete root closure\nduring offline GC, even after the source reference disappears. Pull releases them once its verified objects are\nlocal; \u003ccode>leases ... --read\u003c/code> manages their records after server shutdown.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>GC and packing allow cancellation during verification, planning, construction\nand readback. After their publication/deletion commit point, cleanup finishes\nwithout further callbacks or cancellation. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/maintenance\">maintenance guarantees and limits\u003c/a>.\nProcess-kill recovery remains a separate mechanism described in\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/crash-recovery\">crash recovery\u003c/a>. Object, pointer and C ABI formats remain\nunchanged; upload and read leases negotiate HTTP extensions. C++ callers should rebuild\nagainst the updated headers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">Text differences\u003c/a> are read-only and bounded. \u003ccode>native diff-text SOURCE TARGET PATH\u003c/code> reconstructs and verifies only the requested file; \u003ccode>--staged\u003c/code> compares frozen entries to their original base without reading working files. Oversized inputs are reported before payload reads. Binary/budget results are explicit JSON; unified output refuses these results before stdout. Cancellation and observer failures preserve Store/workspace data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Working text diffs (\u003ccode>native diff-text --working PATH\u003c/code>) target one path against the frozen index/head or \u003ccode>--revision\u003c/code> source. Ignore exclusions, namespace obstructions and detected concurrent modifications fail explicitly. File-size limits prevent payload reads; unknown equality stays null. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff/compare-a-working-file\">working-file read guarantees\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">Partial text staging\u003c/a> has a read-only numbered preview and exact-plan application. It verifies observed working data and complete publication roots, preserves other frozen entries and publishes the new index with its old-index archive atomically. Working files remain untouched; cancellation/observer failure before publication preserves references.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native reset-hunks-plan PATH\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>native reset-hunks PATH --expect-plan ID --hunks 1,3\u003c/code> remove selected prepared text changes against their original baseline. They read frozen snapshots only, keep unselected changes and executable modes, and publish the replacement index with the same retained old-index archive. Pending checkout and stale indexes block both forms of partial staging; ordinary file reset handles additions/deletions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-lines\">Individual line selection\u003c/a> provides \u003ccode>add-lines-plan\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>add-lines\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>reset-lines-plan\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>reset-lines\u003c/code> with the same cancellation, observation and atomic retention boundaries. Each added/removed row has a plan-bound global number. Invalid newline joins and oversized results are refused before ingestion, without normalizing or changing working files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/text-patches\">External patch staging\u003c/a> uses \u003ccode>add-patch-plan\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>add-patch\u003c/code> against a verified frozen index/head file. It never reads the workspace. Patch bytes, source state, limits and result identity are bound by the expected plan. Malformed, binary, oversized or mismatched patches fail before ingestion; cancellation/observer/closure failures preserve references. Replacement index and complete old-index archive publish together.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Progress and cooperative cancellation # The C++ ingestion, native commit, reconstruction and Git-filter interfaces can report progress and accept cancellation. Native commits and clean filters use repository pipeline defaults; explicit IngestOptions replace those defaults. The CLI accepts --workers and --buffer-mib for put , native commit , native add , git clean and git migrate-lfs without changing saved preferences. CLI # text Copy syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress put , get , compare , fsck , pack , gc , native commit/add/staged/reset/status/checkout-plan/checkout/checkout-recover/switch-plan/switch/diff/diff-text/export , git clean/smudge/filter-process , native tag/tags/tag-show/tag-delete/tag-archives/tag-archive/tag-restore , native branch/branches/branch-delete-plan/branch-delete/branch-archives/branch-archive , native branch-restore-plan/branch-restore , native ancestry/cherry-pick-plan/revert-plan/cherry-pick/revert/merge-plan/merge , native reset-plan/squash-plan/squash/archives/archive-show , native rebase-plan/rebase/rebase-index-plan/rebase-index , git lfs-preview/migrate-lfs/retention-scan/retention and agent push/pull accept mutually exclusive --progress and --progress-json . Both write only to stderr; stdout keeps its ordinary JSON, revision ID, Git pointer or binary stream. With neither flag, successful commands stay quiet on stderr. Other commands reject these flags. Plain source data passing through smudge and existing clean pointers do not need block processing and may produce no progress events. Text uses the selected interface language and quotes paths to escape newlines and control characters. JSON is language-independent, with one object per line: json Copy { \"phase\" : \"ingesting\" , \"path\" : \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"bytes\" : 1048576 , \"total_bytes\" : 8388608 , \"complete\" : false , \"buffered_blocks\" : 2 } total_bytes is null when unknown. Native commit paths are relative; put/get report the source/destination argument (Windows paths use forward slashes). Events are throttled to about ten per second for an unchanged phase/path; phase/path changes and file completion are always emitted. Scanning and branch publication events are not aggregate project-byte totals. A nonzero exit code is authoritative even if an earlier file event had complete: true . LFS preview reports verification progress; migration adds ingestion and branch publication events. Its prepared Git transaction finishes without cancellation after the final commit command begins. See LFS migration recovery . File range reads report bytes relative to the requested range. Manifest comparisons use planning counters in block entries across both manifests, rather than bytes. Both support cancellation before output publication. Git retention scans metadata, verifies observed assets and publishes all added protections together. Cancellation before that publication changes no pin; unobserved pins are never removed automatically. Native status and snapshot comparison are read-only: status hashes selected working files, while revision comparison reads only revision/manifest metadata. Planning events count entries rather than bytes; status verification events report bytes within the current file. Cancellation and callback failure produce no partial report or Store publication. Combined status and checkout preflight are also read-only. Combined status keeps the staged and unstaged comparisons separate. Checkout planning inventories ignored/untracked paths, hashes tracked files and optionally verifies the complete target closure with --verify . It ends with a complete planning event; blockers are a completed JSON result with CLI exit 1. Neither operation publishes files/references or emits a publishing callback. Native checkout journals preparation and sequential file moves, with explicit resume/abort. Cancellation after journal creation can leave a partial workspace while the original branch remains unchanged. The last observer before atomic branch/journal publication is publishing with path checkout ; afterwards cleanup has no callbacks or cancellation. A cleanup error can leave a committed journal: inspect native checkout-status and resume, as abort is refused after publication. GC and native workspace writes remain blocked until the active journal has been cleaned. Prepared targets and original backups contain plaintext even when Store objects are encrypted. Native branch switching uses the same executor and commit point. HEAD remains on the source branch until it publishes atomically with the committed journal; the two branch heads are checked and kept. Abort before publication retains the old selection. native head is read-only and accepts no progress options; switch-plan and switch support progress/cancellation. Native tags support progress/cancellation for creation, paged listing, inspection, archived deletion and restoration. Mutations verify the complete retained closure before the publishing event at refs , then publish all tag/annotation/archive roots together. Cancellation before that point keeps the old references; new unreachable metadata may remain. No observer or stop check follows publication. Read-only reports default to metadata inspection; --verify adds complete target verification. Native staging freezes selected files during native add ; staged reads metadata and reset changes only the index. Staged commit never reads working files. Before publication, cancellation or observer failure keeps both branch and index unchanged; preparation may leave unreachable objects. The final publishing event identifies refs . Branch movement and index removal publish together, with no cancellation or observer call after that boundary. Native merge and replay plan without writing objects or files; --verify audits their input closures and any custom resolution manifests. Apply recomputes a verified plan and uses the recoverable checkout executor. Cancellation before journal publication preserves branch/workspace; afterwards inspect/resume/abort the journal as for checkout. Explicit resolution documents bind the inputs and selected parent; partial choices never publish a partial snapshot. Branch reset and squash preserve working files. Plans read metadata, with optional full stored-data verification. Application verifies retained data and atomically publishes branch, index and archive roots; the final observer is publishing at refs , with no callbacks after publication. Archive queries are read-only and verify only returned roots when requested. Cancellation never publishes partial pages or reference changes. These commands handle Ctrl+C, Windows Ctrl+Break and SIGINT/SIGTERM where available. Cooperative cancellation reports an English/French diagnostic and exits with code 130 . The handler only records an atomic flag; an ordinary monitor thread requests the C++ stop token. A read blocked on a pipe or device must return before cancellation can finish. Repeated interrupts request the same stop; forced process termination still uses the separate crash-recovery path. Committed maintenance cleanup finishes without further cancellation. The implementation follows the C++ requirements for signal-safe operations and lock-free atomic flags , and the Windows console handler contract . C++ operations # C++ observers receive a ProgressEvent synchronously on the calling coordinator thread. Phases are scanning, ingesting, reconstructing, verifying, exporting, transferring, planning, packing and publishing. Events contain processed bytes, an optional total, optional path context, a completion flag for the reported scope and the current buffered-block count. Native commits provide each relative file path and its observed size. An ordinary stream import has no known total. Empty files still report progress and completion. Paths are borrowed for the duration of the callback. Copy them if retaining an event. Callbacks must not reenter or mutate the same Store. An exception stops the operation and joins ingest workers. Input-byte progress includes queued work; it does not claim all blocks have already reached durable storage. cpp Copy std :: stop_source cancellation ; auto options = store . settings ( ) . ingest_options ( ) ; options . stop = cancellation . get_token ( ) ; options . progress = [ & ] ( const syrnck :: ProgressEvent & event ) { // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop(). } ; auto revision = store . commit_directory ( project , \"main\" , \"Snapshot\" , options ) ; Cancellation is checked between reads, blocks, directory entries and publications. Current native IO/codec work may finish before control returns. Worker threads only encode data; the coordinator performs writes and callbacks. A stopped import can leave reusable unreferenced blocks. If cancellation arrives after manifest publication, that complete unreferenced manifest can also remain. A completed ingestion event describes the current file's processing, rather than final branch/pin or destination publication. Native commits validate their complete closure before publishing a branch and check cancellation through this traversal and the final atomic reference write. set_ref , compare_set_ref and closure also accept a stop token directly. Cancellation before publication leaves the previous reference intact. A request that races with an atomic publication already in progress can arrive too late; once publication starts, the operation completes that commit point. reconstruct and restore_file accept OperationOptions with a stop token and callback. Direct reconstruction into a caller-owned stream can leave partial output on failure. restore_file stages output atomically, checks cancellation before publication and removes its temporary file on cooperative cancellation. An existing destination is never overwritten. Git's required filters report failure rather than presenting a cancelled conversion as successful; clean emits its pointer only after the manifest has been retained. fsck(OperationOptions) reports aggregate verifying progress without building a complete object list. Bytes count decoded payload reads, including repeated blocks needed for complete file hashes and secondary packed copies; the total is unknown. Completion is emitted only after all checks pass. Stop checks run between objects, blocks and loose-tree entries. Format/index validation already in progress must finish first. Cancellation or an observer exception closes read sessions and restores normal cache use without modifying objects or references. CLI audit failures emit no partial success message on stdout. closure(roots, verify_files, stop, progress) offers the same verification progress while retaining its existing materialized return value. Callers that only need to audit the entire store should use streaming fsck . Multi-file native exports stage a complete tree before exclusive publication. See export guarantees, progress and recovery . Network requests also support these options, with a separate final remote publication commit point and exit 3 for uncertain publication. See transfer cancellation, progress and recovery . Persistent transfer sessions retain their complete source or available downloaded objects during GC until completed or deliberately discarded; records are checked before GC planning. agent prune previews completed receipts and agent prune --delete removes only those receipts. See selective retention and pruning and journal recovery and retention . Legacy server upload leases block offline GC until expiry, publication or deliberate release. Inventory-enabled uploads retain declared objects selectively, with committed page/hash validation. See server leases and fenced publication . Active read leases retain their complete root closure during offline GC, even after the source reference disappears. Pull releases them once its verified objects are local; leases ... --read manages their records after server shutdown. GC and packing allow cancellation during verification, planning, construction and readback. After their publication/deletion commit point, cleanup finishes without further callbacks or cancellation. See maintenance guarantees and limits . Process-kill recovery remains a separate mechanism described in crash recovery . Object, pointer and C ABI formats remain unchanged; upload and read leases negotiate HTTP extensions. C++ callers should rebuild against the updated headers. Text differences are read-only and bounded. native diff-text SOURCE TARGET PATH reconstructs and verifies only the requested file; --staged compares frozen entries to their original base without reading working files. Oversized inputs are reported before payload reads. Binary/budget results are explicit JSON; unified output refuses these results before stdout. Cancellation and observer failures preserve Store/workspace data. Working text diffs ( native diff-text --working PATH ) target one path against the frozen index/head or --revision source. Ignore exclusions, namespace obstructions and detected concurrent modifications fail explicitly. File-size limits prevent payload reads; unknown equality stays null. See working-file read guarantees . Partial text staging has a read-only numbered preview and exact-plan application. It verifies observed working data and complete publication roots, preserves other frozen entries and publishes the new index with its old-index archive atomically. Working files remain untouched; cancellation/observer failure before publication preserves references. native reset-hunks-plan PATH and native reset-hunks PATH --expect-plan ID --hunks 1,3 remove selected prepared text changes against their original baseline. They read frozen snapshots only, keep unselected changes and executable modes, and publish the replacement index with the same retained old-index archive. Pending checkout and stale indexes block both forms of partial staging; ordinary file reset handles additions/deletions. Individual line selection provides add-lines-plan / add-lines and reset-lines-plan / reset-lines with the same cancellation, observation and atomic retention boundaries. Each added/removed row has a plan-bound global number. Invalid newline joins and oversized results are refused before ingestion, without normalizing or changing working files. External patch staging uses add-patch-plan / add-patch against a verified frozen index/head file. It never reads the workspace. Patch bytes, source state, limits and result identity are bound by the expected plan. Malformed, binary, oversized or mismatched patches fail before ingestion; cancellation/observer/closure failures preserve references. Replacement index and complete old-index archive publish together. syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress {\"phase\":\"ingesting\",\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"bytes\":1048576,\"total_bytes\":8388608,\"complete\":false,\"buffered_blocks\":2} std::stop_source cancellation; auto options = store.settings().ingest_options(); options.stop = cancellation.get_token(); options.progress = [&](const syrnck::ProgressEvent &event) { // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop(). }; auto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Snapshot\", options);","headings":[{"id":"progress-and-cooperative-cancellation","text":"Progress and cooperative cancellation","level":1},{"id":"cli","text":"CLI","level":2},{"id":"c-operations","text":"C++ operations","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\nsyrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress\nsyrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json\nsyrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress\n{\"phase\":\"ingesting\",\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"bytes\":1048576,\"total_bytes\":8388608,\"complete\":false,\"buffered_blocks\":2}\nstd::stop_source cancellation;\nauto options = store.settings().ingest_options();\noptions.stop = cancellation.get_token();\noptions.progress = [&](const syrnck::ProgressEvent &event) {\n  // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop().\n};\nauto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Snapshot\", options);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/git-lfs-migration/cancellation-and-recovery","#/en/file-ranges","#/en/file-comparison","#/en/git-retention","#/en/native-comparison","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-branches","#/en/native-tags","#/en/native-staging","#/en/native-merge","#/en/native-replay","#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/native-reset","#/en/native-squash","#/en/native-archives","https://eel.is/c++draft/support.signal","https://eel.is/c++draft/atomics.flag","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/console/handlerroutine","#/en/export","#/en/transfers","#/en/selective-retention","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/maintenance","#/en/crash-recovery","#/en/text-diff","#/en/text-diff/compare-a-working-file","#/en/native-hunks","#/en/native-lines","#/en/text-patches"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"overview","title":"Syrnck","description":"Syrnck","section":"Syrnck","order":0,"revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"getting-started\">Choose your workflow\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/overview/getting-started\" aria-label=\"Link to Choose your workflow\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Syrnck is a C++20 block storage and revision engine for large project assets. A file version is described by a complete manifest, so reconstruction never requires replaying a chain of binary patches. Shared chunks can be reused between versions, paths and transfers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Start with the \u003ca href=\"#/en/usage\">walkthrough\u003c/a>, then choose \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets\">Git assets\u003c/a> or \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-history\">native history\u003c/a>. Inspect \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace\">workspace status\u003c/a> before changes and keep a \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">verified backup\u003c/a> before retiring old versions. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/storage\">storage guide\u003c/a> explains what retains those bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workflows\">What is available\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/overview/workflows\" aria-label=\"Link to What is available\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Git keeps ordinary text/code history, branches, review, merge and other Git features; Syrnck filters store selected assets as pointers. Hosting Git pointers on GitHub/GitLab does not provide a Syrnck block server. A separate block store/server is required for shared asset data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The standalone engine has commits, frozen staging, branches, tags, recoverable checkout, merge, linear/interactive rebase, cherry-pick, revert, squash, reset, stash, exact rename history and bisect. Each command&#39;s guide states its conflict and publication rules. \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfers\">Transfers\u003c/a> publish only complete verified object closures. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent\">resident publication agent\u003c/a> watches local changes and retries native pushes, including after offline edits. Its optional bidirectional mode receives revisions and merges compatible concurrent history. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">Permissions and asset leases\u003c/a> control configured servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/integrations\">C++/C/Go integrations\u003c/a> include a regular-file \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing\">Syncthing BEP adapter\u003c/a>. It is not a resident Syncthing daemon. There is no Unreal plugin; the engine stores ordinary asset bytes without interpreting Unreal objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading\">Use this local manual\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/overview/reading\" aria-label=\"Link to Use this local manual\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The complete reader works through file:// without a server or Internet connection. Search prose and code with the header or Ctrl+K. Use the navigation tree, keyboard Tab/Enter and arrow keys; compact layouts expose a menu button. Theme follows the system initially and can be changed in the theme menu. Both English and French use the same page and section IDs. An unavailable language route returns to English while keeping the destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Bookmarks, notes and reading preferences depend on browser local-storage policy. Page positions are kept during navigation; explicit section links take priority. External reference links are optional and require Internet access only if followed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>syrnck help\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>syrnck help native-tags\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>syrnck --lang fr help native-tags\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--section ID\u003c/code> opens a precise section, \u003ccode>--print-url\u003c/code> prints the address without launching a browser, and \u003ccode>--directory PATH\u003c/code> selects another installed Documentation folder. The terminal&#39;s \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code> remains the concise command reference. This CLI has no help menu or F1 UI to replace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Current boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/overview/limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Current boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Read the \u003ca href=\"#/en/architecture\">architecture\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/protocol\">formats\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/limits\">limits\u003c/a> before embedding or deploying. Encryption protects stored objects, while transfers expose decoded objects to the trusted server. End-to-end encryption, distributed retention and a complete continuous synchronization service are not implemented. Syrnck&#39;s original code is MIT; \u003ca href=\"#/en/licenses\">dependency notices\u003c/a> apply separately.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Choose your workflow # Syrnck is a C++20 block storage and revision engine for large project assets. A file version is described by a complete manifest, so reconstruction never requires replaying a chain of binary patches. Shared chunks can be reused between versions, paths and transfers. Start with the walkthrough , then choose Git assets or native history . Inspect workspace status before changes and keep a verified backup before retiring old versions. The storage guide explains what retains those bytes. What is available # Git keeps ordinary text/code history, branches, review, merge and other Git features; Syrnck filters store selected assets as pointers. Hosting Git pointers on GitHub/GitLab does not provide a Syrnck block server. A separate block store/server is required for shared asset data. The standalone engine has commits, frozen staging, branches, tags, recoverable checkout, merge, linear/interactive rebase, cherry-pick, revert, squash, reset, stash, exact rename history and bisect. Each command's guide states its conflict and publication rules. Transfers publish only complete verified object closures. The resident publication agent watches local changes and retries native pushes, including after offline edits. Its optional bidirectional mode receives revisions and merges compatible concurrent history. Permissions and asset leases control configured servers. The C++/C/Go integrations include a regular-file Syncthing BEP adapter . It is not a resident Syncthing daemon. There is no Unreal plugin; the engine stores ordinary asset bytes without interpreting Unreal objects. Use this local manual # The complete reader works through file:// without a server or Internet connection. Search prose and code with the header or Ctrl+K. Use the navigation tree, keyboard Tab/Enter and arrow keys; compact layouts expose a menu button. Theme follows the system initially and can be changed in the theme menu. Both English and French use the same page and section IDs. An unavailable language route returns to English while keeping the destination. Bookmarks, notes and reading preferences depend on browser local-storage policy. Page positions are kept during navigation; explicit section links take priority. External reference links are optional and require Internet access only if followed. Run syrnck help , syrnck help native-tags , or syrnck --lang fr help native-tags . --section ID opens a precise section, --print-url prints the address without launching a browser, and --directory PATH selects another installed Documentation folder. The terminal's --help remains the concise command reference. This CLI has no help menu or F1 UI to replace. Current boundaries # Read the architecture , formats and limits before embedding or deploying. Encryption protects stored objects, while transfers expose decoded objects to the trusted server. End-to-end encryption, distributed retention and a complete continuous synchronization service are not implemented. Syrnck's original code is MIT; dependency notices apply separately.","headings":[{"id":"getting-started","text":"Choose your workflow","level":2},{"id":"workflows","text":"What is available","level":2},{"id":"reading","text":"Use this local manual","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Current boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/usage","#/en/git-assets","#/en/native-history","#/en/native-workspace","#/en/backups","#/en/storage","#/en/transfers","#/en/resident-agent","#/en/server-access","#/en/integrations","#/en/syncthing","#/en/architecture","#/en/protocol","#/en/limits","#/en/licenses"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"packs","title":"Packs and compaction","description":"Packs and compaction guide.","section":"Storage","order":10,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"packs-et-compaction\">Packs and compaction\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/packs-et-compaction\" aria-label=\"Link to Packs and compaction\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck pack\u003c/code> groups loose objects into indexed immutable packs while preserving IDs, \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code> envelopes, encryption nonces, manifests and references. Ordinary maintenance adds packs; \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> explicitly regroups all objects. Neither removes logical versions. C++: \u003ccode>Store::pack(PackOptions)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store donnees pack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store donnees pack --compact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store donnees gc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store donnees gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"activation-et-compatibilite\">Activation and compatibility\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/activation-et-compatibilite\" aria-label=\"Link to Activation and compatibility\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>New stores remain physical v1 (one file/object). The first explicit pack publishes v2 when one pack suffices, v3 for several; adding a second pack to v2 selects v3. Logical objects/network remain v1. The engine reads all three; older binaries refuse unknown physical versions. Once v3, it stays v3 even with one/no packs. Opening never converts; no downgrade command exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>New writes remain loose. Reading combines loose/active packs; loose+packed duplicates count once and ordinary reads prefer loose. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> checks both physical copies. A key in two active packs is invalid. CLI, filters, server, C and Go read via this core; peers exchange verified logical objects independently of layouts/keys. Maintenance is CLI/C++ only.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"regroupement-incremental-et-complet\">Incremental and full packing\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/regroupement-incremental-et-complet\" aria-label=\"Link to Incremental and full packing\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Default pack selects loose-only keys and appends new packs. Existing names/bytes/timestamps remain; redundant loose copies are removed after verification. With no new objects/copies, v2/v3 does not rewrite pack/index/config, though inactive leftovers may be cleaned. \u003ccode>--compact\u003c/code> regroups everything to reduce pack count while respecting limits; a single pack is not guaranteed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--max-mib\u003c/code>: 18..4096 MiB, default 512; \u003ccode>--max-objects\u003c/code>: 1..1,048,576, default 65,536. The byte budget includes the eight-byte header; whole envelopes up to 17 MiB must fit. C++ fields: \u003ccode>max_pack_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_objects_per_pack\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>compact\u003c/code>; exact minimum 17 MiB+8 bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Planning uses stored lengths and checks actual envelopes again. Insufficient catalog slots fail before building: raise limits or compact. Result final-catalog fields \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_bytes\u003c/code> differ from \u003ccode>new_packs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_written\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_bytes_written\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_bytes_written\u003c/code>. Bytes include whole files. \u003ccode>loose_objects_removed\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>obsolete_pack_files_removed\u003c/code> count physical removals. Existing data is still fully audited; incremental means fewer rewrites, not constant-time validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-et-reprise\">Publication and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/publication-et-reprise\" aria-label=\"Link to Publication and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Under the exclusive Store lock: reload config/indexes and verify all objects/dependencies/file hashes including unpinned data; plan and stream durable packs/indexes; read back every envelope and verify ID/format/authentication; atomically replace \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> to publish the entire catalog; then remove redundant loose/inactive generated files.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Failure before config replacement preserves the old catalog. A batch can leave unpublished packs. After publication, interruption can leave duplicates/old packs, but the catalog names complete copies. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code> cleans after validation. Only inactive generated \u003ccode>pack-&lt;32 lowercase hex&gt;.syp/.syi\u003c/code> names are eligible; symlinks are refused.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A flush can fail after atomic replacement when the new catalog is already visible. The handle reloads effective config before returning the error; failed reload invalidates its in-memory index. Fix the IO issue and reopen or complete a fresh audit before object access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-selectif\">Selective GC\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/gc-selectif\" aria-label=\"Link to Selective GC\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>GC validates every retained closure and complete file hash without caches before deleting. Missing/corrupt retained data stops it. Removing a reference cannot remove blocks shared by another branch/archive/revision/pin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each pack: fully live stays unchanged; fully dead leaves the catalog without copying; partly live gets a replacement with only verified survivors. Catalog changes publish together before old files are removed. Live loose objects stay loose. GC does not merge packs; compact separately. A replacement cannot exceed its source count/size, even for an older large pack.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Preview writes no pack/config. \u003ccode>reclaimable_bytes\u003c/code> sums dead envelope copies excluding headers/indexes. \u003ccode>repack_required\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>packs_to_rewrite\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>packs_to_drop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_bytes_to_write\u003c/code> describe replacement work; the latter includes new pack headers, excludes indexes. \u003ccode>obsolete_pack_files\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>obsolete_pack_bytes\u003c/code> separately describe already inactive leftovers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memoire-du-gc\">GC memory\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/memoire-du-gc\" aria-label=\"Link to GC memory\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>After interrupted GC, dead loose metadata may temporarily outlive dead packed dependencies. Retained versions remain complete but \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> can report orphan dependencies; resume GC before a full audit. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\">recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>GC streams 256-entry pages and uses \u003ccode>sum(ceil(pack_objects/8))\u003c/code> mark bytes:128 KiB for 1,048,576 entries in one pack, before overhead. Only survivor count/size per pack are retained; no dead/survivor-ID lists. Streaming output checks count/order and verifies readback before publication with unchanged formats.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Live keys absent from packs still use an ordered set of 33-byte typed keys plus allocation overhead. Loose-only marking is proportional to live objects. Traversal stack, refs, JSON, codecs, directories/filters/caches add memory; no global ceiling. A first loose traversal validates the tree/counters before any replacement/deletion; a second removes dead loose data after publication. Duplicates count once logically and all dead copies contribute reclaimable bytes. Cleanup uses the new catalog, never reinterpreting old bitmap positions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>packed_mark_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>loose_live_objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>peak_pending_objects\u003c/code> report useful initial bitmap bytes, live keys outside the initial catalog and maximum pending stack keys. They do not measure all allocations/RSS. Root validation ignores caches and both passes/publication stay exclusively locked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limites-actuelles\">Current limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/packs/limites-actuelles\" aria-label=\"Link to Current limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Catalog limits:1,024 packs,1,048,576 objects/pack,4,194,304 packed objects. Index bytes: \u003ccode>16 * packs + 45 * objects\u003c/code>. Opening validates complete indexes then uses 256-record pages, directory/filter and default 4 MiB LRU (\u003ccode>--index-cache-mib\u003c/code>). Overlapping packs use merged duplicate validation. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">snapshot guarantees\u003c/a>. Limits do not establish multi-million-object load validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Writing streams objects without loading whole packs. Compaction needs new-generation disk space before removal and preserves envelopes without recompression/rekeying. Reconstruction/closure/audit sessions share at most eight LRU pack handles, closed on outer return/exception and before publication/removal; isolated reads open independently. Every envelope is reread before cache reuse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Paged metadata trees, automatic merge-pack selection and concurrent online maintenance remain future work. \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> and public closures retain materialized inventories; streaming plans sort only loose keys. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> streams with packed bitmaps. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/compaction\">compaction\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/audit\">audits\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/protocol\">binary format\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance\">resource budgets\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/limits\">limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Packs and compaction # syrnck pack groups loose objects into indexed immutable packs while preserving IDs, SYR1 envelopes, encryption nonces, manifests and references. Ordinary maintenance adds packs; pack --compact explicitly regroups all objects. Neither removes logical versions. C++: Store::pack(PackOptions) . text Copy syrnck --store donnees pack syrnck --store donnees pack --compact syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck --store donnees gc syrnck --store donnees gc --delete Activation and compatibility # New stores remain physical v1 (one file/object). The first explicit pack publishes v2 when one pack suffices, v3 for several; adding a second pack to v2 selects v3. Logical objects/network remain v1. The engine reads all three; older binaries refuse unknown physical versions. Once v3, it stays v3 even with one/no packs. Opening never converts; no downgrade command exists. New writes remain loose. Reading combines loose/active packs; loose+packed duplicates count once and ordinary reads prefer loose. fsck checks both physical copies. A key in two active packs is invalid. CLI, filters, server, C and Go read via this core; peers exchange verified logical objects independently of layouts/keys. Maintenance is CLI/C++ only. Incremental and full packing # Default pack selects loose-only keys and appends new packs. Existing names/bytes/timestamps remain; redundant loose copies are removed after verification. With no new objects/copies, v2/v3 does not rewrite pack/index/config, though inactive leftovers may be cleaned. --compact regroups everything to reduce pack count while respecting limits; a single pack is not guaranteed. --max-mib : 18..4096 MiB, default 512; --max-objects : 1..1,048,576, default 65,536. The byte budget includes the eight-byte header; whole envelopes up to 17 MiB must fit. C++ fields: max_pack_bytes , max_objects_per_pack , compact ; exact minimum 17 MiB+8 bytes. Planning uses stored lengths and checks actual envelopes again. Insufficient catalog slots fail before building: raise limits or compact. Result final-catalog fields objects , pack_count , pack_bytes , index_bytes differ from new_packs , objects_written , pack_bytes_written , index_bytes_written . Bytes include whole files. loose_objects_removed and obsolete_pack_files_removed count physical removals. Existing data is still fully audited; incremental means fewer rewrites, not constant-time validation. Publication and recovery # Under the exclusive Store lock: reload config/indexes and verify all objects/dependencies/file hashes including unpinned data; plan and stream durable packs/indexes; read back every envelope and verify ID/format/authentication; atomically replace config.json to publish the entire catalog; then remove redundant loose/inactive generated files. Failure before config replacement preserves the old catalog. A batch can leave unpublished packs. After publication, interruption can leave duplicates/old packs, but the catalog names complete copies. pack or gc --delete cleans after validation. Only inactive generated pack-\u003c32 lowercase hex>.syp/.syi names are eligible; symlinks are refused. A flush can fail after atomic replacement when the new catalog is already visible. The handle reloads effective config before returning the error; failed reload invalidates its in-memory index. Fix the IO issue and reopen or complete a fresh audit before object access. Selective GC # GC validates every retained closure and complete file hash without caches before deleting. Missing/corrupt retained data stops it. Removing a reference cannot remove blocks shared by another branch/archive/revision/pin. Each pack: fully live stays unchanged; fully dead leaves the catalog without copying; partly live gets a replacement with only verified survivors. Catalog changes publish together before old files are removed. Live loose objects stay loose. GC does not merge packs; compact separately. A replacement cannot exceed its source count/size, even for an older large pack. Preview writes no pack/config. reclaimable_bytes sums dead envelope copies excluding headers/indexes. repack_required , packs_to_rewrite , packs_to_drop , pack_bytes_to_write describe replacement work; the latter includes new pack headers, excludes indexes. obsolete_pack_files / obsolete_pack_bytes separately describe already inactive leftovers. GC memory # After interrupted GC, dead loose metadata may temporarily outlive dead packed dependencies. Retained versions remain complete but fsck can report orphan dependencies; resume GC before a full audit. See recovery . GC streams 256-entry pages and uses sum(ceil(pack_objects/8)) mark bytes:128 KiB for 1,048,576 entries in one pack, before overhead. Only survivor count/size per pack are retained; no dead/survivor-ID lists. Streaming output checks count/order and verifies readback before publication with unchanged formats. Live keys absent from packs still use an ordered set of 33-byte typed keys plus allocation overhead. Loose-only marking is proportional to live objects. Traversal stack, refs, JSON, codecs, directories/filters/caches add memory; no global ceiling. A first loose traversal validates the tree/counters before any replacement/deletion; a second removes dead loose data after publication. Duplicates count once logically and all dead copies contribute reclaimable bytes. Cleanup uses the new catalog, never reinterpreting old bitmap positions. packed_mark_bytes , loose_live_objects , peak_pending_objects report useful initial bitmap bytes, live keys outside the initial catalog and maximum pending stack keys. They do not measure all allocations/RSS. Root validation ignores caches and both passes/publication stay exclusively locked. Current limits # Catalog limits:1,024 packs,1,048,576 objects/pack,4,194,304 packed objects. Index bytes: 16 * packs + 45 * objects . Opening validates complete indexes then uses 256-record pages, directory/filter and default 4 MiB LRU ( --index-cache-mib ). Overlapping packs use merged duplicate validation. See snapshot guarantees . Limits do not establish multi-million-object load validation. Writing streams objects without loading whole packs. Compaction needs new-generation disk space before removal and preserves envelopes without recompression/rekeying. Reconstruction/closure/audit sessions share at most eight LRU pack handles, closed on outer return/exception and before publication/removal; isolated reads open independently. Every envelope is reread before cache reuse. Paged metadata trees, automatic merge-pack selection and concurrent online maintenance remain future work. objects() and public closures retain materialized inventories; streaming plans sort only loose keys. fsck streams with packed bitmaps. See compaction , audits , binary format , resource budgets and limits . syrnck --store donnees pack syrnck --store donnees pack --compact syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck --store donnees gc syrnck --store donnees gc --delete","headings":[{"id":"packs-et-compaction","text":"Packs and compaction","level":1},{"id":"activation-et-compatibilite","text":"Activation and compatibility","level":2},{"id":"regroupement-incremental-et-complet","text":"Incremental and full packing","level":2},{"id":"publication-et-reprise","text":"Publication and recovery","level":2},{"id":"gc-selectif","text":"Selective GC","level":2},{"id":"memoire-du-gc","text":"GC memory","level":2},{"id":"limites-actuelles","text":"Current limits","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store donnees pack\nsyrnck --store donnees pack --compact\nsyrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\nsyrnck --store donnees gc\nsyrnck --store donnees gc --delete","assets":[],"links":["#/en/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection","#/en/indexes","#/en/compaction","#/en/audit","#/en/protocol","#/en/performance","#/en/limits"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"parallel-transfers","title":"Bounded parallel object transfers","description":"Bounded parallel object transfers guide.","section":"Transfers","order":460,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-parallel-object-transfers\">Bounded parallel object transfers\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers/bounded-parallel-object-transfers\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounded parallel object transfers\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The agent can overlap HTTP object requests using libcurl&#39;s\n\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/libcurl-multi.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">multi interface\u003c/a>. Store reads,\nimports, dependency discovery and observer callbacks remain on the calling\ncoordinator thread. No Store handle is shared with HTTP worker threads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These examples require an actual configured server and the existing token/key\nenvironment. The default is \u003cstrong>one object request\u003c/strong>, with a \u003cstrong>64 MiB buffer\nbudget\u003c/strong>. \u003ccode>--requests\u003c/code> accepts 1..32 and \u003ccode>--transfer-buffer-mib\u003c/code> accepts 17..1024.\nIn C++, pass \u003ccode>net::TransferOptions\u003c/code> as the fifth argument of \u003ccode>push\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pull\u003c/code>,\nafter \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>. Existing calls remain valid and run one object request\nat a time by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push negotiates missing keys in batches of at most 4,096, validates its source\nclosure and prepares upload payloads on the coordinator. Pull discovers\ndependencies as verified metadata arrives. A seen set prevents duplicate\ndownloads, including dependencies already scheduled in another active request.\nLocally available pull objects use one verified \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> rather than a separate\npresence read followed by another payload read. Imported objects are validated\nbefore dependency traversal or reference publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-counters\">Limits and counters\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers/limits-and-counters\" aria-label=\"Link to Limits and counters\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The scheduler charges actual upload-vector capacity plus a reserved response\nbuffer for every outstanding object request. Downloads reserve the maximum\nlogical object size, \u003cstrong>16 MiB\u003c/strong>, each. Upload acknowledgements are limited to\n\u003cstrong>4 KiB\u003c/strong> and reserve that much response space. Larger replies fail the object\nrequest. Before decoding another upload, the scheduler also requires enough\nfree budget for a maximum object and its acknowledgement. Consequently actual\nconcurrency can be lower than the requested maximum.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">Negotiated compression\u003c/a> also charges candidate upload\nbuffers while the original bytes remain live, including candidates discarded\nfor insufficient savings. Compressed downloads decode directly into their\nreserved response buffer. Codec contexts remain outside these body credits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Object response vectors reserve their permitted capacity once on first data,\navoiding old-and-new growth buffers during object transfer. Credits remain\ncharged while the coordinator consumes a completed response. Completed requests\nare removed from the multi handle before consumption; other requests remain\nactive. Idle waits use a bounded\n\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_multi_poll.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">multi poll\u003c/a>. The session can reuse\nconnections across object requests in the same batch/walk.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The final statistics retain \u003ccode>transferred_objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>reused_objects\u003c/code>, and add:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>peak_object_requests\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Maximum simultaneous scheduled object requests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>peak_object_buffer_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Maximum charged upload/response-buffer credits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>object_retries\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Additional object attempts scheduled after transient failures\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Encoded object request-body bytes, including repeated attempts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Encoded object response-body bytes, including partial attempts and upload acknowledgements\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The buffer counter includes reserved response capacity, even when a response is\nsmall. It is \u003cstrong>not process RSS\u003c/strong>. It excludes allocator overhead, libcurl/TLS\ninternal buffers, the Store&#39;s caches, codec scratch, closure/seen/pending keys,\nmetadata and sequential control-plane requests. A fully reused transfer can\nreport zero object requests while still negotiating and publishing a reference.\nThe options do not bound all metadata or total process memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-and-publication\">Failure and publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers/failure-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Failure and publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An exhausted/non-retriable request failure, corrupt response, observer exception or cooperative stop\nremoves remaining easy handles and releases their payloads. Removal happens\noutside libcurl callbacks. C callbacks capture exceptions and return an error;\nthe coordinator rethrows the original exception. Previously retained roots stay\ncomplete; already verified unreferenced objects can be reused on retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Transient failures retry with bounded backoff; waiting requests retain their\nslot and buffer credits while other active requests can continue. Optional\nupload/download targets are shared across parallel requests. See the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-policy\">retry and traffic policy\u003c/a> for the allowlist, counters,\nunits, pacing limits and cancellation rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Progress events from different protocol paths can interleave. Each request&#39;s\nbyte counter remains scoped to its own path. Completion of a request does not\npublish a reference. Once every needed object is present, the existing\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfers/publication-boundary\">publication commit point\u003c/a> still applies:\nlocal reference writes check cancellation, and the final remote CAS finishes\nonce dispatched. An ambiguous CAS result still throws \u003ccode>PublicationUncertain\u003c/code>\nand makes the CLI exit 3. There is no automatic CAS retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The production server still serializes Store operations under its mutex while\nHTTP IO can overlap. Metadata traversal and imports remain coordinated rather\nthan parallel. \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">Persistent journals\u003c/a> retain local recovery\nstate and retain available transfer objects during local GC. Compatible servers also use\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">upload leases\u003c/a>, whose dedicated renewal worker leaves\nobject IO running while callbacks and Store access remain coordinated. Its\ncontrol connection/buffers are outside the object counters. \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">Upload inventory\u003c/a>\nregistration also uses foreground control requests outside those counters.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">Read leases\u003c/a> protect pull sources. \u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">Network compression\u003c/a> is negotiated separately. Distributed retention and multi-source scheduling remain future work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Bounded parallel object transfers # The agent can overlap HTTP object requests using libcurl's multi interface . Store reads, imports, dependency discovery and observer callbacks remain on the calling coordinator thread. No Store handle is shared with HTTP worker threads. text Copy syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json These examples require an actual configured server and the existing token/key environment. The default is one object request , with a 64 MiB buffer budget . --requests accepts 1..32 and --transfer-buffer-mib accepts 17..1024. In C++, pass net::TransferOptions as the fifth argument of push or pull , after OperationOptions . Existing calls remain valid and run one object request at a time by default. Push negotiates missing keys in batches of at most 4,096, validates its source closure and prepares upload payloads on the coordinator. Pull discovers dependencies as verified metadata arrives. A seen set prevents duplicate downloads, including dependencies already scheduled in another active request. Locally available pull objects use one verified get rather than a separate presence read followed by another payload read. Imported objects are validated before dependency traversal or reference publication. Limits and counters # The scheduler charges actual upload-vector capacity plus a reserved response buffer for every outstanding object request. Downloads reserve the maximum logical object size, 16 MiB , each. Upload acknowledgements are limited to 4 KiB and reserve that much response space. Larger replies fail the object request. Before decoding another upload, the scheduler also requires enough free budget for a maximum object and its acknowledgement. Consequently actual concurrency can be lower than the requested maximum. Negotiated compression also charges candidate upload buffers while the original bytes remain live, including candidates discarded for insufficient savings. Compressed downloads decode directly into their reserved response buffer. Codec contexts remain outside these body credits. Object response vectors reserve their permitted capacity once on first data, avoiding old-and-new growth buffers during object transfer. Credits remain charged while the coordinator consumes a completed response. Completed requests are removed from the multi handle before consumption; other requests remain active. Idle waits use a bounded multi poll . The session can reuse connections across object requests in the same batch/walk. The final statistics retain transferred_objects , transferred_bytes and reused_objects , and add: Field Meaning peak_object_requests Maximum simultaneous scheduled object requests peak_object_buffer_bytes Maximum charged upload/response-buffer credits object_retries Additional object attempts scheduled after transient failures object_upload_bytes Encoded object request-body bytes, including repeated attempts object_download_bytes Encoded object response-body bytes, including partial attempts and upload acknowledgements The buffer counter includes reserved response capacity, even when a response is small. It is not process RSS . It excludes allocator overhead, libcurl/TLS internal buffers, the Store's caches, codec scratch, closure/seen/pending keys, metadata and sequential control-plane requests. A fully reused transfer can report zero object requests while still negotiating and publishing a reference. The options do not bound all metadata or total process memory. Failure and publication # An exhausted/non-retriable request failure, corrupt response, observer exception or cooperative stop removes remaining easy handles and releases their payloads. Removal happens outside libcurl callbacks. C callbacks capture exceptions and return an error; the coordinator rethrows the original exception. Previously retained roots stay complete; already verified unreferenced objects can be reused on retry. Transient failures retry with bounded backoff; waiting requests retain their slot and buffer credits while other active requests can continue. Optional upload/download targets are shared across parallel requests. See the retry and traffic policy for the allowlist, counters, units, pacing limits and cancellation rules. Progress events from different protocol paths can interleave. Each request's byte counter remains scoped to its own path. Completion of a request does not publish a reference. Once every needed object is present, the existing publication commit point still applies: local reference writes check cancellation, and the final remote CAS finishes once dispatched. An ambiguous CAS result still throws PublicationUncertain and makes the CLI exit 3. There is no automatic CAS retry. The production server still serializes Store operations under its mutex while HTTP IO can overlap. Metadata traversal and imports remain coordinated rather than parallel. Persistent journals retain local recovery state and retain available transfer objects during local GC. Compatible servers also use upload leases , whose dedicated renewal worker leaves object IO running while callbacks and Store access remain coordinated. Its control connection/buffers are outside the object counters. Upload inventory registration also uses foreground control requests outside those counters. Read leases protect pull sources. Network compression is negotiated separately. Distributed retention and multi-source scheduling remain future work. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"bounded-parallel-object-transfers","text":"Bounded parallel object transfers","level":1},{"id":"limits-and-counters","text":"Limits and counters","level":2},{"id":"failure-and-publication","text":"Failure and publication","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["https://curl.se/libcurl/c/libcurl-multi.html","#/en/network-compression","https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_multi_poll.html","#/en/transfer-policy","#/en/transfers/publication-boundary","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/network-compression"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"performance","title":"Performance and resource budgets","description":"Performance and resource budgets","section":"Storage","order":90,"parent":"storage","revision":"2","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"budgets\">Choose budgets for the workload\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/performance/budgets\" aria-label=\"Link to Choose budgets for the workload\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Decoded cache defaults to 64 MiB, verified stamps to 8192 entries and index pages to 4 MiB. These are independent budgets, not a total process-memory bound. Ingestion defaults to one worker with a 32 MiB raw in-flight budget; optional workers add codec/output memory. Transfer request/compression buffers have separate bounds. Tune using \u003ca href=\"#/en/settings\">settings\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">indexes\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers\">parallel transfer\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">network compression\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Objects and flat manifests/revisions are bounded to 16 MiB. Catalog limits are 1024 packs, 1,048,576 entries per pack and 4,194,304 packed entries. Limits do not prove tested project scale. Real Unreal assets may deduplicate poorly when rewritten/compressed/encrypted before ingestion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measurement\">Interpret counters carefully\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/performance/measurement\" aria-label=\"Link to Interpret counters carefully\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>io_stats\u003c/code> counts successful envelope reads/bytes, full decodes and cache hits; index memory fields cover useful directory/filter/page capacities, excluding allocator overhead and OS caches. GC reports packed mark bytes, loose live keys and peak pending traversal keys. None is process RSS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Opening validates full indexes linearly. Streaming audits/GC/compaction reduce packed-ID materialization but loose keys, metadata, stacks, codecs and caches still cost memory. Checkout reconstructs complete changed files, with private disk space and complete validation. Watch can target subtrees while periodically performing full audits. Synthetic benchmark reports and reproduction scripts remain developer records outside this user manual; their timings are not universal throughput promises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Incremental working-file capture\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/performance/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Link to Incremental working-file capture\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Resident native agents and C/Go workspace captures reuse manifests ingested by\ntheir session when the published parent, context and materialization match and\nstrong local filesystem identity/change stamps are unchanged. They enumerate the\ncomplete selected tree, including renames, deletions and ignore rules. Closing\nand reopening the Store retains session proof; status indexes and notifications\nnever establish it. Complete object closure verification still runs, including\nhistorical parents and reused manifests; corrupt/missing data refuses publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Recent files (two-second guard), active Windows writers and unsupported/remote\nfilesystems use ordinary ingestion. Stamp checks support local NTFS/ReFS,\nLinux ext4/XFS/Btrfs/tmpfs and macOS APFS. Restart, explicit invalidation, failed\nnative calls or changed parent/selection discard reuse. One-shot \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code>\ncontinues to ingest all sources.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C capture JSON adds \u003ccode>capture: {version, ingested_files, reused_files, source_bytes_read, source_bytes_reused}\u003c/code>. Go exposes \u003ccode>CaptureReportContext\u003c/code>;\n\u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code> keeps returning its revision ID. C++ exposes \u003ccode>WorkspaceCapture\u003c/code>\nin \u003ccode>&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp&gt;\u003c/code>. Counters cover source reads, excluding metadata,\nclosure verification and OS caches.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Build \u003ccode>syrnck_capture_benchmark\u003c/code>, then run:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe PATH_TO_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Add \u003ccode>--encrypted\u003c/code> for at-rest encryption. This synthetic benchmark alternates\nfull/session captures, includes Store opening and complete publication, records\nStore I/O separately, and verifies every result by a full content scan. It does\nnot establish Unreal-project throughput. Historical verification and flat\nmetadata remain scaling limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Successful workspace captures seed the observer from their complete selected\ninventory, so the next native event tick can target its changed paths. Capture\nconsumes only pre-capture batches; notifications arriving during or after it\nremain queued. A periodic audit still forces a full scan. This is an advisory\nobservation at capture time, not an atomic snapshot of concurrent writers.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Choose budgets for the workload # Decoded cache defaults to 64 MiB, verified stamps to 8192 entries and index pages to 4 MiB. These are independent budgets, not a total process-memory bound. Ingestion defaults to one worker with a 32 MiB raw in-flight budget; optional workers add codec/output memory. Transfer request/compression buffers have separate bounds. Tune using settings , indexes , parallel transfer and network compression . Objects and flat manifests/revisions are bounded to 16 MiB. Catalog limits are 1024 packs, 1,048,576 entries per pack and 4,194,304 packed entries. Limits do not prove tested project scale. Real Unreal assets may deduplicate poorly when rewritten/compressed/encrypted before ingestion. Interpret counters carefully # io_stats counts successful envelope reads/bytes, full decodes and cache hits; index memory fields cover useful directory/filter/page capacities, excluding allocator overhead and OS caches. GC reports packed mark bytes, loose live keys and peak pending traversal keys. None is process RSS. Opening validates full indexes linearly. Streaming audits/GC/compaction reduce packed-ID materialization but loose keys, metadata, stacks, codecs and caches still cost memory. Checkout reconstructs complete changed files, with private disk space and complete validation. Watch can target subtrees while periodically performing full audits. Synthetic benchmark reports and reproduction scripts remain developer records outside this user manual; their timings are not universal throughput promises. Incremental working-file capture # Resident native agents and C/Go workspace captures reuse manifests ingested by their session when the published parent, context and materialization match and strong local filesystem identity/change stamps are unchanged. They enumerate the complete selected tree, including renames, deletions and ignore rules. Closing and reopening the Store retains session proof; status indexes and notifications never establish it. Complete object closure verification still runs, including historical parents and reused manifests; corrupt/missing data refuses publication. Recent files (two-second guard), active Windows writers and unsupported/remote filesystems use ordinary ingestion. Stamp checks support local NTFS/ReFS, Linux ext4/XFS/Btrfs/tmpfs and macOS APFS. Restart, explicit invalidation, failed native calls or changed parent/selection discard reuse. One-shot native commit continues to ingest all sources. C capture JSON adds capture: {version, ingested_files, reused_files, source_bytes_read, source_bytes_reused} . Go exposes CaptureReportContext ; CaptureContext keeps returning its revision ID. C++ exposes WorkspaceCapture in \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> . Counters cover source reads, excluding metadata, closure verification and OS caches. Build syrnck_capture_benchmark , then run: text Copy python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe PATH_TO_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json Add --encrypted for at-rest encryption. This synthetic benchmark alternates full/session captures, includes Store opening and complete publication, records Store I/O separately, and verifies every result by a full content scan. It does not establish Unreal-project throughput. Historical verification and flat metadata remain scaling limits. Successful workspace captures seed the observer from their complete selected inventory, so the next native event tick can target its changed paths. Capture consumes only pre-capture batches; notifications arriving during or after it remain queued. A periodic audit still forces a full scan. This is an advisory observation at capture time, not an atomic snapshot of concurrent writers. python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe PATH_TO_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json","headings":[{"id":"budgets","text":"Choose budgets for the workload","level":2},{"id":"measurement","text":"Interpret counters carefully","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Incremental working-file capture","level":2}],"anchors":["L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe PATH_TO_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json","assets":[],"links":["#/en/settings","#/en/indexes","#/en/parallel-transfers","#/en/network-compression"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"protocol","title":"Formats and protocol","description":"Formats and protocol guide.","section":"Storage","order":10,"parent":"storage","revision":"12","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"formats-et-protocole-experimentaux\">Experimental formats and protocol\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/formats-et-protocole-experimentaux\" aria-label=\"Link to Experimental formats and protocol\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Formats are versioned initial contracts, not a permanent compatibility promise. Object format, product and C ABI versions are independent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identifiants-et-objets\">Identifiers and objects\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/identifiants-et-objets\" aria-label=\"Link to Identifiers and objects\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The ID is BLAKE3 over the domain-separated byte sequence below. Allowed types: \u003ccode>chunk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>; IDs have exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters. The full key is \u003ccode>{kind,id}\u003c/code>, separating identical bytes of different kinds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chunks contain 1 byte..16 MiB; an empty file has zero blocks and the ordinary BLAKE3 hash of the empty stream. All uncompressed objects, including metadata, are limited to 16 MiB.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Manifests are compact UTF-8 JSON without trailing newline, in order \u003ccode>version,size,file_hash,chunking,blocks\u003c/code>; each block has \u003ccode>id,size\u003c/code>. CDC config: \u003ccode>algorithm: fast-cdc2020,min,avg,max\u003c/code>; fixed: \u003ccode>algorithm: fixed,size\u003c/code>. The serialized spelling is authoritative (\u003ccode>inspect manifest\u003c/code>). C++ matches fastcdc 5.0.0 \u003ccode>v2020::StreamCDC::new\u003c/code>, normalization 1/seed 0. Changing boundaries requires a new algorithm/format name.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code> is ordinary BLAKE3 of complete file bytes without domain. Block sizes sum to \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>; reconstruction checks actual sizes and complete hash. Ingest workers/budget do not affect serialized boundaries/order/IDs. Flat manifest size is checked during reading before publication. Peak in-flight statistics are not object fields.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Legacy v1 revisions use compact JSON ordered \u003ccode>version,parents,message,timestamp,files\u003c/code>. UTF-8 path strings sort \u003ccode>files\u003c/code>; entries have \u003ccode>manifest,executable\u003c/code>. String escaping follows v1 canonical output from \u003ccode>nlohmann::ordered_json\u003c/code>; differing reserialization and unknown fields are refused. Independent writers need reference encoding vectors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"authored-revisions\">Authored revisions v2\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/authored-revisions\" aria-label=\"Link to Authored revisions v2\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Opt-in v2 adds \u003ccode>author,committer,signature\u003c/code> between \u003ccode>timestamp\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>files\u003c/code>.\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship/format\">The authorship format\u003c/a> defines strict person stamps,\ncanonical signature fields and the domain-separated signed message. Its reader\nrequirement is published before any v2 object. Manifests stay v1; the object-ID\ndomain, envelopes, packs, dependencies and HTTP object transport are unchanged.\nInternal working snapshots and unconfigured legacy producers remain v1.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stockage-local\">Local storage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/stockage-local\" aria-label=\"Link to Local storage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Binary envelopes begin \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code>, then a mode byte:0 for Zstd;1 for a 24-byte nonce followed by XChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypted Zstd including tag. Associated ASCII is \u003ccode>syrnck-envelope-v1:&lt;type&gt;:&lt;id&gt;\u003c/code>, so moving ciphertext to another key fails authentication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Without packs, the base config has \u003ccode>version:1,key_check\u003c/code>; encrypted key_check is keyed BLAKE3 of \u003ccode>syrnck-store-key-check-v1\u003c/code> with the Store key, otherwise null. Ref names/IDs remain visible in \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The read cache adds no files or fields. It retains nothing and cannot hide missing objects. Entries come from validation in the current handle and are reused only after cryptographic comparison of reread envelopes. Read/decode counts may change, never logical bytes or complete-file validation rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">&lt;store>/config.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">&lt;store>/refs.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">&lt;store>/store.lock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">&lt;store>/objects/&lt;type>/&lt;deux-premiers-caractères>/&lt;id>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch3 id=\"stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs\">Physical v2 packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs\" aria-label=\"Link to Physical v2 packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>An explicit pack may publish the base fields \u003ccode>version:2,key_check,pack\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> is null or a descriptor with exactly \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> (32 lowercase hex), positive \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code>, pack \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_hash\u003c/code> (ordinary BLAKE3 of the whole index). Config is the atomic publication pointer; logical objects/refs/envelopes/network remain v1. V1-only readers must refuse v2.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Files are \u003ccode>packs/pack-&lt;name&gt;.syp/.syi\u003c/code>. Pack header: eight ASCII bytes \u003ccode>SYRPACK1\u003c/code>, then unchanged concatenated \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code> envelopes with no holes/unindexed data. Binary integers are unsigned little-endian.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Index header: eight ASCII bytes \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code>, then 8-byte object count. Each 45-byte record:1-byte kind (0 chunk,1 manifest,2 revision),32-byte binary hash,8-byte absolute envelope offset,4-byte envelope length. Strict kind/ID order, no duplicates; ranges contiguous from offset 8 to \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>. Envelope 5 bytes..17 MiB; at most 1,048,576 entries and pack size \u003ccode>INT64_MAX\u003c/code>. Index length exactly \u003ccode>16+45*objects\u003c/code>, hash equal config.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Opening/audits reload and verify indexes. Read sessions validate pack identity/size/header and share a handle during reconstruction/audit, closing afterward; isolated reads use their own session. Every envelope is reread, authenticated/decompressed/ID/format checked even on cache hits. Index hash detects changes but does not sign/authenticate the catalog. Types/IDs/positions/sizes remain visible when encrypted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Writes remain loose; reads prefer loose duplicates, logical inventory counts once, fsck checks both. Inactive packs retain nothing. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs\">publication/recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs\">Physical v3 pack catalog\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs\" aria-label=\"Link to Physical v3 pack catalog\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The base v3 config has \u003ccode>version:3,key_check,packs\u003c/code>, an array of v2 descriptors. Empty or single-pack arrays stay v3; multiple packs require v3 and older readers must refuse it. Binary pack/index encodings are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Names must be distinct and no typed key may occur in two active packs even with identical bytes. Separate index validation plus merged overlapping traversals detect duplicates. Limits:1,024 packs,4,194,304 total objects,1,048,576 per index. Declared sizes are checked before reading indexes, followed by ranges/content validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ordinary maintenance appends; compact may replace all, GC only some. All files are written/verified before one atomic config replacement. Unpublished batches are inactive and contribute no inventory. Reconstruction/audit reuse at most eight LRU pack handles, closed at completion, separately from page/decoded budgets. Envelope validation is unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format\">Paged access without a format change\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format\" aria-label=\"Link to Paged access without a format change\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>The flat \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code> and complete hash remain. Opening streams 256-record pages to construct fences/digests and a negative-key filter. Those and the cache exist only in RAM; no store descriptor/header/auxiliary file changes. New indexes stream the same binary encoding.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Candidate reread pages must match their validated digest before addresses are used. Hot pages/last address may remain an opened locked catalog snapshot, like the former RAM table. Audits reload and hash complete indexes independently. Actual envelope validation remains mandatory. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">budgets and guarantees\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-head-formats\">Native workspace selection formats\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/native-head-formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Native workspace selection formats\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Named \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> descriptors use canonical v1 JSON; detached descriptors\nuse v2 with \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type: &quot;native-head&quot;\u003c/code> and the canonical \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code>.\nPrivate roots are \u003ccode>workspace/detached\u003c/code> (revision) and \u003ccode>workspace/staging\u003c/code>\n(optional prepared revision). Checkout records involving a detached target use\nv7 with \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> 1..6; all named-only layouts keep their old versions.\nDetached stash records use v2 with target \u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code>; named stash records stay v1.\nThese additions do not change immutable revision v1 or pack/ABI versions. Older\nexecutors must reject unsupported records. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-detached\">detached HEAD\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pointeur-git\">Git pointer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/pointeur-git\" aria-label=\"Link to Git pointer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>All three lines terminate with LF. Encoding is canonical and limited to 1024 bytes; size must match the manifest. A malformed document beginning with the reserved header is an error. This is distinct from Git LFS pointers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">version syrnck-pointer-v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">manifest blake3:&lt;64 caractères hexadécimaux>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">size &lt;entier décimal>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"api-http\">HTTP API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/api-http\" aria-label=\"Link to HTTP API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Every request carries \u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;\u003c/code>. One Store is served, with legacy shared authority or configured \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">users/permissions\u003c/a>. Clients require HTTPS except loopback; the server listens on loopback for deployment behind a TLS proxy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method/path\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Contract\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>POST /v1/missing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>JSON typed keys, absent subset, maximum 4096\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET /v1/objects/{kind}/{id}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logical bytes or negotiated Zstd frame, maximum 16 MiB logical\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/objects/{kind}/{id}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Verify kind/hash/format;201 created,204 present\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET /v1/refs\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Name-to-key JSON table\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/refs/{name}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{root:key,expected:key or null}\u003c/code>, verify complete dependencies\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Remote names use one URL component, local names may contain \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>. Expected null means absent, otherwise exact equality; conflict 409, missing dependency 404, invalid/corrupt object error. Request JSON field ordering is unrestricted; only immutable objects require canonical encoding. Objects may arrive before dependencies; a new version is visible only after verified ref publication. Existing-object PUT is idempotent. Redirects are disabled for authenticated requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This v1 is not BEP, Git LFS or Lore. \u003ccode>GET /v1/capabilities\u003c/code> negotiates \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">upload leases\u003c/a>, lifetime and receipts; protected publications add \u003ccode>upload:{id,fence}\u003c/code>. Legacy requests remain unless leases are required. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">Read leases\u003c/a> advertise \u003ccode>read_leases:1,read_lease_seconds\u003c/code>, acquired/renewed via \u003ccode>PUT /v1/reads/ID\u003c/code>, inspected by GET and released via \u003ccode>POST /v1/reads/ID/release\u003c/code>, retaining an exact root before downloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">Upload inventories\u003c/a> advertise \u003ccode>upload_inventories:1\u003c/code>; lease creation adds \u003ccode>inventory:true\u003c/code>, pages \u003ccode>PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX\u003c/code> declare keys/token before missing queries. Chained page count/final digest are in schema 2; schema 1 retains global protection. \u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">Compression\u003c/a> advertises \u003ccode>object_zstd:1\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding:zstd\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding:zstd\u003c/code> negotiate one frame/object, legacy stays raw. Client validates size/window/integrity/format before import; JSON/CAS representation is unchanged. User/object quotas and server replication remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interface-c\">C interface\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/interface-c\" aria-label=\"Link to C interface\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>include/syrnck.h\u003c/code> defines ABI1 (\u003ccode>syrnck_abi_version()\u003c/code>), independent of objectv1/product 0.1.0. Status 0 success,1 error,2 unknown C++ exception,3 cooperative cancellation for supported calls. No exception crosses C. Strings are NUL-terminated UTF-8; serialize handles including close. Input pointers are not retained.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>object_has\u003c/code> verifies presence and distinguishes corruption. \u003ccode>object_read\u003c/code> gives verified uncompressed bytes: null/zero-capacity query returns size, then provide enough caller buffer; insufficient capacity writes nothing. \u003ccode>closure_json\u003c/code> follows the same rule with NUL included in length. Import checks hash/format without requiring dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ref_set\u003c/code> verifies the complete closure/file hashes then replaces a local pin without CAS; HTTP is distinct CAS. Unpublished imported objects are not protected after close: keep the Store open and serialize transfer/GC, then retain a root before closing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>last_error\u003c/code> is thread-local, valid until a modifying call, bounded 4095 bytes to avoid allocating in error handling. \u003ccode>error_copy\u003c/code> copies without changing it, length includes NUL. Goroutine bindings must retrieve it on the same OS thread. Only allocated \u003ccode>manifest_json\u003c/code> strings use \u003ccode>string_free\u003c/code>; ordinary buffers belong to the caller. Additive range, comparison, file-verification and \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">workspace APIs\u003c/a> require matching rebuilt headers/library. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-c-abi\">C ownership reference\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"linked-workspace-formats\">Linked workspace records\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/linked-workspace-formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Linked workspace records\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">Linked workspaces\u003c/a> use real \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/\u003c/code> refs.\n\u003ccode>registration\u003c/code> retains a canonical v1 native-workspace chunk with its name and\ndirectory; \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> retains the named/detached descriptor. Detached roots\nare private. Named indexes use \u003ccode>staging/BLAKE3(branch-name)\u003c/code>; prepared revisions\nstill record the complete name. Receipts v8 add \u003ccode>context\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code>\n1..6; primary layouts v1..v7 remain unchanged. Selection is handle-local.\nPartial contexts retain a canonical native-materialization v1 chunk at\n\u003ccode>workspace/materialization\u003c/code>, containing sorted disjoint literal \u003ccode>prefixes\u003c/code>.\nAn absent chunk means all paths; an empty array means none. Checkout receipts\nv9 freeze source/target prefix definitions, context and layout (1..6), so recovery\ndoes not infer actions from a mutable current selection. Static v9 records\nrequire equal definitions; raw live policy-reference edits are refused.\nLayout 5 hard-reset recovery contains its full captured physical source tree,\nincluding ancestor obstructions outside the mask; only the logical target is\nprojected. Complete old head/index trees remain parents of that recovery revision.\nPartial hard-reset plan v2 binds the prefix array and its policy object key.\nLayout 6 stash apply/pop projects the logical source and restores the full\nphysical target, including saved ancestor obstructions. Its plan v2 binds the\nsame policy fields. Partial stash descriptors v3 add \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code> to the\nten original fields; legacy full named/detached descriptors remain v1/v2.\nApply requires exact saved/current policy equality; index merging stays logical\nand complete, independently of the physical working projection.\nSelection transitions use v10/layout 7, with different frozen policy definitions\nand the five original-head/index/HEAD/directory-profile fields of layout 5. Source,\ntarget and original head are the same complete logical revision; index is null\nand the directory profile empty. Both policy chunks are pinned while pending.\nOnly a matching applying/installing-to-committed journal/reference transaction\nmay publish the new live policy. Resume/abort before publication uses the source\npolicy; committed cleanup uses the target. HEAD and branch stay unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Feature-free backups with linked machine bindings use marker v2, retaining a portable\nnative-workspace-snapshot descriptor and \u003ccode>snapshot\u003c/code> revision per context instead\nof absolute registration/HEAD/receipt bindings. V1 backups remain readable.\nRestoration explicitly rebinds a portable snapshot in a new directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-archive-formats\">Workspace archive records\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/workspace-archive-formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Workspace archive records\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An unregistered linked context uses a canonical native-workspace-archive v1\nchunk with twelve fields: \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nonce\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>work\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>include_untracked\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>references\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>archive/workspaces/ID/record\u003c/code> pins that chunk; \u003ccode>/head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> pin complete\nlogical HEAD and the physical recovery revision. Each original context reference\nhas an explicit typed \u003ccode>/roots/BLAKE3(original-reference-name)\u003c/code> root. Parsing\nrequires the exact complete group and canonical descriptor/binding recipe.\nThe work revision retains HEAD and the optional index as parents, with timestamp\nzero and message \u003ccode>Syrnck workspace archive work v1: NAME\u003c/code>. Index parents remain\nunchanged on restoration, including stale baselines. A new context also pins\nthe physical work under \u003ccode>workspaces/NEW/restored/ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/code>. These are ordinary\ntyped roots; object, pack and C ABI formats do not change. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-archives\">archive operations\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-relocation-formats\">Workspace relocation records\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/workspace-relocation-formats\" aria-label=\"Link to Workspace relocation records\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation/format\">relocation journal\u003c/a> is an immutable\nnative-workspace-relocation v1 chunk at \u003ccode>workspace-relocations/active\u003c/code>. It freezes\nsource/destination/bridge paths, directory/parent identities, complete old binding\nvalues, the reference-table digest and plan. A new reader requirement precedes\npublication. The central validator accepts only exact start and final reference\ntransactions; other changes are blocked while pending. Physical phase is inferred\nfrom the one directory identity at an exact source/bridge/destination leaf name.\nFinal roots \u003ccode>archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>/aborted\u003c/code> retain\nthe record inline, without hidden object dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"required-store-features\">Required Store features\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/protocol/required-store-features\" aria-label=\"Link to Required Store features\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Owned native checkout journal v11 adds a 64-character lowercase hexadecimal\n\u003ccode>owner\u003c/code>, a context and identical source/target materialization definitions. Its\nlayout is 1 or 3, covering ordinary or namespace application, including linked,\npartial and detached workspaces. The owner and frozen definitions participate in\nthe transaction identity. Unowned layouts retain their existing encodings. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout/owned-recovery\">guarded recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Configurations v1/v2/v3 may add one \u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code> field, a nonempty sorted\narray of unique supported strings (at most 32). Supported names are\n\u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code>. Unknown, duplicate, empty or malformed requirements fail\nStore open. The extra field deliberately makes historical strict readers reject\nthe configuration before reading objects/references. Base pack/index/envelope\nencodings and C ABI 1 are unchanged; no field is added to ordinary legacy Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Enabling publishes the configuration atomically before publishing bindings that\nneed it. Interruption may leave the requirement enabled before a binding exists;\nretry is idempotent. Pack and GC retain the field. It is additive and cannot be\nremoved through the normal API. Reader requirements are distinct from negotiated\nHTTP capabilities and user-adjustable settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Backup marker v3 has the original nine fields plus nonempty \u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code>,\nwhich must equal the destination Store configuration. Its exclusions follow v2.\nMarker v1/v2 applies to feature-free Stores and remains readable. Logical backup,\nrestore and repaired-copy publication preserve source reader requirements.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Experimental formats and protocol # Formats are versioned initial contracts, not a permanent compatibility promise. Object format, product and C ABI versions are independent. Identifiers and objects # The ID is BLAKE3 over the domain-separated byte sequence below. Allowed types: chunk , manifest , revision ; IDs have exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters. The full key is {kind,id} , separating identical bytes of different kinds. Chunks contain 1 byte..16 MiB; an empty file has zero blocks and the ordinary BLAKE3 hash of the empty stream. All uncompressed objects, including metadata, are limited to 16 MiB. Manifests are compact UTF-8 JSON without trailing newline, in order version,size,file_hash,chunking,blocks ; each block has id,size . CDC config: algorithm: fast-cdc2020,min,avg,max ; fixed: algorithm: fixed,size . The serialized spelling is authoritative ( inspect manifest ). C++ matches fastcdc 5.0.0 v2020::StreamCDC::new , normalization 1/seed 0. Changing boundaries requires a new algorithm/format name. file_hash is ordinary BLAKE3 of complete file bytes without domain. Block sizes sum to size ; reconstruction checks actual sizes and complete hash. Ingest workers/budget do not affect serialized boundaries/order/IDs. Flat manifest size is checked during reading before publication. Peak in-flight statistics are not object fields. Legacy v1 revisions use compact JSON ordered version,parents,message,timestamp,files . UTF-8 path strings sort files ; entries have manifest,executable . String escaping follows v1 canonical output from nlohmann::ordered_json ; differing reserialization and unknown fields are refused. Independent writers need reference encoding vectors. text Copy ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé Authored revisions v2 # Opt-in v2 adds author,committer,signature between timestamp and files . The authorship format defines strict person stamps, canonical signature fields and the domain-separated signed message. Its reader requirement is published before any v2 object. Manifests stay v1; the object-ID domain, envelopes, packs, dependencies and HTTP object transport are unchanged. Internal working snapshots and unconfigured legacy producers remain v1. Local storage # Binary envelopes begin SYR1 , then a mode byte:0 for Zstd;1 for a 24-byte nonce followed by XChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypted Zstd including tag. Associated ASCII is syrnck-envelope-v1:\u003ctype>:\u003cid> , so moving ciphertext to another key fails authentication. Without packs, the base config has version:1,key_check ; encrypted key_check is keyed BLAKE3 of syrnck-store-key-check-v1 with the Store key, otherwise null. Ref names/IDs remain visible in refs.json . The read cache adds no files or fields. It retains nothing and cannot hide missing objects. Entries come from validation in the current handle and are reused only after cryptographic comparison of reread envelopes. Read/decode counts may change, never logical bytes or complete-file validation rules. text Copy \u003cstore>/config.json \u003cstore>/refs.json \u003cstore>/store.lock \u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid> Physical v2 packs # An explicit pack may publish the base fields version:2,key_check,pack . pack is null or a descriptor with exactly name (32 lowercase hex), positive objects , pack bytes , index_hash (ordinary BLAKE3 of the whole index). Config is the atomic publication pointer; logical objects/refs/envelopes/network remain v1. V1-only readers must refuse v2. Files are packs/pack-\u003cname>.syp/.syi . Pack header: eight ASCII bytes SYRPACK1 , then unchanged concatenated SYR1 envelopes with no holes/unindexed data. Binary integers are unsigned little-endian. Index header: eight ASCII bytes SYRIDX01 , then 8-byte object count. Each 45-byte record:1-byte kind (0 chunk,1 manifest,2 revision),32-byte binary hash,8-byte absolute envelope offset,4-byte envelope length. Strict kind/ID order, no duplicates; ranges contiguous from offset 8 to bytes . Envelope 5 bytes..17 MiB; at most 1,048,576 entries and pack size INT64_MAX . Index length exactly 16+45*objects , hash equal config. Opening/audits reload and verify indexes. Read sessions validate pack identity/size/header and share a handle during reconstruction/audit, closing afterward; isolated reads use their own session. Every envelope is reread, authenticated/decompressed/ID/format checked even on cache hits. Index hash detects changes but does not sign/authenticate the catalog. Types/IDs/positions/sizes remain visible when encrypted. Writes remain loose; reads prefer loose duplicates, logical inventory counts once, fsck checks both. Inactive packs retain nothing. See publication/recovery . Physical v3 pack catalog # The base v3 config has version:3,key_check,packs , an array of v2 descriptors. Empty or single-pack arrays stay v3; multiple packs require v3 and older readers must refuse it. Binary pack/index encodings are unchanged. Names must be distinct and no typed key may occur in two active packs even with identical bytes. Separate index validation plus merged overlapping traversals detect duplicates. Limits:1,024 packs,4,194,304 total objects,1,048,576 per index. Declared sizes are checked before reading indexes, followed by ranges/content validation. Ordinary maintenance appends; compact may replace all, GC only some. All files are written/verified before one atomic config replacement. Unpublished batches are inactive and contribute no inventory. Reconstruction/audit reuse at most eight LRU pack handles, closed at completion, separately from page/decoded budgets. Envelope validation is unchanged. Paged access without a format change # The flat SYRIDX01 and complete hash remain. Opening streams 256-record pages to construct fences/digests and a negative-key filter. Those and the cache exist only in RAM; no store descriptor/header/auxiliary file changes. New indexes stream the same binary encoding. Candidate reread pages must match their validated digest before addresses are used. Hot pages/last address may remain an opened locked catalog snapshot, like the former RAM table. Audits reload and hash complete indexes independently. Actual envelope validation remains mandatory. See budgets and guarantees . Native workspace selection formats # Named workspace/HEAD descriptors use canonical v1 JSON; detached descriptors use v2 with branch: null , type: \"native-head\" and the canonical directory . Private roots are workspace/detached (revision) and workspace/staging (optional prepared revision). Checkout records involving a detached target use v7 with layout_version 1..6; all named-only layouts keep their old versions. Detached stash records use v2 with target :HEAD ; named stash records stay v1. These additions do not change immutable revision v1 or pack/ABI versions. Older executors must reject unsupported records. See detached HEAD . Git pointer # All three lines terminate with LF. Encoding is canonical and limited to 1024 bytes; size must match the manifest. A malformed document beginning with the reserved header is an error. This is distinct from Git LFS pointers. text Copy version syrnck-pointer-v1 manifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux> size \u003centier décimal> HTTP API # Every request carries Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken> . One Store is served, with legacy shared authority or configured users/permissions . Clients require HTTPS except loopback; the server listens on loopback for deployment behind a TLS proxy. Method/path Contract POST /v1/missing JSON typed keys, absent subset, maximum 4096 GET /v1/objects/{kind}/{id} Logical bytes or negotiated Zstd frame, maximum 16 MiB logical PUT /v1/objects/{kind}/{id} Verify kind/hash/format;201 created,204 present GET /v1/refs Name-to-key JSON table PUT /v1/refs/{name} {root:key,expected:key or null} , verify complete dependencies Remote names use one URL component, local names may contain / . Expected null means absent, otherwise exact equality; conflict 409, missing dependency 404, invalid/corrupt object error. Request JSON field ordering is unrestricted; only immutable objects require canonical encoding. Objects may arrive before dependencies; a new version is visible only after verified ref publication. Existing-object PUT is idempotent. Redirects are disabled for authenticated requests. This v1 is not BEP, Git LFS or Lore. GET /v1/capabilities negotiates upload leases , lifetime and receipts; protected publications add upload:{id,fence} . Legacy requests remain unless leases are required. Read leases advertise read_leases:1,read_lease_seconds , acquired/renewed via PUT /v1/reads/ID , inspected by GET and released via POST /v1/reads/ID/release , retaining an exact root before downloads. Upload inventories advertise upload_inventories:1 ; lease creation adds inventory:true , pages PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX declare keys/token before missing queries. Chained page count/final digest are in schema 2; schema 1 retains global protection. Compression advertises object_zstd:1 ; Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding:zstd and Syrnck-Object-Encoding:zstd negotiate one frame/object, legacy stays raw. Client validates size/window/integrity/format before import; JSON/CAS representation is unchanged. User/object quotas and server replication remain future work. C interface # include/syrnck.h defines ABI1 ( syrnck_abi_version() ), independent of objectv1/product 0.1.0. Status 0 success,1 error,2 unknown C++ exception,3 cooperative cancellation for supported calls. No exception crosses C. Strings are NUL-terminated UTF-8; serialize handles including close. Input pointers are not retained. object_has verifies presence and distinguishes corruption. object_read gives verified uncompressed bytes: null/zero-capacity query returns size, then provide enough caller buffer; insufficient capacity writes nothing. closure_json follows the same rule with NUL included in length. Import checks hash/format without requiring dependencies. ref_set verifies the complete closure/file hashes then replaces a local pin without CAS; HTTP is distinct CAS. Unpublished imported objects are not protected after close: keep the Store open and serialize transfer/GC, then retain a root before closing. last_error is thread-local, valid until a modifying call, bounded 4095 bytes to avoid allocating in error handling. error_copy copies without changing it, length includes NUL. Goroutine bindings must retrieve it on the same OS thread. Only allocated manifest_json strings use string_free ; ordinary buffers belong to the caller. Additive range, comparison, file-verification and workspace APIs require matching rebuilt headers/library. See C ownership reference . Linked workspace records # Linked workspaces use real workspaces/NAME/ refs. registration retains a canonical v1 native-workspace chunk with its name and directory; workspace/HEAD retains the named/detached descriptor. Detached roots are private. Named indexes use staging/BLAKE3(branch-name) ; prepared revisions still record the complete name. Receipts v8 add context and layout_version 1..6; primary layouts v1..v7 remain unchanged. Selection is handle-local. Partial contexts retain a canonical native-materialization v1 chunk at workspace/materialization , containing sorted disjoint literal prefixes . An absent chunk means all paths; an empty array means none. Checkout receipts v9 freeze source/target prefix definitions, context and layout (1..6), so recovery does not infer actions from a mutable current selection. Static v9 records require equal definitions; raw live policy-reference edits are refused. Layout 5 hard-reset recovery contains its full captured physical source tree, including ancestor obstructions outside the mask; only the logical target is projected. Complete old head/index trees remain parents of that recovery revision. Partial hard-reset plan v2 binds the prefix array and its policy object key. Layout 6 stash apply/pop projects the logical source and restores the full physical target, including saved ancestor obstructions. Its plan v2 binds the same policy fields. Partial stash descriptors v3 add materialization to the ten original fields; legacy full named/detached descriptors remain v1/v2. Apply requires exact saved/current policy equality; index merging stays logical and complete, independently of the physical working projection. Selection transitions use v10/layout 7, with different frozen policy definitions and the five original-head/index/HEAD/directory-profile fields of layout 5. Source, target and original head are the same complete logical revision; index is null and the directory profile empty. Both policy chunks are pinned while pending. Only a matching applying/installing-to-committed journal/reference transaction may publish the new live policy. Resume/abort before publication uses the source policy; committed cleanup uses the target. HEAD and branch stay unchanged. Feature-free backups with linked machine bindings use marker v2, retaining a portable native-workspace-snapshot descriptor and snapshot revision per context instead of absolute registration/HEAD/receipt bindings. V1 backups remain readable. Restoration explicitly rebinds a portable snapshot in a new directory. Workspace archive records # An unregistered linked context uses a canonical native-workspace-archive v1 chunk with twelve fields: version , type , name , nonce , branch , revision , index , work , materialization , include_untracked , created_at , references . archive/workspaces/ID/record pins that chunk; /head and /work pin complete logical HEAD and the physical recovery revision. Each original context reference has an explicit typed /roots/BLAKE3(original-reference-name) root. Parsing requires the exact complete group and canonical descriptor/binding recipe. The work revision retains HEAD and the optional index as parents, with timestamp zero and message Syrnck workspace archive work v1: NAME . Index parents remain unchanged on restoration, including stale baselines. A new context also pins the physical work under workspaces/NEW/restored/ARCHIVE_ID . These are ordinary typed roots; object, pack and C ABI formats do not change. See archive operations . Workspace relocation records # The relocation journal is an immutable native-workspace-relocation v1 chunk at workspace-relocations/active . It freezes source/destination/bridge paths, directory/parent identities, complete old binding values, the reference-table digest and plan. A new reader requirement precedes publication. The central validator accepts only exact start and final reference transactions; other changes are blocked while pending. Physical phase is inferred from the one directory identity at an exact source/bridge/destination leaf name. Final roots archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed or /aborted retain the record inline, without hidden object dependencies. Required Store features # Owned native checkout journal v11 adds a 64-character lowercase hexadecimal owner , a context and identical source/target materialization definitions. Its layout is 1 or 3, covering ordinary or namespace application, including linked, partial and detached workspaces. The owner and frozen definitions participate in the transaction identity. Unowned layouts retain their existing encodings. See guarded recovery . Configurations v1/v2/v3 may add one required_features field, a nonempty sorted array of unique supported strings (at most 32). Supported names are authored-revisions-v1 , linked-workspaces-v1 , sparse-workspaces-v1 , materialization-transitions-v1 , resident-sync-v1 and workspace-relocation-v1 . Unknown, duplicate, empty or malformed requirements fail Store open. The extra field deliberately makes historical strict readers reject the configuration before reading objects/references. Base pack/index/envelope encodings and C ABI 1 are unchanged; no field is added to ordinary legacy Stores. Enabling publishes the configuration atomically before publishing bindings that need it. Interruption may leave the requirement enabled before a binding exists; retry is idempotent. Pack and GC retain the field. It is additive and cannot be removed through the normal API. Reader requirements are distinct from negotiated HTTP capabilities and user-adjustable settings. Backup marker v3 has the original nine fields plus nonempty required_features , which must equal the destination Store configuration. Its exclusions follow v2. Marker v1/v2 applies to feature-free Stores and remains readable. Logical backup, restore and repaired-copy publication preserve source reader requirements. ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé \u003cstore>/config.json \u003cstore>/refs.json \u003cstore>/store.lock \u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid> version syrnck-pointer-v1 manifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux> size \u003centier décimal>","headings":[{"id":"formats-et-protocole-experimentaux","text":"Experimental formats and protocol","level":1},{"id":"identifiants-et-objets","text":"Identifiers and objects","level":2},{"id":"authored-revisions","text":"Authored revisions v2","level":2},{"id":"stockage-local","text":"Local storage","level":2},{"id":"stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs","text":"Physical v2 packs","level":3},{"id":"stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs","text":"Physical v3 pack catalog","level":3},{"id":"consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format","text":"Paged access without a format change","level":3},{"id":"native-head-formats","text":"Native workspace selection formats","level":2},{"id":"pointeur-git","text":"Git pointer","level":2},{"id":"api-http","text":"HTTP API","level":2},{"id":"interface-c","text":"C interface","level":2},{"id":"linked-workspace-formats","text":"Linked workspace records","level":2},{"id":"workspace-archive-formats","text":"Workspace archive records","level":2},{"id":"workspace-relocation-formats","text":"Workspace relocation records","level":2},{"id":"required-store-features","text":"Required Store features","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé\n\u003cstore>/config.json\n\u003cstore>/refs.json\n\u003cstore>/store.lock\n\u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid>\nversion syrnck-pointer-v1\nmanifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux>\nsize \u003centier décimal>","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-authorship/format","#/en/packs","#/en/indexes","#/en/native-detached","#/en/server-access","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/network-compression","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/api-c-abi","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/native-workspace-archives","#/en/native-workspace-relocation/format","#/en/native-checkout/owned-recovery"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"reference-tables","title":"Reference table parsing and listing","description":"Reference table parsing and listing guide.","section":"Storage","order":480,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"reference-table-parsing-and-listing\">Reference table parsing and listing\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/reference-tables/reference-table-parsing-and-listing\" aria-label=\"Link to Reference table parsing and listing\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Large Git retention inventories can retain many file manifests independently.\nReference loading and listing now avoid quadratic searches over the complete\ntable without changing its persisted or HTTP representation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code> use a tree-backed JSON object before\nvalidating names and object keys into \u003ccode>Refs\u003c/code>. Serialization appends the sorted,\nunique \u003ccode>Refs\u003c/code> entries directly to the ordered JSON object. The CLI, HTTP server\nand atomic reference writer share that encoder. Output order, field order and\nbytes remain unchanged. There is no new storage format, object ID or ABI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"json-validation\">JSON validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/reference-tables/json-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to JSON validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The depth guard now runs as a SAX pass before ordinary DOM parsing. The previous\nDOM callback parser scanned each parent container for discarded values after\nevery completed child, even though Syrnck&#39;s callback never discarded anything.\nThis made wide arrays of objects quadratic as well as large reference tables.\nThe SAX guard avoids that parent scan and does not construct a DOM of its own.\nImmutable metadata still uses ordered JSON and the same canonical validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The existing 128-level depth rule is preserved, including its boundary behavior\nfor empty containers. Syntax, UTF-8, numbers, names, object kinds, hashes and key\nfields remain validated. Reference tables accept arbitrary field order and\nretain the previous last-value behavior for duplicate JSON names. A malformed\ndocument is rejected without overwriting the persisted table. The 16 MiB\nreference-document limit now applies to reads as well as writes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reference parsing performs O(N log N) table insertions; sorted table encoding\nperforms O(N) entry insertions, excluding key lengths and output bytes. This\ndoes not make all ordered JSON objects linear. Two lexical passes add work for\nsmall documents, and the reference DOM and \u003ccode>Refs\u003c/code> coexist during conversion.\nThe engine still reads the complete reference document on each access. A\npersistent index, incremental reference journal and a global memory budget\nremain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026\">Runtime costs and limits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/reference-tables/measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026\" aria-label=\"Link to Runtime costs and limits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Synthetic measurements do not establish production throughput. Account for object counts, metadata, physical layout, verification and external IO contention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"observer-changes-during-batch-publication\">Observer changes during batch publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/reference-tables/observer-changes-during-batch-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Observer changes during batch publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Batch CAS retains the original bounded reference document while validating its\ncandidate closures and reporting progress. After the last observer, it checks\nthat the persisted document still matches those original bytes. An observer&#39;s\nreference insertion, removal or replacement therefore makes the batch return\n\u003ccode>false\u003c/code> without overwriting that update, including references outside the batch.\nA fresh retry uses the resulting table. Rewriting an identical canonical table\nis a no-op and can proceed. Cancellation still precedes publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The additional check is a linear read/byte comparison of at most 16 MiB; it does\nnot parse another reference DOM or change the disk format. This fences reference\nchanges through callbacks, not arbitrary filesystem tampering or general\nconcurrent access to one Store handle. Normal exclusive Store locking remains\nrequired.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Reference table parsing and listing # Large Git retention inventories can retain many file manifests independently. Reference loading and listing now avoid quadratic searches over the complete table without changing its persisted or HTTP representation. Local refs.json and Remote::refs use a tree-backed JSON object before validating names and object keys into Refs . Serialization appends the sorted, unique Refs entries directly to the ordered JSON object. The CLI, HTTP server and atomic reference writer share that encoder. Output order, field order and bytes remain unchanged. There is no new storage format, object ID or ABI. JSON validation # The depth guard now runs as a SAX pass before ordinary DOM parsing. The previous DOM callback parser scanned each parent container for discarded values after every completed child, even though Syrnck's callback never discarded anything. This made wide arrays of objects quadratic as well as large reference tables. The SAX guard avoids that parent scan and does not construct a DOM of its own. Immutable metadata still uses ordered JSON and the same canonical validation. The existing 128-level depth rule is preserved, including its boundary behavior for empty containers. Syntax, UTF-8, numbers, names, object kinds, hashes and key fields remain validated. Reference tables accept arbitrary field order and retain the previous last-value behavior for duplicate JSON names. A malformed document is rejected without overwriting the persisted table. The 16 MiB reference-document limit now applies to reads as well as writes. Reference parsing performs O(N log N) table insertions; sorted table encoding performs O(N) entry insertions, excluding key lengths and output bytes. This does not make all ordered JSON objects linear. Two lexical passes add work for small documents, and the reference DOM and Refs coexist during conversion. The engine still reads the complete reference document on each access. A persistent index, incremental reference journal and a global memory budget remain future work. Runtime costs and limits # Synthetic measurements do not establish production throughput. Account for object counts, metadata, physical layout, verification and external IO contention. Observer changes during batch publication # Batch CAS retains the original bounded reference document while validating its candidate closures and reporting progress. After the last observer, it checks that the persisted document still matches those original bytes. An observer's reference insertion, removal or replacement therefore makes the batch return false without overwriting that update, including references outside the batch. A fresh retry uses the resulting table. Rewriting an identical canonical table is a no-op and can proceed. Cancellation still precedes publication. The additional check is a linear read/byte comparison of at most 16 MiB; it does not parse another reference DOM or change the disk format. This fences reference changes through callbacks, not arbitrary filesystem tampering or general concurrent access to one Store handle. Normal exclusive Store locking remains required.","headings":[{"id":"reference-table-parsing-and-listing","text":"Reference table parsing and listing","level":1},{"id":"json-validation","text":"JSON validation","level":2},{"id":"measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026","text":"Runtime costs and limits","level":2},{"id":"observer-changes-during-batch-publication","text":"Observer changes during batch publication","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"repair-copy","title":"Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location","description":"Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location guide.","section":"Storage","order":490,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location\">Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/repair-copy/rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location\" aria-label=\"Link to Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Repair copies combine the current Store&#39;s retained roots with a fully verified\nbackup. Intact current objects come from the current Store; missing or corrupt\nobjects come from the backup by their exact kind/ID. The output is a new,\nverified normal Store. Both inputs remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store recovered fsck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The destination must not exist, its parent must exist, and it must be outside\nboth inputs. An existing directory is refused even if empty. Select the new\nStore explicitly for inspection and use; the command does not replace or\nrename the damaged source.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes\">Preserve current history while recovering old bytes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/repair-copy/preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes\" aria-label=\"Link to Preserve current history while recovering old bytes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An older backup does not need to contain all current objects. For example,\nwhen an old retained asset block is corrupt but a newer code revision is still\nintact locally, the copy recovers that block from backup and preserves the\nnewer revision from the source. Current heads, frozen staging snapshots,\narchives, tags and other portable roots are retained together.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Preflight traverses that combined graph, validates object identities and hashes\nevery reconstructed file before creating output. A required object unavailable\nor invalid in both inputs blocks creation. The entire backup is verified,\nincluding its descriptor, settings, retained closure and inventory, even if\nonly one of its objects is needed. \u003ccode>repair-copy-plan\u003c/code> performs those checks\nwithout writing files or references and reports \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>corrupt\u003c/code>,\ncounts, the source references hash and donor snapshot ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Copying freshly imports the verified logical objects using the output key,\ncopies saved ingestion settings and publishes the selected reference table.\nA complete output fsck verifies its object inventory and reconstructed files.\nOnly then does an exclusive directory rename publish the new destination.\nThe final observer runs before source reference/settings and donor descriptor\nchecks, so a callback changing either input cannot publish a stale result.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> and completed checkout receipts are omitted as in\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">backup restore\u003c/a>, since their absolute paths bind another checkout.\nObject retention roots remain intact. The result has no immutable \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code>\ndescriptor and can accept future revisions normally. Current working files,\nuntracked/ignored data and Git repository metadata are not copied as filesystem\nfiles; any retained native versions remain exportable from their revisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes\">Corrupt loose objects and packed envelopes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/repair-copy/corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes\" aria-label=\"Link to Corrupt loose objects and packed envelopes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The read-through applies to chunks, manifests, revisions and opaque retained\nmetadata objects, from loose files or indexed pack envelopes. A corrupt\nenvelope can therefore be recovered into fresh storage without rewriting,\ndeleting or quarantining the original pack. Logical IDs remain the same;\nphysical compression, packing and encryption representations can differ.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The source must still open with its valid key, configuration, reference table,\npack catalog/index, expected pack lengths and headers. Rebuilding damaged or\nmissing pack indexes/headers, damaged config/ref tables and live in-place\nreplacement are separate recovery operations. The command copies retained\nlogical objects; it does not salvage unreachable unknown bytes or create an\nidentical physical pack layout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The original \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups/repair-missing-retained-dependencies\">missing-object repair\u003c/a>\nkeeps its stricter behavior: \u003ccode>backup repair --apply\u003c/code> imports missing objects\nin place, while any corrupt existing object blocks that operation. Use a\nrepair copy when the retained current graph can be recovered from both inputs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"encryption-and-operation-boundaries\">Encryption and operation boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/repair-copy/encryption-and-operation-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to Encryption and operation boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> opens the damaged source; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> opens the donor.\nFor this command, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_RECOVERY_KEY\u003c/code> optionally selects a third independent\noutput key. With no recovery key, the CLI keeps the primary key, or the donor\nkey when only the donor is encrypted. An encrypted input requires an output\nencryption key; the API never silently publishes plaintext from protected input.\nNo command prints key values.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Source and donor Stores are held under their normal exclusive locks and must\nbe quiescent: no active checkout, incomplete transfer or unexpired active\nread/upload lease. This is an offline operation. It is not replication,\nautomatic failover, an encrypted transport or end-to-end encryption.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation and cooperative errors before directory publication remove the\nprivate staging destination. Hard process termination can leave an unpublished\nprivate sibling, while the named destination remains absent or a complete,\nverified Store. Retry with an absent destination, or inspect an already\npublished complete result. The two inputs and their corrupt bytes remain\navailable for independent inspection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The APIs in \u003ccode>syrnck/backup.hpp\u003c/code> are available in core-only builds:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_repaired_copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> backup\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_repaired_copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> backup\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                           destination_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Reports are limited to 16 MiB. Object identity sets and traversal metadata\nremain in memory; payloads are copied and verified one bounded CAS object at\na time. Full preflight/copy/output verification deliberately reread retained\ndata. This is a verified recovery path, without a production throughput claim.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location # Repair copies combine the current Store's retained roots with a fully verified backup. Intact current objects come from the current Store; missing or corrupt objects come from the backup by their exact kind/ID. The output is a new, verified normal Store. Both inputs remain unchanged. text Copy syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered syrnck --store recovered fsck syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files The destination must not exist, its parent must exist, and it must be outside both inputs. An existing directory is refused even if empty. Select the new Store explicitly for inspection and use; the command does not replace or rename the damaged source. Preserve current history while recovering old bytes # An older backup does not need to contain all current objects. For example, when an old retained asset block is corrupt but a newer code revision is still intact locally, the copy recovers that block from backup and preserves the newer revision from the source. Current heads, frozen staging snapshots, archives, tags and other portable roots are retained together. Preflight traverses that combined graph, validates object identities and hashes every reconstructed file before creating output. A required object unavailable or invalid in both inputs blocks creation. The entire backup is verified, including its descriptor, settings, retained closure and inventory, even if only one of its objects is needed. repair-copy-plan performs those checks without writing files or references and reports ready , missing , corrupt , counts, the source references hash and donor snapshot ID. Copying freshly imports the verified logical objects using the output key, copies saved ingestion settings and publishes the selected reference table. A complete output fsck verifies its object inventory and reconstructed files. Only then does an exclusive directory rename publish the new destination. The final observer runs before source reference/settings and donor descriptor checks, so a callback changing either input cannot publish a stale result. workspace/HEAD and completed checkout receipts are omitted as in backup restore , since their absolute paths bind another checkout. Object retention roots remain intact. The result has no immutable backup.json descriptor and can accept future revisions normally. Current working files, untracked/ignored data and Git repository metadata are not copied as filesystem files; any retained native versions remain exportable from their revisions. Corrupt loose objects and packed envelopes # The read-through applies to chunks, manifests, revisions and opaque retained metadata objects, from loose files or indexed pack envelopes. A corrupt envelope can therefore be recovered into fresh storage without rewriting, deleting or quarantining the original pack. Logical IDs remain the same; physical compression, packing and encryption representations can differ. The source must still open with its valid key, configuration, reference table, pack catalog/index, expected pack lengths and headers. Rebuilding damaged or missing pack indexes/headers, damaged config/ref tables and live in-place replacement are separate recovery operations. The command copies retained logical objects; it does not salvage unreachable unknown bytes or create an identical physical pack layout. The original missing-object repair keeps its stricter behavior: backup repair --apply imports missing objects in place, while any corrupt existing object blocks that operation. Use a repair copy when the retained current graph can be recovered from both inputs. Encryption and operation boundaries # SYRNCK_KEY opens the damaged source; SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY opens the donor. For this command, SYRNCK_RECOVERY_KEY optionally selects a third independent output key. With no recovery key, the CLI keeps the primary key, or the donor key when only the donor is encrypted. An encrypted input requires an output encryption key; the API never silently publishes plaintext from protected input. No command prints key values. Source and donor Stores are held under their normal exclusive locks and must be quiescent: no active checkout, incomplete transfer or unexpired active read/upload lease. This is an offline operation. It is not replication, automatic failover, an encrypted transport or end-to-end encryption. Cancellation and cooperative errors before directory publication remove the private staging destination. Hard process termination can leave an unpublished private sibling, while the named destination remains absent or a complete, verified Store. Retry with an absent destination, or inspect an already published complete result. The two inputs and their corrupt bytes remain available for independent inspection. The APIs in syrnck/backup.hpp are available in core-only builds: cpp Copy auto plan = syrnck :: plan_repaired_copy ( source , backup , operation ) ; auto result = syrnck :: create_repaired_copy ( source , backup , new_directory , destination_key , operation ) ; Reports are limited to 16 MiB. Object identity sets and traversal metadata remain in memory; payloads are copied and verified one bounded CAS object at a time. Full preflight/copy/output verification deliberately reread retained data. This is a verified recovery path, without a production throughput claim. syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered syrnck --store recovered fsck syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files auto plan = syrnck::plan_repaired_copy(source, backup, operation); auto result = syrnck::create_repaired_copy(source, backup, new_directory, destination_key, operation);","headings":[{"id":"rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location","text":"Rebuild a damaged Store in a verified new location","level":1},{"id":"preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes","text":"Preserve current history while recovering old bytes","level":2},{"id":"corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes","text":"Corrupt loose objects and packed envelopes","level":2},{"id":"encryption-and-operation-boundaries","text":"Encryption and operation boundaries","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup\nsyrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered\nsyrnck --store recovered fsck\nsyrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_repaired_copy(source, backup, operation);\nauto result = syrnck::create_repaired_copy(source, backup, new_directory,\n                                           destination_key, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/backups","#/en/backups/repair-missing-retained-dependencies"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"resident-agent","title":"Resident native synchronization agent","description":"Watch native workspaces, exchange guarded revisions and merge compatible histories.","section":"Transfers","order":405,"parent":"transfers","revision":"6","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"run\">Run continuously\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/run\" aria-label=\"Link to Run continuously\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These examples require an initialized Store, a real server and \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>--store PATH\u003c/code> selects the Store. Without \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>, the primary workspace\nuses the current directory; a selected linked workspace uses its registered\ndirectory. The current named or detached HEAD is frozen for the session.\n\u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>, when supplied, must match it. The remote branch defaults to\n\u003ccode>main\u003c/code>; remote names are single components of at most 64 bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>By default, the agent observes selected working files and creates a native\nsnapshot when their content changes. This includes deletions and exact renames.\nPartial workspaces preserve the unmaterialized files from their complete parent\nrevision. A change to multiple files is not an atomic filesystem snapshot:\nkeep external writers stable when a consistent application snapshot is needed.\n\u003ccode>--commits-only\u003c/code> disables automatic working-file snapshots. In the default\npublication direction it only publishes existing commits and never changes\nworking files. With bidirectional mode, existing histories may still produce\na merge revision as described below.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Complete unattended folder synchronization,\nOS service installation and complete\nSyncthing daemon interoperability remain unfinished. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/limits\">current limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bidirectional\">Exchange revisions in both directions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/bidirectional\" aria-label=\"Link to Exchange revisions in both directions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> explicitly permits incoming working-file changes. A clean empty\nworkspace can receive its first remote revision. Remote descendants are applied\nthrough the existing recoverable checkout transaction, preserving the previous\nhead and honoring partial materialization and detached HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Concurrent descendants of a shared history use a three-way merge. Compatible\nfile changes and non-overlapping text edits can produce a signed two-parent\nrevision, which is published against the exact observed remote root. Text\ncomposition uses the existing bounded merge engine, including the batch limit\nof 64 attempts and 16 MiB of inputs/results. No unrelated histories or ambiguous\nmerge bases are selected automatically. A remote rewrite outside the acknowledged\nhistory is blocked even if a merge could otherwise combine it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Unresolved binary, overlapping-text and topology conflicts produce a blocked\nstatus with the merge plan. Both local and fetched histories remain retained;\nworking bytes are preserved. Resolve the conflict with the normal\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions\">native merge workflow\u003c/a>; the agent retries the resulting\nlocal history. The optional preservation policy below can retain concurrent\nfile versions automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>--commits-only --bidirectional\u003c/code>, reception and merges still require the\nnormal clean-workspace policy: dirty files are left intact and block application.\nAn edit arriving during a preflight can also block the checkout. Watcher hints\nnever authorize replacement without that fresh verification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conflict-copies\">Retain conflicting assets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/conflict-copies\" aria-label=\"Link to Retain conflicting assets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> requires \u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> and is disabled by default. After\nnormal compatible/text merging, concurrent binary or overlapping-text changes\nand add/add conflicts preserve both versions in the merge snapshot. A stable\nrevision-ID ordering selects the original path; a deterministic sibling retains\nthe other version and common asset extensions. Concurrent delete/modify keeps\nthe modified file. See the exact \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/conflict-copies\">naming and collision rules\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>merge.resolved\u003c/code> report identifies every copy or retained modification.\nCopies are tracked and transferred through the same verified manifests, guarded\npublication and signed two-parent revision. Opposite peer orientations propose\nthe same file namespace; a resolved descendant does not create further copies.\nInspect the copies and commit your eventual choice. Namespace conflicts,\noccupied generated names, untracked obstructions, unrelated/rewritten histories,\nstaging and incomplete recovery retain their blocking behavior. Prepared owned\ntargets resume unchanged even if this flag is omitted after restarting.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Publication and recovery\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/recovery\" aria-label=\"Link to Publication and recovery\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each publication uses the exact observed remote revision as an expected-value\nguard. Complete verified closures, resumable \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">transfer sessions\u003c/a>,\nserver permissions, approved signing keys and optional asset-lock claims apply\nas for ordinary transfers. Use \u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> to require negotiated server GC\nprotection; the agent does not acquire or renew asset-lock claims automatically.\nSupply current claims with \u003ccode>--lock-claims PATH\u003c/code> where branch policy requires them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A successful publication records a typed baseline root and a descriptor under\nthe selected workspace&#39;s \u003ccode>sync/push/SERVICE/\u003c/code> references. The descriptor binds\nthe logical workspace, HEAD target, remote branch and normalized endpoint\nfingerprint. It contains no token or historical filesystem destination. Replaced\nbaselines remain protected by \u003ccode>archive/agent-push/SERVICE/REVISION\u003c/code> roots. GC and\nlogical backups therefore preserve their complete reconstruction dependencies.\nQuiet polls and restarts do not rewrite an unchanged checkpoint.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Remote deletion or rewind after an acknowledged publication is blocked instead\nof being undone. A rewritten local history is also blocked. An unseen remote\nrevision is fetched into a separate \u003ccode>remotes/agent-…/BRANCH\u003c/code> tracking reference,\nwithout switching HEAD or changing working files. A remote that is an ancestor\nof the local HEAD may be advanced. The default publication direction blocks\nremote descendants and divergent histories; \u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> enables the\nreception and compatible merges described above.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If an earlier publication has an unknown outcome, its original durable session\nmust reconcile first, even when local HEAD has since advanced. A receipt proving\npast success does not authorize overwriting a later remote change. A recorded\ntransfer conflict requires explicit resolution; the agent never discards that\njournal automatically. After resolving the underlying history or policy issue,\nthe process retries. Bidirectional mode can \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout/owned-recovery\">resume its own interrupted checkout\u003c/a>\noffline, without re-signing its frozen target. Manual, other-session and\npublication-only checkouts stay blocked. An occupied staging index blocks capture\nand publication until it is cleared.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The typed \u003ccode>sync/push/SERVICE/observed\u003c/code> reference records the latest confirmed\nremote history before incoming file application and before completing a push\nsession. Earlier observations remain under \u003ccode>archive/agent-observation/\u003c/code> roots.\nDeletion or rewind of that observed history is blocked even when a crash happened\nbefore the acknowledged checkpoint. An initial service can import surviving\ncompleted native transfer receipts as historical facts. These facts never\nauthorize overwriting a later remote change. \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> gates this state\nbefore any publication; it remains enabled after cleanup or mode changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Offline local edits can still become signed local snapshots. Reconnecting sends\ntheir missing blocks. Restarting rescans the selected workspace and reuses\ndurable revisions, checkpoints and transfer sessions. The signing identity is\nloaded once at startup and retained in process memory; changing a profile or\nrotating its seed requires restarting the agent. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">identity setup\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"controls\">Scheduling and output\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/controls\" aria-label=\"Link to Scheduling and output\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Default\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Accepted values\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--interval-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20..60000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--debounce-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>75\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0..5000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--audit-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>300000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1000..3600000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--remote-poll-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>5000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>100..3600000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--duration-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 for continuous, or 100..86400000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-updates\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 for unlimited, or 1..1000000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-paths\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4096\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1..65536\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Native notifications are hints; periodic audits and restart scans recover lost\ncontinuity. \u003ccode>--polling\u003c/code> uses conservative rescans. Errors retry after one second.\nThe Store is released before waiting, debouncing or writing a status frame, so\nother CLI commands can run between cycles. Scans and network transfers still\nhold its exclusive lock; large transfers can delay other commands.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Standard output contains NDJSON frames only when semantic status changes.\n\u003ccode>state\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>empty\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pushed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pulled\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>recovered\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>synced\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>uncommitted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blocked\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>unavailable\u003c/code>.\nCanonical reason codes distinguish staging, checkout, remote/local rewrites,\ndeleted references, concurrent histories and uncertain publications. Frames\ninclude local/remote revision IDs and the acknowledged baseline when known.\n\u003ccode>last_observed_remote\u003c/code> reports retained knowledge even before that checkpoint.\n\u003ccode>merged: true\u003c/code> identifies an automatically composed local merge; checkout/merge\nreports describe incoming application or blockers when relevant.\n\u003ccode>working_clean: null\u003c/code> means a new audit is required, including immediately after\nan automatic snapshot. Human messages follow \u003ccode>--lang en|fr\u003c/code>; IDs and JSON keys\nremain unchanged. \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> writes progress to stderr.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>At a configured duration/update limit, exit status reflects the last cycle:\n0 for an ordinary state, 1 for an unavailable operation, 2 for a blocked state,\n3 for an uncertain publication. Cooperative cancellation returns 130. A duration\nis a scheduling limit, not an immediate deadline for in-flight IO.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cpp\">Embed the publication direction\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/cpp\" aria-label=\"Link to Embed the publication direction\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/native_sync.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>net\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeSync \u003cspan class=\"token function\">sync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> sync\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">tick\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> watcher_changes\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Keep this move-only session and its observation state between ticks. Every tick\nuses a currently locked Store handle with the same physical Store, logical\nworkspace, HEAD target and endpoint. Call \u003ccode>invalidate()\u003c/code> when watcher continuity\nis lost; a new session starts with a full audit. The caller owns scheduling and\ncancellation. There is no background thread or implicit CLI profile loading in\nthis C++ API. The options are remote branch, local capture, reception and\ncompatible merges and conflict copies; the last three default to false.\nMerge mode requires reception, and conflict copies require both. For example,\n\u003ccode>NativeSyncOptions{&quot;main&quot;, true, true, true, true}\u003c/code> enables the preservation policy.\nThe earlier \u003ccode>NativePushSync\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>NativePushSyncOptions\u003c/code> names remain\nvalid. Link \u003ccode>syrnck_net\u003c/code> and provide any signing identity explicitly.\nThe CLI uses \u003ccode>Store::take_native_identity()\u003c/code> to move exclusive key ownership\nbetween short-lived handles without exporting seed bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Edits during an owned application\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Link to Edits during an owned application\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>With the existing explicit \u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> policy, owned checkout recovery\nalso preserves regular-file edits made during application. The agent retains\nnative archives before exclusive conflict-copy moves, restores the expected\nintermediate bytes and finishes the unchanged incoming/merged candidate. This\nrecovery works offline and requires no signing private key for a prepared signed\ntarget. Normal later capture records the additional working files. Without that\npolicy, recovery remains strict. Missing files, directory/symlink obstructions\nand ambiguous namespace changes still require resolution. See the shared\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">archive and replay contract\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Capture source I/O\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/resident-agent/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Link to Capture source I/O\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Repeated captures reuse unchanged session-ingested manifests using strong local\nfile stamps. Selected paths are still enumerated and the complete immutable\nclosure verified. Tick reports include \u003ccode>capture\u003c/code> source I/O counters. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/performance/incremental-capture\">performance and conservative fallback\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Run continuously # text Copy syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease These examples require an initialized Store, a real server and SYRNCK_TOKEN . --store PATH selects the Store. Without --directory , the primary workspace uses the current directory; a selected linked workspace uses its registered directory. The current named or detached HEAD is frozen for the session. --branch NAME , when supplied, must match it. The remote branch defaults to main ; remote names are single components of at most 64 bytes. By default, the agent observes selected working files and creates a native snapshot when their content changes. This includes deletions and exact renames. Partial workspaces preserve the unmaterialized files from their complete parent revision. A change to multiple files is not an atomic filesystem snapshot: keep external writers stable when a consistent application snapshot is needed. --commits-only disables automatic working-file snapshots. In the default publication direction it only publishes existing commits and never changes working files. With bidirectional mode, existing histories may still produce a merge revision as described below. Complete unattended folder synchronization, OS service installation and complete Syncthing daemon interoperability remain unfinished. See current limits . Exchange revisions in both directions # --bidirectional explicitly permits incoming working-file changes. A clean empty workspace can receive its first remote revision. Remote descendants are applied through the existing recoverable checkout transaction, preserving the previous head and honoring partial materialization and detached HEAD. Concurrent descendants of a shared history use a three-way merge. Compatible file changes and non-overlapping text edits can produce a signed two-parent revision, which is published against the exact observed remote root. Text composition uses the existing bounded merge engine, including the batch limit of 64 attempts and 16 MiB of inputs/results. No unrelated histories or ambiguous merge bases are selected automatically. A remote rewrite outside the acknowledged history is blocked even if a merge could otherwise combine it. Unresolved binary, overlapping-text and topology conflicts produce a blocked status with the merge plan. Both local and fetched histories remain retained; working bytes are preserved. Resolve the conflict with the normal native merge workflow ; the agent retries the resulting local history. The optional preservation policy below can retain concurrent file versions automatically. With --commits-only --bidirectional , reception and merges still require the normal clean-workspace policy: dirty files are left intact and block application. An edit arriving during a preflight can also block the checkout. Watcher hints never authorize replacement without that fresh verification. Retain conflicting assets # text Copy syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease --conflict-copies requires --bidirectional and is disabled by default. After normal compatible/text merging, concurrent binary or overlapping-text changes and add/add conflicts preserve both versions in the merge snapshot. A stable revision-ID ordering selects the original path; a deterministic sibling retains the other version and common asset extensions. Concurrent delete/modify keeps the modified file. See the exact naming and collision rules . The merge.resolved report identifies every copy or retained modification. Copies are tracked and transferred through the same verified manifests, guarded publication and signed two-parent revision. Opposite peer orientations propose the same file namespace; a resolved descendant does not create further copies. Inspect the copies and commit your eventual choice. Namespace conflicts, occupied generated names, untracked obstructions, unrelated/rewritten histories, staging and incomplete recovery retain their blocking behavior. Prepared owned targets resume unchanged even if this flag is omitted after restarting. Publication and recovery # Each publication uses the exact observed remote revision as an expected-value guard. Complete verified closures, resumable transfer sessions , server permissions, approved signing keys and optional asset-lock claims apply as for ordinary transfers. Use --require-lease to require negotiated server GC protection; the agent does not acquire or renew asset-lock claims automatically. Supply current claims with --lock-claims PATH where branch policy requires them. A successful publication records a typed baseline root and a descriptor under the selected workspace's sync/push/SERVICE/ references. The descriptor binds the logical workspace, HEAD target, remote branch and normalized endpoint fingerprint. It contains no token or historical filesystem destination. Replaced baselines remain protected by archive/agent-push/SERVICE/REVISION roots. GC and logical backups therefore preserve their complete reconstruction dependencies. Quiet polls and restarts do not rewrite an unchanged checkpoint. Remote deletion or rewind after an acknowledged publication is blocked instead of being undone. A rewritten local history is also blocked. An unseen remote revision is fetched into a separate remotes/agent-…/BRANCH tracking reference, without switching HEAD or changing working files. A remote that is an ancestor of the local HEAD may be advanced. The default publication direction blocks remote descendants and divergent histories; --bidirectional enables the reception and compatible merges described above. If an earlier publication has an unknown outcome, its original durable session must reconcile first, even when local HEAD has since advanced. A receipt proving past success does not authorize overwriting a later remote change. A recorded transfer conflict requires explicit resolution; the agent never discards that journal automatically. After resolving the underlying history or policy issue, the process retries. Bidirectional mode can resume its own interrupted checkout offline, without re-signing its frozen target. Manual, other-session and publication-only checkouts stay blocked. An occupied staging index blocks capture and publication until it is cleared. The typed sync/push/SERVICE/observed reference records the latest confirmed remote history before incoming file application and before completing a push session. Earlier observations remain under archive/agent-observation/ roots. Deletion or rewind of that observed history is blocked even when a crash happened before the acknowledged checkpoint. An initial service can import surviving completed native transfer receipts as historical facts. These facts never authorize overwriting a later remote change. resident-sync-v1 gates this state before any publication; it remains enabled after cleanup or mode changes. Offline local edits can still become signed local snapshots. Reconnecting sends their missing blocks. Restarting rescans the selected workspace and reuses durable revisions, checkpoints and transfer sessions. The signing identity is loaded once at startup and retained in process memory; changing a profile or rotating its seed requires restarting the agent. See identity setup . Scheduling and output # Option Default Accepted values --interval-ms 500 20..60000 --debounce-ms 75 0..5000 --audit-ms 300000 1000..3600000 --remote-poll-ms 5000 100..3600000 --duration-ms 0 0 for continuous, or 100..86400000 --max-updates 0 0 for unlimited, or 1..1000000 --max-paths 4096 1..65536 Native notifications are hints; periodic audits and restart scans recover lost continuity. --polling uses conservative rescans. Errors retry after one second. The Store is released before waiting, debouncing or writing a status frame, so other CLI commands can run between cycles. Scans and network transfers still hold its exclusive lock; large transfers can delay other commands. Standard output contains NDJSON frames only when semantic status changes. state is empty , pushed , pulled , recovered , synced , uncommitted , blocked or unavailable . Canonical reason codes distinguish staging, checkout, remote/local rewrites, deleted references, concurrent histories and uncertain publications. Frames include local/remote revision IDs and the acknowledged baseline when known. last_observed_remote reports retained knowledge even before that checkpoint. merged: true identifies an automatically composed local merge; checkout/merge reports describe incoming application or blockers when relevant. working_clean: null means a new audit is required, including immediately after an automatic snapshot. Human messages follow --lang en|fr ; IDs and JSON keys remain unchanged. --progress or --progress-json writes progress to stderr. At a configured duration/update limit, exit status reflects the last cycle: 0 for an ordinary state, 1 for an unavailable operation, 2 for a blocked state, 3 for an uncertain publication. Cooperative cancellation returns 130. A duration is a scheduling limit, not an immediate deadline for in-flight IO. Embed the publication direction # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp> syrnck :: net :: NativeSync sync ( store , project , { \"main\" , true , true , true } ) ; auto status = sync . tick ( store , remote , watcher_changes , operation , transfer ) ; Keep this move-only session and its observation state between ticks. Every tick uses a currently locked Store handle with the same physical Store, logical workspace, HEAD target and endpoint. Call invalidate() when watcher continuity is lost; a new session starts with a full audit. The caller owns scheduling and cancellation. There is no background thread or implicit CLI profile loading in this C++ API. The options are remote branch, local capture, reception and compatible merges and conflict copies; the last three default to false. Merge mode requires reception, and conflict copies require both. For example, NativeSyncOptions{\"main\", true, true, true, true} enables the preservation policy. The earlier NativePushSync / NativePushSyncOptions names remain valid. Link syrnck_net and provide any signing identity explicitly. The CLI uses Store::take_native_identity() to move exclusive key ownership between short-lived handles without exporting seed bytes. Edits during an owned application # With the existing explicit --conflict-copies policy, owned checkout recovery also preserves regular-file edits made during application. The agent retains native archives before exclusive conflict-copy moves, restores the expected intermediate bytes and finishes the unchanged incoming/merged candidate. This recovery works offline and requires no signing private key for a prepared signed target. Normal later capture records the additional working files. Without that policy, recovery remains strict. Missing files, directory/symlink obstructions and ambiguous namespace changes still require resolution. See the shared archive and replay contract . Capture source I/O # Repeated captures reuse unchanged session-ingested manifests using strong local file stamps. Selected paths are still enumerated and the complete immutable closure verified. Tick reports include capture source I/O counters. See performance and conservative fallback . syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease #include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp> syrnck::net::NativeSync sync(store, project, {\"main\", true, true, true}); auto status = sync.tick(store, remote, watcher_changes, operation, transfer);","headings":[{"id":"run","text":"Run continuously","level":2},{"id":"bidirectional","text":"Exchange revisions in both directions","level":2},{"id":"conflict-copies","text":"Retain conflicting assets","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Publication and recovery","level":2},{"id":"controls","text":"Scheduling and output","level":2},{"id":"cpp","text":"Embed the publication direction","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Edits during an owned application","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Capture source I/O","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease\nsyrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease\n#include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp>\n\nsyrnck::net::NativeSync sync(store, project, {\"main\", true, true, true});\nauto status = sync.tick(store, remote, watcher_changes, operation, transfer);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/limits","#/en/native-resolutions","#/en/native-merge/conflict-copies","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/native-checkout/owned-recovery","#/en/native-authorship","#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/en/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"selective-retention","title":"Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning","description":"Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning guide.","section":"Transfers","order":500,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning\">Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/selective-retention/selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning\" aria-label=\"Link to Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Offline GC now treats unfinished local transfers and active server read leases\nas retention roots. Objects unrelated to those roots can be reclaimed while a\ntransfer is interrupted. The server must still be stopped before another process\nopens its Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Protection source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>GC behavior\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Ordinary references and pins\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Verify and retain the complete closure and file hashes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unfinished local push\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Verify and retain its complete source closure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unfinished local pull\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retain every available object reachable through its saved root\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active server read lease\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Verify and retain the complete leased closure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active inventory-enabled server upload lease\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retain existing objects in committed inventory pages\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Active legacy server upload lease\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Block all GC until expiry, publication or deliberate release\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Completed local receipt or inactive server lease\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Does not retain payloads\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Invalid journal or lease metadata\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fail before payload deletion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incomplete-pulls\">Incomplete pulls\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/selective-retention/incomplete-pulls\" aria-label=\"Link to Incomplete pulls\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A pull imports verified metadata before scheduling its dependencies. GC follows\nthat metadata and retains every dependency already present, including blocks\nafter gaps in a manifest. Missing objects are expected for an interrupted pull;\nthey do not enter the live-object marks. A missing root therefore retains no\npayload by itself. Objects whose association cannot be discovered through the\navailable metadata can be collected and will need downloading again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Existing corrupt envelopes, invalid metadata or a wrong length for an available\nfile block stop collection. A complete manifest also has its whole-file hash\nverified. An incomplete manifest cannot yet have its whole-file hash checked;\nordinary final publication still requires full verification. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code>\nremain strict and can reject incomplete orphan metadata while a pull is pending.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>GC first verifies complete roots, then adds available partial closures to the\nsame packed-object bitmap and loose-object marks. It does not materialize a\nsecond complete object inventory. Transfer roots, pending traversal and parsed\nmetadata still consume memory; these allocations are not a fixed RAM cap.\nCancellation and the existing catalog/deletion commit boundary remain in force.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A read lease contributes its exact root at the start of the collection. If it\nexpires while GC is running, this invocation conservatively keeps its objects.\nPreviously observed expired generations are still durably fenced before deleting\npayloads, preventing clock rollback from reviving a collected generation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>New \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">server upload inventories\u003c/a> register batches before\nremote presence queries, retaining declared objects even before manifest arrival.\nLegacy upload leases keep their global hold. Multi-server/distributed retention\nis still future work; local process locks and leases do not implement distributed consensus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pruning-completed-local-receipts\">Pruning completed local receipts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/selective-retention/pruning-completed-local-receipts\" aria-label=\"Link to Pruning completed local receipts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent prune --delete --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default is an inspection only. JSON reports \u003ccode>completed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unfinished\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>removed\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>deleted\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--delete\u003c/code> removes \u003cstrong>all complete receipts\u003c/strong>, regardless\nof age. There is no timestamp or age policy in the current journal schema.\nProgress reports metadata bytes scanned and removed; JSON result counts are records.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both passes stream the journal directory without keeping every ID in memory.\nThe first pass validates the entire inventory before any removal. The second\npass removes only records whose state is \u003ccode>complete\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>transferring\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>uncertain\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>conflict\u003c/code> records remain intact, along with their retention roots.\nMalformed records stop pruning; use explicit \u003ccode>agent forget ID\u003c/code> if deliberately\ndiscarding a damaged or unfinished journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pruning is local, requires no server token and never contacts a server. Opening\nan encrypted Store still requires its key. References, objects and remote leases\nare untouched. A removed receipt can no longer be resumed by its session ID;\na new push/pull creates ordinary transfer state instead of returning that receipt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation or an IO/progress error during deletion can leave a prefix of\ncompleted receipts removed. Retrying is safe. Successful removal flushes the\njournal directory; an interrupted pass also attempts that flush. This is not an\natomic multi-record transaction. Neither \u003ccode>agent prune\u003c/code> nor \u003ccode>agent forget\u003c/code> releases\na remote lease; abandoned leases expire or require explicit administration.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning # Offline GC now treats unfinished local transfers and active server read leases as retention roots. Objects unrelated to those roots can be reclaimed while a transfer is interrupted. The server must still be stopped before another process opens its Store. Protection source GC behavior Ordinary references and pins Verify and retain the complete closure and file hashes Unfinished local push Verify and retain its complete source closure Unfinished local pull Retain every available object reachable through its saved root Active server read lease Verify and retain the complete leased closure Active inventory-enabled server upload lease Retain existing objects in committed inventory pages Active legacy server upload lease Block all GC until expiry, publication or deliberate release Completed local receipt or inactive server lease Does not retain payloads Invalid journal or lease metadata Fail before payload deletion Incomplete pulls # A pull imports verified metadata before scheduling its dependencies. GC follows that metadata and retains every dependency already present, including blocks after gaps in a manifest. Missing objects are expected for an interrupted pull; they do not enter the live-object marks. A missing root therefore retains no payload by itself. Objects whose association cannot be discovered through the available metadata can be collected and will need downloading again. Existing corrupt envelopes, invalid metadata or a wrong length for an available file block stop collection. A complete manifest also has its whole-file hash verified. An incomplete manifest cannot yet have its whole-file hash checked; ordinary final publication still requires full verification. fsck and pack remain strict and can reject incomplete orphan metadata while a pull is pending. GC first verifies complete roots, then adds available partial closures to the same packed-object bitmap and loose-object marks. It does not materialize a second complete object inventory. Transfer roots, pending traversal and parsed metadata still consume memory; these allocations are not a fixed RAM cap. Cancellation and the existing catalog/deletion commit boundary remain in force. A read lease contributes its exact root at the start of the collection. If it expires while GC is running, this invocation conservatively keeps its objects. Previously observed expired generations are still durably fenced before deleting payloads, preventing clock rollback from reviving a collected generation. New server upload inventories register batches before remote presence queries, retaining declared objects even before manifest arrival. Legacy upload leases keep their global hold. Multi-server/distributed retention is still future work; local process locks and leases do not implement distributed consensus. Pruning completed local receipts # text Copy syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete --progress The default is an inspection only. JSON reports completed , unfinished , removed and deleted . --delete removes all complete receipts , regardless of age. There is no timestamp or age policy in the current journal schema. Progress reports metadata bytes scanned and removed; JSON result counts are records. Both passes stream the journal directory without keeping every ID in memory. The first pass validates the entire inventory before any removal. The second pass removes only records whose state is complete . transferring , publishing , uncertain and conflict records remain intact, along with their retention roots. Malformed records stop pruning; use explicit agent forget ID if deliberately discarding a damaged or unfinished journal. Pruning is local, requires no server token and never contacts a server. Opening an encrypted Store still requires its key. References, objects and remote leases are untouched. A removed receipt can no longer be resumed by its session ID; a new push/pull creates ordinary transfer state instead of returning that receipt. Cancellation or an IO/progress error during deletion can leave a prefix of completed receipts removed. Retrying is safe. Successful removal flushes the journal directory; an interrupted pass also attempts that flush. This is not an atomic multi-record transaction. Neither agent prune nor agent forget releases a remote lease; abandoned leases expire or require explicit administration. syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete --progress","headings":[{"id":"selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning","text":"Selective transfer retention and receipt pruning","level":1},{"id":"incomplete-pulls","text":"Incomplete pulls","level":2},{"id":"pruning-completed-local-receipts","text":"Pruning completed local receipts","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent prune\nsyrnck agent prune --delete --progress","assets":[],"links":["#/en/upload-inventories"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"server-access","title":"Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer","description":"Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer guide.","section":"Transfers","order":510,"parent":"transfers","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer\">Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-access/server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer\" aria-label=\"Link to Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Start one server per repository. The configured access mode authenticates each\nrequest as a principal, checks write permissions for repository paths and branch\nnames, and enforces asset leases when publishing a revision. Raw object reads\nare repository-wide: these path rules restrict writes, not confidentiality.\nUse separate repositories when users must not read each other&#39;s objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configure-identities\">Configure identities\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-access/configure-identities\" aria-label=\"Link to Configure identities\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Generate a separate random bearer token for each person or device with\n\u003ccode>syrnck keygen\u003c/code>. Keep tokens in a secret manager or protected environment.\nSet \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code> locally and run \u003ccode>syrnck token-hash\u003c/code> to obtain its BLAKE3\ndigest without opening a Store. Do not place raw tokens in the configuration,\ncommand history, logs or Git. Tokens should have at least 32 random characters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An access file has this exact version-1 schema. Replace each placeholder with\nthe 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters returned by \u003ccode>token-hash\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"principals\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"writer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reviewer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reader\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fast_forward\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"lock_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L22\" data-line=\"22\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The existing HTTPS proxy requirement remains. \u003ccode>--access\u003c/code> replaces the shared\ntoken authentication; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code> on the server is not a fallback. The file\nis validated at startup, and changes require a restart. Removing a principal\nrevokes future requests; replacing its digest rotates its token while keeping\nits identity and lock ownership. Ongoing requests complete under the startup\nconfiguration. This is bearer-token authentication, not end-to-end encryption.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Roles are \u003ccode>reader\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>writer\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>admin\u003c/code>. All authenticated principals may read\nobjects, inspect configured branch references and acquire read-transfer leases.\nWriters/admins may upload objects, but may publish only the explicitly listed\nbranches and allowed paths. An admin has the additional right to prune expired\nasset leases; it does not bypass branch or path rules. Readers must have empty\nwrite-path and branch lists. An empty string in \u003ccode>write_paths\u003c/code> means the whole\nrepository; an empty list grants no writes. A path prefix matches itself and\ndescendants at \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> boundaries, using portable Unicode normalization/case folding.\n\u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> does not grant access to \u003ccode>Contentious\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>IDs and branch names are single reference components, at most 128 bytes.\nIDs are unique after portable case folding; token hashes are unique. Access\nconfiguration is capped at 1 MiB, 256 principals and 1024 branch rules. Lists\nare capped at 1024 entries; individual path prefixes at 4096 UTF-8 bytes.\nTreat the configuration as an administrator-controlled file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"use-remote-asset-leases\">Use remote asset leases\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-access/use-remote-asset-leases\" aria-label=\"Link to Use remote asset leases\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/server-signatures\">Access schema v2\u003c/a> adds registered committer public keys\nand an optional signed-history requirement per branch. V1 above retains its\noriginal behavior. Both modes keep the same independent publisher permissions\nand asset leases below.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Omit \u003ccode>--owner\u003c/code>: the server supplies the authenticated ID. Owner fields in HTTP\nrequests are rejected. Every requested path must be writable by that identity.\nGrouped operations use the same atomic expiry/fencing semantics as the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/asset-locks\">local lease table\u003c/a>. Plans are read-only and do not reserve\npaths. A reader can list leases; only an admin can preview/remove expired\nrecords remotely. Local administrative commands remain available while the\nserver is stopped and its Store lock is released.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Store the \u003ccode>path\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code> fields from acquisition in a JSON array for push:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The server validates the authenticated owner, exact current generation and\nexpiration of every claim. It requires a claim for every changed path under a\nbranch&#39;s \u003ccode>lock_paths\u003c/code>, including deletion and both sides of a case change or\nrename. An active exact lease is honored even outside mandatory prefixes.\nAnother writer cannot use a copied fence to publish an asset. The earliest\nexpiry is checked again after full dependency/file verification, immediately\nbefore the reference transaction. Expiry or an observed backward wall-clock\nchange rejects publication. Callers must keep long-running locks renewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The server mutex and exclusive Store lock serialize operations. Upload/read\ntransfer lease IDs are internally scoped to the authenticated principal; knowing\nanother principal&#39;s external lease ID does not expose its receipt or fencing\ntoken. Stable principal IDs preserve transfer resumption across token rotation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history\">Publish and fetch autonomous history\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-access/publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history\" aria-label=\"Link to Publish and fetch autonomous history\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Push transfers the complete dependency closure of the selected local branch,\nincluding parent history and all asset blocks. It requires the server identity\nextension. A configured server accepts only revision roots on configured branch\nnames. Expected-reference compare-and-swap protects concurrent publication;\n\u003ccode>fast_forward: true\u003c/code> additionally refuses a replacement that does not descend\nfrom the existing head. Uploaded objects may remain unreferenced on rejection,\nbut the branch does not move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before replacing an existing head, configured access mode retains it under\n\u003ccode>archive/publish/REVISION\u003c/code>, including for branches that permit a forced history\nreplacement. The archive is durable before branch publication. A crash or a\nlater rejected publication can leave an extra safety root; it cannot move the\nbranch first and lose its previous history. These roots are hidden from remote\nbranch listings and survive server GC. \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">Explicit archive retirement\u003c/a>\nrequires a verified separate backup before removing selected protection roots.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Write checks cover every newly reachable commit compared with every parent,\nplus changes between the published and proposed heads. Adding a forbidden path\nin an intermediate commit and removing it before push still fails. Merge\npublication is deliberately conservative: differences against either parent\nneed write permission and applicable leases. Publication currently admits at\nmost 100000 new/previous revisions per traversal, 1000000 parent links and\n16384 changed portable paths within a 16 MiB path budget. Lock claims are capped\nat 1024; large protected changes must be divided into smaller publications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fetch downloads and verifies the selected remote revision into an immutable\n\u003ccode>native-fetch/REVISION\u003c/code> root, then atomically updates\n\u003ccode>remotes/REMOTE_NAME/BRANCH\u003c/code>. It archives the previous tracking head under\n\u003ccode>archive/fetch/REVISION\u003c/code>. The selected branch, staging index and working files\nare untouched. Merge/rebase remain explicit, recoverable local operations.\nTracking references are valid explicit revision selectors. Native transfer CLI\nnames are capped at 64 bytes to fit local reference limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Transfers reuse existing resumable sessions, read/upload leases, bounded object\nparallelism, compression and cancellation. \u003ccode>agent resume\u003c/code> can complete an\ninterrupted fetch&#39;s object transfer; rerun \u003ccode>native fetch\u003c/code> to finish tracking\npublication. Retained fetch roots and archives currently require explicit\nadministrative retirement; GC alone does not remove them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"http-extension\">HTTP extension\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-access/http-extension\" aria-label=\"Link to HTTP extension\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>GET /v1/identity\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>id\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>role\u003c/code>. Capabilities advertise\n\u003ccode>asset_locks: 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>branch_policies: 1\u003c/code> in configured access mode. Legacy\nshared-token mode reports zero for both, with a null identity, and refuses\nremote asset-lock operations. Existing shared-token transfer clients remain\nsupported in legacy mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All lock routes use \u003ccode>POST /v1/locks/ACTION\u003c/code>, with JSON bodies capped at 1 MiB:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Exact request fields\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>after\u003c/code> (path or null), \u003ccode>limit\u003c/code> (1..1000)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>acquire-plan\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>acquire\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>paths\u003c/code> (1..1024 strings), \u003ccode>seconds\u003c/code> (1..86400)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>renew\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>claims\u003c/code> (path/fence array), \u003ccode>seconds\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>release\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>claims\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>prune\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>delete\u003c/code> (boolean; false previews)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/refs/NAME\u003c/code> accepts \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>, optional \u003ccode>upload\u003c/code> and optional\n\u003ccode>locks\u003c/code> (the claim array). It enforces the same rules for direct requests and\nCLI requests. Configured reference listing exposes only configured branch\nnames, not local indexes, archives, lock metadata roots or workspace paths.\n401 denotes invalid authentication, 403 denied permissions/policies/claims,\n409 a competing reference update, 412 an invalid upload lease and 428 a missing\nrequired upload lease. Malformed requests and failed lock operations return\n400; missing objects/receipts return 404. Successful plans can contain conflicts\nwithout changing the Store. Mutating requests are not automatically retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>No complete Syncthing interoperability, service-resident watcher,\ndistributed GC, per-path read isolation or remote audit log is implied by this\nextension. Those remain separate development work. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">Signed revision objects\u003c/a>\nare verified during transfer; \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-signatures\">access v2\u003c/a> can additionally\nauthorize their keys and require signed newly admitted history.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer # Start one server per repository. The configured access mode authenticates each request as a principal, checks write permissions for repository paths and branch names, and enforces asset leases when publishing a revision. Raw object reads are repository-wide: these path rules restrict writes, not confidentiality. Use separate repositories when users must not read each other's objects. Configure identities # Generate a separate random bearer token for each person or device with syrnck keygen . Keep tokens in a secret manager or protected environment. Set SYRNCK_TOKEN locally and run syrnck token-hash to obtain its BLAKE3 digest without opening a Store. Do not place raw tokens in the configuration, command history, logs or Git. Tokens should have at least 32 random characters. An access file has this exact version-1 schema. Replace each placeholder with the 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters returned by token-hash : json Copy { \"version\" : 1 , \"principals\" : [ { \"id\" : \"alice\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"writer\" , \"write_paths\" : [ \"Content\" , \"Source\" ] , \"branches\" : [ \"main\" ] } , { \"id\" : \"reviewer\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"reader\" , \"write_paths\" : [ ] , \"branches\" : [ ] } ] , \"branches\" : [ { \"name\" : \"main\" , \"fast_forward\" : true , \"lock_paths\" : [ \"Content\" ] } ] } text Copy syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/ The existing HTTPS proxy requirement remains. --access replaces the shared token authentication; SYRNCK_TOKEN on the server is not a fallback. The file is validated at startup, and changes require a restart. Removing a principal revokes future requests; replacing its digest rotates its token while keeping its identity and lock ownership. Ongoing requests complete under the startup configuration. This is bearer-token authentication, not end-to-end encryption. Roles are reader , writer and admin . All authenticated principals may read objects, inspect configured branch references and acquire read-transfer leases. Writers/admins may upload objects, but may publish only the explicitly listed branches and allowed paths. An admin has the additional right to prune expired asset leases; it does not bypass branch or path rules. Readers must have empty write-path and branch lists. An empty string in write_paths means the whole repository; an empty list grants no writes. A path prefix matches itself and descendants at / boundaries, using portable Unicode normalization/case folding. Content does not grant access to Contentious . IDs and branch names are single reference components, at most 128 bytes. IDs are unique after portable case folding; token hashes are unique. Access configuration is capped at 1 MiB, 256 principals and 1024 branch rules. Lists are capped at 1024 entries; individual path prefixes at 4096 UTF-8 bytes. Treat the configuration as an administrator-controlled file. Use remote asset leases # Access schema v2 adds registered committer public keys and an optional signed-history requirement per branch. V1 above retains its original behavior. Both modes keep the same independent publisher permissions and asset leases below. text Copy syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100 syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Omit --owner : the server supplies the authenticated ID. Owner fields in HTTP requests are rejected. Every requested path must be writable by that identity. Grouped operations use the same atomic expiry/fencing semantics as the local lease table . Plans are read-only and do not reserve paths. A reader can list leases; only an admin can preview/remove expired records remotely. Local administrative commands remain available while the server is stopped and its Store lock is released. Store the path and fence fields from acquisition in a JSON array for push: json Copy [ { \"path\" : \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"fence\" : 1 } , { \"path\" : \"Content/Map.ubulk\" , \"fence\" : 1 } ] text Copy syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease The server validates the authenticated owner, exact current generation and expiration of every claim. It requires a claim for every changed path under a branch's lock_paths , including deletion and both sides of a case change or rename. An active exact lease is honored even outside mandatory prefixes. Another writer cannot use a copied fence to publish an asset. The earliest expiry is checked again after full dependency/file verification, immediately before the reference transaction. Expiry or an observed backward wall-clock change rejects publication. Callers must keep long-running locks renewed. The server mutex and exclusive Store lock serialize operations. Upload/read transfer lease IDs are internally scoped to the authenticated principal; knowing another principal's external lease ID does not expose its receipt or fencing token. Stable principal IDs preserve transfer resumption across token rotation. Publish and fetch autonomous history # text Copy syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main Push transfers the complete dependency closure of the selected local branch, including parent history and all asset blocks. It requires the server identity extension. A configured server accepts only revision roots on configured branch names. Expected-reference compare-and-swap protects concurrent publication; fast_forward: true additionally refuses a replacement that does not descend from the existing head. Uploaded objects may remain unreferenced on rejection, but the branch does not move. Before replacing an existing head, configured access mode retains it under archive/publish/REVISION , including for branches that permit a forced history replacement. The archive is durable before branch publication. A crash or a later rejected publication can leave an extra safety root; it cannot move the branch first and lose its previous history. These roots are hidden from remote branch listings and survive server GC. Explicit archive retirement requires a verified separate backup before removing selected protection roots. Write checks cover every newly reachable commit compared with every parent, plus changes between the published and proposed heads. Adding a forbidden path in an intermediate commit and removing it before push still fails. Merge publication is deliberately conservative: differences against either parent need write permission and applicable leases. Publication currently admits at most 100000 new/previous revisions per traversal, 1000000 parent links and 16384 changed portable paths within a 16 MiB path budget. Lock claims are capped at 1024; large protected changes must be divided into smaller publications. Fetch downloads and verifies the selected remote revision into an immutable native-fetch/REVISION root, then atomically updates remotes/REMOTE_NAME/BRANCH . It archives the previous tracking head under archive/fetch/REVISION . The selected branch, staging index and working files are untouched. Merge/rebase remain explicit, recoverable local operations. Tracking references are valid explicit revision selectors. Native transfer CLI names are capped at 64 bytes to fit local reference limits. Transfers reuse existing resumable sessions, read/upload leases, bounded object parallelism, compression and cancellation. agent resume can complete an interrupted fetch's object transfer; rerun native fetch to finish tracking publication. Retained fetch roots and archives currently require explicit administrative retirement; GC alone does not remove them. HTTP extension # GET /v1/identity returns version , id and role . Capabilities advertise asset_locks: 1 and branch_policies: 1 in configured access mode. Legacy shared-token mode reports zero for both, with a null identity, and refuses remote asset-lock operations. Existing shared-token transfer clients remain supported in legacy mode. All lock routes use POST /v1/locks/ACTION , with JSON bodies capped at 1 MiB: Action Exact request fields list after (path or null), limit (1..1000) acquire-plan , acquire paths (1..1024 strings), seconds (1..86400) renew claims (path/fence array), seconds release claims prune delete (boolean; false previews) PUT /v1/refs/NAME accepts root , expected , optional upload and optional locks (the claim array). It enforces the same rules for direct requests and CLI requests. Configured reference listing exposes only configured branch names, not local indexes, archives, lock metadata roots or workspace paths. 401 denotes invalid authentication, 403 denied permissions/policies/claims, 409 a competing reference update, 412 an invalid upload lease and 428 a missing required upload lease. Malformed requests and failed lock operations return 400; missing objects/receipts return 404. Successful plans can contain conflicts without changing the Store. Mutating requests are not automatically retried. No complete Syncthing interoperability, service-resident watcher, distributed GC, per-path read isolation or remote audit log is implied by this extension. Those remain separate development work. Signed revision objects are verified during transfer; access v2 can additionally authorize their keys and require signed newly admitted history. { \"version\": 1, \"principals\": [ { \"id\": \"alice\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"writer\", \"write_paths\": [\"Content\", \"Source\"], \"branches\": [\"main\"] }, { \"id\": \"reviewer\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"reader\", \"write_paths\": [], \"branches\": [] } ], \"branches\": [ {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"]} ] } syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/ syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100 syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/ [{\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"fence\":1},{\"path\":\"Content/Map.ubulk\",\"fence\":1}] syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main","headings":[{"id":"server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer","text":"Server identities, asset locks and native history transfer","level":1},{"id":"configure-identities","text":"Configure identities","level":2},{"id":"use-remote-asset-leases","text":"Use remote asset leases","level":2},{"id":"publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history","text":"Publish and fetch autonomous history","level":2},{"id":"http-extension","text":"HTTP extension","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21","L22","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4"],"languages":["json","text"],"code":"{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"principals\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"alice\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"writer\",\n      \"write_paths\": [\"Content\", \"Source\"],\n      \"branches\": [\"main\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reviewer\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"reader\",\n      \"write_paths\": [],\n      \"branches\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"branches\": [\n    {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"]}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease\nsyrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/\nsyrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100\nsyrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/\n[{\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"fence\":1},{\"path\":\"Content/Map.ubulk\",\"fence\":1}]\nsyrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease\nsyrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main\nsyrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease\nsyrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main\nsyrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main","assets":[],"links":["#/en/server-signatures","#/en/asset-locks","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/native-authorship","#/en/server-signatures"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"server-read-leases","title":"Server read leases","description":"Server read leases guide.","section":"Transfers","order":520,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"server-read-leases\">Server read leases\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-read-leases/server-read-leases\" aria-label=\"Link to Server read leases\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Pulls automatically negotiate a read lease before their first missing object is\nrequested from a compatible server. It binds the exact root, independently of\nits current reference name. An interrupted download can therefore resume after\nthat reference changes or is removed, provided its lease remains active.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--require-read-lease\u003c/code> rejects a server without this extension before downloading\nany missing object. An entirely local closure needs no remote lease, even with\nthis option. The existing \u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> option remains specific to uploads.\nObject GETs remain available to legacy clients; this is client-negotiated\nretention, not a server access-control policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifecycle-and-retention\">Lifecycle and retention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-read-leases/lifecycle-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Lifecycle and retention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Before acknowledging a new or expired lease, the server verifies the complete\nroot closure and full file hashes. Missing or corrupt sources fail acquisition.\nLive renewal avoids repeating this full scan. All Store operations must be\nserialized by the embedding application; the supplied server uses its Store\nmutex and exclusive process lock.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The TTL accepts \u003cstrong>1..86400 seconds\u003c/strong>, default \u003cstrong>3600\u003c/strong>. Expiry uses server UTC;\nwhole-second timestamps round up by at most one second. The client schedules\nrenewal using a monotonic clock at one third of the TTL. An independent cancellable\nrequest renews without blocking the object scheduler. It never accesses the local\nStore or invokes user progress observers. Control responses are capped at 16 KiB\nand sit outside object-buffer and payload-throughput statistics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Short TTLs, long local validation, user callbacks, server contention or an\nunreachable server can still cause expiry. Acquisition or renewal after expiry\ngets a new fence; a client observing a changed renewal fence aborts and requires\nresume. Protection is not guaranteed across an expiry gap.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Active read leases retain their \u003cstrong>complete verified root closures\u003c/strong> during offline\nGC. Unrelated objects remain collectable. The server can be killed and its\nreference removed offline without releasing this hold. Before deleting objects,\nGC durably marks observed expired leases \u003ccode>expired\u003c/code> and rotates their fence, so a\nlater wall-clock rollback cannot reactivate a generation whose objects were\ncollected. Dry runs do not write expiry transitions. Active upload leases can\nindependently block collection. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">selective retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After all object requests drain, the client cancels and joins any renewal and\nreleases the read lease \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> local reference publication. Source retention\nis no longer needed: verified objects are local and the unfinished local session\nretains those objects during local GC. Local compare-and-set then verifies the closure and file\nhashes before publishing. Cancellation, corruption or a changed local destination\ncannot silently publish an incomplete version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Release is fenced and idempotent. If its reply is lost, the ordinary bounded\nretry policy applies. A release error leaves the local session unfinished;\nresume retries cleanup using the saved root and lease. A missing/pruned lease\ncounts as released once no remote data is needed. A completed local receipt\nresumes without contacting the server or recreating historical references.\nAn abandoned transfer otherwise retains its source until expiry or an explicit\nadministrative release. \u003ccode>agent forget\u003c/code> only removes the local journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol\">Protocol\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-read-leases/protocol\" aria-label=\"Link to Protocol\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>All routes use the existing authenticated HTTP/HTTPS transport and server token.\n\u003ccode>GET /v1/capabilities\u003c/code> advertises \u003ccode>read_leases: 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>read_lease_seconds\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Request\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET /v1/reads/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lease and TTL; 404 if absent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/reads/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Acquire/renew with exactly \u003ccode>{root, fence}\u003c/code>; null fence for creation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>POST /v1/reads/ID/release\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Release with exactly \u003ccode>{fence}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Successful responses contain exactly \u003ccode>{lease, lease_seconds}\u003c/code>. IDs and fences\nare independent random 64-character hexadecimal strings. The strict lease schema\nhas \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expires_at\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>state\u003c/code>. State is \u003ccode>active\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>expired\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>released\u003c/code>. Root identity cannot change. Wrong fences and attempts\nto reactivate a released record return 412. Recreating a pruned ID generates a\nfresh fence. This fences renewal/release, not immutable hash-addressed GETs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Records live in \u003ccode>STORE/reads/ID.json\u003c/code>; at most \u003cstrong>4096\u003c/strong> records, including inactive\nones, are permitted. Symlinks, malformed metadata and unknown schemas fail\nclosed. Files use the existing flushed atomic write machinery.\nTransient foreign \u003ccode>*.tmp\u003c/code> entries may disappear within a bounded 50 ms wait;\npersistent unknown files still fail closed, and canonical lease names are never skipped.\nMetadata contains no tokens, URLs or encryption keys; it has the same filesystem trust boundary\nas references. Stores encrypted at rest still have ordinary lease metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local transfer journals now write schema \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong>, adding nullable \u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code> for\npulls; schemas 1 and 2 remain readable. Transfer statistics add \u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code>\nalongside \u003ccode>upload_lease\u003c/code>. An identifier is a historical association, not evidence\nthat its hold remains active.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A capability 404, explicit \u003ccode>read_leases: 0\u003c/code>, or an older valid upload-only\ncapability response permits legacy fallback unless protection is required.\nMalformed responses and other failures propagate. A saved read lease cannot\nsilently downgrade to an unprotected download.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"offline-administration\">Offline administration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-read-leases/offline-administration\" aria-label=\"Link to Offline administration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Stop the server before opening its Store with the CLI. Encrypted stores require\ntheir key; these local commands do not require the server token.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck leases list --read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck leases release ID --read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck leases prune --read\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Prune validates the entire bounded inventory before removing inactive/expired\nrecords. Deliberate release can interrupt an active reader&#39;s retention guarantee.\nOlder binaries do not understand these holds and must not run GC on this Store.\nLeases are process-local server retention, not replication or\ndistributed consensus. Startup/renewal checks do not provide backup guarantees\nagainst disk corruption, external file deletion or an administrator removing\nthe lease directory.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Server read leases # Pulls automatically negotiate a read lease before their first missing object is requested from a compatible server. It binds the exact root, independently of its current reference name. An interrupted download can therefore resume after that reference changes or is removed, provided its lease remains active. text Copy syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease --require-read-lease rejects a server without this extension before downloading any missing object. An entirely local closure needs no remote lease, even with this option. The existing --require-lease option remains specific to uploads. Object GETs remain available to legacy clients; this is client-negotiated retention, not a server access-control policy. Lifecycle and retention # Before acknowledging a new or expired lease, the server verifies the complete root closure and full file hashes. Missing or corrupt sources fail acquisition. Live renewal avoids repeating this full scan. All Store operations must be serialized by the embedding application; the supplied server uses its Store mutex and exclusive process lock. The TTL accepts 1..86400 seconds , default 3600 . Expiry uses server UTC; whole-second timestamps round up by at most one second. The client schedules renewal using a monotonic clock at one third of the TTL. An independent cancellable request renews without blocking the object scheduler. It never accesses the local Store or invokes user progress observers. Control responses are capped at 16 KiB and sit outside object-buffer and payload-throughput statistics. Short TTLs, long local validation, user callbacks, server contention or an unreachable server can still cause expiry. Acquisition or renewal after expiry gets a new fence; a client observing a changed renewal fence aborts and requires resume. Protection is not guaranteed across an expiry gap. Active read leases retain their complete verified root closures during offline GC. Unrelated objects remain collectable. The server can be killed and its reference removed offline without releasing this hold. Before deleting objects, GC durably marks observed expired leases expired and rotates their fence, so a later wall-clock rollback cannot reactivate a generation whose objects were collected. Dry runs do not write expiry transitions. Active upload leases can independently block collection. See selective retention . After all object requests drain, the client cancels and joins any renewal and releases the read lease before local reference publication. Source retention is no longer needed: verified objects are local and the unfinished local session retains those objects during local GC. Local compare-and-set then verifies the closure and file hashes before publishing. Cancellation, corruption or a changed local destination cannot silently publish an incomplete version. Release is fenced and idempotent. If its reply is lost, the ordinary bounded retry policy applies. A release error leaves the local session unfinished; resume retries cleanup using the saved root and lease. A missing/pruned lease counts as released once no remote data is needed. A completed local receipt resumes without contacting the server or recreating historical references. An abandoned transfer otherwise retains its source until expiry or an explicit administrative release. agent forget only removes the local journal. Protocol # All routes use the existing authenticated HTTP/HTTPS transport and server token. GET /v1/capabilities advertises read_leases: 1 and read_lease_seconds . Request Result GET /v1/reads/ID Lease and TTL; 404 if absent PUT /v1/reads/ID Acquire/renew with exactly {root, fence} ; null fence for creation POST /v1/reads/ID/release Release with exactly {fence} Successful responses contain exactly {lease, lease_seconds} . IDs and fences are independent random 64-character hexadecimal strings. The strict lease schema has version: 1 , id , root , fence , expires_at and state . State is active , expired or released . Root identity cannot change. Wrong fences and attempts to reactivate a released record return 412. Recreating a pruned ID generates a fresh fence. This fences renewal/release, not immutable hash-addressed GETs. Records live in STORE/reads/ID.json ; at most 4096 records, including inactive ones, are permitted. Symlinks, malformed metadata and unknown schemas fail closed. Files use the existing flushed atomic write machinery. Transient foreign *.tmp entries may disappear within a bounded 50 ms wait; persistent unknown files still fail closed, and canonical lease names are never skipped. Metadata contains no tokens, URLs or encryption keys; it has the same filesystem trust boundary as references. Stores encrypted at rest still have ordinary lease metadata. Local transfer journals now write schema 3 , adding nullable read_lease for pulls; schemas 1 and 2 remain readable. Transfer statistics add read_lease alongside upload_lease . An identifier is a historical association, not evidence that its hold remains active. A capability 404, explicit read_leases: 0 , or an older valid upload-only capability response permits legacy fallback unless protection is required. Malformed responses and other failures propagate. A saved read lease cannot silently downgrade to an unprotected download. Offline administration # Stop the server before opening its Store with the CLI. Encrypted stores require their key; these local commands do not require the server token. text Copy syrnck leases list --read syrnck leases release ID --read syrnck leases prune --read Prune validates the entire bounded inventory before removing inactive/expired records. Deliberate release can interrupt an active reader's retention guarantee. Older binaries do not understand these holds and must not run GC on this Store. Leases are process-local server retention, not replication or distributed consensus. Startup/renewal checks do not provide backup guarantees against disk corruption, external file deletion or an administrator removing the lease directory. syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease syrnck leases list --read syrnck leases release ID --read syrnck leases prune --read","headings":[{"id":"server-read-leases","text":"Server read leases","level":1},{"id":"lifecycle-and-retention","text":"Lifecycle and retention","level":2},{"id":"protocol","text":"Protocol","level":2},{"id":"offline-administration","text":"Offline administration","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease\nsyrnck leases list --read\nsyrnck leases release ID --read\nsyrnck leases prune --read","assets":[],"links":["#/en/selective-retention"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"server-signatures","title":"Authorized revision signatures","description":"Bind native committer keys to server accounts and require signed branch history.","section":"Transfers","order":515,"parent":"server-access","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"configuration\">Configure approved keys\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-signatures/configuration\" aria-label=\"Link to Configure approved keys\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The optional access-file schema v2 adds signing authorization to the existing\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">accounts, permissions and asset locks\u003c/a>. V1 remains supported\nwith its exact original fields and behavior. V2 has exactly the same three root\nfields, but every principal must add \u003ccode>signing_keys\u003c/code> and every branch must add\n\u003ccode>require_signatures\u003c/code>. An older server rejects this configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"principals\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"writer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"signing_keys\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">        \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"public_key\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"email\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice@example.invalid\"\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">        \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">      \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fast_forward\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"lock_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"require_signatures\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L22\" data-line=\"22\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Replace the token placeholder using \u003ccode>token-hash\u003c/code> and obtain the public key from\n\u003ccode>native identity\u003c/code> with the signing key loaded, or a verified revision&#39;s signature.\nThe file contains public keys, never signing seeds. Each binding has exactly\n\u003ccode>public_key,name,email\u003c/code>: a canonical Ed25519 public key and the exact UTF-8\ncommitter identity it authorizes. Name/email limits match\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship/identity\">native identities\u003c/a>. Timezone and timestamp remain\nclaims, not proof of when a person signed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each principal accepts at most 32 keys. A key can appear only once across the\nwhole configuration, including other accounts. Malformed, noncanonical,\nnon-curve or small-order public keys are rejected at startup. Readers must have\nan empty key list; writers/admins may have none when they only transport another\napproved committer&#39;s work. Existing 1 MiB and account/branch bounds still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck help server-signatures\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"validation\">Validate every newly admitted commit\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-signatures/validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Validate every newly admitted commit\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>On a branch with \u003ccode>require_signatures: true\u003c/code>, every newly reachable revision must\nbe v2 with a valid Ed25519 signature. Its public key must be registered, and the\ncommitter name/email must exactly match its binding. The signer&#39;s account must\ncurrently have permission to write the target branch and every changed path in\nthat commit, including each merge-parent comparison. A correct tip cannot hide\nan unsigned, unknown-key or mismatched intermediate commit or second merge parent.\nContent that is added and removed before the tip is still checked.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The authenticated publisher independently needs branch/path permissions and\nvalid asset-lock claims. Publisher and signer may be different: a teammate can\ntransport approved history. Admin role provides no signing-policy bypass.\nExisting fast-forward rules, complete-closure verification, final asset-lock\nexpiration check and expected-value reference publication all still apply.\nRejection leaves the published reference unchanged; uploaded objects may remain\nunder the normal transfer/GC rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only ancestors of this branch&#39;s current published head are an admitted baseline.\nEnabling the policy on an existing branch does not require rewriting its legacy\nhistory, but newly introduced commits must satisfy it. An unrelated branch&#39;s\nhead does not admit that history here. Initial publication into an empty branch\ntherefore requires the complete introduced history to satisfy the policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A real forced rewind must also target a currently approved signed tip, even when\nthe tip is already in the baseline; it cannot downgrade to legacy, unsigned or\nrevoked-key metadata. An exact no-op retry of the already published head may\nfinish a transfer after key revocation. It introduces no revision. With\n\u003ccode>require_signatures: false\u003c/code>, ordinary token/path/branch rules apply, while object\nvalidation still checks any signature that is present.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation\">Rotate and revoke\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-signatures/rotation\" aria-label=\"Link to Rotate and revoke\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Treat the access file as administrator-controlled. Changes take effect after a\nserver restart, just like account/token changes. To rotate, register the new\npublic key alongside the old binding, distribute the new private key through\nyour own protected mechanism, then remove the old public key when appropriate.\nRemoving a binding revokes future admission under that key. Removing an account\nor its branch/path permissions also affects future signed publications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Already admitted history remains readable and retains its original signature\nbytes. A new approved child may extend that baseline after an old key is revoked.\nReintroducing old-key commits through newly admitted history is refused. Token\nrotation and signing-key rotation are independent. The policy uses the current\nstartup configuration, not a revision&#39;s self-declared date. Requests already in\nprogress complete under that immutable configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Trust boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-signatures/limits\" aria-label=\"Link to Trust boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Authorization concerns the current committer and the signed complete revision.\nAn original author can differ or be null after a rewrite; its name remains a\nhistorical claim. There is no trusted timestamp, historical certificate validity,\ndistributed revocation feed, live policy reload or OS signing-key vault here.\nThis does not add per-path read confidentiality or end-to-end encryption. The\nserver still receives decoded objects and needs the normal HTTPS deployment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification\">Verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-signatures/verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tests cover strict configuration, key/profile mismatches, every newly admitted\nparent, delegated transport, independent signer/publisher ACLs, admitted legacy\nbaselines, revocation, rotation and forbidden rewinds. Authenticated HTTP tests\nalso transfer signed history through an encrypted Store to a reader without the\nsigning private key, restart with changed policies and audit retained objects.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Configure approved keys # The optional access-file schema v2 adds signing authorization to the existing accounts, permissions and asset locks . V1 remains supported with its exact original fields and behavior. V2 has exactly the same three root fields, but every principal must add signing_keys and every branch must add require_signatures . An older server rejects this configuration. json Copy { \"version\" : 2 , \"principals\" : [ { \"id\" : \"alice\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"writer\" , \"write_paths\" : [ \"Source\" , \"Content\" ] , \"branches\" : [ \"main\" ] , \"signing_keys\" : [ { \"public_key\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\" , \"name\" : \"Alice\" , \"email\" : \"alice@example.invalid\" } ] } ] , \"branches\" : [ { \"name\" : \"main\" , \"fast_forward\" : true , \"lock_paths\" : [ \"Content\" ] , \"require_signatures\" : true } ] } Replace the token placeholder using token-hash and obtain the public key from native identity with the signing key loaded, or a verified revision's signature. The file contains public keys, never signing seeds. Each binding has exactly public_key,name,email : a canonical Ed25519 public key and the exact UTF-8 committer identity it authorizes. Name/email limits match native identities . Timezone and timestamp remain claims, not proof of when a person signed. Each principal accepts at most 32 keys. A key can appear only once across the whole configuration, including other accounts. Malformed, noncanonical, non-curve or small-order public keys are rejected at startup. Readers must have an empty key list; writers/admins may have none when they only transport another approved committer's work. Existing 1 MiB and account/branch bounds still apply. text Copy syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease syrnck help server-signatures Validate every newly admitted commit # On a branch with require_signatures: true , every newly reachable revision must be v2 with a valid Ed25519 signature. Its public key must be registered, and the committer name/email must exactly match its binding. The signer's account must currently have permission to write the target branch and every changed path in that commit, including each merge-parent comparison. A correct tip cannot hide an unsigned, unknown-key or mismatched intermediate commit or second merge parent. Content that is added and removed before the tip is still checked. The authenticated publisher independently needs branch/path permissions and valid asset-lock claims. Publisher and signer may be different: a teammate can transport approved history. Admin role provides no signing-policy bypass. Existing fast-forward rules, complete-closure verification, final asset-lock expiration check and expected-value reference publication all still apply. Rejection leaves the published reference unchanged; uploaded objects may remain under the normal transfer/GC rules. Only ancestors of this branch's current published head are an admitted baseline. Enabling the policy on an existing branch does not require rewriting its legacy history, but newly introduced commits must satisfy it. An unrelated branch's head does not admit that history here. Initial publication into an empty branch therefore requires the complete introduced history to satisfy the policy. A real forced rewind must also target a currently approved signed tip, even when the tip is already in the baseline; it cannot downgrade to legacy, unsigned or revoked-key metadata. An exact no-op retry of the already published head may finish a transfer after key revocation. It introduces no revision. With require_signatures: false , ordinary token/path/branch rules apply, while object validation still checks any signature that is present. Rotate and revoke # Treat the access file as administrator-controlled. Changes take effect after a server restart, just like account/token changes. To rotate, register the new public key alongside the old binding, distribute the new private key through your own protected mechanism, then remove the old public key when appropriate. Removing a binding revokes future admission under that key. Removing an account or its branch/path permissions also affects future signed publications. Already admitted history remains readable and retains its original signature bytes. A new approved child may extend that baseline after an old key is revoked. Reintroducing old-key commits through newly admitted history is refused. Token rotation and signing-key rotation are independent. The policy uses the current startup configuration, not a revision's self-declared date. Requests already in progress complete under that immutable configuration. Trust boundaries # Authorization concerns the current committer and the signed complete revision. An original author can differ or be null after a rewrite; its name remains a historical claim. There is no trusted timestamp, historical certificate validity, distributed revocation feed, live policy reload or OS signing-key vault here. This does not add per-path read confidentiality or end-to-end encryption. The server still receives decoded objects and needs the normal HTTPS deployment. Verification # text Copy python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\" Tests cover strict configuration, key/profile mismatches, every newly admitted parent, delegated transport, independent signer/publisher ACLs, admitted legacy baselines, revocation, rotation and forbidden rewinds. Authenticated HTTP tests also transfer signed history through an encrypted Store to a reader without the signing private key, restart with changed policies and audit retained objects. { \"version\": 2, \"principals\": [ { \"id\": \"alice\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"writer\", \"write_paths\": [\"Source\", \"Content\"], \"branches\": [\"main\"], \"signing_keys\": [ { \"public_key\": \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\", \"name\": \"Alice\", \"email\": \"alice@example.invalid\" } ] } ], \"branches\": [ {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"], \"require_signatures\": true} ] } syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease syrnck help server-signatures python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"","headings":[{"id":"configuration","text":"Configure approved keys","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Validate every newly admitted commit","level":2},{"id":"rotation","text":"Rotate and revoke","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Trust boundaries","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21","L22","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["json","text"],"code":"{\n  \"version\": 2,\n  \"principals\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"alice\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"writer\",\n      \"write_paths\": [\"Source\", \"Content\"],\n      \"branches\": [\"main\"],\n      \"signing_keys\": [\n        {\n          \"public_key\": \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\",\n          \"name\": \"Alice\",\n          \"email\": \"alice@example.invalid\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"branches\": [\n    {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"], \"require_signatures\": true}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease\nsyrnck help server-signatures\npython scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck\npython scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/server-access","#/en/native-authorship/identity"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"server-upload-leases","title":"Persistent server upload leases","description":"Persistent server upload leases guide.","section":"Transfers","order":530,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-server-upload-leases\">Persistent server upload leases\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases/persistent-server-upload-leases\" aria-label=\"Link to Persistent server upload leases\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>New clients automatically protect uploads when the server advertises this\nextension. Active leases survive server restarts and protect uploads during offline GC. A lease\nexpires when its client stops renewing it; published references then provide\nordinary retention after a successful transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Legacy shared-token mode requires \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>; configured identities use \u003ccode>--access\u003c/code>. Both retain loopback binding and an HTTPS proxy for remote access. Encrypted stores use their own \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. English/French CLI\ntext is supported; protocol fields remain English. This extension currently\ncovers \u003cstrong>uploads\u003c/strong>; \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">read leases for pulls\u003c/a> use a separate extension.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifetime-gc-and-administration\">Lifetime, GC and administration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases/lifetime-gc-and-administration\" aria-label=\"Link to Lifetime, GC and administration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The default duration is \u003cstrong>3,600 seconds\u003c/strong>, configurable with \u003ccode>--lease-seconds\u003c/code>\nin \u003cstrong>1..86,400 seconds\u003c/strong>. Whole-second expiry is rounded up by at most one second.\nThe client schedules renewal using its monotonic clock at one third of the\nadvertised duration, without requiring synchronized client/server wall clocks.\nThe server uses its wall clock for persisted expiry, so operators must maintain\nthat clock. A backward jump can extend retention; a forward jump can expire it.\nBefore GC deletes payloads, it durably records observed expirations and replaces\ntheir fences. A later clock rollback cannot revive a fence whose negotiated\nobjects might have been collected. Expired publication rejection also records\nthat observation. GC preview does not write these transitions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The coordinator schedules renewals during object retry and traffic-pacing waits.\nAt most one renewal worker uses a separate HTTP handle, so a delayed control\nreply does not stop object IO. It never accesses the Store or invokes user\nprogress callbacks; the coordinator consumes its result and validates the fence.\nThe worker is cancelled/joined on exit and drained before publication. Its small\ncontrol buffers/connection are outside the object request/buffer counters.\nHandles are independent, following libcurl&#39;s \u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/threadsafe.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">thread-safety requirements\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local IO, user callbacks and synchronous control requests already running can\ndelay scheduling. A short TTL cannot guarantee continuous protection through an\narbitrarily long stall. Keep the normal TTL large enough for those operations;\none-second leases are useful for controlled tests rather than deployment guidance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An active, unexpired legacy lease blocks \u003cstrong>GC preview and deletion for the whole\nserver Store\u003c/strong>. New clients negotiate \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">paged upload inventories\u003c/a>,\nallowing unrelated objects to be collected while declared objects remain retained.\nGC streams and validates the records before planning. An expired,\nreleased or published lease does not retain objects. Expiry never deletes\npayloads itself; an ordinary GC is still required. The server holds the Store&#39;s\nexclusive process lock while running, so current maintenance is offline.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stop the server before local administration:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-data leases list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store server-data leases prune\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>release\u003c/code> deliberately revokes an active lease. It does not modify references\nor payloads and leaves published receipts intact. \u003ccode>prune\u003c/code> removes expired,\nreleased and published records and obsolete inventory generations after validation.\nLive leases and their committed inventories are preserved. Removing a published receipt can reduce what a disconnected\nclient can prove, but never authorizes replaying its old publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The store permits at most \u003cstrong>4,096 lease records\u003c/strong>, including receipts. New lease\ncreation fails at capacity until inactive records are pruned. Records are at\nmost 16 KiB; separately stored inventory pages are capped at 1 MiB each.\nListing materializes at most that bounded lease inventory. Malformed\nrecords and symlinked paths fail closed. Every GC binary must understand this\nextension; older binaries ignore server upload holds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fencing-and-resume\">Fencing and resume\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases/fencing-and-resume\" aria-label=\"Link to Fencing and resume\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The client persists a random lease ID in its local journal before asking the\nserver to create it. The server binds it to the exact root, destination reference\nand expected previous reference. Renewing a live lease keeps its fence. Expiry\nreactivation or recreation after pruning produces a fresh random fence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Every leased reference PUT carries that fence. Expired, released and stale\nfences fail before reference publication. Thus a delayed request cannot publish\nunder a lease reactivated by a later attempt, including when an ID was pruned\nand recreated. This is coordination within one exclusively locked Store, not a\ndistributed consensus or multi-server fencing guarantee.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If renewal changes the fence during an upload, the client stops and requires\nresume to renegotiate missing objects. It cannot rely on a presence query made\nbefore a retention gap. Resume may reupload objects reclaimed after expiry;\nthe unfinished \u003cstrong>local\u003c/strong> journal still protects the source against local GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before publication the server validates the full closure and performs the saved\nreference CAS. It then records a durable \u003ccode>published\u003c/code> receipt. These are two\natomic writes: a crash between them leaves an active lease and a possibly\npublished reference. Resume can confirm a matching root and write the receipt\nwithout another reference PUT.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A published receipt is bound to the original descriptor. Repeating its leased\npublication acknowledges that receipt and never rewrites a reference changed\nlater. A client recovering a lost acknowledgement can therefore finish from the\nreceipt even after another writer changed the reference. If no receipt exists\nand the reference no longer matches, the existing uncertain-publication policy\napplies. Receipts do not pin old versions after their references are removed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation retains the upload lease until expiry or deliberate release. Local\n\u003ccode>agent forget\u003c/code> remains a local command and does not contact the server. Lease\nrecords contain operational IDs/fences and object keys, not bearer tokens or\nencryption keys. Legacy shared-token mode has one authority. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">Configured server identities\u003c/a> add per-user ownership and permissions; use that guide for its authentication policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-and-compatibility\">Protocol and compatibility\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases/protocol-and-compatibility\" aria-label=\"Link to Protocol and compatibility\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>All endpoints use the existing bearer authentication:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Request\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Behavior\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET v1/capabilities\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Advertises \u003ccode>upload_leases: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lease_seconds\u003c/code> and optional \u003ccode>upload_inventories: 1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET v1/uploads/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Returns \u003ccode>lease\u003c/code> metadata and configured \u003ccode>lease_seconds\u003c/code>, or 404\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT v1/uploads/ID\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Creates/renews an exact descriptor; body has \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>, optionally \u003ccode>inventory: true\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Registers a fenced page before querying presence; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">upload inventories\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>POST v1/uploads/ID/confirm\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Confirms a matching reference without publishing it; body has \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT v1/refs/NAME\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>upload: {id, fence}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Validates the lease and performs/reconciles its bound CAS\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Creation sends a null fence. A retried creation is idempotent for the same\ndescriptor; renewals send the last observed fence. Lease request and response\nbodies are capped at 16 KiB in the client; the server rejects oversized control\nrequests before parsing. Server records in \u003ccode>STORE/uploads/ID.json\u003c/code> use a strict\nversion-1 schema for legacy leases: \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>expires_at\u003c/code> (Unix seconds), and \u003ccode>state\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>active\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expired\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>published\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>released\u003c/code>).\nWrites use the same flushed-file and atomic-publication mechanisms as journals.\nInventory-enabled schema-2 records also commit \u003ccode>inventory_pages\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>inventory_hash\u003c/code>;\ntheir separate registration requests permit at most 1 MiB.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HTTP 412 rejects an expired/released/fenced leased publication; 428 rejects an\nunleased publication when the server requires leases. The client treats these\nas known rejection only on its leased publication path. Ambiguous errors still\nexit 3, and final reference PUTs are never automatically retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A 404 capability endpoint or explicit \u003ccode>upload_leases: 0\u003c/code> allows legacy upload\nwhen protection was not required. Network errors, malformed capabilities and\nloss of support for a saved lease fail rather than silently downgrade. Client\n\u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> rejects unsupported servers before object upload. Server\n\u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> rejects unleased final reference PUTs; legacy object PUTs remain\navailable. The flag is for push and resumed push, not pull.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local transfer journals write schema \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong>, retaining nullable \u003ccode>upload_lease\u003c/code>\nfrom schema 2 and adding \u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code> for pulls; schemas 1 and 2 remain readable.\nFinal statistics include nullable \u003ccode>upload_lease\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code>.\nThat ID identifies the negotiated lease, not its current liveness. Logical\nobjects, Git pointers and C ABI formats are unchanged.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Persistent server upload leases # New clients automatically protect uploads when the server advertises this extension. Active leases survive server restarts and protect uploads during offline GC. A lease expires when its client stops renewing it; published references then provide ordinary retention after a successful transfer. text Copy syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease Legacy shared-token mode requires SYRNCK_TOKEN ; configured identities use --access . Both retain loopback binding and an HTTPS proxy for remote access. Encrypted stores use their own SYRNCK_KEY . English/French CLI text is supported; protocol fields remain English. This extension currently covers uploads ; read leases for pulls use a separate extension. Lifetime, GC and administration # The default duration is 3,600 seconds , configurable with --lease-seconds in 1..86,400 seconds . Whole-second expiry is rounded up by at most one second. The client schedules renewal using its monotonic clock at one third of the advertised duration, without requiring synchronized client/server wall clocks. The server uses its wall clock for persisted expiry, so operators must maintain that clock. A backward jump can extend retention; a forward jump can expire it. Before GC deletes payloads, it durably records observed expirations and replaces their fences. A later clock rollback cannot revive a fence whose negotiated objects might have been collected. Expired publication rejection also records that observation. GC preview does not write these transitions. The coordinator schedules renewals during object retry and traffic-pacing waits. At most one renewal worker uses a separate HTTP handle, so a delayed control reply does not stop object IO. It never accesses the Store or invokes user progress callbacks; the coordinator consumes its result and validates the fence. The worker is cancelled/joined on exit and drained before publication. Its small control buffers/connection are outside the object request/buffer counters. Handles are independent, following libcurl's thread-safety requirements . Local IO, user callbacks and synchronous control requests already running can delay scheduling. A short TTL cannot guarantee continuous protection through an arbitrarily long stall. Keep the normal TTL large enough for those operations; one-second leases are useful for controlled tests rather than deployment guidance. An active, unexpired legacy lease blocks GC preview and deletion for the whole server Store . New clients negotiate paged upload inventories , allowing unrelated objects to be collected while declared objects remain retained. GC streams and validates the records before planning. An expired, released or published lease does not retain objects. Expiry never deletes payloads itself; an ordinary GC is still required. The server holds the Store's exclusive process lock while running, so current maintenance is offline. Stop the server before local administration: text Copy syrnck --store server-data leases list syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID syrnck --store server-data leases prune release deliberately revokes an active lease. It does not modify references or payloads and leaves published receipts intact. prune removes expired, released and published records and obsolete inventory generations after validation. Live leases and their committed inventories are preserved. Removing a published receipt can reduce what a disconnected client can prove, but never authorizes replaying its old publication. The store permits at most 4,096 lease records , including receipts. New lease creation fails at capacity until inactive records are pruned. Records are at most 16 KiB; separately stored inventory pages are capped at 1 MiB each. Listing materializes at most that bounded lease inventory. Malformed records and symlinked paths fail closed. Every GC binary must understand this extension; older binaries ignore server upload holds. Fencing and resume # The client persists a random lease ID in its local journal before asking the server to create it. The server binds it to the exact root, destination reference and expected previous reference. Renewing a live lease keeps its fence. Expiry reactivation or recreation after pruning produces a fresh random fence. Every leased reference PUT carries that fence. Expired, released and stale fences fail before reference publication. Thus a delayed request cannot publish under a lease reactivated by a later attempt, including when an ID was pruned and recreated. This is coordination within one exclusively locked Store, not a distributed consensus or multi-server fencing guarantee. If renewal changes the fence during an upload, the client stops and requires resume to renegotiate missing objects. It cannot rely on a presence query made before a retention gap. Resume may reupload objects reclaimed after expiry; the unfinished local journal still protects the source against local GC. Before publication the server validates the full closure and performs the saved reference CAS. It then records a durable published receipt. These are two atomic writes: a crash between them leaves an active lease and a possibly published reference. Resume can confirm a matching root and write the receipt without another reference PUT. A published receipt is bound to the original descriptor. Repeating its leased publication acknowledges that receipt and never rewrites a reference changed later. A client recovering a lost acknowledgement can therefore finish from the receipt even after another writer changed the reference. If no receipt exists and the reference no longer matches, the existing uncertain-publication policy applies. Receipts do not pin old versions after their references are removed. Cancellation retains the upload lease until expiry or deliberate release. Local agent forget remains a local command and does not contact the server. Lease records contain operational IDs/fences and object keys, not bearer tokens or encryption keys. Legacy shared-token mode has one authority. Configured server identities add per-user ownership and permissions; use that guide for its authentication policy. Protocol and compatibility # All endpoints use the existing bearer authentication: Request Behavior GET v1/capabilities Advertises upload_leases: 1 , lease_seconds and optional upload_inventories: 1 GET v1/uploads/ID Returns lease metadata and configured lease_seconds , or 404 PUT v1/uploads/ID Creates/renews an exact descriptor; body has root , reference , expected , fence , optionally inventory: true PUT v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX Registers a fenced page before querying presence; see upload inventories POST v1/uploads/ID/confirm Confirms a matching reference without publishing it; body has fence PUT v1/refs/NAME with upload: {id, fence} Validates the lease and performs/reconciles its bound CAS Creation sends a null fence. A retried creation is idempotent for the same descriptor; renewals send the last observed fence. Lease request and response bodies are capped at 16 KiB in the client; the server rejects oversized control requests before parsing. Server records in STORE/uploads/ID.json use a strict version-1 schema for legacy leases: version , id , reference , root , expected , fence , expires_at (Unix seconds), and state ( active , expired , published , released ). Writes use the same flushed-file and atomic-publication mechanisms as journals. Inventory-enabled schema-2 records also commit inventory_pages and inventory_hash ; their separate registration requests permit at most 1 MiB. HTTP 412 rejects an expired/released/fenced leased publication; 428 rejects an unleased publication when the server requires leases. The client treats these as known rejection only on its leased publication path. Ambiguous errors still exit 3, and final reference PUTs are never automatically retried. A 404 capability endpoint or explicit upload_leases: 0 allows legacy upload when protection was not required. Network errors, malformed capabilities and loss of support for a saved lease fail rather than silently downgrade. Client --require-lease rejects unsupported servers before object upload. Server --require-lease rejects unleased final reference PUTs; legacy object PUTs remain available. The flag is for push and resumed push, not pull. Local transfer journals write schema 3 , retaining nullable upload_lease from schema 2 and adding read_lease for pulls; schemas 1 and 2 remain readable. Final statistics include nullable upload_lease and read_lease . That ID identifies the negotiated lease, not its current liveness. Logical objects, Git pointers and C ABI formats are unchanged. syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease syrnck --store server-data leases list syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID syrnck --store server-data leases prune","headings":[{"id":"persistent-server-upload-leases","text":"Persistent server upload leases","level":1},{"id":"lifetime-gc-and-administration","text":"Lifetime, GC and administration","level":2},{"id":"fencing-and-resume","text":"Fencing and resume","level":2},{"id":"protocol-and-compatibility","text":"Protocol and compatibility","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease\nsyrnck --store server-data leases list\nsyrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID\nsyrnck --store server-data leases prune","assets":[],"links":["#/en/server-read-leases","https://curl.se/libcurl/c/threadsafe.html","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/server-access","#/en/upload-inventories"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"settings","title":"Repository settings","description":"Repository settings guide.","section":"Storage","order":540,"parent":"storage","revision":"7","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"repository-settings\">Repository settings\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/settings/repository-settings\" aria-label=\"Link to Repository settings\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Each store can persist local ingestion and packing preferences in \u003ccode>settings.json\u003c/code>.\nThis optional versioned file is separate from the physical \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code>, keys,\nreferences and logical objects. Old stores without it keep their previous\ndefaults. Merely opening or inspecting such a store does not create the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck config reset\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Updates validate the complete proposed configuration and atomically replace the\nfile under the store&#39;s exclusive lock. Unspecified fields retain their values.\nUnknown fields, unsupported versions, invalid types and out-of-range values are\nrejected. Settings contain no credentials, encryption keys or persisted permission\nto compact/delete data. Symlinks and non-regular settings files are refused.\n\u003ccode>config reset\u003c/code> can replace a malformed regular settings file with defaults.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Defaults are one ingest worker, a 32 MiB buffer budget, 512 MiB per new pack and\n65,536 objects per pack. Workers range from 1 to 64, buffers from 1 to 1,024 MiB,\nand the existing pack limits still apply. The ingest budget must also fit the\nselected maximum chunk size: default CDC needs at least 4 MiB. The budget covers\nqueued raw block bytes, rather than total process memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git clean\u003c/code> read these preferences, then apply explicit\n\u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> overrides. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> applies \u003ccode>--max-mib\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--max-objects\u003c/code> similarly.\nOverrides do not rewrite settings. Compaction and deletion remain explicit\nactions. Cache budgets still use their existing per-process flags.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In C++, use \u003ccode>Store::settings()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Store::set_settings()\u003c/code>. The two-argument\n\u003ccode>ingest(input, chunking)\u003c/code> and zero-argument \u003ccode>pack()\u003c/code> use repository defaults.\nPassing \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>PackOptions\u003c/code> explicitly supplies the entire operation&#39;s\noptions. The default ingestion path is also used by native commits, Git clean\nfilters and C/Go file ingestion. Library names, JSON fields, IDs and pointers\nremain language-independent; the CLI provides English and French diagnostics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Settings do not change existing data or object formats and are not retention\nroots. Integrity auditing and explicit reset remain available when preferences\nare malformed. Different stores, including Git&#39;s common store and a standalone\n\u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code>, have independent preferences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For Git filters, use \u003ccode>--store &lt;common-store&gt;\u003c/code> with the path printed by \u003ccode>git install\u003c/code>.\nAn ordinary standalone \u003ccode>config\u003c/code> command otherwise addresses \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code> by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>See \u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">progress and cancellation\u003c/a> for CLI output modes, Ctrl+C and\nthe C++ callback interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reader-features\">Required reader features\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/settings/reader-features\" aria-label=\"Link to Required reader features\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config required-features\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These requirements describe Store semantics, independently of tunable repository\nsettings. A linked workspace automatically requires \u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> before\nits binding is published. Older executables reject the extended configuration\nat open rather than change shared branches without workspace reservations.\nFor experimental linked Stores created before this gate existed, run the\nexplicit require command before using older tools. Normal reference publication\nwith registered contexts also establishes the gate. A partial workspace additionally\nrequires \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> before publication of its selection.\nChanging an active selection also requires \u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code>\nbefore its recoverable journal is published. Returning to a complete view does\nnot remove these additive reader requirements.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspace-relocation\">Whole-directory relocation\u003c/a> requires\n\u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code> before its journal is published. This remains enabled\nafter completion or abort, so an older reader cannot open a Store during the\ntemporary absence of its old linked directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only supported names are accepted. Enabling is idempotent, preserves physical\nformat and objects, and survives pack/GC, backup, restore and repaired copies.\n\u003ccode>config reset\u003c/code> changes tunable settings while retaining reader requirements.\nThere is no automatic removal or downgrade. C++ exposes \u003ccode>required_features()\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>require_features(names, operation)\u003c/code>. See the \u003ca href=\"#/en/protocol/required-store-features\">format\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A saved \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship/identity\">native identity\u003c/a> is separate from ingestion\npreferences. Saving a profile or a v2 revision enables \u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code>\nbefore writing it; clearing the profile does not downgrade the Store. The profile\nis machine-local and is not copied by logical backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent\">resident synchronization agent\u003c/a> establishes\n\u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> before publication bookkeeping or an owned checkout. This\nprotects retained remote observations and guarded journal v11 recovery. The\nrequirement survives completion, GC and logical backup/restore; merely reading an\nordinary legacy Store does not enable it.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Repository settings # Each store can persist local ingestion and packing preferences in settings.json . This optional versioned file is separate from the physical config.json , keys, references and logical objects. Old stores without it keep their previous defaults. Merely opening or inspecting such a store does not create the file. text Copy syrnck config show syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536 syrnck config reset Updates validate the complete proposed configuration and atomically replace the file under the store's exclusive lock. Unspecified fields retain their values. Unknown fields, unsupported versions, invalid types and out-of-range values are rejected. Settings contain no credentials, encryption keys or persisted permission to compact/delete data. Symlinks and non-regular settings files are refused. config reset can replace a malformed regular settings file with defaults. Defaults are one ingest worker, a 32 MiB buffer budget, 512 MiB per new pack and 65,536 objects per pack. Workers range from 1 to 64, buffers from 1 to 1,024 MiB, and the existing pack limits still apply. The ingest budget must also fit the selected maximum chunk size: default CDC needs at least 4 MiB. The budget covers queued raw block bytes, rather than total process memory. put , native commit and git clean read these preferences, then apply explicit --workers and --buffer-mib overrides. pack applies --max-mib and --max-objects similarly. Overrides do not rewrite settings. Compaction and deletion remain explicit actions. Cache budgets still use their existing per-process flags. In C++, use Store::settings() and Store::set_settings() . The two-argument ingest(input, chunking) and zero-argument pack() use repository defaults. Passing IngestOptions or PackOptions explicitly supplies the entire operation's options. The default ingestion path is also used by native commits, Git clean filters and C/Go file ingestion. Library names, JSON fields, IDs and pointers remain language-independent; the CLI provides English and French diagnostics. Settings do not change existing data or object formats and are not retention roots. Integrity auditing and explicit reset remain available when preferences are malformed. Different stores, including Git's common store and a standalone .syrnck , have independent preferences. For Git filters, use --store \u003ccommon-store> with the path printed by git install . An ordinary standalone config command otherwise addresses .syrnck by default. See progress and cancellation for CLI output modes, Ctrl+C and the C++ callback interface. Required reader features # text Copy syrnck config required-features syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1 These requirements describe Store semantics, independently of tunable repository settings. A linked workspace automatically requires linked-workspaces-v1 before its binding is published. Older executables reject the extended configuration at open rather than change shared branches without workspace reservations. For experimental linked Stores created before this gate existed, run the explicit require command before using older tools. Normal reference publication with registered contexts also establishes the gate. A partial workspace additionally requires sparse-workspaces-v1 before publication of its selection. Changing an active selection also requires materialization-transitions-v1 before its recoverable journal is published. Returning to a complete view does not remove these additive reader requirements. Whole-directory relocation requires workspace-relocation-v1 before its journal is published. This remains enabled after completion or abort, so an older reader cannot open a Store during the temporary absence of its old linked directory. Only supported names are accepted. Enabling is idempotent, preserves physical format and objects, and survives pack/GC, backup, restore and repaired copies. config reset changes tunable settings while retaining reader requirements. There is no automatic removal or downgrade. C++ exposes required_features() and require_features(names, operation) . See the format . A saved native identity is separate from ingestion preferences. Saving a profile or a v2 revision enables authored-revisions-v1 before writing it; clearing the profile does not downgrade the Store. The profile is machine-local and is not copied by logical backups. The resident synchronization agent establishes resident-sync-v1 before publication bookkeeping or an owned checkout. This protects retained remote observations and guarded journal v11 recovery. The requirement survives completion, GC and logical backup/restore; merely reading an ordinary legacy Store does not enable it. syrnck config show syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536 syrnck config reset syrnck config required-features syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1","headings":[{"id":"repository-settings","text":"Repository settings","level":1},{"id":"reader-features","text":"Required reader features","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck config show\nsyrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536\nsyrnck config reset\nsyrnck config required-features\nsyrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1","assets":[],"links":["#/en/operations","#/en/native-workspace-relocation","#/en/protocol/required-store-features","#/en/native-authorship/identity","#/en/resident-agent"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"signing-api","title":"Ed25519 signing API","description":"Move-only in-memory signing keys, standard signatures and strict verification.","section":"API","order":650,"parent":"integrations","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Sign and verify bytes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/signing-api/usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Sign and verify bytes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/signing.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> signer \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">SigningKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">generate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> public_key \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> signer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">public_key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> signature \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> signer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">sign\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>payload\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">bool\u003c/span> valid \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">verify_ed25519\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>public_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> signature\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> payload\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> fingerprint \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">signing_key_id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>public_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>payload\u003c/code> is a span of bytes. Empty messages are supported. Public keys contain\n32 bytes; signatures contain 64 bytes. This is Ed25519 with SHA-512 as specified\nin \u003ca href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RFC 8032\u003c/a>, implemented by the optional\nmodule of vendored Monocypher 4.0.3. The base library&#39;s BLAKE2b EdDSA variant is\na different algorithm. No OpenSSL or network runtime is required by this API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Key ownership\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/signing-api/ownership\" aria-label=\"Link to Key ownership\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>SigningKey::from_seed(bytes)\u003c/code> accepts exactly 32 bytes, copies the caller&#39;s\nseed and expands it privately. The caller retains ownership of its input.\n\u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code> uses the operating system&#39;s random source. The key object is\nmove-only, has no secret export API and wipes its expanded secret on destruction.\nMoved-from objects refuse signing and public-key access. \u003ccode>public_key()\u003c/code> returns\na public copy. Keep signing seeds independent from encryption keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The fingerprint is \u003ccode>ed25519:\u003c/code> followed by BLAKE3 of the public key with the\n\u003ccode>Syrnck Ed25519 public key v1:\u003c/code> domain prefix. It is a key identifier, not proof\nthat a person owns the key. The caller must authorize a public key independently\nbefore attributing a signed payload to an identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification\">Verification contract\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/signing-api/verification\" aria-label=\"Link to Verification contract\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Verification returns false for incorrect lengths, altered messages/signatures,\nnoncanonical point encodings and small-order public keys. The latter cannot\nauthenticate a signer. The upstream curve-equation primitive checks that case;\nthe engine does not implement its own elliptic-curve arithmetic. Encodings also\nreject field elements outside the canonical range and negative-zero points.\nUpstream verification checks the signature scalar and curve equation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This verifies bytes supplied by the caller; applications must define their\nsigned payload and domain. The API does not establish identity trust, a trusted\ntimestamp, signature uniqueness or certificate validity. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-authorship\">Native authorship and commit signing\u003c/a> use the versioned\nrevision payload. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-signatures\">Configured servers\u003c/a> can authorize keys\nand committer profiles. OS key-vault integration remains separate work. Legacy revision encodings remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation\">Build and verification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/signing-api/validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Build and verification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tests cover three RFC 8032 vectors, every single-bit signature mutation in those\nvectors, changed messages, malformed lengths, weak keys, noncanonical encodings\nand key moves. They also construct a noncanonical negative-zero signature that\nsatisfies the upstream equation and require strict engine rejection. These are\nfunctional checks, not a measurement of hardware side channels. Monocypher 4.0.3\ncontains the \u003ca href=\"https://monocypher.org/changelog\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">upstream signing timing fix\u003c/a>.\nRelease provenance and checksums are recorded in\n\u003ccode>third_party/monocypher/UPSTREAM.json\u003c/code>; dependency notices accompany distribution.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Sign and verify bytes # cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp> auto signer = syrnck :: SigningKey :: generate ( ) ; auto public_key = signer . public_key ( ) ; auto signature = signer . sign ( payload ) ; bool valid = syrnck :: verify_ed25519 ( public_key , signature , payload ) ; auto fingerprint = syrnck :: signing_key_id ( public_key ) ; payload is a span of bytes. Empty messages are supported. Public keys contain 32 bytes; signatures contain 64 bytes. This is Ed25519 with SHA-512 as specified in RFC 8032 , implemented by the optional module of vendored Monocypher 4.0.3. The base library's BLAKE2b EdDSA variant is a different algorithm. No OpenSSL or network runtime is required by this API. Key ownership # SigningKey::from_seed(bytes) accepts exactly 32 bytes, copies the caller's seed and expands it privately. The caller retains ownership of its input. generate() uses the operating system's random source. The key object is move-only, has no secret export API and wipes its expanded secret on destruction. Moved-from objects refuse signing and public-key access. public_key() returns a public copy. Keep signing seeds independent from encryption keys. The fingerprint is ed25519: followed by BLAKE3 of the public key with the Syrnck Ed25519 public key v1: domain prefix. It is a key identifier, not proof that a person owns the key. The caller must authorize a public key independently before attributing a signed payload to an identity. Verification contract # Verification returns false for incorrect lengths, altered messages/signatures, noncanonical point encodings and small-order public keys. The latter cannot authenticate a signer. The upstream curve-equation primitive checks that case; the engine does not implement its own elliptic-curve arithmetic. Encodings also reject field elements outside the canonical range and negative-zero points. Upstream verification checks the signature scalar and curve equation. This verifies bytes supplied by the caller; applications must define their signed payload and domain. The API does not establish identity trust, a trusted timestamp, signature uniqueness or certificate validity. Native authorship and commit signing use the versioned revision payload. Configured servers can authorize keys and committer profiles. OS key-vault integration remains separate work. Legacy revision encodings remain unchanged. Build and verification # text Copy python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\" Tests cover three RFC 8032 vectors, every single-bit signature mutation in those vectors, changed messages, malformed lengths, weak keys, noncanonical encodings and key moves. They also construct a noncanonical negative-zero signature that satisfies the upstream equation and require strict engine rejection. These are functional checks, not a measurement of hardware side channels. Monocypher 4.0.3 contains the upstream signing timing fix . Release provenance and checksums are recorded in third_party/monocypher/UPSTREAM.json ; dependency notices accompany distribution. #include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp> auto signer = syrnck::SigningKey::generate(); auto public_key = signer.public_key(); auto signature = signer.sign(payload); bool valid = syrnck::verify_ed25519(public_key, signature, payload); auto fingerprint = syrnck::signing_key_id(public_key); python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Sign and verify bytes","level":2},{"id":"ownership","text":"Key ownership","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification contract","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Build and verification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["cpp","text"],"code":"#include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp>\nauto signer = syrnck::SigningKey::generate();\nauto public_key = signer.public_key();\nauto signature = signer.sign(payload);\nbool valid = syrnck::verify_ed25519(public_key, signature, payload);\nauto fingerprint = syrnck::signing_key_id(public_key);\npython scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests\npython scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"","assets":[],"links":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032","#/en/native-authorship","#/en/server-signatures","https://monocypher.org/changelog"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"storage","title":"Storage and reconstruction","description":"Storage and reconstruction","section":"Storage","order":100,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"objects\">Objects and complete versions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/storage/objects\" aria-label=\"Link to Objects and complete versions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A typed BLAKE3 ID addresses a chunk, manifest or revision. A manifest records the ordered complete block list, total length, file hash and chunking policy. A revision contains a complete path-to-file snapshot and parents. Sharing blocks never creates a dependency on replaying an older file version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code> retains the returned manifest under \u003ccode>files/ID\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> restores it exclusively to a new path. \u003ccode>inspect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>compare\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>diff\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> inspect metadata. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-comparison\">manifest comparison\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/file-ranges\">verified ranges\u003c/a>. Full reconstruction checks every selected chunk and the complete file hash.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Retention before collection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/storage/retention\" aria-label=\"Link to Retention before collection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Branches, tags, staging snapshots, archives and explicit pins retain their dependency closures, including revision parents. Removing a visible commit does not necessarily remove its blocks. First inspect what retains the data; then explicitly retire roots with a \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">verified backup\u003c/a>; only then consider destructive GC. GC previews by default and refuses missing/corrupt retained dependencies before deletion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/checkout-retention\">Completed checkout roots\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">transfer journals\u003c/a> and active leases have their own protection rules. Backup and restore are logical snapshots, not copies of uncommitted working files. Keep externally edited files stable while the engine scans them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"physical\">Physical storage and budgets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/storage/physical\" aria-label=\"Link to Physical storage and budgets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>New stores use loose v1 objects. Explicit packing selects physical v2/v3 while retaining logical v1 IDs and envelopes. Read \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs\">packs\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">paged indexes\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/compaction\">compaction\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/audit\">integrity audits\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/maintenance\">maintenance cancellation\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Defaults: FastCDC 256 KiB minimum, 1 MiB target, 4 MiB maximum; optional fixed chunking. Uncompressed objects and flat manifests/revisions are limited to 16 MiB. Decoded cache defaults to 64 MiB, verified stamps to 8192 entries, index-page cache to 4 MiB. Cache budgets do not bound total process memory. \u003ca href=\"#/en/settings\">Repository preferences\u003c/a> control ingest workers and pack creation separately.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Objects and complete versions # A typed BLAKE3 ID addresses a chunk, manifest or revision. A manifest records the ordered complete block list, total length, file hash and chunking policy. A revision contains a complete path-to-file snapshot and parents. Sharing blocks never creates a dependency on replaying an older file version. put retains the returned manifest under files/ID ; get restores it exclusively to a new path. inspect , compare , diff and refs inspect metadata. See manifest comparison and verified ranges . Full reconstruction checks every selected chunk and the complete file hash. Retention before collection # Branches, tags, staging snapshots, archives and explicit pins retain their dependency closures, including revision parents. Removing a visible commit does not necessarily remove its blocks. First inspect what retains the data; then explicitly retire roots with a verified backup ; only then consider destructive GC. GC previews by default and refuses missing/corrupt retained dependencies before deletion. Completed checkout roots , transfer journals and active leases have their own protection rules. Backup and restore are logical snapshots, not copies of uncommitted working files. Keep externally edited files stable while the engine scans them. Physical storage and budgets # New stores use loose v1 objects. Explicit packing selects physical v2/v3 while retaining logical v1 IDs and envelopes. Read packs , paged indexes , compaction , integrity audits and maintenance cancellation . Defaults: FastCDC 256 KiB minimum, 1 MiB target, 4 MiB maximum; optional fixed chunking. Uncompressed objects and flat manifests/revisions are limited to 16 MiB. Decoded cache defaults to 64 MiB, verified stamps to 8192 entries, index-page cache to 4 MiB. Cache budgets do not bound total process memory. Repository preferences control ingest workers and pack creation separately.","headings":[{"id":"objects","text":"Objects and complete versions","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Retention before collection","level":2},{"id":"physical","text":"Physical storage and budgets","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/file-comparison","#/en/file-ranges","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/checkout-retention","#/en/selective-retention","#/en/packs","#/en/indexes","#/en/compaction","#/en/audit","#/en/maintenance","#/en/settings"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"syncthing","title":"Syncthing fixed-block adapter","description":"Syncthing fixed-block adapter guide.","section":"API","order":660,"parent":"integrations","revision":"9","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"syncthing-fixed-block-adapter\">Syncthing fixed-block adapter\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/syncthing-fixed-block-adapter\" aria-label=\"Link to Syncthing fixed-block adapter\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>This optional Go module connects the C++ engine to Syncthing&#39;s BEP block model.\nIt depends on the unmodified Syncthing library at \u003cstrong>v2.1.7\u003c/strong>, commit\n\u003ccode>d6d0181f7d46\u003c/code>, represented by the Go pseudo-version pinned in \u003ccode>go.mod\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code>.\nThe core Go binding remains a separate module without external Go dependencies.\nThis module requires Go 1.26.2 or later; local validation used Go 1.27.3.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The adapter supports regular-file data, explicitly paired device connections,\na durable causal catalog and a continuous native-workspace controller.\n\u003ccode>Folder\u003c/code> captures local changes, applies verified incoming versions and retains\nrecovery state. \u003ccode>FolderSession\u003c/code> runs that controller over authenticated BEP.\nDiscovery, a pairing UI, OS service installation and complete Syncthing\nfolder semantics remain work to complete; see the controller limits below.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-and-test\">Native library and dependencies\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/build-and-test\" aria-label=\"Link to Native library and dependencies\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Build \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> and prepare the pinned Go dependencies once with \u003ccode>go mod download\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>bindings/syncthing\u003c/code>. The adapter is optional and separate from the core binding. Linux/macOS use cgo and their loader path. The earlier catalog and transport passed Linux CI; this new controller is locally qualified on Windows, with current cross-platform execution pending. Distribute the dependencies and notices required by the chosen integration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apis\">APIs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/apis\" aria-label=\"Link to APIs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>NewFile(ctx, store, manifest, folder, name, blockSize)\u003c/code> creates an immutable\nservable view. It verifies the engine&#39;s complete file hash without exporting a\nfile, then reads verified ranges and constructs Syncthing \u003ccode>protocol.FileInfo\u003c/code>\nSHA-256 descriptors. Engine CDC/fixed chunks and BLAKE3 object IDs stay intact.\nA zero block size chooses Syncthing&#39;s own size policy; explicit sizes must be\npowers of two from 128 KiB through 16 MiB. An index is capped at 65536 blocks.\nEmpty files use the reference implementation&#39;s single empty SHA-256 block.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>File.Info()\u003c/code> returns an independent index copy. Set application-owned version\nvectors, sequence numbers and timestamps on that copy before announcing it.\nNames must be valid UTF-8 relative paths. The adapter normalizes native Darwin\nNFD names to NFC and converts native Windows separators to the protocol&#39;s \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>.\nIndexes, block requests and resume identity use the same canonical name.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>File.ReadRequest(ctx, req)\u003c/code> serves an exact advertised block for the bound\nfolder and file. A supplied SHA-256 hash must match; the protocol&#39;s optional\nhash and older zero/default block number are supported. A temporary-file\nrequest can fall back to this complete immutable source. Other paths, ranges\nor hash generations are refused. Payloads are verified by the engine and\nchecked against the announced SHA-256 digest before returning them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Fetch(ctx, destination, folder, info, request, options)\u003c/code> reconstructs one\nannounced file. Pass a \u003ccode>protocol.Connection.Request\u003c/code> method or another\nauthenticated block requester. Every response must have the exact size/hash.\nAn optional \u003ccode>ReuseManifest\u003c/code> indexes a local immutable file at the incoming\nblock size and reuses matching blocks, including moved/repeated blocks. Only\nmissing data is requested. \u003ccode>FetchResult.Stats\u003c/code> separates requested bytes, local\nreuse and verified staging bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, &quot;project&quot;, &quot;Content/Map.uasset&quot;, 0)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">// Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, &quot;project&quot;, incomingInfo,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">        ReuseManifest: previousManifest,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">        ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">    })\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">// Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After assembling and verifying private staging bytes, Fetch ingests the complete\nfile through the C ABI with fixed engine chunks at the BEP size. It rechecks the\nstored index against the incoming block hashes. The engine retains the complete\nmanifest through its existing \u003ccode>ffi/ID\u003c/code> root. The embedding application can then\nrestore it with the engine&#39;s exclusive file publication or include it in its\nown revision transaction. Fetch never replaces a working file or mutable\nbranch reference.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_file_verify\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>Store.VerifyFile\u003c/code> are the additive C/Go ABI entry points\nfor targeted whole-file validation, including chunks, sizes and \u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code>.\nThey write no destination or reference and stream through bounded native chunk\nbuffers. This binding requires a rebuilt native library exporting that symbol.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-catalog\">Durable folder catalog\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-catalog\" aria-label=\"Link to Durable folder catalog\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenCatalog(ctx, store, CatalogOptions)\u003c/code> loads a folder&#39;s durable catalog or\ncreates an unpublished empty draft. Bind the folder to the full local Device ID\nand the exact trusted peer list. Duplicate devices, local/sentinel peers and\ncolliding 64-bit vector identities are refused. Membership changes require a\nseparate management workflow; changing the options cannot silently replace it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>Catalog\u003c/code> holds no Store or connection. \u003ccode>LocalIndex\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>PeerIndex\u003c/code> return\nindependent snapshots. \u003ccode>RecordLocal\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>RecordLocalChanges\u003c/code> assigns local\nsequence numbers and advances the previous local version vector, with overflow\nchecks. Caller-supplied causal fields must be unset. Batch recording validates\nall changes together and sorts names once. Deletions require explicit tombstones.\nDirectories and symlink descriptors are preserved as metadata; this API does\nnot create directories or follow symlink targets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ReceiveIndex(CatalogIndex)\u003c/code> records a paired device&#39;s full Index or delta. The\ncaller must bind \u003ccode>Device\u003c/code> to the authenticated connection, authorize the folder\nand take \u003ccode>IndexID\u003c/code> from its negotiated ClusterConfig. A new generation requires\na full reset; subsequent batches must continue the persisted previous sequence.\nNames and sequence numbers are unique, vectors are sorted/bounded and duplicate\ncounters, invalid flags, unsupported encrypted entries and malformed block\nlayouts are refused. Equal vectors cannot describe different content metadata.\nPeer announcements do not automatically become local files or download data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Attach(ctx, store, device, name, manifest)\u003c/code> verifies downloaded data against the\ncurrently recorded block descriptors. \u003ccode>Adopt(device, name)\u003c/code> promotes a verified,\ncausally newer peer entry into the local advertised index. Concurrent versions\nreturn \u003ccode>ErrCatalogConflict\u003c/code>; older versions and equal-vector/content mismatches\nare refused. Both versions remain retained, without a silent winner. This changes\nthe advertised catalog only; working-file application needs its own recovery\ntransaction before a service announces that a workspace has caught up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Commit(ctx, store)\u003c/code> verifies retained file data, then publishes state and data\nunder one expected-value guarded \u003ccode>ffi/bep/…\u003c/code> native revision root. Previous\ncatalog revisions are native parents, so an index reset, rename or deletion\nkeeps earlier complete versions reachable by GC and backups. Payload paths use\nhashes of device/name identities; \u003ccode>.git/config\u003c/code>, case-distinct names and paths\nincompatible with a portable workspace can remain lossless in storage. They\nare not materialized by this API. No implicit \u003ccode>ffi/manifestID\u003c/code> pins are created.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A stale draft returns \u003ccode>ErrCatalogStale\u003c/code>. Reload and replan; do not retarget an\nold operation. An IO error during publication has an unknown outcome: reload\nand establish the persisted result before retrying. Unchanged commits are\nno-ops. A fresh local IndexID becomes durable on the first successful commit.\nThe catalog codec rejects duplicate/unknown fields and noncanonical state;\nlimits are 256 paired devices, 256 counters/vector, 65536 entries across all\nindexes and 16 MiB of catalog metadata. Each commit currently verifies its\nretained files and native closure; large-project throughput is not established.\nHistory retirement and catalog sharding remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"staged-download\">Download without holding the Store\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/staged-download\" aria-label=\"Link to Download without holding the Store\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>DownloadFile(ctx, folder, info, request, DownloadOptions)\u003c/code> verifies network\nblocks into private staging without opening a native Store. Optional \u003ccode>ReuseIndex\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>ReadReuse\u003c/code> reuse exact matching local blocks; that callback can open/close\na Store briefly. Different block sizes disable reuse. Caller-owned resume data\nuses the same validated descriptor and range checks as Fetch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The returned \u003ccode>StagedFile\u003c/code> holds no open file or Store. Always \u003ccode>Close()\u003c/code> it.\n\u003ccode>Info()\u003c/code> returns an independent copy and \u003ccode>Stats()\u003c/code> reports transfer/reuse/staging.\nCall \u003ccode>Prepare(ctx, store)\u003c/code> while briefly owning the Store. It imports an unpinned\nmanifest and rechecks stored bytes against the announced blocks. Attach that\nmanifest to the exact current peer entry, optionally adopt it, then Commit\nbefore closing Store or permitting GC. This prevents network waits from holding\nthe native exclusive lock. Revalidate the persisted peer entry after download:\nit may have advanced while the transfer was in flight.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PrepareFetch\u003c/code> is a convenience for a caller that already holds a Store; it has\nthe same unpinned ownership rule. Legacy \u003ccode>Fetch\u003c/code> continues retaining its result\nindependently. Keep these ownership choices explicit; do not remove a shared\nlegacy pin owned by another consumer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"device-connections\">Authenticate a device connection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/device-connections\" aria-label=\"Link to Authenticate a device connection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, DeviceOptions, model)\u003c/code> takes ownership of an\nunsecured connected \u003ccode>net.Conn\u003c/code>, including on failure. Supply one local TLS leaf\ncertificate with its matching \u003ccode>crypto.Signer\u003c/code> private key and the explicitly\npaired peer&#39;s \u003ccode>protocol.DeviceID\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Server: true\u003c/code> selects the accepting side.\nSelf connections, empty/sentinel identities, malformed keys and certificate/key\nmismatches are rejected. Do not mutate the certificate or key while in use.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The connection requires TLS 1.3 or later and \u003ccode>bep/1.0\u003c/code> ALPN. It exchanges the\nbounded upstream Hello before rejecting an unpaired peer, then verifies the\nSHA-256 identity of the exact single peer certificate. This is certificate\npinning: DNS, a Web PKI CA, certificate expiry, discovery and Hello names do not\ngrant membership. \u003ccode>PeerHello\u003c/code> contains untrusted descriptive information.\nNo application model is attached until verification succeeds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    Certificate: deviceCertificate,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">    RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">    Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: &quot;Asset workstation&quot;},\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">}, folderModel)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">// Handle err before use. Start is explicit; ClusterConfig must be the first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">// post-authentication message. The model must authorize every shared folder.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">session.Start()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">// Send a full Index before updates, unless a persisted delta-index agreement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">// permits otherwise. Fetch can use session.Request for verified block data.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>HandshakeTimeout\u003c/code> defaults to 20 seconds and accepts 100 ms through one minute.\nIt covers TLS and Hello; an earlier context deadline wins. Compression defaults\nto the reference metadata policy; Never/Always are also accepted. The context\ncontrols the complete session lifetime, including an authenticated session that\nhas not been started. \u003ccode>Close(reason)\u003c/code> releases the stream; wait for the model&#39;s\n\u003ccode>Closed\u003c/code> callback before closing native Store handles used by its callbacks.\nThe connection metadata contains a stable establishment time and session ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The application supplies certificate storage/rotation, trusted Device IDs and\nconnection scheduling. The low-level connection accepts an application model;\n\u003ccode>FolderSession\u003c/code> supplies the regular-file controller described below. Transport encryption authenticates and protects this peer\nconnection; it does not make an ordinary receiving daemon an untrusted encrypted\nbackup. The complete untrusted-device encryption workflow remains to be integrated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reference-daemon\">Qualify against the reference daemon\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/reference-daemon\" aria-label=\"Link to Qualify against the reference daemon\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This optional test helper compiles \u003ccode>cmd/syncthing\u003c/code> from the exact adapter module\nversion using the checked-in \u003ccode>tests/syncthing-reference/go.mod\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code>.\nIt uses the upstream \u003ccode>noassets\u003c/code> build tag, which omits the web UI assets while\nretaining the real daemon, database, scanner, model, puller and REST API.\nThe output is \u003ccode>build/qualification/syncthing\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>.exe\u003c/code> on Windows). Without\n\u003ccode>--download\u003c/code>, the helper requires a prepared module cache and makes no implicit\nmodule download. It verifies the executable&#39;s embedded upstream module version\nand refuses adapter/reference version mismatches or modified manifests. It also\nresolves the adapter test dependency graph with its platform cgo setting before\noffline tests, including Darwin’s native watcher dependency.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SYNCTHING_BINARY\u003c/code> to that executable&#39;s absolute path and run:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On Linux/macOS provide the built shared library path instead. The three-platform\nCI enables the adapter and this reference test explicitly. The reference daemon\ntest uses fresh test-owned configuration, keys and folder storage, binds only\nloopback, disables discovery/relays/NAT/upgrades/reporting and terminates its\nprocess after the test. It never reads or changes an installed Syncthing profile.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local Windows verification exercised both directions against the unmodified\nupstream daemon: a 384 KiB asset with moved/reused blocks transferred one missing\n128 KiB block in each direction. Reconstruction into encrypted Syrnck storage\nverified the resulting complete file. Real daemon rename/tombstone announcements\nwere also received. This proves transport and regular-file interoperability;\na further real-daemon test persists full/delta indexes and data, closes native\nStores during network waits, then reconnects after an offline rename and\npreserves its tombstone. The production-controller test additionally reconstructs an actual native\nworking folder, returns a post-completion local edit to the daemon, reconnects after\nan offline rename and propagates a local deletion upstream. Without the explicit\nbinary, live-daemon tests report a skip; ordinary adapter tests still run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resume-and-cancellation\">Resume and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/resume-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to Resume and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Without \u003ccode>ResumeDirectory\u003c/code>, Fetch uses a private temporary file and removes it\nwhen the call returns. With it, the caller owns a per-content staging directory\ncontaining \u003ccode>state.json\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>data\u003c/code>. Metadata binds the folder/name/size/block\nsize and the hash of the complete announced block list. A different index,\nunknown file, incomplete descriptor or symlink is refused. Use a private\ndirectory and serialize callers managing it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On retry, every staged range is hashed again. Complete matching ranges are\nreused; partial/corrupt ranges are fetched again. Sparse zero-filled ranges can\nsatisfy matching zero blocks without a request. Progress is flushed every\n16 written blocks and before ingestion. Cancellation preserves the caller-owned\nstaging data. The staging file contains plaintext, including when the Store is\nencrypted; choose an appropriately protected location. Completed staging\ndirectories remain caller-owned and require explicit retirement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Context cancellation is checked between native calls and network/block steps.\nUnpinned preparation propagates cancellation into the native ingestion\noperation. Legacy retained ingest and complete verification finish their current\nsynchronous native call.\nA late cancellation or a detected change during ingestion can therefore leave\na complete internally retained manifest; its ID is returned alongside the\nerror. Callers must handle the error before publishing that result. No partial\nworking file is installed. A process kill can leave private temporary data;\npower-loss durability and automatic staging reclamation are not established.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-controller\">Continuous working-folder controller\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-controller\" aria-label=\"Link to Continuous working-folder controller\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Create the native Store and workspace first, then call \u003ccode>NewFolder(ctx, options)\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>FolderOptions\u003c/code> binds one catalog/local device, its explicit peer list, the\nworking directory, Store and optional registered workspace name. \u003ccode>OpenStore\u003c/code>\nreturns a newly opened handle owned by the controller: an encrypted integration\ncan obtain its key from the embedding application&#39;s provider. No idle Store is\nretained. Close sessions before closing Folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Refresh\u003c/code> captures local working edits using the native watcher/index and\nperiodic full audits (five minutes by default). \u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> requests an immediate\nfull audit. \u003ccode>ReceiveIndex\u003c/code> persists an authenticated paired device&#39;s full/delta\nindex. \u003ccode>Step\u003c/code> downloads one missing file, reuses verified blocks across current\nlocal files, then applies the complete candidate through the native owned\ncheckout. Exact-content renames need no payload transfer. Immutable advertised\nbytes stay servable while a new version downloads; incoming indexes can advance\non a separate worker. Every downloaded descriptor/generation is rechecked before\nattachment, so stale downloads cannot be installed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Incoming application retains three typed roots for its descriptor, native\ncandidate and prepared catalog before touching working files. Protocol publication\nwaits for completed native evidence with this operation&#39;s unique durable owner,\nsource, target and selected branch. \u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext\u003c/code> uses the core receipt\ndecoder, without reconstructing a private record format in Go or diffing entire\nhistorical asset trees. HEAD equality alone is insufficient. A local edit after\nnative completion is captured with the received causal base; it cannot deadlock\nthe acknowledgement or be overwritten. A preparation obstructed before any\nnative journal starts is retired only after checking the exact original native\nstate. Foreign/ambiguous journals remain explicit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The catalog retains previous complete versions through ordinary native revision\nparents; its pending candidates are also GC roots. Process-termination tests kill\na real child before and after native publication, run GC, reopen, recover and\nverify the incoming bytes or a subsequent local edit, with plain/encrypted\nStores. Concurrent protocol versions block by default; the explicit\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">conflict-copy policy\u003c/a> retains both contents.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-conflicts\">Concurrent protocol versions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\" aria-label=\"Link to Concurrent protocol versions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>FolderOptions.ConflictCopies: true\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>&quot;conflict_copies&quot;: true\u003c/code> in the\nstandalone configuration, to resolve concurrent regular-file vectors. The\nstrict default still returns \u003ccode>ErrCatalogConflict\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>Catalog.Adopt\u003c/code> keeps that\nbehavior. Embedders can explicitly call \u003ccode>Catalog.ResolveConcurrent(ctx, store, peer, name)\u003c/code> on a draft, then publish it with the normal verified transaction.\nIts result describes the draft choice, not a working-file receipt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both live inputs are checked against their announced BEP blocks before choosing\na winner. The pinned upstream \u003ccode>FileInfo.WinsConflict\u003c/code> selects the newer modified\ntime, then orders concurrent vectors on a tie. Locally captured versions use the\nnative capture timestamp. The winner keeps its metadata and receives the union\nof both vectors. Concurrent equal contents coalesce by the native whole-file\nhash, independently of chunk boundaries; two tombstones also coalesce.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Different losing contents become a tracked sibling containing \u003ccode>.sync-conflict-\u003c/code>,\nwith a deterministic origin/vector/content identity and the short extension\npreserved. A losing deletion has no contents to copy; a winning deletion keeps\nthe losing live file in a copy. Copy names reserve existing tracked names and\ntombstones, including Unicode case/normalization and file/directory prefix\ncollisions. Bounded alternate names avoid replacing an occupied tracked path.\nOther native namespace or working-file obstructions still block application.\nCopies follow the workspace&#39;s materialization selection; hidden copies remain\nretained logical files. There is no text or binary content merge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The complete winner, copy and causal choices are frozen in the existing native\npending transaction. Recovery applies that candidate even if the option is\nsubsequently disabled. Only its owned completion receipt permits catalog\nacknowledgement. Both inputs remain reachable through catalog history; copies\nare ordinary tracked files and are not automatically retired. Syncthing may\ncreate its own conflict-copy names when it resolves first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tests cover symmetric two-controller convergence without repeated history,\nequal-content coalescing, live/deleted combinations, validation failures and\nUnicode collisions. Actual process termination before/after publication, GC,\nverified backup/restore and recovery preserve both inputs in plain/encrypted\nStores. Standalone processes also retain the losing contents on both devices\nagainst the unmodified reference daemon, with each side winning once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-sessions\">Continuous authenticated sessions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-sessions\" aria-label=\"Link to Continuous authenticated sessions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenFolderSession(ctx, folder, stream, options)\u003c/code> owns the connected transport,\nchecks that the certificate matches the catalog&#39;s complete local Device ID,\nauthenticates the configured paired peer, then negotiates folder generations and\nfull/delta indexes. \u003ccode>PollInterval\u003c/code> defaults to 250 ms (50 ms through one minute).\nThe incoming queue defaults to 32 MiB (1 through 128 MiB), includes the batch\nbeing processed and never blocks the BEP reader waiting for native disk work.\n\u003ccode>Reports\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Errors\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Done\u003c/code> expose progress, blocked operations and shutdown.\n\u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> stops workers and waits for the transport/model shutdown.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The embedding application supplies listening/dialing, certificate/key storage\nand OS lifecycle. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-service\">resident service\u003c/a> provides\nreconnect backoff and independent offline capture. This module is optional; the CMake BEP target packages the standalone executable. The controller currently applies regular files\nand file tombstones, not directory/symlink metadata, empty-directory lifecycle,\nplatform ownership/xattrs or receive-encrypted folders. It retains incoming\nmetadata but does not claim these paths have been applied. It also lacks\npersistent download resume, automatic membership\nchanges/history retirement and authored/signature policy for newly synthesized\nincoming native candidates. Large-project performance and power-loss durability\nare not qualified. The native resident publication agent is a separate feature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>StagedFile.PrepareWithChunking(ctx, store, syrnck.Chunking{})\u003c/code> keeps default\nFastCDC native chunks independent of BEP SHA-256 boundaries. Legacy \u003ccode>Prepare\u003c/code>\nkeeps its fixed-BEP chunking. \u003ccode>Catalog.CommitWithRefs\u003c/code> atomically publishes its\ncatalog with additional typed reference guards; callers must reload after an\nunknown publication outcome, as with the native CAS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An external writer can change a file during active native installation. Default\nrecovery stays strict. Set \u003ccode>FolderOptions.PreserveCheckoutEdits = true\u003c/code> to retain\nregular-file edits as described below. File visibility alone is not a completion\nnotification: use the controller checkpoint/status. An edit after the native\nreceipt has committed is supported independently of this option.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The controller maps native filename spelling to canonical NFC protocol names.\nIt preserves an existing decomposed native spelling when applying edits and\nrejects two native names that normalize to the same protocol path. An echo or\nrescan cannot turn that spelling difference into a deletion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Preserve edits during active checkout\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Link to Preserve edits during active checkout\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The explicit \u003ccode>PreserveCheckoutEdits\u003c/code> option is frozen in each prepared\napplication; changing future configuration cannot retarget an interrupted\noperation. Recovery checks the exact owner, native target, context, directory\nand private staged/backup files. It ingests changed regular files into native\narchive revisions before moving any working bytes. Exclusive conflict-copy\nmoves and exclusive restoration of the expected intermediate bytes are replayed\nfrom retained descriptors. The original strict checkout then rechecks all target\nfiles and publishes its unchanged candidate and receipt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Archive roots use \u003ccode>archive/checkout-edits/TRANSACTION/EDIT/state\u003c/code> (descriptor)\nand \u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> (revision). Their native parents retain the incoming revision and\nits complete file closure. Additional \u003ccode>archive/checkout-edit-versions/TRANSACTION/REVISION\u003c/code> roots retain changes\nto a conflict copy during recovery. Copies are deterministic from each retained\nedit identity, preserve short extensions and are never overwritten. A file that\nappears at a copy name is kept; remaining original edits get a fresh identity.\nThe report&#39;s \u003ccode>preserved_edits\u003c/code> lists source paths, copy paths and archived\nrevision IDs; a copy&#39;s current bytes may be newer than that archived revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After acknowledgement, normal local capture advertises the preserved files.\nThis protects file-content edits before backup, during staging and after file\ninstallation, including an otherwise unchanged tracked file. Prepared signed\ncandidates remain unchanged; recovery needs no signing private key. Archives\nremain GC roots after workspace changes. Automatic retirement of these groups\nstill needs an administrative policy; do not remove their roots independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An actively changing editor can defer completion: each bounded attempt keeps\nits observations for the next retry. Missing-file/deletion intent, directory or\nsymlink obstructions and ambiguous namespace changes still block rather than\ninventing a resolution. This option does not resolve concurrent BEP version\nvectors, implement a complete folder service or establish power-loss safety.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If the original checkout must remove or rename a parent directory, preservation\nplaces the copy outside that directory while keeping the source basename and\nshort extension. This also covers directory-to-file and directory case changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-service\">Resident reconnection service\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-service\" aria-label=\"Link to Resident reconnection service\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenFolderService(ctx, folder, options)\u003c/code> runs one explicitly paired peer across\nconnection loss. Supply \u003ccode>FolderServiceOptions.Session\u003c/code> with the device certificate,\npaired remote ID and session budgets, plus \u003ccode>Connect(ctx)\u003c/code> returning a connected\n\u003ccode>net.Conn\u003c/code>. The factory can dial or accept, must honor cancellation, and transfers\nownership of any returned connection, including alongside an error. TLS/BEP peer\nauthentication always runs after the factory; it cannot authorize a device.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The service observes/captures local changes independently of connection progress,\nincluding offline edits, deletion/rename and an authenticated peer that never\nsends its folder configuration. \u003ccode>OfflineInterval\u003c/code> defaults to 250 ms (50 ms\nthrough one minute). It also runs during downloads: the native folder controller\nserializes state and rejects stale candidates without holding Store during\nnetwork waits. Ordinary full audit scheduling remains enabled.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RetryMin\u003c/code> defaults to 500 ms and \u003ccode>RetryMax\u003c/code> to 30 seconds. Failed connections use\nexponential backoff within configured bounds (50 ms through one minute); a\nsuccessful authenticated connection resets it. Each reconnect sends a fresh\nHello timestamp by default and resumes durable native/catalog state. No discovery\nor implicit pairing is introduced.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Events\u003c/code> reports connecting, connected and retrying; connected means certificate\nauthentication, not completed folder application. \u003ccode>Reports\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Errors\u003c/code> expose\nfolder progress and failures. All three bounded streams are advisory and may drop\nmessages under consumer backpressure; reload durable state for receipts. \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code>\ncancels/joins dialing, TLS and session workers, including a transport completing\nafter cancellation, then closes streams and \u003ccode>Done\u003c/code>. Stop the service before\nclosing its caller-owned Folder. It keeps no idle Store. Storage-key lifetime\nbelongs to the Folder’s application-supplied Store provider; keep the device\nsigning key immutable until shutdown. Close returns any session teardown error.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tests cover plain/encrypted two-workspace offline capture, TCP reconnection,\noffline rename/tombstone convergence, failed factory transport cleanup, stalled\nTLS cancellation and local capture while an authenticated peer withholds folder\nconfiguration. Existing session tests separately qualify the pinned upstream\ndaemon. The application still supplies network listening/dialing, certificates,\nkey storage and OS lifecycle. OS service packaging and\ncomplete directory/symlink semantics remain unfinished.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"standalone-service\">Standalone foreground executable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/standalone-service\" aria-label=\"Link to Standalone foreground executable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The optional \u003ccode>syrnck-bep\u003c/code> executable runs the native-backed resident service for\none existing workspace and one explicitly paired peer. It captures offline\nchanges, authenticates the peer, reconnects after failures and applies guarded\nregular-file versions. It uses no Git process and requires no Go installation at\nruntime. The C++ engine remains the storage/revision implementation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck-bep run --config service.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Provision a distinct device certificate/private key externally and configure its\nprinted full Device ID on the peer. A Syncthing \u003ccode>generate\u003c/code> identity can be used;\nreuse its certificate and matching key together. Self-connections are refused.\nThe CLI imports identities; generation, rotation and OS vault integration remain\nseparate work. Both sides must share the same folder ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Copy the installed \u003ccode>share/Syrnck/examples/bep-service.json\u003c/code> into your configuration\ndirectory and replace its paths, peer ID and address. The example is also in the\nsource \u003ccode>examples\u003c/code> directory. Initialize the workspace Store with the main\n\u003ccode>syrnck\u003c/code> CLI first. Relative paths resolve against the configuration file. The\nStore defaults to \u003ccode>directory/.syrnck\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> can select an existing linked\ncontext. \u003ccode>mode: dial\u003c/code> connects to \u003ccode>address\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>listen\u003c/code> accepts there instead. An\nexplicit \u003ccode>host:port\u003c/code> is required. Each process owns its transport and Folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The PEM private key must be outside the working tree or inside its protected\nStore, including after resolving links. It must not become source content.\n\u003ccode>key_env\u003c/code> names the storage key environment variable (default \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>):\nabsent means no supplied key; otherwise its value must be 64 hexadecimal\ncharacters matching the existing Store. No raw key belongs in JSON or arguments.\nTLS identity and at-rest encryption keys have different roles. The PEM file stays\nunder its filesystem permissions; Store encryption protects stored objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Configuration version 1 rejects duplicate/unknown fields and inputs over 64 KiB.\n\u003ccode>poll_ms\u003c/code> is 50..60000 (zero selects 250); retry bounds default to 500 and 30000 ms.\n\u003ccode>use_permissions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>preserve_checkout_edits\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>conflict_copies\u003c/code> default false.\nThe example explicitly enables \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">active-file preservation\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">concurrent-version copies\u003c/a>. These protect\ndifferent situations and can be configured independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Stop with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM. Restart with the same identity/configuration to\nresume retained native/catalog state. Standard output is canonical JSON;\n\u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> means local initialization/listener setup, not remote synchronization.\nHuman help and CLI-owned diagnostics use English/French; \u003ccode>--lang\u003c/code> overrides\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_LANG\u003c/code>, then English. Technical OS/library details retain their language.\nThe main CLI opens this page with \u003ccode>syrnck help syncthing --section standalone-service\u003c/code>.\nOS service installation, multiple-peer orchestration and full directory/symlink\nsemantics remain unfinished.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-standalone-service\">Build and distribute the executable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/syncthing/build-standalone-service\" aria-label=\"Link to Build and distribute the executable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Enable \u003ccode>-DSYRNCK_BUILD_BEP_SERVICE=ON\u003c/code> in CMake (default on when Syncthing tests\nare enabled). Build \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> first. With Go 1.26.2+ available, prepare the\npinned public dependencies once:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library NATIVE_LIBRARY --output BEP_EXECUTABLE\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>build/Release/syrnck-bep.exe\u003c/code> on the Windows\npreset, \u003ccode>build/libsyrnck_ffi.so\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>build/syrnck-bep\u003c/code> on Linux, and\n\u003ccode>build/libsyrnck_ffi.dylib\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>build/syrnck-bep\u003c/code> on macOS. Then build normally;\nCMake invokes the same helper offline. Unix requires cgo and the matching native\nlibrary. This target uses a separate nonsanitized build from core ASan checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>cmake --install build --config Release --prefix dist\u003c/code> installs the executable,\nmatching native library and local manual. It also retains the actual runtime Go\nmodules’ unmodified source archives, licenses/notices, Go runtime license and\nhash manifest under \u003ccode>share/licenses/Syrnck/bep\u003c/code>. Keep them with the distribution;\nthe project MIT license does not relicense dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local Windows tests run actual standalone processes against the pinned upstream\ndaemon in plaintext/encrypted Stores, with both-direction edits and an upstream\noffline rename across process restart. Separate hard-termination tests reconstruct\nboth old and new native versions. Linux/macOS executable and relocation checks\nremain subject to their CI results; the source configuration alone is not proof.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Syncthing fixed-block adapter # This optional Go module connects the C++ engine to Syncthing's BEP block model. It depends on the unmodified Syncthing library at v2.1.7 , commit d6d0181f7d46 , represented by the Go pseudo-version pinned in go.mod / go.sum . The core Go binding remains a separate module without external Go dependencies. This module requires Go 1.26.2 or later; local validation used Go 1.27.3. The adapter supports regular-file data, explicitly paired device connections, a durable causal catalog and a continuous native-workspace controller. Folder captures local changes, applies verified incoming versions and retains recovery state. FolderSession runs that controller over authenticated BEP. Discovery, a pairing UI, OS service installation and complete Syncthing folder semantics remain work to complete; see the controller limits below. Native library and dependencies # Build syrnck_ffi and prepare the pinned Go dependencies once with go mod download from bindings/syncthing . The adapter is optional and separate from the core binding. Linux/macOS use cgo and their loader path. The earlier catalog and transport passed Linux CI; this new controller is locally qualified on Windows, with current cross-platform execution pending. Distribute the dependencies and notices required by the chosen integration. APIs # NewFile(ctx, store, manifest, folder, name, blockSize) creates an immutable servable view. It verifies the engine's complete file hash without exporting a file, then reads verified ranges and constructs Syncthing protocol.FileInfo SHA-256 descriptors. Engine CDC/fixed chunks and BLAKE3 object IDs stay intact. A zero block size chooses Syncthing's own size policy; explicit sizes must be powers of two from 128 KiB through 16 MiB. An index is capped at 65536 blocks. Empty files use the reference implementation's single empty SHA-256 block. File.Info() returns an independent index copy. Set application-owned version vectors, sequence numbers and timestamps on that copy before announcing it. Names must be valid UTF-8 relative paths. The adapter normalizes native Darwin NFD names to NFC and converts native Windows separators to the protocol's / . Indexes, block requests and resume identity use the same canonical name. File.ReadRequest(ctx, req) serves an exact advertised block for the bound folder and file. A supplied SHA-256 hash must match; the protocol's optional hash and older zero/default block number are supported. A temporary-file request can fall back to this complete immutable source. Other paths, ranges or hash generations are refused. Payloads are verified by the engine and checked against the announced SHA-256 digest before returning them. Fetch(ctx, destination, folder, info, request, options) reconstructs one announced file. Pass a protocol.Connection.Request method or another authenticated block requester. Every response must have the exact size/hash. An optional ReuseManifest indexes a local immutable file at the incoming block size and reuses matching blocks, including moved/repeated blocks. Only missing data is requested. FetchResult.Stats separates requested bytes, local reuse and verified staging bytes. go Copy view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0) // Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model. result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo, connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{ ReuseManifest: previousManifest, ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory, }) // Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision. After assembling and verifying private staging bytes, Fetch ingests the complete file through the C ABI with fixed engine chunks at the BEP size. It rechecks the stored index against the incoming block hashes. The engine retains the complete manifest through its existing ffi/ID root. The embedding application can then restore it with the engine's exclusive file publication or include it in its own revision transaction. Fetch never replaces a working file or mutable branch reference. syrnck_file_verify / Store.VerifyFile are the additive C/Go ABI entry points for targeted whole-file validation, including chunks, sizes and file_hash . They write no destination or reference and stream through bounded native chunk buffers. This binding requires a rebuilt native library exporting that symbol. Durable folder catalog # OpenCatalog(ctx, store, CatalogOptions) loads a folder's durable catalog or creates an unpublished empty draft. Bind the folder to the full local Device ID and the exact trusted peer list. Duplicate devices, local/sentinel peers and colliding 64-bit vector identities are refused. Membership changes require a separate management workflow; changing the options cannot silently replace it. A Catalog holds no Store or connection. LocalIndex and PeerIndex return independent snapshots. RecordLocal or RecordLocalChanges assigns local sequence numbers and advances the previous local version vector, with overflow checks. Caller-supplied causal fields must be unset. Batch recording validates all changes together and sorts names once. Deletions require explicit tombstones. Directories and symlink descriptors are preserved as metadata; this API does not create directories or follow symlink targets. ReceiveIndex(CatalogIndex) records a paired device's full Index or delta. The caller must bind Device to the authenticated connection, authorize the folder and take IndexID from its negotiated ClusterConfig. A new generation requires a full reset; subsequent batches must continue the persisted previous sequence. Names and sequence numbers are unique, vectors are sorted/bounded and duplicate counters, invalid flags, unsupported encrypted entries and malformed block layouts are refused. Equal vectors cannot describe different content metadata. Peer announcements do not automatically become local files or download data. Attach(ctx, store, device, name, manifest) verifies downloaded data against the currently recorded block descriptors. Adopt(device, name) promotes a verified, causally newer peer entry into the local advertised index. Concurrent versions return ErrCatalogConflict ; older versions and equal-vector/content mismatches are refused. Both versions remain retained, without a silent winner. This changes the advertised catalog only; working-file application needs its own recovery transaction before a service announces that a workspace has caught up. Commit(ctx, store) verifies retained file data, then publishes state and data under one expected-value guarded ffi/bep/… native revision root. Previous catalog revisions are native parents, so an index reset, rename or deletion keeps earlier complete versions reachable by GC and backups. Payload paths use hashes of device/name identities; .git/config , case-distinct names and paths incompatible with a portable workspace can remain lossless in storage. They are not materialized by this API. No implicit ffi/manifestID pins are created. A stale draft returns ErrCatalogStale . Reload and replan; do not retarget an old operation. An IO error during publication has an unknown outcome: reload and establish the persisted result before retrying. Unchanged commits are no-ops. A fresh local IndexID becomes durable on the first successful commit. The catalog codec rejects duplicate/unknown fields and noncanonical state; limits are 256 paired devices, 256 counters/vector, 65536 entries across all indexes and 16 MiB of catalog metadata. Each commit currently verifies its retained files and native closure; large-project throughput is not established. History retirement and catalog sharding remain future work. Download without holding the Store # DownloadFile(ctx, folder, info, request, DownloadOptions) verifies network blocks into private staging without opening a native Store. Optional ReuseIndex and ReadReuse reuse exact matching local blocks; that callback can open/close a Store briefly. Different block sizes disable reuse. Caller-owned resume data uses the same validated descriptor and range checks as Fetch. The returned StagedFile holds no open file or Store. Always Close() it. Info() returns an independent copy and Stats() reports transfer/reuse/staging. Call Prepare(ctx, store) while briefly owning the Store. It imports an unpinned manifest and rechecks stored bytes against the announced blocks. Attach that manifest to the exact current peer entry, optionally adopt it, then Commit before closing Store or permitting GC. This prevents network waits from holding the native exclusive lock. Revalidate the persisted peer entry after download: it may have advanced while the transfer was in flight. PrepareFetch is a convenience for a caller that already holds a Store; it has the same unpinned ownership rule. Legacy Fetch continues retaining its result independently. Keep these ownership choices explicit; do not remove a shared legacy pin owned by another consumer. Authenticate a device connection # OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, DeviceOptions, model) takes ownership of an unsecured connected net.Conn , including on failure. Supply one local TLS leaf certificate with its matching crypto.Signer private key and the explicitly paired peer's protocol.DeviceID . Server: true selects the accepting side. Self connections, empty/sentinel identities, malformed keys and certificate/key mismatches are rejected. Do not mutate the certificate or key while in use. The connection requires TLS 1.3 or later and bep/1.0 ALPN. It exchanges the bounded upstream Hello before rejecting an unpaired peer, then verifies the SHA-256 identity of the exact single peer certificate. This is certificate pinning: DNS, a Web PKI CA, certificate expiry, discovery and Hello names do not grant membership. PeerHello contains untrusted descriptive information. No application model is attached until verification succeeds. go Copy session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{ Certificate: deviceCertificate, RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID, Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"}, }, folderModel) // Handle err before use. Start is explicit; ClusterConfig must be the first // post-authentication message. The model must authorize every shared folder. session.Start() session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil) // Send a full Index before updates, unless a persisted delta-index agreement // permits otherwise. Fetch can use session.Request for verified block data. HandshakeTimeout defaults to 20 seconds and accepts 100 ms through one minute. It covers TLS and Hello; an earlier context deadline wins. Compression defaults to the reference metadata policy; Never/Always are also accepted. The context controls the complete session lifetime, including an authenticated session that has not been started. Close(reason) releases the stream; wait for the model's Closed callback before closing native Store handles used by its callbacks. The connection metadata contains a stable establishment time and session ID. The application supplies certificate storage/rotation, trusted Device IDs and connection scheduling. The low-level connection accepts an application model; FolderSession supplies the regular-file controller described below. Transport encryption authenticates and protects this peer connection; it does not make an ordinary receiving daemon an untrusted encrypted backup. The complete untrusted-device encryption workflow remains to be integrated. Qualify against the reference daemon # text Copy python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download This optional test helper compiles cmd/syncthing from the exact adapter module version using the checked-in tests/syncthing-reference/go.mod and go.sum . It uses the upstream noassets build tag, which omits the web UI assets while retaining the real daemon, database, scanner, model, puller and REST API. The output is build/qualification/syncthing ( .exe on Windows). Without --download , the helper requires a prepared module cache and makes no implicit module download. It verifies the executable's embedded upstream module version and refuses adapter/reference version mismatches or modified manifests. It also resolves the adapter test dependency graph with its platform cgo setting before offline tests, including Darwin’s native watcher dependency. Set SYRNCK_SYNCTHING_BINARY to that executable's absolute path and run: text Copy python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers On Linux/macOS provide the built shared library path instead. The three-platform CI enables the adapter and this reference test explicitly. The reference daemon test uses fresh test-owned configuration, keys and folder storage, binds only loopback, disables discovery/relays/NAT/upgrades/reporting and terminates its process after the test. It never reads or changes an installed Syncthing profile. Local Windows verification exercised both directions against the unmodified upstream daemon: a 384 KiB asset with moved/reused blocks transferred one missing 128 KiB block in each direction. Reconstruction into encrypted Syrnck storage verified the resulting complete file. Real daemon rename/tombstone announcements were also received. This proves transport and regular-file interoperability; a further real-daemon test persists full/delta indexes and data, closes native Stores during network waits, then reconnects after an offline rename and preserves its tombstone. The production-controller test additionally reconstructs an actual native working folder, returns a post-completion local edit to the daemon, reconnects after an offline rename and propagates a local deletion upstream. Without the explicit binary, live-daemon tests report a skip; ordinary adapter tests still run. Resume and cancellation # Without ResumeDirectory , Fetch uses a private temporary file and removes it when the call returns. With it, the caller owns a per-content staging directory containing state.json and data . Metadata binds the folder/name/size/block size and the hash of the complete announced block list. A different index, unknown file, incomplete descriptor or symlink is refused. Use a private directory and serialize callers managing it. On retry, every staged range is hashed again. Complete matching ranges are reused; partial/corrupt ranges are fetched again. Sparse zero-filled ranges can satisfy matching zero blocks without a request. Progress is flushed every 16 written blocks and before ingestion. Cancellation preserves the caller-owned staging data. The staging file contains plaintext, including when the Store is encrypted; choose an appropriately protected location. Completed staging directories remain caller-owned and require explicit retirement. Context cancellation is checked between native calls and network/block steps. Unpinned preparation propagates cancellation into the native ingestion operation. Legacy retained ingest and complete verification finish their current synchronous native call. A late cancellation or a detected change during ingestion can therefore leave a complete internally retained manifest; its ID is returned alongside the error. Callers must handle the error before publishing that result. No partial working file is installed. A process kill can leave private temporary data; power-loss durability and automatic staging reclamation are not established. Continuous working-folder controller # Create the native Store and workspace first, then call NewFolder(ctx, options) . FolderOptions binds one catalog/local device, its explicit peer list, the working directory, Store and optional registered workspace name. OpenStore returns a newly opened handle owned by the controller: an encrypted integration can obtain its key from the embedding application's provider. No idle Store is retained. Close sessions before closing Folder. Refresh captures local working edits using the native watcher/index and periodic full audits (five minutes by default). Invalidate requests an immediate full audit. ReceiveIndex persists an authenticated paired device's full/delta index. Step downloads one missing file, reuses verified blocks across current local files, then applies the complete candidate through the native owned checkout. Exact-content renames need no payload transfer. Immutable advertised bytes stay servable while a new version downloads; incoming indexes can advance on a separate worker. Every downloaded descriptor/generation is rechecked before attachment, so stale downloads cannot be installed. Incoming application retains three typed roots for its descriptor, native candidate and prepared catalog before touching working files. Protocol publication waits for completed native evidence with this operation's unique durable owner, source, target and selected branch. CheckoutStatusContext uses the core receipt decoder, without reconstructing a private record format in Go or diffing entire historical asset trees. HEAD equality alone is insufficient. A local edit after native completion is captured with the received causal base; it cannot deadlock the acknowledgement or be overwritten. A preparation obstructed before any native journal starts is retired only after checking the exact original native state. Foreign/ambiguous journals remain explicit. The catalog retains previous complete versions through ordinary native revision parents; its pending candidates are also GC roots. Process-termination tests kill a real child before and after native publication, run GC, reopen, recover and verify the incoming bytes or a subsequent local edit, with plain/encrypted Stores. Concurrent protocol versions block by default; the explicit conflict-copy policy retains both contents. Concurrent protocol versions # Set FolderOptions.ConflictCopies: true , or \"conflict_copies\": true in the standalone configuration, to resolve concurrent regular-file vectors. The strict default still returns ErrCatalogConflict ; Catalog.Adopt keeps that behavior. Embedders can explicitly call Catalog.ResolveConcurrent(ctx, store, peer, name) on a draft, then publish it with the normal verified transaction. Its result describes the draft choice, not a working-file receipt. Both live inputs are checked against their announced BEP blocks before choosing a winner. The pinned upstream FileInfo.WinsConflict selects the newer modified time, then orders concurrent vectors on a tie. Locally captured versions use the native capture timestamp. The winner keeps its metadata and receives the union of both vectors. Concurrent equal contents coalesce by the native whole-file hash, independently of chunk boundaries; two tombstones also coalesce. Different losing contents become a tracked sibling containing .sync-conflict- , with a deterministic origin/vector/content identity and the short extension preserved. A losing deletion has no contents to copy; a winning deletion keeps the losing live file in a copy. Copy names reserve existing tracked names and tombstones, including Unicode case/normalization and file/directory prefix collisions. Bounded alternate names avoid replacing an occupied tracked path. Other native namespace or working-file obstructions still block application. Copies follow the workspace's materialization selection; hidden copies remain retained logical files. There is no text or binary content merge. The complete winner, copy and causal choices are frozen in the existing native pending transaction. Recovery applies that candidate even if the option is subsequently disabled. Only its owned completion receipt permits catalog acknowledgement. Both inputs remain reachable through catalog history; copies are ordinary tracked files and are not automatically retired. Syncthing may create its own conflict-copy names when it resolves first. Tests cover symmetric two-controller convergence without repeated history, equal-content coalescing, live/deleted combinations, validation failures and Unicode collisions. Actual process termination before/after publication, GC, verified backup/restore and recovery preserve both inputs in plain/encrypted Stores. Standalone processes also retain the losing contents on both devices against the unmodified reference daemon, with each side winning once. Continuous authenticated sessions # OpenFolderSession(ctx, folder, stream, options) owns the connected transport, checks that the certificate matches the catalog's complete local Device ID, authenticates the configured paired peer, then negotiates folder generations and full/delta indexes. PollInterval defaults to 250 ms (50 ms through one minute). The incoming queue defaults to 32 MiB (1 through 128 MiB), includes the batch being processed and never blocks the BEP reader waiting for native disk work. Reports , Errors and Done expose progress, blocked operations and shutdown. Close stops workers and waits for the transport/model shutdown. The embedding application supplies listening/dialing, certificate/key storage and OS lifecycle. The resident service provides reconnect backoff and independent offline capture. This module is optional; the CMake BEP target packages the standalone executable. The controller currently applies regular files and file tombstones, not directory/symlink metadata, empty-directory lifecycle, platform ownership/xattrs or receive-encrypted folders. It retains incoming metadata but does not claim these paths have been applied. It also lacks persistent download resume, automatic membership changes/history retirement and authored/signature policy for newly synthesized incoming native candidates. Large-project performance and power-loss durability are not qualified. The native resident publication agent is a separate feature. StagedFile.PrepareWithChunking(ctx, store, syrnck.Chunking{}) keeps default FastCDC native chunks independent of BEP SHA-256 boundaries. Legacy Prepare keeps its fixed-BEP chunking. Catalog.CommitWithRefs atomically publishes its catalog with additional typed reference guards; callers must reload after an unknown publication outcome, as with the native CAS. An external writer can change a file during active native installation. Default recovery stays strict. Set FolderOptions.PreserveCheckoutEdits = true to retain regular-file edits as described below. File visibility alone is not a completion notification: use the controller checkpoint/status. An edit after the native receipt has committed is supported independently of this option. The controller maps native filename spelling to canonical NFC protocol names. It preserves an existing decomposed native spelling when applying edits and rejects two native names that normalize to the same protocol path. An echo or rescan cannot turn that spelling difference into a deletion. Preserve edits during active checkout # The explicit PreserveCheckoutEdits option is frozen in each prepared application; changing future configuration cannot retarget an interrupted operation. Recovery checks the exact owner, native target, context, directory and private staged/backup files. It ingests changed regular files into native archive revisions before moving any working bytes. Exclusive conflict-copy moves and exclusive restoration of the expected intermediate bytes are replayed from retained descriptors. The original strict checkout then rechecks all target files and publishes its unchanged candidate and receipt. Archive roots use archive/checkout-edits/TRANSACTION/EDIT/state (descriptor) and /work (revision). Their native parents retain the incoming revision and its complete file closure. Additional archive/checkout-edit-versions/TRANSACTION/REVISION roots retain changes to a conflict copy during recovery. Copies are deterministic from each retained edit identity, preserve short extensions and are never overwritten. A file that appears at a copy name is kept; remaining original edits get a fresh identity. The report's preserved_edits lists source paths, copy paths and archived revision IDs; a copy's current bytes may be newer than that archived revision. After acknowledgement, normal local capture advertises the preserved files. This protects file-content edits before backup, during staging and after file installation, including an otherwise unchanged tracked file. Prepared signed candidates remain unchanged; recovery needs no signing private key. Archives remain GC roots after workspace changes. Automatic retirement of these groups still needs an administrative policy; do not remove their roots independently. An actively changing editor can defer completion: each bounded attempt keeps its observations for the next retry. Missing-file/deletion intent, directory or symlink obstructions and ambiguous namespace changes still block rather than inventing a resolution. This option does not resolve concurrent BEP version vectors, implement a complete folder service or establish power-loss safety. If the original checkout must remove or rename a parent directory, preservation places the copy outside that directory while keeping the source basename and short extension. This also covers directory-to-file and directory case changes. Resident reconnection service # OpenFolderService(ctx, folder, options) runs one explicitly paired peer across connection loss. Supply FolderServiceOptions.Session with the device certificate, paired remote ID and session budgets, plus Connect(ctx) returning a connected net.Conn . The factory can dial or accept, must honor cancellation, and transfers ownership of any returned connection, including alongside an error. TLS/BEP peer authentication always runs after the factory; it cannot authorize a device. The service observes/captures local changes independently of connection progress, including offline edits, deletion/rename and an authenticated peer that never sends its folder configuration. OfflineInterval defaults to 250 ms (50 ms through one minute). It also runs during downloads: the native folder controller serializes state and rejects stale candidates without holding Store during network waits. Ordinary full audit scheduling remains enabled. RetryMin defaults to 500 ms and RetryMax to 30 seconds. Failed connections use exponential backoff within configured bounds (50 ms through one minute); a successful authenticated connection resets it. Each reconnect sends a fresh Hello timestamp by default and resumes durable native/catalog state. No discovery or implicit pairing is introduced. Events reports connecting, connected and retrying; connected means certificate authentication, not completed folder application. Reports and Errors expose folder progress and failures. All three bounded streams are advisory and may drop messages under consumer backpressure; reload durable state for receipts. Close cancels/joins dialing, TLS and session workers, including a transport completing after cancellation, then closes streams and Done . Stop the service before closing its caller-owned Folder. It keeps no idle Store. Storage-key lifetime belongs to the Folder’s application-supplied Store provider; keep the device signing key immutable until shutdown. Close returns any session teardown error. Tests cover plain/encrypted two-workspace offline capture, TCP reconnection, offline rename/tombstone convergence, failed factory transport cleanup, stalled TLS cancellation and local capture while an authenticated peer withholds folder configuration. Existing session tests separately qualify the pinned upstream daemon. The application still supplies network listening/dialing, certificates, key storage and OS lifecycle. OS service packaging and complete directory/symlink semantics remain unfinished. Standalone foreground executable # The optional syrnck-bep executable runs the native-backed resident service for one existing workspace and one explicitly paired peer. It captures offline changes, authenticates the peer, reconnects after failures and applies guarded regular-file versions. It uses no Git process and requires no Go installation at runtime. The C++ engine remains the storage/revision implementation. text Copy syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem syrnck-bep run --config service.json syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json Provision a distinct device certificate/private key externally and configure its printed full Device ID on the peer. A Syncthing generate identity can be used; reuse its certificate and matching key together. Self-connections are refused. The CLI imports identities; generation, rotation and OS vault integration remain separate work. Both sides must share the same folder ID. Copy the installed share/Syrnck/examples/bep-service.json into your configuration directory and replace its paths, peer ID and address. The example is also in the source examples directory. Initialize the workspace Store with the main syrnck CLI first. Relative paths resolve against the configuration file. The Store defaults to directory/.syrnck ; workspace can select an existing linked context. mode: dial connects to address ; listen accepts there instead. An explicit host:port is required. Each process owns its transport and Folder. The PEM private key must be outside the working tree or inside its protected Store, including after resolving links. It must not become source content. key_env names the storage key environment variable (default SYRNCK_KEY ): absent means no supplied key; otherwise its value must be 64 hexadecimal characters matching the existing Store. No raw key belongs in JSON or arguments. TLS identity and at-rest encryption keys have different roles. The PEM file stays under its filesystem permissions; Store encryption protects stored objects. Configuration version 1 rejects duplicate/unknown fields and inputs over 64 KiB. poll_ms is 50..60000 (zero selects 250); retry bounds default to 500 and 30000 ms. use_permissions , preserve_checkout_edits and conflict_copies default false. The example explicitly enables active-file preservation and concurrent-version copies . These protect different situations and can be configured independently. Stop with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM. Restart with the same identity/configuration to resume retained native/catalog state. Standard output is canonical JSON; ready means local initialization/listener setup, not remote synchronization. Human help and CLI-owned diagnostics use English/French; --lang overrides SYRNCK_LANG , then English. Technical OS/library details retain their language. The main CLI opens this page with syrnck help syncthing --section standalone-service . OS service installation, multiple-peer orchestration and full directory/symlink semantics remain unfinished. Build and distribute the executable # Enable -DSYRNCK_BUILD_BEP_SERVICE=ON in CMake (default on when Syncthing tests are enabled). Build syrnck_ffi first. With Go 1.26.2+ available, prepare the pinned public dependencies once: text Copy python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library NATIVE_LIBRARY --output BEP_EXECUTABLE Use build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll / build/Release/syrnck-bep.exe on the Windows preset, build/libsyrnck_ffi.so / build/syrnck-bep on Linux, and build/libsyrnck_ffi.dylib / build/syrnck-bep on macOS. Then build normally; CMake invokes the same helper offline. Unix requires cgo and the matching native library. This target uses a separate nonsanitized build from core ASan checks. cmake --install build --config Release --prefix dist installs the executable, matching native library and local manual. It also retains the actual runtime Go modules’ unmodified source archives, licenses/notices, Go runtime license and hash manifest under share/licenses/Syrnck/bep . Keep them with the distribution; the project MIT license does not relicense dependencies. Local Windows tests run actual standalone processes against the pinned upstream daemon in plaintext/encrypted Stores, with both-direction edits and an upstream offline rename across process restart. Separate hard-termination tests reconstruct both old and new native versions. Linux/macOS executable and relocation checks remain subject to their CI results; the source configuration alone is not proof. view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0) // Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model. result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo, connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{ ReuseManifest: previousManifest, ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory, }) // Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision. session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{ Certificate: deviceCertificate, RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID, Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"}, }, folderModel) // Handle err before use. Start is explicit; ClusterConfig must be the first // post-authentication message. The model must authorize every shared folder. session.Start() session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil) // Send a full Index before updates, unless a persisted delta-index agreement // permits otherwise. Fetch can use session.Request for verified block data. python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem syrnck-bep run --config service.json syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library NATIVE_LIBRARY --output BEP_EXECUTABLE","headings":[{"id":"syncthing-fixed-block-adapter","text":"Syncthing fixed-block adapter","level":1},{"id":"build-and-test","text":"Native library and dependencies","level":2},{"id":"apis","text":"APIs","level":2},{"id":"folder-catalog","text":"Durable folder catalog","level":2},{"id":"staged-download","text":"Download without holding the Store","level":2},{"id":"device-connections","text":"Authenticate a device connection","level":2},{"id":"reference-daemon","text":"Qualify against the reference daemon","level":2},{"id":"resume-and-cancellation","text":"Resume and cancellation","level":2},{"id":"folder-controller","text":"Continuous working-folder controller","level":2},{"id":"protocol-conflicts","text":"Concurrent protocol versions","level":2},{"id":"folder-sessions","text":"Continuous authenticated sessions","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Preserve edits during active checkout","level":2},{"id":"folder-service","text":"Resident reconnection service","level":2},{"id":"standalone-service","text":"Standalone foreground executable","level":2},{"id":"build-standalone-service","text":"Build and distribute the executable","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-6-L1"],"languages":["go","text"],"code":"view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0)\n// Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model.\nresult, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo,\n    connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{\n        ReuseManifest: previousManifest,\n        ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory,\n    })\n// Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision.\nsession, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{\n    Certificate: deviceCertificate,\n    RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID,\n    Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"},\n}, folderModel)\n// Handle err before use. Start is explicit; ClusterConfig must be the first\n// post-authentication message. The model must authorize every shared folder.\nsession.Start()\nsession.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil)\n// Send a full Index before updates, unless a persisted delta-index agreement\n// permits otherwise. Fetch can use session.Request for verified block data.\npython scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download\npython scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers\nsyrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem\nsyrnck-bep run --config service.json\nsyrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json\npython scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library NATIVE_LIBRARY --output BEP_EXECUTABLE","assets":[],"links":["#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts","#/en/syncthing/folder-service","#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/en/syncthing/protocol-conflicts"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"text-diff","title":"Bounded text differences","description":"Bounded text differences guide.","section":"Version control","order":550,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-text-differences\">Bounded text differences\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-diff/bounded-text-differences\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounded text differences\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The independent C++ \u003ccode>compare_text(source_bytes, target_bytes, options, operation)\u003c/code>\nreturns deterministic line changes without writing objects, references or files.\nIt compares bytes exactly: whitespace, CRLF/LF and the final newline are significant.\nUTF-8 is required. Invalid encodings, NUL and control characters other than tab,\nLF and CR immediately before LF are reported as \u003ccode>status: binary\u003c/code>; no text hunks\nare emitted. UTF-16 and arbitrary assets are not decoded automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>status: text\u003c/code> has \u003ccode>equal\u003c/code>, source/target byte and line counts, total \u003ccode>additions\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>deletions\u003c/code>, and ordered \u003ccode>hunks\u003c/code>. Each hunk has source/target start/count and\n\u003ccode>lines\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>kind\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>context\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>add\u003c/code>), \u003ccode>text\u003c/code> (without LF) and \u003ccode>newline\u003c/code>.\nCR in a CRLF line stays in \u003ccode>text\u003c/code>. Starts are one-based, except a zero-count range\nuses the number of preceding lines, matching unified diff conventions. Empty\nfiles have zero lines. No trailing empty line is invented after a final LF.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The engine removes a common prefix/suffix and computes a shortest line edit\nscript with bounded Myers frontiers. Equal alternatives use a deterministic\ntie-break. Repeated lines need not align like another diff implementation. This\nis a line diff, not word highlighting, rename detection or automatic merging.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Default limits, adjustable through \u003ccode>TextDiffOptions\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Budget\u003c/th>\n\u003cth align=\"right\">Default\u003c/th>\n\u003cth align=\"right\">Maximum\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Context lines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">100\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Bytes per input file\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">1 MiB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">4 MiB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Lines per input file\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">50,000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">100,000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Work units\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">16,777,216\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">268,435,456\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Serialized JSON output\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">4 MiB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">8 MiB\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Retained frontier trace\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">1,048,576 integers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">Fixed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Work charges a unit for each diagonal/backtracking step and conservatively\ncharges the line byte length plus one for an equal-length line comparison.\nInput scanning, UTF-8 validation, splitting and output construction are also\nbounded by their respective byte/line/output limits. Frontier storage is about\n4 MiB on supported targets; line views, edit records, JSON, input buffers and\nallocator/container overhead are additional. These limits are not a total RSS cap.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An exceeded budget returns \u003ccode>status: limited\u003c/code>, an explicit \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>file_limit\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>line_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>work_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>trace_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>output_limit\u003c/code>) and no partial hunks or\nedit counts. \u003ccode>equal\u003c/code> is null if the input byte limit prevented comparison;\notherwise it describes byte equality, even if a later budget is exceeded.\nBinary results also have no line/edit counts. Invalid option values throw.\nCancellation and observer exceptions propagate, rather than becoming a limited\nor successful diff. Progress callbacks use the calling thread and must not\nreenter the Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-a-native-file-between-revisions\">Compare a native file between revisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-diff/compare-a-native-file-between-revisions\" aria-label=\"Link to Compare a native file between revisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Selectors follow the usual native HEAD/branch/tag rules. The C++ equivalent is\n\u003ccode>store.diff_revision_file(source_id, target_id, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code>; either\noptional revision can be null to represent an empty snapshot. The path must be\nan exact safe repository path, present on at least one side. Absence represents\nan added/removed file, independently of whether its content is empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The JSON envelope contains \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target_revision\u003c/code>, nullable\n\u003ccode>source\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>target\u003c/code> file summaries (manifest, size, file hash and executable flag),\n\u003ccode>equal\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>content_changed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>executable_changed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>manifest_changed\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, and the nested \u003ccode>diff\u003c/code> result described above. Envelope\n\u003ccode>equal\u003c/code> compares presence, logical content and executable mode. Nested\n\u003ccode>diff.equal\u003c/code> compares bytes only: an empty-file addition has envelope \u003ccode>equal: false\u003c/code> and nested \u003ccode>equal: true\u003c/code>. Different chunking can change a manifest while\npreserving logical equality.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both file sizes are checked before reading either file&#39;s chunks. A \u003ccode>file_limit\u003c/code>\nreport is metadata-only and has \u003ccode>objects_verified: false\u003c/code>. Otherwise, both\ncomplete files are reconstructed and their whole-file hashes are verified,\nincluding for binary/equal files or later line/work/output limits. Missing or\ncorrupt payloads fail the operation rather than yielding a patch. Only this\npath&#39;s payloads are read; unrelated files and revision parents are not traversed.\nThe wrapper report is additionally capped at 16 MiB, including metadata/path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Default output is canonical JSON independent of the interface language. A\nsuccessful JSON inspection exits 0, including explicit binary/limited reports.\n\u003ccode>--unified\u003c/code> renders text hunks with \u003ccode>a/\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>b/\u003c/code> paths, \u003ccode>/dev/null\u003c/code> for a missing\nside and exact missing-final-newline markers. It fails before stdout output for\nbinary/limited input. An empty hunk list produces empty unified output.\nMode-only changes, alternate manifests and empty-file creation/removal require\nthe JSON metadata; unified output represents text content only. It is not a\ncomplete native revision patch or a native patch-application command.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--context 0..100\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> are supported. All modes\naccept \u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code>,\nusing the defaults and hard maxima in the table above. Options apply to this\ninvocation only; an exceeded runtime budget remains an explicit limited result.\nInvalid limits fail before inspection. Owned errors/help are available\nin English and French.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-frozen-staged-changes\">Inspect frozen staged changes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-diff/inspect-frozen-staged-changes\" aria-label=\"Link to Inspect frozen staged changes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>store.diff_staged_file(branch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code> compares that path in\nthe staging index to its original baseline. Without an index, it compares the\nhead against itself. An initially unborn index has an empty source snapshot.\nStale indexes keep their original base: the engine never substitutes the current\nhead or reads the working directory. Later unsaved/unprepared changes do not\naffect the result. Prepared deletions use a missing target.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The envelope adds \u003ccode>comparison: staged\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, nullable \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>stale\u003c/code>. The index&#39;s internal branch marker, timestamp and parent\nshape are validated before comparison, using the ordinary staging reader.\nBranch selection defaults to native HEAD in the CLI; \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> overrides it.\nOptions, integrity checks, JSON/unified behavior and cancellation follow the\nrevision comparison contract above. The command does not consume/rebase the\nindex, alter references or read working files. A path absent from both frozen\nsnapshots is an error, even if a working file now exists there.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Diff commands are read-only. \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">Numbered hunk selection\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-patches\">strict external patches\u003c/a> prepare frozen changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-a-working-file\">Compare a working file\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-diff/compare-a-working-file\" aria-label=\"Link to Compare a working file\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The default source is the selected branch&#39;s frozen index, or its head when no\nindex exists. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> overrides branch selection. A stale index remains the\nsource, with its original base and current head reported. \u003ccode>--revision SELECTOR\u003c/code>\ninstead chooses an explicit committed source; it cannot be combined with\n\u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--working\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--staged\u003c/code> are mutually exclusive. An unborn branch\nwithout an index compares against an empty snapshot. Working deletions and\nuntracked/empty additions are explicit in JSON.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ entry points:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> explicit_base \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">diff_working_file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source/Player.cpp\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> limits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> unstaged \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">diff_unstaged_file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source/Player.cpp\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> limits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Only ancestor directories are enumerated to resolve the exact path, detect\nportable case collisions and apply the existing nested ignore rules. Unrelated\nsubdirectories and their file contents are not traversed. Ignored paths are\nerrors, including tracked paths and missing ignored names; they are never\nsilently reported as deletions. Links/reparse points, case aliases, directory/\nfile obstructions and the protected Store are refused. The Store must be below\nthe selected workspace. The CLI also checks native HEAD&#39;s workspace identity.\nPaths use the filesystem&#39;s actual UTF-8 spelling, including Unicode normalization.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Inspection has a 128-component depth bound and a 16 MiB budget for enumerated\nnames and ignore-file bytes per pass. Bounded ignore parsing also limits rules\nto 16,384 entries of at most 4,096 bytes. Glob semantics match native staging;\nthe limits apply only to this targeted inspector. Ordinary snapshot scanning\nkeeps its existing policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both source/working sizes are checked before payload reads. Within budget, the\ncomplete source is reconstructed and verified, and the working file is read\nthrough a 64 KiB buffer with growth/shrink checks. Namespace, size, timestamp\nand available mode are rechecked around the read. Detected changes fail with\na retry diagnostic. This is an observed working-file read, not a filesystem\nsnapshot or protection against a hostile writer that restores timestamps or\nswaps paths between checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The envelope has \u003ccode>comparison: working\u003c/code>, canonical \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_revision\u003c/code>\nand a null \u003ccode>target_revision\u003c/code>. Working \u003ccode>target.manifest\u003c/code> is null; \u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code> is\ncomputed only when payloads are read. \u003ccode>workspace_verified\u003c/code> records that the\nworking read/missing-path checks completed; \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code> records source\npayload verification. Both are false on a file-size limit. Size/presence can\nprove \u003ccode>content_changed\u003c/code>; otherwise \u003ccode>content_changed\u003c/code> and envelope \u003ccode>equal\u003c/code> stay\nnull when size limits prevented a content comparison. No equality is invented.\n\u003ccode>manifest_changed\u003c/code> is null because a working file has no stored representation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On Windows, \u003ccode>target.executable\u003c/code> is null, \u003ccode>executable_comparable\u003c/code> is false, and\nmode does not affect envelope equality. On Unix, executable mode is compared.\nBranch-based results additionally include \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>stale\u003c/code>. JSON/unified output, cancellation and binary classification follow the\nearlier contracts. The command imports no objects and changes no working files,\nreferences or staging state.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Bounded text differences # The independent C++ compare_text(source_bytes, target_bytes, options, operation) returns deterministic line changes without writing objects, references or files. It compares bytes exactly: whitespace, CRLF/LF and the final newline are significant. UTF-8 is required. Invalid encodings, NUL and control characters other than tab, LF and CR immediately before LF are reported as status: binary ; no text hunks are emitted. UTF-16 and arbitrary assets are not decoded automatically. status: text has equal , source/target byte and line counts, total additions and deletions , and ordered hunks . Each hunk has source/target start/count and lines with kind ( context , remove , add ), text (without LF) and newline . CR in a CRLF line stays in text . Starts are one-based, except a zero-count range uses the number of preceding lines, matching unified diff conventions. Empty files have zero lines. No trailing empty line is invented after a final LF. The engine removes a common prefix/suffix and computes a shortest line edit script with bounded Myers frontiers. Equal alternatives use a deterministic tie-break. Repeated lines need not align like another diff implementation. This is a line diff, not word highlighting, rename detection or automatic merging. Default limits, adjustable through TextDiffOptions : Budget Default Maximum Context lines 3 100 Bytes per input file 1 MiB 4 MiB Lines per input file 50,000 100,000 Work units 16,777,216 268,435,456 Serialized JSON output 4 MiB 8 MiB Retained frontier trace 1,048,576 integers Fixed Work charges a unit for each diagonal/backtracking step and conservatively charges the line byte length plus one for an equal-length line comparison. Input scanning, UTF-8 validation, splitting and output construction are also bounded by their respective byte/line/output limits. Frontier storage is about 4 MiB on supported targets; line views, edit records, JSON, input buffers and allocator/container overhead are additional. These limits are not a total RSS cap. An exceeded budget returns status: limited , an explicit reason ( file_limit , line_limit , work_limit , trace_limit , output_limit ) and no partial hunks or edit counts. equal is null if the input byte limit prevented comparison; otherwise it describes byte equality, even if a later budget is exceeded. Binary results also have no line/edit counts. Invalid option values throw. Cancellation and observer exceptions propagate, rather than becoming a limited or successful diff. Progress callbacks use the calling thread and must not reenter the Store. Compare a native file between revisions # text Copy syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified Selectors follow the usual native HEAD/branch/tag rules. The C++ equivalent is store.diff_revision_file(source_id, target_id, path, limits, operation) ; either optional revision can be null to represent an empty snapshot. The path must be an exact safe repository path, present on at least one side. Absence represents an added/removed file, independently of whether its content is empty. The JSON envelope contains path , source_revision , target_revision , nullable source / target file summaries (manifest, size, file hash and executable flag), equal , content_changed , executable_changed , manifest_changed , objects_verified , and the nested diff result described above. Envelope equal compares presence, logical content and executable mode. Nested diff.equal compares bytes only: an empty-file addition has envelope equal: false and nested equal: true . Different chunking can change a manifest while preserving logical equality. Both file sizes are checked before reading either file's chunks. A file_limit report is metadata-only and has objects_verified: false . Otherwise, both complete files are reconstructed and their whole-file hashes are verified, including for binary/equal files or later line/work/output limits. Missing or corrupt payloads fail the operation rather than yielding a patch. Only this path's payloads are read; unrelated files and revision parents are not traversed. The wrapper report is additionally capped at 16 MiB, including metadata/path. Default output is canonical JSON independent of the interface language. A successful JSON inspection exits 0, including explicit binary/limited reports. --unified renders text hunks with a/ / b/ paths, /dev/null for a missing side and exact missing-final-newline markers. It fails before stdout output for binary/limited input. An empty hunk list produces empty unified output. Mode-only changes, alternate manifests and empty-file creation/removal require the JSON metadata; unified output represents text content only. It is not a complete native revision patch or a native patch-application command. --context 0..100 , --progress and --progress-json are supported. All modes accept --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes , using the defaults and hard maxima in the table above. Options apply to this invocation only; an exceeded runtime budget remains an explicit limited result. Invalid limits fail before inspection. Owned errors/help are available in English and French. Inspect frozen staged changes # text Copy syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified store.diff_staged_file(branch, path, limits, operation) compares that path in the staging index to its original baseline. Without an index, it compares the head against itself. An initially unborn index has an empty source snapshot. Stale indexes keep their original base: the engine never substitutes the current head or reads the working directory. Later unsaved/unprepared changes do not affect the result. Prepared deletions use a missing target. The envelope adds comparison: staged , branch , nullable base , head and index , and stale . The index's internal branch marker, timestamp and parent shape are validated before comparison, using the ordinary staging reader. Branch selection defaults to native HEAD in the CLI; --branch overrides it. Options, integrity checks, JSON/unified behavior and cancellation follow the revision comparison contract above. The command does not consume/rebase the index, alter references or read working files. A path absent from both frozen snapshots is an error, even if a working file now exists there. Diff commands are read-only. Numbered hunk selection and strict external patches prepare frozen changes. Compare a working file # text Copy syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory . The default source is the selected branch's frozen index, or its head when no index exists. --branch overrides branch selection. A stale index remains the source, with its original base and current head reported. --revision SELECTOR instead chooses an explicit committed source; it cannot be combined with --branch . --working and --staged are mutually exclusive. An unborn branch without an index compares against an empty snapshot. Working deletions and untracked/empty additions are explicit in JSON. C++ entry points: cpp Copy auto explicit_base = store . diff_working_file ( project , revision_id , \"Source/Player.cpp\" , limits , operation ) ; auto unstaged = store . diff_unstaged_file ( project , \"feature\" , \"Source/Player.cpp\" , limits , operation ) ; Only ancestor directories are enumerated to resolve the exact path, detect portable case collisions and apply the existing nested ignore rules. Unrelated subdirectories and their file contents are not traversed. Ignored paths are errors, including tracked paths and missing ignored names; they are never silently reported as deletions. Links/reparse points, case aliases, directory/ file obstructions and the protected Store are refused. The Store must be below the selected workspace. The CLI also checks native HEAD's workspace identity. Paths use the filesystem's actual UTF-8 spelling, including Unicode normalization. Inspection has a 128-component depth bound and a 16 MiB budget for enumerated names and ignore-file bytes per pass. Bounded ignore parsing also limits rules to 16,384 entries of at most 4,096 bytes. Glob semantics match native staging; the limits apply only to this targeted inspector. Ordinary snapshot scanning keeps its existing policy. Both source/working sizes are checked before payload reads. Within budget, the complete source is reconstructed and verified, and the working file is read through a 64 KiB buffer with growth/shrink checks. Namespace, size, timestamp and available mode are rechecked around the read. Detected changes fail with a retry diagnostic. This is an observed working-file read, not a filesystem snapshot or protection against a hostile writer that restores timestamps or swaps paths between checks. The envelope has comparison: working , canonical directory , source_revision and a null target_revision . Working target.manifest is null; file_hash is computed only when payloads are read. workspace_verified records that the working read/missing-path checks completed; objects_verified records source payload verification. Both are false on a file-size limit. Size/presence can prove content_changed ; otherwise content_changed and envelope equal stay null when size limits prevented a content comparison. No equality is invented. manifest_changed is null because a working file has no stored representation. On Windows, target.executable is null, executable_comparable is false, and mode does not affect envelope equality. On Unix, executable mode is compared. Branch-based results additionally include branch , head , base , index and stale . JSON/unified output, cancellation and binary classification follow the earlier contracts. The command imports no objects and changes no working files, references or staging state. syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory . auto explicit_base = store.diff_working_file(project, revision_id, \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation); auto unstaged = store.diff_unstaged_file(project, \"feature\", \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);","headings":[{"id":"bounded-text-differences","text":"Bounded text differences","level":1},{"id":"compare-a-native-file-between-revisions","text":"Compare a native file between revisions","level":2},{"id":"inspect-frozen-staged-changes","text":"Inspect frozen staged changes","level":2},{"id":"compare-a-working-file","text":"Compare a working file","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory .\nauto explicit_base = store.diff_working_file(project, revision_id, \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);\nauto unstaged = store.diff_unstaged_file(project, \"feature\", \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-hunks","#/en/text-patches"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"text-merge","title":"Bounded three-way text merge","description":"Bounded three-way text merge guide.","section":"Version control","order":560,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-three-way-text-merge\">Bounded three-way text merge\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/bounded-three-way-text-merge\" aria-label=\"Link to Bounded three-way text merge\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The C++ core exposes \u003ccode>merge_text(base, current, incoming, limits, operation)\u003c/code>.\nIt compares both versions against the same base and composes independent line\nedits. Identical changes on both sides are emitted once. The result is conservative:\noverlapping changes with different results remain conflicts, including an insertion\nat the boundary of a replacement. There is no character-level merge or rename\ninference.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"result\">Result\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/result\" aria-label=\"Link to Result\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The JSON report contains \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, input byte and line counts,\n\u003ccode>current_changes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>incoming_changes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>conflicts\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>content\u003c/code>.\nChange counts refer to contiguous edit groups against the base.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Content\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact merged UTF-8 string, including the empty string\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>All changes compose\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>conflict\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>null\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>At least one overlapping group differs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>binary\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>null\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An input is invalid UTF-8 or contains disallowed controls\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>limited\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>null\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An input, result, search, trace or output budget was exceeded\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Each conflict contains \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code> ranges with \u003ccode>start\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>count\u003c/code>. Nonempty ranges use one-based line numbers. An empty range names the\npreceding line, including zero at the beginning of a file. Ranges account for\nearlier insertions and deletions on each side. A connected set of overlapping\nchanges produces one conflict. Conflict rows contain coordinates, not marker text.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>LF, CRLF, mixed accepted line endings and a missing final LF are preserved byte\nfor byte. No newline or conflict marker is invented. Binary inputs are rejected\neven when all three inputs are equal. A limited result clears all conflict rows,\nchange counts and content rather than returning a partial composition.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"budgets-and-cancellation\">Budgets and cancellation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/budgets-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to Budgets and cancellation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The operation uses \u003ccode>TextDiffOptions\u003c/code>: by default 1 MiB per input/result, 50,000\nlines per input/result, 16 Mi work units and 4 MiB of serialized output. The hard\nceilings match text diff: 4 MiB, 100,000 lines, 256 Mi work units and 8 MiB.\n\u003ccode>context\u003c/code> is validated but does not affect merge decisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both Myers searches and composition share one work budget. The existing bounded\nMyers trace is reused; it has a 4 MiB ceiling per search. Line tables, change\ngroups and the result remain bounded by the file and line limits. These limits\nare not a total process-memory promise. The complete serialized merge report,\nincluding JSON escaping of clean content, must fit the output budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>reason\u003c/code> identifies the exhausted budget (\u003ccode>file_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>line_limit\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>work_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>trace_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>output_limit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_file_limit\u003c/code> or\n\u003ccode>result_line_limit\u003c/code>). An assembly that would join unterminated lines is refused\nwith \u003ccode>unterminated_line_join\u003c/code>. Cancellation and observer exceptions propagate;\nthe function does not write objects or files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"merge-one-stored-path\">Merge one stored path\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/merge-one-stored-path\" aria-label=\"Link to Merge one stored path\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>store.merge_revision_file(base_id, current_id, incoming_id, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code> previews the same composition using three explicit native snapshots.\nIt does not select a graph merge base. The caller supplies that base, which can\ncome from a native merge/replay plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The envelope contains the three revision IDs, nullable \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>current\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>incoming\u003c/code> file summaries (manifest, size, file hash and executable flag),\n\u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>result_executable\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>merge\u003c/code>.\nOnly a clean result is ready and supplies a result mode. Executable bits merge\nindependently: an unchanged current mode takes the incoming mode, otherwise the\ncurrent mode is retained. Different manifest representations do not change\nlogical text equality.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All three paths must exist. An absent path produces \u003ccode>file_presence\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>merge: null\u003c/code>, so deletion is never confused with empty content. All three\nsizes are checked before any chunks are read; oversized input produces\n\u003ccode>file_limit\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>merge: null\u003c/code>. Both cases have \u003ccode>objects_verified: false\u003c/code>.\nOtherwise the complete requested files and their whole-file hashes are verified,\nand \u003ccode>merge\u003c/code> contains the core report. Binary, conflict and later budget results\nremain non-ready, with the core reason copied into \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. Missing or corrupt\npayloads throw rather than becoming a merge result.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Verification covers the requested files, not other paths or revision ancestors.\nThe operation does not read the working directory, write objects or update\nreferences. The envelope has a separate 16 MiB hard ceiling; the nested core\nreport obeys \u003ccode>max_output_bytes\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\">CLI and reviewed conflict resolution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\" aria-label=\"Link to CLI and reviewed conflict resolution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Selectors use the native HEAD/branch/tag rules. The default output is the JSON\nenvelope; exit status is zero only when \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> is true. A blocked JSON preview\nexits 1 and still writes its complete report to stdout. \u003ccode>--text\u003c/code> writes only\nclean merged bytes, without a trailing newline added by the CLI. On conflicts,\nbinary input, absence or budget limits it fails with no content on stdout.\nDiagnostics and help use English by default, or French with \u003ccode>--lang fr\u003c/code>.\nJSON fields and merged bytes are language independent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--max-file-bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-lines\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-work\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-output-bytes\u003c/code> override\nthe normal text budgets. \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> report on stderr;\nthe operation supports the existing cooperative CLI cancellation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This command is an explicit preview. Native \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\">merge\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition\">cherry-pick/revert\u003c/a> can also\nenable bounded automatic text composition. Rebase still uses its existing\nfile-level decisions. A reviewed clean text result can be applied through a\ncustom file resolution:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Run the native operation&#39;s plan and retain its exact \u003ccode>resolution_context\u003c/code>.\nFor a merge, pass its \u003ccode>base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>source\u003c/code> IDs to \u003ccode>merge-text\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect the JSON report, including \u003ccode>result_executable\u003c/code>. Save clean \u003ccode>--text\u003c/code>\noutput to a temporary file outside the tracked workspace, using a tool that\npreserves stdout bytes. Older shells can transcode redirected native output.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Import that file with \u003ccode>syrnck put FILE\u003c/code>. If GC can run before application,\nretain its manifest with a pin, as described in\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-resolutions/choices\">native conflict resolutions\u003c/a>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a \u003ccode>file\u003c/code> choice using that manifest and the reviewed executable mode to\nthe original resolution context. Preview again with \u003ccode>--resolutions\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>, then apply with the same exact head and resolution document.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>The normal publication checks, recoverable checkout journal and archive roots\nthen protect the resulting commit and both parents. Other unresolved conflicts\nstill block publication. The text preview does not bind or publish a branch\ntransaction by itself. Root/add/delete policy remains operation-specific. \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation\">Virtual preparation\u003c/a> supports successive rebase text composition.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-file-preparation\">Virtual file preparation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation\" aria-label=\"Link to Virtual file preparation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The internal native text batch prepares candidate manifest IDs in memory using\nthe production default CDC boundaries and v1 manifest encoding. It retains clean\ngenerated bytes until application. It writes neither chunks nor manifests during\nplanning. Materialization uses the normal configured ingestion pipeline and\nrequires the resulting manifest ID to match the predicted ID before a revision\ncan use it. Existing representations are reused when the merged logical content\nalready matches one of the inputs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A batch attempts at most 64 content conflicts and charges at most 16 MiB for the\nsum of verified input bytes and clean result bytes. These aggregate ceilings are\nin addition to each file&#39;s text limits. Files outside a budget remain unresolved\nwith \u003ccode>file_count_limit\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>total_bytes_limit\u003c/code>; no text result is guessed. Reports\ncontain status, ranges and target metadata, never generated plaintext.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Bounded three-way text merge # The C++ core exposes merge_text(base, current, incoming, limits, operation) . It compares both versions against the same base and composes independent line edits. Identical changes on both sides are emitted once. The result is conservative: overlapping changes with different results remain conflicts, including an insertion at the boundary of a replacement. There is no character-level merge or rename inference. Result # The JSON report contains version: 1 , input byte and line counts, current_changes , incoming_changes , status , reason , conflicts and content . Change counts refer to contiguous edit groups against the base. Status Content Meaning clean Exact merged UTF-8 string, including the empty string All changes compose conflict null At least one overlapping group differs binary null An input is invalid UTF-8 or contains disallowed controls limited null An input, result, search, trace or output budget was exceeded Each conflict contains base , current and incoming ranges with start and count . Nonempty ranges use one-based line numbers. An empty range names the preceding line, including zero at the beginning of a file. Ranges account for earlier insertions and deletions on each side. A connected set of overlapping changes produces one conflict. Conflict rows contain coordinates, not marker text. LF, CRLF, mixed accepted line endings and a missing final LF are preserved byte for byte. No newline or conflict marker is invented. Binary inputs are rejected even when all three inputs are equal. A limited result clears all conflict rows, change counts and content rather than returning a partial composition. Budgets and cancellation # The operation uses TextDiffOptions : by default 1 MiB per input/result, 50,000 lines per input/result, 16 Mi work units and 4 MiB of serialized output. The hard ceilings match text diff: 4 MiB, 100,000 lines, 256 Mi work units and 8 MiB. context is validated but does not affect merge decisions. Both Myers searches and composition share one work budget. The existing bounded Myers trace is reused; it has a 4 MiB ceiling per search. Line tables, change groups and the result remain bounded by the file and line limits. These limits are not a total process-memory promise. The complete serialized merge report, including JSON escaping of clean content, must fit the output budget. reason identifies the exhausted budget ( file_limit , line_limit , work_limit , trace_limit , output_limit , result_file_limit or result_line_limit ). An assembly that would join unterminated lines is refused with unterminated_line_join . Cancellation and observer exceptions propagate; the function does not write objects or files. Merge one stored path # store.merge_revision_file(base_id, current_id, incoming_id, path, limits, operation) previews the same composition using three explicit native snapshots. It does not select a graph merge base. The caller supplies that base, which can come from a native merge/replay plan. The envelope contains the three revision IDs, nullable base , current and incoming file summaries (manifest, size, file hash and executable flag), ready , objects_verified , result_executable , blockers and merge . Only a clean result is ready and supplies a result mode. Executable bits merge independently: an unchanged current mode takes the incoming mode, otherwise the current mode is retained. Different manifest representations do not change logical text equality. All three paths must exist. An absent path produces file_presence in blockers and merge: null , so deletion is never confused with empty content. All three sizes are checked before any chunks are read; oversized input produces file_limit and merge: null . Both cases have objects_verified: false . Otherwise the complete requested files and their whole-file hashes are verified, and merge contains the core report. Binary, conflict and later budget results remain non-ready, with the core reason copied into blockers . Missing or corrupt payloads throw rather than becoming a merge result. Verification covers the requested files, not other paths or revision ancestors. The operation does not read the working directory, write objects or update references. The envelope has a separate 16 MiB hard ceiling; the nested core report obeys max_output_bytes . CLI and reviewed conflict resolution # text Copy syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text Selectors use the native HEAD/branch/tag rules. The default output is the JSON envelope; exit status is zero only when ready is true. A blocked JSON preview exits 1 and still writes its complete report to stdout. --text writes only clean merged bytes, without a trailing newline added by the CLI. On conflicts, binary input, absence or budget limits it fails with no content on stdout. Diagnostics and help use English by default, or French with --lang fr . JSON fields and merged bytes are language independent. --max-file-bytes , --max-lines , --max-work and --max-output-bytes override the normal text budgets. --progress and --progress-json report on stderr; the operation supports the existing cooperative CLI cancellation. This command is an explicit preview. Native merge and cherry-pick/revert can also enable bounded automatic text composition. Rebase still uses its existing file-level decisions. A reviewed clean text result can be applied through a custom file resolution: Run the native operation's plan and retain its exact resolution_context . For a merge, pass its base , head and source IDs to merge-text . Inspect the JSON report, including result_executable . Save clean --text output to a temporary file outside the tracked workspace, using a tool that preserves stdout bytes. Older shells can transcode redirected native output. Import that file with syrnck put FILE . If GC can run before application, retain its manifest with a pin, as described in native conflict resolutions . Add a file choice using that manifest and the reviewed executable mode to the original resolution context. Preview again with --resolutions and --verify , then apply with the same exact head and resolution document. The normal publication checks, recoverable checkout journal and archive roots then protect the resulting commit and both parents. Other unresolved conflicts still block publication. The text preview does not bind or publish a branch transaction by itself. Root/add/delete policy remains operation-specific. Virtual preparation supports successive rebase text composition. Virtual file preparation # The internal native text batch prepares candidate manifest IDs in memory using the production default CDC boundaries and v1 manifest encoding. It retains clean generated bytes until application. It writes neither chunks nor manifests during planning. Materialization uses the normal configured ingestion pipeline and requires the resulting manifest ID to match the predicted ID before a revision can use it. Existing representations are reused when the merged logical content already matches one of the inputs. A batch attempts at most 64 content conflicts and charges at most 16 MiB for the sum of verified input bytes and clean result bytes. These aggregate ceilings are in addition to each file's text limits. Files outside a budget remain unresolved with file_count_limit or total_bytes_limit ; no text result is guessed. Reports contain status, ranges and target metadata, never generated plaintext. syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text","headings":[{"id":"bounded-three-way-text-merge","text":"Bounded three-way text merge","level":1},{"id":"result","text":"Result","level":2},{"id":"budgets-and-cancellation","text":"Budgets and cancellation","level":2},{"id":"merge-one-stored-path","text":"Merge one stored path","level":2},{"id":"cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution","text":"CLI and reviewed conflict resolution","level":2},{"id":"virtual-file-preparation","text":"Virtual file preparation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition","#/en/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition","#/en/native-resolutions/choices","#/en/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"text-patches","title":"Strict unified text patches","description":"Strict unified text patches guide.","section":"Version control","order":570,"parent":"native-history","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"strict-unified-text-patches\">Strict unified text patches\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-patches/strict-unified-text-patches\" aria-label=\"Link to Strict unified text patches\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The independent C++ \u003ccode>apply_text_patch(source, patch, path, limits, operation)\u003c/code>\nreturns \u003ccode>TextPatchResult\u003c/code> with complete result \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> and hunk/addition/deletion\ncounts. It never writes files, objects or references. Invalid input throws;\nno partial result is returned. Inputs remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"accepted-format\">Accepted format\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-patches/accepted-format\" aria-label=\"Link to Accepted format\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>One existing UTF-8 text file per patch, with exactly matching file labels:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">--- a/Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">+++ b/Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">@@ -1 +1 @@\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">-old line\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">+new line\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Unprefixed matching labels and Git&#39;s trailing TAB label terminator are also\naccepted. An optional exact\n\u003ccode>diff --git a/PATH b/PATH\u003c/code> preamble and \u003ccode>index OLD..NEW [100644|100755]\u003c/code> line are\naccepted. Index hashes are format hints, not verified Git identities; source\nbytes and hunk context determine applicability. Hunk counts may be omitted\nwhen one. Optional section text after \u003ccode>@@\u003c/code> is accepted. Positions and counts\nmust agree on both source and result, including zero-count insertion ranges.\nEvery context/removal row must match the source byte-for-byte at that position.\nThere is no offset search, fuzz, whitespace correction or reverse guessing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>File labels are literal UTF-8, with LF or CRLF metadata terminators. Quoted/\nescaped labels, timestamp suffixes, renames, mode changes, file presence changes,\n\u003ccode>/dev/null\u003c/code>, binary patches, combined/word diffs, multi-file input and mail/commit\nwrappers are outside this subset. A second file or trailing unsupported data\nis refused. An empty patch or one containing no added/removed rows is refused.\nThe selected portable path is validated but is never opened by this function.\nThe index-line mode is informational and never changes the source executable flag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exact-bytes-and-bounds\">Exact bytes and bounds\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-patches/exact-bytes-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Link to Exact bytes and bounds\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Body rows retain their literal content and LF/CRLF bytes. A standard\n\u003ccode>\\ No newline at end of file\u003c/code> marker applies to the preceding body row; markers\ncannot be orphaned or repeated. Patch body rows themselves must end with LF,\nwith the marker representing a missing content LF. A truncated final row is an\nerror. Empty unterminated rows and concatenating content after an unterminated\nline are refused. No newline or encoding normalization is performed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Source, patch and result use the same UTF-8/control-character classification\nas \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text differences\u003c/a>. The limits are \u003ccode>TextDiffOptions\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_file_bytes\u003c/code> bounds source and result independently (default 1 MiB, cap 4 MiB).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_output_bytes\u003c/code> bounds patch input (default 4 MiB, cap 8 MiB).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_lines\u003c/code> bounds each source/result (default 50,000, cap 100,000).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>max_work\u003c/code> charges each consumed patch row&#39;s bytes plus one, matched source\nbytes plus one, and copied unchanged source bytes plus one. Validation scans\nare additionally bounded by the byte limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hunk positions/counts and the number of hunks are capped at 100,000.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>context\u003c/code> remains validated but does not change an externally supplied patch.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Memory holds bounded source line views and result bytes; it does not run an\nedit-distance search or retain a diff frontier. Cancellation is checked during\nvalidation, traversal and output. Synchronous planning observers can abort by\nthrowing; neither failure returns a successful or partial result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index\">Prepare an external patch in the native index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-patches/prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index\" aria-label=\"Link to Prepare an external patch in the native index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>C++ callers use \u003ccode>store.plan_stage_patch(branch, path, patch_bytes, limits, operation)\u003c/code>\nand \u003ccode>store.stage_patch(branch, path, patch_bytes, expected_plan, limits, operation)\u003c/code>.\nThe source is the complete frozen file in \u003ccode>staging/BRANCH\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>heads/BRANCH\u003c/code>\nwithout an index. No working directory argument or working-file read is needed.\nDirty, absent, ignored or directory-replaced working paths do not affect it.\nThe source file must already exist in the stored snapshot; initial preparation\non an unborn branch is supported when an existing initial index contains it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The read-only plan reports \u003ccode>operation: stage-patch\u003c/code>, branch/path/head/index/base,\nsource revision and verified file metadata, raw BLAKE3 \u003ccode>patch_hash\u003c/code>, patch byte\ncount and result byte/hash/hunk/addition/deletion counts. Inspect the original\npatch for its rows; the report does not echo or render the patch. The canonical\npreview JSON, before adding \u003ccode>plan_id\u003c/code>, is hashed in the existing chunk domain.\nNo plan object is written. Options (including validated \u003ccode>context\u003c/code>), patch bytes,\nsource state and result identity are bound to that exact preview.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application recomputes and requires the same expected ID before ingestion.\nInvalid format, missing/oversized sources, binary input and context/range/budget\nfailures throw without writing objects or references. Valid previews may still\nbe blocked by pending checkout, stale staging, an identical result or a\nconflicting old-index archive. Plans verify only the selected file&#39;s payload;\npublication verifies the complete candidate, retained index and branch closures.\nCorrupt unrelated data can therefore block publication after a valid preview.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Application starts from the complete frozen index/head file map, changes only\nthe selected manifest and preserves its executable flag and other preparations.\nIt ingests default CDC with repository compression/worker settings, then uses\nthe same atomic publication as \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">hunk/line selection\u003c/a>: head/index/\nsymbolic HEAD/archive expectations guard the replacement staging root and\n\u003ccode>archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID\u003c/code>. Exact archives can be reused. The previous\nindex and its baseline remain reconstructible after GC and later commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation, observer failure or failed closure checks before publication keep\nthe old references; complete unreachable objects may remain. No workspace\njournal is needed. No observer runs after reference publication. The complete\npreview/application report is capped at 16 MiB, independently of patch input.\nThe caller keeps the borrowed patch bytes stable for the duration of each call.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-workflow\">CLI workflow\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/text-patches/cli-workflow\" aria-label=\"Link to CLI workflow\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Git writes patch bytes directly with \u003ccode>--output\u003c/code>, avoiding shell text encoding\nconversion. Use a single existing file and the intended source state. Git&#39;s\nindex hashes do not authorize or identify Syrnck objects. Modified-file patches\nfrom \u003ccode>native diff-text SOURCE TARGET PATH --unified\u003c/code> are also accepted. Git\nfixtures cover Unicode/spaces, CRLF/LF, missing final LF, empty existing files,\nclearing file contents and multiple hunks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both commands require \u003ccode>--patch FILE\u003c/code>, accept \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code> and the text limits,\nand support \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> and cancellation. They accept no\n\u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>: the patch is applied to stored bytes. The patch file is read once,\nwithin the input byte budget, before planning/application. Its captured bytes\nare bound by the plan hash; a changed file on a later invocation needs a new\npreview. Review the original patch and source/result hashes before application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The default UI is English, with French via \u003ccode>--lang fr\u003c/code>; JSON is identical in\nboth languages. A ready preview exits 0; a blocked preview exits 1 with JSON.\nInvalid patches/options or failed applications exit nonzero without a result.\nSuccessful application returns JSON with \u003ccode>state: applied\u003c/code>, previous/new index,\nresult manifest and file hash, keeping the original preview ID. Progress stays\non stderr. Other prepared files and modes survive the subsequent staged commit.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Strict unified text patches # The independent C++ apply_text_patch(source, patch, path, limits, operation) returns TextPatchResult with complete result content and hunk/addition/deletion counts. It never writes files, objects or references. Invalid input throws; no partial result is returned. Inputs remain unchanged. Accepted format # One existing UTF-8 text file per patch, with exactly matching file labels: text Copy --- a/Source/Player.cpp +++ b/Source/Player.cpp @@ -1 +1 @@ -old line +new line Unprefixed matching labels and Git's trailing TAB label terminator are also accepted. An optional exact diff --git a/PATH b/PATH preamble and index OLD..NEW [100644|100755] line are accepted. Index hashes are format hints, not verified Git identities; source bytes and hunk context determine applicability. Hunk counts may be omitted when one. Optional section text after @@ is accepted. Positions and counts must agree on both source and result, including zero-count insertion ranges. Every context/removal row must match the source byte-for-byte at that position. There is no offset search, fuzz, whitespace correction or reverse guessing. File labels are literal UTF-8, with LF or CRLF metadata terminators. Quoted/ escaped labels, timestamp suffixes, renames, mode changes, file presence changes, /dev/null , binary patches, combined/word diffs, multi-file input and mail/commit wrappers are outside this subset. A second file or trailing unsupported data is refused. An empty patch or one containing no added/removed rows is refused. The selected portable path is validated but is never opened by this function. The index-line mode is informational and never changes the source executable flag. Exact bytes and bounds # Body rows retain their literal content and LF/CRLF bytes. A standard \\ No newline at end of file marker applies to the preceding body row; markers cannot be orphaned or repeated. Patch body rows themselves must end with LF, with the marker representing a missing content LF. A truncated final row is an error. Empty unterminated rows and concatenating content after an unterminated line are refused. No newline or encoding normalization is performed. Source, patch and result use the same UTF-8/control-character classification as text differences . The limits are TextDiffOptions : max_file_bytes bounds source and result independently (default 1 MiB, cap 4 MiB). max_output_bytes bounds patch input (default 4 MiB, cap 8 MiB). max_lines bounds each source/result (default 50,000, cap 100,000). max_work charges each consumed patch row's bytes plus one, matched source bytes plus one, and copied unchanged source bytes plus one. Validation scans are additionally bounded by the byte limits. Hunk positions/counts and the number of hunks are capped at 100,000. context remains validated but does not change an externally supplied patch. Memory holds bounded source line views and result bytes; it does not run an edit-distance search or retain a diff frontier. Cancellation is checked during validation, traversal and output. Synchronous planning observers can abort by throwing; neither failure returns a successful or partial result. Prepare an external patch in the native index # C++ callers use store.plan_stage_patch(branch, path, patch_bytes, limits, operation) and store.stage_patch(branch, path, patch_bytes, expected_plan, limits, operation) . The source is the complete frozen file in staging/BRANCH , or heads/BRANCH without an index. No working directory argument or working-file read is needed. Dirty, absent, ignored or directory-replaced working paths do not affect it. The source file must already exist in the stored snapshot; initial preparation on an unborn branch is supported when an existing initial index contains it. The read-only plan reports operation: stage-patch , branch/path/head/index/base, source revision and verified file metadata, raw BLAKE3 patch_hash , patch byte count and result byte/hash/hunk/addition/deletion counts. Inspect the original patch for its rows; the report does not echo or render the patch. The canonical preview JSON, before adding plan_id , is hashed in the existing chunk domain. No plan object is written. Options (including validated context ), patch bytes, source state and result identity are bound to that exact preview. Application recomputes and requires the same expected ID before ingestion. Invalid format, missing/oversized sources, binary input and context/range/budget failures throw without writing objects or references. Valid previews may still be blocked by pending checkout, stale staging, an identical result or a conflicting old-index archive. Plans verify only the selected file's payload; publication verifies the complete candidate, retained index and branch closures. Corrupt unrelated data can therefore block publication after a valid preview. Application starts from the complete frozen index/head file map, changes only the selected manifest and preserves its executable flag and other preparations. It ingests default CDC with repository compression/worker settings, then uses the same atomic publication as hunk/line selection : head/index/ symbolic HEAD/archive expectations guard the replacement staging root and archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID . Exact archives can be reused. The previous index and its baseline remain reconstructible after GC and later commit. Cancellation, observer failure or failed closure checks before publication keep the old references; complete unreachable objects may remain. No workspace journal is needed. No observer runs after reference publication. The complete preview/application report is capped at 16 MiB, independently of patch input. The caller keeps the borrowed patch bytes stable for the duration of each call. CLI workflow # text Copy git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\" Git writes patch bytes directly with --output , avoiding shell text encoding conversion. Use a single existing file and the intended source state. Git's index hashes do not authorize or identify Syrnck objects. Modified-file patches from native diff-text SOURCE TARGET PATH --unified are also accepted. Git fixtures cover Unicode/spaces, CRLF/LF, missing final LF, empty existing files, clearing file contents and multiple hunks. Both commands require --patch FILE , accept --branch NAME and the text limits, and support --progress / --progress-json and cancellation. They accept no --directory : the patch is applied to stored bytes. The patch file is read once, within the input byte budget, before planning/application. Its captured bytes are bound by the plan hash; a changed file on a later invocation needs a new preview. Review the original patch and source/result hashes before application. The default UI is English, with French via --lang fr ; JSON is identical in both languages. A ready preview exits 0; a blocked preview exits 1 with JSON. Invalid patches/options or failed applications exit nonzero without a result. Successful application returns JSON with state: applied , previous/new index, result manifest and file hash, keeping the original preview ID. Progress stays on stderr. Other prepared files and modes survive the subsequent staged commit. --- a/Source/Player.cpp +++ b/Source/Player.cpp @@ -1 +1 @@ -old line +new line git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"","headings":[{"id":"strict-unified-text-patches","text":"Strict unified text patches","level":1},{"id":"accepted-format","text":"Accepted format","level":2},{"id":"exact-bytes-and-bounds","text":"Exact bytes and bounds","level":2},{"id":"prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index","text":"Prepare an external patch in the native index","level":2},{"id":"cli-workflow","text":"CLI workflow","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"--- a/Source/Player.cpp\n+++ b/Source/Player.cpp\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-old line\n+new line\ngit -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch\nsyrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"","assets":[],"links":["#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-hunks"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"transfer-policy","title":"Retry policy and shared traffic targets","description":"Retry policy and shared traffic targets guide.","section":"Transfers","order":580,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets\">Retry policy and shared traffic targets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-policy/retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets\" aria-label=\"Link to Retry policy and shared traffic targets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The HTTP agent retries transient failures and can pace object traffic in either\ndirection. The same options apply to push and pull:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These commands require a configured server and \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>. Use \u003ccode>--ca\u003c/code> for a\nprivate certificate authority. The CLI supports English/French; JSON fields\nremain canonical English. No credential is persisted by this policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retry-boundaries\">Retry boundaries\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-policy/retry-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to Retry boundaries\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--retries\u003c/code> accepts \u003cstrong>0..8 additional attempts\u003c/strong>, default \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong>. Zero disables\napplication retries. Each request has its own budget. Retriable operations are\n\u003ccode>GET v1/refs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>POST v1/missing\u003c/code> (a presence query), and object \u003ccode>GET\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>PUT\u003c/code>.\nObject PUT is idempotent because the content is addressed by its verified hash.\nA lost upload acknowledgement can therefore cause the same complete object to\nbe sent again. Downloads restart the individual object, not a byte range;\npartial responses never enter the Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Failure\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Policy\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retry within the request&#39;s remaining budget\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DNS resolution, connection, send/receive, empty reply, partial response, request timeout\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Retry within the remaining budget\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Other HTTP statuses, including 401/403/404/409 and 501\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fail immediately\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>TLS verification, malformed protocol, response-size rejection, invalid object or dependency, observer exception, cancellation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fail immediately\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Final reference PUT, including a transient status or lost reply\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Never retry automatically\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>The initial delay is \u003cstrong>250 ms\u003c/strong>, doubled for each additional attempt and capped\nat \u003cstrong>30 seconds\u003c/strong>. A valid \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> integer or HTTP-date can increase the\ndelay up to that same cap; malformed fields are ignored. Waiting uses a monotonic\nclock after converting an HTTP-date. There is currently no randomized jitter.\nAuthentication and payload validation errors are not masked by retries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Object requests waiting for another attempt keep their request slot and charged\nupload/response buffers. They detach from libcurl&#39;s multi handle while waiting.\nOther active requests can continue; a retry cannot bypass the configured buffer\nor request budget. The original upload vector and reserved response capacity\nare reused. Cancellation wakes both retry and traffic waits. Progress observers\nremain on the coordinator thread, and request counters reset on each attempt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The final JSON adds \u003ccode>object_retries\u003c/code>, counting additional \u003cstrong>object\u003c/strong> attempts\nscheduled by this policy. Control-plane retries are excluded. Existing\n\u003ccode>transferred_objects\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> still count successful logical\nobjects once; they are not wire-traffic counters and exclude repeated or partial\nattempts. Exhaustion returns an error without final success statistics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With \u003ca href=\"#/en/network-compression\">negotiated compression\u003c/a>, rate targets and progress\nuse encoded HTTP object-body bytes. \u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code> expose these observed counts, including retries;\n\u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> keeps its logical meaning. Codec/header errors fail without\nretry. Download retries reset the decoder and upload retries reuse their frame.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfers/publication-boundary\">publication boundary\u003c/a> is unchanged:\nafter the final remote CAS is dispatched it completes without observer calls or\ncooperative cancellation. Ambiguous publication exits \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong> and requires checking\nthe remote reference. A retry of the whole command obtains a fresh expected\nreference when starting a fresh session. An unfinished session preserves its\noriginal expectation and uses \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">journal reconciliation\u003c/a>\ninstead of replaying an ambiguous compare-and-set.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-targets\">Traffic targets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-policy/traffic-targets\" aria-label=\"Link to Traffic targets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--upload-kib-s\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--download-kib-s\u003c/code> specify \u003cstrong>KiB/s (1,024 bytes/s)\u003c/strong>, with\n\u003cstrong>0 meaning unlimited\u003c/strong>. Both accept 0..1,073,741,824 KiB/s. The defaults are\nunlimited. They describe aggregate \u003cstrong>object-body\u003c/strong> average targets for the whole\noperation, shared across parallel requests, retries and push batches. They are\nnot multiplied by \u003ccode>--requests\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each active handle receives a share of the target using libcurl&#39;s\n\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">send\u003c/a> and\n\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">receive\u003c/a> limits.\nThe coordinator also accounts libcurl&#39;s upload/download body counters across\nattempts and paces further work against a common elapsed-time budget. It drains\nthat budget before returning to publication, so many small objects cannot each\nclaim a fresh startup allowance. A push keeps the same budget across its missing\nobject batches. Retry delays, local work and network idle time count as elapsed\ntime; actual throughput may be lower than the configured target.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These are software average targets, \u003cstrong>not an instantaneous packet policer\u003c/strong>.\nShort bursts, socket buffers and libcurl scheduling are allowed. HTTP headers,\nTLS framing, reference/presence requests and the final publication are outside\nthe target. Observed object-body bytes from failed/repeated attempts and object\nacknowledgements are included. A cancelled or failed operation can end before\npaying off its last burst; no future session inherits its pacing budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Object request deadlines include the worst-case time for a maximum-sized object\nat the configured target and concurrency, plus the ordinary 120-second allowance.\nConnection establishment still has a 15-second timeout. Control requests and\nthe final CAS retain their 120-second timeout. Limits do not establish a total\noperation deadline, and server/proxy timeouts still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>In C++, \u003ccode>net::TransferOptions\u003c/code> exposes \u003ccode>max_retries\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>retry_delay_ms\u003c/code> (1..30,000),\n\u003ccode>upload_bytes_per_second\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>download_bytes_per_second\u003c/code>. Nonzero rates must be\nat least \u003ccode>max_requests\u003c/code> bytes/s and at most 1 TiB/s. Pass the options after\n\u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>push\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>pull\u003c/code>, or as the second argument to \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>. The\nindependent \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> call supports retries but has no object traffic to limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery-and-validation\">Recovery and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-policy/recovery-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Recovery and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Restarting the command still negotiates remote presence or checks local verified\nobjects and reuses completed imports. \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">Persistent sessions\u003c/a>\nsave the requested root and reference expectation and selectively retain their\navailable objects during local GC until completion or deliberate discard. Retry budgets and pacing credit are not\npersisted. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">Server upload leases\u003c/a> protect uploads while\nrenewed. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">Server read leases\u003c/a> protect exact pull roots until\ntheir objects are local; background resume remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>resilient_transfers\u003c/code> runs a real HTTP peer that returns transient statuses,\nloses acknowledgements and truncates downloads. It exercises control/object\nretries, exhaustion, explicit disable, Retry-After, exponential delay, encrypted\nimports, bounded buffers, cancellation during waits, aggregate pacing over small\nobjects, and single dispatch of uncertain reference publication. Pacing assertions\nmeasure controlled fixture durations, not production throughput. Validation here\nis Windows x64; other platform and sanitizer runs still require CI.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Retry policy and shared traffic targets # The HTTP agent retries transient failures and can pace object traffic in either direction. The same options apply to push and pull: text Copy syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json These commands require a configured server and SYRNCK_TOKEN . Use --ca for a private certificate authority. The CLI supports English/French; JSON fields remain canonical English. No credential is persisted by this policy. Retry boundaries # --retries accepts 0..8 additional attempts , default 3 . Zero disables application retries. Each request has its own budget. Retriable operations are GET v1/refs , POST v1/missing (a presence query), and object GET / PUT . Object PUT is idempotent because the content is addressed by its verified hash. A lost upload acknowledgement can therefore cause the same complete object to be sent again. Downloads restart the individual object, not a byte range; partial responses never enter the Store. Failure Policy HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 Retry within the request's remaining budget DNS resolution, connection, send/receive, empty reply, partial response, request timeout Retry within the remaining budget Other HTTP statuses, including 401/403/404/409 and 501 Fail immediately TLS verification, malformed protocol, response-size rejection, invalid object or dependency, observer exception, cancellation Fail immediately Final reference PUT, including a transient status or lost reply Never retry automatically The initial delay is 250 ms , doubled for each additional attempt and capped at 30 seconds . A valid Retry-After integer or HTTP-date can increase the delay up to that same cap; malformed fields are ignored. Waiting uses a monotonic clock after converting an HTTP-date. There is currently no randomized jitter. Authentication and payload validation errors are not masked by retries. Object requests waiting for another attempt keep their request slot and charged upload/response buffers. They detach from libcurl's multi handle while waiting. Other active requests can continue; a retry cannot bypass the configured buffer or request budget. The original upload vector and reserved response capacity are reused. Cancellation wakes both retry and traffic waits. Progress observers remain on the coordinator thread, and request counters reset on each attempt. The final JSON adds object_retries , counting additional object attempts scheduled by this policy. Control-plane retries are excluded. Existing transferred_objects and transferred_bytes still count successful logical objects once; they are not wire-traffic counters and exclude repeated or partial attempts. Exhaustion returns an error without final success statistics. With negotiated compression , rate targets and progress use encoded HTTP object-body bytes. object_upload_bytes and object_download_bytes expose these observed counts, including retries; transferred_bytes keeps its logical meaning. Codec/header errors fail without retry. Download retries reset the decoder and upload retries reuse their frame. The publication boundary is unchanged: after the final remote CAS is dispatched it completes without observer calls or cooperative cancellation. Ambiguous publication exits 3 and requires checking the remote reference. A retry of the whole command obtains a fresh expected reference when starting a fresh session. An unfinished session preserves its original expectation and uses journal reconciliation instead of replaying an ambiguous compare-and-set. Traffic targets # --upload-kib-s and --download-kib-s specify KiB/s (1,024 bytes/s) , with 0 meaning unlimited . Both accept 0..1,073,741,824 KiB/s. The defaults are unlimited. They describe aggregate object-body average targets for the whole operation, shared across parallel requests, retries and push batches. They are not multiplied by --requests . Each active handle receives a share of the target using libcurl's send and receive limits. The coordinator also accounts libcurl's upload/download body counters across attempts and paces further work against a common elapsed-time budget. It drains that budget before returning to publication, so many small objects cannot each claim a fresh startup allowance. A push keeps the same budget across its missing object batches. Retry delays, local work and network idle time count as elapsed time; actual throughput may be lower than the configured target. These are software average targets, not an instantaneous packet policer . Short bursts, socket buffers and libcurl scheduling are allowed. HTTP headers, TLS framing, reference/presence requests and the final publication are outside the target. Observed object-body bytes from failed/repeated attempts and object acknowledgements are included. A cancelled or failed operation can end before paying off its last burst; no future session inherits its pacing budget. Object request deadlines include the worst-case time for a maximum-sized object at the configured target and concurrency, plus the ordinary 120-second allowance. Connection establishment still has a 15-second timeout. Control requests and the final CAS retain their 120-second timeout. Limits do not establish a total operation deadline, and server/proxy timeouts still apply. In C++, net::TransferOptions exposes max_retries , retry_delay_ms (1..30,000), upload_bytes_per_second and download_bytes_per_second . Nonzero rates must be at least max_requests bytes/s and at most 1 TiB/s. Pass the options after OperationOptions to push / pull , or as the second argument to refs . The independent refs call supports retries but has no object traffic to limit. Recovery and validation # Restarting the command still negotiates remote presence or checks local verified objects and reuses completed imports. Persistent sessions save the requested root and reference expectation and selectively retain their available objects during local GC until completion or deliberate discard. Retry budgets and pacing credit are not persisted. Server upload leases protect uploads while renewed. Server read leases protect exact pull roots until their objects are local; background resume remains future work. resilient_transfers runs a real HTTP peer that returns transient statuses, loses acknowledgements and truncates downloads. It exercises control/object retries, exhaustion, explicit disable, Retry-After, exponential delay, encrypted imports, bounded buffers, cancellation during waits, aggregate pacing over small objects, and single dispatch of uncertain reference publication. Pacing assertions measure controlled fixture durations, not production throughput. Validation here is Windows x64; other platform and sanitizer runs still require CI. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets","text":"Retry policy and shared traffic targets","level":1},{"id":"retry-boundaries","text":"Retry boundaries","level":2},{"id":"traffic-targets","text":"Traffic targets","level":2},{"id":"recovery-and-validation","text":"Recovery and validation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["#/en/network-compression","#/en/transfers/publication-boundary","#/en/transfer-sessions","https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_MAX_SEND_SPEED_LARGE.html","https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_MAX_RECV_SPEED_LARGE.html","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-read-leases"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"transfer-sessions","title":"Persistent transfer sessions","description":"Persistent transfer sessions guide.","section":"Transfers","order":590,"parent":"transfers","revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-transfer-sessions\">Persistent transfer sessions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions/persistent-transfer-sessions\" aria-label=\"Link to Persistent transfer sessions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Each push or pull records local recovery state before transferring objects.\nThe agent can resume in another process, retain verified blocks across a local\nGC attempt and reconcile an acknowledgement lost at publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent sessions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck agent prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck agent prune --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>sessions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>forget\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>prune\u003c/code> are local commands and need no server token. Resume uses\nthe current \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>, optional \u003ccode>--ca\u003c/code> and current transfer options. Encrypted\nstores still require their local \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. Opening a Store starts no network\nactivity or background recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Repeating the same push/pull automatically finds an unfinished session with the\nsame direction, endpoint, exact root and destination reference. Explicit resume\nreads the root from the journal, so the original source reference can have been\nremoved. If a remote reference changed roots, a new \u003ccode>agent pull URL REF LOCAL\u003c/code>\naddresses that new root; use the session ID to resume the original root.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format-and-writes\">Format and writes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions/format-and-writes\" aria-label=\"Link to Format and writes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/en/transfers/expected-publication\">Explicit publication expectations\u003c/a> can bind\na push to an earlier remote observation. A new expectation cannot replace an\nunfinished session&#39;s frozen \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code> value. Explicit resume keeps the original\nvalue, including after the remote has committed and its acknowledgement was lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Records live in \u003ccode>STORE/transfers/ID.json\u003c/code>, with a strict version-3 schema and a\n16 KiB limit. Version-1 and version-2 records remain readable:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Field\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>version\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Journal schema version, currently 3\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stable BLAKE3 identity over direction, endpoint fingerprint, root and reference\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>direction\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>push\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pull\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>endpoint\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hash of the client&#39;s canonical base URL\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>root\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact logical object key requested\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Destination reference name\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Destination reference at session creation, or null\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>state\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Recovery state described below\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>upload_lease\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nullable server upload lease ID (added in version 2)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nullable server read lease ID for pulls (added in version 3)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>URL, token, encryption key, CA filename and exception text are not recorded.\nEndpoint matching is exact after URL normalization; equivalent URL spellings\nare not guaranteed to share a fingerprint. A different endpoint is rejected\nbefore HTTP requests. These are ordinary local metadata, like \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code>,\nrather than encrypted/authenticated journal contents. Filesystem access to the\nStore remains the trust boundary.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Writes use the existing flushed-file and atomic-replacement machinery. On Unix,\ncreating the journal directory also flushes its parent; writes/removals flush\nthe journal directory. Windows uses the existing atomic publication behavior.\nUncommitted \u003ccode>.tmp...\u003c/code> stages do not change canonical state. The Store&#39;s exclusive\nprocess lock coordinates journal/object/reference access. Unknown versions,\nmalformed records, mismatched identities and symlinked paths fail closed.\nPower-loss validation remains separate from process-interruption testing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Only operation boundaries write the journal. There is no per-block bitmap or\nper-block flush: verified CAS objects identify completed payloads. Resume\nrenegotiates missing remote objects or checks local objects. Incomplete object\nrequests restart; complete objects are reused. Retry counts, pacing credit and\nin-flight buffers start fresh in the new process.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"states-and-reference-protection\">States and reference protection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions/states-and-reference-protection\" aria-label=\"Link to States and reference protection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>State\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Resume behavior\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>transferring\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Continue while the destination still matches \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Publication was about to start, or may have completed before a crash\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>uncertain\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remote publication produced no unambiguous result\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>conflict\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Destination changed; preserve it and require deliberate resolution\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>complete\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Historical receipt; no GC hold or publication on explicit resume\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Before final publication the client durably records \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>. A remote\n\u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>uncertain\u003c/code> session reads references: a matching destination root\nrecords completion without another reference PUT. Otherwise it keeps the hold\nand reports \u003ccode>PublicationUncertain\u003c/code> (CLI exit \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong>), even if the destination equals\nthe old \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>. The server could have committed and then been deliberately\nchanged back. The original CAS is never blindly replayed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A local pull validates the complete closure and publishes with compare-and-set\nagainst saved \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>. A changed destination is preserved. After a crash, an\nalready matching local root is verified and marked complete without downloading.\nAny saved read lease is released before completion; cleanup may contact the server.\nIf the local reference still equals \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code>, publication can be retried. A\nknown conflict remains blocked until resolved/discarded; an already matching\ndesired root can also satisfy the session.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Normal success adds \u003ccode>session\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>reconciled\u003c/code> to the existing JSON statistics.\n\u003ccode>reconciled: true\u003c/code> means recovery confirmed publication or returned a previously\ncompleted receipt; object counters are zero because that invocation transferred\nno objects. Resuming a completed receipt does not assert that references still\nhave their historical values or recreate them. A new push/pull after completion\nreuses the ID with a fresh expected-reference snapshot and ordinary transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A completion-journal write can fail after successful publication. Push reports\nexit 3; pull reports an ordinary error. The durable \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> record allows\nresume to inspect the reference. After remote CAS dispatch, cancellation and\ncallbacks remain disabled through completion recording, so a late stop can\nstill return success. The last cancellation boundary is the atomic publishing\nmarker: after it commits, the client finishes dispatch and receipt as well. A\ncrash in the marker/dispatch gap is deliberately treated as uncertain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-and-deliberate-discard\">GC and deliberate discard\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions/gc-and-deliberate-discard\" aria-label=\"Link to GC and deliberate discard\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Every unfinished local session contributes its root to GC retention. Pushes\nrequire a complete verified source closure; pulls retain all available reachable\nobjects even when some dependencies are missing. Unrelated objects are collectable.\nGC streams journals with cancellation checks and keeps the root keys needed for\ntraversal. Invalid journals prevent GC. Packing preserves logical objects, but its\nfull audit can reject incomplete metadata until the pull finishes or its\nunreferenced objects are collected after deliberate discard.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>agent forget ID\u003c/code> removes the journal and releases its hold. It does not alter\nreferences or remove payloads. Ordinary GC can then reclaim unreferenced objects,\nincluding incomplete transfer metadata. A valid filename can be discarded even\nif its contents are malformed. Completed receipts do not pin their roots.\n\u003ccode>agent prune\u003c/code> previews completed/unfinished counts; \u003ccode>agent prune --delete\u003c/code>\nvalidates the full inventory before removing only completed receipts. It leaves\nobjects, references and unfinished records untouched. Cancellation during removal\ncan prune a subset of completed receipts; retry is safe. See\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">selective retention and pruning\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Local holds require journal-aware engines for every GC run. Compatible servers\nalso provide \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">expiring upload leases and publication receipts\u003c/a>.\nThose receipts can confirm a lost acknowledgement even after a later reference\nchange. An expired lease can permit offline server GC; resume renegotiates and\nreuploads missing objects from the protected source. \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">Read leases\u003c/a>\nprotect pull sources until all required objects are local. \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">Upload inventories\u003c/a>\nretain declared server objects selectively. Distributed GC remains future work. The \u003ca href=\"#/en/resident-agent\">resident native publication agent\u003c/a> can resume native pushes; it does not provide general background pull or folder reconciliation. Session\nlisting materializes its result; receipts consume disk space until pruned or forgotten.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Persistent transfer sessions # Each push or pull records local recovery state before transferring objects. The agent can resume in another process, retain verified blocks across a local GC attempt and reconcile an acknowledgement lost at publication. text Copy syrnck agent sessions syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete sessions , forget and prune are local commands and need no server token. Resume uses the current SYRNCK_TOKEN , optional --ca and current transfer options. Encrypted stores still require their local SYRNCK_KEY . Opening a Store starts no network activity or background recovery. Repeating the same push/pull automatically finds an unfinished session with the same direction, endpoint, exact root and destination reference. Explicit resume reads the root from the journal, so the original source reference can have been removed. If a remote reference changed roots, a new agent pull URL REF LOCAL addresses that new root; use the session ID to resume the original root. Format and writes # Explicit publication expectations can bind a push to an earlier remote observation. A new expectation cannot replace an unfinished session's frozen expected value. Explicit resume keeps the original value, including after the remote has committed and its acknowledgement was lost. Records live in STORE/transfers/ID.json , with a strict version-3 schema and a 16 KiB limit. Version-1 and version-2 records remain readable: Field Meaning version Journal schema version, currently 3 id Stable BLAKE3 identity over direction, endpoint fingerprint, root and reference direction push or pull endpoint Hash of the client's canonical base URL root Exact logical object key requested reference Destination reference name expected Destination reference at session creation, or null state Recovery state described below upload_lease Nullable server upload lease ID (added in version 2) read_lease Nullable server read lease ID for pulls (added in version 3) URL, token, encryption key, CA filename and exception text are not recorded. Endpoint matching is exact after URL normalization; equivalent URL spellings are not guaranteed to share a fingerprint. A different endpoint is rejected before HTTP requests. These are ordinary local metadata, like refs.json , rather than encrypted/authenticated journal contents. Filesystem access to the Store remains the trust boundary. Writes use the existing flushed-file and atomic-replacement machinery. On Unix, creating the journal directory also flushes its parent; writes/removals flush the journal directory. Windows uses the existing atomic publication behavior. Uncommitted .tmp... stages do not change canonical state. The Store's exclusive process lock coordinates journal/object/reference access. Unknown versions, malformed records, mismatched identities and symlinked paths fail closed. Power-loss validation remains separate from process-interruption testing. Only operation boundaries write the journal. There is no per-block bitmap or per-block flush: verified CAS objects identify completed payloads. Resume renegotiates missing remote objects or checks local objects. Incomplete object requests restart; complete objects are reused. Retry counts, pacing credit and in-flight buffers start fresh in the new process. States and reference protection # State Resume behavior transferring Continue while the destination still matches expected publishing Publication was about to start, or may have completed before a crash uncertain Remote publication produced no unambiguous result conflict Destination changed; preserve it and require deliberate resolution complete Historical receipt; no GC hold or publication on explicit resume Before final publication the client durably records publishing . A remote publishing / uncertain session reads references: a matching destination root records completion without another reference PUT. Otherwise it keeps the hold and reports PublicationUncertain (CLI exit 3 ), even if the destination equals the old expected . The server could have committed and then been deliberately changed back. The original CAS is never blindly replayed. A local pull validates the complete closure and publishes with compare-and-set against saved expected . A changed destination is preserved. After a crash, an already matching local root is verified and marked complete without downloading. Any saved read lease is released before completion; cleanup may contact the server. If the local reference still equals expected , publication can be retried. A known conflict remains blocked until resolved/discarded; an already matching desired root can also satisfy the session. Normal success adds session and reconciled to the existing JSON statistics. reconciled: true means recovery confirmed publication or returned a previously completed receipt; object counters are zero because that invocation transferred no objects. Resuming a completed receipt does not assert that references still have their historical values or recreate them. A new push/pull after completion reuses the ID with a fresh expected-reference snapshot and ordinary transfer. A completion-journal write can fail after successful publication. Push reports exit 3; pull reports an ordinary error. The durable publishing record allows resume to inspect the reference. After remote CAS dispatch, cancellation and callbacks remain disabled through completion recording, so a late stop can still return success. The last cancellation boundary is the atomic publishing marker: after it commits, the client finishes dispatch and receipt as well. A crash in the marker/dispatch gap is deliberately treated as uncertain. GC and deliberate discard # Every unfinished local session contributes its root to GC retention. Pushes require a complete verified source closure; pulls retain all available reachable objects even when some dependencies are missing. Unrelated objects are collectable. GC streams journals with cancellation checks and keeps the root keys needed for traversal. Invalid journals prevent GC. Packing preserves logical objects, but its full audit can reject incomplete metadata until the pull finishes or its unreferenced objects are collected after deliberate discard. agent forget ID removes the journal and releases its hold. It does not alter references or remove payloads. Ordinary GC can then reclaim unreferenced objects, including incomplete transfer metadata. A valid filename can be discarded even if its contents are malformed. Completed receipts do not pin their roots. agent prune previews completed/unfinished counts; agent prune --delete validates the full inventory before removing only completed receipts. It leaves objects, references and unfinished records untouched. Cancellation during removal can prune a subset of completed receipts; retry is safe. See selective retention and pruning . Local holds require journal-aware engines for every GC run. Compatible servers also provide expiring upload leases and publication receipts . Those receipts can confirm a lost acknowledgement even after a later reference change. An expired lease can permit offline server GC; resume renegotiates and reuploads missing objects from the protected source. Read leases protect pull sources until all required objects are local. Upload inventories retain declared server objects selectively. Distributed GC remains future work. The resident native publication agent can resume native pushes; it does not provide general background pull or folder reconciliation. Session listing materializes its result; receipts consume disk space until pruned or forgotten. syrnck agent sessions syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete","headings":[{"id":"persistent-transfer-sessions","text":"Persistent transfer sessions","level":1},{"id":"format-and-writes","text":"Format and writes","level":2},{"id":"states-and-reference-protection","text":"States and reference protection","level":2},{"id":"gc-and-deliberate-discard","text":"GC and deliberate discard","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent sessions\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress\nsyrnck agent forget SESSION_ID\nsyrnck agent prune\nsyrnck agent prune --delete","assets":[],"links":["#/en/transfers/expected-publication","#/en/selective-retention","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/resident-agent"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"transfers","title":"Transfer progress, cancellation and publication","description":"Transfer progress, cancellation and publication guide.","section":"Transfers","order":400,"revision":"3","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication\">Transfer progress, cancellation and publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Transfer progress, cancellation and publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The one-shot HTTP agent supports the same \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code> as local operations.\n\u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>push\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pull\u003c/code> accept a stop token and a synchronous observer.\nCLI usage keeps the ordinary final statistics on stdout:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Authentication still uses \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>; encrypted local stores use their own\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. These examples need an actual configured service. URL, token and\nkey material are never included in progress events. Events identify the relative\nprotocol path, such as \u003ccode>v1/objects/chunk/ID\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>transferring\u003c/code> bytes are HTTP request-body plus response-body bytes for one\nattempt. They reset between requests and retry attempts. The total may initially be unknown; it\nbecomes known when libcurl learns a content length, and is exact at successful\nrequest completion. Foreground control requests also produce events; background\nlease renewals have no user callbacks. A completed request\ndoes not mean an imported object has passed validation or a reference has been\npublished. The existing final \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> statistic counts only logical\npayloads of newly transferred objects; it excludes control messages and reused\nobjects. Text is localized and JSON keys/phases are canonical English.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The client uses libcurl&#39;s\n\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">transfer-progress callback\u003c/a>\nto abort ordinary requests while they are in progress, including idle waits.\nChecks also run between requests, objects, local validation and reference writes.\nCallbacks run on the calling thread. Exceptions are captured inside C callbacks,\nabort the request and are rethrown in C++ after libcurl returns. They never unwind\nthrough libcurl. Native IO/codecs and resolver work already running may finish\nbefore the next cancellation check; cancellation is cooperative, not an immediate\nsystem-wide timeout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push validates its complete source closure and full file hashes before sending\nobject payloads. It negotiates missing objects in batches, then uploads only\nthose objects. Pull verifies each received object before local import. An\ninterrupted transfer can leave reusable unreferenced objects. Retrying negotiates\nor checks their presence again; incomplete responses are not imported. A pulled\nmanifest may temporarily lack some dependencies until retry finishes, so a full\naudit of unreachable objects can fail during this incomplete state. Previously\nretained roots remain reconstructible. An unfinished local session retains its\nsource or available downloaded objects during GC until completed or deliberately\ndiscarded. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">persistent sessions\u003c/a>.\nNegotiated \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">read leases\u003c/a> protect the exact source root while\nmissing objects are downloaded. They are released before local publication,\nwhen the verified objects and unfinished local journal protect reconstruction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"server-authentication-and-request-bodies\">Server authentication and request bodies\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/server-authentication-and-request-bodies\" aria-label=\"Link to Server authentication and request bodies\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The server consumes the HTTP request body before checking credentials, with the\nexisting \u003cstrong>16 MiB\u003c/strong> payload limit and 30-second read timeout. It then requires\nexactly one \u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;\u003c/code> header before application JSON parsing,\nobject decompression, Store access or publication. Missing, incorrect and duplicate\ncredentials receive \u003ccode>401\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=&quot;Syrnck&quot;\u003c/code>, including\nrequests to unknown routes. Fully receiving the body avoids a Windows TCP reset\nthat could otherwise hide the rejection response when the server closes the\nconnection with unread data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This applies to well-formed, fully received bodies with either \u003ccode>Content-Length\u003c/code>\nor chunked framing. HTTP framing, unsupported transport encoding and payload-size\nerrors can precede authentication and keep their transport error status. A\nprovisional \u003ccode>100 Continue\u003c/code> permits sending the body; it does not authorize access.\nThe server closes connections after HTTP errors. Body reception still occupies\na server worker; deployment quotas and admission controls remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expected-publication\">Publish against an earlier observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/expected-publication\" aria-label=\"Link to Publish against an earlier observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> requires the remote reference to be absent or exactly the supplied\ntyped key (\u003ccode>chunk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, colon and 64 lowercase hex digits).\nThe examples need actual IDs. Use a previously fetched revision ID or the typed\nvalue returned by \u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code> as the observation. A stale value is refused before session\ncreation or object upload, including on a server branch that permits forced\nupdates. A later change during transfer still fails the final expected-value\npublication. Uploaded reusable objects can remain after that later rejection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>optional\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ObjectKey\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span> observed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> previous_remote_root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// null means absent\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">push_expected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> local_root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> observed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                    operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer_options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The session freezes this exact expectation. An existing unfinished session with\na different expectation is refused before writes; resume it explicitly or inspect\nand forget it before planning a new transfer. A new explicit push also refuses\nwhen the remote already equals the desired result but differs from \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>.\nUse \u003ccode>agent resume URL SESSION_ID\u003c/code> to reconcile a lost acknowledgement against\nthe original session. Reconciliation confirms that earlier publication; it does\nnot prove the reference still points there. A completed session starts a fresh\ntransfer with the newly checked expectation. Without \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Remote::push\u003c/code> keeps its existing behavior\nof observing the current value at transfer startup. Object, HTTP and journal\nformats do not change; account, signature, branch and asset-lock rules still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-boundary\">Publication boundary\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/publication-boundary\" aria-label=\"Link to Publication boundary\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Pull checks cancellation throughout the final local closure validation and\natomic reference write. Before that write starts, cancellation preserves the old\nreference. Push emits a \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> event and checks cancellation immediately\nbefore preparing its final remote compare-and-set request. A stop from that\ncallback prevents dispatch and leaves the remote reference unchanged. The\npublishing journal write also checks cancellation before atomic replacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once the publishing marker commits, the client dispatches and finishes the CAS\neven if a stop arrives. A crash between marker and dispatch is conservatively\ntreated as an uncertain publication. Aborting a dispatched CAS cannot undo a\nserver-side publication. There are no\nobserver callbacks after this commit point. A successful response completes the\npush normally; a late Ctrl+C can therefore finish with exit 0. This final request\nretains its existing 120-second timeout. The server publishes only after checking\nthe full dependency closure. A conflict (409) is a normal rejected publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If the CAS response is lost, or its HTTP status does not unambiguously reject\npublication, C++ throws \u003ccode>net::PublicationUncertain\u003c/code>. The CLI emits a localized\ndiagnostic, leaves stdout empty and exits \u003cstrong>3\u003c/strong>, even if an interrupt was also\nrequested. The server may already have updated the reference. Inspect that\nreference before deciding whether to retry. Transport failures, server errors\nand unexpected responses are treated conservatively; 401/403/404/409 remain\nordinary request failures. Resume uses the persistent journal to confirm a\nmatching remote root; it does not automatically repeat an ambiguous CAS. On\ncompatible servers, a \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">published upload receipt\u003c/a> can\nalso complete recovery after a later writer changed the reference. A leased\n412/428 precondition rejection leaves the session available for deliberate retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ordinary cooperative cancellation exits \u003cstrong>130\u003c/strong>. It does not remove already\nverified objects and does not delete existing references. Object formats,\nauthentication rules and the base v1 HTTP protocol remain compatible; upload\nleases use an explicitly negotiated extension.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-remaining-work\">Validation and remaining work\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/validation-and-remaining-work\" aria-label=\"Link to Validation and remaining work\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Object requests default to sequential execution and can run concurrently with\n\u003ccode>--requests\u003c/code> and a buffer budget. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/parallel-transfers\">parallel transfers and resource limits\u003c/a>.\nTransient idempotent requests now support bounded retries with backoff, and\nobject traffic can use shared average targets. See the \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-policy\">retry and traffic policy\u003c/a>.\nPersistent sessions support explicit resume, saved reference expectations and\nlocal GC holds; see \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">journal guarantees and limits\u003c/a>.\nPush still materializes its closure and sorted object list; pull retains a seen\nset and pending traversal. A complete resident sync service and distributed retention remain future work. \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets\">Git push/checkout integration\u003c/a> is available through catalogs, policies and opt-in hooks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-bookkeeping\">Record proven publication before session completion\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/transfers/publication-bookkeeping\" aria-label=\"Link to Record proven publication before session completion\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>C++ overloads of \u003ccode>push_expected\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>resume\u003c/code> accept a final \u003ccode>PublicationObserver\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>net\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>PublicationObserver record \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ObjectKey \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>published\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">set_ref\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"confirmed-publication\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> published\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">push_expected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> observed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> record\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resume\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> session_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> record\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The synchronous callback runs on the caller&#39;s thread after success is proven,\nbefore the session is marked complete. It also runs when recovery confirms an\nolder receipt, or when explicitly resuming a completed push. This is a historical\npublication fact: it does not assert that the remote reference still has that\nvalue. The observer is push-only and must be idempotent. Keep durable bookkeeping\ninside it; the resident agent records its retained remote history this way.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Once this bookkeeping boundary begins, completing its journal is not cancelled\nby a stop arriving inside the callback. For an unfinished push, a callback or\ncompletion-write failure leaves the journal available for recovery; it never\ncauses an automatic CAS replay. An already completed receipt stays complete if\na repeated callback fails, and that observer can be retried explicitly. Existing overloads without an observer keep their behavior. An\nobserver failure does not authorize replacing a remote reference that changed\nlater. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">persistent sessions\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Transfer progress, cancellation and publication # The one-shot HTTP agent supports the same OperationOptions as local operations. Remote::refs , push and pull accept a stop token and a synchronous observer. CLI usage keeps the ordinary final statistics on stdout: text Copy syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json Authentication still uses SYRNCK_TOKEN ; encrypted local stores use their own SYRNCK_KEY . These examples need an actual configured service. URL, token and key material are never included in progress events. Events identify the relative protocol path, such as v1/objects/chunk/ID . transferring bytes are HTTP request-body plus response-body bytes for one attempt. They reset between requests and retry attempts. The total may initially be unknown; it becomes known when libcurl learns a content length, and is exact at successful request completion. Foreground control requests also produce events; background lease renewals have no user callbacks. A completed request does not mean an imported object has passed validation or a reference has been published. The existing final transferred_bytes statistic counts only logical payloads of newly transferred objects; it excludes control messages and reused objects. Text is localized and JSON keys/phases are canonical English. The client uses libcurl's transfer-progress callback to abort ordinary requests while they are in progress, including idle waits. Checks also run between requests, objects, local validation and reference writes. Callbacks run on the calling thread. Exceptions are captured inside C callbacks, abort the request and are rethrown in C++ after libcurl returns. They never unwind through libcurl. Native IO/codecs and resolver work already running may finish before the next cancellation check; cancellation is cooperative, not an immediate system-wide timeout. Push validates its complete source closure and full file hashes before sending object payloads. It negotiates missing objects in batches, then uploads only those objects. Pull verifies each received object before local import. An interrupted transfer can leave reusable unreferenced objects. Retrying negotiates or checks their presence again; incomplete responses are not imported. A pulled manifest may temporarily lack some dependencies until retry finishes, so a full audit of unreachable objects can fail during this incomplete state. Previously retained roots remain reconstructible. An unfinished local session retains its source or available downloaded objects during GC until completed or deliberately discarded. See persistent sessions . Negotiated read leases protect the exact source root while missing objects are downloaded. They are released before local publication, when the verified objects and unfinished local journal protect reconstruction. Server authentication and request bodies # The server consumes the HTTP request body before checking credentials, with the existing 16 MiB payload limit and 30-second read timeout. It then requires exactly one Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken> header before application JSON parsing, object decompression, Store access or publication. Missing, incorrect and duplicate credentials receive 401 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=\"Syrnck\" , including requests to unknown routes. Fully receiving the body avoids a Windows TCP reset that could otherwise hide the rejection response when the server closes the connection with unread data. This applies to well-formed, fully received bodies with either Content-Length or chunked framing. HTTP framing, unsupported transport encoding and payload-size errors can precede authentication and keep their transport error status. A provisional 100 Continue permits sending the body; it does not authorize access. The server closes connections after HTTP errors. Body reception still occupies a server worker; deployment quotas and admission controls remain future work. Publish against an earlier observation # text Copy syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID --expect requires the remote reference to be absent or exactly the supplied typed key ( chunk , manifest or revision , colon and 64 lowercase hex digits). The examples need actual IDs. Use a previously fetched revision ID or the typed value returned by Remote::refs as the observation. A stale value is refused before session creation or object upload, including on a server branch that permits forced updates. A later change during transfer still fails the final expected-value publication. Uploaded reusable objects can remain after that later rejection. cpp Copy std :: optional \u003c syrnck :: ObjectKey > observed = previous_remote_root ; // null means absent auto result = remote . push_expected ( store , local_root , \"main\" , observed , operation , transfer_options ) ; The session freezes this exact expectation. An existing unfinished session with a different expectation is refused before writes; resume it explicitly or inspect and forget it before planning a new transfer. A new explicit push also refuses when the remote already equals the desired result but differs from --expect . Use agent resume URL SESSION_ID to reconcile a lost acknowledgement against the original session. Reconciliation confirms that earlier publication; it does not prove the reference still points there. A completed session starts a fresh transfer with the newly checked expectation. Without --expect , Remote::push keeps its existing behavior of observing the current value at transfer startup. Object, HTTP and journal formats do not change; account, signature, branch and asset-lock rules still apply. Publication boundary # Pull checks cancellation throughout the final local closure validation and atomic reference write. Before that write starts, cancellation preserves the old reference. Push emits a publishing event and checks cancellation immediately before preparing its final remote compare-and-set request. A stop from that callback prevents dispatch and leaves the remote reference unchanged. The publishing journal write also checks cancellation before atomic replacement. Once the publishing marker commits, the client dispatches and finishes the CAS even if a stop arrives. A crash between marker and dispatch is conservatively treated as an uncertain publication. Aborting a dispatched CAS cannot undo a server-side publication. There are no observer callbacks after this commit point. A successful response completes the push normally; a late Ctrl+C can therefore finish with exit 0. This final request retains its existing 120-second timeout. The server publishes only after checking the full dependency closure. A conflict (409) is a normal rejected publication. If the CAS response is lost, or its HTTP status does not unambiguously reject publication, C++ throws net::PublicationUncertain . The CLI emits a localized diagnostic, leaves stdout empty and exits 3 , even if an interrupt was also requested. The server may already have updated the reference. Inspect that reference before deciding whether to retry. Transport failures, server errors and unexpected responses are treated conservatively; 401/403/404/409 remain ordinary request failures. Resume uses the persistent journal to confirm a matching remote root; it does not automatically repeat an ambiguous CAS. On compatible servers, a published upload receipt can also complete recovery after a later writer changed the reference. A leased 412/428 precondition rejection leaves the session available for deliberate retry. Ordinary cooperative cancellation exits 130 . It does not remove already verified objects and does not delete existing references. Object formats, authentication rules and the base v1 HTTP protocol remain compatible; upload leases use an explicitly negotiated extension. Validation and remaining work # Object requests default to sequential execution and can run concurrently with --requests and a buffer budget. See parallel transfers and resource limits . Transient idempotent requests now support bounded retries with backoff, and object traffic can use shared average targets. See the retry and traffic policy . Persistent sessions support explicit resume, saved reference expectations and local GC holds; see journal guarantees and limits . Push still materializes its closure and sorted object list; pull retains a seen set and pending traversal. A complete resident sync service and distributed retention remain future work. Git push/checkout integration is available through catalogs, policies and opt-in hooks. Record proven publication before session completion # C++ overloads of push_expected and resume accept a final PublicationObserver : cpp Copy syrnck :: net :: PublicationObserver record = [ & ] ( const syrnck :: ObjectKey & published ) { store . set_ref ( \"confirmed-publication\" , published ) ; } ; remote . push_expected ( store , root , \"main\" , observed , operation , transfer , record ) ; remote . resume ( store , session_id , operation , transfer , record ) ; The synchronous callback runs on the caller's thread after success is proven, before the session is marked complete. It also runs when recovery confirms an older receipt, or when explicitly resuming a completed push. This is a historical publication fact: it does not assert that the remote reference still has that value. The observer is push-only and must be idempotent. Keep durable bookkeeping inside it; the resident agent records its retained remote history this way. Once this bookkeeping boundary begins, completing its journal is not cancelled by a stop arriving inside the callback. For an unfinished push, a callback or completion-write failure leaves the journal available for recovery; it never causes an automatic CAS replay. An already completed receipt stays complete if a repeated callback fails, and that observer can be retried explicitly. Existing overloads without an observer keep their behavior. An observer failure does not authorize replacing a remote reference that changed later. See persistent sessions . syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID std::optional\u003csyrnck::ObjectKey> observed = previous_remote_root; // null means absent auto result = remote.push_expected(store, local_root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer_options); syrnck::net::PublicationObserver record = [&](const syrnck::ObjectKey &published) { store.set_ref(\"confirmed-publication\", published); }; remote.push_expected(store, root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer, record); remote.resume(store, session_id, operation, transfer, record);","headings":[{"id":"transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication","text":"Transfer progress, cancellation and publication","level":1},{"id":"server-authentication-and-request-bodies","text":"Server authentication and request bodies","level":2},{"id":"expected-publication","text":"Publish against an earlier observation","level":2},{"id":"publication-boundary","text":"Publication boundary","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-remaining-work","text":"Validation and remaining work","level":2},{"id":"publication-bookkeeping","text":"Record proven publication before session completion","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID\nstd::optional\u003csyrnck::ObjectKey> observed = previous_remote_root; // null means absent\nauto result = remote.push_expected(store, local_root, \"main\", observed,\n                                    operation, transfer_options);\nsyrnck::net::PublicationObserver record = [&](const syrnck::ObjectKey &published) {\n  store.set_ref(\"confirmed-publication\", published);\n};\nremote.push_expected(store, root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer, record);\nremote.resume(store, session_id, operation, transfer, record);","assets":[],"links":["https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION.html","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/parallel-transfers","#/en/transfer-policy","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/git-assets","#/en/transfer-sessions"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"upload-inventories","title":"Selective server upload inventories","description":"Selective server upload inventories guide.","section":"Transfers","order":610,"parent":"transfers","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"selective-server-upload-inventories\">Selective server upload inventories\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/upload-inventories/selective-server-upload-inventories\" aria-label=\"Link to Selective server upload inventories\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Compatible clients and servers automatically use a paged inventory to retain\nobjects belonging to an unfinished upload. This works before the root manifest\narrives. Offline GC can reclaim unrelated objects while keeping registered\nobjects, including objects reused from an earlier version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Older upload leases keep their conservative whole-Store hold. Existing command\nlines still work; \u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> requires upload leases, not this optional\ninventory extension. A legacy hold anywhere in the Store still blocks GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registration-and-acknowledgement\">Registration and acknowledgement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/upload-inventories/registration-and-acknowledgement\" aria-label=\"Link to Registration and acknowledgement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The client validates its complete source closure, acquires an inventory-enabled\nlease and registers each batch \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> querying remote presence for that\nbatch. Each page lists all objects in the batch, including reused ones. A page\ncontains 1..4096 strictly ordered unique object keys; ordering continues across\npage boundaries. The client uses the existing deterministic closure ordering.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The server publishes an immutable page, then atomically replaces the lease&#39;s\ncommitted page count and hash. It sends success only after both writes complete.\nThe existing file flush, atomic replacement and parent-directory flush rules\napply. There is one registration per batch, not a new journal write per object.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A lost response can safely retry the same page. A committed page must exist and\nmatch its previous contents. It cannot be replaced through registration. A crash\nafter writing a page but before updating the lease leaves an unacknowledged page;\nthat page is outside the committed prefix. Retrying can validate and commit the\nsame staged page. The client must wait for registration acknowledgement before\nrelying on a presence response or sending objects for that page.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each page includes a BLAKE3 checksum over its canonical descriptor and object\nkeys, linked to the previous page&#39;s hash. The lease commits the final hash along\nwith the page count. GC verifies the full committed chain, so a missing page,\naltered key, broken link or altered page count stops payload deletion. This is\nintegrity checking under the Store&#39;s existing filesystem trust boundary; these\nmetadata are not authenticated against a malicious filesystem administrator.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-expiry-and-cleanup\">GC, expiry and cleanup\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/upload-inventories/gc-expiry-and-cleanup\" aria-label=\"Link to GC, expiry and cleanup\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An active inventory lease retains the \u003cstrong>existing objects explicitly listed in\nits committed pages\u003c/strong>. GC streams pages into the existing packed-object bitmap\nand loose-object marks. Present objects pass the ordinary envelope, hash and\nschema checks. Missing listed objects are allowed: registration can precede\narrival. GC does not materialize a second complete inventory of object IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Registration is not proof that the root closure has arrived. Dependencies absent\nfrom the registered inventory are not implicitly retained by that inventory.\nOrdinary references, read leases and local transfer roots still provide their\nown retention. Full closure and file-hash validation remain mandatory at final\nreference publication. Ordinary object PUTs remain compatible and immutable;\nunregistered uploads have no inventory retention guarantee.\n\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> keep their strict dependency audits and can reject incomplete\nuploaded metadata until the transfer finishes or unretained metadata is collected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Expiry and fencing remain as described in \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">upload leases\u003c/a>.\nBefore deleting payloads, GC durably marks observed expired leases and changes\ntheir fences. Reactivation creates a fresh generation with zero pages and a new\ninitial hash. Resume registers its batches again before renegotiating presence.\nStale fences cannot register pages or publish references, including after a\nlease ID has been pruned and recreated. Published receipts never retain payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>After stopping the server, \u003ccode>leases prune\u003c/code> removes inactive lease records and\nunreferenced inventory generations. It validates lease metadata, committed active\nchains and the complete inventory-directory shape before the first removal.\nActive generations remain. Obsolete generation contents need not be usable\ninventories, but their files must have the expected shape and cannot be symlinks.\nCleanup removes direct regular files and empty generation directories, with no\nrecursive traversal through unknown directories. The command&#39;s \u003ccode>removed\u003c/code> count\nstill counts lease records; it does not count inventory pages or directories.\nInterruption during cleanup is safe to retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-and-disk-format\">Protocol and disk format\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/upload-inventories/protocol-and-disk-format\" aria-label=\"Link to Protocol and disk format\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>GET /v1/capabilities\u003c/code> advertises \u003ccode>upload_inventories: 1\u003c/code> alongside\n\u003ccode>upload_leases: 1\u003c/code>. New lease creation adds \u003ccode>inventory: true\u003c/code> to the existing\n\u003ccode>PUT /v1/uploads/ID\u003c/code> body. That mode is immutable for the lease. Renewal preserves\nthe mode and current committed prefix; a newer server does not silently upgrade\nan active legacy lease. A saved active inventory cannot silently downgrade when\nthe server stops advertising the extension.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX\u003c/code> accepts exactly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"64-character lowercase hex token\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"objects\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"kind\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"chunk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The request cap is \u003cstrong>1 MiB\u003c/strong> and successful registration returns \u003cstrong>204\u003c/strong>. Pages\nmust be contiguous from zero; identical retries below the current count are\naccepted. Wrong/expired/released fences return 412, an unknown lease returns 404,\nand malformed, conflicting, oversized or out-of-order pages are rejected.\nRegistration uses the normal bounded idempotent-request retry policy. Final\nreference CAS is still never automatically retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Inventory-enabled lease records use schema \u003cstrong>2\u003c/strong>: existing fields plus\n\u003ccode>inventory_pages\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>inventory_hash\u003c/code>. Legacy schema-1 leases remain readable.\nThe lease-record cap stays \u003cstrong>16 KiB\u003c/strong>, with at most \u003cstrong>4096\u003c/strong> records per Store.\nEach inventory permits at most \u003cstrong>4096 pages\u003c/strong>, or \u003cstrong>16,777,216 declared objects\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pages live in \u003ccode>STORE/upload-inventories/GENERATION/00000000.json\u003c/code>, where generation\nis BLAKE3 of the JSON tuple \u003ccode>[lease_id, fence]\u003c/code>. Each page is at most 1 MiB and has\nexactly \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>lease\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>checksum\u003c/code>.\nThe canonical checksum payload uses that field order without \u003ccode>checksum\u003c/code>, with\nobject keys encoded as \u003ccode>kind\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>. The initial \u003ccode>previous\u003c/code> hash is BLAKE3 of\nthe JSON tuple \u003ccode>[&quot;syrnck-upload-inventory-v1&quot;, lease_id, fence]\u003c/code>; later pages use\nthe preceding checksum. The lease&#39;s final hash is the last checksum, or the\ninitial hash for zero pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>These are ordinary local metadata: logical IDs and lease identities are visible,\nwithout tokens, URLs or encryption keys. Payload encryption is unchanged. C++\napplications must rebuild against the updated headers; object formats, Git\npointers, pack versions, local session schema and the C ABI are unchanged.\nOnly inventory-aware binaries may run maintenance on these Stores. GC and\ncleanup remain offline, exclusively locked, and local to one server Store.\nDistributed GC, object/user quotas and cross-server replication remain future work.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Selective server upload inventories # Compatible clients and servers automatically use a paged inventory to retain objects belonging to an unfinished upload. This works before the root manifest arrives. Offline GC can reclaim unrelated objects while keeping registered objects, including objects reused from an earlier version. Older upload leases keep their conservative whole-Store hold. Existing command lines still work; --require-lease requires upload leases, not this optional inventory extension. A legacy hold anywhere in the Store still blocks GC. Registration and acknowledgement # The client validates its complete source closure, acquires an inventory-enabled lease and registers each batch before querying remote presence for that batch. Each page lists all objects in the batch, including reused ones. A page contains 1..4096 strictly ordered unique object keys; ordering continues across page boundaries. The client uses the existing deterministic closure ordering. The server publishes an immutable page, then atomically replaces the lease's committed page count and hash. It sends success only after both writes complete. The existing file flush, atomic replacement and parent-directory flush rules apply. There is one registration per batch, not a new journal write per object. A lost response can safely retry the same page. A committed page must exist and match its previous contents. It cannot be replaced through registration. A crash after writing a page but before updating the lease leaves an unacknowledged page; that page is outside the committed prefix. Retrying can validate and commit the same staged page. The client must wait for registration acknowledgement before relying on a presence response or sending objects for that page. Each page includes a BLAKE3 checksum over its canonical descriptor and object keys, linked to the previous page's hash. The lease commits the final hash along with the page count. GC verifies the full committed chain, so a missing page, altered key, broken link or altered page count stops payload deletion. This is integrity checking under the Store's existing filesystem trust boundary; these metadata are not authenticated against a malicious filesystem administrator. GC, expiry and cleanup # An active inventory lease retains the existing objects explicitly listed in its committed pages . GC streams pages into the existing packed-object bitmap and loose-object marks. Present objects pass the ordinary envelope, hash and schema checks. Missing listed objects are allowed: registration can precede arrival. GC does not materialize a second complete inventory of object IDs. Registration is not proof that the root closure has arrived. Dependencies absent from the registered inventory are not implicitly retained by that inventory. Ordinary references, read leases and local transfer roots still provide their own retention. Full closure and file-hash validation remain mandatory at final reference publication. Ordinary object PUTs remain compatible and immutable; unregistered uploads have no inventory retention guarantee. fsck and pack keep their strict dependency audits and can reject incomplete uploaded metadata until the transfer finishes or unretained metadata is collected. Expiry and fencing remain as described in upload leases . Before deleting payloads, GC durably marks observed expired leases and changes their fences. Reactivation creates a fresh generation with zero pages and a new initial hash. Resume registers its batches again before renegotiating presence. Stale fences cannot register pages or publish references, including after a lease ID has been pruned and recreated. Published receipts never retain payloads. After stopping the server, leases prune removes inactive lease records and unreferenced inventory generations. It validates lease metadata, committed active chains and the complete inventory-directory shape before the first removal. Active generations remain. Obsolete generation contents need not be usable inventories, but their files must have the expected shape and cannot be symlinks. Cleanup removes direct regular files and empty generation directories, with no recursive traversal through unknown directories. The command's removed count still counts lease records; it does not count inventory pages or directories. Interruption during cleanup is safe to retry. Protocol and disk format # GET /v1/capabilities advertises upload_inventories: 1 alongside upload_leases: 1 . New lease creation adds inventory: true to the existing PUT /v1/uploads/ID body. That mode is immutable for the lease. Renewal preserves the mode and current committed prefix; a newer server does not silently upgrade an active legacy lease. A saved active inventory cannot silently downgrade when the server stops advertising the extension. PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX accepts exactly: json Copy { \"fence\" : \"64-character lowercase hex token\" , \"objects\" : [ { \"kind\" : \"chunk\" , \"id\" : \"64-character lowercase hex ID\" } ] } The request cap is 1 MiB and successful registration returns 204 . Pages must be contiguous from zero; identical retries below the current count are accepted. Wrong/expired/released fences return 412, an unknown lease returns 404, and malformed, conflicting, oversized or out-of-order pages are rejected. Registration uses the normal bounded idempotent-request retry policy. Final reference CAS is still never automatically retried. Inventory-enabled lease records use schema 2 : existing fields plus inventory_pages and inventory_hash . Legacy schema-1 leases remain readable. The lease-record cap stays 16 KiB , with at most 4096 records per Store. Each inventory permits at most 4096 pages , or 16,777,216 declared objects . Pages live in STORE/upload-inventories/GENERATION/00000000.json , where generation is BLAKE3 of the JSON tuple [lease_id, fence] . Each page is at most 1 MiB and has exactly version , lease , fence , index , previous , objects , and checksum . The canonical checksum payload uses that field order without checksum , with object keys encoded as kind , then id . The initial previous hash is BLAKE3 of the JSON tuple [\"syrnck-upload-inventory-v1\", lease_id, fence] ; later pages use the preceding checksum. The lease's final hash is the last checksum, or the initial hash for zero pages. These are ordinary local metadata: logical IDs and lease identities are visible, without tokens, URLs or encryption keys. Payload encryption is unchanged. C++ applications must rebuild against the updated headers; object formats, Git pointers, pack versions, local session schema and the C ABI are unchanged. Only inventory-aware binaries may run maintenance on these Stores. GC and cleanup remain offline, exclusively locked, and local to one server Store. Distributed GC, object/user quotas and cross-server replication remain future work. {\"fence\":\"64-character lowercase hex token\",\"objects\":[{\"kind\":\"chunk\",\"id\":\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"}]}","headings":[{"id":"selective-server-upload-inventories","text":"Selective server upload inventories","level":1},{"id":"registration-and-acknowledgement","text":"Registration and acknowledgement","level":2},{"id":"gc-expiry-and-cleanup","text":"GC, expiry and cleanup","level":2},{"id":"protocol-and-disk-format","text":"Protocol and disk format","level":2}],"anchors":["L1"],"languages":["json"],"code":"{\"fence\":\"64-character lowercase hex token\",\"objects\":[{\"kind\":\"chunk\",\"id\":\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"}]}","assets":[],"links":["#/en/server-upload-leases"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"usage","title":"Getting started","description":"Getting started guide.","section":"Getting started","order":5,"revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"parcours-du-prototype\">Getting started walkthrough\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/parcours-du-prototype\" aria-label=\"Link to Getting started walkthrough\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Examples assume \u003ccode>syrnck\u003c/code> is on PATH. In PowerShell use \u003ccode>&amp;\u003c/code> with its absolute path if needed. Reuse IDs printed by commands without the example angle brackets. Open contextual help with \u003ccode>syrnck help\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>syrnck help native-tags\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier\">Store and reconstruct a file\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier\" aria-label=\"Link to Store and reconstruct a file\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>init\u003c/code> creates a Store; \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> retains the returned manifest as \u003ccode>files/ID\u003c/code>. Reingest reports new/reused logical bytes; \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> refuses an existing destination. \u003ccode>diff\u003c/code> compares stored manifests. Reusable logical bytes do not equal network traffic: the receiver may already have other objects and repeated blocks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> accepts 1..64, default 1; \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> defaults 32 MiB, must fit a maximum chunk, maximum 1024 MiB. Pending tasks also cap at 2*workers. Read buffers, encoded results, codecs and metadata add memory beyond this raw-byte budget. In-flight peaks are zero on the sequential path. IDs/boundaries stay identical; existing blocks are verified without recompression and pending duplicates encoded once. Gains depend on new data/storage/encryption.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ uses \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code> as third ingest argument and cooperative stop_token between reads/blocks/publications. Workers finish/join before return. Error/cancellation before finalization prevents manifest creation; low-level ingest changes no refs, though orphan chunks may remain for retry/GC. CLI interruption is connected. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/settings\">preferences\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/operations\">progress\u003c/a>. Flat manifests are bounded 16 MiB during reading; paged metadata remains future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck put Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck get &lt;identifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck inspect manifest &lt;identifiant-manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck diff &lt;ancien-manifeste> &lt;nouveau-manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cache-de-lectures-verifiees\">Verified-read cache\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/cache-de-lectures-verifiees\" aria-label=\"Link to Verified-read cache\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Defaults:64 MiB decoded bytes and 8192 verified stamps, independently bounded. Evicting bytes may keep a stamp for presence checks. Every access rereads and BLAKE3-hashes the entire envelope; only exact bytes previously authenticated/decoded/verified in this handle can reuse validation. The cache reduces codec work, not envelope IO, and does not rely on mtime/size.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Global \u003ccode>--cache-mib\u003c/code> accepts 0..1024 MiB;0 disables object caching. Indexes, IO/codecs/pipeline add memory. Cache vanishes with the handle/process; repeated reads/shared blocks in a server benefit most. C++ \u003ccode>StoreOptions\u003c/code> is constructor argument 4: \u003ccode>decoded_cache_bytes=0\u003c/code> keeps stamps, \u003ccode>verified_cache_entries=0\u003c/code> disables object cache. \u003ccode>io_stats()\u003c/code> reports reads/bytes/decodes/hits; reset keeps caches, clear releases object/page caches without removing data/refs. Decoded buffers are cleared on eviction/close.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> bypass caches for current objects/dependencies/file hashes. Closure traversal hashes each manifest while reading its blocks; repeated positions still contribute each time. Index pages independently default 4 MiB, \u003ccode>--index-cache-mib\u003c/code>0..1024 or \u003ccode>pack_index_cache_bytes\u003c/code>. Directory/filter remain without cache; opening/audits verify full indexes, reread pages match validated snapshots. Index memory fields exclude overhead/temporaries. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/indexes\">paged indexes\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --cache-mib 0 get &lt;manifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"regrouper-les-objets-en-packs\">Group objects into packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/regrouper-les-objets-en-packs\" aria-label=\"Link to Group objects into packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> audits then appends packs for loose-only objects, leaving existing packs unchanged. Compact regroups all; defaults 512 MiB/65536 objects. IDs/refs/encryption envelopes stay intact. GC rewrites partly dead packs and drops wholly dead packs only after retained closure validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Streaming GC uses one bit per packed entry. \u003ccode>packed_mark_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>loose_live_objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>peak_pending_objects\u003c/code> report useful marks/keys/stack, not total RAM. \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs/memoire-du-gc\">GC budgets\u003c/a> include loose live keys/metadata.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Maintenance exclusively locks the Store; stop a server using it first. Explicit pack selects physicalv2/v3; opening does not convert, older binaries refuse unknown formats. Limits 1,048,576 objects/pack,1024 packs,4,194,304 packed objects. New packs need disk space before removing old ones. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/packs\">publication/recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck pack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck pack --compact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"historique-autonome\">Standalone history\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/historique-autonome\" aria-label=\"Link to Standalone history\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Adapt \u003ccode>examples/unreal.syrnckignore\u003c/code> into \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>. The Store must be inside the committed root, normally \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code>. Native commands use the current branch, implicitly main in old Stores. Explicit \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> does not change selection. \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>switch-plan\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>switch\u003c/code> inspect/change it recoverably without moving tips. Ordinary commit scans the directory and refuses a staging index. Export requires a new destination with existing parent. Keep files stable while reading.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>add\u003c/code> freezes selected paths including deletions; \u003ccode>add .\u003c/code> selects all. \u003ccode>reset PATH...\u003c/code> resets selected index paths to base, while no-path reset clears the index even if stale; neither edits working files. Staging is retained through GC and consumed atomically with the branch commit. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-staging\">staging\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-hunks\">hunks\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-lines\">lines\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Status hashes without importing; revision/branch choose its base. Diff normally compares snapshots and has explicit \u003ca href=\"#/en/text-diff\">text options\u003c/a>. Both produce JSON with progress/cancellation. Combined status separates base→index and index→work. Checkout-plan is read-only, detects ignored/untracked collisions and requires tracked clean/no index; verify audits the target closure. A blocked plan emits JSON/code 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Checkout moves the selected branch while archiving its old head. Resume/abort are alternatives after interruption. Sequential file moves are journaled; branch/archive/target publish together after verification. After publication only cleanup resumes. GC/native writes block while pending; prepared/backed-up bytes stay plaintext even with encrypted objects. Stop external writers. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-checkout\">recovery\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tags name committed versions only, default HEAD, with optional annotation. Existing names fail. Expected-tip deletion atomically archives target/annotation/receipt; GC retains them. Restore preserves archive and refuses collisions. Selectors allow HEAD, unambiguous names, heads/NAME,tags/NAME; ambiguous names require explicit form. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-tags\">tags\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>History detachment archives the old tip under \u003ccode>archive/ID\u003c/code> before moving the branch. Removing a commit is distinct from releasing roots and physical collection. Use \u003ca href=\"#/en/archive-retention\">backup-protected retirement\u003c/a> before dropping archives. Other roots still retain shared bytes; fully unretained collected versions cannot be reconstructed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native status --revision &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-plan &lt;identifiant-revision> --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native diff &lt;ancienne-revision> &lt;nouvelle-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native branch release &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L10\" data-line=\"10\">syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L11\" data-line=\"11\">syrnck native export &lt;identifiant-revision> ../export-release\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout &lt;identifiant-revision> --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tags --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native tag-archive &lt;identifiant-archive> --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native tag-restore &lt;identifiant-archive>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-11-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-11-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"git-et-assets\">Git and assets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/git-et-assets\" aria-label=\"Link to Git and assets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Inside an existing Git repository, install the filters, then add selected asset patterns to \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code>. The Store is in the common Git directory under syrnck; installation prints its path. Configuration is repository-local with an absolute executable path, needing repair if moved. Source code remains ordinary Git blobs while assets become pointers in Git and full bytes in the workspace. Apply attributes to existing paths with targeted \u003ccode>git add --renormalize\u003c/code>, inspect before commit, and never stack LFS/Syrnck on the same path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Offline \u003ccode>git lfs-preview HEAD\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets\u003c/code> verifies local SHA-256 LFS data, ingests blocks and prepares a child without changing HEAD/index/workspace. To convert ancestry use \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-lfs-history\">full LFS history migration\u003c/a>. \u003ccode>git/ID\u003c/code> pins protect assets independently of Git reachability. Retention-scan inventories refs/reflogs/all registered worktree indexes without opening the Store; retention verifies and previews missing pins, apply adds atomically without deleting unobserved pins. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-retention\">retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Explicit agent transfers by manifest remain available. For normal workflows use \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-assets\">commit catalogs\u003c/a>: configure remote URL and checkout download to fetch missing objects under a read lease, verify complete bytes before output; local remains default. Skip retains pointers and permits status/add without fetching; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1\u003c/code> overrides without Store/key. A policy change does not rewrite existing files. To rehydrate an indexed pointer, preserve wanted edits, remove only that working pointer, then \u003ccode>git checkout -- PATH\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git catalog COMMIT\u003c/code> reads pointers without Store; push-assets/pull-assets transfer their verified closure. Opt-in install-hook sends all ancestors before Git publication and fails on missing old bytes/network errors. Tokens stay in environment. Existing hooks are not replaced; custom core.hooksPath needs manual integration. A local hook can be bypassed and does not replace server-side Git validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#/en/git-filter-process\">resident filter-process\u003c/a> shares one Store/cache across a Git command, holding its exclusive lock until Git closes it. Other operations fail quickly while held. Single clean/smudge remain available.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-12-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>gitattributes\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-gitattributes\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-13-L1\" data-line=\"1\">*.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-13-L2\" data-line=\"2\">*.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L2\" data-line=\"2\">git commit -m \"Carte et code\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L3\" data-line=\"3\">git switch -c feature\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-15-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git> refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-15-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/&lt;manifeste> asset-release\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-16-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-16-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/&lt;manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"serveur-et-agent\">Server and agent\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/serveur-et-agent\" aria-label=\"Link to Server and agent\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Create a separate server Store. Configure \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-access\">users/permissions\u003c/a> or use the legacy shared token, at least 32 characters in SYRNCK_TOKEN on authorized peers. Avoid tokens in URLs/command lines. Server listens loopback; use a TLS proxy for remote access. Clients refuse non-loopback HTTP/redirects and accept a private CA with --ca. Deployment/certificate automation is not provided.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Agent push/pull are one-shot, without disk watching/conflict resolution. Retry reuses verified objects; incomplete refs never publish. The running server owns the exclusive Store lock and does no online GC. Persistent local sessions protect complete sources or partial received objects; resume uses recorded root/expected destination even after source-ref removal. Forget releases protection; prune previews completed receipts, --delete removes only verified completed journals, never incomplete sessions or data. Local administration needs no server token; uncertain publication is checked before retrying CAS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Transfers support request/buffer bounds, retries 0..8(default 3), shared upload/download KiB/s with 0 unlimited. Waits cancel; final ref publication is not automatically retried. Compression auto-negotiates; off uses raw, zstd requires support if missing objects. Small/incompressible data may stay raw. Logical successful bytes differ from actual HTTP attempt bytes; throttling counts network bytes and compression budgets do not bound total RAM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Upload leases/inventories negotiate automatically; require-lease enforces them and server lease-seconds configures lifetime. Offline leases list/release/prune administer them; new uploads declare paged inventories before presence queries for selective GC. Legacy leases without inventories still block GC globally. Expiry permits unretained collection and resume declares anew. Read leases protect exact roots through pull and are released once all data is local, before local publication; require-read-lease enforces support. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/transfer-sessions\">sessions\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-upload-leases\">upload\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/server-read-leases\">read leases\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/en/upload-inventories\">inventories\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/selective-retention\">retention\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-17-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store serveur-data init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-17-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-18-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native log --branch received\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"chiffrement-au-repos\">Encryption at rest\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/chiffrement-au-repos\" aria-label=\"Link to Encryption at rest\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The PowerShell example generates a key into SYRNCK_KEY; save it in a secret manager before closing. Reuse that key on every encrypted Store open. Plain stores must open without it. \u003ca href=\"#/en/backups\">Backups\u003c/a> can copy to another Store/key; in-place rotation, distributed revocation and key recovery remain future work.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Each Store can use its own key. Transfers send decoded objects over secure transport and destinations reencrypt them. This does not provide end-to-end encryption against the server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>powershell\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-powershell\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L1\" data-line=\"1\">$env:SYRNCK_KEY = (&amp; syrnck keygen)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"interface-c\">C interface\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/interface-c\" aria-label=\"Link to C interface\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Build \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi_static\u003c/code> for dynamic/static libraries. Include \u003ccode>syrnck.h\u003c/code>, use UTF-8 paths and close handles. Status codes and thread-local last_error convey failures; serialize Store calls. ABI1 exposes verified object IO, closures, pins, file verification, ranges/comparison and \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">workspace sessions\u003c/a>. Network client is C++. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-c-abi\">ownership\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#/en/go-binding\">Go binding\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ignore-example\">Example Unreal project exclusions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/usage/ignore-example\" aria-label=\"Link to Example Unreal project exclusions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Adapt this sample to your own project before creating commits. Excluded working files are not retained by a native snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>gitignore\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-gitignore\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L1\" data-line=\"1\"># Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L2\" data-line=\"2\">**/Binaries/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L3\" data-line=\"3\">**/DerivedDataCache/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L4\" data-line=\"4\">**/Intermediate/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L5\" data-line=\"5\">**/Saved/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L6\" data-line=\"6\">/.vs/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L7\" data-line=\"7\">/.idea/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L8\" data-line=\"8\">/.tools/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L9\" data-line=\"9\">/target/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L10\" data-line=\"10\">*.sln\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L11\" data-line=\"11\">*.suo\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L12\" data-line=\"12\">*.key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L13\" data-line=\"13\">*.pem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L14\" data-line=\"14\"># Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L15\" data-line=\"15\"># Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Getting started walkthrough # Examples assume syrnck is on PATH. In PowerShell use & with its absolute path if needed. Reuse IDs printed by commands without the example angle brackets. Open contextual help with syrnck help or syrnck help native-tags . Store and reconstruct a file # init creates a Store; put retains the returned manifest as files/ID . Reingest reports new/reused logical bytes; get refuses an existing destination. diff compares stored manifests. Reusable logical bytes do not equal network traffic: the receiver may already have other objects and repeated blocks. --workers accepts 1..64, default 1; --buffer-mib defaults 32 MiB, must fit a maximum chunk, maximum 1024 MiB. Pending tasks also cap at 2*workers. Read buffers, encoded results, codecs and metadata add memory beyond this raw-byte budget. In-flight peaks are zero on the sequential path. IDs/boundaries stay identical; existing blocks are verified without recompression and pending duplicates encoded once. Gains depend on new data/storage/encryption. C++ uses IngestOptions as third ingest argument and cooperative stop_token between reads/blocks/publications. Workers finish/join before return. Error/cancellation before finalization prevents manifest creation; low-level ingest changes no refs, though orphan chunks may remain for retry/GC. CLI interruption is connected. See preferences and progress . Flat manifests are bounded 16 MiB during reading; paged metadata remains future work. text Copy syrnck init syrnck put Content/Map.uasset syrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset syrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> syrnck fsck text Copy syrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste> text Copy syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 Verified-read cache # Defaults:64 MiB decoded bytes and 8192 verified stamps, independently bounded. Evicting bytes may keep a stamp for presence checks. Every access rereads and BLAKE3-hashes the entire envelope; only exact bytes previously authenticated/decoded/verified in this handle can reuse validation. The cache reduces codec work, not envelope IO, and does not rely on mtime/size. Global --cache-mib accepts 0..1024 MiB;0 disables object caching. Indexes, IO/codecs/pipeline add memory. Cache vanishes with the handle/process; repeated reads/shared blocks in a server benefit most. C++ StoreOptions is constructor argument 4: decoded_cache_bytes=0 keeps stamps, verified_cache_entries=0 disables object cache. io_stats() reports reads/bytes/decodes/hits; reset keeps caches, clear releases object/page caches without removing data/refs. Decoded buffers are cleared on eviction/close. fsck / gc bypass caches for current objects/dependencies/file hashes. Closure traversal hashes each manifest while reading its blocks; repeated positions still contribute each time. Index pages independently default 4 MiB, --index-cache-mib 0..1024 or pack_index_cache_bytes . Directory/filter remain without cache; opening/audits verify full indexes, reread pages match validated snapshots. Index memory fields exclude overhead/temporaries. See paged indexes . text Copy syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin Group objects into packs # pack audits then appends packs for loose-only objects, leaving existing packs unchanged. Compact regroups all; defaults 512 MiB/65536 objects. IDs/refs/encryption envelopes stay intact. GC rewrites partly dead packs and drops wholly dead packs only after retained closure validation. Streaming GC uses one bit per packed entry. packed_mark_bytes , loose_live_objects , peak_pending_objects report useful marks/keys/stack, not total RAM. GC budgets include loose live keys/metadata. Maintenance exclusively locks the Store; stop a server using it first. Explicit pack selects physicalv2/v3; opening does not convert, older binaries refuse unknown formats. Limits 1,048,576 objects/pack,1024 packs,4,194,304 packed objects. New packs need disk space before removing old ones. See publication/recovery . text Copy syrnck pack syrnck pack --compact syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck gc syrnck gc --delete Standalone history # Adapt examples/unreal.syrnckignore into .syrnckignore . The Store must be inside the committed root, normally .syrnck . Native commands use the current branch, implicitly main in old Stores. Explicit --branch does not change selection. head , switch-plan , switch inspect/change it recoverably without moving tips. Ordinary commit scans the directory and refuses a staging index. Export requires a new destination with existing parent. Keep files stable while reading. add freezes selected paths including deletions; add . selects all. reset PATH... resets selected index paths to base, while no-path reset clears the index even if stale; neither edits working files. Staging is retained through GC and consumed atomically with the branch commit. See staging , hunks , lines . Status hashes without importing; revision/branch choose its base. Diff normally compares snapshots and has explicit text options . Both produce JSON with progress/cancellation. Combined status separates base→index and index→work. Checkout-plan is read-only, detects ignored/untracked collisions and requires tracked clean/no index; verify audits the target closure. A blocked plan emits JSON/code 1. Checkout moves the selected branch while archiving its old head. Resume/abort are alternatives after interruption. Sequential file moves are journaled; branch/archive/target publish together after verification. After publication only cleanup resumes. GC/native writes block while pending; prepared/backed-up bytes stay plaintext even with encrypted objects. Stop external writers. See recovery . Tags name committed versions only, default HEAD, with optional annotation. Existing names fail. Expected-tip deletion atomically archives target/annotation/receipt; GC retains them. Restore preserves archive and refuses collisions. Selectors allow HEAD, unambiguous names, heads/NAME,tags/NAME; ambiguous names require explicit form. See tags . History detachment archives the old tip under archive/ID before moving the branch. Removing a commit is distinct from releasing roots and physical collection. Use backup-protected retirement before dropping archives. Other roots still retain shared bytes; fully unretained collected versions cannot be reconstructed. text Copy syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\" syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\" syrnck native status --progress syrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native status --combined syrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify syrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision> syrnck native log syrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\" syrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release text Copy syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress syrnck native staged syrnck native reset Source syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\" text Copy syrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress text Copy syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify syrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive> text Copy syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\" syrnck refs syrnck gc text Copy syrnck refs syrnck gc Git and assets # Inside an existing Git repository, install the filters, then add selected asset patterns to .gitattributes . The Store is in the common Git directory under syrnck; installation prints its path. Configuration is repository-local with an absolute executable path, needing repair if moved. Source code remains ordinary Git blobs while assets become pointers in Git and full bytes in the workspace. Apply attributes to existing paths with targeted git add --renormalize , inspect before commit, and never stack LFS/Syrnck on the same path. Offline git lfs-preview HEAD / git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets verifies local SHA-256 LFS data, ingests blocks and prepares a child without changing HEAD/index/workspace. To convert ancestry use full LFS history migration . git/ID pins protect assets independently of Git reachability. Retention-scan inventories refs/reflogs/all registered worktree indexes without opening the Store; retention verifies and previews missing pins, apply adds atomically without deleting unobserved pins. See retention . Explicit agent transfers by manifest remain available. For normal workflows use commit catalogs : configure remote URL and checkout download to fetch missing objects under a read lease, verify complete bytes before output; local remains default. Skip retains pointers and permits status/add without fetching; SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 overrides without Store/key. A policy change does not rewrite existing files. To rehydrate an indexed pointer, preserve wanted edits, remove only that working pointer, then git checkout -- PATH . git catalog COMMIT reads pointers without Store; push-assets/pull-assets transfer their verified closure. Opt-in install-hook sends all ancestors before Git publication and fails on missing old bytes/network errors. Tokens stay in environment. Existing hooks are not replaced; custom core.hooksPath needs manual integration. A local hook can be bypassed and does not replace server-side Git validation. The resident filter-process shares one Store/cache across a Git command, holding its exclusive lock until Git closes it. Other operations fail quickly while held. Single clean/smudge remain available. text Copy syrnck git install gitattributes Copy *.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text *.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text text Copy git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp git commit -m \"Carte et code\" git switch -c feature text Copy syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release text Copy syrnck git install syrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste> Server and agent # Create a separate server Store. Configure users/permissions or use the legacy shared token, at least 32 characters in SYRNCK_TOKEN on authorized peers. Avoid tokens in URLs/command lines. Server listens loopback; use a TLS proxy for remote access. Clients refuse non-loopback HTTP/redirects and accept a private CA with --ca. Deployment/certificate automation is not provided. Agent push/pull are one-shot, without disk watching/conflict resolution. Retry reuses verified objects; incomplete refs never publish. The running server owns the exclusive Store lock and does no online GC. Persistent local sessions protect complete sources or partial received objects; resume uses recorded root/expected destination even after source-ref removal. Forget releases protection; prune previews completed receipts, --delete removes only verified completed journals, never incomplete sessions or data. Local administration needs no server token; uncertain publication is checked before retrying CAS. Transfers support request/buffer bounds, retries 0..8(default 3), shared upload/download KiB/s with 0 unlimited. Waits cancel; final ref publication is not automatically retried. Compression auto-negotiates; off uses raw, zstd requires support if missing objects. Small/incompressible data may stay raw. Logical successful bytes differ from actual HTTP attempt bytes; throttling counts network bytes and compression budgets do not bound total RAM. Upload leases/inventories negotiate automatically; require-lease enforces them and server lease-seconds configures lifetime. Offline leases list/release/prune administer them; new uploads declare paged inventories before presence queries for selective GC. Legacy leases without inventories still block GC globally. Expiry permits unretained collection and resume declares anew. Read leases protect exact roots through pull and are released once all data is local, before local publication; require-read-lease enforces support. See sessions , upload , read leases , inventories and retention . text Copy syrnck --store serveur-data init syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437 text Copy syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main text Copy syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received syrnck native log --branch received syrnck fsck Encryption at rest # The PowerShell example generates a key into SYRNCK_KEY; save it in a secret manager before closing. Reuse that key on every encrypted Store open. Plain stores must open without it. Backups can copy to another Store/key; in-place rotation, distributed revocation and key recovery remain future work. Each Store can use its own key. Transfers send decoded objects over secure transport and destinations reencrypt them. This does not provide end-to-end encryption against the server. powershell Copy $env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen) syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset C interface # Build syrnck_ffi and syrnck_ffi_static for dynamic/static libraries. Include syrnck.h , use UTF-8 paths and close handles. Status codes and thread-local last_error convey failures; serialize Store calls. ABI1 exposes verified object IO, closures, pins, file verification, ranges/comparison and workspace sessions . Network client is C++. See ownership and Go binding . Example Unreal project exclusions # Adapt this sample to your own project before creating commits. Excluded working files are not retained by a native snapshot. gitignore Copy # Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits. **/Binaries/ **/DerivedDataCache/ **/Intermediate/ **/Saved/ /.vs/ /.idea/ /.tools/ /target/ *.sln *.suo *.key *.pem # Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file. # Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case. syrnck init syrnck put Content/Map.uasset syrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset syrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> syrnck fsck syrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste> syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck pack syrnck pack --compact syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck gc syrnck gc --delete syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\" syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\" syrnck native status --progress syrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native status --combined syrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify syrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision> syrnck native log syrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\" syrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress syrnck native staged syrnck native reset Source syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\" syrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify syrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive> syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\" syrnck refs syrnck gc syrnck refs syrnck gc syrnck git install *.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text *.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp git commit -m \"Carte et code\" git switch -c feature syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release syrnck git install syrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste> syrnck --store serveur-data init syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437 syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received syrnck native log --branch received syrnck fsck $env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen) syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset # Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits. **/Binaries/ **/DerivedDataCache/ **/Intermediate/ **/Saved/ /.vs/ /.idea/ /.tools/ /target/ *.sln *.suo *.key *.pem # Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file. # Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.","headings":[{"id":"parcours-du-prototype","text":"Getting started walkthrough","level":1},{"id":"stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier","text":"Store and reconstruct a file","level":2},{"id":"cache-de-lectures-verifiees","text":"Verified-read cache","level":2},{"id":"regrouper-les-objets-en-packs","text":"Group objects into packs","level":2},{"id":"historique-autonome","text":"Standalone history","level":2},{"id":"git-et-assets","text":"Git and assets","level":2},{"id":"serveur-et-agent","text":"Server and agent","level":2},{"id":"chiffrement-au-repos","text":"Encryption at rest","level":2},{"id":"interface-c","text":"C interface","level":2},{"id":"ignore-example","text":"Example Unreal project exclusions","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-5-L5","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-6-L6","code-6-L7","code-6-L8","code-6-L9","code-6-L10","code-6-L11","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-7-L4","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-9-L1","code-9-L2","code-9-L3","code-9-L4","code-9-L5","code-9-L6","code-9-L7","code-10-L1","code-10-L2","code-10-L3","code-11-L1","code-11-L2","code-12-L1","code-13-L1","code-13-L2","code-14-L1","code-14-L2","code-14-L3","code-15-L1","code-15-L2","code-16-L1","code-16-L2","code-17-L1","code-17-L2","code-18-L1","code-19-L1","code-19-L2","code-19-L3","code-20-L1","code-20-L2","code-20-L3","code-21-L1","code-21-L2","code-21-L3","code-21-L4","code-21-L5","code-21-L6","code-21-L7","code-21-L8","code-21-L9","code-21-L10","code-21-L11","code-21-L12","code-21-L13","code-21-L14","code-21-L15"],"languages":["text","gitattributes","powershell","gitignore"],"code":"syrnck init\nsyrnck put Content/Map.uasset\nsyrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset\nsyrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste>\nsyrnck fsck\nsyrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste>\nsyrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\nsyrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin\nsyrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\nsyrnck pack\nsyrnck pack --compact\nsyrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck gc --delete\nsyrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\"\nsyrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\"\nsyrnck native status --progress\nsyrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native status --combined\nsyrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify\nsyrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision>\nsyrnck native log\nsyrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\"\nsyrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release\nsyrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native reset Source\nsyrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\"\nsyrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\"\nsyrnck native tags --limit 100\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\nsyrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify\nsyrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive>\nsyrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\"\nsyrnck refs\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck refs\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck git install\n*.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\n*.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\ngit add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp\ngit commit -m \"Carte et code\"\ngit switch -c feature\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste>\nsyrnck --store serveur-data init\nsyrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437\nsyrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main\nsyrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received\nsyrnck native log --branch received\nsyrnck fsck\n$env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen)\nsyrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted\nsyrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset\n# Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits.\n**/Binaries/\n**/DerivedDataCache/\n**/Intermediate/\n**/Saved/\n/.vs/\n/.idea/\n/.tools/\n/target/\n*.sln\n*.suo\n*.key\n*.pem\n# Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file.\n# Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.","assets":[],"links":["#/en/settings","#/en/operations","#/en/indexes","#/en/packs/memoire-du-gc","#/en/packs","#/en/native-staging","#/en/native-hunks","#/en/native-lines","#/en/text-diff","#/en/native-checkout","#/en/native-tags","#/en/archive-retention","#/en/git-lfs-history","#/en/git-retention","#/en/git-assets","#/en/git-filter-process","#/en/server-access","#/en/transfer-sessions","#/en/server-upload-leases","#/en/server-read-leases","#/en/upload-inventories","#/en/selective-retention","#/en/backups","#/en/workspace-abi","#/en/api-c-abi","#/en/go-binding"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"working-index","title":"Persistent native working index","description":"Persistent native working index guide.","section":"Storage","order":630,"parent":"storage","revision":"1","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-native-working-index\">Persistent native working index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/working-index/persistent-native-working-index\" aria-label=\"Link to Persistent native working index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native status --indexed\u003c/code> opts into a local advisory cache of verified working\nfile hashes. It avoids rereading unchanged asset contents when the filesystem\ncan provide the required identity and change metadata. Ordinary \u003ccode>native status\u003c/code>\ncontinues to hash every selected file in full.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status --indexed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native index-clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The combined form retains the existing branch/index/workspace comparisons,\nincluding stale staging. The cache describes observed working files; it is\nseparate from the immutable staging index and never stages or commits content.\nJSON field names are English in both interface languages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reuse-and-fallback\">Reuse and fallback\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/working-index/reuse-and-fallback\" aria-label=\"Link to Reuse and fallback\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Each cached entry records a repository path, size, full BLAKE3 file hash,\nexecutable metadata and a digest of the filesystem identity/change stamp.\nSupported files are opened for metadata queries before and after observation.\nReuse requires the identity, modification time, change time, size and mode or\nattributes to match the previous verified observation. Recent or future change\nand modification times bypass reuse; a two-second guard is deliberately\nconservative. This detects ordinary in-place writes that restore both size and\nmodification time, as well as replacement by another file with matching bytes\nor timestamps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Platform\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Files eligible for reuse\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Windows\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fixed local NTFS/ReFS volumes; 128-bit file ID, volume identity, last-write/change times and attributes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Linux\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>ext-family, XFS, Btrfs and tmpfs; device/inode, nanosecond mtime/ctime and mode\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>macOS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Local APFS; device/inode, nanosecond mtime/ctime and mode\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Other filesystems/platforms\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Full hashing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>UNC/network paths, unrecognized filesystems, failed identity queries and recent\nwrites use full hashing. Windows metadata handles deny write sharing: an active\nwriter can defer timestamp updates until close, so its file cannot reuse a\ncached hash. A supported identity changing during observation fails the scan\nand requires a retry. Existing ignore rules, reparse/symlink rejection and\nportable namespace checks still apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This is a stat-based acceleration with local cache trust, not an integrity\naudit or an atomic filesystem snapshot. Deliberate metadata forgery, clock\nmanipulation and arbitrary concurrent filesystem replacement are outside its\nassumptions. Use ordinary status for an independent byte comparison.\nCheckout, reset, stash, commit and staging still use their existing complete\nreads and publication fences; they never authorize replacement using this cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"persistence-and-lifetime\">Persistence and lifetime\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/working-index/persistence-and-lifetime\" aria-label=\"Link to Persistence and lifetime\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The Store contains one local \u003ccode>working-index.json\u003c/code> pointer, bound to the hash of\nthe canonical workspace root. Its target is an ordinary content-addressed chunk\ncontaining the index. It uses the Store&#39;s existing compression and, for an\nencrypted Store, object encryption. The pointer contains only identifiers.\nIt is replaced atomically after the complete scan and final progress observer.\nAn observer changing that pointer causes publication to fail instead of\noverwriting the change. Cancellation may leave an unreferenced index chunk,\nbut does not publish a partial pointer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The cache is disposable. It is not a reference or a version-retention root.\nGC can collect its objects; logical backups and replication do not depend on\nit. Missing objects, corrupt envelopes and malformed documents trigger a fresh\nscan and a new cache generation. Linked/nonregular or oversized pointer files\nare rejected rather than overwritten. \u003ccode>index-clear\u003c/code> removes only the local\npointer; ordinary GC can reclaim the remaining chunks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A damaged old object is not repaired in place. It remains visible to a full\nStore audit until ordinary GC collects the unreferenced cache generation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>There is currently one cached workspace per Store. A different canonical root\nrebuilds that cache. Each index object is bounded by 16 MiB; if entries exceed\nits budget, the scan remains complete and only a subset is cached. Reports\nmark \u003ccode>truncated: true\u003c/code>. Snapshots, maps, JSON reports and other allocations are\nadditional memory; this limit is not a total process RAM bound.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reports-c-and-measurement\">Reports, C++ and measurement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/working-index/reports-c-and-measurement\" aria-label=\"Link to Reports, C++ and measurement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Indexed reports preserve the ordinary fields and add \u003ccode>working_index\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>previous\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>invalid\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>other_workspace\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>hashed_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reused_files\u003c/code>: counts for this observation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>supported_files\u003c/code>: files with stable supported identities, including recent\nfiles that still needed full hashing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>cached_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>truncated\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>object\u003c/code>: the new bounded cache contents.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>hashed_bytes\u003c/code> counts actual working content bytes read, excluding stored\nmetadata. A reused file adds zero. Indexed status can write disposable cache\ndata and emit \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> progress; ordinary status remains read-only.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/working_index.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">indexed_native_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> combined \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">indexed_native_workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> cleared \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">clear_native_working_index\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Directory enumeration and ignore evaluation still run on every indexed query,\nso additions, removals, renamed paths and ignore changes are observed. The\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-watch\">resident watcher\u003c/a> triggers queries from native notifications\nand full audits, with bounded hints and overflow recovery. Its complete session\ninventory supports targeted file/subtree replacement for edits, creations,\nremovals, renames, type transitions and nested ignore changes, without unrelated\nsubtree enumeration. Root rules, lost continuity and ambiguous selection still\nforce complete byte scans. Linux creation/import events extend registrations\nbefore returning targeted subtree hints. Directory removals and moves of\nexisting watched directories still force a full scan to cover inotify recovery\ngaps. The watcher offers an optional persistent checkpoint journal; replayable\nevent history and incremental native-watch removal/rename reconciliation remain\nseparate work. \u003ca href=\"#/en/workspace-abi\">C ABI and Go workspace sessions\u003c/a> expose the\nsame native/polling observation and cache, retaining hints across waits while\ncaller-owned Store handles reopen for each query.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Metadata API references: \u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/ns-winbase-file_basic_info\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Windows basic information\u003c/a>,\n\u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-getfinalpathnamebyhandlew\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Windows final paths\u003c/a>\nand \u003ca href=\"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/stat.3type.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Linux stat fields\u003c/a>.\nWindows sharing rules: \u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-createfilea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CreateFile\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Persistent native working index # native status --indexed opts into a local advisory cache of verified working file hashes. It avoids rereading unchanged asset contents when the filesystem can provide the required identity and change metadata. Ordinary native status continues to hash every selected file in full. text Copy syrnck native status --indexed syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION syrnck native index-clear The combined form retains the existing branch/index/workspace comparisons, including stale staging. The cache describes observed working files; it is separate from the immutable staging index and never stages or commits content. JSON field names are English in both interface languages. Reuse and fallback # Each cached entry records a repository path, size, full BLAKE3 file hash, executable metadata and a digest of the filesystem identity/change stamp. Supported files are opened for metadata queries before and after observation. Reuse requires the identity, modification time, change time, size and mode or attributes to match the previous verified observation. Recent or future change and modification times bypass reuse; a two-second guard is deliberately conservative. This detects ordinary in-place writes that restore both size and modification time, as well as replacement by another file with matching bytes or timestamps. Platform Files eligible for reuse Windows Fixed local NTFS/ReFS volumes; 128-bit file ID, volume identity, last-write/change times and attributes Linux ext-family, XFS, Btrfs and tmpfs; device/inode, nanosecond mtime/ctime and mode macOS Local APFS; device/inode, nanosecond mtime/ctime and mode Other filesystems/platforms Full hashing UNC/network paths, unrecognized filesystems, failed identity queries and recent writes use full hashing. Windows metadata handles deny write sharing: an active writer can defer timestamp updates until close, so its file cannot reuse a cached hash. A supported identity changing during observation fails the scan and requires a retry. Existing ignore rules, reparse/symlink rejection and portable namespace checks still apply. This is a stat-based acceleration with local cache trust, not an integrity audit or an atomic filesystem snapshot. Deliberate metadata forgery, clock manipulation and arbitrary concurrent filesystem replacement are outside its assumptions. Use ordinary status for an independent byte comparison. Checkout, reset, stash, commit and staging still use their existing complete reads and publication fences; they never authorize replacement using this cache. Persistence and lifetime # The Store contains one local working-index.json pointer, bound to the hash of the canonical workspace root. Its target is an ordinary content-addressed chunk containing the index. It uses the Store's existing compression and, for an encrypted Store, object encryption. The pointer contains only identifiers. It is replaced atomically after the complete scan and final progress observer. An observer changing that pointer causes publication to fail instead of overwriting the change. Cancellation may leave an unreferenced index chunk, but does not publish a partial pointer. The cache is disposable. It is not a reference or a version-retention root. GC can collect its objects; logical backups and replication do not depend on it. Missing objects, corrupt envelopes and malformed documents trigger a fresh scan and a new cache generation. Linked/nonregular or oversized pointer files are rejected rather than overwritten. index-clear removes only the local pointer; ordinary GC can reclaim the remaining chunks. A damaged old object is not repaired in place. It remains visible to a full Store audit until ordinary GC collects the unreferenced cache generation. There is currently one cached workspace per Store. A different canonical root rebuilds that cache. Each index object is bounded by 16 MiB; if entries exceed its budget, the scan remains complete and only a subset is cached. Reports mark truncated: true . Snapshots, maps, JSON reports and other allocations are additional memory; this limit is not a total process RAM bound. Reports, C++ and measurement # Indexed reports preserve the ordinary fields and add working_index : previous : missing , ready , invalid or other_workspace . hashed_files , reused_files : counts for this observation. supported_files : files with stable supported identities, including recent files that still needed full hashing. cached_files , truncated , object : the new bounded cache contents. hashed_bytes counts actual working content bytes read, excluding stored metadata. A reused file adds zero. Indexed status can write disposable cache data and emit publishing progress; ordinary status remains read-only. cpp Copy # include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp> auto report = syrnck :: indexed_native_status ( store , project , revision , operation ) ; auto combined = syrnck :: indexed_native_workspace_status ( store , project , \"main\" , operation ) ; auto cleared = syrnck :: clear_native_working_index ( store , operation ) ; Directory enumeration and ignore evaluation still run on every indexed query, so additions, removals, renamed paths and ignore changes are observed. The resident watcher triggers queries from native notifications and full audits, with bounded hints and overflow recovery. Its complete session inventory supports targeted file/subtree replacement for edits, creations, removals, renames, type transitions and nested ignore changes, without unrelated subtree enumeration. Root rules, lost continuity and ambiguous selection still force complete byte scans. Linux creation/import events extend registrations before returning targeted subtree hints. Directory removals and moves of existing watched directories still force a full scan to cover inotify recovery gaps. The watcher offers an optional persistent checkpoint journal; replayable event history and incremental native-watch removal/rename reconciliation remain separate work. C ABI and Go workspace sessions expose the same native/polling observation and cache, retaining hints across waits while caller-owned Store handles reopen for each query. Metadata API references: Windows basic information , Windows final paths and Linux stat fields . Windows sharing rules: CreateFile . syrnck native status --indexed syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION syrnck native index-clear #include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp> auto report = syrnck::indexed_native_status(store, project, revision, operation); auto combined = syrnck::indexed_native_workspace_status(store, project, \"main\", operation); auto cleared = syrnck::clear_native_working_index(store, operation);","headings":[{"id":"persistent-native-working-index","text":"Persistent native working index","level":1},{"id":"reuse-and-fallback","text":"Reuse and fallback","level":2},{"id":"persistence-and-lifetime","text":"Persistence and lifetime","level":2},{"id":"reports-c-and-measurement","text":"Reports, C++ and measurement","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status --indexed\nsyrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json\nsyrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION\nsyrnck native index-clear\n#include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp>\nauto report = syrnck::indexed_native_status(store, project, revision, operation);\nauto combined = syrnck::indexed_native_workspace_status(store, project, \"main\", operation);\nauto cleared = syrnck::clear_native_working_index(store, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/en/native-watch","#/en/workspace-abi","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/ns-winbase-file_basic_info","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-getfinalpathnamebyhandlew","https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/stat.3type.html","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/fileapi/nf-fileapi-createfilea"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"workspace-abi","title":"Embedded workspace observation through C and Go","description":"Embedded workspace observation through C and Go guide.","section":"API","order":640,"parent":"integrations","revision":"7","locale":"en","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go\">Embedded workspace observation through C and Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go\" aria-label=\"Link to Embedded workspace observation through C and Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>The resident workspace watcher and its complete session inventory are available\nthrough \u003ccode>SyrnckWorkspace\u003c/code> in the \u003ca href=\"#/en/api-c-abi\">C header\u003c/a> and \u003ccode>Workspace\u003c/code> in\nthe Go binding (\u003ccode>../bindings/go/workspace.go\u003c/code>). They use the same Windows/Linux\nnotifications, polling fallback and targeted/full observation as\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/native-watch\">\u003ccode>native watch\u003c/code>\u003c/a>. These are additive ABI 1 symbols; use the\nheader, binding and native library from the same release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifetime-and-locking\">Lifetime and locking\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/lifetime-and-locking\" aria-label=\"Link to Lifetime and locking\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003col>\n\u003cli>Create the Store and workspace directories first. For primary sessions, the Store must be strictly\nbelow the workspace root. Registered linked sessions use a disjoint Store\nand require their exact registered directory. Both paths are resolved at open.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the workspace session before its initial status. Native notifications\nare already armed when open returns. The session retains its root directory\nidentity, watcher and in-memory inventory, with no Store handle or Store lock.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the Store only for a status query, supplying its encryption key normally.\nQuery then close the Store before consuming output or waiting. The session\ndoes not retain the Store handle or its key.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wait for changes, then reopen the Store for the next status. The first query,\nexplicit invalidation and recovery read all selected working bytes. Ordinary\nnative hints select complete file/subtree prefixes; polling enumerates with\nthe disposable working hash cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Schedule independent complete audits, for example every five minutes, by\ninvalidating before the next query. Idle notifications do not prove that all\nfilesystem changes were reported. Close the workspace explicitly at shutdown.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Calls on each C handle must be serialized, including close. The Go wrapper\nserializes workspace calls with its own mutex and locks the Store during status,\nusing the workspace-then-Store order. A caller can still keep a Store open while\nwaiting; that caller would keep its exclusive Store lock unnecessarily.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The session retains every batch consumed by a wait until a successful status.\nMultiple successful waits and an ignored wait result do not lose hints. Native\ncancellation invalidates continuity even if it interrupts batch processing; the\nnext successful status performs a complete byte scan instead of trusting a\npartial inventory. Coalescing deduplicates paths, preserves\nfull-scan/polling flags and requests a full scan after exceeding the configured\npath count or 1 MiB accounting budget. Failed native waits, status queries and\nJSON allocation/serialization invalidate inventory continuity. Pending hints are\ncleared only after successful status serialization. A changed root identity\nrequires closing/reopening the session; it never silently adopts another root.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Notification delivery is asynchronous. Status collects one currently ready batch;\nan edit whose notification has not arrived can still be absent from that report.\nWait for the relevant hints before requesting event-driven status. Call\n\u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> before a synchronous complete byte comparison instead. Observations\nremain advisory while the filesystem is changing; they authorize no destructive\nworkspace operation to skip its own preflight.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-calls-and-output-ownership\">C calls and output ownership\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/c-calls-and-output-ownership\" aria-label=\"Link to C calls and output ownership\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Call\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Open/arm; NULL options means native notifications with polling fallback, 4096 hints and 65536 directory registrations\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_wait_json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Wait once for 0..60000 ms, retain the batch and return its hints\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_status_json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Collect one immediate native batch and observe all retained hints against a caller-opened Store\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_invalidate\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Require a complete byte scan on the next status\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_close\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Release native resources and inventory; NULL is accepted\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Supplied \u003ccode>SyrnckWorkspaceOptions\u003c/code> has three \u003ccode>uint32_t\u003c/code> fields: \u003ccode>polling\u003c/code> (0/1),\n\u003ccode>max_paths\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>max_directories\u003c/code> (each 1..65536). C zero limits are invalid;\nNULL options selects all defaults. Go zero limits individually select defaults.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Both JSON calls return an allocated \u003ccode>char *\u003c/code> plus a length including its trailing\nNUL. Release it exactly once with \u003ccode>syrnck_string_free\u003c/code>, including results that\nare no longer needed. Output is capped at 128 MiB plus NUL. There is no\nsize-query/re-execution pair for these stateful calls. Output pointers and\nlengths reset to NULL/zero on failure when supplied; copy thread-local diagnostic\ntext before another ABI call. A failed query never returns an older status as\ncurrent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Wait JSON has \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>backend: windows|inotify|polling\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>paths\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>full_scan\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>overflow\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>polling\u003c/code>. It describes that native wait, while the\nsession may retain earlier batches too. Status JSON has the same schema as the\nordinary or combined native status report, including working-index/observation\nmetrics. Full fallback is visible in the working report&#39;s\n\u003ccode>observation.full_scan\u003c/code>, including when a coalesced budget triggered recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Status selection modes are:\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Selector\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>0\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Branch name; NULL follows selected named or detached native HEAD\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Required revision selector, resolved by the native revision resolver\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Combined staging/working status; branch name or NULL for selected HEAD\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>An explicit empty selector is invalid. Unknown working branches have an empty\nbaseline, matching ordinary status. The Store must be the one used at session\nopen. Active recoverable checkout and HEAD bound to another workspace are\nrefused. Status can update the disposable hash cache; it does not ingest working\ncontents, publish a revision or change staging/references.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>c\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-c\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">char\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>json \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">size_t\u003c/span> length \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">int\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_workspace_status_json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_store_close\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">    \u003cspan class=\"token function\">consume_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* consume while allocation is alive */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_string_free\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress\">Cooperative cancellation and C progress\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress\" aria-label=\"Link to Cooperative cancellation and C progress\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The existing calls keep their signatures and delegate to \u003ccode>*_ex\u003c/code> with no\ncancellation source/callback. Additional ABI 1 calls are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>syrnck_cancellation_create\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>syrnck_cancellation_request\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>syrnck_cancellation_close\u003c/code> manage one-shot sources.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open_ex\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_wait_json_ex\u003c/code> accept a\nnullable cancellation source, including recursive registration/recovery.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex\u003c/code> accepts a nullable \u003ccode>SyrnckOperation\u003c/code> with a\nsource, synchronous progress callback and caller-owned user pointer.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Request is idempotent and may run concurrently with the using operation or\nother requests. All using calls and requests must return before closing the\nsource. There is no reset: create a fresh source for another operation. Request\non NULL fails; close on NULL succeeds. No source is stored in the workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cancellation observed by the native operation returns \u003ccode>SYRNCK_CANCELLED\u003c/code> (3),\nwith NULL output and zero length. A cancelled open returns no handle. A cancelled\nwait/status invalidates the session; retry without a requested source requires\na complete observation. A request after the final output check can follow a\nsuccessful result. Individual OS IO, filesystem metadata operations and JSON\nencoding may finish before a cooperative checkpoint; there is no hard latency\nbound. Cancellation can leave updated/cleared disposable hash caches or\ncollectible cache objects. References, staging, retained file data and working\ncontents are not changed by these advisory observations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Progress callbacks execute synchronously on the caller&#39;s thread. \u003ccode>SyrnckProgress\u003c/code>\ncontains the stable \u003ccode>SyrnckProgressPhase\u003c/code> number, borrowed path bytes and\nlength, byte count, optional total, completion flag and buffered-block count.\nPaths are not necessarily NUL terminated. Copy anything needed after the callback\nreturns; neither the event nor the callback/user pointer is retained. Never\nreenter/close the Store or workspace, throw, or unwind through the ABI from a\ncallback. It may call \u003ccode>syrnck_cancellation_request\u003c/code> safely. These callbacks are\navailable in C; the Go context API does not yet expose progress callbacks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>c\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-c\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">static\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">void\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">stop_after_first_chunk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> SyrnckProgress \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">void\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>user\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">->\u003c/span>phase \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">==\u003c/span> SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;&amp;\u003c/span> event\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">->\u003c/span>bytes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1024\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1024\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">        \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_cancellation_request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>SyrnckCancellation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>user\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">SyrnckOperation operation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop_after_first_chunk\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">int\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">                                            \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">   threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"go-usage\">Go usage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/go-usage\" aria-label=\"Link to Go usage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenWorkspace\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>OpenWorkspaceContext\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Wait\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>StatusContext\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> wrap those operations.\n\u003ccode>Wait\u003c/code> returns typed \u003ccode>WorkspaceChanges\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>Status\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>json.RawMessage\u003c/code> so\nall current status fields remain available. Its zero \u003ccode>WorkspaceSelection\u003c/code>\nfollows HEAD, \u003ccode>Branch\u003c/code> overrides it, \u003ccode>Revision\u003c/code> selects an exact/resolved\nsnapshot and \u003ccode>Combined\u003c/code> separates staging from work. Revision excludes the\nother two options. \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> is idempotent; calls on a closed workspace return\n\u003ccode>ErrWorkspaceClosed\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copy\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">defer w.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">closeErr := s.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L10\" data-line=\"10\">if closeErr != nil { return closeErr }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L11\" data-line=\"11\">if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L12\" data-line=\"12\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L13\" data-line=\"13\">changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L14\" data-line=\"14\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L15\" data-line=\"15\">// Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L16\" data-line=\"16\">_ = changes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Go validates UTF-8/NUL inputs, normalizes zero option limits, serializes calls,\ncopies native output into Go memory, frees native allocations and reads\nthread-local errors on the same OS thread as the failed call. Wait accepts\n0..one minute and passes cancellation into a single native wait. Native idle\nwaits check stop requests about every 50 ms; polling becomes actionable at the\nrequested deadline. Registration and status hashing also use cooperative stop\nchecks. \u003ccode>OpenWorkspace\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Status\u003c/code> use \u003ccode>context.Background()\u003c/code> for compatibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Context cancellation maps native status 3 to \u003ccode>context.Canceled\u003c/code> or\n\u003ccode>context.DeadlineExceeded\u003c/code>. The callback that requests the native source is\nstopped/joined before the source is released. Each call owns a fresh source;\na late request cannot cancel a later query. A completed native success/error\nwins over cancellation arriving after its final check. A context that expires\nbefore native execution returns its error without starting the operation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Wait/StatusContext can also abandon the workspace/Store serialization wait when\nthe context expires. Lock order remains workspace-then-Store, with the existing\nstable Store order retained by differential copying. Close still waits for any\nactive call, so cancel a long call before closing. Ordinary Store methods keep\ntheir existing cancellation behavior; this context bridge currently applies to\nworkspace opening, waiting, status and the guarded native actions below.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scope-and-validation\">Scope and validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/scope-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Link to Scope and validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>This is advisory status, not a locked filesystem snapshot or a transfer agent.\nIt does not preserve intermediate file versions, maintain a replayable event\nhistory or expose the CLI&#39;s persistent checkpoint journal. A restart always\ncreates a new inventory and performs a complete scan. Service lifecycle,\ncausal versions and transfers are managed above this observer; see the separate\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/folder-controller\">continuous BEP controller\u003c/a> and its remaining\nfolder/conflict/service limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registered-contexts\">Registered contexts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/registered-contexts\" aria-label=\"Link to Registered contexts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex(directory, store_path, name, options, cancellation, out)\u003c/code> for a registered linked workspace. Before each status,\nselect the same context on the caller-opened Store with\n\u003ccode>syrnck_store_use_workspace(store, name)\u003c/code>. NULL or empty selects primary.\nAn unknown name fails without changing selection; mismatched sessions/Stores\nfail without returning stale output. Never change selection from a progress\ncallback. These additive symbols require matching rebuilt ABI 1 libraries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Go uses \u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code> at open and \u003ccode>store.UseWorkspace(&quot;edit&quot;)\u003c/code>\nbefore status. The session retains no Store/key/lock. Linked native watchers\nobserve external refs/config metadata conservatively and require periodic full\naudits as primary sessions do. Registration and portable restore are exposed by\nthe \u003ca href=\"#/en/native-workspaces\">CLI and C++ API\u003c/a>, not by C/Go management calls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-workspace-actions\">Guarded native workspace actions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\" aria-label=\"Link to Guarded native workspace actions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The additive C ABI also exposes \u003ccode>workspace_native_state_json_ex\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>workspace_capture_json_ex\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>workspace_checkout_plan_json_ex\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>workspace_checkout_owned_json_ex\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>workspace_recover_owned_json_ex\u003c/code>.\nUse the distributed declarations for the complete signatures. Each call binds\nits supplied Store to the session&#39;s exact canonical storage path, registered\ncontext and directory; even another valid Store is refused.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Read state first. The report includes selected \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, its actual reference,\ntyped \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> or null, HEAD, materialization policy, staging flag and the\nselected context&#39;s checkout status. Reading state during an interruption does\nnot recover it. \u003ccode>SyrnckNativeExpected\u003c/code> contains target, nullable revision and\nnullable materialization object ID. NULL means absence, not a wildcard. Capture,\nplan and checkout refuse a changed target, revision or policy before mutations.\nNever update an old expectation merely to force the same operation through.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Capture retains selected working files through the ordinary native commit\ntransaction. It preserves ignored files, unmaterialized paths, detached HEAD\nand authored-commit behavior, and refuses existing staging/checkout work. The\nmessage is at most 64 KiB. Plan verifies the target closure and reports readiness\nwithout moving refs or changing working bytes. A blocked plan is a successful\nreport with \u003ccode>ready:false\u003c/code>. Checkout rechecks those conditions and applies only\nto a clean selected workspace through the recoverable native journal. It uses\nan explicit 64-hex owner identity and the existing reader feature gate.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Before starting a service-owned checkout, durably retain its exact owner,\ncandidate and causal input. Recovery accepts only a currently pending journal\nwith that owner, candidate, selected target, context and physical workspace.\nA foreign/manual journal or no pending journal is refused. Recovery does not\nrequire the private key that signed an already prepared target. Signed target\nobjects and their signatures stay unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>All methods share allocated output ownership and workspace-then-Store locking.\nMutation attempts invalidate observation continuity; the next Status reads all\nselected bytes. Native cancellation remains cooperative. A successful native\npublication wins late cancellation. A nonzero result can follow publication or\nleave a resumable journal: read native state and establish the outcome before\nretrying or recovering. Do not infer absence of changes from empty error output.\nClose Store before waiting for notifications or network responses.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Go provides \u003ccode>NativeState\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>NativeStateContext\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Capture\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>PlanCheckout\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>PlanCheckoutContext\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CheckoutOwned\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>CheckoutOwnedContext\u003c/code> and\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>RecoverOwnedContext\u003c/code> on Workspace. \u003ccode>state.Expected()\u003c/code> carries\nits exact target/revision/materialization snapshot. Capture returns a revision\nID; plan/checkout/recovery return canonical \u003ccode>json.RawMessage\u003c/code>. Context methods\nuse the same safe per-call cancellation bridge. They do not implement a resident\nBEP controller or automatically announce that a working folder has caught up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_checkout_status_json_ex(workspace, store, transaction, operation, out, length)\u003c/code> reads one exact 64-hex transaction through the core\njournal/receipt decoder, without recovery or publication. A completed receipt\nreturns pending=false and state=committed, plus its original owner/source/target/\nbranch/context/directory. It verifies the matching retained target and bound\nworkspace; completed lookup does not diff historical file trees. These facts\nprove past application, not current working cleanliness. Go exposes this as\n\u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext\u003c/code>. Use a unique durable owner per pending application,\nretain its causal inputs before checkout and capture later local edits after\nacknowledging that completed base.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Explicit active-edit preservation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Link to Explicit active-edit preservation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_recover_owned_preserving_json_ex\u003c/code> has the same exact\nStore/workspace/context/target/candidate/owner contract as ordinary owned\nrecovery. It explicitly archives regular-file edits, makes exclusive conflict\ncopies and resumes the original strict journal. Default recovery stays strict.\nGo exposes \u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreserving\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreservingContext\u003c/code>; C++\nuses \u003ccode>Store::recover_checkout_owned_preserving_files\u003c/code>. The JSON report adds\n\u003ccode>preserved_edits\u003c/code> with source path, copy path and archived revision ID. Publication\nerrors still require reloading native state and retained descriptors. See the\n\u003ca href=\"#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">policy and remaining limits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Session capture reuse\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/en/workspace-abi/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Link to Session capture reuse\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Repeated captures reuse session-ingested manifests with unchanged strong local\nfile stamps, while enumerating the full selection and verifying the complete\nobject closure. Capture JSON adds source read/reuse counters. Restart,\ninvalidation and failed native calls discard reuse; the disposable status index\nnever becomes mutation proof. See \u003ca href=\"#/en/performance/incremental-capture\">capture and fallback\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Embedded workspace observation through C and Go # The resident workspace watcher and its complete session inventory are available through SyrnckWorkspace in the C header and Workspace in the Go binding ( ../bindings/go/workspace.go ). They use the same Windows/Linux notifications, polling fallback and targeted/full observation as native watch . These are additive ABI 1 symbols; use the header, binding and native library from the same release. Lifetime and locking # Create the Store and workspace directories first. For primary sessions, the Store must be strictly below the workspace root. Registered linked sessions use a disjoint Store and require their exact registered directory. Both paths are resolved at open. Open the workspace session before its initial status. Native notifications are already armed when open returns. The session retains its root directory identity, watcher and in-memory inventory, with no Store handle or Store lock. Open the Store only for a status query, supplying its encryption key normally. Query then close the Store before consuming output or waiting. The session does not retain the Store handle or its key. Wait for changes, then reopen the Store for the next status. The first query, explicit invalidation and recovery read all selected working bytes. Ordinary native hints select complete file/subtree prefixes; polling enumerates with the disposable working hash cache. Schedule independent complete audits, for example every five minutes, by invalidating before the next query. Idle notifications do not prove that all filesystem changes were reported. Close the workspace explicitly at shutdown. Calls on each C handle must be serialized, including close. The Go wrapper serializes workspace calls with its own mutex and locks the Store during status, using the workspace-then-Store order. A caller can still keep a Store open while waiting; that caller would keep its exclusive Store lock unnecessarily. The session retains every batch consumed by a wait until a successful status. Multiple successful waits and an ignored wait result do not lose hints. Native cancellation invalidates continuity even if it interrupts batch processing; the next successful status performs a complete byte scan instead of trusting a partial inventory. Coalescing deduplicates paths, preserves full-scan/polling flags and requests a full scan after exceeding the configured path count or 1 MiB accounting budget. Failed native waits, status queries and JSON allocation/serialization invalidate inventory continuity. Pending hints are cleared only after successful status serialization. A changed root identity requires closing/reopening the session; it never silently adopts another root. Notification delivery is asynchronous. Status collects one currently ready batch; an edit whose notification has not arrived can still be absent from that report. Wait for the relevant hints before requesting event-driven status. Call Invalidate before a synchronous complete byte comparison instead. Observations remain advisory while the filesystem is changing; they authorize no destructive workspace operation to skip its own preflight. C calls and output ownership # Call Effect syrnck_workspace_open Open/arm; NULL options means native notifications with polling fallback, 4096 hints and 65536 directory registrations syrnck_workspace_wait_json Wait once for 0..60000 ms, retain the batch and return its hints syrnck_workspace_status_json Collect one immediate native batch and observe all retained hints against a caller-opened Store syrnck_workspace_invalidate Require a complete byte scan on the next status syrnck_workspace_close Release native resources and inventory; NULL is accepted Supplied SyrnckWorkspaceOptions has three uint32_t fields: polling (0/1), max_paths and max_directories (each 1..65536). C zero limits are invalid; NULL options selects all defaults. Go zero limits individually select defaults. Both JSON calls return an allocated char * plus a length including its trailing NUL. Release it exactly once with syrnck_string_free , including results that are no longer needed. Output is capped at 128 MiB plus NUL. There is no size-query/re-execution pair for these stateful calls. Output pointers and lengths reset to NULL/zero on failure when supplied; copy thread-local diagnostic text before another ABI call. A failed query never returns an older status as current. Wait JSON has version: 1 , backend: windows|inotify|polling , paths , full_scan , overflow and polling . It describes that native wait, while the session may retain earlier batches too. Status JSON has the same schema as the ordinary or combined native status report, including working-index/observation metrics. Full fallback is visible in the working report's observation.full_scan , including when a coalesced budget triggered recovery. Status selection modes are: Mode Selector 0 Branch name; NULL follows selected named or detached native HEAD 1 Required revision selector, resolved by the native revision resolver 2 Combined staging/working status; branch name or NULL for selected HEAD An explicit empty selector is invalid. Unknown working branches have an empty baseline, matching ordinary status. The Store must be the one used at session open. Active recoverable checkout and HEAD bound to another workspace are refused. Status can update the disposable hash cache; it does not ingest working contents, publish a revision or change staging/references. c Copy /* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */ char * json = NULL ; size_t length = 0 ; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json ( workspace , store , NULL , 0 , & json , & length ) ; /* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */ syrnck_store_close ( store ) ; if ( result == 0 ) { consume_status ( json , length - 1 ) ; /* consume while allocation is alive */ syrnck_string_free ( json ) ; } /* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */ Cooperative cancellation and C progress # The existing calls keep their signatures and delegate to *_ex with no cancellation source/callback. Additional ABI 1 calls are: syrnck_cancellation_create , syrnck_cancellation_request and syrnck_cancellation_close manage one-shot sources. syrnck_workspace_open_ex and syrnck_workspace_wait_json_ex accept a nullable cancellation source, including recursive registration/recovery. syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex accepts a nullable SyrnckOperation with a source, synchronous progress callback and caller-owned user pointer. Request is idempotent and may run concurrently with the using operation or other requests. All using calls and requests must return before closing the source. There is no reset: create a fresh source for another operation. Request on NULL fails; close on NULL succeeds. No source is stored in the workspace. Cancellation observed by the native operation returns SYRNCK_CANCELLED (3), with NULL output and zero length. A cancelled open returns no handle. A cancelled wait/status invalidates the session; retry without a requested source requires a complete observation. A request after the final output check can follow a successful result. Individual OS IO, filesystem metadata operations and JSON encoding may finish before a cooperative checkpoint; there is no hard latency bound. Cancellation can leave updated/cleared disposable hash caches or collectible cache objects. References, staging, retained file data and working contents are not changed by these advisory observations. Progress callbacks execute synchronously on the caller's thread. SyrnckProgress contains the stable SyrnckProgressPhase number, borrowed path bytes and length, byte count, optional total, completion flag and buffered-block count. Paths are not necessarily NUL terminated. Copy anything needed after the callback returns; neither the event nor the callback/user pointer is retained. Never reenter/close the Store or workspace, throw, or unwind through the ABI from a callback. It may call syrnck_cancellation_request safely. These callbacks are available in C; the Go context API does not yet expose progress callbacks. c Copy static void stop_after_first_chunk ( const SyrnckProgress * event , void * user ) { if ( event -> phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event -> bytes >= 1024 * 1024 ) syrnck_cancellation_request ( ( SyrnckCancellation * ) user ) ; } /* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */ SyrnckOperation operation = { cancellation , stop_after_first_chunk , cancellation } ; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex ( workspace , store , NULL , 0 , & operation , & json , & length ) ; /* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */ Go usage # OpenWorkspace , OpenWorkspaceContext , Wait , Status , StatusContext , Invalidate and Close wrap those operations. Wait returns typed WorkspaceChanges ; Status returns json.RawMessage so all current status fields remain available. Its zero WorkspaceSelection follows HEAD, Branch overrides it, Revision selects an exact/resolved snapshot and Combined separates staging from work. Revision excludes the other two options. Close is idempotent; calls on a closed workspace return ErrWorkspaceClosed . go Copy w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{}) if err != nil { return err } defer w.Close() s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key}) if err != nil { return err } report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true}) closeErr := s.Close() if err != nil { return err } if closeErr != nil { return closeErr } if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err } changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second) if err != nil { return err } // Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due. _ = changes Go validates UTF-8/NUL inputs, normalizes zero option limits, serializes calls, copies native output into Go memory, frees native allocations and reads thread-local errors on the same OS thread as the failed call. Wait accepts 0..one minute and passes cancellation into a single native wait. Native idle waits check stop requests about every 50 ms; polling becomes actionable at the requested deadline. Registration and status hashing also use cooperative stop checks. OpenWorkspace and Status use context.Background() for compatibility. Context cancellation maps native status 3 to context.Canceled or context.DeadlineExceeded . The callback that requests the native source is stopped/joined before the source is released. Each call owns a fresh source; a late request cannot cancel a later query. A completed native success/error wins over cancellation arriving after its final check. A context that expires before native execution returns its error without starting the operation. Wait/StatusContext can also abandon the workspace/Store serialization wait when the context expires. Lock order remains workspace-then-Store, with the existing stable Store order retained by differential copying. Close still waits for any active call, so cancel a long call before closing. Ordinary Store methods keep their existing cancellation behavior; this context bridge currently applies to workspace opening, waiting, status and the guarded native actions below. Scope and validation # This is advisory status, not a locked filesystem snapshot or a transfer agent. It does not preserve intermediate file versions, maintain a replayable event history or expose the CLI's persistent checkpoint journal. A restart always creates a new inventory and performs a complete scan. Service lifecycle, causal versions and transfers are managed above this observer; see the separate continuous BEP controller and its remaining folder/conflict/service limits. Registered contexts # Use syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex(directory, store_path, name, options, cancellation, out) for a registered linked workspace. Before each status, select the same context on the caller-opened Store with syrnck_store_use_workspace(store, name) . NULL or empty selects primary. An unknown name fails without changing selection; mismatched sessions/Stores fail without returning stale output. Never change selection from a progress callback. These additive symbols require matching rebuilt ABI 1 libraries. Go uses WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} at open and store.UseWorkspace(\"edit\") before status. The session retains no Store/key/lock. Linked native watchers observe external refs/config metadata conservatively and require periodic full audits as primary sessions do. Registration and portable restore are exposed by the CLI and C++ API , not by C/Go management calls. Guarded native workspace actions # The additive C ABI also exposes workspace_native_state_json_ex , workspace_capture_json_ex , workspace_checkout_plan_json_ex , workspace_checkout_owned_json_ex and workspace_recover_owned_json_ex . Use the distributed declarations for the complete signatures. Each call binds its supplied Store to the session's exact canonical storage path, registered context and directory; even another valid Store is refused. Read state first. The report includes selected target , its actual reference, typed revision or null, HEAD, materialization policy, staging flag and the selected context's checkout status. Reading state during an interruption does not recover it. SyrnckNativeExpected contains target, nullable revision and nullable materialization object ID. NULL means absence, not a wildcard. Capture, plan and checkout refuse a changed target, revision or policy before mutations. Never update an old expectation merely to force the same operation through. Capture retains selected working files through the ordinary native commit transaction. It preserves ignored files, unmaterialized paths, detached HEAD and authored-commit behavior, and refuses existing staging/checkout work. The message is at most 64 KiB. Plan verifies the target closure and reports readiness without moving refs or changing working bytes. A blocked plan is a successful report with ready:false . Checkout rechecks those conditions and applies only to a clean selected workspace through the recoverable native journal. It uses an explicit 64-hex owner identity and the existing reader feature gate. Before starting a service-owned checkout, durably retain its exact owner, candidate and causal input. Recovery accepts only a currently pending journal with that owner, candidate, selected target, context and physical workspace. A foreign/manual journal or no pending journal is refused. Recovery does not require the private key that signed an already prepared target. Signed target objects and their signatures stay unchanged. All methods share allocated output ownership and workspace-then-Store locking. Mutation attempts invalidate observation continuity; the next Status reads all selected bytes. Native cancellation remains cooperative. A successful native publication wins late cancellation. A nonzero result can follow publication or leave a resumable journal: read native state and establish the outcome before retrying or recovering. Do not infer absence of changes from empty error output. Close Store before waiting for notifications or network responses. Go provides NativeState / NativeStateContext , Capture / CaptureContext , PlanCheckout / PlanCheckoutContext , CheckoutOwned / CheckoutOwnedContext and RecoverOwned / RecoverOwnedContext on Workspace. state.Expected() carries its exact target/revision/materialization snapshot. Capture returns a revision ID; plan/checkout/recovery return canonical json.RawMessage . Context methods use the same safe per-call cancellation bridge. They do not implement a resident BEP controller or automatically announce that a working folder has caught up. syrnck_workspace_checkout_status_json_ex(workspace, store, transaction, operation, out, length) reads one exact 64-hex transaction through the core journal/receipt decoder, without recovery or publication. A completed receipt returns pending=false and state=committed, plus its original owner/source/target/ branch/context/directory. It verifies the matching retained target and bound workspace; completed lookup does not diff historical file trees. These facts prove past application, not current working cleanliness. Go exposes this as CheckoutStatusContext . Use a unique durable owner per pending application, retain its causal inputs before checkout and capture later local edits after acknowledging that completed base. Explicit active-edit preservation # syrnck_workspace_recover_owned_preserving_json_ex has the same exact Store/workspace/context/target/candidate/owner contract as ordinary owned recovery. It explicitly archives regular-file edits, makes exclusive conflict copies and resumes the original strict journal. Default recovery stays strict. Go exposes RecoverOwnedPreserving and RecoverOwnedPreservingContext ; C++ uses Store::recover_checkout_owned_preserving_files . The JSON report adds preserved_edits with source path, copy path and archived revision ID. Publication errors still require reloading native state and retained descriptors. See the policy and remaining limits . Session capture reuse # Repeated captures reuse session-ingested manifests with unchanged strong local file stamps, while enumerating the full selection and verifying the complete object closure. Capture JSON adds source read/reuse counters. Restart, invalidation and failed native calls discard reuse; the disposable status index never becomes mutation proof. See capture and fallback . /* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */ char *json = NULL; size_t length = 0; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &json, &length); /* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */ syrnck_store_close(store); if (result == 0) { consume_status(json, length - 1); /* consume while allocation is alive */ syrnck_string_free(json); } /* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */ static void stop_after_first_chunk(const SyrnckProgress *event, void *user) { if (event->phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event->bytes >= 1024 * 1024) syrnck_cancellation_request((SyrnckCancellation *)user); } /* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */ SyrnckOperation operation = {cancellation, stop_after_first_chunk, cancellation}; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &operation, &json, &length); /* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */ w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{}) if err != nil { return err } defer w.Close() s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key}) if err != nil { return err } report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true}) closeErr := s.Close() if err != nil { return err } if closeErr != nil { return closeErr } if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err } changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second) if err != nil { return err } // Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due. _ = changes","headings":[{"id":"embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go","text":"Embedded workspace observation through C and Go","level":1},{"id":"lifetime-and-locking","text":"Lifetime and locking","level":2},{"id":"c-calls-and-output-ownership","text":"C calls and output ownership","level":2},{"id":"cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress","text":"Cooperative cancellation and C progress","level":2},{"id":"go-usage","text":"Go usage","level":2},{"id":"scope-and-validation","text":"Scope and validation","level":2},{"id":"registered-contexts","text":"Registered contexts","level":2},{"id":"native-workspace-actions","text":"Guarded native workspace actions","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Explicit active-edit preservation","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Session capture reuse","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8","code-3-L9","code-3-L10","code-3-L11","code-3-L12","code-3-L13","code-3-L14","code-3-L15","code-3-L16"],"languages":["c","go"],"code":"/* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */\nchar *json = NULL;\nsize_t length = 0;\nint result = syrnck_workspace_status_json(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &json, &length);\n/* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */\nsyrnck_store_close(store);\nif (result == 0) {\n    consume_status(json, length - 1); /* consume while allocation is alive */\n    syrnck_string_free(json);\n}\n/* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */\nstatic void stop_after_first_chunk(const SyrnckProgress *event, void *user) {\n    if (event->phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event->bytes >= 1024 * 1024)\n        syrnck_cancellation_request((SyrnckCancellation *)user);\n}\n/* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */\nSyrnckOperation operation = {cancellation, stop_after_first_chunk, cancellation};\nint result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex(workspace, store, NULL, 0,\n                                            &operation, &json, &length);\n/* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request\n   threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */\nw, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer w.Close()\n\ns, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key})\nif err != nil { return err }\nreport, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true})\ncloseErr := s.Close()\nif err != nil { return err }\nif closeErr != nil { return closeErr }\nif err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err }\n\nchanges, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second)\nif err != nil { return err }\n// Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due.\n_ = changes","assets":[],"links":["#/en/api-c-abi","#/en/native-watch","#/en/syncthing/folder-controller","#/en/native-workspaces","#/en/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/en/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide"},{"id":"api-c-abi","title":"ABI C : propriété et erreurs","description":"ABI C : propriété et erreurs","section":"API","parent":"integrations","order":100,"kind":"api","symbol":"syrnck_store_open","source":"include/syrnck.h:73","sourceRevision":"3a43c7e65bba03865086e2ab8b537c7295dfd209c75e7d3ef8d664edcbafcade","locale":"fr","html":"\u003cp>Inclure \u003ccode>&lt;syrnck.h&gt;\u003c/code> et utiliser la bibliothèque \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> correspondante ; définir SYRNCK_STATIC pour le lien statique.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Handles et tampons\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi/ownership\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Handles et tampons\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ouvrir avec syrnck_store_open puis fermer une seule fois. Sérialiser les appels et la fermeture de chaque handle.\nLa session de workspace ne garde aucun Store ; l&#39;ouvrir pour les requêtes. Chemins et chaînes sont UTF-8. Les tampons appartiennent\nà l&#39;appelant, sauf allocation explicite d&#39;une chaîne à libérer avec syrnck_string_free.\nUn ID demande 65 octets, NUL inclus. Les requêtes de taille diffèrent pour le JSON et les objets\nbinaires : respecter les commentaires de chaque déclaration dans l&#39;en-tête distribué.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors\">Codes de retour et diagnostics\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi/errors\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Codes de retour et diagnostics\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L&#39;ABI 1 rend 0 pour le succès, 1 pour une erreur ordinaire, 2 pour une exception inconnue et 3 pour\nune annulation coopérative dans les appels acceptant une opération. Aucun objet ni exception C++\nne traverse l&#39;interface. Copier syrnck_last_error ou appeler syrnck_error_copy immédiatement après\nl&#39;erreur, sur le même thread OS. Les messages anglais canoniques sont limités à 4095 octets.\nNe pas libérer le pointeur local au thread.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"catalogs\">Préparer et publier les catalogues\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi/catalogs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer et publier les catalogues\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>syrnck_object_put crée un bloc ou objet de métadonnées natif canonique sans référence. C++ calcule\nl&#39;ID typé et refuse les champs JSON en double. syrnck_ingest_buffer_ex (&lt;=16 Mio) et\nsyrnck_prepare_file_ex (flux) préparent des manifests sans référence ni fichier temporaire en clair.\nPublier une racine avant de libérer le Store au GC ; ingest_file_ex garde son pin ancien ffi/ID.\nsyrnck_refs_json lit la table typée. syrnck_refs_compare_json compare un groupe complet de lignes\nname/expected/value et vérifie les fermetures avant une seule publication. Une comparaison échouée\nn&#39;autorise pas à recibler un ancien plan. Sur statut non nul, applied remis à zéro ne prouve rien :\nune erreur IO peut suivre la publication atomique ; relire et établir son résultat avant de réessayer.\nVoir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/go-binding/catalog-transactions\">transactions de catalogue\u003c/a> pour les règles et l&#39;exemple.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-actions\">Actions natives de workspace protégées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi/native-actions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Actions natives de workspace protégées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>workspace_native_state_json_ex lit cible, révision typée, matérialisation et état de checkout.\nCapture et checkout exigent la cible/révision/objet de sélection exacts dans SyrnckNativeExpected.\nCapture conserve le travail local ; plan est consultatif ; checkout_owned utilise le journal\nnatif et refuse le travail modifié. recover_owned exige propriétaire et candidat persistés exacts.\nCapture expose lectures/réutilisations ; les empreintes de session ne prennent pas l’index pour preuve.\nLa fermeture complète reste vérifiée ; voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance/incremental-capture\">capture\u003c/a>.\ncheckout_status_json_ex lit journal/reçu exact par le cœur sans application. Un reçu terminé prouve\nl’application passée par propriétaire/source/cible ; une édition ultérieure reste du travail séparé.\nrecover_owned_preserving_json_ex archive explicitement les éditions ordinaires et crée des copies\nexclusives avant reprise stricte ; recover_owned par défaut garde ses contrôles stricts.\nUne session est liée à un chemin de Store, contexte enregistré et dossier de travail. Les mutations\ninvalident la continuité ; une capture réussie initialise plutôt l’inventaire complet.\nEn erreur, recharger : la publication peut être terminée.\nVoir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\">actions natives\u003c/a> pour reprise et verrouillage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Observation et annulation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi/operations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Observation et annulation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une source d&#39;annulation est à usage unique ; la demande est autorisée depuis un autre thread ou\nun callback de progression. Sérialiser sa destruction avec ses utilisateurs. Les chaînes du\ncallback sont empruntées uniquement pendant l&#39;appel. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions\u003c/a>,\nle \u003ca href=\"#/fr/go-binding\">binding Go\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-ranges\">plages vérifiées\u003c/a> pour les contrats et exemples.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Inclure \u003csyrnck.h> et utiliser la bibliothèque syrnck_ffi correspondante ; définir SYRNCK_STATIC pour le lien statique. Handles et tampons # Ouvrir avec syrnck_store_open puis fermer une seule fois. Sérialiser les appels et la fermeture de chaque handle. La session de workspace ne garde aucun Store ; l'ouvrir pour les requêtes. Chemins et chaînes sont UTF-8. Les tampons appartiennent à l'appelant, sauf allocation explicite d'une chaîne à libérer avec syrnck_string_free. Un ID demande 65 octets, NUL inclus. Les requêtes de taille diffèrent pour le JSON et les objets binaires : respecter les commentaires de chaque déclaration dans l'en-tête distribué. Codes de retour et diagnostics # L'ABI 1 rend 0 pour le succès, 1 pour une erreur ordinaire, 2 pour une exception inconnue et 3 pour une annulation coopérative dans les appels acceptant une opération. Aucun objet ni exception C++ ne traverse l'interface. Copier syrnck_last_error ou appeler syrnck_error_copy immédiatement après l'erreur, sur le même thread OS. Les messages anglais canoniques sont limités à 4095 octets. Ne pas libérer le pointeur local au thread. Préparer et publier les catalogues # syrnck_object_put crée un bloc ou objet de métadonnées natif canonique sans référence. C++ calcule l'ID typé et refuse les champs JSON en double. syrnck_ingest_buffer_ex (\u003c=16 Mio) et syrnck_prepare_file_ex (flux) préparent des manifests sans référence ni fichier temporaire en clair. Publier une racine avant de libérer le Store au GC ; ingest_file_ex garde son pin ancien ffi/ID. syrnck_refs_json lit la table typée. syrnck_refs_compare_json compare un groupe complet de lignes name/expected/value et vérifie les fermetures avant une seule publication. Une comparaison échouée n'autorise pas à recibler un ancien plan. Sur statut non nul, applied remis à zéro ne prouve rien : une erreur IO peut suivre la publication atomique ; relire et établir son résultat avant de réessayer. Voir les transactions de catalogue pour les règles et l'exemple. Actions natives de workspace protégées # workspace_native_state_json_ex lit cible, révision typée, matérialisation et état de checkout. Capture et checkout exigent la cible/révision/objet de sélection exacts dans SyrnckNativeExpected. Capture conserve le travail local ; plan est consultatif ; checkout_owned utilise le journal natif et refuse le travail modifié. recover_owned exige propriétaire et candidat persistés exacts. Capture expose lectures/réutilisations ; les empreintes de session ne prennent pas l’index pour preuve. La fermeture complète reste vérifiée ; voir capture . checkout_status_json_ex lit journal/reçu exact par le cœur sans application. Un reçu terminé prouve l’application passée par propriétaire/source/cible ; une édition ultérieure reste du travail séparé. recover_owned_preserving_json_ex archive explicitement les éditions ordinaires et crée des copies exclusives avant reprise stricte ; recover_owned par défaut garde ses contrôles stricts. Une session est liée à un chemin de Store, contexte enregistré et dossier de travail. Les mutations invalident la continuité ; une capture réussie initialise plutôt l’inventaire complet. En erreur, recharger : la publication peut être terminée. Voir les actions natives pour reprise et verrouillage. Observation et annulation # Une source d'annulation est à usage unique ; la demande est autorisée depuis un autre thread ou un callback de progression. Sérialiser sa destruction avec ses utilisateurs. Les chaînes du callback sont empruntées uniquement pendant l'appel. Voir les sessions , le binding Go et les plages vérifiées pour les contrats et exemples.","headings":[{"id":"ownership","text":"Handles et tampons","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Codes de retour et diagnostics","level":2},{"id":"catalogs","text":"Préparer et publier les catalogues","level":2},{"id":"native-actions","text":"Actions natives de workspace protégées","level":2},{"id":"operations","text":"Observation et annulation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/go-binding/catalog-transactions","#/fr/performance/incremental-capture","#/fr/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions","#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/go-binding","#/fr/file-ranges"],"revision":"2c97dab3021e"},{"id":"api-store","title":"API C++ du Store","description":"API C++ du Store","section":"API","parent":"integrations","order":100,"kind":"api","symbol":"syrnck::Store","source":"include/syrnck/core.hpp:324","sourceRevision":"14995bd6ba7df6651831f0ff47edfe60fc672269d7f0087494b9371ca7e62b74","locale":"fr","html":"\u003cp>Inclure \u003ccode>#include &lt;syrnck/core.hpp&gt;\u003c/code> et lier \u003ccode>Syrnck::core\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Propriété et concurrence\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-store/ownership\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Propriété et concurrence\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store(root, key, create, options)\u003c/code> détient le verrou exclusif du dépôt. Garder le handle\npendant toute l&#39;opération et sérialiser les appels, destruction comprise. Il se déplace\nmais ne se copie pas. Les workers internes ne rendent pas le handle utilisable en concurrence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"objects\">Objets et rétention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-store/objects\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Objets et rétention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ingest(input, chunking)\u003c/code> rend un ID de manifeste et des statistiques, sans publier de référence.\n\u003ccode>get\u003c/code> vérifie l&#39;identité typée ; \u003ccode>reconstruct\u003c/code> vérifie les blocs et le hash complet du fichier.\n\u003ccode>set_ref\u003c/code> contrôle la fermeture complète avant rétention. \u003ccode>compare_update_refs\u003c/code> publie un groupe\nseulement si toutes les valeurs attendues correspondent ; false impose de refaire la lecture.\nVoir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/storage\">stockage\u003c/a>, les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-ranges\">plages\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/reference-tables\">références\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"errors\">Erreurs et annulation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/api-store/errors\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Erreurs et annulation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Intercepter \u003ccode>syrnck::Error\u003c/code>, dont dérivent \u003ccode>MissingObject\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Cancelled\u003c/code>.\nOperationOptions fournit un jeton d&#39;arrêt et un observateur synchrone ; ne pas réentrer\ndans le Store depuis un callback. Respecter les frontières de publication et de rétention\npropres à chaque opération. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">progression\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/settings\">préférences\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Inclure #include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> et lier Syrnck::core . Propriété et concurrence # Store(root, key, create, options) détient le verrou exclusif du dépôt. Garder le handle pendant toute l'opération et sérialiser les appels, destruction comprise. Il se déplace mais ne se copie pas. Les workers internes ne rendent pas le handle utilisable en concurrence. Objets et rétention # ingest(input, chunking) rend un ID de manifeste et des statistiques, sans publier de référence. get vérifie l'identité typée ; reconstruct vérifie les blocs et le hash complet du fichier. set_ref contrôle la fermeture complète avant rétention. compare_update_refs publie un groupe seulement si toutes les valeurs attendues correspondent ; false impose de refaire la lecture. Voir le stockage , les plages et les références . Erreurs et annulation # Intercepter syrnck::Error , dont dérivent MissingObject et Cancelled . OperationOptions fournit un jeton d'arrêt et un observateur synchrone ; ne pas réentrer dans le Store depuis un callback. Respecter les frontières de publication et de rétention propres à chaque opération. Voir la progression et les préférences .","headings":[{"id":"ownership","text":"Propriété et concurrence","level":2},{"id":"objects","text":"Objets et rétention","level":2},{"id":"errors","text":"Erreurs et annulation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/storage","#/fr/file-ranges","#/fr/reference-tables","#/fr/operations","#/fr/settings"],"revision":"06cfbaafb999"},{"id":"architecture","title":"Architecture","description":"Guide : Architecture.","section":"Premiers pas","order":10,"sourceRevision":"4","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"architecture-de-syrnck\">Architecture de Syrnck\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/architecture-de-syrnck\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Architecture de Syrnck\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003ch2 id=\"objectif-et-decisions\">Objectif et décisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/objectif-et-decisions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Objectif et décisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un même moteur doit servir les assets lourds, le contrôle de versions autonome et la synchronisation de fichiers. Le code source doit aussi garder ses diff, merge, rebase, blame, tags, hooks et outils de revue. Le mode Git délègue ces fonctions à Git ; le mode autonome les développe progressivement au-dessus des mêmes objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++20 est retenu pour une bibliothèque native portable, une intégration au développement Unreal et une interface C utilisable depuis Go. CMake construit les bibliothèques et exécutables ; RAII, les tests et les sanitizers encadrent la gestion de mémoire. La bibliothèque de stockage ne dépend ni de Git, ni d&#39;HTTP, ni d&#39;un watcher. Le CLI et les adaptateurs dépendent de la bibliothèque, et non l&#39;inverse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"diagram\">\u003csvg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Syrnck\" viewBox=\"0 0 730 362\" width=\"730\" height=\"362\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\u003cdefs>\u003cmarker id=\"arrow-architecture-0\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\u003cpath d=\"M0 0 10 5 0 10Z\" style=\"fill:var(--accent,#278169);stroke:none\"/>\u003c/marker>\u003c/defs>\u003cpath d=\"M210 31L250 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M355 62L355 140\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M500 31L460 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M250 171L210 311\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M355 202L355 280\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M500 311L460 171\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-architecture-0)\"/>\u003crect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"105\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Git / filters\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">CLI / native history\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"500\" y=\"0\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"605\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">C / Go / BEP adapter\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"140\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">C++20 core\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"0\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"105\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">CAS / manifests / revisions\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"250\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"355\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Refs / retention\u003c/text>\u003crect x=\"500\" y=\"280\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/>\u003ctext x=\"605\" y=\"316\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">HTTP / block server\u003c/text>\u003c/svg>\u003cfigcaption>Syrnck\u003c/figcaption>\u003c/figure>\u003cp>Ce découpage rejoint un principe exposé dans la \u003ca href=\"https://epicgames.github.io/lore/explanation/system-design/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">conception de Lore\u003c/a> : le stockage peut être consommé séparément du contrôle de versions. Il ne crée aucune compatibilité avec les formats Lore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fichiers-et-revisions\">Fichiers et révisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/fichiers-et-revisions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fichiers et révisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un bloc est identifié par BLAKE3 avec un domaine séparant les types d&#39;objets. Un manifeste contient la taille du fichier, son hash complet, la politique de découpage et la liste \u003cstrong>ordonnée et complète\u003c/strong> des blocs. Deux versions peuvent partager un bloc sans partager une relation de parenté.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une révision autonome contient un arbre logique complet \u003ccode>chemin → manifeste + métadonnées\u003c/code>, ses parents et son message. Le prototype représente l&#39;arbre dans une table triée ; une arborescence Merkle paginée remplacera cette représentation pour les grands dépôts. Chaque révision reste un état complet, même après ce changement de représentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Exemple :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>La nouvelle version stocke et transmet le bloc X et son manifeste. Reconstituer B ne demande pas de rejouer A. Si A est supprimée, les blocs 1, 3 et 4 restent vivants tant que B les référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le découpage ne comprend pas les objets Unreal. Une modification de quelques octets peut modifier un bloc entier, parfois plusieurs. Une réécriture, une compression ou un chiffrement préalable d&#39;un asset peut modifier presque tous ses octets et réduire fortement la déduplication.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"politique-de-decoupage\">Politique de découpage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/politique-de-decoupage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Politique de découpage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le prototype propose FastCDC 2020 avec minimum 256 Kio, moyenne cible 1 Mio et maximum 4 Mio, ainsi que des blocs fixes configurables. FastCDC permet de retrouver des frontières communes après une insertion ou une suppression ; la taille moyenne cible n&#39;est pas une taille garantie. Le choix sera ensuite configurable par type de fichier et mesuré sur corpus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00001-fast-cdc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Lore documente son choix de FastCDC\u003c/a>. À l&#39;inverse, le \u003ca href=\"https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/bep-v1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">BEP de Syncthing\u003c/a> utilise des blocs de 128 Kio à 16 Mio, de taille constante dans un fichier sauf le dernier. Le mode fixe de Syrnck ne suffit donc pas à rendre ses objets ou son réseau compatibles avec Syncthing : les hashes, métadonnées, messages, identités et règles de version sont aussi différents.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mode-git\">Mode Git\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/mode-git\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mode Git\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les fichiers texte restent des blobs Git ordinaires. Les assets sélectionnés par \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> passent par \u003ccode>clean\u003c/code>, qui écrit un pointeur vers un manifeste. \u003ccode>smudge\u003c/code> reconstruit les octets lors du checkout. Les filtres sont déclarés obligatoires : une erreur de stockage ne doit pas faire croire à Git qu&#39;un asset a été correctement reconstruit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;adaptateur utilise un format de pointeur Syrnck, distinct de \u003ca href=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git LFS\u003c/a>. GitHub et GitLab peuvent héberger l&#39;historique Git contenant ces pointeurs, mais ne fournissent pas automatiquement le service de blocs Syrnck, les aperçus d&#39;assets ou une interface LFS compatible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration\">migration LFS hors ligne\u003c/a> de \u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> lit un snapshot brut, vérifie les objets SHA-256 pendant leur ingestion et reconstruit uniquement les arbres Git modifiés. Une racine complète protège les assets avant la création d&#39;une branche par transaction Git préparée. Le contrôle de la référence sous verrou protège aussi les références symboliques pendantes. HEAD, index et workspace restent indépendants de cette opération ; les anciens commits ne sont pas réécrits dans ce mode snapshot. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history\">migration complète\u003c/a> convertit aussi l’ascendance dans de nouvelles références.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque manifeste complet passé dans le filtre est épinglé. Un pointeur canonique non hydraté est conservé sans créer de pin incomplet, pour permettre les clones à pointeurs seuls. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-retention\">réconciliation de rétention\u003c/a> inspecte explicitement branches, tags, reflogs, stashes et index de chaque worktree, vérifie les fichiers complets puis ajoute les pins manquants en une transaction. Les pins non observés restent conservés ; leur retrait automatique coordonné reste à développer. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-assets\">catalogues Git\u003c/a> associent une racine de révision sans parent à chaque commit, avec conservation locale et publication distante immuable. \u003ccode>syrnck_git_net\u003c/code> relie ces catalogues aux transferts réseau et vérifie les pointeurs avant publication locale. Un hook pre-push installé explicitement envoie les catalogues de toute l&#39;ascendance avant la publication Git. Les politiques \u003ccode>local\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>download\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>skip\u003c/code> règlent le checkout : le téléchargement opt-in utilise le manifeste du pointeur, un bail de lecture et les sessions persistantes, sans dépendre de HEAD. Avant reconstruction, smudge vérifie taille, blocs et hash complet. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process\">filtre résident\u003c/a> conserve un Store et son cache vérifié entre les fichiers d&#39;une commande Git ; les requêtes restent séquentielles, sans hydratation différée. Le hook local n&#39;assure pas une transaction distribuée avec le serveur Git.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mode-autonome\">Mode autonome\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/mode-autonome\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mode autonome\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le prototype offre la création de révisions, des branches nommées, un journal par premier parent et un export vers un nouveau dossier. Il permet aussi de détacher l&#39;historique en gardant l&#39;état courant et en archivant l&#39;ancienne tête.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-comparison\">comparaisons natives\u003c/a> ajoutent un \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> du dossier par hash complet et un \u003ccode>diff\u003c/code> des métadonnées de snapshots, sans import ni publication. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-staging\">staging natif\u003c/a> conserve une sélection immuable sous \u003ccode>staging/BRANCHE\u003c/code>, protégée contre le GC avec sa base. Le commit préparé vérifie ses dépendances puis déplace la branche et consomme le staging en une transaction de références ; une base périmée est refusée. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-patches\">patches\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-merge\">merges à trois voies\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-merge\">merge natif\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-rebase\">rebase linéaire/interactif\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-replay\">cherry-pick/revert\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stash\">stash\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-file-history\">blame et renommages exacts\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-bisect\">bisect\u003c/a> sont disponibles avec leurs limites documentées. Rebase préservant les merges, auteurs structurés et signatures restent à réaliser.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace\">inspection du workspace\u003c/a> combine base/index/dossier et prépare un plan de checkout sans écriture. Ce plan distingue contenu, mode et représentation, impose les fichiers suivis propres, et inventorie aussi les chemins ignorés pour détecter les collisions. Liens/jonctions, collisions ambiguës et répertoires protégés bloquent ; les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\">transitions propres de casse et fichier/dossier\u003c/a> sont journalisées. La vérification complète des objets cibles reste optionnelle pour le plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">checkout récupérable\u003c/a> vérifie les fermetures source/cible, épingle un journal lié au chemin canonique et prépare des fichiers complets avant les déplacements exclusifs. Les originaux restent dans des slots privés jusqu&#39;à publication atomique de la branche, de l&#39;archive source et de la rétention cible. Une interruption se reprend ou s&#39;annule explicitement ; une publication déjà engagée ne peut que terminer son nettoyage. Les déplacements du workspace sont séquentiels, sans verrou contre les éditeurs externes. Les fichiers privés restent en clair même dans un store chiffré. Le GC et les écritures natives du workspace sont bloqués tant que le journal est actif ; les archives/reçus restent conservés sans purge automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-branches\">HEAD natif\u003c/a> est un chunk canonique sous \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code>, lié au workspace et à une sélection ; son absence sélectionne implicitement main. Les descripteurs nommés gardent v1 ; \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">HEAD détaché\u003c/a> utilise v2 et des racines privées de révision/index. Le changement de branche réutilise le journal v2 (v4 pour les transitions de namespace), ou v7 avec cible détachée, avec les HEAD attendu et nouveau. La publication finale sélectionne HEAD avec le journal terminé en vérifiant et conservant les deux têtes. Les commandes CLI par défaut et \u003ccode>Store::native_target()\u003c/code> résolvent la sélection. Les anciens reçus restent lisibles. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces liés\u003c/a> placent HEAD/index/cache/journal et reçus sous les vraies racines \u003ccode>workspaces/NOM/\u003c/code>, partagent objets/branches et réservent les branches sélectionnées. Leurs reçus v8 enregistrent contexte et layout ; les sauvegardes gardent des sélections portables plutôt que les chemins absolus. Les workspaces partiels statiques conservent révisions/index logiques complets et projettent les fichiers physiques selon une sélection canonique. Leurs reçus v9 figent les deux sélections ; changement dynamique et administration des workspaces restent à compléter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les fichiers source utilisent actuellement le même moteur de stockage en mode autonome. Leurs interfaces de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-merge\">merge\u003c/a> textuels sont explicites et bornées. L&#39;utilisation de blocs pour les stocker n&#39;oblige pas à présenter des différences de blocs à l&#39;utilisateur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-tags\">tags natifs\u003c/a> utilisent une référence directe vers la révision et une référence optionnelle vers un chunk d&#39;annotation. La publication des deux est atomique. La suppression archive la cible, l&#39;annotation et un reçu canonique avec une vérification de l&#39;ID attendu ; leur restauration rétablit le nom et le message sans retirer l&#39;archive. Ces racines continuent de protéger les versions pendant le GC. Les sélecteurs CLI résolvent HEAD, branche ou tag et refusent les noms ambigus. La liste paginée borne son JSON sous 16 Mio, en plus du nombre d&#39;entrées demandé. Les signatures, politiques serveur et transferts atomiques de groupes de racines restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stockage-physique-et-packs\">Stockage physique et packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/stockage-physique-et-packs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage physique et packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les objets logiques v1 peuvent être stockés dans des fichiers isolés ou dans des packs immuables indexés. \u003ccode>Store::pack()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck pack\u003c/code> ajoutent des packs pour les seuls nouveaux objets ; \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> regroupe tout le stock. La configuration physique passe à v2 pour un seul pack, puis v3 pour un catalogue de plusieurs packs ; les nouveaux stores restent v1. Le remplacement atomique de \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> publie le catalogue complet après écriture et relecture vérifiée, avant tout retrait. Le GC préserve les packs entièrement vivants, retire ceux entièrement morts et réécrit les seuls packs partiellement morts. Les références et les blocs partagés conservent les mêmes règles de rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les index sont lus par pages de 256 entrées. Un répertoire de bornes/empreintes et un filtre de clés absentes remplacent la table complète ; une LRU de pages de 4 Mio est configurable indépendamment du cache décodé. L&#39;ouverture vérifie encore chaque index complet et les doublons entre packs par parcours fusionnés. Les limites restent 1 048 576 entrées par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 entrées au total. Le GC et \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> parcourent les inventaires en flux, avec un bit de marquage par objet empaqueté ; le GC recopie également les survivants en flux. Les clés parcourues absentes des packs, la pile de parcours et le JSON restent en mémoire ; \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> et l&#39;API publique \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> gardent encore leurs collections d&#39;identifiants. L&#39;empaquetage et la compaction fusionnent les index par pages et gardent seulement les clés isolées à trier ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/compaction\">garanties de compaction\u003c/a>. Une session réutilise au plus 8 handles de packs avec LRU et les ferme avant publication/suppression. La maintenance conserve le verrou exclusif. L&#39;index ne dispense pas de vérifier les enveloppes réellement lues, et \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> contrôle aussi les doublons physiques. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/audit\">garanties des audits\u003c/a>, les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">budgets des index\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs\">étapes de reprise\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pipeline-d-ingestion\">Pipeline d&#39;ingestion\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/pipeline-d-ingestion\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Pipeline d&#39;ingestion\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L&#39;ingestion C++ peut utiliser un pool de 2 à 64 workers pour la compression et le chiffrement des blocs nouveaux. Le coordinateur conserve le découpage CDC, les hashes, la détection des doublons, l&#39;ordre du manifeste et les écritures atomiques. Les workers ne modifient ni le store ni les références ; chacun possède son contexte Zstd réutilisable. Un budget d&#39;octets et un plafond de \u003ccode>2 × workers\u003c/code> tâches imposent l&#39;attente du producteur quand la file est pleine. Le mode séquentiel reste le choix par défaut.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les buffers bruts sont libérés avant que le résultat d&#39;encodage soit livré au coordinateur. À ce budget s&#39;ajoutent les buffers compressés/chiffrés, le tampon de lecture, les contextes Zstd par worker et les métadonnées. Le manifeste plat est borné pendant la lecture par sa limite sérialisée de 16 Mio ; cette limite ne représente pas sa consommation en mémoire sous forme JSON.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La publication des blocs suit leur première apparition dans le fichier. Le manifeste n&#39;est créé qu&#39;après la réussite de toutes les tâches. Les exceptions des workers remontent au coordinateur ; lecture en échec, écriture refusée et annulation coopérative arrêtent les tâches restantes et joignent les threads. Les références existantes restent intactes. Des blocs orphelins déjà publiés peuvent subsister jusqu&#39;à leur réutilisation ou leur collecte. Le verrou exclusif externe du store reste nécessaire ; le parallélisme interne ne rend pas le handle utilisable simultanément par plusieurs appelants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-et-parcours-de-validation\">Cache et parcours de validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/cache-et-parcours-de-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cache et parcours de validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le cache est local au handle et ne constitue pas une source d&#39;autorité sur disque. Une entrée n&#39;est créée qu&#39;après validation complète d&#39;un objet lu : déchiffrement/authentification si nécessaire, décompression bornée, ID et format. Elle associe la clé typée à l&#39;empreinte BLAKE3 de l&#39;enveloppe exacte. Les accès suivants relisent et hachent cette enveloppe avant de réutiliser l&#39;entrée ; un simple mtime ou une taille ne suffit pas. Une empreinte différente évince l&#39;entrée et impose une nouvelle validation complète. Une absence sur disque reste une absence, même si l&#39;objet était en cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Deux LRU indépendants bornent les empreintes et les données décodées. Le défaut est 8 192 entrées et 64 Mio de données, hors structures d&#39;index et buffers de travail. L&#39;absence de place pour une donnée n&#39;empêche pas de conserver son empreinte. Le cache ne conserve pas les objets contre le GC et ne persiste pas entre ouvertures ; ses buffers décodés sont nettoyés avant libération. Le chiffrement au repos reste distinct de la présence de données déchiffrées dans un processus autorisé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La validation complète d&#39;une fermeture parcourt chaque manifeste et vérifie son hash de fichier en lisant ses blocs dans le même passage. Les révisions, manifestes et blocs rencontrés alimentent l&#39;ensemble des dépendances ; les parents et versions protégées restent couverts. Les blocs partagés entre plusieurs fichiers peuvent être relus pour vérifier chacun de leurs hashes complets. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> réunit les objets énumérés et les racines dans une seule traversée, et \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> valide toutes les racines avant toute suppression. Ces deux opérations contournent le cache, avec restauration de son mode normal même après une erreur.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conservation-et-suppression\">Conservation et suppression\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/conservation-et-suppression\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conservation et suppression\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les racines de rétention sont les branches, archives, pins et références reçues. Le GC marque leurs dépendances transitives puis supprime les objets inaccessibles. Il conserve aussi les parents des révisions vivantes. Un verrou exclusif du store empêche qu&#39;un autre processus publie une référence ou ajoute des objets pendant le nettoyage. Un objet vivant manquant ou corrompu interrompt le GC avant toute suppression.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Trois opérations doivent rester distinctes :\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Retirer un commit d&#39;une branche : son contenu peut rester dans une archive, un autre historique ou un pin.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retirer une référence de rétention : ses objets deviennent éligibles au GC s&#39;ils ne sont plus partagés.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exécuter le GC : les objets réellement inaccessibles sont supprimés physiquement.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native detach-history\u003c/code> archive l&#39;ancienne tête avant de déplacer la branche. Pour libérer ensuite les données anciennes, il faut retirer cette archive puis lancer le GC. Une version volontairement supprimée de toutes les racines et nettoyée ne peut plus être reconstruite. Une suppression définitive et une conservation éternelle des mêmes données seraient incompatibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">journaux de transfert\u003c/a> protègent le store local et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">baux d&#39;envoi serveur\u003c/a> protègent les uploads jusqu&#39;à expiration ou publication. Pour un déploiement réel, compléter les délais de grâce, politiques de rétention, pins de releases et backups vérifiés. Le GC distribué ne pourra pas se contenter des racines d&#39;un seul client.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"synchronisation-et-serveur\">Synchronisation et serveur\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/synchronisation-et-serveur\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Synchronisation et serveur\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L&#39;agent compare des identifiants d&#39;objets, demande les absents puis transfère leur contenu. Les objets reçus sont vérifiés, stockés et réutilisables lors d&#39;une reprise. La nouvelle référence devient visible seulement lorsque toute sa fermeture de dépendances a été validée. Une comparaison avec la valeur attendue protège la publication contre un écrasement concurrent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le serveur est mono-store avec jeton partagé historique ou \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">utilisateurs/permissions\u003c/a> ; il sérialise les accès au stockage. Le GC et les autres processus locaux sont exclus pendant son exécution. Le réseau limite les corps à 16 Mio, les requêtes de présence à 4 096 objets et le pool serveur à 16 threads. L&#39;agent peut chevaucher des requêtes d&#39;objets avec des \u003ca href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers\">plafonds de concurrence et de buffers\u003c/a>, tout en coordonnant les accès au Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un transfert de blocs ne règle pas les conflits entre deux modifications de fichiers. La synchronisation continue devra définir versions causales, suppressions, conflits, renommages et publication multi-fichier, indépendamment de l&#39;algorithme de déduplication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">baux de lecture persistants\u003c/a> protègent la racine exacte d&#39;un pull contre le GC hors ligne tant qu&#39;ils sont actifs, même après suppression de la référence source. Le client les renouvelle par une requête indépendante, puis les libère lorsque tous les objets sont locaux. Le GC protège sélectivement les fermetures complètes des lectures et sources locales, ainsi que les objets disponibles des pulls incomplets. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires d&#39;envoi\u003c/a> ajoutent les objets déclarés avant les demandes de présence, même sans manifeste ; leurs pages chaînées sont validées avant toute suppression. Les anciens baux d&#39;envoi gardent une protection globale. Le GC distribué reste à développer. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/selective-retention\">rétention et la purge des reçus\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;interface C expose l&#39;ingestion, la reconstruction, la présence/lecture/importation des objets, leur fermeture de dépendances et les pins vérifiés. Le binding \u003ccode>bindings/go\u003c/code> utilise cette interface par DLL sous Windows et cgo sous Unix. Son banc de copie entre stores valide le transfert différentiel et la reprise sous Windows x64. L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing\">adaptateur BEP\u003c/a> utilise la bibliothèque Syncthing et des blocs fixes SHA-256 en conservant le stockage propre du moteur. Il ne fournit pas un daemon complet ni de protocole CDC entre pairs Syncthing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chiffrement\">Chiffrement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/chiffrement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Chiffrement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le prototype compresse puis chiffre chaque objet au repos avec XChaCha20-Poly1305, une clé aléatoire de 256 bits et un nonce aléatoire de 192 bits. L&#39;identifiant et le type de l&#39;objet sont authentifiés comme données associées. Les manifestes et révisions sont aussi chiffrés ; la configuration et les noms de références restent visibles. Les identifiants de contenu et tailles stockées ne sont pas masqués.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La clé se fournit via \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>, pas dans la configuration. Il n&#39;y a pas encore de dérivation depuis un mot de passe, rotation, récupération de clé ou coffre système. Le détenteur des droits sur le processus et son environnement reste un acteur de confiance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">compression réseau négociée\u003c/a> utilise Zstandard niveau 1 sur les objets logiques, avec repli brut si le gain ou le budget ne suffit pas. Le décodage vérifie la trame avant import et écrit directement dans le buffer réservé. Les compteurs et cibles de débit réseau incluent les tentatives répétées ; les octets logiques réussis gardent leur compteur distinct.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les objets sont déchiffrés pour le transfert, protégé par HTTPS lorsqu&#39;il est distant. Le serveur est donc de confiance. Un chiffrement de bout en bout devra rendre le serveur opaque, protéger les manifestes et établir une politique explicite de déduplication et de fuite d&#39;égalité par dépôt. Il devra également traiter les attaques par rejeu d&#39;anciennes racines et la révocation des appareils.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/untrusted.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mode appareils non fiables de Syncthing\u003c/a> illustre un autre compromis : son chiffrement de blocs dépend du fichier et de l&#39;offset, ce qui limite les corrélations. Reprendre ce choix tel quel empêcherait la déduplication globale entre fichiers et positions visée ici. Son transport BEP utilise TLS et l&#39;identité des certificats ; le prototype Syrnck utilise un jeton et un proxy TLS, sans identité d&#39;appareil Syncthing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"integrite-et-limites-actuelles\">Intégrité et limites actuelles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/architecture/integrite-et-limites-actuelles\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Intégrité et limites actuelles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les objets utilisent un fichier temporaire puis une publication atomique. Les références sont remplacées atomiquement après validation des dépendances. La reconstruction vérifie chaque bloc et le hash complet du fichier ; l&#39;export d&#39;un fichier ne remplace pas une destination existante. L&#39;export d&#39;une révision entière prépare une arborescence complète puis la publie par renommage exclusif dans un nouveau dossier. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser son dossier temporaire. Le checkout et le changement de branche dans un workspace existant utilisent un journal de reprise et des déplacements par fichier, sans atomicité globale du dossier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le scanner exclut \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code>, le store et les règles \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>. Il refuse actuellement symlinks et fichiers spéciaux. Les chemins doivent être UTF-8 et portables ; utf8proc détecte leurs collisions après normalisation et casefold Unicode. Le prototype n&#39;enregistre pas les ACL, xattrs, liens matériels ou répertoires vides. Les droits exécutables sont conservés sur Unix seulement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le scanner vérifie taille et date de modification autour de chaque lecture. Il ne fournit pas un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers : les fichiers doivent être stables pendant un commit. Les garanties après perte brutale d&#39;alimentation, sur partages réseau ou face à un autre processus modifiant les fichiers du store en contournant son verrou restent à éprouver.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le code original est sous MIT. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/licenses\">notices des dépendances\u003c/a> conservent leurs licences propres.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Architecture de Syrnck # Objectif et décisions # Un même moteur doit servir les assets lourds, le contrôle de versions autonome et la synchronisation de fichiers. Le code source doit aussi garder ses diff, merge, rebase, blame, tags, hooks et outils de revue. Le mode Git délègue ces fonctions à Git ; le mode autonome les développe progressivement au-dessus des mêmes objets. C++20 est retenu pour une bibliothèque native portable, une intégration au développement Unreal et une interface C utilisable depuis Go. CMake construit les bibliothèques et exécutables ; RAII, les tests et les sanitizers encadrent la gestion de mémoire. La bibliothèque de stockage ne dépend ni de Git, ni d'HTTP, ni d'un watcher. Le CLI et les adaptateurs dépendent de la bibliothèque, et non l'inverse. Git / filters CLI / native history C / Go / BEP adapter C++20 core CAS / manifests / revisions Refs / retention HTTP / block server Syrnck Ce découpage rejoint un principe exposé dans la conception de Lore : le stockage peut être consommé séparément du contrôle de versions. Il ne crée aucune compatibilité avec les formats Lore. Fichiers et révisions # Un bloc est identifié par BLAKE3 avec un domaine séparant les types d'objets. Un manifeste contient la taille du fichier, son hash complet, la politique de découpage et la liste ordonnée et complète des blocs. Deux versions peuvent partager un bloc sans partager une relation de parenté. Une révision autonome contient un arbre logique complet chemin → manifeste + métadonnées , ses parents et son message. Le prototype représente l'arbre dans une table triée ; une arborescence Merkle paginée remplacera cette représentation pour les grands dépôts. Chaque révision reste un état complet, même après ce changement de représentation. Exemple : text Copier Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4] Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4] La nouvelle version stocke et transmet le bloc X et son manifeste. Reconstituer B ne demande pas de rejouer A. Si A est supprimée, les blocs 1, 3 et 4 restent vivants tant que B les référence. Le découpage ne comprend pas les objets Unreal. Une modification de quelques octets peut modifier un bloc entier, parfois plusieurs. Une réécriture, une compression ou un chiffrement préalable d'un asset peut modifier presque tous ses octets et réduire fortement la déduplication. Politique de découpage # Le prototype propose FastCDC 2020 avec minimum 256 Kio, moyenne cible 1 Mio et maximum 4 Mio, ainsi que des blocs fixes configurables. FastCDC permet de retrouver des frontières communes après une insertion ou une suppression ; la taille moyenne cible n'est pas une taille garantie. Le choix sera ensuite configurable par type de fichier et mesuré sur corpus. Lore documente son choix de FastCDC . À l'inverse, le BEP de Syncthing utilise des blocs de 128 Kio à 16 Mio, de taille constante dans un fichier sauf le dernier. Le mode fixe de Syrnck ne suffit donc pas à rendre ses objets ou son réseau compatibles avec Syncthing : les hashes, métadonnées, messages, identités et règles de version sont aussi différents. Mode Git # Les fichiers texte restent des blobs Git ordinaires. Les assets sélectionnés par .gitattributes passent par clean , qui écrit un pointeur vers un manifeste. smudge reconstruit les octets lors du checkout. Les filtres sont déclarés obligatoires : une erreur de stockage ne doit pas faire croire à Git qu'un asset a été correctement reconstruit. L'adaptateur utilise un format de pointeur Syrnck, distinct de Git LFS . GitHub et GitLab peuvent héberger l'historique Git contenant ces pointeurs, mais ne fournissent pas automatiquement le service de blocs Syrnck, les aperçus d'assets ou une interface LFS compatible. La migration LFS hors ligne de syrnck_git lit un snapshot brut, vérifie les objets SHA-256 pendant leur ingestion et reconstruit uniquement les arbres Git modifiés. Une racine complète protège les assets avant la création d'une branche par transaction Git préparée. Le contrôle de la référence sous verrou protège aussi les références symboliques pendantes. HEAD, index et workspace restent indépendants de cette opération ; les anciens commits ne sont pas réécrits dans ce mode snapshot. La migration complète convertit aussi l’ascendance dans de nouvelles références. Chaque manifeste complet passé dans le filtre est épinglé. Un pointeur canonique non hydraté est conservé sans créer de pin incomplet, pour permettre les clones à pointeurs seuls. La réconciliation de rétention inspecte explicitement branches, tags, reflogs, stashes et index de chaque worktree, vérifie les fichiers complets puis ajoute les pins manquants en une transaction. Les pins non observés restent conservés ; leur retrait automatique coordonné reste à développer. Les catalogues Git associent une racine de révision sans parent à chaque commit, avec conservation locale et publication distante immuable. syrnck_git_net relie ces catalogues aux transferts réseau et vérifie les pointeurs avant publication locale. Un hook pre-push installé explicitement envoie les catalogues de toute l'ascendance avant la publication Git. Les politiques local / download / skip règlent le checkout : le téléchargement opt-in utilise le manifeste du pointeur, un bail de lecture et les sessions persistantes, sans dépendre de HEAD. Avant reconstruction, smudge vérifie taille, blocs et hash complet. Le filtre résident conserve un Store et son cache vérifié entre les fichiers d'une commande Git ; les requêtes restent séquentielles, sans hydratation différée. Le hook local n'assure pas une transaction distribuée avec le serveur Git. Mode autonome # Le prototype offre la création de révisions, des branches nommées, un journal par premier parent et un export vers un nouveau dossier. Il permet aussi de détacher l'historique en gardant l'état courant et en archivant l'ancienne tête. Les comparaisons natives ajoutent un status du dossier par hash complet et un diff des métadonnées de snapshots, sans import ni publication. Le staging natif conserve une sélection immuable sous staging/BRANCHE , protégée contre le GC avec sa base. Le commit préparé vérifie ses dépendances puis déplace la branche et consomme le staging en une transaction de références ; une base périmée est refusée. Les patches , merges à trois voies , merge natif , rebase linéaire/interactif , cherry-pick/revert , stash , blame et renommages exacts , bisect sont disponibles avec leurs limites documentées. Rebase préservant les merges, auteurs structurés et signatures restent à réaliser. L' inspection du workspace combine base/index/dossier et prépare un plan de checkout sans écriture. Ce plan distingue contenu, mode et représentation, impose les fichiers suivis propres, et inventorie aussi les chemins ignorés pour détecter les collisions. Liens/jonctions, collisions ambiguës et répertoires protégés bloquent ; les transitions propres de casse et fichier/dossier sont journalisées. La vérification complète des objets cibles reste optionnelle pour le plan. Le checkout récupérable vérifie les fermetures source/cible, épingle un journal lié au chemin canonique et prépare des fichiers complets avant les déplacements exclusifs. Les originaux restent dans des slots privés jusqu'à publication atomique de la branche, de l'archive source et de la rétention cible. Une interruption se reprend ou s'annule explicitement ; une publication déjà engagée ne peut que terminer son nettoyage. Les déplacements du workspace sont séquentiels, sans verrou contre les éditeurs externes. Les fichiers privés restent en clair même dans un store chiffré. Le GC et les écritures natives du workspace sont bloqués tant que le journal est actif ; les archives/reçus restent conservés sans purge automatique. Le HEAD natif est un chunk canonique sous workspace/HEAD , lié au workspace et à une sélection ; son absence sélectionne implicitement main. Les descripteurs nommés gardent v1 ; HEAD détaché utilise v2 et des racines privées de révision/index. Le changement de branche réutilise le journal v2 (v4 pour les transitions de namespace), ou v7 avec cible détachée, avec les HEAD attendu et nouveau. La publication finale sélectionne HEAD avec le journal terminé en vérifiant et conservant les deux têtes. Les commandes CLI par défaut et Store::native_target() résolvent la sélection. Les anciens reçus restent lisibles. Les workspaces liés placent HEAD/index/cache/journal et reçus sous les vraies racines workspaces/NOM/ , partagent objets/branches et réservent les branches sélectionnées. Leurs reçus v8 enregistrent contexte et layout ; les sauvegardes gardent des sélections portables plutôt que les chemins absolus. Les workspaces partiels statiques conservent révisions/index logiques complets et projettent les fichiers physiques selon une sélection canonique. Leurs reçus v9 figent les deux sélections ; changement dynamique et administration des workspaces restent à compléter. Les fichiers source utilisent actuellement le même moteur de stockage en mode autonome. Leurs interfaces de diff et merge textuels sont explicites et bornées. L'utilisation de blocs pour les stocker n'oblige pas à présenter des différences de blocs à l'utilisateur. Les tags natifs utilisent une référence directe vers la révision et une référence optionnelle vers un chunk d'annotation. La publication des deux est atomique. La suppression archive la cible, l'annotation et un reçu canonique avec une vérification de l'ID attendu ; leur restauration rétablit le nom et le message sans retirer l'archive. Ces racines continuent de protéger les versions pendant le GC. Les sélecteurs CLI résolvent HEAD, branche ou tag et refusent les noms ambigus. La liste paginée borne son JSON sous 16 Mio, en plus du nombre d'entrées demandé. Les signatures, politiques serveur et transferts atomiques de groupes de racines restent à développer. Stockage physique et packs # Les objets logiques v1 peuvent être stockés dans des fichiers isolés ou dans des packs immuables indexés. Store::pack() et syrnck pack ajoutent des packs pour les seuls nouveaux objets ; pack --compact regroupe tout le stock. La configuration physique passe à v2 pour un seul pack, puis v3 pour un catalogue de plusieurs packs ; les nouveaux stores restent v1. Le remplacement atomique de config.json publie le catalogue complet après écriture et relecture vérifiée, avant tout retrait. Le GC préserve les packs entièrement vivants, retire ceux entièrement morts et réécrit les seuls packs partiellement morts. Les références et les blocs partagés conservent les mêmes règles de rétention. Les index sont lus par pages de 256 entrées. Un répertoire de bornes/empreintes et un filtre de clés absentes remplacent la table complète ; une LRU de pages de 4 Mio est configurable indépendamment du cache décodé. L'ouverture vérifie encore chaque index complet et les doublons entre packs par parcours fusionnés. Les limites restent 1 048 576 entrées par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 entrées au total. Le GC et fsck parcourent les inventaires en flux, avec un bit de marquage par objet empaqueté ; le GC recopie également les survivants en flux. Les clés parcourues absentes des packs, la pile de parcours et le JSON restent en mémoire ; objects() et l'API publique closure() gardent encore leurs collections d'identifiants. L'empaquetage et la compaction fusionnent les index par pages et gardent seulement les clés isolées à trier ; voir les garanties de compaction . Une session réutilise au plus 8 handles de packs avec LRU et les ferme avant publication/suppression. La maintenance conserve le verrou exclusif. L'index ne dispense pas de vérifier les enveloppes réellement lues, et fsck contrôle aussi les doublons physiques. Voir les garanties des audits , les budgets des index et les étapes de reprise . Pipeline d'ingestion # L'ingestion C++ peut utiliser un pool de 2 à 64 workers pour la compression et le chiffrement des blocs nouveaux. Le coordinateur conserve le découpage CDC, les hashes, la détection des doublons, l'ordre du manifeste et les écritures atomiques. Les workers ne modifient ni le store ni les références ; chacun possède son contexte Zstd réutilisable. Un budget d'octets et un plafond de 2 × workers tâches imposent l'attente du producteur quand la file est pleine. Le mode séquentiel reste le choix par défaut. Les buffers bruts sont libérés avant que le résultat d'encodage soit livré au coordinateur. À ce budget s'ajoutent les buffers compressés/chiffrés, le tampon de lecture, les contextes Zstd par worker et les métadonnées. Le manifeste plat est borné pendant la lecture par sa limite sérialisée de 16 Mio ; cette limite ne représente pas sa consommation en mémoire sous forme JSON. La publication des blocs suit leur première apparition dans le fichier. Le manifeste n'est créé qu'après la réussite de toutes les tâches. Les exceptions des workers remontent au coordinateur ; lecture en échec, écriture refusée et annulation coopérative arrêtent les tâches restantes et joignent les threads. Les références existantes restent intactes. Des blocs orphelins déjà publiés peuvent subsister jusqu'à leur réutilisation ou leur collecte. Le verrou exclusif externe du store reste nécessaire ; le parallélisme interne ne rend pas le handle utilisable simultanément par plusieurs appelants. Cache et parcours de validation # Le cache est local au handle et ne constitue pas une source d'autorité sur disque. Une entrée n'est créée qu'après validation complète d'un objet lu : déchiffrement/authentification si nécessaire, décompression bornée, ID et format. Elle associe la clé typée à l'empreinte BLAKE3 de l'enveloppe exacte. Les accès suivants relisent et hachent cette enveloppe avant de réutiliser l'entrée ; un simple mtime ou une taille ne suffit pas. Une empreinte différente évince l'entrée et impose une nouvelle validation complète. Une absence sur disque reste une absence, même si l'objet était en cache. Deux LRU indépendants bornent les empreintes et les données décodées. Le défaut est 8 192 entrées et 64 Mio de données, hors structures d'index et buffers de travail. L'absence de place pour une donnée n'empêche pas de conserver son empreinte. Le cache ne conserve pas les objets contre le GC et ne persiste pas entre ouvertures ; ses buffers décodés sont nettoyés avant libération. Le chiffrement au repos reste distinct de la présence de données déchiffrées dans un processus autorisé. La validation complète d'une fermeture parcourt chaque manifeste et vérifie son hash de fichier en lisant ses blocs dans le même passage. Les révisions, manifestes et blocs rencontrés alimentent l'ensemble des dépendances ; les parents et versions protégées restent couverts. Les blocs partagés entre plusieurs fichiers peuvent être relus pour vérifier chacun de leurs hashes complets. fsck réunit les objets énumérés et les racines dans une seule traversée, et gc valide toutes les racines avant toute suppression. Ces deux opérations contournent le cache, avec restauration de son mode normal même après une erreur. Conservation et suppression # Les racines de rétention sont les branches, archives, pins et références reçues. Le GC marque leurs dépendances transitives puis supprime les objets inaccessibles. Il conserve aussi les parents des révisions vivantes. Un verrou exclusif du store empêche qu'un autre processus publie une référence ou ajoute des objets pendant le nettoyage. Un objet vivant manquant ou corrompu interrompt le GC avant toute suppression. Trois opérations doivent rester distinctes : Retirer un commit d'une branche : son contenu peut rester dans une archive, un autre historique ou un pin. Retirer une référence de rétention : ses objets deviennent éligibles au GC s'ils ne sont plus partagés. Exécuter le GC : les objets réellement inaccessibles sont supprimés physiquement. native detach-history archive l'ancienne tête avant de déplacer la branche. Pour libérer ensuite les données anciennes, il faut retirer cette archive puis lancer le GC. Une version volontairement supprimée de toutes les racines et nettoyée ne peut plus être reconstruite. Une suppression définitive et une conservation éternelle des mêmes données seraient incompatibles. Les journaux de transfert protègent le store local et les baux d'envoi serveur protègent les uploads jusqu'à expiration ou publication. Pour un déploiement réel, compléter les délais de grâce, politiques de rétention, pins de releases et backups vérifiés. Le GC distribué ne pourra pas se contenter des racines d'un seul client. Synchronisation et serveur # L'agent compare des identifiants d'objets, demande les absents puis transfère leur contenu. Les objets reçus sont vérifiés, stockés et réutilisables lors d'une reprise. La nouvelle référence devient visible seulement lorsque toute sa fermeture de dépendances a été validée. Une comparaison avec la valeur attendue protège la publication contre un écrasement concurrent. Le serveur est mono-store avec jeton partagé historique ou utilisateurs/permissions ; il sérialise les accès au stockage. Le GC et les autres processus locaux sont exclus pendant son exécution. Le réseau limite les corps à 16 Mio, les requêtes de présence à 4 096 objets et le pool serveur à 16 threads. L'agent peut chevaucher des requêtes d'objets avec des plafonds de concurrence et de buffers , tout en coordonnant les accès au Store. Un transfert de blocs ne règle pas les conflits entre deux modifications de fichiers. La synchronisation continue devra définir versions causales, suppressions, conflits, renommages et publication multi-fichier, indépendamment de l'algorithme de déduplication. Les baux de lecture persistants protègent la racine exacte d'un pull contre le GC hors ligne tant qu'ils sont actifs, même après suppression de la référence source. Le client les renouvelle par une requête indépendante, puis les libère lorsque tous les objets sont locaux. Le GC protège sélectivement les fermetures complètes des lectures et sources locales, ainsi que les objets disponibles des pulls incomplets. Les inventaires d'envoi ajoutent les objets déclarés avant les demandes de présence, même sans manifeste ; leurs pages chaînées sont validées avant toute suppression. Les anciens baux d'envoi gardent une protection globale. Le GC distribué reste à développer. Voir la rétention et la purge des reçus . L'interface C expose l'ingestion, la reconstruction, la présence/lecture/importation des objets, leur fermeture de dépendances et les pins vérifiés. Le binding bindings/go utilise cette interface par DLL sous Windows et cgo sous Unix. Son banc de copie entre stores valide le transfert différentiel et la reprise sous Windows x64. L’ adaptateur BEP utilise la bibliothèque Syncthing et des blocs fixes SHA-256 en conservant le stockage propre du moteur. Il ne fournit pas un daemon complet ni de protocole CDC entre pairs Syncthing. Chiffrement # Le prototype compresse puis chiffre chaque objet au repos avec XChaCha20-Poly1305, une clé aléatoire de 256 bits et un nonce aléatoire de 192 bits. L'identifiant et le type de l'objet sont authentifiés comme données associées. Les manifestes et révisions sont aussi chiffrés ; la configuration et les noms de références restent visibles. Les identifiants de contenu et tailles stockées ne sont pas masqués. La clé se fournit via SYRNCK_KEY , pas dans la configuration. Il n'y a pas encore de dérivation depuis un mot de passe, rotation, récupération de clé ou coffre système. Le détenteur des droits sur le processus et son environnement reste un acteur de confiance. La compression réseau négociée utilise Zstandard niveau 1 sur les objets logiques, avec repli brut si le gain ou le budget ne suffit pas. Le décodage vérifie la trame avant import et écrit directement dans le buffer réservé. Les compteurs et cibles de débit réseau incluent les tentatives répétées ; les octets logiques réussis gardent leur compteur distinct. Les objets sont déchiffrés pour le transfert, protégé par HTTPS lorsqu'il est distant. Le serveur est donc de confiance. Un chiffrement de bout en bout devra rendre le serveur opaque, protéger les manifestes et établir une politique explicite de déduplication et de fuite d'égalité par dépôt. Il devra également traiter les attaques par rejeu d'anciennes racines et la révocation des appareils. Le mode appareils non fiables de Syncthing illustre un autre compromis : son chiffrement de blocs dépend du fichier et de l'offset, ce qui limite les corrélations. Reprendre ce choix tel quel empêcherait la déduplication globale entre fichiers et positions visée ici. Son transport BEP utilise TLS et l'identité des certificats ; le prototype Syrnck utilise un jeton et un proxy TLS, sans identité d'appareil Syncthing. Intégrité et limites actuelles # Les objets utilisent un fichier temporaire puis une publication atomique. Les références sont remplacées atomiquement après validation des dépendances. La reconstruction vérifie chaque bloc et le hash complet du fichier ; l'export d'un fichier ne remplace pas une destination existante. L'export d'une révision entière prépare une arborescence complète puis la publie par renommage exclusif dans un nouveau dossier. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser son dossier temporaire. Le checkout et le changement de branche dans un workspace existant utilisent un journal de reprise et des déplacements par fichier, sans atomicité globale du dossier. Le scanner exclut .git , .syrnck , le store et les règles .syrnckignore . Il refuse actuellement symlinks et fichiers spéciaux. Les chemins doivent être UTF-8 et portables ; utf8proc détecte leurs collisions après normalisation et casefold Unicode. Le prototype n'enregistre pas les ACL, xattrs, liens matériels ou répertoires vides. Les droits exécutables sont conservés sur Unix seulement. Le scanner vérifie taille et date de modification autour de chaque lecture. Il ne fournit pas un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers : les fichiers doivent être stables pendant un commit. Les garanties après perte brutale d'alimentation, sur partages réseau ou face à un autre processus modifiant les fichiers du store en contournant son verrou restent à éprouver. Le code original est sous MIT. Les notices des dépendances conservent leurs licences propres. Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4] Version B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]","headings":[{"id":"architecture-de-syrnck","text":"Architecture de Syrnck","level":1},{"id":"objectif-et-decisions","text":"Objectif et décisions","level":2},{"id":"fichiers-et-revisions","text":"Fichiers et révisions","level":2},{"id":"politique-de-decoupage","text":"Politique de découpage","level":2},{"id":"mode-git","text":"Mode Git","level":2},{"id":"mode-autonome","text":"Mode autonome","level":2},{"id":"stockage-physique-et-packs","text":"Stockage physique et packs","level":2},{"id":"pipeline-d-ingestion","text":"Pipeline d'ingestion","level":2},{"id":"cache-et-parcours-de-validation","text":"Cache et parcours de validation","level":2},{"id":"conservation-et-suppression","text":"Conservation et suppression","level":2},{"id":"synchronisation-et-serveur","text":"Synchronisation et serveur","level":2},{"id":"chiffrement","text":"Chiffrement","level":2},{"id":"integrite-et-limites-actuelles","text":"Intégrité et limites actuelles","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"Version A : [bloc 1][bloc 2][bloc 3][bloc 4]\nVersion B : [bloc 1][bloc X][bloc 3][bloc 4]","assets":[],"links":["https://epicgames.github.io/lore/explanation/system-design/","https://epicgames.github.io/lore/developing/decisions/00001-fast-cdc/","https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/bep-v1.html","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/main/docs/spec.md","#/fr/git-lfs-migration","#/fr/git-lfs-history","#/fr/git-retention","#/fr/git-assets","#/fr/git-filter-process","#/fr/native-comparison","#/fr/native-staging","#/fr/text-patches","#/fr/text-merge","#/fr/native-merge","#/fr/native-rebase","#/fr/native-replay","#/fr/native-stash","#/fr/native-file-history","#/fr/native-bisect","#/fr/native-workspace","#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions","#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/native-branches","#/fr/native-detached","#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/text-merge","#/fr/native-tags","#/fr/compaction","#/fr/audit","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/packs","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/server-access","#/fr/parallel-transfers","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/upload-inventories","#/fr/selective-retention","#/fr/syncthing","#/fr/network-compression","https://docs.syncthing.net/specs/untrusted.html","#/fr/licenses"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0d376f3baeb6"},{"id":"archive-retention","title":"Retrait des archives","description":"Guide : Retrait des archives.","section":"Stockage","order":20,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup\">Retirer des archives avec une sauvegarde vérifiée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/archive-retention/retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Retirer des archives avec une sauvegarde vérifiée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les archives natives conservent les versions précédentes après reset, rebase, checkout, amend ou remplacement de l’index. Les racines \u003ccode>native-fetch/REVISION\u003c/code> conservent aussi l’historique récupéré. Le GC garde leurs fermetures tant qu’elles restent référencées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’API de \u003ccode>syrnck/retention.hpp\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>backup retire-plan\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>backup retire\u003c/code> retirent les sélections dans une seule transaction. Une sauvegarde indépendante doit d’abord vérifier son descripteur, son inventaire et tous les fichiers : parents des révisions, manifestes et blocs compris. Le retrait ne supprime aucun objet et ne lance pas le GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reprendre exactement le champ \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> de l’aperçu. Ce plan lie la table complète des références, la sélection triée et le snapshot de sauvegarde vérifié. Une modification de ces éléments impose un nouvel aperçu. L’application revérifie la couverture et publie par comparaison-échange ; une annulation avant publication conserve toutes les racines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choisir de 1 à 1024 sélecteurs uniques. Les archives simples reconnues sont \u003ccode>reset\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reset-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>rebase-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stage-index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>squash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>amend\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fetch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>publish\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stash\u003c/code> et les historiques détachés, ainsi que les racines exactes \u003ccode>native-fetch/REVISION\u003c/code>. Les têtes, tags actifs, index et verrous d’assets sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour les archives structurées, sélectionner le groupe entier : \u003ccode>archive/branches/ID\u003c/code> retire ensemble le descripteur, la cible et l’éventuel index figé ; \u003ccode>archive/tags/ID\u003c/code> retire ensemble le descripteur, la cible et l’éventuelle annotation. \u003ccode>native branch-archives\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>native tag-archives\u003c/code> acceptent \u003ccode>--after ID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--limit N\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> pour retrouver les groupes. Un membre \u003ccode>/target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/record\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/index\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>/annotation\u003c/code> ne peut pas être retiré seul. Les groupes incomplets, membres inattendus et liaisons d’annotation/base d’index incorrectes sont refusés. La sauvegarde doit contenir les références exactes du groupe ; les mêmes blocs sous d’autres noms ne constituent pas une recette de restauration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les lignes du plan ajoutent \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, la table \u003ccode>roots\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kind: branch-group\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>tag-group\u003c/code>, et la révision cible. \u003ccode>removed_references\u003c/code> compte les références développées. La sortie reste inférieure à 16 Mio, échappement JSON inclus. Les groupes et racines simples peuvent partager une transaction. Les branches ou tags recréés sous l’ancien nom, HEAD, les fichiers de travail et les autres références restent intacts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le dépôt principal utilise \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>, la sauvegarde secondaire \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> ou aucune clé si cette variable est absente. Les clés peuvent différer. Les deux dépôts restent verrouillés ; toute récupération de checkout doit être terminée auparavant. D’autres racines peuvent encore conserver les versions : aucun gain disque immédiat n’est garanti.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après collecte locale, restaurer la sauvegarde dans un dépôt séparé avec \u003ccode>backup restore\u003c/code>, puis appeler \u003ccode>native branch-restore ID\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>native tag-restore ID\u003c/code>. Modifier directement les références de la sauvegarde invalide son descripteur. Conserver la sauvegarde et sa clé selon la politique voulue ; leur suppression ultérieure reste administrable. Les politiques automatiques par âge/release et le GC distribué restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives\">archives de workspace\u003c/a> utilisent aussi un sélecteur de groupe entier : \u003ccode>archive/workspaces/ID\u003c/code>. Le retrait conserve la même exigence de sauvegarde exacte et retire ensemble descripteur, HEAD, révision physique et toutes les anciennes références du contexte. Aucun membre ne peut être retiré seul. \u003ccode>native workspace-archives\u003c/code> accepte les mêmes options de pagination/vérification ; les lignes du plan utilisent \u003ccode>kind: workspace-group\u003c/code>. Après restauration de la sauvegarde dans un Store séparé, utiliser \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore-plan\u003c/code> puis \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le dernier observateur de progression intervient avant la revérification finale. Cette dernière et le CAS n’appellent plus d’observateur, afin qu’un callback modifiant les références ou la sauvegarde n’autorise pas un retrait périmé. Voir aussi le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention\">retrait des reçus de checkout\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --kind reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Retirer des archives avec une sauvegarde vérifiée # Les archives natives conservent les versions précédentes après reset, rebase, checkout, amend ou remplacement de l’index. Les racines native-fetch/REVISION conservent aussi l’historique récupéré. Le GC garde leurs fermetures tant qu’elles restent référencées. L’API de syrnck/retention.hpp et backup retire-plan / backup retire retirent les sélections dans une seule transaction. Une sauvegarde indépendante doit d’abord vérifier son descripteur, son inventaire et tous les fichiers : parents des révisions, manifestes et blocs compris. Le retrait ne supprime aucun objet et ne lance pas le GC. Reprendre exactement le champ plan de l’aperçu. Ce plan lie la table complète des références, la sélection triée et le snapshot de sauvegarde vérifié. Une modification de ces éléments impose un nouvel aperçu. L’application revérifie la couverture et publie par comparaison-échange ; une annulation avant publication conserve toutes les racines. Choisir de 1 à 1024 sélecteurs uniques. Les archives simples reconnues sont reset , reset-index , rebase-index , stage-index , squash , amend , checkout , fetch , publish , bisect , stash et les historiques détachés, ainsi que les racines exactes native-fetch/REVISION . Les têtes, tags actifs, index et verrous d’assets sont refusés. Pour les archives structurées, sélectionner le groupe entier : archive/branches/ID retire ensemble le descripteur, la cible et l’éventuel index figé ; archive/tags/ID retire ensemble le descripteur, la cible et l’éventuelle annotation. native branch-archives et native tag-archives acceptent --after ID , --limit N et --verify pour retrouver les groupes. Un membre /target , /record , /index ou /annotation ne peut pas être retiré seul. Les groupes incomplets, membres inattendus et liaisons d’annotation/base d’index incorrectes sont refusés. La sauvegarde doit contenir les références exactes du groupe ; les mêmes blocs sous d’autres noms ne constituent pas une recette de restauration. Les lignes du plan ajoutent name , la table roots , kind: branch-group ou tag-group , et la révision cible. removed_references compte les références développées. La sortie reste inférieure à 16 Mio, échappement JSON inclus. Les groupes et racines simples peuvent partager une transaction. Les branches ou tags recréés sous l’ancien nom, HEAD, les fichiers de travail et les autres références restent intacts. Le dépôt principal utilise SYRNCK_KEY , la sauvegarde secondaire SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY ou aucune clé si cette variable est absente. Les clés peuvent différer. Les deux dépôts restent verrouillés ; toute récupération de checkout doit être terminée auparavant. D’autres racines peuvent encore conserver les versions : aucun gain disque immédiat n’est garanti. Après collecte locale, restaurer la sauvegarde dans un dépôt séparé avec backup restore , puis appeler native branch-restore ID ou native tag-restore ID . Modifier directement les références de la sauvegarde invalide son descripteur. Conserver la sauvegarde et sa clé selon la politique voulue ; leur suppression ultérieure reste administrable. Les politiques automatiques par âge/release et le GC distribué restent à développer. Les archives de workspace utilisent aussi un sélecteur de groupe entier : archive/workspaces/ID . Le retrait conserve la même exigence de sauvegarde exacte et retire ensemble descripteur, HEAD, révision physique et toutes les anciennes références du contexte. Aucun membre ne peut être retiré seul. native workspace-archives accepte les mêmes options de pagination/vérification ; les lignes du plan utilisent kind: workspace-group . Après restauration de la sauvegarde dans un Store séparé, utiliser workspace-archive-restore-plan puis workspace-archive-restore . Le dernier observateur de progression intervient avant la revérification finale. Cette dernière et le CAS n’appellent plus d’observateur, afin qu’un callback modifiant les références ou la sauvegarde n’autorise pas un retrait périmé. Voir aussi le retrait des reçus de checkout . text Copier syrnck native archives --kind reset syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck gc text Copier syrnck native branch-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native archives --kind reset syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck gc syrnck native branch-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","headings":[{"id":"retire-native-archives-with-a-verified-backup","text":"Retirer des archives avec une sauvegarde vérifiée","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native archives --kind reset\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire archive/reset/REVISION --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck native branch-archives --verify\nsyrnck native tag-archives --verify\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup retire archive/branches/BRANCH_ID archive/tags/TAG_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspace-archives","#/fr/checkout-retention"],"kind":"guide","revision":"b773d0b1f8cd"},{"id":"asset-locks","title":"Verrous temporaires d’assets","description":"Guide : Verrous temporaires d’assets.","section":"Stockage","order":30,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"repository-local-asset-leases\">Verrous temporaires d’assets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/asset-locks/repository-local-asset-leases\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Verrous temporaires d’assets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck::AssetLocks\u003c/code> fournit une table de baux atomiques dans un Store existant : acquisition groupée, renouvellement, libération et purge des expirations. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">droits serveur\u003c/a> la relient aux identités HTTP authentifiées et aux contrôles de publication des branches. L’appelant C++ local fournit un propriétaire de confiance. Le compteur \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code> bloque les opérations périmées ; ce n’est pas un identifiant d’authentification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"acquire-and-inspect\">Acquérir et consulter\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/asset-locks/acquire-and-inspect\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Acquérir et consulter\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Tous les chemins d’un groupe sont réservés ensemble ou aucun ne l’est. Ils désignent des fichiers exacts relatifs au dépôt, sans verrouiller des sous-arbres ni analyser les fichiers de travail. Normalisation Unicode et casefold identifient les variantes portables ; les doublons équivalents sont refusés. Un verrou actif bloque une nouvelle acquisition, même par son propriétaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan ne lit que les métadonnées et n’écrit aucun objet. Prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : code 1 et conflits JSON. Il ne réserve rien et ne garantit pas une génération future : l’acquisition recalcule l’état sous verrou. Les autres erreurs produisent un diagnostic sans succès partiel. Les clés et motifs JSON restent anglais.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La liste expose propriétaire, orthographe initiale, génération, dates d’acquisition/expiration et \u003ccode>active\u003c/code>. Un enregistrement expiré reste visible jusqu’à remplacement, libération ou purge. Pagination : 100 entrées par défaut, limite 1..1000, \u003ccode>--after PATH\u003c/code> avec la valeur \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> précédente, ordre des chemins normalisés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks list --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --lang fr locks list\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"renew-release-and-prune\">Renouveler, libérer et purger\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/asset-locks/renew-release-and-prune\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Renouveler, libérer et purger\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Utiliser la génération rendue à l’acquisition. Propriétaire et générations de toutes les demandes doivent correspondre, sinon tout le groupe est refusé. Le CLI partage une génération ; l’API C++ accepte une génération par chemin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le renouvellement conserve génération et date d’acquisition, prolonge l’expiration sans la raccourcir et exige que tout le groupe soit encore actif à la publication. Un bail expiré nécessite une nouvelle acquisition. La libération accepte un bail expiré correspondant, mais refuse une ancienne demande après réattribution. \u003ccode>prune\u003c/code> prévisualise les enregistrements expirés ; \u003ccode>--delete\u003c/code> les retire ensemble par transaction de références attendues et conserve les actifs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque acquisition incrémente un compteur 64 bits non signé conservé après libération, purge et GC ; une ancienne génération n’est jamais recyclée par l’API. L’épuisement refuse les acquisitions. Un chemin expiré peut être repris directement, avec une nouvelle génération. Modifier manuellement les racines internes contourne ces garanties.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les durées vont de 1 à 86400 secondes. L’API utilise une horloge Unix de confiance, revérifie l’expiration à la publication et refuse un recul d’horloge observé. L’horloge C++ injectée appartient au service, pas au client ; le CLI ne propose aucun remplacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck locks prune --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"storage-and-api\">Stockage et API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/asset-locks/storage-and-api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage et API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La table utilise des chunks immuables et une mise à jour atomique des références. \u003ccode>asset-locks/clock\u003c/code> conserve la génération ; \u003ccode>asset-locks/paths/BLAKE3_FOLDED_PATH\u003c/code> pointe vers l’enregistrement courant. Celui-ci contient \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>owner\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fence\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expires_at\u003c/code>. Les lecteurs vérifient schéma, octets canoniques, identité du chemin, dates et cohérence avec le compteur. Le chiffrement habituel protège ces objets au repos.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les références courantes survivent à l’empaquetage et au GC ; les anciens enregistrements sans référence sont collectables. Un verrou ne conserve pas les octets d’un asset : branches, révisions, tags et pins assurent cette rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Limites : 4096 enregistrements, expirés compris ; 1..1024 chemins par demande ; 4096 octets UTF-8 par chemin ; propriétaire de 1..256 octets UTF-8 sans caractère de contrôle. Les règles de chemins du dépôt restent applicables. Préserver le verrou exclusif et ne pas réentrer depuis un callback. Une annulation avant publication conserve la table visible et peut laisser des métadonnées complètes non référencées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/asset_locks.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>AssetLocks \u003cspan class=\"token function\">locks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> acquired \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> locks\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">acquire\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> generation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> acquired\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"locks\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">uint64_t\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">locks\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">renew\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> generation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">                       \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> generation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3600\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Verrous temporaires d’assets # syrnck::AssetLocks fournit une table de baux atomiques dans un Store existant : acquisition groupée, renouvellement, libération et purge des expirations. Les droits serveur la relient aux identités HTTP authentifiées et aux contrôles de publication des branches. L’appelant C++ local fournit un propriétaire de confiance. Le compteur fence bloque les opérations périmées ; ce n’est pas un identifiant d’authentification. Acquérir et consulter # Tous les chemins d’un groupe sont réservés ensemble ou aucun ne l’est. Ils désignent des fichiers exacts relatifs au dépôt, sans verrouiller des sous-arbres ni analyser les fichiers de travail. Normalisation Unicode et casefold identifient les variantes portables ; les doublons équivalents sont refusés. Un verrou actif bloque une nouvelle acquisition, même par son propriétaire. Le plan ne lit que les métadonnées et n’écrit aucun objet. Prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : code 1 et conflits JSON. Il ne réserve rien et ne garantit pas une génération future : l’acquisition recalcule l’état sous verrou. Les autres erreurs produisent un diagnostic sans succès partiel. Les clés et motifs JSON restent anglais. La liste expose propriétaire, orthographe initiale, génération, dates d’acquisition/expiration et active . Un enregistrement expiré reste visible jusqu’à remplacement, libération ou purge. Pagination : 100 entrées par défaut, limite 1..1000, --after PATH avec la valeur next précédente, ordre des chemins normalisés. text Copier syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --limit 100 syrnck --lang fr locks list Renouveler, libérer et purger # Utiliser la génération rendue à l’acquisition. Propriétaire et générations de toutes les demandes doivent correspondre, sinon tout le groupe est refusé. Le CLI partage une génération ; l’API C++ accepte une génération par chemin. Le renouvellement conserve génération et date d’acquisition, prolonge l’expiration sans la raccourcir et exige que tout le groupe soit encore actif à la publication. Un bail expiré nécessite une nouvelle acquisition. La libération accepte un bail expiré correspondant, mais refuse une ancienne demande après réattribution. prune prévisualise les enregistrements expirés ; --delete les retire ensemble par transaction de références attendues et conserve les actifs. Chaque acquisition incrémente un compteur 64 bits non signé conservé après libération, purge et GC ; une ancienne génération n’est jamais recyclée par l’API. L’épuisement refuse les acquisitions. Un chemin expiré peut être repris directement, avec une nouvelle génération. Modifier manuellement les racines internes contourne ces garanties. Les durées vont de 1 à 86400 secondes. L’API utilise une horloge Unix de confiance, revérifie l’expiration à la publication et refuse un recul d’horloge observé. L’horloge C++ injectée appartient au service, pas au client ; le CLI ne propose aucun remplacement. text Copier syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune syrnck locks prune --delete Stockage et API # La table utilise des chunks immuables et une mise à jour atomique des références. asset-locks/clock conserve la génération ; asset-locks/paths/BLAKE3_FOLDED_PATH pointe vers l’enregistrement courant. Celui-ci contient version , type , path , owner , fence , created_at , expires_at . Les lecteurs vérifient schéma, octets canoniques, identité du chemin, dates et cohérence avec le compteur. Le chiffrement habituel protège ces objets au repos. Les références courantes survivent à l’empaquetage et au GC ; les anciens enregistrements sans référence sont collectables. Un verrou ne conserve pas les octets d’un asset : branches, révisions, tags et pins assurent cette rétention. Limites : 4096 enregistrements, expirés compris ; 1..1024 chemins par demande ; 4096 octets UTF-8 par chemin ; propriétaire de 1..256 octets UTF-8 sans caractère de contrôle. Les règles de chemins du dépôt restent applicables. Préserver le verrou exclusif et ne pas réentrer depuis un callback. Une annulation avant publication conserve la table visible et peut laisser des métadonnées complètes non référencées. cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp> syrnck :: AssetLocks locks ( store ) ; auto acquired = locks . acquire ( \"alice\" , { \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"Content/Map.ubulk\" } ) ; const auto generation = acquired [ \"locks\" ] [ 0 ] [ \"fence\" ] . get \u003c std :: uint64_t > ( ) ; locks . renew ( \"alice\" , { { \"Content/Map.uasset\" , generation } , { \"Content/Map.ubulk\" , generation } } , 3600 ) ; syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --limit 100 syrnck --lang fr locks list syrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune syrnck locks prune --delete #include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp> syrnck::AssetLocks locks(store); auto acquired = locks.acquire(\"alice\", {\"Content/Map.uasset\", \"Content/Map.ubulk\"}); const auto generation = acquired[\"locks\"][0][\"fence\"].get\u003cstd::uint64_t>(); locks.renew(\"alice\", {{\"Content/Map.uasset\", generation}, {\"Content/Map.ubulk\", generation}}, 3600);","headings":[{"id":"repository-local-asset-leases","text":"Verrous temporaires d’assets","level":1},{"id":"acquire-and-inspect","text":"Acquérir et consulter","level":2},{"id":"renew-release-and-prune","text":"Renouveler, libérer et purger","level":2},{"id":"storage-and-api","text":"Stockage et API","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck locks acquire-plan --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks acquire --owner alice --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks list --limit 100\nsyrnck --lang fr locks list\nsyrnck locks renew --owner alice --fence 1 --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks release --owner alice --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks prune\nsyrnck locks prune --delete\n#include \u003csyrnck/asset_locks.hpp>\n\nsyrnck::AssetLocks locks(store);\nauto acquired = locks.acquire(\"alice\", {\"Content/Map.uasset\", \"Content/Map.ubulk\"});\nconst auto generation = acquired[\"locks\"][0][\"fence\"].get\u003cstd::uint64_t>();\nlocks.renew(\"alice\", {{\"Content/Map.uasset\", generation},\n                       {\"Content/Map.ubulk\", generation}}, 3600);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/server-access"],"kind":"guide","revision":"93d6baa95901"},{"id":"audit","title":"Vérification d’intégrité","description":"Guide : Vérification d’intégrité.","section":"Stockage","order":40,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"streaming-integrity-audits\">Audits d’intégrité en flux\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/audit/streaming-integrity-audits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Audits d’intégrité en flux\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::fsck()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck fsck\u003c/code> vérifient tous les objets logiques actifs, y compris les révisions, manifestes et blocs inaccessibles. L’audit est en lecture seule ; le GC contrôle la rétention et les suppressions. Après un GC interrompu, des métadonnées mortes isolées peuvent temporairement référencer des blocs morts déjà retirés : voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\">reprise du nettoyage\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’audit recharge la configuration et tous les index actifs, contourne les caches décodés et de validation, vérifie les références puis énumère révisions, manifestes et blocs sans inventaire complet d’IDs. Les bornes de pages sélectionnent les types ; les enregistrements mixtes restent filtrés à leur position d’origine. Les métadonnées précèdent les blocs autonomes : chaque hash complet est calculé en lisant les blocs dans l’ordre. Un bloc partagé ou répété peut être relu pour plusieurs fichiers ; aucune garantie d’une lecture unique par objet n’est donnée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque lecture contrôle enveloppe, authentification chiffrée, ID et format. Dépendances absentes ou hash complet incorrect font échouer l’audit, même dans les métadonnées inaccessibles. Un doublon isolé/empaqueté compte une fois mais ses deux copies sont contrôlées. Un pack inactif est hors inventaire et ne retient rien.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-lifetime\">Mémoire et durée de vie\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/audit/memory-and-lifetime\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mémoire et durée de vie\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les objets empaquetés utilisent un bit par entrée, arrondi séparément par pack : 131 072 octets pour 1 048 576 entrées. Répertoire, filtre et cache de pages ont leurs propres coûts : voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">index\u003c/a>. Les objets absents des packs nécessitent un ensemble compact d’IDs typés. Références, pile de dépendances, JSON, allocations et objets décodés s’ajoutent ; un manifeste ou une révision reste matérialisé. La mémoire totale n’est pas indépendante de la taille du projet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque page possède son tampon, conservé lors des lectures imbriquées même sans cache. Les sessions partagent un nombre borné de handles, fermés au retour extérieur ou sur exception. La politique ordinaire du cache décodé est rétablie après succès ou erreur. Le verrou exclusif couvre l’opération. \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> retournent encore des collections complètes ; la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/compaction\">compaction\u003c/a> utilise des plans en flux et trie les clés isolées. Aucun format de stockage, réseau ou ABI ne change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification-and-measurement\">Interpréter les ressources\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/audit/verification-and-measurement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Interpréter les ressources\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance\">budgets et limites de performance\u003c/a> pour les compteurs de mémoire et caches. Un corpus synthétique ne prouve ni débit ni capacité sur un projet Unreal. L’audit contrôle les octets réellement stockés indépendamment des statistiques de cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"progress-and-cancellation\">Progression et annulation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/audit/progress-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Progression et annulation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> accepte \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code>. Les compteurs suivent les lectures décodées sans matérialiser l’inventaire. L’interruption ferme les sessions et rétablit le cache ; objets et rétention restent inchangés. Voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">contrat des opérations\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Audits d’intégrité en flux # Store::fsck() et syrnck fsck vérifient tous les objets logiques actifs, y compris les révisions, manifestes et blocs inaccessibles. L’audit est en lecture seule ; le GC contrôle la rétention et les suppressions. Après un GC interrompu, des métadonnées mortes isolées peuvent temporairement référencer des blocs morts déjà retirés : voir la reprise du nettoyage . L’audit recharge la configuration et tous les index actifs, contourne les caches décodés et de validation, vérifie les références puis énumère révisions, manifestes et blocs sans inventaire complet d’IDs. Les bornes de pages sélectionnent les types ; les enregistrements mixtes restent filtrés à leur position d’origine. Les métadonnées précèdent les blocs autonomes : chaque hash complet est calculé en lisant les blocs dans l’ordre. Un bloc partagé ou répété peut être relu pour plusieurs fichiers ; aucune garantie d’une lecture unique par objet n’est donnée. Chaque lecture contrôle enveloppe, authentification chiffrée, ID et format. Dépendances absentes ou hash complet incorrect font échouer l’audit, même dans les métadonnées inaccessibles. Un doublon isolé/empaqueté compte une fois mais ses deux copies sont contrôlées. Un pack inactif est hors inventaire et ne retient rien. Mémoire et durée de vie # Les objets empaquetés utilisent un bit par entrée, arrondi séparément par pack : 131 072 octets pour 1 048 576 entrées. Répertoire, filtre et cache de pages ont leurs propres coûts : voir les index . Les objets absents des packs nécessitent un ensemble compact d’IDs typés. Références, pile de dépendances, JSON, allocations et objets décodés s’ajoutent ; un manifeste ou une révision reste matérialisé. La mémoire totale n’est pas indépendante de la taille du projet. Chaque page possède son tampon, conservé lors des lectures imbriquées même sans cache. Les sessions partagent un nombre borné de handles, fermés au retour extérieur ou sur exception. La politique ordinaire du cache décodé est rétablie après succès ou erreur. Le verrou exclusif couvre l’opération. objects() et closure() retournent encore des collections complètes ; la compaction utilise des plans en flux et trie les clés isolées. Aucun format de stockage, réseau ou ABI ne change. Interpréter les ressources # Voir les budgets et limites de performance pour les compteurs de mémoire et caches. Un corpus synthétique ne prouve ni débit ni capacité sur un projet Unreal. L’audit contrôle les octets réellement stockés indépendamment des statistiques de cache. Progression et annulation # fsck accepte OperationOptions , --progress et --progress-json . Les compteurs suivent les lectures décodées sans matérialiser l’inventaire. L’interruption ferme les sessions et rétablit le cache ; objets et rétention restent inchangés. Voir le contrat des opérations .","headings":[{"id":"streaming-integrity-audits","text":"Audits d’intégrité en flux","level":1},{"id":"memory-and-lifetime","text":"Mémoire et durée de vie","level":2},{"id":"verification-and-measurement","text":"Interpréter les ressources","level":2},{"id":"progress-and-cancellation","text":"Progression et annulation","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/compaction","#/fr/performance","#/fr/operations"],"kind":"guide","revision":"fcd8ecea5132"},{"id":"backups","title":"Sauvegardes, restauration et réparation","description":"Guide : Sauvegardes, restauration et réparation.","section":"Stockage","order":50,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair\">Sauvegardes vérifiées et restauration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sauvegardes vérifiées et restauration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>L’API \u003ccode>syrnck/backup.hpp\u003c/code> crée un snapshot logique de toutes les racines conservées : parents des révisions, anciennes têtes, tags, index figés, stashes et compteur des verrous d’assets. Les hashes complets des fichiers sont contrôlés avant publication ; la source reste intacte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La destination doit être un nouveau dossier, hors du Store source, avec un parent existant. Même vide, une destination existante est refusée. Création et restauration préparent un dossier frère privé, le synchronisent puis le publient par renommage exclusif. Une annulation ou exception antérieure ne rend pas visible de destination partielle. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser un frère \u003ccode>.syrnck-export-*\u003c/code> non publié : il n’est pas une sauvegarde valide. Réessayer crée une nouvelle préparation ; le nettoyage automatique reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Arrêter le serveur. Le verrou exclusif exclut les écrivains normaux. Checkout actif, transfert incomplet et bail de lecture/envoi actif non expiré bloquent la création ; les terminer ou retirer explicitement auparavant. Les baux d’assets conservent leurs dates d’expiration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ce n’est pas une image du système de fichiers : fichiers de travail, modifications non préparées, ignorés/non suivis, métadonnées Git, objets inaccessibles, journaux de transfert et dossiers privés de checkout ne sont pas copiés. Les liaisons absolues \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> et reçus de checkout terminés sont omis, mais les racines de versions source/cible restent présentes. La restauration produit un Store indépendant ; exporter ensuite la révision choisie pour récupérer les fichiers, puis recréer la liaison au workspace séparément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"verify-and-change-encryption-keys\">Vérifier et changer de clé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/verify-and-change-encryption-keys\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Vérifier et changer de clé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le descripteur canonique \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code> enregistre les hashes des références et préférences, le nombre d’objets, les octets décodés, la date et les références omises. \u003ccode>backup verify\u003c/code> contrôle ce reçu, toutes les dépendances, tous les hashes de fichiers et l’inventaire complet. Changer références/préférences ou ajouter un objet non référencé invalide le contrôle. Le reçu n’est ni signé ni authentifié par une autorité externe : protéger les sauvegardes contre les modifications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> ouvre le Store principal ; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> choisit éventuellement la clé de destination de create/restore. Sans cette dernière, le CLI conserve la clé d’une source chiffrée ; une source en clair reste en clair. L’API C++ exige une clé de destination pour une source chiffrée. Les IDs restent identiques après rechiffrement ; changer de clé ne révoque pas les anciennes copies et ne masque pas les égalités entre IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour vérifier une sauvegarde, fournir sa propre clé dans \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. Pour la restaurer avec une autre clé, fournir cette dernière dans \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code>. Une clé contient 32 octets, encodés en 64 caractères hexadécimaux ; la conserver hors de Git et des chemins de sauvegarde. Descripteur et références restent visibles. Il ne s’agit ni de chiffrement de bout en bout, ni de dérivation de mot de passe, ni de coffre système ou rotation distribuée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La restauration vérifie entièrement la sauvegarde, importe les objets décodés dans de nouvelles enveloppes, conserve références et préférences, puis vérifie le résultat. Le Store restauré n’a pas de reçu \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code>, afin de pouvoir évoluer normalement. Conserver l’original jusqu’au contrôle des fichiers exportés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"repair-missing-retained-dependencies\">Réparer les dépendances manquantes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/repair-missing-retained-dependencies\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Réparer les dépendances manquantes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait d’archives\u003c/a> utilise une sauvegarde vérifiée, un aperçu attendu et une transaction de références ; le GC reste explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour la réparation, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> ouvre la destination abîmée ; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> ouvre la sauvegarde donneuse, en clair si cette variable est absente. Les clés peuvent différer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code>, la commande est un plan en lecture seule : \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>corrupt\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>references_hash\u003c/code>. Un blocage rend le code 1 et du JSON ; appliquer un plan bloqué échoue avant toute réparation partielle. La sauvegarde est vérifiée complètement. Le plan parcourt les racines portables de la destination et n’utilise la sauvegarde qu’en cas d’objet absent. Il vérifie tous les hashes de fichiers dans cette vue combinée avant toute écriture. Une ancienne sauvegarde peut réparer un ancien asset tout en gardant des révisions récentes intactes qu’elle ne possède pas. Un objet absent des deux côtés bloque l’opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application importe uniquement les objets absents, chacun par enveloppe complète atomique. Une annulation ou coupure peut laisser un sous-ensemble réparé ; relancer termine de façon idempotente. Aucun objet existant ni référence n’est remplacé ou retiré. Sortie bornée à 16 Mio, ensembles d’identités encore en mémoire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tout objet existant corrompu bloque la réparation et reste disponible pour inspection. Cette API ne remplace pas les objets corrompus, ne reconstruit pas les packs perdus ni les configurations/références abîmées. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/repair-copy\">copie réparée\u003c/a> reconstruit plutôt un nouveau Store à partir des versions récentes intactes et de la sauvegarde, sans modifier les deux entrées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store damaged-store fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-limits\">Limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/validation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La vérification complète relit volontairement les données conservées. Le parcours ne dispose pas encore d’inventaire sur disque. Ces opérations ne promettent pas une durabilité universelle après panne d’alimentation. Réplication en direct, bascule multi-serveur, scrubbing programmé, rétention distribuée et remplacement automatique des données corrompues restent des fonctions distinctes à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"linked-workspaces\">Snapshots portables des workspaces liés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/linked-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Snapshots portables des workspaces liés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sauvegardes et copies réparées gardent chaque sélection liée dans des métadonnées\nportables avec sa révision exacte conservée et son index figé. Les chemins\nabsolus registration/HEAD et reçus sont omis. Sans capacités requises, ces\nexclusions utilisent le marqueur v2 ; avec exigences de lecteur, v3. Les anciennes\nv1/v2 restent prises en charge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après restauration du Store dans un nouveau dossier principal, lancer\n\u003ccode>native workspace-restore NOM NOUVEAU_DOSSIER [--branch NOM | --detach]\u003c/code>.\nLe défaut reprend la branche originale si sa tête correspond encore et qu’aucun\nautre workspace ne la sélectionne. Sinon choisir un autre nom ou HEAD détaché.\nL’index figé est matérialisé s’il existe ; HEAD garde la révision commitée.\nLes modifications de travail non préparées ne sont pas sauvegardées. Une\nexportation complète interrompue se reprend seulement si tous ses fichiers\ncorrespondent encore. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/restore\">la restauration\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reader-features\">Conservation des exigences de lecteur\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/backups/reader-features\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conservation des exigences de lecteur\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un Store avec capacités requises produit un marqueur v3, liant sa liste non vide\n\u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code> à la configuration sauvegardée. La vérification refuse une\nexigence modifiée ou retirée, y compris un marqueur rétrogradé en v1/v2 alors que\nle Store exige encore des capacités. Les sauvegardes classiques v1/v2 restent\nacceptées. Restauration et copies réparées conservent les exigences source,\navec leur clé de chiffrement indépendante et les règles d’export existantes.\nVoir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/protocol/required-store-features\">la sémantique des exigences\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Sauvegardes vérifiées et restauration # L’API syrnck/backup.hpp crée un snapshot logique de toutes les racines conservées : parents des révisions, anciennes têtes, tags, index figés, stashes et compteur des verrous d’assets. Les hashes complets des fichiers sont contrôlés avant publication ; la source reste intacte. La destination doit être un nouveau dossier, hors du Store source, avec un parent existant. Même vide, une destination existante est refusée. Création et restauration préparent un dossier frère privé, le synchronisent puis le publient par renommage exclusif. Une annulation ou exception antérieure ne rend pas visible de destination partielle. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser un frère .syrnck-export-* non publié : il n’est pas une sauvegarde valide. Réessayer crée une nouvelle préparation ; le nettoyage automatique reste à développer. Arrêter le serveur. Le verrou exclusif exclut les écrivains normaux. Checkout actif, transfert incomplet et bail de lecture/envoi actif non expiré bloquent la création ; les terminer ou retirer explicitement auparavant. Les baux d’assets conservent leurs dates d’expiration. Ce n’est pas une image du système de fichiers : fichiers de travail, modifications non préparées, ignorés/non suivis, métadonnées Git, objets inaccessibles, journaux de transfert et dossiers privés de checkout ne sont pas copiés. Les liaisons absolues workspace/HEAD et reçus de checkout terminés sont omis, mais les racines de versions source/cible restent présentes. La restauration produit un Store indépendant ; exporter ensuite la révision choisie pour récupérer les fichiers, puis recréer la liaison au workspace séparément. text Copier syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files Vérifier et changer de clé # Le descripteur canonique backup.json enregistre les hashes des références et préférences, le nombre d’objets, les octets décodés, la date et les références omises. backup verify contrôle ce reçu, toutes les dépendances, tous les hashes de fichiers et l’inventaire complet. Changer références/préférences ou ajouter un objet non référencé invalide le contrôle. Le reçu n’est ni signé ni authentifié par une autorité externe : protéger les sauvegardes contre les modifications. SYRNCK_KEY ouvre le Store principal ; SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY choisit éventuellement la clé de destination de create/restore. Sans cette dernière, le CLI conserve la clé d’une source chiffrée ; une source en clair reste en clair. L’API C++ exige une clé de destination pour une source chiffrée. Les IDs restent identiques après rechiffrement ; changer de clé ne révoque pas les anciennes copies et ne masque pas les égalités entre IDs. Pour vérifier une sauvegarde, fournir sa propre clé dans SYRNCK_KEY . Pour la restaurer avec une autre clé, fournir cette dernière dans SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY . Une clé contient 32 octets, encodés en 64 caractères hexadécimaux ; la conserver hors de Git et des chemins de sauvegarde. Descripteur et références restent visibles. Il ne s’agit ni de chiffrement de bout en bout, ni de dérivation de mot de passe, ni de coffre système ou rotation distribuée. La restauration vérifie entièrement la sauvegarde, importe les objets décodés dans de nouvelles enveloppes, conserve références et préférences, puis vérifie le résultat. Le Store restauré n’a pas de reçu backup.json , afin de pouvoir évoluer normalement. Conserver l’original jusqu’au contrôle des fichiers exportés. Réparer les dépendances manquantes # Le retrait d’archives utilise une sauvegarde vérifiée, un aperçu attendu et une transaction de références ; le GC reste explicite. Pour la réparation, SYRNCK_KEY ouvre la destination abîmée ; SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY ouvre la sauvegarde donneuse, en clair si cette variable est absente. Les clés peuvent différer. Sans --apply , la commande est un plan en lecture seule : ready , missing , corrupt , references_hash . Un blocage rend le code 1 et du JSON ; appliquer un plan bloqué échoue avant toute réparation partielle. La sauvegarde est vérifiée complètement. Le plan parcourt les racines portables de la destination et n’utilise la sauvegarde qu’en cas d’objet absent. Il vérifie tous les hashes de fichiers dans cette vue combinée avant toute écriture. Une ancienne sauvegarde peut réparer un ancien asset tout en gardant des révisions récentes intactes qu’elle ne possède pas. Un objet absent des deux côtés bloque l’opération. L’application importe uniquement les objets absents, chacun par enveloppe complète atomique. Une annulation ou coupure peut laisser un sous-ensemble réparé ; relancer termine de façon idempotente. Aucun objet existant ni référence n’est remplacé ou retiré. Sortie bornée à 16 Mio, ensembles d’identités encore en mémoire. Tout objet existant corrompu bloque la réparation et reste disponible pour inspection. Cette API ne remplace pas les objets corrompus, ne reconstruit pas les packs perdus ni les configurations/références abîmées. La copie réparée reconstruit plutôt un nouveau Store à partir des versions récentes intactes et de la sauvegarde, sans modifier les deux entrées. text Copier syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply syrnck --store damaged-store fsck Limites # La vérification complète relit volontairement les données conservées. Le parcours ne dispose pas encore d’inventaire sur disque. Ces opérations ne promettent pas une durabilité universelle après panne d’alimentation. Réplication en direct, bascule multi-serveur, scrubbing programmé, rétention distribuée et remplacement automatique des données corrompues restent des fonctions distinctes à développer. Snapshots portables des workspaces liés # Sauvegardes et copies réparées gardent chaque sélection liée dans des métadonnées portables avec sa révision exacte conservée et son index figé. Les chemins absolus registration/HEAD et reçus sont omis. Sans capacités requises, ces exclusions utilisent le marqueur v2 ; avec exigences de lecteur, v3. Les anciennes v1/v2 restent prises en charge. Après restauration du Store dans un nouveau dossier principal, lancer native workspace-restore NOM NOUVEAU_DOSSIER [--branch NOM | --detach] . Le défaut reprend la branche originale si sa tête correspond encore et qu’aucun autre workspace ne la sélectionne. Sinon choisir un autre nom ou HEAD détaché. L’index figé est matérialisé s’il existe ; HEAD garde la révision commitée. Les modifications de travail non préparées ne sont pas sauvegardées. Une exportation complète interrompue se reprend seulement si tous ses fichiers correspondent encore. Voir la restauration . Conservation des exigences de lecteur # Un Store avec capacités requises produit un marqueur v3, liant sa liste non vide required_features à la configuration sauvegardée. La vérification refuse une exigence modifiée ou retirée, y compris un marqueur rétrogradé en v1/v2 alors que le Store exige encore des capacités. Les sauvegardes classiques v1/v2 restent acceptées. Restauration et copies réparées conservent les exigences source, avec leur clé de chiffrement indépendante et les règles d’export existantes. Voir la sémantique des exigences . syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify syrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck syrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 syrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply syrnck --store damaged-store fsck","headings":[{"id":"verified-offline-backups-restore-and-missing-object-repair","text":"Sauvegardes vérifiées et restauration","level":1},{"id":"verify-and-change-encryption-keys","text":"Vérifier et changer de clé","level":2},{"id":"repair-missing-retained-dependencies","text":"Réparer les dépendances manquantes","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-limits","text":"Limites","level":2},{"id":"linked-workspaces","text":"Snapshots portables des workspaces liés","level":2},{"id":"reader-features","text":"Conservation des exigences de lecteur","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store project/.syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001\nsyrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup verify\nsyrnck --store /existing-backup-parent/snapshot-001 backup restore /existing-parent/recovered-store\nsyrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store fsck\nsyrnck --store /existing-parent/recovered-store native export heads/main /existing-parent/recovered-files\nsyrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001\nsyrnck --store damaged-store backup repair snapshot-001 --apply\nsyrnck --store damaged-store fsck","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/repair-copy","#/fr/native-workspaces/restore","#/fr/protocol/required-store-features"],"kind":"guide","revision":"582ce157d32c"},{"id":"checkout-retention","title":"Retrait des reçus de checkout","description":"Guide : Retrait des reçus de checkout.","section":"Stockage","order":60,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"completed-checkout-receipt-retirement\">Retrait des reçus de checkout terminés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention/completed-checkout-receipt-retirement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Retrait des reçus de checkout terminés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les checkouts terminés conservent un reçu immuable et une racine de révision cible ; leur archive source protège indépendamment les anciens fichiers. Ces racines couvrent aussi switch, reset hard et stash et survivent au GC ordinaire. L’API \u003ccode>syrnck/checkout_retention.hpp\u003c/code> fournit un inventaire paginé et un retrait explicite protégé par sauvegarde.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Par défaut, \u003ccode>checkouts/receipts/ID\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>checkouts/completed/ID\u003c/code> sont retirés ensemble. Ajouter \u003ccode>--include-source-archives\u003c/code> au plan et à l’application retire aussi \u003ccode>archive/checkout/ID\u003c/code> dans la même transaction. Aucun octet de fichier, fichier de travail, tête de branche, index ou dossier privé de récupération n’est supprimé. Le GC ultérieur est séparé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"coverage-and-fences\">Couverture et contrôles de publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention/coverage-and-fences\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Couverture et contrôles de publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sélectionner 1..1024 IDs canoniques uniques. Le plan lie l’emplacement source, la table complète des références, les groupes reçu/cible/source choisis, l’option d’archive et le snapshot exact de sauvegarde. Toute modification impose un nouveau plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La sauvegarde distincte doit vérifier descripteur, inventaire et fichiers, puis reconstruire chaque cible et chaque source retirée avec tous ses parents, manifestes et blocs. Les reçus contenant des chemins locaux sont exclus de la sauvegarde ; les racines portables restent présentes. Un Store restauré les affiche avec \u003ccode>retained_only: true\u003c/code> et permet aussi leur retrait protégé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un checkout en cours bloque inspection et retrait. Pins pendants orphelins, groupes contradictoires, liens dans les dossiers et données privées de récupération restantes entraînent un refus. Terminer la récupération normale ; le retrait ne nettoie ni n’adopte de fichier inconnu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le dernier observateur intervient avant revérification du plan et de la sauvegarde. La vérification finale et le CAS n’ont plus de callbacks, mais restent annulables. Un callback ne peut donc pas faire appliquer un plan sur des preuves périmées. Avant publication, l’annulation conserve toutes les références ; une coupure laisse le groupe ancien ou nouveau entier. Après perte de réponse, réinspecter et refaire le plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> ouvre la source, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> la sauvegarde. Conserver copie et clé selon la politique souhaitée. D’autres pins, branches, stashes ou archives peuvent encore garder les mêmes versions : le retrait ne garantit pas un gain immédiat et n’empêche pas un administrateur de supprimer la sauvegarde plus tard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"historical-receipts\">Consulter un reçu historique\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention/historical-receipts\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Consulter un reçu historique\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout-status --id ID\u003c/code> vérifie le reçu immuable sans exiger l’ancienne sélection HEAD ou une source explicitement retirée. Ces liaisons restent en revanche nécessaires pour récupérer activement un journal. Identité et hash du reçu sont toujours contrôlés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Si la source a été retirée puis collectée, le statut rend \u003ccode>files: null\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>files_available: false\u003c/code> plutôt que d’inventer des actions. Pour reconstruire les fichiers, utiliser une racine de révision encore conservée ou la sauvegarde. Le reçu seul ne protège pas les octets retirés. Après retrait du reçu, son statut devient une transaction inconnue ; la restauration d’une sauvegarde ne recrée volontairement pas les reçus liés aux workspaces locaux.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-limits\">Limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention/validation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les pages sont limitées à 1000 entrées. Tables de références et rapports gardent leur plafond de 16 Mio. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> contrôle toutes les fermetures affichées sans écrire de fichier. Les payloads sont vérifiés en flux ; les identités d’objets du parcours restent en mémoire.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Retrait des reçus de checkout terminés # Les checkouts terminés conservent un reçu immuable et une racine de révision cible ; leur archive source protège indépendamment les anciens fichiers. Ces racines couvrent aussi switch, reset hard et stash et survivent au GC ordinaire. L’API syrnck/checkout_retention.hpp fournit un inventaire paginé et un retrait explicite protégé par sauvegarde. Par défaut, checkouts/receipts/ID et checkouts/completed/ID sont retirés ensemble. Ajouter --include-source-archives au plan et à l’application retire aussi archive/checkout/ID dans la même transaction. Aucun octet de fichier, fichier de travail, tête de branche, index ou dossier privé de récupération n’est supprimé. Le GC ultérieur est séparé. text Copier syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100 syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID Couverture et contrôles de publication # Sélectionner 1..1024 IDs canoniques uniques. Le plan lie l’emplacement source, la table complète des références, les groupes reçu/cible/source choisis, l’option d’archive et le snapshot exact de sauvegarde. Toute modification impose un nouveau plan. La sauvegarde distincte doit vérifier descripteur, inventaire et fichiers, puis reconstruire chaque cible et chaque source retirée avec tous ses parents, manifestes et blocs. Les reçus contenant des chemins locaux sont exclus de la sauvegarde ; les racines portables restent présentes. Un Store restauré les affiche avec retained_only: true et permet aussi leur retrait protégé. Un checkout en cours bloque inspection et retrait. Pins pendants orphelins, groupes contradictoires, liens dans les dossiers et données privées de récupération restantes entraînent un refus. Terminer la récupération normale ; le retrait ne nettoie ni n’adopte de fichier inconnu. Le dernier observateur intervient avant revérification du plan et de la sauvegarde. La vérification finale et le CAS n’ont plus de callbacks, mais restent annulables. Un callback ne peut donc pas faire appliquer un plan sur des preuves périmées. Avant publication, l’annulation conserve toutes les références ; une coupure laisse le groupe ancien ou nouveau entier. Après perte de réponse, réinspecter et refaire le plan. SYRNCK_KEY ouvre la source, SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY la sauvegarde. Conserver copie et clé selon la politique souhaitée. D’autres pins, branches, stashes ou archives peuvent encore garder les mêmes versions : le retrait ne garantit pas un gain immédiat et n’empêche pas un administrateur de supprimer la sauvegarde plus tard. Consulter un reçu historique # native checkout-status --id ID vérifie le reçu immuable sans exiger l’ancienne sélection HEAD ou une source explicitement retirée. Ces liaisons restent en revanche nécessaires pour récupérer activement un journal. Identité et hash du reçu sont toujours contrôlés. Si la source a été retirée puis collectée, le statut rend files: null et files_available: false plutôt que d’inventer des actions. Pour reconstruire les fichiers, utiliser une racine de révision encore conservée ou la sauvegarde. Le reçu seul ne protège pas les octets retirés. Après retrait du reçu, son statut devient une transaction inconnue ; la restauration d’une sauvegarde ne recrée volontairement pas les reçus liés aux workspaces locaux. Limites # Les pages sont limitées à 1000 entrées. Tables de références et rapports gardent leur plafond de 16 Mio. --verify contrôle toutes les fermetures affichées sans écrire de fichier. Les payloads sont vérifiés en flux ; les identités d’objets du parcours restent en mémoire. syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify syrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100 syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement syrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","headings":[{"id":"completed-checkout-receipt-retirement","text":"Retrait des reçus de checkout terminés","level":1},{"id":"coverage-and-fences","text":"Couverture et contrôles de publication","level":2},{"id":"historical-receipts","text":"Consulter un reçu historique","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-limits","text":"Limites","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native checkout-receipts --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native checkout-receipts --after TRANSACTION_ID --limit 100\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup checkout-retire-plan TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement\nsyrnck backup checkout-retire TRANSACTION_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/before-retirement --expect-plan PLAN_ID","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"6d1192b60d1f"},{"id":"compaction","title":"Empaquetage et compaction en flux","description":"Guide : Empaquetage et compaction en flux.","section":"Stockage","order":70,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"streaming-packing-and-compaction\">Empaquetage et compaction en flux\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compaction/streaming-packing-and-compaction\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Empaquetage et compaction en flux\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck pack\u003c/code> ajoute des packs pour les nouveaux objets isolés ; \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> regroupe tous les objets actifs, même inaccessibles. Le \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> retire les objets non retenus. Les manifestes complets et leurs blocs sont préservés sans chaîne de deltas binaires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux opérations commencent par un audit complet, hashes de fichiers et doublons isolés/empaquetés compris. Les enveloppes sont conservées exactement, sans recompression ni changement de clé ; les lectures privilégient la copie isolée.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordered-input-and-two-passes\">Entrées triées et deux passages\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compaction/ordered-input-and-two-passes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Entrées triées et deux passages\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque index immuable est déjà trié. La compaction fusionne les index avec une tête et une page possédée de 256 entrées par pack. Les recherches imbriquées n’invalident pas les pages, même sans cache ; la session bornée de handles se ferme à épuisement, annulation ou erreur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seules les clés des objets exclusivement isolés sont collectées et triées : 33 octets utiles par clé avant capacité/allocation. Les doublons empaquetés n’ajoutent aucune clé. Le pack incrémental consomme cette séquence ; la compaction la fusionne avec les index sans liste complète d’IDs empaquetés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le premier passage vérifie ordre strict, nombres, longueurs d’enveloppes, budgets et capacité. Il garde nombre, taille et digest BLAKE3 de séquence par pack de sortie. Une capacité insuffisante ou source malformée échoue avant lecture des payloads ou création des sorties.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le second passage repart d’un curseur neuf et écrit les packs prévus. Le digest lie IDs typés et longueurs au plan ; tout changement d’ID, taille ou nombre, ou erreur source/codec, arrête le lot. Les fichiers déjà terminés restent inactifs jusqu’au nettoyage, sans remplacer le catalogue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Toutes les nouvelles enveloppes sont relues et vérifiées par pages avant une publication atomique unique. Les curseurs et sessions de l’ancien catalogue sont fermés avant publication. Un nouveau parcours des objets isolés retire les copies confirmées dans le catalogue publié ; les packs générés inactifs sont retirés en dernier. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs\">reprise\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-remaining-limits\">Mémoire et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compaction/memory-and-remaining-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mémoire et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Planification, partitions, relecture et nettoyage ne gardent aucune liste complète d’IDs empaquetés. La fusion conserve au plus 11 520 octets de données d’entrées par pack, soit 11,25 Mio à 1 024 packs. Tas, curseurs, snapshots, répertoires/filtres, caches, enveloppes, codecs et audit initial s’ajoutent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le tri des clés isolées, leurs marques d’audit et les métadonnées restent proportionnels. \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> matérialisent leurs résultats. Il n’existe ni garantie globale de mémoire constante, ni relèvement des plafonds du catalogue, ni maintenance concurrente en ligne. Le verrou exclusif couvre l’opération. Formats, IDs, options et ABI C restent identiques.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tests-and-reproduction\">Préparer la maintenance\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compaction/tests-and-reproduction\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer la maintenance\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Prévoir la place d’une génération complète avant retrait des anciens packs. Garder une sauvegarde vérifiée et examiner le résultat avant le GC destructif. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs/publication-et-reprise\">publication et reprise\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026\">Limites de performance\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compaction/windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites de performance\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le débit dépend de la disposition des objets, des codecs, du stockage et des caches. Les plans en flux réduisent les inventaires d’IDs sans garantir un débit supérieur. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance\">budgets\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Empaquetage et compaction en flux # syrnck pack ajoute des packs pour les nouveaux objets isolés ; pack --compact regroupe tous les objets actifs, même inaccessibles. Le gc retire les objets non retenus. Les manifestes complets et leurs blocs sont préservés sans chaîne de deltas binaires. Les deux opérations commencent par un audit complet, hashes de fichiers et doublons isolés/empaquetés compris. Les enveloppes sont conservées exactement, sans recompression ni changement de clé ; les lectures privilégient la copie isolée. Entrées triées et deux passages # Chaque index immuable est déjà trié. La compaction fusionne les index avec une tête et une page possédée de 256 entrées par pack. Les recherches imbriquées n’invalident pas les pages, même sans cache ; la session bornée de handles se ferme à épuisement, annulation ou erreur. Seules les clés des objets exclusivement isolés sont collectées et triées : 33 octets utiles par clé avant capacité/allocation. Les doublons empaquetés n’ajoutent aucune clé. Le pack incrémental consomme cette séquence ; la compaction la fusionne avec les index sans liste complète d’IDs empaquetés. Le premier passage vérifie ordre strict, nombres, longueurs d’enveloppes, budgets et capacité. Il garde nombre, taille et digest BLAKE3 de séquence par pack de sortie. Une capacité insuffisante ou source malformée échoue avant lecture des payloads ou création des sorties. Le second passage repart d’un curseur neuf et écrit les packs prévus. Le digest lie IDs typés et longueurs au plan ; tout changement d’ID, taille ou nombre, ou erreur source/codec, arrête le lot. Les fichiers déjà terminés restent inactifs jusqu’au nettoyage, sans remplacer le catalogue. Toutes les nouvelles enveloppes sont relues et vérifiées par pages avant une publication atomique unique. Les curseurs et sessions de l’ancien catalogue sont fermés avant publication. Un nouveau parcours des objets isolés retire les copies confirmées dans le catalogue publié ; les packs générés inactifs sont retirés en dernier. Voir la reprise . Mémoire et limites # Planification, partitions, relecture et nettoyage ne gardent aucune liste complète d’IDs empaquetés. La fusion conserve au plus 11 520 octets de données d’entrées par pack, soit 11,25 Mio à 1 024 packs. Tas, curseurs, snapshots, répertoires/filtres, caches, enveloppes, codecs et audit initial s’ajoutent. Le tri des clés isolées, leurs marques d’audit et les métadonnées restent proportionnels. objects() et closure() matérialisent leurs résultats. Il n’existe ni garantie globale de mémoire constante, ni relèvement des plafonds du catalogue, ni maintenance concurrente en ligne. Le verrou exclusif couvre l’opération. Formats, IDs, options et ABI C restent identiques. Préparer la maintenance # Prévoir la place d’une génération complète avant retrait des anciens packs. Garder une sauvegarde vérifiée et examiner le résultat avant le GC destructif. Voir la publication et reprise . Limites de performance # Le débit dépend de la disposition des objets, des codecs, du stockage et des caches. Les plans en flux réduisent les inventaires d’IDs sans garantir un débit supérieur. Voir les budgets .","headings":[{"id":"streaming-packing-and-compaction","text":"Empaquetage et compaction en flux","level":1},{"id":"ordered-input-and-two-passes","text":"Entrées triées et deux passages","level":2},{"id":"memory-and-remaining-limits","text":"Mémoire et limites","level":2},{"id":"tests-and-reproduction","text":"Préparer la maintenance","level":2},{"id":"windows-x64-measurement-4-november-2026","text":"Limites de performance","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/packs","#/fr/packs/publication-et-reprise","#/fr/performance"],"kind":"guide","revision":"a4121322cc8b"},{"id":"compatibility","title":"Compatibilité et migration C++20","description":"Compatibilité et migration C++20","section":"Premiers pas","order":20,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"formats\">Formats conservés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compatibility/formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Formats conservés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Moteur, CLI, serveur, agent, filtres et ABI C utilisent C++20/CMake. Les dépendances C natives ne demandent aucun compilateur/runtime Rust. La base de compatibilité conserve IDs BLAKE3 typés v1, manifestes/révisions canoniques, frontières FastCDC 2020 (fastcdc 5.0.0, normalisation 1, seed 0), enveloppes SYR1/Zstd/XChaCha20-Poly1305, configuration/références physiques v1, pointeurs Git et HTTP v1. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/protocol\">formats\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les packs récents demandent un lecteur compatible : pack explicite peut activer v2/v3, jamais l’ouverture seule. V3 reste v3 même après réduction des packs. L’accès paginé conserve octets/hashes d’index sans champ disque supplémentaire. Sauvegarder avant d’activer une fonction inconnue d’anciens binaires.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adaptations\">Différences explicites et distribution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/compatibility/adaptations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Différences explicites et distribution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La normalisation/casefold utf8proc peut refuser des noms équivalents acceptés par un simple lowercase. Les exclusions proposent glob/négations imbriquées avec les limites du scanner. Aucune exception ne traverse C : code 2 signifie exception C++ inconnue ; les appels annulables ajoutent 3. L’ABI 1 gagne des symboles : associer binding, en-têtes et bibliothèque reconstruite compatibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HTTPS utilise libcurl/Schannel Windows et OpenSSL 3 Unix ; redirections désactivées. Le service HTTP écoute seulement loopback. Construire pour chaque OS/architecture ; les archives C++ statiques demandent les dépendances au lien final. Définir SYRNCK_STATIC pour l’ABI statique. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/integrations\">intégrations\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/go-binding\">Go\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/licenses\">licences\u003c/a> et les instructions de build du dépôt.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Formats conservés # Moteur, CLI, serveur, agent, filtres et ABI C utilisent C++20/CMake. Les dépendances C natives ne demandent aucun compilateur/runtime Rust. La base de compatibilité conserve IDs BLAKE3 typés v1, manifestes/révisions canoniques, frontières FastCDC 2020 (fastcdc 5.0.0, normalisation 1, seed 0), enveloppes SYR1/Zstd/XChaCha20-Poly1305, configuration/références physiques v1, pointeurs Git et HTTP v1. Voir les formats . Les packs récents demandent un lecteur compatible : pack explicite peut activer v2/v3, jamais l’ouverture seule. V3 reste v3 même après réduction des packs. L’accès paginé conserve octets/hashes d’index sans champ disque supplémentaire. Sauvegarder avant d’activer une fonction inconnue d’anciens binaires. Différences explicites et distribution # La normalisation/casefold utf8proc peut refuser des noms équivalents acceptés par un simple lowercase. Les exclusions proposent glob/négations imbriquées avec les limites du scanner. Aucune exception ne traverse C : code 2 signifie exception C++ inconnue ; les appels annulables ajoutent 3. L’ABI 1 gagne des symboles : associer binding, en-têtes et bibliothèque reconstruite compatibles. HTTPS utilise libcurl/Schannel Windows et OpenSSL 3 Unix ; redirections désactivées. Le service HTTP écoute seulement loopback. Construire pour chaque OS/architecture ; les archives C++ statiques demandent les dépendances au lien final. Définir SYRNCK_STATIC pour l’ABI statique. Voir intégrations , Go , licences et les instructions de build du dépôt.","headings":[{"id":"formats","text":"Formats conservés","level":2},{"id":"adaptations","text":"Différences explicites et distribution","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/protocol","#/fr/integrations","#/fr/go-binding","#/fr/licenses"],"kind":"guide","revision":"c23a211c930d"},{"id":"crash-recovery","title":"Interruption et récupération","description":"Interruption et récupération","section":"Stockage","order":80,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"before-recovery\">Inspecter avant récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery/before-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspecter avant récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Rouvrir le même Store avec sa clé originale et conserver les traces avant nouvelle tentative. L’ouverture ne modifie jamais les fichiers de travail. Pour checkout/switch/merge/replay/rebase/reset/stash en attente, lire \u003ccode>syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/code>, puis choisir \u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> pour terminer ou \u003ccode>native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/code> pour restaurer les originaux avant publication. Après publication seul le nettoyage peut finir. Ne pas éditer les slots privés/racines internes. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">états et limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une donnée conservée absente/corrompue est une erreur. Employer une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/backups\">sauvegarde vérifiée\u003c/a> ou la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/repair-copy\">réparation par copie\u003c/a> pour les objets absents récupérables, en conservant les traces de corruption. Le GC n’est pas une réparation générale.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interrupted-garbage-collection\">GC interrompu\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers GC interrompu\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Après un GC dont l’interruption est connue, des métadonnées mortes isolées peuvent survivre à leurs blocs morts empaquetés. Les fermetures conservées restent protégées, mais fsck audite aussi les métadonnées inaccessibles et peut signaler ces dépendances. Avec le même Store/clé : prévisualiser \u003ccode>syrnck gc\u003c/code>, examiner les racines, reprendre la suppression déjà demandée avec \u003ccode>syrnck gc --delete\u003c/code>, puis \u003ccode>syrnck fsck\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC revalide toutes les fermetures/hashes conservés avant suppression ; une donnée conservée absente/corrompue bloque. Cela termine un nettoyage sans récupérer de version volontairement privée de racines. Ne pas l’appliquer à une erreur inexpliquée. Garder d’abord pins/archives nécessaires et sauvegardes vérifiées.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-operations\">Autres opérations et temporaires\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery/other-operations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Autres opérations et temporaires\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Reprendre pack/compact après correction de l’IO : la configuration active choisit la génération publiée. Une ingestion peut laisser des blocs complets non référencés réutilisables. Ne reprendre une restauration que si destination absente ; sinon vérifier et utiliser le fichier publié sans écrasement. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/export\">Export de dossier\u003c/a> et sauvegarde peuvent laisser un staging frère privé après kill, sans retrait automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ccode>.tmp&lt;random-id&gt;\u003c/code> obsolètes n’entrent pas dans l’inventaire et ne retiennent rien. Aucun nettoyage global automatique ; le retrait validé des packs/index générés inactifs est distinct. La récupération après terminaison de processus n’établit pas la résistance aux coupures, caches contrôleur, FS réseau ou écrivains hostiles.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Inspecter avant récupération # Rouvrir le même Store avec sa clé originale et conserver les traces avant nouvelle tentative. L’ouverture ne modifie jamais les fichiers de travail. Pour checkout/switch/merge/replay/rebase/reset/stash en attente, lire syrnck native checkout-status , puis choisir native checkout-recover pour terminer ou native checkout-recover --abort pour restaurer les originaux avant publication. Après publication seul le nettoyage peut finir. Ne pas éditer les slots privés/racines internes. Voir les états et limites . Une donnée conservée absente/corrompue est une erreur. Employer une sauvegarde vérifiée ou la réparation par copie pour les objets absents récupérables, en conservant les traces de corruption. Le GC n’est pas une réparation générale. GC interrompu # Après un GC dont l’interruption est connue, des métadonnées mortes isolées peuvent survivre à leurs blocs morts empaquetés. Les fermetures conservées restent protégées, mais fsck audite aussi les métadonnées inaccessibles et peut signaler ces dépendances. Avec le même Store/clé : prévisualiser syrnck gc , examiner les racines, reprendre la suppression déjà demandée avec syrnck gc --delete , puis syrnck fsck . Le GC revalide toutes les fermetures/hashes conservés avant suppression ; une donnée conservée absente/corrompue bloque. Cela termine un nettoyage sans récupérer de version volontairement privée de racines. Ne pas l’appliquer à une erreur inexpliquée. Garder d’abord pins/archives nécessaires et sauvegardes vérifiées. Autres opérations et temporaires # Reprendre pack/compact après correction de l’IO : la configuration active choisit la génération publiée. Une ingestion peut laisser des blocs complets non référencés réutilisables. Ne reprendre une restauration que si destination absente ; sinon vérifier et utiliser le fichier publié sans écrasement. Export de dossier et sauvegarde peuvent laisser un staging frère privé après kill, sans retrait automatique. Les .tmp\u003crandom-id> obsolètes n’entrent pas dans l’inventaire et ne retiennent rien. Aucun nettoyage global automatique ; le retrait validé des packs/index générés inactifs est distinct. La récupération après terminaison de processus n’établit pas la résistance aux coupures, caches contrôleur, FS réseau ou écrivains hostiles.","headings":[{"id":"before-recovery","text":"Inspecter avant récupération","level":2},{"id":"interrupted-garbage-collection","text":"GC interrompu","level":2},{"id":"other-operations","text":"Autres opérations et temporaires","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/backups","#/fr/repair-copy","#/fr/export"],"kind":"guide","revision":"09b636b18a47"},{"id":"export","title":"Export de révisions","description":"Guide : Export de révisions.","section":"Stockage","order":80,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"transactional-native-exports\">Exporter une révision dans un nouveau dossier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/export/transactional-native-exports\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Exporter une révision dans un nouveau dossier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native export REVISION NEW_DIRECTORY\u003c/code> vérifie la révision et sa fermeture complète, reconstruit chaque fichier dans un dossier frère de préparation puis publie l’arbre par renommage exclusif. La destination reste absente tant que l’ensemble n’est pas prêt. Aucun fichier, lien ou dossier existant n’est remplacé, même un dossier vide créé en concurrence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::export_revision(id, destination, OperationOptions)\u003c/code> accepte jeton d’arrêt et observateur synchrone. \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> couvre la fermeture initiale ; \u003ccode>exporting\u003c/code> compte les octets restaurés agrégés avec le total connu du snapshot et le chemin relatif courant, fichiers vides compris. Sa fin signifie préparation terminée. Le \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> suivant est le dernier callback annulable avant renommage. Le succès de la commande ou du retour confirme la publication. Références et objets source restent intacts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La préparation \u003ccode>.syrnck-export-&lt;32 chiffres hexadécimaux minuscules&gt;\u003c/code> est créée dans le parent existant de la destination pour rester sur le même système de fichiers. Sous Unix, le dossier est privé au propriétaire et garde ces permissions après publication. Les fichiers préparés sont décodés ; le bit exécutable est rétabli sous Unix. Annulation, exception d’observateur ou erreur ordinaire nettoient seulement la préparation de cet appel. Les chemins proviennent des révisions validées et les manifestes sont complets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le renommage utilise MoveFileExW sans remplacement sous Windows, \u003ccode>renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE)\u003c/code> sous Linux et \u003ccode>renamex_np(RENAME_EXCL)\u003c/code> sous macOS. Si la primitive exclusive n’est pas disponible, l’export échoue et retire sa préparation ; aucun repli susceptible d’écraser une destination n’est utilisé. Les fichiers sont synchronisés individuellement ; sous Unix, les entrées de dossiers le sont avant publication et le parent après. Une annulation après engagement du renommage peut arriver trop tard. Une erreur de durabilité finale peut laisser un arbre complet publié ; aucune garantie universelle de panne d’alimentation n’en découle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un arrêt forcé peut laisser une préparation, avec destination absente ou complète. Le lecteur ne la nettoie pas automatiquement : vérifier son appartenance et l’utilité des données avant suppression. Réessayer vers une destination absente prépare un nouvel arbre. Ces fichiers temporaires ne sont pas des objets de révision ni des racines de rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La reconstruction lit les blocs en flux, mais la fermeture publique matérialise encore son ensemble de clés et les limites JSON des révisions/manifestes restent valables. Pour modifier un workspace existant, utiliser le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">checkout récupérable\u003c/a>, distinct de cet export vers un nouveau dossier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Exporter une révision dans un nouveau dossier # native export REVISION NEW_DIRECTORY vérifie la révision et sa fermeture complète, reconstruit chaque fichier dans un dossier frère de préparation puis publie l’arbre par renommage exclusif. La destination reste absente tant que l’ensemble n’est pas prêt. Aucun fichier, lien ou dossier existant n’est remplacé, même un dossier vide créé en concurrence. Store::export_revision(id, destination, OperationOptions) accepte jeton d’arrêt et observateur synchrone. verifying couvre la fermeture initiale ; exporting compte les octets restaurés agrégés avec le total connu du snapshot et le chemin relatif courant, fichiers vides compris. Sa fin signifie préparation terminée. Le publishing suivant est le dernier callback annulable avant renommage. Le succès de la commande ou du retour confirme la publication. Références et objets source restent intacts. La préparation .syrnck-export-\u003c32 chiffres hexadécimaux minuscules> est créée dans le parent existant de la destination pour rester sur le même système de fichiers. Sous Unix, le dossier est privé au propriétaire et garde ces permissions après publication. Les fichiers préparés sont décodés ; le bit exécutable est rétabli sous Unix. Annulation, exception d’observateur ou erreur ordinaire nettoient seulement la préparation de cet appel. Les chemins proviennent des révisions validées et les manifestes sont complets. Le renommage utilise MoveFileExW sans remplacement sous Windows, renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE) sous Linux et renamex_np(RENAME_EXCL) sous macOS. Si la primitive exclusive n’est pas disponible, l’export échoue et retire sa préparation ; aucun repli susceptible d’écraser une destination n’est utilisé. Les fichiers sont synchronisés individuellement ; sous Unix, les entrées de dossiers le sont avant publication et le parent après. Une annulation après engagement du renommage peut arriver trop tard. Une erreur de durabilité finale peut laisser un arbre complet publié ; aucune garantie universelle de panne d’alimentation n’en découle. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser une préparation, avec destination absente ou complète. Le lecteur ne la nettoie pas automatiquement : vérifier son appartenance et l’utilité des données avant suppression. Réessayer vers une destination absente prépare un nouvel arbre. Ces fichiers temporaires ne sont pas des objets de révision ni des racines de rétention. La reconstruction lit les blocs en flux, mais la fermeture publique matérialise encore son ensemble de clés et les limites JSON des révisions/manifestes restent valables. Pour modifier un workspace existant, utiliser le checkout récupérable , distinct de cet export vers un nouveau dossier. text Copier syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"transactional-native-exports","text":"Exporter une révision dans un nouveau dossier","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress\nsyrnck native export REVISION restored-project --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-checkout"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f76c101109c6"},{"id":"file-comparison","title":"Comparaison de manifestes","description":"Guide : Comparaison de manifestes.","section":"Stockage","order":90,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans\">Comparer les manifestes et préparer la réutilisation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/file-comparison/manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comparer les manifestes et préparer la réutilisation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::compare_files(source_manifest, target_manifest, operation)\u003c/code> compare deux manifestes v1 vérifiés sans lire les payloads. Le JSON anglais ordonné contient \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, IDs/tailles source et cible, \u003ccode>same_content\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reused_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>new_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unique_new_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blocks\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>needed_chunks\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--summary\u003c/code> retire les deux listes de blocs de stdout après calcul : cela réduit la sortie, pas la mémoire de planification. La progression reste sur stderr, en texte anglais/français ou JSON stable. Aucune référence ne change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’ABI C \u003ccode>syrnck_file_compare_json\u003c/code> utilise un tampon de l’appelant et une requête de taille comprenant NUL. \u003ccode>CompareFiles\u003c/code> en Go rend \u003ccode>FileComparison\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>FileBlock\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>NeededChunk\u003c/code> et borne le JSON à 128 Mio avant décodage. La requête de taille suivie de la lecture calcule deux fois le plan ; aucun pointeur natif n’est conservé. Utiliser en-tête et bibliothèque de la même version.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque bloc cible expose \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>offset\u003c/code> cible, \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>, et \u003ccode>source_offset\u003c/code>, première occurrence correspondante dans la source ou null. Les blocs déplacés/répétés sont réutilisables ; un même ID doit avoir partout la même taille déclarée. \u003ccode>needed_chunks\u003c/code> cite chaque bloc absent une seule fois, dans l’ordre de première apparition cible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>reused_bytes + new_bytes\u003c/code> égale la taille cible. \u003ccode>new_bytes\u003c/code> compte chaque occurrence ; \u003ccode>unique_new_bytes\u003c/code> compte une seule fois chaque bloc absent distinct. Ce sont des octets logiques en clair, sans manifestes, protocole, chiffrement ni compression, pas une mesure réseau. Un destinataire déjà rempli peut encore réduire les besoins.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>same_content\u003c/code> compare taille et hash complet indépendamment du découpage : des octets identiques peuvent n’offrir aucune frontière commune. Le plan ne cherche pas à l’intérieur des blocs et ne génère pas de delta binaire. C’est une recette de reconstruction, sans modification de format ou référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les blocs peuvent être absents : le plan reste une lecture de métadonnées. Un offset ne prouve pas qu’un fichier mutable conserve ces octets. Vérifier les blocs copiés/importés contre leurs IDs et le fichier terminé contre le manifeste avant publication. Les API de lecture, import et publication vérifiée fournissent ces contrôles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un seul manifeste est lu pour deux IDs égaux, sinon deux. Métadonnées et résultat croissent avec les blocs, sous la limite de 16 Mio par manifeste. Coût de recherche : O((blocs source + cible) log(IDs distincts)), sans tampon à la taille du fichier. Il n’existe pas encore d’index de manifeste persistant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La phase \u003ccode>planning\u003c/code> compte les entrées traitées dans les deux manifestes, pas les octets de fichiers. Le contrat d’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">opération\u003c/a> régit callbacks et annulation. Aucun pin n’est créé : l’appelant doit conserver ses racines de rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Comparer les manifestes et préparer la réutilisation # Store::compare_files(source_manifest, target_manifest, operation) compare deux manifestes v1 vérifiés sans lire les payloads. Le JSON anglais ordonné contient version: 1 , IDs/tailles source et cible, same_content , reused_bytes , new_bytes , unique_new_bytes , blocks , needed_chunks . --summary retire les deux listes de blocs de stdout après calcul : cela réduit la sortie, pas la mémoire de planification. La progression reste sur stderr, en texte anglais/français ou JSON stable. Aucune référence ne change. L’ABI C syrnck_file_compare_json utilise un tampon de l’appelant et une requête de taille comprenant NUL. CompareFiles en Go rend FileComparison , FileBlock , NeededChunk et borne le JSON à 128 Mio avant décodage. La requête de taille suivie de la lecture calcule deux fois le plan ; aucun pointeur natif n’est conservé. Utiliser en-tête et bibliothèque de la même version. Chaque bloc cible expose id , offset cible, size , et source_offset , première occurrence correspondante dans la source ou null. Les blocs déplacés/répétés sont réutilisables ; un même ID doit avoir partout la même taille déclarée. needed_chunks cite chaque bloc absent une seule fois, dans l’ordre de première apparition cible. reused_bytes + new_bytes égale la taille cible. new_bytes compte chaque occurrence ; unique_new_bytes compte une seule fois chaque bloc absent distinct. Ce sont des octets logiques en clair, sans manifestes, protocole, chiffrement ni compression, pas une mesure réseau. Un destinataire déjà rempli peut encore réduire les besoins. same_content compare taille et hash complet indépendamment du découpage : des octets identiques peuvent n’offrir aucune frontière commune. Le plan ne cherche pas à l’intérieur des blocs et ne génère pas de delta binaire. C’est une recette de reconstruction, sans modification de format ou référence. Les blocs peuvent être absents : le plan reste une lecture de métadonnées. Un offset ne prouve pas qu’un fichier mutable conserve ces octets. Vérifier les blocs copiés/importés contre leurs IDs et le fichier terminé contre le manifeste avant publication. Les API de lecture, import et publication vérifiée fournissent ces contrôles. Un seul manifeste est lu pour deux IDs égaux, sinon deux. Métadonnées et résultat croissent avec les blocs, sous la limite de 16 Mio par manifeste. Coût de recherche : O((blocs source + cible) log(IDs distincts)), sans tampon à la taille du fichier. Il n’existe pas encore d’index de manifeste persistant. La phase planning compte les entrées traitées dans les deux manifestes, pas les octets de fichiers. Le contrat d’ opération régit callbacks et annulation. Aucun pin n’est créé : l’appelant doit conserver ses racines de rétention. text Copier syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary","headings":[{"id":"manifest-comparison-and-chunk-reuse-plans","text":"Comparer les manifestes et préparer la réutilisation","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --progress-json\nsyrnck compare SOURCE_MANIFEST TARGET_MANIFEST --summary","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/operations"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0fec4afecd22"},{"id":"file-ranges","title":"Lecture vérifiée de plages","description":"Guide : Lecture vérifiée de plages.","section":"Stockage","order":100,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"verified-file-ranges\">Lire des plages vérifiées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/file-ranges/verified-file-ranges\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Lire des plages vérifiées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les méthodes C++ lisent une plage depuis un manifeste sans reconstruire le fichier entier. Le CLI offre le même export exclusif ; \u003ccode>--offset\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--length\u003c/code> doivent être fournis ensemble. Sans les deux, la reconstruction complète habituelle s’applique. Avec \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code>, stdout reste vide et stderr rend des compteurs relatifs à la plage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’ABI ajoute \u003ccode>syrnck_file_read_range\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck_restore_file_range\u003c/code> ; utiliser les en-têtes/binaires correspondants. Go expose \u003ccode>ReadRange\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>RestoreRange\u003c/code>. Les lectures tamponnées C/Go sont plafonnées à 16 Mio et préparent les octets avant copie : une erreur ne modifie pas le tampon appelant. Les exports de fichiers en flux n’ont pas cette limite de longueur ; les limites de bloc et de manifeste restent valables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une requête C \u003ccode>(NULL, 0)\u003c/code> contrôle métadonnées et bornes sans payload. Un pointeur non nul demande une vraie vérification, même pour une plage vide d’un fichier vide. Une capacité insuffisante rend la longueur nécessaire ; les autres erreurs remettent la longueur à zéro. Go alloue la plage demandée et C ajoute un tampon privé de même taille. Les bornes sont contrôlées avant allocation ; le manifeste est relu pendant la reconstruction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Offset et longueur sont des comptes d’octets non signés sur 64 bits. La plage doit tenir exactement dans le fichier, sans troncature ni débordement. Une longueur nulle est permise jusqu’à EOF inclus. La destination contient uniquement les octets choisis, à partir de son offset zéro.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après validation du manifeste, seuls les blocs intersectés sont chargés et entièrement vérifiés : authentification/décompression si nécessaire, ID et taille réelle avant émission. Les blocs non concernés peuvent manquer ; leur disponibilité/intégrité n’est pas auditée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une lecture partielle ne valide pas le hash complet du fichier. Une plage couvrant tout le fichier, même vide, utilise la reconstruction normale et valide aussi ce hash. Conserver une reconstruction ou fermeture complète lorsque toutes les dépendances doivent être auditées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un flux peut avoir émis un préfixe si une erreur de bloc, callback ou écriture survient ensuite. L’export vers un fichier prépare un frère temporaire et ne publie qu’après succès, sans remplacer une destination existante. Annulation, bornes invalides et blocs concernés corrompus ne publient pas de destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La progression \u003ccode>reconstructing\u003c/code> compte les octets de la plage, avec \u003ccode>total_bytes\u003c/code> égal à sa longueur. Callbacks, annulation, verrou exclusif et sérialisation des handles suivent le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">contrat d’opération\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les manifestes v1 et les stockages isolés/mono-pack/multi-packs restent compatibles. Le manifeste complet est analysé pour trouver la plage ; aucun index d’offsets persistant ou manifeste paginé n’est ajouté. La mémoire des payloads C++ reste bornée à un bloc décodé sélectionné, en plus des métadonnées, codecs et caches. Même quelques octets à une frontière peuvent imposer le décodage d’un bloc entier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reconstruct_range\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>manifest\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> output_stream\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> offset\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_file_range\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>manifest\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> offset\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Lire des plages vérifiées # Les méthodes C++ lisent une plage depuis un manifeste sans reconstruire le fichier entier. Le CLI offre le même export exclusif ; --offset et --length doivent être fournis ensemble. Sans les deux, la reconstruction complète habituelle s’applique. Avec --progress-json , stdout reste vide et stderr rend des compteurs relatifs à la plage. L’ABI ajoute syrnck_file_read_range et syrnck_restore_file_range ; utiliser les en-têtes/binaires correspondants. Go expose ReadRange et RestoreRange . Les lectures tamponnées C/Go sont plafonnées à 16 Mio et préparent les octets avant copie : une erreur ne modifie pas le tampon appelant. Les exports de fichiers en flux n’ont pas cette limite de longueur ; les limites de bloc et de manifeste restent valables. Une requête C (NULL, 0) contrôle métadonnées et bornes sans payload. Un pointeur non nul demande une vraie vérification, même pour une plage vide d’un fichier vide. Une capacité insuffisante rend la longueur nécessaire ; les autres erreurs remettent la longueur à zéro. Go alloue la plage demandée et C ajoute un tampon privé de même taille. Les bornes sont contrôlées avant allocation ; le manifeste est relu pendant la reconstruction. Offset et longueur sont des comptes d’octets non signés sur 64 bits. La plage doit tenir exactement dans le fichier, sans troncature ni débordement. Une longueur nulle est permise jusqu’à EOF inclus. La destination contient uniquement les octets choisis, à partir de son offset zéro. Après validation du manifeste, seuls les blocs intersectés sont chargés et entièrement vérifiés : authentification/décompression si nécessaire, ID et taille réelle avant émission. Les blocs non concernés peuvent manquer ; leur disponibilité/intégrité n’est pas auditée. Une lecture partielle ne valide pas le hash complet du fichier. Une plage couvrant tout le fichier, même vide, utilise la reconstruction normale et valide aussi ce hash. Conserver une reconstruction ou fermeture complète lorsque toutes les dépendances doivent être auditées. Un flux peut avoir émis un préfixe si une erreur de bloc, callback ou écriture survient ensuite. L’export vers un fichier prépare un frère temporaire et ne publie qu’après succès, sans remplacer une destination existante. Annulation, bornes invalides et blocs concernés corrompus ne publient pas de destination. La progression reconstructing compte les octets de la plage, avec total_bytes égal à sa longueur. Callbacks, annulation, verrou exclusif et sérialisation des handles suivent le contrat d’opération . Les manifestes v1 et les stockages isolés/mono-pack/multi-packs restent compatibles. Le manifeste complet est analysé pour trouver la plage ; aucun index d’offsets persistant ou manifeste paginé n’est ajouté. La mémoire des payloads C++ reste bornée à un bloc décodé sélectionné, en plus des métadonnées, codecs et caches. Même quelques octets à une frontière peuvent imposer le décodage d’un bloc entier. cpp Copier store . reconstruct_range ( manifest , output_stream , offset , length , operation ) ; store . restore_file_range ( manifest , new_destination , offset , length , operation ) ; text Copier syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress store.reconstruct_range(manifest, output_stream, offset, length, operation); store.restore_file_range(manifest, new_destination, offset, length, operation); syrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress","headings":[{"id":"verified-file-ranges","text":"Lire des plages vérifiées","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1"],"languages":["cpp","text"],"code":"store.reconstruct_range(manifest, output_stream, offset, length, operation);\nstore.restore_file_range(manifest, new_destination, offset, length, operation);\nsyrnck get MANIFEST NEW_FILE --offset 1048576 --length 65536 --progress","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/operations"],"kind":"guide","revision":"5e0c6462a3e0"},{"id":"git-assets","title":"Catalogues d’assets Git","description":"Guide : Catalogues d’assets Git.","section":"Git","order":200,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles\">Catalogues d’assets Git et bundles conservés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-assets/git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Catalogues d’assets Git et bundles conservés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Syrnck lit les pointeurs dans l’arbre Git commité, indépendamment du workspace sale, de l’index et des filtres. Git garde le code et son historique ordinaire ; une révision Syrnck déterministe décrit seulement les assets d’un commit. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration\">migration LFS\u003c/a> séparée vérifie le cache SHA-256 puis retient les blocs/catalogues avant publication d’une branche enfant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>catalog est en lecture seule sans Store ni identifiants. JSON : version, commit Git complet résolu, assets ordonnés avec path, blob, manifest, size logique, executable. Dépôts SHA-1/SHA-256 et tags annotés de commit sont gérés ; résolution source unique avant lecture de l’arbre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>prepare-assets utilise le Store commun git-common-dir/syrnck sauf --store. Initialiser avec git install. Chaque taille de pointeur et fermeture complète/hash de fichier est vérifié avant pin local git-assets/COMMIT. Échec sans perte de pins ; worktrees partagent le Store sous verrou exclusif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le bundle est une révision sans parent, timestamp zéro, message Syrnck Git assets v1 COMMIT et entrées d’assets, sans fichiers source et sans remplacer le commit Git. Même catalogue donne même ID. Chemins portables/collisions et 16 Mio s’appliquent. Le pin protège les versions indépendamment des références Git ; son retrait reste explicite. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-retention\">réconciliation de rétention\u003c/a> ajoute des pins par dépôt depuis références/reflogs/index ; elle ne les retire pas automatiquement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git catalog HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"grouped-transfers\">Transferts groupés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-assets/grouped-transfers\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Transferts groupés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le commit doit déjà exister localement. Store commun par défaut, options habituelles de clés/jetons/CA/compression/reprise/débit/tampon. Résultat : commit, assets, bundle et statistiques/session transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push prépare le bundle vérifié, transfère sa fermeture dédupliquée avec inventaire/bail persistants, puis publie la référence serveur git-COMMIT. Elle est immuable pour cette commande : une cible différente existante échoue avant transfert. Le CAS vérifie la fermeture entière. Même un changement de code seul possède sa référence de commit. Cette commande ne pousse pas Git ; répéter réutilise objets et reçus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pull calcule le bundle attendu depuis les pointeurs locaux et exige la référence serveur correspondante. Téléchargement dans git-stage/BUNDLE_ID, contrôle de tailles/hashes puis publication git-assets/COMMIT et retrait du staging. Échec/coupure peut laisser pin/journal ; refaire pull-assets après correction termine validation/publication. agent resume termine seulement le transfert de staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Aucun fichier de travail ou index Git ne change. Clone neuf : --no-checkout, git install, pull des assets voulus puis checkout explicite, ou politique download ci-dessous. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process\">filtre résident\u003c/a> garde un Store par commande.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transaction couvre un catalogue seulement. Plusieurs commits ont des racines séparées et les serveurs Git/blocs ne partagent pas de transaction distribuée. Un push Git échoué peut laisser des racines complètes : ne pas les retirer tant que l’histoire correspondante doit rester reconstructible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones\">Checkout à la demande et clones de pointeurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-assets/on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Checkout à la demande et clones de pointeurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>SYRNCK_TOKEN sert aux assets manquants ; SYRNCK_KEY ouvre le Store local chiffré initialisé avec --encrypted. Serveur et client peuvent avoir des clés différentes. HTTPS vérifie les certificats ; filtres générés avec CA système, invocation personnalisée pour --ca.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Politique par défaut local : objet absent échoue sans réseau de blocs. download choisit explicitement l’URL de blocs d’un remote Git. Manifeste/chunk absent déclenche un pull persistant du manifeste exact du pointeur stdin, avec bail de lecture serveur renouvelable obligatoire. Ni HEAD, fichier worktree ni catalogue ne déterminent la version : checkout sélectif/ancien fonctionne même avec HEAD ailleurs. Seuls objets absents de ce manifeste sont demandés. Le serveur doit conserver toute la fermeture via catalogues uploadés ; sans bail de lecture, download échoue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Contenu ordinaire ou asset complet local : aucun jeton, endpoint ou réseau requis. Objet local corrompu : erreur, pas remplacement silencieux. Smudge vérifie taille du pointeur, tous les blocs et hash complet avant sortie, puis pin git/MANIFEST. Download échoué ne rend aucun octet reconstruit ; objets vérifiés/journal restent réutilisables. Le pin git-fetch/MANIFEST peut rester après taille invalide ; refaire checkout termine pin final et nettoyage. Resume générique termine seulement le staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>skip garde les pointeurs canoniques sur disque, sans Store/clé/jeton/serveur. Clean accepte un pointeur non hydraté intact pour status/add, sans pin d’une fermeture incomplète. Pré-push/prepare exigent toujours tous les assets. Taille d’un manifeste présent incohérente ou corruption détectée fait échouer clean ; pointeurs mal formés échouent partout. SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 remplace la politique pour l’environnement/invocation, 0 garde la normale, autres valeurs refusées. Retirer la variable après usage si souhaité ; config locale non clonée, à installer explicitement par dépôt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Changer de politique ne change aucun fichier. Git peut garder un pointeur dont index/stat correspondent même avec force. Pour hydrater un tel chemin, sauvegarder les modifications utiles, supprimer seulement son fichier pointeur puis git checkout -- CHEMIN. Une sélection dans un clone --no-checkout doit d’abord restaurer .gitattributes. Le code ordinaire garde Git.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La valeur locale syrnck.checkout vaut local, skip ou download:REMOTE. Les filtres installés passent --checkout-policy ; smudge manuel reste local sans cela. Seule l’ancienne commande générée exacte est mise à niveau ; une personnalisée est préservée par refus. Les filtres résidents traitent séquentiellement, sans hydratation différée ou checkout parallèle. Vérifier avant sortie impose une lecture complète, avec budgets d’objets/métadonnées et clés de fermeture, pas un tampon à la taille du fichier. Le contrat Git n’offre pas d’atomicité multi-fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">cd project\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">git checkout --force HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git configure-checkout skip\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">git checkout --force HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git configure-checkout local\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"opt-in-pre-push-hook\">Hook pre-push explicite\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-assets/opt-in-pre-push-hook\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Hook pre-push explicite\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Installer le hook seulement avec install-hook ; git install seul ne le fait pas. remote.origin.syrnckUrl est distinct de l’URL Git ; configuration refuse identifiants intégrés, query/fragment et HTTP non loopback. Le script contient des chemins cités, hérite clés/jetons et transmet arguments/stdin ; aucun secret n’y est écrit. CA système par défaut ; intégrer manuellement --ca au hook existant si nécessaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le hook résout chaque tête non supprimée en commit et parcourt toute l’ascendance locale, parents de fusion compris, sans croire les anciens commits distants. Tags annotés et pushes multi-références partagent ce parcours. Un asset retiré du dernier arbre doit encore être publié pour son ancien commit. Tout catalogue non vide est vérifié, uploadé et épinglé avant autorisation de Git ; baux d’envoi obligatoires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Client blocs créé à la demande : suppressions seules ou histoire entièrement code sans endpoint/jeton/réseau. Un arbre actuel sans asset ne dispense pas de vérifier ses ancêtres. Manifeste absent, taille fausse, bloc indisponible, racine immuable incorrecte, bail non géré ou erreur réseau font échouer le hook et le push. Corriger puis relancer ; objets/reçus vérifiés se réutilisent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Installation atomique/idempotente seulement pour le script généré exact. Hook différent, chemin lié ou core.hooksPath sont refusés sans écrasement. Un hook existant doit appeler l’équivalent syrnck --store COMMON_STORE git pre-push &quot;$@&quot;, conserver stdin et propager l’échec. Worktrees partagent hooks/Store communs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C’est un hook client contournable par --no-verify, sans enforcement GitHub/GitLab ou transaction distribuée. Annulation ou échec Git ultérieur peuvent laisser des racines conservées ; l’atomicité multi-ref appartient à Git. Contrôle serveur Git, expiration automatique des orphelins et publication distribuée restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Histoires shallow/graft, tags non résolus en commits et données promisor absentes sont refusés : récupérer l’histoire complète auparavant. Bornes : 4096 lignes d’update, 1 Mio stdin, 4 Kio par ligne, 100000 commits uniques, sortie d’histoire 8 Mio. Les arbres sont rescannés à chaque appel ; index de présence/catalogue pour très grands projets reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git install-hook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">git push origin main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-local-git-requirements\">Bornes et exigences Git\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-assets/bounds-and-local-git-requirements\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bornes et exigences Git\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Git 2.45 minimum. --no-replace-objects et --no-lazy-fetch interdisent réécritures implicites/fetch de promisor absent. Énumération NUL avec tailles ; candidats pointeurs = blobs ordinaires de 1024 octets maximum. Les gros blobs de code ne sont pas chargés ; liens et sous-modules sont exclus, ces derniers demandant leur installation propre. Un blob commençant par l’en-tête exact Syrnck doit être canonique ; sinon c’est un blob ordinaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Arbre : 64 Mio et 262144 entrées. Blobs candidats uniques par lots de 128 via cat-file --batch, réponse 160 Kio. Stderr enfant 16 Kio. Le helper prépare les sorties en fichiers temporaires avant lecture bornée : cela ne borne ni disque temporaire ni tout le RSS. Annulation entre commandes/parsing/Store ; un enfant Git en cours peut terminer.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Catalogues d’assets Git et bundles conservés # Syrnck lit les pointeurs dans l’arbre Git commité, indépendamment du workspace sale, de l’index et des filtres. Git garde le code et son historique ordinaire ; une révision Syrnck déterministe décrit seulement les assets d’un commit. La migration LFS séparée vérifie le cache SHA-256 puis retient les blocs/catalogues avant publication d’une branche enfant. catalog est en lecture seule sans Store ni identifiants. JSON : version, commit Git complet résolu, assets ordonnés avec path, blob, manifest, size logique, executable. Dépôts SHA-1/SHA-256 et tags annotés de commit sont gérés ; résolution source unique avant lecture de l’arbre. prepare-assets utilise le Store commun git-common-dir/syrnck sauf --store. Initialiser avec git install. Chaque taille de pointeur et fermeture complète/hash de fichier est vérifié avant pin local git-assets/COMMIT. Échec sans perte de pins ; worktrees partagent le Store sous verrou exclusif. Le bundle est une révision sans parent, timestamp zéro, message Syrnck Git assets v1 COMMIT et entrées d’assets, sans fichiers source et sans remplacer le commit Git. Même catalogue donne même ID. Chemins portables/collisions et 16 Mio s’appliquent. Le pin protège les versions indépendamment des références Git ; son retrait reste explicite. La réconciliation de rétention ajoute des pins par dépôt depuis références/reflogs/index ; elle ne les retire pas automatiquement. text Copier syrnck git catalog HEAD syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD Transferts groupés # Le commit doit déjà exister localement. Store commun par défaut, options habituelles de clés/jetons/CA/compression/reprise/débit/tampon. Résultat : commit, assets, bundle et statistiques/session transfer. Push prépare le bundle vérifié, transfère sa fermeture dédupliquée avec inventaire/bail persistants, puis publie la référence serveur git-COMMIT. Elle est immuable pour cette commande : une cible différente existante échoue avant transfert. Le CAS vérifie la fermeture entière. Même un changement de code seul possède sa référence de commit. Cette commande ne pousse pas Git ; répéter réutilise objets et reçus. Pull calcule le bundle attendu depuis les pointeurs locaux et exige la référence serveur correspondante. Téléchargement dans git-stage/BUNDLE_ID, contrôle de tailles/hashes puis publication git-assets/COMMIT et retrait du staging. Échec/coupure peut laisser pin/journal ; refaire pull-assets après correction termine validation/publication. agent resume termine seulement le transfert de staging. Aucun fichier de travail ou index Git ne change. Clone neuf : --no-checkout, git install, pull des assets voulus puis checkout explicite, ou politique download ci-dessous. Le filtre résident garde un Store par commande. La transaction couvre un catalogue seulement. Plusieurs commits ont des racines séparées et les serveurs Git/blocs ne partagent pas de transaction distribuée. Un push Git échoué peut laisser des racines complètes : ne pas les retirer tant que l’histoire correspondante doit rester reconstructible. text Copier syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease Checkout à la demande et clones de pointeurs # SYRNCK_TOKEN sert aux assets manquants ; SYRNCK_KEY ouvre le Store local chiffré initialisé avec --encrypted. Serveur et client peuvent avoir des clés différentes. HTTPS vérifie les certificats ; filtres générés avec CA système, invocation personnalisée pour --ca. Politique par défaut local : objet absent échoue sans réseau de blocs. download choisit explicitement l’URL de blocs d’un remote Git. Manifeste/chunk absent déclenche un pull persistant du manifeste exact du pointeur stdin, avec bail de lecture serveur renouvelable obligatoire. Ni HEAD, fichier worktree ni catalogue ne déterminent la version : checkout sélectif/ancien fonctionne même avec HEAD ailleurs. Seuls objets absents de ce manifeste sont demandés. Le serveur doit conserver toute la fermeture via catalogues uploadés ; sans bail de lecture, download échoue. Contenu ordinaire ou asset complet local : aucun jeton, endpoint ou réseau requis. Objet local corrompu : erreur, pas remplacement silencieux. Smudge vérifie taille du pointeur, tous les blocs et hash complet avant sortie, puis pin git/MANIFEST. Download échoué ne rend aucun octet reconstruit ; objets vérifiés/journal restent réutilisables. Le pin git-fetch/MANIFEST peut rester après taille invalide ; refaire checkout termine pin final et nettoyage. Resume générique termine seulement le staging. skip garde les pointeurs canoniques sur disque, sans Store/clé/jeton/serveur. Clean accepte un pointeur non hydraté intact pour status/add, sans pin d’une fermeture incomplète. Pré-push/prepare exigent toujours tous les assets. Taille d’un manifeste présent incohérente ou corruption détectée fait échouer clean ; pointeurs mal formés échouent partout. SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 remplace la politique pour l’environnement/invocation, 0 garde la normale, autres valeurs refusées. Retirer la variable après usage si souhaité ; config locale non clonée, à installer explicitement par dépôt. Changer de politique ne change aucun fichier. Git peut garder un pointeur dont index/stat correspondent même avec force. Pour hydrater un tel chemin, sauvegarder les modifications utiles, supprimer seulement son fichier pointeur puis git checkout -- CHEMIN. Une sélection dans un clone --no-checkout doit d’abord restaurer .gitattributes. Le code ordinaire garde Git. La valeur locale syrnck.checkout vaut local, skip ou download:REMOTE. Les filtres installés passent --checkout-policy ; smudge manuel reste local sans cela. Seule l’ancienne commande générée exacte est mise à niveau ; une personnalisée est préservée par refus. Les filtres résidents traitent séquentiellement, sans hydratation différée ou checkout parallèle. Vérifier avant sortie impose une lecture complète, avec budgets d’objets/métadonnées et clés de fermeture, pas un tampon à la taille du fichier. Le contrat Git n’offre pas d’atomicité multi-fichier. text Copier git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project cd project syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin git checkout --force HEAD text Copier syrnck git configure-checkout skip git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout local Hook pre-push explicite # Installer le hook seulement avec install-hook ; git install seul ne le fait pas. remote.origin.syrnckUrl est distinct de l’URL Git ; configuration refuse identifiants intégrés, query/fragment et HTTP non loopback. Le script contient des chemins cités, hérite clés/jetons et transmet arguments/stdin ; aucun secret n’y est écrit. CA système par défaut ; intégrer manuellement --ca au hook existant si nécessaire. Le hook résout chaque tête non supprimée en commit et parcourt toute l’ascendance locale, parents de fusion compris, sans croire les anciens commits distants. Tags annotés et pushes multi-références partagent ce parcours. Un asset retiré du dernier arbre doit encore être publié pour son ancien commit. Tout catalogue non vide est vérifié, uploadé et épinglé avant autorisation de Git ; baux d’envoi obligatoires. Client blocs créé à la demande : suppressions seules ou histoire entièrement code sans endpoint/jeton/réseau. Un arbre actuel sans asset ne dispense pas de vérifier ses ancêtres. Manifeste absent, taille fausse, bloc indisponible, racine immuable incorrecte, bail non géré ou erreur réseau font échouer le hook et le push. Corriger puis relancer ; objets/reçus vérifiés se réutilisent. Installation atomique/idempotente seulement pour le script généré exact. Hook différent, chemin lié ou core.hooksPath sont refusés sans écrasement. Un hook existant doit appeler l’équivalent syrnck --store COMMON_STORE git pre-push \"$@\", conserver stdin et propager l’échec. Worktrees partagent hooks/Store communs. C’est un hook client contournable par --no-verify, sans enforcement GitHub/GitLab ou transaction distribuée. Annulation ou échec Git ultérieur peuvent laisser des racines conservées ; l’atomicité multi-ref appartient à Git. Contrôle serveur Git, expiration automatique des orphelins et publication distribuée restent à développer. Histoires shallow/graft, tags non résolus en commits et données promisor absentes sont refusés : récupérer l’histoire complète auparavant. Bornes : 4096 lignes d’update, 1 Mio stdin, 4 Kio par ligne, 100000 commits uniques, sortie d’histoire 8 Mio. Les arbres sont rescannés à chaque appel ; index de présence/catalogue pour très grands projets reste à développer. text Copier syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git install-hook git push origin main Bornes et exigences Git # Git 2.45 minimum. --no-replace-objects et --no-lazy-fetch interdisent réécritures implicites/fetch de promisor absent. Énumération NUL avec tailles ; candidats pointeurs = blobs ordinaires de 1024 octets maximum. Les gros blobs de code ne sont pas chargés ; liens et sous-modules sont exclus, ces derniers demandant leur installation propre. Un blob commençant par l’en-tête exact Syrnck doit être canonique ; sinon c’est un blob ordinaire. Arbre : 64 Mio et 262144 entrées. Blobs candidats uniques par lots de 128 via cat-file --batch, réponse 160 Kio. Stderr enfant 16 Kio. Le helper prépare les sorties en fichiers temporaires avant lecture bornée : cela ne borne ni disque temporaire ni tout le RSS. Annulation entre commandes/parsing/Store ; un enfant Git en cours peut terminer. syrnck git catalog HEAD syrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json syrnck git prepare-assets HEAD syrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease syrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease git clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project cd project syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout skip git checkout --force HEAD syrnck git configure-checkout local syrnck git install syrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid syrnck git install-hook git push origin main","headings":[{"id":"git-asset-catalogs-and-retained-bundles","text":"Catalogues d’assets Git et bundles conservés","level":1},{"id":"grouped-transfers","text":"Transferts groupés","level":2},{"id":"on-demand-checkout-and-pointer-only-clones","text":"Checkout à la demande et clones de pointeurs","level":2},{"id":"opt-in-pre-push-hook","text":"Hook pre-push explicite","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-local-git-requirements","text":"Bornes et exigences Git","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git catalog HEAD\nsyrnck git catalog release --directory PATH_TO_REPOSITORY --progress-json\nsyrnck git prepare-assets HEAD\nsyrnck git push-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-lease\nsyrnck git pull-assets https://blocks.example.invalid HEAD --requests 4 --require-read-lease\ngit clone --no-checkout GIT_URL project\ncd project\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\nsyrnck git configure-checkout download --remote origin\ngit checkout --force HEAD\nsyrnck git configure-checkout skip\ngit checkout --force HEAD\nsyrnck git configure-checkout local\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git configure-remote origin https://blocks.example.invalid\nsyrnck git install-hook\ngit push origin main","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/git-lfs-migration","#/fr/git-retention","#/fr/git-filter-process"],"kind":"guide","revision":"7891bb6b9180"},{"id":"git-filter-process","title":"Filtres Git résidents","description":"Guide : Filtres Git résidents.","section":"Git","order":120,"parent":"git-assets","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"resident-git-filters\">Filtres Git résidents\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process/resident-git-filters\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Filtres Git résidents\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>git install configure aussi filter.syrnck.process localement. Une commande Git envoie plusieurs demandes clean/smudge au même processus ; le CLI ouvre son Store à la demande et conserve son cache vérifié borné jusqu’à fermeture de stdin, sans service de fond.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les anciens filtres générés restent disponibles et l’ancienne commande smudge exacte peut être mise à niveau. Toute autre commande clean/smudge/process existante est refusée avant écriture. Sans process hérité, retirer localement filter.syrnck.process réactive les filtres simples ; git install remet le résident. Les filtres personnalisés nécessitent une intégration manuelle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">git add Content\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">git checkout another-branch\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"behavior-and-performance-scope\">Comportement et portée des gains\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process/behavior-and-performance-scope\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comportement et portée des gains\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Même moteur clean/smudge et mêmes politiques local/download/skip. Configuration/environnement sont lus au lancement. Skip ne demande ni Store ni clé, même toute une session ; clean demande toujours le stockage local. Download crée le client distant seulement pour un objet manquant et exige les baux de lecture renouvelables. Les objets locaux vérifiés restent hors ligne ; journaux et pins conservent leurs contrats.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le Store garde son verrou exclusif pendant toute la commande Git, attentes entre demandes comprises ; les autres commandes du même Store échouent rapidement si occupé. Requêtes séquentielles. Les worktrees partagent le Store commun sur disque, pas le cache des processus indépendants. Aucune capacité delay, planification parallèle, daemon ou transaction de checkout multi-fichier n’est introduite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le handle évite des initialisations répétées et réutilise les décodages vérifiés. Chaque accès relit et empreinte néanmoins l’enveloppe courante pour détecter les corruptions. La vérification complète avant sortie smudge reste obligatoire ; aucun débit Unreal n’est garanti.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"streaming-and-bounds\">Flux et bornes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process/streaming-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Flux et bornes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le protocole filter-process négocie la version 2 et seulement clean/smudge proposés. Les corps binaires utilisent pkt-line avec 65516 octets de payload maximum par paquet, données vides distinctes du flush, sans transformation. Les lignes texte acceptent un LF final facultatif. Métadonnées : 64 Kio et 256 enregistrements ; en-têtes inconnus ignorés, clés dupliquées ou commande/pathname absent/non géré font échouer le fichier. Le pathname est un contexte emprunté, jamais un chemin ouvert par l’adaptateur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Aucune réponse avant le flush de fin du corps. Clean ingère directement en flux puis émet le petit pointeur. Smudge consomme le pointeur, vérifie sa fermeture et reconstruit directement dans les paquets, sans copie temporaire complète d’asset pour le framing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un handler générique peut produire avant d’avoir fini de lire, comme le passthrough brut de smudge. Au plus 64 Kio sont gardés avant déversement dans un flux temporaire privé. Celui-ci n’est relu par paquets qu’après fin de l’entrée et se ferme/supprime avec la demande, même en échec. Il contient du clair et peut croître avec tout le fichier brut ; le chiffrement du Store ne le protège pas. Paquets, caches, pipeline, clés visitées et métadonnées s’ajoutent : aucun plafond global RSS/disque temporaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failures-and-shutdown\">Erreurs et fin de processus\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process/failures-and-shutdown\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Erreurs et fin de processus\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une erreur de fichier draine le reste de son entrée, écrit le diagnostic traduit sur stderr et répond status=error. La demande suivante peut réussir. Si la sortie avait commencé, la liste finale de statut annule le succès précédent : Git doit rejeter le fichier. filter.syrnck.required=true impose cette règle. Aucun diagnostic ne passe sur stdout binaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Framing invalide, métadonnées excessives, erreur IO protocole ou annulation terminent le processus. Codes : 130 pour arrêt coopératif, 1 pour erreur fatale, 0 pour EOF normal entre demandes, même après erreur de fichier. Le client doit donc lire les statuts encadrés, pas seulement le code du processus. Une lecture de pipe bloquée attend données/EOF avant de terminer l’annulation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’API git::filter_process reçoit des handlers non encadrés, sans HTTP, avec callback facultatif de diagnostic pour erreurs récupérables et exceptions pour erreurs fatales. Respecter l’annulation, les contrôles de sortie et la sérialisation Store/observateur.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Filtres Git résidents # git install configure aussi filter.syrnck.process localement. Une commande Git envoie plusieurs demandes clean/smudge au même processus ; le CLI ouvre son Store à la demande et conserve son cache vérifié borné jusqu’à fermeture de stdin, sans service de fond. Les anciens filtres générés restent disponibles et l’ancienne commande smudge exacte peut être mise à niveau. Toute autre commande clean/smudge/process existante est refusée avant écriture. Sans process hérité, retirer localement filter.syrnck.process réactive les filtres simples ; git install remet le résident. Les filtres personnalisés nécessitent une intégration manuelle. text Copier syrnck git install git add Content git checkout another-branch Comportement et portée des gains # Même moteur clean/smudge et mêmes politiques local/download/skip. Configuration/environnement sont lus au lancement. Skip ne demande ni Store ni clé, même toute une session ; clean demande toujours le stockage local. Download crée le client distant seulement pour un objet manquant et exige les baux de lecture renouvelables. Les objets locaux vérifiés restent hors ligne ; journaux et pins conservent leurs contrats. Le Store garde son verrou exclusif pendant toute la commande Git, attentes entre demandes comprises ; les autres commandes du même Store échouent rapidement si occupé. Requêtes séquentielles. Les worktrees partagent le Store commun sur disque, pas le cache des processus indépendants. Aucune capacité delay, planification parallèle, daemon ou transaction de checkout multi-fichier n’est introduite. Le handle évite des initialisations répétées et réutilise les décodages vérifiés. Chaque accès relit et empreinte néanmoins l’enveloppe courante pour détecter les corruptions. La vérification complète avant sortie smudge reste obligatoire ; aucun débit Unreal n’est garanti. Flux et bornes # Le protocole filter-process négocie la version 2 et seulement clean/smudge proposés. Les corps binaires utilisent pkt-line avec 65516 octets de payload maximum par paquet, données vides distinctes du flush, sans transformation. Les lignes texte acceptent un LF final facultatif. Métadonnées : 64 Kio et 256 enregistrements ; en-têtes inconnus ignorés, clés dupliquées ou commande/pathname absent/non géré font échouer le fichier. Le pathname est un contexte emprunté, jamais un chemin ouvert par l’adaptateur. Aucune réponse avant le flush de fin du corps. Clean ingère directement en flux puis émet le petit pointeur. Smudge consomme le pointeur, vérifie sa fermeture et reconstruit directement dans les paquets, sans copie temporaire complète d’asset pour le framing. Un handler générique peut produire avant d’avoir fini de lire, comme le passthrough brut de smudge. Au plus 64 Kio sont gardés avant déversement dans un flux temporaire privé. Celui-ci n’est relu par paquets qu’après fin de l’entrée et se ferme/supprime avec la demande, même en échec. Il contient du clair et peut croître avec tout le fichier brut ; le chiffrement du Store ne le protège pas. Paquets, caches, pipeline, clés visitées et métadonnées s’ajoutent : aucun plafond global RSS/disque temporaire. Erreurs et fin de processus # Une erreur de fichier draine le reste de son entrée, écrit le diagnostic traduit sur stderr et répond status=error. La demande suivante peut réussir. Si la sortie avait commencé, la liste finale de statut annule le succès précédent : Git doit rejeter le fichier. filter.syrnck.required=true impose cette règle. Aucun diagnostic ne passe sur stdout binaire. Framing invalide, métadonnées excessives, erreur IO protocole ou annulation terminent le processus. Codes : 130 pour arrêt coopératif, 1 pour erreur fatale, 0 pour EOF normal entre demandes, même après erreur de fichier. Le client doit donc lire les statuts encadrés, pas seulement le code du processus. Une lecture de pipe bloquée attend données/EOF avant de terminer l’annulation. L’API git::filter_process reçoit des handlers non encadrés, sans HTTP, avec callback facultatif de diagnostic pour erreurs récupérables et exceptions pour erreurs fatales. Respecter l’annulation, les contrôles de sortie et la sérialisation Store/observateur. syrnck git install git add Content git checkout another-branch","headings":[{"id":"resident-git-filters","text":"Filtres Git résidents","level":1},{"id":"behavior-and-performance-scope","text":"Comportement et portée des gains","level":2},{"id":"streaming-and-bounds","text":"Flux et bornes","level":2},{"id":"failures-and-shutdown","text":"Erreurs et fin de processus","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git install\ngit add Content\ngit checkout another-branch","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"b45244ff2d20"},{"id":"git-lfs-history","title":"Conversion de tout l’historique LFS","description":"Guide : Conversion de tout l’historique LFS.","section":"Git","order":130,"parent":"git-assets","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history\">Convertir tout l’historique LFS accessible\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history/offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Convertir tout l’historique LFS accessible\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> convertit tous les commits accessibles depuis une tête, y compris chaque parent de fusion, sur une nouvelle branche. Références source, HEAD, index et workspace restent intacts. Aucun serveur LFS ni processus git-lfs n’est utilisé. Les deux commandes acceptent \u003ccode>--directory PROJECT\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--lfs-objects CACHE/objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--max-commits 10000\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--strip-signatures\u003c/code>. L’ingestion reprend les préférences du Store sauf substitution ; installer les filtres séparément avec git install.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">git log --graph converted-history\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"complete-preflight\">Contrôle préalable complet\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history/complete-preflight\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Contrôle préalable complet\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le parcours lit les parents bruts, sans remplacements ni simplification. Tout ancêtre manquant échoue ; une limite shallow n’est jamais une nouvelle racine implicite. Le sélecteur est résolu une fois en commit immuable, indépendant d’un déplacement ultérieur de branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant import, chaque arbre source distinct est vérifié pour pointeurs LFS/attributs, chaque objet de cache distinct est audité en SHA-256 et les dépendances Syrnck existantes sont vérifiées. Même un asset historique supprimé du dernier snapshot exige son cache. Objet ancien absent, tronqué ou corrompu bloque avant toute écriture de candidat ou Store. Les octets effectivement ingérés sont revérifiés ensuite pour détecter une mutation tardive du cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’aperçu ne demande ni Store ni clé. Il rend statut des objets, parents/arbre de chaque commit, compteurs et nécessité de retirer les signatures. Comme l’aperçu de snapshot, il audite le cache LFS, pas la disponibilité des assets Syrnck ni la transaction finale de branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preserved-history-and-signatures\">Historique et signatures\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history/preserved-history-and-signatures\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Historique et signatures\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La conversion garde parents ordonnés, fusions, racines, commits vides, en-têtes bruts auteur/committer avec dates/fuseaux, encoding, messages et extensions acceptées. Les commits inchangés gardent leurs IDs. Les arbres modifiés utilisent la conversion de fichiers ordinaires et les attributs littéraux de la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration\">migration de snapshot\u003c/a>. Code inchangé, modes exécutables, blobs de liens et gitlinks conservent leurs objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un commit réécrit ne peut garder une signature devenue fausse. Par défaut, tout gpgsig, gpgsig-sha 256 ou mergetag d’un commit à réécrire bloque. Après examen de l’aperçu, autoriser explicitement leur retrait avec \u003ccode>--strip-signatures\u003c/code>. Ces en-têtes et leurs continuations ne sont retirés que des commits réécrits et sont comptés ; les commits signés inchangés restent exacts. Aucun resignage, changement de tags ou d’autres branches n’est automatique. Répéter sur d’autres têtes si nécessaire : les objets se dédupliquent, mais références/signatures demandent leur propre politique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-publication\">Rétention et publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history/retention-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention et publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque commit historique converti possédant des assets Syrnck reçoit un catalogue complet vérifié sous \u003ccode>git-assets/COMMIT\u003c/code> avant publication de la branche. Une suppression ultérieure d’asset, le nettoyage Git ou le GC/empaquetage Syrnck ne retire donc pas ces versions. Les assets déjà natifs sont inclus ; un catalogue vide ne demande aucune racine Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La branche neuve est créée par transaction Git préparée avec contrôles de référence symbolique/worktree et hooks de la migration simple. Aucune branche existante n’est remplacée. Annulation avant publication ; après engagement, une observation exacte du commit permet de réconcilier un accusé perdu. Sinon code 3 et inspection de la référence/du commit annoncés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une erreur peut laisser des objets valides non référencés et des pins de catalogues déjà préparés, réutilisables, sans journal de migration qui les supprime. Il n’y a pas de transaction distribuée Git/Store : la protection précède la branche. Upload des blocs et publication Git restent séparés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le JSON de succès contient source sélectionnée, nouvelle tête/branche, compteurs, \u003ccode>stripped_signature_headers\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>reconciled\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code>, table source vers commit/arbre/bundle convertis. Conserver cette correspondance pour revue ou migration séparée des tags. Le cache LFS n’est jamais retiré.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-validation\">Bornes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history/bounds-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bornes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Défaut 10000 commits, réglable 1..100000 ; 1024 parents par commit ; 1 Mio par commit brut ; 64 Mio pour octets bruts et estimation des nœuds, avec conteneurs/allocateur en supplément. Au plus 65536 objets LFS distincts, plus les bornes d’arbre/attributs de chaque snapshot. Aperçu et résultat restent sous 16 Mio. Payloads en flux dans le pipeline borné ; arbres, graphe et correspondances en mémoire. Les sous-processus Git sont encore lancés par commit/arbre : c’est un outil hors ligne, pas un serveur optimisé de grands historiques. Les formats de commits et transactions restent ceux de Git.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Convertir tout l’historique LFS accessible # syrnck_git convertit tous les commits accessibles depuis une tête, y compris chaque parent de fusion, sur une nouvelle branche. Références source, HEAD, index et workspace restent intacts. Aucun serveur LFS ni processus git-lfs n’est utilisé. Les deux commandes acceptent --directory PROJECT , --lfs-objects CACHE/objects , --max-commits 10000 , --strip-signatures . L’ingestion reprend les préférences du Store sauf substitution ; installer les filtres séparément avec git install. text Copier syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress git log --graph converted-history Contrôle préalable complet # Le parcours lit les parents bruts, sans remplacements ni simplification. Tout ancêtre manquant échoue ; une limite shallow n’est jamais une nouvelle racine implicite. Le sélecteur est résolu une fois en commit immuable, indépendant d’un déplacement ultérieur de branche. Avant import, chaque arbre source distinct est vérifié pour pointeurs LFS/attributs, chaque objet de cache distinct est audité en SHA-256 et les dépendances Syrnck existantes sont vérifiées. Même un asset historique supprimé du dernier snapshot exige son cache. Objet ancien absent, tronqué ou corrompu bloque avant toute écriture de candidat ou Store. Les octets effectivement ingérés sont revérifiés ensuite pour détecter une mutation tardive du cache. L’aperçu ne demande ni Store ni clé. Il rend statut des objets, parents/arbre de chaque commit, compteurs et nécessité de retirer les signatures. Comme l’aperçu de snapshot, il audite le cache LFS, pas la disponibilité des assets Syrnck ni la transaction finale de branche. Historique et signatures # La conversion garde parents ordonnés, fusions, racines, commits vides, en-têtes bruts auteur/committer avec dates/fuseaux, encoding, messages et extensions acceptées. Les commits inchangés gardent leurs IDs. Les arbres modifiés utilisent la conversion de fichiers ordinaires et les attributs littéraux de la migration de snapshot . Code inchangé, modes exécutables, blobs de liens et gitlinks conservent leurs objets. Un commit réécrit ne peut garder une signature devenue fausse. Par défaut, tout gpgsig, gpgsig-sha 256 ou mergetag d’un commit à réécrire bloque. Après examen de l’aperçu, autoriser explicitement leur retrait avec --strip-signatures . Ces en-têtes et leurs continuations ne sont retirés que des commits réécrits et sont comptés ; les commits signés inchangés restent exacts. Aucun resignage, changement de tags ou d’autres branches n’est automatique. Répéter sur d’autres têtes si nécessaire : les objets se dédupliquent, mais références/signatures demandent leur propre politique. text Copier syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures Rétention et publication # Chaque commit historique converti possédant des assets Syrnck reçoit un catalogue complet vérifié sous git-assets/COMMIT avant publication de la branche. Une suppression ultérieure d’asset, le nettoyage Git ou le GC/empaquetage Syrnck ne retire donc pas ces versions. Les assets déjà natifs sont inclus ; un catalogue vide ne demande aucune racine Store. La branche neuve est créée par transaction Git préparée avec contrôles de référence symbolique/worktree et hooks de la migration simple. Aucune branche existante n’est remplacée. Annulation avant publication ; après engagement, une observation exacte du commit permet de réconcilier un accusé perdu. Sinon code 3 et inspection de la référence/du commit annoncés. Une erreur peut laisser des objets valides non référencés et des pins de catalogues déjà préparés, réutilisables, sans journal de migration qui les supprime. Il n’y a pas de transaction distribuée Git/Store : la protection précède la branche. Upload des blocs et publication Git restent séparés. Le JSON de succès contient source sélectionnée, nouvelle tête/branche, compteurs, stripped_signature_headers , reconciled et commits , table source vers commit/arbre/bundle convertis. Conserver cette correspondance pour revue ou migration séparée des tags. Le cache LFS n’est jamais retiré. Bornes # Défaut 10000 commits, réglable 1..100000 ; 1024 parents par commit ; 1 Mio par commit brut ; 64 Mio pour octets bruts et estimation des nœuds, avec conteneurs/allocateur en supplément. Au plus 65536 objets LFS distincts, plus les bornes d’arbre/attributs de chaque snapshot. Aperçu et résultat restent sous 16 Mio. Payloads en flux dans le pipeline borné ; arbres, graphe et correspondances en mémoire. Les sous-processus Git sont encore lancés par commit/arbre : c’est un outil hors ligne, pas un serveur optimisé de grands historiques. Les formats de commits et transactions restent ceux de Git. syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress git log --graph converted-history syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures syrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures","headings":[{"id":"offline-conversion-of-complete-git-lfs-history","text":"Convertir tout l’historique LFS accessible","level":1},{"id":"complete-preflight","text":"Contrôle préalable complet","level":2},{"id":"preserved-history-and-signatures","text":"Historique et signatures","level":2},{"id":"retention-and-publication","text":"Rétention et publication","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-validation","text":"Bornes","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git lfs-history-preview main --progress\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --workers 4 --progress\ngit log --graph converted-history\nsyrnck git lfs-history-preview main --strip-signatures\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs-history main --branch converted-history --strip-signatures","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/git-lfs-migration"],"kind":"guide","revision":"e0167b065074"},{"id":"git-lfs-migration","title":"Migration d’un snapshot LFS","description":"Guide : Migration d’un snapshot LFS.","section":"Git","order":140,"parent":"git-assets","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"offline-git-lfs-migration\">Migration hors ligne d’un snapshot LFS\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration/offline-git-lfs-migration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Migration hors ligne d’un snapshot LFS\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Un snapshot commité contenant des assets LFS peut être converti en pointeurs Syrnck dans un commit enfant sur une branche neuve. Source, HEAD, index et modifications de travail restent intacts. Les commits antérieurs gardent leurs pointeurs LFS et leur stockage nécessaire. Pour eux, utiliser la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history\">conversion complète d’historique\u003c/a>. Téléchargement d’objets LFS et pipelines d’extensions LFS ne sont pas implémentés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-and-inspect\">Préparer et inspecter\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration/prepare-and-inspect\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer et inspecter\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Rendre les objets LFS disponibles localement avec les outils LFS habituels ou une copie hors ligne. Syrnck ne contacte pas LFS et n’exécute pas git-lfs. L’aperçu lit les blobs commités via Git, sans Store, clé ni checkout ; un clone --no-checkout suffit côté Git.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le cache par défaut est lfs/objects du dossier commun Git, partagé par worktrees. Un objet utilise OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID. Pour un cache copié ou lfs.storage personnalisé, fournir explicitement son dossier objects ; aucune interprétation de ce réglage n’est devinée. Un chemin relatif part du répertoire du processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le JSON liste chemin, blob Git, OID SHA-256 LFS, taille, mode exécutable et statut verified/missing/size_mismatch/hash_mismatch. Les OID dupliqués ne sont hashés qu’une fois. ready exige un catalogue non vide dont tous les objets sont vérifiés ; code 0 si prêt, 1 sinon. Pointeurs mal formés et entrées dangereuses échouent. Racine du cache, deux dossiers de shards et fichiers ne doivent pas être des symlinks ; les ancêtres au-dessus de la racine fournie ne sont pas contrôlés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cet aperçu audite le contenu LFS, pas la possibilité finale d’utiliser branche, attributs, identité auteur, assets Syrnck ou hooks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"convert-a-snapshot\">Convertir le snapshot\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration/convert-a-snapshot\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Convertir le snapshot\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Initialiser Store/filtres puis choisir une branche inutilisée. git install configure les filtres séparément ; la migration ne change pas la configuration et n’installe pas de hook. Il faut Store existant, identité Git locale et tous les objets LFS. Les options de dossier/cache et préférences d’ingestion s’appliquent comme à l’aperçu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque objet unique passe en flux par découpage, compression et chiffrement optionnel. SHA-256 porte sur les octets réellement consommés ; taille et hash doivent correspondre au pointeur. Manifestes/blocs gardent leurs IDs BLAKE3 et formats ; les doublons réutilisent le manifeste.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls pointeurs sélectionnés et attributs nécessaires changent dans l’arbre Git. Chaque chemin reçoit dans son parent immédiat une règle de basename ancrée littérale ajoutée après les règles existantes, avec espaces/crochets échappés. Elle remplace LFS et désactive les transformations texte. Les autres fichiers/sous-arbres, modes, liens et sous-modules gardent leurs IDs. Seuls les pointeurs de fichiers ordinaires sont convertis. Les attributs modifiés doivent être ordinaires, sans NUL et sous leurs limites ; un .gitattributes lui-même sous LFS n’est pas convertible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant la branche, vérifier tout le catalogue natif du nouveau commit, y compris les anciens assets Syrnck, et le retenir sous git-assets/NEW_COMMIT. Git prépare ensuite une transaction et tient le verrou de référence, sous lequel Syrnck revérifie le nom. Cela protège aussi les références symboliques pendantes sur les anciennes versions de Git. Référence existante ou branche sélectionnée par HEAD initial/worktree est refusée. Les hooks reference-transaction peuvent rejeter ; commit-tree ne lance pas les hooks ordinaires de commit ni signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le succès rend source, commit, branch, converted_assets, unique_objects, bundle, assets et reconciled. Examiner puis sélectionner la branche explicitement. Upload de blocs et publication Git restent séparés. Le cache LFS reste en lecture seule et en clair même si le Store cible est chiffré.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">git diff HEAD converted-assets\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cancellation-and-recovery\">Annulation et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration/cancellation-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Annulation et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Progression texte/JSON sur stderr ; Ctrl+C coopératif rend 130 avant engagement. Commandes Git enfants, hooks et lectures de pipe bloquées peuvent retarder l’arrêt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après démarrage du commit Git final, publication ou réconciliation se termine sans nouveau contrôle d’annulation. Un arrêt tardif peut réussir. En cas d’accusé perdu, une référence égale exactement au commit préparé rend reconciled true ; sinon code 3 et inspection avant tout nouvel essai. Ne pas déduire l’absence de publication d’une réponse perdue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant pin de bundle, une erreur peut laisser des objets valides collectables. Après ce pin, les blocs restent protégés même sans branche. Git peut aussi garder blobs/arbres/commit inaccessibles. Aucun journal ni suppression automatique de pins préparés ; inspecter refs, référence Git et catalogue avant retrait volontaire. Réessayer réutilise les objets mais exige une branche cible absente. Le cache LFS n’est jamais supprimé.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"library-and-limits\">Bibliothèque et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration/library-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bibliothèque et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> et git_lfs.hpp exposent parsing, catalogue, résolution du cache, aperçu et migration sans HTTP. L’appelant fournit un Store ouvert et éventuellement IngestOptions ; callbacks selon le contrat d’opération. SHA-256 vient de PicoSHA2.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Bornes : arbre Git 64 Mio et 262144 entrées, dossiers compris ; candidats pointeurs ordinaires au plus 1024 octets, lots de 128 et réponse 160 Kio ; pointeurs LFS canoniques strictement inférieurs à 1024 octets, versions anciennes/extensions/champs inconnus refusés. Migration : 65536 chemins d’assets, 4096 dossiers d’attributs modifiés, 1 Mio par fichier d’attributs et 16 Mio au total, bundle natif aussi limité à 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>SHA-256 travaille par tampons de 64 Kio et les assets via le pipeline borné, sans copie temporaire complète ; arbres, IDs, pointeurs et tables de changements croissent avec le snapshot. Lignes de réponse de transaction : 4 Kio ; diagnostic d’accusé : 16 Kio. Stderr Git est temporaire, sans quota propre de taille ou durée des hooks. Sémantique des pointeurs et écritures d’arbres/références suivent Git LFS et Git.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Migration hors ligne d’un snapshot LFS # Un snapshot commité contenant des assets LFS peut être converti en pointeurs Syrnck dans un commit enfant sur une branche neuve. Source, HEAD, index et modifications de travail restent intacts. Les commits antérieurs gardent leurs pointeurs LFS et leur stockage nécessaire. Pour eux, utiliser la conversion complète d’historique . Téléchargement d’objets LFS et pipelines d’extensions LFS ne sont pas implémentés. Préparer et inspecter # Rendre les objets LFS disponibles localement avec les outils LFS habituels ou une copie hors ligne. Syrnck ne contacte pas LFS et n’exécute pas git-lfs. L’aperçu lit les blobs commités via Git, sans Store, clé ni checkout ; un clone --no-checkout suffit côté Git. Le cache par défaut est lfs/objects du dossier commun Git, partagé par worktrees. Un objet utilise OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID. Pour un cache copié ou lfs.storage personnalisé, fournir explicitement son dossier objects ; aucune interprétation de ce réglage n’est devinée. Un chemin relatif part du répertoire du processus. Le JSON liste chemin, blob Git, OID SHA-256 LFS, taille, mode exécutable et statut verified/missing/size_mismatch/hash_mismatch. Les OID dupliqués ne sont hashés qu’une fois. ready exige un catalogue non vide dont tous les objets sont vérifiés ; code 0 si prêt, 1 sinon. Pointeurs mal formés et entrées dangereuses échouent. Racine du cache, deux dossiers de shards et fichiers ne doivent pas être des symlinks ; les ancêtres au-dessus de la racine fournie ne sont pas contrôlés. Cet aperçu audite le contenu LFS, pas la possibilité finale d’utiliser branche, attributs, identité auteur, assets Syrnck ou hooks. text Copier syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress text Copier OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID text Copier syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects Convertir le snapshot # Initialiser Store/filtres puis choisir une branche inutilisée. git install configure les filtres séparément ; la migration ne change pas la configuration et n’installe pas de hook. Il faut Store existant, identité Git locale et tous les objets LFS. Les options de dossier/cache et préférences d’ingestion s’appliquent comme à l’aperçu. Chaque objet unique passe en flux par découpage, compression et chiffrement optionnel. SHA-256 porte sur les octets réellement consommés ; taille et hash doivent correspondre au pointeur. Manifestes/blocs gardent leurs IDs BLAKE3 et formats ; les doublons réutilisent le manifeste. Seuls pointeurs sélectionnés et attributs nécessaires changent dans l’arbre Git. Chaque chemin reçoit dans son parent immédiat une règle de basename ancrée littérale ajoutée après les règles existantes, avec espaces/crochets échappés. Elle remplace LFS et désactive les transformations texte. Les autres fichiers/sous-arbres, modes, liens et sous-modules gardent leurs IDs. Seuls les pointeurs de fichiers ordinaires sont convertis. Les attributs modifiés doivent être ordinaires, sans NUL et sous leurs limites ; un .gitattributes lui-même sous LFS n’est pas convertible. Avant la branche, vérifier tout le catalogue natif du nouveau commit, y compris les anciens assets Syrnck, et le retenir sous git-assets/NEW_COMMIT. Git prépare ensuite une transaction et tient le verrou de référence, sous lequel Syrnck revérifie le nom. Cela protège aussi les références symboliques pendantes sur les anciennes versions de Git. Référence existante ou branche sélectionnée par HEAD initial/worktree est refusée. Les hooks reference-transaction peuvent rejeter ; commit-tree ne lance pas les hooks ordinaires de commit ni signature. Le succès rend source, commit, branch, converted_assets, unique_objects, bundle, assets et reconciled. Examiner puis sélectionner la branche explicitement. Upload de blocs et publication Git restent séparés. Le cache LFS reste en lecture seule et en clair même si le Store cible est chiffré. text Copier syrnck git install syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress git diff HEAD converted-assets text Copier \"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf Annulation et récupération # Progression texte/JSON sur stderr ; Ctrl+C coopératif rend 130 avant engagement. Commandes Git enfants, hooks et lectures de pipe bloquées peuvent retarder l’arrêt. Après démarrage du commit Git final, publication ou réconciliation se termine sans nouveau contrôle d’annulation. Un arrêt tardif peut réussir. En cas d’accusé perdu, une référence égale exactement au commit préparé rend reconciled true ; sinon code 3 et inspection avant tout nouvel essai. Ne pas déduire l’absence de publication d’une réponse perdue. Avant pin de bundle, une erreur peut laisser des objets valides collectables. Après ce pin, les blocs restent protégés même sans branche. Git peut aussi garder blobs/arbres/commit inaccessibles. Aucun journal ni suppression automatique de pins préparés ; inspecter refs, référence Git et catalogue avant retrait volontaire. Réessayer réutilise les objets mais exige une branche cible absente. Le cache LFS n’est jamais supprimé. Bibliothèque et limites # syrnck_git et git_lfs.hpp exposent parsing, catalogue, résolution du cache, aperçu et migration sans HTTP. L’appelant fournit un Store ouvert et éventuellement IngestOptions ; callbacks selon le contrat d’opération. SHA-256 vient de PicoSHA2. Bornes : arbre Git 64 Mio et 262144 entrées, dossiers compris ; candidats pointeurs ordinaires au plus 1024 octets, lots de 128 et réponse 160 Kio ; pointeurs LFS canoniques strictement inférieurs à 1024 octets, versions anciennes/extensions/champs inconnus refusés. Migration : 65536 chemins d’assets, 4096 dossiers d’attributs modifiés, 1 Mio par fichier d’attributs et 16 Mio au total, bundle natif aussi limité à 16 Mio. SHA-256 travaille par tampons de 64 Kio et les assets via le pipeline borné, sans copie temporaire complète ; arbres, IDs, pointeurs et tables de changements croissent avec le snapshot. Lignes de réponse de transaction : 4 Kio ; diagnostic d’accusé : 16 Kio. Stderr Git est temporaire, sans quota propre de taille ou durée des hooks. Sémantique des pointeurs et écritures d’arbres/références suivent Git LFS et Git. syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress OBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects syrnck git install syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress git diff HEAD converted-assets \"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf","headings":[{"id":"offline-git-lfs-migration","text":"Migration hors ligne d’un snapshot LFS","level":1},{"id":"prepare-and-inspect","text":"Préparer et inspecter","level":2},{"id":"convert-a-snapshot","text":"Convertir le snapshot","level":2},{"id":"cancellation-and-recovery","text":"Annulation et récupération","level":2},{"id":"library-and-limits","text":"Bibliothèque et limites","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --progress\nOBJECTS_DIRECTORY/OID[0:2]/OID[2:4]/OID\nsyrnck git lfs-preview HEAD --directory PROJECT --lfs-objects CACHE/objects\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --progress\ngit diff HEAD converted-assets\n\"/Map.uasset\" filter=syrnck -text -diff -merge -ident -working-tree-encoding -eol -crlf","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/git-lfs-history"],"kind":"guide","revision":"a186c6daa135"},{"id":"git-retention","title":"Rétention des assets Git","description":"Guide : Rétention des assets Git.","section":"Git","order":150,"parent":"git-assets","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection\">Inventorier et protéger les assets Git\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-retention/git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inventorier et protéger les assets Git\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Syrnck inspecte les assets encore observables via Git et ajoute des racines complètes avant maintenance des blocs. L’inspection est explicite, jamais lancée automatiquement par filtres, pack ou GC. Toutes les commandes acceptent --directory ; retention-scan demande Git sans Store, clé, filtre ni contenu du workspace. Les autres utilisent par défaut le Store commun Git ; --store choisit un autre Store initialisé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git retention-scan --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck git retention --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck git retention --apply --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-scan-observes\">Éléments observés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-retention/what-the-scan-observes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Éléments observés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque worktree enregistré, principal ou dépôt bare compris, est parcouru explicitement : références et ancêtres (branches, suivi distant, tags légers/annotés, stash, références privées), objets de tous les reflogs et leur histoire, HEAD même détaché et objets d’index, conflits et dépôt initial vide compris. Ce parcours couvre aussi les références privées omises par le parcours global de Git 2.45.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les objets partagés sont dédupliqués ; seuls les blobs de 1024 octets maximum sont lus comme candidats pointeurs, par lots de métadonnées, sans filtre. Tout blob de forme canonique Syrnck est conservé, quels que soient attributs, chemin ou mode : tags de blobs et blobs de liens peuvent retenir prudemment des assets supplémentaires. Les IDs de manifeste sont fusionnés ; tailles contradictoires et pointeurs mal formés échouent. Aucun LFS n’est converti ou téléchargé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Modifications non préparées, non-suivis, index alternatifs arbitraires, worktrees non enregistrés, dépôts de sous-modules et pseudo-références ORIG_HEAD/FETCH_HEAD n’ajoutent pas de racines. Scanner séparément les sous-modules. Les pins existants demeurent protégés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le JSON anglais donne version, identité repository dérivée du chemin commun canonique, fingerprint des objets, nombres objects/commits et manifestes triés ID/taille. C’est un inventaire, pas la preuve de disponibilité des blocs. Déplacer le dépôt peut changer son namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preview-and-apply\">Prévisualiser et appliquer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-retention/preview-and-apply\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Prévisualiser et appliquer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>git retention vérifie taille déclarée, fermeture complète et hash de chaque manifeste observé. Il ajoute au rapport \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> (pins manquants), \u003ccode>unobserved_pins\u003c/code> avec portée repository/legacy, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>verification_error\u003c/code> canonique, \u003ccode>applied\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>added\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La prévisualisation n’écrit rien. Asset absent/corrompu : ready false, code 1 et aucune addition même avec --apply. Récupérer/restaurer puis réessayer. Un inventaire vide valide est prêt sans pin à ajouter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--apply relit l’inventaire avant publication ; changement d’ensemble exige nouvel essai. Les racines git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID sont revérifiées puis ajoutées ensemble par CAS. Les pins identiques sont réutilisés, une cible contradictoire échoue. Références utilisateur, branches natives, transferts et pins Git restent conservés. Un appel sans ajout rend applied true, added 0. Aucune mutation Git, configuration, workspace, requête réseau ou hook de publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete\">Non observé ne signifie pas supprimable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-retention/unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Non observé ne signifie pas supprimable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Cette fonction ajoute seulement des protections. unobserved_pins n’est ni un plan de retrait ni une estimation d’espace récupérable. Les pins git/MANIFEST ou git-assets/COMMIT peuvent appartenir à un autre dépôt partagé ou conserver volontairement un ancien historique ; les pins par dépôt restent aussi jusqu’au retrait explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Git et Syrnck n’ont pas de verrou transactionnel commun pour références/reflogs/index/worktrees. La deuxième lecture détecte des changements antérieurs mais ne fige pas Git ensuite. Les ajouts conservent sûrement la vue capturée, sans couvrir des changements ultérieurs/concurrents. Coordonner Git et répéter avant maintenance. Les fichiers de travail sales ne deviennent pas des versions conservées par simple inspection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le retrait automatique exige davantage de coordination et une politique de récupération. Examiner les protections avant unpin volontaire. gc prévisualise ; gc --delete collecte seulement ce qui n’est accessible depuis aucune racine restante.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"transactions-cancellation-and-limits\">Transactions, annulation et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/git-retention/transactions-cancellation-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Transactions, annulation et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>RefUpdate et compare_update_refs de l’API Store publient un groupe atomique. Chaque entrée contient nom, ancienne clé attendue optionnelle (null = absent) et nouvelle clé (null = retrait). Noms dupliqués refusés ; discordance rend false sans changement. Les nouvelles fermetures et hashes de fichiers sont vérifiés avant remplacement de refs.json, en gardant les autres références. Sérialiser tous les accès et callbacks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Références bornées à 16 Mio avant publication ; encodage trié sans recherches linéaires répétées. Une sortie excessive laisse l’ancienne table intacte. Progression texte/JSON, arrêt coopératif et absence de callbacks/contrôles après engagement : un arrêt tardif peut réussir. Les commandes Git enfants doivent terminer avant observation de l’arrêt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Bornes : 4096 worktrees ; liste 1 Mio ; 524288 objets Git uniques ; 65536 manifestes ; sortie de parcours 64 Mio par worktree. En-têtes par lots de 512, réponse 64 Kio ; petits blobs par lots de 128, 160 Kio. Les payloads ordinaires ne sont pas gardés entre lots ; IDs, ensembles, empreintes, pins et résultat croissent avec l’histoire. Pas d’inventaire incrémental persistant ; l’histoire partagée est parcourue par worktree.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Histoires shallow/graft et overrides GIT_NAMESPACE, GIT_INDEX_FILE, GIT_SHALLOW_FILE, GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY sont refusés. Worktree inaccessible ou objet Git absent bloque avant publication. Fetch paresseux, replace et fsmonitor sont désactivés. Toute sortie de parcours sur stderr, même avertissement de reflog avec succès Git, est refusée : désactiver tracing ou réparer les métadonnées avant nouvel essai.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Inventorier et protéger les assets Git # Syrnck inspecte les assets encore observables via Git et ajoute des racines complètes avant maintenance des blocs. L’inspection est explicite, jamais lancée automatiquement par filtres, pack ou GC. Toutes les commandes acceptent --directory ; retention-scan demande Git sans Store, clé, filtre ni contenu du workspace. Les autres utilisent par défaut le Store commun Git ; --store choisit un autre Store initialisé. text Copier syrnck git retention-scan --progress syrnck git retention --progress syrnck git retention --apply --progress Éléments observés # Chaque worktree enregistré, principal ou dépôt bare compris, est parcouru explicitement : références et ancêtres (branches, suivi distant, tags légers/annotés, stash, références privées), objets de tous les reflogs et leur histoire, HEAD même détaché et objets d’index, conflits et dépôt initial vide compris. Ce parcours couvre aussi les références privées omises par le parcours global de Git 2.45. Les objets partagés sont dédupliqués ; seuls les blobs de 1024 octets maximum sont lus comme candidats pointeurs, par lots de métadonnées, sans filtre. Tout blob de forme canonique Syrnck est conservé, quels que soient attributs, chemin ou mode : tags de blobs et blobs de liens peuvent retenir prudemment des assets supplémentaires. Les IDs de manifeste sont fusionnés ; tailles contradictoires et pointeurs mal formés échouent. Aucun LFS n’est converti ou téléchargé. Modifications non préparées, non-suivis, index alternatifs arbitraires, worktrees non enregistrés, dépôts de sous-modules et pseudo-références ORIG_HEAD/FETCH_HEAD n’ajoutent pas de racines. Scanner séparément les sous-modules. Les pins existants demeurent protégés. Le JSON anglais donne version, identité repository dérivée du chemin commun canonique, fingerprint des objets, nombres objects/commits et manifestes triés ID/taille. C’est un inventaire, pas la preuve de disponibilité des blocs. Déplacer le dépôt peut changer son namespace. Prévisualiser et appliquer # git retention vérifie taille déclarée, fermeture complète et hash de chaque manifeste observé. Il ajoute au rapport add (pins manquants), unobserved_pins avec portée repository/legacy, ready , verification_error canonique, applied et added . La prévisualisation n’écrit rien. Asset absent/corrompu : ready false, code 1 et aucune addition même avec --apply. Récupérer/restaurer puis réessayer. Un inventaire vide valide est prêt sans pin à ajouter. --apply relit l’inventaire avant publication ; changement d’ensemble exige nouvel essai. Les racines git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID sont revérifiées puis ajoutées ensemble par CAS. Les pins identiques sont réutilisés, une cible contradictoire échoue. Références utilisateur, branches natives, transferts et pins Git restent conservés. Un appel sans ajout rend applied true, added 0. Aucune mutation Git, configuration, workspace, requête réseau ou hook de publication. text Copier git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID Non observé ne signifie pas supprimable # Cette fonction ajoute seulement des protections. unobserved_pins n’est ni un plan de retrait ni une estimation d’espace récupérable. Les pins git/MANIFEST ou git-assets/COMMIT peuvent appartenir à un autre dépôt partagé ou conserver volontairement un ancien historique ; les pins par dépôt restent aussi jusqu’au retrait explicite. Git et Syrnck n’ont pas de verrou transactionnel commun pour références/reflogs/index/worktrees. La deuxième lecture détecte des changements antérieurs mais ne fige pas Git ensuite. Les ajouts conservent sûrement la vue capturée, sans couvrir des changements ultérieurs/concurrents. Coordonner Git et répéter avant maintenance. Les fichiers de travail sales ne deviennent pas des versions conservées par simple inspection. Le retrait automatique exige davantage de coordination et une politique de récupération. Examiner les protections avant unpin volontaire. gc prévisualise ; gc --delete collecte seulement ce qui n’est accessible depuis aucune racine restante. Transactions, annulation et limites # RefUpdate et compare_update_refs de l’API Store publient un groupe atomique. Chaque entrée contient nom, ancienne clé attendue optionnelle (null = absent) et nouvelle clé (null = retrait). Noms dupliqués refusés ; discordance rend false sans changement. Les nouvelles fermetures et hashes de fichiers sont vérifiés avant remplacement de refs.json, en gardant les autres références. Sérialiser tous les accès et callbacks. Références bornées à 16 Mio avant publication ; encodage trié sans recherches linéaires répétées. Une sortie excessive laisse l’ancienne table intacte. Progression texte/JSON, arrêt coopératif et absence de callbacks/contrôles après engagement : un arrêt tardif peut réussir. Les commandes Git enfants doivent terminer avant observation de l’arrêt. Bornes : 4096 worktrees ; liste 1 Mio ; 524288 objets Git uniques ; 65536 manifestes ; sortie de parcours 64 Mio par worktree. En-têtes par lots de 512, réponse 64 Kio ; petits blobs par lots de 128, 160 Kio. Les payloads ordinaires ne sont pas gardés entre lots ; IDs, ensembles, empreintes, pins et résultat croissent avec l’histoire. Pas d’inventaire incrémental persistant ; l’histoire partagée est parcourue par worktree. Histoires shallow/graft et overrides GIT_NAMESPACE, GIT_INDEX_FILE, GIT_SHALLOW_FILE, GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_COMMON_DIR, GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY sont refusés. Worktree inaccessible ou objet Git absent bloque avant publication. Fetch paresseux, replace et fsmonitor sont désactivés. Toute sortie de parcours sur stderr, même avertissement de reflog avec succès Git, est refusée : désactiver tracing ou réparer les métadonnées avant nouvel essai. syrnck git retention-scan --progress syrnck git retention --progress syrnck git retention --apply --progress git-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID","headings":[{"id":"git-retention-inventory-and-verified-protection","text":"Inventorier et protéger les assets Git","level":1},{"id":"what-the-scan-observes","text":"Éléments observés","level":2},{"id":"preview-and-apply","text":"Prévisualiser et appliquer","level":2},{"id":"unobserved-does-not-mean-safe-to-delete","text":"Non observé ne signifie pas supprimable","level":2},{"id":"transactions-cancellation-and-limits","text":"Transactions, annulation et limites","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck git retention-scan --progress\nsyrnck git retention --progress\nsyrnck git retention --apply --progress\ngit-live/REPOSITORY_ID/MANIFEST_ID","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"2167209f8e21"},{"id":"go-binding","title":"Binding Go","description":"Guide : Binding Go.","section":"API","order":10,"parent":"integrations","sourceRevision":"9","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"binding-go-du-moteur-c\">Binding Go du moteur C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/binding-go-du-moteur-c\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Binding Go du moteur C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le module local \u003ccode>syrnck.local/bindings/go\u003c/code> expose les stores, le découpage CDC/fixe, la lecture/importation des objets, leurs dépendances, les pins et la reconstruction vérifiée. Il n&#39;ajoute aucune dépendance Go externe. Dans un projet consommateur, utiliser temporairement un \u003ccode>replace\u003c/code> vers ce dossier, jusqu&#39;à la publication du module sous une adresse définitive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le moteur reste entièrement en C++20 ; ce dossier contient seulement son adaptateur Go. Windows amd64/arm64 utilise le chargeur DLL du système, sans cgo ni compilateur C supplémentaire. Linux/macOS utilise cgo et lie \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code>. Seul Windows amd64 a été exécuté localement ; Linux/macOS sont prévus dans la CI. ARM64 et les autres plateformes restent à valider.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tester\">Charger la bibliothèque native\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/tester\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Charger la bibliothèque native\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compiler la bibliothèque C++ correspondante. Sous Windows, définir \u003ccode>SYRNCK_LIBRARY\u003c/code> au chemin absolu de la DLL de confiance avant le premier \u003ccode>Open\u003c/code> ; elle reste chargée pour la durée du processus. Unix utilise cgo : configurer \u003ccode>CGO_LDFLAGS=-L&lt;dossier&gt;\u003c/code> et le chemin du chargeur ou un rpath de distribution. Go 1.22+ est requis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exemple\">Exemple\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/exemple\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Exemple\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">source, err := syrnck.Open(&quot;source-store&quot;, syrnck.Options{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">defer source.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">destination, err := syrnck.Open(&quot;destination-store&quot;, syrnck.Options{Create: true})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">defer destination.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">id, err := source.Ingest(&quot;Map.uasset&quot;, syrnck.Chunking{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">    syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: &quot;manifest&quot;, ID: id}, &quot;sync/map-v1&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">fmt.Printf(&quot;%d octets copiés\\n&quot;, stats.Bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">return destination.Restore(id, &quot;Map-restored.uasset&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Create\u003c/code> exige un dossier neuf ou vide ; il ne signifie pas « ouvrir ou créer ». \u003ccode>Options.Key\u003c/code> contient zéro ou 32 octets pour le chiffrement au repos. L&#39;appel ne conserve pas le tableau Go. L&#39;application reste responsable du cycle de vie de sa clé et de l&#39;effacement de ses propres copies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"catalog-transactions\">Préparer et publier un état composé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/catalog-transactions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer et publier un état composé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>PrepareBytes(data, chunking)\u003c/code> ingère au plus 16 Mio depuis la mémoire sans\nfichier temporaire en clair. \u003ccode>PrepareFile(path, chunking)\u003c/code> lit un flux sans cette\nlimite d’entrée. Les deux rendent des manifests \u003cstrong>sans référence\u003c/strong>, contrairement\nà l’ancien \u003ccode>Ingest\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Put(kind, data)\u003c/code> crée un objet typé sans référence : octets\nde blocs exacts ; C++ valide et encode canoniquement les JSON de manifest/révision\net calcule leur ID BLAKE3 natif. Champs inconnus, clés dupliquées même échappées,\nschéma invalide, profondeur et tailles excessives sont refusés. Aucun encodeur\ncanonique ni algorithme de hash d’objet natif n’est à réimplémenter en Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Garder le Store ouvert pendant préparation et publication. Après fermeture,\nle GC peut supprimer les objets sans référence. Retenir les dépendances dans une\nrévision native complète ou des racines explicites : un bloc opaque contenant\ndes IDs ne retient pas automatiquement ces objets. Les fichiers et parents d’une\nrévision native tracent leurs dépendances complètes pour GC et sauvegardes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Refs()\u003c/code> rend une \u003ccode>map[string]ObjectKey\u003c/code> indépendante. \u003ccode>CompareRefs(updates)\u003c/code>\nprotège et remplace/supprime atomiquement au plus 65536 références et 16 Mio de\nJSON. Chaque fermeture et hash de fichier complet sont vérifiés avant l’unique\npublication de la table. Les noms en double sont des erreurs. \u003ccode>Expected\u003c/code> nul\nexige l’absence ; \u003ccode>Value\u003c/code> nul supprime. Un groupe nil/vide ne change rien.\nLe booléen n’a de sens que si \u003ccode>err == nil\u003c/code> :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">if old, ok := current[&quot;ffi/catalog/main&quot;]; ok { expected = &amp;old }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">// JSON applicatif d’une révision native complète référençant les manifests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">// préparés et les éventuelles révisions parentes à conserver.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, &quot;revision&quot;, revisionJSON)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">    {Name: &quot;ffi/catalog/main&quot;, Expected: expected, Value: &amp;candidate},\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L13\" data-line=\"13\">if err != nil { return err } // Relire et établir le résultat avant un nouvel essai.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L14\" data-line=\"14\">if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Une condition périmée ne change aucun membre et rend false sans erreur.\nRelire/replanifier au lieu de changer la valeur attendue d’une ancienne opération.\nUne erreur IO peut survenir après publication atomique : sur erreur, le booléen\nne prouve pas l’absence de publication. Rouvrir/relire la table et comparer le\ngroupe exact proposé avant de réessayer. La table est entièrement ancienne ou\nnouvelle, sans mélange produit par cette transaction. Un parcours de haut niveau\ndoit conserver son intention s’il doit réconcilier un résultat inconnu après crash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les cinq opérations possèdent une variante \u003ccode>Context\u003c/code>. Préparation et CAS propagent\nl’annulation native pendant ingestion/vérification et avant publication ; un\nsuccès terminé l’emporte sur une demande tardive. \u003ccode>PutContext\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>RefsContext\u003c/code>\ncontrôlent l’annulation avant leur appel natif borné ; encodage/compression/lecture\ndéjà engagés peuvent finir. Une préparation échouée peut laisser des objets\ncomplets sans référence, sans publication. Les écrivains externes concurrents\nrestent à gérer par l’application. Ces primitives permettent un état protocolaire\npersistant ; elles n’implémentent pas la réconciliation Syncthing ni le checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"concurrence-et-conservation\">Concurrence et conservation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/concurrence-et-conservation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Concurrence et conservation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ne pas copier un \u003ccode>Store\u003c/code>. Les méthodes et \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> partagent un mutex. Chaque séquence native reste sur le même thread OS pour récupérer correctement les erreurs C locales au thread. Aucun pointeur Go n&#39;est conservé par C++. Fermer explicitement chaque store pour libérer son verrou de processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Ingest\u003c/code> conserve automatiquement le fichier avec un pin \u003ccode>ffi/&lt;manifest&gt;\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Import\u003c/code> vérifie l&#39;identifiant et le format, mais ne publie rien. \u003ccode>Pin\u003c/code> vérifie toute la fermeture et les hashes complets avant de remplacer la référence ; conserver plusieurs noms de pins pour garder plusieurs versions. \u003ccode>Unpin\u003c/code> rend éventuellement des objets éligibles à un futur GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CopyMissing\u003c/code> est un banc d&#39;intégration local entre deux handles : il verrouille les deux stores dans un ordre stable, compare les présences vérifiées, copie un objet à la fois, puis publie le pin. Une reprise réutilise les objets déjà écrits, y compris après fermeture/réouverture. L&#39;annulation intervient entre objets. Un transfert interrompu laisse des objets non publiés qui doivent rester protégés contre le GC jusqu&#39;à sa reprise. Il n&#39;y a pas de GC concurrent possible à travers ce binding ; un GC lancé après fermeture peut supprimer ces objets non épinglés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le corps d&#39;un objet est limité à 16 Mio. La liste complète des dépendances est chargée en mémoire, limitée à 128 Mio côté Go. Les lectures par buffers font actuellement une requête de taille puis une lecture, donc deux vérifications natives. La pagination, les leases persistants, les buffers réutilisables et le coût du passage Go/C++ restent des travaux de performance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers\">Plages et comparaison de fichiers\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Plages et comparaison de fichiers\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ReadRange(id, offset, length)\u003c/code> retourne les octets d&#39;une plage exacte, dans la\nlimite de 16 Mio par appel. Les blocs sélectionnés sont vérifiés ; une plage\ncouvrant tout le fichier vérifie aussi son hash complet. \u003ccode>RestoreRange\u003c/code> exporte\nen flux vers un fichier neuf, sans cette limite de longueur, avec publication\nexclusive. Une plage hors limites renvoie une erreur sans troncature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CompareFiles(source, target)\u003c/code> retourne un \u003ccode>FileComparison\u003c/code> typé : compteurs\nd&#39;octets réutilisables/nouveaux, blocs cibles avec offset source éventuel et liste\ndédupliquée des blocs absents du manifeste source. Ce plan repose uniquement sur\nles métadonnées. Vérifier les blocs copiés et le fichier terminé avant publication.\nIl ne prouve pas qu&#39;un fichier de travail contient toujours les octets attendus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces méthodes partagent le mutex et le thread OS des autres appels. Elles ne\nchangent aucun pin et ne sont pas annulables pendant un appel natif. Le JSON de\ncomparaison est plafonné à 128 Mio puis décodé en structures Go. La bibliothèque\net les headers doivent provenir de cette version ; le numéro ABI 1 est conservé\navec des symboles supplémentaires. Voir les contrats des \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-ranges\">plages\u003c/a>\net \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-comparison\">comparaisons\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-observation\">Observation du workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/workspace-observation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Observation du workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenWorkspace(project, storePath, WorkspaceOptions{})\u003c/code> arme le watcher et garde l’inventaire complet sans Store ouvert. Utiliser \u003ccode>Status(store, WorkspaceSelection{})\u003c/code>, ou \u003ccode>Combined: true\u003c/code> pour index+travail. Fermer le Store de l’appelant après chaque requête, puis \u003ccode>Wait(ctx, timeout)\u003c/code> pour des hints natifs/polling typés. Les lots restent retenus jusqu’à status réussi, même après plusieurs waits ou annulation. \u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> demande un audit complet ; les planifier indépendamment des notifications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les options nulles utilisent Windows/Linux natif, fallback polling, 4096 hints et 65536 enregistrements. Appels et \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> sont sérialisés ; fermer explicitement. \u003ccode>Status\u003c/code> retourne le JSON canonique en \u003ccode>json.RawMessage\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>OpenWorkspaceContext\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>StatusContext\u003c/code> transmettent le contexte aux enregistrements récursifs et au hash ; \u003ccode>Wait\u003c/code> interrompt directement l’attente native. L’annulation peut aussi quitter l’attente du mutex workspace/Store. La source native est privée à chaque appel, libérée après arrêt/fin de son callback. Un résultat natif terminé gagne sur une annulation tardive ; IO système/encodage JSON peuvent finir avant le prochain contrôle. Les callbacks C de progression ne sont pas encore exposés en Go. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">ownership et limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"windows-test-cleanup\">Fermeture sous Windows\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/windows-test-cleanup\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fermeture sous Windows\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Fermer explicitement les Stores et sessions avant de retirer leurs répertoires. La DLL de confiance reste chargée pour la durée du processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"syncthing\">Syncthing\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/syncthing\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Syncthing\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le module optionnel \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing\">Syncthing\u003c/a> adapte les manifests aux blocs fixes SHA-256 BEP via la bibliothèque Syncthing v2.1.7 figée. Connexions d’appareils authentifiées et transferts différentiels sont vérifiés contre son daemon réel. Objets et CDC Syrnck gardent leur format. Le catalogue conserve index, vecteurs, tombstones et données vérifiées dans le moteur ; le téléchargement séparé ne garde pas le Store pendant l’attente réseau. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller\">contrôleur continu de fichiers\u003c/a> applique les versions vérifiées aux workspaces natifs avec reprise à propriétaire. Sémantique complète des dossiers/liens, copies pour versions protocolaires concurrentes, découverte, interface d’appairage et installation de service OS restent à terminer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registered-workspaces\">Workspaces enregistrés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/registered-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Workspaces enregistrés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ouvrir avec \u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code> puis sélectionner\n\u003ccode>store.UseWorkspace(&quot;edit&quot;)\u003c/code> avant chaque statut. Un nom vide utilise le principal.\nUn contexte différent échoue ; un nom invalide garde la sélection précédente.\nVoir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/registered-contexts\">les contextes liés\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-workspace-actions\">Capture et reprise natives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/native-workspace-actions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Capture et reprise natives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace.NativeState(store)\u003c/code> lit contexte natif sélectionné, référence,\nrévision, matérialisation et checkout en cours. \u003ccode>state.Expected()\u003c/code> fige les\nidentités cible/révision/politique exactes. L’utiliser pour\n\u003ccode>Capture(store, expected, message)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>PlanCheckout(store, expected, candidate)\u003c/code>\net \u003ccode>CheckoutOwned(store, expected, candidate, owner)\u003c/code>. Capture conserve le travail\nlocal ; checkout exige un workspace propre et garde un journal récupérable.\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned(store, target, candidate, owner)\u003c/code> reprend uniquement cette opération\nen cours exacte. Les cinq méthodes ont des variantes Context et vérifient la\nliaison exacte Store/contexte/dossier. Une erreur de mutation exige une relecture :\nla publication peut déjà avoir eu lieu. Voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\">contrat des actions natives\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext(ctx, store, transaction)\u003c/code> lit un journal ou reçu terminé\nexact par le décodeur du cœur, avec liaison Store/contexte/dossier vérifiée. Un\nreçu committed prouve une application passée, même après une édition locale ; il\nne prétend pas que le workspace est encore propre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreserving[Context]\u003c/code> archive explicitement les éditions de fichiers\nordinaires et crée des copies exclusives avant reprise du journal natif exact.\nRespecter le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits\">contrat des éditions actives\u003c/a> ;\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned\u003c/code> reste strict et toute erreur de mutation exige une relecture.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Rapport des E/S de capture\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/go-binding/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rapport des E/S de capture\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>CaptureReportContext\u003c/code> retourne révision et compteurs sources de lecture et\nréutilisation. \u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code> conserve son résultat limité à l’ID. Les\nmanifestes de session peuvent survivre à la réouverture du Store, avec inventaire\net vérification complète. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance/incremental-capture\">limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Binding Go du moteur C++ # Le module local syrnck.local/bindings/go expose les stores, le découpage CDC/fixe, la lecture/importation des objets, leurs dépendances, les pins et la reconstruction vérifiée. Il n'ajoute aucune dépendance Go externe. Dans un projet consommateur, utiliser temporairement un replace vers ce dossier, jusqu'à la publication du module sous une adresse définitive. Le moteur reste entièrement en C++20 ; ce dossier contient seulement son adaptateur Go. Windows amd64/arm64 utilise le chargeur DLL du système, sans cgo ni compilateur C supplémentaire. Linux/macOS utilise cgo et lie syrnck_ffi . Seul Windows amd64 a été exécuté localement ; Linux/macOS sont prévus dans la CI. ARM64 et les autres plateformes restent à valider. Charger la bibliothèque native # Compiler la bibliothèque C++ correspondante. Sous Windows, définir SYRNCK_LIBRARY au chemin absolu de la DLL de confiance avant le premier Open ; elle reste chargée pour la durée du processus. Unix utilise cgo : configurer CGO_LDFLAGS=-L\u003cdossier> et le chemin du chargeur ou un rpath de distribution. Go 1.22+ est requis. Exemple # go Copier source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{}) if err != nil { return err } defer source.Close() destination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true}) if err != nil { return err } defer destination.Close() id, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{}) if err != nil { return err } stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination, syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\") if err != nil { return err } fmt.Printf(\"%d octets copiés\\n\", stats.Bytes) return destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\") Create exige un dossier neuf ou vide ; il ne signifie pas « ouvrir ou créer ». Options.Key contient zéro ou 32 octets pour le chiffrement au repos. L'appel ne conserve pas le tableau Go. L'application reste responsable du cycle de vie de sa clé et de l'effacement de ses propres copies. Préparer et publier un état composé # PrepareBytes(data, chunking) ingère au plus 16 Mio depuis la mémoire sans fichier temporaire en clair. PrepareFile(path, chunking) lit un flux sans cette limite d’entrée. Les deux rendent des manifests sans référence , contrairement à l’ancien Ingest . Put(kind, data) crée un objet typé sans référence : octets de blocs exacts ; C++ valide et encode canoniquement les JSON de manifest/révision et calcule leur ID BLAKE3 natif. Champs inconnus, clés dupliquées même échappées, schéma invalide, profondeur et tailles excessives sont refusés. Aucun encodeur canonique ni algorithme de hash d’objet natif n’est à réimplémenter en Go. Garder le Store ouvert pendant préparation et publication. Après fermeture, le GC peut supprimer les objets sans référence. Retenir les dépendances dans une révision native complète ou des racines explicites : un bloc opaque contenant des IDs ne retient pas automatiquement ces objets. Les fichiers et parents d’une révision native tracent leurs dépendances complètes pour GC et sauvegardes. Refs() rend une map[string]ObjectKey indépendante. CompareRefs(updates) protège et remplace/supprime atomiquement au plus 65536 références et 16 Mio de JSON. Chaque fermeture et hash de fichier complet sont vérifiés avant l’unique publication de la table. Les noms en double sont des erreurs. Expected nul exige l’absence ; Value nul supprime. Un groupe nil/vide ne change rien. Le booléen n’a de sens que si err == nil : go Copier current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx) if err != nil { return err } var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey if old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old } // JSON applicatif d’une révision native complète référençant les manifests // préparés et les éventuelles révisions parentes à conserver. candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON) if err != nil { return err } applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{ {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate}, }) if err != nil { return err } // Relire et établir le résultat avant un nouvel essai. if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan } Une condition périmée ne change aucun membre et rend false sans erreur. Relire/replanifier au lieu de changer la valeur attendue d’une ancienne opération. Une erreur IO peut survenir après publication atomique : sur erreur, le booléen ne prouve pas l’absence de publication. Rouvrir/relire la table et comparer le groupe exact proposé avant de réessayer. La table est entièrement ancienne ou nouvelle, sans mélange produit par cette transaction. Un parcours de haut niveau doit conserver son intention s’il doit réconcilier un résultat inconnu après crash. Les cinq opérations possèdent une variante Context . Préparation et CAS propagent l’annulation native pendant ingestion/vérification et avant publication ; un succès terminé l’emporte sur une demande tardive. PutContext et RefsContext contrôlent l’annulation avant leur appel natif borné ; encodage/compression/lecture déjà engagés peuvent finir. Une préparation échouée peut laisser des objets complets sans référence, sans publication. Les écrivains externes concurrents restent à gérer par l’application. Ces primitives permettent un état protocolaire persistant ; elles n’implémentent pas la réconciliation Syncthing ni le checkout. Concurrence et conservation # Ne pas copier un Store . Les méthodes et Close partagent un mutex. Chaque séquence native reste sur le même thread OS pour récupérer correctement les erreurs C locales au thread. Aucun pointeur Go n'est conservé par C++. Fermer explicitement chaque store pour libérer son verrou de processus. Ingest conserve automatiquement le fichier avec un pin ffi/\u003cmanifest> . Import vérifie l'identifiant et le format, mais ne publie rien. Pin vérifie toute la fermeture et les hashes complets avant de remplacer la référence ; conserver plusieurs noms de pins pour garder plusieurs versions. Unpin rend éventuellement des objets éligibles à un futur GC. CopyMissing est un banc d'intégration local entre deux handles : il verrouille les deux stores dans un ordre stable, compare les présences vérifiées, copie un objet à la fois, puis publie le pin. Une reprise réutilise les objets déjà écrits, y compris après fermeture/réouverture. L'annulation intervient entre objets. Un transfert interrompu laisse des objets non publiés qui doivent rester protégés contre le GC jusqu'à sa reprise. Il n'y a pas de GC concurrent possible à travers ce binding ; un GC lancé après fermeture peut supprimer ces objets non épinglés. Le corps d'un objet est limité à 16 Mio. La liste complète des dépendances est chargée en mémoire, limitée à 128 Mio côté Go. Les lectures par buffers font actuellement une requête de taille puis une lecture, donc deux vérifications natives. La pagination, les leases persistants, les buffers réutilisables et le coût du passage Go/C++ restent des travaux de performance. Plages et comparaison de fichiers # ReadRange(id, offset, length) retourne les octets d'une plage exacte, dans la limite de 16 Mio par appel. Les blocs sélectionnés sont vérifiés ; une plage couvrant tout le fichier vérifie aussi son hash complet. RestoreRange exporte en flux vers un fichier neuf, sans cette limite de longueur, avec publication exclusive. Une plage hors limites renvoie une erreur sans troncature. CompareFiles(source, target) retourne un FileComparison typé : compteurs d'octets réutilisables/nouveaux, blocs cibles avec offset source éventuel et liste dédupliquée des blocs absents du manifeste source. Ce plan repose uniquement sur les métadonnées. Vérifier les blocs copiés et le fichier terminé avant publication. Il ne prouve pas qu'un fichier de travail contient toujours les octets attendus. Ces méthodes partagent le mutex et le thread OS des autres appels. Elles ne changent aucun pin et ne sont pas annulables pendant un appel natif. Le JSON de comparaison est plafonné à 128 Mio puis décodé en structures Go. La bibliothèque et les headers doivent provenir de cette version ; le numéro ABI 1 est conservé avec des symboles supplémentaires. Voir les contrats des plages et comparaisons . Observation du workspace # OpenWorkspace(project, storePath, WorkspaceOptions{}) arme le watcher et garde l’inventaire complet sans Store ouvert. Utiliser Status(store, WorkspaceSelection{}) , ou Combined: true pour index+travail. Fermer le Store de l’appelant après chaque requête, puis Wait(ctx, timeout) pour des hints natifs/polling typés. Les lots restent retenus jusqu’à status réussi, même après plusieurs waits ou annulation. Invalidate demande un audit complet ; les planifier indépendamment des notifications. Les options nulles utilisent Windows/Linux natif, fallback polling, 4096 hints et 65536 enregistrements. Appels et Close sont sérialisés ; fermer explicitement. Status retourne le JSON canonique en json.RawMessage . OpenWorkspaceContext / StatusContext transmettent le contexte aux enregistrements récursifs et au hash ; Wait interrompt directement l’attente native. L’annulation peut aussi quitter l’attente du mutex workspace/Store. La source native est privée à chaque appel, libérée après arrêt/fin de son callback. Un résultat natif terminé gagne sur une annulation tardive ; IO système/encodage JSON peuvent finir avant le prochain contrôle. Les callbacks C de progression ne sont pas encore exposés en Go. Voir ownership et limites . Fermeture sous Windows # Fermer explicitement les Stores et sessions avant de retirer leurs répertoires. La DLL de confiance reste chargée pour la durée du processus. Syncthing # Le module optionnel Syncthing adapte les manifests aux blocs fixes SHA-256 BEP via la bibliothèque Syncthing v2.1.7 figée. Connexions d’appareils authentifiées et transferts différentiels sont vérifiés contre son daemon réel. Objets et CDC Syrnck gardent leur format. Le catalogue conserve index, vecteurs, tombstones et données vérifiées dans le moteur ; le téléchargement séparé ne garde pas le Store pendant l’attente réseau. Le contrôleur continu de fichiers applique les versions vérifiées aux workspaces natifs avec reprise à propriétaire. Sémantique complète des dossiers/liens, copies pour versions protocolaires concurrentes, découverte, interface d’appairage et installation de service OS restent à terminer. Workspaces enregistrés # Ouvrir avec WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} puis sélectionner store.UseWorkspace(\"edit\") avant chaque statut. Un nom vide utilise le principal. Un contexte différent échoue ; un nom invalide garde la sélection précédente. Voir les contextes liés . Capture et reprise natives # workspace.NativeState(store) lit contexte natif sélectionné, référence, révision, matérialisation et checkout en cours. state.Expected() fige les identités cible/révision/politique exactes. L’utiliser pour Capture(store, expected, message) , PlanCheckout(store, expected, candidate) et CheckoutOwned(store, expected, candidate, owner) . Capture conserve le travail local ; checkout exige un workspace propre et garde un journal récupérable. RecoverOwned(store, target, candidate, owner) reprend uniquement cette opération en cours exacte. Les cinq méthodes ont des variantes Context et vérifient la liaison exacte Store/contexte/dossier. Une erreur de mutation exige une relecture : la publication peut déjà avoir eu lieu. Voir le contrat des actions natives . CheckoutStatusContext(ctx, store, transaction) lit un journal ou reçu terminé exact par le décodeur du cœur, avec liaison Store/contexte/dossier vérifiée. Un reçu committed prouve une application passée, même après une édition locale ; il ne prétend pas que le workspace est encore propre. RecoverOwnedPreserving[Context] archive explicitement les éditions de fichiers ordinaires et crée des copies exclusives avant reprise du journal natif exact. Respecter le contrat des éditions actives ; RecoverOwned reste strict et toute erreur de mutation exige une relecture. Rapport des E/S de capture # CaptureReportContext retourne révision et compteurs sources de lecture et réutilisation. CaptureContext conserve son résultat limité à l’ID. Les manifestes de session peuvent survivre à la réouverture du Store, avec inventaire et vérification complète. Voir les limites . source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{}) if err != nil { return err } defer source.Close() destination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true}) if err != nil { return err } defer destination.Close() id, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{}) if err != nil { return err } stats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination, syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\") if err != nil { return err } fmt.Printf(\"%d octets copiés\\n\", stats.Bytes) return destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\") current, err := store.RefsContext(ctx) if err != nil { return err } var expected *syrnck.ObjectKey if old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old } // JSON applicatif d’une révision native complète référençant les manifests // préparés et les éventuelles révisions parentes à conserver. candidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON) if err != nil { return err } applied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{ {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate}, }) if err != nil { return err } // Relire et établir le résultat avant un nouvel essai. if !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }","headings":[{"id":"binding-go-du-moteur-c","text":"Binding Go du moteur C++","level":1},{"id":"tester","text":"Charger la bibliothèque native","level":2},{"id":"exemple","text":"Exemple","level":2},{"id":"catalog-transactions","text":"Préparer et publier un état composé","level":2},{"id":"concurrence-et-conservation","text":"Concurrence et conservation","level":2},{"id":"plages-et-comparaison-de-fichiers","text":"Plages et comparaison de fichiers","level":2},{"id":"workspace-observation","text":"Observation du workspace","level":2},{"id":"windows-test-cleanup","text":"Fermeture sous Windows","level":2},{"id":"syncthing","text":"Syncthing","level":2},{"id":"registered-workspaces","text":"Workspaces enregistrés","level":2},{"id":"native-workspace-actions","text":"Capture et reprise natives","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Rapport des E/S de capture","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-2-L13","code-2-L14"],"languages":["go"],"code":"source, err := syrnck.Open(\"source-store\", syrnck.Options{})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer source.Close()\ndestination, err := syrnck.Open(\"destination-store\", syrnck.Options{Create: true})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer destination.Close()\n\nid, err := source.Ingest(\"Map.uasset\", syrnck.Chunking{})\nif err != nil { return err }\nstats, err := syrnck.CopyMissing(ctx, source, destination,\n    syrnck.ObjectKey{Kind: \"manifest\", ID: id}, \"sync/map-v1\")\nif err != nil { return err }\nfmt.Printf(\"%d octets copiés\\n\", stats.Bytes)\nreturn destination.Restore(id, \"Map-restored.uasset\")\ncurrent, err := store.RefsContext(ctx)\nif err != nil { return err }\nvar expected *syrnck.ObjectKey\nif old, ok := current[\"ffi/catalog/main\"]; ok { expected = &old }\n\n// JSON applicatif d’une révision native complète référençant les manifests\n// préparés et les éventuelles révisions parentes à conserver.\ncandidate, err := store.PutContext(ctx, \"revision\", revisionJSON)\nif err != nil { return err }\napplied, err := store.CompareRefsContext(ctx, []syrnck.RefUpdate{\n    {Name: \"ffi/catalog/main\", Expected: expected, Value: &candidate},\n})\nif err != nil { return err } // Relire et établir le résultat avant un nouvel essai.\nif !applied { return errRefreshAndReplan }","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/file-ranges","#/fr/file-comparison","#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/syncthing","#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller","#/fr/workspace-abi/registered-contexts","#/fr/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions","#/fr/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits","#/fr/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide","revision":"6114a4e45af0"},{"id":"indexes","title":"Index de packs paginés","description":"Guide : Index de packs paginés.","section":"Stockage","order":10,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs\">Lecture paginée des index de packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/indexes/lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Lecture paginée des index de packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le moteur consulte les index \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code> directement sur disque. Il conserve en mémoire un répertoire de pages, un filtre de clés absentes et un cache de pages borné, au lieu d&#39;une table complète contenant chaque ID et son emplacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette évolution ne change aucun octet du format physique v2/v3, des objets, des enveloppes ou du protocole réseau. Elle ne crée aucun fichier d&#39;index supplémentaire et ne convertit pas les stores à l&#39;ouverture.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-et-recherche\">Validation et recherche\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/indexes/validation-et-recherche\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Validation et recherche\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque ouverture ou audit parcourt \u003cstrong>tout l&#39;index\u003c/strong> avec un tampon de 256 enregistrements, soit \u003cstrong>11 520 octets\u003c/strong>. Le lecteur contrôle l&#39;en-tête, le nombre d&#39;entrées, les types, le tri strict, la continuité des plages et le hash BLAKE3 complet annoncé par la configuration. Il construit pendant ce parcours :\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pour chaque page : sa première et dernière clé et son empreinte BLAKE3.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pour chaque pack : un filtre de présence à 10 bits par entrée environ, avec sept sondes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Le filtre permet de rejeter des clés absentes sans lire une page. Une réponse positive reste à confirmer dans l&#39;index ; le filtre ne valide pas un objet. Les bornes dirigent une recherche binaire vers la page candidate. À l&#39;ouverture du fichier d&#39;index, le moteur vérifie le fichier et sa taille. Chaque page relue est comparée à son empreinte du parcours validé avant utilisation de ses IDs et emplacements. Une page changée, tronquée ou absente produit une erreur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les index des packs sont triés séparément. Quand leurs intervalles de clés se recouvrent, une fusion de leurs parcours vérifie qu&#39;aucune clé n&#39;appartient à deux packs actifs. Ce contrôle garde une page par pack concerné et une file de têtes, sans reconstruire une table de tous les objets. Avec 1 024 packs, les seules pages de ce contrôle temporaire peuvent atteindre 11,25 Mio. Les intervalles entièrement disjoints dispensent de cette fusion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-et-snapshot\">Cache et snapshot\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/indexes/cache-et-snapshot\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cache et snapshot\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les pages déjà vérifiées et la dernière adresse trouvée forment un \u003cstrong>snapshot du catalogue ouvert\u003c/strong>, comme l&#39;ancienne table entièrement en RAM. Une modification externe d&#39;un index pendant que le store détient son verrou peut donc rester invisible sur une entrée chaude. Les pages relues après éviction doivent toujours correspondre à leurs empreintes validées. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> rechargent les index depuis leur début et vérifient leurs hashes complets avant leur travail ; ils n&#39;acceptent pas une ancienne page comme preuve de l&#39;état actuel du disque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette règle porte sur les adresses dans l&#39;index. Les enveloppes d&#39;objets restent relues et contrôlées avant toute réutilisation du cache décodé. Le contenu retourné doit satisfaire les mêmes règles d&#39;ID, format et authentification que précédemment. Le filtre, le répertoire et les pages ne sont pas des racines de rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reglages-et-compteurs\">Réglages et compteurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/indexes/reglages-et-compteurs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Réglages et compteurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le cache de pages d&#39;index vaut \u003cstrong>4 Mio par défaut\u003c/strong>, indépendamment du cache décodé de 64 Mio. L&#39;option globale accepte 0 à 1 024 Mio ; zéro désactive le cache de pages et de la dernière adresse, tout en conservant le répertoire et le filtre nécessaires au lecteur :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get &lt;manifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>En C++, utiliser \u003ccode>StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes\u003c/code> au quatrième argument du constructeur \u003ccode>Store\u003c/code>. Le budget est transmis aux lecteurs temporaires créés pendant la maintenance. Il porte sur les données de pages de chaque lecteur ; les snapshots temporaires, structures de cache et buffers de travail s&#39;ajoutent à ce budget. La LRU est partagée par les index d&#39;un catalogue, sans multiplier le budget par le nombre de packs. Une page trop grande pour le budget est lue dans un tampon temporaire puis libérée/remplacée, sans admission dans le cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>io_stats()\u003c/code> expose quatre instantanés d&#39;allocations utiles au réglage : \u003ccode>index_directory_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_filter_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_pages\u003c/code>. Ils comptent les capacités des répertoires/filtres et les données de pages admises, sans l&#39;overhead des conteneurs/allocateurs, les chemins, métadonnées JSON, buffers temporaires ou pages du système. Ils ne mesurent pas le RSS global du processus. \u003ccode>clear_cache()\u003c/code> libère le cache décodé et le cache de pages, en gardant le répertoire validé ; \u003ccode>reset_io_stats()\u003c/code> ne vide pas ces caches et conserve ces instantanés de mémoire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les index ne contiennent pas les données déchiffrées des fichiers. Les types, IDs, tailles et positions restent visibles comme dans le format précédent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les opérations de reconstruction, fermeture de dépendances, ingestion et inventaire gardent une session de lecture. Elle réutilise au plus \u003cstrong>8 fichiers d&#39;index ouverts\u003c/strong>, avec LRU, en plus des 8 fichiers de données de packs possibles. Les sessions imbriquées partagent ces handles, fermés au retour ou sur exception, et avant le retrait des anciens fichiers. Une recherche isolée hors session ouvre son index pour la page manquante puis le ferme. Cette réutilisation évite de rouvrir le même index à chaque page d&#39;un lot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memoire-et-limites\">Mémoire et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/indexes/memoire-et-limites\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mémoire et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le répertoire utilise 98 octets par page, plus environ 10 bits par entrée pour le filtre. Pour 1 048 576 entrées d&#39;un pack, les données de répertoire occupent 401 408 octets et le filtre 1 310 720 octets, avant cache et overhead. Le fichier d&#39;index correspondant conserve ses 47 185 936 octets. La construction de cet index écrit aussi les enregistrements par pages ; elle ne prépare plus un buffer de tout le fichier d&#39;index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les plafonds restent 1 048 576 objets par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total. L&#39;ouverture garde un coût linéaire de validation ; elle ne se résume pas à quelques lectures de pages. Les recherches aléatoires peuvent nécessiter davantage d&#39;IO qu&#39;une table entièrement en mémoire, surtout avec un cache petit ou nul. Le filtre réduit les recherches inutiles entre packs sans éliminer tous les faux positifs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC utilise désormais ces index pour parcourir les objets par pages et marquer les objets empaquetés avec un bit par entrée. Il ne matérialise plus l&#39;inventaire complet, la liste des objets morts ni les listes de survivants par pack. La réécriture et la relecture des packs de remplacement utilisent aussi ce parcours. Chaque page d&#39;énumération est indépendante du cache : un callback peut effectuer des recherches ou lectures imbriquées sans invalider son buffer, même avec un cache nul.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> utilise également un bitmap et une énumération par type : les bornes des pages sélectionnent les révisions, puis les manifestes, puis les blocs sans constituer un inventaire complet. Les pages aux frontières sont filtrées en conservant les positions d&#39;origine. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/audit\">garanties des audits\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> et l&#39;API publique \u003ccode>closure()\u003c/code> conservent encore leurs collections d&#39;identifiants. L&#39;empaquetage et la compaction utilisent des plans en flux et gardent seulement les clés isolées à trier ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/compaction\">garanties de compaction\u003c/a>. Le GC et \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> gardent un ensemble compact d&#39;IDs pour les objets parcourus absents des packs, une pile de dépendances et les métadonnées JSON du fichier en cours. Le moteur entier ne dispose donc pas encore d&#39;un plafond global de RAM indépendant de la taille du projet. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs/memoire-du-gc\">budgets du GC\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance\">mesures\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Lecture paginée des index de packs # Le moteur consulte les index SYRIDX01 directement sur disque. Il conserve en mémoire un répertoire de pages, un filtre de clés absentes et un cache de pages borné, au lieu d'une table complète contenant chaque ID et son emplacement. Cette évolution ne change aucun octet du format physique v2/v3, des objets, des enveloppes ou du protocole réseau. Elle ne crée aucun fichier d'index supplémentaire et ne convertit pas les stores à l'ouverture. Validation et recherche # Chaque ouverture ou audit parcourt tout l'index avec un tampon de 256 enregistrements, soit 11 520 octets . Le lecteur contrôle l'en-tête, le nombre d'entrées, les types, le tri strict, la continuité des plages et le hash BLAKE3 complet annoncé par la configuration. Il construit pendant ce parcours : Pour chaque page : sa première et dernière clé et son empreinte BLAKE3. Pour chaque pack : un filtre de présence à 10 bits par entrée environ, avec sept sondes. Le filtre permet de rejeter des clés absentes sans lire une page. Une réponse positive reste à confirmer dans l'index ; le filtre ne valide pas un objet. Les bornes dirigent une recherche binaire vers la page candidate. À l'ouverture du fichier d'index, le moteur vérifie le fichier et sa taille. Chaque page relue est comparée à son empreinte du parcours validé avant utilisation de ses IDs et emplacements. Une page changée, tronquée ou absente produit une erreur. Les index des packs sont triés séparément. Quand leurs intervalles de clés se recouvrent, une fusion de leurs parcours vérifie qu'aucune clé n'appartient à deux packs actifs. Ce contrôle garde une page par pack concerné et une file de têtes, sans reconstruire une table de tous les objets. Avec 1 024 packs, les seules pages de ce contrôle temporaire peuvent atteindre 11,25 Mio. Les intervalles entièrement disjoints dispensent de cette fusion. Cache et snapshot # Les pages déjà vérifiées et la dernière adresse trouvée forment un snapshot du catalogue ouvert , comme l'ancienne table entièrement en RAM. Une modification externe d'un index pendant que le store détient son verrou peut donc rester invisible sur une entrée chaude. Les pages relues après éviction doivent toujours correspondre à leurs empreintes validées. fsck et gc rechargent les index depuis leur début et vérifient leurs hashes complets avant leur travail ; ils n'acceptent pas une ancienne page comme preuve de l'état actuel du disque. Cette règle porte sur les adresses dans l'index. Les enveloppes d'objets restent relues et contrôlées avant toute réutilisation du cache décodé. Le contenu retourné doit satisfaire les mêmes règles d'ID, format et authentification que précédemment. Le filtre, le répertoire et les pages ne sont pas des racines de rétention. Réglages et compteurs # Le cache de pages d'index vaut 4 Mio par défaut , indépendamment du cache décodé de 64 Mio. L'option globale accepte 0 à 1 024 Mio ; zéro désactive le cache de pages et de la dernière adresse, tout en conservant le répertoire et le filtre nécessaires au lecteur : text Copier syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck En C++, utiliser StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes au quatrième argument du constructeur Store . Le budget est transmis aux lecteurs temporaires créés pendant la maintenance. Il porte sur les données de pages de chaque lecteur ; les snapshots temporaires, structures de cache et buffers de travail s'ajoutent à ce budget. La LRU est partagée par les index d'un catalogue, sans multiplier le budget par le nombre de packs. Une page trop grande pour le budget est lue dans un tampon temporaire puis libérée/remplacée, sans admission dans le cache. io_stats() expose quatre instantanés d'allocations utiles au réglage : index_directory_bytes , index_filter_bytes , index_cached_bytes , index_cached_pages . Ils comptent les capacités des répertoires/filtres et les données de pages admises, sans l'overhead des conteneurs/allocateurs, les chemins, métadonnées JSON, buffers temporaires ou pages du système. Ils ne mesurent pas le RSS global du processus. clear_cache() libère le cache décodé et le cache de pages, en gardant le répertoire validé ; reset_io_stats() ne vide pas ces caches et conserve ces instantanés de mémoire. Les index ne contiennent pas les données déchiffrées des fichiers. Les types, IDs, tailles et positions restent visibles comme dans le format précédent. Les opérations de reconstruction, fermeture de dépendances, ingestion et inventaire gardent une session de lecture. Elle réutilise au plus 8 fichiers d'index ouverts , avec LRU, en plus des 8 fichiers de données de packs possibles. Les sessions imbriquées partagent ces handles, fermés au retour ou sur exception, et avant le retrait des anciens fichiers. Une recherche isolée hors session ouvre son index pour la page manquante puis le ferme. Cette réutilisation évite de rouvrir le même index à chaque page d'un lot. Mémoire et limites # Le répertoire utilise 98 octets par page, plus environ 10 bits par entrée pour le filtre. Pour 1 048 576 entrées d'un pack, les données de répertoire occupent 401 408 octets et le filtre 1 310 720 octets, avant cache et overhead. Le fichier d'index correspondant conserve ses 47 185 936 octets. La construction de cet index écrit aussi les enregistrements par pages ; elle ne prépare plus un buffer de tout le fichier d'index. Les plafonds restent 1 048 576 objets par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total. L'ouverture garde un coût linéaire de validation ; elle ne se résume pas à quelques lectures de pages. Les recherches aléatoires peuvent nécessiter davantage d'IO qu'une table entièrement en mémoire, surtout avec un cache petit ou nul. Le filtre réduit les recherches inutiles entre packs sans éliminer tous les faux positifs. Le GC utilise désormais ces index pour parcourir les objets par pages et marquer les objets empaquetés avec un bit par entrée. Il ne matérialise plus l'inventaire complet, la liste des objets morts ni les listes de survivants par pack. La réécriture et la relecture des packs de remplacement utilisent aussi ce parcours. Chaque page d'énumération est indépendante du cache : un callback peut effectuer des recherches ou lectures imbriquées sans invalider son buffer, même avec un cache nul. fsck utilise également un bitmap et une énumération par type : les bornes des pages sélectionnent les révisions, puis les manifestes, puis les blocs sans constituer un inventaire complet. Les pages aux frontières sont filtrées en conservant les positions d'origine. Voir les garanties des audits . objects() et l'API publique closure() conservent encore leurs collections d'identifiants. L'empaquetage et la compaction utilisent des plans en flux et gardent seulement les clés isolées à trier ; voir les garanties de compaction . Le GC et fsck gardent un ensemble compact d'IDs pour les objets parcourus absents des packs, une pile de dépendances et les métadonnées JSON du fichier en cours. Le moteur entier ne dispose donc pas encore d'un plafond global de RAM indépendant de la taille du projet. Voir les budgets du GC et les mesures . syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck","headings":[{"id":"lecture-paginee-des-index-de-packs","text":"Lecture paginée des index de packs","level":1},{"id":"validation-et-recherche","text":"Validation et recherche","level":2},{"id":"cache-et-snapshot","text":"Cache et snapshot","level":2},{"id":"reglages-et-compteurs","text":"Réglages et compteurs","level":2},{"id":"memoire-et-limites","text":"Mémoire et limites","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --index-cache-mib 4 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\nsyrnck --cache-mib 0 --index-cache-mib 0 fsck","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/audit","#/fr/compaction","#/fr/packs/memoire-du-gc","#/fr/performance"],"kind":"guide","revision":"ca6b37b5f412"},{"id":"integrations","title":"Intégrations des bibliothèques","description":"Intégrations des bibliothèques","section":"API","order":700,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"cpp\">C++20\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/integrations/cpp\" aria-label=\"Lien vers C++20\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Utiliser les en-têtes distribués et les cibles CMake. \u003ccode>syrnck_core\u003c/code> fournit stockage et opérations natives locales ; \u003ccode>syrnck_git\u003c/code> ajoute Git ; \u003ccode>syrnck_net\u003c/code> ajoute HTTP ; \u003ccode>syrnck_git_net\u003c/code> relie les catalogues Git aux transferts. L’alias \u003ccode>Syrnck::core\u003c/code> est disponible dans la construction source. Les archives C++ statiques demandent aussi l’édition de liens des dépendances natives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/api-store\">API Store\u003c/a> documente verrouillage, vérification et publication. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">Progression\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/backups\">sauvegardes\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff textuel\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-watch\">observation\u003c/a> détaillent chaque contrat. La référence utilise les annotations explicites des en-têtes publics distribués ; elle ne déduit pas la sémantique des surcharges et n’exporte pas les implémentations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-go\">C et Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/integrations/c-go\" aria-label=\"Lien vers C et Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> exporte l’ABI C ; \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi_static\u003c/code> utilise SYRNCK_STATIC. Faire correspondre binaire et en-tête et ne passer aucun objet C++ à travers l’interface. Respecter les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/api-c-abi\">règles de propriété/erreur\u003c/a>, le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/go-binding\">binding Go\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions embarquées\u003c/a>. Windows utilise les appels DLL dynamiques ; Go sur Unix utilise cgo. Sérialiser les handles et récupérer les erreurs sur le thread OS appelant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing\">adaptateur Syncthing\u003c/a> séparé couvre les données de blocs de fichiers ordinaires avec sa dépendance de référence épinglée. Il ne gère ni association d’appareils, ni versions causales, ni suppressions, ni daemon résident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"platforms\">Plateformes et interopérabilité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/integrations/platforms\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Plateformes et interopérabilité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Construire séparément pour Windows, Linux, macOS et chaque architecture. Les sources utilisent MSVC ou GCC/Clang avec C++20. Les dépendances natives incluent BLAKE3, Zstandard, Monocypher, utf8proc, JSON et les composants réseau optionnels. Leurs \u003ca href=\"#/fr/licenses\">notices\u003c/a> accompagnent la distribution.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour une intégration minimale, désactiver CLI, réseau, benchmarks et tests ; la génération documentaire peut aussi être désactivée explicitement. Le moteur ne demande ni en-têtes ni runtime Unreal. Un futur plugin est un projet séparé. Lore et Syncthing inspirent certains choix, mais leurs formats ne sont pas ceux de Syrnck. La compatibilité BEP appartient à l’adaptateur explicite, pas aux objets FastCDC/BLAKE3.\u003c/p>\n","text":"C++20 # Utiliser les en-têtes distribués et les cibles CMake. syrnck_core fournit stockage et opérations natives locales ; syrnck_git ajoute Git ; syrnck_net ajoute HTTP ; syrnck_git_net relie les catalogues Git aux transferts. L’alias Syrnck::core est disponible dans la construction source. Les archives C++ statiques demandent aussi l’édition de liens des dépendances natives. L’ API Store documente verrouillage, vérification et publication. Progression , sauvegardes , diff textuel et observation détaillent chaque contrat. La référence utilise les annotations explicites des en-têtes publics distribués ; elle ne déduit pas la sémantique des surcharges et n’exporte pas les implémentations. C et Go # syrnck_ffi exporte l’ABI C ; syrnck_ffi_static utilise SYRNCK_STATIC. Faire correspondre binaire et en-tête et ne passer aucun objet C++ à travers l’interface. Respecter les règles de propriété/erreur , le binding Go et les sessions embarquées . Windows utilise les appels DLL dynamiques ; Go sur Unix utilise cgo. Sérialiser les handles et récupérer les erreurs sur le thread OS appelant. L’ adaptateur Syncthing séparé couvre les données de blocs de fichiers ordinaires avec sa dépendance de référence épinglée. Il ne gère ni association d’appareils, ni versions causales, ni suppressions, ni daemon résident. Plateformes et interopérabilité # Construire séparément pour Windows, Linux, macOS et chaque architecture. Les sources utilisent MSVC ou GCC/Clang avec C++20. Les dépendances natives incluent BLAKE3, Zstandard, Monocypher, utf8proc, JSON et les composants réseau optionnels. Leurs notices accompagnent la distribution. Pour une intégration minimale, désactiver CLI, réseau, benchmarks et tests ; la génération documentaire peut aussi être désactivée explicitement. Le moteur ne demande ni en-têtes ni runtime Unreal. Un futur plugin est un projet séparé. Lore et Syncthing inspirent certains choix, mais leurs formats ne sont pas ceux de Syrnck. La compatibilité BEP appartient à l’adaptateur explicite, pas aux objets FastCDC/BLAKE3.","headings":[{"id":"cpp","text":"C++20","level":2},{"id":"c-go","text":"C et Go","level":2},{"id":"platforms","text":"Plateformes et interopérabilité","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/api-store","#/fr/operations","#/fr/backups","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/native-watch","#/fr/api-c-abi","#/fr/go-binding","#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/syncthing","#/fr/licenses"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0b1b98ffa820"},{"id":"licenses","title":"Licence et notices tierces","description":"Licence et notices tierces","section":"Syrnck","order":999,"sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"license\">Licence Syrnck\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/licenses/license\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Licence Syrnck\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le code original de Syrnck est sous MIT. Le texte juridique original ci-dessous s’applique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">MIT License\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">copies or substantial portions of the Software.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">SOFTWARE.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"dependencies\">Composants et distribution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/licenses/dependencies\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Composants et distribution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les composants natifs conservent leurs licences propres. Le tableau ci-dessous préserve la notice originale. L’installation du moteur fournit aussi les textes applicables sous share/licenses/Syrnck. Le module Go Syncthing optionnel reste MPL-2.0, téléchargé séparément ; MIT ne le requalifie pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le lecteur local utilise CyDoc 0.1.0 (MIT), marked (MIT) et Prism (MIT) ; esbuild (MIT) construit le lecteur. Leurs textes sont distribués sous DocData/licenses. L’outil est figé par l’archive CyDoc vendored ; aucune bibliothèque distante chargée. Aucun module média optionnel ni Three.js inclus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Pinned version\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>License / notice\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>BLAKE3 C implementation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.8.7\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>c/\u003c/code> of the official blake3 1.8.7 crate, matching the Rust prototype&#39;s hash implementation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Apache-2.0 / CC0; LLVM exception where applicable, included in \u003ccode>third_party/blake3/LICENSE_*\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>FastCDC 2020 port and gear/mask tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nathan Fiedler&#39;s fastcdc crate, \u003ccode>v2020::cut_gear\u003c/code> and tables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; \u003ccode>third_party/fastcdc/LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Monocypher\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4.0.3\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://monocypher.org/download/monocypher-4.0.3.tar.gz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official download\u003c/a>, \u003ccode>src/monocypher.c\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>src/monocypher.h\u003c/code>, optional \u003ccode>monocypher-ed25519.c\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>.h\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BSD-2-Clause / CC0; included \u003ccode>LICENCE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>nlohmann/json\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>3.12.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/tag/v3.12.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official release\u003c/a>, single-header distribution\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>LICENSE.MIT\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>cpp-httplib\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.28.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/tree/v0.28.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>, HTTP server only\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>utf8proc\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>2.10.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/JuliaStrings/utf8proc/tree/v2.10.0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT and Unicode data terms; included \u003ccode>LICENSE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>PicoSHA2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/okdshin/PicoSHA2/tree/161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official repository\u003c/a>, unmodified \u003ccode>picosha2.h\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; included \u003ccode>third_party/picosha2/LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Zstd\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.5.7\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/facebook/zstd/releases/download/v1.5.7/zstd-1.5.7.tar.gz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official release archive\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BSD-3-Clause / GPL-2.0 option; licenses in the fetched archive\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>curl / libcurl\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>8.16.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://curl.se/download/curl-8.16.0.tar.xz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official archive\u003c/a>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>curl license; \u003ccode>COPYING\u003c/code> in the fetched archive\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.1.0, \u003ccode>e038c474577e1f305df3eba421f2b9c03b0f1d8d\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/MrMybal/CyDoc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CyDoc\u003c/a>, packed archive in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/vendor\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/vendor/CyDoc.LICENSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>marked\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>17.0.6\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/marked.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Prism\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1.30.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/Prism.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>esbuild\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0.25.12\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Documentation build tool, pinned in \u003ccode>.CyDocBuild/package-lock.json\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MIT; reader&#39;s \u003ccode>DocData/licenses/esbuild.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n","text":"Licence Syrnck # Le code original de Syrnck est sous MIT. Le texte juridique original ci-dessous s’applique. text Copier MIT License Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE. Composants et distribution # Les composants natifs conservent leurs licences propres. Le tableau ci-dessous préserve la notice originale. L’installation du moteur fournit aussi les textes applicables sous share/licenses/Syrnck. Le module Go Syncthing optionnel reste MPL-2.0, téléchargé séparément ; MIT ne le requalifie pas. Le lecteur local utilise CyDoc 0.1.0 (MIT), marked (MIT) et Prism (MIT) ; esbuild (MIT) construit le lecteur. Leurs textes sont distribués sous DocData/licenses. L’outil est figé par l’archive CyDoc vendored ; aucune bibliothèque distante chargée. Aucun module média optionnel ni Three.js inclus. Component Pinned version Source License / notice BLAKE3 C implementation 1.8.7 c/ of the official blake3 1.8.7 crate, matching the Rust prototype's hash implementation Apache-2.0 / CC0; LLVM exception where applicable, included in third_party/blake3/LICENSE_* FastCDC 2020 port and gear/mask tables fastcdc 5.0.0, normalization 1, seed 0 Nathan Fiedler's fastcdc crate, v2020::cut_gear and tables MIT; third_party/fastcdc/LICENSE Monocypher 4.0.3 Official download , src/monocypher.c , src/monocypher.h , optional monocypher-ed25519.c / .h BSD-2-Clause / CC0; included LICENCE.md nlohmann/json 3.12.0 Official release , single-header distribution MIT; included LICENSE.MIT cpp-httplib 0.28.0 Official repository , HTTP server only MIT; included LICENSE utf8proc 2.10.0 Official repository MIT and Unicode data terms; included LICENSE.md PicoSHA2 161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29 Official repository , unmodified picosha2.h MIT; included third_party/picosha2/LICENSE Zstd 1.5.7 Official release archive BSD-3-Clause / GPL-2.0 option; licenses in the fetched archive curl / libcurl 8.16.0 Official archive curl license; COPYING in the fetched archive CyDoc 0.1.0, e038c474577e1f305df3eba421f2b9c03b0f1d8d CyDoc , packed archive in .CyDocBuild/vendor MIT; .CyDocBuild/vendor/CyDoc.LICENSE marked 17.0.6 CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/marked.txt Prism 1.30.0 CyDoc reader dependency, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/Prism.txt esbuild 0.25.12 Documentation build tool, pinned in .CyDocBuild/package-lock.json MIT; reader's DocData/licenses/esbuild.txt MIT License Copyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.","headings":[{"id":"license","text":"Licence Syrnck","level":2},{"id":"dependencies","text":"Composants et distribution","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21"],"languages":["text"],"code":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 MrMybal and Syrnck contributors\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.","assets":[],"links":["https://monocypher.org/download/monocypher-4.0.3.tar.gz","https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/tag/v3.12.0","https://github.com/yhirose/cpp-httplib/tree/v0.28.0","https://github.com/JuliaStrings/utf8proc/tree/v2.10.0","https://github.com/okdshin/PicoSHA2/tree/161cb3fc4170fa7a3eca9e582cebd27cc4d1fe29","https://github.com/facebook/zstd/releases/download/v1.5.7/zstd-1.5.7.tar.gz","https://curl.se/download/curl-8.16.0.tar.xz","https://github.com/MrMybal/CyDoc"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0fa8e395427a"},{"id":"limits","title":"Limites actuelles et travail prévu","description":"Limites actuelles et travail prévu","section":"Syrnck","order":900,"sourceRevision":"21","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"workspace\">Workspace et versionnage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/limits/workspace\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Workspace et versionnage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">HEAD nommé et détaché\u003c/a> sont pris en charge. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces natifs liés\u003c/a> conservent des HEAD/index séparés et des snapshots portables pour les sauvegardes. Matérialisation partielle, changements de sélection récupérables, reset hard et stash à sélection identique sont disponibles. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives\">désenregistrement archivé et la restauration\u003c/a> gardent travail modifié capturé, arbres HEAD/index complets et racines du contexte. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation\">déplacement lié\u003c/a> conserve le dossier entier sur un même système de fichiers, avec reprise/abort et changements de casse ; déplacements entre systèmes et du principal/Store restent à compléter. L’index de travail persistant et l’observateur évitent certains recalculs de hash, sans produire un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers ni un historique complet d’événements. Les notifications OS peuvent déborder ; réanalyses prudentes et polling restent nécessaires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les renommages de contenu exact sont reconnus, mais renommages avec édition/copies, intégration aux merges/rebases et synthèse récursive des bases multiples restent incomplets. Les révisions anciennes v1 n’ont ni auteur ni signature. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">auteurs v2 facultatifs\u003c/a> enregistrent auteurs/committers et signatures Ed25519 ; les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-signatures\">politiques serveur\u003c/a> peuvent autoriser clés et identités. Enregistrement manuel et redémarrage après changement de politique ; coffres système et révocation distribuée restent incomplets. Respecter les limites de chaque mode de fusion/rebase : la présence d’une commande ne promet pas toute la compatibilité Git.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sync\">Équipe et synchronisation continue\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/limits/sync\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Équipe et synchronisation continue\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les serveurs configurés fournissent utilisateurs/rôles, contrôles de branches et baux d’assets expirables. Transfert de l’historique natif et publication des catalogues Git restent distincts d’une transaction distribuée multi-serveur. L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent\">agent résident de publication\u003c/a> capture changements, suppressions et renommages exacts locaux, garde des snapshots hors ligne et reprend les publications natives protégées. Le mode bidirectionnel facultatif applique les descendants entrants par checkout récupérable et fusionne les historiques concurrents compatibles. Conflits non résolus, suppressions/reculs distants reconnus et réécritures locales sont bloqués. L’agent reprend uniquement ses transactions de checkout/fusion contrôlées, y compris hors ligne pour une cible signée préparée sans clé privée. La reprise manuelle ou étrangère reste explicite. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/conflict-copies\">politique explicite de copies de conflit\u003c/a> conserve les contenus concurrents ; les collisions de structure bloquent toujours. L’installation comme service système reste à terminer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’adaptateur Syncthing traite index/requêtes/réponses de blocs de fichiers ordinaires et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/device-connections\">connexions à appareils épinglés\u003c/a>. Un daemon amont réel vérifie les transferts différentiels dans les deux sens. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-catalog\">catalogue natif de dossier\u003c/a> conserve générations d’index, vecteurs, tombstones et fichiers vérifiés ; les conflits restent explicites et l’historique retenu. La reconnexion après renommage hors ligne est testée contre le daemon réel. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller\">contrôleur continu\u003c/a> applique maintenant fichiers ordinaires/tombstones aux workspaces natifs, avec reprise à propriétaire et tests de reconnexion au daemon réel. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">copies de versions concurrentes\u003c/a> réunissent explicitement les vecteurs des fichiers ordinaires et gardent les contenus perdants distincts. Métadonnées de dossiers/liens, installation du service OS, découverte, interface d’appairage, gestion des membres du catalogue et retrait automatique d’historique restent à réaliser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security\">Sécurité, rétention et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/limits/security\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sécurité, rétention et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les objets peuvent être chiffrés au repos, avec clés indépendantes pour sauvegardes/restaurations. Les serveurs de confiance reçoivent les objets en clair dans le transport sécurisé. Chiffrement de bout en bout, dérivation de mot de passe, coffres système, rotation/révocation distribuée, réparation automatique de répliques et GC distribué restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La rétention est explicite. Les archives conservent les versions tant que leurs racines restent présentes ; une sauvegarde vérifiée peut protéger le retrait manuel, sans politique automatique par âge/release. Arrêt de processus et panne d’alimentation sont des garanties différentes. Aucune opération n’établit une sûreté universelle après coupure électrique, sur stockage réseau ou face à un écrivain externe hostile.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">préservation explicite des éditions actives\u003c/a>\narchive les contenus ordinaires avant copie de conflit et reprend le journal\nstrict à propriétaire. Intentions de suppression/fichiers manquants et structures\nambiguës restent à résoudre. Les groupes d’archives d’édition demandent encore\nune gestion manuelle ; leur retrait automatique selon âge/sauvegarde reste à finir.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Workspace et versionnage # HEAD nommé et détaché sont pris en charge. Les workspaces natifs liés conservent des HEAD/index séparés et des snapshots portables pour les sauvegardes. Matérialisation partielle, changements de sélection récupérables, reset hard et stash à sélection identique sont disponibles. Le désenregistrement archivé et la restauration gardent travail modifié capturé, arbres HEAD/index complets et racines du contexte. Le déplacement lié conserve le dossier entier sur un même système de fichiers, avec reprise/abort et changements de casse ; déplacements entre systèmes et du principal/Store restent à compléter. L’index de travail persistant et l’observateur évitent certains recalculs de hash, sans produire un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers ni un historique complet d’événements. Les notifications OS peuvent déborder ; réanalyses prudentes et polling restent nécessaires. Les renommages de contenu exact sont reconnus, mais renommages avec édition/copies, intégration aux merges/rebases et synthèse récursive des bases multiples restent incomplets. Les révisions anciennes v1 n’ont ni auteur ni signature. Les auteurs v2 facultatifs enregistrent auteurs/committers et signatures Ed25519 ; les politiques serveur peuvent autoriser clés et identités. Enregistrement manuel et redémarrage après changement de politique ; coffres système et révocation distribuée restent incomplets. Respecter les limites de chaque mode de fusion/rebase : la présence d’une commande ne promet pas toute la compatibilité Git. Équipe et synchronisation continue # Les serveurs configurés fournissent utilisateurs/rôles, contrôles de branches et baux d’assets expirables. Transfert de l’historique natif et publication des catalogues Git restent distincts d’une transaction distribuée multi-serveur. L’ agent résident de publication capture changements, suppressions et renommages exacts locaux, garde des snapshots hors ligne et reprend les publications natives protégées. Le mode bidirectionnel facultatif applique les descendants entrants par checkout récupérable et fusionne les historiques concurrents compatibles. Conflits non résolus, suppressions/reculs distants reconnus et réécritures locales sont bloqués. L’agent reprend uniquement ses transactions de checkout/fusion contrôlées, y compris hors ligne pour une cible signée préparée sans clé privée. La reprise manuelle ou étrangère reste explicite. La politique explicite de copies de conflit conserve les contenus concurrents ; les collisions de structure bloquent toujours. L’installation comme service système reste à terminer. L’adaptateur Syncthing traite index/requêtes/réponses de blocs de fichiers ordinaires et connexions à appareils épinglés . Un daemon amont réel vérifie les transferts différentiels dans les deux sens. Le catalogue natif de dossier conserve générations d’index, vecteurs, tombstones et fichiers vérifiés ; les conflits restent explicites et l’historique retenu. La reconnexion après renommage hors ligne est testée contre le daemon réel. Le contrôleur continu applique maintenant fichiers ordinaires/tombstones aux workspaces natifs, avec reprise à propriétaire et tests de reconnexion au daemon réel. Les copies de versions concurrentes réunissent explicitement les vecteurs des fichiers ordinaires et gardent les contenus perdants distincts. Métadonnées de dossiers/liens, installation du service OS, découverte, interface d’appairage, gestion des membres du catalogue et retrait automatique d’historique restent à réaliser. Sécurité, rétention et récupération # Les objets peuvent être chiffrés au repos, avec clés indépendantes pour sauvegardes/restaurations. Les serveurs de confiance reçoivent les objets en clair dans le transport sécurisé. Chiffrement de bout en bout, dérivation de mot de passe, coffres système, rotation/révocation distribuée, réparation automatique de répliques et GC distribué restent à développer. La rétention est explicite. Les archives conservent les versions tant que leurs racines restent présentes ; une sauvegarde vérifiée peut protéger le retrait manuel, sans politique automatique par âge/release. Arrêt de processus et panne d’alimentation sont des garanties différentes. Aucune opération n’établit une sûreté universelle après coupure électrique, sur stockage réseau ou face à un écrivain externe hostile. La préservation explicite des éditions actives archive les contenus ordinaires avant copie de conflit et reprend le journal strict à propriétaire. Intentions de suppression/fichiers manquants et structures ambiguës restent à résoudre. Les groupes d’archives d’édition demandent encore une gestion manuelle ; leur retrait automatique selon âge/sauvegarde reste à finir.","headings":[{"id":"workspace","text":"Workspace et versionnage","level":2},{"id":"sync","text":"Équipe et synchronisation continue","level":2},{"id":"security","text":"Sécurité, rétention et récupération","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-detached","#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/native-workspace-archives","#/fr/native-workspace-relocation","#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/server-signatures","#/fr/resident-agent","#/fr/resident-agent/conflict-copies","#/fr/syncthing/device-connections","#/fr/syncthing/folder-catalog","#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller","#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts","#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits"],"kind":"guide","revision":"81ca0fea1036"},{"id":"localization","title":"Langues et messages","description":"Guide : Langues et messages.","section":"Premiers pas","order":170,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"language-and-localization-policy\">Langue et localisation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/localization/language-and-localization-policy\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Langue et localisation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>L’anglais est la langue source et la langue d’interface par défaut de Syrnck ; le français est intégré. La sélection se fait par session avec repli anglais.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"command-line-selection\">Sélection en ligne de commande\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/localization/command-line-selection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélection en ligne de commande\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--lang\u003c/code> prime sur \u003ccode>SYRNCK_LANG\u003c/code>. Sans les deux, l’anglais s’applique indépendamment de \u003ccode>LANG\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>LC_ALL\u003c/code> ou de la région Windows. \u003ccode>en\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>fr\u003c/code> acceptent aussi \u003ccode>en-US\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>fr_FR\u003c/code>. Une valeur explicite non reconnue échoue. La sortie est UTF-8.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Aide, états, bannière d’écoute et diagnostics appartenant à Syrnck sont traduits. Le catalogue compilé ne demande aucun fichier de langue installé et ne modifie ni langue globale mutable ni locale du processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --help\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --lang fr --help\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --lang en --store data fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"library-and-protocol-boundaries\">Frontières bibliothèque et protocole\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/localization/library-and-protocol-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Frontières bibliothèque et protocole\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le cœur conserve les exceptions canoniques anglaises. Chaque appelant utilise \u003ccode>localize\u003c/code> pour l’affichage, avec \u003ccode>Language::english\u003c/code> par défaut ; plusieurs langues peuvent coexister. Les messages inconnus du système, de la bibliothèque standard ou des dépendances gardent leur langue d’origine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Octets stockés, chemins, messages utilisateur, commandes/options, références, clés JSON, types, IDs, hashes, pointeurs Git et champs de protocole restent stables. Les diagnostics C/Go et HTTP sont canoniques anglais ; la présentation de l’application appelante choisit sa langue. Cela préserve automatisation, hashes et interopérabilité.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/i18n.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> language \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse_language\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fr\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> display \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">localize\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"object hash mismatch\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> language\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"maintaining-translations\">Langues du manuel\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/localization/maintaining-translations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Langues du manuel\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le manuel local possède deux éditions complètes avec les mêmes IDs de pages et d’ancres. L’aide contextuelle reprend la langue du CLI ; le lecteur peut ensuite la changer indépendamment. Une route indisponible revient à l’anglais. Chaque traduction indique la révision anglaise couverte. Les instructions de maintenance sont conservées hors de ce manuel.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Langue et localisation # L’anglais est la langue source et la langue d’interface par défaut de Syrnck ; le français est intégré. La sélection se fait par session avec repli anglais. Sélection en ligne de commande # --lang prime sur SYRNCK_LANG . Sans les deux, l’anglais s’applique indépendamment de LANG , LC_ALL ou de la région Windows. en et fr acceptent aussi en-US et fr_FR . Une valeur explicite non reconnue échoue. La sortie est UTF-8. Aide, états, bannière d’écoute et diagnostics appartenant à Syrnck sont traduits. Le catalogue compilé ne demande aucun fichier de langue installé et ne modifie ni langue globale mutable ni locale du processus. text Copier syrnck --help syrnck --lang fr --help syrnck --lang en --store data fsck Frontières bibliothèque et protocole # Le cœur conserve les exceptions canoniques anglaises. Chaque appelant utilise localize pour l’affichage, avec Language::english par défaut ; plusieurs langues peuvent coexister. Les messages inconnus du système, de la bibliothèque standard ou des dépendances gardent leur langue d’origine. Octets stockés, chemins, messages utilisateur, commandes/options, références, clés JSON, types, IDs, hashes, pointeurs Git et champs de protocole restent stables. Les diagnostics C/Go et HTTP sont canoniques anglais ; la présentation de l’application appelante choisit sa langue. Cela préserve automatisation, hashes et interopérabilité. cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp> auto language = syrnck :: parse_language ( \"fr\" ) ; auto display = syrnck :: localize ( \"object hash mismatch\" , language ) ; Langues du manuel # Le manuel local possède deux éditions complètes avec les mêmes IDs de pages et d’ancres. L’aide contextuelle reprend la langue du CLI ; le lecteur peut ensuite la changer indépendamment. Une route indisponible revient à l’anglais. Chaque traduction indique la révision anglaise couverte. Les instructions de maintenance sont conservées hors de ce manuel. syrnck --help syrnck --lang fr --help syrnck --lang en --store data fsck #include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp> auto language = syrnck::parse_language(\"fr\"); auto display = syrnck::localize(\"object hash mismatch\", language);","headings":[{"id":"language-and-localization-policy","text":"Langue et localisation","level":1},{"id":"command-line-selection","text":"Sélection en ligne de commande","level":2},{"id":"library-and-protocol-boundaries","text":"Frontières bibliothèque et protocole","level":2},{"id":"maintaining-translations","text":"Langues du manuel","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck --help\nsyrnck --lang fr --help\nsyrnck --lang en --store data fsck\n#include \u003csyrnck/i18n.hpp>\n\nauto language = syrnck::parse_language(\"fr\");\nauto display = syrnck::localize(\"object hash mismatch\", language);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"d63eb3328e9c"},{"id":"maintenance","title":"Maintenance et annulation","description":"Guide : Maintenance et annulation.","section":"Stockage","order":180,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"maintenance-progress-and-cancellation\">Progression et annulation de la maintenance\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/maintenance/maintenance-progress-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Progression et annulation de la maintenance\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>gc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> acceptent \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> sur stderr et l’annulation coopérative par Ctrl+C. Le rapport final JSON sur stdout reste identique. En C++, utiliser \u003ccode>Store::gc(remove, OperationOptions)\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>Store::pack(PackOptions, OperationOptions)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La préparation vérifie l’annulation pendant la validation des fichiers conservés, la planification de l’inventaire, le tri des clés isolées, l’écriture des packs et leur relecture. Une exception de l’observateur interrompt aussi cette étape. Catalogue, références et fichiers source existants restent intacts ; les temporaires sont retirés au déroulement coopératif. Des packs immuables complets mais inactifs peuvent rester : la prochaine maintenance réussie nettoie leurs fichiers générés. L’ouverture seule ne les active pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La portée des compteurs dépend de la phase : \u003ccode>verifying\u003c/code> compte les lectures de payloads décodés ; \u003ccode>planning\u003c/code>, les octets d’enveloppes physiques examinés ; \u003ccode>packing\u003c/code>, les enveloppes vérifiées fournies au writer. Ce dernier n’est pas un compteur d’écritures durables et exclut en-têtes et index. Le total d’empaquetage devient connu après le plan. Le GC peut alterner vérification et empaquetage pour chaque pack partiellement vivant. \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> est le dernier callback et contrôle d’annulation avant engagement. Une phase terminée indique la fin de sa préparation ; seul le retour réussi confirme l’opération entière.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après publication du catalogue, le nettoyage des objets isolés et packs obsolètes se termine sans nouveau callback ni contrôle d’annulation. Sans changement de catalogue, cette frontière précède la première suppression. Elle évite de laisser des métadonnées mortes isolées dont les dépendances empaquetées auraient déjà été retirées. Une annulation tardive peut donc finir avec le code 0 et une période sans progression visible. Le GC en simulation reste annulable tout au long du travail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Erreurs IO et arrêt forcé peuvent interrompre ce nettoyage engagé. Appliquer la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery\">procédure de récupération\u003c/a> : les racines conservées restent complètes et un GC interrompu connu peut devoir terminer son nettoyage avant l’audit de toutes les métadonnées inaccessibles. Formats, versions physiques, règles de rétention et verrouillage exclusif ne changent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un appel IO, codec ou contrôle d’index déjà lancé peut se terminer avant le prochain contrôle d’arrêt. Le GC garde un bit par objet empaqueté, l’empaquetage trie les clés isolées et les métadonnées/parcours gardent leurs allocations distinctes. Progression et annulation ajoutent des compteurs/callbacks de taille constante, sans second inventaire complet.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Progression et annulation de la maintenance # gc , gc --delete , pack et pack --compact acceptent --progress et --progress-json sur stderr et l’annulation coopérative par Ctrl+C. Le rapport final JSON sur stdout reste identique. En C++, utiliser Store::gc(remove, OperationOptions) ou Store::pack(PackOptions, OperationOptions) . La préparation vérifie l’annulation pendant la validation des fichiers conservés, la planification de l’inventaire, le tri des clés isolées, l’écriture des packs et leur relecture. Une exception de l’observateur interrompt aussi cette étape. Catalogue, références et fichiers source existants restent intacts ; les temporaires sont retirés au déroulement coopératif. Des packs immuables complets mais inactifs peuvent rester : la prochaine maintenance réussie nettoie leurs fichiers générés. L’ouverture seule ne les active pas. La portée des compteurs dépend de la phase : verifying compte les lectures de payloads décodés ; planning , les octets d’enveloppes physiques examinés ; packing , les enveloppes vérifiées fournies au writer. Ce dernier n’est pas un compteur d’écritures durables et exclut en-têtes et index. Le total d’empaquetage devient connu après le plan. Le GC peut alterner vérification et empaquetage pour chaque pack partiellement vivant. publishing est le dernier callback et contrôle d’annulation avant engagement. Une phase terminée indique la fin de sa préparation ; seul le retour réussi confirme l’opération entière. Après publication du catalogue, le nettoyage des objets isolés et packs obsolètes se termine sans nouveau callback ni contrôle d’annulation. Sans changement de catalogue, cette frontière précède la première suppression. Elle évite de laisser des métadonnées mortes isolées dont les dépendances empaquetées auraient déjà été retirées. Une annulation tardive peut donc finir avec le code 0 et une période sans progression visible. Le GC en simulation reste annulable tout au long du travail. Erreurs IO et arrêt forcé peuvent interrompre ce nettoyage engagé. Appliquer la procédure de récupération : les racines conservées restent complètes et un GC interrompu connu peut devoir terminer son nettoyage avant l’audit de toutes les métadonnées inaccessibles. Formats, versions physiques, règles de rétention et verrouillage exclusif ne changent pas. Un appel IO, codec ou contrôle d’index déjà lancé peut se terminer avant le prochain contrôle d’arrêt. Le GC garde un bit par objet empaqueté, l’empaquetage trie les clés isolées et les métadonnées/parcours gardent leurs allocations distinctes. Progression et annulation ajoutent des compteurs/callbacks de taille constante, sans second inventaire complet.","headings":[{"id":"maintenance-progress-and-cancellation","text":"Progression et annulation de la maintenance","level":1}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/crash-recovery"],"kind":"guide","revision":"09ad1c7463df"},{"id":"native-amend","title":"Modification du dernier commit","description":"Guide : Modification du dernier commit.","section":"Versionnage","order":190,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-commit-amendment\">Modifier le dernier commit natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-amend/native-commit-amendment\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Modifier le dernier commit natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Amend remplace la tête d’une branche en conservant sa liste ordonnée de parents, y compris tous les parents d’une fusion. L’ancienne révision demeure immuable sous \u003ccode>archive/amend/OLD_ID\u003c/code>. Déplacement de branche, archive et consommation éventuelle de l’index sont publiés atomiquement. Les tags existants gardent leur cible initiale.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli\">Commandes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-amend/cli\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Commandes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Tous les modes exigent \u003ccode>-m\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>--message\u003c/code>. La branche sélectionnée est utilisée par défaut ; \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code> la remplace sans modifier HEAD symbolique. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> est l’ID exact de l’ancienne révision, 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules, jamais un sélecteur. Sans cette option, le CLI capture la tête sous verrou exclusif. Il faut une tête existante ; une branche initiale vide ne peut pas être modifiée. \u003ccode>--message-only\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> nécessitent \u003ccode>--amend\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le mode ordinaire réingère le dossier complet avec les exclusions/préférences habituelles et refuse tout index. \u003ccode>--staged\u003c/code> utilise exactement l’index immuable, qui doit exister avec la tête actuelle pour base ; il est consommé atomiquement et aucun fichier de travail n’est lu. \u003ccode>--message-only\u003c/code> reprend toutes les entrées de la tête sans lire le workspace et refuse un index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un index sans changement est accepté pour corriger un message. Modes staged/message-only sont exclusifs et refusent les arguments de dossier/pipeline. Le mode dossier accepte \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> et contrôle la liaison au workspace. Tous refusent une récupération checkout/switch en cours. Un index périmé exige inspection et reset explicites.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La nouvelle révision reçoit le message et la date UTC courante en secondes. Avec une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">identité native\u003c/a>, la v2 préserve l’auteur d’origine et enregistre le committer actuel avec une nouvelle signature facultative. Réécrire une v2 exige une identité ; l’ancienne signature n’est jamais réutilisée. Sans identité, la v1 reste inchangée. Si toute la sérialisation est identique, l’ID existant peut être rendu ; aucun nonce ne force un nouvel ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\"># Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\"># Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native add Content/Maps Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\"># Reuse every existing file entry and change the message.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\"># Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"retention-interruption-and-restoration\">Rétention, interruption et restauration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-amend/retention-interruption-and-restoration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention, interruption et restauration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Avant publication, les fermetures complètes du remplacement et de l’archive sont vérifiées, hashes de fichiers compris. Un ancien bloc absent bloque même un remplacement qui n’en a plus besoin. Les valeurs attendues branche/index/archive sont contrôlées ensemble. Une archive déjà présente n’est réutilisable que si elle pointe vers la même ancienne révision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Annulation, erreur de callback ou entrée invalide avant publication conservent les anciennes références. La préparation peut laisser des objets immuables non référencés, réutilisables ou collectables. Une coupure pendant publication laisse l’ensemble ancien ou nouveau complet. Après engagement, la publication peut terminer malgré l’annulation ; aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt n’intervient après succès.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après interruption, inspecter \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native staged\u003c/code>. Si l’ancienne tête demeure, réessayer avec son ID attendu exact. Si nouvelle tête et archive sont présentes, la publication est terminée. Un nouvel essai avec l’ancien ID refuse alors de modifier la nouvelle tête une seconde fois.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’archive protège l’ancienne révision, ses ancêtres et tous ses blocs sans expiration automatique. Exporter son ID ou créer une branche de récupération, puis suivre les règles de checkout/switch pour revenir dans le workspace. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait protégé par sauvegarde\u003c/a> permet un nettoyage explicite. Retirer la dernière racine rend les données exclusives collectables ; la rétention locale n’est pas une frontière d’autorisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">API C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-amend/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque API exige l’ID exact attendu. La surcharge dossier accepte \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code>, avec préférences du dépôt par défaut ; staged/message acceptent \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>. Les callbacks ne doivent pas réentrer dans le Store. Le choix de snapshot peut être fondé sur les métadonnées, mais la publication vérifie toujours les payloads complets. Progression anglaise/française et Ctrl+C coopératif sont disponibles ; JSON et IDs restent indépendants de la langue. Ces opérations d’historique ne sont pas encore exposées en C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> expected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resolve_native_revision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"heads/main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> corrected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Corrected snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> prepared \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> corrected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Selected correction\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> renamed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">amend_message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> prepared\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Corrected message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Modifier le dernier commit natif # Amend remplace la tête d’une branche en conservant sa liste ordonnée de parents, y compris tous les parents d’une fusion. L’ancienne révision demeure immuable sous archive/amend/OLD_ID . Déplacement de branche, archive et consommation éventuelle de l’index sont publiés atomiquement. Les tags existants gardent leur cible initiale. Commandes # Tous les modes exigent -m / --message . La branche sélectionnée est utilisée par défaut ; --branch NAME la remplace sans modifier HEAD symbolique. --expect est l’ID exact de l’ancienne révision, 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules, jamais un sélecteur. Sans cette option, le CLI capture la tête sous verrou exclusif. Il faut une tête existante ; une branche initiale vide ne peut pas être modifiée. --message-only et --expect nécessitent --amend . Le mode ordinaire réingère le dossier complet avec les exclusions/préférences habituelles et refuse tout index. --staged utilise exactement l’index immuable, qui doit exister avec la tête actuelle pour base ; il est consommé atomiquement et aucun fichier de travail n’est lu. --message-only reprend toutes les entrées de la tête sans lire le workspace et refuse un index. Un index sans changement est accepté pour corriger un message. Modes staged/message-only sont exclusifs et refusent les arguments de dossier/pipeline. Le mode dossier accepte --directory , --workers , --buffer-mib et contrôle la liaison au workspace. Tous refusent une récupération checkout/switch en cours. Un index périmé exige inspection et reset explicites. La nouvelle révision reçoit le message et la date UTC courante en secondes. Avec une identité native , la v2 préserve l’auteur d’origine et enregistre le committer actuel avec une nouvelle signature facultative. Réécrire une v2 exige une identité ; l’ancienne signature n’est jamais réutilisée. Sans identité, la v1 reste inchangée. Si toute la sérialisation est identique, l’ID existant peut être rendu ; aucun nonce ne force un nouvel ID. text Copier # Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory. syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\" # Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone. syrnck native add Content/Maps Source syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\" # Reuse every existing file entry and change the message. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\" # Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\" Rétention, interruption et restauration # Avant publication, les fermetures complètes du remplacement et de l’archive sont vérifiées, hashes de fichiers compris. Un ancien bloc absent bloque même un remplacement qui n’en a plus besoin. Les valeurs attendues branche/index/archive sont contrôlées ensemble. Une archive déjà présente n’est réutilisable que si elle pointe vers la même ancienne révision. Annulation, erreur de callback ou entrée invalide avant publication conservent les anciennes références. La préparation peut laisser des objets immuables non référencés, réutilisables ou collectables. Une coupure pendant publication laisse l’ensemble ancien ou nouveau complet. Après engagement, la publication peut terminer malgré l’annulation ; aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt n’intervient après succès. Après interruption, inspecter native head , refs , native staged . Si l’ancienne tête demeure, réessayer avec son ID attendu exact. Si nouvelle tête et archive sont présentes, la publication est terminée. Un nouvel essai avec l’ancien ID refuse alors de modifier la nouvelle tête une seconde fois. L’archive protège l’ancienne révision, ses ancêtres et tous ses blocs sans expiration automatique. Exporter son ID ou créer une branche de récupération, puis suivre les règles de checkout/switch pour revenir dans le workspace. Le retrait protégé par sauvegarde permet un nettoyage explicite. Retirer la dernière racine rend les données exclusives collectables ; la rétention locale n’est pas une frontière d’autorisation. text Copier syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID API C++ # Chaque API exige l’ID exact attendu. La surcharge dossier accepte IngestOptions , avec préférences du dépôt par défaut ; staged/message acceptent OperationOptions . Les callbacks ne doivent pas réentrer dans le Store. Le choix de snapshot peut être fondé sur les métadonnées, mais la publication vérifie toujours les payloads complets. Progression anglaise/française et Ctrl+C coopératif sont disponibles ; JSON et IDs restent indépendants de la langue. Ces opérations d’historique ne sont pas encore exposées en C/Go. cpp Copier auto expected = store . resolve_native_revision ( \"heads/main\" ) ; auto corrected = store . amend_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected , \"Corrected snapshot\" ) ; auto prepared = store . amend_staged ( \"main\" , corrected , \"Selected correction\" , operation ) ; auto renamed = store . amend_message ( \"main\" , prepared , \"Corrected message\" , operation ) ; # Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory. syrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\" # Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone. syrnck native add Content/Maps Source syrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\" # Reuse every existing file entry and change the message. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\" # Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected. syrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\" syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID auto expected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"heads/main\"); auto corrected = store.amend_directory(project, \"main\", expected, \"Corrected snapshot\"); auto prepared = store.amend_staged(\"main\", corrected, \"Selected correction\", operation); auto renamed = store.amend_message(\"main\", prepared, \"Corrected message\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-commit-amendment","text":"Modifier le dernier commit natif","level":1},{"id":"cli","text":"Commandes","level":2},{"id":"retention-interruption-and-restoration","text":"Rétention, interruption et restauration","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"API C++","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"# Replace the tip's snapshot with the current selected directory.\nsyrnck native commit --amend -m \"Corrected project snapshot\"\n\n# Use exactly the prepared bytes, leaving later working edits alone.\nsyrnck native add Content/Maps Source\nsyrnck native commit --amend --staged -m \"Corrected selected snapshot\"\n\n# Reuse every existing file entry and change the message.\nsyrnck native commit --amend --message-only -m \"Corrected message\"\n\n# Guard scripts against amending a different tip from the one inspected.\nsyrnck native commit --amend --message-only --expect OLD_ID -m \"Correction\"\nsyrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\nauto expected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"heads/main\");\nauto corrected = store.amend_directory(project, \"main\", expected, \"Corrected snapshot\");\nauto prepared = store.amend_staged(\"main\", corrected, \"Selected correction\", operation);\nauto renamed = store.amend_message(\"main\", prepared, \"Corrected message\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/archive-retention"],"kind":"guide","revision":"de433fece27a"},{"id":"native-archives","title":"Archives de l’historique natif","description":"Guide : Archives de l’historique natif.","section":"Versionnage","order":200,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-history-archives\">Archives de l’historique natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-archives/native-history-archives\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Archives de l’historique natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les mutations d’historique conservent anciennes révisions et préparations comme racines ordinaires du GC. L’inventaire les consulte sans modifier références, objets ou fichiers de travail, y compris amend, checkout et détachement d’historique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>reset\u003c/code> conserve l’ancienne tête ; \u003ccode>reset-index\u003c/code> l’ancien index et sa base ; \u003ccode>rebase-index\u003c/code> l’index avant son rejeu ; \u003ccode>stage-index\u003c/code> l’index avant sélection de hunks/lignes ou patch externe ; \u003ccode>squash\u003c/code> toute la chaîne d’origine ; \u003ccode>amend\u003c/code> l’ancienne tête et ses parents ; \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code> la source du checkout terminé ; \u003ccode>detached\u003c/code> la tête d’origine sous \u003ccode>archive/OLD_ID\u003c/code>, après détachement d’historique ou remplacement d’une ligne HEAD détachée inactive. Pour les autres catégories actuellement reconnues, consulter l’inventaire et ses filtres.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les groupes de tags relèvent de \u003ccode>native tag-archives\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>tag-archive\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>tag-restore\u003c/code> ; ceux de branches de \u003ccode>native branch-archives\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>branch-archive\u003c/code>. Ils ne figurent pas dans cet inventaire simple. Les namespaces inconnus/personnalisés sont exclus ; \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> reste l’inventaire générique complet. Les noms reconnus, types révision et correspondances d’ID sont contrôlés. Un nom de checkout désigne une transaction plutôt que sa révision cible. Une archive reconnue corrompue provoque une erreur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"pages-and-verification\">Pagination et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-archives/pages-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Pagination et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--kind\u003c/code> filtre une catégorie ; \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> accepte 1..1000, avec 100 par défaut. Ordre : nom complet de référence. \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> est le curseur exclusif lorsque \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code> vaut true, sinon null. Il reste utilisable après retrait de sa référence, mais sa catégorie doit correspondre à un filtre explicite. Chaque page reflète les références actuelles ; aucune vue persistante ne fige plusieurs appels.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque ligne contient \u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kind\u003c/code>, l’ID exact \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, nombres de fichiers/parents, date de révision et aperçu du message limité à 256 octets UTF-8 sans couper un caractère. \u003ccode>message_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>message_truncated\u003c/code> décrivent le message complet. La date est celle de la révision, pas celle de l’archivage. \u003ccode>archive-show\u003c/code> rend la même ligne pour une référence ; \u003ccode>inspect revision ID\u003c/code> fournit toutes les métadonnées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Par défaut, seules références et révisions sont lues ; les blocs peuvent manquer. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audite la fermeture complète et les hashes de fichiers des lignes affichées uniquement. Les octets conservés absents/corrompus font échouer la requête. Progression/annulation et diagnostics bilingues sont disponibles, JSON anglais et callbacks non réentrants.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sortie bornée à 16 Mio. La table des références est encore chargée intégralement ; la pagination borne lignes et lectures de révisions, pas sa mémoire. Les stockages en clair/chiffrés, isolés/empaquetés utilisent les mêmes racines, sans changement de format ou politique.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovering-stored-versions\">Récupérer les versions conservées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-archives/recovering-stored-versions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Récupérer les versions conservées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Exporter l’ID \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> d’une ligne ou créer une branche de récupération. Les règles de propreté et de récupération de checkout/switch s’appliquent ensuite. Un index archivé s’exporte aussi pour retrouver ses octets figés ; l’inventaire ne restaure pas automatiquement un couple branche/index et ne change pas les modifications de travail. Les fichiers jamais importés sont hors rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les archives n’expirent pas automatiquement. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait protégé par sauvegarde\u003c/a> gère leur nettoyage explicite ; retirer la dernière racine puis lancer un GC destructif peut supprimer leurs objets exclusifs. L’inventaire seul ne promet aucun gain disque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>API disponibles en C++ et CLI, pas encore dans l’historique C/Go. Les index remplacés par sélection textuelle gardent leur fermeture et leur base sous \u003ccode>archive/stage-index/ID\u003c/code>, filtrables avec \u003ccode>--kind stage-index\u003c/code>. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks\">sélection de hunks\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native switch recovered\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_archives\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reset-index\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archive \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive/reset/\"\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">+\u003c/span> old_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Archives de l’historique natif # Les mutations d’historique conservent anciennes révisions et préparations comme racines ordinaires du GC. L’inventaire les consulte sans modifier références, objets ou fichiers de travail, y compris amend, checkout et détachement d’historique. reset conserve l’ancienne tête ; reset-index l’ancien index et sa base ; rebase-index l’index avant son rejeu ; stage-index l’index avant sélection de hunks/lignes ou patch externe ; squash toute la chaîne d’origine ; amend l’ancienne tête et ses parents ; checkout la source du checkout terminé ; detached la tête d’origine sous archive/OLD_ID , après détachement d’historique ou remplacement d’une ligne HEAD détachée inactive. Pour les autres catégories actuellement reconnues, consulter l’inventaire et ses filtres. Les groupes de tags relèvent de native tag-archives / tag-archive / tag-restore ; ceux de branches de native branch-archives / branch-archive . Ils ne figurent pas dans cet inventaire simple. Les namespaces inconnus/personnalisés sont exclus ; refs reste l’inventaire générique complet. Les noms reconnus, types révision et correspondances d’ID sont contrôlés. Un nom de checkout désigne une transaction plutôt que sa révision cible. Une archive reconnue corrompue provoque une erreur. text Copier syrnck native archives --limit 100 syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100 syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify Pagination et vérification # --kind filtre une catégorie ; --limit accepte 1..1000, avec 100 par défaut. Ordre : nom complet de référence. next_after est le curseur exclusif lorsque has_more vaut true, sinon null. Il reste utilisable après retrait de sa référence, mais sa catégorie doit correspondre à un filtre explicite. Chaque page reflète les références actuelles ; aucune vue persistante ne fige plusieurs appels. Chaque ligne contient reference , kind , l’ID exact revision , nombres de fichiers/parents, date de révision et aperçu du message limité à 256 octets UTF-8 sans couper un caractère. message_bytes et message_truncated décrivent le message complet. La date est celle de la révision, pas celle de l’archivage. archive-show rend la même ligne pour une référence ; inspect revision ID fournit toutes les métadonnées. Par défaut, seules références et révisions sont lues ; les blocs peuvent manquer. --verify audite la fermeture complète et les hashes de fichiers des lignes affichées uniquement. Les octets conservés absents/corrompus font échouer la requête. Progression/annulation et diagnostics bilingues sont disponibles, JSON anglais et callbacks non réentrants. Sortie bornée à 16 Mio. La table des références est encore chargée intégralement ; la pagination borne lignes et lectures de révisions, pas sa mémoire. Les stockages en clair/chiffrés, isolés/empaquetés utilisent les mêmes racines, sans changement de format ou politique. Récupérer les versions conservées # Exporter l’ID revision d’une ligne ou créer une branche de récupération. Les règles de propreté et de récupération de checkout/switch s’appliquent ensuite. Un index archivé s’exporte aussi pour retrouver ses octets figés ; l’inventaire ne restaure pas automatiquement un couple branche/index et ne change pas les modifications de travail. Les fichiers jamais importés sont hors rétention. Les archives n’expirent pas automatiquement. Le retrait protégé par sauvegarde gère leur nettoyage explicite ; retirer la dernière racine puis lancer un GC destructif peut supprimer leurs objets exclusifs. L’inventaire seul ne promet aucun gain disque. API disponibles en C++ et CLI, pas encore dans l’historique C/Go. Les index remplacés par sélection textuelle gardent leur fermeture et leur base sous archive/stage-index/ID , filtrables avec --kind stage-index . Voir la sélection de hunks . text Copier syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify syrnck native switch recovered cpp Copier auto page = store . native_archives ( \"reset-index\" , { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto archive = store . native_archive ( \"archive/reset/\" + old_id , true , operation ) ; syrnck native archives --limit 100 syrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify syrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100 syrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify syrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID syrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify syrnck native switch recovered auto page = store.native_archives(\"reset-index\", {}, 100, true, operation); auto archive = store.native_archive(\"archive/reset/\" + old_id, true, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-history-archives","text":"Archives de l’historique natif","level":1},{"id":"pages-and-verification","text":"Pagination et vérification","level":2},{"id":"recovering-stored-versions","text":"Récupérer les versions conservées","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native archives --limit 100\nsyrnck native archives --kind reset-index --verify\nsyrnck native archives --after archive/reset/OLD_ID --limit 100\nsyrnck native archive-show archive/squash/OLD_ID --verify\nsyrnck native export REVISION_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered REVISION_ID\nsyrnck native switch-plan recovered --verify\nsyrnck native switch recovered\nauto page = store.native_archives(\"reset-index\", {}, 100, true, operation);\nauto archive = store.native_archive(\"archive/reset/\" + old_id, true, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-hunks"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f9067414ca40"},{"id":"native-authorship","title":"Auteurs natifs et signatures","description":"Auteur d’origine, committer actuel et signatures Ed25519 facultatives.","section":"Versionnage","order":305,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"identity\">Configurer une identité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/identity\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Configurer une identité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native log --json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native log --show-signature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native identity --clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Nom et adresse sont des textes UTF-8, limités à 256 et 320 octets, sans caractères\nde contrôle. Le fuseau compte les minutes à l’est d’UTC dans -1439..1439, avec 0\npar défaut. L’adresse est enregistrée littéralement sans prouver sa propriété.\nChamps JSON, IDs et empreintes de clés sont identiques dans les interfaces\nanglaise et française.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le fichier facultatif \u003ccode>native-identity.json\u003c/code> du Store, propre à cette machine,\ncontient uniquement version, type, nom, adresse et fuseau. Il s’applique à tous\nles workspaces utilisant ce Store. Les sauvegardes logiques ne le copient pas.\nEffacer le profil préserve révisions existantes et exigence de lecteur additive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La paire \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_NAME\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_EMAIL\u003c/code> remplace le profil pour une\ninvocation ; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_USER_TIMEZONE\u003c/code> fournit un décalage facultatif. Sans identité\nconfigurée, une nouvelle révision reste en v1, y compris après effacement\nexplicite du profil. Réécrire une révision v2 exige une identité actuelle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signing\">Signer sans enregistrer la clé privée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/signing\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Signer sans enregistrer la clé privée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La variable facultative \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SIGNING_SEED\u003c/code> fournit une graine Ed25519 de\n32 octets sous forme de 64 caractères hexadécimaux. Utiliser un environnement\nde processus contrôlé ; ne pas inscrire une vraie graine dans les arguments,\nscripts ou fichiers de profil. Le CLI consomme puis retire la variable de son\nenvironnement et efface ses propres tampons temporaires. Les autres commandes\nla retirent avant de lancer des outils. Cela ne promet pas d’effacer les copies\nantérieures du système ou de l’appelant. Sans graine, une identité crée une\nrévision v2 non signée, avec \u003ccode>signature: null\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Dans la bibliothèque C++, identité et clé sont explicitement liées au handle :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/core.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice@example.invalid\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">60\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                          syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">SigningKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">generate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> revision \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Add asset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">clear_native_identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le Store possède exclusivement la clé déplacée. La bibliothèque ne lit pas\nautomatiquement profil CLI ou environnement. \u003ccode>native_identity()\u003c/code> expose profil\net clé publics ; aucun export de clé privée.\n\u003ccode>take_native_identity()\u003c/code> détache un \u003ccode>NativeIdentityContext\u003c/code> déplaçable contenant\nprofil et éventuelle clé. Déplacer les deux vers le Store suivant avec\n\u003ccode>use_native_identity()\u003c/code> avant de libérer le contexte. Un processus résident peut\nainsi utiliser des handles verrouillés courts sans copier ni exporter la graine.\nL’ancien handle devient non configuré ; prendre un contexte vide renvoie null.\nChanger ou détacher l’identité dans un callback de progression est refusé. Voir la\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/signing-api\">primitive de signature\u003c/a> pour propriété des graines et vérification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rewrites\">Règles d’auteur et de committer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/rewrites\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Règles d’auteur et de committer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une révision v2 enregistre \u003ccode>author\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>committer\u003c/code> et une signature facultative.\nChaque identité datée contient \u003ccode>name,email,timestamp,timezone_offset\u003c/code> ; la date\ndu committer égale celle de la révision. Un auteur d’origine peut être null\nlors d’une réécriture d’histoire ancienne. Le committer actuel signe le résultat\ncomplet, fichiers et parents compris. Une ancienne signature n’est jamais copiée\nsur des métadonnées modifiées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Nouveaux commits de dossier/index, nouveaux merges, reverts et squashes autonomes\nutilisent la personne actuelle comme auteur et committer. Amend, cherry-pick,\nrebase linéaire et détachement d’histoire préservent l’auteur d’origine avec le\nnouveau committer. Squash/fixup du rebase gardent l’auteur du premier commit\nretenu. Résultat inchangé ou fast-forward : révision existante exacte conservée.\nLes snapshots internes d’index, stash et récupération restent v1 sans auteur inventé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le rebase fige une date de committer commune aux deux calculs de candidats.\nAvec une identité, \u003ccode>--committer-time SECONDS\u003c/code> sur rebase-plan/rebase-todo/rebase\nrend l’aperçu reproductible avec les mêmes identité, clé et entrées. Sans cette\noption, des invocations distinctes peuvent choisir des dates et IDs différents.\nLe rapport expose \u003ccode>committer_timestamp\u003c/code>. Une récupération checkout utilise les\ncandidats signés figés sans avoir besoin de la clé privée de signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native log --json\u003c/code>, historique de fichier, blame et résumés d’archives exposent\nauteur, committer et ID public de clé pour v2. Le log texte affiche l’auteur et\nle committer différent ; \u003ccode>--show-signature\u003c/code> ajoute l’ID ou l’état non signé.\nChamps des résumés anciens et parcours par premier parent restent compatibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format\">Octets signés et compatibilité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/format\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Octets signés et compatibilité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La révision v2 est un JSON compact canonique ordonné\n\u003ccode>version,parents,message,timestamp,author,committer,signature,files\u003c/code>.\nLa signature contient exactement \u003ccode>algorithm,key_id,public_key,value\u003c/code> :\n\u003ccode>ed25519\u003c/code>, empreinte, clé publique de 32 octets et signature de 64 octets ; les\nvaleurs binaires utilisent l’hexadécimal minuscule. Le message signé est l’ASCII\n\u003ccode>Syrnck native revision signature v1:\u003c/code> suivi des 64 caractères hexadécimaux\nminuscules du BLAKE3 de la révision v2 canonique avec \u003ccode>signature\u003c/code> à null.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Import, lecture et audit valident signature, empreinte et révision canonique.\nModifier auteur, parents, date, message ou fichiers invalide une signature.\n\u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code> est activé avant stockage d’un objet v2 ou d’un profil.\nLes anciens lecteurs refusent cette configuration étendue ; enveloppe, manifest,\npack, objets HTTP et ABI C restent inchangés. Sauvegardes/restaurations logiques\npréservent l’exigence. Révisions v1 et v2 peuvent coexister dans le même graphe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trust\">Confiance et limites restantes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/trust\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Confiance et limites restantes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une signature valide prouve la possession de sa clé pour ces octets. Nom,\nadresse et date restent des déclarations du signataire. L’accès serveur v1\nutilise principal authentifié, ACL de branches/chemins et verrous d’assets ;\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-signatures\">l’accès v2\u003c/a> peut aussi imposer des signatures approuvées\net lier clés/identités de committer aux comptes. Il vérifie la politique actuelle\npour l’histoire nouvellement admise tout en préservant la base déjà publiée.\nCoffre système de signature et révocation distribuée restent à développer. L’API d’auteur natif est actuellement\nC++/CLI ; import et reconstruction C/Go lisent les objets v2 validés sans graine.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Configurer une identité # text Copier syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60 syrnck native identity syrnck native log --json syrnck native log --show-signature syrnck native identity --clear Nom et adresse sont des textes UTF-8, limités à 256 et 320 octets, sans caractères de contrôle. Le fuseau compte les minutes à l’est d’UTC dans -1439..1439, avec 0 par défaut. L’adresse est enregistrée littéralement sans prouver sa propriété. Champs JSON, IDs et empreintes de clés sont identiques dans les interfaces anglaise et française. Le fichier facultatif native-identity.json du Store, propre à cette machine, contient uniquement version, type, nom, adresse et fuseau. Il s’applique à tous les workspaces utilisant ce Store. Les sauvegardes logiques ne le copient pas. Effacer le profil préserve révisions existantes et exigence de lecteur additive. La paire SYRNCK_USER_NAME et SYRNCK_USER_EMAIL remplace le profil pour une invocation ; SYRNCK_USER_TIMEZONE fournit un décalage facultatif. Sans identité configurée, une nouvelle révision reste en v1, y compris après effacement explicite du profil. Réécrire une révision v2 exige une identité actuelle. Signer sans enregistrer la clé privée # La variable facultative SYRNCK_SIGNING_SEED fournit une graine Ed25519 de 32 octets sous forme de 64 caractères hexadécimaux. Utiliser un environnement de processus contrôlé ; ne pas inscrire une vraie graine dans les arguments, scripts ou fichiers de profil. Le CLI consomme puis retire la variable de son environnement et efface ses propres tampons temporaires. Les autres commandes la retirent avant de lancer des outils. Cela ne promet pas d’effacer les copies antérieures du système ou de l’appelant. Sans graine, une identité crée une révision v2 non signée, avec signature: null . Dans la bibliothèque C++, identité et clé sont explicitement liées au handle : cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> store . use_native_identity ( { \"Alice\" , \"alice@example.invalid\" , 60 } , syrnck :: SigningKey :: generate ( ) ) ; auto revision = store . commit_directory ( project , \"main\" , \"Add asset\" ) ; store . clear_native_identity ( ) ; Le Store possède exclusivement la clé déplacée. La bibliothèque ne lit pas automatiquement profil CLI ou environnement. native_identity() expose profil et clé publics ; aucun export de clé privée. take_native_identity() détache un NativeIdentityContext déplaçable contenant profil et éventuelle clé. Déplacer les deux vers le Store suivant avec use_native_identity() avant de libérer le contexte. Un processus résident peut ainsi utiliser des handles verrouillés courts sans copier ni exporter la graine. L’ancien handle devient non configuré ; prendre un contexte vide renvoie null. Changer ou détacher l’identité dans un callback de progression est refusé. Voir la primitive de signature pour propriété des graines et vérification. Règles d’auteur et de committer # Une révision v2 enregistre author , committer et une signature facultative. Chaque identité datée contient name,email,timestamp,timezone_offset ; la date du committer égale celle de la révision. Un auteur d’origine peut être null lors d’une réécriture d’histoire ancienne. Le committer actuel signe le résultat complet, fichiers et parents compris. Une ancienne signature n’est jamais copiée sur des métadonnées modifiées. Nouveaux commits de dossier/index, nouveaux merges, reverts et squashes autonomes utilisent la personne actuelle comme auteur et committer. Amend, cherry-pick, rebase linéaire et détachement d’histoire préservent l’auteur d’origine avec le nouveau committer. Squash/fixup du rebase gardent l’auteur du premier commit retenu. Résultat inchangé ou fast-forward : révision existante exacte conservée. Les snapshots internes d’index, stash et récupération restent v1 sans auteur inventé. Le rebase fige une date de committer commune aux deux calculs de candidats. Avec une identité, --committer-time SECONDS sur rebase-plan/rebase-todo/rebase rend l’aperçu reproductible avec les mêmes identité, clé et entrées. Sans cette option, des invocations distinctes peuvent choisir des dates et IDs différents. Le rapport expose committer_timestamp . Une récupération checkout utilise les candidats signés figés sans avoir besoin de la clé privée de signature. native log --json , historique de fichier, blame et résumés d’archives exposent auteur, committer et ID public de clé pour v2. Le log texte affiche l’auteur et le committer différent ; --show-signature ajoute l’ID ou l’état non signé. Champs des résumés anciens et parcours par premier parent restent compatibles. Octets signés et compatibilité # La révision v2 est un JSON compact canonique ordonné version,parents,message,timestamp,author,committer,signature,files . La signature contient exactement algorithm,key_id,public_key,value : ed25519 , empreinte, clé publique de 32 octets et signature de 64 octets ; les valeurs binaires utilisent l’hexadécimal minuscule. Le message signé est l’ASCII Syrnck native revision signature v1: suivi des 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules du BLAKE3 de la révision v2 canonique avec signature à null. Import, lecture et audit valident signature, empreinte et révision canonique. Modifier auteur, parents, date, message ou fichiers invalide une signature. authored-revisions-v1 est activé avant stockage d’un objet v2 ou d’un profil. Les anciens lecteurs refusent cette configuration étendue ; enveloppe, manifest, pack, objets HTTP et ABI C restent inchangés. Sauvegardes/restaurations logiques préservent l’exigence. Révisions v1 et v2 peuvent coexister dans le même graphe. Confiance et limites restantes # Une signature valide prouve la possession de sa clé pour ces octets. Nom, adresse et date restent des déclarations du signataire. L’accès serveur v1 utilise principal authentifié, ACL de branches/chemins et verrous d’assets ; l’accès v2 peut aussi imposer des signatures approuvées et lier clés/identités de committer aux comptes. Il vérifie la politique actuelle pour l’histoire nouvellement admise tout en préservant la base déjà publiée. Coffre système de signature et révocation distribuée restent à développer. L’API d’auteur natif est actuellement C++/CLI ; import et reconstruction C/Go lisent les objets v2 validés sans graine. syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60 syrnck native identity syrnck native log --json syrnck native log --show-signature syrnck native identity --clear #include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp> store.use_native_identity({\"Alice\", \"alice@example.invalid\", 60}, syrnck::SigningKey::generate()); auto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Add asset\"); store.clear_native_identity();","headings":[{"id":"identity","text":"Configurer une identité","level":2},{"id":"signing","text":"Signer sans enregistrer la clé privée","level":2},{"id":"rewrites","text":"Règles d’auteur et de committer","level":2},{"id":"format","text":"Octets signés et compatibilité","level":2},{"id":"trust","text":"Confiance et limites restantes","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native identity --name \"Alice\" --email \"alice@example.invalid\" --timezone-offset +60\nsyrnck native identity\nsyrnck native log --json\nsyrnck native log --show-signature\nsyrnck native identity --clear\n#include \u003csyrnck/core.hpp>\nstore.use_native_identity({\"Alice\", \"alice@example.invalid\", 60},\n                          syrnck::SigningKey::generate());\nauto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Add asset\");\nstore.clear_native_identity();","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/signing-api","#/fr/server-signatures"],"kind":"guide","revision":"4a3a2b7068b0"},{"id":"native-bisect","title":"Recherche par bissection","description":"Guide : Recherche par bissection.","section":"Versionnage","order":210,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout\">Bissection persistante sans checkout\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-bisect/persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bissection persistante sans checkout\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck::NativeBisect\u003c/code> réduit une plage connue bonne/mauvaise dans le DAG de révisions, fusions comprises. Son état persiste et chaque résultat exige l’ID d’état attendu. Ni branche, index ni workspace ne bougent ; tester un candidat est une action explicite de l’appelant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les bornes doivent être distinctes, la bonne accessible depuis la mauvaise initiale. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs natifs. Le JSON rend \u003ccode>session\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>state\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>candidate_count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>next_revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>first_bad\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>good\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>skipped\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bad\u003c/code>. Marquer exactement le candidat suggéré avec le state du dernier rapport ; une autre mise à jour invalide l’ancien jeton. Une identité de session aléatoire empêche sa réutilisation lors d’une nouvelle recherche de la même plage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les révisions bonnes excluent tous leurs ancêtres ; les candidats restants sont ancêtres de la mauvaise courante. Pour chaque candidat éligible, le moteur calcule les partitions restantes selon bon/mauvais et choisit la plus équilibrée, départagée par ID. Les bitsets d’ascendance ne comptent pas deux fois les ancêtres partagés des fusions. La mauvaise courante n’est pas reproposée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>status: found\u003c/code> identifie un premier mauvais. Un candidat sauté/non compilable demeure possible ; sans test non sauté restant, le résultat devient ambiguous, first_bad null et possible_first_bad liste les possibilités, sans choisir arbitrairement. Comme toute recherche de régression, utiliser un même critère dont le défaut persiste chez les descendants ; des résultats contradictoires ou critères changeants ne prouvent pas une introduction fiable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native bisect-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"state-retention-and-recovery\">État, rétention et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-bisect/state-retention-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers État, rétention et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une recherche active par contexte de workspace. Le \u003ccode>bisect/active\u003c/code> principal pointe un descripteur chunk canonique strict v1 ; \u003ccode>bisect/pin\u003c/code> conserve la mauvaise initiale, ses ancêtres et fichiers. Le démarrage vérifie les fichiers et publie état/pin ensemble. Déplacer ou retirer une branche ne rend donc pas ces versions collectables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque marquage publie un nouvel état immuable par transaction attendue ; les anciens états sans référence sont collectables, sans archive croissante à chaque étape. Annulation/coupure avant publication gardent l’ancien état et peuvent laisser des métadonnées orphelines complètes. Un nouveau processus retrouve la même recherche par bisect-status, sans cache nécessaire. Descripteur corrompu, pin absent/incohérent, résultat hors historique et métadonnées contradictoires sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Reset termine une recherche, achevée ou non, et publie ensemble la protection \u003ccode>archive/bisect/INITIAL_BAD\u003c/code> et le reçu final \u003ccode>bisect/receipts/SESSION\u003c/code>. Les anciens fichiers restent reconstructibles après GC ; \u003ccode>native archives --kind bisect\u003c/code> les inspecte et le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait avec sauvegarde\u003c/a> permet leur nettoyage. Une nouvelle recherche possède une session indépendante.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Bornes : 100000 révisions, 1000000 liens de parents, budget estimé de métadonnées de graphe 64 Mio, au plus 4096 candidats non résolus. Les bitsets denses utilisent au plus 2 Mio ; conteneurs/caches/allocateur s’ajoutent. Réduire la plage avec une bonne borne plus récente si nécessaire. Aucun index persistant de graphe n’évite encore les relectures de statut.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les contextes liés gardent leurs propres état actif, pin et reçus, sélectionnés\navec \u003ccode>--workspace NOM\u003c/code>. Plusieurs workspaces peuvent garder des recherches\nindépendantes ; les mutations du Store restent sérialisées par son verrou.\nLe \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-file-history\">blame et l’historique des renommages exacts\u003c/a> sont des\nrequêtes distinctes. Checkout bisect interactif, exécution automatique de commande,\nsélection de prédicat tenant compte des renommages restent à réaliser. Les\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">signatures facultatives\u003c/a> sont vérifiées à la lecture\ndes révisions ; elles n’authentifient pas les classements good/bad de l’utilisateur.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Bissection persistante sans checkout # syrnck::NativeBisect réduit une plage connue bonne/mauvaise dans le DAG de révisions, fusions comprises. Son état persiste et chaque résultat exige l’ID d’état attendu. Ni branche, index ni workspace ne bougent ; tester un candidat est une action explicite de l’appelant. Les bornes doivent être distinctes, la bonne accessible depuis la mauvaise initiale. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs natifs. Le JSON rend session , state , status , candidate_count , next_revision , first_bad , good , skipped , bad . Marquer exactement le candidat suggéré avec le state du dernier rapport ; une autre mise à jour invalide l’ancien jeton. Une identité de session aléatoire empêche sa réutilisation lors d’une nouvelle recherche de la même plage. Les révisions bonnes excluent tous leurs ancêtres ; les candidats restants sont ancêtres de la mauvaise courante. Pour chaque candidat éligible, le moteur calcule les partitions restantes selon bon/mauvais et choisit la plus équilibrée, départagée par ID. Les bitsets d’ascendance ne comptent pas deux fois les ancêtres partagés des fusions. La mauvaise courante n’est pas reproposée. status: found identifie un premier mauvais. Un candidat sauté/non compilable demeure possible ; sans test non sauté restant, le résultat devient ambiguous, first_bad null et possible_first_bad liste les possibilités, sans choisir arbitrairement. Comme toute recherche de régression, utiliser un même critère dont le défaut persiste chez les descendants ; des résultats contradictoires ou critères changeants ne prouvent pas une introduction fiable. text Copier syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION syrnck native bisect-status syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID État, rétention et récupération # Une recherche active par contexte de workspace. Le bisect/active principal pointe un descripteur chunk canonique strict v1 ; bisect/pin conserve la mauvaise initiale, ses ancêtres et fichiers. Le démarrage vérifie les fichiers et publie état/pin ensemble. Déplacer ou retirer une branche ne rend donc pas ces versions collectables. Chaque marquage publie un nouvel état immuable par transaction attendue ; les anciens états sans référence sont collectables, sans archive croissante à chaque étape. Annulation/coupure avant publication gardent l’ancien état et peuvent laisser des métadonnées orphelines complètes. Un nouveau processus retrouve la même recherche par bisect-status, sans cache nécessaire. Descripteur corrompu, pin absent/incohérent, résultat hors historique et métadonnées contradictoires sont refusés. Reset termine une recherche, achevée ou non, et publie ensemble la protection archive/bisect/INITIAL_BAD et le reçu final bisect/receipts/SESSION . Les anciens fichiers restent reconstructibles après GC ; native archives --kind bisect les inspecte et le retrait avec sauvegarde permet leur nettoyage. Une nouvelle recherche possède une session indépendante. Bornes : 100000 révisions, 1000000 liens de parents, budget estimé de métadonnées de graphe 64 Mio, au plus 4096 candidats non résolus. Les bitsets denses utilisent au plus 2 Mio ; conteneurs/caches/allocateur s’ajoutent. Réduire la plage avec une bonne borne plus récente si nécessaire. Aucun index persistant de graphe n’évite encore les relectures de statut. Les contextes liés gardent leurs propres état actif, pin et reçus, sélectionnés avec --workspace NOM . Plusieurs workspaces peuvent garder des recherches indépendantes ; les mutations du Store restent sérialisées par son verrou. Le blame et l’historique des renommages exacts sont des requêtes distinctes. Checkout bisect interactif, exécution automatique de commande, sélection de prédicat tenant compte des renommages restent à réaliser. Les signatures facultatives sont vérifiées à la lecture des révisions ; elles n’authentifient pas les classements good/bad de l’utilisateur. syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION syrnck native bisect-status syrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory syrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID syrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID","headings":[{"id":"persistent-native-bisection-without-checkout","text":"Bissection persistante sans checkout","level":1},{"id":"state-retention-and-recovery","text":"État, rétention et récupération","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native bisect-start GOOD_REVISION BAD_REVISION\nsyrnck native bisect-status\nsyrnck native export CANDIDATE_REVISION /new/candidate-directory\nsyrnck native bisect-good CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-bad CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-skip CANDIDATE_REVISION --expect-state STATE_ID\nsyrnck native bisect-reset --expect-state STATE_ID","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-file-history","#/fr/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0802e2fbcbe6"},{"id":"native-blame","title":"Attribution native des lignes","description":"Blame vérifié à travers les merges et renommages exacts.","section":"Versionnage","order":251,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Attribuer les lignes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-blame/usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Attribuer les lignes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native blame REVISION PATH\u003c/code> attribue chaque ligne d’un fichier texte UTF-8 commité\nà la révision et au chemin qui l’ont introduite. Il reconstruit et vérifie les\noctets stockés sans lire le workspace, modifier les références ou importer des\nobjets. L’entrée C++ est \u003ccode>native_blame\u003c/code> dans \u003ccode>syrnck/history.hpp\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le tableau JSON \u003ccode>lines\u003c/code> conserve numéro final, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>path\u003c/code> d’origine,\n\u003ccode>source_line\u003c/code> à partir de 1 et \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> exact, y compris LF/CRLF ou absence de\nsaut de ligne final. Un fichier vide a zéro ligne. \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code> indexe les dates des\nrévisions d’origine et des aperçus de messages bornés. Les entrées v2 exposent\naussi \u003ccode>author\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>committer\u003c/code> et l’ID public \u003ccode>signing_key\u003c/code> (null sans signature).\nLa v1 ancienne n’a pas ces champs ; la commande ne les invente pas.\nVoir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">identité et confiance\u003c/a>.\nLes clés JSON et identifiants restent en anglais.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lineage\">Merges et renommages\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-blame/lineage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Merges et renommages\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Par défaut, tous les parents ordonnés peuvent fournir des lignes inchangées. Le\npremier parent correspondant prime, puis les lignes restantes sont comparées\naux parents suivants. Seules les lignes absentes de tous les parents proviennent\ndu merge lui-même. \u003ccode>--first-parent\u003c/code> limite le parcours à la chaîne des premiers\nparents. Le moteur Myers borné effectue l’alignement ; des lignes répétées peuvent\nadmettre plusieurs alignements valides. Les espaces ne sont pas normalisés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les renommages à contenu exact et correspondance unique sont suivis même si le\ndécoupage change, d’après hash et taille du fichier complet. Le mode exécutable ne\nchange pas l’origine des lignes. Plusieurs sources supprimées ou destinations\najoutées possibles arrêtent ce suivi ; renommages modifiés et copies ne sont pas\ndéduits. \u003ccode>--no-follow\u003c/code> traite le nouveau nom comme un ajout. Un parent binaire ne\nfournit pas d’origines textuelles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Budgets et erreurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-blame/limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Budgets et erreurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le parcours autorise par défaut 1000 révisions distinctes (1..10000), 64 Mio de\nmétadonnées décodées de révisions/manifestes et de fichiers reconstruits cumulés\n(1..256 Mio), ainsi que les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">budgets du moteur textuel\u003c/a>. Ces budgets\nd’entrée/calcul ne bornent pas toutes les allocations du processus. Le contenu\nreconstruit est mis en cache par manifeste pour cette seule requête. Les racines\nde rétention normales doivent toujours protéger l’historique consulté.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>status: complete\u003c/code> termine avec le code 0. Une entrée binaire rend \u003ccode>status: binary\u003c/code>,\nraison \u003ccode>non_text\u003c/code>, code 1. Un budget de révisions/entrée/fichier/lignes/calcul/\ntrace/sortie épuisé rend \u003ccode>status: limited\u003c/code>, une raison et le code 1. Les rapports\nincomplets ont \u003ccode>lines\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>commits\u003c/code> vides ; une attribution partielle n’est jamais\nprésentée comme complète. Chemin absent, objets corrompus/manquants, arguments\ninvalides et annulation font échouer la commande sans rapport partiel. Progression\net diagnostics suivent la langue choisie pour l’interface.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Attribuer les lignes # native blame REVISION PATH attribue chaque ligne d’un fichier texte UTF-8 commité à la révision et au chemin qui l’ont introduite. Il reconstruit et vérifie les octets stockés sans lire le workspace, modifier les références ou importer des objets. L’entrée C++ est native_blame dans syrnck/history.hpp . text Copier syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow Le tableau JSON lines conserve numéro final, revision et path d’origine, source_line à partir de 1 et content exact, y compris LF/CRLF ou absence de saut de ligne final. Un fichier vide a zéro ligne. commits indexe les dates des révisions d’origine et des aperçus de messages bornés. Les entrées v2 exposent aussi author , committer et l’ID public signing_key (null sans signature). La v1 ancienne n’a pas ces champs ; la commande ne les invente pas. Voir identité et confiance . Les clés JSON et identifiants restent en anglais. Merges et renommages # Par défaut, tous les parents ordonnés peuvent fournir des lignes inchangées. Le premier parent correspondant prime, puis les lignes restantes sont comparées aux parents suivants. Seules les lignes absentes de tous les parents proviennent du merge lui-même. --first-parent limite le parcours à la chaîne des premiers parents. Le moteur Myers borné effectue l’alignement ; des lignes répétées peuvent admettre plusieurs alignements valides. Les espaces ne sont pas normalisés. Les renommages à contenu exact et correspondance unique sont suivis même si le découpage change, d’après hash et taille du fichier complet. Le mode exécutable ne change pas l’origine des lignes. Plusieurs sources supprimées ou destinations ajoutées possibles arrêtent ce suivi ; renommages modifiés et copies ne sont pas déduits. --no-follow traite le nouveau nom comme un ajout. Un parent binaire ne fournit pas d’origines textuelles. Budgets et erreurs # Le parcours autorise par défaut 1000 révisions distinctes (1..10000), 64 Mio de métadonnées décodées de révisions/manifestes et de fichiers reconstruits cumulés (1..256 Mio), ainsi que les budgets du moteur textuel . Ces budgets d’entrée/calcul ne bornent pas toutes les allocations du processus. Le contenu reconstruit est mis en cache par manifeste pour cette seule requête. Les racines de rétention normales doivent toujours protéger l’historique consulté. text Copier syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728 syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608 status: complete termine avec le code 0. Une entrée binaire rend status: binary , raison non_text , code 1. Un budget de révisions/entrée/fichier/lignes/calcul/ trace/sortie épuisé rend status: limited , une raison et le code 1. Les rapports incomplets ont lines et commits vides ; une attribution partielle n’est jamais présentée comme complète. Chemin absent, objets corrompus/manquants, arguments invalides et annulation font échouer la commande sans rapport partiel. Progression et diagnostics suivent la langue choisie pour l’interface. syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp syrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728 syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Attribuer les lignes","level":2},{"id":"lineage","text":"Merges et renommages","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Budgets et erreurs","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp\nsyrnck --lang fr native blame tags/release Source/Module.cpp --progress-json\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --first-parent --no-follow\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-revisions 2000 --max-input-bytes 134217728\nsyrnck native blame HEAD Source/Module.cpp --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-lines 60000 --max-work 33554432 --max-output-bytes 8388608","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/text-diff"],"kind":"guide","revision":"237952a2fe25"},{"id":"native-branches","title":"Branches et changement de branche","description":"Guide : Branches et changement de branche.","section":"Versionnage","order":220,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-branches-and-recoverable-switching\">Branches natives et changement récupérable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/native-branches-and-recoverable-switching\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Branches natives et changement récupérable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le CLI sélectionne normalement une branche nommée et prend aussi en charge \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">HEAD détaché\u003c/a>. Sans descripteur HEAD, un ancien Store sélectionne implicitement \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, même non née. Lire HEAD ne le crée pas. Un switch réussi enregistre la sélection liée au répertoire canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native branch NAME REVISION\u003c/code> crée sans sélectionner ni modifier les fichiers. \u003ccode>native switch NAME\u003c/code> restaure la tête exacte et sélectionne la branche sans déplacer aucune tête. \u003ccode>native checkout REVISION\u003c/code> restaure une révision et déplace la cible sélectionnée en archivant son ancienne tête. Les deux utilisent le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">checkout récupérable\u003c/a>. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">\u003ccode>native switch --detach REVISION\u003c/code>\u003c/a> sélectionne une révision indépendamment des branches. Switch ne crée jamais de branche nommée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch feature REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native switch feature --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native switch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"branch-inventory-and-verified-creation\">Inventaire et création vérifiée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/branch-inventory-and-verified-creation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inventaire et création vérifiée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native branches\u003c/code> parcourt les références \u003ccode>heads/\u003c/code> triées par octets. Le JSON contient \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branches\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, le curseur exclusif \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>. Une branche sélectionnée non née apparaît dans \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> sans ligne fictive. Une page vide a un curseur nul. \u003ccode>--limit\u003c/code> vaut 1..1000, défaut 100 ; le curseur peut avoir disparu. Les appels successifs ne forment pas un snapshot partagé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque ligne comprend nom, révision, sélection courante, nombres de fichiers/parents, timestamp et aperçu UTF-8 du message limité à 256 octets avec \u003ccode>message_bytes\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>message_truncated\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stale_index\u003c/code> exposent l’index figé, même périmé, sans lire les fichiers. Les index orphelins restent accessibles par \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>native staged --branch NAME\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>, les payloads ne sont pas audités. Avec cette option, les fermetures complètes des têtes et index affichés, historiques de base compris, sont contrôlées ; la tête sélectionnée hors page n’est pas une racine supplémentaire. Rapports limités à 16 Mio. La table des références est chargée une fois, entièrement, sans copie par branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La création C++ exige une révision exacte existante ; le CLI accepte aussi les sélecteurs. Elle vérifie toutes les données retenues et publie seulement si tête et slot d’index sont absents. Une tête existante, un index orphelin ou un checkout/switch actif bloque ; aucun remplacement forcé. Les fichiers sales restent inchangés. Si HEAD nommait déjà cette branche non née, il résout ensuite la nouvelle tête.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La création CLI reste silencieuse sur stdout ; C++ retourne nom et révision. L’inventaire reste JSON anglais quelle que soit la langue. Progression et annulation ordinaires s’appliquent. Verrou exclusif et attentes d’absence protègent la publication ; annulation, données absentes ou observer en échec avant publication préservent références et workspace. Aucun callback après ce point.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"archived-branch-deletion\">Suppression archivée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/archived-branch-deletion\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Suppression archivée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La suppression d’une branche inactive retire \u003ccode>heads/NAME\u003c/code> et éventuellement \u003ccode>staging/NAME\u003c/code> dans la transaction qui publie une archive retenue. Historique complet, index figé et sa base restent reconstruisibles après GC. La branche symboliquement sélectionnée est protégée même si une autre a le même ID. Un checkout/switch actif bloque les mutations. Fichiers et sélection restent inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tout index, propre ou périmé, exige \u003ccode>--archive-index\u003c/code> ; sinon le plan indique \u003ccode>staging_present\u003c/code>. Le CLI exige \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> avec l’ID exact de tête ; \u003ccode>--expect-index\u003c/code> est optionnel, sinon l’index courant est capturé sous verrou. L’API C++ attend un index optionnel exact : null exige l’absence, un ID autorise ce seul snapshot. Une divergence bloque avant publication. Aucune version non fusionnée n’est implicitement abandonnée : tout l’historique est archivé sans condition d’ancêtre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait protégé par sauvegarde\u003c/a> peut retirer plus tard tout le groupe \u003ccode>archive/branches/ID\u003c/code> seulement si descripteur, tête et index sont couverts par une sauvegarde indépendante vérifiée. Cela ne supprime ni branche recréée du même nom ni objets par GC automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan indique \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> proposé, \u003ccode>archive_existing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archive_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. Plan prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : code 1 avec JSON ; arguments/métadonnées invalides : diagnostic. L’application ajoute \u003ccode>state: deleted\u003c/code>, vérifie toujours toute la fermeture avant l’enregistrement ; les champs précédents décrivent ses entrées. Seul \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> contrôle les payloads dans un plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’identité déterministe dépend du nom, de la tête et de l’index. Répéter le même état réutilise l’archive complète ; un groupe partiel/conflictuel bloque. Avant publication, erreur ou annulation conserve les références mais peut laisser des métadonnées non référencées. Inspecter avant de réessayer. Aucun journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le chunk descripteur JSON canonique contient \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type: native-branch-archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index\u003c/code> nullable. Le groupe retient \u003ccode>record\u003c/code> (chunk), \u003ccode>target\u003c/code> (révision/historique), \u003ccode>index\u003c/code> optionnel (snapshot/base) sous \u003ccode>archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le navigateur contrôle identité, groupe exact et index lié à la branche. \u003ccode>branch-archives\u003c/code> utilise pages triées par ID, curseur exclusif \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, limite 1..1000. Chaque ligne donne \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, nom original et résumé tête/index sans \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>branch-archive\u003c/code> retourne une ligne dans \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audite les racines affichées. Bases périmées ou initialement non nées restent exactes. Aucune expiration automatique ; retirer génériquement ces racines libère leur rétention. \u003ccode>native archives\u003c/code> reste dédié aux archives de révisions comme reset/amend/checkout. Les formats d’objets et stockage ne changent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> deletion \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_delete_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> removed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">delete_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_tip\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> saved \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_branch_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"restore-an-archived-branch\">Restaurer une branche archivée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/restore-an-archived-branch\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Restaurer une branche archivée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La restauration recrée tête et index dans une transaction. Les deux slots doivent être absents, même si une branche existante porte le même ID. Un index orphelin ou checkout/switch actif bloque. L’archive reste retenue. Fichiers et sélection symbolique sont inchangés ; une sélection non née du nom restauré résout sa tête ensuite. Utiliser explicitement switch/checkout pour les fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans \u003ccode>--as\u003c/code>, nom et ID d’index originaux sont restaurés. Avec un autre nom, seul le message interne de liaison de l’index change : fichiers, modes, timestamp et parents restent identiques. Le nouvel ID est déterministe, éventuellement non stocké avant application. Un index non né conserve ses parents vides ; un index périmé le reste. Inspecter \u003ccode>native staged --branch NAME\u003c/code> puis utiliser le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase\">rebase d’index\u003c/a> si nécessaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rapport : \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>original_name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_rewritten\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_base\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stale_index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>current\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blockers\u003c/code>. Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON. L’application vérifie toute l’archive avant tout index réécrit ou publication et ajoute \u003ccode>state: restored\u003c/code>. Le plan ne lit les données qu’avec \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transaction garde le groupe d’archive, les absences des destinations et l’objet HEAD attendus. Les racines sources restent intactes. Avant publication, annulation/observer préserve l’ancienne table ; un index candidat peut rester non référencé. Inspecter archive et destination avant nouvelle tentative : une destination déjà complétée est une collision. Les versions archivées survivent au GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_restore_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>archive_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restored \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>archive_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity\">Défauts CLI et identité du workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Défauts CLI et identité du workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sans \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>, la branche courante ou cible détachée est utilisée pour commit simple/figé, add, staged, reset, status simple/combiné, checkout-plan, checkout, log et détachement d’historique. Une branche explicite n’altère pas HEAD. \u003ccode>status --revision\u003c/code> sélectionne le snapshot demandé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les arguments snapshot acceptent \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-tags\">tags et sélecteurs\u003c/a>. Le switch ordinaire prend un nom littéral de branche ; \u003ccode>native checkout tags/NAME\u003c/code> déplace la cible sélectionnée et archive sa tête. Utiliser \u003ccode>native switch --detach tags/NAME\u003c/code> pour détacher HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> retourne des clés anglaises : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code> (1), \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code> (null si non née), \u003ccode>explicit\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code> (racine canonique ou null pour HEAD implicite). Le descripteur résout la branche dynamiquement sans réécriture à chaque commit. Une suppression générique de sa tête laisse une sélection non née.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avec HEAD explicite, le dossier doit résoudre la même racine canonique. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces liés\u003c/a> gardent des HEAD et index indépendants ; \u003ccode>--workspace NOM\u003c/code> sélectionne leur contexte et utilise par défaut leur chemin enregistré. Le contexte principal garde le défaut \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>. Une branche nommée ne peut être sélectionnée que dans un workspace. Déplacement, matérialisation partielle et switch vers destination non née restent à réaliser. Les méthodes C++ à branche explicite ne résolvent pas implicitement HEAD ; l’appelant peut utiliser \u003ccode>native_target()\u003c/code> pour une branche ou la cible détachée opaque. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">HEAD détaché\u003c/a>, dont \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> indique \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preflight-publication-and-recovery\">Préparation, publication et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/preflight-publication-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparation, publication et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native switch-plan NAME [--verify]\u003c/code> est en lecture seule, basé sur le preflight checkout de la branche courante vers la tête destination. Il ajoute \u003ccode>operation: switch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>destination_branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head_object\u003c/code> (ID ou null), \u003ccode>destination_staging_snapshot\u003c/code>. Un index sur l’une des branches bloque, même propre ; côté destination : \u003ccode>target_staging_present\u003c/code>. Un plan bloqué complet donne JSON/code 1 ; arguments ou objets requis invalides ne donnent pas de rapport.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La destination doit exister et être une révision. Tous les fichiers suivis doivent correspondre par hash complet. Collisions non suivies/ignorées, liens, alias portables et transitions suivent le checkout. Aucun force, écrasement ou reset d’index automatique. Un switch propre vers la branche déjà sélectionnée est un no-op en lecture seule ; deux branches au même ID publient néanmoins une nouvelle sélection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transaction vérifie têtes source/destination, slots d’index et HEAD. Elle audite les fermetures et reconstruit les cibles ; des déplacements exclusifs préservent les originaux. La transaction finale publie HEAD avec journal validé, archive source, rétention cible et reçu sans déplacer les têtes. Avant ce point, HEAD garde l’ancienne sélection même si des fichiers ont changé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant publication, abort restaure les originaux et HEAD ; resume termine la nouvelle sélection. Après publication, seule la fin du nettoyage est possible. Une mutation générique des branches/index/HEAD fait refuser la récupération. Ouvrir le Store ne modifie jamais automatiquement les fichiers. Les déplacements sont séquentiels ; arrêter les éditeurs. Cibles préparées et sauvegardes sont en clair même avec stockage chiffré ; les limites du checkout s’appliquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"representation-and-c-api\">Représentation et API C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-branches/representation-and-c-api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Représentation et API C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> retient un chunk JSON canonique : \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type: native-head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>. Compression, chiffrement et packs ordinaires s’appliquent sans nouveau format. Aucun ID de révision figé : les références de branche retiennent leurs fermetures.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le journal switch v2 ajoute \u003ccode>destination_branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previous_head\u003c/code> nullable et \u003ccode>next_head\u003c/code> à l’identité hashée. Les journaux/reçus v1 restent lisibles ; GC bloqué pendant une transaction. Les anciens descripteurs peuvent être collectés après switch ; les reçus terminés restent consultables sans eux. Journaux actifs et HEAD sont locaux : ne pas les éditer ni les répliquer comme des racines de livraison. Employer un moteur récent après activation. L’ABI C et Go n’exposent pas encore les opérations de branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> head \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> branches \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_branches\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> created \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_native_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_switch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> switched \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">switch_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Branches natives et changement récupérable # Le CLI sélectionne normalement une branche nommée et prend aussi en charge HEAD détaché . Sans descripteur HEAD, un ancien Store sélectionne implicitement main , même non née. Lire HEAD ne le crée pas. Un switch réussi enregistre la sélection liée au répertoire canonique. native branch NAME REVISION crée sans sélectionner ni modifier les fichiers. native switch NAME restaure la tête exacte et sélectionne la branche sans déplacer aucune tête. native checkout REVISION restaure une révision et déplace la cible sélectionnée en archivant son ancienne tête. Les deux utilisent le checkout récupérable . native switch --detach REVISION sélectionne une révision indépendamment des branches. Switch ne crée jamais de branche nommée. text Copier syrnck native head syrnck native branch feature REVISION syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify syrnck native switch feature --progress syrnck native status --combined syrnck native add Source Content/Maps syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\" syrnck native log syrnck native switch main Inventaire et création vérifiée # native branches parcourt les références heads/ triées par octets. Le JSON contient head , branches , has_more , le curseur exclusif next_after et objects_verified . Une branche sélectionnée non née apparaît dans head sans ligne fictive. Une page vide a un curseur nul. --limit vaut 1..1000, défaut 100 ; le curseur peut avoir disparu. Les appels successifs ne forment pas un snapshot partagé. Chaque ligne comprend nom, révision, sélection courante, nombres de fichiers/parents, timestamp et aperçu UTF-8 du message limité à 256 octets avec message_bytes / message_truncated . index , index_base , stale_index exposent l’index figé, même périmé, sans lire les fichiers. Les index orphelins restent accessibles par refs ou native staged --branch NAME . Sans --verify , les payloads ne sont pas audités. Avec cette option, les fermetures complètes des têtes et index affichés, historiques de base compris, sont contrôlées ; la tête sélectionnée hors page n’est pas une racine supplémentaire. Rapports limités à 16 Mio. La table des références est chargée une fois, entièrement, sans copie par branche. La création C++ exige une révision exacte existante ; le CLI accepte aussi les sélecteurs. Elle vérifie toutes les données retenues et publie seulement si tête et slot d’index sont absents. Une tête existante, un index orphelin ou un checkout/switch actif bloque ; aucun remplacement forcé. Les fichiers sales restent inchangés. Si HEAD nommait déjà cette branche non née, il résout ensuite la nouvelle tête. La création CLI reste silencieuse sur stdout ; C++ retourne nom et révision. L’inventaire reste JSON anglais quelle que soit la langue. Progression et annulation ordinaires s’appliquent. Verrou exclusif et attentes d’absence protègent la publication ; annulation, données absentes ou observer en échec avant publication préservent références et workspace. Aucun callback après ce point. text Copier syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100 syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress Suppression archivée # La suppression d’une branche inactive retire heads/NAME et éventuellement staging/NAME dans la transaction qui publie une archive retenue. Historique complet, index figé et sa base restent reconstruisibles après GC. La branche symboliquement sélectionnée est protégée même si une autre a le même ID. Un checkout/switch actif bloque les mutations. Fichiers et sélection restent inchangés. Tout index, propre ou périmé, exige --archive-index ; sinon le plan indique staging_present . Le CLI exige --expect avec l’ID exact de tête ; --expect-index est optionnel, sinon l’index courant est capturé sous verrou. L’API C++ attend un index optionnel exact : null exige l’absence, un ID autorise ce seul snapshot. Une divergence bloque avant publication. Aucune version non fusionnée n’est implicitement abandonnée : tout l’historique est archivé sans condition d’ancêtre. Le retrait protégé par sauvegarde peut retirer plus tard tout le groupe archive/branches/ID seulement si descripteur, tête et index sont couverts par une sauvegarde indépendante vérifiée. Cela ne supprime ni branche recréée du même nom ni objets par GC automatique. Le plan indique branch , archive proposé, archive_existing , archive_index , ready , objects_verified , blockers . Plan prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : code 1 avec JSON ; arguments/métadonnées invalides : diagnostic. L’application ajoute state: deleted , vérifie toujours toute la fermeture avant l’enregistrement ; les champs précédents décrivent ses entrées. Seul --verify contrôle les payloads dans un plan. L’identité déterministe dépend du nom, de la tête et de l’index. Répéter le même état réutilise l’archive complète ; un groupe partiel/conflictuel bloque. Avant publication, erreur ou annulation conserve les références mais peut laisser des métadonnées non référencées. Inspecter avant de réessayer. Aucun journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire. Le chunk descripteur JSON canonique contient version: 1 , type: native-branch-archive , name , revision , index nullable. Le groupe retient record (chunk), target (révision/historique), index optionnel (snapshot/base) sous archive/branches/ARCHIVE_ID/ . Le navigateur contrôle identité, groupe exact et index lié à la branche. branch-archives utilise pages triées par ID, curseur exclusif next_after , has_more , objects_verified , limite 1..1000. Chaque ligne donne archive , nom original et résumé tête/index sans current . branch-archive retourne une ligne dans archive . --verify audite les racines affichées. Bases périmées ou initialement non nées restent exactes. Aucune expiration automatique ; retirer génériquement ces racines libère leur rétention. native archives reste dédié aux archives de révisions comme reset/amend/checkout. Les formats d’objets et stockage ne changent pas. text Copier syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify cpp Copier auto deletion = store . plan_delete_native_branch ( \"feature\" , true , true , operation ) ; auto removed = store . delete_native_branch ( \"feature\" , expected_tip , expected_index , operation ) ; auto saved = store . native_branch_archive ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true , operation ) ; Restaurer une branche archivée # La restauration recrée tête et index dans une transaction. Les deux slots doivent être absents, même si une branche existante porte le même ID. Un index orphelin ou checkout/switch actif bloque. L’archive reste retenue. Fichiers et sélection symbolique sont inchangés ; une sélection non née du nom restauré résout sa tête ensuite. Utiliser explicitement switch/checkout pour les fichiers. Sans --as , nom et ID d’index originaux sont restaurés. Avec un autre nom, seul le message interne de liaison de l’index change : fichiers, modes, timestamp et parents restent identiques. Le nouvel ID est déterministe, éventuellement non stocké avant application. Un index non né conserve ses parents vides ; un index périmé le reste. Inspecter native staged --branch NAME puis utiliser le rebase d’index si nécessaire. Rapport : archive , original_name , name , revision , previous_index , index , index_rewritten , index_base , stale_index , current , ready , objects_verified , blockers . Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON. L’application vérifie toute l’archive avant tout index réécrit ou publication et ajoute state: restored . Le plan ne lit les données qu’avec --verify . La transaction garde le groupe d’archive, les absences des destinations et l’objet HEAD attendus. Les racines sources restent intactes. Avant publication, annulation/observer préserve l’ancienne table ; un index candidat peut rester non référencé. Inspecter archive et destination avant nouvelle tentative : une destination déjà complétée est une collision. Les versions archivées survivent au GC. text Copier syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic cpp Copier auto preview = store . plan_restore_native_branch ( archive_id , \"recovered\" , true , operation ) ; auto restored = store . restore_native_branch ( archive_id , \"recovered\" , operation ) ; Défauts CLI et identité du workspace # Sans --branch , la branche courante ou cible détachée est utilisée pour commit simple/figé, add, staged, reset, status simple/combiné, checkout-plan, checkout, log et détachement d’historique. Une branche explicite n’altère pas HEAD. status --revision sélectionne le snapshot demandé. Les arguments snapshot acceptent tags et sélecteurs . Le switch ordinaire prend un nom littéral de branche ; native checkout tags/NAME déplace la cible sélectionnée et archive sa tête. Utiliser native switch --detach tags/NAME pour détacher HEAD. native head retourne des clés anglaises : version (1), branch , revision (null si non née), explicit , directory (racine canonique ou null pour HEAD implicite). Le descripteur résout la branche dynamiquement sans réécriture à chaque commit. Une suppression générique de sa tête laisse une sélection non née. Avec HEAD explicite, le dossier doit résoudre la même racine canonique. Les workspaces liés gardent des HEAD et index indépendants ; --workspace NOM sélectionne leur contexte et utilise par défaut leur chemin enregistré. Le contexte principal garde le défaut . . Une branche nommée ne peut être sélectionnée que dans un workspace. Déplacement, matérialisation partielle et switch vers destination non née restent à réaliser. Les méthodes C++ à branche explicite ne résolvent pas implicitement HEAD ; l’appelant peut utiliser native_target() pour une branche ou la cible détachée opaque. Voir HEAD détaché , dont native head indique branch: null . Préparation, publication et récupération # native switch-plan NAME [--verify] est en lecture seule, basé sur le preflight checkout de la branche courante vers la tête destination. Il ajoute operation: switch , destination_branch , head_object (ID ou null), destination_staging_snapshot . Un index sur l’une des branches bloque, même propre ; côté destination : target_staging_present . Un plan bloqué complet donne JSON/code 1 ; arguments ou objets requis invalides ne donnent pas de rapport. La destination doit exister et être une révision. Tous les fichiers suivis doivent correspondre par hash complet. Collisions non suivies/ignorées, liens, alias portables et transitions suivent le checkout. Aucun force, écrasement ou reset d’index automatique. Un switch propre vers la branche déjà sélectionnée est un no-op en lecture seule ; deux branches au même ID publient néanmoins une nouvelle sélection. La transaction vérifie têtes source/destination, slots d’index et HEAD. Elle audite les fermetures et reconstruit les cibles ; des déplacements exclusifs préservent les originaux. La transaction finale publie HEAD avec journal validé, archive source, rétention cible et reçu sans déplacer les têtes. Avant ce point, HEAD garde l’ancienne sélection même si des fichiers ont changé. Avant publication, abort restaure les originaux et HEAD ; resume termine la nouvelle sélection. Après publication, seule la fin du nettoyage est possible. Une mutation générique des branches/index/HEAD fait refuser la récupération. Ouvrir le Store ne modifie jamais automatiquement les fichiers. Les déplacements sont séquentiels ; arrêter les éditeurs. Cibles préparées et sauvegardes sont en clair même avec stockage chiffré ; les limites du checkout s’appliquent. text Copier syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID Représentation et API C++ # workspace/HEAD retient un chunk JSON canonique : version: 1 , type: native-head , directory , branch . Compression, chiffrement et packs ordinaires s’appliquent sans nouveau format. Aucun ID de révision figé : les références de branche retiennent leurs fermetures. Le journal switch v2 ajoute destination_branch , previous_head nullable et next_head à l’identité hashée. Les journaux/reçus v1 restent lisibles ; GC bloqué pendant une transaction. Les anciens descripteurs peuvent être collectés après switch ; les reçus terminés restent consultables sans eux. Journaux actifs et HEAD sont locaux : ne pas les éditer ni les répliquer comme des racines de livraison. Employer un moteur récent après activation. L’ABI C et Go n’exposent pas encore les opérations de branche. cpp Copier auto head = store . native_head ( ) ; auto branches = store . native_branches ( { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto created = store . create_native_branch ( \"feature\" , revision_id , operation ) ; auto plan = store . plan_switch ( project , \"feature\" , true , operation ) ; auto switched = store . switch_directory ( project , \"feature\" , operation ) ; syrnck native head syrnck native branch feature REVISION syrnck native switch-plan feature --verify syrnck native switch feature --progress syrnck native status --combined syrnck native add Source Content/Maps syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\" syrnck native log syrnck native switch main syrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100 syrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress syrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify syrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify syrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify auto deletion = store.plan_delete_native_branch(\"feature\", true, true, operation); auto removed = store.delete_native_branch(\"feature\", expected_tip, expected_index, operation); auto saved = store.native_branch_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation); syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID syrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify syrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic auto preview = store.plan_restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", true, operation); auto restored = store.restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", operation); syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID auto head = store.native_head(); auto branches = store.native_branches({}, 100, true, operation); auto created = store.create_native_branch(\"feature\", revision_id, operation); auto plan = store.plan_switch(project, \"feature\", true, operation); auto switched = store.switch_directory(project, \"feature\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-branches-and-recoverable-switching","text":"Branches natives et changement récupérable","level":1},{"id":"branch-inventory-and-verified-creation","text":"Inventaire et création vérifiée","level":2},{"id":"archived-branch-deletion","text":"Suppression archivée","level":2},{"id":"restore-an-archived-branch","text":"Restaurer une branche archivée","level":2},{"id":"cli-defaults-and-workspace-identity","text":"Défauts CLI et identité du workspace","level":2},{"id":"preflight-publication-and-recovery","text":"Préparation, publication et récupération","level":2},{"id":"representation-and-c-api","text":"Représentation et API C++","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-7-L4","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native head\nsyrnck native branch feature REVISION\nsyrnck native switch-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native switch feature --progress\nsyrnck native status --combined\nsyrnck native add Source Content/Maps\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected feature changes\"\nsyrnck native log\nsyrnck native switch main\nsyrnck native branches --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native branches --after feature/topic --limit 100\nsyrnck native branch feature/topic REVISION --progress\nsyrnck native branch-delete-plan feature/topic --archive-index --verify\nsyrnck native branch-delete feature/topic --expect TIP_ID --archive-index --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck native branch-archives --limit 100 --verify\nsyrnck native branch-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nauto deletion = store.plan_delete_native_branch(\"feature\", true, true, operation);\nauto removed = store.delete_native_branch(\"feature\", expected_tip, expected_index, operation);\nauto saved = store.native_branch_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation);\nsyrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID\nsyrnck native branch-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic --verify\nsyrnck native branch-restore ARCHIVE_ID --as recovered/topic\nauto preview = store.plan_restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", true, operation);\nauto restored = store.restore_native_branch(archive_id, \"recovered\", operation);\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\nauto head = store.native_head();\nauto branches = store.native_branches({}, 100, true, operation);\nauto created = store.create_native_branch(\"feature\", revision_id, operation);\nauto plan = store.plan_switch(project, \"feature\", true, operation);\nauto switched = store.switch_directory(project, \"feature\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-detached","#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/native-detached","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-stage-rebase","#/fr/native-tags","#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/native-detached"],"kind":"guide","revision":"c3b78a30a33e"},{"id":"native-checkout","title":"Checkout récupérable","description":"Guide : Checkout récupérable.","section":"Versionnage","order":230,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"recoverable-native-checkout\">Checkout natif récupérable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-native-checkout\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Checkout natif récupérable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout\u003c/code> restaure une révision exacte dans un workspace existant et déplace la branche sélectionnée. Sa tête précédente est archivée avant toute libération de rétention. Il applique le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace\">preflight du workspace\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La branche par défaut est la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-branches\">courante\u003c/a>, implicitement \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> dans les anciens Stores ; répertoire \u003ccode>.\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--branch feature\u003c/code> déplace cette branche en prenant son snapshot comme base. \u003ccode>native switch BRANCH\u003c/code> change la sélection en conservant les deux têtes. Le Store doit être strictement à l’intérieur de la racine canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le CLI résout les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-tags\">sélecteurs\u003c/a>. C++ peut utiliser \u003ccode>resolve_native_revision()\u003c/code> ; l’API checkout prend toujours des IDs exacts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"starting-and-publishing\">Démarrer et publier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/starting-and-publishing\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Démarrer et publier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Tout fichier suivi doit correspondre à sa base par hash complet et métadonnées exécutables prises en charge. Un index, même propre, bloque. Collisions ignorées/non suivies, liens/jonctions, chemins protégés et alias portables ambigus sont refusés. Casse et transitions fichier/dossier propres utilisent l’exécuteur de namespace. Aucun \u003ccode>--force\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un changement répète le preflight et vérifie source, cible, parents et hashes complets avant le journal. Un plan métadonnées seul ne suffit pas. Cible égale à la tête et workspace propre : \u003ccode>state: unchanged\u003c/code>, aucune transaction. Les autres fichiers non suivis/ignorés restent intacts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque cible est reconstruite et vérifiée en slot privé, puis la source revérifiée est déplacée vers une sauvegarde et la cible installée par déplacement exclusif sans écrasement. Une suppression garde l’original ; un changement de représentation seul ne déplace rien ; un changement de mode prépare un fichier complet. Unix applique les bits exécutables ; Windows conserve les métadonnées sans les appliquer ni signaler un changement local.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après le dernier callback public, les fichiers désirés sont vérifiés. Une transaction compare-and-swap déplace la branche, valide le journal, archive la source et retient cible/reçu. Puis nettoyage vérifié et retrait des racines actives. Succès : \u003ccode>state: committed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pending: false\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code> ou null si branche non née.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’atomicité concerne les références, pas les déplacements séquentiels visibles. Arrêter les écrivains externes : aucun snapshot atomique, verrou du workspace ou défense contre remplacement hostile. Une erreur IO/annulation après création du journal demande une récupération explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspecting-resuming-and-aborting\">Inspecter, reprendre et abandonner\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/inspecting-resuming-and-aborting\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspecter, reprendre et abandonner\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sans opération active, \u003ccode>checkout-status\u003c/code> donne \u003ccode>pending: false\u003c/code>. Sinon : identité immuable, branche, source/cible, racine canonique, état, \u003ccode>data_directory\u003c/code> et chemins/slots numériques. \u003ccode>--id\u003c/code> inspecte l’actif ou un reçu terminé. Les champs/états JSON restent anglais.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>États et récupération : \u003ccode>preparing\u003c/code> termine la reconstruction puis revalide/applique ; abort jette les préparations. \u003ccode>applying\u003c/code> vérifie et termine ; abort restaure en ordre inverse. \u003ccode>evacuating\u003c/code> sauvegarde les sources/retire les dossiers requis ; \u003ccode>installing\u003c/code> installe puis publie ; abort désinstalle les cibles avant restauration. \u003ccode>uninstalling\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>restoring\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>aborting\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>discarding\u003c/code> poursuivent leur phase durable, quel que soit le nouvel appel. \u003ccode>aborted\u003c/code> ne fait que nettoyer. \u003ccode>committed\u003c/code> ne fait que nettoyer et refuse abort.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La récupération utilise exactement branche/répertoire enregistrés, sans override. Ouvrir le Store ne touche pas les fichiers ; copier/déplacer un journal n’est pas supporté. Une récupération terminée est idempotente. Si un fichier public/privé a changé, les octets sont préservés et l’étape risquée refusée : inspecter et sauvegarder les edits avant reprise. Une source modifiée juste avant sa sauvegarde est restaurée telle quelle. Le marqueur \u003ccode>aborted\u003c/code> précède le nettoyage pour ne pas confondre un fichier ultérieur. Des parents vides nouveaux peuvent rester ; aucune suppression récursive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pendant l’opération, GC même simulé, commit, add, reset, commit figé et détachement sont bloqués. Pack et consultation restent possibles. Les opérations génériques de références ne contournent pas la récupération : leurs mutations peuvent la bloquer. Ne pas éditer/répliquer les racines actives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"owned-recovery\">Reprise contrôlée par l’agent résident\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/owned-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reprise contrôlée par l’agent résident\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent\">agent résident bidirectionnel\u003c/a> marque ses checkouts entrants\net ceux des fusions avec un propriétaire explicite. Le journal v11 fige propriétaire,\ncontexte et les deux définitions de matérialisation, avec layout 1 ou 3 pour\nl’application ordinaire ou les transitions de namespace. Le propriétaire participe\nà l’ID immuable. \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> est établi avant observations persistantes ou\njournaux avec propriétaire ; les anciens lecteurs refusent le Store au lieu\nd’ignorer ce nouvel état. Les encodages sans propriétaire ne changent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_directory_owned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> owner_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout_owned\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> owner_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>La reprise vérifie propriétaire, contexte sélectionné, workspace physique actuel\net cible HEAD avant toute application. Cet ID sert à la coordination, pas à\nl’authentification face à un écrivain local autorisé du Store. L’agent exige aussi\nque son observation distante conservée soit ancêtre de la cible préparée. Une\nautre adresse, un autre workspace ou une session de publication seule restent bloqués.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une transaction active de l’agent peut finir hors ligne sans clé privée : la\ncible entrante ou de fusion signée est déjà figée et retenue. La reprise ne\nrecompose ni ne signe de révision. Une nouvelle opération signée exige toujours\nl’identité de signature. Les transactions manuelles ou étrangères demandent\n\u003ccode>checkout-recover\u003c/code> explicite ; ouvrir le Store seul ne reprend rien.\nLes reçus terminés restent consultables et retenus suivant les règles existantes.\nLes sauvegardes conservent exigences de lecteur et révisions complètes, sans\nactiver un ancien checkout physique dans un dossier restauré.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"journal-retention-and-private-data\">Journal, rétention et données privées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/journal-retention-and-private-data\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Journal, rétention et données privées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le journal immuable est un chunk ordinaire compressible/chiffrable/empaquetable. Identité : version, nonce, racine canonique, branche, source/cible. Switch v2 ajoute destination et HEAD. Les namespaces utilisent v3/v4 et \u003ccode>namespace_transition: true\u003c/code>, avec lecture v1/v2 conservée. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset\">hard reset\u003c/a> utilise v5 pour distinguer snapshot de travail, tête/index originaux et profil de répertoire. Chaque phase publie un nouveau journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Racines : \u003ccode>checkouts/active\u003c/code> (journal unique) ; \u003ccode>checkouts/pending/ID/target\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>/source\u003c/code> (fermetures jusqu’au nettoyage) ; \u003ccode>archive/checkout/ID\u003c/code> (ancienne tête) ; \u003ccode>checkouts/completed/ID\u003c/code> (cible publiée) ; \u003ccode>checkouts/receipts/ID\u003c/code> (journal validé consultable).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les racines actives partent après nettoyage ; archives, cibles et reçus restent retenus sans purge automatique. Supprimer une branche ne supprime pas cet historique. Retirer toutes les racines puis GC destructif rend ces versions irrécupérables. Aucun reçu permanent pour abort.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les slots \u003ccode>STORE/checkouts/ID/{new,old}/N.bin\u003c/code> contiennent du \u003cstrong>clair même avec Store chiffré\u003c/strong>. Les cibles et sauvegardes ne sont pas protégées par le chiffrement objet. Unix utilise des répertoires privés propriétaire ; Windows hérite des ACL du Store. Utiliser le chiffrement du système de fichiers pour ces octets. Prévoir la place des cibles ; les sauvegardes déplacent les originaux. Tous les chemins doivent supporter les déplacements exclusifs sur le même système de fichiers ; sinon journal conservé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le nettoyage vérifie noms, types et hashes, préserve tout contenu inattendu et ne retire que les dossiers vides. Windows peut réessayer leur retrait non récursif environ 500 ms. Les autres erreurs gardent le journal pour reprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-progress-and-limits\">C++, progression et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/c-progress-and-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers C++, progression et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Callbacks synchrones sans réentrée Store. Scan/plan/vérification gardent leurs événements ; préparation reconstruit par fichier, application/rollback signalent \u003ccode>exporting\u003c/code> avec octets logiques changés et vérifications. Les compteurs peuvent repartir entre phases/reprises. Le dernier observer avant publication est \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>, chemin \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code>. Avant ce point, annulation/erreur préserve la branche mais peut laisser workspace partiel et journal. Après publication, nettoyage sans observer/annulation : une erreur peut donc accompagner une branche déjà validée. Inspecter et reprendre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tampon partagé 1 Mio, tables, caches de manifestes, objets 16 Mio et inventaire borné s’appliquent. Preflight/final hashes et fermetures restent complets ; fichiers changés reconstruits entièrement et sauvegardes hashées avant nettoyage. Pas de patch en place ni matérialisation parallèle ; le cache de travail consultatif ne remplace pas ces validations. L’ABI C et Go n’exposent pas encore checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> pending \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> resumed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> aborted \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> receipt \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">checkout_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>transaction_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"recoverable-namespace-transitions\">Transitions de namespace récupérables\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Transitions de namespace récupérables\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les fichiers suivis propres peuvent changer de casse, parents inclus, ou passer fichier/dossier : \u003ccode>Source/Code.cpp\u003c/code> vers \u003ccode>source/code.cpp\u003c/code>, fichier \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> vers \u003ccode>Content/Map.uasset\u003c/code>, arbre entièrement suivi vers fichier \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code>. Les autres renommages sont suppressions/ajouts ; le suivi historique exact est \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-file-history\">distinct\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan contrôle casse portable exacte et propreté complète, puis indique \u003ccode>namespace_transition\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>remove_directories\u003c/code>. Chaque dossier affecté doit contenir uniquement fichiers sources et parents connus. Descendants ignorés/non suivis, dossiers vides inconnus, liens et stockage protégé bloquent avant journal. Des parents portables de casses incompatibles dans un snapshot sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Toutes les cibles sont préparées, puis \u003cstrong>toutes les sources changées\u003c/strong> évacuées avant installation. Seuls les dossiers dérivés du snapshot nécessaires sont retirés, du plus profond, et seulement vides. \u003ccode>installing\u003c/code> est persisté avant parents et déplacements. Hashes finaux, namespace et publication atomique conservent les garanties.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Abort remet les cibles dans leurs slots, retire les seuls dossiers cibles vides requis, persiste \u003ccode>restoring\u003c/code>, puis rétablit sources et orthographe. Des phases durables distinctes évitent de confondre originaux restaurés et cibles. Les edits ou nouvelles obstructions sont préservés et bloquent la récupération. Aucune récupération automatique à l’ouverture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Switch, merge, replay et rebase linéaire partagent cet exécuteur. Switch préserve les têtes et publie HEAD avec le journal. Aucun nouveau format d’objet/pointeur/révision/réseau ; les vieux moteurs doivent refuser les versions de journal inconnues. V6 prend en charge l’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stash\">application de stash\u003c/a> sans déplacer la tête, avec index et pop publiés ensemble. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention\">retrait des reçus terminés\u003c/a> exige une sauvegarde indépendante ; leur consultation historique n’exige pas l’ancien HEAD ou snapshot source volontairement retiré, contrairement à la récupération active.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Checkout natif récupérable # native checkout restaure une révision exacte dans un workspace existant et déplace la branche sélectionnée. Sa tête précédente est archivée avant toute libération de rétention. Il applique le preflight du workspace . La branche par défaut est la courante , implicitement main dans les anciens Stores ; répertoire . . --branch feature déplace cette branche en prenant son snapshot comme base. native switch BRANCH change la sélection en conservant les deux têtes. Le Store doit être strictement à l’intérieur de la racine canonique. Le CLI résout les sélecteurs . C++ peut utiliser resolve_native_revision() ; l’API checkout prend toujours des IDs exacts. text Copier syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID Démarrer et publier # Tout fichier suivi doit correspondre à sa base par hash complet et métadonnées exécutables prises en charge. Un index, même propre, bloque. Collisions ignorées/non suivies, liens/jonctions, chemins protégés et alias portables ambigus sont refusés. Casse et transitions fichier/dossier propres utilisent l’exécuteur de namespace. Aucun --force . Un changement répète le preflight et vérifie source, cible, parents et hashes complets avant le journal. Un plan métadonnées seul ne suffit pas. Cible égale à la tête et workspace propre : state: unchanged , aucune transaction. Les autres fichiers non suivis/ignorés restent intacts. Chaque cible est reconstruite et vérifiée en slot privé, puis la source revérifiée est déplacée vers une sauvegarde et la cible installée par déplacement exclusif sans écrasement. Une suppression garde l’original ; un changement de représentation seul ne déplace rien ; un changement de mode prépare un fichier complet. Unix applique les bits exécutables ; Windows conserve les métadonnées sans les appliquer ni signaler un changement local. Après le dernier callback public, les fichiers désirés sont vérifiés. Une transaction compare-and-swap déplace la branche, valide le journal, archive la source et retient cible/reçu. Puis nettoyage vérifié et retrait des racines actives. Succès : state: committed , pending: false , id , archive ou null si branche non née. L’atomicité concerne les références, pas les déplacements séquentiels visibles. Arrêter les écrivains externes : aucun snapshot atomique, verrou du workspace ou défense contre remplacement hostile. Une erreur IO/annulation après création du journal demande une récupération explicite. Inspecter, reprendre et abandonner # Sans opération active, checkout-status donne pending: false . Sinon : identité immuable, branche, source/cible, racine canonique, état, data_directory et chemins/slots numériques. --id inspecte l’actif ou un reçu terminé. Les champs/états JSON restent anglais. États et récupération : preparing termine la reconstruction puis revalide/applique ; abort jette les préparations. applying vérifie et termine ; abort restaure en ordre inverse. evacuating sauvegarde les sources/retire les dossiers requis ; installing installe puis publie ; abort désinstalle les cibles avant restauration. uninstalling , restoring , aborting , discarding poursuivent leur phase durable, quel que soit le nouvel appel. aborted ne fait que nettoyer. committed ne fait que nettoyer et refuse abort. La récupération utilise exactement branche/répertoire enregistrés, sans override. Ouvrir le Store ne touche pas les fichiers ; copier/déplacer un journal n’est pas supporté. Une récupération terminée est idempotente. Si un fichier public/privé a changé, les octets sont préservés et l’étape risquée refusée : inspecter et sauvegarder les edits avant reprise. Une source modifiée juste avant sa sauvegarde est restaurée telle quelle. Le marqueur aborted précède le nettoyage pour ne pas confondre un fichier ultérieur. Des parents vides nouveaux peuvent rester ; aucune suppression récursive. Pendant l’opération, GC même simulé, commit, add, reset, commit figé et détachement sont bloqués. Pack et consultation restent possibles. Les opérations génériques de références ne contournent pas la récupération : leurs mutations peuvent la bloquer. Ne pas éditer/répliquer les racines actives. Reprise contrôlée par l’agent résident # L’ agent résident bidirectionnel marque ses checkouts entrants et ceux des fusions avec un propriétaire explicite. Le journal v11 fige propriétaire, contexte et les deux définitions de matérialisation, avec layout 1 ou 3 pour l’application ordinaire ou les transitions de namespace. Le propriétaire participe à l’ID immuable. resident-sync-v1 est établi avant observations persistantes ou journaux avec propriétaire ; les anciens lecteurs refusent le Store au lieu d’ignorer ce nouvel état. Les encodages sans propriétaire ne changent pas. cpp Copier store . checkout_directory_owned ( project , \"main\" , target , owner_id , operation ) ; store . recover_checkout_owned ( project , \"main\" , owner_id , operation ) ; La reprise vérifie propriétaire, contexte sélectionné, workspace physique actuel et cible HEAD avant toute application. Cet ID sert à la coordination, pas à l’authentification face à un écrivain local autorisé du Store. L’agent exige aussi que son observation distante conservée soit ancêtre de la cible préparée. Une autre adresse, un autre workspace ou une session de publication seule restent bloqués. Une transaction active de l’agent peut finir hors ligne sans clé privée : la cible entrante ou de fusion signée est déjà figée et retenue. La reprise ne recompose ni ne signe de révision. Une nouvelle opération signée exige toujours l’identité de signature. Les transactions manuelles ou étrangères demandent checkout-recover explicite ; ouvrir le Store seul ne reprend rien. Les reçus terminés restent consultables et retenus suivant les règles existantes. Les sauvegardes conservent exigences de lecteur et révisions complètes, sans activer un ancien checkout physique dans un dossier restauré. Journal, rétention et données privées # Le journal immuable est un chunk ordinaire compressible/chiffrable/empaquetable. Identité : version, nonce, racine canonique, branche, source/cible. Switch v2 ajoute destination et HEAD. Les namespaces utilisent v3/v4 et namespace_transition: true , avec lecture v1/v2 conservée. Le hard reset utilise v5 pour distinguer snapshot de travail, tête/index originaux et profil de répertoire. Chaque phase publie un nouveau journal. Racines : checkouts/active (journal unique) ; checkouts/pending/ID/target et /source (fermetures jusqu’au nettoyage) ; archive/checkout/ID (ancienne tête) ; checkouts/completed/ID (cible publiée) ; checkouts/receipts/ID (journal validé consultable). Les racines actives partent après nettoyage ; archives, cibles et reçus restent retenus sans purge automatique. Supprimer une branche ne supprime pas cet historique. Retirer toutes les racines puis GC destructif rend ces versions irrécupérables. Aucun reçu permanent pour abort. Les slots STORE/checkouts/ID/{new,old}/N.bin contiennent du clair même avec Store chiffré . Les cibles et sauvegardes ne sont pas protégées par le chiffrement objet. Unix utilise des répertoires privés propriétaire ; Windows hérite des ACL du Store. Utiliser le chiffrement du système de fichiers pour ces octets. Prévoir la place des cibles ; les sauvegardes déplacent les originaux. Tous les chemins doivent supporter les déplacements exclusifs sur le même système de fichiers ; sinon journal conservé. Le nettoyage vérifie noms, types et hashes, préserve tout contenu inattendu et ne retire que les dossiers vides. Windows peut réessayer leur retrait non récursif environ 500 ms. Les autres erreurs gardent le journal pour reprise. C++, progression et limites # Callbacks synchrones sans réentrée Store. Scan/plan/vérification gardent leurs événements ; préparation reconstruit par fichier, application/rollback signalent exporting avec octets logiques changés et vérifications. Les compteurs peuvent repartir entre phases/reprises. Le dernier observer avant publication est publishing , chemin checkout . Avant ce point, annulation/erreur préserve la branche mais peut laisser workspace partiel et journal. Après publication, nettoyage sans observer/annulation : une erreur peut donc accompagner une branche déjà validée. Inspecter et reprendre. Tampon partagé 1 Mio, tables, caches de manifestes, objets 16 Mio et inventaire borné s’appliquent. Preflight/final hashes et fermetures restent complets ; fichiers changés reconstruits entièrement et sauvegardes hashées avant nettoyage. Pas de patch en place ni matérialisation parallèle ; le cache de travail consultatif ne remplace pas ces validations. L’ABI C et Go n’exposent pas encore checkout. cpp Copier auto result = store . checkout_directory ( project , \"main\" , target_id , operation ) ; auto pending = store . checkout_status ( ) ; auto resumed = store . recover_checkout ( false , operation ) ; auto aborted = store . recover_checkout ( true , operation ) ; auto receipt = store . checkout_status ( transaction_id ) ; Transitions de namespace récupérables # Les fichiers suivis propres peuvent changer de casse, parents inclus, ou passer fichier/dossier : Source/Code.cpp vers source/code.cpp , fichier Content vers Content/Map.uasset , arbre entièrement suivi vers fichier Content . Les autres renommages sont suppressions/ajouts ; le suivi historique exact est distinct . Le plan contrôle casse portable exacte et propreté complète, puis indique namespace_transition et remove_directories . Chaque dossier affecté doit contenir uniquement fichiers sources et parents connus. Descendants ignorés/non suivis, dossiers vides inconnus, liens et stockage protégé bloquent avant journal. Des parents portables de casses incompatibles dans un snapshot sont refusés. Toutes les cibles sont préparées, puis toutes les sources changées évacuées avant installation. Seuls les dossiers dérivés du snapshot nécessaires sont retirés, du plus profond, et seulement vides. installing est persisté avant parents et déplacements. Hashes finaux, namespace et publication atomique conservent les garanties. Abort remet les cibles dans leurs slots, retire les seuls dossiers cibles vides requis, persiste restoring , puis rétablit sources et orthographe. Des phases durables distinctes évitent de confondre originaux restaurés et cibles. Les edits ou nouvelles obstructions sont préservés et bloquent la récupération. Aucune récupération automatique à l’ouverture. Switch, merge, replay et rebase linéaire partagent cet exécuteur. Switch préserve les têtes et publie HEAD avec le journal. Aucun nouveau format d’objet/pointeur/révision/réseau ; les vieux moteurs doivent refuser les versions de journal inconnues. V6 prend en charge l’ application de stash sans déplacer la tête, avec index et pop publiés ensemble. Le retrait des reçus terminés exige une sauvegarde indépendante ; leur consultation historique n’exige pas l’ancien HEAD ou snapshot source volontairement retiré, contrairement à la récupération active. syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify syrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID store.checkout_directory_owned(project, \"main\", target, owner_id, operation); store.recover_checkout_owned(project, \"main\", owner_id, operation); auto result = store.checkout_directory(project, \"main\", target_id, operation); auto pending = store.checkout_status(); auto resumed = store.recover_checkout(false, operation); auto aborted = store.recover_checkout(true, operation); auto receipt = store.checkout_status(transaction_id);","headings":[{"id":"recoverable-native-checkout","text":"Checkout natif récupérable","level":1},{"id":"starting-and-publishing","text":"Démarrer et publier","level":2},{"id":"inspecting-resuming-and-aborting","text":"Inspecter, reprendre et abandonner","level":2},{"id":"owned-recovery","text":"Reprise contrôlée par l’agent résident","level":2},{"id":"journal-retention-and-private-data","text":"Journal, rétention et données privées","level":2},{"id":"c-progress-and-limits","text":"C++, progression et limites","level":2},{"id":"recoverable-namespace-transitions","text":"Transitions de namespace récupérables","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native checkout-plan REVISION --branch main --verify\nsyrnck native checkout REVISION --branch main --directory . --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status --id TRANSACTION_ID\nstore.checkout_directory_owned(project, \"main\", target, owner_id, operation);\nstore.recover_checkout_owned(project, \"main\", owner_id, operation);\nauto result = store.checkout_directory(project, \"main\", target_id, operation);\nauto pending = store.checkout_status();\nauto resumed = store.recover_checkout(false, operation);\nauto aborted = store.recover_checkout(true, operation);\nauto receipt = store.checkout_status(transaction_id);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspace","#/fr/native-branches","#/fr/native-tags","#/fr/resident-agent","#/fr/native-hard-reset","#/fr/native-file-history","#/fr/native-stash","#/fr/checkout-retention"],"kind":"guide","revision":"d2a7064b57a1"},{"id":"native-comparison","title":"Statut et comparaison de snapshots","description":"Guide : Statut et comparaison de snapshots.","section":"Versionnage","order":240,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison\">Statut du dossier et comparaison des snapshots\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-comparison/native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Statut du dossier et comparaison des snapshots\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le moteur inspecte un workspace et compare deux snapshots sans importer de fichiers ou publier de références. Résultats JSON anglais canoniques ; progression et diagnostics anglais/français avec les options habituelles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native status --combined --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"working-directory-status\">Statut du dossier de travail\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-comparison/working-directory-status\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Statut du dossier de travail\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’option \u003ca href=\"#/fr/working-index\">--indexed\u003c/a> possède un contrat de réutilisation distinct et peut écrire son cache. Sans elle, le statut lit intégralement les contenus sans écrire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La base par défaut est la branche native courante, main implicitement pour les anciens Stores. --branch choisit une autre branche ; absente, elle forme une base vide et les fichiers sont non suivis. --revision choisit une révision explicite valide et exclut --branch. --combined rend le schéma séparé base/index/workspace et exclut --revision ; le statut ordinaire compare branche et disque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque fichier ordinaire sélectionné est lu et hashé en BLAKE3, même avec taille/date identiques. Champs : base, clean, tracked_files, workspace_files, hashed_bytes, counts, changes triés. Un résultat propre compare les octets aux hashes de manifestes sans auditer leurs blocs stockés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Statuts : untracked sans entrée de base ; removed absent de la sélection de travail ; modified pour contenu ou mode différent ; unchanged dans les compteurs. Chaque changement donne path/status/source/target/content_changed/executable_changed, côtés absents null. Résumés : manifest/size/file_hash/executable ; manifeste de travail null car aucun import. Ajout/retrait implique content_changed ; mode comparé seulement avec deux côtés. Tout changement, non-suivis compris, rend clean false ; le code reste 0 si la requête réussit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le scanner partagé avec commit applique ignores imbriqués et négations, exclusions .git/.syrnck/Store, chemins portables et collisions de casse. Le Store doit être intérieur au dossier ; les ignores sont des fichiers ordinaires ; liens et fichiers spéciaux sélectionnés sont refusés et les exclus ne sont pas suivis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les ignores s’appliquent même aux suivis : ignorer un fichier suivi le signale removed et l’exclut du prochain commit ordinaire, même encore présent sur disque. L’index natif peut figer les entrées indépendamment. Unix inspecte le bit exécutable ; Windows enregistre false et ignore les différences exécutables locales avec un snapshot Unix. Le diff de snapshots continue à les signaler partout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revision-snapshot-comparison\">Comparer deux révisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-comparison/revision-snapshot-comparison\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comparer deux révisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>native diff lit seulement les métadonnées vérifiées des révisions et de tous leurs manifestes, inchangés compris, sans blocs ni parcours des parents. Il peut inspecter un Store partiel de métadonnées ; seul un audit complet prouve la disponibilité des payloads/ancêtres.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le CLI accepte IDs, HEAD et noms non ambigus, ou heads/NAME/tags/NAME. Les méthodes C++ reçoivent des IDs ; la résolution reste séparée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Champs : source, target, equal, source_files, target_files, counts, changes triés. Statuts added/removed/modified et compteur unchanged. Résumés identiques au statut, avec manifest_changed. Un autre découpage peut décrire les mêmes octets : modified avec manifeste changé mais contenu identique. Les modes sont distincts ; messages, dates et parents n’affectent pas l’égalité des entrées. Sans option, un renommage reste suppression/ajout ; \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-file-history\">--renames\u003c/a> détecte les correspondances exactes. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">Diff textuel\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-patches\">patches\u003c/a>, fusion et checkout ont leurs opérations propres.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-and-operation-boundaries\">API et frontières d’observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-comparison/c-and-operation-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API et frontières d’observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>En C++, baseline_revision est optionnel ; sans lui la base est vide, la sélection de branche appartenant au CLI. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions C/Go\u003c/a> fournissent les statuts de workspace ; les mutations générales d’historique restent séparées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Callbacks synchrones non réentrants : scanning pour dossiers, verifying pour octets des fichiers, planning pour comparaisons ; compteurs remis par phase. Annulation/exception : aucun rapport partiel ni changement. JSON écrit au succès seulement ; verrou exclusif et récupération normale restent requis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tampon 1 Mio, FIFO de 1024 résumés de manifeste, snapshots et rapport complets en mémoire, objets de 16 Mio maximum. Le scanner refuse un snapshot plat dont la borne minimale dépasse déjà 16 Mio ; ce n’est pas un plafond global de RAM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Taille/date/type/mode Unix sont contrôlés autour de la lecture, croissance comprise, sans verrou de workspace ni snapshot atomique. Les modifications concurrentes peuvent imposer un nouvel essai et celles postérieures périmer le statut. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/working-index\">Index de travail\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-watch\">observateur\u003c/a> accélèrent des observations avec leurs propres limites, sans remplacer ces garanties.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">status_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> baseline_revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> empty_baseline \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">status_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> difference \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">compare_revisions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Statut du dossier et comparaison des snapshots # Le moteur inspecte un workspace et compare deux snapshots sans importer de fichiers ou publier de références. Résultats JSON anglais canoniques ; progression et diagnostics anglais/français avec les options habituelles. text Copier syrnck native status syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json syrnck native status --combined --branch main syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION Statut du dossier de travail # L’option --indexed possède un contrat de réutilisation distinct et peut écrire son cache. Sans elle, le statut lit intégralement les contenus sans écrire. La base par défaut est la branche native courante, main implicitement pour les anciens Stores. --branch choisit une autre branche ; absente, elle forme une base vide et les fichiers sont non suivis. --revision choisit une révision explicite valide et exclut --branch. --combined rend le schéma séparé base/index/workspace et exclut --revision ; le statut ordinaire compare branche et disque. Chaque fichier ordinaire sélectionné est lu et hashé en BLAKE3, même avec taille/date identiques. Champs : base, clean, tracked_files, workspace_files, hashed_bytes, counts, changes triés. Un résultat propre compare les octets aux hashes de manifestes sans auditer leurs blocs stockés. Statuts : untracked sans entrée de base ; removed absent de la sélection de travail ; modified pour contenu ou mode différent ; unchanged dans les compteurs. Chaque changement donne path/status/source/target/content_changed/executable_changed, côtés absents null. Résumés : manifest/size/file_hash/executable ; manifeste de travail null car aucun import. Ajout/retrait implique content_changed ; mode comparé seulement avec deux côtés. Tout changement, non-suivis compris, rend clean false ; le code reste 0 si la requête réussit. Le scanner partagé avec commit applique ignores imbriqués et négations, exclusions .git/.syrnck/Store, chemins portables et collisions de casse. Le Store doit être intérieur au dossier ; les ignores sont des fichiers ordinaires ; liens et fichiers spéciaux sélectionnés sont refusés et les exclus ne sont pas suivis. Les ignores s’appliquent même aux suivis : ignorer un fichier suivi le signale removed et l’exclut du prochain commit ordinaire, même encore présent sur disque. L’index natif peut figer les entrées indépendamment. Unix inspecte le bit exécutable ; Windows enregistre false et ignore les différences exécutables locales avec un snapshot Unix. Le diff de snapshots continue à les signaler partout. Comparer deux révisions # native diff lit seulement les métadonnées vérifiées des révisions et de tous leurs manifestes, inchangés compris, sans blocs ni parcours des parents. Il peut inspecter un Store partiel de métadonnées ; seul un audit complet prouve la disponibilité des payloads/ancêtres. Le CLI accepte IDs, HEAD et noms non ambigus, ou heads/NAME/tags/NAME. Les méthodes C++ reçoivent des IDs ; la résolution reste séparée. Champs : source, target, equal, source_files, target_files, counts, changes triés. Statuts added/removed/modified et compteur unchanged. Résumés identiques au statut, avec manifest_changed. Un autre découpage peut décrire les mêmes octets : modified avec manifeste changé mais contenu identique. Les modes sont distincts ; messages, dates et parents n’affectent pas l’égalité des entrées. Sans option, un renommage reste suppression/ajout ; --renames détecte les correspondances exactes. Diff textuel , patches , fusion et checkout ont leurs opérations propres. API et frontières d’observation # En C++, baseline_revision est optionnel ; sans lui la base est vide, la sélection de branche appartenant au CLI. Les sessions C/Go fournissent les statuts de workspace ; les mutations générales d’historique restent séparées. Callbacks synchrones non réentrants : scanning pour dossiers, verifying pour octets des fichiers, planning pour comparaisons ; compteurs remis par phase. Annulation/exception : aucun rapport partiel ni changement. JSON écrit au succès seulement ; verrou exclusif et récupération normale restent requis. Tampon 1 Mio, FIFO de 1024 résumés de manifeste, snapshots et rapport complets en mémoire, objets de 16 Mio maximum. Le scanner refuse un snapshot plat dont la borne minimale dépasse déjà 16 Mio ; ce n’est pas un plafond global de RAM. Taille/date/type/mode Unix sont contrôlés autour de la lecture, croissance comprise, sans verrou de workspace ni snapshot atomique. Les modifications concurrentes peuvent imposer un nouvel essai et celles postérieures périmer le statut. Index de travail et observateur accélèrent des observations avec leurs propres limites, sans remplacer ces garanties. cpp Copier auto status = store . status_directory ( project , baseline_revision , operation ) ; auto empty_baseline = store . status_directory ( project ) ; auto difference = store . compare_revisions ( source_id , target_id , operation ) ; syrnck native status syrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT syrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json syrnck native status --combined --branch main syrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION auto status = store.status_directory(project, baseline_revision, operation); auto empty_baseline = store.status_directory(project); auto difference = store.compare_revisions(source_id, target_id, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-working-directory-status-and-snapshot-comparison","text":"Statut du dossier et comparaison des snapshots","level":1},{"id":"working-directory-status","text":"Statut du dossier de travail","level":2},{"id":"revision-snapshot-comparison","text":"Comparer deux révisions","level":2},{"id":"c-and-operation-boundaries","text":"API et frontières d’observation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status\nsyrnck native status --branch feature --directory PROJECT\nsyrnck native status --revision REVISION --progress-json\nsyrnck native status --combined --branch main\nsyrnck native diff SOURCE_REVISION TARGET_REVISION\nauto status = store.status_directory(project, baseline_revision, operation);\nauto empty_baseline = store.status_directory(project);\nauto difference = store.compare_revisions(source_id, target_id, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/working-index","#/fr/native-file-history","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/text-patches","#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/working-index","#/fr/native-watch"],"kind":"guide","revision":"ffa4c58d227c"},{"id":"native-detached","title":"HEAD natif détaché","description":"Explorer des révisions et committer indépendamment des branches nommées.","section":"Versionnage","order":221,"parent":"native-branches","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Sélectionner une révision détachée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-detached/usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélectionner une révision détachée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native switch --detach tags/release\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native branch rescue HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native switch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>switch --detach REVISION\u003c/code> restaure un snapshot committé sans sélectionner ni\ndéplacer une branche nommée. Les sélecteurs de révision sont ceux du checkout.\n\u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> indique \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>mode: &quot;detached&quot;\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>reference: &quot;workspace/detached&quot;\u003c/code> et la révision committée. Aucune fausse branche\nn’apparaît dans \u003ccode>native branches\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>detach-history\u003c/code> est une autre opération :\nelle crée une nouvelle racine d’ascendance pour la cible sélectionnée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Par défaut, commits natifs, préparation, statut, historique, réécritures, stash et\nobservation du workspace utilisent la révision détachée et son index indépendant.\nLes commits prennent la révision détachée précédente comme parent. Une option\nexplicite \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code> opère toujours sur cette branche sans la sélectionner.\nLa publication native distante exige une branche nommée ; créer une branche de\nsauvetage avant de publier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native checkout REVISION\u003c/code> déplace la cible sélectionnée : branche en mode\nattaché, révision privée en mode détaché. \u003ccode>native switch NAME\u003c/code> revient sur une\nbranche et restaure ses fichiers ; aucune tête de branche ne bouge lors du switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Préflight et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-detached/recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préflight et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les plans sont en lecture seule. Fichiers suivis modifiés, index sur l’une des\ncibles, collisions non suivies et namespaces dangereux bloquent le détachement\nou le retour à une branche selon les règles ordinaires du checkout. Aucun\nécrasement ou reset d’index implicite. Les transitions fichier/dossier utilisent\nle même exécuteur récupérable de namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant de détacher un workspace attaché, la destination est vérifiée et retenue\ndans un slot privé. Remplacer une ligne détachée inactive archive son ancienne\ntête sous \u003ccode>archive/OLD_ID\u003c/code>. Une interruption durant cette préparation laisse\nHEAD et les fichiers d’origine intacts, avec un candidat retenu supplémentaire.\nDès que le journal de checkout existe, employer \u003ccode>native checkout-status\u003c/code> puis\n\u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> (ou \u003ccode>--abort\u003c/code>). HEAD change avec le journal terminé.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Rétention et formats\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-detached/retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention et formats\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La racine de révision privée \u003ccode>workspace/detached\u003c/code> et l’index optionnel\n\u003ccode>workspace/staging\u003c/code> retiennent toutes leurs dépendances pendant le GC. Revenir\nsur une branche conserve la tête détachée. Archives, branches de sauvetage et\ntags protègent les anciennes lignes ; les sauvegardes portables conservent ces\nracines de données privées en omettant le HEAD lié à la machine. Les règles de\nretrait explicite habituelles restent applicables aux archives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les descripteurs HEAD nommés gardent leur version canonique 1. Les descripteurs\ndétachés utilisent la version 2 avec \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code> et les mêmes champs directory/type.\nLes reçus de checkout impliquant une cible détachée utilisent la version 7 et\n\u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> pour le layout correspondant, de 1 à 6. Les reçus entre branches\ngardent leurs formats. Les stash détachés passent en version 2, ceux des branches\nrestent en version 1. Les anciens exécuteurs doivent refuser les nouveaux reçus.\nFormats d’objets/révisions, formats physiques des packs et version ABI C inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">Intégration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-detached/api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Intégration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>Store::native_target()\u003c/code> retourne une branche nommée ou le sélecteur opaque \u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code>.\nLe passer aux API natives prenant une branche/cible opérationnelle. Les API de\nsnapshots prennent toujours des IDs : appeler \u003ccode>resolve_native_revision(&quot;HEAD&quot;)\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code> ne peut pas être un nom de branche. Les anciens champs de plan/statut\nnommés \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code> contiennent ce sélecteur en mode détaché, tandis que\n\u003ccode>native_head().branch\u003c/code> vaut null. Les API workspace C/Go suivent aussi HEAD détaché\nlorsque le sélecteur est omis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_detached_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ready\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">bool\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">detach_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> target \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> changes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Chaque handle Store sélectionne un contexte. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces liés\u003c/a>\npartagent objets et branches avec des HEAD et index indépendants. Matérialisation\npartielle, déplacement en place et désenregistrement archivé restent à réaliser.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Sélectionner une révision détachée # text Copier syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify syrnck native switch --detach tags/release syrnck native head syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\" syrnck native branch rescue HEAD syrnck native switch main switch --detach REVISION restaure un snapshot committé sans sélectionner ni déplacer une branche nommée. Les sélecteurs de révision sont ceux du checkout. native head indique branch: null , mode: \"detached\" , reference: \"workspace/detached\" et la révision committée. Aucune fausse branche n’apparaît dans native branches . detach-history est une autre opération : elle crée une nouvelle racine d’ascendance pour la cible sélectionnée. Par défaut, commits natifs, préparation, statut, historique, réécritures, stash et observation du workspace utilisent la révision détachée et son index indépendant. Les commits prennent la révision détachée précédente comme parent. Une option explicite --branch NAME opère toujours sur cette branche sans la sélectionner. La publication native distante exige une branche nommée ; créer une branche de sauvetage avant de publier. native checkout REVISION déplace la cible sélectionnée : branche en mode attaché, révision privée en mode détaché. native switch NAME revient sur une branche et restaure ses fichiers ; aucune tête de branche ne bouge lors du switch. Préflight et récupération # Les plans sont en lecture seule. Fichiers suivis modifiés, index sur l’une des cibles, collisions non suivies et namespaces dangereux bloquent le détachement ou le retour à une branche selon les règles ordinaires du checkout. Aucun écrasement ou reset d’index implicite. Les transitions fichier/dossier utilisent le même exécuteur récupérable de namespace. Avant de détacher un workspace attaché, la destination est vérifiée et retenue dans un slot privé. Remplacer une ligne détachée inactive archive son ancienne tête sous archive/OLD_ID . Une interruption durant cette préparation laisse HEAD et les fichiers d’origine intacts, avec un candidat retenu supplémentaire. Dès que le journal de checkout existe, employer native checkout-status puis native checkout-recover (ou --abort ). HEAD change avec le journal terminé. Rétention et formats # La racine de révision privée workspace/detached et l’index optionnel workspace/staging retiennent toutes leurs dépendances pendant le GC. Revenir sur une branche conserve la tête détachée. Archives, branches de sauvetage et tags protègent les anciennes lignes ; les sauvegardes portables conservent ces racines de données privées en omettant le HEAD lié à la machine. Les règles de retrait explicite habituelles restent applicables aux archives. Les descripteurs HEAD nommés gardent leur version canonique 1. Les descripteurs détachés utilisent la version 2 avec branch: null et les mêmes champs directory/type. Les reçus de checkout impliquant une cible détachée utilisent la version 7 et layout_version pour le layout correspondant, de 1 à 6. Les reçus entre branches gardent leurs formats. Les stash détachés passent en version 2, ceux des branches restent en version 1. Les anciens exécuteurs doivent refuser les nouveaux reçus. Formats d’objets/révisions, formats physiques des packs et version ABI C inchangés. Intégration # Store::native_target() retourne une branche nommée ou le sélecteur opaque :HEAD . Le passer aux API natives prenant une branche/cible opérationnelle. Les API de snapshots prennent toujours des IDs : appeler resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\") . :HEAD ne peut pas être un nom de branche. Les anciens champs de plan/statut nommés branch contiennent ce sélecteur en mode détaché, tandis que native_head().branch vaut null. Les API workspace C/Go suivent aussi HEAD détaché lorsque le sélecteur est omis. cpp Copier auto plan = store . plan_detached_checkout ( workspace , revision , true , operation ) ; if ( plan [ \"ready\" ] . get \u003c bool > ( ) ) store . detach_checkout ( workspace , revision , operation ) ; auto target = store . native_target ( ) ; auto changes = store . workspace_status ( workspace , target , operation ) ; Chaque handle Store sélectionne un contexte. Les workspaces liés partagent objets et branches avec des HEAD et index indépendants. Matérialisation partielle, déplacement en place et désenregistrement archivé restent à réaliser. syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify syrnck native switch --detach tags/release syrnck native head syrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\" syrnck native branch rescue HEAD syrnck native switch main auto plan = store.plan_detached_checkout(workspace, revision, true, operation); if (plan[\"ready\"].get\u003cbool>()) store.detach_checkout(workspace, revision, operation); auto target = store.native_target(); auto changes = store.workspace_status(workspace, target, operation);","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Sélectionner une révision détachée","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Préflight et récupération","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Rétention et formats","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"Intégration","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native switch-plan --detach tags/release --verify\nsyrnck native switch --detach tags/release\nsyrnck native head\nsyrnck native commit -m \"Detached experiment\"\nsyrnck native branch rescue HEAD\nsyrnck native switch main\nauto plan = store.plan_detached_checkout(workspace, revision, true, operation);\nif (plan[\"ready\"].get\u003cbool>())\n  store.detach_checkout(workspace, revision, operation);\nauto target = store.native_target();\nauto changes = store.workspace_status(workspace, target, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspaces"],"kind":"guide","revision":"5898e19025fc"},{"id":"native-file-history","title":"Renommages exacts et historique de fichiers","description":"Guide : Renommages exacts et historique de fichiers.","section":"Versionnage","order":250,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history\">Renommages exacts et historique par premier parent\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-file-history/exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Renommages exacts et historique par premier parent\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les API de \u003ccode>syrnck/history.hpp\u003c/code> comparent les renommages exacts et parcourent l’historique de fichiers en lisant seulement les métadonnées de manifestes vérifiées. Elles ne lisent pas le workspace, ne changent pas les références et ne reconstruisent pas les payloads. Elles ne remplacent pas un audit complet d’intégrité.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"exact-rename-comparison\">Comparer des renommages exacts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-file-history/exact-rename-comparison\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comparer des renommages exacts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native diff --renames\u003c/code> compare les snapshots puis associe un chemin supprimé à un ajouté si taille et hash BLAKE3 complet correspondent exactement, avec correspondance unique dans les deux sens. Cela couvre déplacements, casse/orthographe Unicode et découpages/compressions différents. Le changement de mode exécutable reste distinct.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une ligne \u003ccode>status: renamed\u003c/code> contient \u003ccode>path\u003c/code> cible, \u003ccode>old_path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>similarity: 100\u003c/code>, métadonnées source/cible et indicateurs séparés de contenu/mode/manifeste. Le compteur renamed augmente tandis que les ajouts/suppressions associés diminuent. Sans option, le diff conserve les ajouts/suppressions habituels.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plusieurs correspondances de contenu identique restent des ajouts/suppressions avec \u003ccode>rename_ambiguous\u003c/code> et compteurs d’ambiguïté ; une copie inchangée existante ne devient pas un renommage. Renommages édités, heuristiques de similarité, copies, permutations de chemins existants et résolutions de merge/rebase tenant compte des renommages ne sont pas inférés. Le JSON reste borné à 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"follow-a-file-lineage\">Suivre une lignée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-file-history/follow-a-file-lineage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Suivre une lignée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native log-file REVISION PATH\u003c/code> suit le premier parent et rend les changements du fichier : contenu, mode, représentation et suppression. Il suit en arrière les renommages exacts uniques et saute commits sans rapport ou vides. Un chemin déjà supprimé/renommé au départ peut montrer son passé. Chaque entrée porte révision, chemin à cette révision, aperçu de message borné, date, parents et changement de snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le premier ajout termine la lignée. Une origine ambiguë s’arrête avec \u003ccode>stop_reason: ambiguous_rename\u003c/code>, sans choix arbitraire. \u003ccode>--no-follow\u003c/code> s’arrête aux ajouts du chemin demandé, y compris ceux provenant d’un renommage. Une fusion est comparée à son premier parent ; les autres parents sont décrits mais non parcourus. Ce n’est ni un blame multi-parent ni un moteur de recherche historique général.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Défaut 100 changements, plage 1..1000 ; budget distinct de 1000 révisions inspectées, plage 1..10000. Une page incomplète rend \u003ccode>next_revision\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>next_path\u003c/code>, à reprendre ensemble même après renommage. \u003ccode>complete\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code> distinguent added, ambiguous_rename, root, page_limit, traversal_limit. Ces requêtes ne créent pas de pins ; les racines normales doivent protéger les versions contre un retrait/GC administratif entre appels.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Renommages exacts et historique par premier parent # Les API de syrnck/history.hpp comparent les renommages exacts et parcourent l’historique de fichiers en lisant seulement les métadonnées de manifestes vérifiées. Elles ne lisent pas le workspace, ne changent pas les références et ne reconstruisent pas les payloads. Elles ne remplacent pas un audit complet d’intégrité. text Copier syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000 syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow Comparer des renommages exacts # native diff --renames compare les snapshots puis associe un chemin supprimé à un ajouté si taille et hash BLAKE3 complet correspondent exactement, avec correspondance unique dans les deux sens. Cela couvre déplacements, casse/orthographe Unicode et découpages/compressions différents. Le changement de mode exécutable reste distinct. Une ligne status: renamed contient path cible, old_path , similarity: 100 , métadonnées source/cible et indicateurs séparés de contenu/mode/manifeste. Le compteur renamed augmente tandis que les ajouts/suppressions associés diminuent. Sans option, le diff conserve les ajouts/suppressions habituels. Plusieurs correspondances de contenu identique restent des ajouts/suppressions avec rename_ambiguous et compteurs d’ambiguïté ; une copie inchangée existante ne devient pas un renommage. Renommages édités, heuristiques de similarité, copies, permutations de chemins existants et résolutions de merge/rebase tenant compte des renommages ne sont pas inférés. Le JSON reste borné à 16 Mio. Suivre une lignée # native log-file REVISION PATH suit le premier parent et rend les changements du fichier : contenu, mode, représentation et suppression. Il suit en arrière les renommages exacts uniques et saute commits sans rapport ou vides. Un chemin déjà supprimé/renommé au départ peut montrer son passé. Chaque entrée porte révision, chemin à cette révision, aperçu de message borné, date, parents et changement de snapshot. Le premier ajout termine la lignée. Une origine ambiguë s’arrête avec stop_reason: ambiguous_rename , sans choix arbitraire. --no-follow s’arrête aux ajouts du chemin demandé, y compris ceux provenant d’un renommage. Une fusion est comparée à son premier parent ; les autres parents sont décrits mais non parcourus. Ce n’est ni un blame multi-parent ni un moteur de recherche historique général. Défaut 100 changements, plage 1..1000 ; budget distinct de 1000 révisions inspectées, plage 1..10000. Une page incomplète rend next_revision et next_path , à reprendre ensemble même après renommage. complete et stop_reason distinguent added, ambiguous_rename, root, page_limit, traversal_limit. Ces requêtes ne créent pas de pins ; les racines normales doivent protéger les versions contre un retrait/GC administratif entre appels. text Copier syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25 syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000 syrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow syrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25","headings":[{"id":"exact-renames-and-first-parent-file-history","text":"Renommages exacts et historique par premier parent","level":1},{"id":"exact-rename-comparison","text":"Comparer des renommages exacts","level":2},{"id":"follow-a-file-lineage","text":"Suivre une lignée","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native diff SOURCE TARGET --renames\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --limit 25 --max-revisions 1000\nsyrnck native log-file HEAD Content/Map.uasset --no-follow\nsyrnck native log-file NEXT_REVISION NEXT_PATH --limit 25","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"b834db59db2d"},{"id":"native-hard-reset","title":"Reset hard récupérable","description":"Guide : Reset hard récupérable.","section":"Versionnage","order":260,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"recoverable-native-hard-reset\">Reset hard natif récupérable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/recoverable-native-hard-reset\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reset hard natif récupérable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le reset hard remplace les fichiers suivis du workspace de la branche sélectionnée et vide son index figé. Avant remplacement, il capture les octets locaux complets et conserve ancienne tête et index dans un snapshot de récupération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--directory choisit le workspace. Le Store principal reste strictement intérieur à son workspace ; un workspace lié utilise son dossier enregistré et le Store partagé. --branch doit être la branche sélectionnée. Les contrôles de liaison restent appliqués. Toute récupération checkout/switch/reset doit être terminée/annulée d’abord. Soft/mixed et reset d’index gardent leurs contrats distincts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset HEAD --hard\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"what-is-replaced-and-protected\">Données remplacées et protégées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/what-is-replaced-and-protected\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Données remplacées et protégées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan en lecture seule inventorie le namespace et hashe les fichiers sélectionnés, sans se fier aux ignores, tailles ou dates. Il inclut chemins présents suivis par tête/index, fichiers ordinaires non suivis faisant obstacle à une cible/transition/orthographe, et dossiers nécessaires à retirer/recréer ces transitions, vides compris.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un suivi absent reste absent dans la capture puis revient s’il existe dans la cible. Modifiés, ajouts/suppressions préparés et index périmés peuvent être réinitialisés. Casse et fichier/dossier utilisent le journal d’évacuation. Non-suivis sans rapport restent sur place. obstructing_untracked indique ceux retirés du workspace mais reconstructibles dans le snapshot de récupération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Liens/reparse, fichiers spéciaux, noms portables ambigus et entrées Git/Store protégées ne sont pas adoptés : blocage avant candidat. Retrait de dossier non récursif, vide après évacuation des fichiers capturés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan lie racines canoniques workspace/Store, HEAD sélectionné, tête/index d’origine, cible, orthographes/hashes/modes exacts et profil des dossiers source. Rétablir taille/date n’évite pas ce contrôle. --verify ajoute l’audit complet des fermetures sans changer l’ID. C++ exige cet ID ; le CLI peut capturer un plan frais immédiatement avant application si --expect-plan est omis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"application-and-recovery\">Application et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/application-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Application et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Avant import des données de travail, vérifier anciennes versions conservées et cible. Chaque fichier ingéré doit encore correspondre au hash complet/taille/mode du plan. La révision de récupération contient ses fichiers capturés et comme parents ancienne tête/index éventuel, préservant travail sale et préparation indépendante après GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le journal checkout v5 distingue source de travail et tête, index original, sélection HEAD et profil de dossiers. Publication initiale protège source/cible avant déplacement ; publication finale déplace branche, retire index et crée archive ensemble.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Resume termine installation/nettoyage. Abort avant publication finale restaure octets antérieurs, absences, orthographes et dossiers vides capturés, avec tête/index inchangés. Après publication, nettoyage seulement. Ouvrir le Store ne récupère rien implicitement. Mutations/obstructions nouvelles sont refusées en gardant les données observées pour récupération explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Succès : operation reset, mode hard, ancienne révision/index, recovery_revision, archive, plan et reçu checkout. L’archive cible la récupération ; ses parents gardent tout l’historique et l’index indépendamment de la branche. Exporter récupération pour travail, index pour préparations, ou inspecter les archives checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’export restaure des fichiers ; les dossiers vides ne sont pas suivis dans la révision plate, seulement le journal pour abort. Les archives restent jusqu’au retrait avec sauvegarde vérifiée. Même tête propre sans index : unchanged sans écriture. Erreur de préparation peut laisser des objets valides non référencés ; journal actif à récupérer avant GC. Préparations/backups en clair comme le checkout ordinaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"partial-workspaces\">Workspaces partiels\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Workspaces partiels\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le reset hard accepte les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces partiels\u003c/a> statiques.\nIl remplace les chemins physiques sélectionnés, déplace la branche logique\ncomplète et vide l’index figé complet. Les entrées logiques exclues ne deviennent\nni suppressions physiques ni fichiers de récupération supplémentaires. Une\nsélection explicitement vide permet un reset des seules métadonnées, en gardant\nles fichiers locaux sans rapport.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan v2 lie le tableau canonique \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code> et sa racine conservée\n\u003ccode>materialization_object\u003c/code>. Le journal v9 utilise le layout 5 avec deux définitions\nfigées identiques. La publication vérifie la racine de sélection avec HEAD,\nbranche et index : un changement de politique invalide l’opération. Les plans\net journaux des vues complètes conservent leurs formats.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La récupération conserve l’arbre physique capturé sans lui réappliquer le masque.\nExemple : un fichier ordinaire \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> peut bloquer le chemin sélectionné\n\u003ccode>Content/A/leaf\u003c/code>, tandis que la tête complète contient aussi \u003ccode>Content/B\u003c/code>, exclu.\nLe reset archive le fichier physique, installe la feuille sélectionnée et garde\n\u003ccode>B\u003c/code> absent du disque. Les parents de la récupération conservent tête/index\ncomplets, donc leurs blocs cachés survivent au GC et à la sauvegarde portable.\nAbort rétablit le fichier ordinaire initial. Les chemins locaux exclus qui\nfont obstacle à la cible sont explicitement listés et archivés ; les autres\nrestent sur place.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api-bounds-and-validation\">API et bornes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/api-bounds-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API et bornes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les fonctions de syrnck/workspace.hpp fonctionnent sans réseau. Métadonnées plates, rapports, profil et inventaire gardent chacun 16 Mio. Au plus 100000 entrées de dossiers source, profondeur 128. Les fichiers passent dans le pipeline d’ingestion configuré en flux, avec inventaire/hash des fichiers choisis. Cela ne repose pas sur le cache consultatif ou le watcher.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_hard_reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_hard_reset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                         target\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Reset hard natif récupérable # Le reset hard remplace les fichiers suivis du workspace de la branche sélectionnée et vide son index figé. Avant remplacement, il capture les octets locaux complets et conserve ancienne tête et index dans un snapshot de récupération. --directory choisit le workspace. Le Store principal reste strictement intérieur à son workspace ; un workspace lié utilise son dossier enregistré et le Store partagé. --branch doit être la branche sélectionnée. Les contrôles de liaison restent appliqués. Toute récupération checkout/switch/reset doit être terminée/annulée d’abord. Soft/mixed et reset d’index gardent leurs contrats distincts. text Copier syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native reset HEAD --hard Données remplacées et protégées # Le plan en lecture seule inventorie le namespace et hashe les fichiers sélectionnés, sans se fier aux ignores, tailles ou dates. Il inclut chemins présents suivis par tête/index, fichiers ordinaires non suivis faisant obstacle à une cible/transition/orthographe, et dossiers nécessaires à retirer/recréer ces transitions, vides compris. Un suivi absent reste absent dans la capture puis revient s’il existe dans la cible. Modifiés, ajouts/suppressions préparés et index périmés peuvent être réinitialisés. Casse et fichier/dossier utilisent le journal d’évacuation. Non-suivis sans rapport restent sur place. obstructing_untracked indique ceux retirés du workspace mais reconstructibles dans le snapshot de récupération. Liens/reparse, fichiers spéciaux, noms portables ambigus et entrées Git/Store protégées ne sont pas adoptés : blocage avant candidat. Retrait de dossier non récursif, vide après évacuation des fichiers capturés. Le plan lie racines canoniques workspace/Store, HEAD sélectionné, tête/index d’origine, cible, orthographes/hashes/modes exacts et profil des dossiers source. Rétablir taille/date n’évite pas ce contrôle. --verify ajoute l’audit complet des fermetures sans changer l’ID. C++ exige cet ID ; le CLI peut capturer un plan frais immédiatement avant application si --expect-plan est omis. Application et récupération # Avant import des données de travail, vérifier anciennes versions conservées et cible. Chaque fichier ingéré doit encore correspondre au hash complet/taille/mode du plan. La révision de récupération contient ses fichiers capturés et comme parents ancienne tête/index éventuel, préservant travail sale et préparation indépendante après GC. Le journal checkout v5 distingue source de travail et tête, index original, sélection HEAD et profil de dossiers. Publication initiale protège source/cible avant déplacement ; publication finale déplace branche, retire index et crée archive ensemble. Resume termine installation/nettoyage. Abort avant publication finale restaure octets antérieurs, absences, orthographes et dossiers vides capturés, avec tête/index inchangés. Après publication, nettoyage seulement. Ouvrir le Store ne récupère rien implicitement. Mutations/obstructions nouvelles sont refusées en gardant les données observées pour récupération explicite. Succès : operation reset, mode hard, ancienne révision/index, recovery_revision, archive, plan et reçu checkout. L’archive cible la récupération ; ses parents gardent tout l’historique et l’index indépendamment de la branche. Exporter récupération pour travail, index pour préparations, ou inspecter les archives checkout. L’export restaure des fichiers ; les dossiers vides ne sont pas suivis dans la révision plate, seulement le journal pour abort. Les archives restent jusqu’au retrait avec sauvegarde vérifiée. Même tête propre sans index : unchanged sans écriture. Erreur de préparation peut laisser des objets valides non référencés ; journal actif à récupérer avant GC. Préparations/backups en clair comme le checkout ordinaire. text Copier syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort text Copier syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify Workspaces partiels # Le reset hard accepte les workspaces partiels statiques. Il remplace les chemins physiques sélectionnés, déplace la branche logique complète et vide l’index figé complet. Les entrées logiques exclues ne deviennent ni suppressions physiques ni fichiers de récupération supplémentaires. Une sélection explicitement vide permet un reset des seules métadonnées, en gardant les fichiers locaux sans rapport. Le plan v2 lie le tableau canonique materialization et sa racine conservée materialization_object . Le journal v9 utilise le layout 5 avec deux définitions figées identiques. La publication vérifie la racine de sélection avec HEAD, branche et index : un changement de politique invalide l’opération. Les plans et journaux des vues complètes conservent leurs formats. La récupération conserve l’arbre physique capturé sans lui réappliquer le masque. Exemple : un fichier ordinaire Content peut bloquer le chemin sélectionné Content/A/leaf , tandis que la tête complète contient aussi Content/B , exclu. Le reset archive le fichier physique, installe la feuille sélectionnée et garde B absent du disque. Les parents de la récupération conservent tête/index complets, donc leurs blocs cachés survivent au GC et à la sauvegarde portable. Abort rétablit le fichier ordinaire initial. Les chemins locaux exclus qui font obstacle à la cible sont explicitement listés et archivés ; les autres restent sur place. API et bornes # Les fonctions de syrnck/workspace.hpp fonctionnent sans réseau. Métadonnées plates, rapports, profil et inventaire gardent chacun 16 Mio. Au plus 100000 entrées de dossiers source, profondeur 128. Les fichiers passent dans le pipeline d’ingestion configuré en flux, avec inventaire/hash des fichiers choisis. Cela ne repose pas sur le cache consultatif ou le watcher. cpp Copier auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_hard_reset ( store , project , \"main\" , target , true ) ; auto applied = syrnck :: native_hard_reset ( store , project , \"main\" , expected_head , target , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) ) ; syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify syrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native reset HEAD --hard syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort syrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files syrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files syrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", target, true); auto applied = syrnck::native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", expected_head, target, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>());","headings":[{"id":"recoverable-native-hard-reset","text":"Reset hard natif récupérable","level":1},{"id":"what-is-replaced-and-protected","text":"Données remplacées et protégées","level":2},{"id":"application-and-recovery","text":"Application et récupération","level":2},{"id":"partial-workspaces","text":"Workspaces partiels","level":2},{"id":"api-bounds-and-validation","text":"API et bornes","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native reset-plan TARGET --hard --verify\nsyrnck native reset TARGET --hard --expect OLD_HEAD --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native reset HEAD --hard\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nsyrnck native export RECOVERY_REVISION saved-working-files\nsyrnck native export PREVIOUS_INDEX saved-staged-files\nsyrnck native archives --kind checkout --verify\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", target, true);\nauto applied = syrnck::native_hard_reset(store, project, \"main\", expected_head,\n                                         target, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>());","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspaces"],"kind":"guide","revision":"abc3099f2421"},{"id":"native-history","title":"Ascendance des révisions","description":"Guide : Ascendance des révisions.","section":"Versionnage","order":300,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-revision-ancestry\">Ascendance des révisions natives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-history/native-revision-ancestry\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Ascendance des révisions natives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native ancestry\u003c/code> parcourt le graphe complet accessible depuis deux snapshots et rend les deux directions d’ascendance ainsi que toutes les meilleures bases communes. Il ne choisit pas arbitrairement une base lorsqu’une fusion croisée en possède plusieurs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le JSON contient \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_is_ancestor\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>target_is_ancestor\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>related\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>merge_bases\u003c/code> triées, \u003ccode>visited_revisions\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>parent_links\u003c/code>. Une révision est son propre ancêtre. Des historiques sans lien rendent une liste vide et \u003ccode>related: false\u003c/code>, avec succès. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs natifs usuels ; l’API C++ reçoit des IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le parcours itératif réalise un travail linéaire sur le graphe après décodage des métadonnées. Chaque révision distincte est chargée une fois. Tous les parents, y compris ceux des fusions, participent ; les doublons de parents comptent dans le budget de liens. Défauts : 100000 révisions et 1000000 liens. Réglages explicites : 1..1000000 révisions, 0..10000000 liens. Un dépassement échoue sans résultat partiel. La liste des bases reste conservativement sous 16 Mio JSON ; les révisions conservent aussi cette limite d’objet. La mémoire dépend des révisions et liens visités.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls les objets révision sont lus. Manifestes et blocs ne sont pas nécessaires : cette requête ne prouve pas l’intégrité des fichiers. Toute révision accessible absente ou corrompue provoque une erreur, même si une réponse probable existait déjà. Aucun repli d’historique superficiel ni téléchargement implicite n’est effectué. Utiliser \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> ou une opération vérifiée pour les fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La requête ne change ni références, ni stockage, ni workspace. Progression de planification, annulation et diagnostics bilingues suivent le contrat d’opération ; les callbacks ne doivent pas réentrer dans le Store. L’exposition en C/Go reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_ancestry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>left_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> right_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeGraphLimits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Ascendance des révisions natives # native ancestry parcourt le graphe complet accessible depuis deux snapshots et rend les deux directions d’ascendance ainsi que toutes les meilleures bases communes. Il ne choisit pas arbitrairement une base lorsqu’une fusion croisée en possède plusieurs. Le JSON contient source , target , source_is_ancestor , target_is_ancestor , related , merge_bases triées, visited_revisions et parent_links . Une révision est son propre ancêtre. Des historiques sans lien rendent une liste vide et related: false , avec succès. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs natifs usuels ; l’API C++ reçoit des IDs. Le parcours itératif réalise un travail linéaire sur le graphe après décodage des métadonnées. Chaque révision distincte est chargée une fois. Tous les parents, y compris ceux des fusions, participent ; les doublons de parents comptent dans le budget de liens. Défauts : 100000 révisions et 1000000 liens. Réglages explicites : 1..1000000 révisions, 0..10000000 liens. Un dépassement échoue sans résultat partiel. La liste des bases reste conservativement sous 16 Mio JSON ; les révisions conservent aussi cette limite d’objet. La mémoire dépend des révisions et liens visités. Seuls les objets révision sont lus. Manifestes et blocs ne sont pas nécessaires : cette requête ne prouve pas l’intégrité des fichiers. Toute révision accessible absente ou corrompue provoque une erreur, même si une réponse probable existait déjà. Aucun repli d’historique superficiel ni téléchargement implicite n’est effectué. Utiliser fsck ou une opération vérifiée pour les fichiers. La requête ne change ni références, ni stockage, ni workspace. Progression de planification, annulation et diagnostics bilingues suivent le contrat d’opération ; les callbacks ne doivent pas réentrer dans le Store. L’exposition en C/Go reste à développer. text Copier syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000 cpp Copier auto report = store . native_ancestry ( left_id , right_id , syrnck :: NativeGraphLimits { } , operation ) ; syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json syrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000 auto report = store.native_ancestry(left_id, right_id, syrnck::NativeGraphLimits{}, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-revision-ancestry","text":"Ascendance des révisions natives","level":1}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native ancestry tags/v1 HEAD --progress-json\nsyrnck native ancestry heads/main heads/feature --max-revisions 100000 --max-links 1000000\nauto report = store.native_ancestry(left_id, right_id, syrnck::NativeGraphLimits{}, operation);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"d1d63903f60c"},{"id":"native-hunks","title":"Sélection de hunks textuels","description":"Guide : Sélection de hunks textuels.","section":"Versionnage","order":280,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-partial-text-staging-and-reset\">Sélection partielle de hunks textuels\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/native-partial-text-staging-and-reset\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélection partielle de hunks textuels\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Sélectionner des hunks numérotés d’un fichier existant sans modifier sa copie de travail, en complément de l’ajout par fichier/dossier. Les commandes acceptent branche, dossier, progression/annulation et les options de diff context/max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes. JSON, IDs et numéros restent identiques en anglais/français. L’application rend du JSON, contrairement à l’ID discret de native add. Le budget du diff ne comprend pas numérotation et contexte ; le rapport complet reste sous 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"review-and-bind-the-selection\">Examiner et lier la sélection\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/review-and-bind-the-selection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Examiner et lier la sélection\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan lit l’index figé ou la tête sans index et le fichier de travail observé. Les hunks portent des numéros à partir de 1. Le contexte détermine leur regroupement ; des éditions voisines dans un hunk sont prises ensemble. Les numéros appartiennent uniquement à ce plan. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-lines\">sélection de lignes\u003c/a> offre un autre niveau de choix ; aucune édition libre de patch ou invite interactive n’est proposée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rapport : diff de travail, operation stage-hunks, état branche/tête/index/base, archive optionnelle, text_options, ready, blockers, plan_id. Ce dernier est l’ID BLAKE3 du domaine chunk sur le JSON canonique avant ajout de plan_id, sans objet plan stocké. Il lie dossier/chemin/branche/manifeste/hash observé/mode disponible/hunks/options/disponibilité. La langue ne le change pas. Après application, le rapport garde l’ID d’aperçu, mais ses champs de résultat ne font plus partie de l’entrée de hash initiale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application recalcule et exige l’ID exact attendu. Mutation du fichier, branche/index ou options impose un nouvel aperçu ; passer les mêmes options non par défaut aux deux commandes. Numéros positifs, uniques, présents, non vides et au plus 100000 ; leur ordre d’entrée ne change pas les octets. Refaire un aperçu après succès car les numéros restants peuvent changer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; erreur ordinaire : diagnostic sans résultat. Blocages : checkout en attente, index périmé, diff binaire/limité, absence de changement textuel, changement de présence ou archive contradictoire. L’aperçu ne vérifie que ce fichier, pas toutes les autres données ; les fermetures complètes du remplacement et de l’archive sont vérifiées à la publication et peuvent encore bloquer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scope-and-exact-bytes\">Portée et octets exacts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/scope-and-exact-bytes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Portée et octets exacts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Source et fichier de travail doivent être des fichiers ordinaires existants ; fichiers vides, Unicode, LF/CRLF et absence de newline finale sont gérés. Binaire, nouveau fichier et suppression complète demandent native add ordinaire. Une branche initiale est possible si son index figé contient déjà le fichier. Un index périmé doit être rejoué ou réinitialisé explicitement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le candidat part de la table complète index/tête, ne remplace que le manifeste du chemin et conserve voisins/mode source. Les changements de mode et de découpage ne sont pas du texte sélectionnable. Hunks choisis copiés à l’octet ; régions non choisies gardées de source. Tous les hunks reproduisent le travail observé dans son budget. Un sous-ensemble peut être plus grand que les deux entrées en gardant des suppressions et ajoutant des lignes : refus avant ingestion s’il dépasse la limite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le lecteur ciblé applique ignores, orthographe exacte, protections namespace/liens/Store, bornes et détection des mutations observées. Voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff/compare-a-working-file\">diff de travail\u003c/a>. Aucun fichier de travail, tête de branche ou HEAD symbolique n’est modifié.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"atomic-retention-and-recovery\">Rétention atomique et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention atomique et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les octets choisis sont ingérés avec CDC et préférences du dépôt. Le snapshot d’index v1 possède marqueur lié à la branche, date zéro et parent tête d’origine, ou aucun initialement. Aucun format/chiffrement ne change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transaction contrôle tête, index, HEAD symbolique et archive, puis publie staging/BRANCH et éventuellement archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID avec tout l’ancien index/base. Une archive exacte peut être réutilisée, une cible contradictoire est refusée. Inventaire, inspection et export permettent d’en récupérer les préparations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le chemin de travail est revérifié après ingestion puis immédiatement avant publication, après les observateurs. Annulation, erreur, mutation observée ou fermeture incomplète gardent les anciennes références ; des objets complets non référencés peuvent subsister. L’écriture atomique engagée peut finir malgré le stop ; aucun callback après. Après coupure, inspecter index/archive puis obtenir un nouveau plan : réutiliser un plan réussi ne l’applique pas silencieusement une seconde fois.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pas d’isolation de snapshot filesystem ni protection absolue contre un écrivain hostile falsifiant les dates. Aucun journal workspace nécessaire puisqu’aucun fichier n’est écrit. Les archives restent après GC jusqu’au \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait explicite protégé\u003c/a>. C++ utilise plan_stage_hunks et stage_hunks avec dossier, branche, chemin, limites, opération, plan attendu et numéros.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"remove-prepared-hunks\">Retirer des hunks préparés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/remove-prepared-hunks\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Retirer des hunks préparés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan reset compare la base originale à l’index figé, dans le sens forward du diff staged. Les numéros désignent les changements à retirer ; les autres restent préparés. Le résultat part de la base plus les hunks conservés, sans rechercher des offsets déjà déplacés. Refaire le plan après chaque application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Même branche, progression et cinq options textuelles, mais pas de --directory ni lecture du workspace, ignores ou chemins disque. Le fichier de travail peut être absent, sale, binaire ou devenu dossier sans effet. Le plan operation reset-hunks lie source_revision base, target_revision index, état/options/blocages, sans dossier/hash de travail. Même JSON bilingue, codes prêt/bloqué, validations, budgets et plan attendu que l’ajout partiel.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Il faut un index et le fichier dans base et index. Nouveaux fichiers, suppressions complètes et ajouts initiaux passent par native reset par fichier. Checkout actif, index périmé, binaire/limite, absence de texte changé et archive conflictuelle bloquent. Un mode seul n’est pas retirable en hunk. Tous les hunks rétablissent le contenu de base tout en gardant mode de l’index, voisins et racine staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Même transaction complète vérifiée et archive de l’ancien index, reconstructible après GC ou commit des changements restants. Annulation/exception/dépendance manquante gardent les références, avec objets orphelins possibles. Aucune revérification du workspace ou journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire. C++ utilise plan_reset_hunks et reset_hunks avec branche, chemin, limites, plan attendu et numéros.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Sélection partielle de hunks textuels # Sélectionner des hunks numérotés d’un fichier existant sans modifier sa copie de travail, en complément de l’ajout par fichier/dossier. Les commandes acceptent branche, dossier, progression/annulation et les options de diff context/max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes. JSON, IDs et numéros restent identiques en anglais/français. L’application rend du JSON, contrairement à l’ID discret de native add. Le budget du diff ne comprend pas numérotation et contexte ; le rapport complet reste sous 16 Mio. text Copier syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native staged syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\" Examiner et lier la sélection # Le plan lit l’index figé ou la tête sans index et le fichier de travail observé. Les hunks portent des numéros à partir de 1. Le contexte détermine leur regroupement ; des éditions voisines dans un hunk sont prises ensemble. Les numéros appartiennent uniquement à ce plan. La sélection de lignes offre un autre niveau de choix ; aucune édition libre de patch ou invite interactive n’est proposée. Rapport : diff de travail, operation stage-hunks, état branche/tête/index/base, archive optionnelle, text_options, ready, blockers, plan_id. Ce dernier est l’ID BLAKE3 du domaine chunk sur le JSON canonique avant ajout de plan_id, sans objet plan stocké. Il lie dossier/chemin/branche/manifeste/hash observé/mode disponible/hunks/options/disponibilité. La langue ne le change pas. Après application, le rapport garde l’ID d’aperçu, mais ses champs de résultat ne font plus partie de l’entrée de hash initiale. L’application recalcule et exige l’ID exact attendu. Mutation du fichier, branche/index ou options impose un nouvel aperçu ; passer les mêmes options non par défaut aux deux commandes. Numéros positifs, uniques, présents, non vides et au plus 100000 ; leur ordre d’entrée ne change pas les octets. Refaire un aperçu après succès car les numéros restants peuvent changer. Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; erreur ordinaire : diagnostic sans résultat. Blocages : checkout en attente, index périmé, diff binaire/limité, absence de changement textuel, changement de présence ou archive contradictoire. L’aperçu ne vérifie que ce fichier, pas toutes les autres données ; les fermetures complètes du remplacement et de l’archive sont vérifiées à la publication et peuvent encore bloquer. Portée et octets exacts # Source et fichier de travail doivent être des fichiers ordinaires existants ; fichiers vides, Unicode, LF/CRLF et absence de newline finale sont gérés. Binaire, nouveau fichier et suppression complète demandent native add ordinaire. Une branche initiale est possible si son index figé contient déjà le fichier. Un index périmé doit être rejoué ou réinitialisé explicitement. Le candidat part de la table complète index/tête, ne remplace que le manifeste du chemin et conserve voisins/mode source. Les changements de mode et de découpage ne sont pas du texte sélectionnable. Hunks choisis copiés à l’octet ; régions non choisies gardées de source. Tous les hunks reproduisent le travail observé dans son budget. Un sous-ensemble peut être plus grand que les deux entrées en gardant des suppressions et ajoutant des lignes : refus avant ingestion s’il dépasse la limite. Le lecteur ciblé applique ignores, orthographe exacte, protections namespace/liens/Store, bornes et détection des mutations observées. Voir le diff de travail . Aucun fichier de travail, tête de branche ou HEAD symbolique n’est modifié. Rétention atomique et récupération # Les octets choisis sont ingérés avec CDC et préférences du dépôt. Le snapshot d’index v1 possède marqueur lié à la branche, date zéro et parent tête d’origine, ou aucun initialement. Aucun format/chiffrement ne change. La transaction contrôle tête, index, HEAD symbolique et archive, puis publie staging/BRANCH et éventuellement archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID avec tout l’ancien index/base. Une archive exacte peut être réutilisée, une cible contradictoire est refusée. Inventaire, inspection et export permettent d’en récupérer les préparations. Le chemin de travail est revérifié après ingestion puis immédiatement avant publication, après les observateurs. Annulation, erreur, mutation observée ou fermeture incomplète gardent les anciennes références ; des objets complets non référencés peuvent subsister. L’écriture atomique engagée peut finir malgré le stop ; aucun callback après. Après coupure, inspecter index/archive puis obtenir un nouveau plan : réutiliser un plan réussi ne l’applique pas silencieusement une seconde fois. Pas d’isolation de snapshot filesystem ni protection absolue contre un écrivain hostile falsifiant les dates. Aucun journal workspace nécessaire puisqu’aucun fichier n’est écrit. Les archives restent après GC jusqu’au retrait explicite protégé . C++ utilise plan_stage_hunks et stage_hunks avec dossier, branche, chemin, limites, opération, plan attendu et numéros. text Copier syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY Retirer des hunks préparés # Le plan reset compare la base originale à l’index figé, dans le sens forward du diff staged. Les numéros désignent les changements à retirer ; les autres restent préparés. Le résultat part de la base plus les hunks conservés, sans rechercher des offsets déjà déplacés. Refaire le plan après chaque application. Même branche, progression et cinq options textuelles, mais pas de --directory ni lecture du workspace, ignores ou chemins disque. Le fichier de travail peut être absent, sale, binaire ou devenu dossier sans effet. Le plan operation reset-hunks lie source_revision base, target_revision index, état/options/blocages, sans dossier/hash de travail. Même JSON bilingue, codes prêt/bloqué, validations, budgets et plan attendu que l’ajout partiel. Il faut un index et le fichier dans base et index. Nouveaux fichiers, suppressions complètes et ajouts initiaux passent par native reset par fichier. Checkout actif, index périmé, binaire/limite, absence de texte changé et archive conflictuelle bloquent. Un mode seul n’est pas retirable en hunk. Tous les hunks rétablissent le contenu de base tout en gardant mode de l’index, voisins et racine staging. Même transaction complète vérifiée et archive de l’ancien index, reconstructible après GC ou commit des changements restants. Annulation/exception/dépendance manquante gardent les références, avec objets orphelins possibles. Aucune revérification du workspace ou journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire. C++ utilise plan_reset_hunks et reset_hunks avec branche, chemin, limites, plan attendu et numéros. text Copier syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native staged syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\" syrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify syrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify syrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY syrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3 syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified","headings":[{"id":"native-partial-text-staging-and-reset","text":"Sélection partielle de hunks textuels","level":1},{"id":"review-and-bind-the-selection","text":"Examiner et lier la sélection","level":2},{"id":"scope-and-exact-bytes","text":"Portée et octets exacts","level":2},{"id":"atomic-retention-and-recovery","text":"Rétention atomique et récupération","level":2},{"id":"remove-prepared-hunks","text":"Retirer des hunks préparés","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native add-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Selected changes\"\nsyrnck native archives --kind stage-index --verify\nsyrnck native archive-show archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID --verify\nsyrnck native export OLD_INDEX_ID NEW_DIRECTORY\nsyrnck native reset-hunks-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native reset-hunks Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --hunks 1,3\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-lines","#/fr/text-diff/compare-a-working-file","#/fr/archive-retention"],"kind":"guide","revision":"b53d87c67324"},{"id":"native-lines","title":"Sélection de lignes textuelles","description":"Guide : Sélection de lignes textuelles.","section":"Versionnage","order":290,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-individual-text-line-selection\">Sélection individuelle des lignes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-lines/native-individual-text-line-selection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélection individuelle des lignes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Préparer ou retirer des lignes ajoutées/supprimées à l’intérieur d’un hunk sans écrire le workspace. Les commandes de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks\">hunks entiers\u003c/a> restent disponibles. Choisir les numéros dans le bon aperçu frais avec les mêmes options : les plans add et reset ont des identités et parfois des numérotations différentes. La langue ne modifie ni clés, ni nombres, ni IDs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"numbered-change-rows\">Numérotation des changements\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-lines/numbered-change-rows\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Numérotation des changements\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque plan expose selection_unit line, change_count et diff.hunks numérotés. Chaque ligne add/remove possède en plus un change_number global à partir de 1 dans l’ordre des hunks ; le contexte n’a pas de numéro. --lines désigne ces changements, pas les lignes du fichier ou positions locales. Liste non vide, unique, positive, en plage, au plus 100000 numéros. Au-delà de 100000 changements, tout le plan bloque avec selection_limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Dans un remplacement, retraits et ajouts sont indépendants. Avec les quatre lignes d’exemple, 2,4 remplace old B par new B en gardant old A ; 3 seul garde les deux anciennes lignes puis ajoute new A ; 1 seul retire old A sans remplacement. L’ordre du diff fixe les insertions ; ni réordonnancement ni édition libre. Aucune validation de syntaxe/compilation du résultat.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour reset, la même direction base-vers-index numérote ce qui est retiré. Les autres changements restent préparés ; reconstruire depuis la base et les lignes conservées évite les offsets déplacés. Tout retirer rétablit le contenu de base tout en gardant mode, autres fichiers et racine staging. Obtenir ensuite un nouvel aperçu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">change_number  kind    text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">1              remove old A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">2              remove old B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">3              add    new A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">4              add    new B\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal\">Octets exacts et refus\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-lines/exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Octets exacts et refus\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>UTF-8, LF/CRLF et newline finale restent exacts. Aucune newline n’est inventée. Une sélection qui ajouterait une ligne après une ligne conservée non terminée est refusée avant ingestion. Dans l’exemple, sélectionner seulement l’ajout concaténerait old et new ; sélectionner retrait et ajout pour remplacer. Le reset partiel équivalent est aussi refusé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un plan prêt signifie comparaison éligible, pas validité de toutes les combinaisons. Certaines sélections échouent sur ce raccord ou le budget du résultat, qui peut dépasser les deux entrées en gardant des suppressions non sélectionnées et insérant des ajouts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">source: old (no final LF)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">target: new (with final LF)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"plans-storage-and-retention\">Plans, stockage et rétention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-lines/plans-storage-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Plans, stockage et rétention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan add operation stage-lines compare index/tête figé au fichier observé, avec --directory et les protections du lecteur ciblé : ignores, namespace, liens, tailles et mutations observées. Les métadonnées sont revérifiées après ingestion et après le dernier observateur avant publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le reset operation reset-lines compare base originale et index figé, sans --directory ni contenu de travail. Les chemins disque peuvent être absents, sales, ignorés ou des dossiers. Le fichier doit exister des deux côtés stockés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux formes exigent du texte existant, un index frais s’il existe et aucun checkout actif. Nouveaux fichiers, changements complets de présence, binaire/limites, mode seul et archives suivent les restrictions de hunks. Une branche initiale permet add sur un fichier déjà dans son index initial ; ses ajouts initiaux demandent reset par fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Branche, progression/annulation et cinq options de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff\u003c/a> s’appliquent. Numérotation/contexte sont supplémentaires au budget de diff, avec rapport total de 16 Mio. Changement d’options, diff, branche/index/base, état prêt ou contenu observé impose un nouveau --expect-plan ; un plan hunk n’autorise pas une application ligne. Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; sélection invalide : erreur sans résultat.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transaction vérifiée conserve voisins et mode source, publie l’index avec archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID s’il existait et retient toute l’ancienne préparation/base après GC et commit. Annulation, observateur ou fermeture invalide gardent l’ancien état et peuvent laisser des objets complets inaccessibles ; aucun callback après publication. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery\">récupération des sélections\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ expose plan_stage_lines, stage_lines, plan_reset_lines et reset_lines avec les paramètres des versions hunks. Aucun format logique/chiffrement ne change ; recompiler avec les en-têtes correspondants.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Sélection individuelle des lignes # Préparer ou retirer des lignes ajoutées/supprimées à l’intérieur d’un hunk sans écrire le workspace. Les commandes de hunks entiers restent disponibles. Choisir les numéros dans le bon aperçu frais avec les mêmes options : les plans add et reset ont des identités et parfois des numérotations différentes. La langue ne modifie ni clés, ni nombres, ni IDs. text Copier syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3 syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4 Numérotation des changements # Chaque plan expose selection_unit line, change_count et diff.hunks numérotés. Chaque ligne add/remove possède en plus un change_number global à partir de 1 dans l’ordre des hunks ; le contexte n’a pas de numéro. --lines désigne ces changements, pas les lignes du fichier ou positions locales. Liste non vide, unique, positive, en plage, au plus 100000 numéros. Au-delà de 100000 changements, tout le plan bloque avec selection_limit. Dans un remplacement, retraits et ajouts sont indépendants. Avec les quatre lignes d’exemple, 2,4 remplace old B par new B en gardant old A ; 3 seul garde les deux anciennes lignes puis ajoute new A ; 1 seul retire old A sans remplacement. L’ordre du diff fixe les insertions ; ni réordonnancement ni édition libre. Aucune validation de syntaxe/compilation du résultat. Pour reset, la même direction base-vers-index numérote ce qui est retiré. Les autres changements restent préparés ; reconstruire depuis la base et les lignes conservées évite les offsets déplacés. Tout retirer rétablit le contenu de base tout en gardant mode, autres fichiers et racine staging. Obtenir ensuite un nouvel aperçu. text Copier change_number kind text 1 remove old A 2 remove old B 3 add new A 4 add new B Octets exacts et refus # UTF-8, LF/CRLF et newline finale restent exacts. Aucune newline n’est inventée. Une sélection qui ajouterait une ligne après une ligne conservée non terminée est refusée avant ingestion. Dans l’exemple, sélectionner seulement l’ajout concaténerait old et new ; sélectionner retrait et ajout pour remplacer. Le reset partiel équivalent est aussi refusé. Un plan prêt signifie comparaison éligible, pas validité de toutes les combinaisons. Certaines sélections échouent sur ce raccord ou le budget du résultat, qui peut dépasser les deux entrées en gardant des suppressions non sélectionnées et insérant des ajouts. text Copier source: old (no final LF) target: new (with final LF) Plans, stockage et rétention # Le plan add operation stage-lines compare index/tête figé au fichier observé, avec --directory et les protections du lecteur ciblé : ignores, namespace, liens, tailles et mutations observées. Les métadonnées sont revérifiées après ingestion et après le dernier observateur avant publication. Le reset operation reset-lines compare base originale et index figé, sans --directory ni contenu de travail. Les chemins disque peuvent être absents, sales, ignorés ou des dossiers. Le fichier doit exister des deux côtés stockés. Les deux formes exigent du texte existant, un index frais s’il existe et aucun checkout actif. Nouveaux fichiers, changements complets de présence, binaire/limites, mode seul et archives suivent les restrictions de hunks. Une branche initiale permet add sur un fichier déjà dans son index initial ; ses ajouts initiaux demandent reset par fichier. Branche, progression/annulation et cinq options de diff s’appliquent. Numérotation/contexte sont supplémentaires au budget de diff, avec rapport total de 16 Mio. Changement d’options, diff, branche/index/base, état prêt ou contenu observé impose un nouveau --expect-plan ; un plan hunk n’autorise pas une application ligne. Plan prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; sélection invalide : erreur sans résultat. La transaction vérifiée conserve voisins et mode source, publie l’index avec archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID s’il existait et retient toute l’ancienne préparation/base après GC et commit. Annulation, observateur ou fermeture invalide gardent l’ancien état et peuvent laisser des objets complets inaccessibles ; aucun callback après publication. Voir la récupération des sélections . C++ expose plan_stage_lines, stage_lines, plan_reset_lines et reset_lines avec les paramètres des versions hunks. Aucun format logique/chiffrement ne change ; recompiler avec les en-têtes correspondants. syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3 syrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp syrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4 change_number kind text 1 remove old A 2 remove old B 3 add new A 4 add new B source: old (no final LF) target: new (with final LF)","headings":[{"id":"native-individual-text-line-selection","text":"Sélection individuelle des lignes","level":1},{"id":"numbered-change-rows","text":"Numérotation des changements","level":2},{"id":"exact-bytes-and-selection-refusal","text":"Octets exacts et refus","level":2},{"id":"plans-storage-and-retention","text":"Plans, stockage et rétention","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native add-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 1,3\nsyrnck native reset-lines-plan Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native reset-lines Source/Player.cpp --expect-plan PLAN_ID --lines 2,4\nchange_number  kind    text\n1              remove old A\n2              remove old B\n3              add    new A\n4              add    new B\nsource: old (no final LF)\ntarget: new (with final LF)","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-hunks","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/native-hunks/atomic-retention-and-recovery"],"kind":"guide","revision":"fef0df15b83c"},{"id":"native-merge","title":"Fusion de branches","description":"Guide : Fusion de branches.","section":"Versionnage","order":300,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-merge\">Fusion native\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/native-merge\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fusion native\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>merge-plan calcule topologie et snapshot complet proposé sans modifier objets, références ou workspace. Les décisions de fichiers à trois voies sont partagées avec le replay. Branche sélectionnée et dossier courant par défaut ; options branche/dossier pour les remplacer. Le CLI résout les sélecteurs, C++ utilise IDs exacts. Budgets de révisions/liens identiques à l’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-history\">ascendance\u003c/a>. Le graphe complet est nécessaire, sans repli shallow.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"topology\">Topologie\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/topology\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Topologie\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>up_to_date : source ancêtre de cible, égalité comprise, snapshot actuel exact. fast_forward : cible ancêtre de source, snapshot source exact avec sa représentation. merge : divergence, fusion à trois voies sur la base choisie.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une meilleure base unique est automatique. Plusieurs donnent multiple_merge_bases ; --base doit choisir une des meilleures annoncées, pas un ancêtre plus ancien. Aucune base récursive virtuelle de Git n’est synthétisée. Sans ancêtre, unrelated_histories bloque sauf --allow-unrelated-histories, qui choisit l’arbre vide ; pas de base explicite alors.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les éditions indépendantes et changements contenu/mode indépendants d’un même fichier se combinent. Contenus concurrents différents, add/add et suppression/modification restent en conflit. Les collisions portables de casse/préfixe sont recalculées. L’égalité logique utilise hash complet/taille/mode, pas le seul découpage ; fast-forward adopte néanmoins la révision exacte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans conflit de snapshot, le plan de checkout exige suivis propres et contrôle ignorés/non-suivis, protections, liens, index et récupération. Une divergence de topologie seule peut être prête sans changement d’octets ; up-to-date reste soumis à ce contrôle prudent. --resolutions accepte les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions\">choix explicites\u003c/a>. Composition textuelle optionnelle ci-dessous ; fusion tenant compte des renommages et synthèse récursive restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"opt-in-text-composition\">Composition textuelle explicite\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/opt-in-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Composition textuelle explicite\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>NativeMergeOptions::text_merge active les limites TextDiffOptions ; vide, les décisions de fichiers et l’inspection de métadonnées restent inchangées. Les résolutions explicites passent d’abord ; seuls les conflits content restants sont tentés. Add/add, suppression/modification, binaire, éditions chevauchantes et namespace restent bloquants ; aucune modification de topologie ou représentation fast-forward.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque tentative reconstruit/vérifie les trois entrées même sans audit historique. Limites de taille et de lot avant lecture des payloads. Au plus 64 conflits, budget agrégé de 16 Mio pour entrées vérifiées et résultats propres, plus plafonds par fichier de travail/lignes/trace/sortie. Un refus conserve le conflit initial avec son motif, jamais de résultat partiel pour tenir un budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>CLI plan/application : --text-merge ; max-file-bytes, max-lines, max-work, max-output-bytes exigent cette option. Garder les mêmes limites aux deux étapes ; l’application recalcule avec la tête attendue. Progression/diagnostics bilingues, JSON canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>text_merge rend limites, compteurs, manifestes générés et statuts/plages/métadonnées par chemin, null si désactivé, sans texte fusionné. Les actions réfèrent des IDs prédits qui peuvent ne pas exister ; aucun objet n’est créé au plan, même si un fichier se compose et un autre bloque. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation\">préparation virtuelle\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application recalcule, vérifie histoires et workspace avant toute matérialisation. Chunks/manifestes doivent correspondre aux IDs prédits avant création de la révision à deux parents. Le checkout récupérable publie l’ensemble. Un échec avant journal peut laisser des objets générés non référencés sans changer branche/workspace ; ensuite resume/abort utilise le candidat retenu sans refaire la fusion. Annulation/observateurs respectent ces frontières.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Default text limits, explicit opt-in.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_tip\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"conflict-copies\">Conserver les contenus concurrents\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/conflict-copies\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conserver les contenus concurrents\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> active explicitement une politique de conservation par fichier.\nLes choix \u003ccode>--resolutions\u003c/code> s’appliquent d’abord, puis la composition textuelle\nbornée si activée. Pour les conflits de contenu ou ajout/ajout restants, une\nversion conserve le chemin initial et l’autre devient une copie voisine. Le\nplus petit ID complet de révision dans l’ordre lexicographique garde le chemin\ninitial : inverser les deux pairs produit le même ensemble de fichiers. Cet\nordre déterministe ne désigne pas la version la plus récente ou la meilleure.\nUne suppression concurrente à une modification conserve le fichier modifié au\nchemin initial et annonce \u003ccode>choice: keep_modified\u003c/code>. Les deux historiques parents\nconservent la suppression. Deux suppressions identiques suppriment le fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le nom de copie combine au plus 64 octets UTF-8 du radical, \u003ccode>.syrnck-conflict-\u003c/code>,\nl’ID complet de la révision perdante et 16 chiffres hexadécimaux du hash du chemin\ninitial. Les extensions d’au plus 16 octets UTF-8, dont \u003ccode>.uasset\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>.umap\u003c/code>, sont\nconservées. Date, machine et hasard n’influencent pas le résultat. Les rapports\nincluent \u003ccode>conflict_copies\u003c/code> et les lignes \u003ccode>resolved\u003c/code> avec \u003ccode>choice: conflict_copy\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>copy_path\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>copy_revision\u003c/code>, le \u003ccode>reason\u003c/code> initial et les deux descripteurs.\nLes copies sont des fichiers suivis ordinaires : les examiner puis les supprimer\ndans un commit ultérieur après avoir choisi la version souhaitée de l’asset.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un nom généré ou parent occupé dans les snapshots d’entrée bloque avec\n\u003ccode>conflict_copy_collision\u003c/code>, y compris alias de casse, dossier et fichier qui\naurait autrement été supprimé. Les obstacles de travail ignorés ou non suivis\nbloquent toujours le checkout. Conflits de casse, fichier/dossier, bases ambiguës\net historiques sans lien conservent leurs politiques habituelles. Cette option\nne déduit pas un renommage édité et ne compose pas les données binaires.\nLes copies réutilisent manifests et blocs existants sans payload binaire\nsupplémentaire dans le stockage. L’application vérifie les historiques complets\net utilise signature et checkout récupérable habituels. Le candidat figé, noms\nde copies compris, reprend sans recalculer la politique ni exiger la clé de signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>En C++, définir \u003ccode>NativeMergeOptions::conflict_copies = true\u003c/code>, faux par défaut.\nCherry-pick, revert et rebase gardent leurs politiques de résolution existantes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reports-and-validation\">Rapports et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/reports-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rapports et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Champs : mode, head, source, base choisie, merge_bases, compteurs de graphe, ready, objects_verified, result_files, changes, conflicts, resolved, resolution_context, blockers et workspace. Blocage de topologie : fichiers/workspace null ; conflits fichiers : workspace null ; sinon target du workspace null car aucune révision candidate n’est écrite. Code 0 prêt, 1 bloqué avec JSON, diagnostic pour entrée/métadonnée invalide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--verify audite entièrement les deux histoires et fichiers, résolutions personnalisées comprises. Sans lui, métadonnées et workspace sont contrôlés mais pas les blocs entrants, sauf tentative textuelle ciblée. Rapport/candidat bornés comme le replay ; mémoire selon graphe, snapshots et résultat. Callbacks non réentrants, arrêt coopératif et interface bilingue. Merge natif non exposé en C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>base \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> selected_best_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Optional explicit best ancestor.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-recover\">Appliquer et récupérer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-merge/apply-and-recover\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Appliquer et récupérer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’application recalcule toujours un plan entièrement vérifié et exige la tête exacte attendue, capturée sous verrou par le CLI si --expect est omis. Message par défaut Merge SOURCE_ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Up-to-date rend state unchanged sans écriture. Fast-forward restaure la révision source exacte sans commit supplémentaire. Divergence : révision aux parents [ancienne cible, source], message et date UTC courante, même avec arbre inchangé. Une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">identité configurée\u003c/a> crée une v2 avec auteur/committer actuels et signature facultative ; sinon elle reste v1. Branches/tags source et HEAD symbolique ne bougent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--no-ff exige deux parents même en avance rapide ; --ff-only refuse la divergence avec fast_forward_required. Options exclusives, valides au plan/application, sans contourner base, histoires sans lien, conflits ou workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le candidat passe par journal, sauvegardes de fichiers et publication atomique branche/archive/reçu. Abort avant publication seulement ; après, reprise du nettoyage. Un journal actif bloque GC et nouvelles mutations natives ; ouvrir le Store ne récupère jamais automatiquement le workspace. Des métadonnées préparées avant échec peuvent rester sans référence. Les fichiers privés restent en clair, y compris en Store chiffré.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux histoires sont accessibles via le commit à deux parents ; fast-forward inclut déjà l’ancienne cible et les archives checkout retiennent aussi les têtes. Les blocs restent protégés après GC. Revert de fusion utilise --mainline N.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La réponse directe ajoute operation merge, mode, previous_revision, merged_revision, base aux champs de checkout. La récupération rend le journal générique et sa cible, sans changer les formats révision/journal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge release --ff-only\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Merge feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Fusion native # merge-plan calcule topologie et snapshot complet proposé sans modifier objets, références ou workspace. Les décisions de fichiers à trois voies sont partagées avec le replay. Branche sélectionnée et dossier courant par défaut ; options branche/dossier pour les remplacer. Le CLI résout les sélecteurs, C++ utilise IDs exacts. Budgets de révisions/liens identiques à l’ ascendance . Le graphe complet est nécessaire, sans repli shallow. text Copier syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories Topologie # up_to_date : source ancêtre de cible, égalité comprise, snapshot actuel exact. fast_forward : cible ancêtre de source, snapshot source exact avec sa représentation. merge : divergence, fusion à trois voies sur la base choisie. Une meilleure base unique est automatique. Plusieurs donnent multiple_merge_bases ; --base doit choisir une des meilleures annoncées, pas un ancêtre plus ancien. Aucune base récursive virtuelle de Git n’est synthétisée. Sans ancêtre, unrelated_histories bloque sauf --allow-unrelated-histories, qui choisit l’arbre vide ; pas de base explicite alors. Les éditions indépendantes et changements contenu/mode indépendants d’un même fichier se combinent. Contenus concurrents différents, add/add et suppression/modification restent en conflit. Les collisions portables de casse/préfixe sont recalculées. L’égalité logique utilise hash complet/taille/mode, pas le seul découpage ; fast-forward adopte néanmoins la révision exacte. Sans conflit de snapshot, le plan de checkout exige suivis propres et contrôle ignorés/non-suivis, protections, liens, index et récupération. Une divergence de topologie seule peut être prête sans changement d’octets ; up-to-date reste soumis à ce contrôle prudent. --resolutions accepte les choix explicites . Composition textuelle optionnelle ci-dessous ; fusion tenant compte des renommages et synthèse récursive restent à développer. Composition textuelle explicite # NativeMergeOptions::text_merge active les limites TextDiffOptions ; vide, les décisions de fichiers et l’inspection de métadonnées restent inchangées. Les résolutions explicites passent d’abord ; seuls les conflits content restants sont tentés. Add/add, suppression/modification, binaire, éditions chevauchantes et namespace restent bloquants ; aucune modification de topologie ou représentation fast-forward. Chaque tentative reconstruit/vérifie les trois entrées même sans audit historique. Limites de taille et de lot avant lecture des payloads. Au plus 64 conflits, budget agrégé de 16 Mio pour entrées vérifiées et résultats propres, plus plafonds par fichier de travail/lignes/trace/sortie. Un refus conserve le conflit initial avec son motif, jamais de résultat partiel pour tenir un budget. CLI plan/application : --text-merge ; max-file-bytes, max-lines, max-work, max-output-bytes exigent cette option. Garder les mêmes limites aux deux étapes ; l’application recalcule avec la tête attendue. Progression/diagnostics bilingues, JSON canonique. text_merge rend limites, compteurs, manifestes générés et statuts/plages/métadonnées par chemin, null si désactivé, sans texte fusionné. Les actions réfèrent des IDs prédits qui peuvent ne pas exister ; aucun objet n’est créé au plan, même si un fichier se compose et un autre bloque. Voir la préparation virtuelle . L’application recalcule, vérifie histoires et workspace avant toute matérialisation. Chunks/manifestes doivent correspondre aux IDs prédits avant création de la révision à deux parents. Le checkout récupérable publie l’ensemble. Un échec avant journal peut laisser des objets générés non référencés sans changer branche/workspace ; ensuite resume/abort utilise le candidat retenu sans refaire la fusion. Annulation/observateurs respectent ces frontières. cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; // Default text limits, explicit opt-in. auto preview = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_tip , source_id , \"Merge feature\" , settings , operation ) ; text Copier syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432 Conserver les contenus concurrents # text Copier syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID --conflict-copies active explicitement une politique de conservation par fichier. Les choix --resolutions s’appliquent d’abord, puis la composition textuelle bornée si activée. Pour les conflits de contenu ou ajout/ajout restants, une version conserve le chemin initial et l’autre devient une copie voisine. Le plus petit ID complet de révision dans l’ordre lexicographique garde le chemin initial : inverser les deux pairs produit le même ensemble de fichiers. Cet ordre déterministe ne désigne pas la version la plus récente ou la meilleure. Une suppression concurrente à une modification conserve le fichier modifié au chemin initial et annonce choice: keep_modified . Les deux historiques parents conservent la suppression. Deux suppressions identiques suppriment le fichier. Le nom de copie combine au plus 64 octets UTF-8 du radical, .syrnck-conflict- , l’ID complet de la révision perdante et 16 chiffres hexadécimaux du hash du chemin initial. Les extensions d’au plus 16 octets UTF-8, dont .uasset et .umap , sont conservées. Date, machine et hasard n’influencent pas le résultat. Les rapports incluent conflict_copies et les lignes resolved avec choice: conflict_copy , copy_path , copy_revision , le reason initial et les deux descripteurs. Les copies sont des fichiers suivis ordinaires : les examiner puis les supprimer dans un commit ultérieur après avoir choisi la version souhaitée de l’asset. Un nom généré ou parent occupé dans les snapshots d’entrée bloque avec conflict_copy_collision , y compris alias de casse, dossier et fichier qui aurait autrement été supprimé. Les obstacles de travail ignorés ou non suivis bloquent toujours le checkout. Conflits de casse, fichier/dossier, bases ambiguës et historiques sans lien conservent leurs politiques habituelles. Cette option ne déduit pas un renommage édité et ne compose pas les données binaires. Les copies réutilisent manifests et blocs existants sans payload binaire supplémentaire dans le stockage. L’application vérifie les historiques complets et utilise signature et checkout récupérable habituels. Le candidat figé, noms de copies compris, reprend sans recalculer la politique ni exiger la clé de signature. En C++, définir NativeMergeOptions::conflict_copies = true , faux par défaut. Cherry-pick, revert et rebase gardent leurs politiques de résolution existantes. Rapports et vérification # Champs : mode, head, source, base choisie, merge_bases, compteurs de graphe, ready, objects_verified, result_files, changes, conflicts, resolved, resolution_context, blockers et workspace. Blocage de topologie : fichiers/workspace null ; conflits fichiers : workspace null ; sinon target du workspace null car aucune révision candidate n’est écrite. Code 0 prêt, 1 bloqué avec JSON, diagnostic pour entrée/métadonnée invalide. --verify audite entièrement les deux histoires et fichiers, résolutions personnalisées comprises. Sans lui, métadonnées et workspace sont contrôlés mais pas les blocs entrants, sauf tentative textuelle ciblée. Rapport/candidat bornés comme le replay ; mémoire selon graphe, snapshots et résultat. Callbacks non réentrants, arrêt coopératif et interface bilingue. Merge natif non exposé en C/Go. cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . base = selected_best_base ; // Optional explicit best ancestor. auto plan = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; Appliquer et récupérer # L’application recalcule toujours un plan entièrement vérifié et exige la tête exacte attendue, capturée sous verrou par le CLI si --expect est omis. Message par défaut Merge SOURCE_ID. Up-to-date rend state unchanged sans écriture. Fast-forward restaure la révision source exacte sans commit supplémentaire. Divergence : révision aux parents [ancienne cible, source], message et date UTC courante, même avec arbre inchangé. Une identité configurée crée une v2 avec auteur/committer actuels et signature facultative ; sinon elle reste v1. Branches/tags source et HEAD symbolique ne bougent pas. --no-ff exige deux parents même en avance rapide ; --ff-only refuse la divergence avec fast_forward_required. Options exclusives, valides au plan/application, sans contourner base, histoires sans lien, conflits ou workspace. Le candidat passe par journal, sauvegardes de fichiers et publication atomique branche/archive/reçu. Abort avant publication seulement ; après, reprise du nettoyage. Un journal actif bloque GC et nouvelles mutations natives ; ouvrir le Store ne récupère jamais automatiquement le workspace. Des métadonnées préparées avant échec peuvent rester sans référence. Les fichiers privés restent en clair, y compris en Store chiffré. Les deux histoires sont accessibles via le commit à deux parents ; fast-forward inclut déjà l’ancienne cible et les archives checkout retiennent aussi les têtes. Les blocs restent protégés après GC. Revert de fusion utilise --mainline N. La réponse directe ajoute operation merge, mode, previous_revision, merged_revision, base aux champs de checkout. La récupération rend le journal générique et sa cible, sans changer les formats révision/journal. text Copier syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\" syrnck native merge release --ff-only syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\" text Copier syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort cpp Copier auto applied = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , \"Merge feature\" , settings , operation ) ; syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID syrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default text limits, explicit opt-in. auto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); auto result = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_tip, source_id, \"Merge feature\", settings, operation); syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432 syrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify syrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.base = selected_best_base; // Optional explicit best ancestor. auto plan = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); syrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\" syrnck native merge release --ff-only syrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\" syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort auto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-merge","text":"Fusion native","level":1},{"id":"topology","text":"Topologie","level":2},{"id":"opt-in-text-composition","text":"Composition textuelle explicite","level":2},{"id":"conflict-copies","text":"Conserver les contenus concurrents","level":2},{"id":"reports-and-validation","text":"Rapports et vérification","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-recover","text":"Appliquer et récupérer","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --base BASE_ID\nsyrnck native merge-plan imported --allow-unrelated-histories\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default text limits, explicit opt-in.\nauto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nauto result = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_tip, source_id,\n                                    \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --max-file-bytes 2097152 --max-work 33554432\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --text-merge --conflict-copies --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.base = selected_best_base; // Optional explicit best ancestor.\nauto plan = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nsyrnck native merge feature --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Merge feature\"\nsyrnck native merge release --ff-only\nsyrnck native merge feature --no-ff -m \"Record feature integration\"\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nauto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      \"Merge feature\", settings, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-history","#/fr/native-resolutions","#/fr/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation","#/fr/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide","revision":"2a6fa096e078"},{"id":"native-rebase-todo","title":"Rebase éditable et interactif","description":"Guide : Rebase éditable et interactif.","section":"Versionnage","order":320,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"editable-and-interactive-native-rebase\">Rebase éditable et interactif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase-todo/editable-and-interactive-native-rebase\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rebase éditable et interactif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Un rebase linéaire peut recevoir une séquence complète revue, par JSON ou invite de terminal. Les deux utilisent le même planificateur, résolutions, composition textuelle et checkout récupérable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Garder le document hors des fichiers suivis, par exemple dans le Store, pour ne pas gêner le checkout. Le modèle contient IDs exacts head/base/onto et une étape par commit source. Déplacer les lignes pour réordonner ; conserver chaque source exactement une fois et utiliser drop explicitement. Champs manquants/inconnus, clés/sources dupliquées, commits étrangers, contexte périmé ou plage incomplète échouent avant création de candidat.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Remplacer les IDs illustratifs par les 64 caractères minuscules du modèle. Messages Unicode, multilignes et vides sont possibles. pick rejoue delta en gardant message/date ; drop ne rejoue rien mais archive l’histoire originale ; reword rejoue avec message obligatoire et date conservée ; squash replie le delta sur le résultat choisi précédent avec parent/date conservés et message source ajouté après deux newlines ; fixup replie en gardant aussi son message.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seul reword accepte message. Squash/fixup exigent un résultat choisi antérieur, drops intermédiaires ignorés. Un fold remplace le candidat virtuel précédent sans parent/commit supplémentaire ; ses objets provisoires ne sont pas stockés. L’archive de l’ancienne tête protège les sources, pas les snapshots provisoires non publiés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque delta reste mesuré contre le vrai parent source original malgré réordre/drop : supprimer une dépendance peut créer un conflit, jamais remplacer silencieusement tout le snapshot source. --resolutions/--text-merge peuvent le résoudre. Le contexte inclut la tête virtuelle courante ; changer des actions antérieures périme les choix. Une source dropped ne peut avoir de résolution.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une séquence explicite est honorée même si base = onto. Tout drop déplace la branche sur onto via le journal normal. Sans todo, l’ancien no-op même-base et ordre du plus ancien au plus récent restent disponibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"type\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"native-rebase-todo\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"head\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"HEAD_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"BASE_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"onto\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ONTO_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"steps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"SECOND_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reword\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"New message\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"FIRST_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"squash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"THIRD_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"fixup\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"FOURTH_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"action\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"drop\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L13\" data-line=\"13\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"terminal-input\">Saisie au terminal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase-todo/terminal-input\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Saisie au terminal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’invite affiche IDs et aperçus de messages échappés sur stderr. Entrer la séquence complète avec action et ID entier par ligne. Une ligne point confirme ; point seul choisit la séquence pick initiale. EOF avant confirmation échoue sans candidat ni changement. Ctrl+C demande l’annulation. Les entrées ne sont jamais du texte shell ; les actions restent anglaises. JSON pour messages multilignes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--todo et --interactive sont exclusifs ; pas de progression JSON avec l’invite. rebase-plan --interactive examine sans appliquer. Résultat JSON seul sur stdout. Entrée redirigée UTF-8 ; console Windows lue en UTF-16 puis convertie sans changer codepage ou traiter les backslashes du message comme séparateurs de chemin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">reword SECOND_ID New message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">squash FIRST_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">fixup THIRD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">drop FOURTH_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"limits-recovery-and-scope\">Bornes, récupération et portée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase-todo/limits-recovery-and-scope\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bornes, récupération et portée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les règles de plage linéaire, propreté et vérification complète s’appliquent. Todo JSON et saisie interactive agrégée : 16 Mio ; 100000 lignes ; 1 Mio par message explicite/combiné. Rapport séparément borné à 16 Mio, avec todo, action par étape, replaces des folds, result_commits, dropped_commits. completed_commits/replayed_commits comptent les deltas non dropped ; result_commits la chaîne finale après folds. Le modèle est aussi dans le plan ordinaire ; une plage non linéaire n’en a pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application vérifie les entrées, matérialise les commits survivants puis installe fichiers, branche et archive de l’ancienne tête avec le journal. Après interruption, suivre les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/apply-and-recover\">commandes de rebase\u003c/a>. Une préparation échouée/annulée peut laisser des objets valides non référencés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Il s’agit de réécriture linéaire interactive. Préservation des fusions, lancement d’éditeur, étapes split/edit, association automatique fixup et exécution de tests ne sont pas implémentés par ces actions.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Rebase éditable et interactif # Un rebase linéaire peut recevoir une séquence complète revue, par JSON ou invite de terminal. Les deux utilisent le même planificateur, résolutions, composition textuelle et checkout récupérable. Garder le document hors des fichiers suivis, par exemple dans le Store, pour ne pas gêner le checkout. Le modèle contient IDs exacts head/base/onto et une étape par commit source. Déplacer les lignes pour réordonner ; conserver chaque source exactement une fois et utiliser drop explicitement. Champs manquants/inconnus, clés/sources dupliquées, commits étrangers, contexte périmé ou plage incomplète échouent avant création de candidat. Remplacer les IDs illustratifs par les 64 caractères minuscules du modèle. Messages Unicode, multilignes et vides sont possibles. pick rejoue delta en gardant message/date ; drop ne rejoue rien mais archive l’histoire originale ; reword rejoue avec message obligatoire et date conservée ; squash replie le delta sur le résultat choisi précédent avec parent/date conservés et message source ajouté après deux newlines ; fixup replie en gardant aussi son message. Seul reword accepte message. Squash/fixup exigent un résultat choisi antérieur, drops intermédiaires ignorés. Un fold remplace le candidat virtuel précédent sans parent/commit supplémentaire ; ses objets provisoires ne sont pas stockés. L’archive de l’ancienne tête protège les sources, pas les snapshots provisoires non publiés. Chaque delta reste mesuré contre le vrai parent source original malgré réordre/drop : supprimer une dépendance peut créer un conflit, jamais remplacer silencieusement tout le snapshot source. --resolutions/--text-merge peuvent le résoudre. Le contexte inclut la tête virtuelle courante ; changer des actions antérieures périme les choix. Une source dropped ne peut avoir de résolution. Une séquence explicite est honorée même si base = onto. Tout drop déplace la branche sur onto via le journal normal. Sans todo, l’ancien no-op même-base et ordre du plus ancien au plus récent restent disponibles. text Copier syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD json Copier { \"version\" : 1 , \"type\" : \"native-rebase-todo\" , \"head\" : \"HEAD_ID\" , \"base\" : \"BASE_ID\" , \"onto\" : \"ONTO_ID\" , \"steps\" : [ { \"source\" : \"SECOND_ID\" , \"action\" : \"reword\" , \"message\" : \"New message\" } , { \"source\" : \"FIRST_ID\" , \"action\" : \"squash\" } , { \"source\" : \"THIRD_ID\" , \"action\" : \"fixup\" } , { \"source\" : \"FOURTH_ID\" , \"action\" : \"drop\" } ] } Saisie au terminal # L’invite affiche IDs et aperçus de messages échappés sur stderr. Entrer la séquence complète avec action et ID entier par ligne. Une ligne point confirme ; point seul choisit la séquence pick initiale. EOF avant confirmation échoue sans candidat ni changement. Ctrl+C demande l’annulation. Les entrées ne sont jamais du texte shell ; les actions restent anglaises. JSON pour messages multilignes. --todo et --interactive sont exclusifs ; pas de progression JSON avec l’invite. rebase-plan --interactive examine sans appliquer. Résultat JSON seul sur stdout. Entrée redirigée UTF-8 ; console Windows lue en UTF-16 puis convertie sans changer codepage ou traiter les backslashes du message comme séparateurs de chemin. text Copier syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive text Copier reword SECOND_ID New message squash FIRST_ID fixup THIRD_ID drop FOURTH_ID . Bornes, récupération et portée # Les règles de plage linéaire, propreté et vérification complète s’appliquent. Todo JSON et saisie interactive agrégée : 16 Mio ; 100000 lignes ; 1 Mio par message explicite/combiné. Rapport séparément borné à 16 Mio, avec todo, action par étape, replaces des folds, result_commits, dropped_commits. completed_commits/replayed_commits comptent les deltas non dropped ; result_commits la chaîne finale après folds. Le modèle est aussi dans le plan ordinaire ; une plage non linéaire n’en a pas. L’application vérifie les entrées, matérialise les commits survivants puis installe fichiers, branche et archive de l’ancienne tête avec le journal. Après interruption, suivre les commandes de rebase . Une préparation échouée/annulée peut laisser des objets valides non référencés. Il s’agit de réécriture linéaire interactive. Préservation des fusions, lancement d’éditeur, étapes split/edit, association automatique fixup et exécution de tests ne sont pas implémentés par ces actions. syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD { \"version\": 1, \"type\": \"native-rebase-todo\", \"head\": \"HEAD_ID\", \"base\": \"BASE_ID\", \"onto\": \"ONTO_ID\", \"steps\": [ {\"source\": \"SECOND_ID\", \"action\": \"reword\", \"message\": \"New message\"}, {\"source\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"action\": \"squash\"}, {\"source\": \"THIRD_ID\", \"action\": \"fixup\"}, {\"source\": \"FOURTH_ID\", \"action\": \"drop\"} ] } syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive reword SECOND_ID New message squash FIRST_ID fixup THIRD_ID drop FOURTH_ID .","headings":[{"id":"editable-and-interactive-native-rebase","text":"Rebase éditable et interactif","level":1},{"id":"terminal-input","text":"Saisie au terminal","level":2},{"id":"limits-recovery-and-scope","text":"Bornes, récupération et portée","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-2-L13","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5"],"languages":["text","json"],"code":"syrnck native rebase-todo OLD_BASE --onto upstream > todo.json\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --todo todo.json --expect OLD_HEAD\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"type\": \"native-rebase-todo\",\n  \"head\": \"HEAD_ID\",\n  \"base\": \"BASE_ID\",\n  \"onto\": \"ONTO_ID\",\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\"source\": \"SECOND_ID\", \"action\": \"reword\", \"message\": \"New message\"},\n    {\"source\": \"FIRST_ID\", \"action\": \"squash\"},\n    {\"source\": \"THIRD_ID\", \"action\": \"fixup\"},\n    {\"source\": \"FOURTH_ID\", \"action\": \"drop\"}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --interactive\nreword SECOND_ID New message\nsquash FIRST_ID\nfixup THIRD_ID\ndrop FOURTH_ID\n.","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-rebase/apply-and-recover"],"kind":"guide","revision":"9fdfbce8fb23"},{"id":"native-rebase","title":"Rebase linéaire","description":"Guide : Rebase linéaire.","section":"Versionnage","order":310,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-linear-rebase\">Rebase linéaire natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/native-linear-rebase\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rebase linéaire natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le plan rejoue la plage explicite (BASE, HEAD] sur une autre révision. BASE est exclue et doit être atteinte en suivant les parents uniques de la branche ; aucune base commune implicite. Une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-rebase-todo\">séquence éditable/interactif\u003c/a> peut réordonner, retirer, reformuler, squash/fixup ; sans séquence, les règles ordinaires ci-dessous s’appliquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sélecteurs usuels au CLI, IDs exacts en C++. Branche sélectionnée par défaut, --branch et --directory dans les contraintes de liaison. Workspace avec tous suivis propres, aucun non-suivi/ignoré obstructif, aucun index ni récupération checkout/switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"file-replay-and-topology\">Rejeu des fichiers et topologie\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/file-replay-and-topology\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rejeu des fichiers et topologie\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ordre du plus ancien au plus récent : parent original comme base, résultat virtuel précédent comme current et commit original comme incoming. Changements indépendants de fichier/contenu/mode combinés. Conflit de contenu, ajout/suppression ou namespace arrête au premier commit, sans traiter les suivants comme appliqués. Composition textuelle facultative ; fusion consciente des renommages non implémentée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fusion dans la plage : merge_in_range ; fusion à la borne exclue autorisée. Racine atteinte avant BASE : base_not_on_linear_history. Aucun choix arbitraire ni aplatissement silencieux de parents de merge. Défaut 1000 commits, réglable 1..100000 ; dépassement échoue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>BASE = ONTO sur plage valide : à jour et IDs exacts conservés. HEAD = BASE : plage vide proposant le déplacement sur ONTO. Sinon chaque commit garde son message et reçoit un seul nouveau parent virtuel. Sans identité, les dates anciennes restent conservées ; avec identité, auteur d’origine conservé et committer actuel avec une date commune figée. Les replays logiquement vides sont conservés comme commits ; changement de représentation seul n’est pas une édition de contenu.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-only-report-and-bounds\">Rapport et bornes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/read-only-report-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rapport et bornes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>JSON : head/base/onto/mode, commits ordonnés, steps, completed_commits, target proposée, workspace, ready, blockers. Une étape décrit source/parent d’origine, head virtuelle, target, empty, changements et conflits. Prêt 0 ; bloqué 1 avec JSON ; erreurs d’options/métadonnées en diagnostic. Plage bloquée/conflit : target/workspace null.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les IDs virtuels suivent l’algorithme de révision ordinaire et la validation complète des métadonnées. Aucun candidat n’est écrit ni fichier/référence déplacé ; un ID proposé peut donc être absent du Store. Avec identité, \u003ccode>--committer-time SECONDS\u003c/code> fige la date exposée par \u003ccode>committer_timestamp\u003c/code> ; sinon des invocations distinctes peuvent choisir des dates et IDs différents. Mêmes entrées, identité, clé et date donnent les mêmes IDs. Sans identité, les dates anciennes sont conservées. --verify audite toute l’histoire head/base/onto et les fichiers ; sans cela, pas d’audit de blocs hors fichiers éligibles aux tentatives textuelles. Chaque étape rend resolution_context vide et resolved ; le contexte global est un modèle de série.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rapports et chaque révision plate limités à 16 Mio. Le plan garde liste d’IDs/rapport bornés et snapshots courants, pas toutes les tables candidates ; il peut relire les métadonnées. Beaucoup de changements par étape peuvent atteindre le plafond avant le compte de commits. C++/CLI, progression bilingue, annulation coopérative et callbacks non réentrants. Historique C/Go et préservation des merges restent à développer ; l’édition interactive linéaire est disponible séparément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>max_commits \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">500\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                              settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-recover\">Appliquer et récupérer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/apply-and-recover\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Appliquer et récupérer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’application recalcule/vérifie tout le plan prêt avant stockage, puis répète le calcul déterministe et écrit les candidats complets du plus ancien au plus récent, exigeant l’ID final prédit. Elle ne garde pas toutes leurs tables en mémoire ; révisions v1 ou v2 immuables. Les commits vides suivent les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/rewrites\">règles d’auteur/committer\u003c/a>. La récupération utilise les candidats figés sans clé privée de signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seul le dernier candidat est installé via checkout récupérable ; aucune étape intermédiaire ne bouge branche ou fichiers. L’archive checkout protège l’ancienne tête et tout l’historique d’origine ; la nouvelle chaîne protège ONTO et chaque résultat rejoué, même intermédiaire après GC. Retrouver l’ancienne histoire avec native archives --kind checkout. Branches/tags source et HEAD ne changent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--expect exact, capturé sous verrou si absent au CLI et toujours requis en C++. Up-to-date rend unchanged sans écriture mais vérifie données et workspace. Plage vide restaure ONTO exact sans commit. Conflit, index, suivis sales et histoire incomplète bloquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant journal, annulation conserve branche/workspace, avec candidats complets orphelins possibles. Après, inspecter et reprendre/annuler explicitement ; abort seulement avant publication de branche, ensuite nettoyage. Ouverture sans récupération automatique ; journal actif bloque GC/mutations. Préparations et sauvegardes privées restent en clair.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Réponse directe : operation rebase, mode, previous_revision, base, onto, replayed_commits plus reçu checkout. Reprise : journal/cible génériques. Formats inchangés et racines sans expiration automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                       old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"resolve-conflicts-by-step\">Résoudre par étape\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/resolve-conflicts-by-step\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Résoudre par étape\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>--resolutions prend le contexte global du plan dans un document puis, pour chaque conflit, son contexte d’étape dans steps avec files complété. Garder les anciens choix et replanifier avec mêmes BASE/ONTO ; le modèle global repart toujours avec steps vide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Wrapper exact : version 1, head d’origine, base exclue, onto, steps. Chaque entrée du schéma de résolution utilise operation rebase, source commit original, base/parent parent original, head parent virtuel courant : ONTO d’abord, candidat précédent ensuite. Utiliser les IDs/contextes rendus plutôt que les fabriquer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choix current/incoming/base/remove/file avec mode explicite, limités aux conflits de l’étape. Namespace revérifié ; résolution partielle bloque sans candidat/fichier écrit. Une étape devenue vide reste un commit avec message/date d’origine. Manifestes personnalisés audités par --verify et toujours à l’application ; garder leur pin si GC possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Changer head/BASE/ONTO rejette le wrapper. Modifier un choix antérieur change les IDs suivants et rend leurs contextes périmés. Source étrangère, étapes dupliquées, clés JSON dupliquées, champs inconnus et choix invalides échouent. Les étapes tardives sont contrôlées à leur arrivée ; aucun préfixe conflictuel n’est publié.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Document 16 Mio, 100000 étapes et 100000 choix de fichiers au total ; décodage brut profondeur 64, 1100010 champs totaux. Les décodeurs d’opération gardent leurs limites propres. NativeRebaseResolutionSet range les étapes par ID source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeRebaseResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> step \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"steps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>steps\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> step\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>resolutions \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Composition textuelle bornée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-rebase/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Composition textuelle bornée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>--text-merge tente les conflits content UTF-8 après les choix explicites, en conservant fins de ligne, absence de newline finale et modes indépendants. Binaire, chevauchement, ajouts/suppressions et namespace restent bloquants. Aucun marqueur de conflit ni histoire partielle publiée ; commits vides conservés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un lot couvre toute la plage : 64 tentatives maximum et 16 Mio entrées/résultats propres, plus max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes par fichier. Relire un ancien résultat virtuel le refacture ; les budgets ne repartent pas par commit et leur épuisement bloque tout. Les options de budget exigent --text-merge.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>text_merge global expose compteurs cumulés/limites. Les fichiers par étape décrivent ses tentatives et leurs statuts/motifs/coordonnées/manifestes, avec compteurs cumulatifs, sans texte brut. Choix explicites prioritaires sans tentative automatique. Garder options/budgets/choix précédents pour des résolutions ultérieures : toute modification change la tête virtuelle attendue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un cache mémoire borné garde les contenus générés pour les étapes suivantes sans stockage. IDs CDC et révisions restent reproductibles. L’application vérifie tout le plan/workspace avant de matérialiser tous les manifestes générés, intermédiaires compris, écrire la chaîne puis démarrer le checkout. Aucun préfixe n’est publié si une étape ultérieure bloque. Arrêt avant journal : objets complets non référencés possibles, branche/workspace intacts. Nouvelle chaîne et archive d’origine assurent la rétention après GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                     old_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_base\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Rebase linéaire natif # Le plan rejoue la plage explicite (BASE, HEAD] sur une autre révision. BASE est exclue et doit être atteinte en suivant les parents uniques de la branche ; aucune base commune implicite. Une séquence éditable/interactif peut réordonner, retirer, reformuler, squash/fixup ; sans séquence, les règles ordinaires ci-dessous s’appliquent. Sélecteurs usuels au CLI, IDs exacts en C++. Branche sélectionnée par défaut, --branch et --directory dans les contraintes de liaison. Workspace avec tous suivis propres, aucun non-suivi/ignoré obstructif, aucun index ni récupération checkout/switch. text Copier syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500 Rejeu des fichiers et topologie # Ordre du plus ancien au plus récent : parent original comme base, résultat virtuel précédent comme current et commit original comme incoming. Changements indépendants de fichier/contenu/mode combinés. Conflit de contenu, ajout/suppression ou namespace arrête au premier commit, sans traiter les suivants comme appliqués. Composition textuelle facultative ; fusion consciente des renommages non implémentée. Fusion dans la plage : merge_in_range ; fusion à la borne exclue autorisée. Racine atteinte avant BASE : base_not_on_linear_history. Aucun choix arbitraire ni aplatissement silencieux de parents de merge. Défaut 1000 commits, réglable 1..100000 ; dépassement échoue. BASE = ONTO sur plage valide : à jour et IDs exacts conservés. HEAD = BASE : plage vide proposant le déplacement sur ONTO. Sinon chaque commit garde son message et reçoit un seul nouveau parent virtuel. Sans identité, les dates anciennes restent conservées ; avec identité, auteur d’origine conservé et committer actuel avec une date commune figée. Les replays logiquement vides sont conservés comme commits ; changement de représentation seul n’est pas une édition de contenu. Rapport et bornes # JSON : head/base/onto/mode, commits ordonnés, steps, completed_commits, target proposée, workspace, ready, blockers. Une étape décrit source/parent d’origine, head virtuelle, target, empty, changements et conflits. Prêt 0 ; bloqué 1 avec JSON ; erreurs d’options/métadonnées en diagnostic. Plage bloquée/conflit : target/workspace null. Les IDs virtuels suivent l’algorithme de révision ordinaire et la validation complète des métadonnées. Aucun candidat n’est écrit ni fichier/référence déplacé ; un ID proposé peut donc être absent du Store. Avec identité, --committer-time SECONDS fige la date exposée par committer_timestamp ; sinon des invocations distinctes peuvent choisir des dates et IDs différents. Mêmes entrées, identité, clé et date donnent les mêmes IDs. Sans identité, les dates anciennes sont conservées. --verify audite toute l’histoire head/base/onto et les fichiers ; sans cela, pas d’audit de blocs hors fichiers éligibles aux tentatives textuelles. Chaque étape rend resolution_context vide et resolved ; le contexte global est un modèle de série. Rapports et chaque révision plate limités à 16 Mio. Le plan garde liste d’IDs/rapport bornés et snapshots courants, pas toutes les tables candidates ; il peut relire les métadonnées. Beaucoup de changements par étape peuvent atteindre le plafond avant le compte de commits. C++/CLI, progression bilingue, annulation coopérative et callbacks non réentrants. Historique C/Go et préservation des merges restent à développer ; l’édition interactive linéaire est disponible séparément. cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeRebaseOptions settings ; settings . max_commits = 500 ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase ( project , \"feature\" , old_base , new_base , settings , true , operation ) ; Appliquer et récupérer # L’application recalcule/vérifie tout le plan prêt avant stockage, puis répète le calcul déterministe et écrit les candidats complets du plus ancien au plus récent, exigeant l’ID final prédit. Elle ne garde pas toutes leurs tables en mémoire ; révisions v1 ou v2 immuables. Les commits vides suivent les règles d’auteur/committer . La récupération utilise les candidats figés sans clé privée de signature. Seul le dernier candidat est installé via checkout récupérable ; aucune étape intermédiaire ne bouge branche ou fichiers. L’archive checkout protège l’ancienne tête et tout l’historique d’origine ; la nouvelle chaîne protège ONTO et chaque résultat rejoué, même intermédiaire après GC. Retrouver l’ancienne histoire avec native archives --kind checkout. Branches/tags source et HEAD ne changent pas. --expect exact, capturé sous verrou si absent au CLI et toujours requis en C++. Up-to-date rend unchanged sans écriture mais vérifie données et workspace. Plage vide restaure ONTO exact sans commit. Conflit, index, suivis sales et histoire incomplète bloquent. Avant journal, annulation conserve branche/workspace, avec candidats complets orphelins possibles. Après, inspecter et reprendre/annuler explicitement ; abort seulement avant publication de branche, ensuite nettoyage. Ouverture sans récupération automatique ; journal actif bloque GC/mutations. Préparations et sauvegardes privées restent en clair. Réponse directe : operation rebase, mode, previous_revision, base, onto, replayed_commits plus reçu checkout. Reprise : journal/cible génériques. Formats inchangés et racines sans expiration automatique. text Copier syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort cpp Copier auto applied = store . rebase_directory ( project , \"feature\" , expected_id , old_base , new_base , settings , operation ) ; Résoudre par étape # --resolutions prend le contexte global du plan dans un document puis, pour chaque conflit, son contexte d’étape dans steps avec files complété. Garder les anciens choix et replanifier avec mêmes BASE/ONTO ; le modèle global repart toujours avec steps vide. Wrapper exact : version 1, head d’origine, base exclue, onto, steps. Chaque entrée du schéma de résolution utilise operation rebase, source commit original, base/parent parent original, head parent virtuel courant : ONTO d’abord, candidat précédent ensuite. Utiliser les IDs/contextes rendus plutôt que les fabriquer. Choix current/incoming/base/remove/file avec mode explicite, limités aux conflits de l’étape. Namespace revérifié ; résolution partielle bloque sans candidat/fichier écrit. Une étape devenue vide reste un commit avec message/date d’origine. Manifestes personnalisés audités par --verify et toujours à l’application ; garder leur pin si GC possible. Changer head/BASE/ONTO rejette le wrapper. Modifier un choix antérieur change les IDs suivants et rend leurs contextes périmés. Source étrangère, étapes dupliquées, clés JSON dupliquées, champs inconnus et choix invalides échouent. Les étapes tardives sont contrôlées à leur arrivée ; aucun préfixe conflictuel n’est publié. Document 16 Mio, 100000 étapes et 100000 choix de fichiers au total ; décodage brut profondeur 64, 1100010 champs totaux. Les décodeurs d’opération gardent leurs limites propres. NativeRebaseResolutionSet range les étapes par ID source. text Copier syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID cpp Copier auto choices = syrnck :: NativeRebaseResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; auto step = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"steps\" ] [ 0 ] [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; step . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; choices . steps [ step . source ] = step ; settings . resolutions = choices ; Composition textuelle bornée # --text-merge tente les conflits content UTF-8 après les choix explicites, en conservant fins de ligne, absence de newline finale et modes indépendants. Binaire, chevauchement, ajouts/suppressions et namespace restent bloquants. Aucun marqueur de conflit ni histoire partielle publiée ; commits vides conservés. Un lot couvre toute la plage : 64 tentatives maximum et 16 Mio entrées/résultats propres, plus max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes par fichier. Relire un ancien résultat virtuel le refacture ; les budgets ne repartent pas par commit et leur épuisement bloque tout. Les options de budget exigent --text-merge. text_merge global expose compteurs cumulés/limites. Les fichiers par étape décrivent ses tentatives et leurs statuts/motifs/coordonnées/manifestes, avec compteurs cumulatifs, sans texte brut. Choix explicites prioritaires sans tentative automatique. Garder options/budgets/choix précédents pour des résolutions ultérieures : toute modification change la tête virtuelle attendue. Un cache mémoire borné garde les contenus générés pour les étapes suivantes sans stockage. IDs CDC et révisions restent reproductibles. L’application vérifie tout le plan/workspace avant de matérialiser tous les manifestes générés, intermédiaires compris, écrire la chaîne puis démarrer le checkout. Aucun préfixe n’est publié si une étape ultérieure bloque. Arrêt avant journal : objets complets non référencés possibles, branche/workspace intacts. Nouvelle chaîne et archive d’origine assurent la rétention après GC. text Copier syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeRebaseOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase ( project , \"feature\" , old_base , new_base , settings , true ) ; auto result = store . rebase_directory ( project , \"feature\" , expected_head , old_base , new_base , settings ) ; syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500 syrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings; settings.max_commits = 500; auto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true, operation); syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort auto applied = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_id, old_base, new_base, settings, operation); syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID auto choices = syrnck::NativeRebaseResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); auto step = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"steps\"][0][\"resolution_context\"]); step.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; choices.steps[step.source] = step; settings.resolutions = choices; syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); auto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true); auto result = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_head, old_base, new_base, settings);","headings":[{"id":"native-linear-rebase","text":"Rebase linéaire natif","level":1},{"id":"file-replay-and-topology","text":"Rejeu des fichiers et topologie","level":2},{"id":"read-only-report-and-bounds","text":"Rapport et bornes","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-recover","text":"Appliquer et récupérer","level":2},{"id":"resolve-conflicts-by-step","text":"Résoudre par étape","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Composition textuelle bornée","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto NEW_BASE --max-commits 500\nsyrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.max_commits = 500;\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base,\n                              settings, true, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nauto applied = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_id,\n                                       old_base, new_base, settings, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeRebaseResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nauto step = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"steps\"][0][\"resolution_context\"]);\nstep.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nchoices.steps[step.source] = step;\nsettings.resolutions = choices;\nsyrnck native rebase-plan OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native rebase OLD_BASE --onto upstream --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck::NativeRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace();\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase(project, \"feature\", old_base, new_base, settings, true);\nauto result = store.rebase_directory(project, \"feature\", expected_head,\n                                     old_base, new_base, settings);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-rebase-todo","#/fr/native-authorship/rewrites"],"kind":"guide","revision":"102893f9dac4"},{"id":"native-replay","title":"Cherry-pick et revert","description":"Guide : Cherry-pick et revert.","section":"Versionnage","order":330,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-cherry-pick-and-revert\">Cherry-pick et revert natifs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-replay/native-cherry-pick-and-revert\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cherry-pick et revert natifs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le plan applique le delta de fichiers d’un commit à la branche : parent vers source pour cherry-pick, source vers parent pour revert. Les changements ultérieurs indépendants de la cible peuvent être conservés. Le plan n’écrit rien.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--mainline N choisit un parent à partir de 1, explicitement requis pour une fusion. Commit ordinaire : parent 1 ; racine : arbre vide et option mainline refusée. Sélecteurs au CLI, IDs exacts en C++. Plan prêt : 0 ; vide, conflictuel ou bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; entrée/métadonnée invalide : diagnostic stderr.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"three-way-decisions\">Décisions à trois voies\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-replay/three-way-decisions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Décisions à trois voies\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Base, branche actuelle et entrant sont comparés par hash complet/taille/mode. Rechunking seul n’est pas une édition. Changements identiques ou entrant inchangé gardent le manifeste actuel ; un seul côté changé est retenu ; contenu et mode indépendants d’un même fichier peuvent se combiner.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Conflits : content, add_add, delete_modify, modify_delete ; choix explicites via --resolutions, composition textuelle optionnelle. La fusion des renommages reste hors de ces règles. Les ajouts indépendants sont contrôlés ensemble pour case_collision et file_directory_collision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rapport : operation, branch, head attendue, source, parent/mainline, ready, empty, objects_verified, result_files, changes, conflicts, resolved, resolution_context. Résumés complets manifeste/hash/taille/mode ; conflits avec les trois côtés, namespace avec chemin opposé. Un seul conflit bloque tout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans conflit, workspace contient le préflight habituel : suivis propres, ignorés/non-suivis, protections, liens, collisions, staging et récupération. Sa target reste null sans candidat écrit. Sans delta logique, empty true et non prêt ; avec conflits, workspace null. --verify contrôle les deux histoires et fichiers complets ; sinon seules métadonnées et fichiers de travail, pas blocs entrants/personnalisés hors composition textuelle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>JSON compact limité à 16 Mio ; candidat réservé conservativement avec 4 Kio pour en-tête/message. Mémoire selon trois snapshots, résultat et rapport ; pas d’arbre en flux. Les longs messages gardent la limite de révision. C++/CLI seulement, callbacks non réentrants, progression/erreurs bilingues et raisons JSON stables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                              syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> inverse \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> merge_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                                 syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>revert\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Composition textuelle bornée optionnelle\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-replay/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Composition textuelle bornée optionnelle\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>plan_replay_with_options accepte NativeReplayOptions (mainline, resolutions, text_merge), avec anciennes entrées compatibles et mêmes défauts. Sans text_merge, comportement par fichier inchangé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choix explicites d’abord ; conflits content de fichiers existants seulement. Pour cherry-pick, base textuelle = parent choisi, entrant = source ; pour revert, base = source, entrant = parent choisi ; côté courant = branche. Cette inversion retire le delta tout en gardant les changements ultérieurs indépendants. Racines, ajouts/suppressions, namespace, binaire et chevauchements gardent leurs conflits.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>text_merge null si désactivé ; sinon mêmes limites/compteurs/statuts/plages/métadonnées que merge. Trois entrées reconstruites et hashées à chaque tentative même sans audit d’histoire ; plafonds avant lecture, au plus 64 conflits et 16 Mio agrégés entrées/résultats propres, en plus des limites par fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les résultats propres possèdent des IDs v1 prédits et octets candidats internes, sans stockage ni texte brut dans le plan. Une résolution tout-current peut rester vide ; tout conflit bloque l’ensemble. Tous les contrôles workspace/index/journal/mainline/contexte restent valables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>replay_directory_with_options exige la tête attendue, recalcule et vérifie les deux fermetures avant toute matérialisation. Vide/conflit/index/obstacle ne matérialisent rien. Manifestes générés conformes aux IDs prédits avant révision à un parent. Le journal retient le candidat complet pour resume/abort ; avant journal, seuls des objets non référencés peuvent rester, pas de changement de branche/disque. Le manifeste final conserve directement ses blocs sans dépendre de l’histoire source sans lien ; garder sa branche/tag/pin si cette histoire doit aussi survivre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>CLI : --text-merge et max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes, ces limites exigeant l’activation. Même options au plan/application. Mainline, résolutions, tête attendue, message requis et progression gardent leur sens ; tout conflit/budget restant bloque tous les fichiers générés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Default per-file text limits.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_replay_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">replay_directory_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>revert\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Undo selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">    settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"apply-a-planned-change\">Appliquer un changement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-replay/apply-a-planned-change\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Appliquer un changement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Message obligatoire. Branche/dossier/sélecteurs comme le plan ; --expect est un ID exact minuscule, sinon capture sous verrou au CLI, toujours requis par l’API.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application recalcule et vérifie histoires et fichiers ; elle refuse conflit, résultat vide, récupération checkout/switch et tout index. Tous les suivis et destinations passent clean-tracked, sans force. Le candidat v1 utilise message, date UTC courante et seul parent ancienne tête cible ; cherry-pick n’ajoute pas source comme parent de fusion. Une source fusionnée exige mainline mais produit toujours un parent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le checkout récupérable change les fichiers avant publication atomique branche/archive/reçu ; la branche garde l’ancienne tête jusqu’à vérification de toutes les sorties. Ancienne tête/histoire/blocs restent accessibles par parent et archive. HEAD et références source restent inchangés. Une source sans lien nécessite sa propre racine pour garder toute son ascendance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La réponse directe ajoute operation, replayed_revision, previous_revision et mainline aux champs du reçu checkout (target/state/archive). La récupération rend l’identité générique et sa cible, sans ces labels de réponse. Formats inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">replay_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                      syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeReplayKind\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>cherry_pick\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Apply selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"interruption-abort-and-retention\">Interruption, annulation et rétention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-replay/interruption-abort-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Interruption, annulation et rétention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Avant journal, arrêt/erreur conservent branche et workspace mais peuvent laisser un candidat immuable non référencé. Après journal, inspecter puis checkout-recover ou --abort. Resume installe le candidat enregistré sans recalculer le replay. Abort rétablit les fichiers originaux et garde la branche avant publication ; après publication, seul le nettoyage peut finir.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ouvrir le Store ne change aucun fichier et le GC reste bloqué tant que le journal est actif. Inspecter la tête après perte de réponse ; l’ancien --expect empêche un second commit. Les replays vides sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Révisions finales et parents survivent au GC. Un candidat non publié et abandonné peut être collecté après retrait des pins temporaires. Archives/reçus checkout n’expirent pas automatiquement, leur \u003ca href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention\">retrait protégé\u003c/a> est explicite. Fichiers préparés et sauvegardes privées restent en clair malgré le chiffrement des objets ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">limites du checkout\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\"># Or, before branch publication:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Cherry-pick et revert natifs # Le plan applique le delta de fichiers d’un commit à la branche : parent vers source pour cherry-pick, source vers parent pour revert. Les changements ultérieurs indépendants de la cible peuvent être conservés. Le plan n’écrit rien. --mainline N choisit un parent à partir de 1, explicitement requis pour une fusion. Commit ordinaire : parent 1 ; racine : arbre vide et option mainline refusée. Sélecteurs au CLI, IDs exacts en C++. Plan prêt : 0 ; vide, conflictuel ou bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; entrée/métadonnée invalide : diagnostic stderr. text Copier syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory . syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2 Décisions à trois voies # Base, branche actuelle et entrant sont comparés par hash complet/taille/mode. Rechunking seul n’est pas une édition. Changements identiques ou entrant inchangé gardent le manifeste actuel ; un seul côté changé est retenu ; contenu et mode indépendants d’un même fichier peuvent se combiner. Conflits : content, add_add, delete_modify, modify_delete ; choix explicites via --resolutions, composition textuelle optionnelle. La fusion des renommages reste hors de ces règles. Les ajouts indépendants sont contrôlés ensemble pour case_collision et file_directory_collision. Rapport : operation, branch, head attendue, source, parent/mainline, ready, empty, objects_verified, result_files, changes, conflicts, resolved, resolution_context. Résumés complets manifeste/hash/taille/mode ; conflits avec les trois côtés, namespace avec chemin opposé. Un seul conflit bloque tout. Sans conflit, workspace contient le préflight habituel : suivis propres, ignorés/non-suivis, protections, liens, collisions, staging et récupération. Sa target reste null sans candidat écrit. Sans delta logique, empty true et non prêt ; avec conflits, workspace null. --verify contrôle les deux histoires et fichiers complets ; sinon seules métadonnées et fichiers de travail, pas blocs entrants/personnalisés hors composition textuelle. JSON compact limité à 16 Mio ; candidat réservé conservativement avec 4 Kio pour en-tête/message. Mémoire selon trois snapshots, résultat et rapport ; pas d’arbre en flux. Les longs messages gardent la limite de révision. C++/CLI seulement, callbacks non réentrants, progression/erreurs bilingues et raisons JSON stables. cpp Copier auto plan = store . plan_replay ( project , \"main\" , selected_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , { } , true , operation ) ; auto inverse = store . plan_replay ( project , \"main\" , merge_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: revert , 2 , false , operation ) ; Composition textuelle bornée optionnelle # plan_replay_with_options accepte NativeReplayOptions (mainline, resolutions, text_merge), avec anciennes entrées compatibles et mêmes défauts. Sans text_merge, comportement par fichier inchangé. Choix explicites d’abord ; conflits content de fichiers existants seulement. Pour cherry-pick, base textuelle = parent choisi, entrant = source ; pour revert, base = source, entrant = parent choisi ; côté courant = branche. Cette inversion retire le delta tout en gardant les changements ultérieurs indépendants. Racines, ajouts/suppressions, namespace, binaire et chevauchements gardent leurs conflits. text_merge null si désactivé ; sinon mêmes limites/compteurs/statuts/plages/métadonnées que merge. Trois entrées reconstruites et hashées à chaque tentative même sans audit d’histoire ; plafonds avant lecture, au plus 64 conflits et 16 Mio agrégés entrées/résultats propres, en plus des limites par fichier. Les résultats propres possèdent des IDs v1 prédits et octets candidats internes, sans stockage ni texte brut dans le plan. Une résolution tout-current peut rester vide ; tout conflit bloque l’ensemble. Tous les contrôles workspace/index/journal/mainline/contexte restent valables. replay_directory_with_options exige la tête attendue, recalcule et vérifie les deux fermetures avant toute matérialisation. Vide/conflit/index/obstacle ne matérialisent rien. Manifestes générés conformes aux IDs prédits avant révision à un parent. Le journal retient le candidat complet pour resume/abort ; avant journal, seuls des objets non référencés peuvent rester, pas de changement de branche/disque. Le manifeste final conserve directement ses blocs sans dépendre de l’histoire source sans lien ; garder sa branche/tag/pin si cette histoire doit aussi survivre. CLI : --text-merge et max-file-bytes/max-lines/max-work/max-output-bytes, ces limites exigeant l’activation. Même options au plan/application. Mainline, résolutions, tête attendue, message requis et progression gardent leur sens ; tout conflit/budget restant bloque tous les fichiers générés. cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeReplayOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; // Default per-file text limits. // settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent. auto plan = store . plan_replay_with_options ( project , \"main\" , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , settings , true , operation ) ; cpp Copier auto applied = store . replay_directory_with_options ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: revert , \"Undo selected changes\" , settings , operation ) ; text Copier syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\" syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432 syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\" Appliquer un changement # Message obligatoire. Branche/dossier/sélecteurs comme le plan ; --expect est un ID exact minuscule, sinon capture sous verrou au CLI, toujours requis par l’API. L’application recalcule et vérifie histoires et fichiers ; elle refuse conflit, résultat vide, récupération checkout/switch et tout index. Tous les suivis et destinations passent clean-tracked, sans force. Le candidat v1 utilise message, date UTC courante et seul parent ancienne tête cible ; cherry-pick n’ajoute pas source comme parent de fusion. Une source fusionnée exige mainline mais produit toujours un parent. Le checkout récupérable change les fichiers avant publication atomique branche/archive/reçu ; la branche garde l’ancienne tête jusqu’à vérification de toutes les sorties. Ancienne tête/histoire/blocs restent accessibles par parent et archive. HEAD et références source restent inchangés. Une source sans lien nécessite sa propre racine pour garder toute son ascendance. La réponse directe ajoute operation, replayed_revision, previous_revision et mainline aux champs du reçu checkout (target/state/archive). La récupération rend l’identité générique et sa cible, sans ces labels de réponse. Formats inchangés. text Copier syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\" syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\" cpp Copier auto applied = store . replay_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , syrnck :: NativeReplayKind :: cherry_pick , \"Apply selected changes\" , { } , operation ) ; Interruption, annulation et rétention # Avant journal, arrêt/erreur conservent branche et workspace mais peuvent laisser un candidat immuable non référencé. Après journal, inspecter puis checkout-recover ou --abort. Resume installe le candidat enregistré sans recalculer le replay. Abort rétablit les fichiers originaux et garde la branche avant publication ; après publication, seul le nettoyage peut finir. Ouvrir le Store ne change aucun fichier et le GC reste bloqué tant que le journal est actif. Inspecter la tête après perte de réponse ; l’ancien --expect empêche un second commit. Les replays vides sont refusés. Révisions finales et parents survivent au GC. Un candidat non publié et abandonné peut être collecté après retrait des pins temporaires. Archives/reçus checkout n’expirent pas automatiquement, leur retrait protégé est explicite. Fichiers préparés et sauvegardes privées restent en clair malgré le chiffrement des objets ; voir les limites du checkout . text Copier syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover # Or, before branch publication: syrnck native checkout-recover --abort syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify syrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory . syrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2 auto plan = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", selected_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, {}, true, operation); auto inverse = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", merge_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, 2, false, operation); syrnck::NativeReplayOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default per-file text limits. // settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent. auto plan = store.plan_replay_with_options(project, \"main\", source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, settings, true, operation); auto applied = store.replay_directory_with_options(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, \"Undo selected changes\", settings, operation); syrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify syrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\" syrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432 syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\" syrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\" syrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\" auto applied = store.replay_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, \"Apply selected changes\", {}, operation); syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover # Or, before branch publication: syrnck native checkout-recover --abort","headings":[{"id":"native-cherry-pick-and-revert","text":"Cherry-pick et revert natifs","level":1},{"id":"three-way-decisions","text":"Décisions à trois voies","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Composition textuelle bornée optionnelle","level":2},{"id":"apply-a-planned-change","text":"Appliquer un changement","level":2},{"id":"interruption-abort-and-retention","text":"Interruption, annulation et rétention","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native cherry-pick-plan tags/fix --verify\nsyrnck native revert-plan COMMIT_ID --branch main --directory .\nsyrnck native revert-plan MERGE_ID --mainline 2\nauto plan = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", selected_id,\n                              syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, {}, true, operation);\nauto inverse = store.plan_replay(project, \"main\", merge_id,\n                                 syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, 2, false, operation);\nsyrnck::NativeReplayOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace(); // Default per-file text limits.\n// settings.mainline = 2; // Required for a selected merge parent.\nauto plan = store.plan_replay_with_options(project, \"main\", source_id,\n    syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick, settings, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.replay_directory_with_options(project, \"main\", expected_id,\n    source_id, syrnck::NativeReplayKind::revert, \"Undo selected changes\",\n    settings, operation);\nsyrnck native cherry-pick-plan feature --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native cherry-pick feature --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Apply fix\"\nsyrnck native revert-plan SOURCE_ID --text-merge --max-work 33554432\nsyrnck native revert SOURCE_ID --text-merge --expect OLD_TIP_ID -m \"Undo fix\"\nsyrnck native cherry-pick SOURCE_ID -m \"Apply the asset fix\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native revert SOURCE_ID -m \"Undo the asset fix\"\nsyrnck native revert MERGE_ID --mainline 2 -m \"Undo the selected merge delta\"\nauto applied = store.replay_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      syrnck::NativeReplayKind::cherry_pick,\n                                      \"Apply selected changes\", {}, operation);\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\n# Or, before branch publication:\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/checkout-retention","#/fr/native-checkout"],"kind":"guide","revision":"ba582003ee01"},{"id":"native-reset","title":"Reset soft et mixed","description":"Guide : Reset soft et mixed.","section":"Versionnage","order":340,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-branch-reset\">Reset soft et mixed d’une branche\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-reset/native-branch-reset\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reset soft et mixed d’une branche\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le reset déplace une branche existante vers une révision sans toucher aux fichiers de travail. Ancienne tête et éventuel index sont archivés dans la même transaction. \u003ccode>native reset [PATH...]\u003c/code> garde son sens de retrait de sélection ; déplacer une branche exige un mode explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--soft\u003c/code> conserve les entrées préparées et rebase leur base sur la cible. Sans index, il fige les entrées complètes de l’ancienne tête sur la nouvelle base, permettant ensuite un commit staged enfant de la cible. Les octets préparés restent distincts des modifications ultérieures du workspace. Un index périmé bloque soft ; \u003ccode>--mixed\u003c/code> l’archive puis le vide. Les deux refusent un index dont l’identité ou les métadonnées sont invalides.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La cible peut être toute révision native valide, sans contrainte d’ascendance. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs usuels et \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> sans changer HEAD. L’API utilise des IDs exacts et une branche existante. \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> protège l’ancienne tête ; sans lui, le CLI la capture sous verrou. L’index est capturé à l’invocation et contrôlé à la publication, pas figé par un ancien aperçu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les plans rendent du JSON : code 0 si prêt, code 1 avec blocages sinon. Par défaut seules les métadonnées sont lues ; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> vérifie fermetures complètes tête/cible/index et hashes de fichiers. Le plan soft comprend la comparaison proposée contre la cible. Rapports et révisions gardent le plafond de 16 Mio ; la mémoire croît avec les snapshots plats et la comparaison bornée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le workspace n’est pas analysé : modifications et non-suivis demeurent disponibles. Récupération checkout/switch, archives contradictoires ou index soft périmé bloquent. Vers la même tête, le reset ne fait rien, sauf mixed qui archive et retire tout index existant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-interruption\">Rétention et interruption\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-reset/retention-and-interruption\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention et interruption\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’ancienne tête reste sous \u003ccode>archive/reset/OLD_ID\u003c/code>, l’ancien index sous \u003ccode>archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID\u003c/code>, avec son historique de base. Parents, manifestes et blocs survivent au GC en clair/chiffré et isolés/empaquetés. Tags et autres branches ne bougent pas. Toutes les fermetures anciennes et nouvelles sont vérifiées avant publication ; un ancien bloc absent empêche aussi mixed de retirer sa seule protection.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Branche, index et deux archives sont publiés par remplacement atomique de \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code>. Annulation ou erreur d’observateur conservent les références anciennes ; la préparation soft peut laisser un index immuable non référencé. Une coupure laisse l’ensemble ancien ou nouveau. Inspecter références, tête et index avant nouvel essai avec l’ancienne tête attendue. Aucun journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire puisque le workspace ne change pas. Aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt ne suit la publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Retrouver les versions dans les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-archives\">archives\u003c/a>, exporter leur ID ou créer une branche de récupération. Sans expiration automatique, elles conservent les versions intermédiaires. Retirer la dernière racine puis collecter supprime les données exclusives. Les fichiers jamais ingérés ne sont pas sauvegardés par reset. Pour remplacer le workspace, utiliser le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset\">reset hard récupérable\u003c/a>, une opération distincte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le format v1 ne change pas. Les API C++ et CLI proposent progression/diagnostics bilingues, JSON anglais et callbacks non réentrants ; l’exposition de l’historique natif en C/Go reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_reset_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResetMode\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>soft\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_branch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">                                 syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResetMode\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>soft\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Reset soft et mixed d’une branche # Le reset déplace une branche existante vers une révision sans toucher aux fichiers de travail. Ancienne tête et éventuel index sont archivés dans la même transaction. native reset [PATH...] garde son sens de retrait de sélection ; déplacer une branche exige un mode explicite. --soft conserve les entrées préparées et rebase leur base sur la cible. Sans index, il fige les entrées complètes de l’ancienne tête sur la nouvelle base, permettant ensuite un commit staged enfant de la cible. Les octets préparés restent distincts des modifications ultérieures du workspace. Un index périmé bloque soft ; --mixed l’archive puis le vide. Les deux refusent un index dont l’identité ou les métadonnées sont invalides. La cible peut être toute révision native valide, sans contrainte d’ascendance. Le CLI accepte les sélecteurs usuels et --branch sans changer HEAD. L’API utilise des IDs exacts et une branche existante. --expect protège l’ancienne tête ; sans lui, le CLI la capture sous verrou. L’index est capturé à l’invocation et contrôlé à la publication, pas figé par un ancien aperçu. Les plans rendent du JSON : code 0 si prêt, code 1 avec blocages sinon. Par défaut seules les métadonnées sont lues ; --verify vérifie fermetures complètes tête/cible/index et hashes de fichiers. Le plan soft comprend la comparaison proposée contre la cible. Rapports et révisions gardent le plafond de 16 Mio ; la mémoire croît avec les snapshots plats et la comparaison bornée. Le workspace n’est pas analysé : modifications et non-suivis demeurent disponibles. Récupération checkout/switch, archives contradictoires ou index soft périmé bloquent. Vers la même tête, le reset ne fait rien, sauf mixed qui archive et retire tout index existant. text Copier syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID Rétention et interruption # L’ancienne tête reste sous archive/reset/OLD_ID , l’ancien index sous archive/reset-index/INDEX_ID , avec son historique de base. Parents, manifestes et blocs survivent au GC en clair/chiffré et isolés/empaquetés. Tags et autres branches ne bougent pas. Toutes les fermetures anciennes et nouvelles sont vérifiées avant publication ; un ancien bloc absent empêche aussi mixed de retirer sa seule protection. Branche, index et deux archives sont publiés par remplacement atomique de refs.json . Annulation ou erreur d’observateur conservent les références anciennes ; la préparation soft peut laisser un index immuable non référencé. Une coupure laisse l’ensemble ancien ou nouveau. Inspecter références, tête et index avant nouvel essai avec l’ancienne tête attendue. Aucun journal de fichiers n’est nécessaire puisque le workspace ne change pas. Aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt ne suit la publication. Retrouver les versions dans les archives , exporter leur ID ou créer une branche de récupération. Sans expiration automatique, elles conservent les versions intermédiaires. Retirer la dernière racine puis collecter supprime les données exclusives. Les fichiers jamais ingérés ne sont pas sauvegardés par reset. Pour remplacer le workspace, utiliser le reset hard récupérable , une opération distincte. Le format v1 ne change pas. Les API C++ et CLI proposent progression/diagnostics bilingues, JSON anglais et callbacks non réentrants ; l’exposition de l’historique natif en C/Go reste à développer. text Copier syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files cpp Copier auto plan = store . plan_reset_branch ( \"main\" , target_id , syrnck :: NativeResetMode :: soft , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . reset_branch ( \"main\" , expected_id , target_id , syrnck :: NativeResetMode :: soft , operation ) ; syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify syrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID syrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version syrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID syrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files auto plan = store.plan_reset_branch(\"main\", target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, true, operation); auto result = store.reset_branch(\"main\", expected_id, target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-branch-reset","text":"Reset soft et mixed d’une branche","level":1},{"id":"retention-and-interruption","text":"Rétention et interruption","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native reset-plan REVISION --soft --verify\nsyrnck native reset REVISION --soft --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native reset REVISION --mixed --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nsyrnck native export OLD_ID recovered-version\nsyrnck native branch recovered OLD_ID\nsyrnck native export INDEX_ID recovered-prepared-files\nauto plan = store.plan_reset_branch(\"main\", target_id, syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft,\n                                    true, operation);\nauto result = store.reset_branch(\"main\", expected_id, target_id,\n                                 syrnck::NativeResetMode::soft, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-archives","#/fr/native-hard-reset"],"kind":"guide","revision":"1b818592761a"},{"id":"native-resolutions","title":"Résolution explicite des conflits","description":"Guide : Résolution explicite des conflits.","section":"Versionnage","order":350,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-conflict-resolutions\">Résolutions explicites de conflits\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions/native-conflict-resolutions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Résolutions explicites de conflits\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Merge, cherry-pick, revert, rebase et rebase d’index partagent les choix explicites par fichier. Les plans restent en lecture seule ; fournir des choix ne contourne ni topologie, ni vérification des objets, ni propreté des suivis, ni récupération de checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-and-apply\">Préparer et appliquer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions/prepare-and-apply\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer et appliquer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Copier resolution_context du premier plan dans choices.json puis remplir files sans modifier les autres champs. Un plan bloqué rend 1 avec JSON. Résoudre d’abord les blocages de topologie, dont le contexte est null. Garder les mêmes options de base/mainline à l’aperçu et à l’application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--resolutions fonctionne aussi pour cherry-pick/revert et leurs plans. L’application recalcule, vérifie toutes les histoires et payloads personnalisés puis installe le résultat complet via le journal de checkout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le document lie operation, head exacte, source exacte, base à trois voies et parent de replay. Merge : parent null ; cherry-pick : base = parent choisi ; revert : base = source et incoming = parent choisi ; replay racine : parent null. Changer tête/source/base/parent rejette le document avant publication. Changer mainline d’un revert de fusion ne peut réutiliser les choix de l’autre parent. Les sélecteurs sont résolus en IDs exacts. Remplacer les IDs fictifs de l’exemple par ceux du vrai plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"operation\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"merge\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"head\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"parent\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token null keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"incoming\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"Content/Obsolete.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"remove\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L12\" data-line=\"12\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"choices\">Choix par fichier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions/choices\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Choix par fichier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>current prend toute l’entrée actuelle, mode/manifeste compris ; incoming toute l’entrée entrante (parent choisi pour revert) ; base l’entrée de base ; remove supprime le chemin ; file utilise un manifeste existant avec un mode explicite. Choisir un côté absent supprime. Hors file, seul choice est permis ; file contient exactement choice, manifest et executable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour assembler manuellement un contenu, l’enregistrer hors du workspace suivi, l’importer avec syrnck put puis utiliser son ID. Le CLI put crée sa racine files/ID ; l’API bas niveau ingest seule ne publie pas de pin. Conserver une référence tant que l’opération n’a pas publié, si maintenance possible entre-temps. Les commits conservent ensuite leurs dépendances normalement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\">merge textuel borné\u003c/a> peut produire un fichier personnalisé à revoir depuis trois snapshots, sans changer implicitement les choix de l’opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls chemins du plan initial en conflit ou impliqués dans une collision de namespace peuvent recevoir un choix ; pas de substitution d’un chemin propre. Après tous les choix, les contrôles portables de casse/préfixe recommencent : un blocage peut rester. Les règles du checkout récupérable s’appliquent aux transitions de type/casse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une résolution partielle bloque tout, sans publier séparément les fichiers compatibles. resolved décrit les choix acceptés et leurs entrées ; conflicts conserve les restants. Un replay tout-current sans delta ne peut créer un commit vide. Une fusion divergente peut enregistrer ses deux parents sans changer les fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"choice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"file\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"manifest\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"executable\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-recovery\">Validation et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions/validation-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Validation et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sans --verify, les plans lisent les métadonnées des manifestes personnalisés sans leurs blocs. Vérification et application auditent le fichier complet et son hash ; toute donnée choisie absente/corrompue bloque. Les contrats de lecture seule/callbacks/annulation s’appliquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>JSON éditable limité à 16 Mio, 100000 chemins, profondeur 64, 400010 champs totaux ; exactement sept champs racine. Champs inconnus, doublons, chemins/IDs invalides et mauvais types sont refusés. L’API typée applique même schéma/budget ; decode sur le JSON non fiable détecte les doublons avant perte dans un DOM. IDs : 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le document ne crée ni état persistant de conflit, ni fichier suivi, ni rétention des entrées. Garder branches/tags/pins jusqu’à l’application. Merge, replay, rebase et rebase d’index peuvent activer explicitement la composition textuelle bornée après les choix. Renommages avec édition et éditeur de conflits interactif restent séparés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après début d’application, checkout-status/checkout-recover ou --abort terminent/annulent le candidat enregistré sans exiger le JSON original. Rebase utilise un wrapper liant head/base exclue/onto, avec contextes par étape dans steps. Rebase-index utilise operation rebase-index, source index figé et sa base comme base et parent. Les formats de révision/journal ne changent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ : NativeResolutionSet::parse sur le contexte généré, decode sur un document édité ; options de merge/replay portent les choix. Les anciennes signatures restent disponibles. Ces mutations sont exposées en C++ et CLI, pas dans l’historique natif C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeMergeOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>resolutions \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">merge_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> source_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L7\" data-line=\"7\">                                      \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Resolve asset merge\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Résolutions explicites de conflits # Merge, cherry-pick, revert, rebase et rebase d’index partagent les choix explicites par fichier. Les plans restent en lecture seule ; fournir des choix ne contourne ni topologie, ni vérification des objets, ni propreté des suivis, ni récupération de checkout. Préparer et appliquer # Copier resolution_context du premier plan dans choices.json puis remplir files sans modifier les autres champs. Un plan bloqué rend 1 avec JSON. Résoudre d’abord les blocages de topologie, dont le contexte est null. Garder les mêmes options de base/mainline à l’aperçu et à l’application. --resolutions fonctionne aussi pour cherry-pick/revert et leurs plans. L’application recalcule, vérifie toutes les histoires et payloads personnalisés puis installe le résultat complet via le journal de checkout. Le document lie operation, head exacte, source exacte, base à trois voies et parent de replay. Merge : parent null ; cherry-pick : base = parent choisi ; revert : base = source et incoming = parent choisi ; replay racine : parent null. Changer tête/source/base/parent rejette le document avant publication. Changer mainline d’un revert de fusion ne peut réutiliser les choix de l’autre parent. Les sélecteurs sont résolus en IDs exacts. Remplacer les IDs fictifs de l’exemple par ceux du vrai plan. text Copier syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID json Copier { \"version\" : 1 , \"operation\" : \"merge\" , \"head\" : \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\" , \"source\" : \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\" , \"base\" : \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\" , \"parent\" : null , \"files\" : { \"Content/Map.uasset\" : { \"choice\" : \"incoming\" } , \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\" : { \"choice\" : \"remove\" } } } Choix par fichier # current prend toute l’entrée actuelle, mode/manifeste compris ; incoming toute l’entrée entrante (parent choisi pour revert) ; base l’entrée de base ; remove supprime le chemin ; file utilise un manifeste existant avec un mode explicite. Choisir un côté absent supprime. Hors file, seul choice est permis ; file contient exactement choice, manifest et executable. Pour assembler manuellement un contenu, l’enregistrer hors du workspace suivi, l’importer avec syrnck put puis utiliser son ID. Le CLI put crée sa racine files/ID ; l’API bas niveau ingest seule ne publie pas de pin. Conserver une référence tant que l’opération n’a pas publié, si maintenance possible entre-temps. Les commits conservent ensuite leurs dépendances normalement. Le merge textuel borné peut produire un fichier personnalisé à revoir depuis trois snapshots, sans changer implicitement les choix de l’opération. Seuls chemins du plan initial en conflit ou impliqués dans une collision de namespace peuvent recevoir un choix ; pas de substitution d’un chemin propre. Après tous les choix, les contrôles portables de casse/préfixe recommencent : un blocage peut rester. Les règles du checkout récupérable s’appliquent aux transitions de type/casse. Une résolution partielle bloque tout, sans publier séparément les fichiers compatibles. resolved décrit les choix acceptés et leurs entrées ; conflicts conserve les restants. Un replay tout-current sans delta ne peut créer un commit vide. Une fusion divergente peut enregistrer ses deux parents sans changer les fichiers. json Copier { \"choice\" : \"file\" , \"manifest\" : \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\" , \"executable\" : false } Validation et récupération # Sans --verify, les plans lisent les métadonnées des manifestes personnalisés sans leurs blocs. Vérification et application auditent le fichier complet et son hash ; toute donnée choisie absente/corrompue bloque. Les contrats de lecture seule/callbacks/annulation s’appliquent. JSON éditable limité à 16 Mio, 100000 chemins, profondeur 64, 400010 champs totaux ; exactement sept champs racine. Champs inconnus, doublons, chemins/IDs invalides et mauvais types sont refusés. L’API typée applique même schéma/budget ; decode sur le JSON non fiable détecte les doublons avant perte dans un DOM. IDs : 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules. Le document ne crée ni état persistant de conflit, ni fichier suivi, ni rétention des entrées. Garder branches/tags/pins jusqu’à l’application. Merge, replay, rebase et rebase d’index peuvent activer explicitement la composition textuelle bornée après les choix. Renommages avec édition et éditeur de conflits interactif restent séparés. Après début d’application, checkout-status/checkout-recover ou --abort terminent/annulent le candidat enregistré sans exiger le JSON original. Rebase utilise un wrapper liant head/base exclue/onto, avec contextes par étape dans steps. Rebase-index utilise operation rebase-index, source index figé et sa base comme base et parent. Les formats de révision/journal ne changent pas. C++ : NativeResolutionSet::parse sur le contexte généré, decode sur un document édité ; options de merge/replay portent les choix. Les anciennes signatures restent disponibles. Ces mutations sont exposées en C++ et CLI, pas dans l’historique natif C/Go. cpp Copier auto choices = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; choices . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; syrnck :: NativeMergeOptions settings ; settings . resolutions = choices ; auto preview = store . plan_merge ( project , \"main\" , source_id , settings , true , operation ) ; auto applied = store . merge_directory ( project , \"main\" , expected_id , source_id , \"Resolve asset merge\" , settings , operation ) ; syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify syrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID { \"version\": 1, \"operation\": \"merge\", \"head\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\", \"source\": \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\", \"base\": \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\", \"parent\": null, \"files\": { \"Content/Map.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"incoming\"}, \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"remove\"} } } {\"choice\": \"file\", \"manifest\": \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\", \"executable\": false} auto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); choices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; syrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings; settings.resolutions = choices; auto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation); auto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id, \"Resolve asset merge\", settings, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-conflict-resolutions","text":"Résolutions explicites de conflits","level":1},{"id":"prepare-and-apply","text":"Préparer et appliquer","level":2},{"id":"choices","text":"Choix par fichier","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-recovery","text":"Validation et récupération","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-2-L12","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5","code-4-L6","code-4-L7"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native merge-plan feature --verify\nsyrnck native merge-plan feature --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native merge feature --resolutions choices.json --expect OLD_TIP_ID\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"operation\": \"merge\",\n  \"head\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n  \"source\": \"bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\",\n  \"base\": \"cccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccc\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"files\": {\n    \"Content/Map.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"incoming\"},\n    \"Content/Obsolete.uasset\": {\"choice\": \"remove\"}\n  }\n}\n{\"choice\": \"file\", \"manifest\": \"dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd\", \"executable\": false}\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nchoices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nsyrnck::NativeMergeOptions settings;\nsettings.resolutions = choices;\nauto preview = store.plan_merge(project, \"main\", source_id, settings, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.merge_directory(project, \"main\", expected_id, source_id,\n                                      \"Resolve asset merge\", settings, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution"],"kind":"guide","revision":"9b06414ca1ff"},{"id":"native-squash","title":"Regroupement de commits","description":"Guide : Regroupement de commits.","section":"Versionnage","order":360,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-commit-squash\">Regrouper des commits natifs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-squash/native-commit-squash\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Regrouper des commits natifs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Squash remplace les 2 à 100000 derniers commits linéaires par un seul, avec exactement les entrées de fichiers de l’ancienne tête. Il ne change ni fichiers préparés ni workspace et conserve tout l’historique d’origine dans une archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La plage inclut la tête et suit ses parents uniques. Le nouveau parent est celui du commit le plus ancien inclus, ou aucun si la racine est atteinte. Manifestes, bits exécutables et représentations viennent de l’ancienne tête, sans reconstruction ni réingestion pour créer le candidat. Message obligatoire, date UTC courante et limite de révision de 16 Mio s’appliquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une fusion dans la plage bloque avec \u003ccode>merge_in_range\u003c/code> : aucun parent n’est choisi silencieusement. Une fusion immédiatement avant la plage reste le nouveau parent avec toute son ascendance. Une plage trop longue donne \u003ccode>count_exceeds_history\u003c/code>. Tout index, même propre, une récupération checkout en cours ou une archive contradictoire bloque. Terminer ou retirer explicitement l’index auparavant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan en lecture seule expose \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revisions\u003c/code> du plus récent au plus ancien, \u003ccode>parents\u003c/code> proposés, nombre de fichiers, référence d’archive, \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> et blocages. Une plage incomplète a des parents null. Code 0 si prêt, 1 avec JSON si bloqué ; comptes/métadonnées invalides produisent un diagnostic. La sortie est bornée à 16 Mio ; la mémoire dépend des IDs visités et des révisions plates décodées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> contrôle toute la fermeture de l’ancienne tête et les hashes de fichiers. L’application le fait toujours avant candidat, puis vérifie remplacement et archive. Un aperçu de métadonnées ne prouve pas la présence des blocs. Un bloc d’ancêtre manquant bloque même si la nouvelle tête n’en a plus besoin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"publication-and-retained-history\">Publication et historique conservé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-squash/publication-and-retained-history\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Publication et historique conservé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une seule transaction déplace la branche et crée \u003ccode>archive/squash/OLD_HEAD_ID\u003c/code> pour toute la chaîne d’origine. L’absence attendue de l’index est contrôlée ensemble. Tags, autres branches et sélection HEAD ne changent pas. Les modifications de travail ne sont ni lues ni incluses.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> choisit la branche ; \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> est l’ID exact de l’ancienne tête, capturé sous verrou si omis au CLI et toujours requis en C++. Une coupure laisse toutes les anciennes ou nouvelles références. Une annulation/erreur de callback avant publication conserve l’ancien état mais peut laisser un candidat immuable non référencé. Aucun journal de fichiers n’existe. Inspecter tête et références avant nouvel essai avec l’ancien ID ; aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt après publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-archives\">archives\u003c/a> permettent de retrouver têtes et versions préparées. Elles n’expirent pas automatiquement et gardent toutes les versions intermédiaires après GC. Exporter une révision ou créer une branche pour récupérer les fichiers. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait avec sauvegarde\u003c/a> est explicite ; squash raccourcit l’historique visible sans récupérer automatiquement l’espace archivé. Retirer la dernière racine autorise la collecte des données exclusives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avec une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">identité native\u003c/a>, le squash autonome crée une v2 avec la personne actuelle comme auteur et committer et une nouvelle signature facultative. Le squash du rebase préserve, lui, le premier auteur retenu. Sans identité, la v1 reste identique. C++ et CLI appliquent progression bilingue, annulation coopérative et callbacks non réentrants. L’historique natif n’est pas encore exposé dans les bindings C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_squash\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> applied \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">squash_commits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                    \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Implement asset streaming\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Regrouper des commits natifs # Squash remplace les 2 à 100000 derniers commits linéaires par un seul, avec exactement les entrées de fichiers de l’ancienne tête. Il ne change ni fichiers préparés ni workspace et conserve tout l’historique d’origine dans une archive. La plage inclut la tête et suit ses parents uniques. Le nouveau parent est celui du commit le plus ancien inclus, ou aucun si la racine est atteinte. Manifestes, bits exécutables et représentations viennent de l’ancienne tête, sans reconstruction ni réingestion pour créer le candidat. Message obligatoire, date UTC courante et limite de révision de 16 Mio s’appliquent. Une fusion dans la plage bloque avec merge_in_range : aucun parent n’est choisi silencieusement. Une fusion immédiatement avant la plage reste le nouveau parent avec toute son ascendance. Une plage trop longue donne count_exceeds_history . Tout index, même propre, une récupération checkout en cours ou une archive contradictoire bloque. Terminer ou retirer explicitement l’index auparavant. Le plan en lecture seule expose head , count , revisions du plus récent au plus ancien, parents proposés, nombre de fichiers, référence d’archive, ready et blocages. Une plage incomplète a des parents null. Code 0 si prêt, 1 avec JSON si bloqué ; comptes/métadonnées invalides produisent un diagnostic. La sortie est bornée à 16 Mio ; la mémoire dépend des IDs visités et des révisions plates décodées. --verify contrôle toute la fermeture de l’ancienne tête et les hashes de fichiers. L’application le fait toujours avant candidat, puis vérifie remplacement et archive. Un aperçu de métadonnées ne prouve pas la présence des blocs. Un bloc d’ancêtre manquant bloque même si la nouvelle tête n’en a plus besoin. text Copier syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID Publication et historique conservé # Une seule transaction déplace la branche et crée archive/squash/OLD_HEAD_ID pour toute la chaîne d’origine. L’absence attendue de l’index est contrôlée ensemble. Tags, autres branches et sélection HEAD ne changent pas. Les modifications de travail ne sont ni lues ni incluses. --branch choisit la branche ; --expect est l’ID exact de l’ancienne tête, capturé sous verrou si omis au CLI et toujours requis en C++. Une coupure laisse toutes les anciennes ou nouvelles références. Une annulation/erreur de callback avant publication conserve l’ancien état mais peut laisser un candidat immuable non référencé. Aucun journal de fichiers n’existe. Inspecter tête et références avant nouvel essai avec l’ancien ID ; aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt après publication. Les archives permettent de retrouver têtes et versions préparées. Elles n’expirent pas automatiquement et gardent toutes les versions intermédiaires après GC. Exporter une révision ou créer une branche pour récupérer les fichiers. Le retrait avec sauvegarde est explicite ; squash raccourcit l’historique visible sans récupérer automatiquement l’espace archivé. Retirer la dernière racine autorise la collecte des données exclusives. Avec une identité native , le squash autonome crée une v2 avec la personne actuelle comme auteur et committer et une nouvelle signature facultative. Le squash du rebase préserve, lui, le premier auteur retenu. Sans identité, la v1 reste identique. C++ et CLI appliquent progression bilingue, annulation coopérative et callbacks non réentrants. L’historique natif n’est pas encore exposé dans les bindings C/Go. cpp Copier auto preview = store . plan_squash ( \"main\" , 3 , true , operation ) ; auto applied = store . squash_commits ( \"main\" , expected_id , 3 , \"Implement asset streaming\" , operation ) ; syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify syrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID auto preview = store.plan_squash(\"main\", 3, true, operation); auto applied = store.squash_commits(\"main\", expected_id, 3, \"Implement asset streaming\", operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-commit-squash","text":"Regrouper des commits natifs","level":1},{"id":"publication-and-retained-history","text":"Publication et historique conservé","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native squash-plan 3 --verify\nsyrnck native squash 3 -m \"Implement asset streaming\" --expect OLD_TIP_ID\nauto preview = store.plan_squash(\"main\", 3, true, operation);\nauto applied = store.squash_commits(\"main\", expected_id, 3,\n                                    \"Implement asset streaming\", operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-archives","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-authorship"],"kind":"guide","revision":"3507880252a6"},{"id":"native-stage-rebase","title":"Rejeu d’un index périmé","description":"Guide : Rejeu d’un index périmé.","section":"Versionnage","order":370,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"rebase-a-native-staging-index\">Rejouer un index préparé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase/rebase-a-native-staging-index\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rejouer un index préparé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>rebase-index-plan rejoue les changements figés de l’index sur la tête actuelle de sa branche en lisant les snapshots/manifestes stockés uniquement. Aucun scan, import ou modification du workspace ; fichiers sales, ignorés et non suivis n’affectent pas le plan. L’index Git est indépendant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"replay-and-preview\">Rejeu et aperçu\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase/replay-and-preview\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rejeu et aperçu\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Base = parent enregistré de l’index ; current = tête ; incoming = index complet. Les changements indépendants de fichier/mode se combinent. Contenu concurrent, ajout/suppression, casse ou préfixe fichier/dossier bloquent ; texte optionnel, prise en compte des renommages non implémentée. Un journal checkout/switch en attente bloque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Il faut tête et index. Un index préparé avant premier commit peut ensuite se rejouer sur une base vide ; aucune ascendance imposée à la nouvelle tête. Le CLI suit la branche choisie, --branch ne change pas HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Index frais : inchangé avec ID/octets exacts. Périmé : candidat v1 daté zéro, marqueur de branche et parent tête actuelle. Un résultat vide reste un index explicite. ID déterministe prédit mais non stocké. Un changement de représentation préparé seul reste si la nouvelle tête garde contenu/manifeste de base, éventuellement avec mode indépendant ; si upstream a aussi changé la représentation à contenu égal, upstream gagne. Un contenu upstream changé ne conserve pas une ancienne représentation de contenu obsolète.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rapport : head,index,base nullable,stale,changed,target,archive,ready,objects_verified,result_files,empty,counts,changes,conflicts,resolution_context,blockers et resolved. Comparaison du candidat avec la tête, séparant contenu/mode/manifeste. Bloqué : comparaison provisoire, target/result_files null. Prêt 0 ; bloqué 1 avec JSON ; entrée invalide/absente diagnostic.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plan sans écriture. --verify contrôle les deux fermetures et hashes historiques ; hors texte, métadonnées seules ne prouvent pas les blocs. Texte activé vérifie les fichiers tentés même sans --verify. Snapshots plats, rapport et révision de 16 Mio maximum. Callbacks synchrones non réentrants, progression/arrêt et interface bilingue ; historique C/Go reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes\">Appliquer et conserver les préparations\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase/apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Appliquer et conserver les préparations\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’application vérifie le plan prêt avant candidat et exige son ID prédit. Une transaction contrôle tête et index attendus, archive l’ancien sous archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID puis remplace staging/BRANCH, sans changer branche, HEAD ou workspace. IDs exacts capturés sous verrou au CLI si omis, explicites en C++. Conflits et journal actif bloquent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Index frais rend unchanged sans écriture mais avec audit complet. Sinon applied, previous_index et nouvel index, plus champs du plan ; changed/stale décrivent l’entrée, staged le nouvel état. Un résultat égal à la tête demeure un index propre explicite, qui ne suffit pas au commit staged exigeant des différences.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Arrêt, bloc absent, callback ou archive contradictoire avant publication gardent les anciennes références ; le candidat peut rester complet non référencé. Aucun journal de fichiers nécessaire. Après engagement, aucun callback/stop ; coupure ancien ou nouvel ensemble complet. Inspecter staged avant nouvel essai.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’archive protège tout l’ancien index, ses blocs et sa base après GC, même après commit/retrait du remplacement. Pas d’expiration automatique ; format/règles de chiffrement inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"resolve-index-conflicts\">Résoudre les conflits d’index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase/resolve-index-conflicts\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Résoudre les conflits d’index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>--resolutions prend resolution_context du plan et files complété. operation rebase-index ; head tête actuelle ; source index figé ; base et parent son ancienne base, null pour l’index initial. Tout ID doit correspondre : changement de tête/index impose nouveau plan et choix.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choix d’entrée entière current (nouvelle tête), incoming (ancienne préparation), base, remove ou file personnalisé avec mode. Seulement chemins initialement conflictuels ; collisions recalculées, résolution incomplète sans publication. Aucun remplacement de chemin propre. Choix vides sur index frais gardent l’ID exact.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le décodage rejette doublons/champs inconnus/chemins/IDs/choix invalides avec les limites communes de 16 Mio, 100000 chemins et profondeur/champs. Plans par métadonnées contrôlent les manifestes personnalisés ; --verify/application vérifient leurs payloads. Conserver un pin avant publication si maintenance possible ; le nouvel index puis commit conservent ensuite les blocs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> choices \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">NativeResolutionSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">parse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"resolution_context\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>files\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeResolutionChoice\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>incoming\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage_resolved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage_resolved\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                          choices\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"optional-bounded-text-composition\">Composition textuelle optionnelle\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase/optional-bounded-text-composition\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Composition textuelle optionnelle\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>--text-merge combine les changements UTF-8 indépendants entre base enregistrée, tête et index figé, sans lire le travail ultérieur. Résolutions explicites prioritaires ; binaire, chevauchements, ajouts/suppressions et namespace bloquent l’ensemble. Fins de ligne conservées et mode combiné indépendamment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Même lot de 64 tentatives et 16 Mio entrées/résultats propres, plus limites par fichier. Les options de budget exigent l’activation. text_merge décrit statuts/motifs/coordonnées/métadonnées par tentative, sans texte fusionné. Index déjà frais : ID exact et zéro tentative.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan reconstruit/vérifie seulement les conflits éligibles et prédit les manifestes CDC sans écrire. L’application audite les entrées avant matérialisation et candidat ; ancienne archive et nouvel index publient dans le même CAS tête/index. Aucun groupe partiel en conflit. Avant transaction, arrêt peut laisser des objets complets mais garde références/workspace. Archive/base restent reconstructibles après GC et le commit ultérieur conserve le snapshot fusionné. Les méthodes nommées with_options gardent les entrées plain/resolved compatibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeStageRebaseOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>text_merge\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">emplace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_rebase_stage_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">rebase_stage_with_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> expected_head\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L5\" data-line=\"5\">                                               expected_index\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Rejouer un index préparé # rebase-index-plan rejoue les changements figés de l’index sur la tête actuelle de sa branche en lisant les snapshots/manifestes stockés uniquement. Aucun scan, import ou modification du workspace ; fichiers sales, ignorés et non suivis n’affectent pas le plan. L’index Git est indépendant. text Copier syrnck native staged syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature Rejeu et aperçu # Base = parent enregistré de l’index ; current = tête ; incoming = index complet. Les changements indépendants de fichier/mode se combinent. Contenu concurrent, ajout/suppression, casse ou préfixe fichier/dossier bloquent ; texte optionnel, prise en compte des renommages non implémentée. Un journal checkout/switch en attente bloque. Il faut tête et index. Un index préparé avant premier commit peut ensuite se rejouer sur une base vide ; aucune ascendance imposée à la nouvelle tête. Le CLI suit la branche choisie, --branch ne change pas HEAD. Index frais : inchangé avec ID/octets exacts. Périmé : candidat v1 daté zéro, marqueur de branche et parent tête actuelle. Un résultat vide reste un index explicite. ID déterministe prédit mais non stocké. Un changement de représentation préparé seul reste si la nouvelle tête garde contenu/manifeste de base, éventuellement avec mode indépendant ; si upstream a aussi changé la représentation à contenu égal, upstream gagne. Un contenu upstream changé ne conserve pas une ancienne représentation de contenu obsolète. Rapport : head,index,base nullable,stale,changed,target,archive,ready,objects_verified,result_files,empty,counts,changes,conflicts,resolution_context,blockers et resolved. Comparaison du candidat avec la tête, séparant contenu/mode/manifeste. Bloqué : comparaison provisoire, target/result_files null. Prêt 0 ; bloqué 1 avec JSON ; entrée invalide/absente diagnostic. Plan sans écriture. --verify contrôle les deux fermetures et hashes historiques ; hors texte, métadonnées seules ne prouvent pas les blocs. Texte activé vérifie les fichiers tentés même sans --verify. Snapshots plats, rapport et révision de 16 Mio maximum. Callbacks synchrones non réentrants, progression/arrêt et interface bilingue ; historique C/Go reste à développer. cpp Copier auto plan = store . plan_rebase_stage ( \"feature\" , true , operation ) ; Appliquer et conserver les préparations # L’application vérifie le plan prêt avant candidat et exige son ID prédit. Une transaction contrôle tête et index attendus, archive l’ancien sous archive/rebase-index/INDEX_ID puis remplace staging/BRANCH, sans changer branche, HEAD ou workspace. IDs exacts capturés sous verrou au CLI si omis, explicites en C++. Conflits et journal actif bloquent. Index frais rend unchanged sans écriture mais avec audit complet. Sinon applied, previous_index et nouvel index, plus champs du plan ; changed/stale décrivent l’entrée, staged le nouvel état. Un résultat égal à la tête demeure un index propre explicite, qui ne suffit pas au commit staged exigeant des différences. Arrêt, bloc absent, callback ou archive contradictoire avant publication gardent les anciennes références ; le candidat peut rester complet non référencé. Aucun journal de fichiers nécessaire. Après engagement, aucun callback/stop ; coupure ancien ou nouvel ensemble complet. Inspecter staged avant nouvel essai. L’archive protège tout l’ancien index, ses blocs et sa base après GC, même après commit/retrait du remplacement. Pas d’expiration automatique ; format/règles de chiffrement inchangés. text Copier syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native staged syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\" syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify cpp Copier auto result = store . rebase_stage ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , operation ) ; Résoudre les conflits d’index # --resolutions prend resolution_context du plan et files complété. operation rebase-index ; head tête actuelle ; source index figé ; base et parent son ancienne base, null pour l’index initial. Tout ID doit correspondre : changement de tête/index impose nouveau plan et choix. Choix d’entrée entière current (nouvelle tête), incoming (ancienne préparation), base, remove ou file personnalisé avec mode. Seulement chemins initialement conflictuels ; collisions recalculées, résolution incomplète sans publication. Aucun remplacement de chemin propre. Choix vides sur index frais gardent l’ID exact. Le décodage rejette doublons/champs inconnus/chemins/IDs/choix invalides avec les limites communes de 16 Mio, 100000 chemins et profondeur/champs. Plans par métadonnées contrôlent les manifestes personnalisés ; --verify/application vérifient leurs payloads. Conserver un pin avant publication si maintenance possible ; le nouvel index puis commit conservent ensuite les blocs. text Copier syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID cpp Copier auto choices = syrnck :: NativeResolutionSet :: parse ( plan [ \"resolution_context\" ] ) ; choices . files [ \"Content/Map.uasset\" ] = { syrnck :: NativeResolutionChoice :: incoming } ; auto preview = store . plan_rebase_stage_resolved ( \"feature\" , choices , true , operation ) ; auto result = store . rebase_stage_resolved ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , choices , operation ) ; Composition textuelle optionnelle # --text-merge combine les changements UTF-8 indépendants entre base enregistrée, tête et index figé, sans lire le travail ultérieur. Résolutions explicites prioritaires ; binaire, chevauchements, ajouts/suppressions et namespace bloquent l’ensemble. Fins de ligne conservées et mode combiné indépendamment. Même lot de 64 tentatives et 16 Mio entrées/résultats propres, plus limites par fichier. Les options de budget exigent l’activation. text_merge décrit statuts/motifs/coordonnées/métadonnées par tentative, sans texte fusionné. Index déjà frais : ID exact et zéro tentative. Le plan reconstruit/vérifie seulement les conflits éligibles et prédit les manifestes CDC sans écrire. L’application audite les entrées avant matérialisation et candidat ; ancienne archive et nouvel index publient dans le même CAS tête/index. Aucun groupe partiel en conflit. Avant transaction, arrêt peut laisser des objets complets mais garde références/workspace. Archive/base restent reconstructibles après GC et le commit ultérieur conserve le snapshot fusionné. Les méthodes nommées with_options gardent les entrées plain/resolved compatibles. text Copier syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID cpp Copier syrnck :: NativeStageRebaseOptions settings ; settings . text_merge . emplace ( ) ; auto plan = store . plan_rebase_stage_with_options ( \"feature\" , settings , true ) ; auto result = store . rebase_stage_with_options ( \"feature\" , expected_head , expected_index , settings ) ; syrnck native staged syrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify syrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature auto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage(\"feature\", true, operation); syrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck native staged syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\" syrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify auto result = store.rebase_stage(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, operation); syrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify syrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID auto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]); choices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming}; auto preview = store.plan_rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", choices, true, operation); auto result = store.rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, choices, operation); syrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify syrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID syrnck::NativeStageRebaseOptions settings; settings.text_merge.emplace(); auto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", settings, true); auto result = store.rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, settings);","headings":[{"id":"rebase-a-native-staging-index","text":"Rejouer un index préparé","level":1},{"id":"replay-and-preview","text":"Rejeu et aperçu","level":2},{"id":"apply-and-retain-prepared-bytes","text":"Appliquer et conserver les préparations","level":2},{"id":"resolve-index-conflicts","text":"Résoudre les conflits d’index","level":2},{"id":"optional-bounded-text-composition","text":"Composition textuelle optionnelle","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-8-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native staged\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --branch feature\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage(\"feature\", true, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Publish the rebased selection\"\nsyrnck native archives --kind rebase-index --verify\nauto result = store.rebase_stage(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --resolutions choices.json --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index --resolutions choices.json --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nauto choices = syrnck::NativeResolutionSet::parse(plan[\"resolution_context\"]);\nchoices.files[\"Content/Map.uasset\"] = {syrnck::NativeResolutionChoice::incoming};\nauto preview = store.plan_rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", choices, true, operation);\nauto result = store.rebase_stage_resolved(\"feature\", expected_head, expected_index,\n                                          choices, operation);\nsyrnck native rebase-index-plan --text-merge --verify\nsyrnck native rebase-index --text-merge --expect HEAD_ID --expect-index INDEX_ID\nsyrnck::NativeStageRebaseOptions settings;\nsettings.text_merge.emplace();\nauto plan = store.plan_rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", settings, true);\nauto result = store.rebase_stage_with_options(\"feature\", expected_head,\n                                               expected_index, settings);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"32b4256ae8c8"},{"id":"native-staging","title":"Index de préparation et commits sélectionnés","description":"Guide : Index de préparation et commits sélectionnés.","section":"Versionnage","order":380,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-staging-and-selected-commits\">Préparation native et commits sélectionnés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/native-staging-and-selected-commits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparation native et commits sélectionnés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le moteur autonome fige des fichiers choisis, inspecte le snapshot, retire des sélections puis commite sans relire le workspace. Cela concerne les branches natives ; Git garde son propre index. add . sélectionne tout le candidat ; reset sans chemin vide l’index. Aucun fichier de travail n’est modifié. Branche courante par défaut, main pour les anciens Stores ; --branch en choisit une autre sans changer HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset Source --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"selection-and-frozen-contents\">Sélection et octets figés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/selection-and-frozen-contents\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélection et octets figés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un chemin sélectionne un fichier exact ou tout un préfixe de dossier, relatif à --directory (défaut .), pas à une racine Git. Le CLI normalise séparateurs, ./ et slash final ; C++ attend des chemins validés ou . Wildcards, absolus et sorties de racine sont refusés ; -- permet un chemin commençant par tiret.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque add modifie l’index existant, initialement copié de la branche. Les entrées non choisies gardent leurs octets préparés ; les fichiers choisis sont ingérés immédiatement. Un suivi/préparé absent de la sélection actuelle est retiré, y compris exclu par ignore. Aucun match côté travail ou base/index est une erreur ; les dossiers vides non suivis n’ont pas d’entrée. Sélections répétées/chevauchantes autorisées ; dir ne prend pas dir 2. Pour une transition de type/casse qui laisserait des entrées incompatibles, choisir leur parent commun.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Scanner/exclusions identiques au commit/statut : ignores imbriqués, chemins/casse portables et fichiers ordinaires. Le Store est intérieur. L’ajout visite encore le candidat entier et n’ingère que les fichiers sélectionnés : un chemin non choisi invalide peut faire échouer. Il ne s’autorise pas sur le cache consultatif du watcher. Scan et métadonnées plates gardent leurs plafonds de 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Add rend l’ID immuable du snapshot complet, voisins inchangés compris, inspectable/exportable. Même base/entrées donne même ID. Les modifications ultérieures du disque ne changent pas les préparations ; refaire add remplace explicitement les octets choisis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-reset-and-commit\">Inspecter, réinitialiser et commiter\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/inspect-reset-and-commit\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspecter, réinitialiser et commiter\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>native staged ne lit que révisions/manifestes et expose branch, base enregistrée ou null, head actuelle ou null, snapshot ou null, stale si base différente de tête, clean si entrées égales à la base, staged_files total et counts/changes. Les lignes distinguent contenu, mode et manifeste ; une représentation seule peut être préparée. Sans index, rapport propre contre la tête ou base vide initiale. L’index périmé reste inspectable sur son ancienne base ; cela n’audite pas les payloads, contrairement à publication/GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Status ordinaire compare branche/disque ; --revision STAGING_ID compare à l’index ; --combined rend les deux côtés avec staleness. Reset PATH rétablit les entrées de la base enregistrée ou retire les nouveaux ajouts, sans lire/restaurer le disque et en gardant un index explicite même propre. Reset sans chemin retire tout l’index, de façon idempotente.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Commit --staged publie exactement l’index comme enfant de sa base et le consomme atomiquement ; branche initiale = commit racine. Il exige des entrées changées, accepte progression/arrêt mais refuse dossier/pipeline puisqu’il ne lit pas le workspace. Le commit ordinaire du dossier refuse tout index existant : commiter ou retirer la sélection auparavant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Branche déplacée/retirée rend l’index périmé et bloque add, reset sélectif et commit staged. Inspecter/exporter puis reset complet, ou \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stage-rebase\">rejouer l’index\u003c/a> sur une tête existante, avec archivage et refus des conflits. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks\">Hunks\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-lines\">lignes\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-patches\">patches externes\u003c/a> permettent la préparation partielle exacte ; l’éditeur de patch interactif reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-publication\">Rétention et publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/retention-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention et publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>staging/BRANCH pointe une révision v1 ordinaire avec date fixe, marqueur lié à la branche et parent de base facultatif. C’est une table complète, pas une chaîne d’éditions ; heads/BRANCH ne bouge qu’au commit. Le snapshot interne ne devient pas un parent de commit utilisateur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La racine conserve tous les manifestes/blocs préparés et l’histoire de base contre le GC, même sans branche visible, sur tous les stockages et avec chiffrement habituel. Ne pas retirer manuellement staging pour un index actif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Add/reset sélectif vérifient la fermeture du remplacement ; commit staged vérifie puis publie branche et retrait d’index ensemble par refs.json atomique, avec attentes branche/index contrôlées. Reset complet retire uniquement l’index. Verrou exclusif pendant toute l’opération ; aucun callback/stop après publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Annulation, mutation source, chemin/dépendance invalide ou callback en échec avant publication gardent l’ancienne branche/index. Des objets préparés immuables peuvent rester sans référence, réutilisables ou collectables. Aucun rollback de fichiers puisque staging n’en écrit pas. Le scan n’est pas un snapshot atomique : les fichiers sont contrôlés autour de l’ingestion et une mutation concurrente peut demander un nouvel essai.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">API C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Amend --staged réutilise le même index, même propre pour corriger le message. Il conserve les parents de l’ancienne tête et archive cette dernière en consommant l’index atomiquement. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-amend\">amend\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>stage_directory possède une surcharge IngestOptions ; le défaut utilise les préférences d’ingestion. Les autres opérations acceptent OperationOptions. Callbacks synchrones non réentrants, progression scan/ingestion puis vérification et dernier publishing/refs. Les mutations d’historique natives restent hors bindings C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> snapshot \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">stage_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Maps\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">staged_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> remaining \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> commit \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Selected changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">reset_stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-prepared-text\">Inspecter le texte préparé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-staging/inspect-prepared-text\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspecter le texte préparé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>native diff-text --staged PATH compare de façon vérifiée et bornée le fichier figé à la base originale de son index. Un index périmé garde sa base et les modifications ultérieures du workspace ne sont pas lues. --unified rend le patch ; voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff textuel\u003c/a> et la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks\">sélection de hunks\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Préparation native et commits sélectionnés # Le moteur autonome fige des fichiers choisis, inspecte le snapshot, retire des sélections puis commite sans relire le workspace. Cela concerne les branches natives ; Git garde son propre index. add . sélectionne tout le candidat ; reset sans chemin vide l’index. Aucun fichier de travail n’est modifié. Branche courante par défaut, main pour les anciens Stores ; --branch en choisit une autre sans changer HEAD. text Copier syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress syrnck native staged --branch main syrnck native reset Source --branch main syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main Sélection et octets figés # Un chemin sélectionne un fichier exact ou tout un préfixe de dossier, relatif à --directory (défaut .), pas à une racine Git. Le CLI normalise séparateurs, ./ et slash final ; C++ attend des chemins validés ou . Wildcards, absolus et sorties de racine sont refusés ; -- permet un chemin commençant par tiret. Chaque add modifie l’index existant, initialement copié de la branche. Les entrées non choisies gardent leurs octets préparés ; les fichiers choisis sont ingérés immédiatement. Un suivi/préparé absent de la sélection actuelle est retiré, y compris exclu par ignore. Aucun match côté travail ou base/index est une erreur ; les dossiers vides non suivis n’ont pas d’entrée. Sélections répétées/chevauchantes autorisées ; dir ne prend pas dir 2. Pour une transition de type/casse qui laisserait des entrées incompatibles, choisir leur parent commun. Scanner/exclusions identiques au commit/statut : ignores imbriqués, chemins/casse portables et fichiers ordinaires. Le Store est intérieur. L’ajout visite encore le candidat entier et n’ingère que les fichiers sélectionnés : un chemin non choisi invalide peut faire échouer. Il ne s’autorise pas sur le cache consultatif du watcher. Scan et métadonnées plates gardent leurs plafonds de 16 Mio. Add rend l’ID immuable du snapshot complet, voisins inchangés compris, inspectable/exportable. Même base/entrées donne même ID. Les modifications ultérieures du disque ne changent pas les préparations ; refaire add remplace explicitement les octets choisis. Inspecter, réinitialiser et commiter # native staged ne lit que révisions/manifestes et expose branch, base enregistrée ou null, head actuelle ou null, snapshot ou null, stale si base différente de tête, clean si entrées égales à la base, staged_files total et counts/changes. Les lignes distinguent contenu, mode et manifeste ; une représentation seule peut être préparée. Sans index, rapport propre contre la tête ou base vide initiale. L’index périmé reste inspectable sur son ancienne base ; cela n’audite pas les payloads, contrairement à publication/GC. Status ordinaire compare branche/disque ; --revision STAGING_ID compare à l’index ; --combined rend les deux côtés avec staleness. Reset PATH rétablit les entrées de la base enregistrée ou retire les nouveaux ajouts, sans lire/restaurer le disque et en gardant un index explicite même propre. Reset sans chemin retire tout l’index, de façon idempotente. Commit --staged publie exactement l’index comme enfant de sa base et le consomme atomiquement ; branche initiale = commit racine. Il exige des entrées changées, accepte progression/arrêt mais refuse dossier/pipeline puisqu’il ne lit pas le workspace. Le commit ordinaire du dossier refuse tout index existant : commiter ou retirer la sélection auparavant. Branche déplacée/retirée rend l’index périmé et bloque add, reset sélectif et commit staged. Inspecter/exporter puis reset complet, ou rejouer l’index sur une tête existante, avec archivage et refus des conflits. Hunks , lignes et patches externes permettent la préparation partielle exacte ; l’éditeur de patch interactif reste à développer. Rétention et publication # staging/BRANCH pointe une révision v1 ordinaire avec date fixe, marqueur lié à la branche et parent de base facultatif. C’est une table complète, pas une chaîne d’éditions ; heads/BRANCH ne bouge qu’au commit. Le snapshot interne ne devient pas un parent de commit utilisateur. La racine conserve tous les manifestes/blocs préparés et l’histoire de base contre le GC, même sans branche visible, sur tous les stockages et avec chiffrement habituel. Ne pas retirer manuellement staging pour un index actif. Add/reset sélectif vérifient la fermeture du remplacement ; commit staged vérifie puis publie branche et retrait d’index ensemble par refs.json atomique, avec attentes branche/index contrôlées. Reset complet retire uniquement l’index. Verrou exclusif pendant toute l’opération ; aucun callback/stop après publication. Annulation, mutation source, chemin/dépendance invalide ou callback en échec avant publication gardent l’ancienne branche/index. Des objets préparés immuables peuvent rester sans référence, réutilisables ou collectables. Aucun rollback de fichiers puisque staging n’en écrit pas. Le scan n’est pas un snapshot atomique : les fichiers sont contrôlés autour de l’ingestion et une mutation concurrente peut demander un nouvel essai. API C++ # Amend --staged réutilise le même index, même propre pour corriger le message. Il conserve les parents de l’ancienne tête et archive cette dernière en consommant l’index atomiquement. Voir amend . stage_directory possède une surcharge IngestOptions ; le défaut utilise les préférences d’ingestion. Les autres opérations acceptent OperationOptions. Callbacks synchrones non réentrants, progression scan/ingestion puis vérification et dernier publishing/refs. Les mutations d’historique natives restent hors bindings C/Go. cpp Copier auto snapshot = store . stage_directory ( project , \"main\" , { \"Source\" , \"Content/Maps\" } ) ; auto report = store . staged_status ( \"main\" , operation ) ; auto remaining = store . reset_stage ( \"main\" , { \"Source\" } , operation ) ; auto commit = store . commit_staged ( \"main\" , \"Selected changes\" , operation ) ; store . reset_stage ( \"main\" ) ; Inspecter le texte préparé # native diff-text --staged PATH compare de façon vérifiée et bornée le fichier figé à la base originale de son index. Un index périmé garde sa base et les modifications ultérieures du workspace ne sont pas lues. --unified rend le patch ; voir le diff textuel et la sélection de hunks . syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress syrnck native staged --branch main syrnck native reset Source --branch main syrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main auto snapshot = store.stage_directory(project, \"main\", {\"Source\", \"Content/Maps\"}); auto report = store.staged_status(\"main\", operation); auto remaining = store.reset_stage(\"main\", {\"Source\"}, operation); auto commit = store.commit_staged(\"main\", \"Selected changes\", operation); store.reset_stage(\"main\");","headings":[{"id":"native-staging-and-selected-commits","text":"Préparation native et commits sélectionnés","level":1},{"id":"selection-and-frozen-contents","text":"Sélection et octets figés","level":2},{"id":"inspect-reset-and-commit","text":"Inspecter, réinitialiser et commiter","level":2},{"id":"retention-and-publication","text":"Rétention et publication","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"API C++","level":2},{"id":"inspect-prepared-text","text":"Inspecter le texte préparé","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native staged --branch main\nsyrnck native reset Source --branch main\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"Update the selected maps\" --branch main\nauto snapshot = store.stage_directory(project, \"main\", {\"Source\", \"Content/Maps\"});\nauto report = store.staged_status(\"main\", operation);\nauto remaining = store.reset_stage(\"main\", {\"Source\"}, operation);\nauto commit = store.commit_staged(\"main\", \"Selected changes\", operation);\nstore.reset_stage(\"main\");","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-stage-rebase","#/fr/native-hunks","#/fr/native-lines","#/fr/text-patches","#/fr/native-amend","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/native-hunks"],"kind":"guide","revision":"79fb1b711c44"},{"id":"native-stash","title":"Stash natif","description":"Guide : Stash natif.","section":"Versionnage","order":390,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-stash\">Stash natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/native-stash\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stash natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Stash capture fichiers de travail complets et index figé avant nettoyage du workspace sélectionné ; l’application ultérieure restaure ce travail sans avancer la branche. API syrnck/stash.hpp sans dépendance réseau/CLI.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Utiliser l’ID canonique de 64 caractères rendu par push/list, sans alias positionnel mutable. --directory choisit le workspace principal ou le dossier lié enregistré partageant le Store ; --branch doit identifier la cible nommée ou détachée sélectionnée. Terminer d’abord toute récupération checkout/switch/reset/stash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash list --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"save-and-scope\">Sauvegarde et portée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/save-and-scope\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sauvegarde et portée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La sauvegarde inclut chemins suivis par tête/index, indépendamment des ignores, et non-suivis ordinaires obstructifs aux transitions. --include-untracked ajoute les fichiers ordinaires sélectionnés par le scanner natif avec ignores ; ignorés sans rapport restent sur place. Liens/reparse, spéciaux, entrées Store/Git et noms ambigus sont refusés s’ils obstruent ; le scanner optionnel refuse aussi les liens sélectionnés. Profondeur 128.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le snapshot de travail a pour parents tête et index éventuel. Ses fichiers gardent les octets du workspace ; son parent index garde les préparations même différentes. Les suivis manquants restent absents. Le nettoyage vise la tête courante. Les dossiers vides ne sont pas versionnés, mais le journal de reset garde ceux nécessaires à abort.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Métadonnées stash, génération monotone et journal hard-reset v5/pins sont publiés atomiquement avant tout déplacement. Une erreur antérieure peut laisser des objets complets non référencés ; ensuite stash conservé et nettoyage récupérable. Abort restaure workspace/index tout en gardant le stash comme copie supplémentaire. État propre sans index : stash null sans écriture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>List page du plus récent avec --before GENERATION, --limit N ; validation de métadonnées/références de travail sans tous les payloads. Aperçu de message 256 octets UTF-8, show rend le complet et --verify sa fermeture. JSON, IDs, messages utilisateur et noms d’opération restent stables entre langues.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reapply-conflicts-and-index\">Réapplication, conflits et index\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/reapply-conflicts-and-index\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Réapplication, conflits et index\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>apply-plan lit sans écrire ; application exige --expect-plan exact et recalcule avant candidats. Suivis sales, index existant, checkout actif et non-suivis obstructifs bloquent pour ne pas écraser un travail local.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le travail sauvegardé fusionne contre tête d’origine et tête sélectionnée actuelle. --index fusionne séparément les préparations contre le parent propre de l’ancien index, même périmé, pour garder les changements de tête ultérieurs. Le nouvel index a pour base la tête courante. Sans --index, les préparations appartiennent au résultat de travail sans nouvel index figé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tout conflit bloque l’ensemble. --text-merge active le moteur borné ; binaire demande un choix explicite. Deux contextes working.resolution_context et index.resolution_context acceptent current/incoming/base/remove/file avec manifeste vérifié. Opérations stash et stash-index et IDs head/source/base/parent lient les choix. Le texte partage limites agrégées de tentatives/octets entre les deux résultats ; garder les mêmes options à l’application.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Journal checkout v6 distingue tête publiée et cible de travail. La tête ne bouge pas ; publication finale installe index éventuel et, pour pop, archive/retire le stash dans le même CAS que le reçu engagé. Apply garde le stash actif. La révision de travail conservée protège stash original et nouvel index après GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant publication finale, abort restaure le workspace propre précédent et laisse le stash actif. Après, récupération du nettoyage. Fichiers de travail/préparation/rollback en clair selon les limites checkout habituelles ; ouverture sans reprise implicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"partial-workspaces\">Workspaces partiels\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/partial-workspaces\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Workspaces partiels\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Stash accepte les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces partiels\u003c/a> statiques, avec\nHEAD détaché ou sélection explicitement vide. La sauvegarde capture l’arbre\nphysique sélectionné et les fichiers ordinaires faisant obstacle à son nettoyage.\n--include-untracked suit cette même sélection et les règles d’exclusion. Les\nfichiers locaux exclus sans rapport restent sur place. Tête/index anciens restent\nparents complets, avec toutes les entrées cachées et leurs blocs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les descripteurs partiels utilisent v3 avec tableau canonique \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code>\nintégré. Les vues complètes nommées/détachées gardent v1/v2. Apply exige des\ndéfinitions sauvegardée/actuelle strictement identiques ; passage complet/partiel\nou changement de sélection refusé avant écriture des candidats. Le plan partiel\nv2 lie définition et racine de l’objet de politique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le rejeu de travail compare base/tête projetées à l’arbre physique sauvegardé.\nLe rejeu d’index reste complet : --index restaure les préparations cachées tout\nen conservant les changements cachés indépendants d’une tête plus récente. Sans\n--index, les données préparées cachées restent dans le parent index sauvegardé,\nsans apparaître hors sélection sur disque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Save utilise le journal v9 layout 5 ; apply/pop le layout 6 avec définitions\nfigées identiques. Le layout 6 projette la source logique et restaure le candidat\nphysique complet. Un fichier sauvegardé \u003ccode>Content\u003c/code> peut ainsi remplacer le chemin\nsélectionné \u003ccode>Content/A/leaf\u003c/code>, sans introduire le \u003ccode>Content/B\u003c/code> logique exclu dans\nce namespace physique. Abort restaure la vue projetée propre précédente ; la\npublication finale combine index complet et archivage du pop, avec HEAD logique\ninchangé. GC et sauvegarde portable conservent descripteur, travail physique et\nindex/historique complets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drop-restore-and-retention\">Drop, restauration et rétention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/drop-restore-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Drop, restauration et rétention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Drop exige l’ID d’enregistrement immuable attendu et déplace références actives de métadonnées/travail vers archives. Pop protège de la même façon ; aucun objet supprimé. Restore conserve la génération originale et exige l’ID actif absent. Archives et racines de travail survivent à sauvegarde/restauration et GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les racines de travail peuvent être \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retirées avec sauvegarde\u003c/a>. Un stash retiré ne se restaure plus depuis le Store courant sans réimporter protection/données de sa sauvegarde. D’autres archives/reçus checkout peuvent encore garder des blocs partagés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-verification\">Bornes et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-stash/bounds-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bornes et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Au plus 4096 stashes actifs et 1000 lignes par page ; message 1 Mio. Révisions, inventaires et rapports gardent 16 Mio. Les octets passent en flux par ingestion configurée, mais inventaire et hashing restent nécessaires, distincts du watcher/index de travail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sous Windows à limite canonique de chemin ancienne, l’OS peut refuser un chemin avant profondeur 128 ; la prise en charge complète des longs chemins reste un travail de compatibilité distinct.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Stash natif # Stash capture fichiers de travail complets et index figé avant nettoyage du workspace sélectionné ; l’application ultérieure restaure ce travail sans avancer la branche. API syrnck/stash.hpp sans dépendance réseau/CLI. Utiliser l’ID canonique de 64 caractères rendu par push/list, sans alias positionnel mutable. --directory choisit le workspace principal ou le dossier lié enregistré partageant le Store ; --branch doit identifier la cible nommée ou détachée sélectionnée. Terminer d’abord toute récupération checkout/switch/reset/stash. text Copier syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\" syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\" syrnck native stash list --limit 100 syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID Sauvegarde et portée # La sauvegarde inclut chemins suivis par tête/index, indépendamment des ignores, et non-suivis ordinaires obstructifs aux transitions. --include-untracked ajoute les fichiers ordinaires sélectionnés par le scanner natif avec ignores ; ignorés sans rapport restent sur place. Liens/reparse, spéciaux, entrées Store/Git et noms ambigus sont refusés s’ils obstruent ; le scanner optionnel refuse aussi les liens sélectionnés. Profondeur 128. Le snapshot de travail a pour parents tête et index éventuel. Ses fichiers gardent les octets du workspace ; son parent index garde les préparations même différentes. Les suivis manquants restent absents. Le nettoyage vise la tête courante. Les dossiers vides ne sont pas versionnés, mais le journal de reset garde ceux nécessaires à abort. Métadonnées stash, génération monotone et journal hard-reset v5/pins sont publiés atomiquement avant tout déplacement. Une erreur antérieure peut laisser des objets complets non référencés ; ensuite stash conservé et nettoyage récupérable. Abort restaure workspace/index tout en gardant le stash comme copie supplémentaire. État propre sans index : stash null sans écriture. List page du plus récent avec --before GENERATION, --limit N ; validation de métadonnées/références de travail sans tous les payloads. Aperçu de message 256 octets UTF-8, show rend le complet et --verify sa fermeture. JSON, IDs, messages utilisateur et noms d’opération restent stables entre langues. Réapplication, conflits et index # apply-plan lit sans écrire ; application exige --expect-plan exact et recalcule avant candidats. Suivis sales, index existant, checkout actif et non-suivis obstructifs bloquent pour ne pas écraser un travail local. Le travail sauvegardé fusionne contre tête d’origine et tête sélectionnée actuelle. --index fusionne séparément les préparations contre le parent propre de l’ancien index, même périmé, pour garder les changements de tête ultérieurs. Le nouvel index a pour base la tête courante. Sans --index, les préparations appartiennent au résultat de travail sans nouvel index figé. Tout conflit bloque l’ensemble. --text-merge active le moteur borné ; binaire demande un choix explicite. Deux contextes working.resolution_context et index.resolution_context acceptent current/incoming/base/remove/file avec manifeste vérifié. Opérations stash et stash-index et IDs head/source/base/parent lient les choix. Le texte partage limites agrégées de tentatives/octets entre les deux résultats ; garder les mêmes options à l’application. Journal checkout v6 distingue tête publiée et cible de travail. La tête ne bouge pas ; publication finale installe index éventuel et, pour pop, archive/retire le stash dans le même CAS que le reçu engagé. Apply garde le stash actif. La révision de travail conservée protège stash original et nouvel index après GC. Avant publication finale, abort restaure le workspace propre précédent et laisse le stash actif. Après, récupération du nettoyage. Fichiers de travail/préparation/rollback en clair selon les limites checkout habituelles ; ouverture sans reprise implicite. text Copier syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID text Copier syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort Workspaces partiels # Stash accepte les workspaces partiels statiques, avec HEAD détaché ou sélection explicitement vide. La sauvegarde capture l’arbre physique sélectionné et les fichiers ordinaires faisant obstacle à son nettoyage. --include-untracked suit cette même sélection et les règles d’exclusion. Les fichiers locaux exclus sans rapport restent sur place. Tête/index anciens restent parents complets, avec toutes les entrées cachées et leurs blocs. Les descripteurs partiels utilisent v3 avec tableau canonique materialization intégré. Les vues complètes nommées/détachées gardent v1/v2. Apply exige des définitions sauvegardée/actuelle strictement identiques ; passage complet/partiel ou changement de sélection refusé avant écriture des candidats. Le plan partiel v2 lie définition et racine de l’objet de politique. Le rejeu de travail compare base/tête projetées à l’arbre physique sauvegardé. Le rejeu d’index reste complet : --index restaure les préparations cachées tout en conservant les changements cachés indépendants d’une tête plus récente. Sans --index, les données préparées cachées restent dans le parent index sauvegardé, sans apparaître hors sélection sur disque. Save utilise le journal v9 layout 5 ; apply/pop le layout 6 avec définitions figées identiques. Le layout 6 projette la source logique et restaure le candidat physique complet. Un fichier sauvegardé Content peut ainsi remplacer le chemin sélectionné Content/A/leaf , sans introduire le Content/B logique exclu dans ce namespace physique. Abort restaure la vue projetée propre précédente ; la publication finale combine index complet et archivage du pop, avec HEAD logique inchangé. GC et sauvegarde portable conservent descripteur, travail physique et index/historique complets. Drop, restauration et rétention # Drop exige l’ID d’enregistrement immuable attendu et déplace références actives de métadonnées/travail vers archives. Pop protège de la même façon ; aucun objet supprimé. Restore conserve la génération originale et exige l’ID actif absent. Archives et racines de travail survivent à sauvegarde/restauration et GC. Les racines de travail peuvent être retirées avec sauvegarde . Un stash retiré ne se restaure plus depuis le Store courant sans réimporter protection/données de sa sauvegarde. D’autres archives/reçus checkout peuvent encore garder des blocs partagés. text Copier syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify Bornes et vérification # Au plus 4096 stashes actifs et 1000 lignes par page ; message 1 Mio. Révisions, inventaires et rapports gardent 16 Mio. Les octets passent en flux par ingestion configurée, mais inventaire et hashing restent nécessaires, distincts du watcher/index de travail. Sous Windows à limite canonique de chemin ancienne, l’OS peut refuser un chemin avant profondeur 128 ; la prise en charge complète des longs chemins reste un travail de compatibilité distinct. syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\" syrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\" syrnck native stash list --limit 100 syrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify syrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge syrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json syrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover syrnck native checkout-recover --abort syrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID syrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify syrnck native stash restore STASH_ID syrnck native archives --kind stash --verify","headings":[{"id":"native-stash","text":"Stash natif","level":1},{"id":"save-and-scope","text":"Sauvegarde et portée","level":2},{"id":"reapply-conflicts-and-index","text":"Réapplication, conflits et index","level":2},{"id":"partial-workspaces","text":"Workspaces partiels","level":2},{"id":"drop-restore-and-retention","text":"Drop, restauration et rétention","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-verification","text":"Bornes et vérification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native stash push -m \"WIP: lighting\"\nsyrnck native stash push --include-untracked -m \"WIP: new assets\"\nsyrnck native stash list --limit 100\nsyrnck native stash show STASH_ID --verify\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --verify\nsyrnck native stash pop STASH_ID --index --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --text-merge\nsyrnck native stash apply-plan STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json\nsyrnck native stash apply STASH_ID --index --resolutions work.json --index-resolutions index.json --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort\nsyrnck native stash drop STASH_ID --expect-record RECORD_ID\nsyrnck native stash archive STASH_ID --verify\nsyrnck native stash restore STASH_ID\nsyrnck native archives --kind stash --verify","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/archive-retention"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f498c16b413c"},{"id":"native-tags","title":"Tags et sélecteurs de révision","description":"Guide : Tags et sélecteurs de révision.","section":"Versionnage","order":400,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives\">Tags natifs, sélecteurs et archives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-tags/native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Tags natifs, sélecteurs et archives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Un tag nomme une révision commitée indépendamment d’une branche. Léger, il conserve sa cible ; avec un message, il ajoute une annotation UTF-8 et sa date. Ces opérations ne lisent ni ne modifient workspace, index ou HEAD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La cible par défaut est HEAD commité. Modifications de travail et préparations locales ne sont pas incluses. Un HEAD initial vide exige une révision existante explicite. La création vérifie toute la fermeture cible, parents et hashes complets compris. Un nom existant est refusé même pour la même cible ; aucune option force/move n’est fournie.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Noms : lettres/chiffres ASCII, slash, point, tiret, underscore ; aucun composant vide, \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>..\u003c/code>, aucun tiret initial ; au plus 187 octets pour tenir dans les deux namespaces. Messages : 65536 octets UTF-8 maximum ; un message vide explicite crée une annotation. L’OS peut limiter davantage la ligne de commande. Auteur de tag et signatures restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag release/v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tags --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"read-only-reports-and-pagination\">Rapports en lecture seule et pagination\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-tags/read-only-reports-and-pagination\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rapports en lecture seule et pagination\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native tags\u003c/code> rend \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tags\u003c/code> triés, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code>. Limite 1..1000, défaut 100, et plafond de JSON sérialisé inférieur à 16 Mio, échappement inclus. Chaque tag expose \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>annotation\u003c/code> (ID chunk ou null), \u003ccode>message\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>timestamp\u003c/code> ; les deux derniers sont null pour un tag léger. Dates Unix UTC, JSON anglais et messages utilisateur inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le curseur \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code> est exclusif et reste utilisable si le tag a été supprimé. Chaque appel utilise son snapshot de références, sans inventaire persistant ni calcul du total ; les mutations entre pages peuvent changer la vue. Une page peut contenir moins que sa limite. Le plafond de sortie ne borne pas la RAM : références, caches, annotation et buffers s’ajoutent. Les annotations affichées et racines orphelines sont contrôlées sans lire tous les arbres ni payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>tag-show NAME\u003c/code> rend \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tag\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>tag-archive ID\u003c/code> ajoute l’ID d’archive et \u003ccode>deleted_at\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audite la fermeture complète de la cible. Par défaut, références/annotations/descripteurs sont inspectés sans preuve de disponibilité des blocs/parents. Annotations invalides, identités incohérentes et groupes incomplets ou avec membres inattendus sont refusés, sans réparation ni écriture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>tag-archives [--after ID] [--limit N] [--verify]\u003c/code> liste les tags supprimés par ID d’archive. Champs : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>archives\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>has_more\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>next_after\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_verified\u003c/code> ; lignes \u003ccode>archive\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tag\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>deleted_at\u003c/code>. Même défaut/plafond de lignes et de JSON. Le curseur exclusif survit au retrait de l’archive. Chaque page contrôle ses groupes complets et annotations ; \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> audite seulement les fermetures affichées. Aucune vue figée sur plusieurs requêtes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"archived-deletion-and-restoration\">Suppression archivée et restauration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-tags/archived-deletion-and-restoration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Suppression archivée et restauration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La suppression exige \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> avec l’ID exact inspecté, pas un sélecteur mobile. Une transaction retire tag/annotation publics et publie descripteur, cible et annotation éventuelle de l’archive, dont l’ID est rendu. Un échec de vérification/attente ne supprime rien. L’archive conserve tout l’historique et ses blocs partagés après GC, même sans branche ni tag public.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après perte de réponse, inspecter le tag et \u003ccode>tag-archives\u003c/code>, puis \u003ccode>tag-archive ID\u003c/code>. Les groupes portent \u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/record\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La restauration vérifie l’archive et recrée nom, cible, message et date d’annotation ensemble. Un emplacement déjà occupé par un tag ou une annotation est refusé ; l’archive reste conservée. On peut recréer volontairement un tag à une autre révision sans perdre l’ancienne archive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait protégé par sauvegarde\u003c/a> accepte le groupe entier \u003ccode>archive/tags/ID\u003c/code>. Sa recette et ses payloads restent dans la sauvegarde indépendante vérifiée. Pas de purge automatique. Les API administratives peuvent contourner ces racines : l’immutabilité n’est ni autorisation ni signature. Retirer toutes les protections puis collecter rend volontairement cette version indisponible. Le descripteur seul ne conserve pas sa cible : préserver tout le groupe lors d’une copie.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tag-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"native-revision-selectors\">Sélecteurs de révisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-tags/native-revision-selectors\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sélecteurs de révisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Création de tags/branches, \u003ccode>status --revision\u003c/code>, diff de snapshots, checkout et export acceptent : ID exact de 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules (prioritaire sur un nom identique) ; HEAD nommé ou détaché committé ; \u003ccode>heads/NAME\u003c/code> ; \u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code> avec contrôle de l’annotation ; ou NAME non ambigu entre branche et tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Même si leurs cibles sont identiques, un nom partagé est ambigu : préciser le namespace. Un tag nommé HEAD ou comme un hash reste accessible par \u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code>. La résolution vérifie les métadonnées de la révision seulement, pas ses parents, manifestes ou blocs ; la vérification complète reste distincte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>switch NAME\u003c/code> sélectionne toujours une branche littérale. \u003ccode>checkout TAG\u003c/code> restaure la révision et déplace la cible sélectionnée en archivant sa tête ; utiliser \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">\u003ccode>native switch --detach TAG\u003c/code>\u003c/a> pour détacher HEAD. Les méthodes C++ gardent leurs IDs exacts ; appeler \u003ccode>resolve_native_revision()\u003c/code> pour les sélecteurs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-cancellation-and-api\">Stockage, annulation et API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-tags/storage-cancellation-and-api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage, annulation et API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>tags/NAME\u003c/code> cible une révision ; \u003ccode>tag-metadata/NAME\u003c/code> pointe l’annotation canonique facultative. \u003ccode>archive/tags/ID/record\u003c/code> porte le descripteur chunk d’ID identique ; \u003ccode>/target\u003c/code> la révision ; \u003ccode>/annotation\u003c/code> le chunk initial éventuel. Les schémas stricts lient nom, cible et annotation. L’archive ajoute un nonce et une date de suppression. Compression/chiffrement au repos habituels, aucun nouveau type d’objet, pointeur, ABI ou format physique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les transferts génériques ne négocient pas encore une publication atomique des groupes de tags annotés/archives. Politiques serveur de tags et réplication coordonnée restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Toutes les commandes permettent progression et arrêt coopératif. Les mutations publient par CAS après fermeture complète. Avant publication, arrêt/exception de callback conservent les racines et peuvent laisser des chunks complets non référencés. Aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt après publication ; une coupure laisse l’ancien ou le nouvel ensemble complet. Les tags restent utilisables pendant un checkout en attente puisqu’ils ne changent ni branche ni racines de récupération. Callbacks non réentrants. Les opérations de tags ne sont pas encore exposées en C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> selected \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resolve_native_revision\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"HEAD\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> created \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Release notes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> shown \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> removed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">delete_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"release/v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> selected\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archived_page \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag_archives\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> saved \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_tag_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restored \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_tag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>removed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Tags natifs, sélecteurs et archives # Un tag nomme une révision commitée indépendamment d’une branche. Léger, il conserve sa cible ; avec un message, il ajoute une annotation UTF-8 et sa date. Ces opérations ne lisent ni ne modifient workspace, index ou HEAD. La cible par défaut est HEAD commité. Modifications de travail et préparations locales ne sont pas incluses. Un HEAD initial vide exige une révision existante explicite. La création vérifie toute la fermeture cible, parents et hashes complets compris. Un nom existant est refusé même pour la même cible ; aucune option force/move n’est fournie. Noms : lettres/chiffres ASCII, slash, point, tiret, underscore ; aucun composant vide, . ou .. , aucun tiret initial ; au plus 187 octets pour tenir dans les deux namespaces. Messages : 65536 octets UTF-8 maximum ; un message vide explicite crée une annotation. L’OS peut limiter davantage la ligne de commande. Auteur de tag et signatures restent à développer. text Copier syrnck native tag release/v1 syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify Rapports en lecture seule et pagination # native tags rend version , tags triés, has_more , next_after . Limite 1..1000, défaut 100, et plafond de JSON sérialisé inférieur à 16 Mio, échappement inclus. Chaque tag expose name , revision , annotation (ID chunk ou null), message , timestamp ; les deux derniers sont null pour un tag léger. Dates Unix UTC, JSON anglais et messages utilisateur inchangés. Le curseur next_after est exclusif et reste utilisable si le tag a été supprimé. Chaque appel utilise son snapshot de références, sans inventaire persistant ni calcul du total ; les mutations entre pages peuvent changer la vue. Une page peut contenir moins que sa limite. Le plafond de sortie ne borne pas la RAM : références, caches, annotation et buffers s’ajoutent. Les annotations affichées et racines orphelines sont contrôlées sans lire tous les arbres ni payloads. tag-show NAME rend version , tag , objects_verified . tag-archive ID ajoute l’ID d’archive et deleted_at . --verify audite la fermeture complète de la cible. Par défaut, références/annotations/descripteurs sont inspectés sans preuve de disponibilité des blocs/parents. Annotations invalides, identités incohérentes et groupes incomplets ou avec membres inattendus sont refusés, sans réparation ni écriture. tag-archives [--after ID] [--limit N] [--verify] liste les tags supprimés par ID d’archive. Champs : version , archives , has_more , next_after , objects_verified ; lignes archive , tag , deleted_at . Même défaut/plafond de lignes et de JSON. Le curseur exclusif survit au retrait de l’archive. Chaque page contrôle ses groupes complets et annotations ; --verify audite seulement les fermetures affichées. Aucune vue figée sur plusieurs requêtes. Suppression archivée et restauration # La suppression exige --expect avec l’ID exact inspecté, pas un sélecteur mobile. Une transaction retire tag/annotation publics et publie descripteur, cible et annotation éventuelle de l’archive, dont l’ID est rendu. Un échec de vérification/attente ne supprime rien. L’archive conserve tout l’historique et ses blocs partagés après GC, même sans branche ni tag public. Après perte de réponse, inspecter le tag et tag-archives , puis tag-archive ID . Les groupes portent archive/tags/ID/record . La restauration vérifie l’archive et recrée nom, cible, message et date d’annotation ensemble. Un emplacement déjà occupé par un tag ou une annotation est refusé ; l’archive reste conservée. On peut recréer volontairement un tag à une autre révision sans perdre l’ancienne archive. Le retrait protégé par sauvegarde accepte le groupe entier archive/tags/ID . Sa recette et ses payloads restent dans la sauvegarde indépendante vérifiée. Pas de purge automatique. Les API administratives peuvent contourner ces racines : l’immutabilité n’est ni autorisation ni signature. Retirer toutes les protections puis collecter rend volontairement cette version indisponible. Le descripteur seul ne conserve pas sa cible : préserver tout le groupe lors d’une copie. text Copier syrnck native tag-show release/v1 syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck gc --delete syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID Sélecteurs de révisions # Création de tags/branches, status --revision , diff de snapshots, checkout et export acceptent : ID exact de 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules (prioritaire sur un nom identique) ; HEAD nommé ou détaché committé ; heads/NAME ; tags/NAME avec contrôle de l’annotation ; ou NAME non ambigu entre branche et tag. Même si leurs cibles sont identiques, un nom partagé est ambigu : préciser le namespace. Un tag nommé HEAD ou comme un hash reste accessible par tags/NAME . La résolution vérifie les métadonnées de la révision seulement, pas ses parents, manifestes ou blocs ; la vérification complète reste distincte. switch NAME sélectionne toujours une branche littérale. checkout TAG restaure la révision et déplace la cible sélectionnée en archivant sa tête ; utiliser native switch --detach TAG pour détacher HEAD. Les méthodes C++ gardent leurs IDs exacts ; appeler resolve_native_revision() pour les sélecteurs. Stockage, annulation et API # tags/NAME cible une révision ; tag-metadata/NAME pointe l’annotation canonique facultative. archive/tags/ID/record porte le descripteur chunk d’ID identique ; /target la révision ; /annotation le chunk initial éventuel. Les schémas stricts lient nom, cible et annotation. L’archive ajoute un nonce et une date de suppression. Compression/chiffrement au repos habituels, aucun nouveau type d’objet, pointeur, ABI ou format physique. Les transferts génériques ne négocient pas encore une publication atomique des groupes de tags annotés/archives. Politiques serveur de tags et réplication coordonnée restent à développer. Toutes les commandes permettent progression et arrêt coopératif. Les mutations publient par CAS après fermeture complète. Avant publication, arrêt/exception de callback conservent les racines et peuvent laisser des chunks complets non référencés. Aucun callback ni contrôle d’arrêt après publication ; une coupure laisse l’ancien ou le nouvel ensemble complet. Les tags restent utilisables pendant un checkout en attente puisqu’ils ne changent ni branche ni racines de récupération. Callbacks non réentrants. Les opérations de tags ne sont pas encore exposées en C/Go. cpp Copier auto selected = store . resolve_native_revision ( \"HEAD\" , operation ) ; auto created = store . create_native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , selected , \"Release notes\" , operation ) ; auto page = store . native_tags ( { } , 100 , operation ) ; auto shown = store . native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , true , operation ) ; auto removed = store . delete_native_tag ( \"release/v1\" , selected , operation ) ; auto archived_page = store . native_tag_archives ( { } , 100 , true , operation ) ; auto saved = store . native_tag_archive ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true , operation ) ; auto restored = store . restore_native_tag ( removed [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , operation ) ; syrnck native tag release/v1 syrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify syrnck native tag-show release/v1 syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID syrnck native tag-archives --verify syrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck gc --delete syrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID auto selected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\", operation); auto created = store.create_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, \"Release notes\", operation); auto page = store.native_tags({}, 100, operation); auto shown = store.native_tag(\"release/v1\", true, operation); auto removed = store.delete_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, operation); auto archived_page = store.native_tag_archives({}, 100, true, operation); auto saved = store.native_tag_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation); auto restored = store.restore_native_tag(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-tags-revision-selectors-and-retained-archives","text":"Tags natifs, sélecteurs et archives","level":1},{"id":"read-only-reports-and-pagination","text":"Rapports en lecture seule et pagination","level":2},{"id":"archived-deletion-and-restoration","text":"Suppression archivée et restauration","level":2},{"id":"native-revision-selectors","text":"Sélecteurs de révisions","level":2},{"id":"storage-cancellation-and-api","text":"Stockage, annulation et API","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native tag release/v1\nsyrnck native tag release/v2 HEAD -m \"Release notes\"\nsyrnck native tags --limit 100\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\nsyrnck native checkout-plan tags/release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1\nsyrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect EXACT_REVISION_ID\nsyrnck native tag-archives --verify\nsyrnck native tag-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck gc --delete\nsyrnck native tag-restore ARCHIVE_ID\nauto selected = store.resolve_native_revision(\"HEAD\", operation);\nauto created = store.create_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, \"Release notes\", operation);\nauto page = store.native_tags({}, 100, operation);\nauto shown = store.native_tag(\"release/v1\", true, operation);\nauto removed = store.delete_native_tag(\"release/v1\", selected, operation);\nauto archived_page = store.native_tag_archives({}, 100, true, operation);\nauto saved = store.native_tag_archive(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true, operation);\nauto restored = store.restore_native_tag(removed[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-detached"],"kind":"guide","revision":"9a6c9a9d1b72"},{"id":"native-watch","title":"Surveillance résidente du workspace","description":"Guide : Surveillance résidente du workspace.","section":"Versionnage","order":410,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"resident-native-workspace-observation\">Observation résidente du workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/resident-native-workspace-observation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Observation résidente du workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native watch\u003c/code> observe l’état courant : notifications récursives Windows, inotify récursif Linux, polling ailleurs ou avec \u003ccode>--polling\u003c/code>, en cas d’indisponibilité/limite native.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une ligne JSON compacte est émise puis flushée par frame. Clés/types/raisons restent anglais ; erreurs/progression stderr sont bilingues. Sans durée/limite d’updates, arrêt par interruption. Il observe sans commit, modification de fichiers, publication, transfert, verrou d’asset ni résolution de concurrence. C’est une fondation pour un futur agent résident. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions C/Go\u003c/a> exposent watcher/inventaire avec lots retenus et Store appartenant à l’appelant, sans journal CLI.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native watch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native watch --journal --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native watch-journal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native watch-journal --clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"status-and-event-lifecycle\">Cycle des événements et états\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/status-and-event-lifecycle\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cycle des événements et états\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le watcher est armé avant la première requête. Le démarrage efface le cache de hash consultatif et lit tous les fichiers sélectionnés, couvrant les edits hors ligne. Les notifications sont coalescées sur un intervalle fixe borné puis remplacent les sous-arbres concernés de l’inventaire complet en mémoire. Les règles ignore ordinaires s’appliquent. Les fichiers concernés sont intégralement lus même à taille/dates identiques ; les autres sous-arbres ne sont ni énumérés ni lus. Les observations ciblées ne réécrivent pas le cache persisté.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Créations, suppressions, renommages et transitions remplacent leurs préfixes complets. Un ancêtre absent/ignoré/fichier élargit la portée pour retirer les entrées périmées ; \u003ccode>asset\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>assets\u003c/code> sont distincts. Les hints imbriqués fusionnent. Un \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code> imbriqué resélectionne son sous-arbre, avec règles enfants ; celui de racine impose le scan complet. Collisions portables fichier/dossier/casse et borne de métadonnées v1 sont contrôlées sur l’union retenue+nouvelle avant hash. Sélection ambiguë, portée protégée ou budget dépassé impose un scan complet ; une erreur persistante reste une erreur. Liens/reparse pour ignore files sont refusés ; un pattern invalide donne une erreur de scan récupérable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les noms d’événements sont des indices d’état courant, pas un log transactionnel ni une paire de renommage garantie. \u003ccode>--combined\u003c/code> sépare index et travail, index périmé inclus. Branche/révision suivent status ; combined refuse une révision explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’attente native ne parcourt ni dossiers ni Store. Le Store est ouvert seulement pendant la requête puis fermé avant sortie/attente/coalescence pour laisser les autres commandes prendre le verrou. Cache/objets/.git sont exclus de la boucle ; les vrais \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code> restent visibles. Windows vérifie l’identité des alias de Store ; \u003ccode>Vault\u003c/code> distinct de \u003ccode>vault\u003c/code> sur FS sensible à la casse reste observé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls un état sémantique changé, un scan complet terminé ou une nouvelle erreur émettent. Compteurs de hash/réutilisation et générations seuls restent silencieux. Le polling énumère à chaque intervalle mais n’émet pas les résultats identiques.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le champ \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> reprend les rapports ordinaires/combinés. \u003ccode>observation\u003c/code> donne \u003ccode>mode: full|indexed|targeted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>full_scan\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>inspected_files\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>retained_files\u003c/code>; les ciblés ajoutent \u003ccode>scanned_scopes\u003c/code> triés après coalescence/ancêtres. Une suppression peut lire zéro fichier/octet. Seuls les scans énumérés incluent les métriques \u003ccode>working_index\u003c/code>; l’inventaire de session reste complet même pour les fichiers non cachables. Le fallback marque aussi la frame \u003ccode>full_scan\u003c/code>. Comparer les maps et lire les métadonnées reste proportionnel, sans status constant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sequence\u003c/code> croît dans le processus, erreurs comprises. Backends : \u003ccode>windows\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>inotify\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>polling\u003c/code>. Raisons : \u003ccode>initial\u003c/code> première réussite ; \u003ccode>filesystem\u003c/code> notification avec changement ; \u003ccode>polling\u003c/code> changement observé ; \u003ccode>audit\u003c/code> lecture complète périodique ; \u003ccode>rescan\u003c/code> topologie/enregistrement ; \u003ccode>overflow\u003c/code> perte/budget ; \u003ccode>recovered\u003c/code> scan complet après indisponibilité.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"sequence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"type\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"status\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"reason\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"initial\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"backend\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"windows\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"full_scan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"overflow\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"status\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"base\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token null keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"clean\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"tracked_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"workspace_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"hashed_bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"counts\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"untracked\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"removed\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"modified\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"unchanged\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"changes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"working_index\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"mode\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"stat-v1\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"previous\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"missing\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"hashed_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"reused_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"supported_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"cached_files\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"truncated\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"object\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations\">Pertes d’événements, audits et indisponibilité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Pertes d’événements, audits et indisponibilité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Overflow, enregistrement incomplet/malformé, budget de hints ou panne native abandonnent les listes partielles et exigent un scan complet sans cache. Noms non UTF-8 : hints abandonnés, scan complet, affichage avec remplacement des octets invalides pour garder un JSON valide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Linux arme les nouveaux dossiers/imports avant d’énumérer leurs enfants sur la même instance inotify, puis observe leur sous-arbre entier ; les écritures ultérieures restent en queue. Enregistrements existants conservés, additions coalescées et budgets persistants appliqués. Suppression, sortie, self-move, watch ignoré ou démontage reconstruisent l’instance et imposent un scan complet ; aucun renommage des chemins par cookie. Descripteurs inconnus/alias, trames invalides, overflow et extension ratée/dépassée exigent récupération. Instance partielle fermée ; une annulation invalide la continuité. Une indisponibilité/quota peut revenir au polling.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les audits complets périodiques ont un calendrier indépendant des notifications/polling : défaut cinq minutes, réglable. Ils couvrent notamment FS distants, notifications manquées et écritures mappées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Store occupé, checkout actif ou scan raté : frame \u003ccode>type: error\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>code: scan_failed\u003c/code>, message traduit, \u003ccode>status: null\u003c/code>. L’ancien succès n’est pas présenté comme courant. Erreurs consécutives identiques supprimées ; reprise au moins une seconde plus tard sans verrou conservé. Le retour au succès impose un scan complet. Un changement permanent d’identité de racine demande un redémarrage ; un handle de dossier retient cette identité sans interdire les edits ni verrouiller le Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’observation ne fournit ni snapshot atomique/verrouillé, ni versions intermédiaires, ni autorisation de sauter les validations des mutations.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal\">Journal de checkpoint optionnel\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Journal de checkpoint optionnel\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--journal\u003c/code> écrit le lot coalescé avant traitement, puis \u003ccode>observed\u003c/code> avant sortie réussie ou \u003ccode>unavailable\u003c/code> après erreur si stockage accessible. C’est un enregistrement borné remplacé, pas un historique rejouable : digests workspace/sélection, session aléatoire, génération, ancienne session/état, backend, hints bornés, digest sémantique, base, propreté et nombre de fichiers, sans contenu ni rapport complet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les frames ajoutent \u003ccode>journal: {session,generation,durable,previous}\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>durable: true\u003c/code> signifie sauvegarde avant stdout, sans accusé du consommateur. \u003ccode>sequence\u003c/code> reste local ; un arrêt entre publication et livraison exige de rétablir l’état à la reconnexion. Un Store indisponible peut donner \u003ccode>durable: false\u003c/code>. Un journal corrompu est préservé et bloque ce mode jusqu’à effacement explicite ; le mode ordinaire reste utilisable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native watch-journal\u003c/code> lit le dernier record ou \u003ccode>state: absent\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>observed\u003c/code> décrit une ancienne requête, pas un watcher vivant ni un état encore courant. \u003ccode>pending\u003c/code> peut subsister après arrêt. Tout démarrage crée une nouvelle session et lit tous les octets, même après un checkpoint valide ; le premier record conserve l’ancienne session/état.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--clear\u003c/code> retire explicitement le journal local, même malformé dans la borne de taille, sans modifier objets/références/index/workspace. Le mode journal détient \u003ccode>watch.lock\u003c/code>, interdisant second propriétaire/clear concurrent. Le Store reste libre entre requêtes et le checkpoint consultable ; sans journal, pas de verrou de session.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fichier autonome : \u003ccode>SYW1\u003c/code>, digest de chunk sur 64 octets et enveloppe Store compressée, chiffrée par la clé AEAD existante si nécessaire, noms inclus. Digest et mode restent visibles. Bornes : clair 2 Mio, fichier 3 Mio, 65536 chemins, budget de noms 1 Mio. Écriture temporaire, flush et remplacement atomique durable, publication après callbacks. Aucun objet/racine temporaire ; GC/compact ne l’effacent pas, backups logiques l’omettent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Attente native et polling inchangé n’écrivent rien. Un changement polling est checkpointé avant sortie ; audit écrit pending puis observed. Versions causales, outbox et publication réseau exactly-once restent distinctes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-resource-use\">Limites et ressources\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/limits-and-resource-use\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites et ressources\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Racines workspace/Store doivent exister, être lisibles et identifiables.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Défaut\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Valeurs\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--interval-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20..60000, polling et réveil natif\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--debounce-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>75\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0..5000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--audit-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>300000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1000..3600000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--duration-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>illimité, sinon 100..86400000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-updates\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>illimité, sinon 1..1000000 succès\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-paths\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4096\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1..65536 hints uniques\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Durée contrôlée entre observations, sans timeout de syscall ; erreurs hors quota succès. À expiration :1 si dernier état indisponible, sinon 0. Interruption selon le CLI ordinaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Windows : tampon 64 Kio aligné DWORD et copie bornée. Linux : lecture 64 Kio,65536 enregistrements,128 niveaux,16 Mio de noms, avec quotas OS potentiellement inférieurs. Hints natifs et lot CLI :1 Mio de noms chacun. Sets/maps/paths/snapshots/rapports/queues noyau/allocations s’ajoutent ; aucun budget total RAM. Un consommateur bloqué peut faire déborder les événements et imposer un scan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Observation ciblée C++ :65536 hints/1 Mio ; noms de dossiers/ancêtres et ignore bytes 16 Mio par portée, noms sélectionnés 16 Mio conservateurs. Dépassement : scan complet. Scan complet et union retenue/remplacement appliquent la borne de métadonnées v1, sans plafond RAM/syscalls global.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-api-and-validation\">API C++ et validation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/c-api-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++ et validation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>API à appelant unique ; un autre thread peut demander l’annulation. \u003ccode>invalidate()\u003c/code> force le prochain scan immédiatement sans jeter les notifications. \u003ccode>wait\u003c/code> accepte 0..60000 ms,0 immédiat ; attente native/polling vérifie l’annulation au plus toutes les 50 ms hors latence FS/syscall. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions C/Go\u003c/a> exposent observation et lots, sans journal CLI.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>WorkspaceObservation(project,store_path)\u003c/code> fournit \u003ccode>status(store,revision,hints,operation)\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>workspace_status(store,branch,hints,operation)\u003c/code>. Armer d’abord le watcher puis transmettre chaque lot sans trou ; nouvelle observation, invalidate, erreur ou audit abandonnent les hypothèses incrémentales. Le polling peut utiliser le stat-cache entre audits d’octets. Fermer/réouvrir le Store ne perd pas l’inventaire, ni collecter les objets de cache non retenus. Les mutations revalident indépendamment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/watcher.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>WorkspaceWatcher \u003cspan class=\"token function\">watcher\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store_path\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> hints \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> watcher\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">wait\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>chrono\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">milliseconds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">500\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>hints\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>full_scan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">    \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">watcher\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">invalidate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// The caller lost its own observation state.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"synthetic-performance-measurement\">Limites d’observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-watch/synthetic-performance-measurement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites d’observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Quand la continuité est préservée, fichiers/sous-arbres utilisent l’observation ciblée. Linux étend les créations/imports ; suppressions/mouvements de dossiers déjà suivis reconstruisent l’instance et imposent une lecture complète. La validation réelle Linux/macOS reste nécessaire. Historique rejouable, causalité, tombstones, ordonnanceur de transfert, packaging service OS et interopérabilité daemon/dossier Syncthing restent à développer. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance\">ressources\u003c/a> et les références officielles \u003ca href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Windows\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">inotify\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Observation résidente du workspace # native watch observe l’état courant : notifications récursives Windows, inotify récursif Linux, polling ailleurs ou avec --polling , en cas d’indisponibilité/limite native. Une ligne JSON compacte est émise puis flushée par frame. Clés/types/raisons restent anglais ; erreurs/progression stderr sont bilingues. Sans durée/limite d’updates, arrêt par interruption. Il observe sans commit, modification de fichiers, publication, transfert, verrou d’asset ni résolution de concurrence. C’est une fondation pour un futur agent résident. Les sessions C/Go exposent watcher/inventaire avec lots retenus et Store appartenant à l’appelant, sans journal CLI. text Copier syrnck native watch syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10 syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000 syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048 syrnck native watch --journal --combined syrnck native watch-journal syrnck native watch-journal --clear Cycle des événements et états # Le watcher est armé avant la première requête. Le démarrage efface le cache de hash consultatif et lit tous les fichiers sélectionnés, couvrant les edits hors ligne. Les notifications sont coalescées sur un intervalle fixe borné puis remplacent les sous-arbres concernés de l’inventaire complet en mémoire. Les règles ignore ordinaires s’appliquent. Les fichiers concernés sont intégralement lus même à taille/dates identiques ; les autres sous-arbres ne sont ni énumérés ni lus. Les observations ciblées ne réécrivent pas le cache persisté. Créations, suppressions, renommages et transitions remplacent leurs préfixes complets. Un ancêtre absent/ignoré/fichier élargit la portée pour retirer les entrées périmées ; asset et assets sont distincts. Les hints imbriqués fusionnent. Un .syrnckignore imbriqué resélectionne son sous-arbre, avec règles enfants ; celui de racine impose le scan complet. Collisions portables fichier/dossier/casse et borne de métadonnées v1 sont contrôlées sur l’union retenue+nouvelle avant hash. Sélection ambiguë, portée protégée ou budget dépassé impose un scan complet ; une erreur persistante reste une erreur. Liens/reparse pour ignore files sont refusés ; un pattern invalide donne une erreur de scan récupérable. Les noms d’événements sont des indices d’état courant, pas un log transactionnel ni une paire de renommage garantie. --combined sépare index et travail, index périmé inclus. Branche/révision suivent status ; combined refuse une révision explicite. L’attente native ne parcourt ni dossiers ni Store. Le Store est ouvert seulement pendant la requête puis fermé avant sortie/attente/coalescence pour laisser les autres commandes prendre le verrou. Cache/objets/.git sont exclus de la boucle ; les vrais refs.json / config.json restent visibles. Windows vérifie l’identité des alias de Store ; Vault distinct de vault sur FS sensible à la casse reste observé. Seuls un état sémantique changé, un scan complet terminé ou une nouvelle erreur émettent. Compteurs de hash/réutilisation et générations seuls restent silencieux. Le polling énumère à chaque intervalle mais n’émet pas les résultats identiques. Le champ status reprend les rapports ordinaires/combinés. observation donne mode: full|indexed|targeted , full_scan , inspected_files , retained_files ; les ciblés ajoutent scanned_scopes triés après coalescence/ancêtres. Une suppression peut lire zéro fichier/octet. Seuls les scans énumérés incluent les métriques working_index ; l’inventaire de session reste complet même pour les fichiers non cachables. Le fallback marque aussi la frame full_scan . Comparer les maps et lire les métadonnées reste proportionnel, sans status constant. sequence croît dans le processus, erreurs comprises. Backends : windows , inotify , polling . Raisons : initial première réussite ; filesystem notification avec changement ; polling changement observé ; audit lecture complète périodique ; rescan topologie/enregistrement ; overflow perte/budget ; recovered scan complet après indisponibilité. json Copier { \"version\" : 1 , \"sequence\" : 1 , \"type\" : \"status\" , \"reason\" : \"initial\" , \"backend\" : \"windows\" , \"full_scan\" : true , \"overflow\" : false , \"paths\" : [ ] , \"status\" : { \"version\" : 1 , \"base\" : null , \"clean\" : true , \"tracked_files\" : 0 , \"workspace_files\" : 0 , \"hashed_bytes\" : 0 , \"counts\" : { \"untracked\" : 0 , \"removed\" : 0 , \"modified\" : 0 , \"unchanged\" : 0 } , \"changes\" : [ ] , \"working_index\" : { \"version\" : 1 , \"mode\" : \"stat-v1\" , \"previous\" : \"missing\" , \"hashed_files\" : 0 , \"reused_files\" : 0 , \"supported_files\" : 0 , \"cached_files\" : 0 , \"truncated\" : false , \"object\" : \"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\" } } } Pertes d’événements, audits et indisponibilité # Overflow, enregistrement incomplet/malformé, budget de hints ou panne native abandonnent les listes partielles et exigent un scan complet sans cache. Noms non UTF-8 : hints abandonnés, scan complet, affichage avec remplacement des octets invalides pour garder un JSON valide. Linux arme les nouveaux dossiers/imports avant d’énumérer leurs enfants sur la même instance inotify, puis observe leur sous-arbre entier ; les écritures ultérieures restent en queue. Enregistrements existants conservés, additions coalescées et budgets persistants appliqués. Suppression, sortie, self-move, watch ignoré ou démontage reconstruisent l’instance et imposent un scan complet ; aucun renommage des chemins par cookie. Descripteurs inconnus/alias, trames invalides, overflow et extension ratée/dépassée exigent récupération. Instance partielle fermée ; une annulation invalide la continuité. Une indisponibilité/quota peut revenir au polling. Les audits complets périodiques ont un calendrier indépendant des notifications/polling : défaut cinq minutes, réglable. Ils couvrent notamment FS distants, notifications manquées et écritures mappées. Store occupé, checkout actif ou scan raté : frame type: error , code: scan_failed , message traduit, status: null . L’ancien succès n’est pas présenté comme courant. Erreurs consécutives identiques supprimées ; reprise au moins une seconde plus tard sans verrou conservé. Le retour au succès impose un scan complet. Un changement permanent d’identité de racine demande un redémarrage ; un handle de dossier retient cette identité sans interdire les edits ni verrouiller le Store. L’observation ne fournit ni snapshot atomique/verrouillé, ni versions intermédiaires, ni autorisation de sauter les validations des mutations. Journal de checkpoint optionnel # --journal écrit le lot coalescé avant traitement, puis observed avant sortie réussie ou unavailable après erreur si stockage accessible. C’est un enregistrement borné remplacé, pas un historique rejouable : digests workspace/sélection, session aléatoire, génération, ancienne session/état, backend, hints bornés, digest sémantique, base, propreté et nombre de fichiers, sans contenu ni rapport complet. Les frames ajoutent journal: {session,generation,durable,previous} . durable: true signifie sauvegarde avant stdout, sans accusé du consommateur. sequence reste local ; un arrêt entre publication et livraison exige de rétablir l’état à la reconnexion. Un Store indisponible peut donner durable: false . Un journal corrompu est préservé et bloque ce mode jusqu’à effacement explicite ; le mode ordinaire reste utilisable. native watch-journal lit le dernier record ou state: absent . observed décrit une ancienne requête, pas un watcher vivant ni un état encore courant. pending peut subsister après arrêt. Tout démarrage crée une nouvelle session et lit tous les octets, même après un checkpoint valide ; le premier record conserve l’ancienne session/état. --clear retire explicitement le journal local, même malformé dans la borne de taille, sans modifier objets/références/index/workspace. Le mode journal détient watch.lock , interdisant second propriétaire/clear concurrent. Le Store reste libre entre requêtes et le checkpoint consultable ; sans journal, pas de verrou de session. Fichier autonome : SYW1 , digest de chunk sur 64 octets et enveloppe Store compressée, chiffrée par la clé AEAD existante si nécessaire, noms inclus. Digest et mode restent visibles. Bornes : clair 2 Mio, fichier 3 Mio, 65536 chemins, budget de noms 1 Mio. Écriture temporaire, flush et remplacement atomique durable, publication après callbacks. Aucun objet/racine temporaire ; GC/compact ne l’effacent pas, backups logiques l’omettent. Attente native et polling inchangé n’écrivent rien. Un changement polling est checkpointé avant sortie ; audit écrit pending puis observed. Versions causales, outbox et publication réseau exactly-once restent distinctes. Limites et ressources # Racines workspace/Store doivent exister, être lisibles et identifiables. Option Défaut Valeurs --interval-ms 500 20..60000, polling et réveil natif --debounce-ms 75 0..5000 --audit-ms 300000 1000..3600000 --duration-ms 0 illimité, sinon 100..86400000 --max-updates 0 illimité, sinon 1..1000000 succès --max-paths 4096 1..65536 hints uniques Durée contrôlée entre observations, sans timeout de syscall ; erreurs hors quota succès. À expiration :1 si dernier état indisponible, sinon 0. Interruption selon le CLI ordinaire. Windows : tampon 64 Kio aligné DWORD et copie bornée. Linux : lecture 64 Kio,65536 enregistrements,128 niveaux,16 Mio de noms, avec quotas OS potentiellement inférieurs. Hints natifs et lot CLI :1 Mio de noms chacun. Sets/maps/paths/snapshots/rapports/queues noyau/allocations s’ajoutent ; aucun budget total RAM. Un consommateur bloqué peut faire déborder les événements et imposer un scan. Observation ciblée C++ :65536 hints/1 Mio ; noms de dossiers/ancêtres et ignore bytes 16 Mio par portée, noms sélectionnés 16 Mio conservateurs. Dépassement : scan complet. Scan complet et union retenue/remplacement appliquent la borne de métadonnées v1, sans plafond RAM/syscalls global. API C++ et validation # API à appelant unique ; un autre thread peut demander l’annulation. invalidate() force le prochain scan immédiatement sans jeter les notifications. wait accepte 0..60000 ms,0 immédiat ; attente native/polling vérifie l’annulation au plus toutes les 50 ms hors latence FS/syscall. Les sessions C/Go exposent observation et lots, sans journal CLI. WorkspaceObservation(project,store_path) fournit status(store,revision,hints,operation) et workspace_status(store,branch,hints,operation) . Armer d’abord le watcher puis transmettre chaque lot sans trou ; nouvelle observation, invalidate, erreur ou audit abandonnent les hypothèses incrémentales. Le polling peut utiliser le stat-cache entre audits d’octets. Fermer/réouvrir le Store ne perd pas l’inventaire, ni collecter les objets de cache non retenus. Les mutations revalident indépendamment. cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp> syrnck :: WorkspaceWatcher watcher ( project , store_path , { } , stop ) ; // Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction. auto hints = watcher . wait ( std :: chrono :: milliseconds ( 500 ) , stop ) ; if ( hints . full_scan ) { // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints. } watcher . invalidate ( ) ; // The caller lost its own observation state. Limites d’observation # Quand la continuité est préservée, fichiers/sous-arbres utilisent l’observation ciblée. Linux étend les créations/imports ; suppressions/mouvements de dossiers déjà suivis reconstruisent l’instance et imposent une lecture complète. La validation réelle Linux/macOS reste nécessaire. Historique rejouable, causalité, tombstones, ordonnanceur de transfert, packaging service OS et interopérabilité daemon/dossier Syncthing restent à développer. Voir les ressources et les références officielles Windows et inotify . syrnck native watch syrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10 syrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000 syrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048 syrnck native watch --journal --combined syrnck native watch-journal syrnck native watch-journal --clear {\"version\":1,\"sequence\":1,\"type\":\"status\",\"reason\":\"initial\",\"backend\":\"windows\",\"full_scan\":true,\"overflow\":false,\"paths\":[],\"status\":{\"version\":1,\"base\":null,\"clean\":true,\"tracked_files\":0,\"workspace_files\":0,\"hashed_bytes\":0,\"counts\":{\"untracked\":0,\"removed\":0,\"modified\":0,\"unchanged\":0},\"changes\":[],\"working_index\":{\"version\":1,\"mode\":\"stat-v1\",\"previous\":\"missing\",\"hashed_files\":0,\"reused_files\":0,\"supported_files\":0,\"cached_files\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"object\":\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"}}} #include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp> syrnck::WorkspaceWatcher watcher(project, store_path, {}, stop); // Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction. auto hints = watcher.wait(std::chrono::milliseconds(500), stop); if (hints.full_scan) { // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints. } watcher.invalidate(); // The caller lost its own observation state.","headings":[{"id":"resident-native-workspace-observation","text":"Observation résidente du workspace","level":1},{"id":"status-and-event-lifecycle","text":"Cycle des événements et états","level":2},{"id":"lost-events-audits-and-unavailable-observations","text":"Pertes d’événements, audits et indisponibilité","level":2},{"id":"optional-persistent-checkpoint-journal","text":"Journal de checkpoint optionnel","level":2},{"id":"limits-and-resource-use","text":"Limites et ressources","level":2},{"id":"c-api-and-validation","text":"API C++ et validation","level":2},{"id":"synthetic-performance-measurement","text":"Limites d’observation","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native watch\nsyrnck native watch --combined --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native watch --directory PROJECT --max-updates 10\nsyrnck native watch --polling --interval-ms 1000 --duration-ms 10000\nsyrnck native watch --audit-ms 60000 --debounce-ms 150 --max-paths 2048\nsyrnck native watch --journal --combined\nsyrnck native watch-journal\nsyrnck native watch-journal --clear\n{\"version\":1,\"sequence\":1,\"type\":\"status\",\"reason\":\"initial\",\"backend\":\"windows\",\"full_scan\":true,\"overflow\":false,\"paths\":[],\"status\":{\"version\":1,\"base\":null,\"clean\":true,\"tracked_files\":0,\"workspace_files\":0,\"hashed_bytes\":0,\"counts\":{\"untracked\":0,\"removed\":0,\"modified\":0,\"unchanged\":0},\"changes\":[],\"working_index\":{\"version\":1,\"mode\":\"stat-v1\",\"previous\":\"missing\",\"hashed_files\":0,\"reused_files\":0,\"supported_files\":0,\"cached_files\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"object\":\"INDEX_OBJECT_ID\"}}}\n#include \u003csyrnck/watcher.hpp>\nsyrnck::WorkspaceWatcher watcher(project, store_path, {}, stop);\n// Perform the caller's initial complete observation after construction.\nauto hints = watcher.wait(std::chrono::milliseconds(500), stop);\nif (hints.full_scan) {\n    // Reestablish a complete observation before trusting incremental hints.\n}\nwatcher.invalidate(); // The caller lost its own observation state.","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/workspace-abi","#/fr/performance","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw","https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inotify.7.html"],"kind":"guide","revision":"1d714a4a332d"},{"id":"native-workspace-archives","title":"Archiver et restaurer un workspace","description":"Désenregistrer un workspace lié en conservant les fichiers modifiés, la préparation et l’historique.","section":"Gestion de versions","order":223,"parent":"native-workspaces","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"unregister\">Désenregistrer un workspace lié\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives/unregister\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Désenregistrer un workspace lié\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-archives --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Reprendre le champ \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> de l’aperçu, avec le même choix pour\n\u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code>. Ces commandes sélectionnent elles-mêmes le contexte lié\nnommé. Le workspace principal ne peut pas être désenregistré et les checkouts\nen attente doivent d’abord être récupérés. L’aperçu lit les fichiers et références\nsans publier d’objets ni de métadonnées. Une modification du contenu capturé,\nde la sélection ou des références du contexte invalide le plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application capture l’état physique et conserve une archive canonique avant\nde retirer l’inscription et toutes les références \u003ccode>workspaces/NOM/\u003c/code> dans une seule\ntransaction. Le dossier original et tous ses fichiers restent en place ; la\nréservation de branche est libérée. Les branches partagées et HEAD principal\nrestent inchangés. Le handle C++ revient au contexte principal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contents\">Contenu conservé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives/contents\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Contenu conservé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’archive conserve trois états indépendants :\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>HEAD commité complet, y compris les fichiers hors sélection partielle.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>L’index figé complet, s’il existe, avec son parent original même s’il est périmé.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Une révision de récupération physique contenant les fichiers suivis sélectionnés,\nleurs modifications locales et les fichiers ordinaires faisant obstacle à\nl’arbre logique. Les suppressions restent absentes de ce snapshot physique.\n\u003ccode>--include-untracked\u003c/code> ajoute les fichiers ordinaires sélectionnés, non ignorés,\ntrouvés par le parcours natif.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Chaque référence originale du contexte possède une racine d’archive typée\nexplicite, notamment les index inactifs et références de récupération ou\nd’historique terminées. GC, packs et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/backups\">sauvegardes\u003c/a> conservent leurs\nfermetures d’objets. Les fichiers locaux ignorés ou hors sélection restent dans\nle dossier original ; leur présence dans le snapshot n’est pas garantie. Les\ndossiers vides ne sont pas versionnés. Des chemins dangereux, liens ou métadonnées\nprotégées peuvent bloquer le plan ; inspecter \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> et les obstacles signalés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’inventaire accepte \u003ccode>--after ID\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--limit N\u003c/code> (1..1000, 100 par défaut) et\n\u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code>. Les identifiants stables sont ceux des objets d’archive ; la pagination\nles trie par identifiant. \u003ccode>workspace-archive\u003c/code> affiche la recette des références\nsauvegardées et la révision physique.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore\">Restaurer dans un nouveau dossier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives/restore\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Restaurer dans un nouveau dossier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Exécuter la restauration depuis le contexte principal. Le parent du dossier\ncible doit exister ; la cible ne doit chevaucher ni le principal, ni le Store,\nni un autre workspace inscrit. Un dossier existant n’est réutilisé que si ses\nfichiers ordinaires et modes correspondent à tout le snapshot physique. Les\nfichiers ajoutés ou modifiés et les liens sont refusés. Des dossiers vides\nsupplémentaires restent permis dans un export exact.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La sélection originale, nommée ou détachée, est utilisée par défaut.\n\u003ccode>--branch NOUVEAU_NOM\u003c/code> restaure sous une autre branche ; \u003ccode>--detach\u003c/code> crée un HEAD\nprivé. Une branche nommée doit être absente ou encore pointer sur HEAD sauvegardé,\nsans réservation concurrente. Une branche avancée n’est jamais réinitialisée.\nHEAD garde la révision commitée, le dossier contient les octets modifiés capturés\net l’index préparé reste distinct. Changer son sélecteur réécrit uniquement le\nmessage de l’index, sans rebaser implicitement un index périmé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’export physique est publié avant l’inscription. Après interruption, relancer\nla même commande avec le même plan : un export terminé exact est réutilisé et\nune inscription terminée exacte est idempotente. Les fichiers ou liaisons\nmodifiés sont refusés. La sélection partielle est restaurée, mais les fichiers\nancêtres faisant obstacle sont restitués intégralement. Les anciennes références\ninactives restent accessibles dans l’archive sans être réactivées sous le nouveau\nnom. Le nouveau contexte conserve aussi sa propre racine de récupération physique.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Sauvegardes et retrait des archives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives/retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sauvegardes et retrait des archives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le retrait exige une sauvegarde vérifiée contenant le groupe exact complet.\nUn membre \u003ccode>/record\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>/roots/...\u003c/code> ne peut pas être retiré seul.\nLe retrait supprime des racines, sans effacer les fichiers de travail ni lancer\nle GC. Un contexte restauré garde sa racine de récupération physique après le\nretrait de l’archive source. HEAD et l’index sélectionnés restent des racines\nordinaires ; les anciennes références inactives dépendent ensuite de la\nsauvegarde vérifiée ou d’autres racines restantes. Voir le\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait des archives\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour récupérer après GC local, restaurer cette sauvegarde dans un Store séparé,\npuis y exécuter \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore-plan\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>workspace-archive-restore\u003c/code>\nen indiquant le dossier principal récupéré avec \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>. Les liaisons\nabsolues historiques sont des recettes, sans inscription active ; le dossier\noriginal n’a pas besoin d’exister.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">API C++ et limites actuelles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives/api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++ et limites actuelles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_unregistration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> archived \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">unregister_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> id \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> archived\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"archive\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L8\" data-line=\"8\">settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>detached \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L9\" data-line=\"9\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> restore \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_archive_restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L10\" data-line=\"10\">    store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L11\" data-line=\"11\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">restore_native_workspace_archive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"recovered\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L12\" data-line=\"12\">    destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> restore\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> settings\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le header expose aussi l’inspection et l’inventaire paginé. Les opérations\nacceptent annulation et progression. Fichiers et références sont revérifiés\naprès le dernier callback de publication, sans nouvel observateur pendant la\npublication finale. Les écritures externes exigent toujours une coordination ;\nil ne s’agit pas d’un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers. Les tests\nd’interruption de processus ne démontrent pas la résistance aux coupures\nd’alimentation. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation\">déplacement lié sur un même système de fichiers\u003c/a>\nest disponible ; déplacements entre systèmes et politiques automatiques par âge/release restent à développer. Les Stores chiffrés protègent les objets\narchivés au repos ; les exports et leurs fichiers temporaires ont la sémantique\nhabituelle d’un workspace en clair.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Désenregistrer un workspace lié # text Copier syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native workspace-archives --verify syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify Reprendre le champ plan de l’aperçu, avec le même choix pour --include-untracked . Ces commandes sélectionnent elles-mêmes le contexte lié nommé. Le workspace principal ne peut pas être désenregistré et les checkouts en attente doivent d’abord être récupérés. L’aperçu lit les fichiers et références sans publier d’objets ni de métadonnées. Une modification du contenu capturé, de la sélection ou des références du contexte invalide le plan. L’application capture l’état physique et conserve une archive canonique avant de retirer l’inscription et toutes les références workspaces/NOM/ dans une seule transaction. Le dossier original et tous ses fichiers restent en place ; la réservation de branche est libérée. Les branches partagées et HEAD principal restent inchangés. Le handle C++ revient au contexte principal. Contenu conservé # L’archive conserve trois états indépendants : HEAD commité complet, y compris les fichiers hors sélection partielle. L’index figé complet, s’il existe, avec son parent original même s’il est périmé. Une révision de récupération physique contenant les fichiers suivis sélectionnés, leurs modifications locales et les fichiers ordinaires faisant obstacle à l’arbre logique. Les suppressions restent absentes de ce snapshot physique. --include-untracked ajoute les fichiers ordinaires sélectionnés, non ignorés, trouvés par le parcours natif. Chaque référence originale du contexte possède une racine d’archive typée explicite, notamment les index inactifs et références de récupération ou d’historique terminées. GC, packs et sauvegardes conservent leurs fermetures d’objets. Les fichiers locaux ignorés ou hors sélection restent dans le dossier original ; leur présence dans le snapshot n’est pas garantie. Les dossiers vides ne sont pas versionnés. Des chemins dangereux, liens ou métadonnées protégées peuvent bloquer le plan ; inspecter ready et les obstacles signalés. L’inventaire accepte --after ID , --limit N (1..1000, 100 par défaut) et --verify . Les identifiants stables sont ceux des objets d’archive ; la pagination les trie par identifiant. workspace-archive affiche la recette des références sauvegardées et la révision physique. Restaurer dans un nouveau dossier # text Copier syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined Exécuter la restauration depuis le contexte principal. Le parent du dossier cible doit exister ; la cible ne doit chevaucher ni le principal, ni le Store, ni un autre workspace inscrit. Un dossier existant n’est réutilisé que si ses fichiers ordinaires et modes correspondent à tout le snapshot physique. Les fichiers ajoutés ou modifiés et les liens sont refusés. Des dossiers vides supplémentaires restent permis dans un export exact. La sélection originale, nommée ou détachée, est utilisée par défaut. --branch NOUVEAU_NOM restaure sous une autre branche ; --detach crée un HEAD privé. Une branche nommée doit être absente ou encore pointer sur HEAD sauvegardé, sans réservation concurrente. Une branche avancée n’est jamais réinitialisée. HEAD garde la révision commitée, le dossier contient les octets modifiés capturés et l’index préparé reste distinct. Changer son sélecteur réécrit uniquement le message de l’index, sans rebaser implicitement un index périmé. L’export physique est publié avant l’inscription. Après interruption, relancer la même commande avec le même plan : un export terminé exact est réutilisé et une inscription terminée exacte est idempotente. Les fichiers ou liaisons modifiés sont refusés. La sélection partielle est restaurée, mais les fichiers ancêtres faisant obstacle sont restitués intégralement. Les anciennes références inactives restent accessibles dans l’archive sans être réactivées sous le nouveau nom. Le nouveau contexte conserve aussi sa propre racine de récupération physique. Sauvegardes et retrait des archives # text Copier syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN Le retrait exige une sauvegarde vérifiée contenant le groupe exact complet. Un membre /record , /head , /work ou /roots/... ne peut pas être retiré seul. Le retrait supprime des racines, sans effacer les fichiers de travail ni lancer le GC. Un contexte restauré garde sa racine de récupération physique après le retrait de l’archive source. HEAD et l’index sélectionnés restent des racines ordinaires ; les anciennes références inactives dépendent ensuite de la sauvegarde vérifiée ou d’autres racines restantes. Voir le retrait des archives . Pour récupérer après GC local, restaurer cette sauvegarde dans un Store séparé, puis y exécuter workspace-archive-restore-plan et workspace-archive-restore en indiquant le dossier principal récupéré avec --directory . Les liaisons absolues historiques sont des recettes, sans inscription active ; le dossier original n’a pas besoin d’exister. API C++ et limites actuelles # cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store . use_native_workspace ( \"edit\" ) ; auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_unregistration ( store , true , true ) ; auto archived = syrnck :: unregister_native_workspace ( store , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , true ) ; auto id = archived [ \"archive\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) ; syrnck :: NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings ; settings . detached = true ; auto restore = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_archive_restore ( store , primary , id , \"recovered\" , destination , settings , true ) ; syrnck :: restore_native_workspace_archive ( store , primary , id , \"recovered\" , destination , restore [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , settings ) ; Le header expose aussi l’inspection et l’inventaire paginé. Les opérations acceptent annulation et progression. Fichiers et références sont revérifiés après le dernier callback de publication, sans nouvel observateur pendant la publication finale. Les écritures externes exigent toujours une coordination ; il ne s’agit pas d’un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers. Les tests d’interruption de processus ne démontrent pas la résistance aux coupures d’alimentation. Le déplacement lié sur un même système de fichiers est disponible ; déplacements entre systèmes et politiques automatiques par âge/release restent à développer. Les Stores chiffrés protègent les objets archivés au repos ; les exports et leurs fichiers temporaires ont la sémantique habituelle d’un workspace en clair. syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify syrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native workspace-archives --verify syrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify syrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined syrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive syrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN #include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store.use_native_workspace(\"edit\"); auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_unregistration(store, true, true); auto archived = syrnck::unregister_native_workspace( store, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true); auto id = archived[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>(); syrnck::NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings; settings.detached = true; auto restore = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_archive_restore( store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, settings, true); syrnck::restore_native_workspace_archive(store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, restore[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), settings);","headings":[{"id":"unregister","text":"Désenregistrer un workspace lié","level":2},{"id":"contents","text":"Contenu conservé","level":2},{"id":"restore","text":"Restaurer dans un nouveau dossier","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Sauvegardes et retrait des archives","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"API C++ et limites actuelles","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5","code-4-L6","code-4-L7","code-4-L8","code-4-L9","code-4-L10","code-4-L11","code-4-L12"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-unregister-plan edit --include-untracked --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-unregister edit --include-untracked --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native workspace-archives --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive ARCHIVE_ID --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive-restore-plan ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --verify\nsyrnck native workspace-archive-restore ARCHIVE_ID recovered ../recovered --detach --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace recovered native status --combined\nsyrnck backup create /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\nsyrnck backup retire-plan archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive\nsyrnck backup retire archive/workspaces/ARCHIVE_ID --from /existing-backup-parent/with-workspace-archive --expect-plan RETIRE_PLAN\n#include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp>\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"edit\");\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_unregistration(store, true, true);\nauto archived = syrnck::unregister_native_workspace(\n    store, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), true);\nauto id = archived[\"archive\"].get\u003cstd::string>();\nsyrnck::NativeWorkspaceRestoreOptions settings;\nsettings.detached = true;\nauto restore = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_archive_restore(\n    store, primary, id, \"recovered\", destination, settings, true);\nsyrnck::restore_native_workspace_archive(store, primary, id, \"recovered\",\n    destination, restore[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), settings);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/backups","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-workspace-relocation"],"kind":"guide","revision":"a76dd98bba12"},{"id":"native-workspace-relocation","title":"Déplacer un workspace lié","description":"Déplacement de dossier récupérable, changements de casse et données locales non versionnées compris.","section":"Gestion de versions","order":224,"parent":"native-workspaces","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Aperçu et déplacement\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Aperçu et déplacement\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Utiliser le contexte principal et le champ \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> exact de l’aperçu. La source\ndoit être un workspace lié inscrit. Le parent cible doit exister sur le même\nsystème de fichiers ; la cible doit être absente. Un alias de casse de la source\nreste permis, par exemple \u003ccode>project-edit\u003c/code> vers \u003ccode>Project-Edit\u003c/code>. Une variante\noccupée indépendamment est refusée sur un système sensible à la casse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’opération déplace le dossier entier : fichiers de travail modifiés ou préparés,\nfichiers ignorés ou hors sélection, dossiers vides et liens descendants. Elle ne\nparcourt pas les liens, ne reconstruit pas les fichiers depuis HEAD et ne réingère\npas le travail. HEAD logique, index, sélection partielle et branches partagées\nrestent inchangés. Seuls les dossiers de l’inscription et de HEAD lié changent.\nLe dossier principal et le Store restent en place.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’aperçu lit identités et métadonnées sans écrire d’objets ni changer les fichiers.\nSon identité lie toutes les références, les anciennes liaisons, l’identité du\ndossier source et celles des deux parents. Elle ne fige pas le contenu des\nfichiers : les modifications dans ce même dossier sont déplacées avec lui.\nCoordonner éditeurs, observateurs et autres outils pendant l’opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Reprendre ou annuler un déplacement en attente\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reprendre ou annuler un déplacement en attente\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Utiliser le contexte principal pour la récupération, même si l’ancien dossier\nlié est temporairement absent. Une transaction indique la position physique :\n\u003ccode>source\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>destination\u003c/code>. La reprise atteint la destination ; abort\nramène le même dossier au chemin original avant de conserver les liaisons\noriginales. Aucune de ces opérations ne supprime récursivement les données.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le journal est conservé avant tout déplacement. Chaque opération passe par un\ndossier frère unique \u003ccode>.syrnck-relocate-NONCE\u003c/code>, changements de casse compris.\nLes renommages exclusifs refusent une cible occupée. Inscription et HEAD sont\npubliés ensemble après vérification de l’identité physique à destination.\nLa reprise inspecte les identités réelles sans présumer qu’un renommage est\nterminé. Ne pas supprimer ni réutiliser le dossier intermédiaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Relancer \u003ccode>workspace-relocate\u003c/code> avec les mêmes nom, destination et plan reprend\nle déplacement correspondant. Une opération déjà terminée renvoie son reçu si\nles liaisons et l’identité cible correspondent encore. Une liaison modifiée ou\nun dossier remplacé exigent une inspection, sans adoption implicite. Sans\ntransaction active, statut et récupération renvoient\n\u003ccode>pending: false, state: none\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety\">Sécurité et périmètre\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/safety\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sécurité et périmètre\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La capacité \u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code> est activée avant le journal ; les anciens\nlecteurs refusent le Store. Pendant le déplacement, autres changements de\nréférences, mutations natives, GC et création de sauvegarde sont bloqués jusqu’à\nrécupération. Remplacement du dossier ou des parents, liens/reparse points à la\nracine, identités ambiguës, chevauchements et chemins étrangers occupés sont\nrefusés. Une édition directe des références ne peut pas contourner le protocole\npour relier une inscription active à un autre dossier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les identités utilisent volume/file ID Windows ou device/inode POSIX. Un\nfournisseur non pris en charge entraîne un refus prudent. Les derniers callbacks\nprécèdent une nouvelle vérification d’identité et de références ; la publication\nfinale n’appelle plus d’observateur. Les écritures Syrnck sont coordonnées, mais\nles autres écritures du système de fichiers demandent une coordination. Les\ntests d’interruption ne démontrent ni la résistance aux coupures d’alimentation\nni la prise en charge de tous les systèmes réseau.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces commandes ne déplacent pas entre systèmes de fichiers, ni le workspace\nprincipal ou le Store. Les reçus terminés restent conservés, sans nettoyage\nautomatique par âge. Une sauvegarde du Store conserve objets logiques et reçus ;\nles octets locaux non préparés nécessitent toujours une\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives\">archive de workspace\u003c/a> ou une sauvegarde des\nfichiers. Déplacer un dossier ne chiffre pas ses fichiers de travail en clair.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">API C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_native_workspace_relocation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">relocate_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> destination\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">    plan\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"plan\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic-function\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token generic class-name\">\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>string\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> pending \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_workspace_relocation_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">recover_native_workspace_relocation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Passer \u003ccode>true\u003c/code> pour annuler. Ces opérations sont C++ ; interfaces C et Go gardent\nleur ABI actuelle. Progression et annulation peuvent laisser un déplacement en\nattente ; son journal conservé est nécessaire pour reprendre ou annuler ensuite.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"format\">Format du journal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/format\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Format du journal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-relocations/active\u003c/code> conserve un chunk canonique native-workspace-relocation\nv1 à quatorze champs : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nonce\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>destination\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>identity\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source_parent\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>destination_parent\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>registration\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>refs_hash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code>. Les liaisons sont incluses dans le\ndescripteur, sans dépendances d’objets cachées. Les racines du contexte restent\nen place pendant l’attente. Le validateur central des références n’accepte que\nles transitions exactes de début et de fin. La finalisation retire la racine\nactive et ajoute \u003ccode>archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>/aborted\u003c/code>,\nconservant le reçu. Versions d’objets, révisions, packs et ABI C inchangées.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Aperçu et déplacement # text Copier syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head Utiliser le contexte principal et le champ plan exact de l’aperçu. La source doit être un workspace lié inscrit. Le parent cible doit exister sur le même système de fichiers ; la cible doit être absente. Un alias de casse de la source reste permis, par exemple project-edit vers Project-Edit . Une variante occupée indépendamment est refusée sur un système sensible à la casse. L’opération déplace le dossier entier : fichiers de travail modifiés ou préparés, fichiers ignorés ou hors sélection, dossiers vides et liens descendants. Elle ne parcourt pas les liens, ne reconstruit pas les fichiers depuis HEAD et ne réingère pas le travail. HEAD logique, index, sélection partielle et branches partagées restent inchangés. Seuls les dossiers de l’inscription et de HEAD lié changent. Le dossier principal et le Store restent en place. L’aperçu lit identités et métadonnées sans écrire d’objets ni changer les fichiers. Son identité lie toutes les références, les anciennes liaisons, l’identité du dossier source et celles des deux parents. Elle ne fige pas le contenu des fichiers : les modifications dans ce même dossier sont déplacées avec lui. Coordonner éditeurs, observateurs et autres outils pendant l’opération. Reprendre ou annuler un déplacement en attente # text Copier syrnck native workspace-relocation-status syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort Utiliser le contexte principal pour la récupération, même si l’ancien dossier lié est temporairement absent. Une transaction indique la position physique : source , bridge ou destination . La reprise atteint la destination ; abort ramène le même dossier au chemin original avant de conserver les liaisons originales. Aucune de ces opérations ne supprime récursivement les données. Le journal est conservé avant tout déplacement. Chaque opération passe par un dossier frère unique .syrnck-relocate-NONCE , changements de casse compris. Les renommages exclusifs refusent une cible occupée. Inscription et HEAD sont publiés ensemble après vérification de l’identité physique à destination. La reprise inspecte les identités réelles sans présumer qu’un renommage est terminé. Ne pas supprimer ni réutiliser le dossier intermédiaire. Relancer workspace-relocate avec les mêmes nom, destination et plan reprend le déplacement correspondant. Une opération déjà terminée renvoie son reçu si les liaisons et l’identité cible correspondent encore. Une liaison modifiée ou un dossier remplacé exigent une inspection, sans adoption implicite. Sans transaction active, statut et récupération renvoient pending: false, state: none . Sécurité et périmètre # La capacité workspace-relocation-v1 est activée avant le journal ; les anciens lecteurs refusent le Store. Pendant le déplacement, autres changements de références, mutations natives, GC et création de sauvegarde sont bloqués jusqu’à récupération. Remplacement du dossier ou des parents, liens/reparse points à la racine, identités ambiguës, chevauchements et chemins étrangers occupés sont refusés. Une édition directe des références ne peut pas contourner le protocole pour relier une inscription active à un autre dossier. Les identités utilisent volume/file ID Windows ou device/inode POSIX. Un fournisseur non pris en charge entraîne un refus prudent. Les derniers callbacks précèdent une nouvelle vérification d’identité et de références ; la publication finale n’appelle plus d’observateur. Les écritures Syrnck sont coordonnées, mais les autres écritures du système de fichiers demandent une coordination. Les tests d’interruption ne démontrent ni la résistance aux coupures d’alimentation ni la prise en charge de tous les systèmes réseau. Ces commandes ne déplacent pas entre systèmes de fichiers, ni le workspace principal ou le Store. Les reçus terminés restent conservés, sans nettoyage automatique par âge. Une sauvegarde du Store conserve objets logiques et reçus ; les octets locaux non préparés nécessitent toujours une archive de workspace ou une sauvegarde des fichiers. Déplacer un dossier ne chiffre pas ses fichiers de travail en clair. API C++ # cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store . use_native_workspace ( \"\" ) ; auto plan = syrnck :: plan_native_workspace_relocation ( store , \"edit\" , destination ) ; syrnck :: relocate_native_workspace ( store , \"edit\" , destination , plan [ \"plan\" ] . get \u003c std :: string > ( ) , operation ) ; auto pending = syrnck :: native_workspace_relocation_status ( store ) ; syrnck :: recover_native_workspace_relocation ( store , false , operation ) ; Passer true pour annuler. Ces opérations sont C++ ; interfaces C et Go gardent leur ABI actuelle. Progression et annulation peuvent laisser un déplacement en attente ; son journal conservé est nécessaire pour reprendre ou annuler ensuite. Format du journal # workspace-relocations/active conserve un chunk canonique native-workspace-relocation v1 à quatorze champs : version , type , name , nonce , source , destination , bridge , identity , source_parent , destination_parent , registration , head , refs_hash , plan . Les liaisons sont incluses dans le descripteur, sans dépendances d’objets cachées. Les racines du contexte restent en place pendant l’attente. Le validateur central des références n’accepte que les transitions exactes de début et de fin. La finalisation retire la racine active et ajoute archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed ou /aborted , conservant le reçu. Versions d’objets, révisions, packs et ABI C inchangées. syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit syrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck native workspace-relocation-status syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover syrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort #include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> store.use_native_workspace(\"\"); auto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_relocation(store, \"edit\", destination); syrnck::relocate_native_workspace(store, \"edit\", destination, plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation); auto pending = syrnck::native_workspace_relocation_status(store); syrnck::recover_native_workspace_relocation(store, false, operation);","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Aperçu et déplacement","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Reprendre ou annuler un déplacement en attente","level":2},{"id":"safety","text":"Sécurité et périmètre","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"API C++","level":2},{"id":"format","text":"Format du journal","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-relocate-plan edit ../project-edit\nsyrnck native workspace-relocate edit ../project-edit --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-status\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-recover\nsyrnck native workspace-relocation-recover --abort\n#include \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp>\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"\");\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_native_workspace_relocation(store, \"edit\", destination);\nsyrnck::relocate_native_workspace(store, \"edit\", destination,\n    plan[\"plan\"].get\u003cstd::string>(), operation);\nauto pending = syrnck::native_workspace_relocation_status(store);\nsyrnck::recover_native_workspace_relocation(store, false, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-workspace-archives"],"kind":"guide","revision":"1c446acee863"},{"id":"native-workspace","title":"Inspection du workspace","description":"Guide : Inspection du workspace.","section":"Versionnage","order":420,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight\">Inspection du workspace et aperçu de checkout\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace/native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspection du workspace et aperçu de checkout\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le statut combiné branche/index/workspace et le plan de checkout ordinaire sont en lecture seule : aucune modification de fichier, objet, référence ou préparation. Un plan n’exécute rien ; le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">checkout récupérable\u003c/a> prépare les sorties vérifiées, revérifie le workspace et journalise les déplacements avant publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le CLI utilise la branche native courante, main par défaut historique, et le dossier courant. Le Store doit se trouver dans le workspace, strictement sous sa racine pour le checkout. Un HEAD explicite lie les commandes à la racine canonique. Clés JSON, actions et motifs de conflit restent anglais.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"combined-status\">Statut combiné\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace/combined-status\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Statut combiné\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>--indexed active facultativement le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/working-index\">cache persistant de hashes\u003c/a> côté non préparé ; ce mode peut écrire des données jetables. Sans lui, le statut garde ses lectures complètes sans écriture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--combined rend version, branch, clean, staged, unstaged. Staged compare la base enregistrée à l’index figé ; unstaged compare cet index au workspace. Sans index, comparer à la tête ; branche initiale vide, base vide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un chemin peut apparaître des deux côtés : préparer des octets puis remettre le fichier à sa base rend le statut de branche propre, mais conserve un changement préparé et un autre non préparé dans le rapport combiné. Un index périmé reste inspectable sur sa base d’origine ; le workspace se compare toujours à ses octets figés. clean exige deux comparaisons propres et un index frais ; périmé seul suffit à rendre faux. Un rapport sale réussi rend 0. --combined et --revision sont exclusifs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le scan conserve ignores, fichiers ordinaires et hash BLAKE3 complet ; il n’ingère rien et n’audite pas les payloads stockés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"checkout-plan-and-verification\">Plan de checkout et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace/checkout-plan-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Plan de checkout et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan compare heads/BRANCH à une révision cible exacte, inventorie le disque et hashe tous les fichiers suivis de la base. Taille/date ne prouvent jamais l’égalité. La politique clean-tracked-v1 exige tous les suivis propres, même inchangés dans la cible. Tout index explicite bloque, même sans différences préparées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Champs : source/target ; staging_snapshot ; policy/ready ; objects_verified ; executable_metadata_supported (false sur Windows) ; inventory_entries ; hashed_bytes (lecture des suivis, hors audit Store) ; restore_bytes (octets logiques des créations/remplacements) ; counts/actions ; create_directories ; conflicts triés uniques avec path/reason/obstruction. Les transitions de namespace ajoutent namespace_transition et remove_directories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Actions triées par chemin, avec résumés source/cible (manifest, size, file_hash, executable), côté absent null. Ce sont les changements du snapshot proposé même bloqué, pas un programme ordonné de déplacements. create : nouvel entrant ; replace : contenu différent ; remove : sortie absente de cible ; set_executable : mode seul ; reuse : même contenu/mode, autre manifeste ; unchanged : compteur sans ligne. restore_bytes n’est ni trafic réseau ni nombre unique de blocs ; voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-comparison\">réutilisation de manifestes\u003c/a>. Windows conserve les changements de mode souhaités sans en faire un test de propreté locale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Par défaut, seules révisions et métadonnées de manifestes nécessaires sont lues, sans blocs cibles ni parents historiques ; un Store partiel de métadonnées peut donc rendre ready true et objects_verified false sans garantir la reconstruction. --verify audite toute la fermeture cible, parents et hashes compris, même si le disque présente des obstacles. Dépendance invalide/absente : erreur sans plan partiel.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Plan prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : JSON et code 1 ; argument, IO ou métadonnée invalide : diagnostic stderr sans JSON.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits\">Chemins protégés et limites de politique\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace/protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Chemins protégés et limites de politique\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le plan n’applique pas .syrnckignore : les ignorés restent visibles pour empêcher écrasement/collision. Les non-suivis sans rapport sont conservés sans hash ; une cible non suivie bloque même si ses octets correspondent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Motifs : tracked_modified/tracked_missing ; tracked_mode_changed sous Unix ; staging_present/stale_staging ; checkout_pending ; untracked_collision ; file_directory_transition lorsqu’une entrée extérieure à la transition suivie gêne ; path_spelling pour orthographe portable incohérente ; portable_collision pour plusieurs noms physiques équivalents ; symlink_obstruction/special_file ; protected_path pour Store/autre entrée protégée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Symlinks et points de réanalyse/jonctions Windows sont opaques, jamais parcourus volontairement. .git, .syrnck et le Store réel même personnalisé sont protégés et exclus. Changements propres de casse/normalisation et transitions fichier/dossier passent par l’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions\">exécuteur récupérable\u003c/a>. Seuls fichiers appartenant au snapshot et parents connus peuvent être déplacés ; dossiers vides inconnus et descendants ignorés restent protégés. L’orthographe des parents de chaque snapshot doit être cohérente sous casefold. Aucun force, écrasement, reset d’index ou adoption automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-progress-and-resource-boundaries\">API C++, progression et ressources\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspace/c-progress-and-resource-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API C++, progression et ressources\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Callbacks synchrones non réentrants. scanning compte dossiers/entrées ; verifying les octets du workspace ou de fermeture ; planning les métadonnées/actions, avec compteurs par phase. La planification finit par planning complet, jamais publishing. Annulation/exception ne rendent pas de résultat partiel et ne changent rien. Verrou exclusif et règles normales d’ouverture/récupération restent requis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tampon de lecture 1 Mio ; cache FIFO de 1024 résumés de manifeste par chargement de snapshot. Inventaire : budget d’entrées 16 Mio, coût 64 + octets UTF-8 du chemin + chemin normalisé ; profondeur maximale 128. Ce n’est pas la RAM totale : maps, rapports, JSON, allocations et fermetures s’ajoutent. Objets limités à 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’observation ne verrouille pas le workspace et ne constitue pas un snapshot atomique. Changement observé de taille/type/date/mode autour d’une lecture fait échouer ; une modification ultérieure peut périmer le résultat. L’exécuteur doit donc préparer, revérifier et journaliser, même après ready. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions C/Go\u003c/a> exposent l’observation et les statuts ; elles ne fournissent pas toutes les mutations natives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> preview \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> verified \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> target_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Inspection du workspace et aperçu de checkout # Le statut combiné branche/index/workspace et le plan de checkout ordinaire sont en lecture seule : aucune modification de fichier, objet, référence ou préparation. Un plan n’exécute rien ; le checkout récupérable prépare les sorties vérifiées, revérifie le workspace et journalise les déplacements avant publication. Le CLI utilise la branche native courante, main par défaut historique, et le dossier courant. Le Store doit se trouver dans le workspace, strictement sous sa racine pour le checkout. Un HEAD explicite lie les commandes à la racine canonique. Clés JSON, actions et motifs de conflit restent anglais. text Copier syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify Statut combiné # --indexed active facultativement le cache persistant de hashes côté non préparé ; ce mode peut écrire des données jetables. Sans lui, le statut garde ses lectures complètes sans écriture. --combined rend version, branch, clean, staged, unstaged. Staged compare la base enregistrée à l’index figé ; unstaged compare cet index au workspace. Sans index, comparer à la tête ; branche initiale vide, base vide. Un chemin peut apparaître des deux côtés : préparer des octets puis remettre le fichier à sa base rend le statut de branche propre, mais conserve un changement préparé et un autre non préparé dans le rapport combiné. Un index périmé reste inspectable sur sa base d’origine ; le workspace se compare toujours à ses octets figés. clean exige deux comparaisons propres et un index frais ; périmé seul suffit à rendre faux. Un rapport sale réussi rend 0. --combined et --revision sont exclusifs. Le scan conserve ignores, fichiers ordinaires et hash BLAKE3 complet ; il n’ingère rien et n’audite pas les payloads stockés. Plan de checkout et vérification # Le plan compare heads/BRANCH à une révision cible exacte, inventorie le disque et hashe tous les fichiers suivis de la base. Taille/date ne prouvent jamais l’égalité. La politique clean-tracked-v1 exige tous les suivis propres, même inchangés dans la cible. Tout index explicite bloque, même sans différences préparées. Champs : source/target ; staging_snapshot ; policy/ready ; objects_verified ; executable_metadata_supported (false sur Windows) ; inventory_entries ; hashed_bytes (lecture des suivis, hors audit Store) ; restore_bytes (octets logiques des créations/remplacements) ; counts/actions ; create_directories ; conflicts triés uniques avec path/reason/obstruction. Les transitions de namespace ajoutent namespace_transition et remove_directories. Actions triées par chemin, avec résumés source/cible (manifest, size, file_hash, executable), côté absent null. Ce sont les changements du snapshot proposé même bloqué, pas un programme ordonné de déplacements. create : nouvel entrant ; replace : contenu différent ; remove : sortie absente de cible ; set_executable : mode seul ; reuse : même contenu/mode, autre manifeste ; unchanged : compteur sans ligne. restore_bytes n’est ni trafic réseau ni nombre unique de blocs ; voir réutilisation de manifestes . Windows conserve les changements de mode souhaités sans en faire un test de propreté locale. Par défaut, seules révisions et métadonnées de manifestes nécessaires sont lues, sans blocs cibles ni parents historiques ; un Store partiel de métadonnées peut donc rendre ready true et objects_verified false sans garantir la reconstruction. --verify audite toute la fermeture cible, parents et hashes compris, même si le disque présente des obstacles. Dépendance invalide/absente : erreur sans plan partiel. Plan prêt : code 0 ; bloqué : JSON et code 1 ; argument, IO ou métadonnée invalide : diagnostic stderr sans JSON. Chemins protégés et limites de politique # Le plan n’applique pas .syrnckignore : les ignorés restent visibles pour empêcher écrasement/collision. Les non-suivis sans rapport sont conservés sans hash ; une cible non suivie bloque même si ses octets correspondent. Motifs : tracked_modified/tracked_missing ; tracked_mode_changed sous Unix ; staging_present/stale_staging ; checkout_pending ; untracked_collision ; file_directory_transition lorsqu’une entrée extérieure à la transition suivie gêne ; path_spelling pour orthographe portable incohérente ; portable_collision pour plusieurs noms physiques équivalents ; symlink_obstruction/special_file ; protected_path pour Store/autre entrée protégée. Symlinks et points de réanalyse/jonctions Windows sont opaques, jamais parcourus volontairement. .git, .syrnck et le Store réel même personnalisé sont protégés et exclus. Changements propres de casse/normalisation et transitions fichier/dossier passent par l’ exécuteur récupérable . Seuls fichiers appartenant au snapshot et parents connus peuvent être déplacés ; dossiers vides inconnus et descendants ignorés restent protégés. L’orthographe des parents de chaque snapshot doit être cohérente sous casefold. Aucun force, écrasement, reset d’index ou adoption automatique. API C++, progression et ressources # Callbacks synchrones non réentrants. scanning compte dossiers/entrées ; verifying les octets du workspace ou de fermeture ; planning les métadonnées/actions, avec compteurs par phase. La planification finit par planning complet, jamais publishing. Annulation/exception ne rendent pas de résultat partiel et ne changent rien. Verrou exclusif et règles normales d’ouverture/récupération restent requis. Tampon de lecture 1 Mio ; cache FIFO de 1024 résumés de manifeste par chargement de snapshot. Inventaire : budget d’entrées 16 Mio, coût 64 + octets UTF-8 du chemin + chemin normalisé ; profondeur maximale 128. Ce n’est pas la RAM totale : maps, rapports, JSON, allocations et fermetures s’ajoutent. Objets limités à 16 Mio. L’observation ne verrouille pas le workspace et ne constitue pas un snapshot atomique. Changement observé de taille/type/date/mode autour d’une lecture fait échouer ; une modification ultérieure peut périmer le résultat. L’exécuteur doit donc préparer, revérifier et journaliser, même après ready. Les sessions C/Go exposent l’observation et les statuts ; elles ne fournissent pas toutes les mutations natives. cpp Copier auto status = store . workspace_status ( project , \"main\" , operation ) ; auto preview = store . plan_checkout ( project , \"main\" , target_id ) ; auto verified = store . plan_checkout ( project , \"main\" , target_id , true , operation ) ; syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json syrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify auto status = store.workspace_status(project, \"main\", operation); auto preview = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id); auto verified = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id, true, operation);","headings":[{"id":"native-workspace-inspection-and-checkout-preflight","text":"Inspection du workspace et aperçu de checkout","level":1},{"id":"combined-status","text":"Statut combiné","level":2},{"id":"checkout-plan-and-verification","text":"Plan de checkout et vérification","level":2},{"id":"protected-paths-and-first-policy-limits","text":"Chemins protégés et limites de politique","level":2},{"id":"c-progress-and-resource-boundaries","text":"API C++, progression et ressources","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status --combined --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --branch main --progress-json\nsyrnck native checkout-plan TARGET_REVISION --verify\nauto status = store.workspace_status(project, \"main\", operation);\nauto preview = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id);\nauto verified = store.plan_checkout(project, \"main\", target_id, true, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/working-index","#/fr/file-comparison","#/fr/native-checkout/recoverable-namespace-transitions","#/fr/workspace-abi"],"kind":"guide","revision":"00c20ecda62c"},{"id":"native-workspaces","title":"Workspaces natifs liés","description":"Dossiers de travail, HEAD et index indépendants sur un Store partagé.","section":"Versionnage","order":222,"parent":"native-branches","sourceRevision":"9","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Créer et sélectionner un workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Créer et sélectionner un workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native workspaces\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le workspace principal conserve son organisation historique. Un workspace lié\nenregistré possède son répertoire canonique, HEAD, index préparé, révision\ndétachée, reçus de checkout, état bisect, cache de travail et journal d’observation.\nObjets et branches nommées restent partagés ; les données ne sont pas copiées\nentre plusieurs Stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-add NAME DIRECTORY\u003c/code> s’exécute dans le contexte principal. Le dossier\nparent doit exister. Une destination absente est créée ; les fichiers existants\nsont conservés et les règles habituelles de collision s’appliquent. \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>\nexige une branche existante et disponible. \u003ccode>--revision REVISION\u003c/code> initialise HEAD\ndétaché ; sans ces options, la révision principale committée est utilisée en mode\ndétaché. Un principal non né initialise un snapshot vide retenu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les noms contiennent 1 à 64 lettres ASCII minuscules, chiffres ou tirets internes.\n\u003ccode>primary\u003c/code> et les noms de périphériques Windows sont réservés. Les racines ne\npeuvent chevaucher ni un autre workspace, ni le principal, ni le Store. Liens et\nalias portables ne contournent pas ces contrôles. Maximum : 1 024 contextes liés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--workspace NAME\u003c/code> sélectionne le contexte de l’invocation ; \u003ccode>primary\u003c/code> désigne\nexplicitement le défaut. Utiliser \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> pour retrouver le Store partagé depuis\nun autre dossier. En contexte lié, les commandes natives prennent le répertoire\nenregistré comme \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code> par défaut ; une valeur explicite doit résoudre\ncette même racine. \u003ccode>native workspaces\u003c/code> affiche aussi le principal, les répertoires,\nbranches/révisions et checkouts en attente.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial\">Dossiers de travail partiels\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/partial\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Dossiers de travail partiels\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les options répétées \u003ccode>--include CHEMIN\u003c/code> sélectionnent des fichiers ou préfixes\nde dossiers relatifs au dépôt, avec correspondance Unicode/casse portable.\nLes préfixes doivent être disjoints ; ni jokers ni exclusions. \u003ccode>--no-files\u003c/code>\nsélectionne explicitement un dossier vide. Sans ces options, la matérialisation\nreste complète. Les contrôles \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code> à la racine et dans les ancêtres\nnécessaires sont inclus automatiquement, avec leur orthographe canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Révisions et index figés conservent toujours l’arbre logique complet et tous\nses objets localement. Commit, ajout et amendement d’un dossier préservent les\nentrées omises ; statut, cache et observation comparent seulement la sélection\nphysique. Une édition explicite d’un fichier de travail hors sélection est\nrefusée. Les différences entre snapshots et \u003ccode>native export\u003c/code> portent toujours\nsur l’arbre complet. Ajout et commit du dossier exigent une révision cible\nexistante pour préserver les entrées omises ; créer d’abord la branche depuis\nla révision sélectionnée complète. Ce mode ne télécharge pas les blocs à la\ndemande et ne réduit pas la taille du Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Checkout, switch, HEAD détaché et restauration portable utilisent la sélection\nenregistrée, y compris pour les transitions fichier/dossier et de casse. Les\nfichiers locaux exclus sont préservés ; une obstruction de namespace est\nrefusée. Les sauvegardes conservent sélection et index complet. La restauration\nmatérialise les fichiers sélectionnés de l’index et vérifie exactement cet\narbre projeté avant de lier le workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La modification directe de la référence de sélection est refusée tant que le\nworkspace est actif. Utiliser la transition récupérable ci-dessous pour la changer.\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\">Reset hard\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stash/partial-workspaces\">stash\u003c/a>\nacceptent les fichiers partiels. En C++ : \u003ccode>register_partial_native_workspace( primary, name, directory, prefixes, operation)\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>native_materialization()\u003c/code>.\nC/Go peuvent sélectionner et observer un contexte partiel créé par CLI/C++.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"materialization-transitions\">Changer la sélection courante\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/materialization-transitions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Changer la sélection courante\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Choisir exactement une forme : \u003ccode>--include CHEMIN\u003c/code> répété, \u003ccode>--no-files\u003c/code> ou\n\u003ccode>--all-files\u003c/code>. Le plan en lecture seule doit être prêt ; l’application exige son\nID exact. \u003ccode>--expect ID\u003c/code> peut aussi verrouiller explicitement le HEAD complet.\nLes API C++ de \u003ccode>syrnck/workspace.hpp\u003c/code> sont \u003ccode>plan_native_materialization\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>set_native_materialization\u003c/code> : vecteur de préfixes optionnel nul pour tous les\nfichiers, vecteur explicitement vide pour aucun.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cela fonctionne pour les dossiers principaux et liés, en HEAD nommé ou détaché.\nUn changement exige des fichiers suivis propres et un index figé vide. Conserver\nd’abord les modifications par stash ou commit. L’extension refuse les obstacles\nnon suivis ou ignorés ; la réduction retire seulement les suivis vérifiés. Les\ndonnées locales sans rapport restent sur place. Les dossiers vides, non versionnés,\npeuvent rester. Une sélection identique rend \u003ccode>unchanged\u003c/code>, sans écriture et en\ngardant travail sale et index.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La transition garde historique logique complet et HEAD inchangés. Le journal\nv10, layout 7, fige les deux projections. Le CAS final publie nouvelle sélection\net reçu engagé ensemble. Avant cette publication, une interruption garde l’ancienne\npolitique active : \u003ccode>native checkout-recover\u003c/code> reprend ; l’option \u003ccode>--abort\u003c/code> restaure\nfichiers et politique antérieurs. Après publication, seul le nettoyage reste.\nObservateur et index de travail adoptent la projection à l’observation suivante.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> sont établies avant le\njournal, pour faire refuser le Store aux anciens lecteurs. Une interruption peut\nlaisser ces exigences additives actives ; la relance est sûre. Les sauvegardes\nconservent exigences et métadonnées de sélection ; les dossiers de travail\ndemandent toujours une restauration explicite. Tous les objets retenus restent\nlocaux ; aucun téléchargement différé d’assets n’est fourni.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"isolation\">Isolation et réservations de branches\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/isolation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Isolation et réservations de branches\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La préparation d’un workspace ne consomme jamais l’index d’un autre. Commits et\nréécritures utilisent la cible sélectionnée. Une branche nommée ne peut être\nsélectionnée que dans un workspace ; les plans signalent \u003ccode>branch_in_other_workspace\u003c/code>.\nLa publication des références contrôle aussi les réservations : une branche\nexplicite ne permet pas de déplacer celle d’un autre workspace derrière ses\nfichiers. Changer sa sélection ou détacher HEAD pour libérer cette branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque handle Store garde son verrou exclusif de dépôt. Les processus l’ouvrent\nautour des opérations et les observateurs le libèrent pendant l’attente. Un\ncheckout en attente bloque mutations natives et GC du Store jusqu’à sa récupération\ndans le contexte propriétaire. L’application est journalisée par workspace, sans\natomicité globale entre les dossiers de travail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"observation\">Observation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/observation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Observation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>L’observation liée utilise les notifications natives disponibles et contrôle\nl’identité des références et de la configuration du Store externe. Une publication\nde métadonnées impose une observation complète sans émettre de chemin extérieur\nau workspace. Stamps non pris en charge et échecs de notifications replient vers\nle polling. Les audits complets périodiques restent nécessaires. Cache et journal\nde chaque contexte sont séparés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"performance\">Mesure de l’index de travail\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/performance\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mesure de l’index de travail\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Omettre \u003ccode>--linked\u003c/code> pour le principal ; sous Unix utiliser \u003ccode>build/syrnck\u003c/code>.\nLe jeu de données synthétique de 64 Mio vérifie l’équivalence des statuts complet/avec\nindex et détecte une modification de même taille avec date de modification\nrestaurée. Sous Windows x64 local, le 8 novembre 2026, les cinq observations\nliées à chaud réutilisent chacune un fichier et hashent zéro octet ; les\nobservations complètes hashent chacune 64 Mio. Médianes CLI : 68 ms complètes,\n24 ms avec index (principal : 75 ms et 20 ms). Le cache OS était chaud et d’autres\ntests tournaient. Ces mesures montrent les lectures évitées sur ce cas ; elles\nne prédisent ni le débit d’un projet Unreal ni les latences multiplateformes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cette autre mesure utilise deux assets synthétiques totalisant 128 Mio et\n25 octets de code. Sur Windows x64 local, le 8 novembre 2026, cinq observations\nsans cache d’index : la vue complète hashe 134 217 753 octets par observation,\nla vue \u003ccode>Source\u003c/code> seulement 25 octets. Médianes CLI : 105 ms et 18 ms. Les fichiers\nvenaient d’être écrits/ingérés, sans purge du cache OS, et d’autres régressions\ntournaient. Le Store conserve tous\nles objets ; seule l’observation du dossier de travail est mesurée.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore\">Sauvegarde et restauration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/restore\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sauvegarde et restauration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les sauvegardes omettent les liaisons actives aux dossiers et les reçus locaux.\nElles conservent index, données détachées et racines de cibles terminées, avec un\ndescripteur de sélection portable et une racine de révision exacte par workspace\nlié. Le format v2 enregistre ces exclusions pour les sources expérimentales sans\ncapacités requises. Un Store avec capacités de lecteur requises utilise le\nmarqueur v3, qui les lie aussi à la sauvegarde. Les v1/v2 restent lisibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>workspace-restore NAME DIRECTORY\u003c/code> lie le snapshot portable à un nouveau dossier.\nLa branche d’origine est réutilisée si sa tête est inchangée et non réservée.\n\u003ccode>--branch NEW_NAME\u003c/code> peut créer une branche de sauvetage ; \u003ccode>--detach\u003c/code> restaure un\nHEAD indépendant. Une autre tête existante n’est jamais réinitialisée implicitement.\nL’index figé est matérialisé en fichiers de travail, avec HEAD committé conservé\nséparément. Les modifications non préparées ne font pas partie d’une sauvegarde\nlogique du Store. Changer la cible de l’index archive son snapshot d’origine avant\nde publier le remplacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La restauration publie exclusivement la sélection physique exportée avant de lier\nle contexte. Après interruption avant liaison, relancer : une arborescence\nexistante doit correspondre exactement au contenu sélectionné et aux modes sauvegardés.\nExports modifiés, fichiers supplémentaires, liens et métadonnées protégées sont\nrefusés sans écrasement. Les racines portables restent conservées jusqu’à un\nretrait explicite de rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native workspaces\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>L’enregistrement retient atomiquement un état détaché vide valide avant la première\nmatérialisation. Si celle-ci bloque ou s’interrompt, le contexte reste consultable ;\ny effectuer switch, checkout ou récupération. HEAD et préparations du principal\nne sont pas déplacés. Les reçus lient contexte et répertoire canonique exact ;\nun autre contexte ne peut les exécuter. Les reçus terminés restent locaux et\nconsultables après changement de sélection et GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La campagne Windows x64 a vérifié les 808 points IO observés d’arrêt forcé pour\nl’enregistrement et l’entrée/sortie/déplacement du checkout lié : Stores en clair\net chiffrés, reprise/annulation, transitions de namespace, inspection indépendante\nde production et GC après récupération. Ces essais gardent OS et système de\nfichiers actifs ; les coupures de courant demandent une validation distincte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La campagne des workspaces partiels a aussi vérifié les 716 frontières d’arrêt\nde processus observées pour enregistrement et checkout, avec les mêmes contrôles\nclair/chiffré, reprise/annulation, lecteur indépendant et GC après récupération.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api\">C++, C et Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/api\" aria-label=\"Lien vers C++, C et Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L1\" data-line=\"1\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">register_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>primary\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L2\" data-line=\"2\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"edit\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L3\" data-line=\"3\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">switch_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> changes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">native_target\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L5\" data-line=\"5\">store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">use_native_workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// principal, sélection locale au handle\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>En C : \u003ccode>syrnck_store_use_workspace\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex\u003c/code>.\nUne session liée exige un Store sélectionné sur le même nom. Un échec de sélection\nconserve le nom précédent. Go expose \u003ccode>Store.UseWorkspace(name)\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code> ; annulation et sérialisation des handles restent\napplicables. Un nom vide sélectionne le principal. Ces symboles s’ajoutent à\nl’ABI 1 et demandent une bibliothèque correspondante.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"formats\">Formats et périmètre actuel\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Formats et périmètre actuel\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les références principales et reçus v1 à v7 restent inchangés. Les métadonnées\nliées utilisent de vraies références \u003ccode>workspaces/NAME/\u003c/code>. Le slot d’un index nommé\nutilise le digest BLAKE3 du nom de branche, pour préserver la longueur maximale\nexistante ; le snapshot garde le nom complet. Les reçus liés passent en v8 avec\n\u003ccode>context\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> de 1 à 6 pour les vues complètes. Les reçus partiels\nutilisent v9 avec définitions figées \u003ccode>source_materialization\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>target_materialization\u003c/code>, contexte et layout de 1 à 6. Le layout 5 accepte le\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces\">reset hard partiel récupérable\u003c/a> ; le\nlayout 6 restaure les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-stash/partial-workspaces\">stashes partiels\u003c/a>. La sélection statique\nv9 exige deux définitions identiques. Les changements utilisent v10/layout 7\navec deux définitions figées différentes. Un chunk canonique v1 est conservé\nsous \u003ccode>workspaces/NOM/workspace/materialization\u003c/code>. Versions d’objets/révisions,\npacks et ABI C inchangées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les contextes liés peuvent être \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives\">archivés, désenregistrés et restaurés\u003c/a>,\navec travail modifié distinct de HEAD et de l’index. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation\">déplacement du dossier entier\u003c/a>\nprend en charge les contextes liés sur un même système de fichiers, changements de casse\ncompris. Déplacements entre systèmes et du principal/Store restent à compléter. Chaque workspace exige\ntoujours ses objets retenus complets localement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compatibility\">Compatibilité des lecteurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces/compatibility\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Compatibilité des lecteurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Enregistrement et restauration portable établissent l’exigence de lecteur\n\u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> avant publication de la liaison. Les anciens exécutables\nrefusent le Store, même pour les commandes de références ordinaires, et ne\npeuvent donc contourner les réservations de branches. Les lecteurs à jour\nacceptent toujours les configurations classiques. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/settings/reader-features\">inspection et mise à\nniveau explicite\u003c/a>. Une création partielle établit\naussi \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> avant publication de sa sélection. Un lecteur\nprenant en charge seulement les contextes liés refuse cette exigence supplémentaire.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Créer et sélectionner un workspace # text Copier syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\" Le workspace principal conserve son organisation historique. Un workspace lié enregistré possède son répertoire canonique, HEAD, index préparé, révision détachée, reçus de checkout, état bisect, cache de travail et journal d’observation. Objets et branches nommées restent partagés ; les données ne sont pas copiées entre plusieurs Stores. workspace-add NAME DIRECTORY s’exécute dans le contexte principal. Le dossier parent doit exister. Une destination absente est créée ; les fichiers existants sont conservés et les règles habituelles de collision s’appliquent. --branch NAME exige une branche existante et disponible. --revision REVISION initialise HEAD détaché ; sans ces options, la révision principale committée est utilisée en mode détaché. Un principal non né initialise un snapshot vide retenu. Les noms contiennent 1 à 64 lettres ASCII minuscules, chiffres ou tirets internes. primary et les noms de périphériques Windows sont réservés. Les racines ne peuvent chevaucher ni un autre workspace, ni le principal, ni le Store. Liens et alias portables ne contournent pas ces contrôles. Maximum : 1 024 contextes liés. --workspace NAME sélectionne le contexte de l’invocation ; primary désigne explicitement le défaut. Utiliser --store pour retrouver le Store partagé depuis un autre dossier. En contexte lié, les commandes natives prennent le répertoire enregistré comme --directory par défaut ; une valeur explicite doit résoudre cette même racine. native workspaces affiche aussi le principal, les répertoires, branches/révisions et checkouts en attente. Dossiers de travail partiels # text Copier syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files Les options répétées --include CHEMIN sélectionnent des fichiers ou préfixes de dossiers relatifs au dépôt, avec correspondance Unicode/casse portable. Les préfixes doivent être disjoints ; ni jokers ni exclusions. --no-files sélectionne explicitement un dossier vide. Sans ces options, la matérialisation reste complète. Les contrôles .syrnckignore à la racine et dans les ancêtres nécessaires sont inclus automatiquement, avec leur orthographe canonique. Révisions et index figés conservent toujours l’arbre logique complet et tous ses objets localement. Commit, ajout et amendement d’un dossier préservent les entrées omises ; statut, cache et observation comparent seulement la sélection physique. Une édition explicite d’un fichier de travail hors sélection est refusée. Les différences entre snapshots et native export portent toujours sur l’arbre complet. Ajout et commit du dossier exigent une révision cible existante pour préserver les entrées omises ; créer d’abord la branche depuis la révision sélectionnée complète. Ce mode ne télécharge pas les blocs à la demande et ne réduit pas la taille du Store. Checkout, switch, HEAD détaché et restauration portable utilisent la sélection enregistrée, y compris pour les transitions fichier/dossier et de casse. Les fichiers locaux exclus sont préservés ; une obstruction de namespace est refusée. Les sauvegardes conservent sélection et index complet. La restauration matérialise les fichiers sélectionnés de l’index et vérifie exactement cet arbre projeté avant de lier le workspace. La modification directe de la référence de sélection est refusée tant que le workspace est actif. Utiliser la transition récupérable ci-dessous pour la changer. Reset hard et stash acceptent les fichiers partiels. En C++ : register_partial_native_workspace( primary, name, directory, prefixes, operation) et native_materialization() . C/Go peuvent sélectionner et observer un contexte partiel créé par CLI/C++. Changer la sélection courante # text Copier syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID Choisir exactement une forme : --include CHEMIN répété, --no-files ou --all-files . Le plan en lecture seule doit être prêt ; l’application exige son ID exact. --expect ID peut aussi verrouiller explicitement le HEAD complet. Les API C++ de syrnck/workspace.hpp sont plan_native_materialization et set_native_materialization : vecteur de préfixes optionnel nul pour tous les fichiers, vecteur explicitement vide pour aucun. Cela fonctionne pour les dossiers principaux et liés, en HEAD nommé ou détaché. Un changement exige des fichiers suivis propres et un index figé vide. Conserver d’abord les modifications par stash ou commit. L’extension refuse les obstacles non suivis ou ignorés ; la réduction retire seulement les suivis vérifiés. Les données locales sans rapport restent sur place. Les dossiers vides, non versionnés, peuvent rester. Une sélection identique rend unchanged , sans écriture et en gardant travail sale et index. La transition garde historique logique complet et HEAD inchangés. Le journal v10, layout 7, fige les deux projections. Le CAS final publie nouvelle sélection et reçu engagé ensemble. Avant cette publication, une interruption garde l’ancienne politique active : native checkout-recover reprend ; l’option --abort restaure fichiers et politique antérieurs. Après publication, seul le nettoyage reste. Observateur et index de travail adoptent la projection à l’observation suivante. materialization-transitions-v1 et sparse-workspaces-v1 sont établies avant le journal, pour faire refuser le Store aux anciens lecteurs. Une interruption peut laisser ces exigences additives actives ; la relance est sûre. Les sauvegardes conservent exigences et métadonnées de sélection ; les dossiers de travail demandent toujours une restauration explicite. Tous les objets retenus restent locaux ; aucun téléchargement différé d’assets n’est fourni. Isolation et réservations de branches # La préparation d’un workspace ne consomme jamais l’index d’un autre. Commits et réécritures utilisent la cible sélectionnée. Une branche nommée ne peut être sélectionnée que dans un workspace ; les plans signalent branch_in_other_workspace . La publication des références contrôle aussi les réservations : une branche explicite ne permet pas de déplacer celle d’un autre workspace derrière ses fichiers. Changer sa sélection ou détacher HEAD pour libérer cette branche. Chaque handle Store garde son verrou exclusif de dépôt. Les processus l’ouvrent autour des opérations et les observateurs le libèrent pendant l’attente. Un checkout en attente bloque mutations natives et GC du Store jusqu’à sa récupération dans le contexte propriétaire. L’application est journalisée par workspace, sans atomicité globale entre les dossiers de travail. Observation # text Copier syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal L’observation liée utilise les notifications natives disponibles et contrôle l’identité des références et de la configuration du Store externe. Une publication de métadonnées impose une observation complète sans émettre de chemin extérieur au workspace. Stamps non pris en charge et échecs de notifications replient vers le polling. Les audits complets périodiques restent nécessaires. Cache et journal de chaque contexte sont séparés. Mesure de l’index de travail # text Copier python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json Omettre --linked pour le principal ; sous Unix utiliser build/syrnck . Le jeu de données synthétique de 64 Mio vérifie l’équivalence des statuts complet/avec index et détecte une modification de même taille avec date de modification restaurée. Sous Windows x64 local, le 8 novembre 2026, les cinq observations liées à chaud réutilisent chacune un fichier et hashent zéro octet ; les observations complètes hashent chacune 64 Mio. Médianes CLI : 68 ms complètes, 24 ms avec index (principal : 75 ms et 20 ms). Le cache OS était chaud et d’autres tests tournaient. Ces mesures montrent les lectures évitées sur ce cas ; elles ne prédisent ni le débit d’un projet Unreal ni les latences multiplateformes. text Copier python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json Cette autre mesure utilise deux assets synthétiques totalisant 128 Mio et 25 octets de code. Sur Windows x64 local, le 8 novembre 2026, cinq observations sans cache d’index : la vue complète hashe 134 217 753 octets par observation, la vue Source seulement 25 octets. Médianes CLI : 105 ms et 18 ms. Les fichiers venaient d’être écrits/ingérés, sans purge du cache OS, et d’autres régressions tournaient. Le Store conserve tous les objets ; seule l’observation du dossier de travail est mesurée. Sauvegarde et restauration # text Copier syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project Les sauvegardes omettent les liaisons actives aux dossiers et les reçus locaux. Elles conservent index, données détachées et racines de cibles terminées, avec un descripteur de sélection portable et une racine de révision exacte par workspace lié. Le format v2 enregistre ces exclusions pour les sources expérimentales sans capacités requises. Un Store avec capacités de lecteur requises utilise le marqueur v3, qui les lie aussi à la sauvegarde. Les v1/v2 restent lisibles. workspace-restore NAME DIRECTORY lie le snapshot portable à un nouveau dossier. La branche d’origine est réutilisée si sa tête est inchangée et non réservée. --branch NEW_NAME peut créer une branche de sauvetage ; --detach restaure un HEAD indépendant. Une autre tête existante n’est jamais réinitialisée implicitement. L’index figé est matérialisé en fichiers de travail, avec HEAD committé conservé séparément. Les modifications non préparées ne font pas partie d’une sauvegarde logique du Store. Changer la cible de l’index archive son snapshot d’origine avant de publier le remplacement. La restauration publie exclusivement la sélection physique exportée avant de lier le contexte. Après interruption avant liaison, relancer : une arborescence existante doit correspondre exactement au contenu sélectionné et aux modes sauvegardés. Exports modifiés, fichiers supplémentaires, liens et métadonnées protégées sont refusés sans écrasement. Les racines portables restent conservées jusqu’à un retrait explicite de rétention. Récupération # text Copier syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort L’enregistrement retient atomiquement un état détaché vide valide avant la première matérialisation. Si celle-ci bloque ou s’interrompt, le contexte reste consultable ; y effectuer switch, checkout ou récupération. HEAD et préparations du principal ne sont pas déplacés. Les reçus lient contexte et répertoire canonique exact ; un autre contexte ne peut les exécuter. Les reçus terminés restent locaux et consultables après changement de sélection et GC. La campagne Windows x64 a vérifié les 808 points IO observés d’arrêt forcé pour l’enregistrement et l’entrée/sortie/déplacement du checkout lié : Stores en clair et chiffrés, reprise/annulation, transitions de namespace, inspection indépendante de production et GC après récupération. Ces essais gardent OS et système de fichiers actifs ; les coupures de courant demandent une validation distincte. La campagne des workspaces partiels a aussi vérifié les 716 frontières d’arrêt de processus observées pour enregistrement et checkout, avec les mêmes contrôles clair/chiffré, reprise/annulation, lecteur indépendant et GC après récupération. C++, C et Go # cpp Copier store . register_native_workspace ( primary , \"edit\" , directory , operation ) ; store . use_native_workspace ( \"edit\" ) ; store . switch_directory ( directory , \"feature\" , operation ) ; auto changes = store . workspace_status ( directory , store . native_target ( ) , operation ) ; store . use_native_workspace ( \"\" ) ; // principal, sélection locale au handle En C : syrnck_store_use_workspace et syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex . Une session liée exige un Store sélectionné sur le même nom. Un échec de sélection conserve le nom précédent. Go expose Store.UseWorkspace(name) et WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} ; annulation et sérialisation des handles restent applicables. Un nom vide sélectionne le principal. Ces symboles s’ajoutent à l’ABI 1 et demandent une bibliothèque correspondante. Formats et périmètre actuel # Les références principales et reçus v1 à v7 restent inchangés. Les métadonnées liées utilisent de vraies références workspaces/NAME/ . Le slot d’un index nommé utilise le digest BLAKE3 du nom de branche, pour préserver la longueur maximale existante ; le snapshot garde le nom complet. Les reçus liés passent en v8 avec context et layout_version de 1 à 6 pour les vues complètes. Les reçus partiels utilisent v9 avec définitions figées source_materialization et target_materialization , contexte et layout de 1 à 6. Le layout 5 accepte le reset hard partiel récupérable ; le layout 6 restaure les stashes partiels . La sélection statique v9 exige deux définitions identiques. Les changements utilisent v10/layout 7 avec deux définitions figées différentes. Un chunk canonique v1 est conservé sous workspaces/NOM/workspace/materialization . Versions d’objets/révisions, packs et ABI C inchangées. Les contextes liés peuvent être archivés, désenregistrés et restaurés , avec travail modifié distinct de HEAD et de l’index. Le déplacement du dossier entier prend en charge les contextes liés sur un même système de fichiers, changements de casse compris. Déplacements entre systèmes et du principal/Store restent à compléter. Chaque workspace exige toujours ses objets retenus complets localement. Compatibilité des lecteurs # Enregistrement et restauration portable établissent l’exigence de lecteur linked-workspaces-v1 avant publication de la liaison. Les anciens exécutables refusent le Store, même pour les commandes de références ordinaires, et ne peuvent donc contourner les réservations de branches. Les lecteurs à jour acceptent toujours les configurations classiques. Voir inspection et mise à niveau explicite . Une création partielle établit aussi sparse-workspaces-v1 avant publication de sa sélection. Un lecteur prenant en charge seulement les contextes liés refuse cette exigence supplémentaire. syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\" syrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization syrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files syrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal python scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json python scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json syrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot syrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck syrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project syrnck native workspaces syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover syrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort store.register_native_workspace(primary, \"edit\", directory, operation); store.use_native_workspace(\"edit\"); store.switch_directory(directory, \"feature\", operation); auto changes = store.workspace_status(directory, store.native_target(), operation); store.use_native_workspace(\"\"); // principal, sélection locale au handle","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Créer et sélectionner un workspace","level":2},{"id":"partial","text":"Dossiers de travail partiels","level":2},{"id":"materialization-transitions","text":"Changer la sélection courante","level":2},{"id":"isolation","text":"Isolation et réservations de branches","level":2},{"id":"observation","text":"Observation","level":2},{"id":"performance","text":"Mesure de l’index de travail","level":2},{"id":"restore","text":"Sauvegarde et restauration","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Récupération","level":2},{"id":"api","text":"C++, C et Go","level":2},{"id":"formats","text":"Formats et périmètre actuel","level":2},{"id":"compatibility","text":"Compatibilité des lecteurs","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-6-L1","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-9-L1","code-9-L2","code-9-L3","code-9-L4","code-9-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native workspace-add edit ../project-edit --branch feature\nsyrnck native workspaces\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native head\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native status --combined\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native add Source\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native commit --staged -m \"Feature work\"\nsyrnck native workspace-add code ../project-code --branch feature --include Source --include Config\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace code native materialization\nsyrnck native workspace-add metadata ../project-metadata --no-files\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --include Source --include Config --verify\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --include Source --include Config --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-plan --all-files\nsyrnck --workspace code native materialization-set --all-files --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native watch --combined --journal\npython scripts/benchmark_working_index.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --linked --asset-mib 64 --iterations 5 --output linked-index.json\npython scripts/benchmark_partial_workspace.py --cli build/Release/syrnck.exe --asset-mib 128 --iterations 5 --output partial-status.json\nsyrnck --store .syrnck backup create /backup/snapshot\nsyrnck --store /backup/snapshot backup restore /recovered/project/.syrnck\nsyrnck --store /recovered/project/.syrnck native workspace-restore edit /recovered/edit --directory /recovered/project\nsyrnck native workspaces\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-status\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover\nsyrnck --store .syrnck --workspace edit native checkout-recover --abort\nstore.register_native_workspace(primary, \"edit\", directory, operation);\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"edit\");\nstore.switch_directory(directory, \"feature\", operation);\nauto changes = store.workspace_status(directory, store.native_target(), operation);\nstore.use_native_workspace(\"\"); // principal, sélection locale au handle","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces","#/fr/native-stash/partial-workspaces","#/fr/native-hard-reset/partial-workspaces","#/fr/native-stash/partial-workspaces","#/fr/native-workspace-archives","#/fr/native-workspace-relocation","#/fr/settings/reader-features"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f1c6d560e03e"},{"id":"network-compression","title":"Compression réseau négociée","description":"Guide : Compression réseau négociée.","section":"Transferts","order":430,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"negotiated-object-compression\">Compression négociée des objets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/network-compression/negotiated-object-compression\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Compression négociée des objets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Uploads et downloads utilisent Zstandard par défaut si le serveur l’annonce. Seule la représentation réseau change : IDs BLAKE3, manifestes complets, compression/chiffrement au repos, journaux et publication gardent leur contrat.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les exemples demandent Store initialisé, jeton et HTTPS. \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code> est le défaut ; \u003ccode>off\u003c/code> utilise les octets logiques ; \u003ccode>zstd\u003c/code> exige le support lorsqu’un objet manque réellement, mais autorise encore une représentation brute pour un objet petit/incompressible. \u003ccode>serve --no-compression\u003c/code> désactive l’extension, comme \u003ccode>ServeOptions::object_compression=false\u003c/code>. C++ utilise \u003ccode>TransferOptions::compression\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>net::ObjectCompression::{automatic, disabled, zstd}\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck serve --no-compression\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"negotiation-and-representation\">Négociation et représentation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/network-compression/negotiation-and-representation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Négociation et représentation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>GET /v1/capabilities ajoute \u003ccode>object_zstd\u003c/code>, entier 0 ou 1. La négociation est différée jusqu’au premier objet absent, une fois par opération. Champ absent ou endpoint 404 permettent le brut en auto ; types/versions invalides, refus d’identité et autres erreurs échouent. Un transfert réutilisé ou reçu complet ne négocie pas. Resume peut changer de mode sans modifier identité ni attente de référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>PUT avec \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code> transporte une trame Zstandard ; sans cet en-tête, les octets logiques. GET avec \u003ccode>Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code> accepte les deux, sinon seulement le brut. Une réponse compressée porte \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code>, une brute aucun. Type application/octet-stream ; chunks, manifestes et révisions concernés. Contrôles JSON et accusés restent bruts. Les réponses GET portent \u003ccode>Vary: Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces en-têtes Syrnck sont distincts de Content-Encoding HTTP pour maîtriser comptage encodé et décompression bornée. Le client demande identity et désactive le décodage HTTP automatique. Codages HTTP inattendus, en-têtes objet dupliqués/inconnus, trailers de codage ou compression non acceptée sont refusés. Le serveur désactivé refuse les PUT encodés, codages inconnus/dupliqués et contrôles encodés avec 415 ; la valeur est exactement zstd.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le corps borné est reçu avant refus du codage personnalisé pour éviter de masquer le 415 par reset. Le codage HTTP non géré peut être refusé plus tôt par la bibliothèque et provoquer une erreur de connexion. Les proxies doivent préserver en-têtes, Vary et octets : retirer le codage entraîne un échec de vérification, jamais un repli automatique après erreur opaque. Les anciens clients reçoivent toujours du brut.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bounds-and-verification\">Bornes et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/network-compression/bounds-and-verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bornes et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Corps encodés et logiques sont limités à 16 Mio. Trame acceptée : taille connue, sans dictionnaire, fenêtre au plus 16 Mio. Trames skippable/concaténées, suffixes, troncatures, taille inconnue et déclarations excessives sont refusés. L’en-tête est contrôlé avant allocation et le décodeur en flux borne sa fenêtre. La trame n’est pas une autorité d’authentification : le Store vérifie encore type, BLAKE3 et schéma, puis la publication contrôle fermeture et hashes de fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Encodage Zstd niveau 1, tentative à partir de 1 Kio, gain minimal 64 octets. Si le candidat ne tient pas dans les crédits disponibles ou n’atteint pas ce gain, le corps initial est utilisé sans allocation spéculative plus grande. Compression et Store restent sur le coordinateur ; annulation contrôlée avant/après compression et entre étapes de décodage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le budget facture original, candidat tant qu’ils coexistent et accusé réservé, même si le candidat est rejeté. Après choix, seul le vecteur retenu reste facturé. Downloads réservent 16 Mio et décodent directement dans la réponse sans garder une copie encodée complète ; le minimum CLI de 17 Mio reste valable. Contextes, transport, caches et métadonnées restent hors crédits, qui ne sont pas le RSS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-retries-and-recovery\">Trafic, reprises et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/network-compression/traffic-retries-and-recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Trafic, reprises et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> compte les octets logiques réussis une fois. \u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code> comptent les corps HTTP objet observés sur toutes les tentatives, accusés PUT compris côté download. En-têtes, TLS et contrôles capacités/présence/baux/références sont exclus. Tentatives partielles et répétées comptent ; réutilisation complète rend zéro. Débit cible et progression utilisent aussi les octets encodés, pas le compteur logique. Échec/annulation ne rendent pas de statistiques de succès.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les reprises idempotentes d’upload gardent le même corps ; downloads reprennent l’objet avec décodeur neuf, sans importer du partiel. Erreurs de format/codage/en-têtes ne sont pas reprises. Les objets déjà vérifiés se réutilisent via sessions et baux. Le CAS de référence n’est jamais repris automatiquement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-remaining-work\">Limites restantes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/network-compression/validation-and-remaining-work\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites restantes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les gains sur un corpus synthétique ne promettent pas un débit de production Unreal. Réutilisation des contextes, budgets CPU de compression, mesures sur assets réels et cache de présence restent des travaux distincts. Les contrats de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers\">transfert\u003c/a>, de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers\">budget parallèle\u003c/a> et de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy\">reprise\u003c/a> restent applicables.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Compression négociée des objets # Uploads et downloads utilisent Zstandard par défaut si le serveur l’annonce. Seule la représentation réseau change : IDs BLAKE3, manifestes complets, compression/chiffrement au repos, journaux et publication gardent leur contrat. Les exemples demandent Store initialisé, jeton et HTTPS. auto est le défaut ; off utilise les octets logiques ; zstd exige le support lorsqu’un objet manque réellement, mais autorise encore une représentation brute pour un objet petit/incompressible. serve --no-compression désactive l’extension, comme ServeOptions::object_compression=false . C++ utilise TransferOptions::compression et net::ObjectCompression::{automatic, disabled, zstd} . text Copier syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off syrnck serve --no-compression Négociation et représentation # GET /v1/capabilities ajoute object_zstd , entier 0 ou 1. La négociation est différée jusqu’au premier objet absent, une fois par opération. Champ absent ou endpoint 404 permettent le brut en auto ; types/versions invalides, refus d’identité et autres erreurs échouent. Un transfert réutilisé ou reçu complet ne négocie pas. Resume peut changer de mode sans modifier identité ni attente de référence. PUT avec Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd transporte une trame Zstandard ; sans cet en-tête, les octets logiques. GET avec Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd accepte les deux, sinon seulement le brut. Une réponse compressée porte Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd , une brute aucun. Type application/octet-stream ; chunks, manifestes et révisions concernés. Contrôles JSON et accusés restent bruts. Les réponses GET portent Vary: Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding . Ces en-têtes Syrnck sont distincts de Content-Encoding HTTP pour maîtriser comptage encodé et décompression bornée. Le client demande identity et désactive le décodage HTTP automatique. Codages HTTP inattendus, en-têtes objet dupliqués/inconnus, trailers de codage ou compression non acceptée sont refusés. Le serveur désactivé refuse les PUT encodés, codages inconnus/dupliqués et contrôles encodés avec 415 ; la valeur est exactement zstd. Le corps borné est reçu avant refus du codage personnalisé pour éviter de masquer le 415 par reset. Le codage HTTP non géré peut être refusé plus tôt par la bibliothèque et provoquer une erreur de connexion. Les proxies doivent préserver en-têtes, Vary et octets : retirer le codage entraîne un échec de vérification, jamais un repli automatique après erreur opaque. Les anciens clients reçoivent toujours du brut. Bornes et vérification # Corps encodés et logiques sont limités à 16 Mio. Trame acceptée : taille connue, sans dictionnaire, fenêtre au plus 16 Mio. Trames skippable/concaténées, suffixes, troncatures, taille inconnue et déclarations excessives sont refusés. L’en-tête est contrôlé avant allocation et le décodeur en flux borne sa fenêtre. La trame n’est pas une autorité d’authentification : le Store vérifie encore type, BLAKE3 et schéma, puis la publication contrôle fermeture et hashes de fichiers. Encodage Zstd niveau 1, tentative à partir de 1 Kio, gain minimal 64 octets. Si le candidat ne tient pas dans les crédits disponibles ou n’atteint pas ce gain, le corps initial est utilisé sans allocation spéculative plus grande. Compression et Store restent sur le coordinateur ; annulation contrôlée avant/après compression et entre étapes de décodage. Le budget facture original, candidat tant qu’ils coexistent et accusé réservé, même si le candidat est rejeté. Après choix, seul le vecteur retenu reste facturé. Downloads réservent 16 Mio et décodent directement dans la réponse sans garder une copie encodée complète ; le minimum CLI de 17 Mio reste valable. Contextes, transport, caches et métadonnées restent hors crédits, qui ne sont pas le RSS. Trafic, reprises et récupération # transferred_bytes compte les octets logiques réussis une fois. object_upload_bytes et object_download_bytes comptent les corps HTTP objet observés sur toutes les tentatives, accusés PUT compris côté download. En-têtes, TLS et contrôles capacités/présence/baux/références sont exclus. Tentatives partielles et répétées comptent ; réutilisation complète rend zéro. Débit cible et progression utilisent aussi les octets encodés, pas le compteur logique. Échec/annulation ne rendent pas de statistiques de succès. Les reprises idempotentes d’upload gardent le même corps ; downloads reprennent l’objet avec décodeur neuf, sans importer du partiel. Erreurs de format/codage/en-têtes ne sont pas reprises. Les objets déjà vérifiés se réutilisent via sessions et baux. Le CAS de référence n’est jamais repris automatiquement. Limites restantes # Les gains sur un corpus synthétique ne promettent pas un débit de production Unreal. Réutilisation des contextes, budgets CPU de compression, mesures sur assets réels et cache de présence restent des travaux distincts. Les contrats de transfert , de budget parallèle et de reprise restent applicables. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off syrnck serve --no-compression","headings":[{"id":"negotiated-object-compression","text":"Compression négociée des objets","level":1},{"id":"negotiation-and-representation","text":"Négociation et représentation","level":2},{"id":"bounds-and-verification","text":"Bornes et vérification","level":2},{"id":"traffic-retries-and-recovery","text":"Trafic, reprises et récupération","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-remaining-work","text":"Limites restantes","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --compression auto\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --compression zstd --transfer-buffer-mib 17\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION --compression off\nsyrnck serve --no-compression","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/transfers","#/fr/parallel-transfers","#/fr/transfer-policy"],"kind":"guide","revision":"637ef76a9ff6"},{"id":"operations","title":"Progression et annulation coopérative","description":"Guide : Progression et annulation coopérative.","section":"Premiers pas","order":440,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"progress-and-cooperative-cancellation\">Progression et annulation coopérative\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/operations/progress-and-cooperative-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Progression et annulation coopérative\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Ingestion C++, commit natif, reconstruction et filtres Git acceptent progression et annulation. Commits et clean utilisent les préférences de pipeline du dépôt ; IngestOptions explicite les remplace. Le CLI accepte workers/buffer pour put, commit, add, clean et migration LFS sans modifier les préférences enregistrées.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli\">CLI\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/operations/cli\" aria-label=\"Lien vers CLI\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les commandes qui traitent des blocs, métadonnées, snapshots, maintenance, migrations ou transferts acceptent --progress ou --progress-json, mutuellement exclusifs. Les deux écrivent exclusivement sur stderr ; stdout conserve son JSON, ID, pointeur Git ou flux binaire habituel. Sans option, le succès reste silencieux sur stderr. Les commandes sans progression refusent ces options. Un smudge brut ou clean de pointeur existant peut n’émettre aucun événement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le texte suit la langue de session et cite les chemins en échappant contrôles/newlines. Le JSON est canonique, un objet par ligne ; total_bytes null si inconnu. Chemins relatifs pour commit, arguments source/destination pour put/get, slash Windows normalisé. Pour une même phase/chemin, environ dix événements par seconde ; changements de portée et fins de fichier toujours émis. Scanning/publication ne sont pas des totaux d’octets projet. Le code d’erreur final prime sur un événement complete antérieur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>LFS preview vérifie ; migration ingère puis publie sa transaction Git sans annulation après engagement. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-ranges\">plages\u003c/a> comptent leurs octets relatifs ; \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-comparison\">compare\u003c/a> compte des entrées de blocs. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-retention\">rétention Git\u003c/a> vérifie et publie tous ses pins ensemble, sans retrait automatique. Statut ordinaire et diff sont en lecture seule, sans rapport partiel en échec ; le mode \u003ca href=\"#/fr/working-index\">indexé\u003c/a> peut écrire un cache jetable. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace\">statut combiné et plan de checkout\u003c/a> séparent staged/unstaged, inventorient les collisions et finissent en planning sans publishing. Un plan bloqué est un résultat JSON terminé avec code 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">checkout\u003c/a> journalise préparation et déplacements. Avant journal, annulation sans changement ; ensuite le workspace peut être partiel avec ancienne branche : inspecter, reprendre ou annuler. Le dernier callback publishing/checkout précède branche/journal atomiques ; après publication, seul le nettoyage continue sans arrêt/callback. Une erreur peut laisser un journal engagé à reprendre, non annulable. GC et écritures natives restent bloqués jusqu’à nettoyage ; cibles préparées et sauvegardes privées sont en clair même pour un Store chiffré. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-branches\">Switch\u003c/a> publie HEAD/journal ensemble en gardant les deux têtes ; abort antérieur garde l’ancienne sélection. native head est une lecture sans progression.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-tags\">Tags\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-staging\">staging\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-reset\">reset\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-squash\">squash\u003c/a> et sélections textuelles publient leurs groupes de références après fermeture complète vérifiée. Arrêt/exception avant publication garde les anciennes racines, avec objets préparés éventuellement orphelins ; aucun callback après. Staged commit ne lit pas le workspace. Reset soft/mixed et squash ne le modifient pas. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-archives\">archives\u003c/a> ne vérifient que les lignes affichées si demandé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-merge\">Merge\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-replay\">replay\u003c/a> et rebase planifient sans écrire ; --verify audite entrées et résolutions personnalisées. L’application recalcule le plan vérifié puis utilise le checkout récupérable. Les documents de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions\">résolution\u003c/a> lient les entrées et le parent choisi ; aucun snapshot partiel n’est publié. Hunks/lignes préservent les autres entrées et archivent l’ancien index ; reset-hunks et reset-lines lisent seulement les snapshots figés. Index périmé ou checkout actif bloque. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-patches\">patch externe\u003c/a> ne lit pas le workspace et lie octets, source, limites et résultat au plan attendu ; erreur de patch/budget/contexte avant ingestion sans référence changée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Break Windows et SIGINT/SIGTERM disponibles demandent l’arrêt coopératif, code 130 et diagnostic traduit. Le handler ne note qu’un flag atomique ; un thread normal demande le stop C++. Pipe/périphérique bloqué doit rendre la main ; les interruptions répétées demandent le même arrêt. La terminaison forcée suit la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery\">récupération\u003c/a>, distincte. Le nettoyage de maintenance engagé se termine sans nouvelle annulation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"phase\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"ingesting\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1048576\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"total_bytes\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">8388608\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"complete\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"buffered_blocks\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"c-operations\">Opérations C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/operations/c-operations\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Opérations C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’observateur reçoit ProgressEvent synchronement sur le coordinateur. Phases : scanning, ingesting, reconstructing, verifying, exporting, transferring, planning, packing, publishing. Événement : octets traités, total optionnel, chemin optionnel, complete pour cette portée, blocs tamponnés. Commit fournit chemin relatif/taille observée ; un flux ordinaire n’a pas de total connu ; les fichiers vides rendent aussi fin/progression.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les chemins sont empruntés durant le callback, à copier si conservés. Ne pas réentrer ou modifier le Store. Une exception arrête le travail et joint les workers. Les octets d’entrée peuvent être encore en file, sans preuve d’écriture durable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Arrêt vérifié entre lectures, blocs, dossiers et publications ; IO/codecs déjà lancés peuvent finir. Les workers encodent, le coordinateur écrit et appelle les observateurs. Un import arrêté peut laisser blocs et manifeste complet non référencés. Une fin d’ingestion concerne le fichier, pas une branche/pin/destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les commits vérifient leur fermeture avant déplacement de branche et contrôlent le stop jusqu’à l’écriture atomique. set_ref, compare_set_ref et closure acceptent aussi un stop_token. Avant engagement, ancienne référence intacte ; un stop concurrent au point atomique peut arriver trop tard.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>reconstruct vers un flux appelant peut laisser une sortie partielle. restore_file prépare un frère temporaire, contrôle l’arrêt avant publication exclusive et nettoie à l’annulation ; aucune destination existante n’est écrasée. Les filtres required signalent l’échec ; clean n’émet le pointeur qu’après rétention du manifeste.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>fsck(OperationOptions) vérifie en flux sans liste complète d’objets. Les octets décodés incluent répétitions nécessaires aux hashes et copies physiques secondaires ; total inconnu, fin seulement après succès. Arrêts entre objets/blocs/entrées ; validation format/index en cours peut finir. Échec ferme les sessions, rétablit le cache et ne modifie pas les données ; aucun faux succès stdout. closure garde, elle, son résultat matérialisé. Export multi-fichier prépare un arbre complet avant publication exclusive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers\">réseau\u003c/a> possède son point de CAS distant et son code 3 incertain. Journaux/baux/inventaires conservent sources ou objets reçus pendant GC ; prune ne retire que les reçus complets. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/maintenance\">Maintenance\u003c/a> s’annule pendant vérification/plan/construction/relecture, puis termine le nettoyage engagé sans callback. Les formats d’objet/pointeur/ABI ne changent pas ; les baux négocient des extensions HTTP. Recompiler les consommateurs C++ avec leurs en-têtes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diffs textuels\u003c/a> reconstruisent seulement le chemin demandé, refusent les budgets avant lecture, distinguent binaire/limites et ne publient pas de patch partiel. Le diff de travail contrôle ignores, obstructions et mutations observées ; une égalité inconnue reste null. Hunks, lignes et patches ont leurs propres refus de résultats surdimensionnés ou assemblages de newlines invalides, sans normaliser le workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>stop_source cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> options \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">settings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">ingest_options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>stop \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">get_token\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>progress \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ProgressEvent \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">  \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop().\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> revision \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">commit_directory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Snapshot\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Progression et annulation coopérative # Ingestion C++, commit natif, reconstruction et filtres Git acceptent progression et annulation. Commits et clean utilisent les préférences de pipeline du dépôt ; IngestOptions explicite les remplace. Le CLI accepte workers/buffer pour put, commit, add, clean et migration LFS sans modifier les préférences enregistrées. CLI # Les commandes qui traitent des blocs, métadonnées, snapshots, maintenance, migrations ou transferts acceptent --progress ou --progress-json, mutuellement exclusifs. Les deux écrivent exclusivement sur stderr ; stdout conserve son JSON, ID, pointeur Git ou flux binaire habituel. Sans option, le succès reste silencieux sur stderr. Les commandes sans progression refusent ces options. Un smudge brut ou clean de pointeur existant peut n’émettre aucun événement. Le texte suit la langue de session et cite les chemins en échappant contrôles/newlines. Le JSON est canonique, un objet par ligne ; total_bytes null si inconnu. Chemins relatifs pour commit, arguments source/destination pour put/get, slash Windows normalisé. Pour une même phase/chemin, environ dix événements par seconde ; changements de portée et fins de fichier toujours émis. Scanning/publication ne sont pas des totaux d’octets projet. Le code d’erreur final prime sur un événement complete antérieur. LFS preview vérifie ; migration ingère puis publie sa transaction Git sans annulation après engagement. Les plages comptent leurs octets relatifs ; compare compte des entrées de blocs. La rétention Git vérifie et publie tous ses pins ensemble, sans retrait automatique. Statut ordinaire et diff sont en lecture seule, sans rapport partiel en échec ; le mode indexé peut écrire un cache jetable. Le statut combiné et plan de checkout séparent staged/unstaged, inventorient les collisions et finissent en planning sans publishing. Un plan bloqué est un résultat JSON terminé avec code 1. Le checkout journalise préparation et déplacements. Avant journal, annulation sans changement ; ensuite le workspace peut être partiel avec ancienne branche : inspecter, reprendre ou annuler. Le dernier callback publishing/checkout précède branche/journal atomiques ; après publication, seul le nettoyage continue sans arrêt/callback. Une erreur peut laisser un journal engagé à reprendre, non annulable. GC et écritures natives restent bloqués jusqu’à nettoyage ; cibles préparées et sauvegardes privées sont en clair même pour un Store chiffré. Switch publie HEAD/journal ensemble en gardant les deux têtes ; abort antérieur garde l’ancienne sélection. native head est une lecture sans progression. Tags , staging , reset , squash et sélections textuelles publient leurs groupes de références après fermeture complète vérifiée. Arrêt/exception avant publication garde les anciennes racines, avec objets préparés éventuellement orphelins ; aucun callback après. Staged commit ne lit pas le workspace. Reset soft/mixed et squash ne le modifient pas. Les archives ne vérifient que les lignes affichées si demandé. Merge , replay et rebase planifient sans écrire ; --verify audite entrées et résolutions personnalisées. L’application recalcule le plan vérifié puis utilise le checkout récupérable. Les documents de résolution lient les entrées et le parent choisi ; aucun snapshot partiel n’est publié. Hunks/lignes préservent les autres entrées et archivent l’ancien index ; reset-hunks et reset-lines lisent seulement les snapshots figés. Index périmé ou checkout actif bloque. Le patch externe ne lit pas le workspace et lie octets, source, limites et résultat au plan attendu ; erreur de patch/budget/contexte avant ingestion sans référence changée. Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Break Windows et SIGINT/SIGTERM disponibles demandent l’arrêt coopératif, code 130 et diagnostic traduit. Le handler ne note qu’un flag atomique ; un thread normal demande le stop C++. Pipe/périphérique bloqué doit rendre la main ; les interruptions répétées demandent le même arrêt. La terminaison forcée suit la récupération , distincte. Le nettoyage de maintenance engagé se termine sans nouvelle annulation. text Copier syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress json Copier { \"phase\" : \"ingesting\" , \"path\" : \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"bytes\" : 1048576 , \"total_bytes\" : 8388608 , \"complete\" : false , \"buffered_blocks\" : 2 } Opérations C++ # L’observateur reçoit ProgressEvent synchronement sur le coordinateur. Phases : scanning, ingesting, reconstructing, verifying, exporting, transferring, planning, packing, publishing. Événement : octets traités, total optionnel, chemin optionnel, complete pour cette portée, blocs tamponnés. Commit fournit chemin relatif/taille observée ; un flux ordinaire n’a pas de total connu ; les fichiers vides rendent aussi fin/progression. Les chemins sont empruntés durant le callback, à copier si conservés. Ne pas réentrer ou modifier le Store. Une exception arrête le travail et joint les workers. Les octets d’entrée peuvent être encore en file, sans preuve d’écriture durable. Arrêt vérifié entre lectures, blocs, dossiers et publications ; IO/codecs déjà lancés peuvent finir. Les workers encodent, le coordinateur écrit et appelle les observateurs. Un import arrêté peut laisser blocs et manifeste complet non référencés. Une fin d’ingestion concerne le fichier, pas une branche/pin/destination. Les commits vérifient leur fermeture avant déplacement de branche et contrôlent le stop jusqu’à l’écriture atomique. set_ref, compare_set_ref et closure acceptent aussi un stop_token. Avant engagement, ancienne référence intacte ; un stop concurrent au point atomique peut arriver trop tard. reconstruct vers un flux appelant peut laisser une sortie partielle. restore_file prépare un frère temporaire, contrôle l’arrêt avant publication exclusive et nettoie à l’annulation ; aucune destination existante n’est écrasée. Les filtres required signalent l’échec ; clean n’émet le pointeur qu’après rétention du manifeste. fsck(OperationOptions) vérifie en flux sans liste complète d’objets. Les octets décodés incluent répétitions nécessaires aux hashes et copies physiques secondaires ; total inconnu, fin seulement après succès. Arrêts entre objets/blocs/entrées ; validation format/index en cours peut finir. Échec ferme les sessions, rétablit le cache et ne modifie pas les données ; aucun faux succès stdout. closure garde, elle, son résultat matérialisé. Export multi-fichier prépare un arbre complet avant publication exclusive. Le réseau possède son point de CAS distant et son code 3 incertain. Journaux/baux/inventaires conservent sources ou objets reçus pendant GC ; prune ne retire que les reçus complets. Maintenance s’annule pendant vérification/plan/construction/relecture, puis termine le nettoyage engagé sans callback. Les formats d’objet/pointeur/ABI ne changent pas ; les baux négocient des extensions HTTP. Recompiler les consommateurs C++ avec leurs en-têtes. Les diffs textuels reconstruisent seulement le chemin demandé, refusent les budgets avant lecture, distinguent binaire/limites et ne publient pas de patch partiel. Le diff de travail contrôle ignores, obstructions et mutations observées ; une égalité inconnue reste null. Hunks, lignes et patches ont leurs propres refus de résultats surdimensionnés ou assemblages de newlines invalides, sans normaliser le workspace. cpp Copier std :: stop_source cancellation ; auto options = store . settings ( ) . ingest_options ( ) ; options . stop = cancellation . get_token ( ) ; options . progress = [ & ] ( const syrnck :: ProgressEvent & event ) { // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop(). } ; auto revision = store . commit_directory ( project , \"main\" , \"Snapshot\" , options ) ; syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress syrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json syrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress {\"phase\":\"ingesting\",\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"bytes\":1048576,\"total_bytes\":8388608,\"complete\":false,\"buffered_blocks\":2} std::stop_source cancellation; auto options = store.settings().ingest_options(); options.stop = cancellation.get_token(); options.progress = [&](const syrnck::ProgressEvent &event) { // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop(). }; auto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Snapshot\", options);","headings":[{"id":"progress-and-cooperative-cancellation","text":"Progression et annulation coopérative","level":1},{"id":"cli","text":"CLI","level":2},{"id":"c-operations","text":"Opérations C++","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7"],"languages":["text","json","cpp"],"code":"syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\nsyrnck put Content/Map.uasset --progress\nsyrnck native commit -m \"Snapshot\" --progress-json\nsyrnck get MANIFEST restored.uasset --progress\n{\"phase\":\"ingesting\",\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"bytes\":1048576,\"total_bytes\":8388608,\"complete\":false,\"buffered_blocks\":2}\nstd::stop_source cancellation;\nauto options = store.settings().ingest_options();\noptions.stop = cancellation.get_token();\noptions.progress = [&](const syrnck::ProgressEvent &event) {\n  // Observe progress here; another thread may call cancellation.request_stop().\n};\nauto revision = store.commit_directory(project, \"main\", \"Snapshot\", options);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/file-ranges","#/fr/file-comparison","#/fr/git-retention","#/fr/working-index","#/fr/native-workspace","#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/native-branches","#/fr/native-tags","#/fr/native-staging","#/fr/native-reset","#/fr/native-squash","#/fr/native-archives","#/fr/native-merge","#/fr/native-replay","#/fr/native-resolutions","#/fr/text-patches","#/fr/crash-recovery","#/fr/transfers","#/fr/maintenance","#/fr/text-diff"],"kind":"guide","revision":"6a90fa37a734"},{"id":"overview","title":"Syrnck","description":"Syrnck","section":"Syrnck","order":0,"sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"getting-started\">Choisir son parcours\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/overview/getting-started\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Choisir son parcours\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Syrnck est un moteur C++20 de stockage de blocs et de révisions pour les assets volumineux. Chaque version possède un manifeste complet : sa reconstruction n’exige pas de rejouer des patches binaires. Les blocs communs se réutilisent entre versions, chemins et transferts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Commencer par le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/usage\">parcours de démarrage\u003c/a>, puis choisir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-assets\">assets Git\u003c/a> ou l’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-history\">historique natif\u003c/a>. Inspecter le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace\">workspace\u003c/a> avant les changements et conserver une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/backups\">sauvegarde vérifiée\u003c/a> avant tout retrait d’anciennes versions. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/storage\">guide du stockage\u003c/a> explique leur rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workflows\">Fonctions disponibles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/overview/workflows\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fonctions disponibles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Git conserve l’historique ordinaire du code, les branches, revues, fusions et autres fonctions Git ; les filtres Syrnck représentent les assets choisis par des pointeurs. Héberger ces pointeurs sur GitHub/GitLab ne fournit pas un serveur de blocs Syrnck : les données partagées demandent un stockage/serveur séparé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le moteur autonome propose commits, index figé, branches, tags, checkout récupérable, merge, rebase linéaire/interactif, cherry-pick, revert, squash, reset, stash, historique des renommages exacts et bisect. Chaque guide précise les règles de conflit et de publication. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers\">transferts\u003c/a> ne publient que des fermetures complètes vérifiées. L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent\">agent résident de publication\u003c/a> surveille les changements locaux et reprend les publications natives, y compris après des modifications hors ligne. Son mode bidirectionnel facultatif reçoit les révisions et fusionne les historiques concurrents compatibles. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">permissions et baux d’assets\u003c/a> contrôlent les serveurs configurés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/integrations\">intégrations C++/C/Go\u003c/a> incluent un \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing\">adaptateur BEP Syncthing\u003c/a> pour fichiers ordinaires, sans constituer un daemon Syncthing résident. Aucun plugin Unreal n’est fourni ; le moteur stocke des octets d’assets sans interpréter leurs objets Unreal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading\">Lire ce manuel local\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/overview/reading\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Lire ce manuel local\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le lecteur complet fonctionne par file:// sans serveur ni connexion Internet. Rechercher texte et code dans l’en-tête ou avec Ctrl+K. L’arbre de navigation accepte Tab/Entrée et les flèches ; un bouton de menu apparaît sur les petits écrans. Le thème suit initialement le système puis se choisit dans son menu. Anglais et français partagent les IDs de pages et sections. Une langue indisponible revient à l’anglais en gardant la destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Signets, notes et préférences dépendent du stockage local autorisé par le navigateur. Les positions sont gardées pendant la navigation ; les liens de section explicites sont prioritaires. Les références externes sont facultatives et ne demandent Internet que lorsqu’on les ouvre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Utiliser \u003ccode>syrnck help\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>syrnck help native-tags\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>syrnck --lang fr help native-tags\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>--section ID\u003c/code> ouvre une section précise ; \u003ccode>--print-url\u003c/code> affiche l’adresse sans lancer de navigateur ; \u003ccode>--directory PATH\u003c/code> choisit un autre dossier Documentation installé. \u003ccode>--help\u003c/code> reste la référence courte du terminal. Cette CLI ne possède ni menu d’aide ni interface F1 à remplacer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Limites actuelles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/overview/limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites actuelles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Lire l’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/architecture\">architecture\u003c/a>, les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/protocol\">formats\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/limits\">limites\u003c/a> avant intégration ou déploiement. Le chiffrement protège les objets au repos, tandis que les transferts fournissent des objets décodés au serveur de confiance. Chiffrement de bout en bout, rétention distribuée et service complet de synchronisation continue restent à développer. Le code original de Syrnck est sous MIT ; les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/licenses\">notices des dépendances\u003c/a> s’appliquent séparément.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Choisir son parcours # Syrnck est un moteur C++20 de stockage de blocs et de révisions pour les assets volumineux. Chaque version possède un manifeste complet : sa reconstruction n’exige pas de rejouer des patches binaires. Les blocs communs se réutilisent entre versions, chemins et transferts. Commencer par le parcours de démarrage , puis choisir les assets Git ou l’ historique natif . Inspecter le workspace avant les changements et conserver une sauvegarde vérifiée avant tout retrait d’anciennes versions. Le guide du stockage explique leur rétention. Fonctions disponibles # Git conserve l’historique ordinaire du code, les branches, revues, fusions et autres fonctions Git ; les filtres Syrnck représentent les assets choisis par des pointeurs. Héberger ces pointeurs sur GitHub/GitLab ne fournit pas un serveur de blocs Syrnck : les données partagées demandent un stockage/serveur séparé. Le moteur autonome propose commits, index figé, branches, tags, checkout récupérable, merge, rebase linéaire/interactif, cherry-pick, revert, squash, reset, stash, historique des renommages exacts et bisect. Chaque guide précise les règles de conflit et de publication. Les transferts ne publient que des fermetures complètes vérifiées. L’ agent résident de publication surveille les changements locaux et reprend les publications natives, y compris après des modifications hors ligne. Son mode bidirectionnel facultatif reçoit les révisions et fusionne les historiques concurrents compatibles. Les permissions et baux d’assets contrôlent les serveurs configurés. Les intégrations C++/C/Go incluent un adaptateur BEP Syncthing pour fichiers ordinaires, sans constituer un daemon Syncthing résident. Aucun plugin Unreal n’est fourni ; le moteur stocke des octets d’assets sans interpréter leurs objets Unreal. Lire ce manuel local # Le lecteur complet fonctionne par file:// sans serveur ni connexion Internet. Rechercher texte et code dans l’en-tête ou avec Ctrl+K. L’arbre de navigation accepte Tab/Entrée et les flèches ; un bouton de menu apparaît sur les petits écrans. Le thème suit initialement le système puis se choisit dans son menu. Anglais et français partagent les IDs de pages et sections. Une langue indisponible revient à l’anglais en gardant la destination. Signets, notes et préférences dépendent du stockage local autorisé par le navigateur. Les positions sont gardées pendant la navigation ; les liens de section explicites sont prioritaires. Les références externes sont facultatives et ne demandent Internet que lorsqu’on les ouvre. Utiliser syrnck help , syrnck help native-tags ou syrnck --lang fr help native-tags . --section ID ouvre une section précise ; --print-url affiche l’adresse sans lancer de navigateur ; --directory PATH choisit un autre dossier Documentation installé. --help reste la référence courte du terminal. Cette CLI ne possède ni menu d’aide ni interface F1 à remplacer. Limites actuelles # Lire l’ architecture , les formats et les limites avant intégration ou déploiement. Le chiffrement protège les objets au repos, tandis que les transferts fournissent des objets décodés au serveur de confiance. Chiffrement de bout en bout, rétention distribuée et service complet de synchronisation continue restent à développer. Le code original de Syrnck est sous MIT ; les notices des dépendances s’appliquent séparément.","headings":[{"id":"getting-started","text":"Choisir son parcours","level":2},{"id":"workflows","text":"Fonctions disponibles","level":2},{"id":"reading","text":"Lire ce manuel local","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limites actuelles","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/usage","#/fr/git-assets","#/fr/native-history","#/fr/native-workspace","#/fr/backups","#/fr/storage","#/fr/transfers","#/fr/resident-agent","#/fr/server-access","#/fr/integrations","#/fr/syncthing","#/fr/architecture","#/fr/protocol","#/fr/limits","#/fr/licenses"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f8c86367f8ba"},{"id":"packs","title":"Packs et compaction","description":"Guide : Packs et compaction.","section":"Stockage","order":10,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"packs-et-compaction\">Packs et compaction\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/packs-et-compaction\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Packs et compaction\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck pack\u003c/code> regroupe les objets isolés dans des packs immuables indexés. Il conserve les identifiants, enveloppes \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code>, nonces de chiffrement, manifestes et références. La maintenance ordinaire ajoute des packs sans réécrire ceux déjà actifs ; \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> regroupe explicitement tout le stock. Aucune de ces commandes ne supprime de version logique. L&#39;API C++ correspondante est \u003ccode>Store::pack(PackOptions)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store donnees pack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store donnees pack --compact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store donnees gc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck --store donnees gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"activation-et-compatibilite\">Activation et compatibilité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/activation-et-compatibilite\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Activation et compatibilité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un store neuf reste au format physique v1, avec un fichier par objet. La première commande \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> publie une configuration \u003cstrong>v2\u003c/strong> si un seul pack suffit, ou \u003cstrong>v3\u003c/strong> si plusieurs packs sont nécessaires. Ajouter un deuxième pack à un store v2 passe à v3. Les objets logiques et le protocole réseau restent v1. Le moteur lit les trois configurations ; les binaires plus anciens refusent les versions physiques qu&#39;ils ne comprennent pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le format v3 contient une liste de packs actifs. Une fois activé, il est conservé même si une compaction ou un GC réduit cette liste à un seul pack ou à zéro. Les stores existants ne sont pas convertis automatiquement à l&#39;ouverture. Il n&#39;existe pas de commande de retour à une ancienne version physique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les nouvelles écritures continuent à produire des fichiers isolés. La lecture combine ces objets et tous les packs actifs. Une clé peut avoir une copie isolée et une copie dans un pack ; elle compte une fois dans l&#39;inventaire logique. La copie isolée est prioritaire pour les lectures ordinaires. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> contrôle aussi la copie du pack pour qu&#39;un doublon valide ne masque pas une corruption. Une même clé dans deux packs actifs est refusée comme catalogue incohérent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le CLI, les filtres Git, le serveur, l&#39;ABI C et le binding Go lisent ce stockage par le même moteur. Le réseau échange toujours des objets logiques vérifiés ; les pairs peuvent employer des dispositions physiques et des clés différentes. La maintenance est exposée par le CLI et l&#39;API C++, sans nouvelle fonction C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"regroupement-incremental-et-complet\">Regroupement incrémental et complet\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/regroupement-incremental-et-complet\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Regroupement incrémental et complet\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Par défaut, \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> sélectionne les objets isolés qui ne figurent dans aucun pack actif. Il les répartit entre de nouveaux packs, puis ajoute ces packs au catalogue. Les fichiers existants gardent leurs noms, octets et dates d&#39;écriture. Les copies isolées redondantes sont retirées après validation. Dans un store déjà au format v2/v3, sans objet nouveau ni copie à nettoyer, la commande ne réécrit ni pack, ni index, ni configuration ; des restes de générations non publiées peuvent toutefois être nettoyés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> rassemble les objets de tous les packs et les objets isolés dans un nouvel ensemble de packs. Cette opération permet de réduire leur nombre. Elle respecte les mêmes limites de taille et de nombre d&#39;objets ; elle ne garantit donc pas de produire un pack unique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--max-mib\u003c/code> accepte \u003cstrong>18 à 4 096 Mio\u003c/strong>, avec \u003cstrong>512 Mio\u003c/strong> par défaut. \u003ccode>--max-objects\u003c/code> accepte \u003cstrong>1 à 1 048 576\u003c/strong>, avec \u003cstrong>65 536\u003c/strong> par défaut. Ces plafonds s&#39;appliquent aux packs créés par la commande, en-tête de 8 octets inclus pour le plafond d&#39;octets. Les enveloppes sont conservées entières ; une enveloppe maximale de 17 Mio doit pouvoir tenir. Les réglages C++ sont \u003ccode>PackOptions::max_pack_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_objects_per_pack\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>compact\u003c/code>. Le minimum C++ exact est 17 Mio + 8 octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan est calculé à partir des tailles stockées, puis les limites sont à nouveau appliquées aux enveloppes effectivement lues. Un plan dépassant le nombre disponible de packs est refusé avant la construction. Il faut alors augmenter les limites de création ou utiliser \u003ccode>--compact\u003c/code> pour regrouper les packs existants.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le résultat JSON distingue \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_count\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>index_bytes\u003c/code> du catalogue final, de \u003ccode>new_packs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>objects_written\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pack_bytes_written\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>index_bytes_written\u003c/code> pour les nouvelles publications physiques. Les compteurs d&#39;octets portent sur les fichiers complets pack/index. \u003ccode>loose_objects_removed\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>obsolete_pack_files_removed\u003c/code> décrivent les retraits physiques. La validation complète lit toujours le stock existant : le regroupement incrémental réduit les réécritures, sans rendre cette validation constante en temps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-et-reprise\">Publication et reprise\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/publication-et-reprise\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Publication et reprise\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sous le verrou exclusif du store, \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> :\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Recharge la configuration et les index, vérifie les objets, leurs dépendances et les hashes complets des fichiers, y compris les objets non épinglés.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prépare le plan, écrit les nouveaux packs en flux, puis leurs index, avec les publications atomiques et flushs du moteur.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relit les nouvelles enveloppes et revalide leurs identifiants, formats et authentification.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remplace atomiquement \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code>, qui publie le catalogue complet.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retire les copies isolées et les générations devenues inactives.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Une erreur avant le remplacement de la configuration conserve l&#39;ancien catalogue et ses copies. Un lot peut laisser des packs non publiés si la construction d&#39;un pack suivant échoue. Après publication, une interruption peut laisser des doublons et d&#39;anciens packs ; le catalogue désigne déjà les nouvelles copies complètes. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>gc --delete\u003c/code> nettoient ces restes après validation. Seuls les noms générés \u003ccode>pack-&lt;32 caractères hexadécimaux&gt;.syp/.syi\u003c/code> inactifs sont concernés ; les liens symboliques à ces emplacements sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un échec de flush après le remplacement atomique peut survenir alors que le nouveau catalogue est déjà visible. Le handle recharge alors la configuration effective avant de rendre l&#39;erreur ; un échec de rechargement invalide l&#39;index en mémoire. Il faut corriger le problème et rouvrir le store ou réussir un nouvel audit avant de poursuivre les accès aux objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-selectif\">GC sélectif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/gc-selectif\" aria-label=\"Lien vers GC sélectif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le GC commence par valider la fermeture de toutes les références et les hashes des fichiers conservés, sans utiliser les données du cache. Une racine absente ou corrompue arrête l&#39;opération avant toute suppression. Supprimer une référence ne supprime pas un bloc encore utilisé par une autre branche, archive, révision ou pin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque pack est traité séparément :\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tous ses objets sont conservés : ses fichiers restent inchangés.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Aucun n&#39;est conservé : il est retiré du catalogue, sans recopier son contenu.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Une partie est conservée : un nouveau pack contient ses seuls objets vivants, avec relecture vérifiée avant publication.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Toutes les modifications de catalogue du GC sont publiées ensemble avant le retrait des anciens fichiers. Les objets isolés conservés restent isolés. Le GC ne fusionne pas les packs ; utiliser \u003ccode>pack --compact\u003c/code> pour cela. Le pack de remplacement ne dépasse pas le nombre d&#39;objets ni la taille du pack source, même si celui-ci provient d&#39;un ancien store avec des packs plus grands que les réglages actuels par défaut.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC simulé ne crée ni pack ni configuration. \u003ccode>reclaimable_bytes\u003c/code> additionne les enveloppes inaccessibles de toutes leurs copies physiques, hors en-têtes/index. \u003ccode>repack_required\u003c/code> indique si le catalogue contient des objets à retirer. \u003ccode>packs_to_rewrite\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>packs_to_drop\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>pack_bytes_to_write\u003c/code> annoncent les packs à recopier, les packs entièrement morts et les octets des seuls nouveaux packs de remplacement, en-têtes inclus mais index exclus. \u003ccode>obsolete_pack_files\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>obsolete_pack_bytes\u003c/code> décrivent séparément les restes déjà inactifs au début de l&#39;opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memoire-du-gc\">Mémoire du GC\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/memoire-du-gc\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mémoire du GC\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Après un GC interrompu, des métadonnées isolées non référencées peuvent encore attendre leur suppression alors que leurs blocs morts ont quitté le catalogue. Les versions conservées restent complètes, mais \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> peut signaler ces dépendances orphelines. Reprendre le GC termine le nettoyage avant l&#39;audit complet ; voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection\">procédure et ses limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC parcourt les index par pages de 256 entrées. Le marquage des objets empaquetés utilise \u003ccode>somme(ceil(objets_du_pack / 8))\u003c/code> octets, soit \u003cstrong>128 Kio pour 1 048 576 objets dans un pack\u003c/strong>, hors overhead des conteneurs. Il conserve seulement un compteur de survivants et une taille par pack. Les objets morts et les survivants à recopier ne sont plus rassemblés dans des listes d&#39;identifiants. Le constructeur de pack en flux vérifie le nombre annoncé et l&#39;ordre strict des clés, puis la relecture contrôle toutes les nouvelles enveloppes avant publication. Les formats physiques restent identiques.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les objets vivants absents du catalogue de packs utilisent encore un ensemble de clés binaires typées de 33 octets, avec l&#39;overhead d&#39;un arbre ordonné. Un store uniquement en fichiers isolés conserve donc un coût de marquage proportionnel à ses objets vivants. La pile de parcours, les références, les métadonnées JSON, les codecs, les répertoires/filtres des index et leurs caches s&#39;ajoutent au bitmap. Le GC n&#39;annonce pas un plafond global de RAM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;inventaire des objets isolés est parcouru une première fois pour valider toute son arborescence et préparer les compteurs, avant toute écriture de remplacement ou suppression. Après publication éventuelle, un second parcours retire les objets isolés morts. Les copies à la fois isolées et empaquetées comptent une fois dans \u003ccode>live_objects\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>unreachable_objects\u003c/code>, et chaque copie morte contribue aux octets récupérables. Le nettoyage utilise le nouveau catalogue après publication : les positions du bitmap de l&#39;ancien catalogue ne sont jamais réinterprétées dans le nouveau.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le résultat JSON ajoute trois compteurs décrivant le travail de l&#39;opération : \u003ccode>packed_mark_bytes\u003c/code> pour les octets utiles du bitmap initial, \u003ccode>loose_live_objects\u003c/code> pour les clés vivantes absentes du catalogue initial et \u003ccode>peak_pending_objects\u003c/code> pour le maximum de clés en attente dans la pile. Ils ne mesurent ni le RSS ni toutes les allocations. Les caches vérifiés restent exclus de la validation des racines. Le verrou exclusif couvre les deux parcours et la publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limites-actuelles\">Limites actuelles\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/packs/limites-actuelles\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Limites actuelles\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le catalogue accepte \u003cstrong>1 024 packs\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>1 048 576 objets par pack\u003c/strong> et \u003cstrong>4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total\u003c/strong>. La taille binaire totale des index reste \u003ccode>16 × packs + 45 × objets\u003c/code> octets. Le lecteur les parcourt à l&#39;ouverture puis consulte des pages de 256 entrées, avec un répertoire, un filtre et une LRU de \u003cstrong>4 Mio\u003c/strong> par défaut (\u003ccode>--index-cache-mib\u003c/code>). La validation des doublons entre packs utilise des parcours fusionnés. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">garanties de snapshot et budgets mémoire\u003c/a>. Ces plafonds ne constituent pas une validation de charge du moteur complet à plusieurs millions d&#39;objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La construction lit les objets en flux, sans charger les packs entiers en RAM. La compaction nécessite l&#39;espace des nouveaux packs avant de retirer les anciens. Elle conserve les enveloppes, sans les recompresser ni changer la clé. Une reconstruction, une fermeture de dépendances ou un audit réutilise au plus \u003cstrong>8 handles de packs\u003c/strong> dans sa session, avec éviction LRU ; les appels imbriqués partagent cette session. Tous ces handles sont fermés au retour, y compris sur exception, et avant publication/suppression de générations. Les lectures isolées ouvrent leur propre handle. Chaque enveloppe reste relue et contrôlée avant tout hit du cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les arbres de métadonnées paginés, le choix automatique des packs à fusionner et la maintenance en ligne avec lecteurs concurrents restent à développer. \u003ccode>objects()\u003c/code> et l&#39;API publique de fermeture des dépendances matérialisent encore des inventaires proportionnels au nombre d&#39;objets. Les plans d&#39;empaquetage/compaction sont désormais en flux : les index empaquetés sont fusionnés par pages et seules les clés isolées restent à trier. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/compaction\">garanties de compaction\u003c/a>. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> parcourt désormais l&#39;inventaire en flux avec un bitmap pour les objets empaquetés ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/audit\">garanties et limites des audits\u003c/a>. Voir aussi le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/protocol\">format binaire\u003c/a>, les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance\">mesures\u003c/a> et la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/limits\">feuille de route\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Packs et compaction # syrnck pack regroupe les objets isolés dans des packs immuables indexés. Il conserve les identifiants, enveloppes SYR1 , nonces de chiffrement, manifestes et références. La maintenance ordinaire ajoute des packs sans réécrire ceux déjà actifs ; pack --compact regroupe explicitement tout le stock. Aucune de ces commandes ne supprime de version logique. L'API C++ correspondante est Store::pack(PackOptions) . text Copier syrnck --store donnees pack syrnck --store donnees pack --compact syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck --store donnees gc syrnck --store donnees gc --delete Activation et compatibilité # Un store neuf reste au format physique v1, avec un fichier par objet. La première commande pack publie une configuration v2 si un seul pack suffit, ou v3 si plusieurs packs sont nécessaires. Ajouter un deuxième pack à un store v2 passe à v3. Les objets logiques et le protocole réseau restent v1. Le moteur lit les trois configurations ; les binaires plus anciens refusent les versions physiques qu'ils ne comprennent pas. Le format v3 contient une liste de packs actifs. Une fois activé, il est conservé même si une compaction ou un GC réduit cette liste à un seul pack ou à zéro. Les stores existants ne sont pas convertis automatiquement à l'ouverture. Il n'existe pas de commande de retour à une ancienne version physique. Les nouvelles écritures continuent à produire des fichiers isolés. La lecture combine ces objets et tous les packs actifs. Une clé peut avoir une copie isolée et une copie dans un pack ; elle compte une fois dans l'inventaire logique. La copie isolée est prioritaire pour les lectures ordinaires. fsck contrôle aussi la copie du pack pour qu'un doublon valide ne masque pas une corruption. Une même clé dans deux packs actifs est refusée comme catalogue incohérent. Le CLI, les filtres Git, le serveur, l'ABI C et le binding Go lisent ce stockage par le même moteur. Le réseau échange toujours des objets logiques vérifiés ; les pairs peuvent employer des dispositions physiques et des clés différentes. La maintenance est exposée par le CLI et l'API C++, sans nouvelle fonction C/Go. Regroupement incrémental et complet # Par défaut, pack sélectionne les objets isolés qui ne figurent dans aucun pack actif. Il les répartit entre de nouveaux packs, puis ajoute ces packs au catalogue. Les fichiers existants gardent leurs noms, octets et dates d'écriture. Les copies isolées redondantes sont retirées après validation. Dans un store déjà au format v2/v3, sans objet nouveau ni copie à nettoyer, la commande ne réécrit ni pack, ni index, ni configuration ; des restes de générations non publiées peuvent toutefois être nettoyés. pack --compact rassemble les objets de tous les packs et les objets isolés dans un nouvel ensemble de packs. Cette opération permet de réduire leur nombre. Elle respecte les mêmes limites de taille et de nombre d'objets ; elle ne garantit donc pas de produire un pack unique. --max-mib accepte 18 à 4 096 Mio , avec 512 Mio par défaut. --max-objects accepte 1 à 1 048 576 , avec 65 536 par défaut. Ces plafonds s'appliquent aux packs créés par la commande, en-tête de 8 octets inclus pour le plafond d'octets. Les enveloppes sont conservées entières ; une enveloppe maximale de 17 Mio doit pouvoir tenir. Les réglages C++ sont PackOptions::max_pack_bytes , max_objects_per_pack et compact . Le minimum C++ exact est 17 Mio + 8 octets. Le plan est calculé à partir des tailles stockées, puis les limites sont à nouveau appliquées aux enveloppes effectivement lues. Un plan dépassant le nombre disponible de packs est refusé avant la construction. Il faut alors augmenter les limites de création ou utiliser --compact pour regrouper les packs existants. Le résultat JSON distingue objects , pack_count , pack_bytes et index_bytes du catalogue final, de new_packs , objects_written , pack_bytes_written et index_bytes_written pour les nouvelles publications physiques. Les compteurs d'octets portent sur les fichiers complets pack/index. loose_objects_removed et obsolete_pack_files_removed décrivent les retraits physiques. La validation complète lit toujours le stock existant : le regroupement incrémental réduit les réécritures, sans rendre cette validation constante en temps. Publication et reprise # Sous le verrou exclusif du store, pack : Recharge la configuration et les index, vérifie les objets, leurs dépendances et les hashes complets des fichiers, y compris les objets non épinglés. Prépare le plan, écrit les nouveaux packs en flux, puis leurs index, avec les publications atomiques et flushs du moteur. Relit les nouvelles enveloppes et revalide leurs identifiants, formats et authentification. Remplace atomiquement config.json , qui publie le catalogue complet. Retire les copies isolées et les générations devenues inactives. Une erreur avant le remplacement de la configuration conserve l'ancien catalogue et ses copies. Un lot peut laisser des packs non publiés si la construction d'un pack suivant échoue. Après publication, une interruption peut laisser des doublons et d'anciens packs ; le catalogue désigne déjà les nouvelles copies complètes. pack ou gc --delete nettoient ces restes après validation. Seuls les noms générés pack-\u003c32 caractères hexadécimaux>.syp/.syi inactifs sont concernés ; les liens symboliques à ces emplacements sont refusés. Un échec de flush après le remplacement atomique peut survenir alors que le nouveau catalogue est déjà visible. Le handle recharge alors la configuration effective avant de rendre l'erreur ; un échec de rechargement invalide l'index en mémoire. Il faut corriger le problème et rouvrir le store ou réussir un nouvel audit avant de poursuivre les accès aux objets. GC sélectif # Le GC commence par valider la fermeture de toutes les références et les hashes des fichiers conservés, sans utiliser les données du cache. Une racine absente ou corrompue arrête l'opération avant toute suppression. Supprimer une référence ne supprime pas un bloc encore utilisé par une autre branche, archive, révision ou pin. Chaque pack est traité séparément : Tous ses objets sont conservés : ses fichiers restent inchangés. Aucun n'est conservé : il est retiré du catalogue, sans recopier son contenu. Une partie est conservée : un nouveau pack contient ses seuls objets vivants, avec relecture vérifiée avant publication. Toutes les modifications de catalogue du GC sont publiées ensemble avant le retrait des anciens fichiers. Les objets isolés conservés restent isolés. Le GC ne fusionne pas les packs ; utiliser pack --compact pour cela. Le pack de remplacement ne dépasse pas le nombre d'objets ni la taille du pack source, même si celui-ci provient d'un ancien store avec des packs plus grands que les réglages actuels par défaut. Le GC simulé ne crée ni pack ni configuration. reclaimable_bytes additionne les enveloppes inaccessibles de toutes leurs copies physiques, hors en-têtes/index. repack_required indique si le catalogue contient des objets à retirer. packs_to_rewrite , packs_to_drop et pack_bytes_to_write annoncent les packs à recopier, les packs entièrement morts et les octets des seuls nouveaux packs de remplacement, en-têtes inclus mais index exclus. obsolete_pack_files et obsolete_pack_bytes décrivent séparément les restes déjà inactifs au début de l'opération. Mémoire du GC # Après un GC interrompu, des métadonnées isolées non référencées peuvent encore attendre leur suppression alors que leurs blocs morts ont quitté le catalogue. Les versions conservées restent complètes, mais fsck peut signaler ces dépendances orphelines. Reprendre le GC termine le nettoyage avant l'audit complet ; voir la procédure et ses limites . Le GC parcourt les index par pages de 256 entrées. Le marquage des objets empaquetés utilise somme(ceil(objets_du_pack / 8)) octets, soit 128 Kio pour 1 048 576 objets dans un pack , hors overhead des conteneurs. Il conserve seulement un compteur de survivants et une taille par pack. Les objets morts et les survivants à recopier ne sont plus rassemblés dans des listes d'identifiants. Le constructeur de pack en flux vérifie le nombre annoncé et l'ordre strict des clés, puis la relecture contrôle toutes les nouvelles enveloppes avant publication. Les formats physiques restent identiques. Les objets vivants absents du catalogue de packs utilisent encore un ensemble de clés binaires typées de 33 octets, avec l'overhead d'un arbre ordonné. Un store uniquement en fichiers isolés conserve donc un coût de marquage proportionnel à ses objets vivants. La pile de parcours, les références, les métadonnées JSON, les codecs, les répertoires/filtres des index et leurs caches s'ajoutent au bitmap. Le GC n'annonce pas un plafond global de RAM. L'inventaire des objets isolés est parcouru une première fois pour valider toute son arborescence et préparer les compteurs, avant toute écriture de remplacement ou suppression. Après publication éventuelle, un second parcours retire les objets isolés morts. Les copies à la fois isolées et empaquetées comptent une fois dans live_objects / unreachable_objects , et chaque copie morte contribue aux octets récupérables. Le nettoyage utilise le nouveau catalogue après publication : les positions du bitmap de l'ancien catalogue ne sont jamais réinterprétées dans le nouveau. Le résultat JSON ajoute trois compteurs décrivant le travail de l'opération : packed_mark_bytes pour les octets utiles du bitmap initial, loose_live_objects pour les clés vivantes absentes du catalogue initial et peak_pending_objects pour le maximum de clés en attente dans la pile. Ils ne mesurent ni le RSS ni toutes les allocations. Les caches vérifiés restent exclus de la validation des racines. Le verrou exclusif couvre les deux parcours et la publication. Limites actuelles # Le catalogue accepte 1 024 packs , 1 048 576 objets par pack et 4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total . La taille binaire totale des index reste 16 × packs + 45 × objets octets. Le lecteur les parcourt à l'ouverture puis consulte des pages de 256 entrées, avec un répertoire, un filtre et une LRU de 4 Mio par défaut ( --index-cache-mib ). La validation des doublons entre packs utilise des parcours fusionnés. Voir les garanties de snapshot et budgets mémoire . Ces plafonds ne constituent pas une validation de charge du moteur complet à plusieurs millions d'objets. La construction lit les objets en flux, sans charger les packs entiers en RAM. La compaction nécessite l'espace des nouveaux packs avant de retirer les anciens. Elle conserve les enveloppes, sans les recompresser ni changer la clé. Une reconstruction, une fermeture de dépendances ou un audit réutilise au plus 8 handles de packs dans sa session, avec éviction LRU ; les appels imbriqués partagent cette session. Tous ces handles sont fermés au retour, y compris sur exception, et avant publication/suppression de générations. Les lectures isolées ouvrent leur propre handle. Chaque enveloppe reste relue et contrôlée avant tout hit du cache. Les arbres de métadonnées paginés, le choix automatique des packs à fusionner et la maintenance en ligne avec lecteurs concurrents restent à développer. objects() et l'API publique de fermeture des dépendances matérialisent encore des inventaires proportionnels au nombre d'objets. Les plans d'empaquetage/compaction sont désormais en flux : les index empaquetés sont fusionnés par pages et seules les clés isolées restent à trier. Voir les garanties de compaction . fsck parcourt désormais l'inventaire en flux avec un bitmap pour les objets empaquetés ; voir les garanties et limites des audits . Voir aussi le format binaire , les mesures et la feuille de route . syrnck --store donnees pack syrnck --store donnees pack --compact syrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck --store donnees gc syrnck --store donnees gc --delete","headings":[{"id":"packs-et-compaction","text":"Packs et compaction","level":1},{"id":"activation-et-compatibilite","text":"Activation et compatibilité","level":2},{"id":"regroupement-incremental-et-complet","text":"Regroupement incrémental et complet","level":2},{"id":"publication-et-reprise","text":"Publication et reprise","level":2},{"id":"gc-selectif","text":"GC sélectif","level":2},{"id":"memoire-du-gc","text":"Mémoire du GC","level":2},{"id":"limites-actuelles","text":"Limites actuelles","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store donnees pack\nsyrnck --store donnees pack --compact\nsyrnck --store donnees pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\nsyrnck --store donnees gc\nsyrnck --store donnees gc --delete","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/crash-recovery/interrupted-garbage-collection","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/compaction","#/fr/audit","#/fr/protocol","#/fr/performance","#/fr/limits"],"kind":"guide","revision":"e9d858274907"},{"id":"parallel-transfers","title":"Transferts parallèles bornés","description":"Guide : Transferts parallèles bornés.","section":"Transferts","order":460,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-parallel-object-transfers\">Transferts parallèles bornés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers/bounded-parallel-object-transfers\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Transferts parallèles bornés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>L’agent chevauche les requêtes HTTP d’objets avec l’interface multi de libcurl. Lectures/imports du Store, découverte de dépendances et callbacks restent sur le coordinateur appelant ; aucun handle Store n’est partagé avec des threads HTTP.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Défauts : une requête objet et budget de 64 Mio. \u003ccode>--requests\u003c/code> accepte 1..32 ; \u003ccode>--transfer-buffer-mib\u003c/code>, 17..1024. C++ reçoit \u003ccode>net::TransferOptions\u003c/code> après \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code>, cinquième argument de push/pull. Les appels antérieurs restent séquentiels.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push négocie les clés absentes par lots de 4096 maximum, vérifie sa source et prépare les payloads sur le coordinateur. Pull découvre les dépendances dans les métadonnées reçues vérifiées. L’ensemble visité évite les téléchargements répétés, même déjà planifiés. Les objets locaux sont lus une fois par \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> vérifié, sans lecture de présence supplémentaire. L’import est validé avant parcours ou publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-counters\">Budgets et compteurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers/limits-and-counters\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Budgets et compteurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’ordonnanceur facture la capacité réelle du vecteur d’envoi et un tampon de réponse réservé par requête. Chaque téléchargement réserve 16 Mio logiques ; chaque accusé d’upload réserve 4 Kio et une réponse plus grande échoue. Avant décodage d’un nouvel upload, le budget doit contenir un objet maximum et son accusé : la concurrence réelle peut être inférieure à la demande.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">compression négociée\u003c/a> facture aussi le candidat compressé tant que l’original vit, même s’il est abandonné. Les téléchargements compressés décodent directement dans la réponse réservée. Contextes des codecs hors crédits. Les vecteurs de réponse réservent leur capacité à la première donnée, évitant doubles tampons de croissance. Les crédits restent dus pendant consommation ; la requête terminée est retirée de multi avant cela, les autres restent actives. Attentes multi-poll bornées et réutilisation des connexions sont possibles dans une session.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les statistiques conservent objets/octets transférés et objets réutilisés. \u003ccode>peak_object_requests\u003c/code> est le maximum de requêtes planifiées simultanées ; \u003ccode>peak_object_buffer_bytes\u003c/code>, le maximum des crédits ; \u003ccode>object_retries\u003c/code>, les tentatives objet supplémentaires ; \u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code>, les corps encodés observés, reprises et réponses partielles/accusés compris.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces crédits incluent les capacités réservées, pas seulement les octets reçus ; ils ne sont pas le RSS. Ils excluent allocations internes, libcurl/TLS, caches Store, codecs, clés de parcours, métadonnées et contrôles séquentiels. Un transfert entièrement réutilisé peut avoir zéro requête objet tout en négociant/publiant une référence. Aucun budget mémoire global n’est promis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-and-publication\">Échec et publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers/failure-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Échec et publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Échec non récupérable ou budget de reprises épuisé, réponse corrompue, exception d’observateur ou arrêt coopératif retirent les handles restants et libèrent les payloads hors callbacks libcurl. Les exceptions C sont capturées puis relancées par le coordinateur. Les anciennes racines restent complètes ; les objets vérifiés non référencés se réutilisent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les attentes de reprise gardent slot et crédits tandis que d’autres requêtes avancent. Les cibles de débit sont partagées ; voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy\">politique\u003c/a>. Les événements de chemins différents peuvent s’entrelacer, avec compteurs propres à chaque requête. La fin d’une requête ne publie rien. Quand tout est présent, la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers/publication-boundary\">frontière de publication\u003c/a> reste inchangée : écriture locale annulable avant engagement, CAS distant terminé après envoi, code 3 en cas d’incertitude et aucun rejeu automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le serveur sérialise toujours les accès Store sous mutex pendant que les IO HTTP se chevauchent. Imports et parcours restent coordonnés. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">journaux\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">baux d’envoi\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">baux de lecture\u003c/a> protègent la reprise. Le renouvellement utilise un worker/une connexion de contrôle indépendants, hors compteurs objet ; les inscriptions d’inventaire sont aussi hors crédits. La compression est disponible par \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">négociation\u003c/a>. Rétention distribuée et planification multi-source restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Transferts parallèles bornés # L’agent chevauche les requêtes HTTP d’objets avec l’interface multi de libcurl. Lectures/imports du Store, découverte de dépendances et callbacks restent sur le coordinateur appelant ; aucun handle Store n’est partagé avec des threads HTTP. Défauts : une requête objet et budget de 64 Mio. --requests accepte 1..32 ; --transfer-buffer-mib , 17..1024. C++ reçoit net::TransferOptions après OperationOptions , cinquième argument de push/pull. Les appels antérieurs restent séquentiels. Push négocie les clés absentes par lots de 4096 maximum, vérifie sa source et prépare les payloads sur le coordinateur. Pull découvre les dépendances dans les métadonnées reçues vérifiées. L’ensemble visité évite les téléchargements répétés, même déjà planifiés. Les objets locaux sont lus une fois par get vérifié, sans lecture de présence supplémentaire. L’import est validé avant parcours ou publication. text Copier syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json Budgets et compteurs # L’ordonnanceur facture la capacité réelle du vecteur d’envoi et un tampon de réponse réservé par requête. Chaque téléchargement réserve 16 Mio logiques ; chaque accusé d’upload réserve 4 Kio et une réponse plus grande échoue. Avant décodage d’un nouvel upload, le budget doit contenir un objet maximum et son accusé : la concurrence réelle peut être inférieure à la demande. La compression négociée facture aussi le candidat compressé tant que l’original vit, même s’il est abandonné. Les téléchargements compressés décodent directement dans la réponse réservée. Contextes des codecs hors crédits. Les vecteurs de réponse réservent leur capacité à la première donnée, évitant doubles tampons de croissance. Les crédits restent dus pendant consommation ; la requête terminée est retirée de multi avant cela, les autres restent actives. Attentes multi-poll bornées et réutilisation des connexions sont possibles dans une session. Les statistiques conservent objets/octets transférés et objets réutilisés. peak_object_requests est le maximum de requêtes planifiées simultanées ; peak_object_buffer_bytes , le maximum des crédits ; object_retries , les tentatives objet supplémentaires ; object_upload_bytes et object_download_bytes , les corps encodés observés, reprises et réponses partielles/accusés compris. Ces crédits incluent les capacités réservées, pas seulement les octets reçus ; ils ne sont pas le RSS. Ils excluent allocations internes, libcurl/TLS, caches Store, codecs, clés de parcours, métadonnées et contrôles séquentiels. Un transfert entièrement réutilisé peut avoir zéro requête objet tout en négociant/publiant une référence. Aucun budget mémoire global n’est promis. Échec et publication # Échec non récupérable ou budget de reprises épuisé, réponse corrompue, exception d’observateur ou arrêt coopératif retirent les handles restants et libèrent les payloads hors callbacks libcurl. Les exceptions C sont capturées puis relancées par le coordinateur. Les anciennes racines restent complètes ; les objets vérifiés non référencés se réutilisent. Les attentes de reprise gardent slot et crédits tandis que d’autres requêtes avancent. Les cibles de débit sont partagées ; voir la politique . Les événements de chemins différents peuvent s’entrelacer, avec compteurs propres à chaque requête. La fin d’une requête ne publie rien. Quand tout est présent, la frontière de publication reste inchangée : écriture locale annulable avant engagement, CAS distant terminé après envoi, code 3 en cas d’incertitude et aucun rejeu automatique. Le serveur sérialise toujours les accès Store sous mutex pendant que les IO HTTP se chevauchent. Imports et parcours restent coordonnés. Les journaux , baux d’envoi , inventaires et baux de lecture protègent la reprise. Le renouvellement utilise un worker/une connexion de contrôle indépendants, hors compteurs objet ; les inscriptions d’inventaire sont aussi hors crédits. La compression est disponible par négociation . Rétention distribuée et planification multi-source restent à développer. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"bounded-parallel-object-transfers","text":"Transferts parallèles bornés","level":1},{"id":"limits-and-counters","text":"Budgets et compteurs","level":2},{"id":"failure-and-publication","text":"Échec et publication","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/network-compression","#/fr/transfer-policy","#/fr/transfers/publication-boundary","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/upload-inventories","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/network-compression"],"kind":"guide","revision":"3b24cff557ad"},{"id":"performance","title":"Performance et budgets de ressources","description":"Performance et budgets de ressources","section":"Stockage","order":90,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"2","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"budgets\">Choisir les budgets du corpus\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/performance/budgets\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Choisir les budgets du corpus\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Cache décodé : 64 Mio ; empreintes : 8192 entrées ; pages d’index : 4 Mio. Ces budgets indépendants ne bornent pas toute la RAM. L’ingestion utilise un worker et 32 Mio bruts en attente par défaut ; les workers ajoutent codecs/sorties. Requêtes/compression réseau ont leurs bornes distinctes. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/settings\">réglages\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">index\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers\">transferts parallèles\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">compression\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Objets et manifestes/révisions plats : 16 Mio. Catalogue : 1024 packs, 1 048 576 entrées par pack, 4 194 304 au total. Les plafonds ne prouvent pas une échelle validée. Des assets Unreal réécrits/compressés/chiffrés en amont peuvent peu se dédupliquer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measurement\">Interpréter les compteurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/performance/measurement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Interpréter les compteurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>io_stats\u003c/code> compte lectures/octets d’enveloppes réussis, décodages et hits ; les champs mémoire d’index couvrent capacités utiles de répertoire/filtre/pages sans allocateurs ni cache système. Le GC expose marques empaquetées, clés vivantes isolées et pic de pile. Aucun ne mesure le RSS global.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’ouverture valide linéairement les index complets. Audits/GC/compaction en flux réduisent les inventaires empaquetés, mais clés isolées, métadonnées, piles, codecs et caches coûtent toujours. Checkout reconstruit les fichiers changés complets et utilise espace privé/validation complète. Watch cible des sous-arbres entre audits complets. Rapports synthétiques et scripts de reproduction restent des données développeur hors manuel ; leurs durées ne promettent pas un débit universel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Capture incrémentale des fichiers de travail\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/performance/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Capture incrémentale des fichiers de travail\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Agents natifs résidents et captures C/Go réutilisent les manifestes ingérés par\nleur session si parent publié, contexte et matérialisation correspondent, avec\nidentité et empreinte de changement fiables inchangées. Ils inventorient toute\nla sélection : renommages, suppressions et exclusions. Fermer puis rouvrir le\nStore conserve la preuve de session ; index de statut et notifications ne la\nfournissent jamais. La fermeture complète reste vérifiée, parents historiques et\nmanifestes réutilisés compris ; corruption ou absence refusent la publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fichiers récents (garde de deux secondes), écrivains Windows actifs et systèmes\ndistants/non pris en charge utilisent l’ingestion ordinaire. Empreintes sur\nNTFS/ReFS local, ext4/XFS/Btrfs/tmpfs Linux et APFS macOS. Redémarrage,\ninvalidation explicite, échec natif ou changement de parent/sélection effacent la\nréutilisation. \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code> ponctuel continue de lire toutes les sources.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le JSON C ajoute \u003ccode>capture: {version, ingested_files, reused_files, source_bytes_read, source_bytes_reused}\u003c/code>. Go expose \u003ccode>CaptureReportContext\u003c/code> ;\n\u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code> retourne toujours l’ID. C++ expose \u003ccode>WorkspaceCapture\u003c/code> dans\n\u003ccode>&lt;syrnck/workspace.hpp&gt;\u003c/code>. Les compteurs couvrent les sources, sans métadonnées,\nvérification de fermeture ni cache système.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Construire \u003ccode>syrnck_capture_benchmark\u003c/code>, puis lancer :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe CHEMIN_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ajouter \u003ccode>--encrypted\u003c/code> pour le chiffrement au repos. Ce banc synthétique alterne\ncaptures complètes/de session, inclut ouverture et publication complète, sépare\nles E/S du Store et vérifie chaque résultat par lecture complète. Il ne garantit\naucun débit sur un projet Unreal. Vérification historique et métadonnées plates\nrestent des limites de passage à l’échelle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une capture réussie initialise l’observateur avec son inventaire sélectionné\ncomplet ; le tick natif suivant peut cibler les chemins changés. Seuls les lots\nantérieurs sont consommés ; les notifications pendant/après capture restent en\nattente. Un audit périodique impose toujours une lecture complète. Ce statut\nreste une observation à l’instant de capture, pas un instantané atomique des\nécritures concurrentes.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Choisir les budgets du corpus # Cache décodé : 64 Mio ; empreintes : 8192 entrées ; pages d’index : 4 Mio. Ces budgets indépendants ne bornent pas toute la RAM. L’ingestion utilise un worker et 32 Mio bruts en attente par défaut ; les workers ajoutent codecs/sorties. Requêtes/compression réseau ont leurs bornes distinctes. Voir réglages , index , transferts parallèles , compression . Objets et manifestes/révisions plats : 16 Mio. Catalogue : 1024 packs, 1 048 576 entrées par pack, 4 194 304 au total. Les plafonds ne prouvent pas une échelle validée. Des assets Unreal réécrits/compressés/chiffrés en amont peuvent peu se dédupliquer. Interpréter les compteurs # io_stats compte lectures/octets d’enveloppes réussis, décodages et hits ; les champs mémoire d’index couvrent capacités utiles de répertoire/filtre/pages sans allocateurs ni cache système. Le GC expose marques empaquetées, clés vivantes isolées et pic de pile. Aucun ne mesure le RSS global. L’ouverture valide linéairement les index complets. Audits/GC/compaction en flux réduisent les inventaires empaquetés, mais clés isolées, métadonnées, piles, codecs et caches coûtent toujours. Checkout reconstruit les fichiers changés complets et utilise espace privé/validation complète. Watch cible des sous-arbres entre audits complets. Rapports synthétiques et scripts de reproduction restent des données développeur hors manuel ; leurs durées ne promettent pas un débit universel. Capture incrémentale des fichiers de travail # Agents natifs résidents et captures C/Go réutilisent les manifestes ingérés par leur session si parent publié, contexte et matérialisation correspondent, avec identité et empreinte de changement fiables inchangées. Ils inventorient toute la sélection : renommages, suppressions et exclusions. Fermer puis rouvrir le Store conserve la preuve de session ; index de statut et notifications ne la fournissent jamais. La fermeture complète reste vérifiée, parents historiques et manifestes réutilisés compris ; corruption ou absence refusent la publication. Fichiers récents (garde de deux secondes), écrivains Windows actifs et systèmes distants/non pris en charge utilisent l’ingestion ordinaire. Empreintes sur NTFS/ReFS local, ext4/XFS/Btrfs/tmpfs Linux et APFS macOS. Redémarrage, invalidation explicite, échec natif ou changement de parent/sélection effacent la réutilisation. native commit ponctuel continue de lire toutes les sources. Le JSON C ajoute capture: {version, ingested_files, reused_files, source_bytes_read, source_bytes_reused} . Go expose CaptureReportContext ; CaptureContext retourne toujours l’ID. C++ expose WorkspaceCapture dans \u003csyrnck/workspace.hpp> . Les compteurs couvrent les sources, sans métadonnées, vérification de fermeture ni cache système. Construire syrnck_capture_benchmark , puis lancer : text Copier python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe CHEMIN_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json Ajouter --encrypted pour le chiffrement au repos. Ce banc synthétique alterne captures complètes/de session, inclut ouverture et publication complète, sépare les E/S du Store et vérifie chaque résultat par lecture complète. Il ne garantit aucun débit sur un projet Unreal. Vérification historique et métadonnées plates restent des limites de passage à l’échelle. Une capture réussie initialise l’observateur avec son inventaire sélectionné complet ; le tick natif suivant peut cibler les chemins changés. Seuls les lots antérieurs sont consommés ; les notifications pendant/après capture restent en attente. Un audit périodique impose toujours une lecture complète. Ce statut reste une observation à l’instant de capture, pas un instantané atomique des écritures concurrentes. python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe CHEMIN_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json","headings":[{"id":"budgets","text":"Choisir les budgets du corpus","level":2},{"id":"measurement","text":"Interpréter les compteurs","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Capture incrémentale des fichiers de travail","level":2}],"anchors":["L1"],"languages":["text"],"code":"python scripts/benchmark_capture.py --exe CHEMIN_BENCHMARK --mib 128 --files 64 --iterations 5 --output capture.json","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/settings","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/parallel-transfers","#/fr/network-compression"],"kind":"guide","revision":"6fa189080679"},{"id":"protocol","title":"Formats et protocole","description":"Guide : Formats et protocole.","section":"Stockage","order":10,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"12","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"formats-et-protocole-experimentaux\">Formats et protocole expérimentaux\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/formats-et-protocole-experimentaux\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Formats et protocole expérimentaux\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le format est un contrat initial à versionner, pas une promesse de compatibilité définitive. La version du format d&#39;objet est distincte de la version du binaire et de l&#39;ABI C.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identifiants-et-objets\">Identifiants et objets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/identifiants-et-objets\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Identifiants et objets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L&#39;identifiant est le BLAKE3 sur :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les types autorisés sont \u003ccode>chunk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>. L&#39;ID contient exactement 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules. La clé complète est \u003ccode>{kind, id}\u003c/code>. L&#39;espace de noms sépare donc un bloc des métadonnées contenant les mêmes octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un bloc contient de 1 octet à 16 Mio. Un fichier vide possède zéro bloc et le hash BLAKE3 du flux vide. Tous les objets non compressés sont limités à 16 Mio, y compris les métadonnées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les manifestes sont du JSON UTF-8 compact, sans espaces superflus ni newline finale. Les champs sont écrits dans cet ordre : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>chunking\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blocks\u003c/code>. Chaque bloc contient \u003ccode>id\u003c/code>, puis \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>. La configuration CDC contient \u003ccode>algorithm: &quot;fast-cdc2020&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>min\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>avg\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max\u003c/code> ; le mode fixe contient \u003ccode>algorithm: &quot;fixed&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>. Le nom sérialisé exact fait foi et est vérifiable avec \u003ccode>inspect manifest\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La politique CDC C++ reproduit les frontières de \u003ccode>fastcdc::v2020::StreamCDC::new\u003c/code> de fastcdc 5.0.0, normalisation par défaut niveau 1, seed par défaut 0. Les paramètres sont enregistrés pour ne pas dépendre du choix par défaut d&#39;une future version du CLI. Une modification des frontières produites exigera une nouvelle valeur d&#39;algorithme ou de format.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le hash \u003ccode>file_hash\u003c/code> est le BLAKE3 ordinaire des octets complets du fichier, sans domaine. La somme des tailles de blocs doit égaler \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>. La reconstruction vérifie aussi les tailles réelles et ce hash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le nombre de workers et le budget de l&#39;ingestion ne sont pas sérialisés dans le manifeste : ils ne modifient ni les frontières, ni l&#39;ordre des blocs, ni leurs identifiants. La taille sérialisée du manifeste plat est contrôlée pendant la lecture, avant sa publication. Les statistiques d&#39;ingestion exposent séparément \u003ccode>peak_in_flight_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>peak_in_flight_blocks\u003c/code> ; ces compteurs ne font pas partie du format d&#39;objet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les révisions sont du JSON compact dans l&#39;ordre \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>parents\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>message\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>timestamp\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>files\u003c/code>. Les chemins de \u003ccode>files\u003c/code> sont triés selon l&#39;ordre des chaînes UTF-8. Une entrée contient \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code>, puis \u003ccode>executable\u003c/code>. L&#39;échappement des chaînes conserve le format v1 du prototype initial, vérifié par les fixtures de migration et produit par \u003ccode>nlohmann::ordered_json\u003c/code> ; tout document dont la re-sérialisation diffère est refusé. Les champs inconnus sont refusés. Une implémentation indépendante devra tester ses encodages contre des vecteurs de référence avant de produire des objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authored-revisions\">Révisions avec auteurs v2\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/authored-revisions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Révisions avec auteurs v2\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La v2 facultative ajoute \u003ccode>author,committer,signature\u003c/code> entre \u003ccode>timestamp\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>files\u003c/code>.\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/format\">Le format des auteurs\u003c/a> définit identités datées,\nchamps stricts de signature et message signé avec domaine. L’exigence de lecteur\nprécède tout objet v2. Manifests v1, domaine d’ID, enveloppes, packs, dépendances\net transport HTTP d’objets restent inchangés. Snapshots de travail internes et\nproducteurs anciens sans identité restent v1.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stockage-local\">Stockage local\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/stockage-local\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage local\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">&lt;store>/config.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">&lt;store>/refs.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">&lt;store>/store.lock\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">&lt;store>/objects/&lt;type>/&lt;deux-premiers-caractères>/&lt;id>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>L&#39;enveloppe binaire commence par \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code>, puis un octet de mode. Mode 0 : contenu Zstd. Mode 1 : nonce de 24 octets, puis chiffrement XChaCha20-Poly1305 du contenu Zstd, tag inclus. Les données associées sont l&#39;ASCII de \u003ccode>syrnck-envelope-v1:&lt;type&gt;:&lt;id&gt;\u003c/code>. Un objet chiffré ne peut pas être déplacé sous une autre clé sans échec d&#39;authentification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans packs, la configuration de base contient \u003ccode>version: 1\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>key_check\u003c/code>. Pour un store chiffré, \u003ccode>key_check\u003c/code> est BLAKE3 keyed du texte \u003ccode>syrnck-store-key-check-v1\u003c/code> avec la clé du store ; sinon il vaut \u003ccode>null\u003c/code>. Les noms de références et IDs restent en clair dans \u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le cache de lecture est uniquement en mémoire et n&#39;ajoute aucun fichier ni champ à ce format. Ses entrées ne sont ni des racines de rétention ni une autorité permettant d&#39;ignorer un objet manquant. Elles proviennent d&#39;un objet validé dans le handle courant et ne sont réutilisées qu&#39;après comparaison cryptographique de l&#39;enveloppe relue. Le nombre de lectures et de décodages peut changer sans modifier les octets logiques des objets ou les règles de validation des hashes de fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs\">Stockage physique v2 avec packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage physique v2 avec packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Pour un seul pack, l&#39;appel explicite à \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> peut publier une configuration de base de trois champs : \u003ccode>version: 2\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>key_check\u003c/code> inchangé, et \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> vaut \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> s&#39;il n&#39;y a aucun pack actif ; sinon il contient exactement \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> (32 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules), \u003ccode>objects\u003c/code> (nombre entier positif), \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code> (taille du pack), \u003ccode>index_hash\u003c/code> (BLAKE3 ordinaire de tout l&#39;index, hexadécimal minuscule). La configuration est le pointeur de publication atomique. Les objets logiques, références, enveloppes et messages réseau gardent leur format v1. Les anciens lecteurs de configuration v1 doivent refuser v2.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les fichiers actifs se trouvent à \u003ccode>packs/pack-&lt;name&gt;.syp\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>packs/pack-&lt;name&gt;.syi\u003c/code>. Le pack commence par les 8 octets ASCII \u003ccode>SYRPACK1\u003c/code>, suivis des enveloppes \u003ccode>SYR1\u003c/code> concaténées sans modification. Il n&#39;y a ni espace libre ni donnée non indexée dans une génération. Tous les entiers binaires sont non signés en little-endian.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;index commence par les 8 octets ASCII \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code>, puis un entier de 8 octets donnant le nombre d&#39;objets. Chaque entrée de 45 octets contient :\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Champ\u003c/th>\n\u003cth align=\"right\">Taille\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Représentation\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Type\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">1 octet\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 = chunk, 1 = manifest, 2 = revision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ID\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">32 octets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hash binaire, sans encodage hexadécimal\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Offset\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">8 octets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Position absolue du début de l&#39;enveloppe dans le pack\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Longueur\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd align=\"right\">4 octets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Taille de l&#39;enveloppe complète\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Les entrées sont strictement triées par type puis ID ; aucun doublon n&#39;est admis. Les plages doivent être contiguës depuis l&#39;offset 8 jusqu&#39;à \u003ccode>bytes\u003c/code>. La longueur d&#39;une enveloppe est comprise entre 5 octets et 17 Mio, limite existante du lecteur d&#39;objets. Le nombre d&#39;entrées actuel est limité à 1 048 576 et la taille du pack à \u003ccode>INT64_MAX\u003c/code>. La taille de l&#39;index doit être exactement \u003ccode>16 + 45 × objects\u003c/code> et son hash doit correspondre à la configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;ouverture et les audits rechargent et vérifient cet index. À l&#39;ouverture d&#39;une session de lecture du pack, le moteur contrôle le fichier, sa taille et son en-tête. Une reconstruction ou un audit réutilise ce handle pendant son opération puis le ferme ; les lectures isolées ouvrent leur propre session. Chaque objet relit les octets de l&#39;enveloppe ciblée, dont le contenu doit ensuite satisfaire les mêmes règles d&#39;authentification, décompression, ID et format que les fichiers isolés. Le cache ne dispense pas de relire l&#39;enveloppe. Le hash d&#39;index détecte les altérations mais ne constitue pas une signature authentifiée du catalogue. Les IDs, types, positions et tailles restent visibles dans l&#39;index, même si les enveloppes sont chiffrées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les écritures nouvelles restent à \u003ccode>objects/...\u003c/code>. La lecture ordinaire privilégie un fichier isolé lorsqu&#39;un objet existe aussi dans le pack ; l&#39;inventaire retourne une seule clé logique. \u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> vérifie les deux copies. Les fichiers de packs non désignés par la configuration n&#39;ajoutent aucune racine de rétention. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs\">publication, reprise et compaction\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs\">Stockage physique v3 : catalogue de packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage physique v3 : catalogue de packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>La configuration v3 de base contient \u003ccode>version: 3\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>key_check\u003c/code> inchangé et \u003ccode>packs\u003c/code>, un tableau de descripteurs de packs au format v2 décrit ci-dessus. Le tableau peut être vide ou contenir un seul pack ; le moteur ne rétrograde pas implicitement un store v3. Plusieurs packs exigent v3, que les lecteurs v1/v2 doivent refuser. Les formats binaires \u003ccode>SYRPACK1\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code> restent inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les noms de packs doivent être distincts. Une clé \u003ccode>{kind, id}\u003c/code> ne peut figurer que dans un seul pack actif, même si deux copies physiques auraient un contenu identique. Les index sont vérifiés séparément, puis des parcours fusionnés contrôlent les clés lorsque leurs intervalles se recouvrent. Le catalogue est limité à 1 024 packs et à 4 194 304 objets au total, avec toujours au maximum 1 048 576 objets dans chaque index. Les tailles annoncées sont contrôlées avant la lecture des index ; les plages et contenus restent vérifiés ensuite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La maintenance ordinaire ajoute les nouveaux packs au tableau. Une compaction explicite peut en remplacer tout le contenu ; le GC peut en remplacer seulement une partie. Tous les fichiers d&#39;un lot sont écrits et vérifiés avant le remplacement atomique unique de la configuration. Un lot non publié reste inactif et ne remplace aucune ancienne copie. La présence d&#39;un fichier hors catalogue n&#39;ajoute aucun objet à l&#39;inventaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les sessions de reconstruction et d&#39;audit réutilisent au plus 8 handles de packs, avec éviction LRU et fermeture en fin d&#39;opération. Leur limite de handles est distincte des plafonds d&#39;index et du cache décodé. La validation de chaque enveloppe est identique au stockage v1/v2.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch3 id=\"consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format\">Consultation paginée sans changement de format\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Consultation paginée sans changement de format\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h3>\u003cp>Le lecteur conserve le format plat \u003ccode>SYRIDX01\u003c/code> et le même hash global. À l&#39;ouverture, il le parcourt en flux, construit des bornes et empreintes de pages de 256 enregistrements ainsi qu&#39;un filtre de clés absentes. Ces structures et le cache de pages sont uniquement en mémoire ; aucun en-tête, descripteur ou fichier auxiliaire n&#39;est ajouté au store. La construction des nouveaux index écrit également les enregistrements en flux, avec le même encodage binaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les recherches consultent les pages candidates ; chaque page relue doit correspondre à l&#39;empreinte du parcours validé avant utilisation de ses adresses. Les pages chaudes et la dernière adresse trouvée peuvent rester un snapshot du catalogue ouvert sous verrou, comme l&#39;ancienne table en RAM. Les audits rechargent les index et recontrôlent leurs hashes complets, indépendamment de ce cache. La vérification des enveloppes réellement lues reste obligatoire avant de retourner un objet. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">budgets, garanties et limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-head-formats\">Formats de sélection du workspace natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/native-head-formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Formats de sélection du workspace natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les descripteurs nommés \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> utilisent le JSON canonique v1 ; les\ndescripteurs détachés passent en v2 avec \u003ccode>branch: null\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type: &quot;native-head&quot;\u003c/code>\net le \u003ccode>directory\u003c/code> canonique. Racines privées : \u003ccode>workspace/detached\u003c/code> (révision)\net \u003ccode>workspace/staging\u003c/code> (révision préparée optionnelle). Les reçus de checkout\navec cible détachée utilisent v7 et \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> de 1 à 6 ; les layouts entre\nbranches gardent leurs anciennes versions. Les stash détachés utilisent v2 avec\nla cible \u003ccode>:HEAD\u003c/code>, les stash nommés restent en v1. Ces ajouts ne changent ni les\nrévisions immuables v1 ni les versions packs/ABI. Les anciens exécuteurs doivent\nrefuser les reçus inconnus. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-detached\">HEAD détaché\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pointeur-git\">Pointeur Git\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/pointeur-git\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Pointeur Git\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">version syrnck-pointer-v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">manifest blake3:&lt;64 caractères hexadécimaux>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">size &lt;entier décimal>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les trois lignes se terminent par LF. L&#39;encodage est canonique et le pointeur est limité à 1 024 octets. La taille doit correspondre au manifeste. Un document commençant par l&#39;en-tête réservé mais mal formé est une erreur. Ce format est distinct des pointeurs Git LFS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api-http\">API HTTP\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/api-http\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API HTTP\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque requête porte \u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer &lt;jeton&gt;\u003c/code>. Le serveur expose un seul store, avec autorité partagée historique ou \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">utilisateurs et permissions configurés\u003c/a>. HTTPS est requis côté client, à l&#39;exception de loopback. Le service HTTP écoute uniquement sur loopback et peut être exposé derrière un proxy TLS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Méthode et chemin\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Corps / réponse\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>POST /v1/missing\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tableau JSON de clés ; retourne le sous-ensemble absent ; maximum 4 096 entrées\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET /v1/objects/{kind}/{id}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Octets logiques ou trame Zstandard négociée, maximum 16 Mio\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/objects/{kind}/{id}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Même représentation ; vérifie type, hash et format ; 201 si créé, 204 si déjà présent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>GET /v1/refs\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Table JSON \u003ccode>nom → clé\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PUT /v1/refs/{name}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>{root: clé, expected: clé ou null}\u003c/code> ; valide toutes les dépendances avant publication\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Les noms distants utilisent un seul composant d&#39;URL. Les références locales peuvent employer \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>expected: null\u003c/code> exige l&#39;absence de référence ; une valeur exige une égalité exacte. Un conflit renvoie 409. Une dépendance absente renvoie 404 ; une corruption ou un objet invalide renvoie une erreur. Les réponses JSON peuvent employer n&#39;importe quel ordre de champs : la contrainte d&#39;encodage canonique porte sur les objets immuables, pas sur les enveloppes de requêtes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les objets peuvent arriver avant leurs dépendances. Ils ne sont pas publiés comme nouvelle version tant que la référence n&#39;a pas été validée et remplacée. Un retry du PUT d&#39;un objet déjà présent est idempotent. Le client désactive les redirections pour ne pas déplacer les requêtes authentifiées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette v1 n&#39;est compatible ni avec BEP, ni avec l&#39;API Git LFS, ni avec le protocole Lore. L&#39;extension négociée \u003ccode>GET /v1/capabilities\u003c/code> annonce les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">baux d&#39;envoi persistants\u003c/a>, leur durée et les reçus de publication. Les publications protégées ajoutent \u003ccode>upload: {id, fence}\u003c/code> au corps existant ; les requêtes historiques restent acceptées sauf si le serveur impose les baux. L&#39;extension \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">baux de lecture\u003c/a> annonce \u003ccode>read_leases: 1\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>read_lease_seconds\u003c/code>, avec acquisition/renouvellement sur \u003ccode>PUT /v1/reads/ID\u003c/code>, consultation sur \u003ccode>GET /v1/reads/ID\u003c/code> et libération sur \u003ccode>POST /v1/reads/ID/release\u003c/code>. Elle protège une racine exacte avant le téléchargement des blocs manquants. Les quotas d&#39;objets/utilisateurs et la réplication serveur restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;extension \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires d&#39;envoi\u003c/a> annonce \u003ccode>upload_inventories: 1\u003c/code>. La création du bail ajoute \u003ccode>inventory: true\u003c/code> ; \u003ccode>PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX\u003c/code> enregistre un lot de clés et son jeton avant la demande de présence. Les pages sont chaînées et leur nombre/empreinte finale sont conservés dans le bail de schéma 2. Les baux historiques de schéma 1 gardent leur protection globale. Le format logique des objets et l&#39;ABI C restent inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;extension de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">compression réseau\u003c/a> annonce \u003ccode>object_zstd: 1\u003c/code>. Les en-têtes \u003ccode>Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd\u003c/code> négocient une trame par objet. Les clients historiques continuent de recevoir des octets logiques. Le client vérifie taille, fenêtre, intégrité et format avant import ; les corps JSON et la publication CAS gardent leur représentation existante.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interface-c\">Interface C\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/interface-c\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Interface C\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L&#39;en-tête \u003ccode>include/syrnck.h\u003c/code> définit l&#39;ABI 1, annoncée par \u003ccode>syrnck_abi_version()\u003c/code>. Cette version est indépendante du format d&#39;objet v1 et de la version 0.1.0 du produit. Les fonctions renvoient 0 en cas de succès, 1 pour une erreur et 2 pour une exception C++ inconnue et 3 pour une annulation coopérative des opérations qui la prennent en charge. Aucune exception ne franchit l&#39;interface. Les chaînes sont UTF-8 terminées par NUL ; les handles doivent être sérialisés, fermeture comprise. Aucun pointeur d&#39;entrée n&#39;est conservé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_object_has\u003c/code> vérifie un objet présent ; une corruption n&#39;est pas assimilée à une absence. \u003ccode>syrnck_object_read\u003c/code> retourne les octets non compressés vérifiés. Une requête avec pointeur nul et capacité zéro donne la taille ; la lecture exige ensuite un buffer assez grand et n&#39;écrit rien si sa capacité est insuffisante. \u003ccode>syrnck_closure_json\u003c/code> suit le même contrat, mais sa longueur inclut le NUL final. \u003ccode>syrnck_object_import\u003c/code> valide hash et format, sans exiger que les dépendances soient déjà présentes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après import de tous les objets, \u003ccode>syrnck_ref_set\u003c/code> valide les dépendances et les hashes complets avant de publier le pin. Cette fonction remplace une référence locale sans compare-and-swap ; l&#39;API HTTP conserve son contrat CAS distinct. Les objets reçus avant publication ne sont pas automatiquement protégés du GC après fermeture du store. L&#39;intégrateur doit garder le store ouvert et sérialiser transfert/GC, puis conserver une racine avant fermeture pour garantir la rétention.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_last_error\u003c/code> est local au thread et reste valide jusqu&#39;au prochain appel qui modifie l&#39;erreur. Le diagnostic est borné à 4 095 octets pour éviter d&#39;allouer de la mémoire pendant le signalement d&#39;une erreur. \u003ccode>syrnck_error_copy\u003c/code> permet de le copier sans le modifier ; sa longueur inclut NUL. Un binding avec goroutines doit récupérer l&#39;erreur sur le même thread OS que l&#39;appel. Les buffers d&#39;objets appartiennent à l&#39;appelant ; seules les chaînes allouées par \u003ccode>syrnck_manifest_json\u003c/code> passent par \u003ccode>syrnck_string_free\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"linked-workspace-formats\">Enregistrements des workspaces liés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/linked-workspace-formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Enregistrements des workspaces liés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">workspaces liés\u003c/a> utilisent les vraies références\n\u003ccode>workspaces/NOM/\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>registration\u003c/code> conserve un chunk native-workspace v1 canonique\navec nom et dossier ; \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> conserve la sélection nommée/détachée.\nLes racines détachées sont privées. Les index nommés utilisent\n\u003ccode>staging/BLAKE3(nom-branche)\u003c/code> ; la révision préparée garde le nom complet.\nLes reçus v8 ajoutent \u003ccode>context\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>layout_version\u003c/code> 1..6 ; les layouts principaux\nv1..v7 ne changent pas. La sélection appartient au handle.\nUn contexte partiel conserve un chunk canonique native-materialization v1 sous\n\u003ccode>workspace/materialization\u003c/code>, avec tableau de préfixes littéraux disjoints triés.\nAbsence de chunk : tous les chemins ; tableau vide : aucun. Les reçus v9 figent\nles définitions source/cible, contexte et layout (1..6), sans déduire les actions\nde reprise depuis une sélection actuelle mutable. Les reçus statiques v9 exigent\ndeux définitions identiques ; l’édition directe d’une référence active est refusée.\nLe layout 5 du reset hard garde l’arbre source physique capturé complet, y compris\nles ancêtres faisant obstacle hors masque ; seule la cible logique est projetée.\nTête/index complets restent parents de cette récupération. Le plan de reset hard\npartiel v2 lie le tableau de préfixes et la clé de son objet de politique.\nLe layout 6 d’apply/pop projette la source logique et restaure la cible physique\ncomplète, ancêtres obstructifs sauvegardés compris. Son plan v2 lie les mêmes\nchamps de politique. Un descripteur stash partiel v3 ajoute \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code>\naux dix champs initiaux ; les descripteurs complets nommés/détachés restent\nv1/v2. Apply exige une politique sauvegardée/actuelle strictement identique ;\nla fusion d’index reste logique et complète, indépendante de la projection.\nLes transitions utilisent v10/layout 7 avec définitions figées différentes et\nles cinq champs tête/index/HEAD/profil de dossiers du layout 5. Source, cible et\ntête originale désignent la même révision logique complète ; index nul et profil\nde dossiers vide. Les deux chunks de politique restent protégés pendant la\ntransaction. Seul un CAS de journal/références correspondant, de applying/installing\nvers committed, peut publier la nouvelle sélection active. Reprise/abort avant\npublication utilisent la source ; nettoyage engagé utilise la cible. HEAD et\nbranche restent inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sans capacités requises, une sauvegarde de ces liaisons utilise le marqueur v2, conservant par\ncontexte un descripteur portable native-workspace-snapshot et la révision\n\u003ccode>snapshot\u003c/code>, sans liaisons absolues registration/HEAD/reçus. Les sauvegardes v1\nrestent lisibles. La restauration relie explicitement le snapshot à un nouveau dossier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-archive-formats\">Enregistrements d’archives de workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/workspace-archive-formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Enregistrements d’archives de workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un contexte lié désenregistré utilise un chunk canonique native-workspace-archive\nv1 à douze champs : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>type\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nonce\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>branch\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>index\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>work\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>materialization\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>include_untracked\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>references\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>archive/workspaces/ID/record\u003c/code> conserve ce chunk ; \u003ccode>/head\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> conservent\nHEAD logique complet et la révision physique de récupération. Chaque référence\noriginale possède une racine typée \u003ccode>/roots/BLAKE3(nom-original-référence)\u003c/code>.\nLa lecture exige le groupe exact complet et la recette canonique de liaisons.\nLa révision physique garde HEAD et l’éventuel index comme parents, avec timestamp\nnul et message \u003ccode>Syrnck workspace archive work v1: NOM\u003c/code>. La restauration garde\nles parents de l’index, même périmés. Le nouveau contexte conserve aussi le travail\nphysique sous \u003ccode>workspaces/NOUVEAU/restored/ARCHIVE_ID\u003c/code>. Ces racines sont typées\nordinaires ; formats d’objets, packs et ABI C inchangés. Voir les\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-archives\">opérations d’archive\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"workspace-relocation-formats\">Enregistrements de déplacement de workspace\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/workspace-relocation-formats\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Enregistrements de déplacement de workspace\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/format\">journal de déplacement\u003c/a> est un chunk\nimmuable native-workspace-relocation v1 sous \u003ccode>workspace-relocations/active\u003c/code>.\nIl fige chemins source/cible/intermédiaire, identités du dossier et des parents,\nvaleurs complètes des anciennes liaisons, hash des références et plan.\nUne nouvelle exigence de lecteur précède la publication. Le validateur central\nn’accepte que les transactions exactes de début et de fin ; les autres mutations\nsont bloquées pendant l’attente. La phase physique dépend de l’identité unique\nau nom exact source/intermédiaire/cible. Les racines finales\n\u003ccode>archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>/aborted\u003c/code> gardent le reçu inclus,\nsans dépendances d’objets cachées.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"required-store-features\">Capacités requises du Store\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/protocol/required-store-features\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Capacités requises du Store\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le journal natif v11 ajoute un \u003ccode>owner\u003c/code> hexadécimal minuscule de 64 caractères,\nun contexte et des définitions source/cible de matérialisation identiques. Son\nlayout 1 ou 3 couvre application ordinaire ou namespace, y compris workspaces\nliés, partiels et détachés. Propriétaire et définitions figées participent à\nl’identité de transaction. Les layouts sans propriétaire gardent leurs encodages.\nVoir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/owned-recovery\">reprise contrôlée\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les configurations v1/v2/v3 peuvent ajouter \u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code>, tableau non vide\ntrié de noms uniques pris en charge (au plus 32). Les noms acceptés sont\n\u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code>. Nom inconnu, doublon, liste vide ou type invalide bloquent\nl’ouverture. Le champ supplémentaire fait volontairement refuser la configuration\naux anciens lecteurs stricts avant lecture des objets/références. Les encodages\npacks/index/enveloppes et l’ABI C 1 ne changent pas ; les Stores classiques ne\nreçoivent aucun champ supplémentaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’activation publie atomiquement la configuration avant les liaisons qui\nl’exigent. Une interruption peut laisser l’exigence active sans liaison ; la\nreprise est idempotente. Packs et GC conservent le champ. Ajout seulement, sans\nretrait par l’API normale. Ces exigences de lecteur sont distinctes des capacités\nHTTP négociées et des réglages ajustables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le marqueur de sauvegarde v3 ajoute aux neuf champs existants un tableau non vide\n\u003ccode>required_features\u003c/code>, égal à celui de la configuration destination. Exclusions\nidentiques à v2. Les marqueurs v1/v2 décrivent des Stores sans capacités requises\net restent lisibles. Sauvegarde logique, restauration et copie réparée conservent\nles exigences de lecteur source.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Formats et protocole expérimentaux # Le format est un contrat initial à versionner, pas une promesse de compatibilité définitive. La version du format d'objet est distincte de la version du binaire et de l'ABI C. Identifiants et objets # L'identifiant est le BLAKE3 sur : text Copier ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé Les types autorisés sont chunk , manifest , revision . L'ID contient exactement 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules. La clé complète est {kind, id} . L'espace de noms sépare donc un bloc des métadonnées contenant les mêmes octets. Un bloc contient de 1 octet à 16 Mio. Un fichier vide possède zéro bloc et le hash BLAKE3 du flux vide. Tous les objets non compressés sont limités à 16 Mio, y compris les métadonnées. Les manifestes sont du JSON UTF-8 compact, sans espaces superflus ni newline finale. Les champs sont écrits dans cet ordre : version , size , file_hash , chunking , blocks . Chaque bloc contient id , puis size . La configuration CDC contient algorithm: \"fast-cdc2020\" , min , avg , max ; le mode fixe contient algorithm: \"fixed\" , size . Le nom sérialisé exact fait foi et est vérifiable avec inspect manifest . La politique CDC C++ reproduit les frontières de fastcdc::v2020::StreamCDC::new de fastcdc 5.0.0, normalisation par défaut niveau 1, seed par défaut 0. Les paramètres sont enregistrés pour ne pas dépendre du choix par défaut d'une future version du CLI. Une modification des frontières produites exigera une nouvelle valeur d'algorithme ou de format. Le hash file_hash est le BLAKE3 ordinaire des octets complets du fichier, sans domaine. La somme des tailles de blocs doit égaler size . La reconstruction vérifie aussi les tailles réelles et ce hash. Le nombre de workers et le budget de l'ingestion ne sont pas sérialisés dans le manifeste : ils ne modifient ni les frontières, ni l'ordre des blocs, ni leurs identifiants. La taille sérialisée du manifeste plat est contrôlée pendant la lecture, avant sa publication. Les statistiques d'ingestion exposent séparément peak_in_flight_bytes et peak_in_flight_blocks ; ces compteurs ne font pas partie du format d'objet. Les révisions sont du JSON compact dans l'ordre version , parents , message , timestamp , files . Les chemins de files sont triés selon l'ordre des chaînes UTF-8. Une entrée contient manifest , puis executable . L'échappement des chaînes conserve le format v1 du prototype initial, vérifié par les fixtures de migration et produit par nlohmann::ordered_json ; tout document dont la re-sérialisation diffère est refusé. Les champs inconnus sont refusés. Une implémentation indépendante devra tester ses encodages contre des vecteurs de référence avant de produire des objets. Révisions avec auteurs v2 # La v2 facultative ajoute author,committer,signature entre timestamp et files . Le format des auteurs définit identités datées, champs stricts de signature et message signé avec domaine. L’exigence de lecteur précède tout objet v2. Manifests v1, domaine d’ID, enveloppes, packs, dépendances et transport HTTP d’objets restent inchangés. Snapshots de travail internes et producteurs anciens sans identité restent v1. Stockage local # text Copier \u003cstore>/config.json \u003cstore>/refs.json \u003cstore>/store.lock \u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid> L'enveloppe binaire commence par SYR1 , puis un octet de mode. Mode 0 : contenu Zstd. Mode 1 : nonce de 24 octets, puis chiffrement XChaCha20-Poly1305 du contenu Zstd, tag inclus. Les données associées sont l'ASCII de syrnck-envelope-v1:\u003ctype>:\u003cid> . Un objet chiffré ne peut pas être déplacé sous une autre clé sans échec d'authentification. Sans packs, la configuration de base contient version: 1 et key_check . Pour un store chiffré, key_check est BLAKE3 keyed du texte syrnck-store-key-check-v1 avec la clé du store ; sinon il vaut null . Les noms de références et IDs restent en clair dans refs.json . Le cache de lecture est uniquement en mémoire et n'ajoute aucun fichier ni champ à ce format. Ses entrées ne sont ni des racines de rétention ni une autorité permettant d'ignorer un objet manquant. Elles proviennent d'un objet validé dans le handle courant et ne sont réutilisées qu'après comparaison cryptographique de l'enveloppe relue. Le nombre de lectures et de décodages peut changer sans modifier les octets logiques des objets ou les règles de validation des hashes de fichiers. Stockage physique v2 avec packs # Pour un seul pack, l'appel explicite à pack peut publier une configuration de base de trois champs : version: 2 , key_check inchangé, et pack . pack vaut null s'il n'y a aucun pack actif ; sinon il contient exactement name (32 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules), objects (nombre entier positif), bytes (taille du pack), index_hash (BLAKE3 ordinaire de tout l'index, hexadécimal minuscule). La configuration est le pointeur de publication atomique. Les objets logiques, références, enveloppes et messages réseau gardent leur format v1. Les anciens lecteurs de configuration v1 doivent refuser v2. Les fichiers actifs se trouvent à packs/pack-\u003cname>.syp et packs/pack-\u003cname>.syi . Le pack commence par les 8 octets ASCII SYRPACK1 , suivis des enveloppes SYR1 concaténées sans modification. Il n'y a ni espace libre ni donnée non indexée dans une génération. Tous les entiers binaires sont non signés en little-endian. L'index commence par les 8 octets ASCII SYRIDX01 , puis un entier de 8 octets donnant le nombre d'objets. Chaque entrée de 45 octets contient : Champ Taille Représentation Type 1 octet 0 = chunk, 1 = manifest, 2 = revision ID 32 octets Hash binaire, sans encodage hexadécimal Offset 8 octets Position absolue du début de l'enveloppe dans le pack Longueur 4 octets Taille de l'enveloppe complète Les entrées sont strictement triées par type puis ID ; aucun doublon n'est admis. Les plages doivent être contiguës depuis l'offset 8 jusqu'à bytes . La longueur d'une enveloppe est comprise entre 5 octets et 17 Mio, limite existante du lecteur d'objets. Le nombre d'entrées actuel est limité à 1 048 576 et la taille du pack à INT64_MAX . La taille de l'index doit être exactement 16 + 45 × objects et son hash doit correspondre à la configuration. L'ouverture et les audits rechargent et vérifient cet index. À l'ouverture d'une session de lecture du pack, le moteur contrôle le fichier, sa taille et son en-tête. Une reconstruction ou un audit réutilise ce handle pendant son opération puis le ferme ; les lectures isolées ouvrent leur propre session. Chaque objet relit les octets de l'enveloppe ciblée, dont le contenu doit ensuite satisfaire les mêmes règles d'authentification, décompression, ID et format que les fichiers isolés. Le cache ne dispense pas de relire l'enveloppe. Le hash d'index détecte les altérations mais ne constitue pas une signature authentifiée du catalogue. Les IDs, types, positions et tailles restent visibles dans l'index, même si les enveloppes sont chiffrées. Les écritures nouvelles restent à objects/... . La lecture ordinaire privilégie un fichier isolé lorsqu'un objet existe aussi dans le pack ; l'inventaire retourne une seule clé logique. fsck vérifie les deux copies. Les fichiers de packs non désignés par la configuration n'ajoutent aucune racine de rétention. Voir publication, reprise et compaction . Stockage physique v3 : catalogue de packs # La configuration v3 de base contient version: 3 , key_check inchangé et packs , un tableau de descripteurs de packs au format v2 décrit ci-dessus. Le tableau peut être vide ou contenir un seul pack ; le moteur ne rétrograde pas implicitement un store v3. Plusieurs packs exigent v3, que les lecteurs v1/v2 doivent refuser. Les formats binaires SYRPACK1 et SYRIDX01 restent inchangés. Les noms de packs doivent être distincts. Une clé {kind, id} ne peut figurer que dans un seul pack actif, même si deux copies physiques auraient un contenu identique. Les index sont vérifiés séparément, puis des parcours fusionnés contrôlent les clés lorsque leurs intervalles se recouvrent. Le catalogue est limité à 1 024 packs et à 4 194 304 objets au total, avec toujours au maximum 1 048 576 objets dans chaque index. Les tailles annoncées sont contrôlées avant la lecture des index ; les plages et contenus restent vérifiés ensuite. La maintenance ordinaire ajoute les nouveaux packs au tableau. Une compaction explicite peut en remplacer tout le contenu ; le GC peut en remplacer seulement une partie. Tous les fichiers d'un lot sont écrits et vérifiés avant le remplacement atomique unique de la configuration. Un lot non publié reste inactif et ne remplace aucune ancienne copie. La présence d'un fichier hors catalogue n'ajoute aucun objet à l'inventaire. Les sessions de reconstruction et d'audit réutilisent au plus 8 handles de packs, avec éviction LRU et fermeture en fin d'opération. Leur limite de handles est distincte des plafonds d'index et du cache décodé. La validation de chaque enveloppe est identique au stockage v1/v2. Consultation paginée sans changement de format # Le lecteur conserve le format plat SYRIDX01 et le même hash global. À l'ouverture, il le parcourt en flux, construit des bornes et empreintes de pages de 256 enregistrements ainsi qu'un filtre de clés absentes. Ces structures et le cache de pages sont uniquement en mémoire ; aucun en-tête, descripteur ou fichier auxiliaire n'est ajouté au store. La construction des nouveaux index écrit également les enregistrements en flux, avec le même encodage binaire. Les recherches consultent les pages candidates ; chaque page relue doit correspondre à l'empreinte du parcours validé avant utilisation de ses adresses. Les pages chaudes et la dernière adresse trouvée peuvent rester un snapshot du catalogue ouvert sous verrou, comme l'ancienne table en RAM. Les audits rechargent les index et recontrôlent leurs hashes complets, indépendamment de ce cache. La vérification des enveloppes réellement lues reste obligatoire avant de retourner un objet. Voir les budgets, garanties et limites . Formats de sélection du workspace natif # Les descripteurs nommés workspace/HEAD utilisent le JSON canonique v1 ; les descripteurs détachés passent en v2 avec branch: null , type: \"native-head\" et le directory canonique. Racines privées : workspace/detached (révision) et workspace/staging (révision préparée optionnelle). Les reçus de checkout avec cible détachée utilisent v7 et layout_version de 1 à 6 ; les layouts entre branches gardent leurs anciennes versions. Les stash détachés utilisent v2 avec la cible :HEAD , les stash nommés restent en v1. Ces ajouts ne changent ni les révisions immuables v1 ni les versions packs/ABI. Les anciens exécuteurs doivent refuser les reçus inconnus. Voir HEAD détaché . Pointeur Git # text Copier version syrnck-pointer-v1 manifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux> size \u003centier décimal> Les trois lignes se terminent par LF. L'encodage est canonique et le pointeur est limité à 1 024 octets. La taille doit correspondre au manifeste. Un document commençant par l'en-tête réservé mais mal formé est une erreur. Ce format est distinct des pointeurs Git LFS. API HTTP # Chaque requête porte Authorization: Bearer \u003cjeton> . Le serveur expose un seul store, avec autorité partagée historique ou utilisateurs et permissions configurés . HTTPS est requis côté client, à l'exception de loopback. Le service HTTP écoute uniquement sur loopback et peut être exposé derrière un proxy TLS. Méthode et chemin Corps / réponse POST /v1/missing Tableau JSON de clés ; retourne le sous-ensemble absent ; maximum 4 096 entrées GET /v1/objects/{kind}/{id} Octets logiques ou trame Zstandard négociée, maximum 16 Mio PUT /v1/objects/{kind}/{id} Même représentation ; vérifie type, hash et format ; 201 si créé, 204 si déjà présent GET /v1/refs Table JSON nom → clé PUT /v1/refs/{name} {root: clé, expected: clé ou null} ; valide toutes les dépendances avant publication Les noms distants utilisent un seul composant d'URL. Les références locales peuvent employer / . expected: null exige l'absence de référence ; une valeur exige une égalité exacte. Un conflit renvoie 409. Une dépendance absente renvoie 404 ; une corruption ou un objet invalide renvoie une erreur. Les réponses JSON peuvent employer n'importe quel ordre de champs : la contrainte d'encodage canonique porte sur les objets immuables, pas sur les enveloppes de requêtes. Les objets peuvent arriver avant leurs dépendances. Ils ne sont pas publiés comme nouvelle version tant que la référence n'a pas été validée et remplacée. Un retry du PUT d'un objet déjà présent est idempotent. Le client désactive les redirections pour ne pas déplacer les requêtes authentifiées. Cette v1 n'est compatible ni avec BEP, ni avec l'API Git LFS, ni avec le protocole Lore. L'extension négociée GET /v1/capabilities annonce les baux d'envoi persistants , leur durée et les reçus de publication. Les publications protégées ajoutent upload: {id, fence} au corps existant ; les requêtes historiques restent acceptées sauf si le serveur impose les baux. L'extension baux de lecture annonce read_leases: 1 et read_lease_seconds , avec acquisition/renouvellement sur PUT /v1/reads/ID , consultation sur GET /v1/reads/ID et libération sur POST /v1/reads/ID/release . Elle protège une racine exacte avant le téléchargement des blocs manquants. Les quotas d'objets/utilisateurs et la réplication serveur restent à développer. L'extension inventaires d'envoi annonce upload_inventories: 1 . La création du bail ajoute inventory: true ; PUT /v1/uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX enregistre un lot de clés et son jeton avant la demande de présence. Les pages sont chaînées et leur nombre/empreinte finale sont conservés dans le bail de schéma 2. Les baux historiques de schéma 1 gardent leur protection globale. Le format logique des objets et l'ABI C restent inchangés. L'extension de compression réseau annonce object_zstd: 1 . Les en-têtes Syrnck-Accept-Object-Encoding: zstd et Syrnck-Object-Encoding: zstd négocient une trame par objet. Les clients historiques continuent de recevoir des octets logiques. Le client vérifie taille, fenêtre, intégrité et format avant import ; les corps JSON et la publication CAS gardent leur représentation existante. Interface C # L'en-tête include/syrnck.h définit l'ABI 1, annoncée par syrnck_abi_version() . Cette version est indépendante du format d'objet v1 et de la version 0.1.0 du produit. Les fonctions renvoient 0 en cas de succès, 1 pour une erreur et 2 pour une exception C++ inconnue et 3 pour une annulation coopérative des opérations qui la prennent en charge. Aucune exception ne franchit l'interface. Les chaînes sont UTF-8 terminées par NUL ; les handles doivent être sérialisés, fermeture comprise. Aucun pointeur d'entrée n'est conservé. syrnck_object_has vérifie un objet présent ; une corruption n'est pas assimilée à une absence. syrnck_object_read retourne les octets non compressés vérifiés. Une requête avec pointeur nul et capacité zéro donne la taille ; la lecture exige ensuite un buffer assez grand et n'écrit rien si sa capacité est insuffisante. syrnck_closure_json suit le même contrat, mais sa longueur inclut le NUL final. syrnck_object_import valide hash et format, sans exiger que les dépendances soient déjà présentes. Après import de tous les objets, syrnck_ref_set valide les dépendances et les hashes complets avant de publier le pin. Cette fonction remplace une référence locale sans compare-and-swap ; l'API HTTP conserve son contrat CAS distinct. Les objets reçus avant publication ne sont pas automatiquement protégés du GC après fermeture du store. L'intégrateur doit garder le store ouvert et sérialiser transfert/GC, puis conserver une racine avant fermeture pour garantir la rétention. syrnck_last_error est local au thread et reste valide jusqu'au prochain appel qui modifie l'erreur. Le diagnostic est borné à 4 095 octets pour éviter d'allouer de la mémoire pendant le signalement d'une erreur. syrnck_error_copy permet de le copier sans le modifier ; sa longueur inclut NUL. Un binding avec goroutines doit récupérer l'erreur sur le même thread OS que l'appel. Les buffers d'objets appartiennent à l'appelant ; seules les chaînes allouées par syrnck_manifest_json passent par syrnck_string_free . Enregistrements des workspaces liés # Les workspaces liés utilisent les vraies références workspaces/NOM/ . registration conserve un chunk native-workspace v1 canonique avec nom et dossier ; workspace/HEAD conserve la sélection nommée/détachée. Les racines détachées sont privées. Les index nommés utilisent staging/BLAKE3(nom-branche) ; la révision préparée garde le nom complet. Les reçus v8 ajoutent context et layout_version 1..6 ; les layouts principaux v1..v7 ne changent pas. La sélection appartient au handle. Un contexte partiel conserve un chunk canonique native-materialization v1 sous workspace/materialization , avec tableau de préfixes littéraux disjoints triés. Absence de chunk : tous les chemins ; tableau vide : aucun. Les reçus v9 figent les définitions source/cible, contexte et layout (1..6), sans déduire les actions de reprise depuis une sélection actuelle mutable. Les reçus statiques v9 exigent deux définitions identiques ; l’édition directe d’une référence active est refusée. Le layout 5 du reset hard garde l’arbre source physique capturé complet, y compris les ancêtres faisant obstacle hors masque ; seule la cible logique est projetée. Tête/index complets restent parents de cette récupération. Le plan de reset hard partiel v2 lie le tableau de préfixes et la clé de son objet de politique. Le layout 6 d’apply/pop projette la source logique et restaure la cible physique complète, ancêtres obstructifs sauvegardés compris. Son plan v2 lie les mêmes champs de politique. Un descripteur stash partiel v3 ajoute materialization aux dix champs initiaux ; les descripteurs complets nommés/détachés restent v1/v2. Apply exige une politique sauvegardée/actuelle strictement identique ; la fusion d’index reste logique et complète, indépendante de la projection. Les transitions utilisent v10/layout 7 avec définitions figées différentes et les cinq champs tête/index/HEAD/profil de dossiers du layout 5. Source, cible et tête originale désignent la même révision logique complète ; index nul et profil de dossiers vide. Les deux chunks de politique restent protégés pendant la transaction. Seul un CAS de journal/références correspondant, de applying/installing vers committed, peut publier la nouvelle sélection active. Reprise/abort avant publication utilisent la source ; nettoyage engagé utilise la cible. HEAD et branche restent inchangés. Sans capacités requises, une sauvegarde de ces liaisons utilise le marqueur v2, conservant par contexte un descripteur portable native-workspace-snapshot et la révision snapshot , sans liaisons absolues registration/HEAD/reçus. Les sauvegardes v1 restent lisibles. La restauration relie explicitement le snapshot à un nouveau dossier. Enregistrements d’archives de workspace # Un contexte lié désenregistré utilise un chunk canonique native-workspace-archive v1 à douze champs : version , type , name , nonce , branch , revision , index , work , materialization , include_untracked , created_at , references . archive/workspaces/ID/record conserve ce chunk ; /head et /work conservent HEAD logique complet et la révision physique de récupération. Chaque référence originale possède une racine typée /roots/BLAKE3(nom-original-référence) . La lecture exige le groupe exact complet et la recette canonique de liaisons. La révision physique garde HEAD et l’éventuel index comme parents, avec timestamp nul et message Syrnck workspace archive work v1: NOM . La restauration garde les parents de l’index, même périmés. Le nouveau contexte conserve aussi le travail physique sous workspaces/NOUVEAU/restored/ARCHIVE_ID . Ces racines sont typées ordinaires ; formats d’objets, packs et ABI C inchangés. Voir les opérations d’archive . Enregistrements de déplacement de workspace # Le journal de déplacement est un chunk immuable native-workspace-relocation v1 sous workspace-relocations/active . Il fige chemins source/cible/intermédiaire, identités du dossier et des parents, valeurs complètes des anciennes liaisons, hash des références et plan. Une nouvelle exigence de lecteur précède la publication. Le validateur central n’accepte que les transactions exactes de début et de fin ; les autres mutations sont bloquées pendant l’attente. La phase physique dépend de l’identité unique au nom exact source/intermédiaire/cible. Les racines finales archive/workspace-relocations/ID/completed ou /aborted gardent le reçu inclus, sans dépendances d’objets cachées. Capacités requises du Store # Le journal natif v11 ajoute un owner hexadécimal minuscule de 64 caractères, un contexte et des définitions source/cible de matérialisation identiques. Son layout 1 ou 3 couvre application ordinaire ou namespace, y compris workspaces liés, partiels et détachés. Propriétaire et définitions figées participent à l’identité de transaction. Les layouts sans propriétaire gardent leurs encodages. Voir la reprise contrôlée . Les configurations v1/v2/v3 peuvent ajouter required_features , tableau non vide trié de noms uniques pris en charge (au plus 32). Les noms acceptés sont authored-revisions-v1 , linked-workspaces-v1 , sparse-workspaces-v1 , materialization-transitions-v1 , resident-sync-v1 et workspace-relocation-v1 . Nom inconnu, doublon, liste vide ou type invalide bloquent l’ouverture. Le champ supplémentaire fait volontairement refuser la configuration aux anciens lecteurs stricts avant lecture des objets/références. Les encodages packs/index/enveloppes et l’ABI C 1 ne changent pas ; les Stores classiques ne reçoivent aucun champ supplémentaire. L’activation publie atomiquement la configuration avant les liaisons qui l’exigent. Une interruption peut laisser l’exigence active sans liaison ; la reprise est idempotente. Packs et GC conservent le champ. Ajout seulement, sans retrait par l’API normale. Ces exigences de lecteur sont distinctes des capacités HTTP négociées et des réglages ajustables. Le marqueur de sauvegarde v3 ajoute aux neuf champs existants un tableau non vide required_features , égal à celui de la configuration destination. Exclusions identiques à v2. Les marqueurs v1/v2 décrivent des Stores sans capacités requises et restent lisibles. Sauvegarde logique, restauration et copie réparée conservent les exigences de lecteur source. ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé \u003cstore>/config.json \u003cstore>/refs.json \u003cstore>/store.lock \u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid> version syrnck-pointer-v1 manifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux> size \u003centier décimal>","headings":[{"id":"formats-et-protocole-experimentaux","text":"Formats et protocole expérimentaux","level":1},{"id":"identifiants-et-objets","text":"Identifiants et objets","level":2},{"id":"authored-revisions","text":"Révisions avec auteurs v2","level":2},{"id":"stockage-local","text":"Stockage local","level":2},{"id":"stockage-physique-v2-avec-packs","text":"Stockage physique v2 avec packs","level":3},{"id":"stockage-physique-v3-catalogue-de-packs","text":"Stockage physique v3 : catalogue de packs","level":3},{"id":"consultation-paginee-sans-changement-de-format","text":"Consultation paginée sans changement de format","level":3},{"id":"native-head-formats","text":"Formats de sélection du workspace natif","level":2},{"id":"pointeur-git","text":"Pointeur Git","level":2},{"id":"api-http","text":"API HTTP","level":2},{"id":"interface-c","text":"Interface C","level":2},{"id":"linked-workspace-formats","text":"Enregistrements des workspaces liés","level":2},{"id":"workspace-archive-formats","text":"Enregistrements d’archives de workspace","level":2},{"id":"workspace-relocation-formats","text":"Enregistrements de déplacement de workspace","level":2},{"id":"required-store-features","text":"Capacités requises du Store","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"ASCII(\"syrnck-object-v1\") || 0x00 || ASCII(type) || 0x00 || contenu_non_compressé\n\u003cstore>/config.json\n\u003cstore>/refs.json\n\u003cstore>/store.lock\n\u003cstore>/objects/\u003ctype>/\u003cdeux-premiers-caractères>/\u003cid>\nversion syrnck-pointer-v1\nmanifest blake3:\u003c64 caractères hexadécimaux>\nsize \u003centier décimal>","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-authorship/format","#/fr/packs","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/native-detached","#/fr/server-access","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/upload-inventories","#/fr/network-compression","#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/native-workspace-archives","#/fr/native-workspace-relocation/format","#/fr/native-checkout/owned-recovery"],"kind":"guide","revision":"a262168bf3fb"},{"id":"reference-tables","title":"Tables de références","description":"Guide : Tables de références.","section":"Stockage","order":480,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"reference-table-parsing-and-listing\">Tables de références\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/reference-tables/reference-table-parsing-and-listing\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Tables de références\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les grands inventaires de rétention Git peuvent garder de nombreux manifestes indépendants. Le chargement et la liste évitent les recherches quadratiques sans modifier le stockage ou HTTP.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>refs.json\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code> passent par un objet JSON arborescent avant validation des noms et clés dans \u003ccode>Refs\u003c/code>. La sérialisation ajoute directement les entrées triées et uniques à l’objet JSON ordonné. CLI, serveur et writer atomique partagent cet encodage. Ordre des entrées/champs et octets restent identiques, sans nouveau format, ID ni ABI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"json-validation\">Validation du JSON\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/reference-tables/json-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Validation du JSON\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un garde de profondeur SAX précède le DOM normal, sans construire de second DOM. Il évite les recherches répétées dans les parents de l’ancien callback de parsing. Les métadonnées immuables conservent JSON ordonné et validation canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La profondeur maximale de 128 niveaux reste inchangée, y compris pour les conteneurs vides. Syntaxe, UTF-8, nombres, noms, types, hashes et clés sont contrôlés. L’ordre des champs est libre dans les tables de références et les noms JSON dupliqués gardent le comportement antérieur de dernière valeur. Un document invalide ne remplace pas la table persistée. La limite de 16 Mio s’applique en lecture comme en écriture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le parsing coûte O(N log N), l’encodage d’une table triée O(N), hors longueurs des clés et octets de sortie. Cela ne rend pas tous les objets JSON ordonnés linéaires. Deux passes lexicales ajoutent du travail aux petits documents ; DOM et \u003ccode>Refs\u003c/code> coexistent pendant conversion. Le document complet est relu à chaque accès. Index persistant, journal incrémental et budget mémoire global restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026\">Coûts et limites en exécution\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/reference-tables/measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Coûts et limites en exécution\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les mesures synthétiques ne garantissent pas les performances de production. Tenir compte du nombre d’objets, des métadonnées, de la disposition physique, de la vérification et de la contention IO externe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"observer-changes-during-batch-publication\">Mutations des observateurs pendant une publication groupée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/reference-tables/observer-changes-during-batch-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Mutations des observateurs pendant une publication groupée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le CAS groupé conserve les octets originaux bornés des références pendant vérification et progression. Après le dernier observateur, il vérifie que le fichier reste identique. Insertion, retrait ou remplacement par un callback fait rendre false sans écraser sa modification, même pour une référence hors du groupe. Refaire la lecture avant nouvel essai. Une réécriture canonique identique peut continuer ; l’annulation reste vérifiée avant publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ce contrôle est une lecture/comparaison linéaire d’au plus 16 Mio, sans second DOM ni changement de format. Il protège les mutations par callback, pas une altération arbitraire du système de fichiers ou des accès concurrents généraux au même handle. Le verrou exclusif et la sérialisation restent requis.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Tables de références # Les grands inventaires de rétention Git peuvent garder de nombreux manifestes indépendants. Le chargement et la liste évitent les recherches quadratiques sans modifier le stockage ou HTTP. refs.json et Remote::refs passent par un objet JSON arborescent avant validation des noms et clés dans Refs . La sérialisation ajoute directement les entrées triées et uniques à l’objet JSON ordonné. CLI, serveur et writer atomique partagent cet encodage. Ordre des entrées/champs et octets restent identiques, sans nouveau format, ID ni ABI. Validation du JSON # Un garde de profondeur SAX précède le DOM normal, sans construire de second DOM. Il évite les recherches répétées dans les parents de l’ancien callback de parsing. Les métadonnées immuables conservent JSON ordonné et validation canonique. La profondeur maximale de 128 niveaux reste inchangée, y compris pour les conteneurs vides. Syntaxe, UTF-8, nombres, noms, types, hashes et clés sont contrôlés. L’ordre des champs est libre dans les tables de références et les noms JSON dupliqués gardent le comportement antérieur de dernière valeur. Un document invalide ne remplace pas la table persistée. La limite de 16 Mio s’applique en lecture comme en écriture. Le parsing coûte O(N log N), l’encodage d’une table triée O(N), hors longueurs des clés et octets de sortie. Cela ne rend pas tous les objets JSON ordonnés linéaires. Deux passes lexicales ajoutent du travail aux petits documents ; DOM et Refs coexistent pendant conversion. Le document complet est relu à chaque accès. Index persistant, journal incrémental et budget mémoire global restent à développer. Coûts et limites en exécution # Les mesures synthétiques ne garantissent pas les performances de production. Tenir compte du nombre d’objets, des métadonnées, de la disposition physique, de la vérification et de la contention IO externe. Mutations des observateurs pendant une publication groupée # Le CAS groupé conserve les octets originaux bornés des références pendant vérification et progression. Après le dernier observateur, il vérifie que le fichier reste identique. Insertion, retrait ou remplacement par un callback fait rendre false sans écraser sa modification, même pour une référence hors du groupe. Refaire la lecture avant nouvel essai. Une réécriture canonique identique peut continuer ; l’annulation reste vérifiée avant publication. Ce contrôle est une lecture/comparaison linéaire d’au plus 16 Mio, sans second DOM ni changement de format. Il protège les mutations par callback, pas une altération arbitraire du système de fichiers ou des accès concurrents généraux au même handle. Le verrou exclusif et la sérialisation restent requis.","headings":[{"id":"reference-table-parsing-and-listing","text":"Tables de références","level":1},{"id":"json-validation","text":"Validation du JSON","level":2},{"id":"measured-cli-listing-windows-x64-4-november-2026","text":"Coûts et limites en exécution","level":2},{"id":"observer-changes-during-batch-publication","text":"Mutations des observateurs pendant une publication groupée","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"660ba29b6f1b"},{"id":"repair-copy","title":"Reconstruction d’un dépôt endommagé","description":"Guide : Reconstruction d’un dépôt endommagé.","section":"Stockage","order":490,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location\">Reconstruire un Store endommagé ailleurs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/repair-copy/rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reconstruire un Store endommagé ailleurs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>La copie réparée associe les racines conservées de la source à une sauvegarde intégralement vérifiée. Les objets courants intacts viennent de la source ; les objets absents ou corrompus viennent de la sauvegarde sous leur type/ID exact. Le résultat est un nouveau Store normal vérifié ; les deux entrées restent intactes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La destination doit être absente, avoir un parent existant et se situer hors des deux entrées. Un dossier existant, même vide, est refusé. Sélectionner ensuite explicitement le nouveau Store : la commande ne remplace ni ne renomme la source abîmée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store recovered fsck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes\">Conserver les versions récentes et réparer les anciennes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/repair-copy/preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conserver les versions récentes et réparer les anciennes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une ancienne sauvegarde n’a pas besoin de posséder toutes les versions actuelles : elle peut fournir un ancien bloc corrompu tandis qu’une nouvelle révision intacte reste copiée depuis la source. Têtes, index figés, archives, tags et autres racines portables sont conservés ensemble.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le contrôle préalable parcourt la vue combinée, vérifie les identités et tous les hashes de fichiers avant création de la sortie. Un objet nécessaire invalide ou absent des deux côtés bloque la copie. Toute la sauvegarde, y compris reçu, préférences, fermeture et inventaire, est auditée même pour un seul objet donneur. Le plan n’écrit rien et rend \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>missing\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>corrupt\u003c/code>, les compteurs, le hash des références source et l’ID du snapshot donneur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La copie réimporte les objets vérifiés avec la clé de sortie, conserve les préférences et publie les références. Un fsck complet vérifie le résultat avant son renommage exclusif. Le dernier observateur précède la revérification des références/préférences source et du descripteur donneur : une mutation du callback bloque la publication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Comme pour une restauration, \u003ccode>workspace/HEAD\u003c/code> et reçus locaux sont omis, mais les racines de données restent présentes. Aucun \u003ccode>backup.json\u003c/code> immuable n’est conservé. Les fichiers de travail, ignorés/non suivis et métadonnées Git ne sont pas copiés comme fichiers ; leurs versions natives conservées restent exportables.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes\">Objets isolés et enveloppes de packs corrompus\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/repair-copy/corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Objets isolés et enveloppes de packs corrompus\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La lecture combinée couvre chunks, manifestes, révisions et métadonnées opaques conservées, isolés ou empaquetés. Une enveloppe abîmée peut ainsi être récupérée sans réécrire, supprimer ou mettre en quarantaine le pack original. Les IDs logiques restent identiques ; compression, chiffrement et disposition physique peuvent différer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La source doit encore s’ouvrir avec sa clé, sa configuration, ses références, son catalogue et ses index valides, ainsi que des tailles/en-têtes de packs conformes. La reconstruction d’index/en-têtes perdus, les tables/configurations abîmées et la substitution en place restent hors de cette opération. Aucun octet inconnu inaccessible n’est récupéré et aucune disposition physique identique n’est promise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>backup repair --apply\u003c/code> conserve son contrat plus strict : importer seulement les objets absents, avec refus de tout objet existant corrompu. Choisir une copie réparée quand la vue combinée des deux entrées permet de reconstruire le graphe conservé.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"encryption-and-operation-boundaries\">Clés et limites de l’opération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/repair-copy/encryption-and-operation-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Clés et limites de l’opération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> ouvre la source, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY\u003c/code> le donneur, et \u003ccode>SYRNCK_RECOVERY_KEY\u003c/code> choisit éventuellement une troisième clé indépendante pour la sortie. Sans cette dernière, le CLI conserve la clé principale, ou celle du donneur si lui seul est chiffré. Une entrée chiffrée impose une sortie chiffrée ; aucune clé n’est affichée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les entrées sont verrouillées exclusivement et doivent être au repos : pas de checkout actif, transfert incomplet ou bail de lecture/envoi actif non expiré. Il s’agit d’une récupération hors ligne, sans réplication, bascule automatique ou chiffrement de bout en bout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Annulation et erreurs coopératives avant publication retirent la préparation privée. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser un dossier frère non publié ; la destination nommée est absente ou complète et vérifiée. Réessayer vers une destination absente, ou inspecter le résultat déjà publié. Les entrées corrompues restent disponibles pour analyse indépendante.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces API de \u003ccode>syrnck/backup.hpp\u003c/code> fonctionnent aussi sans les composants réseau. Les rapports sont bornés à 16 Mio. Identités et métadonnées de parcours restent en mémoire ; les payloads sont copiés et vérifiés un objet CAS borné à la fois. Les contrôles préalable, de copie et de sortie relisent les données ; aucun débit de production n’est garanti.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> plan \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">plan_repaired_copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> backup\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">create_repaired_copy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>source\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> backup\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> new_directory\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                           destination_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Reconstruire un Store endommagé ailleurs # La copie réparée associe les racines conservées de la source à une sauvegarde intégralement vérifiée. Les objets courants intacts viennent de la source ; les objets absents ou corrompus viennent de la sauvegarde sous leur type/ID exact. Le résultat est un nouveau Store normal vérifié ; les deux entrées restent intactes. La destination doit être absente, avoir un parent existant et se situer hors des deux entrées. Un dossier existant, même vide, est refusé. Sélectionner ensuite explicitement le nouveau Store : la commande ne remplace ni ne renomme la source abîmée. text Copier syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered syrnck --store recovered fsck syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files Conserver les versions récentes et réparer les anciennes # Une ancienne sauvegarde n’a pas besoin de posséder toutes les versions actuelles : elle peut fournir un ancien bloc corrompu tandis qu’une nouvelle révision intacte reste copiée depuis la source. Têtes, index figés, archives, tags et autres racines portables sont conservés ensemble. Le contrôle préalable parcourt la vue combinée, vérifie les identités et tous les hashes de fichiers avant création de la sortie. Un objet nécessaire invalide ou absent des deux côtés bloque la copie. Toute la sauvegarde, y compris reçu, préférences, fermeture et inventaire, est auditée même pour un seul objet donneur. Le plan n’écrit rien et rend ready , missing , corrupt , les compteurs, le hash des références source et l’ID du snapshot donneur. La copie réimporte les objets vérifiés avec la clé de sortie, conserve les préférences et publie les références. Un fsck complet vérifie le résultat avant son renommage exclusif. Le dernier observateur précède la revérification des références/préférences source et du descripteur donneur : une mutation du callback bloque la publication. Comme pour une restauration, workspace/HEAD et reçus locaux sont omis, mais les racines de données restent présentes. Aucun backup.json immuable n’est conservé. Les fichiers de travail, ignorés/non suivis et métadonnées Git ne sont pas copiés comme fichiers ; leurs versions natives conservées restent exportables. Objets isolés et enveloppes de packs corrompus # La lecture combinée couvre chunks, manifestes, révisions et métadonnées opaques conservées, isolés ou empaquetés. Une enveloppe abîmée peut ainsi être récupérée sans réécrire, supprimer ou mettre en quarantaine le pack original. Les IDs logiques restent identiques ; compression, chiffrement et disposition physique peuvent différer. La source doit encore s’ouvrir avec sa clé, sa configuration, ses références, son catalogue et ses index valides, ainsi que des tailles/en-têtes de packs conformes. La reconstruction d’index/en-têtes perdus, les tables/configurations abîmées et la substitution en place restent hors de cette opération. Aucun octet inconnu inaccessible n’est récupéré et aucune disposition physique identique n’est promise. backup repair --apply conserve son contrat plus strict : importer seulement les objets absents, avec refus de tout objet existant corrompu. Choisir une copie réparée quand la vue combinée des deux entrées permet de reconstruire le graphe conservé. Clés et limites de l’opération # SYRNCK_KEY ouvre la source, SYRNCK_BACKUP_KEY le donneur, et SYRNCK_RECOVERY_KEY choisit éventuellement une troisième clé indépendante pour la sortie. Sans cette dernière, le CLI conserve la clé principale, ou celle du donneur si lui seul est chiffré. Une entrée chiffrée impose une sortie chiffrée ; aucune clé n’est affichée. Les entrées sont verrouillées exclusivement et doivent être au repos : pas de checkout actif, transfert incomplet ou bail de lecture/envoi actif non expiré. Il s’agit d’une récupération hors ligne, sans réplication, bascule automatique ou chiffrement de bout en bout. Annulation et erreurs coopératives avant publication retirent la préparation privée. Un arrêt forcé peut laisser un dossier frère non publié ; la destination nommée est absente ou complète et vérifiée. Réessayer vers une destination absente, ou inspecter le résultat déjà publié. Les entrées corrompues restent disponibles pour analyse indépendante. Ces API de syrnck/backup.hpp fonctionnent aussi sans les composants réseau. Les rapports sont bornés à 16 Mio. Identités et métadonnées de parcours restent en mémoire ; les payloads sont copiés et vérifiés un objet CAS borné à la fois. Les contrôles préalable, de copie et de sortie relisent les données ; aucun débit de production n’est garanti. cpp Copier auto plan = syrnck :: plan_repaired_copy ( source , backup , operation ) ; auto result = syrnck :: create_repaired_copy ( source , backup , new_directory , destination_key , operation ) ; syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered syrnck --store recovered fsck syrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files auto plan = syrnck::plan_repaired_copy(source, backup, operation); auto result = syrnck::create_repaired_copy(source, backup, new_directory, destination_key, operation);","headings":[{"id":"rebuild-a-damaged-store-in-a-verified-new-location","text":"Reconstruire un Store endommagé ailleurs","level":1},{"id":"preserve-current-history-while-recovering-old-bytes","text":"Conserver les versions récentes et réparer les anciennes","level":2},{"id":"corrupt-loose-objects-and-packed-envelopes","text":"Objets isolés et enveloppes de packs corrompus","level":2},{"id":"encryption-and-operation-boundaries","text":"Clés et limites de l’opération","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy-plan verified-backup\nsyrnck --store damaged backup repair-copy verified-backup recovered\nsyrnck --store recovered fsck\nsyrnck --store recovered native export HEAD recovered-files\nauto plan = syrnck::plan_repaired_copy(source, backup, operation);\nauto result = syrnck::create_repaired_copy(source, backup, new_directory,\n                                           destination_key, operation);","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"e0a0dbc58cfa"},{"id":"resident-agent","title":"Agent résident de synchronisation native","description":"Surveiller les workspaces, échanger les révisions protégées et fusionner les historiques compatibles.","section":"Transfers","order":405,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"6","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"run\">Fonctionnement continu\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/run\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fonctionnement continu\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ces exemples nécessitent un stockage initialisé, un vrai serveur et \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>--store PATH\u003c/code> sélectionne le stockage. Sans \u003ccode>--directory\u003c/code>, le workspace principal\nutilise le dossier courant ; un workspace lié sélectionné utilise son dossier\nenregistré. HEAD, nommé ou détaché, reste figé pour la session. \u003ccode>--branch NAME\u003c/code>,\nsi fourni, doit lui correspondre. La branche distante vaut \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> par défaut ;\nles noms distants sont des composants uniques d’au plus 64 octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Par défaut, l’agent observe les fichiers matérialisés et crée un snapshot natif\nquand leur contenu change, y compris suppressions et renommages exacts. Un\nworkspace partiel conserve les fichiers non matérialisés de sa révision parente\ncomplète. Plusieurs fichiers ne constituent pas un snapshot atomique du système\nde fichiers : stabiliser les écrivains externes pour capturer un état applicatif\ncohérent. \u003ccode>--commits-only\u003c/code> désactive les snapshots automatiques des fichiers.\nDans le sens de publication par défaut, il publie uniquement les commits\nexistants sans changer les fichiers. En mode bidirectionnel, les historiques\nexistants peuvent encore produire une révision de fusion comme décrit ci-dessous.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Synchronisation entièrement autonome de dossiers, installation\ncomme service système et interopérabilité complète avec un daemon Syncthing\nrestent à terminer. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/limits\">limites actuelles\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bidirectional\">Échanger les révisions dans les deux sens\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/bidirectional\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Échanger les révisions dans les deux sens\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> autorise explicitement les changements entrants des fichiers.\nUn workspace vide et propre peut recevoir sa première révision distante. Les\ndescendants distants s’appliquent par la transaction de checkout récupérable,\nqui conserve l’ancien état et respecte matérialisation partielle et HEAD détaché.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Des descendants concurrents d’un historique partagé utilisent une fusion à trois\nvoies. Les changements de fichiers compatibles et éditions textuelles sans\nchevauchement peuvent produire une révision signée à deux parents, publiée\ncontre la racine distante exactement observée. La composition textuelle utilise\nle moteur borné existant, avec au plus 64 essais et 16 Mio d’entrées/résultats par\nlot. Historiques sans lien et bases de fusion ambiguës ne sont pas choisis\nautomatiquement. Une réécriture distante hors de l’historique reconnu est bloquée\nmême si une fusion pourrait autrement la combiner.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les conflits binaires, textuels chevauchants ou topologiques non résolus bloquent\nl’agent avec le plan de fusion. Historiques local et distant récupéré restent\nconservés ; les octets de travail sont préservés. Résoudre avec le\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-resolutions\">parcours de fusion native\u003c/a> ; l’agent réessaie l’historique\nlocal résultant. La politique optionnelle ci-dessous peut conserver\nautomatiquement les versions concurrentes des fichiers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avec \u003ccode>--commits-only --bidirectional\u003c/code>, réception et fusion exigent toujours un\nworkspace propre : les fichiers sales sont conservés et bloquent l’application.\nUne édition arrivant pendant le précontrôle peut aussi bloquer le checkout.\nLes indications du watcher n’autorisent jamais un remplacement sans vérification.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conflict-copies\">Conserver les assets conflictuels\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/conflict-copies\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conserver les assets conflictuels\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> exige \u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> et reste désactivé par défaut. Après\nles fusions compatibles/textuelles normales, les modifications binaires ou\ntextuelles chevauchantes et les ajouts concurrents conservent les deux versions\ndans le snapshot de fusion. L’ordre stable des IDs de révisions sélectionne le\nchemin initial ; une copie voisine déterministe conserve l’autre version et les\nextensions d’assets courantes. Suppression/modification garde le fichier modifié.\nConsulter les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-merge/conflict-copies\">règles exactes de nommage et collision\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>merge.resolved\u003c/code> identifie chaque copie ou modification conservée. Les copies\nsont suivies et transférées par les mêmes manifests vérifiés, publication\nprotégée et révision signée à deux parents. Inverser les pairs propose le même\nensemble de fichiers ; un descendant résolu ne crée pas de nouvelles copies.\nExaminer les copies puis committer le choix final. Conflits de structure,\nnoms générés occupés, obstacles non suivis, historiques sans lien/réécrits,\nindex et récupération incomplète continuent de bloquer. Un candidat déjà préparé\nreprend à l’identique même si l’option est omise après redémarrage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Publication et récupération\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/recovery\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Publication et récupération\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Chaque publication exige la révision distante observée comme valeur attendue.\nFermetures complètes vérifiées, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">sessions de transfert\u003c/a>,\npermissions, clés de signature approuvées et éventuelles preuves de verrous\nd’assets s’appliquent comme aux transferts ordinaires. \u003ccode>--require-lease\u003c/code> exige la\nprotection négociée contre le GC distant ; l’agent n’acquiert ni ne renouvelle\nautomatiquement les verrous d’assets. Fournir les preuves actuelles avec\n\u003ccode>--lock-claims PATH\u003c/code> lorsque la politique de branche les exige.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une publication réussie conserve une racine typée de référence et un descripteur\nsous les références \u003ccode>sync/push/SERVICE/\u003c/code> du workspace sélectionné. Le descripteur\nlie workspace logique, cible HEAD, branche distante et empreinte de l’adresse\nnormalisée. Il ne contient ni jeton ni ancienne destination physique. Les états\nremplacés restent protégés par \u003ccode>archive/agent-push/SERVICE/REVISION\u003c/code>. GC et\nsauvegardes logiques conservent ainsi toutes les dépendances de reconstruction.\nLes interrogations et redémarrages sans changement ne réécrivent pas le point\nde reprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une suppression ou un recul distant après publication reconnue est bloqué au\nlieu d’être annulé. Une réécriture locale est également bloquée. Une révision\ndistante inconnue est récupérée dans une référence distincte\n\u003ccode>remotes/agent-…/BRANCH\u003c/code>, sans changer HEAD ni les fichiers. Un ancêtre distant\nde HEAD peut être avancé. Le sens de publication par défaut bloque descendants\ndistants et historiques divergents ; \u003ccode>--bidirectional\u003c/code> permet la réception et\nles fusions compatibles décrites ci-dessus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Si une publication précédente a une issue inconnue, sa session persistante\nd’origine doit d’abord se réconcilier, même si HEAD local a progressé. Un reçu\nde succès passé n’autorise pas à écraser un changement distant ultérieur. Un\nconflit enregistré demande une résolution explicite ; l’agent ne supprime jamais\nautomatiquement ce journal. Après résolution du problème d’historique ou de\npolitique, il réessaie. Le mode bidirectionnel peut \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout/owned-recovery\">reprendre son propre checkout interrompu\u003c/a>\nhors ligne sans signer à nouveau sa cible figée. Les checkouts manuels, d’autres\nsessions ou en mode publication seule restent bloqués. Un index de préparation\noccupé bloque capture et publication jusqu’à sa libération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La référence typée \u003ccode>sync/push/SERVICE/observed\u003c/code> conserve le dernier historique\ndistant confirmé avant application des fichiers et avant de terminer une session\nd’envoi. Les observations précédentes restent sous \u003ccode>archive/agent-observation/\u003c/code>.\nSuppression ou recul de cet historique est bloqué même si un crash précède le\npoint de reprise reconnu. Une nouvelle session peut importer les reçus natifs\ncomplets encore disponibles comme faits historiques, sans autoriser l’écrasement\nd’un changement distant ultérieur. \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code> protège cet état avant\npublication et reste activé après nettoyage ou changement de mode.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Des changements hors ligne peuvent toujours devenir des snapshots locaux signés.\nLa reconnexion transfère leurs blocs manquants. Le redémarrage rescane le\nworkspace et réutilise révisions, points de reprise et sessions persistantes.\nL’identité de signature est chargée une fois au démarrage et gardée en mémoire ;\nchanger le profil ou faire tourner sa graine exige de redémarrer l’agent. Voir\nla \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">configuration d’identité\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"controls\">Planification et sortie\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/controls\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Planification et sortie\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Défaut\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Valeurs acceptées\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--interval-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>500\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20..60000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--debounce-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>75\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0..5000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--audit-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>300000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1000..3600000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--remote-poll-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>5000\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>100..3600000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--duration-ms\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 pour continu, ou 100..86400000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-updates\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>0 pour illimité, ou 1..1000000\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--max-paths\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>4096\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>1..65536\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\n\u003cp>Les notifications natives sont indicatives ; audits périodiques et rescans au\nredémarrage récupèrent la perte de continuité. \u003ccode>--polling\u003c/code> utilise des rescans\nprudents. Une erreur est réessayée après une seconde. Le stockage est libéré\navant attente, regroupement d’événements et écriture d’un état : les autres\ncommandes CLI peuvent passer entre les cycles. Scans et transferts réseau\ngardent son verrou exclusif ; un transfert volumineux peut retarder les commandes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La sortie standard produit des lignes NDJSON lorsque l’état sémantique change.\n\u003ccode>state\u003c/code> vaut \u003ccode>empty\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pushed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pulled\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>recovered\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>synced\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>uncommitted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>blocked\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>unavailable\u003c/code>.\nLes codes de raison distinguent index, checkout, réécritures, références\nsupprimées, historiques concurrents et publications incertaines. Les lignes\nincluent les révisions locales/distantes et la référence reconnue si connues.\n\u003ccode>last_observed_remote\u003c/code> expose l’historique conservé avant même ce point de reprise.\n\u003ccode>merged: true\u003c/code> signale une fusion locale automatique ; les rapports checkout/merge\ndécrivent l’application entrante ou ses blocages lorsque nécessaire.\n\u003ccode>working_clean: null\u003c/code> demande un nouvel audit, notamment juste après un snapshot\nautomatique. Les messages suivent \u003ccode>--lang en|fr\u003c/code> ; IDs et clés JSON restent\nidentiques. \u003ccode>--progress\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>--progress-json\u003c/code> écrit la progression sur stderr.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>À une limite de durée ou de mises à jour, le code de sortie reflète le dernier\ncycle : 0 pour un état ordinaire, 1 pour une opération indisponible, 2 pour un\nblocage, 3 pour une publication incertaine. L’annulation coopérative renvoie 130.\nLa durée limite la planification, sans interrompre immédiatement les IO en cours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cpp\">Intégration C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/cpp\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Intégration C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/native_sync.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>net\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>NativeSync \u003cspan class=\"token function\">sync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> status \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> sync\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">tick\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> watcher_changes\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Conserver cette session déplaçable et son observation entre les cycles. Chaque\ncycle utilise un stockage actuellement verrouillé, avec mêmes stockage physique,\nworkspace logique, cible HEAD et adresse distante. Appeler \u003ccode>invalidate()\u003c/code> après\nperte de continuité du watcher ; une nouvelle session commence par un audit\ncomplet. L’appelant gère planification et annulation. Cette API ne lance aucun\nthread de fond et ne charge pas implicitement le profil CLI. Les options sont\nbranche distante, capture locale, réception, fusions compatibles et copies de\nconflit ; les trois dernières valent false par défaut. La fusion exige la\nréception et les copies exigent les deux. Par exemple,\n\u003ccode>NativeSyncOptions{&quot;main&quot;, true, true, true, true}\u003c/code> active la conservation. Les noms\n\u003ccode>NativePushSync\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>NativePushSyncOptions\u003c/code> restent valides. Lier \u003ccode>syrnck_net\u003c/code> et\nfournir explicitement l’identité éventuelle. La CLI utilise\n\u003ccode>Store::take_native_identity()\u003c/code> pour déplacer la propriété exclusive de la clé\nentre handles courts sans exporter les octets de la graine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Éditions pendant une application à propriétaire\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Éditions pendant une application à propriétaire\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La politique existante explicite \u003ccode>--conflict-copies\u003c/code> conserve aussi les éditions\nde fichiers ordinaires survenues pendant l’application. L’agent retient des\narchives natives avant les déplacements exclusifs vers des copies de conflit,\nrestaure les octets intermédiaires attendus et termine le candidat entrant/fusionné\ninchangé. Cette reprise fonctionne hors ligne sans clé privée pour une cible\nsignée préparée. La capture locale suivante enregistre les fichiers ajoutés.\nSans cette politique, la reprise reste stricte. Fichiers manquants, obstructions\nde dossiers/liens et structures ambiguës demandent encore une résolution. Voir le\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">contrat partagé d’archive et de reprise\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">E/S sources des captures\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/resident-agent/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Lien vers E/S sources des captures\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les captures répétées réutilisent les manifestes de session inchangés grâce aux\nempreintes fiables des fichiers locaux. Inventaire sélectionné et vérification\nde toute la fermeture restent requis. Le rapport de tick expose les compteurs\nsources \u003ccode>capture\u003c/code>. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance/incremental-capture\">performance et repli\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Fonctionnement continu # text Copier syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease Ces exemples nécessitent un stockage initialisé, un vrai serveur et SYRNCK_TOKEN . --store PATH sélectionne le stockage. Sans --directory , le workspace principal utilise le dossier courant ; un workspace lié sélectionné utilise son dossier enregistré. HEAD, nommé ou détaché, reste figé pour la session. --branch NAME , si fourni, doit lui correspondre. La branche distante vaut main par défaut ; les noms distants sont des composants uniques d’au plus 64 octets. Par défaut, l’agent observe les fichiers matérialisés et crée un snapshot natif quand leur contenu change, y compris suppressions et renommages exacts. Un workspace partiel conserve les fichiers non matérialisés de sa révision parente complète. Plusieurs fichiers ne constituent pas un snapshot atomique du système de fichiers : stabiliser les écrivains externes pour capturer un état applicatif cohérent. --commits-only désactive les snapshots automatiques des fichiers. Dans le sens de publication par défaut, il publie uniquement les commits existants sans changer les fichiers. En mode bidirectionnel, les historiques existants peuvent encore produire une révision de fusion comme décrit ci-dessous. Synchronisation entièrement autonome de dossiers, installation comme service système et interopérabilité complète avec un daemon Syncthing restent à terminer. Voir les limites actuelles . Échanger les révisions dans les deux sens # --bidirectional autorise explicitement les changements entrants des fichiers. Un workspace vide et propre peut recevoir sa première révision distante. Les descendants distants s’appliquent par la transaction de checkout récupérable, qui conserve l’ancien état et respecte matérialisation partielle et HEAD détaché. Des descendants concurrents d’un historique partagé utilisent une fusion à trois voies. Les changements de fichiers compatibles et éditions textuelles sans chevauchement peuvent produire une révision signée à deux parents, publiée contre la racine distante exactement observée. La composition textuelle utilise le moteur borné existant, avec au plus 64 essais et 16 Mio d’entrées/résultats par lot. Historiques sans lien et bases de fusion ambiguës ne sont pas choisis automatiquement. Une réécriture distante hors de l’historique reconnu est bloquée même si une fusion pourrait autrement la combiner. Les conflits binaires, textuels chevauchants ou topologiques non résolus bloquent l’agent avec le plan de fusion. Historiques local et distant récupéré restent conservés ; les octets de travail sont préservés. Résoudre avec le parcours de fusion native ; l’agent réessaie l’historique local résultant. La politique optionnelle ci-dessous peut conserver automatiquement les versions concurrentes des fichiers. Avec --commits-only --bidirectional , réception et fusion exigent toujours un workspace propre : les fichiers sales sont conservés et bloquent l’application. Une édition arrivant pendant le précontrôle peut aussi bloquer le checkout. Les indications du watcher n’autorisent jamais un remplacement sans vérification. Conserver les assets conflictuels # text Copier syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease --conflict-copies exige --bidirectional et reste désactivé par défaut. Après les fusions compatibles/textuelles normales, les modifications binaires ou textuelles chevauchantes et les ajouts concurrents conservent les deux versions dans le snapshot de fusion. L’ordre stable des IDs de révisions sélectionne le chemin initial ; une copie voisine déterministe conserve l’autre version et les extensions d’assets courantes. Suppression/modification garde le fichier modifié. Consulter les règles exactes de nommage et collision . merge.resolved identifie chaque copie ou modification conservée. Les copies sont suivies et transférées par les mêmes manifests vérifiés, publication protégée et révision signée à deux parents. Inverser les pairs propose le même ensemble de fichiers ; un descendant résolu ne crée pas de nouvelles copies. Examiner les copies puis committer le choix final. Conflits de structure, noms générés occupés, obstacles non suivis, historiques sans lien/réécrits, index et récupération incomplète continuent de bloquer. Un candidat déjà préparé reprend à l’identique même si l’option est omise après redémarrage. Publication et récupération # Chaque publication exige la révision distante observée comme valeur attendue. Fermetures complètes vérifiées, sessions de transfert , permissions, clés de signature approuvées et éventuelles preuves de verrous d’assets s’appliquent comme aux transferts ordinaires. --require-lease exige la protection négociée contre le GC distant ; l’agent n’acquiert ni ne renouvelle automatiquement les verrous d’assets. Fournir les preuves actuelles avec --lock-claims PATH lorsque la politique de branche les exige. Une publication réussie conserve une racine typée de référence et un descripteur sous les références sync/push/SERVICE/ du workspace sélectionné. Le descripteur lie workspace logique, cible HEAD, branche distante et empreinte de l’adresse normalisée. Il ne contient ni jeton ni ancienne destination physique. Les états remplacés restent protégés par archive/agent-push/SERVICE/REVISION . GC et sauvegardes logiques conservent ainsi toutes les dépendances de reconstruction. Les interrogations et redémarrages sans changement ne réécrivent pas le point de reprise. Une suppression ou un recul distant après publication reconnue est bloqué au lieu d’être annulé. Une réécriture locale est également bloquée. Une révision distante inconnue est récupérée dans une référence distincte remotes/agent-…/BRANCH , sans changer HEAD ni les fichiers. Un ancêtre distant de HEAD peut être avancé. Le sens de publication par défaut bloque descendants distants et historiques divergents ; --bidirectional permet la réception et les fusions compatibles décrites ci-dessus. Si une publication précédente a une issue inconnue, sa session persistante d’origine doit d’abord se réconcilier, même si HEAD local a progressé. Un reçu de succès passé n’autorise pas à écraser un changement distant ultérieur. Un conflit enregistré demande une résolution explicite ; l’agent ne supprime jamais automatiquement ce journal. Après résolution du problème d’historique ou de politique, il réessaie. Le mode bidirectionnel peut reprendre son propre checkout interrompu hors ligne sans signer à nouveau sa cible figée. Les checkouts manuels, d’autres sessions ou en mode publication seule restent bloqués. Un index de préparation occupé bloque capture et publication jusqu’à sa libération. La référence typée sync/push/SERVICE/observed conserve le dernier historique distant confirmé avant application des fichiers et avant de terminer une session d’envoi. Les observations précédentes restent sous archive/agent-observation/ . Suppression ou recul de cet historique est bloqué même si un crash précède le point de reprise reconnu. Une nouvelle session peut importer les reçus natifs complets encore disponibles comme faits historiques, sans autoriser l’écrasement d’un changement distant ultérieur. resident-sync-v1 protège cet état avant publication et reste activé après nettoyage ou changement de mode. Des changements hors ligne peuvent toujours devenir des snapshots locaux signés. La reconnexion transfère leurs blocs manquants. Le redémarrage rescane le workspace et réutilise révisions, points de reprise et sessions persistantes. L’identité de signature est chargée une fois au démarrage et gardée en mémoire ; changer le profil ou faire tourner sa graine exige de redémarrer l’agent. Voir la configuration d’identité . Planification et sortie # Option Défaut Valeurs acceptées --interval-ms 500 20..60000 --debounce-ms 75 0..5000 --audit-ms 300000 1000..3600000 --remote-poll-ms 5000 100..3600000 --duration-ms 0 0 pour continu, ou 100..86400000 --max-updates 0 0 pour illimité, ou 1..1000000 --max-paths 4096 1..65536 Les notifications natives sont indicatives ; audits périodiques et rescans au redémarrage récupèrent la perte de continuité. --polling utilise des rescans prudents. Une erreur est réessayée après une seconde. Le stockage est libéré avant attente, regroupement d’événements et écriture d’un état : les autres commandes CLI peuvent passer entre les cycles. Scans et transferts réseau gardent son verrou exclusif ; un transfert volumineux peut retarder les commandes. La sortie standard produit des lignes NDJSON lorsque l’état sémantique change. state vaut empty , pushed , pulled , recovered , synced , uncommitted , blocked ou unavailable . Les codes de raison distinguent index, checkout, réécritures, références supprimées, historiques concurrents et publications incertaines. Les lignes incluent les révisions locales/distantes et la référence reconnue si connues. last_observed_remote expose l’historique conservé avant même ce point de reprise. merged: true signale une fusion locale automatique ; les rapports checkout/merge décrivent l’application entrante ou ses blocages lorsque nécessaire. working_clean: null demande un nouvel audit, notamment juste après un snapshot automatique. Les messages suivent --lang en|fr ; IDs et clés JSON restent identiques. --progress ou --progress-json écrit la progression sur stderr. À une limite de durée ou de mises à jour, le code de sortie reflète le dernier cycle : 0 pour un état ordinaire, 1 pour une opération indisponible, 2 pour un blocage, 3 pour une publication incertaine. L’annulation coopérative renvoie 130. La durée limite la planification, sans interrompre immédiatement les IO en cours. Intégration C++ # cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp> syrnck :: net :: NativeSync sync ( store , project , { \"main\" , true , true , true } ) ; auto status = sync . tick ( store , remote , watcher_changes , operation , transfer ) ; Conserver cette session déplaçable et son observation entre les cycles. Chaque cycle utilise un stockage actuellement verrouillé, avec mêmes stockage physique, workspace logique, cible HEAD et adresse distante. Appeler invalidate() après perte de continuité du watcher ; une nouvelle session commence par un audit complet. L’appelant gère planification et annulation. Cette API ne lance aucun thread de fond et ne charge pas implicitement le profil CLI. Les options sont branche distante, capture locale, réception, fusions compatibles et copies de conflit ; les trois dernières valent false par défaut. La fusion exige la réception et les copies exigent les deux. Par exemple, NativeSyncOptions{\"main\", true, true, true, true} active la conservation. Les noms NativePushSync / NativePushSyncOptions restent valides. Lier syrnck_net et fournir explicitement l’identité éventuelle. La CLI utilise Store::take_native_identity() pour déplacer la propriété exclusive de la clé entre handles courts sans exporter les octets de la graine. Éditions pendant une application à propriétaire # La politique existante explicite --conflict-copies conserve aussi les éditions de fichiers ordinaires survenues pendant l’application. L’agent retient des archives natives avant les déplacements exclusifs vers des copies de conflit, restaure les octets intermédiaires attendus et termine le candidat entrant/fusionné inchangé. Cette reprise fonctionne hors ligne sans clé privée pour une cible signée préparée. La capture locale suivante enregistre les fichiers ajoutés. Sans cette politique, la reprise reste stricte. Fichiers manquants, obstructions de dossiers/liens et structures ambiguës demandent encore une résolution. Voir le contrat partagé d’archive et de reprise . E/S sources des captures # Les captures répétées réutilisent les manifestes de session inchangés grâce aux empreintes fiables des fichiers locaux. Inventaire sélectionné et vérification de toute la fermeture restent requis. Le rapport de tick expose les compteurs sources capture . Voir performance et repli . syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease syrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease #include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp> syrnck::net::NativeSync sync(store, project, {\"main\", true, true, true}); auto status = sync.tick(store, remote, watcher_changes, operation, transfer);","headings":[{"id":"run","text":"Fonctionnement continu","level":2},{"id":"bidirectional","text":"Échanger les révisions dans les deux sens","level":2},{"id":"conflict-copies","text":"Conserver les assets conflictuels","level":2},{"id":"recovery","text":"Publication et récupération","level":2},{"id":"controls","text":"Planification et sortie","level":2},{"id":"cpp","text":"Intégration C++","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Éditions pendant une application à propriétaire","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"E/S sources des captures","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --directory PROJECT --remote-branch main --require-lease\nsyrnck --workspace edit agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --remote-branch feature --require-lease\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --commits-only --polling\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --require-lease --require-read-lease\nsyrnck agent run https://blocks.example.invalid --bidirectional --conflict-copies --require-lease\n#include \u003csyrnck/native_sync.hpp>\n\nsyrnck::net::NativeSync sync(store, project, {\"main\", true, true, true});\nauto status = sync.tick(store, remote, watcher_changes, operation, transfer);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/limits","#/fr/native-resolutions","#/fr/native-merge/conflict-copies","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/native-checkout/owned-recovery","#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/fr/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide","revision":"6ea184c6db1e"},{"id":"selective-retention","title":"Rétention des transferts et purge","description":"Guide : Rétention des transferts et purge.","section":"Transferts","order":500,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning\">Rétention sélective des transferts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/selective-retention/selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention sélective des transferts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Le GC hors ligne traite les transferts locaux inachevés et baux de lecture actifs comme racines ; les autres objets peuvent être collectés pendant interruption. Arrêter le serveur avant une autre ouverture du Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Références/pins et push inachevé : vérifier et garder fermeture complète/hashs. Pull inachevé : garder chaque objet accessible disponible. Bail de lecture actif : fermeture complète vérifiée. Upload avec inventaire actif : objets présents déclarés. Upload historique actif : bloquer tout GC jusqu’à expiration/publication/libération. Reçus locaux complets et baux inactifs : aucun payload retenu. Métadonnées de journal/bail invalides : échec avant suppression.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incomplete-pulls\">Pulls incomplets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/selective-retention/incomplete-pulls\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Pulls incomplets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Pull importe les métadonnées vérifiées avant leurs dépendances ; le GC suit ce qui est disponible, blocs après un trou compris. Les absents sont normaux et non marqués ; racine absente seule ne conserve rien. Une association impossible à déduire des métadonnées présentes peut être collectée puis redemandée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Enveloppe corrompue, métadonnée invalide ou taille d’un bloc présent fausse arrête le GC. Un manifeste complet vérifie son hash entier ; un incomplet ne peut pas encore le faire, mais publication finale l’exige toujours. fsck/pack restent stricts sur les métadonnées orphelines incomplètes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC vérifie d’abord racines complètes puis ajoute les fermetures partielles aux mêmes bitmap/marques, sans second inventaire complet. Racines, pile et JSON consomment encore de la mémoire ; pas de plafond global. Annulation et point d’engagement catalogue/suppression restent applicables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un bail de lecture actif au début conserve prudemment sa racine pour ce GC même s’il expire pendant le parcours. Une expiration déjà observée est durablement fenced avant suppression pour empêcher un retour d’horloge. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires\u003c/a> protègent même avant manifeste ; les anciens baux gardent la protection globale. Verrous locaux/baux ne constituent pas un consensus distribué.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pruning-completed-local-receipts\">Purger les reçus locaux complets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/selective-retention/pruning-completed-local-receipts\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Purger les reçus locaux complets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>agent prune inspecte par défaut ; JSON completed, unfinished, removed, deleted. --delete retire tous les reçus complets, sans critère d’âge, absent du schéma actuel. La progression compte octets de métadonnées lus/retirés, le rapport compte des records.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Deux passages en flux, sans tous les IDs en mémoire : validation totale avant retrait, puis seuls state complete supprimés. Transferring/publishing/uncertain/conflict restent avec leurs protections. Un journal invalide bloque ; agent forget ID permet un abandon explicitement choisi même abîmé/inachevé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Opération locale sans token/réseau, clé toujours nécessaire au Store chiffré. Références/objets/baux distants intacts. Un reçu retiré n’est plus reprenable par ID ; un nouveau transfert recrée un état ordinaire au lieu de rendre ce reçu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Annulation/erreur IO ou observateur pendant suppression peut laisser un préfixe retiré ; réessayer est sûr. Le dossier est synchronisé au succès et cette synchronisation est tentée après interruption. Pas de transaction atomique multi-record. Prune/forget ne libèrent jamais un bail distant : expiration ou administration explicite restent nécessaires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent prune --delete --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Rétention sélective des transferts # Le GC hors ligne traite les transferts locaux inachevés et baux de lecture actifs comme racines ; les autres objets peuvent être collectés pendant interruption. Arrêter le serveur avant une autre ouverture du Store. Références/pins et push inachevé : vérifier et garder fermeture complète/hashs. Pull inachevé : garder chaque objet accessible disponible. Bail de lecture actif : fermeture complète vérifiée. Upload avec inventaire actif : objets présents déclarés. Upload historique actif : bloquer tout GC jusqu’à expiration/publication/libération. Reçus locaux complets et baux inactifs : aucun payload retenu. Métadonnées de journal/bail invalides : échec avant suppression. Pulls incomplets # Pull importe les métadonnées vérifiées avant leurs dépendances ; le GC suit ce qui est disponible, blocs après un trou compris. Les absents sont normaux et non marqués ; racine absente seule ne conserve rien. Une association impossible à déduire des métadonnées présentes peut être collectée puis redemandée. Enveloppe corrompue, métadonnée invalide ou taille d’un bloc présent fausse arrête le GC. Un manifeste complet vérifie son hash entier ; un incomplet ne peut pas encore le faire, mais publication finale l’exige toujours. fsck/pack restent stricts sur les métadonnées orphelines incomplètes. Le GC vérifie d’abord racines complètes puis ajoute les fermetures partielles aux mêmes bitmap/marques, sans second inventaire complet. Racines, pile et JSON consomment encore de la mémoire ; pas de plafond global. Annulation et point d’engagement catalogue/suppression restent applicables. Un bail de lecture actif au début conserve prudemment sa racine pour ce GC même s’il expire pendant le parcours. Une expiration déjà observée est durablement fenced avant suppression pour empêcher un retour d’horloge. Les inventaires protègent même avant manifeste ; les anciens baux gardent la protection globale. Verrous locaux/baux ne constituent pas un consensus distribué. Purger les reçus locaux complets # agent prune inspecte par défaut ; JSON completed, unfinished, removed, deleted. --delete retire tous les reçus complets, sans critère d’âge, absent du schéma actuel. La progression compte octets de métadonnées lus/retirés, le rapport compte des records. Deux passages en flux, sans tous les IDs en mémoire : validation totale avant retrait, puis seuls state complete supprimés. Transferring/publishing/uncertain/conflict restent avec leurs protections. Un journal invalide bloque ; agent forget ID permet un abandon explicitement choisi même abîmé/inachevé. Opération locale sans token/réseau, clé toujours nécessaire au Store chiffré. Références/objets/baux distants intacts. Un reçu retiré n’est plus reprenable par ID ; un nouveau transfert recrée un état ordinaire au lieu de rendre ce reçu. Annulation/erreur IO ou observateur pendant suppression peut laisser un préfixe retiré ; réessayer est sûr. Le dossier est synchronisé au succès et cette synchronisation est tentée après interruption. Pas de transaction atomique multi-record. Prune/forget ne libèrent jamais un bail distant : expiration ou administration explicite restent nécessaires. text Copier syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete --progress syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete --progress","headings":[{"id":"selective-transfer-retention-and-receipt-pruning","text":"Rétention sélective des transferts","level":1},{"id":"incomplete-pulls","text":"Pulls incomplets","level":2},{"id":"pruning-completed-local-receipts","text":"Purger les reçus locaux complets","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent prune\nsyrnck agent prune --delete --progress","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/upload-inventories"],"kind":"guide","revision":"b72677c79240"},{"id":"server-access","title":"Identités et permissions serveur","description":"Guide : Identités et permissions serveur.","section":"Transferts","order":510,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer\">Identités, verrous et historique serveur\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-access/server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Identités, verrous et historique serveur\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Un serveur par dépôt. Le mode configuré authentifie chaque requête comme un principal, vérifie chemins/branches en écriture et impose les baux d’assets lors d’une publication. La lecture brute des objets reste globale au dépôt : ces règles ne fournissent pas de confidentialité par chemin. Séparer les dépôts si les utilisateurs ne doivent pas lire les mêmes objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configure-identities\">Configurer les identités\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-access/configure-identities\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Configurer les identités\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-signatures\">schéma d’accès v2\u003c/a> ajoute clés publiques de committers\net exigence facultative d’histoire signée par branche. La v1 présentée ici\nconserve son comportement, permissions séparées du publieur et baux d’assets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Générer un bearer aléatoire distinct par personne/appareil avec keygen et le garder dans un gestionnaire ou environnement protégé. Avec SYRNCK_TOKEN, token-hash rend son digest BLAKE3 sans ouvrir de Store. Aucun secret brut dans config, historique, logs ou Git ; au moins 32 caractères aléatoires. Remplacer les placeholders JSON par 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules de token-hash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--access remplace l’authentification partagée : le jeton serveur n’est pas un repli. Fichier validé au démarrage, redémarrage requis après modification. Retirer un principal révoque ses prochaines requêtes ; changer son digest renouvelle le token sans changer identité/propriété des verrous. Les requêtes engagées suivent la configuration du démarrage. Proxy HTTPS toujours requis ; ce n’est pas du chiffrement de bout en bout.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Rôles reader, writer, admin. Tous peuvent lire objets/références configurées et acquérir des baux de transfert de lecture. Writer/admin uploadent, mais ne publient que branches listées et chemins permis. Admin ajoute seulement le droit de purger les verrous expirés, sans contourner branches/chemins. Reader exige listes d’écriture/branches vides. Dans write_paths, chaîne vide signifie dépôt entier ; liste vide, aucun droit. Préfixes aux frontières slash sous normalisation/casefold Unicode : Content ne couvre pas Contentious.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>IDs et branches : composant unique, 128 octets maximum. IDs uniques sous casefold portable et hashes de jetons uniques. Config administrateur : 1 Mio, 256 principaux, 1024 règles de branches, 1024 entrées par liste et 4096 octets UTF-8 par préfixe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"principals\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"writer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reviewer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"reader\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fast_forward\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"lock_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L22\" data-line=\"22\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"use-remote-asset-leases\">Baux d’assets distants\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-access/use-remote-asset-leases\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Baux d’assets distants\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ne pas fournir --owner : le serveur utilise l’identité authentifiée et refuse owner dans HTTP. Tout chemin demandé doit être inscriptible. Même atomicité, expiration et génération que les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/asset-locks\">baux locaux\u003c/a>. Les plans ne réservent rien ; reader peut lister et seul admin peut prévisualiser/purger les expirés à distance. Administration locale possible serveur arrêté et verrou libéré.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Conserver path/fence de l’acquisition dans claims.json puis fournir --lock-claims au push. Le serveur contrôle propriétaire authentifié, génération exacte et expiration de chaque claim. Tout chemin changé sous lock_paths exige un claim, suppressions et deux côtés des renommages/changements de casse compris. Un bail exact actif est respecté même hors préfixe obligatoire. Copier une génération d’autrui ne permet pas de publier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La première expiration est revérifiée après contrôle complet des dépendances/fichiers, juste avant transaction. Expiration ou recul d’horloge observé rejette ; renouveler les baux longs. Mutex serveur et verrou Store sérialisent les opérations. Les IDs de baux de transfert sont isolés par principal en interne ; connaître l’ID d’autrui ne révèle ni reçu ni fence. L’identité stable permet la reprise après rotation du token.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.uasset\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"path\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content/Map.ubulk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history\">Publier et récupérer l’historique autonome\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-access/publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Publier et récupérer l’historique autonome\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Push transfère la fermeture complète de la branche, parents et blocs d’assets compris, et exige l’extension identity. Un serveur configuré n’accepte que des racines révision sur ses noms de branches configurés. CAS contre la référence attendue protège les concurrences ; fast_forward true exige aussi une descendance de l’ancienne tête. Les objets uploadés peuvent rester non référencés après rejet, sans déplacement de branche.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant remplacement, l’ancienne tête est conservée sous archive/publish/REVISION, même si le remplacement forcé est autorisé. Cette protection précède durablement la publication ; une coupure/refus peut laisser une racine supplémentaire mais pas perdre d’abord l’ancienne histoire. Cachées aux listes distantes, ces racines survivent au GC ; leur \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait\u003c/a> exige une sauvegarde distincte vérifiée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les droits couvrent chaque nouveau commit contre tous ses parents et la différence entre anciennes/nouvelles têtes. Ajouter un chemin interdit puis le supprimer avant push reste interdit. Une fusion est conservatrice : différences contre l’un ou l’autre parent exigent droits/baux. Bornes par parcours : 100000 révisions nouvelles/anciennes, 1000000 liens, 16384 chemins portables changés dans 16 Mio, 1024 claims. Découper les grandes publications protégées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fetch vérifie la révision distante sous native-fetch/REVISION puis met à jour remotes/REMOTE_NAME/BRANCH atomiquement avec archive/fetch/REVISION de l’ancien suivi. Branche choisie, index et workspace ne changent pas ; merge/rebase restent explicites. Les refs de suivi sont des sélecteurs valides. Noms CLI de transfert limités à 64 octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sessions, baux, parallélisme borné, compression et annulation sont réutilisés. agent resume termine le transfert interrompu ; refaire native fetch termine le suivi. Racines/archives récupérées exigent un retrait administratif explicite ; le GC seul ne les enlève pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"http-extension\">Extension HTTP\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-access/http-extension\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Extension HTTP\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>GET /v1/identity rend version/id/role. Les capacités asset_locks et branch_policies valent 1 en mode configuré ; mode partagé historique : zéro, identité null et verrous distants refusés. Les anciens clients restent utilisables dans ce mode historique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>POST /v1/locks/ACTION, JSON au plus 1 Mio : list avec after null/chemin et limit 1..1000 ; acquire-plan/acquire avec paths 1..1024 et seconds 1..86400 ; renew avec claims et seconds ; release avec claims ; prune avec delete booléen, false pour aperçu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>PUT /v1/refs/NAME accepte root, expected, upload facultatif et locks facultatif. Même contrôle pour clients directs/CLI. La liste configurée ne révèle que branches configurées, pas index, archives, racines de verrous ou chemins de workspace.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>401 identité invalide ; 403 droits/politique/claim refusés ; 409 concurrence ; 412 bail d’envoi invalide ; 428 bail requis absent ; 400 requête/lock invalide ; 404 objet/reçu absent. Un plan réussi peut contenir des conflits sans mutation. Pas de reprise automatique des requêtes mutantes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette extension n’implique ni synchronisation complète Syncthing, ni service résident, GC distribué, lecture isolée par chemin ou journal d’audit distant. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">révisions signées\u003c/a> sont vérifiées pendant le transfert ; \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-signatures\">l’accès v2\u003c/a> peut aussi autoriser leurs clés et exiger une histoire nouvellement admise signée. L’adaptateur BEP séparé couvre uniquement le sous-ensemble décrit dans son \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing\">guide\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Identités, verrous et historique serveur # Un serveur par dépôt. Le mode configuré authentifie chaque requête comme un principal, vérifie chemins/branches en écriture et impose les baux d’assets lors d’une publication. La lecture brute des objets reste globale au dépôt : ces règles ne fournissent pas de confidentialité par chemin. Séparer les dépôts si les utilisateurs ne doivent pas lire les mêmes objets. Configurer les identités # Le schéma d’accès v2 ajoute clés publiques de committers et exigence facultative d’histoire signée par branche. La v1 présentée ici conserve son comportement, permissions séparées du publieur et baux d’assets. Générer un bearer aléatoire distinct par personne/appareil avec keygen et le garder dans un gestionnaire ou environnement protégé. Avec SYRNCK_TOKEN, token-hash rend son digest BLAKE3 sans ouvrir de Store. Aucun secret brut dans config, historique, logs ou Git ; au moins 32 caractères aléatoires. Remplacer les placeholders JSON par 64 caractères hexadécimaux minuscules de token-hash. --access remplace l’authentification partagée : le jeton serveur n’est pas un repli. Fichier validé au démarrage, redémarrage requis après modification. Retirer un principal révoque ses prochaines requêtes ; changer son digest renouvelle le token sans changer identité/propriété des verrous. Les requêtes engagées suivent la configuration du démarrage. Proxy HTTPS toujours requis ; ce n’est pas du chiffrement de bout en bout. Rôles reader, writer, admin. Tous peuvent lire objets/références configurées et acquérir des baux de transfert de lecture. Writer/admin uploadent, mais ne publient que branches listées et chemins permis. Admin ajoute seulement le droit de purger les verrous expirés, sans contourner branches/chemins. Reader exige listes d’écriture/branches vides. Dans write_paths, chaîne vide signifie dépôt entier ; liste vide, aucun droit. Préfixes aux frontières slash sous normalisation/casefold Unicode : Content ne couvre pas Contentious. IDs et branches : composant unique, 128 octets maximum. IDs uniques sous casefold portable et hashes de jetons uniques. Config administrateur : 1 Mio, 256 principaux, 1024 règles de branches, 1024 entrées par liste et 4096 octets UTF-8 par préfixe. json Copier { \"version\" : 1 , \"principals\" : [ { \"id\" : \"alice\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"writer\" , \"write_paths\" : [ \"Content\" , \"Source\" ] , \"branches\" : [ \"main\" ] } , { \"id\" : \"reviewer\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"reader\" , \"write_paths\" : [ ] , \"branches\" : [ ] } ] , \"branches\" : [ { \"name\" : \"main\" , \"fast_forward\" : true , \"lock_paths\" : [ \"Content\" ] } ] } text Copier syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Baux d’assets distants # Ne pas fournir --owner : le serveur utilise l’identité authentifiée et refuse owner dans HTTP. Tout chemin demandé doit être inscriptible. Même atomicité, expiration et génération que les baux locaux . Les plans ne réservent rien ; reader peut lister et seul admin peut prévisualiser/purger les expirés à distance. Administration locale possible serveur arrêté et verrou libéré. Conserver path/fence de l’acquisition dans claims.json puis fournir --lock-claims au push. Le serveur contrôle propriétaire authentifié, génération exacte et expiration de chaque claim. Tout chemin changé sous lock_paths exige un claim, suppressions et deux côtés des renommages/changements de casse compris. Un bail exact actif est respecté même hors préfixe obligatoire. Copier une génération d’autrui ne permet pas de publier. La première expiration est revérifiée après contrôle complet des dépendances/fichiers, juste avant transaction. Expiration ou recul d’horloge observé rejette ; renouveler les baux longs. Mutex serveur et verrou Store sérialisent les opérations. Les IDs de baux de transfert sont isolés par principal en interne ; connaître l’ID d’autrui ne révèle ni reçu ni fence. L’identité stable permet la reprise après rotation du token. text Copier syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100 syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/ json Copier [ { \"path\" : \"Content/Map.uasset\" , \"fence\" : 1 } , { \"path\" : \"Content/Map.ubulk\" , \"fence\" : 1 } ] text Copier syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease Publier et récupérer l’historique autonome # Push transfère la fermeture complète de la branche, parents et blocs d’assets compris, et exige l’extension identity. Un serveur configuré n’accepte que des racines révision sur ses noms de branches configurés. CAS contre la référence attendue protège les concurrences ; fast_forward true exige aussi une descendance de l’ancienne tête. Les objets uploadés peuvent rester non référencés après rejet, sans déplacement de branche. Avant remplacement, l’ancienne tête est conservée sous archive/publish/REVISION, même si le remplacement forcé est autorisé. Cette protection précède durablement la publication ; une coupure/refus peut laisser une racine supplémentaire mais pas perdre d’abord l’ancienne histoire. Cachées aux listes distantes, ces racines survivent au GC ; leur retrait exige une sauvegarde distincte vérifiée. Les droits couvrent chaque nouveau commit contre tous ses parents et la différence entre anciennes/nouvelles têtes. Ajouter un chemin interdit puis le supprimer avant push reste interdit. Une fusion est conservatrice : différences contre l’un ou l’autre parent exigent droits/baux. Bornes par parcours : 100000 révisions nouvelles/anciennes, 1000000 liens, 16384 chemins portables changés dans 16 Mio, 1024 claims. Découper les grandes publications protégées. Fetch vérifie la révision distante sous native-fetch/REVISION puis met à jour remotes/REMOTE_NAME/BRANCH atomiquement avec archive/fetch/REVISION de l’ancien suivi. Branche choisie, index et workspace ne changent pas ; merge/rebase restent explicites. Les refs de suivi sont des sélecteurs valides. Noms CLI de transfert limités à 64 octets. Sessions, baux, parallélisme borné, compression et annulation sont réutilisés. agent resume termine le transfert interrompu ; refaire native fetch termine le suivi. Racines/archives récupérées exigent un retrait administratif explicite ; le GC seul ne les enlève pas. text Copier syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main Extension HTTP # GET /v1/identity rend version/id/role. Les capacités asset_locks et branch_policies valent 1 en mode configuré ; mode partagé historique : zéro, identité null et verrous distants refusés. Les anciens clients restent utilisables dans ce mode historique. POST /v1/locks/ACTION, JSON au plus 1 Mio : list avec after null/chemin et limit 1..1000 ; acquire-plan/acquire avec paths 1..1024 et seconds 1..86400 ; renew avec claims et seconds ; release avec claims ; prune avec delete booléen, false pour aperçu. PUT /v1/refs/NAME accepte root, expected, upload facultatif et locks facultatif. Même contrôle pour clients directs/CLI. La liste configurée ne révèle que branches configurées, pas index, archives, racines de verrous ou chemins de workspace. 401 identité invalide ; 403 droits/politique/claim refusés ; 409 concurrence ; 412 bail d’envoi invalide ; 428 bail requis absent ; 400 requête/lock invalide ; 404 objet/reçu absent. Un plan réussi peut contenir des conflits sans mutation. Pas de reprise automatique des requêtes mutantes. Cette extension n’implique ni synchronisation complète Syncthing, ni service résident, GC distribué, lecture isolée par chemin ou journal d’audit distant. Les révisions signées sont vérifiées pendant le transfert ; l’accès v2 peut aussi autoriser leurs clés et exiger une histoire nouvellement admise signée. L’adaptateur BEP séparé couvre uniquement le sous-ensemble décrit dans son guide . { \"version\": 1, \"principals\": [ { \"id\": \"alice\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"writer\", \"write_paths\": [\"Content\", \"Source\"], \"branches\": [\"main\"] }, { \"id\": \"reviewer\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"reader\", \"write_paths\": [], \"branches\": [] } ], \"branches\": [ {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"]} ] } syrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease syrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/ syrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100 syrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk syrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/ [{\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"fence\":1},{\"path\":\"Content/Map.ubulk\",\"fence\":1}] syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease syrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main syrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease syrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main syrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main","headings":[{"id":"server-identities-asset-locks-and-native-history-transfer","text":"Identités, verrous et historique serveur","level":1},{"id":"configure-identities","text":"Configurer les identités","level":2},{"id":"use-remote-asset-leases","text":"Baux d’assets distants","level":2},{"id":"publish-and-fetch-autonomous-history","text":"Publier et récupérer l’historique autonome","level":2},{"id":"http-extension","text":"Extension HTTP","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21","L22","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4"],"languages":["json","text"],"code":"{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"principals\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"alice\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_ALICE_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"writer\",\n      \"write_paths\": [\"Content\", \"Source\"],\n      \"branches\": [\"main\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"reviewer\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_REVIEWER_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"reader\",\n      \"write_paths\": [],\n      \"branches\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"branches\": [\n    {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"]}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck --store server-store serve --access access.json --require-lease\nsyrnck locks identity --remote https://blocks.example.test/\nsyrnck locks acquire-plan --remote https://blocks.example.test/ Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks acquire --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --seconds 3600 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks list --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --limit 100\nsyrnck locks renew --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks release --remote https://blocks.example.test/ --fence 1 Content/Map.uasset Content/Map.ubulk\nsyrnck locks prune --remote https://blocks.example.test/\n[{\"path\":\"Content/Map.uasset\",\"fence\":1},{\"path\":\"Content/Map.ubulk\",\"fence\":1}]\nsyrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --lock-claims claims.json --require-lease\nsyrnck native push https://blocks.example.test/ --branch main --remote-branch main\nsyrnck native fetch https://blocks.example.test/ --remote-branch main --remote-name origin --require-read-lease\nsyrnck native ancestry HEAD remotes/origin/main\nsyrnck native merge-plan remotes/origin/main","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/server-signatures","#/fr/asset-locks","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/server-signatures","#/fr/syncthing"],"kind":"guide","revision":"1970d2132590"},{"id":"server-read-leases","title":"Baux de lecture serveur","description":"Guide : Baux de lecture serveur.","section":"Transferts","order":520,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"server-read-leases\">Baux de lecture serveur\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases/server-read-leases\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Baux de lecture serveur\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Un pull négocie automatiquement un bail avant son premier objet manquant avec un serveur compatible. La racine exacte est liée indépendamment de son nom de référence ; une reprise reste possible après déplacement/suppression de celle-ci tant que le bail est actif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>--require-read-lease refuse un serveur non compatible avant download. Une fermeture entièrement locale n’a pas besoin de bail même avec cette option. --require-lease reste réservé aux envois. Les anciens clients peuvent encore faire des GET : c’est une rétention négociée par client, pas une politique d’autorisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"lifecycle-and-retention\">Cycle de vie et rétention\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases/lifecycle-and-retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cycle de vie et rétention\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Avant accusé d’un bail neuf/expiré, le serveur vérifie toute sa fermeture et ses hashes de fichiers ; donnée absente/corrompue bloque. Le renouvellement actif évite cet audit répété. L’application hôte sérialise les accès Store ; le serveur fourni utilise mutex et verrou exclusif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>TTL 1..86400 secondes, défaut 3600 ; date UTC serveur arrondie vers le haut d’au plus une seconde. Le client renouvelle au tiers du TTL avec horloge monotone, par requête indépendante annulable qui n’accède pas au Store local ni aux callbacks. Réponses de contrôle limitées à 16 Kio, hors budgets/compteurs objet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>TTL court, validation locale longue, callback, contention ou panne réseau peuvent faire expirer. Une acquisition/relance après expiration change de fence ; un client observant ce changement au renouvellement s’arrête pour reprise. Aucune protection continue n’est garantie pendant le trou d’expiration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC hors ligne conserve toute la fermeture des baux actifs, laissant les autres objets collectables, même serveur tué/référence retirée. Avant suppression, il inscrit durablement expired et change le fence observé, empêchant un recul d’horloge de réactiver une génération collectée. La simulation n’écrit pas ces transitions ; un bail d’envoi peut imposer une protection supplémentaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après vidage des requêtes, le client annule/joint le renouvellement puis libère le bail avant publication locale. Les objets vérifiés et le journal local inachevé assurent alors la rétention. Le CAS local revérifie fermeture/fichiers, sans publier de version incomplète après annulation, corruption ou changement de destination.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La libération est fenced/idempotente, avec reprises bornées si réponse perdue. Une erreur garde la session inachevée ; resume réessaie avec racine/bail sauvés. Un bail absent/purgé est considéré libéré dès qu’aucune donnée distante n’est requise. Le reçu local complet ne contacte plus le serveur. Un abandon laisse autrement la protection jusqu’à expiration/libération admin ; agent forget ne retire que le journal local.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol\">Protocole\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases/protocol\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Protocole\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>HTTP/HTTPS authentifié existant. Les capacités annoncent read_leases 1 et read_lease_seconds. GET /v1/reads/ID rend bail/TTL ou 404 ; PUT acquiert/renouvelle avec exactement root et fence, null à création ; POST /release demande exactement fence. Succès : lease et lease_seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>IDs et fences sont deux aléas indépendants de 64 caractères hexadécimaux. Schéma strict v1 : version/id/root/fence/expires_at/state, actif/expiré/libéré. Racine immuable ; mauvais fence ou réactivation d’un released = 412. ID purgé recréé = nouveau fence. Ce contrôle concerne renouvellement/libération, pas les GET immuables adressés par hash.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Dossier STORE/reads/ID.json, maximum 4096 enregistrements inactifs compris. Liens, schémas inconnus et métadonnées invalides sont refusés. Écriture atomique synchronisée. Les *.tmp étrangers transitoires peuvent disparaître pendant une attente bornée de 50 ms ; les inconnus persistants échouent et les noms canoniques ne sont jamais ignorés. Métadonnées ordinaires même si objets chiffrés, sans jetons/URL/clés et même confiance filesystem que les références.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Journal local v3 ajoute read_lease nullable pour pull ; v1/v2 restent lisibles. Statistiques ajoutent read_lease à upload_lease, sans prouver leur activité actuelle. Endpoint capacités 404, read_leases 0 ou ancienne réponse valide upload-only permettent le mode ancien sauf option obligatoire. Réponse mal formée/autre erreur propagée ; un bail sauvegardé ne peut être dégradé silencieusement en download non protégé.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"offline-administration\">Administration hors ligne\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases/offline-administration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Administration hors ligne\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Arrêter le serveur avant ouverture CLI de son Store. La clé du Store chiffré est nécessaire, pas le jeton serveur. Prune valide tout l’inventaire borné puis retire les inactifs/expirés. Une libération volontaire peut interrompre la protection d’un lecteur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ne jamais lancer le GC d’un ancien binaire ignorant ces racines. La rétention concerne un seul Store serveur, sans réplication ni consensus distribué. Ni démarrage ni renouvellement ne remplacent une sauvegarde contre corruption, retrait externe ou suppression administrative du dossier de baux.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck leases list --read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck leases release ID --read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck leases prune --read\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Baux de lecture serveur # Un pull négocie automatiquement un bail avant son premier objet manquant avec un serveur compatible. La racine exacte est liée indépendamment de son nom de référence ; une reprise reste possible après déplacement/suppression de celle-ci tant que le bail est actif. --require-read-lease refuse un serveur non compatible avant download. Une fermeture entièrement locale n’a pas besoin de bail même avec cette option. --require-lease reste réservé aux envois. Les anciens clients peuvent encore faire des GET : c’est une rétention négociée par client, pas une politique d’autorisation. text Copier syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease Cycle de vie et rétention # Avant accusé d’un bail neuf/expiré, le serveur vérifie toute sa fermeture et ses hashes de fichiers ; donnée absente/corrompue bloque. Le renouvellement actif évite cet audit répété. L’application hôte sérialise les accès Store ; le serveur fourni utilise mutex et verrou exclusif. TTL 1..86400 secondes, défaut 3600 ; date UTC serveur arrondie vers le haut d’au plus une seconde. Le client renouvelle au tiers du TTL avec horloge monotone, par requête indépendante annulable qui n’accède pas au Store local ni aux callbacks. Réponses de contrôle limitées à 16 Kio, hors budgets/compteurs objet. TTL court, validation locale longue, callback, contention ou panne réseau peuvent faire expirer. Une acquisition/relance après expiration change de fence ; un client observant ce changement au renouvellement s’arrête pour reprise. Aucune protection continue n’est garantie pendant le trou d’expiration. Le GC hors ligne conserve toute la fermeture des baux actifs, laissant les autres objets collectables, même serveur tué/référence retirée. Avant suppression, il inscrit durablement expired et change le fence observé, empêchant un recul d’horloge de réactiver une génération collectée. La simulation n’écrit pas ces transitions ; un bail d’envoi peut imposer une protection supplémentaire. Après vidage des requêtes, le client annule/joint le renouvellement puis libère le bail avant publication locale. Les objets vérifiés et le journal local inachevé assurent alors la rétention. Le CAS local revérifie fermeture/fichiers, sans publier de version incomplète après annulation, corruption ou changement de destination. La libération est fenced/idempotente, avec reprises bornées si réponse perdue. Une erreur garde la session inachevée ; resume réessaie avec racine/bail sauvés. Un bail absent/purgé est considéré libéré dès qu’aucune donnée distante n’est requise. Le reçu local complet ne contacte plus le serveur. Un abandon laisse autrement la protection jusqu’à expiration/libération admin ; agent forget ne retire que le journal local. Protocole # HTTP/HTTPS authentifié existant. Les capacités annoncent read_leases 1 et read_lease_seconds. GET /v1/reads/ID rend bail/TTL ou 404 ; PUT acquiert/renouvelle avec exactement root et fence, null à création ; POST /release demande exactement fence. Succès : lease et lease_seconds. IDs et fences sont deux aléas indépendants de 64 caractères hexadécimaux. Schéma strict v1 : version/id/root/fence/expires_at/state, actif/expiré/libéré. Racine immuable ; mauvais fence ou réactivation d’un released = 412. ID purgé recréé = nouveau fence. Ce contrôle concerne renouvellement/libération, pas les GET immuables adressés par hash. Dossier STORE/reads/ID.json, maximum 4096 enregistrements inactifs compris. Liens, schémas inconnus et métadonnées invalides sont refusés. Écriture atomique synchronisée. Les *.tmp étrangers transitoires peuvent disparaître pendant une attente bornée de 50 ms ; les inconnus persistants échouent et les noms canoniques ne sont jamais ignorés. Métadonnées ordinaires même si objets chiffrés, sans jetons/URL/clés et même confiance filesystem que les références. Journal local v3 ajoute read_lease nullable pour pull ; v1/v2 restent lisibles. Statistiques ajoutent read_lease à upload_lease, sans prouver leur activité actuelle. Endpoint capacités 404, read_leases 0 ou ancienne réponse valide upload-only permettent le mode ancien sauf option obligatoire. Réponse mal formée/autre erreur propagée ; un bail sauvegardé ne peut être dégradé silencieusement en download non protégé. Administration hors ligne # Arrêter le serveur avant ouverture CLI de son Store. La clé du Store chiffré est nécessaire, pas le jeton serveur. Prune valide tout l’inventaire borné puis retire les inactifs/expirés. Une libération volontaire peut interrompre la protection d’un lecteur. Ne jamais lancer le GC d’un ancien binaire ignorant ces racines. La rétention concerne un seul Store serveur, sans réplication ni consensus distribué. Ni démarrage ni renouvellement ne remplacent une sauvegarde contre corruption, retrait externe ou suppression administrative du dossier de baux. text Copier syrnck leases list --read syrnck leases release ID --read syrnck leases prune --read syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4 syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease syrnck leases list --read syrnck leases release ID --read syrnck leases prune --read","headings":[{"id":"server-read-leases","text":"Baux de lecture serveur","level":1},{"id":"lifecycle-and-retention","text":"Cycle de vie et rétention","level":2},{"id":"protocol","text":"Protocole","level":2},{"id":"offline-administration","text":"Administration hors ligne","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck serve --read-lease-seconds 3600\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --require-read-lease --requests 4\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --require-read-lease\nsyrnck leases list --read\nsyrnck leases release ID --read\nsyrnck leases prune --read","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"93d6c85ac0de"},{"id":"server-signatures","title":"Signatures de révision autorisées","description":"Lier clés de committer et comptes serveur, puis imposer une histoire signée.","section":"Transferts","order":515,"parent":"server-access","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"configuration\">Configurer les clés approuvées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-signatures/configuration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Configurer les clés approuvées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le schéma facultatif v2 du fichier d’accès ajoute l’autorisation des signatures\naux \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">comptes, permissions et verrous\u003c/a>. La v1 garde ses champs\net son comportement exacts. La v2 conserve les trois champs racine, mais chaque\nprincipal ajoute \u003ccode>signing_keys\u003c/code> et chaque branche \u003ccode>require_signatures\u003c/code>.\nUn ancien serveur refuse cette configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"version\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"principals\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"token_hash\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"role\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"writer\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"write_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">      \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"signing_keys\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">        \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L12\" data-line=\"12\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"public_key\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L13\" data-line=\"13\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Alice\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L14\" data-line=\"14\">          \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"email\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"alice@example.invalid\"\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L15\" data-line=\"15\">        \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L16\" data-line=\"16\">      \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L17\" data-line=\"17\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L18\" data-line=\"18\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L19\" data-line=\"19\">  \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"branches\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L20\" data-line=\"20\">    \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"name\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fast_forward\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"lock_paths\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Content\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"require_signatures\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token boolean\">true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L21\" data-line=\"21\">  \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L22\" data-line=\"22\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Remplacer le placeholder de jeton avec \u003ccode>token-hash\u003c/code>. Obtenir la clé publique via\n\u003ccode>native identity\u003c/code> avec la clé de signature chargée, ou la signature d’une\nrévision vérifiée. Le fichier contient des clés publiques, jamais de graines.\nChaque liaison contient exactement \u003ccode>public_key,name,email\u003c/code> : clé Ed25519\ncanonique et identité UTF-8 exacte du committer autorisé. Limites de nom/adresse\nidentiques aux \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/identity\">identités natives\u003c/a>. Fuseau et date\nrestent des déclarations, sans preuve du moment de signature.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Au plus 32 clés par principal. Une clé n’apparaît qu’une fois dans toute la\nconfiguration, autres comptes compris. Clé mal formée, non canonique, hors courbe\nou de petit ordre : refus au démarrage. Reader exige une liste vide ; writer/admin\npeut n’avoir aucune clé s’il transporte seulement le travail d’un autre committer\napprouvé. Plafonds existants de 1 Mio et de comptes/branches conservés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck help server-signatures\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"validation\">Valider chaque nouveau commit admis\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-signatures/validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Valider chaque nouveau commit admis\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sur une branche avec \u003ccode>require_signatures: true\u003c/code>, chaque révision nouvellement\natteignable doit être v2 avec une signature Ed25519 valide. Sa clé publique doit\nêtre enregistrée et les nom/adresse du committer doivent correspondre exactement.\nLe compte signataire doit actuellement pouvoir écrire la branche cible et chaque\nchemin changé dans ce commit, comparaisons avec chaque parent de merge comprises.\nUne tête correcte ne masque pas un commit intermédiaire ou second parent non\nsigné, à clé inconnue ou identité différente. Un contenu ajouté puis retiré avant\nla tête reste contrôlé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le publieur authentifié exige séparément permissions de branche/chemins et\nrevendications de verrous valides. Publieur et signataire peuvent différer :\nun collègue peut transporter l’histoire approuvée. Le rôle admin ne contourne pas\nla politique de signature. Fast-forward, validation complète des dépendances,\ncontrôle final d’expiration des verrous et publication sous valeur attendue\nrestent applicables. Un refus conserve la référence publiée ; les objets déjà\nenvoyés suivent les règles ordinaires de transfert et GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls les ancêtres de la tête publiée actuelle de cette branche constituent une\nbase déjà admise. Activer la politique sur une branche existante n’exige pas de\nréécrire son histoire ancienne, mais chaque nouveau commit doit la respecter.\nUne tête d’une autre branche n’admet pas cette histoire ici. Une publication\ninitiale sur branche vide exige donc toute l’histoire introduite conforme.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un véritable retour forcé doit aussi cibler une tête signée actuellement approuvée,\nmême déjà dans la base ; aucun retour vers métadonnées anciennes, non signées ou\nà clé révoquée. Une reprise strictement identique de la tête publiée peut terminer\nun transfert après révocation : elle n’introduit aucune révision. Avec\n\u003ccode>require_signatures: false\u003c/code>, règles usuelles de jeton/chemins/branches, tout en\nvérifiant cryptographiquement les signatures présentes dans les objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation\">Renouveler et révoquer\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-signatures/rotation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Renouveler et révoquer\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le fichier d’accès appartient à l’administration. Les modifications exigent un\nredémarrage serveur, comme comptes et jetons. Pour renouveler, enregistrer la\nnouvelle clé publique avec l’ancienne, distribuer la nouvelle clé privée par un\nmécanisme protégé, puis retirer l’ancienne clé publique au moment choisi.\nRetirer une liaison révoque les admissions futures avec cette clé. Retirer un\ncompte ou ses permissions de branche/chemins affecte aussi les publications futures.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’histoire déjà admise reste lisible avec ses octets de signature d’origine.\nUn nouvel enfant approuvé peut prolonger cette base après révocation d’une clé\nancienne. Réintroduire des commits à clé ancienne par une nouvelle histoire est\nrefusé. Rotation du jeton et rotation de signature sont indépendantes. La politique\nutilise la configuration actuelle du démarrage, pas la date déclarée du commit.\nLes requêtes engagées suivent cette configuration immuable jusqu’à leur fin.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Frontières de confiance\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-signatures/limits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Frontières de confiance\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’autorisation concerne le committer actuel et la révision complète signée.\nL’auteur d’origine peut différer ou être null après réécriture ; son nom reste\nune déclaration historique. Aucun horodatage fiable, validité historique de\ncertificat, flux distribué de révocation, rechargement à chaud ou coffre système\nde clé de signature ici. Ni confidentialité de lecture par chemin ni chiffrement\nde bout en bout : le serveur reçoit encore les objets décodés et exige le\ndéploiement HTTPS habituel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification\">Vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-signatures/verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tests de schéma strict, clés/identités différentes, tous les nouveaux parents,\ntransport délégué, ACL séparées signataire/publieur, bases anciennes déjà admises,\nrévocation, rotation et retours interdits. Les tests HTTP authentifiés transfèrent\naussi une histoire signée via un Store chiffré vers un lecteur sans clé privée,\nredémarrent avec de nouvelles politiques et auditent les objets conservés.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Configurer les clés approuvées # Le schéma facultatif v2 du fichier d’accès ajoute l’autorisation des signatures aux comptes, permissions et verrous . La v1 garde ses champs et son comportement exacts. La v2 conserve les trois champs racine, mais chaque principal ajoute signing_keys et chaque branche require_signatures . Un ancien serveur refuse cette configuration. json Copier { \"version\" : 2 , \"principals\" : [ { \"id\" : \"alice\" , \"token_hash\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\" , \"role\" : \"writer\" , \"write_paths\" : [ \"Source\" , \"Content\" ] , \"branches\" : [ \"main\" ] , \"signing_keys\" : [ { \"public_key\" : \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\" , \"name\" : \"Alice\" , \"email\" : \"alice@example.invalid\" } ] } ] , \"branches\" : [ { \"name\" : \"main\" , \"fast_forward\" : true , \"lock_paths\" : [ \"Content\" ] , \"require_signatures\" : true } ] } Remplacer le placeholder de jeton avec token-hash . Obtenir la clé publique via native identity avec la clé de signature chargée, ou la signature d’une révision vérifiée. Le fichier contient des clés publiques, jamais de graines. Chaque liaison contient exactement public_key,name,email : clé Ed25519 canonique et identité UTF-8 exacte du committer autorisé. Limites de nom/adresse identiques aux identités natives . Fuseau et date restent des déclarations, sans preuve du moment de signature. Au plus 32 clés par principal. Une clé n’apparaît qu’une fois dans toute la configuration, autres comptes compris. Clé mal formée, non canonique, hors courbe ou de petit ordre : refus au démarrage. Reader exige une liste vide ; writer/admin peut n’avoir aucune clé s’il transporte seulement le travail d’un autre committer approuvé. Plafonds existants de 1 Mio et de comptes/branches conservés. text Copier syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease syrnck help server-signatures Valider chaque nouveau commit admis # Sur une branche avec require_signatures: true , chaque révision nouvellement atteignable doit être v2 avec une signature Ed25519 valide. Sa clé publique doit être enregistrée et les nom/adresse du committer doivent correspondre exactement. Le compte signataire doit actuellement pouvoir écrire la branche cible et chaque chemin changé dans ce commit, comparaisons avec chaque parent de merge comprises. Une tête correcte ne masque pas un commit intermédiaire ou second parent non signé, à clé inconnue ou identité différente. Un contenu ajouté puis retiré avant la tête reste contrôlé. Le publieur authentifié exige séparément permissions de branche/chemins et revendications de verrous valides. Publieur et signataire peuvent différer : un collègue peut transporter l’histoire approuvée. Le rôle admin ne contourne pas la politique de signature. Fast-forward, validation complète des dépendances, contrôle final d’expiration des verrous et publication sous valeur attendue restent applicables. Un refus conserve la référence publiée ; les objets déjà envoyés suivent les règles ordinaires de transfert et GC. Seuls les ancêtres de la tête publiée actuelle de cette branche constituent une base déjà admise. Activer la politique sur une branche existante n’exige pas de réécrire son histoire ancienne, mais chaque nouveau commit doit la respecter. Une tête d’une autre branche n’admet pas cette histoire ici. Une publication initiale sur branche vide exige donc toute l’histoire introduite conforme. Un véritable retour forcé doit aussi cibler une tête signée actuellement approuvée, même déjà dans la base ; aucun retour vers métadonnées anciennes, non signées ou à clé révoquée. Une reprise strictement identique de la tête publiée peut terminer un transfert après révocation : elle n’introduit aucune révision. Avec require_signatures: false , règles usuelles de jeton/chemins/branches, tout en vérifiant cryptographiquement les signatures présentes dans les objets. Renouveler et révoquer # Le fichier d’accès appartient à l’administration. Les modifications exigent un redémarrage serveur, comme comptes et jetons. Pour renouveler, enregistrer la nouvelle clé publique avec l’ancienne, distribuer la nouvelle clé privée par un mécanisme protégé, puis retirer l’ancienne clé publique au moment choisi. Retirer une liaison révoque les admissions futures avec cette clé. Retirer un compte ou ses permissions de branche/chemins affecte aussi les publications futures. L’histoire déjà admise reste lisible avec ses octets de signature d’origine. Un nouvel enfant approuvé peut prolonger cette base après révocation d’une clé ancienne. Réintroduire des commits à clé ancienne par une nouvelle histoire est refusé. Rotation du jeton et rotation de signature sont indépendantes. La politique utilise la configuration actuelle du démarrage, pas la date déclarée du commit. Les requêtes engagées suivent cette configuration immuable jusqu’à leur fin. Frontières de confiance # L’autorisation concerne le committer actuel et la révision complète signée. L’auteur d’origine peut différer ou être null après réécriture ; son nom reste une déclaration historique. Aucun horodatage fiable, validité historique de certificat, flux distribué de révocation, rechargement à chaud ou coffre système de clé de signature ici. Ni confidentialité de lecture par chemin ni chiffrement de bout en bout : le serveur reçoit encore les objets décodés et exige le déploiement HTTPS habituel. Vérification # text Copier python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\" Tests de schéma strict, clés/identités différentes, tous les nouveaux parents, transport délégué, ACL séparées signataire/publieur, bases anciennes déjà admises, révocation, rotation et retours interdits. Les tests HTTP authentifiés transfèrent aussi une histoire signée via un Store chiffré vers un lecteur sans clé privée, redémarrent avec de nouvelles politiques et auditent les objets conservés. { \"version\": 2, \"principals\": [ { \"id\": \"alice\", \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\", \"role\": \"writer\", \"write_paths\": [\"Source\", \"Content\"], \"branches\": [\"main\"], \"signing_keys\": [ { \"public_key\": \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\", \"name\": \"Alice\", \"email\": \"alice@example.invalid\" } ] } ], \"branches\": [ {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"], \"require_signatures\": true} ] } syrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease syrnck help server-signatures python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck python scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"","headings":[{"id":"configuration","text":"Configurer les clés approuvées","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Valider chaque nouveau commit admis","level":2},{"id":"rotation","text":"Renouveler et révoquer","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Frontières de confiance","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Vérification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","L12","L13","L14","L15","L16","L17","L18","L19","L20","L21","L22","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2"],"languages":["json","text"],"code":"{\n  \"version\": 2,\n  \"principals\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"alice\",\n      \"token_hash\": \"REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_HASH\",\n      \"role\": \"writer\",\n      \"write_paths\": [\"Source\", \"Content\"],\n      \"branches\": [\"main\"],\n      \"signing_keys\": [\n        {\n          \"public_key\": \"REPLACE_WITH_64_LOWERCASE_HEX_PUBLIC_KEY\",\n          \"name\": \"Alice\",\n          \"email\": \"alice@example.invalid\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"branches\": [\n    {\"name\": \"main\", \"fast_forward\": true, \"lock_paths\": [\"Content\"], \"require_signatures\": true}\n  ]\n}\nsyrnck --store server-store serve --access access-v2.json --require-lease\nsyrnck help server-signatures\npython scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_server_signatures_tests syrnck\npython scripts/build.py test -R \"^server_(signatures|signatures_policy|access_policy)$\"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/server-access","#/fr/native-authorship/identity"],"kind":"guide","revision":"71a0652d9bc6"},{"id":"server-upload-leases","title":"Baux d’envoi persistants","description":"Guide : Baux d’envoi persistants.","section":"Transferts","order":530,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-server-upload-leases\">Baux d’envoi persistants\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases/persistent-server-upload-leases\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Baux d’envoi persistants\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les nouveaux clients protègent automatiquement leurs envois si le serveur annonce l’extension. Les baux survivent aux redémarrages et protègent pendant le GC hors ligne ; ils expirent sans renouvellement, puis les références publiées prennent le relais au succès.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le serveur utilise son authentification partagée ou les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">identités configurées\u003c/a>, une écoute loopback et un proxy HTTPS pour le distant. Les Stores chiffrés ont leur propre clé. Texte bilingue, protocole anglais. Cette extension concerne l’upload ; les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">baux de lecture\u003c/a> protègent les pulls séparément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"lifetime-gc-and-administration\">Durée, GC et administration\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases/lifetime-gc-and-administration\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Durée, GC et administration\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Défaut 3600 secondes, réglage 1..86400, arrondi d’expiration au plus une seconde. Renouvellement au tiers du TTL via horloge monotone client, sans synchronisation des horloges ; le serveur conserve l’expiration sur son horloge murale à maintenir correctement. Recul peut prolonger, avance expirer. Avant suppression GC, une expiration observée est inscrite durablement avec nouveau fence ; recul ultérieur ne réactive pas une génération collectée. Un refus de publication expirée l’enregistre aussi ; pas d’écriture en simulation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le coordinateur planifie les renouvellements durant attentes de reprise/débit. Un worker au plus, HTTP indépendant pour ne pas bloquer les objets, sans accès Store ni callbacks utilisateur. Résultat/fence contrôlés par le coordinateur ; worker annulé/joint en sortie et terminé avant publication. Sa connexion/tampons de contrôle sont hors compteurs objet. IO locale, callback et contrôles synchrones peuvent retarder la planification : choisir un TTL suffisant, aucun maintien pendant une pause arbitraire n’est garanti.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un ancien bail actif non expiré bloque tout GC du Store, simulation comprise. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires paginés\u003c/a> ne gardent que les objets déclarés. Les expirés/libérés/publiés ne conservent rien ; expiration ne supprime pas les payloads sans GC explicite. Maintenance hors ligne, serveur tenant son verrou exclusif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après arrêt, list/release/prune administrent les baux. Release révoque sans modifier références/payloads ni reçu publié. Prune valide puis retire inactifs et générations d’inventaire obsolètes ; actifs conservés. Retirer un reçu peut réduire les preuves d’un client déconnecté, jamais autoriser un ancien CAS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Au plus 4096 enregistrements reçus compris, création bloquée à capacité ; 16 Kio par record, 1 Mio par page d’inventaire. La liste matérialise cet inventaire borné. Métadonnées mal formées/liées refusées. Tous les moteurs de GC doivent connaître l’extension.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store server-data leases list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store server-data leases prune\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"fencing-and-resume\">Fencing et reprise\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases/fencing-and-resume\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fencing et reprise\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le client persiste un ID aléatoire avant création serveur ; le bail lie racine exacte, référence et ancienne valeur attendue. Renouvellement vivant garde le fence, réactivation/recréation en change. Toute publication louée porte ce fence ; expiré/libéré/périmé est refusé avant publication, même après purge/recréation d’ID. C’est une coordination locale sous verrou, pas un consensus multi-serveur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un changement de fence pendant renouvellement interrompt pour resume : une requête de présence avant un trou de rétention n’est plus fiable. Des objets collectés peuvent être renvoyés ; le journal local garde la source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le serveur vérifie toute la fermeture, applique le CAS sauvegardé puis écrit un reçu durable published. Deux écritures atomiques distinctes : une coupure entre elles laisse bail actif et référence possiblement publiée. La confirmation à la reprise peut enregistrer le reçu d’une racine correspondante sans nouveau PUT de référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le reçu lie le descripteur d’origine. Répéter sa publication accuse le reçu, sans modifier une référence déplacée ensuite ; la reprise peut donc réussir après un autre écrivain. Sans reçu et référence non correspondante, la publication reste incertaine. Le reçu seul ne conserve pas d’ancienne version après retrait des références.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’annulation laisse le bail jusqu’à expiration/libération ; agent forget ne contacte pas le serveur. Les records ont IDs/fences/clés d’objets, pas jetons ni clés de chiffrement. En mode configuré, leur propriété est isolée par principal ; le mode partagé historique conserve son identité partagée.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-and-compatibility\">Protocole et compatibilité\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases/protocol-and-compatibility\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Protocole et compatibilité\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Capacités : upload_leases 1, lease_seconds, éventuellement upload_inventories 1. GET uploads/ID rend lease/TTL ou 404 ; PUT crée/renouvelle avec root/reference/expected/fence et inventory true optionnel. PUT inventory/INDEX inscrit une page avant présence ; POST confirm avec fence confirme une référence correspondante sans la publier. PUT refs/NAME avec upload {id,fence} applique/réconcilie le CAS lié.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Création fence null, idempotente pour descripteur identique ; renouvellement avec dernier fence. Requêtes/réponses de contrôle 16 Kio, grandes entrées rejetées avant parsing. STORE/uploads/ID.json v1 strict : version,id,reference,root,expected,fence,expires_at,state active/expired/published/released, avec écriture synchronisée atomique. V2 ajoute inventory_pages/inventory_hash ; inscriptions séparées au plus 1 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>412 refuse publication expirée/libérée/fenced ; 428 refuse absence de bail obligatoire. Le client les interprète comme rejets connus seulement sur son chemin loué ; l’ambiguïté reste code 3 et aucun PUT final n’est repris automatiquement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>404 des capacités ou upload_leases 0 permet l’ancien mode sauf protection exigée. Erreur réseau, schéma invalide ou perte de support d’un bail enregistré échoue sans dégradation. --require-lease client refuse avant upload ; côté serveur, il refuse le PUT final non loué, pas les PUT d’objets historiques. Cette option concerne push/resume de push, pas pull.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les journaux locaux écrivent v3 avec upload_lease nullable hérité de v2 et read_lease ; v1/v2 lisibles. Les statistiques portent ces IDs historiques, pas la preuve de validité actuelle. Objets logiques, pointeurs Git et ABI C restent identiques.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Baux d’envoi persistants # Les nouveaux clients protègent automatiquement leurs envois si le serveur annonce l’extension. Les baux survivent aux redémarrages et protègent pendant le GC hors ligne ; ils expirent sans renouvellement, puis les références publiées prennent le relais au succès. Le serveur utilise son authentification partagée ou les identités configurées , une écoute loopback et un proxy HTTPS pour le distant. Les Stores chiffrés ont leur propre clé. Texte bilingue, protocole anglais. Cette extension concerne l’upload ; les baux de lecture protègent les pulls séparément. text Copier syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease Durée, GC et administration # Défaut 3600 secondes, réglage 1..86400, arrondi d’expiration au plus une seconde. Renouvellement au tiers du TTL via horloge monotone client, sans synchronisation des horloges ; le serveur conserve l’expiration sur son horloge murale à maintenir correctement. Recul peut prolonger, avance expirer. Avant suppression GC, une expiration observée est inscrite durablement avec nouveau fence ; recul ultérieur ne réactive pas une génération collectée. Un refus de publication expirée l’enregistre aussi ; pas d’écriture en simulation. Le coordinateur planifie les renouvellements durant attentes de reprise/débit. Un worker au plus, HTTP indépendant pour ne pas bloquer les objets, sans accès Store ni callbacks utilisateur. Résultat/fence contrôlés par le coordinateur ; worker annulé/joint en sortie et terminé avant publication. Sa connexion/tampons de contrôle sont hors compteurs objet. IO locale, callback et contrôles synchrones peuvent retarder la planification : choisir un TTL suffisant, aucun maintien pendant une pause arbitraire n’est garanti. Un ancien bail actif non expiré bloque tout GC du Store, simulation comprise. Les inventaires paginés ne gardent que les objets déclarés. Les expirés/libérés/publiés ne conservent rien ; expiration ne supprime pas les payloads sans GC explicite. Maintenance hors ligne, serveur tenant son verrou exclusif. Après arrêt, list/release/prune administrent les baux. Release révoque sans modifier références/payloads ni reçu publié. Prune valide puis retire inactifs et générations d’inventaire obsolètes ; actifs conservés. Retirer un reçu peut réduire les preuves d’un client déconnecté, jamais autoriser un ancien CAS. Au plus 4096 enregistrements reçus compris, création bloquée à capacité ; 16 Kio par record, 1 Mio par page d’inventaire. La liste matérialise cet inventaire borné. Métadonnées mal formées/liées refusées. Tous les moteurs de GC doivent connaître l’extension. text Copier syrnck --store server-data leases list syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID syrnck --store server-data leases prune Fencing et reprise # Le client persiste un ID aléatoire avant création serveur ; le bail lie racine exacte, référence et ancienne valeur attendue. Renouvellement vivant garde le fence, réactivation/recréation en change. Toute publication louée porte ce fence ; expiré/libéré/périmé est refusé avant publication, même après purge/recréation d’ID. C’est une coordination locale sous verrou, pas un consensus multi-serveur. Un changement de fence pendant renouvellement interrompt pour resume : une requête de présence avant un trou de rétention n’est plus fiable. Des objets collectés peuvent être renvoyés ; le journal local garde la source. Le serveur vérifie toute la fermeture, applique le CAS sauvegardé puis écrit un reçu durable published. Deux écritures atomiques distinctes : une coupure entre elles laisse bail actif et référence possiblement publiée. La confirmation à la reprise peut enregistrer le reçu d’une racine correspondante sans nouveau PUT de référence. Le reçu lie le descripteur d’origine. Répéter sa publication accuse le reçu, sans modifier une référence déplacée ensuite ; la reprise peut donc réussir après un autre écrivain. Sans reçu et référence non correspondante, la publication reste incertaine. Le reçu seul ne conserve pas d’ancienne version après retrait des références. L’annulation laisse le bail jusqu’à expiration/libération ; agent forget ne contacte pas le serveur. Les records ont IDs/fences/clés d’objets, pas jetons ni clés de chiffrement. En mode configuré, leur propriété est isolée par principal ; le mode partagé historique conserve son identité partagée. Protocole et compatibilité # Capacités : upload_leases 1, lease_seconds, éventuellement upload_inventories 1. GET uploads/ID rend lease/TTL ou 404 ; PUT crée/renouvelle avec root/reference/expected/fence et inventory true optionnel. PUT inventory/INDEX inscrit une page avant présence ; POST confirm avec fence confirme une référence correspondante sans la publier. PUT refs/NAME avec upload {id,fence} applique/réconcilie le CAS lié. Création fence null, idempotente pour descripteur identique ; renouvellement avec dernier fence. Requêtes/réponses de contrôle 16 Kio, grandes entrées rejetées avant parsing. STORE/uploads/ID.json v1 strict : version,id,reference,root,expected,fence,expires_at,state active/expired/published/released, avec écriture synchronisée atomique. V2 ajoute inventory_pages/inventory_hash ; inscriptions séparées au plus 1 Mio. 412 refuse publication expirée/libérée/fenced ; 428 refuse absence de bail obligatoire. Le client les interprète comme rejets connus seulement sur son chemin loué ; l’ambiguïté reste code 3 et aucun PUT final n’est repris automatiquement. 404 des capacités ou upload_leases 0 permet l’ancien mode sauf protection exigée. Erreur réseau, schéma invalide ou perte de support d’un bail enregistré échoue sans dégradation. --require-lease client refuse avant upload ; côté serveur, il refuse le PUT final non loué, pas les PUT d’objets historiques. Cette option concerne push/resume de push, pas pull. Les journaux locaux écrivent v3 avec upload_lease nullable hérité de v2 et read_lease ; v1/v2 lisibles. Les statistiques portent ces IDs historiques, pas la preuve de validité actuelle. Objets logiques, pointeurs Git et ABI C restent identiques. syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease syrnck --store server-data leases list syrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID syrnck --store server-data leases prune","headings":[{"id":"persistent-server-upload-leases","text":"Baux d’envoi persistants","level":1},{"id":"lifetime-gc-and-administration","text":"Durée, GC et administration","level":2},{"id":"fencing-and-resume","text":"Fencing et reprise","level":2},{"id":"protocol-and-compatibility","text":"Protocole et compatibilité","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck --store server-data serve --lease-seconds 3600 --require-lease\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --require-lease\nsyrnck --store server-data leases list\nsyrnck --store server-data leases release LEASE_ID\nsyrnck --store server-data leases prune","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/server-access","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/upload-inventories"],"kind":"guide","revision":"438bd5ede7c7"},{"id":"settings","title":"Préférences du dépôt","description":"Guide : Préférences du dépôt.","section":"Stockage","order":540,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"7","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"repository-settings\">Préférences du dépôt\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/settings/repository-settings\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préférences du dépôt\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Chaque Store peut conserver les préférences locales d’ingestion et d’empaquetage dans \u003ccode>settings.json\u003c/code>, un fichier optionnel versionné distinct de \u003ccode>config.json\u003c/code>, des clés, références et objets logiques. Sans ce fichier, les anciens défauts s’appliquent ; ouvrir ou inspecter le dépôt ne le crée pas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les mises à jour valident toute la configuration proposée puis remplacent atomiquement le fichier sous verrou exclusif. Les champs non précisés sont conservés. Champs inconnus, versions non gérées, types et valeurs invalides sont refusés. Aucun identifiant secret, clé ou autorisation persistante de compacter/supprimer n’y est enregistré. Liens et fichiers non ordinaires sont refusés. \u003ccode>config reset\u003c/code> peut remplacer un fichier ordinaire mal formé par les défauts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Défauts : 1 worker, tampon de 32 Mio, 512 Mio par nouveau pack et 65536 objets par pack. Workers : 1..64 ; tampon : 1..1024 Mio, avec place pour un bloc maximum, donc au moins 4 Mio pour le CDC par défaut. Les limites de packs existantes restent applicables. Le budget couvre les octets bruts en attente, pas toute la RAM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>native commit\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>git clean\u003c/code> lisent ces valeurs puis appliquent \u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> applique \u003ccode>--max-mib\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>--max-objects\u003c/code>. Ces substitutions ne réécrivent pas les préférences ; compaction et suppression restent explicites. Les caches gardent leurs options par processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>En C++, \u003ccode>Store::settings()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Store::set_settings()\u003c/code> gèrent ces préférences. \u003ccode>ingest(input, chunking)\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>pack()\u003c/code> sans options les utilisent ; des \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>PackOptions\u003c/code> explicites remplacent l’ensemble des options de l’opération. L’ingestion native, les filtres Git et l’ABI C/Go partagent ce chemin par défaut. Noms d’API, champs JSON, IDs et pointeurs restent indépendants de la langue ; le CLI traduit les diagnostics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ces préférences ne modifient ni les données existantes ni leur format et ne sont pas des racines de rétention. L’audit et le reset restent disponibles si elles sont invalides. Chaque Store a ses préférences propres. Pour un filtre Git, utiliser \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> avec le magasin commun affiché par \u003ccode>git install\u003c/code> ; sinon \u003ccode>config\u003c/code> adresse \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code>. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/operations\">progression et l’annulation\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck config reset\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"reader-features\">Capacités de lecteur requises\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/settings/reader-features\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Capacités de lecteur requises\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck config required-features\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ces exigences décrivent la sémantique du Store, indépendamment des réglages de\nperformance. Un workspace lié exige automatiquement \u003ccode>linked-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> avant\npublication de sa liaison. Les anciens exécutables refusent la configuration\nétendue à l’ouverture, évitant de déplacer une branche partagée sans réservation.\nPour un Store lié expérimental créé avant cette protection, lancer la commande\nexplicite avant d’utiliser d’anciens outils. Une publication normale de références\navec contextes enregistrés établit aussi la protection. Un workspace partiel exige\naussi \u003ccode>sparse-workspaces-v1\u003c/code> avant publication de sa sélection.\nChanger une sélection active exige aussi \u003ccode>materialization-transitions-v1\u003c/code> avant\npublication de son journal récupérable. Revenir à une vue complète ne retire\npas ces exigences de lecteur additives.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace-relocation\">déplacement du dossier entier\u003c/a> exige\n\u003ccode>workspace-relocation-v1\u003c/code> avant publication de son journal. L’exigence reste\nactive après réussite ou annulation, pour empêcher un ancien lecteur d’ouvrir\nle Store lorsque l’ancien dossier lié est temporairement absent.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls les noms pris en charge sont acceptés. Activation idempotente, format\nphysique et objets préservés ; les exigences survivent aux packs/GC, sauvegarde,\nrestauration et copies réparées. \u003ccode>config reset\u003c/code> change les réglages ajustables en\ngardant ces exigences. Aucun retrait ou downgrade automatique. C++ expose\n\u003ccode>required_features()\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>require_features(names, operation)\u003c/code>. Voir\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/protocol/required-store-features\">le format\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship/identity\">identité native\u003c/a> est distincte des préférences\nd’ingestion. Sauver un profil ou une révision v2 active \u003ccode>authored-revisions-v1\u003c/code>\navant écriture ; effacer le profil ne rétrograde pas le Store. Le profil reste\npropre à la machine et n’est pas copié par les sauvegardes logiques.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent\">agent résident de synchronisation\u003c/a> établit \u003ccode>resident-sync-v1\u003c/code>\navant les traces de publication ou un checkout avec propriétaire. Cette exigence\nprotège observations distantes conservées et reprise contrôlée du journal v11.\nElle survit à la fin, au GC et aux sauvegardes/restaurations logiques ; lire un\nStore classique seul ne l’active pas.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Préférences du dépôt # Chaque Store peut conserver les préférences locales d’ingestion et d’empaquetage dans settings.json , un fichier optionnel versionné distinct de config.json , des clés, références et objets logiques. Sans ce fichier, les anciens défauts s’appliquent ; ouvrir ou inspecter le dépôt ne le crée pas. Les mises à jour valident toute la configuration proposée puis remplacent atomiquement le fichier sous verrou exclusif. Les champs non précisés sont conservés. Champs inconnus, versions non gérées, types et valeurs invalides sont refusés. Aucun identifiant secret, clé ou autorisation persistante de compacter/supprimer n’y est enregistré. Liens et fichiers non ordinaires sont refusés. config reset peut remplacer un fichier ordinaire mal formé par les défauts. Défauts : 1 worker, tampon de 32 Mio, 512 Mio par nouveau pack et 65536 objets par pack. Workers : 1..64 ; tampon : 1..1024 Mio, avec place pour un bloc maximum, donc au moins 4 Mio pour le CDC par défaut. Les limites de packs existantes restent applicables. Le budget couvre les octets bruts en attente, pas toute la RAM. put , native commit et git clean lisent ces valeurs puis appliquent --workers / --buffer-mib . pack applique --max-mib / --max-objects . Ces substitutions ne réécrivent pas les préférences ; compaction et suppression restent explicites. Les caches gardent leurs options par processus. En C++, Store::settings() et Store::set_settings() gèrent ces préférences. ingest(input, chunking) et pack() sans options les utilisent ; des IngestOptions ou PackOptions explicites remplacent l’ensemble des options de l’opération. L’ingestion native, les filtres Git et l’ABI C/Go partagent ce chemin par défaut. Noms d’API, champs JSON, IDs et pointeurs restent indépendants de la langue ; le CLI traduit les diagnostics. Ces préférences ne modifient ni les données existantes ni leur format et ne sont pas des racines de rétention. L’audit et le reset restent disponibles si elles sont invalides. Chaque Store a ses préférences propres. Pour un filtre Git, utiliser --store avec le magasin commun affiché par git install ; sinon config adresse .syrnck . Voir la progression et l’annulation . text Copier syrnck config show syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536 syrnck config reset Capacités de lecteur requises # text Copier syrnck config required-features syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1 Ces exigences décrivent la sémantique du Store, indépendamment des réglages de performance. Un workspace lié exige automatiquement linked-workspaces-v1 avant publication de sa liaison. Les anciens exécutables refusent la configuration étendue à l’ouverture, évitant de déplacer une branche partagée sans réservation. Pour un Store lié expérimental créé avant cette protection, lancer la commande explicite avant d’utiliser d’anciens outils. Une publication normale de références avec contextes enregistrés établit aussi la protection. Un workspace partiel exige aussi sparse-workspaces-v1 avant publication de sa sélection. Changer une sélection active exige aussi materialization-transitions-v1 avant publication de son journal récupérable. Revenir à une vue complète ne retire pas ces exigences de lecteur additives. Le déplacement du dossier entier exige workspace-relocation-v1 avant publication de son journal. L’exigence reste active après réussite ou annulation, pour empêcher un ancien lecteur d’ouvrir le Store lorsque l’ancien dossier lié est temporairement absent. Seuls les noms pris en charge sont acceptés. Activation idempotente, format physique et objets préservés ; les exigences survivent aux packs/GC, sauvegarde, restauration et copies réparées. config reset change les réglages ajustables en gardant ces exigences. Aucun retrait ou downgrade automatique. C++ expose required_features() et require_features(names, operation) . Voir le format . Une identité native est distincte des préférences d’ingestion. Sauver un profil ou une révision v2 active authored-revisions-v1 avant écriture ; effacer le profil ne rétrograde pas le Store. Le profil reste propre à la machine et n’est pas copié par les sauvegardes logiques. L’ agent résident de synchronisation établit resident-sync-v1 avant les traces de publication ou un checkout avec propriétaire. Cette exigence protège observations distantes conservées et reprise contrôlée du journal v11. Elle survit à la fin, au GC et aux sauvegardes/restaurations logiques ; lire un Store classique seul ne l’active pas. syrnck config show syrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536 syrnck config reset syrnck config required-features syrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1","headings":[{"id":"repository-settings","text":"Préférences du dépôt","level":1},{"id":"reader-features","text":"Capacités de lecteur requises","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck config show\nsyrnck config set --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --pack-max-mib 512 --pack-max-objects 65536\nsyrnck config reset\nsyrnck config required-features\nsyrnck config require-feature linked-workspaces-v1","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/operations","#/fr/native-workspace-relocation","#/fr/protocol/required-store-features","#/fr/native-authorship/identity","#/fr/resident-agent"],"kind":"guide","revision":"8c3af3c95d7f"},{"id":"signing-api","title":"API de signature Ed25519","description":"Clés en mémoire à déplacement exclusif, signatures standard et vérification stricte.","section":"API","order":650,"parent":"integrations","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"usage\">Signer et vérifier des octets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/signing-api/usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Signer et vérifier des octets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/signing.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> signer \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">SigningKey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">generate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> public_key \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> signer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">public_key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> signature \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> signer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">sign\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>payload\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">bool\u003c/span> valid \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">verify_ed25519\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>public_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> signature\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> payload\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> fingerprint \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">signing_key_id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>public_key\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>payload\u003c/code> est une vue d’octets ; les messages vides sont acceptés. Clés publiques\nde 32 octets, signatures de 64 octets. Il s’agit d’Ed25519 avec SHA-512, défini\npar la \u003ca href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RFC 8032\u003c/a>, fourni par le module\noptionnel de Monocypher 4.0.3 embarqué. La variante EdDSA avec BLAKE2b de base\nest un autre algorithme. Cette API n’exige ni OpenSSL ni exécution réseau.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership\">Propriété des clés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/signing-api/ownership\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Propriété des clés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>SigningKey::from_seed(bytes)\u003c/code> accepte exactement 32 octets, copie l’entrée et\ndéveloppe sa clé privée en interne. L’appelant reste propriétaire de son entrée.\n\u003ccode>generate()\u003c/code> utilise l’aléa du système. L’objet se déplace sans pouvoir être\ncopié, n’expose aucun export secret et efface son secret développé à destruction.\nUn objet déplacé refuse signature et accès à sa clé publique. \u003ccode>public_key()\u003c/code>\nrenvoie une copie publique. Utiliser des graines de signature indépendantes des\nclés de chiffrement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’empreinte commence par \u003ccode>ed25519:\u003c/code>, suivi du BLAKE3 de la clé publique précédée\ndu domaine \u003ccode>Syrnck Ed25519 public key v1:\u003c/code>. Cet identifiant ne prouve pas qu’une\npersonne possède la clé. L’appelant doit autoriser la clé publique séparément\navant d’attribuer un contenu signé à une identité.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification\">Contrat de vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/signing-api/verification\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Contrat de vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>La vérification renvoie faux pour longueurs incorrectes, messages ou signatures\naltérés, encodages de points non canoniques et clés publiques de petit ordre.\nCes dernières ne permettent pas d’authentifier un signataire. La primitive amont\nd’équation de courbe vérifie ce cas ; le moteur n’implémente pas son propre\ncalcul elliptique. L’encodage refuse aussi les éléments hors plage canonique et\nles zéros négatifs. L’amont vérifie scalaire de signature et équation de courbe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’appelant définit les octets signés et leur domaine. Cette API n’établit pas\nconfiance d’identité, horodatage fiable, unicité de signature ou validité de\ncertificat. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-authorship\">Auteurs natifs et signatures de commits\u003c/a> utilisent un\npayload de révision versionné. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-signatures\">serveurs configurés\u003c/a>\npeuvent autoriser clés et profils de committer. Le coffre système reste à développer. Les encodages des révisions anciennes restent inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation\">Construction et vérification\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/signing-api/validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Construction et vérification\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les tests couvrent trois vecteurs RFC 8032, chaque modification d’un bit de leurs\nsignatures, messages modifiés, longueurs incorrectes, clés faibles, encodages non\ncanoniques et déplacements de clé. Un test construit une signature avec zéro\nnégatif satisfaisant l’équation amont, puis exige son refus strict par le moteur.\nCes contrôles fonctionnels ne mesurent pas les canaux auxiliaires matériels.\nMonocypher 4.0.3 contient le \u003ca href=\"https://monocypher.org/changelog\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">correctif temporel amont\u003c/a>.\nProvenance et sommes de contrôle figurent dans\n\u003ccode>third_party/monocypher/UPSTREAM.json\u003c/code> ; les notices accompagnent la distribution.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Signer et vérifier des octets # cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp> auto signer = syrnck :: SigningKey :: generate ( ) ; auto public_key = signer . public_key ( ) ; auto signature = signer . sign ( payload ) ; bool valid = syrnck :: verify_ed25519 ( public_key , signature , payload ) ; auto fingerprint = syrnck :: signing_key_id ( public_key ) ; payload est une vue d’octets ; les messages vides sont acceptés. Clés publiques de 32 octets, signatures de 64 octets. Il s’agit d’Ed25519 avec SHA-512, défini par la RFC 8032 , fourni par le module optionnel de Monocypher 4.0.3 embarqué. La variante EdDSA avec BLAKE2b de base est un autre algorithme. Cette API n’exige ni OpenSSL ni exécution réseau. Propriété des clés # SigningKey::from_seed(bytes) accepte exactement 32 octets, copie l’entrée et développe sa clé privée en interne. L’appelant reste propriétaire de son entrée. generate() utilise l’aléa du système. L’objet se déplace sans pouvoir être copié, n’expose aucun export secret et efface son secret développé à destruction. Un objet déplacé refuse signature et accès à sa clé publique. public_key() renvoie une copie publique. Utiliser des graines de signature indépendantes des clés de chiffrement. L’empreinte commence par ed25519: , suivi du BLAKE3 de la clé publique précédée du domaine Syrnck Ed25519 public key v1: . Cet identifiant ne prouve pas qu’une personne possède la clé. L’appelant doit autoriser la clé publique séparément avant d’attribuer un contenu signé à une identité. Contrat de vérification # La vérification renvoie faux pour longueurs incorrectes, messages ou signatures altérés, encodages de points non canoniques et clés publiques de petit ordre. Ces dernières ne permettent pas d’authentifier un signataire. La primitive amont d’équation de courbe vérifie ce cas ; le moteur n’implémente pas son propre calcul elliptique. L’encodage refuse aussi les éléments hors plage canonique et les zéros négatifs. L’amont vérifie scalaire de signature et équation de courbe. L’appelant définit les octets signés et leur domaine. Cette API n’établit pas confiance d’identité, horodatage fiable, unicité de signature ou validité de certificat. Auteurs natifs et signatures de commits utilisent un payload de révision versionné. Les serveurs configurés peuvent autoriser clés et profils de committer. Le coffre système reste à développer. Les encodages des révisions anciennes restent inchangés. Construction et vérification # text Copier python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\" Les tests couvrent trois vecteurs RFC 8032, chaque modification d’un bit de leurs signatures, messages modifiés, longueurs incorrectes, clés faibles, encodages non canoniques et déplacements de clé. Un test construit une signature avec zéro négatif satisfaisant l’équation amont, puis exige son refus strict par le moteur. Ces contrôles fonctionnels ne mesurent pas les canaux auxiliaires matériels. Monocypher 4.0.3 contient le correctif temporel amont . Provenance et sommes de contrôle figurent dans third_party/monocypher/UPSTREAM.json ; les notices accompagnent la distribution. #include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp> auto signer = syrnck::SigningKey::generate(); auto public_key = signer.public_key(); auto signature = signer.sign(payload); bool valid = syrnck::verify_ed25519(public_key, signature, payload); auto fingerprint = syrnck::signing_key_id(public_key); python scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests python scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"","headings":[{"id":"usage","text":"Signer et vérifier des octets","level":2},{"id":"ownership","text":"Propriété des clés","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Contrat de vérification","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Construction et vérification","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","code-2-L1","code-2-L2"],"languages":["cpp","text"],"code":"#include \u003csyrnck/signing.hpp>\nauto signer = syrnck::SigningKey::generate();\nauto public_key = signer.public_key();\nauto signature = signer.sign(payload);\nbool valid = syrnck::verify_ed25519(public_key, signature, payload);\nauto fingerprint = syrnck::signing_key_id(public_key);\npython scripts/build.py build --target syrnck_signing_tests\npython scripts/build.py test -R \"^signing$\"","assets":[],"links":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032","#/fr/native-authorship","#/fr/server-signatures","https://monocypher.org/changelog"],"kind":"guide","revision":"536e53ab9afb"},{"id":"storage","title":"Stockage et reconstruction","description":"Stockage et reconstruction","section":"Stockage","order":100,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch2 id=\"objects\">Objets et versions complètes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/storage/objects\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Objets et versions complètes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un ID BLAKE3 typé adresse un chunk, manifeste ou révision. Le manifeste conserve la liste complète ordonnée des blocs, longueur, hash complet et découpage. Une révision contient un snapshot complet des chemins et ses parents. Partager des blocs ne crée aucune dépendance à rejouer une ancienne version de fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code> conserve le manifeste sous \u003ccode>files/ID\u003c/code> ; \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> le restaure exclusivement vers un nouveau chemin. \u003ccode>inspect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>compare\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>diff\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>refs\u003c/code> consultent les métadonnées. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-comparison\">comparaison\u003c/a> et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/file-ranges\">plages vérifiées\u003c/a>. La reconstruction complète vérifie chaque bloc et le hash du fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention\">Rétention avant collecte\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/storage/retention\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rétention avant collecte\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Branches, tags, index, archives et pins conservent leurs dépendances, parents de révisions compris. Retirer un commit visible ne supprime pas nécessairement ses blocs. Inspecter d’abord les protections, retirer ensuite explicitement les racines avec une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">sauvegarde vérifiée\u003c/a>, puis seulement envisager le GC destructif. Le GC prévisualise par défaut et refuse toute dépendance conservée absente/corrompue avant suppression.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/checkout-retention\">racines de checkout\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/selective-retention\">journaux de transfert\u003c/a> et baux actifs ont leurs règles propres. Sauvegarde et restauration sont des snapshots logiques, pas des copies de fichiers de travail non commités. Stabiliser les fichiers modifiés par d’autres programmes pendant leur analyse.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"physical\">Stockage physique et budgets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/storage/physical\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stockage physique et budgets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les nouveaux dépôts stockent des objets isolés v1. L’empaquetage explicite sélectionne le stockage physique v2/v3 tout en gardant les IDs et enveloppes logiques v1. Voir \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs\">packs\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">index paginés\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/compaction\">compaction\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/audit\">audits\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/maintenance\">annulation de maintenance\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Défauts FastCDC : minimum 256 Kio, cible 1 Mio, maximum 4 Mio ; découpage fixe optionnel. Les objets décompressés et manifestes/révisions plats sont limités à 16 Mio. Cache décodé : 64 Mio ; empreintes vérifiées : 8192 entrées ; pages d’index : 4 Mio. Ces budgets ne bornent pas toute la mémoire du processus. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/settings\">préférences\u003c/a> contrôlent séparément workers d’ingestion et création des packs.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Objets et versions complètes # Un ID BLAKE3 typé adresse un chunk, manifeste ou révision. Le manifeste conserve la liste complète ordonnée des blocs, longueur, hash complet et découpage. Une révision contient un snapshot complet des chemins et ses parents. Partager des blocs ne crée aucune dépendance à rejouer une ancienne version de fichier. put conserve le manifeste sous files/ID ; get le restaure exclusivement vers un nouveau chemin. inspect , compare , diff et refs consultent les métadonnées. Voir la comparaison et les plages vérifiées . La reconstruction complète vérifie chaque bloc et le hash du fichier. Rétention avant collecte # Branches, tags, index, archives et pins conservent leurs dépendances, parents de révisions compris. Retirer un commit visible ne supprime pas nécessairement ses blocs. Inspecter d’abord les protections, retirer ensuite explicitement les racines avec une sauvegarde vérifiée , puis seulement envisager le GC destructif. Le GC prévisualise par défaut et refuse toute dépendance conservée absente/corrompue avant suppression. Les racines de checkout , journaux de transfert et baux actifs ont leurs règles propres. Sauvegarde et restauration sont des snapshots logiques, pas des copies de fichiers de travail non commités. Stabiliser les fichiers modifiés par d’autres programmes pendant leur analyse. Stockage physique et budgets # Les nouveaux dépôts stockent des objets isolés v1. L’empaquetage explicite sélectionne le stockage physique v2/v3 tout en gardant les IDs et enveloppes logiques v1. Voir packs , index paginés , compaction , audits et annulation de maintenance . Défauts FastCDC : minimum 256 Kio, cible 1 Mio, maximum 4 Mio ; découpage fixe optionnel. Les objets décompressés et manifestes/révisions plats sont limités à 16 Mio. Cache décodé : 64 Mio ; empreintes vérifiées : 8192 entrées ; pages d’index : 4 Mio. Ces budgets ne bornent pas toute la mémoire du processus. Les préférences contrôlent séparément workers d’ingestion et création des packs.","headings":[{"id":"objects","text":"Objets et versions complètes","level":2},{"id":"retention","text":"Rétention avant collecte","level":2},{"id":"physical","text":"Stockage physique et budgets","level":2}],"anchors":[],"languages":[],"code":"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/file-comparison","#/fr/file-ranges","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/checkout-retention","#/fr/selective-retention","#/fr/packs","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/compaction","#/fr/audit","#/fr/maintenance","#/fr/settings"],"kind":"guide","revision":"c9b75c0246f9"},{"id":"syncthing","title":"Adaptateur de blocs Syncthing","description":"Guide : Adaptateur de blocs Syncthing.","section":"API","order":660,"parent":"integrations","sourceRevision":"9","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"syncthing-fixed-block-adapter\">Adaptateur Syncthing à blocs fixes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/syncthing-fixed-block-adapter\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Adaptateur Syncthing à blocs fixes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Ce module Go optionnel relie le C++ au modèle de blocs BEP via la bibliothèque Syncthing non modifiée \u003cstrong>v2.1.7\u003c/strong>, commit \u003ccode>d6d0181f7d46\u003c/code>, pseudo-version figée dans go.mod/go.sum. Le binding cœur reste séparé, sans dépendances Go externes. Le module demande Go 1.26.2+.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le module gère données de fichiers ordinaires, connexions à appareils associés,\ncatalogue causal durable et contrôleur continu de workspace natif. \u003ccode>Folder\u003c/code>\ncapture les changements locaux, applique les versions entrantes vérifiées et\nconserve l’état de reprise ; \u003ccode>FolderSession\u003c/code> le fait fonctionner sur BEP\nauthentifié. Découverte, interface d’appairage, installation du service OS et\nsémantique complète des dossiers Syncthing restent à terminer, selon les limites\nprécisées ci-dessous.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-and-test\">Bibliothèque native et dépendances\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/build-and-test\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Bibliothèque native et dépendances\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compiler \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> et télécharger une fois les dépendances Go figées avec \u003ccode>go mod download\u003c/code> depuis \u003ccode>bindings/syncthing\u003c/code>. L’adaptateur est optionnel, séparé du binding cœur. Linux/macOS utilisent cgo et leur chemin de chargeur. Catalogue et transport précédents ont passé la CI Linux ; ce nouveau contrôleur est vérifié localement sous Windows, son exécution multiplateforme reste à qualifier. Ne distribuer que les dépendances et notices requises pour l’intégration choisie.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apis\">API\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/apis\" aria-label=\"Lien vers API\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>NewFile(ctx,store,manifest,folder,name,blockSize)\u003c/code> crée une vue immuable servable. Il vérifie le hash complet sans exporter puis lit des plages vérifiées pour construire les \u003ccode>protocol.FileInfo\u003c/code> SHA-256. CDC/blocs fixes et IDs BLAKE3 du moteur restent intacts. Taille 0 : politique Syncthing ; sinon puissance de 2 de 128 Kio à 16 Mio. Index limité 65536 blocs. Fichier vide : bloc SHA-256 vide unique de référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Info()\u003c/code> copie l’index indépendamment ; l’application y pose vecteurs de version, séquences et timestamps avant annonce. Noms relatifs UTF-8 valides : noms natifs Darwin NFD normalisés en NFC et séparateurs Windows convertis en \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> protocolaire. Index, requêtes de blocs et identité de reprise utilisent le même nom canonique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ReadRequest(ctx,req)\u003c/code> sert exactement un bloc annoncé pour ce folder/fichier. Un SHA-256 fourni doit correspondre ; hash optionnel et ancien numéro de bloc 0/défaut sont acceptés. Une requête temporaire peut revenir à cette source immuable complète. Autres chemins/plages/générations refusés. Le payload est vérifié par le moteur puis par SHA-256 avant retour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Fetch(ctx,destination,folder,info,request,options)\u003c/code> reconstruit un fichier annoncé. Fournir \u003ccode>protocol.Connection.Request\u003c/code> ou un autre demandeur authentifié. Chaque réponse doit avoir taille/hash exacts. \u003ccode>ReuseManifest\u003c/code> indexe une version locale à la taille reçue pour réutiliser blocs identiques, déplacés ou répétés ; seuls les absents sont demandés. \u003ccode>FetchResult.Stats\u003c/code> sépare octets demandés, réemploi local et staging vérifié.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après assemblage/vérification privée, Fetch ingère le fichier complet via ABI C avec blocs fixes BEP et recontrôle l’index stocké contre les SHA-256 reçus. \u003ccode>ffi/ID\u003c/code> retient le manifeste. L’application peut restaurer exclusivement ou l’inclure dans sa propre transaction ; Fetch ne remplace ni fichier de travail ni branche mutable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_file_verify\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>Store.VerifyFile\u003c/code> vérifient fichier complet, blocs, tailles et file_hash, sans destination ni référence, en buffers natifs bornés. Une bibliothèque reconstruite avec ce symbole est nécessaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, &quot;project&quot;, &quot;Content/Map.uasset&quot;, 0)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">// Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, &quot;project&quot;, incomingInfo,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">    connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">        ReuseManifest: previousManifest,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">        ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">    })\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">// Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"folder-catalog\">Catalogue persistant de dossier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-catalog\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Catalogue persistant de dossier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenCatalog(ctx, store, CatalogOptions)\u003c/code> charge le catalogue persistant ou crée\nun brouillon vide non publié. Associer le dossier à l’ID complet de l’appareil\nlocal et à la liste exacte des pairs de confiance. Doublons, appareil local,\nsentinelles et collisions des identités 64 bits des vecteurs sont refusés.\nChanger la liste d’options ne modifie pas implicitement les autorisations.\nUne procédure distincte de gestion des membres reste à réaliser.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Catalog\u003c/code> ne garde ni Store ni connexion. \u003ccode>LocalIndex\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>PeerIndex\u003c/code> rendent des\ncopies indépendantes. \u003ccode>RecordLocal\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>RecordLocalChanges\u003c/code> attribue les séquences\nlocales et avance le vecteur local antérieur avec contrôle de débordement ; les\nchamps causaux fournis doivent être vides. Un lot est validé entièrement avec un\nseul tri des noms. Les suppressions doivent être des tombstones explicites.\nDossiers et liens symboliques sont conservés comme métadonnées ; aucune création\nde dossier ni résolution de cible de lien n’est faite par cette API.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ReceiveIndex(CatalogIndex)\u003c/code> conserve un Index complet ou un delta d’un pair.\nL’appelant doit lier \u003ccode>Device\u003c/code> à la connexion authentifiée, autoriser le dossier\net prendre \u003ccode>IndexID\u003c/code> dans son ClusterConfig négocié. Une nouvelle génération\nexige un index complet ; les lots suivants continuent la séquence persistée.\nNoms et séquences uniques, vecteurs triés et bornés : compteurs dupliqués,\nflags invalides, entrées chiffrées non prises en charge et blocs mal formés\nsont refusés. Un vecteur identique ne peut décrire d’autres métadonnées de\ncontenu. Recevoir une annonce ne la transforme pas en fichier local et ne\nlance aucun téléchargement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Attach(ctx, store, device, name, manifest)\u003c/code> vérifie les données contre les blocs\nde l’entrée actuellement enregistrée. \u003ccode>Adopt(device, name)\u003c/code> promeut une entrée\nvérifiée causalement plus récente dans l’index local annoncé. Une concurrence\nrend \u003ccode>ErrCatalogConflict\u003c/code> ; versions antérieures et contenu incohérent à vecteur\nidentique sont refusés. Les deux contenus restent conservés sans gagnant\nimplicite. Seul le catalogue annoncé change : l’application au workspace doit\navoir sa transaction de reprise avant qu’un service annonce un dossier à jour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Commit(ctx, store)\u003c/code> vérifie les données retenues puis publie état et données\nsous une racine unique \u003ccode>ffi/bep/…\u003c/code> de révision native protégée par valeur attendue.\nLes catalogues précédents sont parents natifs : reset d’index, renommage et\nsuppression conservent donc les versions complètes au GC et en sauvegarde.\nLes chemins de payload sont les hashes des identités appareil/nom : noms\n\u003ccode>.git/config\u003c/code>, distincts par la casse et incompatibles avec un workspace portable\nrestent stockables sans perte. Cette API ne les matérialise pas. Aucune racine\nimplicite \u003ccode>ffi/manifestID\u003c/code> n’est ajoutée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un brouillon périmé rend \u003ccode>ErrCatalogStale\u003c/code> : recharger et replanifier sans\nrecibler l’opération ancienne. Une erreur d’IO de publication laisse un résultat\ninconnu ; recharger et établir le résultat persistant avant toute reprise.\nUn commit inchangé ne fait rien. Le nouvel IndexID local devient persistant au\npremier commit réussi. Le codec refuse champs dupliqués/inconnus et état non\ncanonique. Limites : 256 pairs, 256 compteurs/vecteur, 65536 entrées au total et\n16 Mio de métadonnées. Chaque commit vérifie actuellement fichiers retenus et\nfermeture native ; débit sur grand projet non établi. Retrait de l’historique\net partitionnement du catalogue restent à réaliser.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"staged-download\">Télécharger sans garder le Store ouvert\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/staged-download\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Télécharger sans garder le Store ouvert\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>DownloadFile(ctx, folder, info, request, DownloadOptions)\u003c/code> vérifie les blocs\nréseau dans un staging privé sans ouvrir le Store natif. \u003ccode>ReuseIndex\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>ReadReuse\u003c/code> réutilisent les blocs locaux identiques ; le callback peut ouvrir\net fermer brièvement un Store. Des tailles de blocs différentes désactivent\nle réemploi. La reprise utilise les mêmes descripteurs et contrôles que Fetch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le \u003ccode>StagedFile\u003c/code> retourné ne garde ni fichier ouvert ni Store. Toujours appeler\n\u003ccode>Close()\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>Info()\u003c/code> copie indépendamment ; \u003ccode>Stats()\u003c/code> décrit transfert, réemploi\net staging. Appeler \u003ccode>Prepare(ctx, store)\u003c/code> en possédant brièvement le Store :\nimport sans racine implicite et recontrôle contre les blocs annoncés. Attacher\nle manifeste à la version courante exacte du pair, l’adopter si nécessaire et\nfaire Commit avant de fermer le Store ou d’autoriser le GC. L’attente réseau ne\ngarde ainsi pas le verrou exclusif natif. Revérifier l’entrée persistée après\ntransfert, car elle peut avoir avancé pendant le téléchargement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PrepareFetch\u003c/code> simplifie l’appel si un Store est déjà ouvert, avec la même règle\nde rétention explicite. \u003ccode>Fetch\u003c/code> garde sa rétention indépendante historique.\nNe pas supprimer une racine partagée historique appartenant à un autre appelant.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"device-connections\">Authentifier une connexion d’appareil\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/device-connections\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Authentifier une connexion d’appareil\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenDeviceConnection(ctx,stream,DeviceOptions,model)\u003c/code> prend possession d’un\n\u003ccode>net.Conn\u003c/code> connecté et non chiffré, y compris en cas d’échec. Fournir un certificat\nTLS local unique, sa clé privée \u003ccode>crypto.Signer\u003c/code> correspondante et le\n\u003ccode>protocol.DeviceID\u003c/code> du pair explicitement associé. \u003ccode>Server: true\u003c/code> sélectionne le\ncôté acceptant. Auto-connexion, identités vides/réservées, clés malformées et\ndiscordance certificat/clé sont refusées. Ne modifier ni certificat ni clé en cours d’utilisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>TLS 1.3 ou ultérieur et ALPN \u003ccode>bep/1.0\u003c/code> sont exigés. Le Hello amont borné est\néchangé avant de rejeter un pair non associé, puis l’identité SHA-256 de son\nunique certificat est comparée exactement. C’est un épinglage de certificat :\nDNS, CA Web PKI, expiration, découverte et nom Hello n’accordent pas l’accès.\n\u003ccode>PeerHello\u003c/code> contient des descriptions non fiables. Aucun modèle applicatif\nn’est attaché avant la vérification réussie.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    Certificate: deviceCertificate,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">    RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">    Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: &quot;Asset workstation&quot;},\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">}, folderModel)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">// Traiter err avant utilisation. Démarrage explicite, puis ClusterConfig comme\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">// premier message après authentification. Le modèle autorise chaque dossier.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">session.Start()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">// Envoyer un Index complet avant les mises à jour, sauf accord persistant\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L11\" data-line=\"11\">// d’index delta. Fetch peut utiliser session.Request pour les blocs vérifiés.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>HandshakeTimeout\u003c/code> vaut 20 secondes par défaut et accepte de 100 ms à une minute.\nIl couvre TLS et Hello ; une échéance du contexte plus proche l’emporte. La\ncompression des métadonnées amont est le défaut ; Never/Always sont acceptés.\nLe contexte contrôle toute la session, même authentifiée mais pas encore démarrée.\n\u003ccode>Close(reason)\u003c/code> libère le flux ; attendre le callback \u003ccode>Closed\u003c/code> du modèle avant\nde fermer les handles natifs qu’utilisent ses callbacks. Les métadonnées de\nconnexion gardent une date d’établissement et un ID de session stables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application fournit stockage/rotation des certificats, Device ID de confiance,\nplanification, transactions du catalogue, reprise du workspace et résolution\ndes conflits. Le chiffrement du transport protège et\nauthentifie ce pair ; un daemon recevant des données ordinaires ne devient pas\nune sauvegarde chiffrée non fiable. Le parcours complet de chiffrement vers un\nappareil non fiable reste à intégrer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reference-daemon\">Vérifier avec le daemon de référence\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/reference-daemon\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Vérifier avec le daemon de référence\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Ce helper de test facultatif compile \u003ccode>cmd/syncthing\u003c/code> à la version exacte du module\nde l’adaptateur avec les manifests figés \u003ccode>tests/syncthing-reference/go.mod\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>go.sum\u003c/code>. Le tag amont \u003ccode>noassets\u003c/code> omet les ressources d’interface web et conserve\ndaemon réel, base de données, scanner, modèle, puller et API REST. La sortie est\n\u003ccode>build/qualification/syncthing\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>.exe\u003c/code> sous Windows). Sans \u003ccode>--download\u003c/code>, le cache\nde modules doit être prêt : aucun téléchargement implicite. La version amont\nembarquée est contrôlée ; version différente ou manifests modifiés sont refusés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Définir \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SYNCTHING_BINARY\u003c/code> avec son chemin absolu puis exécuter :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Sous Linux/macOS, fournir le chemin de la bibliothèque partagée construite. La\nCI des trois plateformes active explicitement l’adaptateur et ce test. Le daemon\nutilise configuration, clés et dossier neufs propres au test, écoute uniquement\nen loopback, désactive découverte/relais/NAT/mises à jour/rapports et est terminé\naprès le test. Aucun profil Syncthing installé n’est lu ou modifié.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La vérification locale Windows a exercé les deux sens contre le daemon amont\nnon modifié : un asset de 384 Kio à blocs déplacés/réutilisés transfère uniquement\nle bloc absent de 128 Kio dans chaque sens. La reconstruction dans un stockage\nSyrnck chiffré vérifie le fichier complet. Les annonces réelles de renommage et\ntombstone ont aussi été reçues. Cela prouve transport et interopérabilité des\nfichiers ordinaires. Un autre test réel conserve index complets/deltas et données,\nferme les Stores pendant l’attente réseau puis se reconnecte après un renommage\nhors ligne avec tombstone persistant. Le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/standalone-service\">service autonome\u003c/a> est disponible pour les fichiers ordinaires.\nSans binaire explicite, ce test annonce qu’il est ignoré ; les tests ordinaires\nde l’adaptateur continuent de s’exécuter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resume-and-cancellation\">Reprise et annulation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/resume-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reprise et annulation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Sans \u003ccode>ResumeDirectory\u003c/code>, Fetch utilise un fichier temporaire privé retiré au retour. Avec cette option, le dossier par contenu appartient à l’appelant : \u003ccode>state.json\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>data\u003c/code>. Le descripteur lie folder/nom/taille/taille de bloc et hash de liste complète. Autre index, fichier inconnu, descripteur incomplet ou lien est refusé. Utiliser un dossier privé et sérialiser ses gestionnaires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque plage de staging est rehashée au retry. Seules les plages complètes conformes sont réutilisées ; partielles/corrompues redemandées. Les trous remplis de zéro peuvent satisfaire un bloc zéro sans requête. Flush tous les 16 blocs et avant ingestion. Annulation préserve le staging fourni par l’appelant, qui reste \u003cstrong>en clair même avec Store chiffré\u003c/strong> ; protéger l’emplacement. Le retrait des dossiers terminés est explicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le contexte est vérifié entre appels natifs/étapes réseau/blocs. La préparation sans racine implicite propage l’annulation dans l’ingestion native ; l’ingestion retenue historique et la vérification complète terminent leur appel natif synchrone courant. Une annulation tardive ou changement détecté peut laisser un manifeste complet retenu, dont l’ID accompagne l’erreur. Traiter l’erreur avant publication. Aucun fichier de travail partiel installé. Un kill peut laisser du privé ; durabilité après coupure et retrait automatique ne sont pas établis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-controller\">Contrôleur continu de dossier de travail\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Contrôleur continu de dossier de travail\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Créer d’abord Store et workspace natifs puis appeler \u003ccode>NewFolder(ctx, options)\u003c/code>.\n\u003ccode>FolderOptions\u003c/code> lie catalogue, Device ID local, pairs explicites, dossier, Store\net contexte enregistré facultatif. \u003ccode>OpenStore\u003c/code> retourne un nouveau handle que le\ncontrôleur possède et ferme ; une intégration chiffrée obtient sa clé auprès du\nfournisseur de l’application. Aucun Store n’est gardé au repos. Fermer les sessions\navant Folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Refresh\u003c/code> capture les changements avec watcher/index natifs et audits complets\npériodiques (cinq minutes par défaut). \u003ccode>Invalidate\u003c/code> demande un audit immédiat.\n\u003ccode>ReceiveIndex\u003c/code> conserve l’index complet/delta d’un appareil associé authentifié.\n\u003ccode>Step\u003c/code> télécharge un fichier manquant, réutilise les blocs vérifiés des fichiers\nlocaux, puis applique le candidat complet par checkout natif à propriétaire.\nUn renommage exact ne transfère aucun payload. Les octets immuables annoncés\nrestent servables pendant le téléchargement ; un worker séparé reçoit les index.\nGénération et descripteur téléchargé sont recontrôlés avant attachement : une\nréponse périmée ne peut pas être installée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Trois racines typées conservent descripteur, candidat natif et catalogue préparé\navant l’application. La publication protocolaire attend la preuve native de fin\navec propriétaire durable unique, source, cible et branche sélectionnée exacts.\n\u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext\u003c/code> utilise le décodeur de reçus du cœur, sans réimplémenter\nson format privé en Go ni comparer tous les arbres historiques d’assets. Une\négalité de HEAD seule ne suffit pas. Une édition après fin native est capturée\navec la base causale reçue, sans blocage de l’accusé ni écrasement. Une préparation\nobstruée avant tout journal natif est retirée uniquement après vérification de\nl’état natif original exact. Les journaux étrangers ou ambigus restent explicites.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les parents des révisions du catalogue conservent les versions complètes ; les\ncandidats en attente sont aussi des racines de GC. Les tests terminent brutalement\nun vrai processus avant/après publication native, exécutent le GC, rouvrent,\nreprennent et vérifient les données reçues ou leur édition locale ultérieure,\navec Stores chiffrés et non chiffrés. Les versions protocolaires concurrentes\nbloquent par défaut ; la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">politique explicite de copies\u003c/a>\nconserve les deux contenus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-conflicts\">Versions protocolaires concurrentes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Versions protocolaires concurrentes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Activer \u003ccode>FolderOptions.ConflictCopies: true\u003c/code>, ou \u003ccode>&quot;conflict_copies&quot;: true\u003c/code> dans\nla configuration autonome, pour résoudre les vecteurs concurrents de fichiers\nordinaires. Le défaut strict rend toujours \u003ccode>ErrCatalogConflict\u003c/code> ; \u003ccode>Catalog.Adopt\u003c/code>\ngarde ce comportement. Une intégration peut appeler explicitement\n\u003ccode>Catalog.ResolveConcurrent(ctx, store, peer, name)\u003c/code> sur un brouillon, puis le\npublier par la transaction vérifiée habituelle. Le résultat décrit le choix du\nbrouillon, pas un reçu d’application aux fichiers de travail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux contenus vivants sont vérifiés contre leurs blocs BEP annoncés avant\narbitrage. \u003ccode>FileInfo.WinsConflict\u003c/code> de la version amont figée choisit la date de\nmodification la plus récente, puis ordonne les vecteurs concurrents en cas\nd’égalité. Une version capturée localement utilise le timestamp de capture\nnative. Le gagnant conserve ses métadonnées et reçoit l’union des deux vecteurs.\nDes contenus concurrents identiques sont regroupés par hash natif du fichier\ncomplet, indépendamment du découpage ; deux tombstones sont aussi regroupés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le contenu perdant distinct devient une copie voisine suivie avec \u003ccode>.sync-conflict-\u003c/code>,\nune identité déterministe origine/vecteur/contenu et l’extension courte conservée.\nUne suppression perdante n’a rien à copier ; une suppression gagnante garde le\nfichier vivant perdant dans une copie. Les noms suivis et tombstones existants\nsont réservés, avec casse/normalisation Unicode et collisions de préfixes\nfichier/dossier. Des variantes bornées évitent d’écraser un chemin suivi occupé.\nLes autres obstacles natifs de structure ou de fichiers de travail bloquent.\nLes copies respectent la sélection matérialisée ; une copie masquée reste un\nfichier logique retenu. Il n’y a pas de fusion de contenu texte ou binaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Gagnant, copie et causalité sont figés dans la transaction native en attente.\nLa reprise applique ce candidat même après désactivation de l’option. Seul son\nreçu de fin à propriétaire autorise l’acquittement du catalogue. Les deux entrées\nrestent accessibles dans l’historique ; les copies sont des fichiers suivis sans\nretrait automatique. Syncthing peut créer ses propres noms de copie s’il résout\nle conflit en premier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les tests couvrent convergence symétrique de deux contrôleurs sans historique\nrépété, contenus identiques, combinaisons vivant/supprimé, refus de données\ninvalides et collisions Unicode. Arrêts forcés avant/après publication, GC,\nsauvegarde/restauration vérifiées et reprise gardent les deux entrées dans des\nStores clairs/chiffrés. Les processus autonomes conservent aussi le perdant sur\nles deux appareils face au daemon amont non modifié, avec chaque côté gagnant.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-sessions\">Sessions authentifiées continues\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-sessions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sessions authentifiées continues\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenFolderSession(ctx, folder, stream, options)\u003c/code> possède le transport connecté,\nvérifie le Device ID local complet du certificat, authentifie le pair configuré,\npuis négocie générations et index complets/deltas du dossier. \u003ccode>PollInterval\u003c/code> vaut\n250 ms par défaut (50 ms à une minute). La file entrante vaut 32 Mio par défaut\n(1 à 128 Mio), inclut le batch en traitement et ne bloque jamais le lecteur BEP\nsur le travail disque natif. \u003ccode>Reports\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Errors\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Done\u003c/code> exposent progression,\nblocages et fin. \u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> arrête les workers et attend transport/callback de fin.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application fournit écoute/connexion, certificats/clés et cycle de vie OS.\nLe \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-service\">service résident\u003c/a> gère attente de reconnexion\net capture hors ligne indépendante. Ce module est optionnel ; la cible BEP CMake distribue l’exécutable autonome. Le contrôleur applique les fichiers ordinaires et\nleurs tombstones, pas les métadonnées de dossiers/liens symboliques, dossiers\nvides, propriétaires/xattrs ni dossiers receive-encrypted. Les métadonnées\nentrantes sont conservées, sans prétendre que ces éléments ont été appliqués.\nReprise persistante de téléchargement, gestion automatique\ndes membres/retrait d’historique et politique d’auteur/signature des nouveaux\ncandidats natifs entrants restent à réaliser. Performances à grande échelle et\ndurabilité face à une coupure électrique ne sont pas qualifiées. L’agent natif\nrésident de publication est une autre fonctionnalité.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>StagedFile.PrepareWithChunking(ctx, store, syrnck.Chunking{})\u003c/code> conserve les blocs\nFastCDC natifs par défaut indépendamment des frontières SHA-256 BEP. \u003ccode>Prepare\u003c/code>\ngarde ses blocs fixes BEP. \u003ccode>Catalog.CommitWithRefs\u003c/code> publie atomiquement catalogue\net gardes de références typées additionnelles ; recharger après un résultat de\npublication inconnu, selon les règles du CAS natif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une édition externe pendant installation native reste bloquée par la reprise\nstandard. Activer \u003ccode>FolderOptions.PreserveCheckoutEdits = true\u003c/code> pour conserver\nles éditions de fichiers ordinaires selon la politique ci-dessous. La visibilité\ndu fichier seule ne signale pas la fin : utiliser checkpoint/statut. Une édition\naprès le reçu natif committed reste prise en charge indépendamment de cette option.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le contrôleur associe l’orthographe native aux noms NFC du protocole, conserve\nl’orthographe décomposée existante lors des éditions et refuse deux noms natifs\nqui se normalisent vers le même chemin. Écho et nouveau scan ne transforment\npas cette différence d’orthographe en suppression.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Conserver les éditions pendant le checkout actif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conserver les éditions pendant le checkout actif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’option explicite \u003ccode>PreserveCheckoutEdits\u003c/code> est figée dans chaque application\npréparée ; une nouvelle configuration ne peut pas recibler l’opération interrompue.\nLa reprise vérifie propriétaire, cible, contexte, dossier et fichiers privés de\nstaging/sauvegarde exacts. Elle ingère les fichiers ordinaires modifiés dans des\nrévisions d’archive avant tout déplacement. Déplacement exclusif vers une copie\nde conflit et restauration exclusive des octets intermédiaires attendus sont\nrejouables depuis leurs descripteurs retenus. Le checkout strict original\nrevérifie ensuite tous les fichiers cibles et publie son candidat/reçu inchangés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les racines \u003ccode>archive/checkout-edits/TRANSACTION/EDIT/state\u003c/code> (descripteur) et\n\u003ccode>/work\u003c/code> (révision) conservent les versions ; les parents natifs gardent la révision\nentrante et ses fichiers complets. Les racines \u003ccode>archive/checkout-edit-versions/TRANSACTION/REVISION\u003c/code> retiennent les éditions\nsupplémentaires d’une copie pendant la reprise. Chaque nom est déterminé par\nl’identité persistée de l’édition, conserve les extensions courtes et n’est jamais\nécrasé. Un fichier apparu au nom réservé est conservé ; les éditions restantes de\nl’original reçoivent une nouvelle identité. \u003ccode>preserved_edits\u003c/code> rend chemins source,\ncopie et IDs de révisions archivées ; les octets actuels de la copie peuvent être\nplus récents que cette révision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après l’accusé, la capture locale ordinaire annonce les fichiers conservés.\nCela protège les éditions de contenu avant sauvegarde, pendant staging et après\ninstallation, y compris sur un fichier suivi normalement inchangé. La cible signée\npréparée reste identique ; aucune clé privée de signature n’est requise pour la\nreprise. Les archives restent des racines de GC après les changements du workspace.\nLe retrait automatique de ces groupes demande encore une politique administrative ;\nne pas retirer leurs racines indépendamment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un éditeur qui continue à écrire peut différer la fin : chaque tentative bornée\ngarde ses observations pour la suivante. Intentions de suppression/fichiers\nmanquants, obstructions de dossiers/liens et structures ambiguës bloquent encore\nplutôt que d’inventer une résolution. Cette option ne résout pas les vecteurs BEP\nconcurrents, ne fournit pas un service de dossiers complet et ne prouve pas la\ndurabilité face à une coupure électrique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Si le checkout doit retirer ou renommer un dossier parent, la copie est placée\nhors de ce dossier en gardant le nom de base et l’extension courte. Cela couvre\naussi dossier vers fichier et changement de casse d’un dossier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"folder-service\">Service résident de reconnexion\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-service\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Service résident de reconnexion\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>OpenFolderService(ctx, folder, options)\u003c/code> maintient un pair explicitement appairé\nmalgré les coupures. Fournir \u003ccode>FolderServiceOptions.Session\u003c/code> avec certificat,\nDevice ID distant et budgets, puis \u003ccode>Connect(ctx)\u003c/code> retournant un \u003ccode>net.Conn\u003c/code>\nconnecté. Cette fabrique peut connecter ou accepter, doit respecter l’annulation\net transfère tout transport retourné, même accompagné d’une erreur. TLS/BEP\nvérifie ensuite le pair ; la fabrique ne peut pas autoriser un appareil.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le service observe/capture les modifications locales indépendamment du réseau :\néditions hors ligne, suppression/renommage et pair authentifié qui n’envoie\njamais sa configuration de dossier. \u003ccode>OfflineInterval\u003c/code> vaut 250 ms par défaut\n(50 ms à une minute). Il fonctionne aussi pendant les téléchargements : le\ncontrôleur natif sérialise l’état et refuse les candidats périmés sans conserver\nle Store pendant les attentes réseau. Les audits complets restent programmés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RetryMin\u003c/code> vaut 500 ms et \u003ccode>RetryMax\u003c/code> 30 secondes par défaut. L’attente exponentielle\nreste entre les bornes configurées (50 ms à une minute) ; une connexion\nauthentifiée la réinitialise. Chaque reconnexion utilise un nouveau timestamp\nHello par défaut et reprend l’état natif/catalogue durable. Aucune découverte\nni appairage implicite ne sont ajoutés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Events\u003c/code> expose connecting, connected et retrying ; connected signifie certificat\nauthentifié, pas application du dossier terminée. \u003ccode>Reports\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>Errors\u003c/code> rendent\nprogression et échecs. Ces trois flux bornés sont consultatifs et peuvent perdre\ndes messages si le consommateur ralentit ; relire l’état durable pour les reçus.\n\u003ccode>Close\u003c/code> annule et attend connexion, TLS et sessions, y compris un transport\nterminant après annulation, puis ferme flux et \u003ccode>Done\u003c/code>. Arrêter le service avant\nson Folder, qui appartient à l’appelant. Aucun Store n’est gardé au repos. Le\nfournisseur du Folder contrôle la durée de vie des clés de stockage ; garder la\nclé de signature d’appareil immuable jusqu’à l’arrêt. Close rend les erreurs de\nfermeture des sessions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les tests couvrent deux workspaces clairs/chiffrés : capture hors ligne,\nreconnexion TCP, renommage/tombstone, fermeture des transports échoués,\nannulation TLS bloquée et capture locale sans configuration distante. Les tests\nde session qualifient séparément le daemon amont figé. L’application fournit\nencore écoute/connexion réseau, certificats, stockage des clés et cycle de vie OS.\nInstallation de service OS et métadonnées complètes des dossiers/liens\nrestent à terminer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"standalone-service\">Exécutable autonome au premier plan\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/standalone-service\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Exécutable autonome au premier plan\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’exécutable optionnel \u003ccode>syrnck-bep\u003c/code> maintient le service résident pour un workspace\nexistant et un pair explicitement associé. Il capture hors ligne, authentifie le\npair, se reconnecte et applique les versions ordinaires contrôlées. Aucun processus\nGit ni installation Go ne sont nécessaires à l’exécution. Le moteur C++ conserve\nstockage et révisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck-bep run --config service.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Fournir un certificat et une clé privée d’appareil distincts, puis autoriser le\nDevice ID complet affiché chez le pair. Une identité créée par \u003ccode>syncthing generate\u003c/code>\nconvient ; conserver ensemble certificat et clé correspondante. Une connexion à\nsoi-même est refusée. Le CLI importe les identités ; génération, rotation et\ncoffre système restent des travaux distincts. L’ID de dossier doit être commun.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Copier \u003ccode>share/Syrnck/examples/bep-service.json\u003c/code> installé dans le dossier de\nconfiguration puis adapter chemins, pair et adresse. L’exemple existe aussi dans\n\u003ccode>examples\u003c/code> des sources. Initialiser d’abord le Store avec le CLI \u003ccode>syrnck\u003c/code> principal.\nLes chemins relatifs partent du fichier de configuration. Le Store vaut\n\u003ccode>directory/.syrnck\u003c/code> par défaut ; \u003ccode>workspace\u003c/code> choisit un contexte lié existant.\n\u003ccode>mode: dial\u003c/code> se connecte à \u003ccode>address\u003c/code> ; \u003ccode>listen\u003c/code> y accepte une connexion. L’adresse\n\u003ccode>hôte:port\u003c/code> est explicite. Chaque processus possède transport et Folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La clé PEM privée doit rester hors du dossier de travail ou dans son Store\nprotégé, après résolution des liens. Elle ne doit pas devenir du contenu source.\n\u003ccode>key_env\u003c/code> nomme la variable de clé de stockage (\u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> par défaut) : absente,\naucune clé n’est fournie ; sinon, 64 caractères hexadécimaux doivent correspondre\nau Store existant. Aucun secret brut dans JSON ou arguments. Identité TLS et clé\nde chiffrement au repos ont des rôles différents. Le fichier PEM reste protégé\npar les droits du système de fichiers ; le chiffrement concerne les objets du Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La configuration version 1 refuse doublons/champs inconnus et plus de 64 Kio.\n\u003ccode>poll_ms\u003c/code> vaut 50..60000 (zéro choisit 250) ; les délais de reconnexion valent\n500/30000 ms par défaut. \u003ccode>use_permissions\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>preserve_checkout_edits\u003c/code> valent\nfalse ; l’exemple active la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">préservation des éditions\u003c/a>.\n\u003ccode>conflict_copies\u003c/code> vaut également false par défaut ; l’exemple active les\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts\">copies pour vecteurs concurrents\u003c/a>. Ces deux\npolitiques protègent des situations distinctes et se configurent séparément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ctrl+C ou SIGTERM arrête le processus. Redémarrer avec la même identité et la\nmême configuration reprend état natif/catalogue. La sortie JSON reste canonique ;\n\u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> indique initialisation locale/écoute, pas synchronisation terminée.\nAide et diagnostics propres au CLI sont anglais/français ; \u003ccode>--lang\u003c/code> prime sur\n\u003ccode>SYRNCK_LANG\u003c/code>, puis anglais. Les détails système/bibliothèque gardent leur langue.\nLe CLI principal ouvre cette page par \u003ccode>syrnck --lang fr help syncthing --section standalone-service\u003c/code>.\nInstallation de service OS, orchestration de plusieurs pairs et sémantique\ncomplète des dossiers/liens restent à terminer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-standalone-service\">Construire et distribuer l’exécutable\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/syncthing/build-standalone-service\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Construire et distribuer l’exécutable\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Activer \u003ccode>-DSYRNCK_BUILD_BEP_SERVICE=ON\u003c/code> dans CMake (par défaut avec les tests\nSyncthing). Construire d’abord \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code>. Avec Go 1.26.2+, préparer une fois les\ndépendances publiques figées :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library BIBLIOTHEQUE_NATIVE --output EXECUTABLE_BEP\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le preset Windows utilise \u003ccode>build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>build/Release/syrnck-bep.exe\u003c/code> ; Linux : \u003ccode>build/libsyrnck_ffi.so\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>build/syrnck-bep\u003c/code> ; macOS : \u003ccode>build/libsyrnck_ffi.dylib\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>build/syrnck-bep\u003c/code>.\nConstruire ensuite normalement : CMake appelle le même outil hors ligne. Unix\nnécessite cgo et la bibliothèque native correspondante. Utiliser un build sans\nsanitizer pour cet exécutable, séparé des contrôles ASan du cœur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>cmake --install build --config Release --prefix dist\u003c/code> installe exécutable,\nbibliothèque correspondante et manuel local. Les archives sources inchangées\ndes modules Go réellement utilisés, licences/notices, licence du runtime Go et\nmanifest d’empreintes sont conservés dans \u003ccode>share/licenses/Syrnck/bep\u003c/code>. Les garder\navec la distribution ; MIT du projet ne remplace pas les licences tierces.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les tests Windows utilisent de vrais processus autonomes et le daemon amont figé,\navec Stores clairs/chiffrés, éditions dans les deux sens et renommage distant hors\nligne après redémarrage. Des arrêts brutaux distincts vérifient la reconstruction\ndes anciennes/nouvelles versions. Exécution et déplacement de distribution\nLinux/macOS dépendent encore de leur CI ; la configuration ne les prouve pas.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Adaptateur Syncthing à blocs fixes # Ce module Go optionnel relie le C++ au modèle de blocs BEP via la bibliothèque Syncthing non modifiée v2.1.7 , commit d6d0181f7d46 , pseudo-version figée dans go.mod/go.sum. Le binding cœur reste séparé, sans dépendances Go externes. Le module demande Go 1.26.2+. Le module gère données de fichiers ordinaires, connexions à appareils associés, catalogue causal durable et contrôleur continu de workspace natif. Folder capture les changements locaux, applique les versions entrantes vérifiées et conserve l’état de reprise ; FolderSession le fait fonctionner sur BEP authentifié. Découverte, interface d’appairage, installation du service OS et sémantique complète des dossiers Syncthing restent à terminer, selon les limites précisées ci-dessous. Bibliothèque native et dépendances # Compiler syrnck_ffi et télécharger une fois les dépendances Go figées avec go mod download depuis bindings/syncthing . L’adaptateur est optionnel, séparé du binding cœur. Linux/macOS utilisent cgo et leur chemin de chargeur. Catalogue et transport précédents ont passé la CI Linux ; ce nouveau contrôleur est vérifié localement sous Windows, son exécution multiplateforme reste à qualifier. Ne distribuer que les dépendances et notices requises pour l’intégration choisie. API # NewFile(ctx,store,manifest,folder,name,blockSize) crée une vue immuable servable. Il vérifie le hash complet sans exporter puis lit des plages vérifiées pour construire les protocol.FileInfo SHA-256. CDC/blocs fixes et IDs BLAKE3 du moteur restent intacts. Taille 0 : politique Syncthing ; sinon puissance de 2 de 128 Kio à 16 Mio. Index limité 65536 blocs. Fichier vide : bloc SHA-256 vide unique de référence. Info() copie l’index indépendamment ; l’application y pose vecteurs de version, séquences et timestamps avant annonce. Noms relatifs UTF-8 valides : noms natifs Darwin NFD normalisés en NFC et séparateurs Windows convertis en / protocolaire. Index, requêtes de blocs et identité de reprise utilisent le même nom canonique. ReadRequest(ctx,req) sert exactement un bloc annoncé pour ce folder/fichier. Un SHA-256 fourni doit correspondre ; hash optionnel et ancien numéro de bloc 0/défaut sont acceptés. Une requête temporaire peut revenir à cette source immuable complète. Autres chemins/plages/générations refusés. Le payload est vérifié par le moteur puis par SHA-256 avant retour. Fetch(ctx,destination,folder,info,request,options) reconstruit un fichier annoncé. Fournir protocol.Connection.Request ou un autre demandeur authentifié. Chaque réponse doit avoir taille/hash exacts. ReuseManifest indexe une version locale à la taille reçue pour réutiliser blocs identiques, déplacés ou répétés ; seuls les absents sont demandés. FetchResult.Stats sépare octets demandés, réemploi local et staging vérifié. Après assemblage/vérification privée, Fetch ingère le fichier complet via ABI C avec blocs fixes BEP et recontrôle l’index stocké contre les SHA-256 reçus. ffi/ID retient le manifeste. L’application peut restaurer exclusivement ou l’inclure dans sa propre transaction ; Fetch ne remplace ni fichier de travail ni branche mutable. syrnck_file_verify / Store.VerifyFile vérifient fichier complet, blocs, tailles et file_hash, sans destination ni référence, en buffers natifs bornés. Une bibliothèque reconstruite avec ce symbole est nécessaire. go Copier view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0) // Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model. result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo, connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{ ReuseManifest: previousManifest, ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory, }) // Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision. Catalogue persistant de dossier # OpenCatalog(ctx, store, CatalogOptions) charge le catalogue persistant ou crée un brouillon vide non publié. Associer le dossier à l’ID complet de l’appareil local et à la liste exacte des pairs de confiance. Doublons, appareil local, sentinelles et collisions des identités 64 bits des vecteurs sont refusés. Changer la liste d’options ne modifie pas implicitement les autorisations. Une procédure distincte de gestion des membres reste à réaliser. Catalog ne garde ni Store ni connexion. LocalIndex et PeerIndex rendent des copies indépendantes. RecordLocal ou RecordLocalChanges attribue les séquences locales et avance le vecteur local antérieur avec contrôle de débordement ; les champs causaux fournis doivent être vides. Un lot est validé entièrement avec un seul tri des noms. Les suppressions doivent être des tombstones explicites. Dossiers et liens symboliques sont conservés comme métadonnées ; aucune création de dossier ni résolution de cible de lien n’est faite par cette API. ReceiveIndex(CatalogIndex) conserve un Index complet ou un delta d’un pair. L’appelant doit lier Device à la connexion authentifiée, autoriser le dossier et prendre IndexID dans son ClusterConfig négocié. Une nouvelle génération exige un index complet ; les lots suivants continuent la séquence persistée. Noms et séquences uniques, vecteurs triés et bornés : compteurs dupliqués, flags invalides, entrées chiffrées non prises en charge et blocs mal formés sont refusés. Un vecteur identique ne peut décrire d’autres métadonnées de contenu. Recevoir une annonce ne la transforme pas en fichier local et ne lance aucun téléchargement. Attach(ctx, store, device, name, manifest) vérifie les données contre les blocs de l’entrée actuellement enregistrée. Adopt(device, name) promeut une entrée vérifiée causalement plus récente dans l’index local annoncé. Une concurrence rend ErrCatalogConflict ; versions antérieures et contenu incohérent à vecteur identique sont refusés. Les deux contenus restent conservés sans gagnant implicite. Seul le catalogue annoncé change : l’application au workspace doit avoir sa transaction de reprise avant qu’un service annonce un dossier à jour. Commit(ctx, store) vérifie les données retenues puis publie état et données sous une racine unique ffi/bep/… de révision native protégée par valeur attendue. Les catalogues précédents sont parents natifs : reset d’index, renommage et suppression conservent donc les versions complètes au GC et en sauvegarde. Les chemins de payload sont les hashes des identités appareil/nom : noms .git/config , distincts par la casse et incompatibles avec un workspace portable restent stockables sans perte. Cette API ne les matérialise pas. Aucune racine implicite ffi/manifestID n’est ajoutée. Un brouillon périmé rend ErrCatalogStale : recharger et replanifier sans recibler l’opération ancienne. Une erreur d’IO de publication laisse un résultat inconnu ; recharger et établir le résultat persistant avant toute reprise. Un commit inchangé ne fait rien. Le nouvel IndexID local devient persistant au premier commit réussi. Le codec refuse champs dupliqués/inconnus et état non canonique. Limites : 256 pairs, 256 compteurs/vecteur, 65536 entrées au total et 16 Mio de métadonnées. Chaque commit vérifie actuellement fichiers retenus et fermeture native ; débit sur grand projet non établi. Retrait de l’historique et partitionnement du catalogue restent à réaliser. Télécharger sans garder le Store ouvert # DownloadFile(ctx, folder, info, request, DownloadOptions) vérifie les blocs réseau dans un staging privé sans ouvrir le Store natif. ReuseIndex et ReadReuse réutilisent les blocs locaux identiques ; le callback peut ouvrir et fermer brièvement un Store. Des tailles de blocs différentes désactivent le réemploi. La reprise utilise les mêmes descripteurs et contrôles que Fetch. Le StagedFile retourné ne garde ni fichier ouvert ni Store. Toujours appeler Close() . Info() copie indépendamment ; Stats() décrit transfert, réemploi et staging. Appeler Prepare(ctx, store) en possédant brièvement le Store : import sans racine implicite et recontrôle contre les blocs annoncés. Attacher le manifeste à la version courante exacte du pair, l’adopter si nécessaire et faire Commit avant de fermer le Store ou d’autoriser le GC. L’attente réseau ne garde ainsi pas le verrou exclusif natif. Revérifier l’entrée persistée après transfert, car elle peut avoir avancé pendant le téléchargement. PrepareFetch simplifie l’appel si un Store est déjà ouvert, avec la même règle de rétention explicite. Fetch garde sa rétention indépendante historique. Ne pas supprimer une racine partagée historique appartenant à un autre appelant. Authentifier une connexion d’appareil # OpenDeviceConnection(ctx,stream,DeviceOptions,model) prend possession d’un net.Conn connecté et non chiffré, y compris en cas d’échec. Fournir un certificat TLS local unique, sa clé privée crypto.Signer correspondante et le protocol.DeviceID du pair explicitement associé. Server: true sélectionne le côté acceptant. Auto-connexion, identités vides/réservées, clés malformées et discordance certificat/clé sont refusées. Ne modifier ni certificat ni clé en cours d’utilisation. TLS 1.3 ou ultérieur et ALPN bep/1.0 sont exigés. Le Hello amont borné est échangé avant de rejeter un pair non associé, puis l’identité SHA-256 de son unique certificat est comparée exactement. C’est un épinglage de certificat : DNS, CA Web PKI, expiration, découverte et nom Hello n’accordent pas l’accès. PeerHello contient des descriptions non fiables. Aucun modèle applicatif n’est attaché avant la vérification réussie. go Copier session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{ Certificate: deviceCertificate, RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID, Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"}, }, folderModel) // Traiter err avant utilisation. Démarrage explicite, puis ClusterConfig comme // premier message après authentification. Le modèle autorise chaque dossier. session.Start() session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil) // Envoyer un Index complet avant les mises à jour, sauf accord persistant // d’index delta. Fetch peut utiliser session.Request pour les blocs vérifiés. HandshakeTimeout vaut 20 secondes par défaut et accepte de 100 ms à une minute. Il couvre TLS et Hello ; une échéance du contexte plus proche l’emporte. La compression des métadonnées amont est le défaut ; Never/Always sont acceptés. Le contexte contrôle toute la session, même authentifiée mais pas encore démarrée. Close(reason) libère le flux ; attendre le callback Closed du modèle avant de fermer les handles natifs qu’utilisent ses callbacks. Les métadonnées de connexion gardent une date d’établissement et un ID de session stables. L’application fournit stockage/rotation des certificats, Device ID de confiance, planification, transactions du catalogue, reprise du workspace et résolution des conflits. Le chiffrement du transport protège et authentifie ce pair ; un daemon recevant des données ordinaires ne devient pas une sauvegarde chiffrée non fiable. Le parcours complet de chiffrement vers un appareil non fiable reste à intégrer. Vérifier avec le daemon de référence # text Copier python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download Ce helper de test facultatif compile cmd/syncthing à la version exacte du module de l’adaptateur avec les manifests figés tests/syncthing-reference/go.mod et go.sum . Le tag amont noassets omet les ressources d’interface web et conserve daemon réel, base de données, scanner, modèle, puller et API REST. La sortie est build/qualification/syncthing ( .exe sous Windows). Sans --download , le cache de modules doit être prêt : aucun téléchargement implicite. La version amont embarquée est contrôlée ; version différente ou manifests modifiés sont refusés. Définir SYRNCK_SYNCTHING_BINARY avec son chemin absolu puis exécuter : text Copier python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers Sous Linux/macOS, fournir le chemin de la bibliothèque partagée construite. La CI des trois plateformes active explicitement l’adaptateur et ce test. Le daemon utilise configuration, clés et dossier neufs propres au test, écoute uniquement en loopback, désactive découverte/relais/NAT/mises à jour/rapports et est terminé après le test. Aucun profil Syncthing installé n’est lu ou modifié. La vérification locale Windows a exercé les deux sens contre le daemon amont non modifié : un asset de 384 Kio à blocs déplacés/réutilisés transfère uniquement le bloc absent de 128 Kio dans chaque sens. La reconstruction dans un stockage Syrnck chiffré vérifie le fichier complet. Les annonces réelles de renommage et tombstone ont aussi été reçues. Cela prouve transport et interopérabilité des fichiers ordinaires. Un autre test réel conserve index complets/deltas et données, ferme les Stores pendant l’attente réseau puis se reconnecte après un renommage hors ligne avec tombstone persistant. Le service autonome est disponible pour les fichiers ordinaires. Sans binaire explicite, ce test annonce qu’il est ignoré ; les tests ordinaires de l’adaptateur continuent de s’exécuter. Reprise et annulation # Sans ResumeDirectory , Fetch utilise un fichier temporaire privé retiré au retour. Avec cette option, le dossier par contenu appartient à l’appelant : state.json , data . Le descripteur lie folder/nom/taille/taille de bloc et hash de liste complète. Autre index, fichier inconnu, descripteur incomplet ou lien est refusé. Utiliser un dossier privé et sérialiser ses gestionnaires. Chaque plage de staging est rehashée au retry. Seules les plages complètes conformes sont réutilisées ; partielles/corrompues redemandées. Les trous remplis de zéro peuvent satisfaire un bloc zéro sans requête. Flush tous les 16 blocs et avant ingestion. Annulation préserve le staging fourni par l’appelant, qui reste en clair même avec Store chiffré ; protéger l’emplacement. Le retrait des dossiers terminés est explicite. Le contexte est vérifié entre appels natifs/étapes réseau/blocs. La préparation sans racine implicite propage l’annulation dans l’ingestion native ; l’ingestion retenue historique et la vérification complète terminent leur appel natif synchrone courant. Une annulation tardive ou changement détecté peut laisser un manifeste complet retenu, dont l’ID accompagne l’erreur. Traiter l’erreur avant publication. Aucun fichier de travail partiel installé. Un kill peut laisser du privé ; durabilité après coupure et retrait automatique ne sont pas établis. Contrôleur continu de dossier de travail # Créer d’abord Store et workspace natifs puis appeler NewFolder(ctx, options) . FolderOptions lie catalogue, Device ID local, pairs explicites, dossier, Store et contexte enregistré facultatif. OpenStore retourne un nouveau handle que le contrôleur possède et ferme ; une intégration chiffrée obtient sa clé auprès du fournisseur de l’application. Aucun Store n’est gardé au repos. Fermer les sessions avant Folder. Refresh capture les changements avec watcher/index natifs et audits complets périodiques (cinq minutes par défaut). Invalidate demande un audit immédiat. ReceiveIndex conserve l’index complet/delta d’un appareil associé authentifié. Step télécharge un fichier manquant, réutilise les blocs vérifiés des fichiers locaux, puis applique le candidat complet par checkout natif à propriétaire. Un renommage exact ne transfère aucun payload. Les octets immuables annoncés restent servables pendant le téléchargement ; un worker séparé reçoit les index. Génération et descripteur téléchargé sont recontrôlés avant attachement : une réponse périmée ne peut pas être installée. Trois racines typées conservent descripteur, candidat natif et catalogue préparé avant l’application. La publication protocolaire attend la preuve native de fin avec propriétaire durable unique, source, cible et branche sélectionnée exacts. CheckoutStatusContext utilise le décodeur de reçus du cœur, sans réimplémenter son format privé en Go ni comparer tous les arbres historiques d’assets. Une égalité de HEAD seule ne suffit pas. Une édition après fin native est capturée avec la base causale reçue, sans blocage de l’accusé ni écrasement. Une préparation obstruée avant tout journal natif est retirée uniquement après vérification de l’état natif original exact. Les journaux étrangers ou ambigus restent explicites. Les parents des révisions du catalogue conservent les versions complètes ; les candidats en attente sont aussi des racines de GC. Les tests terminent brutalement un vrai processus avant/après publication native, exécutent le GC, rouvrent, reprennent et vérifient les données reçues ou leur édition locale ultérieure, avec Stores chiffrés et non chiffrés. Les versions protocolaires concurrentes bloquent par défaut ; la politique explicite de copies conserve les deux contenus. Versions protocolaires concurrentes # Activer FolderOptions.ConflictCopies: true , ou \"conflict_copies\": true dans la configuration autonome, pour résoudre les vecteurs concurrents de fichiers ordinaires. Le défaut strict rend toujours ErrCatalogConflict ; Catalog.Adopt garde ce comportement. Une intégration peut appeler explicitement Catalog.ResolveConcurrent(ctx, store, peer, name) sur un brouillon, puis le publier par la transaction vérifiée habituelle. Le résultat décrit le choix du brouillon, pas un reçu d’application aux fichiers de travail. Les deux contenus vivants sont vérifiés contre leurs blocs BEP annoncés avant arbitrage. FileInfo.WinsConflict de la version amont figée choisit la date de modification la plus récente, puis ordonne les vecteurs concurrents en cas d’égalité. Une version capturée localement utilise le timestamp de capture native. Le gagnant conserve ses métadonnées et reçoit l’union des deux vecteurs. Des contenus concurrents identiques sont regroupés par hash natif du fichier complet, indépendamment du découpage ; deux tombstones sont aussi regroupés. Le contenu perdant distinct devient une copie voisine suivie avec .sync-conflict- , une identité déterministe origine/vecteur/contenu et l’extension courte conservée. Une suppression perdante n’a rien à copier ; une suppression gagnante garde le fichier vivant perdant dans une copie. Les noms suivis et tombstones existants sont réservés, avec casse/normalisation Unicode et collisions de préfixes fichier/dossier. Des variantes bornées évitent d’écraser un chemin suivi occupé. Les autres obstacles natifs de structure ou de fichiers de travail bloquent. Les copies respectent la sélection matérialisée ; une copie masquée reste un fichier logique retenu. Il n’y a pas de fusion de contenu texte ou binaire. Gagnant, copie et causalité sont figés dans la transaction native en attente. La reprise applique ce candidat même après désactivation de l’option. Seul son reçu de fin à propriétaire autorise l’acquittement du catalogue. Les deux entrées restent accessibles dans l’historique ; les copies sont des fichiers suivis sans retrait automatique. Syncthing peut créer ses propres noms de copie s’il résout le conflit en premier. Les tests couvrent convergence symétrique de deux contrôleurs sans historique répété, contenus identiques, combinaisons vivant/supprimé, refus de données invalides et collisions Unicode. Arrêts forcés avant/après publication, GC, sauvegarde/restauration vérifiées et reprise gardent les deux entrées dans des Stores clairs/chiffrés. Les processus autonomes conservent aussi le perdant sur les deux appareils face au daemon amont non modifié, avec chaque côté gagnant. Sessions authentifiées continues # OpenFolderSession(ctx, folder, stream, options) possède le transport connecté, vérifie le Device ID local complet du certificat, authentifie le pair configuré, puis négocie générations et index complets/deltas du dossier. PollInterval vaut 250 ms par défaut (50 ms à une minute). La file entrante vaut 32 Mio par défaut (1 à 128 Mio), inclut le batch en traitement et ne bloque jamais le lecteur BEP sur le travail disque natif. Reports , Errors et Done exposent progression, blocages et fin. Close arrête les workers et attend transport/callback de fin. L’application fournit écoute/connexion, certificats/clés et cycle de vie OS. Le service résident gère attente de reconnexion et capture hors ligne indépendante. Ce module est optionnel ; la cible BEP CMake distribue l’exécutable autonome. Le contrôleur applique les fichiers ordinaires et leurs tombstones, pas les métadonnées de dossiers/liens symboliques, dossiers vides, propriétaires/xattrs ni dossiers receive-encrypted. Les métadonnées entrantes sont conservées, sans prétendre que ces éléments ont été appliqués. Reprise persistante de téléchargement, gestion automatique des membres/retrait d’historique et politique d’auteur/signature des nouveaux candidats natifs entrants restent à réaliser. Performances à grande échelle et durabilité face à une coupure électrique ne sont pas qualifiées. L’agent natif résident de publication est une autre fonctionnalité. StagedFile.PrepareWithChunking(ctx, store, syrnck.Chunking{}) conserve les blocs FastCDC natifs par défaut indépendamment des frontières SHA-256 BEP. Prepare garde ses blocs fixes BEP. Catalog.CommitWithRefs publie atomiquement catalogue et gardes de références typées additionnelles ; recharger après un résultat de publication inconnu, selon les règles du CAS natif. Une édition externe pendant installation native reste bloquée par la reprise standard. Activer FolderOptions.PreserveCheckoutEdits = true pour conserver les éditions de fichiers ordinaires selon la politique ci-dessous. La visibilité du fichier seule ne signale pas la fin : utiliser checkpoint/statut. Une édition après le reçu natif committed reste prise en charge indépendamment de cette option. Le contrôleur associe l’orthographe native aux noms NFC du protocole, conserve l’orthographe décomposée existante lors des éditions et refuse deux noms natifs qui se normalisent vers le même chemin. Écho et nouveau scan ne transforment pas cette différence d’orthographe en suppression. Conserver les éditions pendant le checkout actif # L’option explicite PreserveCheckoutEdits est figée dans chaque application préparée ; une nouvelle configuration ne peut pas recibler l’opération interrompue. La reprise vérifie propriétaire, cible, contexte, dossier et fichiers privés de staging/sauvegarde exacts. Elle ingère les fichiers ordinaires modifiés dans des révisions d’archive avant tout déplacement. Déplacement exclusif vers une copie de conflit et restauration exclusive des octets intermédiaires attendus sont rejouables depuis leurs descripteurs retenus. Le checkout strict original revérifie ensuite tous les fichiers cibles et publie son candidat/reçu inchangés. Les racines archive/checkout-edits/TRANSACTION/EDIT/state (descripteur) et /work (révision) conservent les versions ; les parents natifs gardent la révision entrante et ses fichiers complets. Les racines archive/checkout-edit-versions/TRANSACTION/REVISION retiennent les éditions supplémentaires d’une copie pendant la reprise. Chaque nom est déterminé par l’identité persistée de l’édition, conserve les extensions courtes et n’est jamais écrasé. Un fichier apparu au nom réservé est conservé ; les éditions restantes de l’original reçoivent une nouvelle identité. preserved_edits rend chemins source, copie et IDs de révisions archivées ; les octets actuels de la copie peuvent être plus récents que cette révision. Après l’accusé, la capture locale ordinaire annonce les fichiers conservés. Cela protège les éditions de contenu avant sauvegarde, pendant staging et après installation, y compris sur un fichier suivi normalement inchangé. La cible signée préparée reste identique ; aucune clé privée de signature n’est requise pour la reprise. Les archives restent des racines de GC après les changements du workspace. Le retrait automatique de ces groupes demande encore une politique administrative ; ne pas retirer leurs racines indépendamment. Un éditeur qui continue à écrire peut différer la fin : chaque tentative bornée garde ses observations pour la suivante. Intentions de suppression/fichiers manquants, obstructions de dossiers/liens et structures ambiguës bloquent encore plutôt que d’inventer une résolution. Cette option ne résout pas les vecteurs BEP concurrents, ne fournit pas un service de dossiers complet et ne prouve pas la durabilité face à une coupure électrique. Si le checkout doit retirer ou renommer un dossier parent, la copie est placée hors de ce dossier en gardant le nom de base et l’extension courte. Cela couvre aussi dossier vers fichier et changement de casse d’un dossier. Service résident de reconnexion # OpenFolderService(ctx, folder, options) maintient un pair explicitement appairé malgré les coupures. Fournir FolderServiceOptions.Session avec certificat, Device ID distant et budgets, puis Connect(ctx) retournant un net.Conn connecté. Cette fabrique peut connecter ou accepter, doit respecter l’annulation et transfère tout transport retourné, même accompagné d’une erreur. TLS/BEP vérifie ensuite le pair ; la fabrique ne peut pas autoriser un appareil. Le service observe/capture les modifications locales indépendamment du réseau : éditions hors ligne, suppression/renommage et pair authentifié qui n’envoie jamais sa configuration de dossier. OfflineInterval vaut 250 ms par défaut (50 ms à une minute). Il fonctionne aussi pendant les téléchargements : le contrôleur natif sérialise l’état et refuse les candidats périmés sans conserver le Store pendant les attentes réseau. Les audits complets restent programmés. RetryMin vaut 500 ms et RetryMax 30 secondes par défaut. L’attente exponentielle reste entre les bornes configurées (50 ms à une minute) ; une connexion authentifiée la réinitialise. Chaque reconnexion utilise un nouveau timestamp Hello par défaut et reprend l’état natif/catalogue durable. Aucune découverte ni appairage implicite ne sont ajoutés. Events expose connecting, connected et retrying ; connected signifie certificat authentifié, pas application du dossier terminée. Reports et Errors rendent progression et échecs. Ces trois flux bornés sont consultatifs et peuvent perdre des messages si le consommateur ralentit ; relire l’état durable pour les reçus. Close annule et attend connexion, TLS et sessions, y compris un transport terminant après annulation, puis ferme flux et Done . Arrêter le service avant son Folder, qui appartient à l’appelant. Aucun Store n’est gardé au repos. Le fournisseur du Folder contrôle la durée de vie des clés de stockage ; garder la clé de signature d’appareil immuable jusqu’à l’arrêt. Close rend les erreurs de fermeture des sessions. Les tests couvrent deux workspaces clairs/chiffrés : capture hors ligne, reconnexion TCP, renommage/tombstone, fermeture des transports échoués, annulation TLS bloquée et capture locale sans configuration distante. Les tests de session qualifient séparément le daemon amont figé. L’application fournit encore écoute/connexion réseau, certificats, stockage des clés et cycle de vie OS. Installation de service OS et métadonnées complètes des dossiers/liens restent à terminer. Exécutable autonome au premier plan # L’exécutable optionnel syrnck-bep maintient le service résident pour un workspace existant et un pair explicitement associé. Il capture hors ligne, authentifie le pair, se reconnecte et applique les versions ordinaires contrôlées. Aucun processus Git ni installation Go ne sont nécessaires à l’exécution. Le moteur C++ conserve stockage et révisions. text Copier syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem syrnck-bep run --config service.json syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json Fournir un certificat et une clé privée d’appareil distincts, puis autoriser le Device ID complet affiché chez le pair. Une identité créée par syncthing generate convient ; conserver ensemble certificat et clé correspondante. Une connexion à soi-même est refusée. Le CLI importe les identités ; génération, rotation et coffre système restent des travaux distincts. L’ID de dossier doit être commun. Copier share/Syrnck/examples/bep-service.json installé dans le dossier de configuration puis adapter chemins, pair et adresse. L’exemple existe aussi dans examples des sources. Initialiser d’abord le Store avec le CLI syrnck principal. Les chemins relatifs partent du fichier de configuration. Le Store vaut directory/.syrnck par défaut ; workspace choisit un contexte lié existant. mode: dial se connecte à address ; listen y accepte une connexion. L’adresse hôte:port est explicite. Chaque processus possède transport et Folder. La clé PEM privée doit rester hors du dossier de travail ou dans son Store protégé, après résolution des liens. Elle ne doit pas devenir du contenu source. key_env nomme la variable de clé de stockage ( SYRNCK_KEY par défaut) : absente, aucune clé n’est fournie ; sinon, 64 caractères hexadécimaux doivent correspondre au Store existant. Aucun secret brut dans JSON ou arguments. Identité TLS et clé de chiffrement au repos ont des rôles différents. Le fichier PEM reste protégé par les droits du système de fichiers ; le chiffrement concerne les objets du Store. La configuration version 1 refuse doublons/champs inconnus et plus de 64 Kio. poll_ms vaut 50..60000 (zéro choisit 250) ; les délais de reconnexion valent 500/30000 ms par défaut. use_permissions et preserve_checkout_edits valent false ; l’exemple active la préservation des éditions . conflict_copies vaut également false par défaut ; l’exemple active les copies pour vecteurs concurrents . Ces deux politiques protègent des situations distinctes et se configurent séparément. Ctrl+C ou SIGTERM arrête le processus. Redémarrer avec la même identité et la même configuration reprend état natif/catalogue. La sortie JSON reste canonique ; ready indique initialisation locale/écoute, pas synchronisation terminée. Aide et diagnostics propres au CLI sont anglais/français ; --lang prime sur SYRNCK_LANG , puis anglais. Les détails système/bibliothèque gardent leur langue. Le CLI principal ouvre cette page par syrnck --lang fr help syncthing --section standalone-service . Installation de service OS, orchestration de plusieurs pairs et sémantique complète des dossiers/liens restent à terminer. Construire et distribuer l’exécutable # Activer -DSYRNCK_BUILD_BEP_SERVICE=ON dans CMake (par défaut avec les tests Syncthing). Construire d’abord syrnck_ffi . Avec Go 1.26.2+, préparer une fois les dépendances publiques figées : text Copier python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library BIBLIOTHEQUE_NATIVE --output EXECUTABLE_BEP Le preset Windows utilise build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll et build/Release/syrnck-bep.exe ; Linux : build/libsyrnck_ffi.so et build/syrnck-bep ; macOS : build/libsyrnck_ffi.dylib et build/syrnck-bep . Construire ensuite normalement : CMake appelle le même outil hors ligne. Unix nécessite cgo et la bibliothèque native correspondante. Utiliser un build sans sanitizer pour cet exécutable, séparé des contrôles ASan du cœur. cmake --install build --config Release --prefix dist installe exécutable, bibliothèque correspondante et manuel local. Les archives sources inchangées des modules Go réellement utilisés, licences/notices, licence du runtime Go et manifest d’empreintes sont conservés dans share/licenses/Syrnck/bep . Les garder avec la distribution ; MIT du projet ne remplace pas les licences tierces. Les tests Windows utilisent de vrais processus autonomes et le daemon amont figé, avec Stores clairs/chiffrés, éditions dans les deux sens et renommage distant hors ligne après redémarrage. Des arrêts brutaux distincts vérifient la reconstruction des anciennes/nouvelles versions. Exécution et déplacement de distribution Linux/macOS dépendent encore de leur CI ; la configuration ne les prouve pas. view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0) // Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model. result, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo, connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{ ReuseManifest: previousManifest, ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory, }) // Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision. session, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{ Certificate: deviceCertificate, RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID, Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"}, }, folderModel) // Traiter err avant utilisation. Démarrage explicite, puis ClusterConfig comme // premier message après authentification. Le modèle autorise chaque dossier. session.Start() session.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil) // Envoyer un Index complet avant les mises à jour, sauf accord persistant // d’index delta. Fetch peut utiliser session.Request pour les blocs vérifiés. python scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download python scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers syrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem syrnck-bep run --config service.json syrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json python scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library BIBLIOTHEQUE_NATIVE --output EXECUTABLE_BEP","headings":[{"id":"syncthing-fixed-block-adapter","text":"Adaptateur Syncthing à blocs fixes","level":1},{"id":"build-and-test","text":"Bibliothèque native et dépendances","level":2},{"id":"apis","text":"API","level":2},{"id":"folder-catalog","text":"Catalogue persistant de dossier","level":2},{"id":"staged-download","text":"Télécharger sans garder le Store ouvert","level":2},{"id":"device-connections","text":"Authentifier une connexion d’appareil","level":2},{"id":"reference-daemon","text":"Vérifier avec le daemon de référence","level":2},{"id":"resume-and-cancellation","text":"Reprise et annulation","level":2},{"id":"folder-controller","text":"Contrôleur continu de dossier de travail","level":2},{"id":"protocol-conflicts","text":"Versions protocolaires concurrentes","level":2},{"id":"folder-sessions","text":"Sessions authentifiées continues","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Conserver les éditions pendant le checkout actif","level":2},{"id":"folder-service","text":"Service résident de reconnexion","level":2},{"id":"standalone-service","text":"Exécutable autonome au premier plan","level":2},{"id":"build-standalone-service","text":"Construire et distribuer l’exécutable","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-2-L11","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-6-L1"],"languages":["go","text"],"code":"view, err := syncthing.NewFile(ctx, source, manifest, \"project\", \"Content/Map.uasset\", 0)\n// Check err, authenticate the peer and announce view.Info() through its model.\nresult, err := syncthing.Fetch(ctx, destination, \"project\", incomingInfo,\n    connection.Request, syncthing.FetchOptions{\n        ReuseManifest: previousManifest,\n        ResumeDirectory: perContentPrivateDirectory,\n    })\n// Check err before using result.Manifest or publishing a workspace revision.\nsession, err := syncthing.OpenDeviceConnection(ctx, stream, syncthing.DeviceOptions{\n    Certificate: deviceCertificate,\n    RemoteDevice: pairedDeviceID,\n    Hello: protocol.Hello{DeviceName: \"Asset workstation\"},\n}, folderModel)\n// Traiter err avant utilisation. Démarrage explicite, puis ClusterConfig comme\n// premier message après authentification. Le modèle autorise chaque dossier.\nsession.Start()\nsession.ClusterConfig(clusterConfig, nil)\n// Envoyer un Index complet avant les mises à jour, sauf accord persistant\n// d’index delta. Fetch peut utiliser session.Request pour les blocs vérifiés.\npython scripts/build_syncthing_reference.py --download\npython scripts/test_go.py --library build/Release/syrnck_ffi.dll --module syncthing --check-pointers\nsyrnck-bep device-id --certificate device/cert.pem\nsyrnck-bep run --config service.json\nsyrnck-bep --lang fr run --config service.json\npython scripts/build_bep_service.py --download --library BIBLIOTHEQUE_NATIVE --output EXECUTABLE_BEP","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/syncthing/standalone-service","#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts","#/fr/syncthing/folder-service","#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/fr/syncthing/protocol-conflicts"],"kind":"guide","revision":"c618da096480"},{"id":"text-diff","title":"Différences textuelles bornées","description":"Guide : Différences textuelles bornées.","section":"Versionnage","order":550,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-text-differences\">Différences textuelles bornées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-diff/bounded-text-differences\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Différences textuelles bornées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>compare_text(source_bytes, target_bytes, options, operation) calcule des changements de lignes déterministes sans écrire. Comparaison exacte des espaces, LF/CRLF et newline finale, avec UTF-8 requis. Encodage invalide, NUL ou contrôle autre que tabulation/LF/CR immédiatement avant LF donne status binary sans hunks ; aucun décodage automatique d’UTF-16 ou d’assets arbitraires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un résultat text contient equal, tailles et lignes source/cible, additions/deletions, hunks ordonnés. Chaque hunk donne plages start/count et lignes kind context/remove/add, text sans LF, newline. Le CR de CRLF reste dans text. Numérotation à partir de 1 ; plage vide indique le nombre de lignes précédentes. Fichier vide = zéro ligne, sans ligne vide inventée après LF final.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le moteur retire préfixe/suffixe communs puis cherche un script minimal de lignes par Myers borné avec départage déterministe. Des répétitions peuvent s’aligner différemment d’un autre outil. Aucun diff de mots, renommage ou merge implicite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Défauts/maxima : contexte 3/100 ; octets par entrée 1/4 Mio ; lignes 50000/100000 ; travail 16777216/268435456 unités ; sortie JSON 4/8 Mio. Trace fixe de 1048576 entiers, environ 4 Mio. Chaque diagonale/backtrack et comparaison de ligne de même longueur facture ses octets plus un. Scan, UTF-8, découpage et sortie sont aussi bornés. Vues de lignes, éditions, JSON, buffers et allocations s’ajoutent : pas de plafond RSS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Dépassement : limited avec file_limit/line_limit/work_limit/trace_limit/output_limit, sans hunks/comptes partiels. equal est null si la taille empêche la comparaison ; sinon égalité d’octets même après limite ultérieure. Binaire sans comptes de lignes/éditions. Options invalides, annulation et exceptions propagées, jamais transformées en succès/limite. Callbacks sur appelant, non réentrants.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compare-a-native-file-between-revisions\">Comparer un fichier entre révisions\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-diff/compare-a-native-file-between-revisions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comparer un fichier entre révisions\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les sélecteurs suivent HEAD/branche/tag. C++ : diff_revision_file, révision optionnelle null = snapshot vide. Chemin exact portable présent au moins d’un côté ; présence distincte d’un contenu vide.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Enveloppe : path, source_revision/target_revision, résumés nullable source/target avec manifeste/taille/hash/mode, equal, content_changed, executable_changed, manifest_changed, objects_verified et diff. Equal de l’enveloppe inclut présence/contenu/mode ; diff.equal ne compare que les octets. Un ajout de fichier vide peut donc être inégal dans l’enveloppe et égal dans diff. Découpage différent peut changer le manifeste sans changer les octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux tailles sont contrôlées avant toute lecture de bloc. File_limit = métadonnées seulement, objects_verified false. Sinon les deux fichiers complets sont reconstruits/hashés même si binaire/égaux ou limite ultérieure. Donnée absente/corrompue échoue sans patch. Seul ce chemin est lu, pas les voisins/parents. Enveloppe totale 16 Mio.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>JSON canonique par défaut, code 0 pour inspection réussie même binaire/limited. --unified émet a/ et b/, /dev/null si absent et marqueurs exacts de newline manquante ; binaire/limited refusés avant stdout, aucun hunk = sortie vide. Modes/représentations/présence vide nécessitent le JSON : le patch ne représente que le texte, pas toute une révision native.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Contexte 0..100 et options de taille/lignes/travail/sortie s’appliquent par invocation selon la table. Limites invalides avant inspection ; dépassement runtime décrit explicitement. Progression stderr et diagnostics bilingues.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"inspect-frozen-staged-changes\">Inspecter les changements figés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-diff/inspect-frozen-staged-changes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inspecter les changements figés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>diff_staged_file compare chemin dans index et base d’origine. Sans index, tête contre elle-même ; index initial, source vide. Un index périmé garde sa base sans substituer la tête courante ni lire le disque. Les éditions ultérieures n’interviennent pas ; suppression préparée = cible absente.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’enveloppe ajoute comparison staged, branch, base/head/index nullable et stale. Marqueur de branche, date et forme des parents de l’index sont contrôlés. Le CLI suit HEAD sauf --branch. Mêmes options, intégrité, JSON/unified et annulation que la comparaison de révisions. Aucune consommation/rebase/référence ou lecture de workspace. Chemin absent des deux snapshots = erreur même s’il existe maintenant sur disque. \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-hunks\">Hunks\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-lines\">lignes\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-patches\">patches externes\u003c/a> offrent la préparation explicite séparément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"compare-a-working-file\">Comparer un fichier de travail\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-diff/compare-a-working-file\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Comparer un fichier de travail\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Source par défaut = index figé de la branche choisie, sinon sa tête ; --branch remplace la branche. Un index périmé reste source avec sa base/tête décrites. --revision choisit plutôt une source commitée explicite et exclut --branch. --working exclut --staged. Branche initiale sans index = base vide ; suppressions et ajouts non suivis/vides explicites.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Seuls dossiers ancêtres sont énumérés pour orthographe exacte, collisions portables et ignores imbriqués, sans parcourir les sous-arbres sans rapport. Ignoré = erreur, suivi ou absent compris, jamais suppression silencieuse. Liens/reparse, alias de casse, obstructions de type et Store protégé refusés. Le Store est sous le workspace et HEAD doit correspondre à sa racine. Orthographe UTF-8 physique, normalisation comprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Inspection : profondeur 128 composants, budget de 16 Mio pour noms énumérés et ignores par passage ; au plus 16384 règles de 4096 octets. Sémantique des globs identique au staging, limites propres à ce lecteur ciblé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tailles source/travail contrôlées avant payload. Ensuite source complète reconstruite/vérifiée, travail lu par 64 Kio avec détection croissance/réduction. Namespace/taille/date/mode disponible revérifiés autour de la lecture ; mutation observée = refaire. Aucun snapshot atomique ni défense universelle contre falsification de dates ou échanges entre contrôles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Enveloppe comparison working, directory canonique, source_revision, target_revision null. target.manifest et manifest_changed null ; hash calculé seulement si lu. workspace_verified et objects_verified indiquent contrôles terminés, tous deux false à limite de taille. Présence/taille peuvent prouver content_changed ; sinon contenu/égalité restent null si non comparés. Windows : target.executable null et executable_comparable false, mode ignoré pour égalité ; Unix compare le mode. La variante branche ajoute branch/head/base/index/stale. Aucun import, changement de fichier, index ou référence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> explicit_base \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">diff_working_file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source/Player.cpp\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> limits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> unstaged \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">diff_unstaged_file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"feature\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"Source/Player.cpp\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> limits\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Différences textuelles bornées # compare_text(source_bytes, target_bytes, options, operation) calcule des changements de lignes déterministes sans écrire. Comparaison exacte des espaces, LF/CRLF et newline finale, avec UTF-8 requis. Encodage invalide, NUL ou contrôle autre que tabulation/LF/CR immédiatement avant LF donne status binary sans hunks ; aucun décodage automatique d’UTF-16 ou d’assets arbitraires. Un résultat text contient equal, tailles et lignes source/cible, additions/deletions, hunks ordonnés. Chaque hunk donne plages start/count et lignes kind context/remove/add, text sans LF, newline. Le CR de CRLF reste dans text. Numérotation à partir de 1 ; plage vide indique le nombre de lignes précédentes. Fichier vide = zéro ligne, sans ligne vide inventée après LF final. Le moteur retire préfixe/suffixe communs puis cherche un script minimal de lignes par Myers borné avec départage déterministe. Des répétitions peuvent s’aligner différemment d’un autre outil. Aucun diff de mots, renommage ou merge implicite. Défauts/maxima : contexte 3/100 ; octets par entrée 1/4 Mio ; lignes 50000/100000 ; travail 16777216/268435456 unités ; sortie JSON 4/8 Mio. Trace fixe de 1048576 entiers, environ 4 Mio. Chaque diagonale/backtrack et comparaison de ligne de même longueur facture ses octets plus un. Scan, UTF-8, découpage et sortie sont aussi bornés. Vues de lignes, éditions, JSON, buffers et allocations s’ajoutent : pas de plafond RSS. Dépassement : limited avec file_limit/line_limit/work_limit/trace_limit/output_limit, sans hunks/comptes partiels. equal est null si la taille empêche la comparaison ; sinon égalité d’octets même après limite ultérieure. Binaire sans comptes de lignes/éditions. Options invalides, annulation et exceptions propagées, jamais transformées en succès/limite. Callbacks sur appelant, non réentrants. Comparer un fichier entre révisions # Les sélecteurs suivent HEAD/branche/tag. C++ : diff_revision_file, révision optionnelle null = snapshot vide. Chemin exact portable présent au moins d’un côté ; présence distincte d’un contenu vide. Enveloppe : path, source_revision/target_revision, résumés nullable source/target avec manifeste/taille/hash/mode, equal, content_changed, executable_changed, manifest_changed, objects_verified et diff. Equal de l’enveloppe inclut présence/contenu/mode ; diff.equal ne compare que les octets. Un ajout de fichier vide peut donc être inégal dans l’enveloppe et égal dans diff. Découpage différent peut changer le manifeste sans changer les octets. Les deux tailles sont contrôlées avant toute lecture de bloc. File_limit = métadonnées seulement, objects_verified false. Sinon les deux fichiers complets sont reconstruits/hashés même si binaire/égaux ou limite ultérieure. Donnée absente/corrompue échoue sans patch. Seul ce chemin est lu, pas les voisins/parents. Enveloppe totale 16 Mio. JSON canonique par défaut, code 0 pour inspection réussie même binaire/limited. --unified émet a/ et b/, /dev/null si absent et marqueurs exacts de newline manquante ; binaire/limited refusés avant stdout, aucun hunk = sortie vide. Modes/représentations/présence vide nécessitent le JSON : le patch ne représente que le texte, pas toute une révision native. Contexte 0..100 et options de taille/lignes/travail/sortie s’appliquent par invocation selon la table. Limites invalides avant inspection ; dépassement runtime décrit explicitement. Progression stderr et diagnostics bilingues. text Copier syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified Inspecter les changements figés # diff_staged_file compare chemin dans index et base d’origine. Sans index, tête contre elle-même ; index initial, source vide. Un index périmé garde sa base sans substituer la tête courante ni lire le disque. Les éditions ultérieures n’interviennent pas ; suppression préparée = cible absente. L’enveloppe ajoute comparison staged, branch, base/head/index nullable et stale. Marqueur de branche, date et forme des parents de l’index sont contrôlés. Le CLI suit HEAD sauf --branch. Mêmes options, intégrité, JSON/unified et annulation que la comparaison de révisions. Aucune consommation/rebase/référence ou lecture de workspace. Chemin absent des deux snapshots = erreur même s’il existe maintenant sur disque. Hunks , lignes et patches externes offrent la préparation explicite séparément. text Copier syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified Comparer un fichier de travail # Source par défaut = index figé de la branche choisie, sinon sa tête ; --branch remplace la branche. Un index périmé reste source avec sa base/tête décrites. --revision choisit plutôt une source commitée explicite et exclut --branch. --working exclut --staged. Branche initiale sans index = base vide ; suppressions et ajouts non suivis/vides explicites. Seuls dossiers ancêtres sont énumérés pour orthographe exacte, collisions portables et ignores imbriqués, sans parcourir les sous-arbres sans rapport. Ignoré = erreur, suivi ou absent compris, jamais suppression silencieuse. Liens/reparse, alias de casse, obstructions de type et Store protégé refusés. Le Store est sous le workspace et HEAD doit correspondre à sa racine. Orthographe UTF-8 physique, normalisation comprise. Inspection : profondeur 128 composants, budget de 16 Mio pour noms énumérés et ignores par passage ; au plus 16384 règles de 4096 octets. Sémantique des globs identique au staging, limites propres à ce lecteur ciblé. Tailles source/travail contrôlées avant payload. Ensuite source complète reconstruite/vérifiée, travail lu par 64 Kio avec détection croissance/réduction. Namespace/taille/date/mode disponible revérifiés autour de la lecture ; mutation observée = refaire. Aucun snapshot atomique ni défense universelle contre falsification de dates ou échanges entre contrôles. Enveloppe comparison working, directory canonique, source_revision, target_revision null. target.manifest et manifest_changed null ; hash calculé seulement si lu. workspace_verified et objects_verified indiquent contrôles terminés, tous deux false à limite de taille. Présence/taille peuvent prouver content_changed ; sinon contenu/égalité restent null si non comparés. Windows : target.executable null et executable_comparable false, mode ignoré pour égalité ; Unix compare le mode. La variante branche ajoute branch/head/base/index/stale. Aucun import, changement de fichier, index ou référence. text Copier syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory . cpp Copier auto explicit_base = store . diff_working_file ( project , revision_id , \"Source/Player.cpp\" , limits , operation ) ; auto unstaged = store . diff_unstaged_file ( project , \"feature\" , \"Source/Player.cpp\" , limits , operation ) ; syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature syrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory . auto explicit_base = store.diff_working_file(project, revision_id, \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation); auto unstaged = store.diff_unstaged_file(project, \"feature\", \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);","headings":[{"id":"bounded-text-differences","text":"Différences textuelles bornées","level":1},{"id":"compare-a-native-file-between-revisions","text":"Comparer un fichier entre révisions","level":2},{"id":"inspect-frozen-staged-changes","text":"Inspecter les changements figés","level":2},{"id":"compare-a-working-file","text":"Comparer un fichier de travail","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native diff-text SOURCE TARGET Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native diff-text HEAD release Source/Player.cpp --context 5 --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --branch feature --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --branch feature\nsyrnck native diff-text --working Source/Player.cpp --revision HEAD --directory .\nauto explicit_base = store.diff_working_file(project, revision_id, \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);\nauto unstaged = store.diff_unstaged_file(project, \"feature\", \"Source/Player.cpp\", limits, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-hunks","#/fr/native-lines","#/fr/text-patches"],"kind":"guide","revision":"0113a6462c37"},{"id":"text-merge","title":"Fusion textuelle à trois voies","description":"Guide : Fusion textuelle à trois voies.","section":"Versionnage","order":560,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"bounded-three-way-text-merge\">Fusion textuelle à trois voies bornée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/bounded-three-way-text-merge\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fusion textuelle à trois voies bornée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>merge_text(base,current,incoming,limits,operation) compare les deux côtés à une base et combine les éditions indépendantes. Un changement identique des deux côtés apparaît une fois. Politique prudente : chevauchement donnant des résultats différents reste conflit, insertion à la frontière d’un remplacement comprise. Pas de merge par caractère ou inférence de renommage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"result\">Résultat\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/result\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Résultat\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le JSON v1 donne tailles/lignes d’entrée, current_changes/incoming_changes (groupes d’éditions contigus contre base), status, reason, conflicts et content. Clean : chaîne UTF-8 exacte, même vide ; conflict, binary et limited : content null. Binary signifie UTF-8/contrôles invalides ; limited signale une borne d’entrée/résultat/recherche/trace/sortie.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque conflit contient plages base/current/incoming start/count. Plage non vide à partir de 1 ; vide désigne la ligne précédente, zéro au début. Les coordonnées tiennent compte des insertions/retraits antérieurs et un groupe connecté de chevauchements donne un conflit. Seulement des coordonnées, sans marqueurs textuels.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>LF, CRLF, mélanges acceptés et absence de LF finale sont conservés exactement, sans newline ou marqueur inventé. Une entrée binaire est refusée même si les trois sont égales. Une limite efface conflits, comptes et contenu plutôt que rendre une fusion partielle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"budgets-and-cancellation\">Budgets et annulation\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/budgets-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Budgets et annulation\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>TextDiffOptions : défaut 1 Mio par entrée/résultat, 50000 lignes, 16777216 unités de travail, 4 Mio JSON ; plafonds 4 Mio, 100000 lignes, 268435456 unités, 8 Mio. Context est validé sans affecter les décisions. Les deux recherches Myers et l’assemblage partagent le budget ; trace réutilisée plafonnée à environ 4 Mio par recherche. Tables de lignes, groupes et résultat bornés, sans plafond global de RAM. Le JSON du résultat propre, échappement compris, doit tenir.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Motifs : file_limit, line_limit, work_limit, trace_limit, output_limit, result_file_limit, result_line_limit. Une jonction après ligne non terminée est refusée par unterminated_line_join. Annulation/exception propagées ; aucune écriture de fichier ou objet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"merge-one-stored-path\">Fusionner un chemin stocké\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/merge-one-stored-path\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Fusionner un chemin stocké\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>merge_revision_file reçoit trois snapshots explicites et un chemin, sans choisir de base de graphe ; l’appelant peut prendre celle du plan natif. Enveloppe avec trois IDs, résumés de fichiers base/current/incoming nullable, ready, objects_verified, result_executable, blockers et merge. Seul clean est prêt et donne un mode final. Les modes fusionnent séparément : current inchangé prend incoming, sinon current est gardé. La représentation du manifeste n’altère pas l’égalité de texte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les trois chemins doivent exister. Absent : file_presence et merge null ; taille excessive contrôlée avant blocs : file_limit et merge null ; les deux ont objects_verified false. Sinon fichiers complets et hashes sont vérifiés, même si binaire/conflit/limite ultérieure rendent non prêt avec motif dans blockers. Donnée absente/corrompue lance une erreur, pas un résultat fusionné.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Audit seulement des chemins demandés, sans voisins/ancêtres, sans workspace ou publication. Enveloppe bornée à 16 Mio, rapport interne à max_output_bytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\">CLI et résolution revue\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution\" aria-label=\"Lien vers CLI et résolution revue\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le CLI résout les sélecteurs natifs. JSON par défaut ; succès zéro seulement si ready. Bloqué : code 1 avec rapport complet. --text écrit uniquement les octets propres, sans newline finale ajoutée ; conflit, binaire, absence ou limite échouent sans contenu stdout. Texte/IDs/JSON indépendants de la langue, diagnostics bilingues. Les quatre limites textuelles et progression/annulation sont disponibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette commande est un aperçu explicite ; merge, cherry-pick/revert, rebase et rebase-index ont aussi leur composition opt-in. Pour un fichier personnalisé : garder le contexte exact du plan ; passer base/head/source au merge-text ; vérifier JSON/mode ; enregistrer --text hors du workspace avec un outil préservant les octets (d’anciens shells peuvent transcoder) ; importer via put et conserver sa racine ; ajouter le choix file/manifeste/mode au contexte ; replanifier --resolutions --verify puis appliquer avec même tête/document.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les contrôles de publication, journal et archives protègent ensuite commit/parents. Les autres conflits bloquent toujours. Le preview seul ne lie ni ne publie une branche. Les cas racine/ajout/suppression gardent les règles de conflit de leur opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-file-preparation\">Préparation virtuelle\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-merge/virtual-file-preparation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparation virtuelle\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le lot textuel interne prédit les manifestes en mémoire avec frontières CDC et encodage v1 de production. Les octets propres sont gardés jusqu’à l’application, sans écriture au plan. La matérialisation utilise le pipeline configuré et exige l’ID prédit avant usage par une révision. Une représentation existante est reprise si le contenu logique fusionné égale une entrée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Au plus 64 conflits tentés et 16 Mio pour somme des entrées vérifiées/résultats propres, en supplément des limites par fichier. Hors budget, le conflit reste avec file_count_limit/total_bytes_limit, jamais une approximation. Les rapports exposent statut/plages/métadonnées cibles, pas les octets générés.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Fusion textuelle à trois voies bornée # merge_text(base,current,incoming,limits,operation) compare les deux côtés à une base et combine les éditions indépendantes. Un changement identique des deux côtés apparaît une fois. Politique prudente : chevauchement donnant des résultats différents reste conflit, insertion à la frontière d’un remplacement comprise. Pas de merge par caractère ou inférence de renommage. Résultat # Le JSON v1 donne tailles/lignes d’entrée, current_changes/incoming_changes (groupes d’éditions contigus contre base), status, reason, conflicts et content. Clean : chaîne UTF-8 exacte, même vide ; conflict, binary et limited : content null. Binary signifie UTF-8/contrôles invalides ; limited signale une borne d’entrée/résultat/recherche/trace/sortie. Chaque conflit contient plages base/current/incoming start/count. Plage non vide à partir de 1 ; vide désigne la ligne précédente, zéro au début. Les coordonnées tiennent compte des insertions/retraits antérieurs et un groupe connecté de chevauchements donne un conflit. Seulement des coordonnées, sans marqueurs textuels. LF, CRLF, mélanges acceptés et absence de LF finale sont conservés exactement, sans newline ou marqueur inventé. Une entrée binaire est refusée même si les trois sont égales. Une limite efface conflits, comptes et contenu plutôt que rendre une fusion partielle. Budgets et annulation # TextDiffOptions : défaut 1 Mio par entrée/résultat, 50000 lignes, 16777216 unités de travail, 4 Mio JSON ; plafonds 4 Mio, 100000 lignes, 268435456 unités, 8 Mio. Context est validé sans affecter les décisions. Les deux recherches Myers et l’assemblage partagent le budget ; trace réutilisée plafonnée à environ 4 Mio par recherche. Tables de lignes, groupes et résultat bornés, sans plafond global de RAM. Le JSON du résultat propre, échappement compris, doit tenir. Motifs : file_limit, line_limit, work_limit, trace_limit, output_limit, result_file_limit, result_line_limit. Une jonction après ligne non terminée est refusée par unterminated_line_join. Annulation/exception propagées ; aucune écriture de fichier ou objet. Fusionner un chemin stocké # merge_revision_file reçoit trois snapshots explicites et un chemin, sans choisir de base de graphe ; l’appelant peut prendre celle du plan natif. Enveloppe avec trois IDs, résumés de fichiers base/current/incoming nullable, ready, objects_verified, result_executable, blockers et merge. Seul clean est prêt et donne un mode final. Les modes fusionnent séparément : current inchangé prend incoming, sinon current est gardé. La représentation du manifeste n’altère pas l’égalité de texte. Les trois chemins doivent exister. Absent : file_presence et merge null ; taille excessive contrôlée avant blocs : file_limit et merge null ; les deux ont objects_verified false. Sinon fichiers complets et hashes sont vérifiés, même si binaire/conflit/limite ultérieure rendent non prêt avec motif dans blockers. Donnée absente/corrompue lance une erreur, pas un résultat fusionné. Audit seulement des chemins demandés, sans voisins/ancêtres, sans workspace ou publication. Enveloppe bornée à 16 Mio, rapport interne à max_output_bytes. CLI et résolution revue # Le CLI résout les sélecteurs natifs. JSON par défaut ; succès zéro seulement si ready. Bloqué : code 1 avec rapport complet. --text écrit uniquement les octets propres, sans newline finale ajoutée ; conflit, binaire, absence ou limite échouent sans contenu stdout. Texte/IDs/JSON indépendants de la langue, diagnostics bilingues. Les quatre limites textuelles et progression/annulation sont disponibles. Cette commande est un aperçu explicite ; merge, cherry-pick/revert, rebase et rebase-index ont aussi leur composition opt-in. Pour un fichier personnalisé : garder le contexte exact du plan ; passer base/head/source au merge-text ; vérifier JSON/mode ; enregistrer --text hors du workspace avec un outil préservant les octets (d’anciens shells peuvent transcoder) ; importer via put et conserver sa racine ; ajouter le choix file/manifeste/mode au contexte ; replanifier --resolutions --verify puis appliquer avec même tête/document. Les contrôles de publication, journal et archives protègent ensuite commit/parents. Les autres conflits bloquent toujours. Le preview seul ne lie ni ne publie une branche. Les cas racine/ajout/suppression gardent les règles de conflit de leur opération. text Copier syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text Préparation virtuelle # Le lot textuel interne prédit les manifestes en mémoire avec frontières CDC et encodage v1 de production. Les octets propres sont gardés jusqu’à l’application, sans écriture au plan. La matérialisation utilise le pipeline configuré et exige l’ID prédit avant usage par une révision. Une représentation existante est reprise si le contenu logique fusionné égale une entrée. Au plus 64 conflits tentés et 16 Mio pour somme des entrées vérifiées/résultats propres, en supplément des limites par fichier. Hors budget, le conflit reste avec file_count_limit/total_bytes_limit, jamais une approximation. Les rapports exposent statut/plages/métadonnées cibles, pas les octets générés. syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp syrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text","headings":[{"id":"bounded-three-way-text-merge","text":"Fusion textuelle à trois voies bornée","level":1},{"id":"result","text":"Résultat","level":2},{"id":"budgets-and-cancellation","text":"Budgets et annulation","level":2},{"id":"merge-one-stored-path","text":"Fusionner un chemin stocké","level":2},{"id":"cli-and-reviewed-conflict-resolution","text":"CLI et résolution revue","level":2},{"id":"virtual-file-preparation","text":"Préparation virtuelle","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck native merge-text BASE CURRENT INCOMING Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native merge-text BASE HEAD feature Source/Player.cpp --text","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"772deeac3690"},{"id":"text-patches","title":"Patches unifiés stricts","description":"Guide : Patches unifiés stricts.","section":"Versionnage","order":570,"parent":"native-history","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"strict-unified-text-patches\">Patches textuels unifiés stricts\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-patches/strict-unified-text-patches\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Patches textuels unifiés stricts\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>apply_text_patch(source,patch,path,limits,operation) rend TextPatchResult avec contenu complet et comptes hunks/ajouts/retraits sans écrire fichiers, objets ou références. Une entrée invalide lance une erreur, sans résultat partiel et sans modifier les entrées.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"accepted-format\">Format accepté\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-patches/accepted-format\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Format accepté\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un fichier UTF-8 existant par patch, labels exacts. Labels identiques sans préfixe et terminateur TAB final Git acceptés. Préambule exact diff --git a/PATH b/PATH et ligne index OLD..NEW avec 100644/100755 optionnelle possibles. Les hashes index sont des indications de format, pas des identités Git vérifiées : octets source/contexte fixent l’application. Compte de hunk omis si un ; texte de section après @@ accepté. Positions/comptes source et résultat, insertions de compte zéro comprises, doivent correspondre. Toute ligne contexte/retrait doit être identique à la position exacte, sans recherche d’offset, fuzz, correction d’espace ou inversion devinée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Labels UTF-8 littéraux, métadonnées LF/CRLF. Hors sous-ensemble : labels cités/échappés, dates, renommages, modes, présence de fichiers, /dev/null, binaire, diff combiné/de mots, multi-fichier et wrappers mail/commit. Second fichier ou suffixe non géré refusé ; patch vide ou sans ajout/retrait aussi. Chemin portable validé, jamais ouvert. Le mode index ne change pas le mode exécutable source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">--- a/Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">+++ b/Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">@@ -1 +1 @@\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">-old line\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">+new line\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"exact-bytes-and-bounds\">Octets exacts et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-patches/exact-bytes-and-bounds\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Octets exacts et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les lignes de corps gardent contenu et LF/CRLF. Le marqueur standard de newline finale manquante s’applique à la ligne précédente, sans répétition ni orphelin. Le corps du patch doit lui-même se terminer par LF, avec le marqueur pour l’absence de LF de contenu ; ligne tronquée, ligne vide non terminée ou concaténation après une ligne non terminée refusées. Aucune normalisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Classification UTF-8/contrôles identique au \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff\u003c/a>. max_file_bytes borne indépendamment source/résultat (1 Mio, plafond 4) ; max_output_bytes borne le patch entrant (4 Mio, plafond 8) ; max_lines borne chaque fichier (50000, plafond 100000). max_work facture octets de chaque ligne de patch +1, octets source comparés +1 et octets inchangés copiés +1 ; scans de validation aussi bornés par tailles. Positions/comptes et nombre de hunks au plus 100000. Context validé mais sans modifier un patch externe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Mémoire : vues de lignes source et résultat bornés, sans recherche de distance ou trace de diff. Stop pendant validation/parcours/sortie ; observateur planning synchrone peut lancer une erreur, jamais de succès partiel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index\">Préparer un patch dans l’index natif\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-patches/prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préparer un patch dans l’index natif\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>plan_stage_patch/stage_patch utilisent le fichier figé complet de staging/BRANCH, sinon heads/BRANCH. Pas de dossier ou lecture de travail ; sale, absent, ignoré ou devenu dossier sans effet. Le fichier source doit exister dans le snapshot ; une branche initiale est possible avec index initial contenant ce fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le plan donne operation stage-patch, branche/chemin/tête/index/base, révision source et métadonnées vérifiées, patch_hash BLAKE3 brut, taille du patch et comptes/taille/hash du résultat. Il ne réaffiche pas le patch : examiner l’original. plan_id est l’ID chunk du JSON canonique avant son ajout, sans objet plan. Options, patch, source et résultat sont liés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’application recalcule l’ID exact avant ingestion. Format, source absente/trop grande, binaire, contexte/plage/budget invalides échouent sans écriture. Un aperçu valide peut encore être bloqué par checkout, index périmé, résultat identique ou archive contradictoire. Il vérifie seulement le fichier ; publication vérifie candidat, ancienne préparation et branche, donc une autre donnée corrompue peut bloquer ensuite.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le candidat garde toute la table figée, change le seul manifeste et conserve mode/voisins. Ingestion CDC et préférences habituelles, puis CAS tête/index/HEAD symbolique/archive publiant staging et archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID. Archive exacte réutilisable ; toute l’ancienne préparation/base reste reconstructible après GC et commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Arrêt/observateur/fermeture en échec avant publication gardent l’état ancien, objets complets inaccessibles possibles. Aucun journal workspace ni callback après publication. Rapport complet 16 Mio indépendamment du patch. Garder les octets empruntés stables pendant chaque appel.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-workflow\">Parcours CLI\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/text-patches/cli-workflow\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Parcours CLI\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Git --output écrit directement le patch sans transcodage shell. Choisir un seul fichier existant et la bonne source. Les hashes Git index n’autorisent aucun objet Syrnck. Les patches de modification générés par native diff-text --unified sont aussi acceptés, avec chemins Unicode/espaces, LF/CRLF, fichier existant vide, contenu vidé et plusieurs hunks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux commandes exigent --patch FILE et acceptent --branch, limites, progression et arrêt, mais pas --directory : application sur octets stockés. Patch lu une fois dans son budget avant plan/application ; changement lors d’une invocation ultérieure impose nouvel aperçu. Revoir patch et hashes source/résultat avant appliquer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>JSON identique en anglais/français. Prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; patch/options/application invalides : non-zéro sans résultat. Succès : state applied, index précédent/nouveau, manifeste/hash résultat et ancien ID de plan conservé. Progression stderr ; voisins/modes préparés conservés au commit staged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Patches textuels unifiés stricts # apply_text_patch(source,patch,path,limits,operation) rend TextPatchResult avec contenu complet et comptes hunks/ajouts/retraits sans écrire fichiers, objets ou références. Une entrée invalide lance une erreur, sans résultat partiel et sans modifier les entrées. Format accepté # Un fichier UTF-8 existant par patch, labels exacts. Labels identiques sans préfixe et terminateur TAB final Git acceptés. Préambule exact diff --git a/PATH b/PATH et ligne index OLD..NEW avec 100644/100755 optionnelle possibles. Les hashes index sont des indications de format, pas des identités Git vérifiées : octets source/contexte fixent l’application. Compte de hunk omis si un ; texte de section après @@ accepté. Positions/comptes source et résultat, insertions de compte zéro comprises, doivent correspondre. Toute ligne contexte/retrait doit être identique à la position exacte, sans recherche d’offset, fuzz, correction d’espace ou inversion devinée. Labels UTF-8 littéraux, métadonnées LF/CRLF. Hors sous-ensemble : labels cités/échappés, dates, renommages, modes, présence de fichiers, /dev/null, binaire, diff combiné/de mots, multi-fichier et wrappers mail/commit. Second fichier ou suffixe non géré refusé ; patch vide ou sans ajout/retrait aussi. Chemin portable validé, jamais ouvert. Le mode index ne change pas le mode exécutable source. text Copier --- a/Source/Player.cpp +++ b/Source/Player.cpp @@ -1 +1 @@ -old line +new line Octets exacts et limites # Les lignes de corps gardent contenu et LF/CRLF. Le marqueur standard de newline finale manquante s’applique à la ligne précédente, sans répétition ni orphelin. Le corps du patch doit lui-même se terminer par LF, avec le marqueur pour l’absence de LF de contenu ; ligne tronquée, ligne vide non terminée ou concaténation après une ligne non terminée refusées. Aucune normalisation. Classification UTF-8/contrôles identique au diff . max_file_bytes borne indépendamment source/résultat (1 Mio, plafond 4) ; max_output_bytes borne le patch entrant (4 Mio, plafond 8) ; max_lines borne chaque fichier (50000, plafond 100000). max_work facture octets de chaque ligne de patch +1, octets source comparés +1 et octets inchangés copiés +1 ; scans de validation aussi bornés par tailles. Positions/comptes et nombre de hunks au plus 100000. Context validé mais sans modifier un patch externe. Mémoire : vues de lignes source et résultat bornés, sans recherche de distance ou trace de diff. Stop pendant validation/parcours/sortie ; observateur planning synchrone peut lancer une erreur, jamais de succès partiel. Préparer un patch dans l’index natif # plan_stage_patch/stage_patch utilisent le fichier figé complet de staging/BRANCH, sinon heads/BRANCH. Pas de dossier ou lecture de travail ; sale, absent, ignoré ou devenu dossier sans effet. Le fichier source doit exister dans le snapshot ; une branche initiale est possible avec index initial contenant ce fichier. Le plan donne operation stage-patch, branche/chemin/tête/index/base, révision source et métadonnées vérifiées, patch_hash BLAKE3 brut, taille du patch et comptes/taille/hash du résultat. Il ne réaffiche pas le patch : examiner l’original. plan_id est l’ID chunk du JSON canonique avant son ajout, sans objet plan. Options, patch, source et résultat sont liés. L’application recalcule l’ID exact avant ingestion. Format, source absente/trop grande, binaire, contexte/plage/budget invalides échouent sans écriture. Un aperçu valide peut encore être bloqué par checkout, index périmé, résultat identique ou archive contradictoire. Il vérifie seulement le fichier ; publication vérifie candidat, ancienne préparation et branche, donc une autre donnée corrompue peut bloquer ensuite. Le candidat garde toute la table figée, change le seul manifeste et conserve mode/voisins. Ingestion CDC et préférences habituelles, puis CAS tête/index/HEAD symbolique/archive publiant staging et archive/stage-index/OLD_INDEX_ID. Archive exacte réutilisable ; toute l’ancienne préparation/base reste reconstructible après GC et commit. Arrêt/observateur/fermeture en échec avant publication gardent l’état ancien, objets complets inaccessibles possibles. Aucun journal workspace ni callback après publication. Rapport complet 16 Mio indépendamment du patch. Garder les octets empruntés stables pendant chaque appel. Parcours CLI # Git --output écrit directement le patch sans transcodage shell. Choisir un seul fichier existant et la bonne source. Les hashes Git index n’autorisent aucun objet Syrnck. Les patches de modification générés par native diff-text --unified sont aussi acceptés, avec chemins Unicode/espaces, LF/CRLF, fichier existant vide, contenu vidé et plusieurs hunks. Les deux commandes exigent --patch FILE et acceptent --branch, limites, progression et arrêt, mais pas --directory : application sur octets stockés. Patch lu une fois dans son budget avant plan/application ; changement lors d’une invocation ultérieure impose nouvel aperçu. Revoir patch et hashes source/résultat avant appliquer. JSON identique en anglais/français. Prêt : 0 ; bloqué : 1 avec JSON ; patch/options/application invalides : non-zéro sans résultat. Succès : state applied, index précédent/nouveau, manifeste/hash résultat et ancien ID de plan conservé. Progression stderr ; voisins/modes préparés conservés au commit staged. text Copier git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\" --- a/Source/Player.cpp +++ b/Source/Player.cpp @@ -1 +1 @@ -old line +new line git -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp syrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch syrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID syrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified syrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"","headings":[{"id":"strict-unified-text-patches","text":"Patches textuels unifiés stricts","level":1},{"id":"accepted-format","text":"Format accepté","level":2},{"id":"exact-bytes-and-bounds","text":"Octets exacts et limites","level":2},{"id":"prepare-an-external-patch-in-the-native-index","text":"Préparer un patch dans l’index natif","level":2},{"id":"cli-workflow","text":"Parcours CLI","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"--- a/Source/Player.cpp\n+++ b/Source/Player.cpp\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-old line\n+new line\ngit -c core.quotepath=false diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv --output=change.patch -- Source/Player.cpp\nsyrnck native add-patch-plan Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch\nsyrnck native add-patch Source/Player.cpp --patch change.patch --expect-plan PLAN_ID\nsyrnck native diff-text --staged Source/Player.cpp --unified\nsyrnck native commit --staged -m \"External text changes\"","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/text-diff"],"kind":"guide","revision":"b1e111b7638d"},{"id":"transfer-policy","title":"Reprise et objectifs de débit","description":"Guide : Reprise et objectifs de débit.","section":"Transferts","order":580,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets\">Reprises et cibles de trafic partagées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy/retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reprises et cibles de trafic partagées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Push et pull peuvent reprendre les erreurs transitoires et réguler le trafic objet dans chaque direction. Le service et \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code> doivent être configurés ; \u003ccode>--ca\u003c/code> fournit une autorité privée. Le CLI traduit ses messages, garde son JSON anglais et ne persiste aucun identifiant secret dans cette politique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"retry-boundaries\">Frontières des reprises\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy/retry-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Frontières des reprises\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--retries\u003c/code> vaut 0..8 tentatives supplémentaires, 3 par défaut ; zéro désactive les reprises applicatives. Chaque requête possède son budget. Opérations concernées : GET refs, POST missing et GET/PUT d’objets. PUT est idempotent par son hash vérifié : un accusé perdu peut entraîner le renvoi complet. Un download recommence l’objet, pas une plage ; aucune réponse partielle n’est importée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 et erreurs DNS/connexion/envoi/réception/réponse vide/partielle/timeout sont repris dans le budget restant. Autres statuts, dont 401/403/404/409/501, échec TLS, protocole/taille/objet/dépendance invalide, erreur de callback et annulation échouent immédiatement. Le PUT final de référence n’est jamais repris automatiquement, même avec statut transitoire ou réponse perdue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Attente initiale 250 ms, doublée à chaque tentative, plafond 30 secondes. Retry-After entier ou date HTTP valide peut augmenter l’attente jusqu’au même plafond ; un champ mal formé est ignoré. L’attente utilise ensuite une horloge monotone, sans jitter aléatoire. Les erreurs d’identité et de payload ne sont pas masquées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une requête en attente garde slot et tampons facturés mais quitte le multi libcurl. Les autres avancent ; aucun budget ne peut être contourné. Vecteur d’envoi et capacité de réponse sont réutilisés. L’annulation réveille attentes de reprise et débit. Callbacks sur le coordinateur, compteurs remis à zéro par tentative.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>object_retries\u003c/code> compte seulement les nouvelles tentatives objet, pas les contrôles. Les compteurs logiques de succès ne comptent chaque objet qu’une fois, hors tentatives partielles/répétées ; un échec final n’a pas de statistiques de succès. Avec compression, progression/débit et compteurs réseau portent sur les corps encodés. Erreurs de codec/en-têtes ne sont pas reprises ; download réinitialise son décodeur et upload conserve sa trame.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers/publication-boundary\">publication\u003c/a> garde son point d’engagement : aucun callback/arrêt coopératif après envoi du CAS. L’incertitude rend 3 et demande inspection. Une nouvelle session capture une nouvelle référence attendue ; une session inachevée garde l’ancienne et utilise la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">réconciliation\u003c/a> sans rejeu aveugle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-targets\">Cibles de débit\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy/traffic-targets\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cibles de débit\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--upload-kib-s\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--download-kib-s\u003c/code> utilisent des Kio/s (1024 octets/s), plage 0..1073741824 ; zéro, valeur par défaut, signifie illimité. Ce sont des cibles moyennes agrégées de corps d’objets pour l’opération entière, partagées entre requêtes, reprises et lots, sans multiplication par \u003ccode>--requests\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque handle reçoit sa part des limites libcurl. Le coordinateur cumule les corps de toutes les tentatives et règle la suite sur un budget commun de temps écoulé, soldé avant publication : de petits objets n’obtiennent pas chacun une nouvelle tolérance de démarrage. Le budget d’un push couvre tous ses lots. Reprises, travail local et inactivité comptent dans le temps ; le débit réel peut rester inférieur à la cible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ce n’est pas un policier instantané de paquets : rafales courtes, buffers socket et ordonnancement sont permis. En-têtes HTTP, TLS, requêtes de contrôle et CAS final sont exclus. Corps des tentatives échouées/répétées et accusés sont inclus. Une opération annulée/échouée peut terminer avant compensation de sa dernière rafale ; aucune session suivante n’hérite du budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le délai objet couvre le temps maximal d’un objet à la cible/concurrence choisie plus 120 secondes. Connexion : 15 secondes ; contrôles et CAS : 120 secondes. Aucun délai global n’est garanti et les timeouts proxy/serveur restent applicables.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C++ expose \u003ccode>max_retries\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>retry_delay_ms\u003c/code> (1..30000), \u003ccode>upload_bytes_per_second\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>download_bytes_per_second\u003c/code>. Un débit non nul vaut au moins \u003ccode>max_requests\u003c/code> octets/s et au plus 1 Tio/s. Passer les options après OperationOptions pour push/pull, en deuxième argument de refs. Refs permet les reprises mais n’a pas de trafic objet à limiter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery-and-validation\">Reprise après interruption\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy/recovery-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Reprise après interruption\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Relancer renégocie la présence distante ou vérifie les objets locaux et réutilise les imports terminés. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">sessions persistantes\u003c/a> gardent racine et référence attendue, et protègent sélectivement les objets pendant le GC jusqu’à achèvement ou abandon. Budgets de reprises et crédits de débit ne persistent pas. Baux d’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">envoi\u003c/a> et de \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">lecture\u003c/a> assurent leurs protections respectives tant qu’ils restent actifs. La reprise automatique en arrière-plan reste à développer.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Reprises et cibles de trafic partagées # Push et pull peuvent reprendre les erreurs transitoires et réguler le trafic objet dans chaque direction. Le service et SYRNCK_TOKEN doivent être configurés ; --ca fournit une autorité privée. Le CLI traduit ses messages, garde son JSON anglais et ne persiste aucun identifiant secret dans cette politique. text Copier syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json Frontières des reprises # --retries vaut 0..8 tentatives supplémentaires, 3 par défaut ; zéro désactive les reprises applicatives. Chaque requête possède son budget. Opérations concernées : GET refs, POST missing et GET/PUT d’objets. PUT est idempotent par son hash vérifié : un accusé perdu peut entraîner le renvoi complet. Un download recommence l’objet, pas une plage ; aucune réponse partielle n’est importée. HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504 et erreurs DNS/connexion/envoi/réception/réponse vide/partielle/timeout sont repris dans le budget restant. Autres statuts, dont 401/403/404/409/501, échec TLS, protocole/taille/objet/dépendance invalide, erreur de callback et annulation échouent immédiatement. Le PUT final de référence n’est jamais repris automatiquement, même avec statut transitoire ou réponse perdue. Attente initiale 250 ms, doublée à chaque tentative, plafond 30 secondes. Retry-After entier ou date HTTP valide peut augmenter l’attente jusqu’au même plafond ; un champ mal formé est ignoré. L’attente utilise ensuite une horloge monotone, sans jitter aléatoire. Les erreurs d’identité et de payload ne sont pas masquées. Une requête en attente garde slot et tampons facturés mais quitte le multi libcurl. Les autres avancent ; aucun budget ne peut être contourné. Vecteur d’envoi et capacité de réponse sont réutilisés. L’annulation réveille attentes de reprise et débit. Callbacks sur le coordinateur, compteurs remis à zéro par tentative. object_retries compte seulement les nouvelles tentatives objet, pas les contrôles. Les compteurs logiques de succès ne comptent chaque objet qu’une fois, hors tentatives partielles/répétées ; un échec final n’a pas de statistiques de succès. Avec compression, progression/débit et compteurs réseau portent sur les corps encodés. Erreurs de codec/en-têtes ne sont pas reprises ; download réinitialise son décodeur et upload conserve sa trame. La publication garde son point d’engagement : aucun callback/arrêt coopératif après envoi du CAS. L’incertitude rend 3 et demande inspection. Une nouvelle session capture une nouvelle référence attendue ; une session inachevée garde l’ancienne et utilise la réconciliation sans rejeu aveugle. Cibles de débit # --upload-kib-s et --download-kib-s utilisent des Kio/s (1024 octets/s), plage 0..1073741824 ; zéro, valeur par défaut, signifie illimité. Ce sont des cibles moyennes agrégées de corps d’objets pour l’opération entière, partagées entre requêtes, reprises et lots, sans multiplication par --requests . Chaque handle reçoit sa part des limites libcurl. Le coordinateur cumule les corps de toutes les tentatives et règle la suite sur un budget commun de temps écoulé, soldé avant publication : de petits objets n’obtiennent pas chacun une nouvelle tolérance de démarrage. Le budget d’un push couvre tous ses lots. Reprises, travail local et inactivité comptent dans le temps ; le débit réel peut rester inférieur à la cible. Ce n’est pas un policier instantané de paquets : rafales courtes, buffers socket et ordonnancement sont permis. En-têtes HTTP, TLS, requêtes de contrôle et CAS final sont exclus. Corps des tentatives échouées/répétées et accusés sont inclus. Une opération annulée/échouée peut terminer avant compensation de sa dernière rafale ; aucune session suivante n’hérite du budget. Le délai objet couvre le temps maximal d’un objet à la cible/concurrence choisie plus 120 secondes. Connexion : 15 secondes ; contrôles et CAS : 120 secondes. Aucun délai global n’est garanti et les timeouts proxy/serveur restent applicables. C++ expose max_retries , retry_delay_ms (1..30000), upload_bytes_per_second , download_bytes_per_second . Un débit non nul vaut au moins max_requests octets/s et au plus 1 Tio/s. Passer les options après OperationOptions pour push/pull, en deuxième argument de refs. Refs permet les reprises mais n’a pas de trafic objet à limiter. Reprise après interruption # Relancer renégocie la présence distante ou vérifie les objets locaux et réutilise les imports terminés. Les sessions persistantes gardent racine et référence attendue, et protègent sélectivement les objets pendant le GC jusqu’à achèvement ou abandon. Budgets de reprises et crédits de débit ne persistent pas. Baux d’ envoi et de lecture assurent leurs protections respectives tant qu’ils restent actifs. La reprise automatique en arrière-plan reste à développer. syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096 syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json","headings":[{"id":"retry-policy-and-shared-traffic-targets","text":"Reprises et cibles de trafic partagées","level":1},{"id":"retry-boundaries","text":"Frontières des reprises","level":2},{"id":"traffic-targets","text":"Cibles de débit","level":2},{"id":"recovery-and-validation","text":"Reprise après interruption","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --requests 4 --retries 3 --upload-kib-s 4096\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --requests 4 --download-kib-s 8192 --progress-json","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/transfers/publication-boundary","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/server-read-leases"],"kind":"guide","revision":"42de9be553ab"},{"id":"transfer-sessions","title":"Sessions de transfert persistantes","description":"Guide : Sessions de transfert persistantes.","section":"Transferts","order":590,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-transfer-sessions\">Sessions de transfert persistantes\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions/persistent-transfer-sessions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Sessions de transfert persistantes\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Chaque push/pull inscrit son état local avant envoi des objets. Un autre processus peut reprendre, conserver les blocs vérifiés malgré un GC local et réconcilier un accusé perdu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>sessions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>forget\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>prune\u003c/code> sont locaux et ne demandent aucun jeton serveur. Resume utilise les identifiants, autorité privée et options de transfert actuels ; le Store chiffré garde sa clé locale. Ouvrir le Store ne démarre ni réseau ni récupération automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Répéter un transfert retrouve une session inachevée de mêmes direction, endpoint, racine exacte et référence. Resume explicite lit la racine du journal, même après retrait de la référence source. Si la référence distante a changé, une nouvelle commande pull vise la nouvelle racine ; son ancien ID de session reprend l’ancienne.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent sessions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck agent prune\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck agent prune --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"format-and-writes\">Format et écritures\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions/format-and-writes\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Format et écritures\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfers/expected-publication\">attente de publication explicite\u003c/a> peut\nlier un push à une observation distante antérieure. Une nouvelle attente ne\nremplace pas le \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code> figé d’une session inachevée. La reprise explicite\nconserve l’original, même après commit distant dont l’accusé a été perdu.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les journaux \u003ccode>STORE/transfers/ID.json\u003c/code> utilisent un schéma v3 strict, limité à 16 Kio, avec lecture des versions 1 et 2. Champs : \u003ccode>version\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>id\u003c/code> BLAKE3 stable calculé sur direction/empreinte endpoint/racine/référence, \u003ccode>direction\u003c/code> push ou pull, \u003ccode>endpoint\u003c/code> hash de l’URL canonique, \u003ccode>root\u003c/code> clé exacte, \u003ccode>reference\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>expected\u003c/code> ou null, \u003ccode>state\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>upload_lease\u003c/code> nullable depuis v2 et \u003ccode>read_lease\u003c/code> nullable depuis v3.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>URL, jeton, clé, chemin CA et diagnostic ne sont pas enregistrés. Le matching endpoint est exact après normalisation, sans garantie pour toutes les écritures équivalentes. Un autre endpoint est refusé avant HTTP. Comme refs.json, les journaux sont des métadonnées ordinaires locales, sans chiffrement/authentification propre ; l’accès au Store reste la frontière de confiance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Écriture synchronisée puis remplacement atomique. Sous Unix, création du dossier synchronise son parent et écriture/retrait synchronisent le dossier. Windows garde sa publication atomique existante. Les temporaires non engagés ne changent pas l’état canonique. Verrou exclusif pour journaux/objets/références ; versions inconnues, contenu mal formé, identité incohérente ou chemins liés sont refusés. La durabilité électrique est une question distincte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les écritures suivent les frontières d’opération, sans bitmap ni flush par bloc : les objets CAS vérifiés décrivent les payloads terminés. Les requêtes partielles reprennent l’objet entier ; les complets se réutilisent. Reprises, débit et tampons redémarrent à zéro dans le nouveau processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"states-and-reference-protection\">États et protection des références\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions/states-and-reference-protection\" aria-label=\"Lien vers États et protection des références\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>transferring\u003c/code> continue si la destination correspond à expected. \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code> indique une publication imminente ou possiblement achevée ; \u003ccode>uncertain\u003c/code>, une absence de résultat clair ; \u003ccode>conflict\u003c/code>, une destination modifiée à préserver ; \u003ccode>complete\u003c/code>, un reçu historique sans maintien GC ni republication à la reprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le marqueur publishing est durable avant publication. Pour un push publishing/uncertain, une référence distante égale à la racine voulue confirme l’achèvement sans nouveau PUT. Sinon le maintien reste actif et \u003ccode>PublicationUncertain\u003c/code> rend le code 3, même si la destination égale l’ancienne valeur attendue : elle peut avoir été publiée puis remise volontairement. Le CAS initial n’est jamais rejoué aveuglément.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pull vérifie sa fermeture puis publie par CAS contre expected sauvegardé. Une destination changée est conservée. Après coupure, une racine locale déjà correspondante est vérifiée et marquée complète sans téléchargement. Un bail de lecture enregistré est libéré avant achèvement ; ce nettoyage peut contacter le serveur. Si la référence égale encore expected, publication locale réessayable. Un conflit connu reste bloqué jusqu’à résolution/abandon, sauf racine finale déjà présente.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les statistiques ajoutent \u003ccode>session\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>reconciled\u003c/code>. True signifie publication confirmée ou ancien reçu complet, avec zéro objet transféré par cet appel. Un reçu complet n’affirme pas que les références ont encore leurs valeurs historiques et ne les recrée pas. Un nouveau transfert achevé réutilise l’ID avec de nouvelles attentes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’écriture du reçu final peut échouer après publication : code 3 pour push, erreur ordinaire pour pull. Le marqueur permet alors l’inspection à la reprise. Après envoi du CAS distant, callbacks et annulation restent désactivés jusqu’au reçu ; un arrêt tardif peut réussir. La dernière frontière annulable est l’engagement du marqueur ; l’intervalle marqueur/envoi reste prudemment incertain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-and-deliberate-discard\">GC et abandon explicite\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions/gc-and-deliberate-discard\" aria-label=\"Lien vers GC et abandon explicite\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Toute session inachevée contribue sa racine au GC. Push exige une source complète vérifiée ; pull conserve les objets accessibles disponibles même si des dépendances manquent. Les autres objets restent collectables. Le GC lit les journaux en flux avec contrôles d’arrêt et conserve les racines nécessaires ; un journal invalide le bloque. L’audit complet de pack peut refuser des métadonnées incomplètes jusqu’à fin du pull ou collecte après abandon.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>agent forget ID\u003c/code> retire le journal et sa protection, sans modifier références ou payloads. Le GC peut ensuite collecter les objets inaccessibles. Un nom de fichier valide peut être abandonné même si son contenu est mal formé. Les reçus complets ne protègent pas leurs racines. Prune prévisualise nombres complets/inachevés ; \u003ccode>--delete\u003c/code> valide tout l’inventaire puis retire uniquement les complets. Une annulation peut en retirer un sous-ensemble, avec nouvel essai sûr. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/selective-retention\">rétention sélective\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Tous les GC doivent comprendre ces journaux. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">baux d’envoi et reçus\u003c/a> peuvent confirmer un accusé perdu après changement de référence ; l’expiration permet la collecte serveur et la reprise renégocie/réenvoie depuis la source protégée. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">baux de lecture\u003c/a> protègent le pull et les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires\u003c/a> les objets déclarés. Le GC distribué reste à développer. L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/resident-agent\">agent résident de publication native\u003c/a> peut reprendre les envois natifs ; il ne fournit pas de pull général en arrière-plan ni de réconciliation de dossiers. La liste des sessions matérialise son résultat ; les reçus occupent du disque jusqu’à purge/abandon.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Sessions de transfert persistantes # Chaque push/pull inscrit son état local avant envoi des objets. Un autre processus peut reprendre, conserver les blocs vérifiés malgré un GC local et réconcilier un accusé perdu. sessions , forget , prune sont locaux et ne demandent aucun jeton serveur. Resume utilise les identifiants, autorité privée et options de transfert actuels ; le Store chiffré garde sa clé locale. Ouvrir le Store ne démarre ni réseau ni récupération automatique. Répéter un transfert retrouve une session inachevée de mêmes direction, endpoint, racine exacte et référence. Resume explicite lit la racine du journal, même après retrait de la référence source. Si la référence distante a changé, une nouvelle commande pull vise la nouvelle racine ; son ancien ID de session reprend l’ancienne. text Copier syrnck agent sessions syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete Format et écritures # Une attente de publication explicite peut lier un push à une observation distante antérieure. Une nouvelle attente ne remplace pas le expected figé d’une session inachevée. La reprise explicite conserve l’original, même après commit distant dont l’accusé a été perdu. Les journaux STORE/transfers/ID.json utilisent un schéma v3 strict, limité à 16 Kio, avec lecture des versions 1 et 2. Champs : version , id BLAKE3 stable calculé sur direction/empreinte endpoint/racine/référence, direction push ou pull, endpoint hash de l’URL canonique, root clé exacte, reference , expected ou null, state , upload_lease nullable depuis v2 et read_lease nullable depuis v3. URL, jeton, clé, chemin CA et diagnostic ne sont pas enregistrés. Le matching endpoint est exact après normalisation, sans garantie pour toutes les écritures équivalentes. Un autre endpoint est refusé avant HTTP. Comme refs.json, les journaux sont des métadonnées ordinaires locales, sans chiffrement/authentification propre ; l’accès au Store reste la frontière de confiance. Écriture synchronisée puis remplacement atomique. Sous Unix, création du dossier synchronise son parent et écriture/retrait synchronisent le dossier. Windows garde sa publication atomique existante. Les temporaires non engagés ne changent pas l’état canonique. Verrou exclusif pour journaux/objets/références ; versions inconnues, contenu mal formé, identité incohérente ou chemins liés sont refusés. La durabilité électrique est une question distincte. Les écritures suivent les frontières d’opération, sans bitmap ni flush par bloc : les objets CAS vérifiés décrivent les payloads terminés. Les requêtes partielles reprennent l’objet entier ; les complets se réutilisent. Reprises, débit et tampons redémarrent à zéro dans le nouveau processus. États et protection des références # transferring continue si la destination correspond à expected. publishing indique une publication imminente ou possiblement achevée ; uncertain , une absence de résultat clair ; conflict , une destination modifiée à préserver ; complete , un reçu historique sans maintien GC ni republication à la reprise. Le marqueur publishing est durable avant publication. Pour un push publishing/uncertain, une référence distante égale à la racine voulue confirme l’achèvement sans nouveau PUT. Sinon le maintien reste actif et PublicationUncertain rend le code 3, même si la destination égale l’ancienne valeur attendue : elle peut avoir été publiée puis remise volontairement. Le CAS initial n’est jamais rejoué aveuglément. Pull vérifie sa fermeture puis publie par CAS contre expected sauvegardé. Une destination changée est conservée. Après coupure, une racine locale déjà correspondante est vérifiée et marquée complète sans téléchargement. Un bail de lecture enregistré est libéré avant achèvement ; ce nettoyage peut contacter le serveur. Si la référence égale encore expected, publication locale réessayable. Un conflit connu reste bloqué jusqu’à résolution/abandon, sauf racine finale déjà présente. Les statistiques ajoutent session et reconciled . True signifie publication confirmée ou ancien reçu complet, avec zéro objet transféré par cet appel. Un reçu complet n’affirme pas que les références ont encore leurs valeurs historiques et ne les recrée pas. Un nouveau transfert achevé réutilise l’ID avec de nouvelles attentes. L’écriture du reçu final peut échouer après publication : code 3 pour push, erreur ordinaire pour pull. Le marqueur permet alors l’inspection à la reprise. Après envoi du CAS distant, callbacks et annulation restent désactivés jusqu’au reçu ; un arrêt tardif peut réussir. La dernière frontière annulable est l’engagement du marqueur ; l’intervalle marqueur/envoi reste prudemment incertain. GC et abandon explicite # Toute session inachevée contribue sa racine au GC. Push exige une source complète vérifiée ; pull conserve les objets accessibles disponibles même si des dépendances manquent. Les autres objets restent collectables. Le GC lit les journaux en flux avec contrôles d’arrêt et conserve les racines nécessaires ; un journal invalide le bloque. L’audit complet de pack peut refuser des métadonnées incomplètes jusqu’à fin du pull ou collecte après abandon. agent forget ID retire le journal et sa protection, sans modifier références ou payloads. Le GC peut ensuite collecter les objets inaccessibles. Un nom de fichier valide peut être abandonné même si son contenu est mal formé. Les reçus complets ne protègent pas leurs racines. Prune prévisualise nombres complets/inachevés ; --delete valide tout l’inventaire puis retire uniquement les complets. Une annulation peut en retirer un sous-ensemble, avec nouvel essai sûr. Voir la rétention sélective . Tous les GC doivent comprendre ces journaux. Les baux d’envoi et reçus peuvent confirmer un accusé perdu après changement de référence ; l’expiration permet la collecte serveur et la reprise renégocie/réenvoie depuis la source protégée. Les baux de lecture protègent le pull et les inventaires les objets déclarés. Le GC distribué reste à développer. L’ agent résident de publication native peut reprendre les envois natifs ; il ne fournit pas de pull général en arrière-plan ni de réconciliation de dossiers. La liste des sessions matérialise son résultat ; les reçus occupent du disque jusqu’à purge/abandon. syrnck agent sessions syrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress syrnck agent forget SESSION_ID syrnck agent prune syrnck agent prune --delete","headings":[{"id":"persistent-transfer-sessions","text":"Sessions de transfert persistantes","level":1},{"id":"format-and-writes","text":"Format et écritures","level":2},{"id":"states-and-reference-protection","text":"États et protection des références","level":2},{"id":"gc-and-deliberate-discard","text":"GC et abandon explicite","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5"],"languages":["text"],"code":"syrnck agent sessions\nsyrnck agent resume https://blocks.example.invalid SESSION_ID --requests 4 --progress\nsyrnck agent forget SESSION_ID\nsyrnck agent prune\nsyrnck agent prune --delete","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/transfers/expected-publication","#/fr/selective-retention","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/upload-inventories","#/fr/resident-agent"],"kind":"guide","revision":"59c5b630e1de"},{"id":"transfers","title":"Transferts et publication","description":"Guide : Transferts et publication.","section":"Transferts","order":400,"sourceRevision":"3","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication\">Transfert, progression et publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Transfert, progression et publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>L’agent HTTP ponctuel accepte \u003ccode>OperationOptions\u003c/code> : \u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code>, push et pull utilisent un jeton d’arrêt et un observateur synchrone. Le CLI garde les statistiques finales sur stdout. Utiliser un service réellement configuré, \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code> et, pour le Store local chiffré, sa propre \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code>. Événements et progression ne contiennent ni URL, ni jeton, ni clé ; ils identifient des chemins relatifs du protocole.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La phase \u003ccode>transferring\u003c/code> compte corps de requête et de réponse HTTP d’une tentative ; les compteurs repartent par requête et tentative. Le total peut être inconnu puis devient connu avec Content-Length et exact au succès de la requête. Les contrôles au premier plan rendent aussi des événements ; les renouvellements de baux en arrière-plan n’appellent pas l’utilisateur. Fin de requête ne signifie ni import vérifié ni référence publiée. \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> final ne compte qu’une fois les payloads logiques des nouveaux objets, hors contrôles et objets réutilisés. Texte traduit, clés/phases JSON anglaises.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Libcurl peut arrêter une requête en cours ou une attente inactive. Des contrôles ont aussi lieu entre requêtes, objets, validations et écritures. Les callbacks sont sur le thread appelant ; leurs exceptions sont interceptées à la frontière C, font interrompre la requête puis sont relancées en C++, jamais à travers libcurl. IO/codecs/résolution déjà lancés peuvent finir avant le contrôle suivant : l’arrêt est coopératif, pas un délai global immédiat.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Push vérifie toute la fermeture source et les hashes complets avant envoi, négocie la présence par lots puis transmet les absents. Pull vérifie chaque objet avant import. Une interruption laisse des objets complets réutilisables ; les réponses partielles ne sont pas importées. Un manifeste téléchargé peut temporairement manquer de dépendances, faisant échouer un audit de métadonnées inaccessibles ; les anciennes racines restent reconstructibles. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">session locale\u003c/a> inachevée protège la source ou les objets reçus. Un \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">bail de lecture\u003c/a> protège la racine distante exacte jusqu’à disponibilité locale ; il est libéré avant publication locale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"server-authentication-and-request-bodies\">Authentification serveur et corps HTTP\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/server-authentication-and-request-bodies\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Authentification serveur et corps HTTP\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le serveur reçoit le corps avant contrôle d’identité, sous limite de 16 Mio et timeout de lecture de 30 secondes. Il exige ensuite exactement un en-tête \u003ccode>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;\u003c/code> avant parsing JSON applicatif, décompression, accès Store ou publication. Identifiants absents, faux ou dupliqués donnent 401 et \u003ccode>WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=&quot;Syrnck&quot;\u003c/code>, même pour une route inconnue. Recevoir d’abord le corps évite un reset TCP Windows masquant le refus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ce contrat concerne les corps bien formés complètement reçus avec Content-Length ou framing chunked. Erreurs de framing, codage transport non géré et dépassement de taille peuvent précéder l’authentification. \u003ccode>100 Continue\u003c/code> permet l’envoi, sans autoriser l’accès. Les connexions sont fermées après erreur HTTP. La réception occupe encore un worker ; quotas de déploiement et contrôle d’admission restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expected-publication\">Publier selon une observation antérieure\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/expected-publication\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Publier selon une observation antérieure\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code> exige une référence distante absente ou exactement égale à la clé\ntypée indiquée (\u003ccode>chunk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>manifest\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>revision\u003c/code>, deux-points et 64 chiffres\nhexadécimaux minuscules). Remplacer les IDs par une révision précédemment\ntéléchargée ou une valeur typée obtenue par \u003ccode>Remote::refs\u003c/code>. Une observation périmée\nest refusée avant création de session ou envoi d’objet, même si la branche serveur\nautorise les changements forcés. Un changement ultérieur pendant le transfert\nfait encore échouer la publication sous valeur attendue ; les objets réutilisables\ndéjà envoyés peuvent subsister après ce refus plus tardif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">std\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>optional\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&lt;\u003c/span>syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ObjectKey\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>\u003c/span> observed \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> previous_remote_root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">// null means absent\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">push_expected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> local_root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> observed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">                                    operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer_options\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>La session fige cette valeur attendue. Une session inachevée existante avec une\nautre attente est refusée avant écriture ; la reprendre explicitement ou l’inspecter\npuis l’oublier avant de planifier un nouveau transfert. Un nouveau push explicite\nrefuse aussi une cible déjà égale au résultat désiré si elle diffère de \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>.\nUtiliser \u003ccode>agent resume URL SESSION_ID\u003c/code> pour réconcilier un accusé perdu selon sa\nsession d’origine. La réconciliation confirme cette publication passée, sans\nprouver que la référence y pointe encore. Une session terminée démarre un\nnouveau transfert avec la nouvelle attente vérifiée. Sans \u003ccode>--expect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Remote::push\u003c/code> garde son observation au démarrage du\ntransfert. Formats d’objets, HTTP et journaux inchangés ; comptes, signatures,\nbranches et verrous d’assets restent applicables.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-boundary\">Frontière de publication\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/publication-boundary\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Frontière de publication\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Pull vérifie l’annulation jusqu’à validation finale et écriture atomique locale ; un arrêt avant celle-ci garde l’ancienne référence. Push émet \u003ccode>publishing\u003c/code>, contrôle l’arrêt avant préparation du CAS distant et pendant l’écriture du marqueur de journal. Un arrêt à ce callback empêche l’envoi.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Après engagement du marqueur, le CAS est envoyé et terminé même si l’arrêt arrive ensuite. Une coupure entre marqueur et envoi est traitée comme publication incertaine. Annuler un CAS déjà envoyé ne défait pas une publication serveur. Aucun callback ne suit ce point d’engagement ; un Ctrl+C tardif peut rendre le code 0. Le timeout final reste 120 secondes. Le serveur vérifie la fermeture complète avant publication ; un conflit 409 est un refus normal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Réponse perdue ou statut ambigu : C++ lance \u003ccode>net::PublicationUncertain\u003c/code>, le CLI affiche un diagnostic, garde stdout vide et rend le code 3, même si un arrêt est demandé. Le serveur peut déjà avoir publié : inspecter sa référence. Les erreurs de transport, serveur et réponses inattendues sont interprétées prudemment ; 401/403/404/409 restent des erreurs ordinaires. La reprise consulte le journal et confirme une racine correspondante sans rejouer aveuglément le CAS. Un \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">reçu d’envoi publié\u003c/a> peut confirmer le succès même après déplacement ultérieur de la référence. Un rejet loué 412/428 garde la session pour un nouvel essai volontaire.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’annulation coopérative ordinaire rend 130, sans retirer objets vérifiés ou références existantes. Formats et base HTTP v1 restent compatibles ; les baux utilisent une extension négociée.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validation-and-remaining-work\">Ressources et limites\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/validation-and-remaining-work\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Ressources et limites\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les requêtes d’objets sont séquentielles par défaut ; \u003ca href=\"#/fr/parallel-transfers\">parallélisme borné\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy\">reprises et débit\u003c/a> et \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">journaux persistants\u003c/a> sont optionnels ou explicitement configurés. Push matérialise encore fermeture et liste triée ; pull conserve un ensemble visité et une file de parcours. Le suivi local ne constitue pas une synchronisation continue complète ; la rétention distribuée reste à développer. L’intégration Git dispose séparément de ses \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-assets\">catalogues et hooks\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publication-bookkeeping\">Conserver la publication prouvée avant de terminer la session\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/transfers/publication-bookkeeping\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Conserver la publication prouvée avant de terminer la session\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Des surcharges C++ de \u003ccode>push_expected\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>resume\u003c/code> acceptent un \u003ccode>PublicationObserver\u003c/code>\nen dernier argument :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>net\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>PublicationObserver record \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>ObjectKey \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>published\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">  store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">set_ref\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"confirmed-publication\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> published\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L4\" data-line=\"4\">remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">push_expected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> root\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> observed\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> record\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L5\" data-line=\"5\">remote\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">resume\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> session_id\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> transfer\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> record\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le callback synchrone s’exécute sur le thread appelant après preuve du succès,\navant de marquer la session complète. Il s’exécute aussi quand une reprise confirme\nun ancien reçu, ou lors d’une reprise explicite d’un envoi déjà terminé. C’est un\nfait de publication passé, pas une affirmation sur la valeur actuelle de la\nréférence distante. Cet observateur est réservé à l’envoi et doit être idempotent.\nY conserver les traces durables ; l’agent résident y retient l’historique distant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une fois cette frontière commencée, terminer le journal n’est pas annulé par un\narrêt demandé dans le callback. Pour un envoi inachevé, un échec du callback ou\nde l’écriture de fin laisse le journal récupérable, sans rejouer automatiquement\nle CAS. Un reçu déjà terminé le reste si un callback répété échoue ; cet\nobservateur peut être réessayé explicitement.\nLes surcharges sans observateur gardent leur comportement. L’échec n’autorise pas\nle remplacement d’une référence changée ensuite. Voir les\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">sessions persistantes\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Transfert, progression et publication # L’agent HTTP ponctuel accepte OperationOptions : Remote::refs , push et pull utilisent un jeton d’arrêt et un observateur synchrone. Le CLI garde les statistiques finales sur stdout. Utiliser un service réellement configuré, SYRNCK_TOKEN et, pour le Store local chiffré, sa propre SYRNCK_KEY . Événements et progression ne contiennent ni URL, ni jeton, ni clé ; ils identifient des chemins relatifs du protocole. La phase transferring compte corps de requête et de réponse HTTP d’une tentative ; les compteurs repartent par requête et tentative. Le total peut être inconnu puis devient connu avec Content-Length et exact au succès de la requête. Les contrôles au premier plan rendent aussi des événements ; les renouvellements de baux en arrière-plan n’appellent pas l’utilisateur. Fin de requête ne signifie ni import vérifié ni référence publiée. transferred_bytes final ne compte qu’une fois les payloads logiques des nouveaux objets, hors contrôles et objets réutilisés. Texte traduit, clés/phases JSON anglaises. Libcurl peut arrêter une requête en cours ou une attente inactive. Des contrôles ont aussi lieu entre requêtes, objets, validations et écritures. Les callbacks sont sur le thread appelant ; leurs exceptions sont interceptées à la frontière C, font interrompre la requête puis sont relancées en C++, jamais à travers libcurl. IO/codecs/résolution déjà lancés peuvent finir avant le contrôle suivant : l’arrêt est coopératif, pas un délai global immédiat. Push vérifie toute la fermeture source et les hashes complets avant envoi, négocie la présence par lots puis transmet les absents. Pull vérifie chaque objet avant import. Une interruption laisse des objets complets réutilisables ; les réponses partielles ne sont pas importées. Un manifeste téléchargé peut temporairement manquer de dépendances, faisant échouer un audit de métadonnées inaccessibles ; les anciennes racines restent reconstructibles. La session locale inachevée protège la source ou les objets reçus. Un bail de lecture protège la racine distante exacte jusqu’à disponibilité locale ; il est libéré avant publication locale. text Copier syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json Authentification serveur et corps HTTP # Le serveur reçoit le corps avant contrôle d’identité, sous limite de 16 Mio et timeout de lecture de 30 secondes. Il exige ensuite exactement un en-tête Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken> avant parsing JSON applicatif, décompression, accès Store ou publication. Identifiants absents, faux ou dupliqués donnent 401 et WWW-Authenticate: Bearer realm=\"Syrnck\" , même pour une route inconnue. Recevoir d’abord le corps évite un reset TCP Windows masquant le refus. Ce contrat concerne les corps bien formés complètement reçus avec Content-Length ou framing chunked. Erreurs de framing, codage transport non géré et dépassement de taille peuvent précéder l’authentification. 100 Continue permet l’envoi, sans autoriser l’accès. Les connexions sont fermées après erreur HTTP. La réception occupe encore un worker ; quotas de déploiement et contrôle d’admission restent à développer. Publier selon une observation antérieure # text Copier syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID --expect exige une référence distante absente ou exactement égale à la clé typée indiquée ( chunk , manifest ou revision , deux-points et 64 chiffres hexadécimaux minuscules). Remplacer les IDs par une révision précédemment téléchargée ou une valeur typée obtenue par Remote::refs . Une observation périmée est refusée avant création de session ou envoi d’objet, même si la branche serveur autorise les changements forcés. Un changement ultérieur pendant le transfert fait encore échouer la publication sous valeur attendue ; les objets réutilisables déjà envoyés peuvent subsister après ce refus plus tardif. cpp Copier std :: optional \u003c syrnck :: ObjectKey > observed = previous_remote_root ; // null means absent auto result = remote . push_expected ( store , local_root , \"main\" , observed , operation , transfer_options ) ; La session fige cette valeur attendue. Une session inachevée existante avec une autre attente est refusée avant écriture ; la reprendre explicitement ou l’inspecter puis l’oublier avant de planifier un nouveau transfert. Un nouveau push explicite refuse aussi une cible déjà égale au résultat désiré si elle diffère de --expect . Utiliser agent resume URL SESSION_ID pour réconcilier un accusé perdu selon sa session d’origine. La réconciliation confirme cette publication passée, sans prouver que la référence y pointe encore. Une session terminée démarre un nouveau transfert avec la nouvelle attente vérifiée. Sans --expect , Remote::push garde son observation au démarrage du transfert. Formats d’objets, HTTP et journaux inchangés ; comptes, signatures, branches et verrous d’assets restent applicables. Frontière de publication # Pull vérifie l’annulation jusqu’à validation finale et écriture atomique locale ; un arrêt avant celle-ci garde l’ancienne référence. Push émet publishing , contrôle l’arrêt avant préparation du CAS distant et pendant l’écriture du marqueur de journal. Un arrêt à ce callback empêche l’envoi. Après engagement du marqueur, le CAS est envoyé et terminé même si l’arrêt arrive ensuite. Une coupure entre marqueur et envoi est traitée comme publication incertaine. Annuler un CAS déjà envoyé ne défait pas une publication serveur. Aucun callback ne suit ce point d’engagement ; un Ctrl+C tardif peut rendre le code 0. Le timeout final reste 120 secondes. Le serveur vérifie la fermeture complète avant publication ; un conflit 409 est un refus normal. Réponse perdue ou statut ambigu : C++ lance net::PublicationUncertain , le CLI affiche un diagnostic, garde stdout vide et rend le code 3, même si un arrêt est demandé. Le serveur peut déjà avoir publié : inspecter sa référence. Les erreurs de transport, serveur et réponses inattendues sont interprétées prudemment ; 401/403/404/409 restent des erreurs ordinaires. La reprise consulte le journal et confirme une racine correspondante sans rejouer aveuglément le CAS. Un reçu d’envoi publié peut confirmer le succès même après déplacement ultérieur de la référence. Un rejet loué 412/428 garde la session pour un nouvel essai volontaire. L’annulation coopérative ordinaire rend 130, sans retirer objets vérifiés ou références existantes. Formats et base HTTP v1 restent compatibles ; les baux utilisent une extension négociée. Ressources et limites # Les requêtes d’objets sont séquentielles par défaut ; parallélisme borné , reprises et débit et journaux persistants sont optionnels ou explicitement configurés. Push matérialise encore fermeture et liste triée ; pull conserve un ensemble visité et une file de parcours. Le suivi local ne constitue pas une synchronisation continue complète ; la rétention distribuée reste à développer. L’intégration Git dispose séparément de ses catalogues et hooks . Conserver la publication prouvée avant de terminer la session # Des surcharges C++ de push_expected et resume acceptent un PublicationObserver en dernier argument : cpp Copier syrnck :: net :: PublicationObserver record = [ & ] ( const syrnck :: ObjectKey & published ) { store . set_ref ( \"confirmed-publication\" , published ) ; } ; remote . push_expected ( store , root , \"main\" , observed , operation , transfer , record ) ; remote . resume ( store , session_id , operation , transfer , record ) ; Le callback synchrone s’exécute sur le thread appelant après preuve du succès, avant de marquer la session complète. Il s’exécute aussi quand une reprise confirme un ancien reçu, ou lors d’une reprise explicite d’un envoi déjà terminé. C’est un fait de publication passé, pas une affirmation sur la valeur actuelle de la référence distante. Cet observateur est réservé à l’envoi et doit être idempotent. Y conserver les traces durables ; l’agent résident y retient l’historique distant. Une fois cette frontière commencée, terminer le journal n’est pas annulé par un arrêt demandé dans le callback. Pour un envoi inachevé, un échec du callback ou de l’écriture de fin laisse le journal récupérable, sans rejouer automatiquement le CAS. Un reçu déjà terminé le reste si un callback répété échoue ; cet observateur peut être réessayé explicitement. Les surcharges sans observateur gardent leur comportement. L’échec n’autorise pas le remplacement d’une référence changée ensuite. Voir les sessions persistantes . syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress syrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID std::optional\u003csyrnck::ObjectKey> observed = previous_remote_root; // null means absent auto result = remote.push_expected(store, local_root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer_options); syrnck::net::PublicationObserver record = [&](const syrnck::ObjectKey &published) { store.set_ref(\"confirmed-publication\", published); }; remote.push_expected(store, root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer, record); remote.resume(store, session_id, operation, transfer, record);","headings":[{"id":"transfer-progress-cancellation-and-publication","text":"Transfert, progression et publication","level":1},{"id":"server-authentication-and-request-bodies","text":"Authentification serveur et corps HTTP","level":2},{"id":"expected-publication","text":"Publier selon une observation antérieure","level":2},{"id":"publication-boundary","text":"Frontière de publication","level":2},{"id":"validation-and-remaining-work","text":"Ressources et limites","level":2},{"id":"publication-bookkeeping","text":"Conserver la publication prouvée avant de terminer la session","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-4-L3","code-4-L4","code-4-L5"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --progress\nsyrnck agent pull https://blocks.example.invalid asset received --progress-json\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect absent\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid heads/main main --expect revision:OLD_ID\nsyrnck agent push https://blocks.example.invalid files/MANIFEST asset --expect manifest:OLD_ID\nstd::optional\u003csyrnck::ObjectKey> observed = previous_remote_root; // null means absent\nauto result = remote.push_expected(store, local_root, \"main\", observed,\n                                    operation, transfer_options);\nsyrnck::net::PublicationObserver record = [&](const syrnck::ObjectKey &published) {\n  store.set_ref(\"confirmed-publication\", published);\n};\nremote.push_expected(store, root, \"main\", observed, operation, transfer, record);\nremote.resume(store, session_id, operation, transfer, record);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/parallel-transfers","#/fr/transfer-policy","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/git-assets","#/fr/transfer-sessions"],"kind":"guide","revision":"e189498a25e4"},{"id":"upload-inventories","title":"Inventaires d’envoi paginés","description":"Guide : Inventaires d’envoi paginés.","section":"Transferts","order":610,"parent":"transfers","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"selective-server-upload-inventories\">Inventaires d’envoi sélectifs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories/selective-server-upload-inventories\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inventaires d’envoi sélectifs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les pairs compatibles utilisent automatiquement un inventaire paginé pour conserver les objets d’un upload inachevé, même avant arrivée de son manifeste racine. Le GC hors ligne peut collecter les objets sans rapport tout en gardant déclarés et réutilisés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les anciens baux conservent leur protection globale ; --require-lease exige les baux, pas cette extension optionnelle. Un seul bail ancien actif suffit encore à bloquer tout le GC.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registration-and-acknowledgement\">Inscription et accusé\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories/registration-and-acknowledgement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Inscription et accusé\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le client vérifie la source complète, acquiert un bail avec inventaire et inscrit chaque lot avant sa requête de présence, réutilisés compris. Page de 1..4096 clés uniques strictement ordonnées, ordre continu entre pages, selon la fermeture déterministe existante.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le serveur publie la page immuable puis remplace atomiquement compteur/hash engagés du bail ; succès seulement après les deux écritures synchronisées. Une inscription par lot, pas par objet. Réponse perdue : même page réessayable si octets identiques. Une page engagée doit exister et ne peut être remplacée. Une coupure avant engagement laisse une page non accusée hors préfixe engagé, que le nouvel essai peut valider/engager. Attendre l’accusé avant de se fier à la présence ou d’envoyer ses objets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque page contient un checksum BLAKE3 canonique lié à la précédente. Le bail engage compte et hash final ; GC vérifie toute la chaîne, avec refus de suppression si page manquante, clé/hash/lien/compte altéré. C’est de l’intégrité dans la confiance du filesystem, pas une authentification contre son administrateur malveillant.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gc-expiry-and-cleanup\">GC, expiration et nettoyage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories/gc-expiry-and-cleanup\" aria-label=\"Lien vers GC, expiration et nettoyage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un bail actif à inventaire conserve les objets existants explicitement listés dans ses pages engagées. Le GC les ajoute en flux au bitmap de packs et marques isolées ; enveloppes/hashes/schémas présents sont vérifiés. Les absents sont permis car inscrits avant arrivée. Aucun second inventaire complet d’IDs n’est matérialisé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L’inscription ne prouve pas la fermeture racine complète et ne protège pas implicitement des dépendances non listées. Références, baux de lecture et journaux locaux gardent leurs propres protections. Le CAS final exige toujours fermeture et hashes complets. PUT ordinaires restent immuables mais sans garantie d’inventaire s’ils ne sont pas inscrits. fsck/pack restent stricts et peuvent refuser des métadonnées uploadées incomplètes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Expiration/fencing selon les baux : GC inscrit les expirés et change leurs fences avant suppression. Réactivation = génération neuve, zéro page, hash initial neuf. Resume réinscrit avant présence. Un fence périmé ne peut inscrire ou publier, même après purge/recréation. Les reçus publiés ne gardent pas les payloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Serveur arrêté, leases prune valide métadonnées, chaînes actives et forme complète du dossier avant retrait d’inactifs/générations non référencées. Les actifs restent. Les anciennes générations peuvent avoir du contenu inutilisable, mais doivent conserver une forme régulière sans lien. Nettoyage de fichiers ordinaires directs et dossiers vides uniquement, jamais parcours récursif d’inconnus. removed compte les records de bail, pas pages/dossiers. Reprise sûre après interruption.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protocol-and-disk-format\">Protocole et format disque\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories/protocol-and-disk-format\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Protocole et format disque\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Capacités upload_inventories 1 avec upload_leases 1. Création PUT uploads/ID ajoute inventory true, mode ensuite immuable. Renouveler conserve mode/préfixe ; aucun upgrade silencieux d’ancien bail ni downgrade d’un inventaire actif enregistré.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>PUT uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX accepte exactement fence et objects {kind,id}. Limite 1 Mio, succès 204. Pages contiguës depuis zéro ; reprises identiques d’index antérieurs admises. Fence faux/expiré/libéré : 412 ; bail inconnu : 404 ; page mal formée, conflictuelle, trop grande ou hors ordre : refus. Reprise idempotente bornée habituelle, jamais CAS final automatique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Record bail v2 = anciens champs plus inventory_pages et inventory_hash ; v1 lisible. Record 16 Kio, 4096 records ; au plus 4096 pages par inventaire, soit 16777216 objets déclarés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chemin STORE/upload-inventories/GENERATION/00000000.json ; génération = BLAKE3 du tuple JSON [lease_id,fence]. Page 1 Mio, champs exacts version,lease,fence,index,previous,objects,checksum. Checksum sur ces champs dans cet ordre hors checksum, clés kind puis id. previous initial = BLAKE3 de [&quot;syrnck-upload-inventory-v1&quot;,lease_id,fence], puis checksum précédent. Hash du bail = dernier checksum ou initial si zéro page.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Métadonnées locales ordinaires visibles, sans jetons/URL/clés ; chiffrement des payloads inchangé. Recompiler C++ avec en-têtes correspondants. Formats objets/pointeurs/packs/journal local/ABI C inchangés. Seuls moteurs conscients de l’inventaire peuvent maintenir ces Stores. Maintenance hors ligne, verrouillée, sur un serveur unique ; GC distribué, quotas objet/utilisateur et réplication multi-serveur restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>json\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-json\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"fence\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"64-character lowercase hex token\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"objects\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"kind\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"chunk\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token property\">\"id\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Inventaires d’envoi sélectifs # Les pairs compatibles utilisent automatiquement un inventaire paginé pour conserver les objets d’un upload inachevé, même avant arrivée de son manifeste racine. Le GC hors ligne peut collecter les objets sans rapport tout en gardant déclarés et réutilisés. Les anciens baux conservent leur protection globale ; --require-lease exige les baux, pas cette extension optionnelle. Un seul bail ancien actif suffit encore à bloquer tout le GC. Inscription et accusé # Le client vérifie la source complète, acquiert un bail avec inventaire et inscrit chaque lot avant sa requête de présence, réutilisés compris. Page de 1..4096 clés uniques strictement ordonnées, ordre continu entre pages, selon la fermeture déterministe existante. Le serveur publie la page immuable puis remplace atomiquement compteur/hash engagés du bail ; succès seulement après les deux écritures synchronisées. Une inscription par lot, pas par objet. Réponse perdue : même page réessayable si octets identiques. Une page engagée doit exister et ne peut être remplacée. Une coupure avant engagement laisse une page non accusée hors préfixe engagé, que le nouvel essai peut valider/engager. Attendre l’accusé avant de se fier à la présence ou d’envoyer ses objets. Chaque page contient un checksum BLAKE3 canonique lié à la précédente. Le bail engage compte et hash final ; GC vérifie toute la chaîne, avec refus de suppression si page manquante, clé/hash/lien/compte altéré. C’est de l’intégrité dans la confiance du filesystem, pas une authentification contre son administrateur malveillant. GC, expiration et nettoyage # Un bail actif à inventaire conserve les objets existants explicitement listés dans ses pages engagées. Le GC les ajoute en flux au bitmap de packs et marques isolées ; enveloppes/hashes/schémas présents sont vérifiés. Les absents sont permis car inscrits avant arrivée. Aucun second inventaire complet d’IDs n’est matérialisé. L’inscription ne prouve pas la fermeture racine complète et ne protège pas implicitement des dépendances non listées. Références, baux de lecture et journaux locaux gardent leurs propres protections. Le CAS final exige toujours fermeture et hashes complets. PUT ordinaires restent immuables mais sans garantie d’inventaire s’ils ne sont pas inscrits. fsck/pack restent stricts et peuvent refuser des métadonnées uploadées incomplètes. Expiration/fencing selon les baux : GC inscrit les expirés et change leurs fences avant suppression. Réactivation = génération neuve, zéro page, hash initial neuf. Resume réinscrit avant présence. Un fence périmé ne peut inscrire ou publier, même après purge/recréation. Les reçus publiés ne gardent pas les payloads. Serveur arrêté, leases prune valide métadonnées, chaînes actives et forme complète du dossier avant retrait d’inactifs/générations non référencées. Les actifs restent. Les anciennes générations peuvent avoir du contenu inutilisable, mais doivent conserver une forme régulière sans lien. Nettoyage de fichiers ordinaires directs et dossiers vides uniquement, jamais parcours récursif d’inconnus. removed compte les records de bail, pas pages/dossiers. Reprise sûre après interruption. Protocole et format disque # Capacités upload_inventories 1 avec upload_leases 1. Création PUT uploads/ID ajoute inventory true, mode ensuite immuable. Renouveler conserve mode/préfixe ; aucun upgrade silencieux d’ancien bail ni downgrade d’un inventaire actif enregistré. PUT uploads/ID/inventory/INDEX accepte exactement fence et objects {kind,id}. Limite 1 Mio, succès 204. Pages contiguës depuis zéro ; reprises identiques d’index antérieurs admises. Fence faux/expiré/libéré : 412 ; bail inconnu : 404 ; page mal formée, conflictuelle, trop grande ou hors ordre : refus. Reprise idempotente bornée habituelle, jamais CAS final automatique. Record bail v2 = anciens champs plus inventory_pages et inventory_hash ; v1 lisible. Record 16 Kio, 4096 records ; au plus 4096 pages par inventaire, soit 16777216 objets déclarés. Chemin STORE/upload-inventories/GENERATION/00000000.json ; génération = BLAKE3 du tuple JSON [lease_id,fence]. Page 1 Mio, champs exacts version,lease,fence,index,previous,objects,checksum. Checksum sur ces champs dans cet ordre hors checksum, clés kind puis id. previous initial = BLAKE3 de [\"syrnck-upload-inventory-v1\",lease_id,fence], puis checksum précédent. Hash du bail = dernier checksum ou initial si zéro page. Métadonnées locales ordinaires visibles, sans jetons/URL/clés ; chiffrement des payloads inchangé. Recompiler C++ avec en-têtes correspondants. Formats objets/pointeurs/packs/journal local/ABI C inchangés. Seuls moteurs conscients de l’inventaire peuvent maintenir ces Stores. Maintenance hors ligne, verrouillée, sur un serveur unique ; GC distribué, quotas objet/utilisateur et réplication multi-serveur restent à développer. json Copier { \"fence\" : \"64-character lowercase hex token\" , \"objects\" : [ { \"kind\" : \"chunk\" , \"id\" : \"64-character lowercase hex ID\" } ] } {\"fence\":\"64-character lowercase hex token\",\"objects\":[{\"kind\":\"chunk\",\"id\":\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"}]}","headings":[{"id":"selective-server-upload-inventories","text":"Inventaires d’envoi sélectifs","level":1},{"id":"registration-and-acknowledgement","text":"Inscription et accusé","level":2},{"id":"gc-expiry-and-cleanup","text":"GC, expiration et nettoyage","level":2},{"id":"protocol-and-disk-format","text":"Protocole et format disque","level":2}],"anchors":["L1"],"languages":["json"],"code":"{\"fence\":\"64-character lowercase hex token\",\"objects\":[{\"kind\":\"chunk\",\"id\":\"64-character lowercase hex ID\"}]}","assets":[],"links":[],"kind":"guide","revision":"236110d5392b"},{"id":"usage","title":"Parcours de démarrage","description":"Guide : Parcours de démarrage.","section":"Premiers pas","order":5,"sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"parcours-du-prototype\">Parcours du prototype\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/parcours-du-prototype\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Parcours du prototype\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Les exemples supposent que le binaire \u003ccode>syrnck\u003c/code> compilé est dans le \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code>. Sous PowerShell, on peut aussi appeler son chemin absolu avec \u003ccode>&amp;\u003c/code>. Les identifiants affichés par le CLI sont à réutiliser sans les chevrons des exemples.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier\">Stocker et reconstruire un fichier\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Stocker et reconstruire un fichier\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck put Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck get &lt;identifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck inspect manifest &lt;identifiant-manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code> épingle automatiquement \u003ccode>files/&lt;identifiant-manifeste&gt;\u003c/code>. Réingérer un fichier modifié affiche les octets nouveaux et réutilisés. \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> refuse d&#39;écraser un fichier existant. Pour comparer deux versions déjà ingérées :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck diff &lt;ancien-manifeste> &lt;nouveau-manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les octets réutilisables sont une mesure logique des blocs communs. Les octets effectivement transmis dépendent aussi des autres objets déjà présents sur le destinataire et des doublons au sein du fichier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour compresser et, si le store est chiffré, chiffrer plusieurs blocs en parallèle :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>--workers\u003c/code> accepte 1 à 64 ; 1 reste la valeur par défaut. \u003ccode>--buffer-mib\u003c/code> borne les octets bruts des tâches en attente de publication, à 32 Mio par défaut. La limite doit contenir au moins un bloc maximum et ne pas dépasser 1 024 Mio. Un second plafond limite les tâches à \u003ccode>2 × workers\u003c/code>, même avec de petits blocs fixes. Le tampon de lecture, les buffers encodés, les contextes des codecs et les métadonnées s&#39;ajoutent à ce budget ; il ne s&#39;agit pas d&#39;un plafond sur la RAM du processus.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>stats.peak_in_flight_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>stats.peak_in_flight_blocks\u003c/code> donnent les maxima des tâches en attente ; ils valent zéro sur le chemin séquentiel. Les identifiants et frontières de blocs restent identiques. Les blocs déjà présents sont vérifiés et réutilisés sans recompression ; les doublons encore en attente ne sont encodés qu&#39;une fois. Le gain dépend donc de la quantité de nouvelles données, du disque et du chiffrement.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La bibliothèque C++ reçoit ces réglages dans \u003ccode>IngestOptions\u003c/code>, troisième argument de \u003ccode>Store::ingest\u003c/code>. Son \u003ccode>std::stop_token\u003c/code> permet l&#39;annulation entre lectures, blocs et publications. Une opération native en cours peut finir avant le retour ; les workers sont joints avant de rendre la main. Une erreur ou annulation avant la finalisation empêche la création du manifeste. \u003ccode>ingest\u003c/code> ne modifie aucune référence. Des blocs déjà écrits peuvent rester non référencés, réutilisables au retry ou supprimables par GC. L’interruption du CLI est reliée à l’annulation coopérative. Les préférences d’ingestion s’appliquent aux commandes compatibles ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/settings\">réglages\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le manifeste v1 reste limité à 16 Mio. L&#39;ingestion vérifie sa taille sérialisée pendant la lecture et s&#39;arrête avant de consommer tout un fichier trop fragmenté. Les manifestes paginés restent à développer pour dépasser cette limite.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-de-lectures-verifiees\">Cache de lectures vérifiées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/cache-de-lectures-verifiees\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Cache de lectures vérifiées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Un store garde par défaut jusqu&#39;à \u003cstrong>64 Mio de données décodées\u003c/strong> et \u003cstrong>8 192 empreintes d&#39;objets vérifiés\u003c/strong> en mémoire. Les deux limites sont indépendantes : une donnée peut être évincée alors que son empreinte reste disponible pour un test de présence. Les objets, leurs IDs et les références de rétention gardent le format v1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>À chaque accès, le moteur relit l&#39;enveloppe complète et calcule son empreinte BLAKE3. Il ne réutilise une validation antérieure que si les octets correspondent à une enveloppe déjà déchiffrée, décodée et vérifiée dans ce même handle. Une modification de taille ou de date n&#39;est pas nécessaire pour invalider le cache. Le cache réduit le travail des codecs ; il ne supprime pas cette lecture disque.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;option globale du CLI permet de régler le budget de données décodées ou de désactiver tout le cache :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-4-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --cache-mib 0 get &lt;manifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le budget accepte 0 à 1 024 Mio. Les structures d&#39;index, buffers IO, codecs et pipeline s&#39;ajoutent à ce budget. Le cache disparaît avec le processus ou le handle ; il n&#39;est pas conservé entre deux commandes CLI séparées. Les gains les plus directs concernent les lectures répétées dans un même handle, les serveurs et les références partageant des blocs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La bibliothèque C++ configure ces limites avec \u003ccode>StoreOptions\u003c/code>, quatrième argument du constructeur \u003ccode>Store\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>decoded_cache_bytes = 0\u003c/code> garde seulement les empreintes ; \u003ccode>verified_cache_entries = 0\u003c/code> désactive le cache d&#39;objets. \u003ccode>io_stats()\u003c/code> expose les lectures d&#39;enveloppes réussies, octets lus, décodages complets et hits ; \u003ccode>reset_io_stats()\u003c/code> remet ces compteurs à zéro sans vider les caches. \u003ccode>clear_cache()\u003c/code> efface les caches d&#39;objets et de pages d&#39;index sans supprimer d&#39;objet ni de référence. Les buffers décodés sont nettoyés à l&#39;éviction et à la fermeture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>fsck\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>gc\u003c/code> ignorent les validations et données du cache pour vérifier les objets actuels, leurs dépendances et les hashes complets des fichiers. La validation d&#39;une fermeture avec fichiers intègre maintenant le hash du fichier pendant sa traversée : elle ne parcourt plus tous les blocs une première fois avant de les relire pour ce hash. Un bloc répété dans un fichier reste nécessaire à chaque position dans le hash complet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le cache de pages d&#39;index est indépendant : \u003cstrong>4 Mio\u003c/strong> par défaut, avec \u003ccode>--index-cache-mib 0..1024\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes\u003c/code> en C++. Le répertoire de pages et le filtre restent en mémoire lorsque ce cache est désactivé. L&#39;ouverture et les audits vérifient tout l&#39;index ; les pages relues sont contrôlées contre ce snapshot. Les champs \u003ccode>index_directory_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_filter_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_cached_pages\u003c/code> de \u003ccode>io_stats()\u003c/code> décrivent leurs allocations utiles, hors overhead et buffers temporaires. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/indexes\">détails des index paginés\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"regrouper-les-objets-en-packs\">Regrouper les objets en packs\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/regrouper-les-objets-en-packs\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Regrouper les objets en packs\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck pack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck pack --compact\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-5-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck gc --delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>pack\u003c/code> vérifie le store puis regroupe les nouveaux objets isolés dans des packs immuables indexés. Les packs existants restent inchangés. \u003ccode>--compact\u003c/code> regroupe explicitement tout le stock ; les plafonds de création sont 512 Mio et 65 536 objets par défaut. Les identifiants, références et enveloppes chiffrées sont conservés. Le GC réécrit seulement les packs partiellement morts et retire les packs entièrement morts après validation des versions conservées.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le GC parcourt les inventaires en flux et utilise un bit par objet empaqueté pour le marquage. Son résultat ajoute \u003ccode>packed_mark_bytes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>loose_live_objects\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>peak_pending_objects\u003c/code> : octets utiles du bitmap initial, nombre de clés vivantes absentes des packs et maximum de clés en attente de parcours. Ces compteurs ne représentent pas toute la RAM du processus. Les clés vivantes isolées et les métadonnées restent en mémoire ; voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs/memoire-du-gc\">budgets du GC\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cette maintenance exige le verrou exclusif ; arrêter un serveur qui utilise le même store avant de la lancer. Elle active la configuration physique v2 pour un seul pack, puis v3 pour plusieurs packs ; les anciens binaires refusent les formats qu&#39;ils ne comprennent pas. Les stores existants ne sont pas convertis à l&#39;ouverture. Les index sont lus par pages, avec 1 048 576 objets maximum par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total. Produire de nouveaux packs nécessite de l&#39;espace avant de supprimer les anciens. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/packs\">garanties de publication, reprise et limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"historique-autonome\">Historique autonome\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/historique-autonome\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Historique autonome\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Copier les exclusions Unreal de \u003ccode>examples/unreal.syrnckignore\u003c/code> dans \u003ccode>.syrnckignore\u003c/code>, après les avoir adaptées au projet. Le store natif doit se trouver dans le dossier commité, normalement \u003ccode>.syrnck\u003c/code> à la racine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native status --revision &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native status --combined\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native checkout-plan &lt;identifiant-revision> --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native diff &lt;ancienne-revision> &lt;nouvelle-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L8\" data-line=\"8\">syrnck native log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L9\" data-line=\"9\">syrnck native branch release &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L10\" data-line=\"10\">syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-6-L11\" data-line=\"11\">syrnck native export &lt;identifiant-revision> ../export-release\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les commandes natives utilisent la branche courante, implicitement \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> pour les anciens stores. \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code> choisit une autre branche pour l&#39;opération sans changer cette sélection. \u003ccode>native head\u003c/code> l&#39;affiche ; \u003ccode>native switch-plan release\u003c/code> puis \u003ccode>native switch release\u003c/code> préparent et exécutent un changement de branche récupérable sans déplacer les têtes. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-branches\">contrats du HEAD et des branches natives\u003c/a>. Le commit ordinaire scanne tout le dossier et refuse si un staging existe pour cette branche. L&#39;export exige un nouveau dossier dont le parent existe. Les fichiers doivent être stables pendant la lecture.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour préparer une sélection, puis publier les octets figés même après d&#39;autres modifications du dossier :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native staged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native reset Source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-7-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\"\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>native add .\u003c/code> prépare tout le snapshot candidat, y compris les suppressions. \u003ccode>native reset CHEMIN...\u003c/code> restaure la sélection depuis la base dans le staging ; \u003ccode>native reset\u003c/code> sans chemin efface tout le staging, y compris si la branche a avancé. Ces commandes ne modifient pas les fichiers de travail. Le staging conserve ses blocs contre le GC et le commit publie la branche et consomme l&#39;index atomiquement. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-staging\">contrats du staging natif\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native status\u003c/code> lit les fichiers sélectionnés et compare leur hash complet à \u003ccode>heads/main\u003c/code>, à une autre branche avec \u003ccode>--branch\u003c/code>, ou à un snapshot avec \u003ccode>--revision\u003c/code>. Il n&#39;importe aucun fichier. \u003ccode>native diff\u003c/code> compare les métadonnées de deux snapshots ; ses options textuelles explicites produisent aussi des différences lisibles ; voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/text-diff\">diff textuel\u003c/a>. Les deux renvoient un rapport JSON et acceptent progression/annulation. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-comparison\">contrats de comparaison native\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>native status --combined\u003c/code> renvoie séparément la base vers le staging et le staging vers le dossier de travail. \u003ccode>native checkout-plan RÉVISION\u003c/code> propose les créations/remplacements/suppressions et signale les obstacles sans modifier les fichiers. Sa première politique exige tous les fichiers suivis propres et refuse un staging existant ; les fichiers ignorés restent visibles pour détecter les collisions. \u003ccode>--verify\u003c/code> vérifie aussi toute la fermeture stockée de la cible. Un plan bloqué renvoie son JSON avec le code 1. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspace\">contrats d&#39;inspection du workspace\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour appliquer une révision sur ce workspace propre, en déplaçant la branche choisie et en conservant son ancienne tête :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native checkout &lt;identifiant-revision> --branch main --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native checkout-status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native checkout-recover --progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-8-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les deux dernières commandes sont les alternatives de reprise ou d&#39;annulation après une interruption. Les fichiers sont déplacés séquentiellement sous journal ; la branche, l&#39;archive source et la rétention cible sont publiées ensemble une fois les fichiers vérifiés. Après publication, seule la fin du nettoyage est possible. Le GC et les écritures natives restent bloqués tant que l&#39;opération est en attente. Les sauvegardes et fichiers préparés restent en clair, même si les objets sont chiffrés. Arrêter les éditeurs externes pendant l&#39;opération. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-checkout\">limites, la rétention et les états de récupération du checkout\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour nommer une version commitée et conserver son annotation :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native tags --limit 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L5\" data-line=\"5\">syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect &lt;identifiant-revision>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L6\" data-line=\"6\">syrnck native tag-archive &lt;identifiant-archive> --verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-9-L7\" data-line=\"7\">syrnck native tag-restore &lt;identifiant-archive>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>La création vise HEAD par défaut et n&#39;inclut aucune modification locale non commitée. Un nom existant est refusé. La suppression vérifie l&#39;ID attendu puis archive la cible et l&#39;annotation atomiquement ; le GC conserve cette version même après retrait des autres branches/tags. La restauration garde l&#39;archive et refuse un nom déjà repris. Les commandes de snapshots acceptent les noms non ambigus ainsi que \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>heads/NOM\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>tags/NOM\u003c/code>. Un nom partagé par une branche et un tag exige une forme explicite. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-tags\">contrats des tags, de la pagination et des archives\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour raccourcir l&#39;historique tout en gardant une archive :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-10-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>L&#39;ancienne tête est conservée sous \u003ccode>archive/&lt;ancienne-tête&gt;\u003c/code>. Avant de retirer une archive, utiliser le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/archive-retention\">retrait protégé par sauvegarde\u003c/a>, puis inspecter les racines et prévisualiser le GC :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-11-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-11-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck gc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Les branches, autres archives et pins continuent de protéger leurs données. Après retrait de toutes les racines et nettoyage, les versions supprimées ne sont plus récupérables depuis ce store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"git-et-assets\">Git et assets\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/git-et-assets\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Git et assets\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Dans un dépôt Git existant :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-12-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le store est créé dans le répertoire Git commun sous \u003ccode>syrnck\u003c/code>. Le chemin exact est affiché à l&#39;installation ; \u003ccode>--store\u003c/code> permet ensuite de l&#39;utiliser avec le CLI. La configuration est locale au dépôt et pointe vers le chemin absolu du binaire. Après déplacement du binaire, les chemins des filtres doivent être corrigés dans la configuration Git locale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ajouter par exemple dans \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code> :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>gitattributes\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-gitattributes\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-13-L1\" data-line=\"1\">*.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-13-L2\" data-line=\"2\">*.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Puis utiliser Git :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L1\" data-line=\"1\">git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L2\" data-line=\"2\">git commit -m \"Carte et code\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-14-L3\" data-line=\"3\">git switch -c feature\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le contenu de \u003ccode>Source/Game.cpp\u003c/code> reste dans Git ; \u003ccode>Content/Map.uasset\u003c/code> est représenté dans Git par un pointeur et reconstruit dans le workspace. Pour convertir des fichiers déjà suivis, appliquer les attributs avec \u003ccode>git add --renormalize\u003c/code> aux chemins concernés et examiner le diff avant de commiter. Ne pas superposer filtres LFS et Syrnck sur les mêmes chemins.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour les pointeurs Git LFS déjà commités, utiliser \u003ccode>syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD\u003c/code> puis \u003ccode>syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets\u003c/code>. La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-migration\">migration LFS hors ligne\u003c/a> contrôle le cache SHA-256, importe les blocs et prépare un commit enfant sans modifier HEAD/index/workspace. Elle ajuste les attributs exacts et conserve le catalogue complet avant de créer la branche. Pour convertir également l’ascendance, utiliser la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-lfs-history\">migration de tout l’historique LFS\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les pins \u003ccode>git/&lt;manifeste&gt;\u003c/code> protègent les assets même si un commit disparaît du graphe Git. Leur retrait est manuel dans ce prototype. Git conserve ses propres règles pour l&#39;historique de code, indépendamment du GC Syrnck.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck git retention-scan\u003c/code> inventorie les pointeurs des références, reflogs et index de chaque worktree enregistré sans ouvrir le store. \u003ccode>syrnck git retention\u003c/code> vérifie leurs fichiers complets et prévisualise les pins manquants ; \u003ccode>--apply\u003c/code> les ajoute en une transaction. Les pins non observés sont signalés sans suppression automatique. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-retention\">garanties et limites de la rétention Git\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un transfert explicite par référence de manifeste reste possible :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-15-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git> refs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-15-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/&lt;manifeste> asset-release\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Sur le destinataire, installer le filtre puis récupérer les blocs avant de demander à Git de matérialiser l&#39;asset :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-16-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck git install\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-16-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store &lt;store-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/&lt;manifeste>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Le checkout reste local par défaut. Après \u003ccode>git clone --no-checkout\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck git install\u003c/code>, configurer le serveur de blocs avec \u003ccode>git configure-remote origin URL\u003c/code>, puis activer \u003ccode>git configure-checkout download --remote origin\u003c/code> pour récupérer seulement les objets manquants, avec bail de lecture et vérification complète avant sortie. \u003ccode>git configure-checkout skip\u003c/code> conserve les pointeurs et permet \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>git add\u003c/code> sans télécharger les assets ; \u003ccode>SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1\u003c/code> fournit aussi un override ponctuel sans store ni clé. Un changement de politique ne réécrit pas les fichiers déjà présents, même avec \u003ccode>checkout-index --force\u003c/code>. Pour réhydrater un chemin indexé, préserver les modifications souhaitées, supprimer seulement son fichier pointeur de travail (\u003ccode>Remove-Item -LiteralPath Map.uasset\u003c/code> sous PowerShell), puis lancer \u003ccode>git checkout -- Map.uasset\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>git catalog COMMIT\u003c/code> inspecte les pointeurs sans store ; \u003ccode>git push-assets URL COMMIT\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>git pull-assets URL COMMIT\u003c/code> transfèrent leur fermeture vérifiée en groupe. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-assets\">catalogues, transferts et politiques de checkout Git\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le hook d&#39;envoi s&#39;active explicitement avec \u003ccode>syrnck git configure-remote origin URL_BLOCS\u003c/code> puis \u003ccode>syrnck git install-hook\u003c/code>. Il vérifie et envoie les assets de tous les commits ancêtres avant de laisser Git publier ; une erreur réseau ou des blocs historiques manquants bloque ce push. Les jetons restent dans l&#39;environnement. Les hooks existants ne sont pas remplacés et \u003ccode>core.hooksPath\u003c/code> demande une intégration manuelle. Ce hook local reste contournable avec \u003ccode>--no-verify\u003c/code> ; il ne remplace pas une politique de validation côté serveur Git.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>L&#39;installation active aussi le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/git-filter-process\">filtre résident Git\u003c/a> : une commande Git réutilise le même Store et son cache vérifié pour plusieurs fichiers. Le verrou exclusif reste détenu jusqu&#39;à la fermeture du processus par Git ; les autres commandes visant ce store échouent rapidement pendant ce temps. Les commandes simples \u003ccode>git clean\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>git smudge\u003c/code> restent disponibles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"serveur-et-agent\">Serveur et agent\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/serveur-et-agent\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Serveur et agent\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Créer un store serveur séparé, puis configurer les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-access\">utilisateurs et permissions\u003c/a>, ou définir pour le mode partagé historique un jeton d&#39;au moins 32 caractères dans \u003ccode>SYRNCK_TOKEN\u003c/code>, identique sur les clients autorisés. Ne pas placer le jeton dans l&#39;URL. Le CLI accepte \u003ccode>--token\u003c/code>, mais l&#39;environnement évite sa présence dans la ligne de commande.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-17-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck --store serveur-data init\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-17-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Dans un autre terminal, depuis le projet client :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-18-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Depuis un autre store client initialisé :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native log --branch received\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-19-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck fsck\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>L&#39;agent est une commande ponctuelle. Il ne surveille pas le disque et ne résout pas de conflits. Une interruption laisse des objets réutilisables mais aucune nouvelle référence incomplète. Relancer reprend à partir des objets présents. Une interruption peut laisser des objets non épinglés ; le serveur conserve son verrou pendant son exécution et n&#39;effectue pas de GC en ligne.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>agent sessions\u003c/code> affiche les journaux locaux persistants. \u003ccode>agent resume URL SESSION_ID\u003c/code> reprend la racine et la référence attendue enregistrées, même si la référence source a disparu. Une session inachevée protège sa source complète ou les objets déjà reçus pendant le GC local ; les objets sans lien avec le transfert restent collectables. \u003ccode>agent forget SESSION_ID\u003c/code> abandonne son journal et libère cette protection. \u003ccode>agent prune\u003c/code> prévisualise les reçus terminés ; \u003ccode>agent prune --delete\u003c/code> les supprime après validation de tous les journaux, sans supprimer les sessions inachevées ni les données. Ces commandes locales ne demandent aucun jeton serveur. Une publication distante incertaine est vérifiée sans renvoyer aveuglément le CAS. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-sessions\">garanties des sessions\u003c/a> et la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/selective-retention\">rétention sélective\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les transferts acceptent \u003ccode>--requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>--retries 0..8\u003c/code> (3 par défaut), \u003ccode>--upload-kib-s\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>--download-kib-s\u003c/code> (Kio/s partagés, 0 pour illimité). Les attentes sont annulables ; la publication finale de référence n&#39;est jamais relancée automatiquement. Voir la \u003ca href=\"#/fr/transfer-policy\">politique de reprise et de débit\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Pour un accès distant, publier le serveur derrière un proxy HTTPS. Le client refuse HTTP hors loopback et les redirections. Un certificat d&#39;autorité privé peut être fourni avec \u003ccode>--ca racine.pem\u003c/code>. Le prototype ne fournit pas encore de configuration de déploiement ou de gestion de certificats.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>La \u003ca href=\"#/fr/network-compression\">compression réseau\u003c/a> est négociée automatiquement\nquand des objets manquent. \u003ccode>--compression off\u003c/code> demande des octets bruts et\n\u003ccode>--compression zstd\u003c/code> exige la capacité du serveur pour les objets à transférer.\nLes objets petits ou incompressibles peuvent rester bruts. \u003ccode>serve --no-compression\u003c/code>\ndésactive l&#39;extension. \u003ccode>transferred_bytes\u003c/code> compte les octets logiques réussis ;\n\u003ccode>object_upload_bytes\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>object_download_bytes\u003c/code> comptent les corps HTTP réellement\ntransférés, tentatives répétées comprises. Les cibles de débit portent sur ces\noctets réseau. Le budget inclut les candidats compressés, sans borner toute la RAM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les envois négocient automatiquement les baux persistants quand le serveur les annonce. \u003ccode>agent push ... --require-lease\u003c/code> exige leur prise en charge ; \u003ccode>serve --require-lease --lease-seconds 3600\u003c/code> impose les publications protégées. Après arrêt du serveur, \u003ccode>leases list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>leases release ID\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>leases prune\u003c/code> permettent l&#39;administration. Les nouveaux envois négocient aussi des \u003ca href=\"#/fr/upload-inventories\">inventaires paginés\u003c/a>, enregistrés avant les demandes de présence : le GC protège les objets déclarés et peut collecter les autres. Un ancien bail sans inventaire bloque encore tout le GC serveur. L&#39;expiration autorise la collecte des objets non référencés et la reprise redéclare son inventaire sous un nouveau jeton. \u003ccode>leases prune\u003c/code> nettoie aussi les générations obsolètes. Voir les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-upload-leases\">baux d&#39;envoi, jetons et reçus\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les pulls négocient aussi un \u003ca href=\"#/fr/server-read-leases\">bail de lecture\u003c/a> avant leur première demande de bloc manquant. \u003ccode>agent pull ... --require-read-lease\u003c/code> ou \u003ccode>agent resume ... --require-read-lease\u003c/code> exige cette prise en charge. \u003ccode>serve --read-lease-seconds 3600\u003c/code> règle leur durée. Après arrêt du serveur, les commandes \u003ccode>leases list --read\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>leases release ID --read\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>leases prune --read\u003c/code> administrent ces baux. La racine reste protégée même sans référence tant que le bail est actif ; le client le libère lorsque tous les objets sont locaux, avant la publication de la référence locale.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chiffrement-au-repos\">Chiffrement au repos\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/chiffrement-au-repos\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Chiffrement au repos\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Exemple PowerShell :\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>powershell\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-powershell\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L1\" data-line=\"1\">$env:SYRNCK_KEY = (&amp; syrnck keygen)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-20-L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Conserver cette clé dans un gestionnaire de secrets avant de fermer le terminal. Le même \u003ccode>SYRNCK_KEY\u003c/code> doit être fourni à chaque ouverture du store chiffré. Un store non chiffré doit s&#39;ouvrir sans cette variable. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/backups\">sauvegardes\u003c/a> peuvent recopier les objets dans un nouveau store avec une autre clé ; la rotation en place, la révocation distribuée et la récupération des clés restent à développer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque store peut employer sa propre clé. L&#39;agent transmet le contenu déchiffré via le transport sécurisé ; le store destinataire le chiffre avec sa propre clé. Il ne s&#39;agit pas de chiffrement de bout en bout contre le serveur.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interface-c\">Interface C\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/interface-c\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Interface C\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compiler les cibles \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>syrnck_ffi_static\u003c/code> produit une DLL sous Windows, une bibliothèque dynamique sous Linux/macOS et une bibliothèque statique. Inclure \u003ccode>include/syrnck.h\u003c/code>, utiliser des chemins UTF-8 et fermer les handles. Les fonctions retournent un code d&#39;état ; \u003ccode>syrnck_last_error()\u003c/code> donne le dernier message du thread. Le handle ne doit pas être utilisé simultanément par plusieurs threads.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le manifeste JSON expose les identifiants et tailles des blocs. L&#39;ABI C 1 fournit aussi leur présence, lecture/importation vérifiée, la fermeture des dépendances, les pins et la vérification du store. Le client réseau reste dans la bibliothèque C++. Voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/go-binding\">binding Go\u003c/a> pour une copie différentielle entre stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ignore-example\">Exemple d’exclusions de projet Unreal\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/usage/ignore-example\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Exemple d’exclusions de projet Unreal\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Adapter cet exemple au projet avant de créer des commits. Les fichiers de travail exclus ne sont pas conservés dans le snapshot natif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>gitignore\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-gitignore\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L1\" data-line=\"1\"># Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L2\" data-line=\"2\">**/Binaries/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L3\" data-line=\"3\">**/DerivedDataCache/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L4\" data-line=\"4\">**/Intermediate/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L5\" data-line=\"5\">**/Saved/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L6\" data-line=\"6\">/.vs/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L7\" data-line=\"7\">/.idea/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L8\" data-line=\"8\">/.tools/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L9\" data-line=\"9\">/target/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L10\" data-line=\"10\">*.sln\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L11\" data-line=\"11\">*.suo\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L12\" data-line=\"12\">*.key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L13\" data-line=\"13\">*.pem\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L14\" data-line=\"14\"># Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-21-L15\" data-line=\"15\"># Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Parcours du prototype # Les exemples supposent que le binaire syrnck compilé est dans le PATH . Sous PowerShell, on peut aussi appeler son chemin absolu avec & . Les identifiants affichés par le CLI sont à réutiliser sans les chevrons des exemples. Stocker et reconstruire un fichier # text Copier syrnck init syrnck put Content/Map.uasset syrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset syrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> syrnck fsck put épingle automatiquement files/\u003cidentifiant-manifeste> . Réingérer un fichier modifié affiche les octets nouveaux et réutilisés. get refuse d'écraser un fichier existant. Pour comparer deux versions déjà ingérées : text Copier syrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste> Les octets réutilisables sont une mesure logique des blocs communs. Les octets effectivement transmis dépendent aussi des autres objets déjà présents sur le destinataire et des doublons au sein du fichier. Pour compresser et, si le store est chiffré, chiffrer plusieurs blocs en parallèle : text Copier syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 --workers accepte 1 à 64 ; 1 reste la valeur par défaut. --buffer-mib borne les octets bruts des tâches en attente de publication, à 32 Mio par défaut. La limite doit contenir au moins un bloc maximum et ne pas dépasser 1 024 Mio. Un second plafond limite les tâches à 2 × workers , même avec de petits blocs fixes. Le tampon de lecture, les buffers encodés, les contextes des codecs et les métadonnées s'ajoutent à ce budget ; il ne s'agit pas d'un plafond sur la RAM du processus. stats.peak_in_flight_bytes et stats.peak_in_flight_blocks donnent les maxima des tâches en attente ; ils valent zéro sur le chemin séquentiel. Les identifiants et frontières de blocs restent identiques. Les blocs déjà présents sont vérifiés et réutilisés sans recompression ; les doublons encore en attente ne sont encodés qu'une fois. Le gain dépend donc de la quantité de nouvelles données, du disque et du chiffrement. La bibliothèque C++ reçoit ces réglages dans IngestOptions , troisième argument de Store::ingest . Son std::stop_token permet l'annulation entre lectures, blocs et publications. Une opération native en cours peut finir avant le retour ; les workers sont joints avant de rendre la main. Une erreur ou annulation avant la finalisation empêche la création du manifeste. ingest ne modifie aucune référence. Des blocs déjà écrits peuvent rester non référencés, réutilisables au retry ou supprimables par GC. L’interruption du CLI est reliée à l’annulation coopérative. Les préférences d’ingestion s’appliquent aux commandes compatibles ; voir les réglages . Le manifeste v1 reste limité à 16 Mio. L'ingestion vérifie sa taille sérialisée pendant la lecture et s'arrête avant de consommer tout un fichier trop fragmenté. Les manifestes paginés restent à développer pour dépasser cette limite. Cache de lectures vérifiées # Un store garde par défaut jusqu'à 64 Mio de données décodées et 8 192 empreintes d'objets vérifiés en mémoire. Les deux limites sont indépendantes : une donnée peut être évincée alors que son empreinte reste disponible pour un test de présence. Les objets, leurs IDs et les références de rétention gardent le format v1. À chaque accès, le moteur relit l'enveloppe complète et calcule son empreinte BLAKE3. Il ne réutilise une validation antérieure que si les octets correspondent à une enveloppe déjà déchiffrée, décodée et vérifiée dans ce même handle. Une modification de taille ou de date n'est pas nécessaire pour invalider le cache. Le cache réduit le travail des codecs ; il ne supprime pas cette lecture disque. L'option globale du CLI permet de régler le budget de données décodées ou de désactiver tout le cache : text Copier syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin Le budget accepte 0 à 1 024 Mio. Les structures d'index, buffers IO, codecs et pipeline s'ajoutent à ce budget. Le cache disparaît avec le processus ou le handle ; il n'est pas conservé entre deux commandes CLI séparées. Les gains les plus directs concernent les lectures répétées dans un même handle, les serveurs et les références partageant des blocs. La bibliothèque C++ configure ces limites avec StoreOptions , quatrième argument du constructeur Store . decoded_cache_bytes = 0 garde seulement les empreintes ; verified_cache_entries = 0 désactive le cache d'objets. io_stats() expose les lectures d'enveloppes réussies, octets lus, décodages complets et hits ; reset_io_stats() remet ces compteurs à zéro sans vider les caches. clear_cache() efface les caches d'objets et de pages d'index sans supprimer d'objet ni de référence. Les buffers décodés sont nettoyés à l'éviction et à la fermeture. fsck et gc ignorent les validations et données du cache pour vérifier les objets actuels, leurs dépendances et les hashes complets des fichiers. La validation d'une fermeture avec fichiers intègre maintenant le hash du fichier pendant sa traversée : elle ne parcourt plus tous les blocs une première fois avant de les relire pour ce hash. Un bloc répété dans un fichier reste nécessaire à chaque position dans le hash complet. Le cache de pages d'index est indépendant : 4 Mio par défaut, avec --index-cache-mib 0..1024 et StoreOptions::pack_index_cache_bytes en C++. Le répertoire de pages et le filtre restent en mémoire lorsque ce cache est désactivé. L'ouverture et les audits vérifient tout l'index ; les pages relues sont contrôlées contre ce snapshot. Les champs index_directory_bytes , index_filter_bytes , index_cached_bytes , index_cached_pages de io_stats() décrivent leurs allocations utiles, hors overhead et buffers temporaires. Voir les détails des index paginés . Regrouper les objets en packs # text Copier syrnck pack syrnck pack --compact syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck gc syrnck gc --delete pack vérifie le store puis regroupe les nouveaux objets isolés dans des packs immuables indexés. Les packs existants restent inchangés. --compact regroupe explicitement tout le stock ; les plafonds de création sont 512 Mio et 65 536 objets par défaut. Les identifiants, références et enveloppes chiffrées sont conservés. Le GC réécrit seulement les packs partiellement morts et retire les packs entièrement morts après validation des versions conservées. Le GC parcourt les inventaires en flux et utilise un bit par objet empaqueté pour le marquage. Son résultat ajoute packed_mark_bytes , loose_live_objects et peak_pending_objects : octets utiles du bitmap initial, nombre de clés vivantes absentes des packs et maximum de clés en attente de parcours. Ces compteurs ne représentent pas toute la RAM du processus. Les clés vivantes isolées et les métadonnées restent en mémoire ; voir les budgets du GC . Cette maintenance exige le verrou exclusif ; arrêter un serveur qui utilise le même store avant de la lancer. Elle active la configuration physique v2 pour un seul pack, puis v3 pour plusieurs packs ; les anciens binaires refusent les formats qu'ils ne comprennent pas. Les stores existants ne sont pas convertis à l'ouverture. Les index sont lus par pages, avec 1 048 576 objets maximum par pack, 1 024 packs et 4 194 304 objets empaquetés au total. Produire de nouveaux packs nécessite de l'espace avant de supprimer les anciens. Voir les garanties de publication, reprise et limites . Historique autonome # Copier les exclusions Unreal de examples/unreal.syrnckignore dans .syrnckignore , après les avoir adaptées au projet. Le store natif doit se trouver dans le dossier commité, normalement .syrnck à la racine. text Copier syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\" syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\" syrnck native status --progress syrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native status --combined syrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify syrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision> syrnck native log syrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\" syrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release Les commandes natives utilisent la branche courante, implicitement main pour les anciens stores. --branch choisit une autre branche pour l'opération sans changer cette sélection. native head l'affiche ; native switch-plan release puis native switch release préparent et exécutent un changement de branche récupérable sans déplacer les têtes. Voir les contrats du HEAD et des branches natives . Le commit ordinaire scanne tout le dossier et refuse si un staging existe pour cette branche. L'export exige un nouveau dossier dont le parent existe. Les fichiers doivent être stables pendant la lecture. Pour préparer une sélection, puis publier les octets figés même après d'autres modifications du dossier : text Copier syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress syrnck native staged syrnck native reset Source syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\" native add . prépare tout le snapshot candidat, y compris les suppressions. native reset CHEMIN... restaure la sélection depuis la base dans le staging ; native reset sans chemin efface tout le staging, y compris si la branche a avancé. Ces commandes ne modifient pas les fichiers de travail. Le staging conserve ses blocs contre le GC et le commit publie la branche et consomme l'index atomiquement. Voir les contrats du staging natif . native status lit les fichiers sélectionnés et compare leur hash complet à heads/main , à une autre branche avec --branch , ou à un snapshot avec --revision . Il n'importe aucun fichier. native diff compare les métadonnées de deux snapshots ; ses options textuelles explicites produisent aussi des différences lisibles ; voir le diff textuel . Les deux renvoient un rapport JSON et acceptent progression/annulation. Voir les contrats de comparaison native . native status --combined renvoie séparément la base vers le staging et le staging vers le dossier de travail. native checkout-plan RÉVISION propose les créations/remplacements/suppressions et signale les obstacles sans modifier les fichiers. Sa première politique exige tous les fichiers suivis propres et refuse un staging existant ; les fichiers ignorés restent visibles pour détecter les collisions. --verify vérifie aussi toute la fermeture stockée de la cible. Un plan bloqué renvoie son JSON avec le code 1. Voir les contrats d'inspection du workspace . Pour appliquer une révision sur ce workspace propre, en déplaçant la branche choisie et en conservant son ancienne tête : text Copier syrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress Les deux dernières commandes sont les alternatives de reprise ou d'annulation après une interruption. Les fichiers sont déplacés séquentiellement sous journal ; la branche, l'archive source et la rétention cible sont publiées ensemble une fois les fichiers vérifiés. Après publication, seule la fin du nettoyage est possible. Le GC et les écritures natives restent bloqués tant que l'opération est en attente. Les sauvegardes et fichiers préparés restent en clair, même si les objets sont chiffrés. Arrêter les éditeurs externes pendant l'opération. Voir les limites, la rétention et les états de récupération du checkout . Pour nommer une version commitée et conserver son annotation : text Copier syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify syrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive> La création vise HEAD par défaut et n'inclut aucune modification locale non commitée. Un nom existant est refusé. La suppression vérifie l'ID attendu puis archive la cible et l'annotation atomiquement ; le GC conserve cette version même après retrait des autres branches/tags. La restauration garde l'archive et refuse un nom déjà repris. Les commandes de snapshots acceptent les noms non ambigus ainsi que HEAD , heads/NOM et tags/NOM . Un nom partagé par une branche et un tag exige une forme explicite. Voir les contrats des tags, de la pagination et des archives . Pour raccourcir l'historique tout en gardant une archive : text Copier syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\" syrnck refs syrnck gc L'ancienne tête est conservée sous archive/\u003cancienne-tête> . Avant de retirer une archive, utiliser le retrait protégé par sauvegarde , puis inspecter les racines et prévisualiser le GC : text Copier syrnck refs syrnck gc Les branches, autres archives et pins continuent de protéger leurs données. Après retrait de toutes les racines et nettoyage, les versions supprimées ne sont plus récupérables depuis ce store. Git et assets # Dans un dépôt Git existant : text Copier syrnck git install Le store est créé dans le répertoire Git commun sous syrnck . Le chemin exact est affiché à l'installation ; --store permet ensuite de l'utiliser avec le CLI. La configuration est locale au dépôt et pointe vers le chemin absolu du binaire. Après déplacement du binaire, les chemins des filtres doivent être corrigés dans la configuration Git locale. Ajouter par exemple dans .gitattributes : gitattributes Copier *.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text *.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text Puis utiliser Git : text Copier git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp git commit -m \"Carte et code\" git switch -c feature Le contenu de Source/Game.cpp reste dans Git ; Content/Map.uasset est représenté dans Git par un pointeur et reconstruit dans le workspace. Pour convertir des fichiers déjà suivis, appliquer les attributs avec git add --renormalize aux chemins concernés et examiner le diff avant de commiter. Ne pas superposer filtres LFS et Syrnck sur les mêmes chemins. Pour les pointeurs Git LFS déjà commités, utiliser syrnck git lfs-preview HEAD puis syrnck git migrate-lfs HEAD --branch converted-assets . La migration LFS hors ligne contrôle le cache SHA-256, importe les blocs et prépare un commit enfant sans modifier HEAD/index/workspace. Elle ajuste les attributs exacts et conserve le catalogue complet avant de créer la branche. Pour convertir également l’ascendance, utiliser la migration de tout l’historique LFS . Les pins git/\u003cmanifeste> protègent les assets même si un commit disparaît du graphe Git. Leur retrait est manuel dans ce prototype. Git conserve ses propres règles pour l'historique de code, indépendamment du GC Syrnck. syrnck git retention-scan inventorie les pointeurs des références, reflogs et index de chaque worktree enregistré sans ouvrir le store. syrnck git retention vérifie leurs fichiers complets et prévisualise les pins manquants ; --apply les ajoute en une transaction. Les pins non observés sont signalés sans suppression automatique. Voir les garanties et limites de la rétention Git . Un transfert explicite par référence de manifeste reste possible : text Copier syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release Sur le destinataire, installer le filtre puis récupérer les blocs avant de demander à Git de matérialiser l'asset : text Copier syrnck git install syrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste> Le checkout reste local par défaut. Après git clone --no-checkout et syrnck git install , configurer le serveur de blocs avec git configure-remote origin URL , puis activer git configure-checkout download --remote origin pour récupérer seulement les objets manquants, avec bail de lecture et vérification complète avant sortie. git configure-checkout skip conserve les pointeurs et permet git status / git add sans télécharger les assets ; SYRNCK_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 fournit aussi un override ponctuel sans store ni clé. Un changement de politique ne réécrit pas les fichiers déjà présents, même avec checkout-index --force . Pour réhydrater un chemin indexé, préserver les modifications souhaitées, supprimer seulement son fichier pointeur de travail ( Remove-Item -LiteralPath Map.uasset sous PowerShell), puis lancer git checkout -- Map.uasset . git catalog COMMIT inspecte les pointeurs sans store ; git push-assets URL COMMIT et git pull-assets URL COMMIT transfèrent leur fermeture vérifiée en groupe. Voir les catalogues, transferts et politiques de checkout Git . Le hook d'envoi s'active explicitement avec syrnck git configure-remote origin URL_BLOCS puis syrnck git install-hook . Il vérifie et envoie les assets de tous les commits ancêtres avant de laisser Git publier ; une erreur réseau ou des blocs historiques manquants bloque ce push. Les jetons restent dans l'environnement. Les hooks existants ne sont pas remplacés et core.hooksPath demande une intégration manuelle. Ce hook local reste contournable avec --no-verify ; il ne remplace pas une politique de validation côté serveur Git. L'installation active aussi le filtre résident Git : une commande Git réutilise le même Store et son cache vérifié pour plusieurs fichiers. Le verrou exclusif reste détenu jusqu'à la fermeture du processus par Git ; les autres commandes visant ce store échouent rapidement pendant ce temps. Les commandes simples git clean et git smudge restent disponibles. Serveur et agent # Créer un store serveur séparé, puis configurer les utilisateurs et permissions , ou définir pour le mode partagé historique un jeton d'au moins 32 caractères dans SYRNCK_TOKEN , identique sur les clients autorisés. Ne pas placer le jeton dans l'URL. Le CLI accepte --token , mais l'environnement évite sa présence dans la ligne de commande. text Copier syrnck --store serveur-data init syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437 Dans un autre terminal, depuis le projet client : text Copier syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main Depuis un autre store client initialisé : text Copier syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received syrnck native log --branch received syrnck fsck L'agent est une commande ponctuelle. Il ne surveille pas le disque et ne résout pas de conflits. Une interruption laisse des objets réutilisables mais aucune nouvelle référence incomplète. Relancer reprend à partir des objets présents. Une interruption peut laisser des objets non épinglés ; le serveur conserve son verrou pendant son exécution et n'effectue pas de GC en ligne. agent sessions affiche les journaux locaux persistants. agent resume URL SESSION_ID reprend la racine et la référence attendue enregistrées, même si la référence source a disparu. Une session inachevée protège sa source complète ou les objets déjà reçus pendant le GC local ; les objets sans lien avec le transfert restent collectables. agent forget SESSION_ID abandonne son journal et libère cette protection. agent prune prévisualise les reçus terminés ; agent prune --delete les supprime après validation de tous les journaux, sans supprimer les sessions inachevées ni les données. Ces commandes locales ne demandent aucun jeton serveur. Une publication distante incertaine est vérifiée sans renvoyer aveuglément le CAS. Voir les garanties des sessions et la rétention sélective . Les transferts acceptent --requests 4 --transfer-buffer-mib 64 , --retries 0..8 (3 par défaut), --upload-kib-s et --download-kib-s (Kio/s partagés, 0 pour illimité). Les attentes sont annulables ; la publication finale de référence n'est jamais relancée automatiquement. Voir la politique de reprise et de débit . Pour un accès distant, publier le serveur derrière un proxy HTTPS. Le client refuse HTTP hors loopback et les redirections. Un certificat d'autorité privé peut être fourni avec --ca racine.pem . Le prototype ne fournit pas encore de configuration de déploiement ou de gestion de certificats. La compression réseau est négociée automatiquement quand des objets manquent. --compression off demande des octets bruts et --compression zstd exige la capacité du serveur pour les objets à transférer. Les objets petits ou incompressibles peuvent rester bruts. serve --no-compression désactive l'extension. transferred_bytes compte les octets logiques réussis ; object_upload_bytes et object_download_bytes comptent les corps HTTP réellement transférés, tentatives répétées comprises. Les cibles de débit portent sur ces octets réseau. Le budget inclut les candidats compressés, sans borner toute la RAM. Les envois négocient automatiquement les baux persistants quand le serveur les annonce. agent push ... --require-lease exige leur prise en charge ; serve --require-lease --lease-seconds 3600 impose les publications protégées. Après arrêt du serveur, leases list , leases release ID et leases prune permettent l'administration. Les nouveaux envois négocient aussi des inventaires paginés , enregistrés avant les demandes de présence : le GC protège les objets déclarés et peut collecter les autres. Un ancien bail sans inventaire bloque encore tout le GC serveur. L'expiration autorise la collecte des objets non référencés et la reprise redéclare son inventaire sous un nouveau jeton. leases prune nettoie aussi les générations obsolètes. Voir les baux d'envoi, jetons et reçus . Les pulls négocient aussi un bail de lecture avant leur première demande de bloc manquant. agent pull ... --require-read-lease ou agent resume ... --require-read-lease exige cette prise en charge. serve --read-lease-seconds 3600 règle leur durée. Après arrêt du serveur, les commandes leases list --read , leases release ID --read et leases prune --read administrent ces baux. La racine reste protégée même sans référence tant que le bail est actif ; le client le libère lorsque tous les objets sont locaux, avant la publication de la référence locale. Chiffrement au repos # Exemple PowerShell : powershell Copier $env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen) syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset Conserver cette clé dans un gestionnaire de secrets avant de fermer le terminal. Le même SYRNCK_KEY doit être fourni à chaque ouverture du store chiffré. Un store non chiffré doit s'ouvrir sans cette variable. Les sauvegardes peuvent recopier les objets dans un nouveau store avec une autre clé ; la rotation en place, la révocation distribuée et la récupération des clés restent à développer. Chaque store peut employer sa propre clé. L'agent transmet le contenu déchiffré via le transport sécurisé ; le store destinataire le chiffre avec sa propre clé. Il ne s'agit pas de chiffrement de bout en bout contre le serveur. Interface C # Compiler les cibles syrnck_ffi et syrnck_ffi_static produit une DLL sous Windows, une bibliothèque dynamique sous Linux/macOS et une bibliothèque statique. Inclure include/syrnck.h , utiliser des chemins UTF-8 et fermer les handles. Les fonctions retournent un code d'état ; syrnck_last_error() donne le dernier message du thread. Le handle ne doit pas être utilisé simultanément par plusieurs threads. Le manifeste JSON expose les identifiants et tailles des blocs. L'ABI C 1 fournit aussi leur présence, lecture/importation vérifiée, la fermeture des dépendances, les pins et la vérification du store. Le client réseau reste dans la bibliothèque C++. Voir le binding Go pour une copie différentielle entre stores. Exemple d’exclusions de projet Unreal # Adapter cet exemple au projet avant de créer des commits. Les fichiers de travail exclus ne sont pas conservés dans le snapshot natif. gitignore Copier # Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits. **/Binaries/ **/DerivedDataCache/ **/Intermediate/ **/Saved/ /.vs/ /.idea/ /.tools/ /target/ *.sln *.suo *.key *.pem # Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file. # Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case. syrnck init syrnck put Content/Map.uasset syrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset syrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> syrnck fsck syrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste> syrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32 syrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin syrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin syrnck pack syrnck pack --compact syrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536 syrnck gc syrnck gc --delete syrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\" syrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\" syrnck native status --progress syrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native status --combined syrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify syrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision> syrnck native log syrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\" syrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release syrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress syrnck native staged syrnck native reset Source syrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\" syrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress syrnck native checkout-status syrnck native checkout-recover --progress syrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress syrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\" syrnck native tags --limit 100 syrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify syrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD syrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision> syrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify syrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive> syrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\" syrnck refs syrnck gc syrnck refs syrnck gc syrnck git install *.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text *.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text git add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp git commit -m \"Carte et code\" git switch -c feature syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs syrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release syrnck git install syrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste> syrnck --store serveur-data init syrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437 syrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main syrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received syrnck native log --branch received syrnck fsck $env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen) syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted syrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset # Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits. **/Binaries/ **/DerivedDataCache/ **/Intermediate/ **/Saved/ /.vs/ /.idea/ /.tools/ /target/ *.sln *.suo *.key *.pem # Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file. # Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.","headings":[{"id":"parcours-du-prototype","text":"Parcours du prototype","level":1},{"id":"stocker-et-reconstruire-un-fichier","text":"Stocker et reconstruire un fichier","level":2},{"id":"cache-de-lectures-verifiees","text":"Cache de lectures vérifiées","level":2},{"id":"regrouper-les-objets-en-packs","text":"Regrouper les objets en packs","level":2},{"id":"historique-autonome","text":"Historique autonome","level":2},{"id":"git-et-assets","text":"Git et assets","level":2},{"id":"serveur-et-agent","text":"Serveur et agent","level":2},{"id":"chiffrement-au-repos","text":"Chiffrement au repos","level":2},{"id":"interface-c","text":"Interface C","level":2},{"id":"ignore-example","text":"Exemple d’exclusions de projet Unreal","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","code-2-L1","code-3-L1","code-4-L1","code-4-L2","code-5-L1","code-5-L2","code-5-L3","code-5-L4","code-5-L5","code-6-L1","code-6-L2","code-6-L3","code-6-L4","code-6-L5","code-6-L6","code-6-L7","code-6-L8","code-6-L9","code-6-L10","code-6-L11","code-7-L1","code-7-L2","code-7-L3","code-7-L4","code-8-L1","code-8-L2","code-8-L3","code-8-L4","code-9-L1","code-9-L2","code-9-L3","code-9-L4","code-9-L5","code-9-L6","code-9-L7","code-10-L1","code-10-L2","code-10-L3","code-11-L1","code-11-L2","code-12-L1","code-13-L1","code-13-L2","code-14-L1","code-14-L2","code-14-L3","code-15-L1","code-15-L2","code-16-L1","code-16-L2","code-17-L1","code-17-L2","code-18-L1","code-19-L1","code-19-L2","code-19-L3","code-20-L1","code-20-L2","code-20-L3","code-21-L1","code-21-L2","code-21-L3","code-21-L4","code-21-L5","code-21-L6","code-21-L7","code-21-L8","code-21-L9","code-21-L10","code-21-L11","code-21-L12","code-21-L13","code-21-L14","code-21-L15"],"languages":["text","gitattributes","powershell","gitignore"],"code":"syrnck init\nsyrnck put Content/Map.uasset\nsyrnck get \u003cidentifiant-manifeste> copie.uasset\nsyrnck inspect manifest \u003cidentifiant-manifeste>\nsyrnck fsck\nsyrnck diff \u003cancien-manifeste> \u003cnouveau-manifeste>\nsyrnck put gros-fichier.bin --workers 4 --buffer-mib 32\nsyrnck --cache-mib 32 put fichier.bin\nsyrnck --cache-mib 0 get \u003cmanifeste> nouvelle-copie.bin\nsyrnck pack\nsyrnck pack --compact\nsyrnck pack --max-mib 512 --max-objects 65536\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck gc --delete\nsyrnck native commit --message \"Import du projet\"\nsyrnck native commit --message \"Modification de la carte\"\nsyrnck native status --progress\nsyrnck native status --revision \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native status --combined\nsyrnck native checkout-plan \u003cidentifiant-revision> --verify\nsyrnck native diff \u003cancienne-revision> \u003cnouvelle-revision>\nsyrnck native log\nsyrnck native branch release \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native commit --branch release --message \"Préparation de la release\"\nsyrnck native export \u003cidentifiant-revision> ../export-release\nsyrnck native add Source Content/Maps --progress\nsyrnck native staged\nsyrnck native reset Source\nsyrnck native commit --staged --message \"Cartes sélectionnées\"\nsyrnck native checkout \u003cidentifiant-revision> --branch main --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-status\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --progress\nsyrnck native checkout-recover --abort --progress\nsyrnck native tag release/v1 HEAD -m \"Version de livraison\"\nsyrnck native tags --limit 100\nsyrnck native tag-show release/v1 --verify\nsyrnck native diff tags/release/v1 HEAD\nsyrnck native tag-delete release/v1 --expect \u003cidentifiant-revision>\nsyrnck native tag-archive \u003cidentifiant-archive> --verify\nsyrnck native tag-restore \u003cidentifiant-archive>\nsyrnck native detach-history --message \"Nouveau départ\"\nsyrnck refs\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck refs\nsyrnck gc\nsyrnck git install\n*.uasset filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\n*.umap filter=syrnck -diff -merge -text\ngit add .gitattributes Content/Map.uasset Source/Game.cpp\ngit commit -m \"Carte et code\"\ngit switch -c feature\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git> refs\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git> agent push https://blocs.exemple.test git/\u003cmanifeste> asset-release\nsyrnck git install\nsyrnck --store \u003cstore-git-destinataire> agent pull https://blocs.exemple.test asset-release git/\u003cmanifeste>\nsyrnck --store serveur-data init\nsyrnck --store serveur-data serve --listen 127.0.0.1:7437\nsyrnck agent push http://127.0.0.1:7437 heads/main main\nsyrnck agent pull http://127.0.0.1:7437 main heads/received\nsyrnck native log --branch received\nsyrnck fsck\n$env:SYRNCK_KEY = (& syrnck keygen)\nsyrnck --store donnees-chiffrees init --encrypted\nsyrnck --store donnees-chiffrees put Content/Map.uasset\n# Review for your project; copy to .syrnckignore for native commits.\n**/Binaries/\n**/DerivedDataCache/\n**/Intermediate/\n**/Saved/\n/.vs/\n/.idea/\n/.tools/\n/target/\n*.sln\n*.suo\n*.key\n*.pem\n# Retain Content, Config, Source, Plugins and the .uproject file.\n# Some plugins need prebuilt Binaries: adjust the exclusions in that case.","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/settings","#/fr/indexes","#/fr/packs/memoire-du-gc","#/fr/packs","#/fr/native-branches","#/fr/native-staging","#/fr/text-diff","#/fr/native-comparison","#/fr/native-workspace","#/fr/native-checkout","#/fr/native-tags","#/fr/archive-retention","#/fr/git-lfs-migration","#/fr/git-lfs-history","#/fr/git-retention","#/fr/git-assets","#/fr/git-filter-process","#/fr/server-access","#/fr/transfer-sessions","#/fr/selective-retention","#/fr/transfer-policy","#/fr/network-compression","#/fr/upload-inventories","#/fr/server-upload-leases","#/fr/server-read-leases","#/fr/backups","#/fr/go-binding"],"kind":"guide","revision":"eb0cc6ebe4c5"},{"id":"working-index","title":"Index de travail persistant","description":"Guide : Index de travail persistant.","section":"Stockage","order":630,"parent":"storage","sourceRevision":"1","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"persistent-native-working-index\">Index de travail persistant\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/working-index/persistent-native-working-index\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Index de travail persistant\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>native status --indexed active un cache local consultatif de hashes vérifiés, évitant de relire les assets inchangés lorsque le système de fichiers fournit identité et métadonnées nécessaires. Le statut ordinaire continue à tout hasher. Le mode combined garde les comparaisons branche/index/workspace, y compris un index périmé. Ce cache décrit les fichiers observés ; il est distinct de l’index immuable de préparation et ne prépare/commite jamais de contenu. JSON anglais dans les deux langues.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>text\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-text\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">syrnck native status --indexed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">syrnck native index-clear\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"reuse-and-fallback\">Réutilisation et repli\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/working-index/reuse-and-fallback\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Réutilisation et repli\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Une entrée contient chemin, taille, hash complet BLAKE3, mode et digest d’identité/métadonnées filesystem. Des handles interrogent les métadonnées avant/après observation. Réutilisation seulement si identité, mtime, ctime, taille et mode/attributs correspondent à l’observation vérifiée. Dates récentes ou futures contournent le cache avec une garde prudente de deux secondes ; écritures remettant taille/mtime et remplacement de fichier restent détectables par les autres champs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Windows : volumes locaux fixes NTFS/ReFS, identité de volume et fichier 128 bits, dates d’écriture/changement et attributs. Linux : familles ext, XFS, Btrfs, tmpfs, device/inode, mtime/ctime nanoseconde et mode. macOS : APFS local avec les mêmes champs. Autres plateformes/filesystems : hash complet.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>UNC/réseau, filesystem inconnu, échec d’identité et écriture récente repliquent sur lecture complète. Sous Windows, les handles de métadonnées refusent le partage en écriture : un writer actif pouvant différer ses dates ne peut réutiliser un ancien hash. Une identité changeant pendant observation fait échouer et impose un nouvel essai. Ignores, refus de liens/reparse et namespace portable restent appliqués.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>C’est une accélération stat fondée sur la confiance du cache local, pas un audit ni snapshot atomique. Falsification délibérée des métadonnées, horloge manipulée et remplacements arbitraires concurrents sont hors hypothèses. Le statut ordinaire compare indépendamment les octets. Checkout/reset/stash/commit/staging gardent leurs lectures complètes et ne s’autorisent jamais à remplacer un fichier sur ce cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"persistence-and-lifetime\">Persistance et durée de vie\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/working-index/persistence-and-lifetime\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Persistance et durée de vie\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Le pointeur working-index.json du Store lie un chunk CAS à l’empreinte de la racine canonique du workspace. Le chunk d’index est compressé et chiffré avec les objets habituels ; le pointeur ne contient que des IDs. Il est remplacé atomiquement après scan complet et dernier observateur. Une mutation du pointeur par callback bloque la publication au lieu de l’écraser. L’annulation peut laisser un chunk non référencé mais jamais un pointeur partiel.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ce cache jetable n’est pas une racine de versions. Le GC peut collecter ses objets ; sauvegarde/réplication logiques n’en dépendent pas. Objet absent, enveloppe corrompue ou document mal formé entraînent un nouveau scan/cache. Un pointeur lié, non ordinaire ou trop grand est refusé plutôt qu’écrasé. index-clear retire seulement le pointeur ; le GC reprend ensuite les chunks. Un ancien objet abîmé n’est pas réparé et reste visible à l’audit complet jusqu’à sa collecte.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Un workspace mis en cache par Store ; une autre racine canonique le reconstruit. Objet limité à 16 Mio : si trop d’entrées, le scan reste complet mais seule une partie est mémorisée, avec truncated true. Snapshots, maps, JSON et autres allocations s’ajoutent à ce budget.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reports-c-and-measurement\">Rapports et API C++\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/working-index/reports-c-and-measurement\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Rapports et API C++\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les rapports gardent leurs champs et ajoutent working_index : previous = missing/ready/invalid/other_workspace ; hashed_files/reused_files ; supported_files (identités stables, même trop récentes pour réutilisation) ; cached_files/truncated/object. hashed_bytes compte seulement les octets de travail lus, zéro pour une réutilisation, hors métadonnées stockées. Le mode indexé peut écrire et émettre publishing ; l’ordinaire reste en lecture seule.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque requête indexée énumère encore dossiers et ignores pour voir ajouts/retraits/renommages/règles. L’\u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-watch\">observateur résident\u003c/a> utilise notifications et audits avec indices bornés et récupération de débordement. Son inventaire complet en session permet de remplacer un fichier/sous-arbre pour éditions, créations, retraits, renommages, types et ignores imbriqués sans énumérer ailleurs. Règles racine, perte de continuité et sélection ambiguë imposent une relecture complète des octets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Linux étend les inscriptions de dossiers créés/importés avant les indices ciblés ; retraits/déplacements d’anciens dossiers observés imposent encore un scan complet pour couvrir les trous inotify. Un checkpoint persistant est optionnel ; historique rejouable d’événements et réconciliation incrémentale native des retraits/renommages restent distincts. Les \u003ca href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi\">sessions C/Go\u003c/a> offrent cette observation native/polling et conservent les indices pendant les attentes, avec réouverture de Store à chaque requête. Les gains dépendent des fichiers et du filesystem ; aucun débit de corpus Unreal n’est garanti.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>cpp\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-cpp\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token macro property\">\u003cspan class=\"token directive-hash\">#\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token directive keyword\">include\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">&lt;syrnck/working_index.hpp>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> report \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">indexed_native_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> revision\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> combined \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">indexed_native_workspace_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> project\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token string\">\"main\"\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">auto\u003c/span> cleared \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> syrnck\u003cspan class=\"token double-colon punctuation\">::\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token function\">clear_native_working_index\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","text":"Index de travail persistant # native status --indexed active un cache local consultatif de hashes vérifiés, évitant de relire les assets inchangés lorsque le système de fichiers fournit identité et métadonnées nécessaires. Le statut ordinaire continue à tout hasher. Le mode combined garde les comparaisons branche/index/workspace, y compris un index périmé. Ce cache décrit les fichiers observés ; il est distinct de l’index immuable de préparation et ne prépare/commite jamais de contenu. JSON anglais dans les deux langues. text Copier syrnck native status --indexed syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION syrnck native index-clear Réutilisation et repli # Une entrée contient chemin, taille, hash complet BLAKE3, mode et digest d’identité/métadonnées filesystem. Des handles interrogent les métadonnées avant/après observation. Réutilisation seulement si identité, mtime, ctime, taille et mode/attributs correspondent à l’observation vérifiée. Dates récentes ou futures contournent le cache avec une garde prudente de deux secondes ; écritures remettant taille/mtime et remplacement de fichier restent détectables par les autres champs. Windows : volumes locaux fixes NTFS/ReFS, identité de volume et fichier 128 bits, dates d’écriture/changement et attributs. Linux : familles ext, XFS, Btrfs, tmpfs, device/inode, mtime/ctime nanoseconde et mode. macOS : APFS local avec les mêmes champs. Autres plateformes/filesystems : hash complet. UNC/réseau, filesystem inconnu, échec d’identité et écriture récente repliquent sur lecture complète. Sous Windows, les handles de métadonnées refusent le partage en écriture : un writer actif pouvant différer ses dates ne peut réutiliser un ancien hash. Une identité changeant pendant observation fait échouer et impose un nouvel essai. Ignores, refus de liens/reparse et namespace portable restent appliqués. C’est une accélération stat fondée sur la confiance du cache local, pas un audit ni snapshot atomique. Falsification délibérée des métadonnées, horloge manipulée et remplacements arbitraires concurrents sont hors hypothèses. Le statut ordinaire compare indépendamment les octets. Checkout/reset/stash/commit/staging gardent leurs lectures complètes et ne s’autorisent jamais à remplacer un fichier sur ce cache. Persistance et durée de vie # Le pointeur working-index.json du Store lie un chunk CAS à l’empreinte de la racine canonique du workspace. Le chunk d’index est compressé et chiffré avec les objets habituels ; le pointeur ne contient que des IDs. Il est remplacé atomiquement après scan complet et dernier observateur. Une mutation du pointeur par callback bloque la publication au lieu de l’écraser. L’annulation peut laisser un chunk non référencé mais jamais un pointeur partiel. Ce cache jetable n’est pas une racine de versions. Le GC peut collecter ses objets ; sauvegarde/réplication logiques n’en dépendent pas. Objet absent, enveloppe corrompue ou document mal formé entraînent un nouveau scan/cache. Un pointeur lié, non ordinaire ou trop grand est refusé plutôt qu’écrasé. index-clear retire seulement le pointeur ; le GC reprend ensuite les chunks. Un ancien objet abîmé n’est pas réparé et reste visible à l’audit complet jusqu’à sa collecte. Un workspace mis en cache par Store ; une autre racine canonique le reconstruit. Objet limité à 16 Mio : si trop d’entrées, le scan reste complet mais seule une partie est mémorisée, avec truncated true. Snapshots, maps, JSON et autres allocations s’ajoutent à ce budget. Rapports et API C++ # Les rapports gardent leurs champs et ajoutent working_index : previous = missing/ready/invalid/other_workspace ; hashed_files/reused_files ; supported_files (identités stables, même trop récentes pour réutilisation) ; cached_files/truncated/object. hashed_bytes compte seulement les octets de travail lus, zéro pour une réutilisation, hors métadonnées stockées. Le mode indexé peut écrire et émettre publishing ; l’ordinaire reste en lecture seule. Chaque requête indexée énumère encore dossiers et ignores pour voir ajouts/retraits/renommages/règles. L’ observateur résident utilise notifications et audits avec indices bornés et récupération de débordement. Son inventaire complet en session permet de remplacer un fichier/sous-arbre pour éditions, créations, retraits, renommages, types et ignores imbriqués sans énumérer ailleurs. Règles racine, perte de continuité et sélection ambiguë imposent une relecture complète des octets. Linux étend les inscriptions de dossiers créés/importés avant les indices ciblés ; retraits/déplacements d’anciens dossiers observés imposent encore un scan complet pour couvrir les trous inotify. Un checkpoint persistant est optionnel ; historique rejouable d’événements et réconciliation incrémentale native des retraits/renommages restent distincts. Les sessions C/Go offrent cette observation native/polling et conservent les indices pendant les attentes, avec réouverture de Store à chaque requête. Les gains dépendent des fichiers et du filesystem ; aucun débit de corpus Unreal n’est garanti. cpp Copier # include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp> auto report = syrnck :: indexed_native_status ( store , project , revision , operation ) ; auto combined = syrnck :: indexed_native_workspace_status ( store , project , \"main\" , operation ) ; auto cleared = syrnck :: clear_native_working_index ( store , operation ) ; syrnck native status --indexed syrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json syrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION syrnck native index-clear #include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp> auto report = syrnck::indexed_native_status(store, project, revision, operation); auto combined = syrnck::indexed_native_workspace_status(store, project, \"main\", operation); auto cleared = syrnck::clear_native_working_index(store, operation);","headings":[{"id":"persistent-native-working-index","text":"Index de travail persistant","level":1},{"id":"reuse-and-fallback","text":"Réutilisation et repli","level":2},{"id":"persistence-and-lifetime","text":"Persistance et durée de vie","level":2},{"id":"reports-c-and-measurement","text":"Rapports et API C++","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4"],"languages":["text","cpp"],"code":"syrnck native status --indexed\nsyrnck native status --indexed --combined --progress-json\nsyrnck native status --indexed --revision REVISION\nsyrnck native index-clear\n#include \u003csyrnck/working_index.hpp>\nauto report = syrnck::indexed_native_status(store, project, revision, operation);\nauto combined = syrnck::indexed_native_workspace_status(store, project, \"main\", operation);\nauto cleared = syrnck::clear_native_working_index(store, operation);","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/native-watch","#/fr/workspace-abi"],"kind":"guide","revision":"f7c12f7d5c5f"},{"id":"workspace-abi","title":"Observation embarquée en C et Go","description":"Guide : Observation embarquée en C et Go.","section":"API","order":640,"parent":"integrations","sourceRevision":"7","locale":"fr","html":"\u003ch1 id=\"embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go\">Observation embarquée en C et Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Observation embarquée en C et Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h1>\u003cp>SyrnckWorkspace dans l’en-tête C et Workspace dans le binding Go exposent le watcher résident et son inventaire complet. Ils partagent notifications Windows/Linux, repli polling et observations ciblées/complètes de native watch. Ce sont des symboles additifs ABI 1 : utiliser ensemble en-tête, binding et bibliothèque de la même version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifetime-and-locking\">Durée de vie et verrouillage\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/lifetime-and-locking\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Durée de vie et verrouillage\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Créer d’abord Store et workspace. Pour le contexte principal, le Store est strictement sous la racine. Une session liée utilise un Store disjoint et exige son dossier enregistré exact. Les chemins sont résolus à l’ouverture. Ouvrir la session avant son premier statut : les notifications sont déjà armées au retour. Elle garde identité de racine, watcher et inventaire mémoire, sans handle Store, clé ou verrou Store.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Ouvrir le Store seulement pour le statut, puis le fermer avant consommation du résultat/attente. Après changement, le rouvrir pour une nouvelle requête. Premier statut, invalidation explicite et récupération relisent tous les octets choisis ; indices natifs ordinaires ciblent des préfixes fichier/sous-arbre, tandis que le polling énumère avec le cache de hashes jetable. Planifier des audits complets indépendants, par exemple toutes les cinq minutes via Invalidate : le silence des notifications ne prouve pas l’absence de changements. Fermer explicitement la session à l’arrêt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Sérialiser chaque handle, fermeture comprise. Go protège workspace par mutex et Store pendant statut dans l’ordre workspace puis Store. Garder un Store ouvert pendant Wait conserve inutilement son verrou exclusif.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Chaque batch consommé par Wait reste accumulé jusqu’à statut réussi ; plusieurs attentes ou résultat ignoré ne perdent pas les indices. Une annulation native invalide la continuité même au milieu d’un batch : le prochain statut relit tout. Les chemins sont dédupliqués ; dépasser le nombre configuré ou le budget comptable de 1 Mio impose un scan complet. Attente/statut/JSON en échec invalident aussi l’inventaire. Les indices ne sont effacés qu’après sérialisation réussie. Une identité de racine changée impose fermeture/réouverture, jamais adoption silencieuse.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les événements arrivent de façon asynchrone ; le statut prend un batch prêt, donc peut manquer une notification encore en transit. Attendre les indices pertinents ou invalider pour une comparaison complète synchrone. Ces observations consultatives n’autorisent aucune opération destructive à sauter son propre contrôle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"c-calls-and-output-ownership\">Appels C et propriété des sorties\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/c-calls-and-output-ownership\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Appels C et propriété des sorties\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>workspace_open arme l’observation ; options NULL choisissent natif avec repli, 4096 indices et 65536 inscriptions de dossiers. wait_json attend 0..60000 ms, conserve le batch et rend ses indices. status_json collecte un batch immédiat et traite tous les indices accumulés avec un Store ouvert par l’appelant. invalidate impose une lecture complète suivante ; close libère ressources/inventaire et accepte NULL.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>SyrnckWorkspaceOptions contient polling (0/1), max_paths et max_directories (1..65536). En C, limites zéro invalides, NULL pour tous les défauts. En Go, chaque limite zéro sélectionne son défaut.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Les deux sorties JSON sont des char* alloués et une longueur incluant NUL, à libérer exactement une fois avec syrnck_string_free, même si inutiles. Plafond 128 Mio plus NUL. Pas de requête de taille/réexécution pour ces appels avec état. En échec, pointeur/longueur fournis deviennent NULL/zéro ; copier le diagnostic du thread avant un autre appel ABI. Aucun ancien statut n’est présenté comme courant.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Wait rend version 1, backend windows/inotify/polling, paths, full_scan, overflow, polling. Il décrit cette attente, tandis que d’anciens batches peuvent encore être retenus. Le statut garde le schéma ordinaire/combiné, compteurs d’index et d’observation compris ; observation.full_scan révèle les replis.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Modes de sélection : 0 branche, NULL suit HEAD nommé ou détaché ; 1 sélecteur de révision requis et résolu ; 2 statut combiné, branche ou NULL. Chaîne explicite vide refusée. Branche inconnue = base vide. Le Store doit être celui de l’ouverture ; checkout actif et HEAD lié à un autre workspace refusés. Le statut peut écrire son cache jetable, sans ingestion, révision, index préparé ou référence publiés.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>c\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-c\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L2\" data-line=\"2\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">char\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>json \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L3\" data-line=\"3\">\u003cspan class=\"token class-name\">size_t\u003c/span> length \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">int\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_workspace_status_json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L6\" data-line=\"6\">\u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_store_close\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L8\" data-line=\"8\">    \u003cspan class=\"token function\">consume_status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> length \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* consume while allocation is alive */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L9\" data-line=\"9\">    \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_string_free\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L10\" data-line=\"10\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"L11\" data-line=\"11\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress\">Annulation coopérative et progression C\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Annulation coopérative et progression C\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les appels existants délèguent aux versions *_ex sans source/callback. cancellation_create/request/close gèrent une source à usage unique. workspace_open_ex et wait_json_ex acceptent une source nullable, y compris pour inscription/récupération récursive. status_json_ex accepte SyrnckOperation nullable avec source, callback synchrone et pointeur utilisateur.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Request est idempotent et concurrent avec l’opération ou d’autres demandes. Tous les utilisateurs/demandeurs doivent revenir avant close ; aucun reset, créer une nouvelle source. Request NULL échoue ; close NULL réussit. Le workspace ne garde pas la source.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Une annulation observée rend SYRNCK_CANCELLED (3), sortie NULL et longueur zéro ; ouverture annulée sans handle. Wait/statut annulés invalident la session, imposant ensuite une observation complète. Une demande après le dernier contrôle peut laisser un succès. IO, métadonnées et encodage JSON peuvent terminer avant le prochain point coopératif, sans délai maximal garanti. Le cache jetable peut être modifié/vidé et des objets collectables rester, mais pas les références, index, données conservées ou fichiers de travail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le callback C reçoit SyrnckProgress avec numéro de phase stable, chemin emprunté et longueur, compte d’octets, total optionnel, indicateur complet et blocs tamponnés. Le chemin n’est pas forcément terminé par NUL ; copier ce qui doit survivre. Aucun événement/pointeur callback/utilisateur n’est retenu. Ne jamais réentrer/fermer Store ou workspace, lancer une exception ou dérouler l’ABI depuis ce callback ; demander cancellation_request y est permis. Les callbacks de progression ne sont pas encore exposés par l’API context Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>c\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-c\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L1\" data-line=\"1\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">static\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">void\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">stop_after_first_chunk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">const\u003c/span> SyrnckProgress \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">void\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>user\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L2\" data-line=\"2\">    \u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>event\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">->\u003c/span>phase \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">==\u003c/span> SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;&amp;\u003c/span> event\u003cspan class=\"token operator\">->\u003c/span>bytes \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">>=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1024\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">1024\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L3\" data-line=\"3\">        \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_cancellation_request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>SyrnckCancellation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>user\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L4\" data-line=\"4\">\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L5\" data-line=\"5\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L6\" data-line=\"6\">SyrnckOperation operation \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">{\u003c/span>cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> stop_after_first_chunk\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> cancellation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L7\" data-line=\"7\">\u003cspan class=\"token keyword\">int\u003c/span> result \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">=\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token function\">syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">(\u003c/span>workspace\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> store\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token constant\">NULL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token number\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L8\" data-line=\"8\">                                            \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>operation\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>json\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"token operator\">&amp;\u003c/span>length\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"token punctuation\">;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L9\" data-line=\"9\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">/* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-2-L10\" data-line=\"10\">\u003cspan class=\"token comment\">   threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"go-usage\">Utilisation Go\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/go-usage\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Utilisation Go\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>OpenWorkspace, OpenWorkspaceContext, Wait, Status, StatusContext, Invalidate et Close encapsulent ces opérations. Wait rend WorkspaceChanges typé ; Status rend json.RawMessage pour conserver tous les champs. WorkspaceSelection vide suit HEAD ; Branch remplace la branche, Revision choisit un snapshot exact/résolu et Combined sépare index/travail. Revision exclut les deux autres. Close est idempotent et les appels après fermeture rendent ErrWorkspaceClosed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Go valide UTF-8/NUL, normalise les options zéro, sérialise les appels, copie la sortie en mémoire Go, libère le natif et lit l’erreur sur le même thread OS. Wait accepte de zéro à une minute et transmet l’annulation à une seule attente native. Le natif contrôle l’arrêt environ toutes les 50 ms lorsqu’inactif ; polling devient exploitable à l’échéance. Inscription et hashing sont aussi coopératifs. Les méthodes sans Context utilisent context.Background pour compatibilité.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Le statut natif 3 devient context.Canceled ou context.DeadlineExceeded. Le callback de demande est arrêté/joint avant libération de sa source ; une source neuve par appel empêche l’annulation tardive d’un appel suivant. Le résultat natif achevé prime sur une annulation après son dernier contrôle ; un contexte expiré avant exécution ne démarre pas l’opération.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Wait/StatusContext peuvent abandonner l’attente de sérialisation workspace/Store à l’expiration. Ordre workspace puis Store, avec ordre stable des Stores pour copie différentielle. Close attend encore les appels actifs : les annuler avant fermeture. Ce pont context concerne seulement ouverture/attente/statut de workspace ; les autres méthodes Store gardent leur comportement propre.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code-block\">\u003cdiv class=\"code-toolbar\">\u003cspan>go\u003c/span>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" data-copy-code>Copier\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-go\">\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L1\" data-line=\"1\">w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L2\" data-line=\"2\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L3\" data-line=\"3\">defer w.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L4\" data-line=\"4\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L5\" data-line=\"5\">s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L6\" data-line=\"6\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L7\" data-line=\"7\">report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true})\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L8\" data-line=\"8\">closeErr := s.Close()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L9\" data-line=\"9\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L10\" data-line=\"10\">if closeErr != nil { return closeErr }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L11\" data-line=\"11\">if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L12\" data-line=\"12\"> \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L13\" data-line=\"13\">changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L14\" data-line=\"14\">if err != nil { return err }\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L15\" data-line=\"15\">// Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"code-line\" id=\"code-3-L16\" data-line=\"16\">_ = changes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch2 id=\"scope-and-validation\">Portée\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/scope-and-validation\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Portée\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>C’est une observation consultative, pas un snapshot verrouillé ou un agent de transfert. Elle ne conserve pas les versions intermédiaires, ne maintient pas d’historique rejouable et n’expose pas le journal de checkpoint du CLI. Un redémarrage recrée l’inventaire et refait un scan complet. Causalité et transferts sont gérés au-dessus de cet observateur ; voir le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller\">contrôleur BEP continu\u003c/a> et ses limites de dossiers, conflits et service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registered-contexts\">Contextes enregistrés\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/registered-contexts\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Contextes enregistrés\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Utiliser \u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex(directory, store_path, name, options, cancellation, out)\u003c/code> pour un workspace lié enregistré. Avant chaque\nstatut, sélectionner le même contexte sur le Store ouvert par l’appelant via\n\u003ccode>syrnck_store_use_workspace(store, name)\u003c/code>. NULL ou vide sélectionne le principal.\nUn nom inconnu échoue sans changer la sélection ; une session/contexte différent\néchoue sans sortie périmée. Ne pas changer de sélection dans un callback de\nprogression. Symboles additifs exigeant une bibliothèque ABI 1 reconstruite adaptée.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Go utilise \u003ccode>WorkspaceOptions{Name: &quot;edit&quot;}\u003c/code> à l’ouverture puis\n\u003ccode>store.UseWorkspace(&quot;edit&quot;)\u003c/code> avant statut. La session ne garde ni Store, clé ou\nverrou. Les notifications liées surveillent prudemment refs/config externes ;\nles audits complets périodiques restent nécessaires. Enregistrement et\nrestauration portable sont exposés par le \u003ca href=\"#/fr/native-workspaces\">CLI et C++\u003c/a>,\npas par des appels d’administration C/Go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-workspace-actions\">Actions natives de workspace protégées\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/native-workspace-actions\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Actions natives de workspace protégées\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>L’ABI C enrichie expose \u003ccode>workspace_native_state_json_ex\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>workspace_capture_json_ex\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>workspace_checkout_plan_json_ex\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>workspace_checkout_owned_json_ex\u003c/code> et \u003ccode>workspace_recover_owned_json_ex\u003c/code>.\nLes déclarations distribuées donnent les signatures complètes. Chaque appel\nvérifie le chemin canonique exact du Store, le contexte enregistré et le dossier\nde la session. Même un autre Store valide est refusé.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Lire l’état d’abord : cible sélectionnée, référence réelle, révision typée ou\nnull, HEAD, matérialisation, présence de staging et statut du checkout du\ncontexte. Cette lecture ne reprend rien. \u003ccode>SyrnckNativeExpected\u003c/code> contient cible,\nrévision nullable et ID nullable de l’objet de sélection partielle. NULL exige\nl’absence, ce n’est pas un joker. Capture, plan et checkout refusent toute cible,\nrévision ou politique modifiée avant mutation. Ne pas actualiser l’attente d’une\nancienne opération uniquement pour la faire passer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Capture conserve les fichiers sélectionnés par la transaction de commit native.\nElle respecte fichiers ignorés, chemins non matérialisés, HEAD détaché et auteurs,\net refuse staging/checkout en cours. Message limité à 64 Kio. Plan vérifie la\nfermeture cible et décrit sa faisabilité sans déplacer de référence ni modifier\nles octets de travail. Un plan bloqué est un rapport réussi avec \u003ccode>ready:false\u003c/code>.\nCheckout recontrôle tout et n’applique qu’à un workspace sélectionné propre,\nvia le journal récupérable natif et un propriétaire explicite de 64 caractères\nhexadécimaux. La barrière de version de lecteur existante s’applique.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Avant un checkout contrôlé par le service, conserver durablement propriétaire,\ncandidat et entrée causale exacts. La reprise exige un journal actuellement en\ncours avec ces propriétaire, candidat, cible sélectionnée, contexte et dossier.\nJournal manuel/étranger ou absence de journal sont refusés. La reprise n’exige\npas la clé privée ayant signé une cible déjà préparée ; objet et signature sont\nconservés sans modification.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Propriété des sorties allouées et ordre de verrouillage workspace puis Store\nrestent communs. Les tentatives de mutation invalident la continuité ; le prochain\nStatus relit tous les octets sélectionnés. Annulation native coopérative ; une\npublication réussie l’emporte sur une annulation tardive. Un statut non nul peut\nsuivre une publication ou laisser un journal reprenable : relire l’état et\nétablir le résultat avant toute nouvelle tentative. Une sortie vide en erreur\nne prouve aucune absence de modification. Fermer le Store avant l’attente réseau\nou des notifications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Go expose \u003ccode>NativeState\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>NativeStateContext\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Capture\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>CaptureContext\u003c/code>,\n\u003ccode>PlanCheckout\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>PlanCheckoutContext\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CheckoutOwned\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>CheckoutOwnedContext\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwned\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>RecoverOwnedContext\u003c/code> sur Workspace. \u003ccode>state.Expected()\u003c/code> transporte\nl’attente exacte cible/révision/sélection. Capture rend l’ID ; plan, checkout et\nreprise rendent le \u003ccode>json.RawMessage\u003c/code> canonique. Les variantes Context gardent\nla passerelle d’annulation par appel. Elles n’implémentent pas le contrôleur BEP\nrésident et n’annoncent pas automatiquement un dossier de travail à jour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_checkout_status_json_ex(workspace, store, transaction, operation, out, length)\u003c/code> lit une transaction 64-hex exacte par le décodeur du\ncœur, sans reprise ni publication. Un reçu terminé rend pending=false et\nstate=committed avec propriétaire/source/cible/branche/contexte/dossier originaux.\nLa cible retenue correspondante et la liaison au workspace sont vérifiées ; la\nlecture d’un reçu terminé ne compare pas les arbres historiques. Ces faits\nprouvent une application passée, pas la propreté actuelle. Go l’expose par\n\u003ccode>CheckoutStatusContext\u003c/code>. Utiliser un propriétaire durable unique par application,\nconserver les entrées causales avant checkout et capturer les éditions locales\nultérieures après reconnaissance de la base terminée.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-checkout-edits\">Préservation explicite des éditions actives\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/active-checkout-edits\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Préservation explicite des éditions actives\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ccode>syrnck_workspace_recover_owned_preserving_json_ex\u003c/code> garde le contrat exact\nStore/workspace/contexte/cible/candidat/propriétaire de la reprise ordinaire.\nIl archive explicitement les éditions de fichiers ordinaires, crée des copies de\nconflit exclusives et reprend le journal strict original. La reprise par défaut\nreste stricte. Go expose \u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreserving\u003c/code> et\n\u003ccode>RecoverOwnedPreservingContext\u003c/code> ; C++ fournit\n\u003ccode>Store::recover_checkout_owned_preserving_files\u003c/code>. Le rapport JSON ajoute\n\u003ccode>preserved_edits\u003c/code> : chemin source, chemin de copie et ID de révision archivée.\nEn erreur de publication, relire état natif et descripteurs conservés. Voir la\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits\">politique et ses limites\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incremental-capture\">Réutilisation des captures de session\u003ca class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#/fr/workspace-abi/incremental-capture\" aria-label=\"Lien vers Réutilisation des captures de session\">#\u003c/a>\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Les captures répétées réutilisent leurs manifestes à empreintes locales fiables\ninchangées, avec inventaire complet et vérification de toute la fermeture. Le\nJSON ajoute les compteurs sources. Redémarrage, invalidation et échec natif\neffacent cette preuve ; l’index jetable de statut ne la remplace jamais. Voir\n\u003ca href=\"#/fr/performance/incremental-capture\">capture et repli\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>\n","text":"Observation embarquée en C et Go # SyrnckWorkspace dans l’en-tête C et Workspace dans le binding Go exposent le watcher résident et son inventaire complet. Ils partagent notifications Windows/Linux, repli polling et observations ciblées/complètes de native watch. Ce sont des symboles additifs ABI 1 : utiliser ensemble en-tête, binding et bibliothèque de la même version. Durée de vie et verrouillage # Créer d’abord Store et workspace. Pour le contexte principal, le Store est strictement sous la racine. Une session liée utilise un Store disjoint et exige son dossier enregistré exact. Les chemins sont résolus à l’ouverture. Ouvrir la session avant son premier statut : les notifications sont déjà armées au retour. Elle garde identité de racine, watcher et inventaire mémoire, sans handle Store, clé ou verrou Store. Ouvrir le Store seulement pour le statut, puis le fermer avant consommation du résultat/attente. Après changement, le rouvrir pour une nouvelle requête. Premier statut, invalidation explicite et récupération relisent tous les octets choisis ; indices natifs ordinaires ciblent des préfixes fichier/sous-arbre, tandis que le polling énumère avec le cache de hashes jetable. Planifier des audits complets indépendants, par exemple toutes les cinq minutes via Invalidate : le silence des notifications ne prouve pas l’absence de changements. Fermer explicitement la session à l’arrêt. Sérialiser chaque handle, fermeture comprise. Go protège workspace par mutex et Store pendant statut dans l’ordre workspace puis Store. Garder un Store ouvert pendant Wait conserve inutilement son verrou exclusif. Chaque batch consommé par Wait reste accumulé jusqu’à statut réussi ; plusieurs attentes ou résultat ignoré ne perdent pas les indices. Une annulation native invalide la continuité même au milieu d’un batch : le prochain statut relit tout. Les chemins sont dédupliqués ; dépasser le nombre configuré ou le budget comptable de 1 Mio impose un scan complet. Attente/statut/JSON en échec invalident aussi l’inventaire. Les indices ne sont effacés qu’après sérialisation réussie. Une identité de racine changée impose fermeture/réouverture, jamais adoption silencieuse. Les événements arrivent de façon asynchrone ; le statut prend un batch prêt, donc peut manquer une notification encore en transit. Attendre les indices pertinents ou invalider pour une comparaison complète synchrone. Ces observations consultatives n’autorisent aucune opération destructive à sauter son propre contrôle. Appels C et propriété des sorties # workspace_open arme l’observation ; options NULL choisissent natif avec repli, 4096 indices et 65536 inscriptions de dossiers. wait_json attend 0..60000 ms, conserve le batch et rend ses indices. status_json collecte un batch immédiat et traite tous les indices accumulés avec un Store ouvert par l’appelant. invalidate impose une lecture complète suivante ; close libère ressources/inventaire et accepte NULL. SyrnckWorkspaceOptions contient polling (0/1), max_paths et max_directories (1..65536). En C, limites zéro invalides, NULL pour tous les défauts. En Go, chaque limite zéro sélectionne son défaut. Les deux sorties JSON sont des char* alloués et une longueur incluant NUL, à libérer exactement une fois avec syrnck_string_free, même si inutiles. Plafond 128 Mio plus NUL. Pas de requête de taille/réexécution pour ces appels avec état. En échec, pointeur/longueur fournis deviennent NULL/zéro ; copier le diagnostic du thread avant un autre appel ABI. Aucun ancien statut n’est présenté comme courant. Wait rend version 1, backend windows/inotify/polling, paths, full_scan, overflow, polling. Il décrit cette attente, tandis que d’anciens batches peuvent encore être retenus. Le statut garde le schéma ordinaire/combiné, compteurs d’index et d’observation compris ; observation.full_scan révèle les replis. Modes de sélection : 0 branche, NULL suit HEAD nommé ou détaché ; 1 sélecteur de révision requis et résolu ; 2 statut combiné, branche ou NULL. Chaîne explicite vide refusée. Branche inconnue = base vide. Le Store doit être celui de l’ouverture ; checkout actif et HEAD lié à un autre workspace refusés. Le statut peut écrire son cache jetable, sans ingestion, révision, index préparé ou référence publiés. c Copier /* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */ char * json = NULL ; size_t length = 0 ; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json ( workspace , store , NULL , 0 , & json , & length ) ; /* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */ syrnck_store_close ( store ) ; if ( result == 0 ) { consume_status ( json , length - 1 ) ; /* consume while allocation is alive */ syrnck_string_free ( json ) ; } /* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */ Annulation coopérative et progression C # Les appels existants délèguent aux versions *_ex sans source/callback. cancellation_create/request/close gèrent une source à usage unique. workspace_open_ex et wait_json_ex acceptent une source nullable, y compris pour inscription/récupération récursive. status_json_ex accepte SyrnckOperation nullable avec source, callback synchrone et pointeur utilisateur. Request est idempotent et concurrent avec l’opération ou d’autres demandes. Tous les utilisateurs/demandeurs doivent revenir avant close ; aucun reset, créer une nouvelle source. Request NULL échoue ; close NULL réussit. Le workspace ne garde pas la source. Une annulation observée rend SYRNCK_CANCELLED (3), sortie NULL et longueur zéro ; ouverture annulée sans handle. Wait/statut annulés invalident la session, imposant ensuite une observation complète. Une demande après le dernier contrôle peut laisser un succès. IO, métadonnées et encodage JSON peuvent terminer avant le prochain point coopératif, sans délai maximal garanti. Le cache jetable peut être modifié/vidé et des objets collectables rester, mais pas les références, index, données conservées ou fichiers de travail. Le callback C reçoit SyrnckProgress avec numéro de phase stable, chemin emprunté et longueur, compte d’octets, total optionnel, indicateur complet et blocs tamponnés. Le chemin n’est pas forcément terminé par NUL ; copier ce qui doit survivre. Aucun événement/pointeur callback/utilisateur n’est retenu. Ne jamais réentrer/fermer Store ou workspace, lancer une exception ou dérouler l’ABI depuis ce callback ; demander cancellation_request y est permis. Les callbacks de progression ne sont pas encore exposés par l’API context Go. c Copier static void stop_after_first_chunk ( const SyrnckProgress * event , void * user ) { if ( event -> phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event -> bytes >= 1024 * 1024 ) syrnck_cancellation_request ( ( SyrnckCancellation * ) user ) ; } /* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */ SyrnckOperation operation = { cancellation , stop_after_first_chunk , cancellation } ; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex ( workspace , store , NULL , 0 , & operation , & json , & length ) ; /* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */ Utilisation Go # OpenWorkspace, OpenWorkspaceContext, Wait, Status, StatusContext, Invalidate et Close encapsulent ces opérations. Wait rend WorkspaceChanges typé ; Status rend json.RawMessage pour conserver tous les champs. WorkspaceSelection vide suit HEAD ; Branch remplace la branche, Revision choisit un snapshot exact/résolu et Combined sépare index/travail. Revision exclut les deux autres. Close est idempotent et les appels après fermeture rendent ErrWorkspaceClosed. Go valide UTF-8/NUL, normalise les options zéro, sérialise les appels, copie la sortie en mémoire Go, libère le natif et lit l’erreur sur le même thread OS. Wait accepte de zéro à une minute et transmet l’annulation à une seule attente native. Le natif contrôle l’arrêt environ toutes les 50 ms lorsqu’inactif ; polling devient exploitable à l’échéance. Inscription et hashing sont aussi coopératifs. Les méthodes sans Context utilisent context.Background pour compatibilité. Le statut natif 3 devient context.Canceled ou context.DeadlineExceeded. Le callback de demande est arrêté/joint avant libération de sa source ; une source neuve par appel empêche l’annulation tardive d’un appel suivant. Le résultat natif achevé prime sur une annulation après son dernier contrôle ; un contexte expiré avant exécution ne démarre pas l’opération. Wait/StatusContext peuvent abandonner l’attente de sérialisation workspace/Store à l’expiration. Ordre workspace puis Store, avec ordre stable des Stores pour copie différentielle. Close attend encore les appels actifs : les annuler avant fermeture. Ce pont context concerne seulement ouverture/attente/statut de workspace ; les autres méthodes Store gardent leur comportement propre. go Copier w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{}) if err != nil { return err } defer w.Close() s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key}) if err != nil { return err } report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true}) closeErr := s.Close() if err != nil { return err } if closeErr != nil { return closeErr } if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err } changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second) if err != nil { return err } // Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due. _ = changes Portée # C’est une observation consultative, pas un snapshot verrouillé ou un agent de transfert. Elle ne conserve pas les versions intermédiaires, ne maintient pas d’historique rejouable et n’expose pas le journal de checkpoint du CLI. Un redémarrage recrée l’inventaire et refait un scan complet. Causalité et transferts sont gérés au-dessus de cet observateur ; voir le contrôleur BEP continu et ses limites de dossiers, conflits et service. Contextes enregistrés # Utiliser syrnck_workspace_open_registered_ex(directory, store_path, name, options, cancellation, out) pour un workspace lié enregistré. Avant chaque statut, sélectionner le même contexte sur le Store ouvert par l’appelant via syrnck_store_use_workspace(store, name) . NULL ou vide sélectionne le principal. Un nom inconnu échoue sans changer la sélection ; une session/contexte différent échoue sans sortie périmée. Ne pas changer de sélection dans un callback de progression. Symboles additifs exigeant une bibliothèque ABI 1 reconstruite adaptée. Go utilise WorkspaceOptions{Name: \"edit\"} à l’ouverture puis store.UseWorkspace(\"edit\") avant statut. La session ne garde ni Store, clé ou verrou. Les notifications liées surveillent prudemment refs/config externes ; les audits complets périodiques restent nécessaires. Enregistrement et restauration portable sont exposés par le CLI et C++ , pas par des appels d’administration C/Go. Actions natives de workspace protégées # L’ABI C enrichie expose workspace_native_state_json_ex , workspace_capture_json_ex , workspace_checkout_plan_json_ex , workspace_checkout_owned_json_ex et workspace_recover_owned_json_ex . Les déclarations distribuées donnent les signatures complètes. Chaque appel vérifie le chemin canonique exact du Store, le contexte enregistré et le dossier de la session. Même un autre Store valide est refusé. Lire l’état d’abord : cible sélectionnée, référence réelle, révision typée ou null, HEAD, matérialisation, présence de staging et statut du checkout du contexte. Cette lecture ne reprend rien. SyrnckNativeExpected contient cible, révision nullable et ID nullable de l’objet de sélection partielle. NULL exige l’absence, ce n’est pas un joker. Capture, plan et checkout refusent toute cible, révision ou politique modifiée avant mutation. Ne pas actualiser l’attente d’une ancienne opération uniquement pour la faire passer. Capture conserve les fichiers sélectionnés par la transaction de commit native. Elle respecte fichiers ignorés, chemins non matérialisés, HEAD détaché et auteurs, et refuse staging/checkout en cours. Message limité à 64 Kio. Plan vérifie la fermeture cible et décrit sa faisabilité sans déplacer de référence ni modifier les octets de travail. Un plan bloqué est un rapport réussi avec ready:false . Checkout recontrôle tout et n’applique qu’à un workspace sélectionné propre, via le journal récupérable natif et un propriétaire explicite de 64 caractères hexadécimaux. La barrière de version de lecteur existante s’applique. Avant un checkout contrôlé par le service, conserver durablement propriétaire, candidat et entrée causale exacts. La reprise exige un journal actuellement en cours avec ces propriétaire, candidat, cible sélectionnée, contexte et dossier. Journal manuel/étranger ou absence de journal sont refusés. La reprise n’exige pas la clé privée ayant signé une cible déjà préparée ; objet et signature sont conservés sans modification. Propriété des sorties allouées et ordre de verrouillage workspace puis Store restent communs. Les tentatives de mutation invalident la continuité ; le prochain Status relit tous les octets sélectionnés. Annulation native coopérative ; une publication réussie l’emporte sur une annulation tardive. Un statut non nul peut suivre une publication ou laisser un journal reprenable : relire l’état et établir le résultat avant toute nouvelle tentative. Une sortie vide en erreur ne prouve aucune absence de modification. Fermer le Store avant l’attente réseau ou des notifications. Go expose NativeState / NativeStateContext , Capture / CaptureContext , PlanCheckout / PlanCheckoutContext , CheckoutOwned / CheckoutOwnedContext et RecoverOwned / RecoverOwnedContext sur Workspace. state.Expected() transporte l’attente exacte cible/révision/sélection. Capture rend l’ID ; plan, checkout et reprise rendent le json.RawMessage canonique. Les variantes Context gardent la passerelle d’annulation par appel. Elles n’implémentent pas le contrôleur BEP résident et n’annoncent pas automatiquement un dossier de travail à jour. syrnck_workspace_checkout_status_json_ex(workspace, store, transaction, operation, out, length) lit une transaction 64-hex exacte par le décodeur du cœur, sans reprise ni publication. Un reçu terminé rend pending=false et state=committed avec propriétaire/source/cible/branche/contexte/dossier originaux. La cible retenue correspondante et la liaison au workspace sont vérifiées ; la lecture d’un reçu terminé ne compare pas les arbres historiques. Ces faits prouvent une application passée, pas la propreté actuelle. Go l’expose par CheckoutStatusContext . Utiliser un propriétaire durable unique par application, conserver les entrées causales avant checkout et capturer les éditions locales ultérieures après reconnaissance de la base terminée. Préservation explicite des éditions actives # syrnck_workspace_recover_owned_preserving_json_ex garde le contrat exact Store/workspace/contexte/cible/candidat/propriétaire de la reprise ordinaire. Il archive explicitement les éditions de fichiers ordinaires, crée des copies de conflit exclusives et reprend le journal strict original. La reprise par défaut reste stricte. Go expose RecoverOwnedPreserving et RecoverOwnedPreservingContext ; C++ fournit Store::recover_checkout_owned_preserving_files . Le rapport JSON ajoute preserved_edits : chemin source, chemin de copie et ID de révision archivée. En erreur de publication, relire état natif et descripteurs conservés. Voir la politique et ses limites . Réutilisation des captures de session # Les captures répétées réutilisent leurs manifestes à empreintes locales fiables inchangées, avec inventaire complet et vérification de toute la fermeture. Le JSON ajoute les compteurs sources. Redémarrage, invalidation et échec natif effacent cette preuve ; l’index jetable de statut ne la remplace jamais. Voir capture et repli . /* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */ char *json = NULL; size_t length = 0; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &json, &length); /* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */ syrnck_store_close(store); if (result == 0) { consume_status(json, length - 1); /* consume while allocation is alive */ syrnck_string_free(json); } /* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */ static void stop_after_first_chunk(const SyrnckProgress *event, void *user) { if (event->phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event->bytes >= 1024 * 1024) syrnck_cancellation_request((SyrnckCancellation *)user); } /* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */ SyrnckOperation operation = {cancellation, stop_after_first_chunk, cancellation}; int result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &operation, &json, &length); /* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */ w, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{}) if err != nil { return err } defer w.Close() s, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key}) if err != nil { return err } report, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true}) closeErr := s.Close() if err != nil { return err } if closeErr != nil { return closeErr } if err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err } changes, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second) if err != nil { return err } // Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due. _ = changes","headings":[{"id":"embedded-workspace-observation-through-c-and-go","text":"Observation embarquée en C et Go","level":1},{"id":"lifetime-and-locking","text":"Durée de vie et verrouillage","level":2},{"id":"c-calls-and-output-ownership","text":"Appels C et propriété des sorties","level":2},{"id":"cooperative-cancellation-and-c-progress","text":"Annulation coopérative et progression C","level":2},{"id":"go-usage","text":"Utilisation Go","level":2},{"id":"scope-and-validation","text":"Portée","level":2},{"id":"registered-contexts","text":"Contextes enregistrés","level":2},{"id":"native-workspace-actions","text":"Actions natives de workspace protégées","level":2},{"id":"active-checkout-edits","text":"Préservation explicite des éditions actives","level":2},{"id":"incremental-capture","text":"Réutilisation des captures de session","level":2}],"anchors":["L1","L2","L3","L4","L5","L6","L7","L8","L9","L10","L11","code-2-L1","code-2-L2","code-2-L3","code-2-L4","code-2-L5","code-2-L6","code-2-L7","code-2-L8","code-2-L9","code-2-L10","code-3-L1","code-3-L2","code-3-L3","code-3-L4","code-3-L5","code-3-L6","code-3-L7","code-3-L8","code-3-L9","code-3-L10","code-3-L11","code-3-L12","code-3-L13","code-3-L14","code-3-L15","code-3-L16"],"languages":["c","go"],"code":"/* After opening a session and caller-owned Store: */\nchar *json = NULL;\nsize_t length = 0;\nint result = syrnck_workspace_status_json(workspace, store, NULL, 0, &json, &length);\n/* On failure, copy syrnck_last_error() before close/free/another ABI call. */\nsyrnck_store_close(store);\nif (result == 0) {\n    consume_status(json, length - 1); /* consume while allocation is alive */\n    syrnck_string_free(json);\n}\n/* The next wait holds no Store lock. Free its JSON result in the same way. */\nstatic void stop_after_first_chunk(const SyrnckProgress *event, void *user) {\n    if (event->phase == SYRNCK_PHASE_VERIFYING && event->bytes >= 1024 * 1024)\n        syrnck_cancellation_request((SyrnckCancellation *)user);\n}\n/* Keep cancellation and operation alive for the complete status call. */\nSyrnckOperation operation = {cancellation, stop_after_first_chunk, cancellation};\nint result = syrnck_workspace_status_json_ex(workspace, store, NULL, 0,\n                                            &operation, &json, &length);\n/* Inspect/copy diagnostics first. Close cancellation only after all request\n   threads have also returned. Free json only when the result owns it. */\nw, err := syrnck.OpenWorkspaceContext(ctx, project, storePath, syrnck.WorkspaceOptions{})\nif err != nil { return err }\ndefer w.Close()\n\ns, err := syrnck.Open(storePath, syrnck.Options{Key: key})\nif err != nil { return err }\nreport, err := w.StatusContext(ctx, s, syrnck.WorkspaceSelection{Combined: true})\ncloseErr := s.Close()\nif err != nil { return err }\nif closeErr != nil { return closeErr }\nif err := consumeStatus(report); err != nil { return err }\n\nchanges, err := w.Wait(ctx, time.Second)\nif err != nil { return err }\n// Reopen s and query again when changes.Changed(), or when an audit is due.\n_ = changes","assets":[],"links":["#/fr/syncthing/folder-controller","#/fr/native-workspaces","#/fr/syncthing/active-checkout-edits","#/fr/performance/incremental-capture"],"kind":"guide","revision":"4100b130e44d"}]};
